888, 853, 1991, 11652, 484, 665, 413, 3291, 11, 10112, 5862, 1215, 2846, 220, 667, 20, 581, 679, 3561, 1888, 3291, 30, 50557, 3561, 1888, 5862, 1215, 28, 667, 20, 382, 1888, 328, 8663, 220, 16, 316, 2846, 220, 667, 20, 12, 16, 314, 220, 667, 19, 8663, 31571, 591, 220, 667, 19, 1917, 316, 220, 16, 11, 813, 9560, 13, 2632, 625, 3291, 13, 623, 11085, 49895, 328, 3561, 1888, 6960, 21241, 22991, 2870, 220, 16, 326, 220, 667, 19, 10112, 1919, 1991, 11652, 3291, 30, 3072, 2928, 49895, 19919, 4410, 2236, 1991, 15448, 4410, 11652, 364, 84787, 47685, 7890, 19786, 364, 2167, 665, 37071, 138920, 18294, 25, 2214, 1215, 72554, 220, 667, 20, 11, 2454, 2914, 350, 87, 533, 575, 8, 395, 575, 306, 451, 382, 72554, 350, 87, 533, 220, 667, 20, 8, 659, 350, 667, 20, 533, 575, 8, 10112, 13, 16454, 364, 7943, 581, 665, 3587, 261, 4705, 10383, 25, 2214, 1062, 1374, 1215, 11, 290, 17786, 1432, 328, 3561, 1888, 382, 540, 5153, 290, 1888, 328, 17786, 4824, 328, 290, 1920, 1849, 5941, 350, 21910, 2454, 2914, 402, 3561, 382, 540, 5153, 472, 4410, 306, 17786, 1432, 472, 2454, 2914, 402, 1849, 11, 1261, 17889, 54286, 741, 4037, 86130, 395, 1215, 2870, 220, 16, 326, 220, 667, 20, 364, 49377, 87130, 920, 25, 12265, 451, 8895, 8663, 591, 220, 16, 485, 667, 19, 11, 326, 460, 8895, 220, 667, 20, 326, 220, 667, 21, 11, 290, 920, 451, 382, 261, 1982, 70664, 328, 354, 16, 35229, 11, 667, 19, 16255, 2214, 1062, 1215, 11, 581, 665, 10610, 2454, 1849, 12332, 483, 261, 16913, 3561, 12332, 484, 382, 20039, 316, 1215, 30, 4037, 35737, 364, 96308, 25, 7649, 80149, 25, 2214, 1062, 1374, 260, 11, 1022, 83, 533, 261, 91, 99731, 1022, 83, 533, 287, 91, 538, 261, 72554, 287, 326, 260, 72554, 350, 64, 76609, 11876, 17, 30, 1416, 8535, 16218, 364, 125072, 6960, 68524, 19545, 13031, 364, 198140, 19957, 2086, 139842, 314, 220, 667, 21, 364, 84787, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 8973, "words": 5566, "band_lo": 2783.0, "band_hi": 11132.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have an equation: (x-1)(x-2)...(x-2016) = (x-1)(x-2)...(x-2016). It's identical on both sides. We can erase some linear factors from both sides (i.e., remove same factor from each side?) Actually \"erase some linear factors from both sides so that each side still has at least one factor\". Means we can cross out some factors on left and some possibly different on right, but we must erase factors from both sides (like we can delete any subset of factors from LHS and any subset from RHS) as long as each side still has at least one factor remaining. After erasing, we get an equation of the form product of remaining factors on left = product of remaining factors on right. We want the resulting equation to have no real roots. Find minimal total number of factors erased.\n\nInterpretation: Initially both sides identical sets of 2016 factors. After erasing, we choose two (possibly different) subsets of indices to keep. The sides could have different multiplicities of particular factors.\n\nWe want resulting equation no real solution. That means the equation f(x)=g(x) where f and g are products of linear terms (with integer constants 1..2016) has no real x satisfying equality.\n\nWe can think of moving all to one side: f(x)-g(x)=0. But we want no real roots. Equivalent to f(x)≠g(x) for all real x.\n\nWe can't erase all factors; each side must have at least one factor.\n\nGoal: minimize total number of erased factors = (2016 - |L|) + (2016 - |R|) = 4032 - (|L|+|R|). So maximizing total remaining factors while still no real roots.\n\nWe need to choose subsets L,R of {1,...,2016} with nonempty, such that the equation ∏_{i∈L}(x-i) = ∏_{j∈R}(x-j) has no real solution.\n\nWe need to find maximal |L|+|R| under this condition. Then answer = 4032 - maxSum.\n\nObservation: Both sides are polynomials. For large |L|+|R|, likely there will be some solution due to continuity? Let's think.\n\nIf L=R, then identity holds for all x → infinite roots; not allowed. If L and R are different sets, maybe equality holds at some x.\n\nTrivial case: L contains one factor (x-a), R contains a different factor (x-b). Equation (x-a) = (x-b) => a = b, contradiction if a≠b then equation simplifies to x-a = x-b => -a = -b => a=b, false, thus no solution? Wait compute: (x-a) = (x-b) => x - a = x - b => subtract x both sides => -a = -b -> a=b. Since a≠b, this equality never holds for any x because both sides are linear with same slope; they are parallel lines with zero? Actually they are same slope 1, different intercepts, they never intersect. So no real solution. Good. So with one factor each, any distinct a,b yields no solution.\n\nThus we could keep 1 factor left, 1 factor right, distinct. That would erase 2015+2015=4030 factors. But perhaps we can keep more factors while still no solution.\n\nGoal maximize total remaining factors while still inequality for all real x.\n\nConsider L contains two factors (x-a)(x-c) and R contains one factor (x-b). Equation quadratic = linear. Might have up to 2 solutions; may have none depending on signs? Let's analyze possibilities.\n\nWe need product of left factor set equals product of right factor set; can we avoid intersection? Likely with more factors there will be some solution due to continuity and sign changes.\n\nWe can treat functions f(x) and g(x) as polynomials. Consider h(x)=f(x)-g(x). If degrees differ, as x→±∞, leading term dominates. For no real roots, h(x) must keep same sign for all real x (strictly >0 or <0). Is that possible? For polynomials of different degree, leading term sign dominates at infinities, but could there be sign change causing at least one root by intermediate value if sign differs at infinities? If leading term same sign both infinities (i.e., degree even and leading coefficient positive), could stay positive. But we need to check if it's possible without root.\n\nBut we can also consider that f and g share factors maybe; but they are distinct sets.\n\nSimplify: we can think of canceling common factors first. Since we are allowed to delete arbitrary factors, we could also delete some common factor from both sides; that doesn't affect equality. But we might want sets with no common factor because then equality would reduce to something else.\n\nBut we can keep common factors; they factor out. Suppose we keep common factors C (product). Then equation C * f1(x) = C * g1(x) => f1(x)=g1(x). So common factors cancel out (assuming not zero). So we might as well consider sets L,R after removing common elements (they have no intersection). Therefore we can assume L and R are disjoint.\n\nThus we need two disjoint nonempty subsets of {1..2016} such that ∏_{i∈L}(x-i) = ∏_{j∈R}(x-j) has no real solution.\n\nGoal maximize |L|+|R| with L,R disjoint nonempty.\n\nNow think about monotonicity: For x less than all indices (i.e., x<1), each factor (x-i) is negative (since i≥1). So each product's sign is (-1)^{|L|} and (-1)^{|R|}. Equality would require same sign because values are both negative or positive? Actually the magnitude may differ.\n\nSimilarly for x greater than all indices (>2016), each factor positive, product positive.\n\nConsider large x: both sides positive; equality can happen maybe.\n\nWe might use ordering to ensure one side always larger than other, e.g., one side always larger magnitude.\n\nObservation: If we take L consisting of the smallest k indices, and R consisting of the largest m indices, perhaps for all x, product left < product right (or >) thus no equality.\n\nWe need to guarantee inequality for all real x. Since both are continuous functions, if one is always > other, then fine.\n\nSo problem reduces to find largest total size of two disjoint subsets such that one product is always greater than other for all real x.\n\nBecause all factors are linear with zero at their index. The sign of each factor changes at x = i.\n\nLet's think about ordering: For x between some indices, some factors negative, some positive; product magnitude may vary.\n\nWe can perhaps use the fact that the sets a