y). Then h positive at both ends.\n\nThus choose L with even size greater than R size, and both subsets disjoint. So total number of kept factors = |L|+|R|, with |L| even > |R| maybe odd or even.\n\nTotal can be up to 2016 while maintaining |L| even > |R|.\n\nIf we take |L| = 2016 (i.e., keep all factors on left) and |R| = 0 not allowed (must have at least one). So cannot.\n\nWe need both nonempty.\n\nTake |L| = 2015 (odd), not even, fails sign same at infinities (since larger degree odd gives sign change). So need |L| be even and >|R|≥1.\n\nMaximum total with |L| even and >|R| and |L|+|R| ≤ 2016.\n\nWe can set |L| = 2014 (even), |R| = 2 (any). Then total = 2016. L even, bigger degree 2014 >2. h positive at both infinities (since leading term x^{2014} positive). Need to verify no zero in between. Likely not? Need to ensure.\n\nConsider L = {1,...,2014} and R = {2015,2016}. Then h(x) = ∏_{i=1}^{2014}(x-i) - (x-2015)(x-2016). For x between 2014 and 2015, left product small? Let's reason.\n\nAt x close to 2015, left product includes factor (x-2014) which is ~1, but also many others huge? Actually for x near 2015, each factor (x-i) for i up to 2014 is around 1 to 2015 - i (positive). So left product is huge (product of many numbers up to ~2015). Right product is small (~0)*(1) etc. So left >> right => h positive.\n\nAt x near say 0, left product includes many negative terms, overall sign maybe (-1)^{2014}= positive (even), magnitude huge? Actually product of 2014 terms large magnitude; right product is product of negative numbers roughly 2015*2016 positive as both negative? At x=0, left = (-1)(-2)...(-2014) = (-1)^{2014} * 2014! = 2014! positive. Right = (-2015)(-2016)= 2015*2016 positive. 2014! is astronomically larger than 2015*2016, so h positive. So seems h always positive.\n\nThus seems with left containing many factors and right just few large indices, h>0 always, no root.\n\nHence we can keep all 2016 factors? Wait we need both sides nonempty and disjoint. Partition with L size 2015 (even? 2015 odd) not suitable. Partition with L size 2014 even, R size 2, total 2016. This gives no common factors, fine.\n\nWe need to verify that equation has no real root; seems likely because left product dominates for all x.\n\nProof: Because L contains all small numbers up to 2014, which are <=2014, while right side contains only large numbers 2015,2016 > all elements of L. For any real x, consider the magnitude of each factor:\n\nFor any factor (x - a) with a in L, and (x - b) with b in R, we have b > a. For any x, the factor (x-b) is <= (x-a) if x is >= b? Let's analyze.\n\nTake any x. Since b > a, we have (x-b) = (x-a) - (b-a). So (x-b) = (x-a) - Δ where Δ>0. If (x-a) is positive large, subtracting positive makes it smaller, possibly negative if (x-a) small.\n\nThus magnitude of (x-b) ≤ magnitude of (x-a) may not hold for negative values. But perhaps product inequality holds.\n\nHowever numeric demonstration suggests left product > right product for all x. Let's test x > 2016: then all factors positive, left product includes many terms each larger than corresponding right factor? Actually right side has only two terms (x-2015)(x-2016) which are both less than many of left terms like (x-1) etc. So product left larger.\n\nFor x between say 1000, many left factors negative? For x less than 1, all left factors negative (even count => positive), right factors also negative (even two => positive). But each absolute value of left factor is larger? Let's compare magnitude: For x<1, (x - i) for i≥1 is ≤ (x - j) for j≥2015? Actually for x negative large magnitude, (x- i) ≈ x (large negative) similar for all i; but left product involves many more factors thus magnitude huge.\n\nThus likely left product dominates magnitude for all x. To make rigorous maybe using that for any x, |x - a| ≥ |x - b| for a ≤ b when |x| large? Not always if x between a and b.\n\nConsider x=2015. Then (x-2015)=0 making right product zero, left product includes factor (x-2014)=1 etc, non-zero => h>0. So equality impossible. At x=2015.5, (x-2015)=0.5, (x-2016)=-0.5. Right product = -0.25. Left product includes (x-2014)=1.5 etc, magnitude huge positive. So h positive.\n\nThus I think no root.\n\nThus we can retain all 2016 factors total (i.e., erase 4032-2016=2016). This seems minimal erasures.\n\nBut can we do better (i.e., erase fewer than 2016)? That would require total kept > 2016, impossible since each side can have at most 2016 factors, but total kept = left kept + right kept, which max is 4032 if we keep all on both sides, but we must avoid common factors. If we allow common factors we get roots at those points. So must have disjoint sets. So total kept ≤ 2016 (since each index can appear at most on one side). So erased ≥ 4032-2016 = 2016.\n\nThus minimal erased is at least 2016. We have a construction achieving exactly 2016 erased (keeping 2016 total). Therefore answer is 2016.\n\nDouble-check that the equation with L = {1,...,2014}, R = {2015,2016} indeed has no real root. Let's examine possibility of root where left product equals right product maybe when left product is small (like maybe near some root where left product zero). For those x equal to one of indices 1..2014, left product zero, right product ≠0 (since right indices are 2015,2016 not equal), so equality fails. For x = 2015 or 2016, right product zero, left product ≠0; equality fails. So zeros not solutions.\n\nCould there be x where both products equal non-zero? Since left product has many factors that can be small, maybe near x around 2015 we have left product small? Actually left product near x=2015 is product of numbers 1 to around 2015-1 = 2014 numbers ranging from 2014 down to 1, so huge. So not small. The minimum magnitude of left product likely occurs somewhere between 1 and 2014 maybe where many factors small? But still magnitude relatively large because many remaining large factors.\n\nThus equality seems impossible.\n\nWe can argue monotonic: For x ≤ 2015, each term (x - i) for i in L is ≤ (x - 2015) + (2015 - i) maybe. Hard.\n\nBut we can provide a simple argument: For any real x, the absolute value of left product is at least the product of absolute values of the two right terms (since each term on left is at least as large in absolute value as each term on right, when ordered appropriately). Not trivial for x between 1 and 2015.\n\nConsider splitting set: Since L contains numbers from 1..2014, and R contains 2015 and 2016, the set L is a superset of {1,...,2014}. For any x, we can pair each right factor with a distinct left factor that is closer to x? Not straightforward.\n\nAlternative: Use inequality: For any real t, |t - a| ≥ |t - b| if a ≤ b and t ≤ (a+b)/2? We can't guarantee.\n\nNevertheless likely rigorous proof exists.\n\nHence minimal number erased = 2016.\n\nThus answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 2016", "answer": 2016, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.4305109630186053e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999985694900602, "logp_correct": -1.4305109630186053e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6594878264347128, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3649556827777687, "frac_below_-5": 0.01395400759098013, "digit_lp_min": -3.168003559112549, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 8958, "lp_end": -0.002660308964550495, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -4.768360213347478e-06, "logp_nocot": -4.109584150835872, "p_nocot": 0.01641459910451384, "uplift": 4.109582720324909}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "263dd033-d636-4c26-b6b8-396519113929", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Let $n$ be a positive integer. We arrange $1+2+\\cdots+n$ circles in a triangle with $n$ rows, such that the $i^{\\text {th }}$ row contains exactly $i$ circles. The following figure shows the case $n=6$. In this triangle, a ninja-path is a sequence of circles obtained by repeatedly going from a circle to one of the two circles directly below it. In terms of $n$, find the largest value of $k$ such that if one circle from every row is coloured red, we can always find a ninja-path in which at least $k$ of the circles are red. Please provide the smallest value of $n$ for which $k = 11$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Let $n$ be a positive integer. We arrange $1+2+\\cdots+n$ circles in a triangle with $n$ rows, such that the $i^{\\text {th }}$ row contains exactly $i$ circles. The following figure shows the case $n=6$. In this triangle, a ninja-path is a sequence of circles obtained by repeatedly going from a circle to one of the two circles directly below it. In terms of $n$, find the largest value of $k$ such that if one circle from every row is coloured red, we can always find a ninja-path in which at least $k$ of the circles are red. Please provide the smallest value of $n$ for which $k = 11$.", "true_answer": 1024, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "263dd033-d636-4c26-b6b8-396519113929:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 679, 316, 18135, 290, 4792, 25, 41487, 328, 45131, 13585, 575, 314, 16, 485, 77, 13, 355, 112647, 73630, 382, 261, 3104, 8601, 591, 1236, 22005, 306, 2344, 5225, 30, 392, 47173, 328, 45131, 17747, 656, 45605, 2966, 591, 261, 22005, 316, 1001, 328, 290, 1920, 45131, 8516, 4895, 480, 3692, 2632, 1062, 3104, 328, 5517,