8, 8350, 25116, 17, 297, 16161, 16, 13, 26632, 15600, 22311, 25, 2454, 1058, 481, 665, 3503, 290, 9279, 1432, 30, 20064, 10383, 25, 395, 1062, 8441, 11, 3331, 15600, 87130, 328, 13585, 4122, 402, 5588, 261, 10853, 72554, 1236, 24313, 30, 17158, 1199, 177804, 55043, 402, 49900, 364, 40, 2411, 290, 11608, 382, 484, 481, 665, 31512, 13585, 1511, 8374, 7065, 316, 27742, 328, 1920, 25, 1354, 553, 540, 1645, 220, 17, 42923, 74, 12, 16, 92, 13585, 483, 197896, 72554, 372, 13, 13602, 316, 8397, 261, 13464, 328, 5517, 372, 10, 16, 11, 581, 1309, 2454, 261, 10853, 316, 413, 540, 1645, 220, 17, 42923, 74, 12, 16, 16255, 13602, 316, 717, 261, 10853, 4410, 481, 1309, 1991, 6217, 13585, 30, 2761, 122961, 19545, 25, 9024, 451, 413, 5517, 328, 37511, 2893, 613, 57252, 19874, 6975, 13, 7801, 1354, 553, 540, 1645, 220, 17, 42923, 43, 12, 16, 92, 13585, 13, 41351, 13686, 1534, 25, 290, 2349, 328, 261, 16281, 483, 197896, 72554, 451, 382, 540, 1645, 6958, 42600, 11075, 12, 16, 659, 3543, 81192, 4037, 3239, 364, 7943, 6960, 11608, 15248, 27742, 328, 1920, 25, 75745, 16281, 1919, 261, 10853, 382, 290, 4010, 328, 5153, 6933, 220, 16, 306, 15600, 22311, 328, 575, 350, 267, 3543, 8, 15286, 316, 197896, 314, 2969, 234, 232, 2133, 17, 297, 139819, 233, 659, 16, 364, 39520, 4792, 2713, 11, 6052, 220, 7672, 19, 7890, 120152, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 25, 220, 7672, 19, 13], "n_tokens": 8343, "words": 5618, "band_lo": 2809.0, "band_hi": 11236.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have to interpret the problem: triangle of circles rows i =1..n. A ninja-path is a path starting from some circle in top row? \"sequence of circles obtained by repeatedly going from a circle to one of the two circles directly below it.\" So any path of length n (including each row). So choose one circle from each row (a path). Equivalent to root-to-leaf path in a binary tree formed by triangle.\n\nWe color one circle per row red (so total n red circles, each in distinct rows, not necessarily forming a path). Need guarantee that there exists a path containing at least k red circles, regardless of which circles are colored (adversarial). Find largest k with this property, as function of n. Then ask smallest n where that k equals 11.\n\nSo we need to compute the Ramsey-type value: For any selection of one red per row, the maximum guaranteed minimal number of reds intersecting some root-to-leaf path. Equivalent to: For any n-element set with one per level, what's the minimal possible maximal intersection with a path? We want largest k such that guarantee at least k. So we need to find worst-case coloring minimizing the maximal overlap with any path, then k = that minimal maximal overlap + 1? Wait careful.\n\nDefine for a given coloring (set R of red circles, exactly one per row). For each path P (size n), define |P ∩ R|. Let M(R) = max_{paths P} |P ∩ R|. The guarantee is that for all R, M(R) ≥ k. So the largest k is the minimum over all R of M(R). So k = min_R max_path |intersection|.\n\nThus we need to compute function f(n) = min_R max_path overlap. This is classic: In binary tree of height n (rows), picking one red per level, we want minimal maximal number of reds on a root-leaf path. This reminiscent of Dilworth or pigeonhole? Might be floor(log2 n) + 1? Let's think.\n\nWe can think of each red selection per level is a node. The set R has exactly one node per depth (row). We consider all root-to-leaf paths, each selects one node per depth as well (the node on that path). The overlap count is number of depths where the red node coincides with the path node.\n\nGoal: choose reds to minimize the maximum coincidences with any leaf path.\n\nThis is similar to constructing an antichain? Not.\n\nInterpret binary strings of length n-1 maybe? Represent positions: at depth i (row i), there are i nodes (positions 1..i). But the branching is not symmetric binary tree because each node has two children: left and right, giving a Pascal triangle. This structure is equivalent to binomial coefficient lattice.\n\nAlternative approach: Since at each step you can go down left or right, each path corresponds to a sequence of choices (L/R) length n-1, ending at one of n nodes in bottom row (positions 1..n). The circle at row i along a given path corresponds to position equal to number of Right moves among first i-1 steps +1? Indeed.\n\nThus red selection per row is picking a column j_i where 1 ≤ j_i ≤ i.\n\nWe need to pick (j_i) such that for any binary sequence b_1...b_{n-1}, the number of i where j_i = 1 + (# of rights among first i-1) is minimized maximal.\n\nGoal: minimize maximum matches.\n\nObservation: As we go down, the possible positions spread. This resembles minimizing coincidences with any monotone path.\n\nPerhaps the optimal strategy is to choose red circles along a \"central\" diagonal to force any path to intersect them many times? Actually we as adversary want to minimize intersections, so we choose reds to be as \"far apart\" as possible across the tree, making it hard for any single path to hit many of them.\n\nThis looks similar to problem of finding width of \"sperner family\"? Not.\n\nWe need formula for f(n). Let's try small n compute.\n\nn=1: only one row, one red, path includes that circle, so M=1. f(1)=1.\n\nn=2: rows: top (row1) only 1 node, row2 has 2 nodes. We must pick red in row1 (that node) and red in row2 (choose left or right). Paths: two possible paths: left path goes through left node row2; right path through right node. The red in row1 is always intersected, plus maybe also row2 if matches. So max overlap is either 2 (if row2 red matches that path) else 1. Since we as adversary want to minimize max, we choose row2 red such that each path has at most 1? But there are two paths, we can pick row2 red to be left, then right path gets only row1 red =1, left path gets both =2, so max=2. Similarly any choice gives max=2. So M(R)=2. Thus f(2)=2.\n\nn=3: rows 1,2,3. Red choices: row1 forced, row2 choose left/right, row3 choose one of three positions. Need to find configuration minimizing the max matches across 4 paths? Actually there are 2^(2)=4 paths (binary choices length2). Let's brute think: maybe we can achieve max=2? Let's see if we can have any path intersect at most 2 reds. Since there are 3 reds total, pigeonhole maybe max at least ceil(3/2)=2? Could we guarantee 2? Need to see if we can make max=2 (cannot be 1 because top red always present). So f(3)=2? Let's test possible arrangement: pick row2 red = left, row3 red = rightmost (position3). Paths:\n\nPaths enumerated by sequence: LL (left-left) goes through positions: row1 pos1, row2 left (pos1), row3 leftmost (pos1). Overlap with reds: row1 yes, row2 yes, row3 no =>2.\n\nLR (left then right) positions row1 pos1, row2 left pos1, row3 middle pos2. reds: row1 yes, row2 yes, row3 no =>2.\n\nRL (right then left) positions row1 pos1, row2 right pos2, row3 middle pos2. reds: row1 yes, row2 no, row3 no =>1.\n\nRR: row1 pos1, row2 right pos2, row3 rightmost pos3. reds: row1 yes, row2 no, row3 yes =>2.\n\nMaximum =2. So M(R)=2. Could we get max=1? Not possible because row1 always intersect, that's 1. But need max across paths; could we have some path with only row1 red and no other? Possibly but other paths could have more. To have max=1, we would need every path intersect exactly row1 only (no other reds). But row2 red is at left or right, then that path that goes through that same node would have 2. So impossible. Therefore f(3)=2.\n\nn=4: Let's attempt compute minimally achievable max. There are total 4 reds. Could we arrange such that any path hits at most 2? maybe 3? Let's try to see lower bound: by pigeonhole principle, there are 2^{n-1} paths. Each red circle lies on some subset of paths (those that go through that node). Number of paths through a given node at row i (position j) equals C(i-1, j-1) * 2^{n-i}?? Actually from top to that node there are C(i-1, j-1) ways, from node to bottom there are 2^{n-i} (since after row i, each step splits). So each red lies on C(i-1, j-1) * 2^{n-i} paths. Sum over reds of coverage counts = total over rows of that. Since each path can be covered by multiple reds (the ones on that path). If we want to keep max coverage ≤ m, then total covered counts ≤ m * total paths.\n\nThus we can get lower bound from sum of coverages.\n\nLet total paths T=2^{n-1}. For each row i, we must select a node j_i. Its coverage count = C(i-1, j_i-1) * 2^{n-i} = 2^{n-i} * C(i-1, j_i-1). Sum S = Σ_{i=1..n} 2^{n-i} * C(i-1, j_i-1). Since we can choose j_i to minimize each term (prefer small binomial coefficient). Minimum binomial coefficient in row i is 1 (at edges j=1 or j=i). So minimal S_min = Σ 2^{n-i} * 1 = Σ_{k=0}^{n-1} 2^{k} = 2^{n} -1.\n\nThus any selection yields total coverage at least 2^{n} -1.\n\nIf each path can be covered at most m times, then S ≤ m * T = m * 2^{n-1}. Hence we need m ≥ S / 2^{n-1} ≥ (2^{n} -1) / 2^{n-1} = 2 - 2^{-(n-1)}. So m ≥ 2 - tiny => m ≥ 2 (since integer). So guarantee at least 2 reds on some path for any n≥? Looks like lower bound m ≥2. So f(n) ≥2 for all n≥2. But we already have f(3)=2, f(2)=2. For higher n, maybe larger lower bound? Let's improve bound using better selection maybe edges give minimal coverage. The total S_min = 2^{n} -1. To get m =2, need (2^{n} -1) ≤ 2 * 2^{n-1}= 2^{n}. Holds, since 2^{n} -1 < 2^{n}. So m could be 2; not forcing larger.\n\nBut perhaps there is stronger bound using that coverage at each level cannot all be at edges due to selection constraints? Actually we are free to choose edges each row: choose leftmost nodes all the time (j=1). That's permissible. Then each red lies on exactly 2^{n-i} paths (since C(i-1,0)=1). So S = Σ 2^{n-i} = 2^{n} -1. So maximum per path may be as low as? Let's test n=4 with all left edge reds. Compute overlaps: Paths are sequences of L/R. Any path will intersect red at row i if after first i-1 steps you have taken 0 rights (i.e., all L up to that point). Because leftmost node corresponds to j=1, which is reached only if you never go right before row i. Therefore only the all-left path hits all reds (4). Other paths intersect fewer. Let's compute maximum intersection: The path that goes left for first i-1 steps and then anything later? Actually if you take a right at step r, then at rows > r the position j will be >1, so you miss the left edge reds for those later rows. So the number of reds intersected equals the number of initial consecutive left moves plus 1 (row1 always). So worst case path is all left giving n reds. So M = n. That's large. Not minimal. So we need to choose reds to spread to minimize max. Perhaps choose alternating edges to balance? The edge choice leads to some path hitting many.\n\nGoal is to minimize maximal overlap, we can try to design reds such that any path can match at most about log2 n? Might be related to binary representation because each red corresponds to a prefix condition.\n\nObservation: each red at row i defines a set of paths determined by a binary string of length i-1 that leads to that node. That's exactly a prefix condition (the pattern of L/R moves to reach that node). For node j, the prefix corresponds to having exactly (j-1) rights among first i-1 moves, but also order matters. However each node corresponds to set of length i-1 sequences with exactly j-1 rights, not a single prefix. So many paths.\n\nThus the earlier coverage bound may be sharp that we can achieve maximum 2 with appropriate choice? Let's test small n=4 by trying to achieve max=2. Need arrangement such that any path hits at most 2 reds. Is it possible? Let's attempt search manually maybe.\n\nRows 1-4. Row1 red (forced). So each path already has 1 red. Need to ensure no path hits two more reds (i.e., at most one additional red). So we need the other three reds placed such that any path passes through at most one of them besides row1.\n\nThink of sets of paths through each red: for row i, node j corresponds to set of paths where among first i-1 steps you have exactly j-1 rights. That's a subset of size C(i-1, j-1) * 2^{n-i}.\n\nWe need to ensure that these subsets are pairwise disjoint (except can intersect at paths that also include row1 red which is all). Actually row1 red is in every path, ignore.\n\nThus we need to choose nodes so that sets of paths they define are disjoint to avoid a path containing two of them. Is that possible for n=4? Let's examine.\n\nRow2: two nodes. Their path sets: left node (j=1) corresponds to sequences with 0 rights in first1 step (i.e., first step L). That's half of paths (those starting L). Right node corresponds to sequences starting R (first step R). They are disjoint. Good.\n\nRow3: three nodes. Their sets correspond to sequences of first2 steps with 0,1,2 rights respectively. So strings: LL, LR/RL (two strings), RR. Each set includes all continuations. These three subsets partition the paths based on first two steps. Disjoint as well. Good.\n\nRow4: four nodes partition based on first three steps. Also disjoint.\n\nThus actually for each row, the subsets partition the entire set of paths based on prefix of length i-1. So they are disjoint across nodes of same row, but across different rows they overlap (since earlier prefix condition is coarser). For a path to include reds from two different rows, need that its prefix of length i-1 matches node at row i, and also prefix of length j-1 matches node at row j. That's possible because if the node at lower row corresponds to longer prefix, it refines earlier condition. So any path that matches a node at row i also matches exactly one node at each earlier row (the one containing its prefix). So if we select reds at multiple rows, they may be aligned along a chain (consistent prefixes) causing a path to hit multiple reds. To avoid multiple hits, need to ensure that reds are not aligned along any same path; i.e., for any path, at most one of the selected nodes (besides row1) lies on it.\n\nThat is like we need to select at most one node from each \"rooted chain\"? But each path defines a chain of nodes (one per row). To have at most one selected node besides row1, we need the selected nodes to be pairwise incomparable (no one is ancestor of another). This is a classic antichain problem in the binomial poset (Pascal triangle). The partial order: node a at (i,j) is ancestor of node b at (i',j') if i≤i' and j≤j' (since moving down-left/right). Indeed path corresponds to monotone nondecreasing column index.\n\nThus our problem reduces: choose one node per row (any), such that the antichain condition maybe not necessary; we as adversary want to minimize maximum hits, so we would like the chosen nodes to form an antichain (no two on same root-to-leaf path). But we must have exactly one per row, which forces some comparabilities because you have nodes across rows; any two nodes at different rows might be comparable (if column indices monotonic). Can we avoid comparability? For rows i<j, need j_i > j_j? Actually for two nodes (i,a) and (j,b) with i<j, they are comparable if a ≤ b (since you can move right to increase column). To be incomparable, need a > b (i.e., column index decreasing), which is impossible because column index cannot decrease along a path (you can stay same (left child) or increase by 1 (right child)). So for i<j, to be incomparable we need a > b, which is allowed? You can have node at higher row with larger column index than later row? Since column index cannot decrease as you go down, but we are just picking arbitrary nodes, not necessarily along same path; you can pick (i, larger column) and later row lower column smaller; they are incomparable (no path contains both) because moving down you cannot go left (decrease column). So we can choose decreasing column indices to avoid comparability.\n\nThus we can choose j_i decreasing with i to keep nodes antichain. Since j_i ∈ [1,i], we can start with j_1=1, then pick j_2=2? That's increase, comparable. Need decreasing: j_2 must be ≤1? Not possible. So we cannot have decreasing from row1 because j_1=1 is minimal. So any node at row2 will have column 1 or 2. If column 1, then comparable (ancestor) with node at row1 (since same column). So any choice will be comparable with row1 (inevitable). Therefore any path will contain at least row1 red and possibly more if later reds are in ancestors.\n\nThus we cannot avoid a path hitting more than one red, maybe we can limit to at most 2? We need to find maximum size of antichain with one per level given that top is fixed as ancestor to all. So the conflict is that row1 red is ancestor of all; thus any other red will be on same path as row1 regardless. So any path will have at least 2 reds (row1 + whichever red lies on its chain). But we can try to ensure that no path contains more than 2 reds, meaning the selected reds besides row1 are arranged so that any path contains at most one of them, i.e., the set of reds excluding row1 forms an antichain (no two comparable). Since comparability would imply a path hits both. So the problem reduces to: we need to select one node per row (excluding row1) so that the set of selected nodes forms an antichain. Is that possible? Since we need exactly one per each remaining row, we need n-1 nodes, one per row 2..n, all mutually incomparable. Is that feasible? In poset of binomial lattice, Dilworth's theorem: width = size of largest antichain = floor(n/2)+1? Actually the largest antichain in Pascal's triangle of size n rows is the middle row size which is C(n, floor(n/2)). That's huge. But we also have restriction of at most one per row; need n-1 nodes each from distinct rows but incomparable. Are there n-1 rows that can be selected as an antichain? Since antichain cannot have two nodes from same row because nodes in same row are incomparable? Actually nodes in same row are incomparable (they are not comparable via ≤ because column difference but row same). So antichain can include at most one per row automatically? No they could include multiple in same row because not comparable, but our restriction is exactly one per row.\n\nQuestion: Can we have one node per row 2..n such that no two are comparable? This is equivalent to constructing a chain-free transversal. In binomial poset, this is possible: choose nodes on \"alternating\" edges maybe j_i = i (rightmost) for even rows and j_i=1 for odd rows? Let's test comparability: Node (i,i) (rightmost) is comparable to (j,j) if i≤j (since columns increasing). Indeed (i,i) ≤ (j,j) for i≤j because you can always go right each step. So all rightmost nodes form a chain, not antichain. Similarly leftmost nodes form chain.\n\nNeed to pick sequence of column indices that sometimes increase, sometimes decrease to break comparability. For two nodes (i,a) and (j,b) with i<j, they are comparable if a ≤ b (since can increase). So to be incomparable we need a > b. Therefore the column index must strictly decrease as row index increases. So we need a decreasing sequence a_i for i increasing. Starting at row2, a_2 can be at most 2, but must be > a_3 > a_4 ... > a_n ≥1. That's possible only for at most a_2 distinct decreasing steps limited by a_2 value. Since a_2 ≤2, the maximum length of strictly decreasing sequence starting ≤2 is at most 2 (e.g., 2,1). So we can have at most 2 rows besides row1 with incomparable reds. Thus we cannot achieve antichain of size n-1 for n>3. So the maximum we can avoid comparisons is small.\n\nThus we can bound the maximal number of reds on a path at least something like floor(log2 n)+? Need to compute minimal maximal overlap.\n\nWe can think of building a chain of comparabilities: Since the column index must eventually decrease, the longest decreasing sequence length is limited by starting column. The starting column at row2 can be at most 2, giving at most 2 reds after row1 that are mutually incomparable? Actually we can have chain where each later red is ancestor of previous? Wait we want to minimize max per path, so we want to avoid long chains (since a chain would be present in a single path). The poset property indicates that any two reds with i<j and a_i ≤ a_j are comparable and lie on same path. So to limit chain length, we need to have many instances where a_i > a_j (decrease). But since a_i cannot drop below 1, after we go down to 1 cannot decrease further, so chain will start again? Let's think in terms of constructing selection to keep longest chain length minimal. This is reminiscent of Dilworth: the poset has dimension? The minimum possible size of the largest chain in a transversal of size n maybe ceil(log2 n)+1.\n\nBetter approach: Equivalent to binary representation of numbers? Consider representing each red at row i as a binary word of length i-1 (the set of prefixes that lead to it). Two reds are comparable if the earlier word is a prefix of some longer word? Actually not exactly, but maybe we can map to intervals.\n\nAlternative: This is the classic problem of \"Erdős–Szekeres\" monotone subsequence: In any sequence of length n of numbers (here column indices j_i), there exists a monotone (nondecreasing) subsequence of length at least √n? Actually Erdős–Szekeres says any sequence of length (r-1)(s-1)+1 contains increasing subsequence length r or decreasing length s. Here we have column indices bounded by i (max), not arbitrary. But we want longest nondecreasing subsequence corresponds to chain where path includes those reds. Since row1 is column 1, which is minimal, we already have nondecreasing from row1 onward as long as j_i are nondecreasing (or stay). The maximal size of a monotone nondecreasing subsequence of a sequence of length n with entries bounded by their position? Not exactly.\n\nWe can think of the following: For any selection (j_i), consider longest nondecreasing subsequence of indices i where j_i does not decrease. That subsequence corresponds to a chain (path) containing those reds. Since we must include row1, the longest nondecreasing subsequence length is the max number of reds on a path. So the problem reduces to: given a sequence a_i (i=1..n) where a_i ∈ [1,i], what is the minimum possible length of its longest nondecreasing subsequence? +? Actually we also consider monotone decreasing subsequence could also be chain? Because comparability condition is a_i ≤ a_j for i<j leads to chain; if a_i > a_j, they are incomparable, not on same path. So path can only include reds where indices are nondecreasing. So longest nondecreasing subsequence length L gives path with L reds. Therefore M(R) = L (since we can choose path matching those). So we need minimal possible L over sequences a_i ∈ [1,i] with each level exactly one red.\n\nThus f(n) = min_{a_i} L_n where L_n = length of longest nondecreasing subsequence of (a_i).\n\nNow this is a known problem: Given constraints a_i ≤ i, we can try to keep the sequence \"oscillating\" to avoid long nondecreasing subsequence. The classic Erdos–Szekeres result says any sequence of length (r-1)(s-1)+1 contains increasing subsequence of length r or decreasing of length s. Here we allow decreasing (incomparable) but decreasing subsequence not helpful, we only care about increasing. So to bound minimal possible length of longest increasing subsequence, we can construct a sequence with longest increasing subsequence of size about √n (using permutations). The classic result: There exists a permutation of length n with longest increasing subsequence length about √n (by constructing decreasing blocks). But our values a_i are not a permutation, they are limited.\n\nWe need to construct a_i such that L is minimized. A known construction: Write numbers in decreasing blocks of size k: sequence k,k-1,...,1, 2k,2k-1,...,k+1, etc yields L = k. For n = k^2 we get L = k. Because each block is decreasing of size k, and there are k blocks, forming sequence of length k^2, longest increasing subsequence length = k.\n\nIn our case, we also have constraint a_i ≤ i, which is automatically satisfied if we set a_i = some numbers maybe > i? Not allowed. In block construction, numbers increase overall; after first block ends at 1, next block starts at k+1 which might be > index position? Let's test: for i= k+1 (the start of second block), we need a_{k+1}=k+1 which is ≤ i = k+1, okay. At each position, the value equals something ≤ i typically because we are using numbers up to n. So this construction satisfies a_i ≤ i trivially because we can set a_i = i map? Wait a_i = some permutation of {1..n} satisfies a_i ≤ n but may exceed i for early positions (e.g., at i=1 we cannot set a_1=5). So constraint is stricter: a_i cannot be greater than i. So the block decreasing construction may violate early positions where value > i. To respect constraint, we need a_i ≤ i. So we can only use values up to current index, which suggests natural construction: let a_i = i mod k plus something? Actually to keep decreasing within each block, need large values early; but can't exceed i. Thus we can mimic the block construction by using values equal to the position within block relative to block start: e.g., first block of size k: set a_i = k - (i-1) (i=1..k) gives decreasing from k to 1, each ≤ i? Let's check i=1 gives a_1=k which must be ≤1 => impossible unless k=1. So cannot start with large value at early rows. So the decreasing must start with small numbers early, cannot have large numbers early.\n\nThus constraint heavily forces sequence to be nondecreasing initially, because a_i cannot exceed i, and a_{i+1} can be at most i+1, but could be smaller than a_i (decrease). So you can decrease, but only down to 1. The maximum you can increase per step is +1? Since a_{i+1} ≤ i+1, and a_i ≤ i, the difference a_{i+1} - a_i ≤ (i+1) - a_i, which could be more than 1 if a_i small. E.g., a_i=1, then a_{i+1} can be up to i+1 (big). So big jumps upward are allowed later.\n\nThus we can construct sequence that stays low early, then jumps high later, then decreasing etc. This could keep longest nondecreasing subsequence length small? Since we can have big jump yields many larger numbers later, which could produce longer increasing subsequence.\n\nGoal: Minimize longest nondecreasing subsequence length. Equivalent to constructing sequence with small patience sorting heap size.\n\nI suspect the minimal longest increasing subsequence length equals ceil(log2 n)+1 perhaps.\n\nWe can test for n=3 gave f=2, log2(3)=1.58 ceil+? ceil(log2 n)+1 = 3? Not match.\n\nMaybe f(n) = floor(log2 n)+1? For n=3, floor log2=1, +1=2 matches. For n=2: floor log2 2 =1 +1=2 matches. For n=4: floor log2 4=2 +1=3? Let's test if we can achieve max=2? Let's attempt compute if minimal possible longest nondecreasing subsequence length for n=4 maybe is 3? Let's try to construct sequence a_i to keep L=2.\n\nWe need a sequence of length 4 with no increasing subsequence length 3. Classic example is 2,1,4,3 which has LIS length 2. Does it satisfy a_i ≤ i? Check: i=1 a1=2 >1 invalid. So can't. Let's try sequence 1,2,1,2? This has LIS length 2? Actually 1,2 then maybe 1,2 increasing? the subsequence 1,2 (first two) length2, also 1,2 (positions 1,4) length2. No length3. Check constraints: a1=1 ≤1 good. a2=2 ≤2 good. a3=1 ≤3 good. a4=2 ≤4 good. So this works! So we have maximal LIS length 2 for n=4. Therefore f(4)=2? Let's see if any path can get at most 2 reds. Let's compute using column indices: rows: (1,1), (2,2), (3,1), (4,2). Let's test path that picks reds: need to find a path with three reds? Row1 always; Row2 red at column2 (rightmost). Row3 red column1 (leftmost). Row4 red column2. Can there be a path containing row1, row2, row4 reds? For row2 column2 we must have first step right. Then at row3 column? To stay on same path, row3 column could be 2 or 3 (since after right you can stay or go right again). But red at row3 column1 not on path, so path does not get that red. At row4 column2 is reachable: after being at column2 at row2, you could go left (stay column2) then right? Actually staying left child keep column same (j). From column2 at row2, left child at row3 is column2, right child column3. Then at row4, to get column2, you need to have taken left child at row3 (staying column2) then left child again? Wait need to compute: Starting at top column1 (row1). Move right to column2 row2. At row3, if you go left (stay column2), you are at column2. Then at row4, left again gives column2. So indeed there exists a path (R, L, L) that goes through row2 col2, row3 col2, row4 col2. This path includes reds at rows1,2,4 (since row4 red col2). That's 3 reds! Did we miss that row3 red col1 not on path, that's okay. So path length includes 3 reds >2. So our earlier mapping to LIS may not directly equal path reds because path includes all rows, not just subsequence. The LIS corresponds to a subsequence (not necessarily consecutive rows). But a path must include exactly one node per row, so if we have red at row i with column a_i, and we have another red at later row j with column a_j ≥ a_i (nondecreasing), then there exists a path that passes through both (since you can choose moves to achieve column indices consistent). So any nondecreasing subsequence yields a path containing those reds. So to keep max reds small, we need to limit length of any nondecreasing subsequence. However the path may also include reds where column indices are decreasing relative to previous red, but then they cannot be on same path because column can't decrease. So you cannot have reds with decreasing column in same path.\n\nThus longest nondecreasing subsequence length L gives lower bound on maximum reds on some path (≥ L). And it's achievable: there exists a path containing exactly those L reds (and perhaps other reds where column equal? Wait if equal also nondecreasing). So M(R) = L? Actually there may be other reds that also lie on that path whose columns also fit, but those would form another nondecreasing subsequence, making longer length. So M(R) = length of longest nondecreasing subsequence. Good.\n\nThus our constructed sequence 1,2,1,2 has LIS length 2. So any path can have at most 2 reds. Yet earlier we found a path with 3 reds? Let's verify that the red at row4 column2 and row2 column2 are both equal (nondecreasing). Sequence rows: (1,1) red row1 column1, (2,2) red, (3,1) red, (4,2) red.\n\nLongest nondecreasing subsequence: pick rows 1 (col1),2(col2),4(col2) gives 1≤2≤2, length 3! Oops I missed that; it's nondecreasing as well. Indeed 1,2,2 is nondecreasing. So LIS length is 3 not 2. Because we can select rows 1,2,4. So our earlier calculation of LIS incorrectly. Check optimal LIS of sequence 1,2,1,2 is 3. So cannot have LIS 2.\n\nThus min possible LIS for n=4 appears to be 3? Let's test other sequences. Need sequence a_i within limits that avoids length-3 increasing subsequence. Known minimal LIS for permutations length 4 is 2 (like 2,1,4,3). But with a_i ≤ i restriction, maybe minimal is 3.\n\nWe can try to search manually. Need a_i values such that any three indices i<j<k have not nondecreasing a_i ≤ a_j ≤ a_k. Equivalent to with three indices at least one decrease.\n\nTry sequence: 1,1,2,1? Values: a1=1, a2=1, a3=2, a4=1. Check LIS: we can take 1,1,2 length3 (rows1,2,3). So 3.\n\nTry 1,2,2,1: LIS can be 1,2,2 length3.\n\nTry 1,1,1,2: LIS=1,1,1,2 length4? Actually 1,1,1,2 is nondecreasing of length4.\n\nThus seems LIS≥3 for n=4. So f(4)=3? Let's test lower bound: using pigeonhole earlier gave only 2. But maybe tighter bound.\n\nWe can try to compute minimal possible maximum reds k_n for n.\n\nWe have condition a_i ≤ i. The minimal LIS length maybe equals floor(log2 n)+1? Let's test n=4 gives floor(log2 4)=2+1=3 matches. n=3 gives floor(log2 3)=1+1=2 matches. n=2 gives floor(log2 2)=1+1=2 matches. n=5: floor(log2 5)=2+1=3. Let's see if we can achieve LIS=3 for n=5? Perhaps can't be less than 3. Could we achieve LIS=3? Likely yes. Then k=3 for n up to 7 maybe? Let's test n=6: floor(log2 6)=2+1=3. But maybe achievable LIS=3 still, not 4. For n=8: floor(log2 8)=3+1=4.\n\nThus formula maybe k = ⌊log2 n⌋ + 1.\n\nWe need smallest n where k =11 => floor(log2 n) +1 =11 => floor(log2 n) =10 => 2^{10} ≤ n < 2^{11}. So n in [1024, 2047]. Smallest n = 1024.\n\nCheck if this aligns with earlier small n values: n=1 => floor(log2 1)=0+1=1 matches f(1). Good.\n\nThus answer likely 1024.\n\nWe should ensure that construction achieving LIS = floor(log2 n)+1 exists with the constraint a_i ≤ i, and that lower bound holds (cannot be less). Lower bound: by Dilworth/Erdos–Szekeres, any sequence of length n with values in [1,n] has increasing subsequence length at least √n. But with our extra constraint, we can get better bound? Need to prove lower bound of floor(log2 n)+1. Consider binary representation: each time you can double the allowed value? Another argument: for any selection, consider binary splitting of rows based on whether a_i ≤ some threshold? Maybe use pigeonhole on intervals.\n\nI think the bound is that you can partition rows into levels according to powers of two: there are at most 2^{k-1} rows with LIS ≤ k. Because to avoid a chain of length k+1, we need each a_i to be at most 2^{k-1}. Because to get a_i large you need many prior rows? Inductive proof: Let L be length of longest nondecreasing subsequence. Then there are at most 2^{L-1} rows. Indeed classic result: the size of a sequence with LIS ≤ L is at most binomial(L-1 + something?). Not sure.\n\nBut likely bound matches powers of two: constructing sequence where a_i is the position of least significant 1 in binary representation of i (or something) leads to LIS = ⌊log2 n⌋ +1.\n\nGiven problem style, answer 1024 seems plausible.\n\nThus final answer: 1024.", "response": "ANSWER: 1024", "answer": 1024, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.4305104514278355e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999985694905718, "logp_correct": -1.4305104514278355e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6498130024432472, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3544499058773543, "frac_below_-5": 0.010086455331412104, "digit_lp_min": -2.463940382003784, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 8328, "lp_end": -1.1324817933200393e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -4.8040190449682996e-05, "logp_nocot": -14.185515701770782, "p_nocot": 6.907307776427472e-07, "uplift": 14.185514271260331}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "338e47b1-f1a6-4109-8657-07769c364dcb", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Determine the largest integer $N$ for which there exists a $6 \\times N$ table $T$ that has the following properties:\n\n- Every column contains the numbers $1, 2, \\ldots, 6$ in some ordering.\n- For any two columns $i \\ne j$, there exists a row $r$ such that $T(r,i) = T(r,j)$.\n- For any two columns $i \\ne j$, there exists a row $s$ such that $T(s,i) \\ne T(s,j)$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Determine the largest integer $N$ for which there exists a $6 \\times N$ table $T$ that has the following properties:\n\n- Every column contains the numbers $1, 2, \\ldots, 6$ in some ordering.\n- For any two columns $i \\ne j$, there exists a row $r$ such that $T(r,i) = T(r,j)$.\n- For any two columns $i \\ne j$, there exists a row $s$ such that $T(s,i) \\ne T(s,j)$.", "true_answer": 120, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "338e47b1-f1a6-4109-8657-07769c364dcb:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 10574, 478, 2238, 484, 1354, 13031, 261, 220, 21, 87, 45, 12248, 483, 2454, 6000, 2447, 261, 135200, 328, 220, 16, 485, 21, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 261, 28974, 6000, 741, 30255, 25, 2214, 1062, 1920, 16913, 15697, 575, 22712, 11, 1354, 382, 540, 5153, 1001, 5225, 1919, 1023, 679, 2684, 7251, 350, 786, 1023, 10758, 22991, 936, 326, 1354, 382, 540, 5153, 1001, 5225, 1919, 1023, 2171, 350, 786, 625, 32820, 15697, 741, 65418, 15697, 2804, 413, 16913, 174595, 11, 326, 2454, 10610, 2804, 10758, 306, 540, 5153, 1001, 4010, 889, 1217, 2171, 306, 540, 5153, 1001, 4010, 364, 5808, 581, 1309, 290, 10562, 2349, 328, 261, 3502, 328, 174595, 328, 723, 21, 60, 483, 487, 62981, 9324, 2870, 1062, 1920, 174595, 2447, 540, 5153, 220, 16, 350, 127306, 8, 326, 540, 1645, 220, 20, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 1023, 6284, 413, 9141, 2647, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 9324, 220, 21, 8, 2236, 1023, 2804, 679, 540, 5153, 1001, 30380, 12629, 741, 2632, 290, 7060, 382, 25, 3004, 1920, 174595, 553, 9141, 829, 69164, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 679, 860, 13213, 2438, 306, 5355, 741, 197203, 316, 174595, 2447, 625, 261, 1227, 58322, 328, 2454, 1273, 350, 3086, 27524, 328, 1001, 483, 53743, 328, 290, 1273, 853, 860, 13213, 5571, 741, 2632, 581, 1309, 10574, 920, 328, 174595, 2238, 484, 395, 1062, 1920, 174595, 3669, 11, 2244, 11, 290, 135200, 3669, 183239, 16, 92, 2244, 853, 540, 5153, 1001, 13213, 2438, 13, 2632, 581, 1682, 10574, 3502, 328, 174595, 328, 336, 62, 21, 483, 10610, 7480, 4566, 540, 5153, 1001, 5355, 2438, 364, 2500, 382, 261, 13686, 4792, 25, 10574, 2349, 328, 261, 3502, 328, 174595, 328, 723, 77, 60, 483, 10610, 7480, 64353, 289, 4322, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 1023, 10758, 540, 1236, 4010, 741, 1328, 382, 5542, 472, 290, 104051, 53922, 1585, 33185, 1585, 49, 1064, 177750, 395, 174595, 13, 623, 65414, 64353, 289, 3502, 328, 174595, 328, 723, 77, 60, 853, 2349, 350, 77, 12, 16, 8, 22683, 2214, 297, 99731, 220, 17, 13, 1958, 290, 1606, 8771, 280, 11512, 553, 290, 8974, 1719, 328, 2438, 23666, 24223, 25, 722, 174595, 56239, 261, 5024, 2438, 13, 350, 2653, 297, 28, 21, 11, 350, 21, 12, 16, 63426, 314, 220, 6106, 8476, 5551, 7477, 581, 1217, 1841, 484, 1062, 1920, 174595, 553, 625, 32820, 30, 50557, 1023, 2804, 1217, 2171, 22991, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 625, 32820, 13, 623, 64353, 289, 7060, 1481, 3763, 2684, 135200, 7598, 4238, 889, 581, 1606, 3331, 16913, 15697, 13, 1416, 1309, 16913, 15697, 11, 14531, 13, 2632, 581, 1309, 261, 3502, 328, 16913, 174595, 483, 64353, 289, 4322, 13, 121064, 2349, 382, 350, 77, 12, 16, 63426, 314, 220, 6106, 13, 3072, 1217, 581, 1309, 484, 1062, 1920, 15697, 2171, 22991, 11, 1118, 382, 22981, 538, 15697, 553, 16913, 13, 2632, 290, 10562, 478, 1757, 413, 220, 6106, 364, 7943, 1309, 316, 14866, 484, 581, 665, 679, 220, 6106, 15697, 11, 2454, 261, 135200, 328, 220, 16, 485, 21, 11, 2238, 484, 1062, 1920, 10758, 22991, 13, 4569, 382, 9707, 290, 23666, 4492, 328, 261, 2438, 350, 64494, 174595, 1919, 3577, 220, 16, 24267, 316, 220, 16, 8, 8967, 220, 20, 0, 314, 220, 6106, 174595, 13, 3164, 722, 9295, 4010, 220, 16, 350, 843, 220, 16, 8, 29702, 220, 16, 306, 2454, 6000, 13, 2632, 1062, 1920, 15697, 679, 2684, 7251, 350, 16, 8, 306, 5225, 16, 13, 3164, 1217, 2171, 22991, 2236, 174595, 553, 16913, 13, 2632, 478, 665, 413, 220, 6106, 364, 7943, 665, 581, 679, 12384, 1572, 220, 6106, 30, 623, 457, 67815, 395, 174595, 5003, 10562, 64353, 289, 3502, 2349, 382, 350, 77, 12, 16, 8, 27942, 12604, 297, 28, 17, 11, 18, 10112, 1236, 39181, 30, 2214, 297, 87319, 17, 1354, 382, 47685, 326, 105230, 395, 297, 29, 17, 13, 2214, 297, 28, 21, 11, 350, 77, 12, 16, 63426, 314, 220, 6106, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 6106, 364, 4383, 7060, 25, 2454, 6000, 8895, 8663, 220, 16, 485, 21, 350, 543, 92896, 741, 11377, 13, 18215, 16, 1023, 722, 679, 220, 16, 13, 2632, 7060, 220, 17, 23121, 13, 30255, 220, 18, 25, 16913, 15697, 16218, 540, 5153, 1001, 5225, 1919, 1023, 2171, 11, 22476, 1023, 2171, 2236, 174595, 553, 16913, 26, 2023, 1023, 413, 2684, 306, 722, 13585, 30, 860, 364, 84787, 10562, 478, 28, 6106, 364, 16577, 25, 30456, 1354, 413, 261, 3502, 12384, 484, 6664, 174595, 625, 722, 9295, 2684, 2438, 889, 2928, 64353, 10610, 7480, 30, 16344, 316, 457, 67815, 177750, 11, 10562, 382, 220, 6106, 13, 2632, 6052, 364, 84787, 4733, 364], "n_tokens": 780, "words": 531, "band_lo": 276.75, "band_hi": 1107.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need largest N such that there exists a 6xN matrix with each column being a permutation of 1..6 (i.e., a Latin column). Condition: For any two distinct columns i,j, there is at least one row where they have same entry (so they agree somewhere), and there is at least one row where they differ (so not identical columns). Basically columns must be distinct permutations, and each pair must agree in at least one position but also differ in at least one position.\n\nSo we need the maximum size of a family of permutations of [6] with Hamming distance between any two permutations being at least 1 (different) and at most 5 (i.e., they cannot be completely different (i.e., distance 6) because they must have at least one coordinate equal). So the condition is: No two permutations are completely disjoint (i.e., have no fixed point in common). Equivalent to permutations being not a derangement of each other (the composition of one with inverse of the other has no fixed points). So we need largest set of permutations such that for any two permutations σ, τ, the permutation σ^{-1} τ has at least one fixed point. So we want largest family of permutations of S_6 with pairwise having at least one common point.\n\nThis is a classic problem: largest size of a family of permutations of [n] with pairwise intersecting property (i.e., they agree at some position). This is known as the Erdős–Ko–Rado theorem for permutations. The maximal intersecting family of permutations of [n] has size (n-1)!. For n ≥ 2. And the only extremal families are the cosets of point stabilizers: all permutations fixing a particular point. (For n=6, (6-1)! = 120.) However note we also require that any two permutations are not identical? Actually they must also differ somewhere, i.e., not identical. The intersecting condition would allow same permutation multiple times but we only consider distinct columns. We need distinct columns, yes. So we need a family of distinct permutations with intersecting property. Largest size is (n-1)! = 120. But also we need that any two columns differ somewhere, which is automatic if columns are distinct. So the maximum N should be 120.\n\nBut need to verify that we can have 120 columns, each a permutation of 1..6, such that any two agree somewhere. That is exactly the stabilizer of a point (say permutations where element 1 maps to 1) gives 5! = 120 permutations. They all fix position 1 (row 1) equals 1 in each column. So any two columns have same entry (1) in row1. They also differ somewhere because permutations are distinct. So N can be 120.\n\nBut can we have larger than 120? The EKR for permutations says maximum intersecting family size is (n-1)!, unless n=2,3 maybe some exceptions? For n≥2 there is equality and uniqueness for n>2. For n=6, (n-1)! = 120.\n\nThus answer: 120.\n\nCheck condition: each column contains numbers 1..6 (permutation). Yes. Row1 they all have 1. So condition 2 satisfied. Condition 3: distinct columns guarantee at least one row where they differ, indeed they differ because permutations are distinct; could they be same in all rows? no.\n\nThus maximum N=120.\n\nEdge: Could there be a family larger that includes permutations not all fix same point but still intersect pairwise? According to EKR theorem, maximum is 120. So answer.\n\nThus output.", "response": "ANSWER: 120", "answer": 120, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.4662663716080715e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999853374437803, "logp_correct": -1.4662663716080715e-05, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6124682834238738, "cot_lp_min64": -1.100628744968109, "frac_below_-5": 0.007832898172323759, "digit_lp_min": -2.2404654026031494, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.005233753472566605, "n_cot_tokens": 766, "lp_end": -3.135155202471651e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -9.059865078597795e-06, "logp_nocot": -22.519163608550002, "p_nocot": 1.6597837476636134e-10, "uplift": 22.519148945886286}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "5b5665ef-cc5c-43f0-aec2-e846dd0758f9", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Let $\\mathcal{A}$ denote the set of all polynomials in three variables $x, y, z$ with integer coefficients. Let $\\mathcal{B}$ denote the subset of $\\mathcal{A}$ formed by all polynomials which can be expressed as:\n\\[\n(x + y + z)P(x, y, z) + (xy + yz + zx)Q(x, y, z) + xyzR(x, y, z)\n\\]\nwith $P, Q, R \\in \\mathcal{A}$. Find the smallest non-negative integer $n$ such that $x^i y^j z^k \\in \\mathcal{B}$ for all non-negative integers $i, j, k$ satisfying $i + j + k \\geq n$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Let $\\mathcal{A}$ denote the set of all polynomials in three variables $x, y, z$ with integer coefficients. Let $\\mathcal{B}$ denote the subset of $\\mathcal{A}$ formed by all polynomials which can be expressed as:\n\\[\n(x + y + z)P(x, y, z) + (xy + yz + zx)Q(x, y, z) + xyzR(x, y, z)\n\\]\nwith $P, Q, R \\in \\mathcal{A}$. Find the smallest non-negative integer $n$ such that $x^i y^j z^k \\in \\mathcal{B}$ for all non-negative integers $i, j, k$ satisfying $i + j + k \\geq n$.", "true_answer": 4, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "5b5665ef-cc5c-43f0-aec2-e846dd0758f9:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 3331, 290, 4918, 418, 13768, 656, 9064, 13768, 656, 123676, 73121, 265, 16, 314, 1215, 102753, 10, 89, 11, 265, 17, 314, 53634, 10, 26947, 10, 141497, 11, 265, 18, 314, 82501, 1072, 1489, 17295, 16103, 54920, 1651, 623, 920, 418, 382, 290, 9064, 357, 314, 350, 82, 16, 11, 265, 17, 11, 265, 18, 741, 2632, 418, 314, 357, 13, 623, 4928, 25, 9764, 42173, 297, 2238, 484, 1062, 1767, 42600, 1215, 61, 72, 342, 61, 73, 579, 61, 74, 483, 3609, 9882, 5064, 297, 24384, 306, 290, 9064, 357, 364, 5808, 581, 1309, 290, 392, 98659