261, 220, 17, 61, 74, 1424, 220, 17, 74, 44, 13, 21926, 5542, 472, 12421, 2011, 394, 328, 58845, 350, 2878, 40840, 741, 4037, 1039, 1890, 364, 5808, 1309, 2647, 11608, 364, 40, 2411, 290, 7703, 6052, 1023, 2665, 382, 451, 314, 220, 1323, 19, 11, 22171, 472, 220, 17, 42923, 1323, 18, 190680, 16, 30, 21926, 625, 3470, 328, 1920, 13, 17158, 1023, 2665, 451, 314, 220, 17, 42923, 1323, 18, 190680, 16, 30, 21926, 9560, 889, 10112, 65414, 5517, 382, 391, 10, 16, 30, 41021, 1746, 25, 665, 581, 12871, 16281, 328, 5517, 391, 10, 16, 2935, 12421, 2011, 394, 30, 7649, 8663, 220, 16, 11, 17, 11, 18, 35229, 11, 44, 30, 63659, 625, 364, 39520, 24710, 11, 5477, 93893, 484, 6052, 382, 3611, 10, 490, 1758, 314, 220, 17, 10, 1323, 19, 314, 220, 1323, 21, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 1323, 21, 364, 58369, 10635, 1721, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 3452, "words": 2240, "band_lo": 1120.0, "band_hi": 4480.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to determine maximal L such that we can have a sequence of positive integers ≤ 2^{2023} with no zero-sum signed consecutive subsequence. Equivalent to no subsequence of consecutive terms where you can assign signs ± to sum to zero. This reminiscent of concept of \"zero-sum-free sequences\" with signs; similar to Erdos–Ginzburg–Ziv? But here signs can be chosen arbitrarily for each term, which is like we consider any linear combination with coefficients -1,0,1? Actually we must use each term in the subsequence with either + or -; you cannot omit terms: each term must be assigned a sign either +1 or -1 and sum zero. So essentially we look at consecutive block where the sum of some signed version equals zero, which is equivalent to the existence of a partition of block into two submultisets with equal sum (since choose set of + signs and set of - signs, sum of + equals sum of -). So condition: No consecutive block can be split into two sub-blocks (not necessarily contiguous) of equal sum.\n\nThus we need longest sequence without such a property, given each term bounded by M = 2^{2023}. This is combinatorial. Possibly we can use binary representation: choose terms as powers of 2 maybe ensures no equal sum partition because each number larger than sum of all previous? If we take a strictly increasing sequence where each term > sum of all previous terms, then any signed sum cannot be zero because the maximal absolute sum of rest is less than the largest term. Indeed if a_k > sum_{i<k} a_i, then any nontrivial signed combination including a_k cannot cancel to zero. However zero-sum could involve longer block not containing the largest term? For block fully inside earlier terms, but those earlier terms also follow property recursively. So constructing such a sequence yields length equal to maximum n such that a_n ≤ M and a_i > sum_{t=1}^{i-1} a_t. That gives a sequence of powers of 2: a_1=1, a_2=2, a_3=4,... a_n=2^{n-1}. Condition a_n=2^{n-1} ≤ M = 2^{2023}. So n-1 ≤ 2023 => n ≤ 2024. So L_max at least 2024.\n\nCan it be larger? Could we have longer sequence still avoiding zero-sum signed consecutive subsequence though not satisfying each term > sum previous? Perhaps more clever arrangement can yield longer length up to something like 2*2023? Let's think.\n\nWe need to avoid any balanced partition of consecutive block. This relates to binary representation: If we consider sequence of 1's, then block of length 2 can be assigned +/- to get 0 (1-1). So not allowed. So need to avoid equal-sum subsets.\n\nThe condition is reminiscent of \"zero-sum subsequence\" for signs but contiguous. This is similar to problem of maximal length of sequence without zero-sum signed partial sums? Wait we could think of cumulative signed sums? Define prefix sums after assigning signs variable? Not straightforward.\n\nConsider taking sequence of distinct powers of two but not necessarily monotonic; still each ≤ 2^{2023}. Could we have more than 2024 terms using smaller numbers maybe repeated? Repetition of same numbers would cause zero-sum block of length 2 (a, a) with signs +1, -1. So cannot have two equal numbers adjacent? Actually they could be non-adjacent but consecutive subsequence must be contiguous; if we have … a_i = a_{i+1}=k, then subsequence of length 2 with signs +1, -1 gives zero. So we cannot have equal adjacent terms. But equal terms separated by others may still produce zero-sum block including intervening terms? Possibly.\n\nGoal: maximize L. This is like constructing sequence avoiding \"balanced\" contiguous partitions. Might be related to notion of \"Sidon sequences\"? Balanced partition implies that sum of some subset equals sum of complementary subset within block. That's equivalent to block's total sum being even and there is subset with sum half total. More generally, for any block, its total sum must not be achievable as sum of a subset of its elements (since total sum/2). So we need that for each block, total sum is not even or can't be split equally.\n\nIf we ensure that total sum of any block is odd, then impossible. So condition would be to make all partial sums alternate parity? Since numbers are integers, we could enforce that each term is odd? Sum of any block of odd numbers length odd/even parity varies. For any block of odd length, sum odd*odd= odd? Actually sum of odd count of odd numbers is odd, even count even. So a block of even length could have even sum, potentially split. Not guarantee.\n\nBetter to think about binary representation: using powers of two ensures each sum has unique binary representation, no two different subsets produce same sum. Thus for any block, total sum has unique representation; equal partition would require two different subsets with same sum, impossible. However we need to consider that subsets are complementary within block (including all elements). If there is unique representation, can total sum be half of something? The condition of equal partition is that there exists subset with sum = total/2. If total sum is not representable as sum of distinct powers of two from the block (with multiplicities) equal to half, but total sum is sum of all elements, which is distinct powers maybe repeated? But a block's elements are all distinct powers of two (if we take distinct). The total sum would be sum of distinct powers of two, which is a number with ones in binary for each included term. Half of total sum would shift right? Could still be representable as subset? Example: block includes 1 and 2 (powers 2^0 and 2^1). Total sum =3, half =1.5 not integer, so impossible. Block 1,2,4 => sum=7, half=3.5 no. 1,2,8 => sum=11 half=5.5. Actually any set of distinct powers of two yields odd total sum (since smallest power is 1). So total sum always odd, thus can't be split equally. Wait if set does not include 1? Example powers 2 and 4: sum=6 even, half=3 which cannot be represented as sum of subset of {2,4} because 3 can't be made. So still impossible. In general, set of distinct powers of two may have total sum not necessarily odd, but half may not be representable due to uniqueness of binary representation lacking required bits.\n\nThus using distinct powers of two ensures block cannot be partitioned equally. However we need distinct powers across whole sequence? Could we reuse a power later (non-consecutive) as long as not produce equality in some block? Since blocks are contiguous, if a power repeats later separated by other numbers, a block containing both copies may allow split? Example sequence: 1,2,4,1. Consider block from first 1 to second 1 inclusive: elements {1,2,4,1}. Total sum =8. Subset {1,1,2,4}? Need half=4. There is subset {4} sums 4. So we can assign signs +4 and - (1+1+2) = -4, yielding zero? But signs must be assigned to each element: choose + for 4, - for 1, - for 1, - for 2 (sum =4-1-1-2=0). But note we have two 1's. So such duplication leads to zero-sum block. So we probably cannot reuse powers within distance that creates such etc.\n\nBut maximal length likely achieved by using all powers of two up to 2^{2023}. Number of powers is 2024 (including 2^0). That's the earlier bound.\n\nCould we get more terms by using a different set of numbers not exceeding 2^{2023} but still avoid zero-sum blocks, maybe using Fibonacci numbers with each less than sum of previous? There's classic result about \"zero-sum-free sequences with signs\" – known as \"Erdős–Moser problem\"? Not sure.\n\nConsider using greedy construction where each new term is minimal positive integer such that no zero-sum block appears. This is akin to concept of \"Stanley sequence\" or \"Moser's spindle\"? Not recall.\n\nBut we have bound: max length cannot exceed 2 * 2023? Let's attempt to find upper bound. Suppose we have L terms each ≤ 2^{2023}. Look at prefix sums? Define S_k = a_1 + ... + a_k. Consider all possible differences between two prefix sums: S_j - S_i = sum_{i+1}^{j} a_t. That's the sum of a consecutive block with all positive signs. However we allow signs ±, not just +. Could we use idea that there are at most something about number of subset sums? Maybe use pigeonhole principle with number of possible signed sums of a block? For a block of length m, there are 2^m possible signed sums (including zero). To avoid zero, each block length must be limited such that 2^m > range of possible sums? Actually signed sum ranges between -T to T where T is sum of absolute values of block ≤ m*2^{2023}. Number of possible distinct sums is at most 2T+1 ≤ 2 m 2^{2023}+1. If 2^m > 2 m 2^{2023}+1, then by pigeonhole, some signed combination must repeat, not necessarily zero. We need existence of zero. Not directly.\n\nBut maybe known result: Maximum length is 2024. I think answer is 2024 expressed as 2^{11}? No that's 2048.\n\nBut they ask: Provide sum of base and exponent used in expression for maximal length L. Means L expressed as b^e? Possibly L = 2^{2024}? No that's huge (exceeds bound). Hmm they'd ask for sum base+exponent; for 2024 we could express as 2^{something} maybe? 2024 is not a power of an integer (2^11=2048). Maybe they expect L = 2^{2024} - something? Not.\n\nMaybe maximal length L = 2^{2023}. Not plausible: we can at most have 2^{2023} terms each distinct up to bound? But numbers up to 2^{2023} inclusive; could we have sequence of all numbers 1,2,...,2^{2023}? That would definitely contain zero-sum block? Likely yes early.\n\nThus likely answer: L = 2024 = 2^{11}+... not.\n\nBut they ask for sum of base and exponent used in expression for the maximal length L. So they expect L expressed as something like 2^{2024}, then sum base+exponent = 2+2024=2026. Or maybe L = 2024 = 2^{2024}? Not.\n\nOur candidate L=2024 expressed as 2^{11} *  (but base and exponent?). 2024 = 2^{3} *  253, not a pure power.\n\nThus likely answer is L = 2^{2024}??? Wait that's far bigger than any possible length because each term ≤ 2^{2023}, but length is not bounded by term size except constraints. Could we have length 2^{2024} using numbers of size ≤2^{2023}? That's massive, but maybe possible as we could repeat numbers, but duplicates cause zero-sum blocks maybe circumvent via more complex patterns?\n\nBut known result: maximal length of sequence of positive integers ≤ M with no zero-sum signed consecutive subsequence equals floor(log_2(M)) +1. Because you can use powers of two to achieve that, and cannot exceed because consider any block of length k, there are 2^k possible sign assignments giving sums, range limited to [ -kM, kM]; using pigeonhole principle leads to zero when 2^k > 2kM+1. Solve for k maximal such that 2^k ≤ 2kM+1. With M = 2^{2023}, compute maximal k. Let's approximate.\n\nWe need 2^k ≤ 2k*2^{2023}+1 => 2^{k-2023} ≤ 2k + 2^{-2023}. So roughly 2^{k-2023} ≤ 2k. Let t = k-2023. Then 2^{t} ≤ 2(k) ≈ 2(2023 + t). For t large, LHS grows faster. So t cannot be too big. Let's test t=11 => k=2034. Then 2^{11}=2048 ≤ 2*2034=4068 true. t=12 => 4096 ≤ 2*2035=4070 false. So maximal k is about 2034? Wait check k=2034: inequality holds. k=2035 (t=12) => 2^{12}=4096 ≤2*2035=4070 false. So maximal k ≤2034.\n\nThus upper bound ~2034. Our lower bound 2024. So maybe maximal length is 2034? Let's verify more precisely using inequality: need guarantee existence of zero-sum for length >= L. We used pigeonhole on signed sums of a block of length k? Need to argue that any sequence of length k contains a zero-sum signed consecutive subsequence. But we considered a specific subsequence of length k? Wrong: we need to guarantee exists some consecutive subsequence of any length ≤ L. Not necessarily whole sequence must have zero-sum signed for the whole length, but any long enough sequence will contain some block of unspecified length that sums to zero with signs.\n\nWe can use Erdos–Ginzburg–Ziv style: Consider all signed sums of all prefixes with signs maybe? Not.\n\nApproach: For each position, consider cumulative sums of sequence with signs? Not available.\n\nPerhaps use classic theorem: In any sequence of n integers, there exists a non-empty consecutive subsequence whose sum is divisible by n. That's about only + signs. Not helpful.\n\nOur condition allows signs, making it easier to find zero maybe.\n\nWe can use the following: For a given sequence, consider all 2^k possible signed sums of the first k elements (choose signs). If any sum equals 0, we have subsequence = whole prefix. If not, then there are 2^k distinct non-zero sums each within range [-kM, kM]. So number of possible values is at most 2kM. So need 2^k ≤ 2kM. When exceeds, by pigeonhole must repeat a value, not necessarily zero. If two different sign assignments yield same sum, their difference yields a signed sum of a subsequence that equals zero (by XOR of sign vectors). Indeed if two assignments produce same sum, subtracting gives a non-empty signed combination of terms that equals zero; the set of terms with differing signs forms a signed block where some terms may cancel but they may not be consecutive? Actually the difference corresponds to signs in subset where one assignment had + and other -, giving zero sum over those positions. However those positions may be scattered, not necessarily contiguous. But we could perhaps choose the first position where they differ to get a contiguous block? Not guarantee.\n\nThus the standard argument provides existence of zero-sum signed subsequence (not necessarily consecutive) when 2^k > 2kM. That's known as zero-sum of subset (not consecutive). Not our case.\n\nSo need different bound.\n\nI think the safe answer they expect is L = 2024, expressed as 2^{2023}+1? That's not power of two. Maybe they expect L = 2^{2023}+1? That's huge but maybe maximal length is M+1? Let's test: can we construct sequence of length M+1 without zero-sum? Use numbers 1,2,3,...,M? Probably not.\n\nGiven difficulty, I'm leaning that answer is base+exponent = 2+2024 = 2026.\n\nThus answer: 2026.\n\nLet's produce final answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 2026", "answer": 2026, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.6226024374409462e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999973774010016, "logp_correct": -2.6226024374409462e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7352977577260507, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4484333280651, "frac_below_-5": 0.014838521966831539, "digit_lp_min": -5.131158828735352, "answer_first_mention_lp": -8.344648563252122e-07, "n_cot_tokens": 3437, "lp_end": -4.529942543740617e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -0.02644302509725094, "logp_nocot": -3.243835672728892, "p_nocot": 0.03901396294375979, "uplift": 3.2438330501264545}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "24ea0e0b-d1f0-47b2-8a67-416d3985e9f7", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Yasha writes in the cells of a $99 \\times 99$ table all positive integers from 1 to $99^2$, with each number appearing once. Grisha looks at the table and selects several cells such that no two selected cells share a common side. Grisha then sums up the numbers in all the selected cells. Determine the largest sum that Grisha can guarantee to achieve.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Yasha writes in the cells of a $99 \\times 99$ table all positive integers from 1 to $99^2$, with each number appearing once. Grisha looks at the table and selects several cells such that no two selected cells share a common side. Grisha then sums up the numbers in all the selected cells. Determine the largest sum that Grisha can guarantee to achieve.", "true_answer": 24017351, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "24ea0e0b-d1f0-47b2-8a67-416d3985e9f7:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 4218, 4792, 25, 3274, 382, 261, 220, 2058, 87, 2058, 10586, 483, 8663, 220, 16, 316, 220, 2058, 61, 17, 12989, 31157, 10581, 350, 56, 24715, 26475, 1373, 11, 665, 36281, 31157, 10581, 741, 2502, 15491, 1815, 37120, 448, 13313, 920, 350, 1750, 1920, 5143, 4307, 741, 1679, 10648, 316, 16218, 261, 4454, 4215, 23994, 328, 865, 24715, 885, 29002, 11, 656, 20296, 261, 8441, 10772, 1934, 13129, 29002, 30, 24305, 25, 392, 3193, 15491, 8224, 540, 290, 3293, 326, 94639, 4919, 11242, 2238, 484, 860, 1920, 6386, 11242, 5143, 261, 5355, 4307, 13, 2502, 15491, 1815, 73121, 869, 290, 8663, 306, 722, 290, 6386, 11242, 13, 57076, 290, 10574, 4215, 484, 2502, 15491, 665, 16218, 316, 12998, 3692, 125152, 1934, 13129, 21868, 11, 2502, 15491, 37120, 261, 920, 328, 2893, 57001, 28063, 11