4, 84787, 581, 1309, 316, 6757, 484, 290, 30248, 21868, 4787, 1349, 25363, 4321, 6049, 9707, 484, 3291, 4307, 6049, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 860, 3432, 4321, 364, 3031, 1354, 261, 5542, 1534, 25, 730, 54467, 497, 651, 7465, 483, 25363, 28460, 11, 1349, 6049, 25363, 4321, 29702, 2999, 6049, 20238, 1919, 22569, 679, 6049, 30, 50557, 15333, 8496, 328, 392, 80931, 6049, 25363, 4321, 1, 1919, 2454, 25363, 6049, 11, 1309, 316, 4321, 722, 22569, 26, 665, 413, 38560, 316, 1349, 6049, 11165, 4321, 30, 4037, 30890, 364, 7943, 12951, 395, 261, 5533, 54467, 497, 651, 30, 41021, 2411, 25, 1843, 581, 14975, 8663, 2238, 484, 2454, 10610, 328, 23821, 40521, 11242, 350, 57001, 28063, 30, 4037, 722, 24702, 553, 39069, 741, 5551, 2454, 6461, 4206, 382, 39069, 316, 869, 316, 220, 19, 5960, 11242, 13, 2632, 316, 4321, 722, 22569, 2935, 31615, 484, 6461, 11, 481, 2804, 4736, 722, 1617, 78186, 5960, 11242, 13, 2632, 538, 6461, 6049, 382, 19919, 4410, 11, 481, 2023, 13284, 480, 656, 1617, 78186, 106143, 11, 889, 1815, 1309, 1991, 328, 1373, 13, 730, 1039, 9770, 11, 290, 45716, 484, 679, 1339, 125210, 316, 413, 12384, 8663, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 11095, 316, 274, 591, 220, 16, 485, 3796, 8, 553, 290, 8663, 483, 1932, 4824, 30, 50557, 1934, 132194, 11, 1236, 45716, 5025, 4410, 8663, 350, 3086, 4410, 76088, 741, 15273, 39069, 5960, 11242, 553, 19919, 3291, 8663, 350, 3086, 261, 10853, 741, 2514, 4321, 22569, 20934, 316, 484, 6461, 11, 481, 2023, 7665, 4736, 290, 5960, 13034, 1858, 8, 1118, 553, 3291, 11, 889, 35174, 1309, 12951, 7598, 5960, 11242, 316, 4321, 722, 22569, 591, 484, 6461, 26, 889, 2454, 11165, 382, 9599, 483, 14836, 16913, 5960, 11242, 13, 12265, 2454, 6461, 4206, 853, 869, 316, 220, 19, 20934, 22569, 11, 35174, 1309, 316, 3931, 869, 316, 220, 19, 5960, 11242, 316, 13284, 24878, 484, 6461, 13, 34138, 328, 2617, 5960, 11242, 3572, 2928, 413, 4465, 4951, 316, 10389, 3609, 6049, 13, 16454, 364, 7943, 19271, 16218, 4792, 306, 2238, 21387, 6960, 6052, 382, 35964, 7014, 14, 17, 741, 2632, 357, 2411, 4275, 6145, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 25, 220, 13312, 20898, 6231, 364, 4383, 81645, 25, 14228, 311, 39744, 14, 17, 8, 314, 350, 3519, 11, 40850, 11, 34968, 659, 16, 11876, 17, 314, 220, 3519, 11, 40850, 11, 38732, 14, 17, 314, 220, 1494, 11, 30526, 11, 33219, 13, 9700, 364, 84787, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 7368, "words": 4601, "band_lo": 2129.5, "band_hi": 8518.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to understand problem: There is a 99x99 grid with numbers 1 to 99^2 placed arbitrarily (Yasha writes them, can arrange arbitrarily). Grisha then picks an independent set (no two share side). He wants to guarantee a certain sum regardless of Yasha's placement, by choosing a selection strategy after seeing placement? Wait: \"Grisha looks at the table and selects several cells such that no two selected cells share a common side. Grisha then sums up the numbers in all the selected cells. Determine the largest sum that Grisha can guarantee to achieve.\" Means after seeing arrangement, Grisha picks a set of nonadjacent cells to maximize sum. But Yasha gets to arrange numbers arbitrarily to minimize the maximal achievable sum (since we want guarantee). So it's a two-player game: Yasha places numbers 1..N in cells. Then Grisha chooses an independent set to maximize sum; Yasha wants keep that sum low. We need value of game: max over strategies of Grisha of min over Yasha placements of sum that Grisha can achieve (with optimal selection after seeing placement). Equivalent to: For any placement, Grisha can achieve at least some sum S. Find maximal S such that for any placement, there exists independent set with sum ≥ S. That is the guarantee.\n\nThus we need lower bound: Show any arrangement has an independent set with sum at least something. Upper bound: Show Yasha can arrange to make any independent set sum ≤ something. Find exact value.\n\nWe have grid bipartite (checkerboard). Independent set is subset of one color (since no two adjacent share side, any independent set can be partitioned into at most two colors?), Actually maximum independent set for bipartite grid is max of two color classes. Since any adjacency is between opposite colors. Independent set can include both colors as long as they are not adjacent; but in grid, you could have shape that includes many of both colors. However maximum cardinality independent set size is ceil(N/2) =  (99*99+1)/2 = 4901? Let's compute: 99^2 = 9801. ceil(9801/2)=4901. That's one color class size (since odd). The grid has 4901 of one color, 4900 of other. So any independent set can't exceed 4901 cells. But we care sum.\n\nGoal: guarantee large sum, likely by picking all cells of the larger color class (or near). But Yasha can place small numbers on that color. So worst-case sum for that color class if Yasha assigns smallest numbers to that color. So we need to consider optimum for Grisha after seeing arrangement; he can pick whichever independent set yields max sum, not necessarily a whole color.\n\nObservation: For any placement, the sum of numbers in larger color class is at least something (since Yasha can assign numbers arbitrarily). But Yasha could make that sum minimal. Let's compute minimal possible sum of numbers in the larger color class (size 4901) given numbers 1..9801.\n\nYasha aims to minimize the maximum independent set sum. He can assign smallest numbers to one color (say black cells) and largest to white cells; then the best Grisha can do is pick independent set maybe of white cells but must avoid adjacency, could mix colors. However the worst case might be that all large numbers are placed so that any independent set cannot collect too many large numbers.\n\nWe need to find guarantee value: It's a classic \"half the sum\" maybe? Since independent set at most half cells, maybe guarantee sum at least half of total sum? But numbers vary.\n\nTotal sum = (1+9801)*9801/2 = (9802*9801)/2 = 9802*4900.5? Compute: 9801*9802/2 = (9801*9802)/2. Let's compute numeric: 9801*9802 = 9801*(9800+2)=9801*9800 + 9801*2 = 960,49800? Let's compute precisely: 9801*9800 = 9801*98*100 = (9801*98)*100. 9801*100=980,100; minus 9801*2=19,602 gives 960,498. Wait that's off due to decimal. Let's do direct: 9801*9800 = 9801*(98*100) = (9801*98)*100. 9801*98 = 9801*(100-2)=980,100-19,602=960,498. Then times 100=96,049,800. Then add 9801*2 = 19,602 -> total 96,069,402. Divide by 2: 48,034,701. So total sum = 48,034,701.\n\nHalf of that ≈ 24,017,350.5.\n\nBut independent set max size is 4901 < half cells (which is 4900.5?). Actually half cells = 9801/2 = 4900.5, independent set size is 4901 (greater by 0.5 rounding up). So independent set could be at most 1 more cell than half. So sum guarantee maybe close to half of sum plus something.\n\nBut Yasha can hide largest numbers in cells such that no independent set can capture many of them because they are arranged adjacent to each other, forcing at least one of each pair not selected.\n\nConsidering bipartite nature: any independent set can be at most all cells of one color plus maybe some from the other color if they are not adjacent to selected cells of first color. But to maximize sum, Grisha will likely choose cells of one color with larger numbers, maybe sacrifice a few to get some of the other color.\n\nPerhaps optimal guarantee is sum of the larger half of numbers? Since Grisha can guarantee at least sum of the 4901 largest numbers? Yasha could place those largest numbers all on the smaller color class (size 4900). Then Grisha cannot pick them all because he can't select adjacent cells; but they are all on same color (say white). If Yasha puts largest numbers on white, which has 4900 cells. Then Grisha could select all white cells (they form independent set? Wait white cells are not adjacent to each other—they are same color, so they are independent. Indeed in a checkerboard, no two same color share a side. So all white cells is independent set! So Grisha could select all whites, getting sum of all numbers placed on whites. So Yasha wouldn't do that; they'd put large numbers on black (the larger color class) maybe? Let's define colors: Suppose board colored like chess, with black cells count = 4901 (since odd). Grisha could pick all black cells (independent set) and get sum of numbers on blacks. Yasha wants to minimize that sum, thus put smallest numbers on black cells. So sum on blacks would be sum of smallest 4901 numbers = 1+2+...+4901 = 4901*4902/2 = 4901*2451 =? Compute: 4901*2000=9,802,000; *400=1,960,400; *50=245,050; *1=4,901. Sum = 9,802,000+1,960,400=11,762,400; +245,050=12,007,450; +4,901=12,012,351. Let's check calculation: Actually 2451 = 2000+400+50+1, yes.\n\nSo worst-case sum if Grisha picks all blacks = 12,012,351.\n\nBut Grisha is not forced to pick all blacks; he can pick a better independent set mixing colors to get higher sum maybe by picking some white cells (which may contain larger numbers). However Yasha can place large numbers on whites but can also make them adjacent to many large numbers to limit selection? But whites are independent themselves, so Grisha could also take all whites (size 4900) sum of numbers on whites = total sum - sum on blacks = 48,034,701 - 12,012,351 = 36,022,350. That's larger. So Grisha would prefer whites if they contain large numbers. Yasha can choose which color gets small numbers.\n\nThus Yasha will assign the smallest 4901 numbers to the larger color (black), and the remaining larger numbers to whites. Then Grisha can pick all whites (4900 cells) for sum 36,022,350. That's big guarantee? But can Yasha do better to reduce Grisha's maximal independent set sum below that? Maybe by mixing numbers across colors such that any large-mass independent set must include some black cells (which contain small numbers) or cannot take all whites because adjacency to selected blacks? Wait independent set can include both colors. Since whites form independent set, Grisha could just take all whites (since no adjacency). So the independent set of all whites is always allowable, regardless of which black cells are also selected. So Grisha can always achieve at least sum of numbers on the larger color class (whites?) Actually whichever color class has size 4900 (the smaller). Since whites are independent, picking all whites always permissible. So guarantee at least sum of numbers on the color class of size 4900 (the smaller class). However Yasha can assign the smallest numbers to whites to make this sum low. But we earlier assigned smallest to black (bigger class) to minimize sum of blacks; but for whites (smaller class), Yasha would put the largest numbers? That would increase sum on whites, which is bad for Yasha. So Yasha wants to minimize max achievable sum: Since Grisha can pick whichever color class yields larger sum (maybe he picks larger-sum color). Yasha must decide distribution to minimize the maximum of sum over black set and sum over white set (since Grisha can take either full color class). However Grisha can also take subset mixing both, potentially exceeding both color sums? Could any independent set have sum larger than max of full color sums? Possibly, by taking many high numbers from both colors while respecting adjacency constraints, maybe achieve higher than max of color sums.\n\nBut we can at least bound guarantee by max of sums of color classes: Grisha can achieve at least max(S_black, S_white), where S_black is sum of numbers placed on black cells, similarly white. Because he could just select all cells of that color. So guarantee = min over placements of max(S_black, S_white). Yasha wants to minimize that.\n\nGiven numbers total T, and sizes: |B|=4901, |W|=4900. Yasha can allocate numbers to minimize the larger of the two color sums. This is like partition numbers into two sets of specified sizes to minimize the larger sum.\n\nGoal: find minimal possible value of max of sums for partition of 1..N into sets of sizes 4901 and 4900.\n\nSince total sum T, the larger sum will be at least ceil(T/2). But because sizes differ, the minimal possible max sum may be greater than ceil(T/2). Let's compute.\n\nLet sum of numbers assigned to black be S_b (size 4901), white sum S_w = T - S_b. Want to minimize max(S_b, S_w). Equivalent to make S_b as close as possible to T/2, but limited by size constraints.\n\nWe can think of selecting 4901 numbers to black to get sum as close as possible to T/2*? Since black larger size, they may get slightly larger sum.\n\nWe need to find minimal possible max, i.e., smallest possible value M such that there exists a subset of size 4901 with sum ≤ M and complement sum ≤ M (since M ≥ both). So we need subset sum S_b such that both S_b ≤ M and T - S_b ≤ M -> S_b between T-M and M. So we need interval overlap: Need to find M minimal s.t. there exists subset of size 4901 with sum within [T-M, M].\n\nSince |B| > |W|, likely S_b will be ≥ T/2 due to more items (by average). The average of numbers = (N+1)/2 = (9801+1)/2 = 4901. So expected sum for 4901 items is 4901*4901 = 4901^2 = 24,019,? compute: 4900^2=24,010,000; plus 2*4900+1 = 9,801? Actually (a+1)^2 = a^2+2a+1. So 4900^2=24,010,000. Add 2*4900+1 = 9,801. Sum = 24,019,801. So average sum = 24,019,801.\n\nThus S_b typical ~24M, which is about half total (48M). So we can possibly get S_b close to half.\n\nWe need to find minimal possible max = minimal possible larger of the two sums. Since total T =48,034,701, half = 24,017,350.5. So minimal max must be at least ceiling of half = 24,017,351.\n\nBut due to size imbalance, maybe the minimal max is higher, maybe 24,027,xxx.\n\nWe need to know if we can select 4901 numbers with sum ≤ 24,017,351 (i.e., close to half) while leaving complement also ≤ that (i.e., other sum = T - S_b also ≤ 24,017,351). Since T is odd? T = 48,034,701 odd; half not integer. If S_b = 24,017,351, then T - S_b = 24,017,350, which is ≤ 24,017,351. That's okay. So we need subset of 4901 numbers adding to 24,017,351.\n\nIs that possible? Let's consider selecting the smallest 4901 numbers gave sum 12,012,351 far too low. So we can increase.\n\nGoal is to achieve sum as close as possible to half but with size 4901. Since average of numbers 1..N is 4901, the sum of any 4901 numbers could range from minimum 12,012,351 to maximum sum of largest 4901 numbers = sum from 4901 to 9801? Wait largest 4901 numbers are from 9801-4900=4901? Indeed the set {4901,...,9801} has 4901 numbers. Their sum = total sum - sum of 1..4900 = T - (4900*4901/2). Compute sum 1..4900 = 4900*4901/2 = 4900*2450.5 = 4900*2450 + 4900*0.5= 12,005,000 + 2,450 =12,007,450? Wait compute accurately: 4900*4901 = 24,? 4900*4901 = 4900*(4900+1)=4900*4900 + 4900 = 24,010,000 + 4,900 = 24,014,900. Divide by 2 => 12,007,450. Yes.\n\nThus sum of largest 4901 numbers = T - 12,007,450 = 48,034,701 - 12,007,450 = 36,027,251.\n\nThat's much larger than half.\n\nThus we can achieve a sum close to half by selecting mix of small and large numbers.\n\nQuestion reduces to: Can we partition numbers into two sets of sizes 4901 and 4900 such that sums are as balanced as possible (difference minimized). This is reminiscent of \"balanced partition with given sizes\".\n\nSince total numbers symmetrical around 4901 average, we expect we can get perfect balance (difference 1 possibly). Because we have one extra element in larger set, the sum difference will be at least average of extra element (?) Actually if we split numbers equally by size (4900 each) we could have perfect balance. But we have 4901 vs 4900. So the larger set will have one extra number, whose contribution (its value) will tip the sum. To minimize max sum, we should give the extra number to be as small as possible (i.e., near average). So we can aim for sums: S_b = floor(T/2) + x, S_w = ceil(T/2) - x perhaps? Let's compute.\n\nLet S_b = (T + d)/2, S_w = (T - d)/2 where d = S_b - S_w. Since sizes differ by 1, we may have d roughly median value.\n\nSum over all numbers is T. The average per element is 4901. The extra element in black contributes its value; the sum of black minus white = (average of numbers in black) *4901 - (average in white)*4900 = ?\n\nLet total sum = sum_B + sum_W. Let extra element value = e is the element that is in black but not in white (since black has one extra). Let the remaining 4900 elements be paired one-to-one between black and white. If we pair each black element b_i with white w_i, we can think of difference sum_B - sum_W = e + Σ (b_i - w_i). To minimize max, we want differences as small as possible, maybe pairing to offset.\n\nMaybe we can achieve sums differing by as little as possible, maybe difference = some value in range? Since total sum odd, difference must be odd? Actually T odd => S_b and S_w one integer each, difference odd. Since T = 48,034,701 odd, then S_b and S_w cannot both be integer with same parity? Their sum odd, difference odd. So minimal max would be (T+1)/2 = 24,017,351 (ceil half). That leads to S_b = 24,017,351, S_w = 24,017,350. Difference =1. This seems achievable if we can pick black set size 4901 with sum exactly ceil(T/2). Is that possible? Let's see if there exists subset of size 4901 summing to 24,017,351.\n\nWe need to find whether such subset exists. Likely yes due to flexibility.\n\nGiven the numbers 1..9801, we can think of taking all numbers except some set of 4900 numbers for white, so black sum = total - sum of white. So we need white sum = 24,017,350 (the smaller). White size=4900. So can we select 4900 numbers summing to exactly 24,017,350? Average would be approx 24,017,350 / 4900 ≈ 4900.48, close to average 4901. That seems plausible.\n\nI suspect feasible.\n\nThus minimal possible max sum = ceil(T/2) = 24,017,351. So Yasha can arrange so that both color sums are at most that; then Grisha can guarantee at most that (since he can pick the larger color sum). But could Grisha do better by a mixed independent set achieving sum greater than ceil(T/2) even under this optimal partition? Need to consider.\n\nGiven any placement, Grisha can choose any independent set, not limited to full color. Could there be an independent set with sum > max(color sums)? Potentially if large numbers are placed such that they are not all same color but interspersed, enabling a set that grabs many large numbers across colors while respecting adjacency. The maximum independent set weight problem on bipartite graph with given vertex weights (numbers). The guarantee we need is the value of the game: min over assignments of max weight independent set. That is the weighted bipartite independence number (maximum weight independent set) for worst-case weights.\n\nSince the graph is bipartite, maximum weight independent set = total weight - minimum weight vertex cover (König's theorem weighted version). Minimum weight vertex cover can be found using Hungarian-like algorithm; but guarantee we need worst-case minimal value.\n\nObservation: For any assignment of weights, max weight independent set ≥ half of total weight (by selecting all vertices of one side? Actually could be less if one side has much larger total weight than other; but we can take the heavier side as independent set, giving weight at least half of total weight? Not necessarily half, because the heavier side could be > half. Wait max of side sums is at least half (since sum of both sides = total). So we already have lower bound ceil(T/2). So guarantee cannot be less than ceil(T/2). And we have construction achieving that? Need to show Yasha can arrange weights such that any independent set weight ≤ ceil(T/2). That would give answer = 24,017,351.\n\nWe need to construct placement where maximum weight independent set = ceil(T/2). So Yasha must assign numbers to make the min vertex cover weight = floor(T/2). Equivalent to ensure that the minimum weight vertex cover is at most floor(T/2). Since total weight is T, max independent = T - min vertex cover. So need min vertex cover weight = floor(T/2) = 24,017,350.\n\nIf Yasha can ensure there exists a vertex cover (set of cells covering all edges) with total weight = floor(T/2), then independent set max ≤ ceil(T/2). Since we also have lower bound, equality holds.\n\nThus need to construct placement where there exists a set of cells covering all adjacent pairs (i.e., a set that contains at least one endpoint of each adjacency) with total weight ≤ floor(T/2). Simple candidate: take all cells of one color (say black) as vertex cover? Wait in bipartite graph, one color class is a vertex cover? Actually each edge connects black-white, so taking all black cells covers all edges (since each edge incident to a black). Yes whole black side is a vertex cover. Its sum is S_b. Similarly whites also vertex cover. Minimum vertex cover weight will be min(S_b, S_w). So min vertex cover weight = min(S_b, S_w). Because any vertex cover must contain at least one endpoint of each edge; but picking entire one side suffices, and any other cover may be smaller but cannot be less than min side? In unweighted bipartite, min vertex cover size = max matching size =... but weighted case, the minimum weight vertex cover may be lower than min side weight by selecting subset of both sides. But we can still guarantee there is a cover of weight = min(S_b,S_w). So min vertex cover weight ≤ min(S_b,S_w). So max independent weight ≥ T - min(S_b,S_w) = max(S_b,S_w). So indeed max independent weight equals max of side sums? Wait for weighted bipartite, König's theorem: maximum weight matching corresponds to min weight vertex cover with relation? Actually there is a weighted version where each vertex has weight, we consider vertex cover weight sum of selected vertices, want minimal sum. It equals maximum weight of independent set? Not directly; but independent set weight + vertex cover weight = total weight (since complement of independent set is vertex cover?). Indeed complement of an independent set is a vertex cover (since any edge not covered would have both ends not in cover = both in independent set, contradiction). So for any independent set I, its complement V\\I is a vertex cover. Thus weight(I)+weight(V\\I)=T. Hence max weight independent = T - min weight vertex cover. So min vertex cover weight = min_{cover} weight. Not necessarily the whole smaller side; but could be lower.\n\nThus to achieve max independent = ceil(T/2), we need min vertex cover weight = floor(T/2). That would be attained if there exists a vertex cover of weight floor(T/2). Since we can always take the smaller side with weight at most floor(T/2) if we can arrange side sums such that the smaller side weight = floor(T/2). The smaller side weight would be the smaller of S_b,S_w. If we can make min(S_b,S_w)= floor(T/2), then max independent = T - floor(T/2)=ceil(T/2). That's enough: we need to partition numbers so that one color sum is floor(T/2) (or less). Since we can pick which color has more cells? Black larger side 4901; white smaller side 4900. The smaller side weight could be the smaller sum.\n\nWe want min(S_b,S_w) = floor(T/2) = 24,017,350. Since total is odd, the two sums must be 24,017,351 and 24,017,350. So min = 24,017,350. That matches exactly the partition we considered earlier (balanced). So we need to partition into colors with these sums. That's possible if we can assign numbers appropriately.\n\nThus Yasha can arrange numbers such that sum on whites = 24,017,350 (the smaller side). Then the min vertex cover weight = 24,017,350 (by taking whites). Then max independent = 48,034,701 - 24,017,350 = 24,017,351. Grisha can't exceed that.\n\nHence guarantee = 24,017,351.\n\nNeed to confirm existence of such placement: need subset of size 4900 with sum 24,017,350. Equivalent to subset of size 4901 with sum 24,017,351. I think it's feasible; we can construct by pairing numbers i and (9802 - i) (summing to 9802). For each pair, one is small, one large. For each pair, choose one to go to black, the other to white to balance. Since there are 4900 pairs (i from 1 to 4900). Each pair sum = 9802. If we assign one element of each pair to each color, each color gets total sum = 4900*9802/2 = 4900*4901 = 24,? compute: 4900*4901 = 24,? earlier we computed 24,014,900? wait 4900*4901 = as above 24,014,900. That's exactly half of total without middle element 4901? Let's compute total sum = sum_{i=1}^{9801} i = sum_{i=1}^{4900} i + 4901 + sum_{i=1}^{4900} (9802 - i). Pair sums 9802 each pair gives 4900*9802 = 48,? compute: 9802*4900 = 9802*49*100 = (9802*49)*100. 9802*50 = 490,100; minus 9802 = 480,298; times100 => 48,029,800. Then plus middle 4901 => total 48,034,701 matches.\n\nIf we assign from each pair one number to black and one to white, each side gets sum = 4900*9802/2 = 24,014,900 plus possibly extra element 4901 goes to black (since black larger size). Then black sum = 24,014,900 + 4901 = 24,019,801. White sum = 24,014,900. These are not balanced; difference = 4,901. That's larger than needed.\n\nBut we can adjust by some pairs assigning both elements to same side, shifting sums.\n\nGoal: achieve sums difference 1. Since we have flexibility, we can move some larger numbers from black to white and their smaller counterparts opposite, adjusting balance.\n\nWe need to achieve white sum = 24,017,350 (increase from 24,014,900 by 2,450). That means we need to shift net extra 2,450 to white from black. By swapping in some pairs: take a pair (a,b) where a<b (a small, b large). Currently a assigned to white, b to black (or vice). To increase white sum, we move b to white and a to black, net increase of (b - a) for white, decrease same for black. Since b - a = (9802 - a) - a = 9802 - 2a. So we can select a's such that sum of (9802 - 2a) = 2,450.\n\nThus need to pick a subset of a-values (from 1..4900) such that sum of (9802 - 2a) = 2450. That equals sum of (2*(4901 - a)) = 2 * sum (4901 - a). So we need sum (4901 - a) = 1225.\n\nSo pick a set of a's where sum of (4901 - a) =1225. Equivalent to pick a set of numbers c_i = 4901 - a_i (these are numbers from 1..4900) whose sum is 1225. This is possible using e.g., 1225 = 1225*1? Just pick a_i = 4900 (c=1) 1225 times, but can't exceed 4900 elements. Since we have only 4900 possible distinct a's. Need multiset of c's distinct? a_i must be distinct because each pair used at most once. So we need distinct c_i values summing to 1225, each between 1 and 4900. Can we find distinct numbers summing to 1225? Yes e.g., 1+2+...+49 = 1225 (since sum 1..49 = 49*50/2 = 1225). So we can select a_i = 4901 - c_i = 4900, 4899, ..., 4852? Actually c from 1..49, a = 4901 - c => from 4900 down to 4852. Distinct, fine.\n\nThus by swapping the assignment for these 49 pairs, we adjust the sum by exactly 2,450 (since each swap adds 2*(c_i) to white). Starting from white sum 24,014,900, plus 2,450 = 24,017,350 as needed. Then black sum becomes total - that = 24,017,351. So possible.\n\nThus we have explicit construction: start with checkerboard where each pair (i, 9802-i) placed opposite colors, give black extra 4901. Then swap colors for pairs corresponding to a_i = 4900, 4899, ..., 4852 (i.e., numbers 4900 down to 4852 on black originally, now move them to white, and their counterparts (i.e., 2..52?) to black). That yields required sums.\n\nThus Yasha can achieve partition with sums exactly floor/ceil half. Therefore maximal guaranteed sum is 24,017,351.\n\nThus answer.\n\nCheck that independent set cannot exceed that: by max weighted independent set equals max side sum? In this arrangement, maybe there exists independent set with larger weight than the larger side (black) due to mixing? Let's consider: Since we have min vertex cover weight = 24,017,350 (white side). Could there be a vertex cover with lower weight? The min side has weight 24,017,350, could there be other cover with even lower weight using both colors? Possibly, but then max independent would be larger, which would break guarantee.\n\nNeed to argue that min vertex cover weight = smaller side sum. In bipartite weighted case, the minimum weight vertex cover is not necessarily the smaller side; but we can maybe show that any vertex cover must have weight at least the sum of the smaller side because the smaller side already covers all edges, and any other cover may have weight at least that (since each edge must be covered, you need at least one endpoint from each pair; but could replace many small-weight vertices from small side with larger-weight vertices from other side, increasing weight). However maybe there exists a cover mixing to get lower weight than the small side if some vertices on small side have large weight and some on large side have small weight. In our construction, the small side is white with sum = floor(T/2), but some whites may have relatively large numbers (because we swapped). Could we replace those large whites with their adjacent black vertices which might be smaller to reduce total cover weight? However to cover edges incident to a white vertex, you could also select the black neighbor(s). But a white vertex may have multiple neighboring black vertices; to replace that white you might need to include multiple black vertices potentially increasing weight. Since each white cell has up to 4 black neighbors. Might be possible to lower total weight.\n\nNevertheless, for a bipartite graph which is a grid, the minimum vertex cover problem corresponds to a maximum matching with weights (König-Egérváry for unweighted). Weighted version is more complex: Not simply min side.\n\nThus we need to ensure that the constructed arrangement makes min vertex cover weight exactly that small side weight, i.e., no better cover.\n\nIs there a known result: In bipartite graph with vertex weights, min weight vertex cover equals max weight matching where edges have weight? Actually there's concept of \"minimum weight vertex cover\" where each vertex weight, need to cover all edges; can be transformed to min weight edge cover? Not recall.\n\nBut perhaps for a complete bipartite? Let's think: If we assign numbers such that each pair of opposite colored cells (adjacent? Not all pairs are adjacent). However each white cell is adjacent to up to 4 black cells. So to cover all edges without selecting that white, you must select all its neighboring black cells. So if white weight is relatively large, you could replace it by its neighboring blacks, but then need many of them. In our construction, the whites that have been swapped to be larger numbers (i.e., correspond to c from 1..49) are the numbers with high values? Actually after swapping, some whites become large numbers (the large counterparts). Their adjacent black cells are relatively small numbers (the a_i). To cover edges incident to that white, you could instead select the black neighbor(s) which are small, but you'd need perhaps multiple black cells to cover all edges from that white; but each edge is shared with possibly distinct black cells. Since each white cell has up to 4 incident edges, you'd need to include up to 4 black cells to replace covering that white. Sum of those black cells might still be low enough to reduce total weight. Hard.\n\nBut typical guarantee problem in such contest likely answer is ceil(T/2). So I think it's correct.\n\nThus final answer: 24017351.\n\nCheck arithmetic: Ceil(total/2) = (48,034,701 +1)/2 = 48,034,702/2 = 24,017,351. Good.\n\nThus answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 24017351", "answer": 24017351, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -7.152555951961403e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999992847446606, "logp_correct": -7.152555951961403e-07, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6258019726773857, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4184348750969207, "frac_below_-5": 0.00911316648531012, "digit_lp_min": -4.7921528816223145, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.14267736673355103, "n_cot_tokens": 7352, "lp_end": -0.004445195198059082, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -4.529942543740617e-06, "logp_nocot": -44.049328073320794, "p_nocot": 7.406616975630716e-20, "uplift": 44.0493273580652}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
