, 185130, 57269, 12, 16, 472, 11965, 1562, 26, 12698, 3981, 220, 22, 503, 220, 1422, 13, 2632, 28336, 364, 7, 24, 11, 1125, 3127, 275, 28, 23, 625, 9197, 364, 7, 899, 11, 899, 3127, 275, 28, 994, 11, 2335, 28, 994, 350, 48581, 8, 871, 297, 28, 14503, 350, 994, 61, 17, 741, 47320, 7, 14503, 25987, 16, 10, 994, 10, 14503, 28, 16518, 625, 220, 15363, 13, 2632, 625, 364, 84787, 297, 483, 47320, 28, 15363, 7111, 5192, 316, 100482, 7060, 364, 4827, 1078, 47320, 28, 18566, 350, 1055, 61, 17, 741, 14720, 1646, 297, 483, 47320, 28, 18566, 13, 63743, 220, 18566, 28, 18, 61, 17, 9, 20, 61, 17, 364, 12211, 395, 1888, 350, 79, 10, 16, 4898, 80, 10, 16, 25987, 18566, 13, 41250, 350, 24, 11, 1161, 3127, 275, 28, 23, 625, 9197, 13, 350, 1055, 11, 1055, 3127, 275, 28, 1265, 625, 9197, 13, 350, 20, 11, 2548, 3127, 275, 28, 19, 625, 9197, 13, 2632, 1309, 10112, 3407, 104042, 364, 3335, 297, 314, 275, 136288, 92096, 11, 47320, 314, 350, 79, 10, 16, 4898, 80, 10, 16, 4898, 81, 10, 16, 8, 314, 220, 18566, 13, 19792, 2454, 2914, 1424, 17, 11, 12332, 2860, 220, 18566, 28, 18, 9, 18, 9, 20, 9, 20, 13, 2632, 2023, 920, 275, 10, 16, 28, 18, 11, 2335, 10, 16, 28, 18, 11, 428, 10, 16, 28, 1161, 871, 275, 28, 17, 9197, 11, 2335, 28, 17, 9197, 11, 428, 28, 1494, 625, 9197, 13, 2632, 625, 364, 34013, 297, 28, 220, 17, 9, 18, 9, 30, 4037, 364, 84787, 12951, 1606, 297, 28, 16, 326, 220, 18, 35441, 7060, 364, 2167, 2804, 413, 25120, 25, 297, 2023, 413, 13749, 1299, 297, 28, 2636, 30, 47320, 625, 13749, 11, 813, 842, 364, 198140, 4215, 314, 16, 10, 18, 28, 19, 364, 58369, 3503, 30411, 297, 28, 17, 30, 28336, 13, 297, 28, 21, 30, 47320, 625, 13749, 11, 813, 1062, 7578, 2804, 679, 47320, 13749, 364, 84787, 6052, 6960, 220, 19, 364], "n_tokens": 6385, "words": 2497, "band_lo": 1726.75, "band_hi": 6907.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need positive integers n such that its divisors can be ordered so that cumulative sums are perfect squares.\n\nLet divisors of n: d1,...,dk. Need permutation where prefix sums s_i = sum_{j=1}^i d_j are squares.\n\nThus s_1 = d_1 must be a perfect square, so one divisor is a perfect square (maybe 1). Since 1 divides all n, we can set d1=1, as 1 is square.\n\nThen s_2 = 1 + d2 must be a perfect square => d2 = m^2 -1 for some integer m>1, and must be a divisor of n.\n\nSimilarly s_3 = m^2 + d3 must be square: let s_3 = t^2, so d3 = t^2 - m^2 = (t-m)(t+m) must be divisor.\n\nWe need to arrange all divisors covering them.\n\nObservation: along the path, s_i are increasing squares, and the differences between successive squares are the subsequent divisors.\n\nThus the sequence of squares: s_1 = a_1^2, s_2 = a_2^2, ..., s_k = a_k^2 (where a_i positive integers, strictly increasing). And d_i = a_i^2 - a_{i-1}^2 for i>1, with a_0 =0? Wait s_1 = d1 = a_1^2, but also a_1^2 -0 =a_1^2 fits.\n\nThus d_i = a_i^2 - a_{i-1}^2 = (a_i - a_{i-1})(a_i + a_{i-1}).\n\nAll d_i must be divisors of n, and collectively they are all divisors of n exactly once. Also sum of all d_i = s_k = a_k^2 = sum of all divisors of n = sigma(n). So sigma(n) must be a perfect square.\n\nAlso each divisor can be expressed as difference of consecutive squares in a chain.\n\nSo we need n such that sigma(n) is a perfect square, and the multiset of divisor differences can be arranged as differences of increasing squares.\n\nBut we also require that each divisor appears exactly once; sequence of differences between consecutive squares must be a permutation of divisors.\n\nThus the set of gaps {a_i - a_{i-1}} times {a_i + a_{i-1}} equals each divisor.\n\nConsider small n.\n\nTry n=1: divisors {1}. Sequence: d1=1, s1=1 which is square. Works. So n=1 qualifies.\n\nn=2: divisors {1,2}. Need order where prefix sums squares: start with 1, then need 1+2=3 not square. Could start with 2 then 2 is not square. So fails.\n\nn=3: {1,3}. 1+3=4 square! Start with 1, then 3. s1=1 square, s2=4 square. Works. So n=3 qualifies.\n\nCheck sigma(3)=1+3=4 square, matches.\n\nn=4: divisors {1,2,4}. We need ordering. Try start 1 (square). Need next divisor d such that 1+d = square, possibilities: d=3? Not divisor. So can't start with 1. Could start with 4? 4 square, then need 4+d some square. Remaining divisors 1,2. 4+? = square: 4+5=9? Need 5 not divisor. 4+12=16 need 12 not divisor. So fail. So n=4 fails.\n\nn=5: divisors {1,5}. 1+5=6 not square. Starting 5, 5 not square. So fails.\n\nn=6: divisors {1,2,3,6}. sigma=12 not square.\n\nMaybe need sigma square. Let's list numbers with sigma perfect square.\n\nKnown sequence: n=1 sigma=1; n=3 sigma=4; n=6 sigma=12 not square; n=7 sigma=8 not; n=8 sigma=15; n=9 sigma=13; n=10 sigma=18; n=14 sigma=24; n=15 sigma=24; n=21 sigma=32; n=22 sigma=36? Compute sigma(22)=1+2+11+22=36 square! So candidate n=22.\n\nCheck if ordering exists. Divisors: 1,2,11,22. Need permutation such that cumulative sums squares. Start with 1 (square). Need next d such that 1+d = square. Options: square numbers >1: 4 => d=3 not divisor; 9 => d=8; 16 => d=15; 25 => d=24; 36 => d=35; none match divisor (2,11,22). So can't start with 1. Start with 2 (square? 2 not). Start with 11 not square. 22 not square. So fails.\n\nBut maybe we could start with d1 not 1? d1 must be square divisor of n. So we need a square divisor of n. For n that is not a square, only square divisor may be 1. So 1 must be first. So constraint: n must have divisor that is perfect square >1 maybe if n is square itself, could have d1 being other square.\n\nThus for non-square n, d1=1. Hence we need a divisor d such that 1+d is square.\n\nSo we need n with divisor d = m^2 -1 where m^2-1 divides n.\n\nThus n must be multiple of some number of form m^2-1.\n\nLet’s search small n.\n\nWe already have n=3 works with m=2 (since d2=3 =2^2-1). sigma=4 square.\n\nNext n=8? divisors include 1,3? 3 not divisor; So fails.\n\nn=15? divisors 1,3,5,15. need d2=3? m=2 => d=3 present! Good. Let's test ordering: d1=1, d2=3 (cumulative 4). Next need d3 such that sum now 4 + d3 = square; squares >4: 9 => d3=5 which is divisor! Good. Next sum =9 + d4 = square; remaining divisor is 15. Need 9+15=24 not square. But we could reorder maybe earlier: after 1,3, next could be 15? 4+15=19 not square. So maybe different sequence? Let's attempt using squares: we have squares 1,4,9,? final sum sigma=1+3+5+15=24 not square, sigma must be square for any solution (since final sum is a_k^2). Indeed sigma(15)=24 not square, so fails anyway.\n\nThus sigma must be square. So search n with sigma square and also property that after ordering possible. Let's list numbers wherein sigma is perfect square up to some bound.\n\nCompute sigma for n:\n\n1:1\n2:3\n3:4 yes\n4:7\n5:6\n6:12\n7:8\n8:15\n9:13\n10:18\n11:12\n12:28\n13:14\n14:24\n15:24\n16:31\n17:18\n18:39\n19:20\n20:42\n21:32\n22:36 yes\n23:24\n24:60\n25:31\n26:42\n27:40\n28:56\n30:72\n31:32\n32:63\n33:48\n34:54\n35:48\n36:91\n37:38\n38:60\n39:80? (1+3+13+39=56?) Wait compute sigma(39)=1+3+13+39=56 not square.\n\nLet's compute systematically maybe use known OEOE sequence: n such that sigma(n) is a perfect square. Known small solutions: n=1,3, 7? sigma(7)=8 not square. 11? sigma=12 not. 14?24. I recall known sequence A001844 maybe? Let's quickly compute for n up to 50 using mental.\n\nI'll write quick table:\n\nn: prime factorization sigma formula.\n\n1:1\n2:1+2=3\n3:1+3=4 (square)\n4:1+2+4=7\n5:6\n6: (1+2)(1+3)=3*4=12\n7:8\n8:1+2+4+8=15\n9:1+3+9=13\n10:(1+2)(1+5)=3*6=18\n11:12\n12:(1+2+4)(1+3)=7*4=28\n13:14\n14:(1+2)(1+7)=3*8=24\n15:(1+3)(1+5)=4*6=24\n16:1+2+4+8+16=31\n17:18\n18:(1+2+3+6)(1+3)=12*4=48? Wait sigma(18)=1+2+3+6+9+18=39 actually compute formula (2*3^2): sigma= (2^2-1)/(2-1)*(3^3-1)/(3-1) = (4-1)/1 * (27-1)/2 =3*13 =39 correct\n19:20\n20:(1+2+4+5+10+20)=42\n21:(1+3)(1+7)=4*8=32 (square?) 32 not square.\n22:(1+2)(1+11)=3*12=36 square! So n=22 candidate but earlier failed due to ordering because after 1 need d=3? wait m=2 gives 3 divisor not present. 3 not divisor of 22. So cannot.\n\nBut maybe there exists alternative ordering where d1 not 1? Since n is not square, only square divisor is 1. So must start with 1. So need a divisor equal to square -1. For sigma(22) =36 we have final sum 36. Starting with 1, we need a square after adding some divisor: suppose after first step, sum = a2^2. So a2^2 -1 must be divisor. Let's list possible squares up to 36: 4,9,16,25,36. Differences from 1: 3,8,15,24,35. None of those are divisors of 22. So impossible. Hence n=22 fails.\n\nNext candidate n where sigma is square: n where sigma = 49? Need numbers with sigma=49. Try n=48? sigma(48) = (1+2+4+8+16+32?) Wait 48=2^4*3, sigma= (2^5-1)/(2-1) * (3^2-1)/(3-1)=31*4=124 not. Let's search known sequence: n= 1,3, 8? sigma 15 not. 14?24 no. 15?24. 20?42.\n\nMaybe next is n= 49? sigma(49)=1+7+49=57.\n\nn= 70 (2*5*7): sigma = (1+2)*(1+5)*(1+7)=3*6*8=144 square! So n=70 candidate.\n\nCheck ordering. n=70 divisors: list: 1,2,5,7,10,14,35,70. sigma=144. Need sequence of squares ending at 144. Start 1. Need a2^2 -1 divisor among remaining. Differences from squares: 4-1=3 not divisor. 9-1=8 not. 16-1=15 not. 25-1=24 not. 36-1=35 divisor! Good, we have 35. So d2=35, sum=36 (6^2). Remaining divisors: 2,5,7,10,14,70. Next need a3^2 -36 divisor. squares >36 up to 144: 49 (diff 13), 64 (diff 28), 81 (diff 45), 100 (diff 64), 121 (diff 85), 144 (diff 108). None of these diffs are among remaining divisors. So maybe different ordering: after 1, pick d2 =? we can also have a2^2 maybe 9? diff=8 not divisor. 16 diff=15 not. 25 diff=24 not. So 35 is only possible. So fails.\n\nThus n=70 fails.\n\nNext sigma maybe 64. Find n with sigma=64. Need numbers where sigma=64. Try n=63? sigma(63)= (1+3+9+27)*(1+7)=40*8=320 not. n=31? sigma 32. n=24 sigma 60. n=27 sigma 40. n=48 sigma 124. n=41 prime sigma=42. n=43 sigma=44. n=45 sigma? 45=3^2*5 => sigma=(3^3-1)/(3-1)*(1+5)=26/2*6=13*6=78.\n\nLet's use known solutions: The equation sigma(n)=m^2. This is known as \"square sigma numbers\". Known small solutions: n=1,3, 8? not; 49? 57; 70? sigma=144 yes we saw. Others: n=71? prime =>72 not square. n=84 sigma? 84=2^2*3*7 => sigma=(2^3-1)/(2-1)*(1+3)*(1+7)=7*4*8=224 not square.\n\nLet's maybe search with factorization approach: sigma multiplicative; we need product of terms each of form (p^{a+1}-1)/(p-1) to be square.\n\nSmall cases: n = p (prime) => sigma = p+1 must be square. So p+1 = m^2 => p = m^2 -1 = (m-1)(m+1). For prime, need m-1=1 => m=2 => p=3. So prime solution n=3 already.\n\nn = p^2: sigma = 1 + p + p^2 = (p^3 -1)/(p-1). Need square. test p=2 => sigma=7 not. p=3 => sigma=13. p=5=> sigma=31. Not.\n\nn = p^a with a>=1, need sigma square. Hard.\n\nComposite with two primes: n = p*q distinct. sigma = (p+1)(q+1). Need square. So product of two numbers each one more than a prime. Set (p+1)(q+1)=m^2.\n\nLet p+1 = u^2, q+1 = v^2 to make product square. Then p = u^2 -1 = (u-1)(u+1). For prime, must have u-1=1 => u=2 => p=3. Similarly q must be 3 as well? But distinct? Alternative maybe u and v such that product square but not each square individually if share common factor. Since (p+1) and (q+1) could each be non-square but their product a square if each is k * s^2 with same k.\n\nLet p+1 = k a^2, q+1 = k b^2, with k squarefree. Then product = k^2 a^2 b^2 = (k a b)^2 square. Need p,q primes.\n\nSearch small primes: compute p+1 values: for primes up to say 50: 2->3,3->4,5->6,7->8,11->12,13->14,17->18,19->20,23->24,29->30,31->32,37->38,41->42,43->44,47->48,53->54,...\n\nWe need two of these numbers whose product is square. Look for common factorization. For product to be square, each prime factor's exponent in the product must be even. So combine numbers such that exponents complement.\n\nLet’s factor each: \n3=3\n4=2^2\n6=2*3\n8=2^3\n12=2^2*3\n14=2*7\n18=2*3^2\n20=2^2*5\n24=2^3*3\n30=2*3*5\n32=2^5\n38=2*19\n42=2*3*7\n44=2^2*11\n48=2^4*3\n54=2*3^3\n\nPick two numbers whose combined exponents even. For example 3 (2^0 3^1) and 12 (2^2 3^1) product=36=6^2 square! So p+1=3 => p=2 (prime), q+1=12 => q=11 (prime). Indeed p=2, q=11 gives sigma = (2+1)(11+1)=3*12=36 square. So n = 2*11 =22 which we already considered. But ordering fails due to lacking (square-1) divisor.\n\nBut maybe we can have ordering not necessarily starting with 1? Since n is not square, only square divisor is 1, so must start with 1. We need a divisor equal to difference between two squares after previous sum. The first square is a1^2 = 1. Next square a2^2 must be divisor +1.\n\nThus we need some divisor d = a2^2 -1. For n=22, we have divisors 2,11,22. None equal to square-1 for a2 up to sqrt(sigma)=6. So fails.\n\nThus we need n where among divisors there exists a number of form m^2 -1.\n\nGeneral requirement: there exists a divisor d of n (not 1) that is one less than a perfect square. Moreover after that, we need the remaining divisors can be expressed as successive differences of squares leading to total sigma.\n\nMaybe only few n work.\n\nLet's search by constructing sequence of squares and corresponding differences forming a divisor set that is closed under multiplication (since divisors of n form multiplicative set). Actually set of divisors of n is closed under gcd but not additive.\n\nBrute force reasoning limited. Let's try to continue search manually up to maybe 100.\n\nWrite program? Not possible. Let's reason.\n\nSuppose we have sequence a1 < a2 < ... < a_k where a1=1? Actually a1^2 = d1 maybe 1? Since need first term square and divisor. Could be 4 if n is square multiple? If n is perfect square, could have d1=4 etc. But we consider general.\n\nConsider possibility that n itself is a perfect square. Then all its divisors include squares. We could start with d1 being some square >1.\n\nBut also sigma must be square. For n square, maybe more flexibility.\n\nLet's test n=4 (square). We found fail. n=9? divisors 1,3,9. sigma=13 not square.\n\nn=16: divisors 1,2,4,8,16 sigma=31 not square.\n\nn=36: divisors many sigma=91 not square.\n\nThus maybe only n=1 works among squares.\n\nNow consider n = p*q where sigma square as earlier, e.g., n=22, 33? Let's check p=3 (p+1=4), need q+1 such that product 4*(q+1)=square => q+1 must be square. So need prime q where q+1 is square => q=3 again (but same). So n=9? Not product of distinct primes. So only n=22 maybe.\n\nBut n may have three prime factors: n = p*q*r distinct. sigma = (p+1)(q+1)(r+1). Need square. Could have each term 2*2*... hmm.\n\nFind small primes where each p+1 is 2 times a square? For example p=2 =>3 not even. p=3=>4=2^2. p=7=>8=2^3 not square but maybe 8=2*2^2? Actually 8=2*2^2? 2*4 =8 yes. So p+1=2*2^2. So factorize.\n\nGoal product of three such numbers to be square. Could be (3)(12)(27?) not.\n\nMaybe easier to search known sequence in OEIS: sigma(n) perfect square numbers: 1,3,20? Wait compute sigma(20)=42 not square. Let's quickly search mental: n= 48? sigma=124. n= 70 we have 144. n= 84 sigma 224 not square. n= 90 sigma? 90=2*3^2*5 => sigma = (1+2)*(1+3+9)*(1+5)=3*13*6=234 not square. n= 105 sigma? (1+3)*(1+5)*(1+7)=4*6*8=192 not. n= 130? 130=2*5*13 => sigma =3*6*14=252 not square.\n\nBut we might miss n where sigma is square and also sequence works. Let's attempt to construct from squares differences directly to produce divisor set that is closed under multiplication.\n\nSuppose we select squares a_i^2 such that differences are all distinct and factors multiply to n's divisors. The set D = {a_i^2 - a_{i-1}^2}. The product of all D? Not directly.\n\nBut note that product of divisors = n^{k/2}. Not helpful.\n\nMaybe only n=1 and n=3 satisfy condition. Let's test n=6? Sigma=12 not square. n=10 sigma=18 not. n=15 sigma 24 not. n=21 sigma 32 not. n=22 sigma 36 square but ordering fails as we saw.\n\nMaybe there is also n= 60? sigma=168? not square.\n\nn= 84? no.\n\nn= 85? 85=5*17 sigma=(6)*(18)=108 not.\n\nn= 105? 192 not.\n\nn= 130? 252.\n\nn= 165? 165=3*5*11 sigma=4*6*12=288 not square. 288 not square.\n\nn= 190=2*5*19 sigma=3*6*20=360.\n\nn= 221=13*17 sigma=14*18=252.\n\nn= 255=3*5*17 sigma=4*6*18=432 (not square).\n\nn= 286=2*11*13 sigma=3*12*14=504.\n\nWe'll compute maybe n up to 200, but manual heavy.\n\nGiven problem likely answer small sum.\n\nThus sum maybe 1+3 =4.\n\nBut need verify no other n.\n\nCheck n= 325? 5^2*13? Not.\n\nSearch n where sigma square and also have divisor m^2 -1. Let's list sigma square numbers up to maybe 200 from known list: 1,4,9,16,25,36,49,64,81,100,121,144,169,196,225,256,... We need sigma(n) equals one of these squares.\n\nWe can attempt to find n with sigma = 9. Need sum of divisors =9. Which n? possibilities: n=8? sigma 15 no. n=4?7. n=2?3. n=3?4. n=5?6. n=6?12. So none.\n\nSigma=16: need n with sigma 16. Try n=15?24. n=14?24. n=11?12. n=7?8. n=8?15. n=9?13. n=10?18. So none.\n\nSigma=25: maybe n=12?28. n=13?14. n=24?60. Not.\n\nSigma=36 we have n=22, also n= 30? sigma 72 no. n=14?24. n= 18?39. n= 20?42. n= 35?48.\n\nAny other n with sigma=36? Let's compute n= 6?12. n= 16?31. n= 24?60. n= 25?31. n= 27?40. n= 28?56. n= 31?32. n= 32?63. So only n=22 maybe.\n\nsigma=49: need n? try n= 48?124. n= 20?42. Not.\n\nsigma=64: maybe n= 63?320. Not.\n\nsigma=81: maybe n= 64?127? Wait sigma(64)=127. Not.\n\nsigma=100: maybe n= 96? sigma maybe 252.\n\nSo likely only sigma squares are rare; 1,4,36,144? We have n=70 sigma=144.\n\nCheck if ordering possible for n=70 we saw fails. Maybe other n with sigma=144: need n such that sigma=144. Search factorization: 144 = 2^4 *3^2. Need sigma multiplicative produce this.\n\nConsider n = p^a where sigma = (p^{a+1}-1)/(p-1) = 144.\n\nSolve for p small. For p=2: sigma =2^{a+1}-1 =144 => 2^{a+1}=145 not power of 2. p=3: (3^{a+1}-1)/2 =144 => 3^{a+1}=289 => not power of 3. p=5: (5^{a+1}-1)/4=144 =>5^{a+1}=577 => not. So none.\n\nConsider n = p*q: sigma = (p+1)(q+1)=144. Factor 144 into pairs of numbers each one more than a prime.\n\nList factor pairs (1,144) etc but p+1≥3. So possibilities:\n\n(p+1,q+1) = (3,48) => p=2, q=47 (prime). Works: n=94, sigma=144? Let's compute sigma(94)= (1+2)*(1+47)=3*48=144. So n=94 candidate.\n\nCheck ordering: divisors of 94: 1,2,47,94. Start with 1. Need a2^2-1 divisor among remaining. Squares: 4 diff 3 not. 9 diff 8 not. 16 diff15 no. 25 diff24 no. 36 diff35 no. 49 diff48 not divisor. 64 diff63 no. 81 diff80 not. 100 diff99 not. 121 diff120 not. 144 diff143 not. So fails.\n\nNext pair (4,36): p=3, q=35 not prime. So not.\n\n(6,24): p=5, q=23 prime -> n=115, sigma=144. Divisors: 1,5,23,115. Need divisor 5? Check 1 + d = square. Need square-1 = divisor. possible d=3,8,15,24,35,48,63,80,99,120. None are 5,23,115. So fails.\n\n(8,18): p=7, q=17 prime => n=119, sigma=144. Divisors: 1,7,17,119. Differences squares-1 as earlier list; none match 7 or 17. So fails.\n\n(9,16): p=8 not prime.\n\n(12,12): p=11, q=11 (same) => n=121 (11^2). sigma(121)=1+11+121=133 not 144. So not.\n\nThus n with sigma=144 fail due to divisor condition.\n\nWhat about sigma=225 (15^2). Try find n with sigma=225. Factor 225=3^2*5^2.\n\nLook for product (p+1)(q+1)=225. Pair (9,25): p=8 not prime. (15,15): p=14 not prime. (5,45): p=4 not prime. So need maybe three primes.\n\nIf n = p*q*r, sigma = (p+1)(q+1)(r+1) = 225. Need each term >2, factorization 225=3*3*5*5. So could set p+1=3, q+1=3, r+1=25 => p=2 prime, q=2 prime, r=24 not prime. So not.\n\nMaybe n= 2*3*? Not.\n\nThus perhaps only n=1 and 3 satisfy condition.\n\nWe must be careful: n could be square like n=36? sigma not square, so out.\n\nHence sum =1+3=4.\n\nLet's double-check n=2? fails. n=6? sigma not square, so any solution must have sigma square.\n\nThus answer likely 4.", "response": "ANSWER: 4", "answer": 4, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.0994386861493695e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999969005661171, "logp_correct": -3.0994386861493695e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4731378548990413, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1693533811589987, "frac_below_-5": 0.010045518756867053, "digit_lp_min": -7.713699817657471, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 6371, "lp_end": -0.6865571141242981, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.6702524337451905e-05, "logp_nocot": -8.179225385189056, "p_nocot": 0.0002804190727878205, "uplift": 8.17922228575037}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "87932845-b928-4989-a61f-a8f0934e8497", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: In a simple graph with 300 vertices, no two vertices of the same degree are adjacent. What is the maximal possible number of edges in such a graph?\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: In a simple graph with 300 vertices, no two vertices of the same degree are adjacent. What is the maximal possible number of edges in such a graph?", "true_answer": 42550, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "87932845-b928-4989-a61f-a8f0934e8497:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 10562, 22569, 306, 261, 4705, 7465, 350, 1750, 57130, 11, 860, 7598, 22569, 8, 483, 297, 314, 220, 4095, 38360, 11, 2238, 484, 860, 1920, 39069, 38360, 679, 2684, 9882, 13, 4569, 382, 11, 395, 1753, 11165, 31432, 11, 5597, 17057, 8, 42308, 254, 5597, 6932, 741, 197203, 25, 290, 7465, 382, 392, 178297, 423, 40521, 1, 656, 9882, 26, 39069, 38360, 679, 16913, 18210, 364, 2167, 1309, 316, 44207, 3609, 22569, 1641, 495, 44762, 364, 2167, 1309, 316, 12871, 7465, 483, 472, 1991, 22569, 472, 4149, 11, 136775, 484, 7060, 13, 8820, 1309, 316, 1646, 13057, 11608, 364, 88748, 25, 158585, 553, 54912, 591, 220, 15, 316, 297, 12, 16, 350, 3228, 220, 25431, 741, 730, 261, 4705, 7465, 11, 9882, 328, 25363, 665, 413, 1062, 1432, 3518, 3352, 17227, 177015, 31060, 364, 3335, 860, 1920, 39069, 38360, 679, 2684, 9882, 11, 1815, 395, 1062, 9882, 1432, 272, 11, 38360, 483, 9882, 272, 1625, 448, 13313, 920, 350, 1750, 22569, 6200, 1373, 741, 2632, 2454, 9882, 744, 382, 13313, 364, 84787, 11, 290, 7465, 665, 413, 6177, 472, 261, 392, 7978, 328, 9882, 9778, 1, 1919, 22569, 1606, 2870, 38360, 328, 2647, 18210, 364, 42087, 25, 44207, 22569, 11, 5655, 9882, 12545, 364, 8270, 22569, 314, 220, 16, 14, 17, 214