183159, 625, 722, 4149, 8088, 22569, 553, 3333, 26, 484, 1481, 10389, 22569, 11, 625, 7064, 13, 2632, 316, 44207, 22569, 581, 1757, 3931, 472, 1991, 8088, 22569, 472, 4149, 2049, 26513, 9882, 31060, 13, 623, 5533, 137883, 651, 7890, 65414, 4335, 290, 31512, 16174, 364, 7943, 2023, 1354, 413, 261, 25697, 1919, 1236, 18210, 553, 12486, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 625, 1753, 9882, 1432, 14518, 8, 889, 581, 665, 679, 945, 22569, 1572, 290, 5533, 137883, 651, 483, 16913, 997, 16174, 30, 26632, 4566, 12384, 9882, 9778, 350, 8229, 38360, 2684, 9882, 8, 1481, 10389, 8812, 2893, 12, 39127, 2236, 1023, 553, 3518, 2684, 9882, 744, 350, 1750, 22569, 741, 72560, 8812, 22569, 553, 220, 15, 306, 137883, 651, 13, 1843, 581, 7064, 2349, 328, 261, 744, 11, 581, 1147, 8812, 2893, 12, 39127, 350, 8229, 12486, 22569, 936, 1118, 38566, 3609, 22569, 11, 813, 625, 1899, 13, 2632, 1636, 382, 316, 3357, 744, 16174, 472, 3291, 472, 4149, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 1991, 9778, 328, 2349, 220, 16, 11, 17, 35229, 5178, 13, 623, 19957, 4215, 328, 57269, 21241, 483, 1991, 3291, 7881, 326, 16913, 364, 84787, 6052, 220, 31665, 1434, 364, 58369, 6757, 484, 290, 9882, 220, 15, 38360, 553, 625, 9279, 30, 12265, 9882, 220, 15, 25363, 1481, 413, 42329, 11, 997, 2349, 314, 220, 4095, 350, 21910, 9882, 314, 297, 533, 265, 871, 9882, 220, 15, 871, 265, 314, 297, 314, 4095, 8, 625, 58866, 483, 1273, 9778, 2236, 91671, 1309, 316, 413, 13313, 591, 722, 5036, 11, 326, 39069, 38360, 6284, 679, 2684, 9882, 11, 889, 42329, 25363, 853, 860, 177015, 31060, 13, 5551, 4566, 42329, 38360, 1481, 10389, 22569, 13, 2632, 625, 27102, 364, 7942, 31512, 6664, 997, 16174, 869, 316, 220, 1494, 11, 1118, 3644, 9882, 1349, 220, 30895, 1424, 15, 11, 722, 38360, 679, 1932, 9882, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 5351, "words": 2630, "band_lo": 1132.75, "band_hi": 4531.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need maximum edges in a simple graph (no loops, no multiple edges) with n = 300 vertices, such that no two adjacent vertices have same degree. That is, for every edge uv, deg(u) ≠ deg(v). Equivalent: the graph is \"properly colored\" by degree; adjacent vertices have distinct degrees.\n\nWe need to maximize total edges under this constraint.\n\nWe need to construct graph with as many edges as possible, respecting that condition. Also need to find upper bound.\n\nObservation: Degrees are integers from 0 to n-1 (max 299). In a simple graph, degree of vertex can be any value within range considering adjacency constraints.\n\nIf no two adjacent vertices have same degree, then for any degree value d, vertices with degree d form an independent set (no edges among them). So each degree class is independent.\n\nThus, the graph can be seen as a \"graph of degree classes\" where edges only between vertices of different degrees.\n\nGoal: maximize edges, choose degree distribution.\n\nTotal edges = 1/2 Σ deg(v). We need to assign degrees to vertices (d_i) satisfying: each vertex v has degree d_i, and there are no edges among vertices of same degree.\n\nBut also the degree sequence must be graphic (realizable) and satisfy the extra condition: vertices of same degree cannot be adjacent, but that is already enforced by degree class being independent. However, we also must ensure that each vertex of degree d is adjacent to exactly d vertices, none of which share same degree as itself.\n\nThus, for each degree class D (set of vertices with degree d), each vertex in D must connect to vertices all of which are from other classes (i.e., degrees ≠ d). That's fine.\n\nGoal maximize sum of degrees given constraints. Intuitively we want many high-degree vertices, but high degree requires many neighbors, which must be distinct degree values from theirs? Actually they can connect to multiple vertices sharing same neighbor degree (as long as neighbor degree differs from theirs). For a vertex of degree d, its d neighbors must be chosen among vertices whose degrees are not d. They can have same degree among themselves.\n\nSo perhaps we can assign many vertices degree 299? impossible because each would need to be adjacent to all other vertices, including those also degree 299, which would violate condition (same degree adjacency). So at most one vertex can have degree 299, because any two such vertices would be adjacent (since they're connected to all others). But the degree-299 vertex would be adjacent to all other 299 vertices; they must have degree not equal to 299. That's okay.\n\nSimilarly, we can perhaps have many vertices of degree 298? If two vertices both degree 298, they cannot be adjacent, but they both need to be adjacent to 298 vertices each, i.e., all vertices except themselves and one non-neighbor. Since they cannot be adjacent to each other, that \"missing\" neighbor must be each other? Actually for vertex A of degree 298, there are 299 other vertices; it must miss exactly one non-neighbor. If we have another vertex B also degree 298, then A cannot be adjacent to B (since same degree) thus B is the missed neighbor for A. Similarly, B cannot be adjacent to A, fine. For any other vertex C (different degree), both A and B could be adjacent to C. So it's feasible to have many degree-298 vertices provided they are mutually nonadjacent (forming independent set) and each misses adjacency only among vertices of same degree (i.e., they must not be adjacent to any vertex of degree 298). But if we have k vertices with degree 298, each must be nonadjacent to all the other k-1 vertices of degree 298 (since same degree). Since each such vertex must have degree 298, they need to be adjacent to all other vertices except themselves and the other k-1 degree-298 vertices? Wait each vertex cannot be adjacent to any vertex of same degree => all other k-1 vertices of degree 298 must be non-neighbors. So each vertex's non-neighbors count = 1 (itself) + (k-1) = k. That would mean its degree = n-1 - (k-1) = n - k. Since n=300, degree would be 300 - k. To have degree 298, we need 300 - k = 298 → k = 2. So at most 2 vertices can have degree 298, and they must be nonadjacent to each other but adjacent to all other 298 vertices.\n\nGeneralizing: For degree d, if there are k vertices of degree d, then each such vertex cannot be adjacent to the other k-1 same-degree vertices. Thus its degree is at most n-1 - (k-1) = n - k. For it to have degree exactly d, we must have d ≤ n - k. Equality can be achieved if they are adjacent to all vertices outside its own class. So condition: d = n - k (if we maximize edges). Therefore, for a given degree d, the size k of its class must be k = n - d.\n\nThus, at most n-d vertices can have degree d (if we design them to be adjacent to all others). For example, d = 299 => k = 1 (makes sense). d=298 => k=2. d=297 => k=3, etc.\n\nThus we can possibly have for each d, up to n-d vertices of degree d, forming independent set, each adjacent to all vertices not in its class (i.e., degrees not equal to d). This structure seems feasible if the degree requirements of other vertices are consistent.\n\nIf we assign classes with sizes k_d = n - d for some set of degrees d. Note that sum over all k_d = total vertices = n = 300.\n\nSo we need to select subset of degrees D such that sum_{d in D} (n - d) = n.\n\nLet n = 300. Define t = n - d, which equals size. So t runs from 1 to n (since d from n-1 down to 0). Relationship d = n - t.\n\nThus we need a multiset of sizes t_i adding to n, where each t_i corresponds to degree d_i = n - t_i. However note that degree d_i must be nonnegative; t_i ≤ n.\n\nIf we use this construction, each vertex in class of size t (degree n - t) connects to all vertices not in its class, i.e., the complement size = n - t. Since there are n - t vertices outside its class, its degree will be exactly n - t, matches.\n\nThus the constructed graph would have edges between every pair of vertices whose degrees differ (i.e., they are from different classes). That's a complete multipartite graph where parts are degree classes. This graph indeed satisfies condition: no edge within same part (same degree). Within each part, no edges; across parts, edge exists (graph is complete multipartite). Let's verify degrees: In a complete k-partite graph, vertex in part of size s has degree n - s. So indeed each vertex's degree equals total vertices minus size of its own part. So degree value for part size s is n - s. So each part size s corresponds to degree d = n - s.\n\nHence the condition becomes that parts sizes must all be distinct? Wait degree values may repeat if different part sizes give same degree, but part sizes correspond uniquely to degree; however if we have two parts with same size, they'd have same degree, but they would be nonadjacent across parts because they are different parts but same degree, that would produce edges between same-degree vertices (since across parts they are adjacent). That would violate condition. So we cannot have two parts with same size because they'd have same degree, and edges would exist between them (since complete multipartite includes edges across all parts). So each part size must be unique.\n\nThus the vertex partition into parts (independent sets) must have distinct sizes.\n\nNow we need to partition 300 into distinct positive integers (part sizes), where each part size s corresponds to degree d = 300 - s. The total edges in such a complete multipartite graph: total possible edges = total possible pairs minus sum of internal edges (which are zero). In complete multipartite, number of edges = total pairs - sum_{parts} C(s_i,2). Since total pairs = C(300,2) = 300*299/2 = 44850.\n\nThus maximizing edges is equivalent to minimizing sum of intra-part pairs Σ C(s_i,2) = Σ s_i(s_i -1)/2.\n\nGiven we must partition 300 into distinct positive integers s_i, minimize sum of s_i choose 2. That tends to make the parts as equal as possible? Wait for fixed sum, sum of squares is minimized when numbers are as equal as possible (by convexity). Since C(s,2) approx s^2/2, minimizing sum of squares -> parts as equal as possible. However we need distinct parts, so they cannot be equal. To minimize sum of squares with distinct positive integers summing to 300, we should use a set of consecutive integers as close as possible around the average.\n\nThus to maximize edges, use partition of 300 into distinct sizes that are as balanced as possible (i.e., set of consecutive integers centered around average = 300 / k, where k is number of parts). Need to find integer k and set.\n\nLet sizes be a, a+1, ..., a+k-1 (k distinct consecutive). Sum = k*a + k(k-1)/2 = 300.\n\nWe need integer solutions with a ≥1.\n\nSo we need to find k such that 300 - k(k-1)/2 is divisible by k, giving a integer a≥1.\n\nAlso we can consider not necessarily consecutive but near equal; but the minimal sum of squares for distinct integers given sum is achieved by the most balanced set (i.e., consecutive or as close as possible). So compute possibilities.\n\nLet S = 300.\n\nFor each k from 1 up to maybe sqrt(2S) about 24, compute a = (S - k(k-1)/2)/k. Must be integer ≥1.\n\nCompute k values:\n\nk=1 -> a=300. Partition: [300] not allowed because need distinct positive integers, but only one part size 300 meaning graph has no edges (since only one part) => edges 0. Not max.\n\nk=2: a = (300 - 2*1/2) /2 = (300 -1)/2 = 299/2 = 149.5 not integer.\n\nk=3: a = (300 - 3*2/2)/3 = (300 -3)/3 = 297/3 = 99. So sizes 99,100,101 sum=300. Distinct yes.\n\nk=4: a = (300 - 4*3/2)/4 = (300 -6)/4 = 294/4 = 73.5 not int.\n\nk=5: a = (300 -5*4/2)/5 = (300 -10)/5 = 290/5 = 58. So sizes 58..62 sum? 58+59+60+61+62=300. Good.\n\nk=6: a = (300 -6*5/2)/6 = (300 -15)/6 = 285/6 = 47.5 not int.\n\nk=7: a = (300 -7*6/2)/7 = (300 -21)/7 = 279/7 = 39.857... not int.\n\nk=8: a = (300 -8*7/2)/8 = (300 -28)/8 = 272/8 = 34. So sizes 34..41 (8 numbers) sum? 34+35+36+37+38+39+40+41 = (average 37.5)*8=300. Good.\n\nk=9: a = (300 -9*8/2)/9 = (300 -36)/9 = 264/9 = 29.333 not int.\n\nk=10: a = (300 -10*9/2)/10 = (300 -45)/10 = 255/10 =25.5 not int.\n\nk=11: a = (300 -11*10/2)/11 = (300 -55)/11 =245/11 =22.2727 not int.\n\nk=12: a = (300 -12*11/2)/12 = (300 -66)/12 =234/12=19.5 not int.\n\nk=13: a = (300 -13*12/2)/13 = (300 -78)/13 =222/13 =17.0769 not int.\n\nk=14: a = (300 -14*13/2)/14 = (300 -91)/14 =209/14 =14.928 not int.\n\nk=15: a = (300 -15*14/2)/15 = (300 -105)/15 =195/15=13. So sizes 13..27? Wait k=15, a=13, sizes 13 to 27 (since a + k -1 = 13+14 =27). Sum = average (13+27)/2 =20 *15 =300 yes.\n\nk=16: a = (300 -16*15/2)/16 = (300 -120)/16 =180/16=11.25 not int.\n\nk=17: a = (300 -17*16/2)/17 = (300 -136)/17 =164/17=9.647 not int.\n\nk=18: a = (300 -18*17/2)/18 = (300 -153)/18 =147/18=8.166... not int.\n\nk=19: a = (300 -19*18/2)/19 = (300 -171)/19 =129/19=6.789 not int.\n\nk=20: a = (300 -20*19/2)/20 = (300 -190)/20 =110/20=5.5 not int.\n\nk=21: a = (300 -21*20/2)/21 = (300 -210)/21 =90/21=4.285 not int.\n\nk=22: a = (300 -22*21/2)/22 = (300 -231)/22 =69/22=3.136 not int.\n\nk=23: a = (300 -23*22/2)/23 = (300 -253)/23 =47/23=2.043 not int.\n\nk=24: a = (300 -24*23/2)/24 = (300 -276)/24 =24/24=1. So sizes 1..24 sum 300? Indeed sum 1..24 = 24*25/2 =300.\n\nk=25: a = (300 -25*24/2)/25 = (300 -300)/25 =0 => a=0 not allowed (size zero). So max k=24.\n\nThus we have partitions for k=3,5,8,15,24 possible using consecutive integers.\n\nWe need to minimize sum of C(s,2) among these feasible partitions. Usually more parts (i.e., larger k) yields smaller sizes on average => reduces intra-part pairs sum? Let's compute sum_s C(s,2) = (∑ s^2 - ∑ s)/2.\n\nGiven total sum S=300 fixed, sum_s s = 300, so minimizing sum of squares will minimize sum of C(s,2). Using more parts reduces each size leading to smaller squares, but also increases number of parts, potentially raising sum of squares? Let's compute for each feasible k.\n\nWe can compute sum of squares for consecutive integers.\n\nFormula for consecutive integers starting at a, length k: sum_{i=0}^{k-1} (a+i)^2 = k a^2 + a k(k-1) + (k-1)k(2k-1)/6.\n\nWe can compute for each k.\n\nAlternatively compute sum C(s,2) directly maybe easier.\n\nLet's compute for each viable k:\n\n1) k=24, sizes 1..24.\n\nCompute sum of C(s,2) = Σ s(s-1)/2 = (1*0 + 2*1 + 3*2 + ... + 24*23)/2.\n\nBut we can compute sum of s^2 and sum of s.\n\nSum s = 300.\n\nSum s^2 for 1..24 = n(n+1)(2n+1)/6 with n=24 => 24*25*49 /6 = (24*25=600) *49 /6 = 600*49/6 = 100*49 = 4900.\n\nThus sum C(s,2) = (sum s^2 - sum s)/2 = (4900 - 300)/2 = 4600/2 = 2300.\n\nEdges = total pairs - intra = 44850 - 2300 = 42550.\n\n2) k=15, sizes 13..27.\n\nCompute sum s = number of terms 15, average (13+27)/2=20, sum=20*15=300 good.\n\nCompute sum of squares: Use formula or compute.\n\nWe can compute using sum of squares from 1..27 minus sum up to 12.\n\nSum squares 1..27 = 27*28*55 /6. Compute 27*28=756; 756*55= 756*50+756*5=37800+3780=41580. Divide by 6 => 6930.\n\nSum squares 1..12 = 12*13*25/6 = 12*13=156; 156*25=3900; /6=650.\n\nThus sum squares 13..27 = 6930 - 650 = 6280.\n\nNow sum C(s,2) = (6280 - 300)/2 = 5980/2 = 2990.\n\nEdges = 44850 - 2990 = 41860.\n\nLess than previous (42550). So k=24 better.\n\n3) k=8, sizes 34..41.\n\nSum squares for 1..41 minus 1..33.\n\nFirst compute sum squares 1..41: n=41 => 41*42*83/6.\n\n41*42=1722; *83 = 1722*83 = 1722*80 +1722*3 = 137760 + 5166 = 142926. Divide by 6 => 23821 (since 6*23821 = 142926). Good.\n\nNow sum squares 1..33: n=33 => 33*34*67 /6.\n\n33*34=1122; *67 = 1122*67 = 1122*60 +1122*7 = 67320 + 7854 = 75174. /6 = 12529 (since 6*12529=75174). Good.\n\nThus sum squares 34..41 = 23821 -12529 = 11292.\n\nNow sum C(s,2) = (11292 - 300)/2 = 10992/2 = 5496.\n\nEdges = 44850 - 5496 = 39354. Lower.\n\n4) k=5, sizes 58..62.\n\nCompute sum squares 1..62 minus 1..57.\n\nSum squares 1..62: n=62 => 62*63*125 /6.\n\n62*63 = 3906; *125 = 3906*125 = 3906*100 +3906*25 = 390600 + 97650 = 488250. /6 = 81375.\n\nSum squares 1..57: n=57 => 57*58*115 /6.\n\n57*58 = 3306; *115 = 3306*115 = 3306*100 +3306*15 = 330600 + 49590 = 380190. /6 = 63365.\n\nThus sum squares 58..62 = 81375 - 63365 = 18010.\n\nSum C(s,2) = (18010 - 300)/2 = 17710/2 = 8855.\n\nEdges = 44850 - 8855 = 35995.\n\n5) k=3, sizes 99,100,101.\n\nCompute sum squares: 99^2=9801, 100^2=10000, 101^2=10201 sum=30002.\n\nSum C(s,2) = (30002 -300)/2 = 29702/2 = 14851.\n\nEdges = 44850 -14851 = 29999.\n\nThus best is k=24 giving edges 42550.\n\nBut need to ensure that graph with the partition 1..24 (sizes 1,2,...,24) satisfies degree condition: each vertex in part of size s has degree n - s = 300 - s. Since parts have distinct sizes, degrees are distinct: degrees are 300-1=299,298,...,276. Wait compute: sizes 1..24 produce degrees from 299 down to 276 (since 300-24=276). That's 24 distinct degrees, each class (size s) vertices share same degree (within part). Since parts are independent sets, no edges among same degree vertices. However there are edges between vertices of different degrees, okay.\n\nThus the condition satisfied. Number of edges is as computed: total pairs minus internal pairs within parts: Σ C(s,2) sum we computed 2300. So edges = 44850 - 2300 = 42550.\n\nNow, could we do better than this? Could we use non-consecutive but still distinct sizes that result in even smaller sum of squares? For given sum and distinct positive integers, the minimal sum of squares is achieved by the set that is as equal as possible, i.e., numbers as consecutive as possible starting from floor and adjusting. Indeed, the optimal set is the \"most balanced\" distinct integer set, which indeed is consecutive numbers (maybe with some shift). Since we have exact sum 300, we found a solution with consecutive numbers of length 24 (1..24). This is the maximal possible number of parts (since sum of 1..24 =300). That's the most balanced because numbers are smallest possible unique sizes to sum to 300. Having many small sizes reduces squares significantly.\n\nBut is there any possibility using 25 parts with some zeros? Not allowed (size must be positive). So max parts is 24.\n\nThus this partition likely yields minimal intra-part pairs, maximizing edges.\n\nBut need to ensure graph with these part sizes is realizable (complete multipartite). Yes, complete multipartite graph with parts of given sizes always exists.\n\nThus maximum edges = 42550.\n\nLet's double-check calculations.\n\nTotal pairs = C(300,2) = 300*299/2 = 149500/2? Wait compute: 300*299 = 89700. Divide by 2 = 44850 correct.\n\nIntra pairs sum for sizes 1..24 compute again: Σ s(s-1)/2. We computed sum squares = 4900. Let's verify sum squares of 1..24: formula n(n+1)(2n+1)/6 = 24*25*49/6. Compute 24*25=600; 600*49=29400. Divide by6 -> 4900 correct. Then sum C(s,2) = (4900 - 300)/2=4600/2=2300 correct.\n\nThus edges = 44850 - 2300 = 42550.\n\nCheck: Could we have a graph not complete multipartite but still satisfying condition with more edges? Suppose not all possible cross edges are present; that would reduce edges, not increase. So to maximize edges we should include as many cross edges as possible while maintaining degree constraints. The complete multipartite seems maximal given the partition sizes.\n\nBut could there be a scenario where some degrees are missing (i.e., not every degree value appears) but we can have more edges than the complete multipartite with distinct part sizes? Consider having larger degree classes (more vertices same degree) would reduce internal non-edges because they are within same degree class (no edges). Already internal edges are 0 in multipartite. If we increase size of a class, we add internal non-edges (more missing edges), which reduces total edges, so not good. So best is to keep class sizes as small as possible, i.e., many classes of size 1,2,... etc. The minimal sum of squares occurs with many small parts and distinct.\n\nThus answer 42550.\n\nLet's ensure that the degree 0 vertices are not allowed? Since degree 0 vertex would be isolated, part size = 300 (since degree = n - s => degree 0 => s = n =300) not feasible with other classes because they'd need to be independent from all others, and adjacent vertices cannot have same degree, but isolated vertex has no adjacency constraints. However having isolated vertices would reduce edges. So not beneficial.\n\nOur partition includes part sizes up to 24, which give degree min 276 >0, all vertices have high degree.\n\nThus final answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 42550", "answer": 42550, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.0265570981337078e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999979734449553, "logp_correct": -2.0265570981337078e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4763807394906125, "cot_lp_min64": -1.349479512672076, "frac_below_-5": 0.008245877061469266, "digit_lp_min": -5.132156848907471, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0010674261720851064, "n_cot_tokens": 5336, "lp_end": -0.0513395331799984, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.576278118089249e-07, "logp_nocot": -17.393935850501286, "p_nocot": 2.7919628905208813e-08, "uplift": 17.393933823944188}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "fc9a7564-9aaa-40fa-a6f7-20ce9df71767", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: We are given some three-element subsets of $\\{1,2, \\dots ,n\\}$ for which any two of them have at most one common element. We call a subset of $\\{1,2, \\dots ,n\\}$ nice if it doesn't include any of the given subsets. If, no matter how the three-element subsets are selected initially, we can add one more element to every 29-element nice subset while keeping it nice, find the minimum value of $n$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: We are given some three-element subsets of $\\{1,2, \\dots ,n\\}$ for which any two of them have at most one common element. We call a subset of $\\{1,2, \\dots ,n\\}$ nice if it doesn't include any of the given subsets. If, no matter how the three-element subsets are selected initially, we can add one more element to every 29-element nice subset while keeping it nice, find the minimum value of $n$.", "true_answer": 436, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "fc9a7564-9aaa-40fa-a6f7-20ce9df71767:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 290, 4792, 364, 2167, 679, 1236, 5801, 328, 220, 18, 73011, 165377, 328, 354, 16, 35229, 11, 77, 92, 483, 290, 4322, 484, 1062, 1920, 328, 1373, 64353, 306, 540, 1645, 1001, 3577, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 1023, 1625, 261, 23876, 220, 18, 30296, 11633, 22725, 7978, 741, 1416, 13174, 261, 58845, 328, 354, 16, 35229, 11, 77, 92, 316, 413, 392, 36202, 1, 538, 480, 2226, 625, 10232, 1062, 328, 2617, 4335, 220, 18, 73011, 165377, 472, 261, 58845, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 480, 382, 13313, 920, 483, 5618, 316, 484, 22725, 7978, 741, 623, 7060, 25, 3004, 7165, 1495, 290, 220, 18, 73011, 165377, 553, 6386, 25998, 350, 24110, 316, 290, 10610, 7480, 42002, 72554, 16, 936, 581, 665, 1147, 