, 14081, 261, 58845, 328, 290, 620, 920, 11, 110841, 21130, 36280, 13, 1843, 1354, 382, 860, 2238, 11165, 11, 1815, 323, 2023, 413, 5768, 13, 2632, 2454, 12229, 25363, 2804, 413, 306, 540, 5153, 1001, 11165, 483, 1920, 38360, 591, 336, 13, 623, 5151, 57927, 18305, 13, 30456, 1354, 413, 448, 11165, 483, 323, 326, 1001, 25363, 591, 336, 326, 3613, 591, 12229, 920, 30, 4569, 11165, 1481, 413, 45298, 6772, 336, 6, 35353, 103, 354, 85, 4180, 889, 480, 8895, 1920, 12229, 38360, 11, 625, 78655, 6772, 336, 18085, 103, 90, 85, 16255, 2214, 336, 18085, 103, 90, 85, 92, 316, 10232, 290, 6508, 41563, 11, 722, 3407, 38360, 2804, 413, 306, 336, 18085, 103, 90, 85, 16255, 1843, 290, 11165, 6664, 3613, 12229, 25363, 286, 625, 306, 336, 18085, 103, 90, 85, 4180, 1815, 625, 78655, 6772, 11, 813, 2226, 625, 4355, 323, 13, 2632, 1606, 22569, 483, 2973, 1273, 38360, 306, 336, 4355, 323, 13, 23586, 8155, 7060, 25, 2454, 12229, 25363, 33063, 316, 540, 5153, 1001, 11165, 483, 9707, 1920, 38360, 591, 336, 364, 84787, 1039, 11608, 20862, 364, 16577, 1890, 25, 1412, 538, 1236, 12229, 25363, 382, 8807, 997, 328, 945, 1572, 1001, 2238, 11165, 350, 127306, 24702, 741, 21926, 25435, 889, 2928, 2454, 12229, 25363, 24267, 316, 1236, 10610, 26, 105230, 5251, 16913, 12229, 38360, 395, 23876, 7060, 364, 84787, 65414, 2086, 328, 12229, 38360, 382, 540, 1645, 2086, 328, 24702, 13, 19233, 297, 72554, 220, 33488, 4149, 316, 679, 261, 65414, 13313, 920, 328, 2349, 220, 2270, 13, 2214, 297, 28, 33488, 1354, 382, 261, 9770, 472, 13394, 25, 14975, 2454, 12229, 25363, 316, 261, 16913, 10610, 13, 1328, 5882, 472, 1701, 472, 581, 679, 9707, 220, 31541, 12229, 38360, 26, 1309, 9707, 220, 31541, 16913, 12229, 38360, 350, 786, 297, 28, 33488, 741, 21926, 25435, 364, 84787, 19957, 297, 484, 49460, 7060, 382, 220, 39437, 364, 4383, 484, 7060, 392, 1252, 1920, 328, 1373, 679, 540, 1645, 1001, 5355, 3577, 1, 18305, 395, 495, 9770, 25, 22569, 11095, 316, 24702, 350, 64, 17568, 8, 591, 336, 2932, 5746, 12229, 25363, 323, 22305, 378, 16255, 13311, 2647, 22569, 5143, 540, 1645, 1001, 25363, 25, 1023, 2023, 5143, 261, 25363, 261, 538, 24702, 350, 64, 17568, 8, 326, 350, 64, 22261, 8, 5143, 261, 11, 42002, 354, 64, 16255, 21926, 9279, 13, 3164, 6284, 5143, 2973, 261, 326, 287, 2236, 484, 1481, 413, 2684, 10610, 21482, 316, 1920, 12229, 38360, 11, 1118, 581, 8397, 13, 2632, 23876, 7060, 23121, 364, 84787, 6052, 220, 39437, 364, 2167, 2804, 4733, 392, 160761, 25, 220, 39437, 4050], "n_tokens": 2323, "words": 1766, "band_lo": 812.25, "band_hi": 3249.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse the problem.\n\nWe have some collection of 3-element subsets of {1,...,n} with the property that any two of them intersect in at most one element (i.e., they form a linear 3-uniform hypergraph). We define a subset of {1,...,n} to be \"nice\" if it does not contain any of those given 3-element subsets as a subset (i.e., it is independent set with respect to that hypergraph). The condition: No matter how the 3-element subsets are selected initially (subject to the pairwise intersection ≤1), we can add one more element to every 29-element nice subset while keeping it nice. That is: For any such hypergraph, for any nice set of size 29, there exists at least one element not in the set that can be added to maintain niceness (i.e., the expanded set size 30 still does not contain any prohibited triple). Wait: \"we can add one more element to every 29-element nice subset while keeping it nice\" means: For each 29-element nice subset S, there exists an element x ∉ S such that S ∪ {x} is also nice. The condition must hold for any possible hypergraph (any family of triples with pairwise intersections ≤1). And we want the minimal n such that this holds.\n\nEssentially: Find minimal n such that for any linear 3-uniform hypergraph H on vertex set [n], the independence number α(H) is at least 30? Not exactly. Because we require that any independent set of size 29 is not maximal; i.e., can be extended to size 30 independent set. That implies that there is no maximal independent set of size 29. Equivalent: The hypergraph does not have any independent set of size 29 that is maximal.\n\nThus we need n such that any linear 3-uniform hypergraph on n vertices has no maximal independent set of size 29. Need minimal n guaranteeing this property.\n\nIf we can construct a hypergraph with a maximal independent set of size 29, then n is too small. So we need minimal n where impossible.\n\nThus we need to find maximal possible size of a maximal independent set in linear 3-uniform hypergraph on n vertices, and find n where this max < 30 (i.e., <29? Actually we want guarantee that max independent set size > 29, or at least any size 29 independent set is not maximal). So we need to guarantee no maximal independent set of size 29. Equivalent to: α(H) ≥ 30? Not sufficient because there could be maximal independent sets of size 29 even though there is larger independent set elsewhere. But if there is any maximal independent set of size 29, condition fails because that specific set cannot be extended. So we need to ensure that every independent set of size 29 can be extended. That's stronger.\n\nBut perhaps the worst-case hypergraph that creates a maximal independent set of size 29 is a \"Steiner system\" type? Let's think: Maximal independent set means its complement (i.e., the set of vertices not in the independent set) is a vertex cover (hits every edge). So a maximal independent set of size k corresponds to a vertex cover of size n-k that is minimal (i.e., removing any vertex from the cover leaves an uncovered edge). So our condition is that there is no minimal vertex cover of size n-29.\n\nThus we need n such that any linear 3-uniform hypergraph has no minimal vertex cover of size n-29.\n\nAlternative: Equivalent to: The transversal number τ(H) (size of smallest vertex cover) might be small, but we want prevent existence of minimal vertex cover of size exactly n-29? Wait minimal vertex cover can be larger than τ. There could be minimal covers of size larger than minimum. We need to guarantee that there is no minimal cover of size n-29.\n\nWe are looking for minimal n where for any linear 3-uniform hypergraph on n vertices, every minimal vertex cover has size ≤ n-30 (i.e., at most n-30). Because if there is a minimal cover of size n-29, its complement is a maximal independent set of size 29, violating condition.\n\nThus we need to ensure that any linear 3-uniform hypergraph has a vertex whose removal from any cover of size n-29 still leaves a cover? Hard.\n\nMaybe easier to consider constructing hypergraphs with a maximal independent set of size 29: need to find for given n the largest possible size of a maximal independent set (the independence number of a maximal independent set) given the linear condition.\n\nThis resembles problem of \"largest possible size of a maximal independent set in a linear 3-uniform hypergraph on n vertices\". For graphs (edges size 2), known results: maximal independent set can be as large as floor(n/2) maybe? For hypergraphs size 3, maybe larger.\n\nWe can try to construct hypergraph such that we have a set S of size 29, and each vertex outside S belongs to an edge that is entirely within the complement? Wait to make S maximal independent, each vertex v not in S must be part of some edge entirely contained in S ∪ {v}. Since S is independent, no edge fully inside S. To block adding v, there must be some edge e such that e⊆S∪{v} and v∈e. Since e size 3, the other two vertices of e must be in S. So for each exterior vertex v, there must be at least one pair {a,b}⊆S such that {a,b,v} is a prohibited triple. Moreover, the hypergraph must be linear: any two triples intersect in ≤1 element.\n\nThus the collection of edges that involve each external vertex v correspond to distinct pairs from S, and these edges must not intersect each other in more than one element. Since each edge with external vertex v contains exactly two vertices from S, and each edge is unique.\n\nIf two external vertices v and w share a pair {a,b} from S, then edges {a,b,v} and {a,b,w} intersect in two vertices {a,b} => not allowed. So each pair of vertices from S can be used in at most one edge (with at most one external vertex). Therefore there is an injection from external vertices to unordered pairs of S. So number of external vertices ≤ number of pairs in S = C(29,2) = 406.\n\nThus we can have at most 406 external vertices, i.e., n ≤ 29 + 406 = 435. If n = 435, we could have edges for each pair paired with a distinct external vertex, giving a maximal independent set S size 29. Need also ensure that edges among external vertices themselves do not violate linear condition. But there are none, only edges defined as above (triples containing two S and one external). Also need to ensure that there are no edges entirely among externals (since that would intersect S's edges maybe but could cause additional constraints). We can simply define hypergraph to consist exactly of these edges; the linear condition holds because any two edges share at most one element: edges from different pairs intersect at most in one S vertex (if the pairs share a single S element) – they intersect in exactly one vertex if the pairs share a vertex, else zero, which is acceptable.\n\nThus we have constructed a linear 3-uniform hypergraph on up to 435 vertices that has a maximal independent set of size 29. So for n up to 435, it's possible to have a violation. Therefore minimal n must be at least 436.\n\nNow need to verify whether for n=436, the condition always holds (i.e., cannot have such a maximal independent set of size 29). Using the pair injection argument, external vertices count ≤406, so if n>435 then external vertices >406 so pigeonhole principle forces some pair to be used by at least two external vertices, which would create two edges intersecting in two vertices → not allowed. Hence no linear hypergraph can have a maximal independent set of size 29 when n >= 436? Wait need to ensure that any linear hypergraph with a maximal independent set S of size 29 leads to external vertices count ≤C(29,2). Indeed each external vertex must be associated to a unique unordered pair of S for which the edge {a,b,v} exists. Thus external vertices ≤406. Therefore if n-29 > 406 => n > 435, then impossible. So for n≥436, any linear hypergraph cannot have maximal independent set of size 29. Therefore condition holds for n=436 and above. So minimal n is 436.\n\nBut we must also consider possibility of hypergraph where some external vertex is blocked not by a triple consisting of two vertices from S, but perhaps we could block adding v via multiple edges overlapping. However to prevent adding v, it's sufficient that there exists at least one edge within S∪{v} containing v, because if v is added to S, that edge becomes a subset of the new set, violating niceness. If there is no such edge, then v could be added. So each external vertex must be in at least one edge with two vertices from S. The above reasoning holds. Could there be an edge with v and one vertex from S and another from external set? That edge would be partially inside S' ∪ {v}, but it contains two external vertices, not wholly inside S∪{v}. For S∪{v} to contain the entire triple, all three vertices must be in S∪{v}. If the edge includes another external vertex w not in S∪{v}, then not wholly inside, so does not block v. So only edges with both other vertices in S block v. Thus necessary condition: each external vertex belongs to at least one edge with exactly two vertices from S.\n\nThus our bound stands.\n\nEdge case: what if some external vertex is itself part of more than one such edge (different pairs). That's okay but still each external vertex maps to some pair; uniqueness across distinct external vertices for linear condition.\n\nThus maximal number of external vertices is at most number of pairs. Therefore n ≤ 435 possible to have a maximal independent set of size 29. For n=435 there is a construction as described: assign each external vertex to a distinct pair. This works as long as we have exactly 406 external vertices; need exactly 406 distinct external vertices (so n=435). That's okay.\n\nThus minimal n that guarantees condition is 436.\n\nCheck that condition \"any two of them have at most one common element\" holds for this construction: edges correspond to pairs (a,b) from S plus unique external vertex v_{ab}. Two different edges share at most one vertex: they could share a vertex a if pairs (a,b) and (a,c) share a, intersection {a}. That's allowed. They cannot share both a and b because that would be same pair assigned to two external vertices, which we avoid. So linear condition satisfied.\n\nThus answer 436.\n\nWe must output \"ANSWER: 436\".", "response": "ANSWER: 436", "answer": 436, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.503393488950678e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999974966096445, "logp_correct": -2.503393488950678e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6034454424204027, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3141098561340652, "frac_below_-5": 0.010827197921177999, "digit_lp_min": -5.9267377853393555, "answer_first_mention_lp": -7.986990567587782e-06, "n_cot_tokens": 2309, "lp_end": -7.152531907195225e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -4.172316494077677e-06, "logp_nocot": -18.569404244168254, "p_nocot": 8.618073715106578e-09, "uplift": 18.569401740774765}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "25f28d8f-466c-48a1-9eec-6f34287c3047", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: In the plane, $2013$ red points and $2014$ blue points are marked so that no three of the marked points are collinear. One needs to draw $k$ lines not passing through the marked points and dividing the plane into several regions. The goal is to do it in such a way that no region contains points of both colors. Find the minimal value of $k$ such that the goal is attainable for every possible configuration of $4027$ points. Please provide the value of k.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: In the plane, $2013$ red points and $2014$ blue points are marked so that no three of the marked points are collinear. One needs to draw $k$ lines not passing through the marked points and dividing the plane into several regions. The goal is to do it in such a way that no region contains points of both colors. Find the minimal value of $k$ such that the goal is attainable for every possible configuration of $4027$ points. Please provide the value of k.", "true_answer": 2013, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "25f28d8f-466c-48a1-9eec-6f34287c3047:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 19957, 2086, 328, 8698, 350, 2878, 21178, 1819, 1062, 2438, 8, 316, 13574, 3592, 326, 9861, 5571, 306, 16499, 11, 860, 7967, 8895, 2973, 10803, 13, 197203, 316, 581, 1309, 316, 31512, 16499, 1511, 21043, 2454, 15683, 5571, 328, 1606, 1001, 3089, 350, 267, 8213, 741, 1416, 665, 2475, 8698, 15907, 350, 2878, 1819, 5571, 741, 1416, 2804, 16218, 395, 1062, 21868, 328, 220, 667, 18, 3592, 326, 220, 667, 19, 9861, 5571, 306, 5985, 4010, 350, 1750, 3407, 996, 41454, 741, 4614, 382, 19957, 2086, 328, 8698, 3759, 306, 20486, 1890, 364, 2167, 1309, 316, 13574, 1920, 2438, 11982, 236