{2}) * (1/x^{2}) ln(1 + x^{2}) dx = ∫_0^1 ( ln(1 + x^{2}) )/(1 - x^{2}) dx.\n\nThus B = I1 + I2 = -ln2 ∫_0^1 dx/(1 - x^{2}) + ∫_0^1 ln(1 + x^{2})/(1 - x^{2}) dx.\n\nCombine into single integral: B = ∫_0^1 [ ln(1 + x^{2}) - ln 2 ] / (1 - x^{2}) dx.\n\nThus S = -B = -∫_0^1 [ ln(1 + x^{2}) - ln 2 ] / (1 - x^{2}) dx = ∫_0^1 [ ln 2 - ln(1 + x^{2}) ] / (1 - x^{2}) dx.\n\nNow compute integral I = ∫_0^1 (ln 2 - ln(1 + x^{2})) / (1 - x^{2}) dx.\n\nSimplify: I = ∫_0^1 \\frac{\\ln \\frac{2}{1 + x^{2}}}{1 - x^{2}} dx.\n\nLet’s evaluate.\n\nMake substitution x = tan θ? Not good because range 0 to 1 corresponds θ from 0 to π/4.\n\nLet x = tan θ, then 1 + x^2 = 1 + tan^2 θ = sec^2 θ. Also 1 - x^2 = 1 - tan^2 θ = (cos^2 θ - sin^2 θ)/cos^2 θ = cos 2θ / cos^2 θ = (cos 2θ)/cos^2 θ. dx = sec^2 θ dθ.\n\nCompute integrand: numerator ln(2/(1+x^2)) = ln(2 / sec^2 θ) = ln(2 cos^2 θ) = ln 2 + ln(cos^2 θ) = ln 2 + 2 ln cos θ.\n\nDenominator 1 - x^2 = (cos 2θ) / cos^2 θ.\n\nThus fraction = (ln 2 + 2 ln cos θ) / ( (cos 2θ)/cos^2 θ ) = (ln 2 + 2 ln cos θ) * (cos^2 θ / cos 2θ).\n\ndx = sec^2 θ dθ = 1/ cos^2 θ dθ.\n\nMultiplying integrand by dx gives:\n\nI = ∫_{θ=0}^{π/4} (ln 2 + 2 ln cos θ) * (cos^2 θ / cos 2θ) * (1/ cos^2 θ) dθ = ∫_0^{π/4} (ln 2 + 2 ln cos θ) / cos 2θ dθ.\n\nGreat simplification: I = ∫_0^{π/4} (ln 2 + 2 ln cos θ) / cos 2θ dθ.\n\nNow note cos 2θ = 2 cos^2 θ -1.\n\nBut maybe integrate by splitting: I = ln 2 ∫_0^{π/4} dθ / cos 2θ + 2 ∫_0^{π/4} (ln cos θ) / cos 2θ dθ.\n\nCompute integral J1 = ∫_0^{π/4} dθ / cos 2θ. Use substitution u = 2θ => du = 2 dθ => dθ = du/2. Limits u: 0 to π/2. Then J1 = ∫_0^{π/2} (du/2) / cos u = (1/2) ∫_0^{π/2} sec u du = (1/2) [ln | sec u + tan u| ]_0^{π/2}. As u→π/2-, sec u → ∞, tan u → ∞, sum ~ huge, log diverges. So J1 diverges. But expectation total I finite due to cancellation with second integral which also diverges.\n\nThus we need evaluate combined integral perhaps analytically.\n\nConsider combine: I = ∫_0^{π/4} \\frac{\\ln (2 cos^2 θ)}{cos 2θ} dθ