055, 625, 9197, 2747, 28336, 13, 2632, 860, 364, 84787, 1606, 297, 1919, 17116, 8895, 41974, 104042, 1606, 14836, 3291, 13, 41021, 28035, 297, 28, 20, 25, 17116, 3536, 20, 10, 17, 11876, 17, 28, 18, 13, 20, 11, 20, 60, 6664, 9197, 220, 20, 1606, 11, 41974, 30, 220, 20, 12, 17, 28, 18, 9197, 14531, 13, 30255, 29169, 11, 889, 11965, 28336, 5192, 316, 220, 17, 14, 18, 51159, 13, 30391, 1309, 1217, 4951, 51159, 395, 220, 17, 11, 18, 13, 54458, 1309, 297, 2238, 484, 1888, 51159, 23275, 323, 1359, 2406, 19406, 395, 722, 275, 364, 2167, 665, 2075, 23864, 395, 297, 28, 22, 3505, 13, 13886, 17098, 10112, 297, 28, 1055, 30, 889, 220, 1055, 625, 9197, 11, 17116, 3536, 1055, 10, 17, 11876, 17, 28, 23, 13, 20, 11, 1055, 60, 6664, 104042, 220, 994, 11, 1311, 13, 220, 994, 625, 41974, 11, 7111, 364, 77, 28, 858, 17116, 1424, 702, 13, 20, 6664, 220, 994, 11, 1311, 11, 1422, 11, 858, 26, 220, 994, 625, 41974, 11, 7111, 364, 84787, 1606, 31061, 553, 1261, 297, 72554, 220, 17, 79, 13731, 30, 4037, 3239, 364, 34013, 1606, 297, 28, 22, 5882, 364, 4383, 297, 28, 18, 30, 17116, 1424, 17, 13, 20, 6664, 220, 18, 1118, 382, 41974, 30, 220, 18, 12, 17, 28, 16, 625, 9197, 26, 28336, 364, 84787, 6052, 6960, 297, 28, 22, 1606, 364, 2167, 1757, 1217, 1746, 297, 28, 21, 30, 17116, 1424, 19, 6664, 220, 20, 13, 220, 20, 41974, 30, 14531, 350, 18, 741, 3072, 1309, 1217, 679, 51159, 328, 220, 17, 4951, 30, 1888, 2684, 472, 297, 28, 20, 350, 17713, 8, 483, 323, 17, 28, 18, 30, 41021, 23864, 323, 17, 7, 21, 19406, 28, 8350, 21, 14, 17, 28, 18, 10, 8350, 21, 14, 19, 28, 16, 314, 19, 13, 1888, 853, 220, 17, 885, 591, 73121, 25, 220, 23, 8967, 220, 18, 11, 220, 702, 8967, 16, 11, 3609, 220, 19, 30, 24305, 1888, 395, 104042, 869, 316, 20, 350, 17, 11, 18, 11, 20, 8, 3931, 73121, 25, 350, 17, 11, 18, 25987, 20, 11, 350, 17, 11, 20, 25987, 22, 11, 350, 18, 11, 20, 25987, 23, 871, 1606, 1001, 1952, 4215, 220, 23, 350, 17, 61, 18, 8, 8967, 220, 18, 5432, 365, 11, 625, 220, 19, 13, 2632, 323, 17, 28, 18, 464, 19, 11, 7111, 364, 84787, 297, 28, 22, 1606, 364, 58369, 413, 21716, 364, 17045, 25, 2127, 314, 220, 22, 13], "n_tokens": 5670, "words": 2700, "band_lo": 1073.5, "band_hi": 4294.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to find all n>2 such that n! divides product over all unordered pairs of primes p<q ≤ n of (p+q). So consider product over all pairs of distinct primes up to n inclusive.\n\nLet primes up to n: p1<p2<...<pk where pk ≤ n. Number of primes ≤ n is π(n)=k.\n\nThe product is Π_{1≤i<j≤k} (p_i + p_j). Need n! | that product.\n\nObserve v_p (exponent of prime p) in n! is floor(n/p)+ floor(n/p^2)+...\n\nNeed to ensure each prime ≤ n appears enough times in product.\n\nWe can attempt to bound. For each prime r ≤ n, we must have exponent of r in product at least v_r(n!).\n\nThe product involves sums of two primes, may have many factors divisible by r.\n\nGoal: find n such that condition holds.\n\nLet's try small n.\n\nCompute for n=3: primes ≤3 are 2,3. Pairs: (2,3) sum=5. Product=5. n! =6. 6 does not divide 5 => no.\n\nn=4: primes up to 4: 2,3. Same product=5. 4! =24. 24 does not divide 5.\n\nn=5: primes up to5: 2,3,5. Pairs: (2,3)=5, (2,5)=7, (3,5)=8. Product=5*7*8=280. 5! =120. Does 120 divide 280? 280/120=2.333, not integer. So no.\n\nn=6: primes up to6: 2,3,5. Same product 280. 6! =720. 720 does not divide 280.\n\nn=7: primes up to7: 2,3,5,7. Compute all pairs sums:\n2+3=5\n2+5=7\n2+7=9\n3+5=8\n3+7=10\n5+7=12\nProduct =5*7*9*8*10*12 = let's compute: 5*7=35, *9=315, *8=2520, *10=25200, *12=302400. So product =302,400. 7! =5040. Does 5040 divide 302400? 302400/5040 = 60. So yes! So n=7 works.\n\nn=8: primes up to8 are 2,3,5,7. Same product 302400. 8! =40320. Does it divide? 302400/40320 = 7.5 not integer. So fails.\n\nn=9: primes up to9: 2,3,5,7. Same product 302400. 9! =362880. 302400 < 362880 => impossible. So fails for all n≥9? Wait product grows slowly; n! grows faster. But adding new primes adds more factors. For n=10, primes: 2,3,5,7. same. n! huge > product. So only possible n where n! <= product. Let's see n=10 product same but n! 3,628,800 > product. So fails.\n\nHowever n may increase to include new primes: at n=11, primes up to11: add 11. Then product includes new pair sums with 11: (2,11)=13, (3,11)=14, (5,11)=16, (7,11)=18. Also we must include previous pairs among old primes (6 pairs). So total pairs = C(5,2)=10. Product becomes previous product 302400 *13*14*16*18. Compute factor:13*14=182; 16*18=288; 182*288= (182*200)+(182*88)=36400+16016=52416. Multiply 302400*52416 ≈ 302,400 * 52,416 = need exact maybe not needed, but huge. Let's compute approximate: 3e5*5.2e4 = ~1.56e10. n! for n=11 is 39916800 ≈ 4e7. Product likely larger, divisibility may hold? Need exponent check.\n\nWe need to test condition for n=11 maybe works.\n\nBut we must check for all n>2, maybe only n=7 and 11?, etc.\n\nLet's attempt reasoning: For n large, the product grows as roughly exp( sum_{i<j} log(p_i+p_j) ). Asymptotically, number of primes up to n ≈ n /log n = k. Number of pairs ≈ k^2/2. Each term about O(n). So log product ≈ (k^2/2) * log n ~ (n^2/(2 log^2 n)) * log n = n^2/(2 log n). Meanwhile log n! ~ n log n. So product exponent grows like n^2/(2 log n) which dominates n log n for sufficiently large n. So product eventually exceeds n!. So maybe infinitely many n satisfy? But need divisibility, not just size.\n\nNeed each prime power dividing n! appears enough in product. Since product contains many factors, maybe eventually always satisfied beyond some n. Need to test further.\n\nBut compute for n=11.\n\nLet's compute exponent for each prime r ≤11 in product.\n\nList primes up to 11: 2,3,5,7,11.\n\nWe need v_r(product) >= v_r(11!). Let's compute v_2(11!) = floor(11/2)+floor(11/4)+floor(11/8)=5+2+1=8. v_3= floor(11/3)+floor(11/9)=3+1=4. v_5=2, v_7=1, v_11=1.\n\nNow compute product factors: all sums of two distinct primes among set.\n\nLet's list sums:\n\n2+3=5\n2+5=7\n2+7=9\n2+11=13\n3+5=8\n3+7=10\n3+11=14\n5+7=12\n5+11=16\n7+11=18\n\nNow factor each:\n\n5=5\n7=7\n9=3^2\n13=13 (prime > n, irrelevant for our prime considerations)\n8=2^3\n10=2*5\n14=2*7\n12=2^2*3\n16=2^4\n18=2*3^2\n\nNow compute total exponent for each prime ≤11.\n\nCount 2's: from 8 (2^3) gives 3, from 10 gives 1, from 14 gives1, from12 gives2, from16 gives4, from18 gives1. Total =3+1+1+2+4+1=12.\n\nThus v_2(product)=12 ≥8 good.\n\n3's: from 9 gives2, from12 gives1, from18 gives2. Total=5. Also any from 15? not present. So v_3(product)=5 ≥4 good.\n\n5's: from 5 gives1, from10 gives1. Also maybe from 15 none. So total=2 ≥2 good.\n\n7's: from7 gives1, from14 gives1. total=2 ≥1.\n\n11's: none of sums are multiples of 11? Check 22 maybe not present. So v_11(product)=0, but need v_11(11!)=1. So fails because product has no factor 11. Indeed none of sums p+q with p,q primes up to 11 equals multiple of 11. Check pairs: 2+...9? Actually 2+? = 11? 2+9 not prime; 3+8; 5+6; 7+4; 11+0. So no. So n=11 fails.\n\nThus presence of prime equal to n may cause issue: for prime n itself, we need a factor divisible by that prime in product. That would require p+q ≡0 mod n with p,q primes ≤ n. Since n is prime, only possibility is p+q = n or 2n etc. Both p,q < n, sum n requires one of them to be n-p, which is also prime. For n prime >2, can we have representation as sum of two primes ≤ n? Goldbach-like. For odd prime n, need representation as sum of two primes (both odd) gives even sum, so not possible unless one prime is 2 (even) and other is n-2 which is odd. So need n-2 also prime. So condition for prime n: n-2 must be prime. That's twin prime pair. So for n=7, n-2=5 prime, so we have pair (2,5) sum 7, giving factor divisible by 7. Indeed product includes 7 from 2+5. And also 7 appears from other sums (like 7+...?), but at least one. For n=11, n-2=9 not prime, so product lacks factor 11. Hence fails. For n=13, n-2=11 prime, maybe works?\n\nLet's test n=13 (prime, n-2=11 prime). primes up to13: 2,3,5,7,11,13 (k=6). Need product of sums of all 15 pairs. Need to ensure each prime ≤13 has enough exponent.\n\nFirst check prime 13: need factor multiple of 13. Since 13 is prime, the only way is pair sum =13 or 26 etc. Pair (2,11)=13 includes 13. So we have factor 13. Good.\n\nNow need to ensure exponent for others. Likely enough.\n\nBut must also consider prime 11: need factor multiple of 11. Since 11 appears as prime ≤ n, we need at least one sum divisible by 11. Possible sums: 2+9 not, 3+8 not,5+6 not,7+4 not,13+? -2? Actually 13+? negative. Also 11 itself could be sum of two primes: 2+9 not; 3+8;5+6; etc none. To get multiple of 11 maybe 22 etc: pair sums =22: possibilities (11,11) not allowed distinct? p<q so distinct, cannot both be 11. (2,20) not prime, etc. So maybe no factor 11? Wait we have pair (2,9) invalid, (3,19) >13. So maybe no sum multiple of 11. However note that prime 11 could divide sum of two other primes: e.g., 5+13=18 not; 7+13=20 not; 3+13=16; 2+13=15; 5+7=12; 5+11=16; 7+11=18; 3+11=14; 2+11=13 (not multiple of 11). None are multiples of 11. So exponent of 11 in product may be 0, but v_11(13!) = floor(13/11)=1. So fails. Therefore any prime n where n-2 is prime gives factor for n, but internal smaller prime p may still lack factor. In particular prime 11 itself needs factor multiple of 11. For n=13, we don't have any sum divisible by 11. So fails.\n\nThus condition seems to require that for each prime p ≤ n, there exists at least one pair of primes (a,b) with a<b≤n such that p | (a+b). In other words, p divides some sum.\n\nIf p is odd prime, to have p | a+b, we can have a ≡ -b mod p. Since a,b<n≤... we can try a = p - b . But both need to be primes ≤ n, distinct.\n\nObservation: For each odd prime p ≤ n, if p-2 is prime, then a=2, b=p-2 works (sum = p). So suffices that p-2 prime. But not for all primes p. Need at least one representation of some multiple of p as sum of two primes ≤ n. That is reminiscent of \"Goldbach's weak\" for p, but we can also consider 2p (even). For odd p, 2p is even and could be sum of two odd primes (both odd). So if there exist primes a,b ≤ n with a+b=2p. Since a,b odd, that's plausible.\n\nParameter: For prime p ≤ n, need a,b primes ≤ n, distinct, such that a+b ≡0 mod p. Since a+b ≤ 2n, the only multiples possible are p or 2p (since 3p>2n for p > n/2). So need either a+b = p or =2p.\n\nWhen p ≤ n, p is ≤ n, so a+b=p requires a and b less than p, sum odd? If p is odd, one must be 2. So requires p-2 prime (twin). If p>2, sum=2p: need two odd primes adding to 2p, each <2p. Since they must be ≤ n, and n ≥ p, that's okay if n ≥ p and also each prime ≤ n. So we need representation of 2p as sum of two primes ≤ n. Since 2p is even, Goldbach conjecture asserts it is sum of two primes (for p>2). For small p maybe works. However we also need distinct primes. Could be same? a≠b? The product over p<q, so distinct primes. So need two different primes adding to 2p. Example p=5, 2p=10 =3+7 (both primes distinct). So gives factor 10 divisible by 5. Good. For p=7, 14=3+11 (both ≤ n if n≥11). For n=7, 14 uses prime 11 > n, not allowed. So at n=7, we used sum =7 (2+5) to get factor divisible by 7.\n\nThus for prime p up to n, we need either twin condition (p-2 prime) or Goldbach representation within bound n (both ≤ n). Since n may be less than one of the primes needed.\n\nSo for n=7, primes: 2,3,5,7. Check each:\n\np=2: need factor multiple of 2. Many sums even: 3+5=8 etc.\n\np=3: need multiple of3: 2+7=9 works.\n\np=5: need multiple of5: 2+3=5 works (or 2+13 but not needed).\n\np=7: need multiple of7: 2+5=7 works (twin).\n\nAll satisfied.\n\nNow for n=8, same primes, but 7 still okay, 5 etc. Why n=8 fails? Because exponent of 2 maybe insufficient? Let's compute v_2(8!)= floor(8/2)+4+2+1=7. v_2(product)= we earlier computed product for n=7 product =302400. Let's compute exponent of 2 in that product (we had for n=7 earlier). Let's compute quickly: list sums for primes up to7: we have earlier factorization maybe compute exponents:\n\nSums:5 (none),7 (none),9 (3^2),8 (2^3),10 (2*5),12 (2^2*3). Also 5+7=12 (already), 2+7=9, 3+7=10, etc. Let's tally 2's: 8 gives 3, 10 gives1, 12 gives2. Total 6. Also maybe 14 not present as 7+7 not allowed. So exponent 6 <7, thus fails for n=8 (v2 deficiency). So condition fails because not enough 2's as n increases but prime set unchanged until next prime introduced at 11, adding many even sums with higher powers of 2. So n=8 fails.\n\nThus to satisfy for n where primes set unchanged, need n! not increase by too many 2's. Once n includes new primes, product gains many new even sums raising exponent of 2.\n\nSo perhaps n values that work are those where n is prime and n-2 is prime (twin) and also the next prime after n is > 2n? Not sure.\n\nLet's test n=13 again with exponent of 2 etc.\n\nPrimes up to13: 2,3,5,7,11,13. Need to compute v_2 in product: many even sums. Might be enough for v_2(13!) which is floor(13/2)+6+3+1=13? Actually compute: 13/2=6, 13/4=3, 13/8=1, 13/16=0 => total 10. Wait correct: floor13/2=6, floor13/4=3, floor13/8=1 => sum 10. So need at least 10 twos. Likely product has many.\n\nBut need to check prime 11 lacking factor earlier. Let's see if within primes up to13 we can get sum multiple of 11: possibilities p,q such that p+q=11 or 22. 11 can be 2+9 (no), 3+8 (no),5+6 (no), 7+4 (no). 22 possibilities: 5+17 (17>13), 11+11 (not distinct), 2+20 (no), 3+19 (no), 13+9 (9 not prime), etc. So none. Thus 11 still missing. So condition fails.\n\nTherefore requirement: for each odd prime p≤n, there must be a sum of two distinct primes ≤ n that is either p or 2p (or possibly multiple kp but >2n impossible). For p around n, 2p may exceed 2n, so only possible is p. Thus for the largest prime ≤ n, need p-2 prime. That's why n=7 works (largest prime =7, twin). For n where largest prime p is such that 2p ≤ 2n (always true), but need representation with both primes ≤ n. For p close to n, representation might involve a prime > n unless p-2 prime. So condition for n to work is that the largest prime ≤ n must be in a twin pair (i.e., p-2 prime). Additionally, for the second largest prime perhaps also? Let's consider n between twin primes.\n\nSuppose n between twin primes (p, p+2) where p and p+2 are primes, and n = p+2 maybe that works as we saw for 7. What about n=5? Largest prime 5 has twin 3, but n=5 fails earlier due to 2 exponent deficiency maybe because product too small. Let's test n=5 earlier product=280, v2(5!)= floor5/2=2 + floor5/4=1 =>3. v2(product): factors: sums:5,7,8. 8 contributes 3 twos. So v2=3 equal, v3? need v3(5!)=1, product has from 5? actually 5 not multiple3, 7 not, 8 not, so none. So v3(product)=0 <1. That's why fails. So need also enough of other primes.\n\nThus larger n may satisfy multiple conditions.\n\nWe can brute think next candidate may be n=9? fails due to size.\n\nn=13 fails due to 11.\n\nMaybe need n such that every odd prime ≤ n has either twin (p-2 prime) or there exists representation of 2p as sum of two primes ≤ n (distinct). For prime p where p-2 not prime, need 2p representation with both primes ≤ n. Since 2p ≤ 2n, we need primes a,b ≤ n such that a+b=2p. This means a = 2p - b. Since both ≤ n, and b prime ≤ n, a also ≤ n; need both prime, distinct. This is essentially Goldbach for even number 2p with primes ≤ n. The classic Goldbach says any even >2 is sum of two primes (not necessarily ≤ n but ≤ 2p). Since n ≥ p, and p may be up to n, the primes could be up to 2p - smallest prime (2). So the larger summand could be as high as 2p-2 which may exceed n when p is near n. Need both ≤ n, so we need a,b ≤ n => the larger summand ≤ n => 2p - 2 ≤ n => p ≤ (n+2)/2.\n\nThus for primes p > (n+2)/2, the only possible multiple is p itself, requiring twin. Hence all primes in interval ((n+2)/2, n] must be twin primes (i.e., p-2 also prime). That's a strong condition.\n\nFor n=7, (n+2)/2 = 4.5, primes >4.5 up to 7 are 5 and7. Both are twin: 5-2=3 prime;7-2=5 prime. So condition satisfied.\n\nFor n=11, (11+2)/2=6.5, primes >6.5 up to 11 are 7,11. 7's twin? 5 prime yes - okay. 11's twin? 9 not prime -> fails. Hence n=11 fails.\n\nFor n=13, (13+2)/2=7.5, primes >7.5 up to 13: 11,13. 11 twin? no -> fails.\n\nFor n=17, (17+2)/2=9.5, primes >9.5 up to 17: 11,13,17. Need each be twin: 11 no, 13? 11 prime (13-2=11) yes twin, 17? 15 not prime. So fails.\n\nThus candidate n must have all primes in interval ((n+2)/2, n] being twin primes. That implies the largest prime ≤ n must be twin, and there cannot be any prime in that interval that is not twin. That limits n maybe to small numbers where twin clusters exist.\n\nWhat intervals could have only twin primes? Consider n=7 works. What about n=9? primes up to9: 2,3,5,7. Interval ((9+2)/2=5.5,9] includes 7 only (since 5 not >5.5). 7 twin yes. So condition satisfied? But earlier n=9 fails because product smaller than n!. However condition about divisibility may also fail due to not enough 2 powers etc. But maybe after adding prime 11, new interval bigger.\n\nBut n=9 not prime, largest prime 7 twin satisfied, but lack of 5? but 5 is within lower half, maybe can be handled via 2p representation. Let's test n=9 again: product same as n=7 (since primes up to9 same). v2 needed is 7 (since 9! exponent 2 is floor9/2=4+2+1=7). product exponent 6 (as computed) insufficient, hence fails. So the deficiency arises because 2 exponent insufficient when n includes composite numbers up to n that add to v_2 requirement. So need enough even sums to increase 2 exponent; adding more composites doesn't help, only adding new primes adds many even sums, especially with 2.\n\nThus n must be such that after adding new primes up to n, the exponent v_2 in product at least v_2(n!). May hold for n where new prime added gives boost.\n\nLet's test n=11 (fails due to 11 factor). n=13 fails due to 11 factor. n=17 maybe fails due to 17 factor not twin.\n\nWhat about n=23? Let's see interval ((23+2)/2=12.5,23] includes primes 13,17,19,23. Need each twin: 13-2=11 prime yes ; 17-2=15 not prime -> fails. So no.\n\nThus only n where interval contains twin primes only possibly small. Let's examine n=5: interval ((5+2)/2=3.5,5] includes prime 5 only, twin? 5-2=3 prime yes. Condition passes, but earlier fails due to 2/3 exponent. Perhaps need also enough exponent for 2,3. Might need n such that product exponent exceed v_p(n!) for all p.\n\nWe can try compute for n=7 success. Next candidate maybe n=15? but 15 not prime, interval ((15+2)/2=8.5,15] includes primes 11,13. 11 not twin, fail.\n\nn=19 interval >10.5 includes 11,13,17,19; 11 not twin, fail.\n\nThus only possibilities are when n ≤ 2p_max? Not sure.\n\nMaybe only n=7 works.\n\nCheck n=3? interval >2.5 includes 3 which is twin? 3-2=1 not prime; fails.\n\nThus answer likely n=7 only.\n\nWe should also test n=6? interval >4 includes 5. 5 twin? yes (3). But need also have exponent of 2 enough? product same as n=5 (280) with v2=3? Let's compute v2(6!)= floor6/2=3+ floor6/4=1 =4. product has 2's from sums: 8 gives 3, 10 gives1, total 4? Wait product for primes up to5 (2,3,5) include sums: (2,3)=5, (2,5)=7, (3,5)=8 => only one even sum 8 (2^3) gives 3 twos, not 4. So v2=3 <4, fail.\n\nThus n=7 only.\n\nLet's be confident.\n\nAnswer: X = 7.", "response": "ANSWER: 7", "answer": 7, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.2397698469612806e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999876023783816, "logp_correct": -1.2397698469612806e-05, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5391598442439927, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3248756716467085, "frac_below_-5": 0.009547383309759547, "digit_lp_min": -5.689261436462402, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 5656, "lp_end": -1.7881233361549675e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.2291887944447808e-05, "logp_nocot": -14.26374340057373, "p_nocot": 6.387559371160744e-07, "uplift": 14.26373100287526}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "e5d8e598-739a-407e-addb-ba931ad9a32b", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Given positive integers $m$ and $n \\geq m$, determine the largest number of dominoes ( $1 \\times 2$ or $2 \\times 1$ rectangles) that can be placed on a rectangular board with $m$ rows and $2 n$ columns consisting of cells ( $1 \\times 1$ squares) so that:  \n$\\mathrm{(i)}$ each domino covers exactly two adjacent cells of the board;  \n$\\mathrm{(ii)}$ no two dominoes overlap;  \n$\\mathrm{(iii)}$no two form a $2 \\times 2$ square; and  \n$\\mathrm{(iv)}$the bottom row of the board is completely covered by $n$ dominoes.\nPlease calculate the number of dominoes when $m = 6$ and $n = 5$\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Given positive integers $m$ and $n \\geq m$, determine the largest number of dominoes ( $1 \\times 2$ or $2 \\times 1$ rectangles) that can be placed on a rectangular board with $m$ rows and $2 n$ columns consisting of cells ( $1 \\times 1$ squares) so that:  \n$\\mathrm{(i)}$ each domino covers exactly two adjacent cells of the board;  \n$\\mathrm{(ii)}$ no two dominoes overlap;  \n$\\mathrm{(iii)}$no two form a $2 \\times 2$ square; and  \n$\\mathrm{(iv)}$the bottom row of the board is completely covered by $n$ dominoes.\nPlease calculate the number of dominoes when $m = 6$ and $n = 5$", "true_answer": 27, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "e5d8e598-739a-407e-addb-ba931ad9a32b:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 4218, 290, 4792, 25, 29844, 284, 13585, 11, 220, 17, 77, 15697, 13, 20953, 2475, 133589, 268, 24878, 39069, 11242, 11, 860, 56631, 11, 860, 1920, 1625, 261, 220, 17, 87, 17, 13749, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 860, 39069, 133589, 268, 484, 4717, 89046, 261, 220, 17, 87, 17, 4355, 741, 623, 8725, 5225, 2804,