158, 236, 99, 659, 2969, 236, 96, 1353, 22, 14, 2092, 158, 236, 99, 659, 2969, 236, 96, 1353, 22, 14, 9989, 158, 236, 99, 659, 2969, 236, 96, 1353, 22, 14, 25150, 158, 236, 99, 659, 2969, 236, 96, 1353, 22, 14, 43698, 158, 236, 99, 364, 56734, 1402, 1353, 22, 14, 18, 314, 220, 29587, 13, 19837, 871, 8350, 220, 29587, 364, 1353, 22, 14, 24, 314, 220, 8780, 13, 11399, 871, 220, 8780, 364, 1353, 22, 14, 2092, 314, 220, 2991, 13, 31788, 871, 220, 2991, 364, 1353, 22, 14, 9989, 314, 220, 899, 13, 30729, 871, 220, 899, 364, 1353, 22, 14, 25150, 314, 220, 19, 13, 16620, 871, 220, 19, 364, 1353, 22, 14, 43698, 314, 220, 16, 13, 35527, 871, 220, 16, 364, 7695, 220, 24437, 22, 1424, 1353, 22, 871, 5666, 364, 2578, 25, 220, 29587, 10, 8780, 28, 38884, 13, 220, 38884, 10, 2991, 28, 40681, 13, 220, 40681, 10, 899, 28, 36491, 13, 220, 36491, 10, 19, 28, 29483, 13, 220, 29483, 10, 16, 28, 3234, 364, 84787, 336, 314, 220, 3234, 364, 72816, 372, 62, 18, 314, 220, 1353, 22, 659, 220, 3234, 314, 220, 5215, 22, 364, 10620, 1309, 372, 62, 17, 659, 372, 62, 18, 314, 220, 17, 11, 42363, 11, 11442, 659, 220, 16, 11, 37516, 314, 220, 17, 11, 24628, 11, 38189, 364, 4383, 5856, 25, 220, 17, 11, 42363, 11, 11442, 659, 220, 16, 11, 3234, 314, 220, 17, 11, 24628, 11, 37142, 26, 2932, 220, 22, 314, 220, 17, 11, 24628, 11, 38189, 13, 11377, 364, 17045, 220, 14754, 41957, 17, 364, 58369, 3503, 30411, 18485, 364, 2167, 37703, 478, 314, 2086, 328, 57364, 1430, 75935, 1072, 85509, 7, 17, 741, 623, 7060, 21681, 14210, 2747, 174922, 14210, 1072, 1489, 30, 3072, 113181, 1606, 20102, 402, 18614, 1812, 220, 17, 11, 326, 113181, 328, 21681, 29702, 174922, 1812, 220, 17, 13, 41351, 21681, 1812, 220, 17, 29702, 174922, 1812, 220, 17, 350, 21910, 2232, 12332, 21382, 16, 8, 42923, 3085, 77, 7, 1168, 15468, 38566, 316, 220, 16, 1812, 220, 17, 741, 2632, 14210, 21681, 4748, 174922, 42308, 94, 16, 1812, 220, 17, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 174922, 382, 14210, 13, 7931, 85509, 7, 17, 936, 1062, 14210, 16336, 382, 220, 16, 1812, 220, 17, 11, 813, 290, 174922, 1812, 220, 17, 382, 220, 16, 11, 10915, 12248, 382, 57364, 1430, 1072, 85509, 7, 17, 741, 5551, 11, 382, 1354, 1062, 12248, 483, 174922, 14210, 889, 48540, 1072, 85509, 7, 17, 20707, 63499, 258, 493, 14210, 134080, 625, 167813, 656, 220, 17, 134080, 174922, 472, 16336, 382, 14210, 26, 889, 174922, 46716, 1072, 54912, 591, 15600, 12248, 1481, 413, 16336, 13, 1843, 174922, 14210, 11, 1815, 112372, 220, 17, 4275, 220, 16, 11, 33317, 57364, 1430, 1072, 85509, 7, 17, 741, 2632, 56509, 1082, 18305, 364, 84787, 3605, 328, 57364, 1430, 15600, 75935, 25, 20690, 1888, 472, 2061, 364, 2594, 1899, 364, 109213, 161791, 25, 623, 12248, 18614, 553, 220, 15, 14, 16, 26, 174922, 46716, 1072, 54912, 10112, 2023, 413, 1952, 889, 12248, 2928, 57364, 1430, 1072, 85509, 7, 17, 8, 6961, 50557, 57364, 4295, 1072, 85509, 7, 17, 8, 1327, 10860, 174922, 1812, 220, 17, 2893, 79097, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 14210, 13, 2632, 581, 32571, 7060, 364, 84787, 6052, 364, 58369, 4733, 364], "n_tokens": 2257, "words": 923, "band_lo": 427.5, "band_hi": 1710.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to count N = number of 2015-tuples of subsets S_i of [2015] such that the number of permutations σ with σ(i) in S_i for all i is odd.\n\nDefine a 0-1 matrix A where A_{i,j}=1 if j∈S_i. For each tuple S_i, we have a matrix of size n=2015 with rows indicating allowed positions for each i. The number of permutations σ such that σ(i)∈S_i is the number of perfect matchings in bipartite graph defined by A, which equals permanent of A (since each permutation contributes 1 if allowed). So we need permanent of A to be odd.\n\nWe need count of binary n×n matrices whose permanent is odd.\n\nWe work modulo 2. Over GF(2), permanent equals determinant because sign doesn't matter (sign is ±1 mod 2 = 1). Indeed over GF(2), permanent = determinant (since sign factor is irrelevant). So mod 2, permanent odd ⇔ determinant over GF(2) = 1 (nonzero). So we need matrices over GF(2) that are invertible (non-singular). So A must be an n×n matrix over GF(2) with full rank.\n\nThus N = number of n×n binary matrices that are invertible over GF(2). But wait: S_i are subsets of {1..n}, no condition that each column's entries count? Actually A_{i,j}=1 if j∈S_i; any binary matrix corresponds to a tuple of subsets (each row is subset). So indeed N equals number of invertible n×n matrices over field GF(2).\n\nThus N = number of invertible matrices of size n over GF(2), where n=2015.\n\nNumber of invertible n×n matrices over GF(q) is (q^n -1)(q^n - q)(q^n - q^2)...(q^n - q^{n-1}). For q=2: N = ∏_{i=0}^{n-1} (2^n - 2^i).\n\nCompute prime power exponents for 2 and 3 in N.\n\nFirst, N has factor 2^{?} times odd part. Need k_2 maximal exponent of 2 dividing N. Similarly for 3.\n\nWe need exponent of 2 in product ∏_{i=0}^{n-1} (2^n - 2^i). Write each term as 2^i (2^{n-i} -1). Because 2^n - 2^i = 2^i (2^{n-i} -1). So product = ∏_{i=0}^{n-1} 2^i ∏_{i=0}^{n-1} (2^{n-i} -1).\n\nThe first product ∏_{i=0}^{n-1} 2^i = 2^{sum_{i=0}^{n-1} i}=2^{n(n-1)/2}.\n\nThe second product is product over j = n-i (i from 0..n-1) yields ∏_{j=1}^{n} (2^{j} -1). Indeed as i runs 0..n-1, n-i runs n..1. So product = ∏_{j=1}^{n} (2^{j} -1).\n\nThus N = 2^{n(n-1)/2} * ∏_{j=1}^{n} (2^{j} -1). The second factor is odd because each term (2^j -1) is odd.\n\nHence exponent of 2 in N is n(n-1)/2.\n\nGiven n=2015. Compute n(n-1)/2 = 2015*2014/2 = 2015*1007 = ?\n\n2015*1000 = 2,015,000, plus 2015*7=14,105, total 2,029,105.\n\nSo k_2 = 2029105.\n\nNow exponent of 3: we need exponent of 3 in ∏_{j=1}^{n} (2^{j} -1). Since 2^{j} -1 can be divisible by powers of 3 for certain j.\n\nWe need to compute v_3(N) = sum_{j=1}^{n} v_3(2^{j} -1). Since the powers of 2 factor contributes none.\n\nThus k_3 = sum_{j=1}^{2015} v_3(2^{j}-1).\n\nWe need to compute the exponent of 3 dividing each term. Let's analyze pattern.\n\nRecall that 2 modulo powers of 3: 2 mod 3 = -1. So 2^j -1 ≡ (-1)^j -1 mod 3. So when j is even, (-1)^j =1 => 2^j -1 ≡ 0 mod 3. When j odd, ≡ -2 mod 3 = 1? Wait compute: (-1)^odd = -1, -1 -1 = -2 ≡ 1 mod 3, not divisible. So 3 divides 2^j-1 iff j even.\n\nHigher powers: Use lifting exponent lemma (LTE). For odd prime p=3, v_3(2^{2m} -1) = v_3(2^{2} -1) + v_3(m). Since 2 is not divisible by 3, and 2+1 =3 divisible by 3. Use LTE for a^n - b^n with n even? Let's recall: For p odd prime dividing a-b, v_p(a^n - b^n) = v_p(a-b) + v_p(n). Here a=2, b=1, so a-b =1, not divisible by 3. Wait we need a+b case because 2^{2m} -1 = (2^m -1)(2^m +1). But better use known formula for p=3 dividing 2^{2k} -1: Since 2^2=4 ≡1 mod3, we have 2^{2k} -1 = (4)^k -1. So we are essentially looking at 4^k -1. Then apply LTE for p=3 dividing 4-1 =3. So v_3(4^k -1) = v_3(4-1) + v_3(k) = 1 + v_3(k). Good.\n\nThus for even j = 2k, we have v_3(2^{2k} -1) = 1 + v_3(k). (because 2^{2k}=4^k). That gives power of 3.\n\nFor odd j, v_3 =0.\n\nTherefore k_3 = sum_{k=1}^{⌊n/2⌋} (1 + v_3(k)) where n=2015, n/2 floor = 1007.\n\nThus k_3 = sum_{k=1}^{1007} 1 + sum_{k=1}^{1007} v_3(k) = 1007 + S, where S = sum_{k=1}^{1007} v_3(k).\n\nWe need S.\n\nWe can compute v_3(k) counts: For each integer k, its exponent of 3.\n\nSum over k up to N =1007: sum_{k=1}^{N} v_3(k) = floor(N/3) + floor(N/9) + floor(N/27) + floor(N/81) + floor(N/243) + floor(N/729) + floor(N/2187)... stop when power > N.\n\nBecause each multiple of 3 contributes at least 1, each multiple of 9 contributes an extra 1, etc.\n\nThus S = ⎣1007/3⎦ + ⎣1007/9⎦ + ⎣1007/27⎦ + ⎣1007/81⎦ + ⎣1007/243⎦ + ⎣1007/729⎦.\n\nCompute:\n\n1007/3 = 335.666 => floor 335.\n\n1007/9 = 111.888 => 111.\n\n1007/27 = 37.296 => 37.\n\n1007/81 = 12.432 => 12.\n\n1007/243 = 4.145 => 4.\n\n1007/729 = 1.381 => 1.\n\nNext 2187 >1007 => stop.\n\nAdd: 335+111=446. 446+37=483. 483+12=495. 495+4=499. 499+1=500.\n\nThus S = 500.\n\nTherefore k_3 = 1007 + 500 = 1507.\n\nNow need k_2 + k_3 = 2,029,105 + 1,507 = 2,030,612.\n\nCheck addition: 2,029,105 + 1,500 = 2,030,605; plus 7 = 2,030,612. Yes.\n\nAnswer 2030612.\n\nLet's double-check logic.\n\nWe assumed N = number of invertible matrices over GF(2). The condition permanent odd -> determinant odd over Z? But parity only depends on entries mod 2, and parity of permanent equals determinant mod 2. Indeed permanent mod 2 equals determinant mod 2 (since sign factor (-1)^{sgn(σ)} reduces to 1 mod 2). So odd permanent means determinant ≡1 mod 2, i.e., determinant is odd. Over GF(2), any odd integer is 1 mod 2, so the determinant mod 2 is 1, meaning matrix is invertible over GF(2). However, is there any matrix with determinant odd but singular over GF(2)? Determinant odd ⇒ not divisible by 2 ⇒ determinant as integer is odd; but determinant computed over integers from binary matrix would be integer. If determinant odd, then modulo 2 it's 1, hence invertible over GF(2). So equivalence holds.\n\nThus count of invertible binary matrices: formula product as used.\n\nAll good.\n\nPotential nuance: The matrix entries are 0/1; determinant computed over integers maybe could be even but matrix still invertible over GF(2)?? Actually invertibility over GF(2) just requires determinant mod 2 non-zero, i.e., odd. So we captured condition.\n\nThus answer.\n\nLet's output.", "response": "ANSWER: 2030612", "answer": 2030612, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -7.152555099310121e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999992847447459, "logp_correct": -7.152555099310121e-07, "cot_lp_mean": -0.3785727159172213, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9672257193842668, "frac_below_-5": 0.005800981704596163, "digit_lp_min": -4.141036510467529, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.4740857481956482, "n_cot_tokens": 2241, "lp_end": -1.5497195136049413e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -42.98608682974009, "p_nocot": 2.1447648882109367e-19, "uplift": 42.98608611448458}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "2162ca74-50f1-4080-92d0-fe9c7c285c41", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: In a table with $88$ rows and $253$ columns, each cell is colored either purple or yellow. Suppose that for each yellow cell $c$, the following condition holds:  \n$$x(c) \\cdot y(c) \\geq 184,$$  \nwhere $x(c)$ is the number of purple cells in the same row as $c$, and $y(c)$ is the number of purple cells in the same column as $c$.  \nFind the least possible number of cells that are colored purple.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: In a table with $88$ rows and $253$ columns, each cell is colored either purple or yellow. Suppose that for each yellow cell $c$, the following condition holds:  \n$$x(c) \\cdot y(c) \\geq 184,$$  \nwhere $x(c)$ is the number of purple cells in the same row as $c$, and $y(c)$ is the number of purple cells in the same column as $c$.  \nFind the least possible number of cells that are colored purple.", "true_answer": 2024, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "2162ca74-50f1-4080-92d0-fe9c7c285c41:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 41776, 3609, 37896, 11242, 306, 220, 2843, 87, 26340, 10586, 4335, 484, 395, 2454, 19691, 4206, 274, 11, 1888, 328, 2086, 328, 37896, 11242, 306, 1617, 5225, 350, 87, 8, 326, 6000, 350, 88, 8, 5064, 15980, 13, 125152, 13585, 326, 15697, 679, 1236, 28991, 328, 37896, 11242, 364, 12845, 290, 2086, 328, 37896, 11242, 306, 5225, 575, 413, 428, 10853, 350, 15, 72554, 428, 10853, 72554, 220, 26340, 741, 9184, 328, 37896, 11242, 306, 6000, 441, 413, 274, 12021, 350, 15, 72554, 274, 12021, 72554, 220, 2843, 741, 2214, 2454, 19691, 4206, 540, 64353, 350, 72, 22712, 8, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 4206, 625, 37896, 936, 581, 1309, 428, 10853, 425, 274, 12021, 5064, 220, 15980, 364, 42087, 25, 41776