9, 13585, 220, 2843, 11, 15697, 220, 26340, 11, 3609, 8104, 220, 1323, 19, 13, 1328, 382, 261, 54467, 497, 651, 6953, 8789, 25, 5225, 9882, 220, 1860, 11, 6000, 9882, 220, 23, 13, 12265, 220, 1860, 9, 2843, 314, 220, 1323, 19, 314, 220, 23, 9, 26340, 11, 18210, 35441, 1803, 1116, 5016, 50015, 13, 1771, 78521, 328, 54467, 497, 651, 7465, 483, 2617, 18210, 30, 19792, 101848, 12401, 88, 599, 6409, 13, 2214, 54467, 497, 651, 7465, 483, 3561, 4307, 220, 2843, 13585, 18210, 220, 1860, 2454, 11, 1849, 4307, 220, 26340, 15697, 18210, 220, 23, 2454, 13, 1416, 665, 2371, 290, 8155, 7060, 25, 10562, 9882, 402, 1849, 4307, 72554, 2086, 328, 3561, 38360, 350, 2843, 8, 1118, 18305, 350, 23, 72554, 220, 2843, 741, 623, 4215, 15248, 13, 3274, 382, 5542, 24140, 538, 9882, 45665, 553, 94357, 13, 7649, 101848, 40924, 403, 4356, 30, 357, 2411, 4275, 58866, 2236, 18210, 553, 625, 23700, 13, 12265, 220, 1860, 72554, 220, 26340, 11, 2454, 5225, 665, 4837, 316, 220, 1860, 15697, 842, 328, 220, 26340, 13, 19792, 316, 48502, 2454, 6000, 316, 220, 23, 13585, 13, 1328, 382, 1299, 75745, 261, 350, 1860, 11, 23, 14080, 32790, 54467, 497, 651, 3469, 83902, 13, 12265, 106918, 328, 18210, 10112, 30, 220, 1860, 326, 220, 23, 19919, 9197, 11, 889, 484, 8740, 139266, 4705, 7465, 24140, 13, 14346, 885, 7060, 6960, 23121, 364, 40, 2411, 2238, 261, 12248, 13031, 13, 47682, 19957, 4149, 2086, 328, 37896, 11242, 382, 220, 1323, 19, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 1323, 19, 364], "n_tokens": 2535, "words": 1591, "band_lo": 1540.0, "band_hi": 6160.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need minimize total purple cells in 88x253 grid given that for each yellow cell c, product of number of purple cells in its row (x) and column (y) >=184. Means rows and columns have some counts of purple cells.\n\nLet the number of purple cells in row i be r_i (0 ≤ r_i ≤ 253). Number of purple cells in column j be c_j (0 ≤ c_j ≤ 88). For each yellow cell at intersect (i,j) (i.e., cell not purple), we need r_i * c_j >= 184.\n\nGoal: minimize total purple cells = sum_i r_i = sum_j c_j = P.\n\nWe can arrange placement of purple cells arbitrarily. For each row i and column j, if the cell is yellow then condition applies. If cell is purple, condition irrelevant.\n\nThus to minimize P, perhaps we can have many yellow cells but need condition holds. The condition may fail if some row i and column j have small r_i and c_j both. To avoid violation, for any pair (i,j) where r_i*c_j <184, the cell must be purple (i.e., we need to place purple there). So any \"dangerous\" pair must be forced purple.\n\nThus we need to choose r_i and c_j (nonnegative integers) and a set of forced purple cells such that for any (i,j) with r_i*c_j <184, we set that cell purple (so that condition doesn't need to hold). For cells where product >=184, we may leave them yellow (or purple if we like). Minimizing total purple cells means we want as many pairs as possible to have product >=184, so we can keep them yellow.\n\nThe forced purple cells count is at least number of low-product pairs. However also we must ensure the row/column counts match the placement; i.e., r_i is exactly the number of purple cells in row i, counting forced ones plus maybe extra. Similarly c_j count.\n\nThus we have a combinatorial optimization: Given row counts r_i and column counts c_j, the number of forced purple cells is at least the size of a set covering all pairs (i,j) with r_i*c_j <184 such that each forced purple contributes to counts. In other words, the purple cells must form a bipartite graph with degrees r_i left, c_j right. The edges correspond to purple cells. Requirement: the edge set must include all \"bad\" edges (i,j) where r_i*c_j <184. Therefore the degree sequence must be able to accommodate all those required edges.\n\nThus we need to find degree sequences minimizing total edges (P) such that there exists a bipartite graph with given degrees containing all required edges.\n\nObservation: If we let rows with high r_i and columns with high c_j, product may exceed threshold, reducing required edges. So we may concentrate purple cells into some rows and columns, leaving others with zero? If a row has r_i=0 then any column j with c_j arbitrary gives product 0<184, so every cell in that row must be purple => that row would have 253 purple cells, contradict r_i=0. So cannot have r_i=0 unless also every column j has c_j high? Wait product r_i*c_j =0 if r_i=0 regardless of c_j, so all cells in that row must be purple => r_i would be 253, not 0. So impossible. So each row must have r_i >0. Similarly each column must have c_j >0.\n\nLet’s denote minimal positive values.\n\nGoal: Minimize sum r_i given constraints that there exists bipartite graph with degree sequence (r_i,c_j) and contains all edges where product <184.\n\nIf product condition fails for some pair we force edge. That increases degrees of those rows/cols, may increase product further... cyclic.\n\nWe can think of constructing a matrix of purple cells (1) such that for any zero entry we have r_i*c_j >=184.\n\nLet’s denote binary matrix A_{i,j} =1 if purple else 0. Row sums = r_i, column sums = c_j. Condition: If A_{i,j}=0 then r_i*c_j >=184.\n\nGoal: minimize total ones.\n\nThis is reminiscent of problem to minimize number of ones given product constraints for zeros: i.e., we want as many zeros as possible but must keep r_i*c_j large.\n\nWe could try to make all rows have same r and all columns same c to simplify. Let r rows equal a, c columns equal b. Then total purple = 88a = 253b => a/b = 253/88 ≈ 2.875. But a and b must be integers; we can have total P = lcm? Actually we need integer a,b satisfying 88a = 253b = P. Minimal positive solution: a = 253k, b = 88k with k integer gives huge counts. Not minimal.\n\nBetter to allow varied.\n\nConsider setting r_i values maybe high for some rows and low for others, but note low r_i forces many forced edges with many columns.\n\nSuppose we set all rows to have same r, similarly columns to have same c? Let's test. If we have r per row, c per column, then product = rc. For any zero cell, need rc >=184. So we can have zeros only if rc>=184. If rc<184 then all cells must be ones (purple). That would make row fully purple (253) which would make r=253, not low. So need rc >=184 to allow zeros. So we want to choose r,c such that rc >=184 but also degrees match. Since total purple = 88 r = 253 c => r = 253c/88. Plug into rc >=184 => (253c/88)*c >=184 => 253 c^2 >=184*88 = 16192 => c^2 >=16192/253 ≈ 64.032 => c >= 9 (since integer). c=9 gives r = 253*9/88 = 2277/88 ≈25.88 not integer. But r must be integer; we can have varying.\n\nBut this suggests maybe we can have column counts around 9, rows around 26 (since 26*9=234 >=184). Then each zero cell product >=234 >184, OK. So we could set each row about 26 purple cells, each column about 9. Need total purple = 88*26 = 2288. Columns total would be 253*9 = 2277 mismatch by 11. So adjust some rows/cols.\n\nOur goal is to minimize total P. The uniform estimate would be around (total rows * r) where r ~ sqrt(184)=13.6? Wait rc >=184, the product of average row and column should exceed 184 to allow many zeros. Minimizing total edges given rc >=184? The total edges P = sum r_i = sum c_j. For uniform case, r = P/88, c = P/253. Condition rc = (P/88)*(P/253) = P^2/(88*253) >=184 => P^2 >=184*88*253. Compute 88*253 = 22264. Multiply 184*22264 = 184*22264 = (200-16)*22264 = 4,452,800 - 356,224 = 4,096,576. So P >= sqrt(4,096,576) =? Let's compute sqrt. 2024^2 = 4,096,576? Let's check: 2000^2=4,000,000; 2020^2=4,080,400; 2024^2 = (2000+24)^2 =4,000,000+96,000+576=4,096,576. Exactly! So P >= 2024. Nice! So uniform distribution gives lower bound P >=2024. And equality occurs when P=2024 and uniform rows and columns satisfy rc=184 exactly? Let's compute r = P/88 = 2024/88 = 23.0? 88*23 = 2024, yes r=23. c = P/253 = 2024/253 ≈ 8.0? 253*8 = 2024, yes c=8. So uniform counts: each row has 23 purple cells, each column 8 purple cells. Then product rc = 23*8 = 184 exactly. Then for any yellow cell, need rc >=184 satisfied as equality. Since rc=184, condition holds (>=). So we can have any arrangement where each row has exactly 23 purple cells and each column exactly 8 purple cells; then all yellow cells satisfy condition because row count*column count = 184 for any row, column pair (since uniform). Indeed any yellow cell (i,j) is in row with 23 purple, column with 8 purple, product 184 meets condition. So this arrangement works.\n\nThus minimal P is at most 2024. Need to verify if lower possible using non-uniform distribution? The lower bound derived from arithmetic mean? Let's prove minimal must be at least ceil(sqrt(184*88*253)) = 2024. Because consider any row i and column j: For each yellow cell we need product >=184. For any row i, let its r_i. For each column j that corresponds to a yellow cell in row i, we must have c_j >= ceil(184 / r_i). For a given row, the number of yellow cells in that row = 253 - r_i. So sum over those columns' contributions maybe?\n\nAlternative bound: Use inequality: For each row i, we have sum over columns j of indicator of yellow? Might use double counting. However the lower bound from uniform seems plausible minimal due to convexity (by AM-GM). Since total edges P = sum r_i, we have average r = P/88. Similarly average c = P/253. For any row i and column j with a yellow cell, product r_i * c_j >=184. In particular, pick the row i with minimal r_i = r_min, and column j with minimal c_j = c_min; if cell (i,j) is yellow then r_min * c_min >=184, else that cell must be purple, increasing counts possibly. But worst-case maybe all low rows intersect low columns are made purple, increasing P. The uniform distribution often minimizes sum given product constraints (by rearrangement inequality). I think 2024 is minimal.\n\nWe need to construct explicit arrangement achieving 2024 purple cells with required row/col counts. Is it possible to have a 0-1 matrix with row sums 23 and column sums 8? Since total rows 88, columns 253, total ones 2024. This is a bipartite regular situation: row degree 23, column degree 8. Since 23*88 = 2024 = 8*253, degrees satisfy handshaking lemma. Existence of bipartite graph with those degrees? Need Gale-Ryser conditions. For bipartite graph with left side 88 rows degrees 23 each, right side 253 columns degrees 8 each. We can check the necessary condition: maximum degree on right side ≤ number of left vertices (88) which holds (8 ≤ 88). The sum matches. There is known existence if degree sequences are graphical. Use Gale-Shapley? I think it's feasible because degrees are not extreme. Since 23 ≤ 253, each row can connect to 23 columns out of 253. Need to allocate each column to 8 rows. This is like constructing a (23,8)-regular bipartite multigraph. Since gcd of degrees maybe? 23 and 8 relatively prime, but that doesn't impede simple graph existence. Hall's condition likely satisfied.\n\nI think such a matrix exists. Hence minimal possible number of purple cells is 2024.\n\nThus answer: 2024.", "response": "ANSWER: 2024", "answer": 2024, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.1920925544472993e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999988079081561, "logp_correct": -1.1920925544472993e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6338635161703315, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2725238016174316, "frac_below_-5": 0.011507936507936509, "digit_lp_min": -4.349249839782715, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.09287245711311698, "n_cot_tokens": 2520, "lp_end": -2.5070934295654297, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.2278481335670222e-05, "logp_nocot": -8.542179316282272, "p_nocot": 0.0001950646889355806, "uplift": 8.542178124189718}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "78f824a2-0210-45bb-b88f-2f11c487c46b", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Determine all integers $m$ for which the $m \\times m$ square can be dissected into five rectangles, the side lengths of which are the integers $1,2,3, \\ldots, 10$ in some order. Please provide the sum of all possible values of $m$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Determine all integers $m$ for which the $m \\times m$ square can be dissected into five rectangles, the side lengths of which are the integers $1,2,3, \\ldots, 10$ in some order. Please provide the sum of all possible values of $m$.", "true_answer": 24, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "78f824a2-0210-45bb-b88f-2f11c487c46b:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 146417, 448, 284, 1215, 284, 13749, 1511, 6468, 164265, 350, 2878, 23517, 57269, 741, 623, 4307, 51957, 328, 290, 164265, 553, 54912, 220, 16, 11, 17, 35229, 11, 702, 11, 2061, 472, 23995, 350, 27252, 189592, 2454, 45808, 853, 16336, 4307, 51957, 11, 326, 290, 920, 328, 290, 4325, 8663, 553, 9707, 290, 51957, 328, 290, 45808, 18089, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 2454, 45808, 8844, 1920, 4307, 51957, 11, 3609, 328, 220, 20, 9, 17, 28, 702, 8663, 741, 2632, 581, 1309, 316, 10610, 290, 8663, 354, 16, 35229, 11, 702, 92, 1511, 220, 20, 131528, 24702, 11, 2454, 10610, 2447, 290, 4307, 51957, 350, 6015, 326, 4679, 8, 328, 261, 45808, 13, 7801, 1879, 220, 20, 164265, 2804, 413, 3741, 316, 17664, 448, 284, 12378, 76, 13749, 9707, 350, 1750, 173439, 11, 860, 52008, 741, 22765, 34011, 665, 413, 93819, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 29546, 665, 413, 125210, 364, 42087, 25, 1646, 722, 16336, 284, 395, 1118, 2238, 261, 3453, 289, 13031, 364, 2167, 1309, 3624, 7060, 25, 4215, 328, 6723, 328, 164265, 314, 284, 61, 17, 13, 11555, 45808, 3624