, 20, 11, 21, 11, 22, 16255, 19792, 1920, 24702, 4215, 1398, 13, 22483, 25, 350, 22, 11, 20, 25987, 2467, 659, 350, 21, 11, 16, 25987, 21, 28, 4987, 13, 350, 22, 11, 18, 25987, 2040, 31717, 21, 11, 20, 25987, 1130, 28, 6231, 13, 350, 21, 11, 20, 25987, 1130, 31717, 22, 11, 17, 25987, 1265, 28, 3336, 13, 350, 21, 11, 19, 58473, 625, 364, 34013, 284, 28, 899, 625, 4149, 364, 10620, 284, 28, 1311, 350, 7502, 220, 20335, 741, 19792, 18800, 314, 220, 20335, 12, 7920, 314, 4621, 7064, 13, 19792, 4215, 328, 57269, 328, 19504, 12629, 220, 4621, 4493, 150699, 350, 366, 118877, 741, 220, 4621, 665, 413, 22171, 472, 4215, 328, 57269, 25, 220, 3796, 10, 24, 10, 16, 314, 4621, 350, 22, 61, 17, 10, 18, 61, 17, 10, 16, 61, 17, 741, 2632, 1309, 3407, 829, 69164, 150699, 483, 19504, 220, 22, 11, 18, 11, 16, 13, 4569, 7890, 120152, 364, 84787, 284, 28, 1311, 6960, 4149, 364, 7943, 1217, 1309, 24140, 328, 3453, 289, 13, 63659, 2973, 220, 994, 326, 220, 1311, 4149, 11, 220, 899, 625, 364, 2167, 1757, 3587, 4215, 328, 722, 4149, 284, 314, 220, 994, 10, 1311, 28, 1494, 364, 58369, 413, 21716, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 1494, 13], "n_tokens": 10045, "words": 3780, "band_lo": 1890.0, "band_hi": 7560.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to dissect an m x m square into five rectangles (not necessarily squares). The side lengths of the rectangles are integers 1,2,...,10, used as dimensions (presumably each rectangle has integer side lengths, and the set of the ten numbers are exactly the lengths of the rectangle sides, i.e., each rectangle uses two side lengths, total of 5*2=10 numbers). So we need to pair the numbers {1,...,10} into 5 unordered pairs, each pair being the side lengths (width and height) of a rectangle. Then these 5 rectangles must be able to tile an m×m square exactly (no overlaps, no gaps). Rectangles can be rotated, i.e., orientation can be swapped.\n\nGoal: find all integer m for which such a tiling exists.\n\nWe need area condition: sum of areas of rectangles = m^2. Each rectangle area = product of its two side lengths (pair). So sum_{pairs} a_i * b_i = m^2.\n\nWe also need that the tiling can actually be arranged into a square; combinatorial geometry.\n\nWe need to consider all possible pairings of numbers 1..10 into five pairs. Then compute sum of products. Then check if can form square tiling.\n\nWe also need to consider that the side lengths of square must be integer m, so m^2 equals sum of products which must be integer, obviously.\n\nFirst compute total sum of numbers = 55. Not directly needed.\n\nWe need to pair numbers such that sum of products yields a perfect square. Let's enumerate possibilities? Could use reasoning.\n\nWe can think of sum of products of pairs = 1/2[(sum of numbers)^2 - sum of squares of numbers] if we pair arbitrarily? Let's check: Suppose we have numbers x1..x10. Pair them, sum_{pairs} a_i b_i. If we consider sum_{i<j} x_i x_j = ( (sum)^2 - sum of squares ) /2. That's sum over all unordered pairs, but we only select 5 disjoint pairs covering all numbers. That's a perfect matching. The sum of products of the matching is some subset of the total sum of all 45 pairwise products (since there are 10 choose 2 =45). The total sum of all pairwise products is fixed: T = (55^2 - sum_{i} i^2)/2. Compute sum of squares 1^2+...+10^2 = 10*11*21/6 = 385. Then (55^2 =3025). So T = (3025-385)/2 = 2640/2 =1320. So total of all 45 products =1320. Our matching selects exactly 5 of these 45 products, sum S. So S can be any sum of 5 pairwise products with each number used exactly once.\n\nThus S = m^2.\n\nWe need to find possible perfect squares achievable.\n\nAlso need tiling feasibility. Might be limited set.\n\nFirst find all possible matchings sum to square.\n\nLet's attempt to search conceptually. We could brute force using reasoning but as chatGPT we can do some reasoning.\n\nLet's think of pairing extremes possibly.\n\nWe could try to find squares near total area maybe.\n\nMaximum possible sum S_m