-i, partner of j is 11-j. Then delta = (i - j)*[(11 - j) - (11 - i)] = (i - j)*(i - j) = (i - j)^2. So each swap between i and j (i>j) increases sum by (i - j)^2. Good! So from minimal configuration, any swap of numbers i and j results in increase of square of difference. And multiple swaps combine (since after swap the pairing changes). But we can think of applying sequence of swaps; total delta will be sum of squares of differences of swapped pairs, provided swaps are independent (i.e., each number swapped at most once). Since we can partition the set into new pairs; essentially we can think of a permutation where each number may be paired with new partner, and the total increase from minimal equals sum of (i - newpartner_i)^2 /2? Might be more complex.\n\nBut we can try to achieve total delta 34 as sum of squares of differences (distinct numbers) where each number appears at most once. Since you can think of we start with pairing a with 11-a. We can \"swap\" the partners of two numbers i and j, which replaces pairs (i,11-i) and (j,11-j) with (i,11-j) and (j,11-i). The increase is (i - j)^2 as shown.\n\nThus the total increase is sum over swaps of squares of differences, where swaps are disjoint (no number involved in multiple swaps). So we need to select a set of disjoint swaps such that sum of squares of differences = desired delta.\n\nWe need delta = 34 for m=12.\n\nFind squares up to (9)^2=81: possible squares: 1,4,9,16,25,36,49,64,81.\n\nWe need sum 34 = can be 9+25 = 34. So we need two swaps: one with difference 3 (square 9) and another with difference 5 (square 25). The swaps must involve distinct numbers (no overlap).\n\nThus pick i and j with difference 3 and another pair with difference5, non overlapping.\n\nExample: swap numbers 10 and 7 (difference 3) -> increase 9. Swap numbers 9 and 4 (difference5) -> increase25. Check they are disjoint sets: {10,7} and {9,4} distinct, okay.\n\nNow apply these swaps to minimal pairing.\n\nOriginal pairs: (10,1), (9,2