: (9,6), (10,2), (8,3), (7,4), (5,1). Compute sum: Let's calculate products: 9*6=54, 10*2=20, 8*3=24,7*4=28,5*1=5. Sum =54+20+24+28+5=131? Wait compute: 54+20=74, +24=98, +28=126, +5=131. But we expected increase total 25+9=34 from 110 gives 144. However we got 131. Something off because after first swap sum was 110+25=135, after second swap we added 9 should be 144. But my recalculation gave 131 meaning my first sum maybe miscalculated. Let's recompute correctly.\n\nOriginal minimal sum 110.\n\nAfter swapping 10 with5: new pairs: (10,5) product 50, (6,1) product6, others unchanged: (9,2)=18, (8,3)=24, (7,4)=28. Sum =50+6+18+24+28 =126. Wait earlier I thought increase 25 leading to 135 but actually increase is 25? Let's compute delta: originally (10,1)=10 and (6,5)=30 sum 40. New pair (10,5)=50 and (6,1)=6 sum 56. Increase 16, not 25. Hmm our formula earlier gave (i - j)^2 where i=10, j=5 diff=5 =>25. But we observed increase of 16. Let's verify formula with minimal pairing: partner of i is 11 - i. Pair (i, 11-i). For i=10 partner 1. For j=5 partner 6. Swap to (i, 11 - j) = (10,6) and (j, 11 - i) = (5,1). Wait earlier we swapped to (10,5) and (6,1) which is different! Actually swapping i and j means exchange their partners, not pair them together. The rule: start with (i, a) where a=11-i, (j, b) where b=11-j. After swapping partners, we have (i, b) and (j, a). That results in pairs (10,6) and (5,1), not (10,5) and (6,1). That's the correct operation preserving each number's partner being the partner of the other original. So my earlier swapping was incorrect. The increase formula should be (i - j)^2 indeed for that swap. So after swap (10,5) is not allowed directly. We must instead swap partners.\n\nThus to achieve desired pairings we need to apply swaps that result in final matching. Our earlier successful 121 solution involved (10,5) pairing, which is not a simple swap from minimal; it required more complex re-pairing.\n\nThus using swaps approach might be messy.\n\nGiven time, assume m=11 possible (we found matching, likely tiling possible). Need also test m=12 and 13.\n\nLet's try find matching sum 144 using more intelligent method maybe via solving for delta =34 as sum of squares of odd numbers? Could be possible.\n\nWe can attempt to find explicit pairing for 144 by solving equations.\n\nLet’s attempt to find pairing manually: need sum 144.\n\nTry to use moderate products: Maybe (10,6)=60, (9,5)=45 total 105. Need 39 from remaining numbers {1,2,3,4,7,8}. Need two pairs sum 39. Options: (8,4)=32 and (7,2)=14 too high (46). (8,3)=24 and (7,2)=14 total 38 close, need 1 more. (8,1)=8 and (7,4)=28 total 36. (8,2)=16 and (7,3)=21 total 37. (8,7)=56 too high. So not.\n\nTry (10,6)=60, (9,4)=36 total 96, need 48 from {1,2,3,5,7,8}. Need two pairs sum 48. Possibilities: (8,5)=40 + (7,2)=14 =54 too high, (8,3)=24 + (7,5)=35=59 high, (8,2)=16 + (7,5)=35=51, (8,1)=8+(7,5)=35=43, (8,7)=56 + (5,2)=10=66, not.\n\nTry (10,5)=50, (9,6)=54 total 104, need 40 from {1,2,3,4,7,8}. Need two pairs sum 40. Options: (8,4)=32 + (7,2)=14 =46 too high. (8,3)=24 + (7,2)=14 =38 close, need 2 more maybe (1,? ) not. (8,1)=8 + (7,4)=28 =36. (8,2)=16 + (7,3)=21 =37. (8,7)=56 + (4,2)=8 =64. So not.\n\nTry (10,4)=40, (9,5)=45 total 85, need 59 from {1,2,3,6,7,8}. Need two pairs sum 59. Options: (8,7)=56 + (6,1)=6 =62 too high. (8,6)=48 + (7,2)=14 =62. (8,3)=24 + (7,6)=42 =66. (8,2)=16 + (7,6)=42 =58 close need 1. (8,1)=8 + (7,6)=42 =50. Not.\n\nTry (10,7)=70, (9,5)=45 sum115 need 29 from {1,2,3,4,6,8}. Need two pairs sum 29. Options: (8,3)=24 + (6,1)=6 =30 close, (8,2)=16+(6,3)=18=34, (8,1)=8+(6,4)=24=32, (6,2)=12+(8,4)=32=44. Not.\n\nTry (10,7)=70,(9,6)=54 total124 need20 from {1,2,3,4,5,8}. Need two pairs sum 20. Options: (8,2)=16+(5,1)=5=21, (8,1)=8+(5,3)=15=23, (5,4)=20 + (8,1)=8 ->30. (4,3)=12+(5,2)=10=22. (8,4)=32 too high.\n\nTry (10,8)=80, (9,4)=36 total116 need28 from {1,2,3,5,6,7}. Need two pairs sum28. Options: (7,4) not available. (7,3)=21 + (6,1)=6 =27 close. (7,2)=14+(6,5)=30=44. (7,6)=42 + (5,1)=5=47. (5,3)=15+(6,2)=12=27. (5,4) not.\n\nTry (10,8)=80,(9,5)=45 total125 need19 from {1,2,3,4,6,7}. need two pairs sum19. (7,2)=14 + (6,1)=6=20 close. (7,1)=7 + (6,3)=18=25. (7,3)=21 + (6,1)=6=27. (4,3)=12 + (6,1)=6=18 close.\n\nTry (10,9) not allowed.\n\nTry (9,8)=72,(10,5)=50 total122 need22 from {1,2,3,4,6,7}. Need two pairs sum22. Options: (7,3)=21 + (6,1)=6 =27. (7,2)=14+(6,3)=18=32. (6,4)=24 + (7,1)=7=31. (6,2)=12+(7,3)=21=33. (4,3)=12+(7,2)=14=26. (4,1)=4+(7,6)=42=46. Not.\n\nTry (9,8)=72,(10,4)=40 total112 need32 from {1,2,3,5,6,7}. Need two pairs sum32. Options: (7,5)=35 + (6,1)=6=41. (7,3)=21+(6,5)=30=51. (6,5)=30+(7,2)=14=44. (6,4)... not.\n\nMaybe m=12 not possible.\n\nNow m=13 (area 169). Need delta = 169-110 =59 increase. Need sum of squares of differences equal 59 via swaps (if achievable). 59 can be expressed as sum of squares: 49+9+1 =59 (7^2+3^2+1^2). So need three disjoint swaps with differences 7,3,1. That seems plausible.\n\nThus m=13 likely possible.\n\nBut also need existence of tiling. Probably both 11 and 13 possible, 12 not.\n\nWe should provide sum of all possible m = 11+13=24.\n\nLet's be confident.\n\nThus answer: 24.", "response": "ANSWER: 24", "answer": 24, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.54972008203913e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999984502811188, "logp_correct": -1.54972008203913e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4643467848412652, "cot_lp_min64": -1.5504777329303456, "frac_below_-5": 0.00867311334861928, "digit_lp_min": -7.1995720863342285, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 10031, "lp_end": -8.344646857949556e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -5.602820692729438e-06, "logp_nocot": -2.8994125413737493, "p_nocot": 0.05505555341799968, "uplift": 2.8994109916536672}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "282f315e-f668-434d-8037-2b112af067b8", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Given an integer $n>1$, let $S_{n}$ be the group of permutations of the numbers $1,2, \\\\ldots, n$. Two players, A and B, play the following game. Taking turns, they select elements (one element at a time) from the group $S_{n}$. It is forbidden to select an element that has already been selected. The game ends when the selected elements generate the whole group $S_{n}$. The player who made the last move loses the game. The first move is made by A. For which values of n does player A have a winning strategy? Please provide the sum of these values of n.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Given an integer $n>1$, let $S_{n}$ be the group of permutations of the numbers $1,2, \\\\ldots, n$. Two players, A and B, play the following game. Taking turns, they select elements (one element at a time) from the group $S_{n}$. It is forbidden to select an element that has already been selected. The game ends when the selected elements generate the whole group $S_{n}$. The player who made the last move loses the game. The first move is made by A. For which values of n does player A have a winning strategy? Please provide the sum of these values of n.", "true_answer": 5, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "282f315e-f668-434d-8037-2b112af067b8:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 30532, 132717, 25307, 68745, 2813, 1919, 7238, 7310, 3721, 4736, 174595, 591, 336, 2170, 2935, 100556, 26, 2813, 17095, 1261, 290, 920, 328, 6386, 174595, 10419, 290, 6062, 3566, 336, 2170, 26, 2174, 5275, 63582, 350, 18320, 4346, 7060, 741, 2632, 581, 1309, 316, 1761, 1218, 665, 9578, 38519, 316, 413, 19387, 316, 1520, 1721, 5275, 484, 82313, 13986, 364, 2167, 1309, 3