 2632, 6052, 6960, 220, 1353, 364, 16577, 1890, 25, 30456, 581, 10389, 8812, 3605, 656, 2360, 17679, 8812, 133589, 268, 306, 1577, 6000, 889, 10112, 21700, 483, 12229, 15248, 316, 10389, 3605, 316, 220, 15, 395, 1236, 4355, 656, 2360, 261, 945, 42218, 8302, 484, 8844, 1236, 11242, 591, 1577, 6000, 31366, 71327, 4493, 20404, 30, 3004, 71327, 12229, 13, 2632, 2454, 4355, 306, 1577, 6000, 2804, 679, 540, 5153, 1001, 8812, 133589, 2236, 290, 1920, 11242, 306, 6000, 15, 6284, 2973, 413, 31366, 113807, 25, 5207, 25, 306, 4355, 11, 1354, 553, 1920, 15697, 25, 220, 15, 326, 220, 16, 13, 2214, 261, 4206, 306, 6000, 15, 11, 1617, 1606, 4149, 12229, 13034, 382, 94774, 1917, 5323, 538, 4275, 14210, 5225, 350, 288, 11965, 741, 3072, 3518, 290, 4355, 1354, 553, 1920, 11242, 306, 6000, 15, 350, 2668, 220, 17, 74, 11, 17, 74, 10, 16, 741, 623, 13057, 4206, 350, 28689, 5225, 8, 853, 860, 12229, 13034, 350, 76543, 810, 869, 11, 3561, 11, 1849, 8812, 11, 1917, 8812, 2245, 13972, 2804, 413, 8812, 13, 2632, 2454, 4355, 10860, 540, 5153, 1001, 8812, 133589, 24878, 484, 13057, 4206, 483, 3543, 350, 105049, 25511, 483, 6000, 16, 8812, 11, 503, 17679, 483, 6931, 4206, 8812, 741, 730, 1062, 1890, 8812, 133589, 3605, 540, 5153, 220, 16, 777, 4355, 13, 65037, 395, 2174, 6000, 11, 290, 13057, 4206, 328, 6000, 2058, 350, 28689, 5225, 8, 1217, 853, 860, 12229, 13034, 316, 1849, 11, 813, 4414, 8812, 13, 2632, 19957, 8812, 777, 4355, 540, 22569, 382, 220, 16, 13, 23586, 6931, 11608, 220, 1353, 18305, 364, 84787, 19957, 2086, 328, 133589, 268, 19449, 6772, 1236, 220, 17, 87, 17, 13749, 382, 220, 1353, 364, 17045, 25, 220, 1353, 13], "n_tokens": 10205, "words": 6979, "band_lo": 3489.5, "band_hi": 13958.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have a 100x100 grid (presumably unit squares). Tiled in two ways: one tiling uses only dominoes (1x2 rectangles) covering the board entirely, no overlap, covering all squares. Another tiling uses only 2x2 squares (i.e., each tile is a block of four unit squares). Both are perfect tilings.\n\nWe consider the domino tiling. Among its dominoes, some are entirely inside some 2x2 square (i.e., there exists a location of a 2x2 block of unit squares such that the domino lies completely within it; i.e., the domino occupies two of the four cells of that 2x2 block). We want the minimal possible number of dominoes that are wholly inside some 2x2 block, over all possible pairs of such tilings? Or for a given domino tiling, we consider the 2x2 tiling also? The problem says \"tiled in two ways: only with dominoes and only with 2×2 squares.\" So we have two separate tilings of the same board: one domino tiling, one 2x2 tiling. We ask minimal number of dominoes that are entirely inside some 2x2 square. Means we compare the two tilings: count dominoes from the domino tiling that happen to be completely contained within one of the 2x2 squares of the second tiling.\n\nWe need to find minimal possible count given we can choose both tilings arbitrarily (subject to covering board). Equivalent to we want to arrange a domino tiling that avoids being inside a 2x2 region as much as possible, while also there exists a 2x2 tiling of board (which trivially always exists because 100 even). The 2x2 tiling is just dividing board into 50x50 blocks of size 2x2.\n\nWe can choose orientation of those blocks arbitrarily? The tiling by 2x2 squares must partition into disjoint 2x2 squares covering the board. Since grid dimensions are multiples of 2, one simple tiling is standard aligning to the overall grid lines: blocks at coordinates (2i,2j) to (2i+1,2j+1). However, we could also shift the blocks by one? But 2x2 squares must align with grid lines; the board is 100x100 unit squares with integer coordinates. A 2x2 square can start at any integer coordinate (x,y) where 0≤x≤98, same parity? Actually a 2x2 square occupies cells (x+1..x+2, y+1..y+2). So start positions can be any (i,j) where i and j are integers; to tile the board, the collection of start positions must partition all cells. Essentially we need a perfect matching of unit squares into 2x2 blocks. This is equivalent to choosing a checkerboard coloring? Not exactly.\n\nSince board is even dimensions, there are many tilings of 2x2 squares (like a domino tiling but of 2x2). The 2x2 squares can be arranged in patterns; essentially we can tile with a \"checkerboard\" of size 2 blocks offset every row/column? Let's think: Suppose we use a standard tiling: blocks anchored at even coordinates. Another tiling could be offset by one in both directions leading to overlapping? But offset by 1 would cause misalignment at borders; can't cover edges. To tile whole board, the set of start positions must be such that each row is partitioned into intervals of length 2. That means for each row, the positions of squares that include that row must start either at column 0,2,... or maybe some rows start at column 1,3,... but then adjacency constraints cause alternating pattern similar to a checkerboard of 2x2 blocks. Indeed you can have tiling where rows are alternately offset: e.g., row pairs (0-1) start at even columns, next pair (2-3) start at odd columns, etc. That works because each 2x2 block occupies two consecutive rows; offsetting by one column across pairs is possible.\n\nThus we have flexibility: essentially we can tile board by partitioning into 2x2 blocks where each pair of rows (rows 2k,2k+1) can be either aligned (even columns) or shifted (odd columns), as long as adjacent pairs are consistent? Let's formalize: Represent board with coordinates (i,j) where i rows (0..99) and j columns (0..99). A 2x2 block can be identified by its top-left cell (i,j) where i,j are between 0 and 98 inclusive. The board tiling constraints: each cell belongs to exactly one block. Since blocks are 2 rows tall, the row parity determines grouping of rows into pairs. So we must partition rows into groups of size 2: either (0,1), (2,3) etc. There's no flexibility there because blocks cannot cross row boundaries beyond 2. Because any block occupies exactly two consecutive rows, so rows are paired. Since the board height is even, we can pair rows as (0,1), (2,3), ... essentially fixed. However, we could also pair rows as (1,2), (3,4), ...? But then row 0 would be unpaired. Not allowed. So rows must be paired as (0,1), (2,3), ..., (98,99). Thus block vertical positions are fixed.\n\nWithin each row-pair (i,i+1), we need to tile the 100 columns with 2x2 blocks. Within that pair, blocks can be placed such that their columns start at either even or odd positions, but they must not overlap. So we need to partition 100 columns into intervals of length 2; we can choose start positions either 0,2,4,... (even) or 1,3,5,... (odd) for each block. However, the start columns across that row-pair must all have same parity because otherwise overlapping. So each row-pair can be either \"aligned\" (even) or \"shifted\" (odd). This yields 2^{50} tilings.\n\nThus we can choose for each of the 50 horizontal stripes of height 2 whether the 2x2 blocks start at column 0 or 1 (mod 2). Good.\n\nNow, given such a 2x2 tiling, we want to place dominoes (1x2) covering board. Dominoes can be placed horizontally or vertically. We want to minimize number of dominoes that sit completely inside some 2x2 block. That occurs when a domino's two cells are within same 2x2 block. Domino can be horizontal (occupying two adjacent cells in same row) or vertical (two cells in same column across two rows). For a domino to be fully inside a 2x2 block, its cells must be among the four cells of that block.\n\nObservation: Within each 2x2 block, there are exactly 2 possible domino placements that stay inside: one horizontal in top row, one horizontal in bottom row, and two vertical (left column, right column). That's total 4 domino placements inside the block (if considering orientation). But a domino tiling cannot place overlapping dominoes; each block of 4 cells will be tiled by exactly two dominoes (since area 4). So each 2x2 block will contain either: two dominoes both wholly inside it (if dominoes do not cross block boundaries) OR at least one domino crossing the block boundary (i.e., a domino that uses a cell from this block and cell outside). Since each block contains 4 cells, the total number of dominoes covering those cells is 2 (some could be partially inside). So we can define for each block: if both dominoes are internal, then we count 2 internal dominoes contributed; if one internal and one crossing, then count 1 internal; if none internal (both crossing), then 0.\n\nGoal: minimize total internal domino count across all blocks.\n\nConstraints come from adjacency: a domino that crosses a block boundary must share an edge with adjacent block. Since block boundaries are either vertical lines between columns (if block orientation shifted) or horizontal lines between row-pairs? Actually block boundaries are between the two rows within a pair? No, each block occupies two rows; there is no internal horizontal boundary inside block. Between row-pairs, there is a horizontal line after each 2 rows, which is also a block boundary between blocks of vertical adjacency. Domino crossing that would be vertical across rows belonging to different row-pairs (i.e., spanning rows 1-2). However dominoes crossing such boundary would have to be vertical of length 2 covering rows 1 and 2 (which belong to different row-pairs). That's allowed.\n\nThus we must consider a grid of 2x2 blocks (50 rows of blocks, 50 columns of blocks? Actually columns number = 100/2 =50; also rows = 50). So we have a 50x50 array of blocks. Within each block, we can decide pattern of dominoes.\n\nWe are asked minimal number of internal dominoes across whole board given that we can also choose the block orientation (shift parity) for each stripe.\n\nWe can treat this as a combinatorial optimization: find a domino tiling of 100x100 such that few dominoes are completely contained within any 2x2 block (with respect to a given 2x2 tiling). Equivalent to we want many dominoes to cross block boundaries.\n\nObservation: Any domino that is vertical and spans two rows belongs to exactly one of two possibilities: either both rows belong to same row-pair (i.e., rows 2k,2k+1) i.e., inside one block vertically (if also columns same block), or it spans rows across a boundary between row-pairs (i.e., rows 2k+1 and 2k+2). The latter crosses block horizontal boundary and thus cannot be fully inside any block (because any block is only 2 rows tall, can't include both rows 2k+1 and 2k+2). So vertical dominoes crossing between row-pairs are automatically non-internal.\n\nSimilarly for horizontal dominoes: they occupy two adjacent columns. They can be within same block (if columns are within same 2-column group of a block) or cross block vertical boundaries (between adjacent blocks in same row-pair). Horizontal domino crossing between blocks is also non-internal.\n\nThus to minimize internal dominoes, we want as many dominoes as possible crossing block boundaries either horizontally or vertically.\n\nConstraints of domino tiling: each cell must be covered.\n\nLet's consider each block row (pair of rows). Within that stripe, we have a 2x100 rectangle (2 rows, 100 columns). We also have a tiling of dominoes within that stripe, possibly interacting with adjacent stripes via vertical dominoes crossing the stripe boundary (between rows 2k+1 and 2k+2). So we can view the whole board as stacking of 50 stripes each of height 2.\n\nGoal: minimize internal dominoes within each 2x2 block.\n\nLet’s denote for each block column (of width 2), the configuration of dominoes in the two rows of that stripe.\n\nWithin a stripe, options for covering cells of a 2x2 block column (two rows, two columns) using dominoes are:\n\n- Two horizontal dominoes (top and bottom) each internal (count 2 internal).\n- Two vertical dominoes (left column and right column) each internal (count 2 internal).\n- One vertical domino spanning the two rows in column 1 (internal?) That's within block, but then remaining two cells (top-right, bottom-right) must be covered by a horizontal domino crossing block boundary? Wait they lie in same block column? Actually they share column2 same rows, could be vertical as well. But if we use one vertical internal left column and then horizontal domino covering top-right and top-right+1? But top-right adjacent to top-right+1 is column 3 which belongs to next block horizontally; that horizontal domino would cross block boundary, which is allowed but then top-right cell together with top-left? No horizontal domino must be adjacent cells horizontally; top-right cell is column j+1 (within same block). Its neighbor column j+2 belongs to next block. That is possible. However then bottom-right cell must be covered by vertical domino crossing downward? Complex.\n\nWe need to view as global tiling constraints.\n\nMaybe easier: we can think of representing domino tiling as perfect matching on grid graph. Count internal dominoes = number of edges completely inside a 2x2 block (i.e., both endpoints in same block). Want minimal number.\n\nWe can treat each block as a set of four vertices; internal edges are those within that 4-vertex subgraph.\n\nGoal: minimize total internal edges across all blocks. This is reminiscent of minimizing number of monochromatic edges under a partition.\n\nGiven the 2x2 block partition (parity pattern per stripe), we want a domino matching with minimal internal edges. This is equivalent to we want a perfect matching of the whole 100x100 grid graph that uses as few edges that are inside the 2x2 blocks as possible.\n\nObservation: Edges crossing block boundaries are edges between vertices belonging to different blocks (adjacent cells either horizontally across block column boundary, or vertically across block row boundary). Since each vertex belongs to exactly one block, each block has 4 vertices. In the matching, each vertex is matched with one neighbor. If the matched neighbor lies in another block, then that edge is not internal; else internal.\n\nThus we want to maximize number of matching edges that go between blocks. This is similar to a graph bipartite matching maximizing inter-block edges.\n\nConsider representation: Create a graph where each block is a supernode of four vertices; we want a perfect matching on entire vertex set; internal edges correspond to matching within a supernode. Since each supernode has even number of vertices (4), we could match all its vertices with vertices outside, resulting in 0 internal edges, but constraints may prevent that globally because of parity flows.\n\nIs it possible to have a perfect matching with zero internal edges? That would mean each block's four vertices are matched to vertices in other blocks. Let's test feasibility.\n\nConsider a block at position (row pair r, column pair c). Its four cells are (r*2, c*2) top-left, (r*2, c*2+1) top-right, (r*2+1, c*2) bottom-left, (r*2+1, c*2+1) bottom-right. For each cell, its neighbors are up/down/left/right (if exists). For cells on the interior of board, they have up to 4 neighbors. Among those, edges to cells within same block are internal; edges to cells outside are crossing.\n\nIf we want no internal matches, each cell must be matched to a neighbor outside its block. Let's see if we can pair them accordingly without conflict.\n\nPotential strategy: Use only vertical dominoes crossing between row-pairs (i.e., spanning rows 1-2, 3-4 etc). However those dominoes connect cells from different row-pairs but same column. Consider a cell in row 0 (top of first stripe). Its only vertical neighbor inside same stripe is row1 same column (still within same block). To avoid internal, the cell must be matched horizontally to adjacent column (within same stripe) or vertically to row -1 (nonexistent) or row2 (next stripe) if that neighbor cell belongs to another block? Actually row 0 cell at (0, j) vertical neighbor down is (1,j) which is same stripe (2x2 block row). That's internal within block if j within same column pair? Wait block column width is 2. So if j is even (block's left column), (0,j) and (1,j) share same block (both rows 0,1, column j). So that would be internal. To avoid internal, we must not match vertically within same stripe. So we must match horizontally with (0,j±1). That neighbor could be either inside same block (if j is even then right neighbor j+1 is within same block) which would be internal as well. So we need to pair cell with a neighbor that belongs to a different block; that requires crossing both row-pair and column-group maybe.\n\nLet's examine parity arrangement possibilities.\n\nMaybe we can achieve a perfect matching with 0 internal edges using a checkerboard of dominoes that are all oriented such that they cross block boundaries. Is that possible? Let's attempt small case: For a 2x2 block grid (i.e., 2 rows, 2 columns), can we tile with dominoes such that no domino lies completely inside the block? That's impossible because there are only 4 cells; any domino covers two cells. If we avoid internal edges, each domino must go to a cell outside block, but there are no outside cells. So minimal internal for a single 2x2 isolated region is at least 1? Actually need to consider entire board; blocks are adjacent, so dominoes can cross between neighboring blocks. For a 2x2 block adjacent to others, you could have each of its four cells matched to a neighbor in adjacent block. This would mean each of its four cells matched outward, resulting in zero internal dominoes for that block. Is that possible globally? Likely yes: think of a checkerboard pattern of dominoes oriented horizontally across the boundaries between columns of blocks, and vertically across boundaries between rows of blocks, forming a lattice that pairs cells of adjacent blocks. For example, consider pairing each cell with neighbor to the right (horizontal) for cells in even columns of blocks, and left for odd columns, generating dominoes that bridge block columns. Similarly vertical bridging might be needed for cells on boundaries where horizontal not possible.\n\nWe need a perfect matching that avoids internal edges altogether. Let's see if we can construct such a matching on a large even-by-even board with 2x2 block partition.\n\nConsider tiling the board with dominoes each of which straddles a block boundary either horizontally or vertically, never staying entirely inside a block. This seems plausible by using a \"checkerboard of size 2\". Example: For each row, place horizontal dominoes spanning columns (1,2), (3,4), etc offset such that each 2x2 block gets its left column cells matched with right column cells of neighboring block. But then the right column cells also used, could conflict.\n\nLet’s attempt constructive pattern: Use only horizontal dominoes, each covering two cells that belong to adjacent columns. Align such that block columns of width 2; if we place dominoes covering cells with columns (1,2), (3,4)... then the domino occupies cells whose column pair may be within the same block (if block columns are (0,1), (2,3) etc). To avoid internal, we need domino to cross from column 1 (right column of block 0) to column 2 (left column of block 1). This would happen if we offset by one column: domino covering columns (1,2), (3,4) etc. That means dominoes start at odd columns. Within each block column pair (even, odd), the domino crossing the boundary uses the odd column of block A and the even column of block B. So each block contributes its odd column cells matched outward, but its even column cells will be matched to previous block's odd column. So each cell is matched across block boundary. No internal edges.\n\nHowever this pattern requires that the board has width 100, which is even; starting at column 1 (odd) produce dominoes covering columns 1-2, 3-4,... up to 99-100? Actually column indices 0..99. Starting at 1 gives pairs (1,2), (3,4), ..., (97,98), (99,100?) but 100 out of range. So we would have leftover column 0 cell unmatched. Therefore we need to also use vertical dominoes for those cells perhaps. Could design a checkerboard pattern mixing horizontals and verticals that together avoid internal.\n\nPerhaps easier: treat as a bipartite graph where each block is considered; we want a perfect matching where each vertex is matched to a vertex in neighboring block. This is like a \"perfect matching in the line graph\" etc. Might be feasible because the block adjacency graph is 4-regular (each block has 4 neighboring blocks). Since each block has 4 cells, we need to match its cells to neighboring blocks each providing one edge. Could assign each of the four cells to a distinct neighboring block (up, down, left, right). That would give zero internal.\n\nThus minimal number could be 0. But we must ensure such matching exists globally with boundary conditions (edges at board perimeter). Cells on outer boundary have fewer neighboring blocks (edge cells). For a boundary cell, some adjacent neighbor may be outside board; cannot match outward. So those cells must be matched internally (or with another boundary cell of same block). Thus interior blocks may have zero internal, but edge blocks may force some internal dominoes.\n\nThus minimal count likely determined by boundary.\n\nLet's compute minimal possible internal dominoes forced by edges.\n\nConsider board perimeter: cells on outermost rows (0 and 99) and columns (0 and 99). They have at most 3 neighboring cells (some outside). For a corner cell (0,0), its neighbors are (0,1) right, (1,0) down. Both are inside same block? (0,0) block includes (0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1). So right neighbor (0,1) is same block; down neighbor (1,0) same block. No neighbor outside block. Therefore corner cell must be matched internally. Thus each corner forces at least one internal domino covering two corner cells? Wait we have two corners? Actually each corner cell can be matched to its adjacent cell within same block; there are two corners per block maybe. The block in the corner (top-left block) includes both corner cells (0,0) and (0,1) top row, and (1,0),(1,1). The only way to match the corner cells without internal edges is impossible because they have no external neighbors. So at least one internal domino must be within that corner block.\n\nSimilarly for any block touching the outer boundary: cells on the outer edge have fewer external options; may still have external neighbor along the edge direction (e.g., a cell on top row of a non-corner block has neighbor above outside board, not allowed, but left or right neighbor may be external block horizontally). For a cell on top edge but not at corner, its possible external neighbor is left or right across block boundary (if block column has internal crossing). So could match outward horizontally.\n\nThus the only forced internal dominoes are those due to corners. Let's examine corner blocks:\n\nTop-left corner block positions rows 0-1, columns 0-1. Its four cells are all at outermost top and left edges, no external neighbor (outside board). So all dominoes covering these cells must stay within the block. Therefore this block will contain exactly two internal dominoes (since its four cells must be tiled). Minimal internal contribution from this block: at least 2 internal dominoes (since all dominoes inside). Similarly top-right corner block (rows0-1, columns98-99) will have both top edge and right edge outside; but cells have external neighbor to left (inside board) across column 97? Actually rightmost block columns 98-99. Its cells can match leftwards into column 97, which belongs to adjacent block. So those cells have external neighbor horizontally. So they could avoid internal? Let's test: cell (0,98) right neighbor (0,99) internal; left neighbor (0,97) belongs to previous block (since column 97 is odd, which block may depend on parity). If that is allowed, then possible to match outward. So not forced.\n\nSimilarly bottom-left corner block may have external neighbor upward? Not.\n\nThus only corner block at top-left (and maybe bottom-left??) Let's examine bottom-left block rows 98-99, columns 0-1. Cells have neighbor below outside board, left outside board, but can match upward or rightward. Upward is row 97 which belongs to previous stripe, which is external block. So they can avoid internal. So only the top-left corner block is forced.\n\nBut also top-right corner block may have no external neighbor to the right, but can have left neighbor; bottom-left can have top neighbor; bottom-right can have left or top neighbor.\n\nThus minimal forced internal domino count is at least 2 (from that corner block). But is there also forced internal due to parity of number of blocks with odd degree? Let's consider a flow argument: consider graph whose vertices are blocks; each internal domino corresponds to an edge within same block (i.e., consumes two vertices of block). External domino corresponds to edge between two blocks (matching those particular cells). For each block, number of external domino endpoints equal number of cells matched outward = some integer between 0 and 4, and the remainder cells are matched internally (forming internal dominoes). Since internal dominoes use two cells inside block, the count of internal dominoes in a block equals (4 - external_endpoints)/2. So internal count per block = (4 - outdegree)/2 where outdegree is number of incident external matched edges (each counts 1 per cell). Since outdegree can be 0,2,4 (must be even because number of unmatched cells must be even). So internal domino count per block can be 2,1,0 respectively.\n\nThus total internal dominoes = sum over blocks of (4 - outdeg)/2 = 2*B - (1/2) Σ outdeg, where B = total number of blocks = 2500 (since 50x50). Σ outdeg = total number of external matched edges incident to blocks = 2 * (# external dominoes) because each external domino connects two blocks, contributing 1 to each block's outdeg. So Σ outdeg = 2E_ext, where E_ext is number of external dominoes. Number of total dominoes = (100*100)/2 = 5000. So let I = internal domino count, E_ext = external domino count = 5000 - I.\n\nThen Σ outdeg = 2E_ext = 2(5000 - I) = 10000 - 2I.\n\nPlug into I = 2B - (1/2)(10000 - 2I) = 2B - 5000 + I.\n\nThus I cancels? Let's compute: I = 2B - 5000 + I => subtract I both sides gives 0 = 2B - 5000 => 2B = 5000 => B = 2500. Indeed B =2500 matches. So identity holds for any tiling (no constraint). So internal count not directly limited by counts.\n\nWe need minimization given boundary constraints.\n\nConsider blocks at the outermost rows/columns. For each block on the boundary, some of its cells lack external neighboring cells beyond board. Yet those cells may still connect to adjacent block along the boundary direction (horizontal for top row, vertical for left column). For top row blocks, cells have neighbor above none; but they can connect downwards (horizontal neighbor across columns) or vertically downwards into row 1 (but that's internal to same block). Wait vertical neighbor downwards for cell (0,j) is (1,j) which is same block (since rows 0,1). So internal. Thus the only way for a top-row block cell to avoid internal is via horizontal edge to left/right neighbor (which may be in same block if same column pair) or if parity shift leads to neighbor belonging to adjacent block? horizontal neighbor column difference 1 may belong to same block if columns within same 2-width group. For top row block, its two columns are either (even, odd) etc. The horizontal neighbor of the left column cell is right column (within same block) internal. The right column cell's right neighbor is column+1 which belongs to next block (if block columns are width 2). Hence only the rightmost column cells can have external horizontal neighbor crossing block boundary (if there is a block to the right). Similarly leftmost column cells could have external neighbor to left if there is block left (except for left edge). So for a block not at leftmost edge, its left column cells could connect leftwards to previous block; its right column cells could connect rightwards to next block.\n\nThus for top row, each block (except leftmost) can have its left column cells match leftwards (external) and right column cells maybe match rightwards as well, but those would cause interactions with neighboring blocks.\n\nIt looks plausible to arrange all interior cells to be external, except those at the top-left corner block forced internal.\n\nLet's attempt constructive tiling:\n\nWe can use a pattern of dominoes that form a \"checkerboard\" of horizontal dominoes across each row starting at column 1 (odd) for all rows except maybe first row? Let's analyze.\n\nIf we place horizontal dominoes covering cells (i, j) and (i, j+1) for every row i and for every odd j (i.e., j = 1,3,5,...,99). This uses dominoes from odd to even column (odd-even). Each such domino spans columns from odd to even (e.g., 1-2, 3-4...). This means within each block (columns 0-1,2-3,...), the domino covering columns 1-2 crosses block boundary (since column1 is right column of block0, column2 left column of block1). Similarly domino covering 3-4 crosses between blocks 1 and2. So each block of columns (0,1) will have its column1 cells matched outward to block1, while its column0 cells will need to be matched some other way (maybe vertical). We can then match column0 cells vertically with cells below (within same block) but that would be internal. However we could also match column0 cells vertically downward across row-pair boundary (i.e., to row i+1?), but that stays within same block rows (since block rows are pairs). Example: cells at (0,0) top-left cannot go downward because row1 same block, internal. Could go upward not possible. Could go left none. So corner cell must be internal.\n\nFor other rows (i not 0), column0 cells could be matched vertically downwards to cell (i+1,0). That is within same block (since rows i,i+1 both belong to same stripe pair maybe). Actually if i is even (i = 2k), then (i,0) and (i+1,0) are within same 2x2 block (since block rows 2k,2k+1). So internal again. So vertical matching does not help.\n\nThus column0 cells may need internal matches unless they can match horizontally leftwards, but leftwards would be column -1 nonexistent for column0 blocks.\n\nThus each block column0 (the leftmost column of each block) may need internal matching for its cells, unless we offset the block arrangement such that column0 is actually the right column of a shifted block? But blocks fixed to columns of size 2. The leftmost column of board is column 0; there is no column -1, so cells in column0 cannot match horizontally outward. Thus they must be matched vertically within same block column. That leads to internal vertical dominoes for each column0 cell pair (rows 0-1, 2-3, etc). That gives for each block in column 0 (i.e., all 50 blocks in first column), we have two vertical internal dominoes? Actually each block in first column comprises rows (2k,2k+1) and columns 0,1. Its column0 cells (top and bottom) can be matched vertically internal within block (cells (2k,0)-(2k+1,0)). That's one internal domino per block. Similarly column1 cells are already matched outward via horizontal domino (odd-even crossing). So each block in first column would have exactly 1 internal domino (the vertical left column). That adds 50 internal dominoes total.\n\nThus minimal internal count maybe at least 50 (from first column) plus 2 from top-left corner block already counted? Actually the top-left block already has that internal vertical domino in column0 plus maybe another internal if its column1 cells don't cross? But column1 cells can cross to right block (since it's not blocked). So top-left block would have exactly 1 internal domino (vertical column0). Wait earlier we thought must have at least 2 internal dominoes because no external neighbor. But with horizontal crossing, column1 cells can match to block to the right, leaving only column0 vertical internal. That's feasible because column0 cells have neighbor downwards internal, column1 cells have external neighbor to the right (block column1). So top-left block can have only 1 internal domino, not 2. Yes because the block's top-left corner cell (0,0) matched vertically to (1,0) internal. Its top-right cell (0,1) matched horizontally to (0,2) which belongs to block (col pair 2-3). So allowed.\n\nThus only forced internal edges are for cells in column0 that cannot cross to left; they need internal vertical matches. Similarly cells in row0 cannot match upward, but they may match horizontally outward if not in column0. For cell (0,2) etc, they can match horizontally to (0,1) internal? Wait (0,2) is column2 (even) left column of block (2,3). Its left neighbor (0,1) belongs to previous block (1-2). Good external. So top row cells are fine except column0.\n\nSimilarly for rightmost column 99 (odd). Cells there have no right neighbor; but they can match leftwards to column98 which belongs to same block (since block columns 98-99). That's internal. However they could also match vertically internal. So rightmost column may force internal edges? Let's examine column 99 is odd (right column of block). Its left neighbor column98 is even (left column of same block). That edge is internal to block. There is no external neighbor to the right. So cells in column99 cannot have external horizontal match; they could have vertical match to row+1 (if not bottom row) internal; or can match vertically crossing row-pair boundary? That's internal within block because vertically still same block. So they seem forced internal as well. But they could match vertically with cell below (if not bottommost row) which belongs to block (rows 1-2) same block column pair 98-99. Still internal. So column99 cells seem forced to internal matches.\n\nThus each block in last column also will have at least 1 internal domino (vertical on right column?). Wait each block includes columns 98 (even) and 99 (odd). Since column99 cells have no external neighbor horizontally, they must match within block: vertical internal domino with cell (row+1,99) or perhaps horizontal internal with column98 (same block). So each block in last column contributes at least 1 internal domino.\n\nSimilarly first row cells in row0 have no upward neighbor, but they can match horizontally outward (if not column0) as earlier; column0 already accounted. Row0 cells at column even? they can match horizontally to right (cross block) as we used. For column 99 (odd) they cannot match right, but could match left internal. So still internal.\n\nThus seems each block in the first column and last column each contributes at least 1 internal domino, total 2*50 = 100 internal dominoes; maybe plus something for corners counted twice but that's okay.\n\nBut could we reduce further by using vertical external dominoes between first column blocks and second column blocks? No because vertical external means crossing row-pair boundary but staying same column, still column0. That still cells column0 matched to cell below in next stripe (row 1 to row2). However column0 cell (row0,0) cannot match downwards because row1 same block; but if we use vertical domino spanning rows1-2, that involves cell (1,0) and (2,0), which are in different row-pairs: (1,0) is in top block, (2,0) is in block below (since block rows 2-3). But these two cells belong to different blocks? Actually (1,0) is bottom row of block top (rows0-1). (2,0) is top row of block below (rows2-3). They are in same column0, but different blocks (since block rows differ). However vertical domino spanning rows1-2 crosses the horizontal block boundary, and thus is external (since cells in different blocks). So column0 cell (1,0) could be matched vertically outward rather than internally. That would reduce internal count, but then cell (0,0) remains unmatched; must be matched internal (vertical with (1,0) not possible then). Could match (0,0) horizontally? No left neighbor; could match vertically with (1,0) internal (as earlier) but that uses (1,0) which now matched externally onward - conflict. So we need a configuration where both cells of column0 in a block maybe both matched externally: top cell matches downwards to (1,0) internal? That's internal. bottom cell matches downwards to (2,0) external crossing boundary but that cell (2,0) belongs to block below, which would need to match upward etc. This seems create a chain; perhaps we can have alternating pattern where internal count per block reduces.\n\nLet's attempt systematic approach using parity flow.\n\nThink of each column of cells (each column index j). For j=0 (leftmost), we have a column of 100 cells. We need to match them via dominoes possibly horizontal (to column1) or vertical (within column). Since column1 cells exist, we could match many of column0 cells horizontally to column1 cells, but those edges are internal to block? Actually column0 cell (i,0) adjacent right column1 is same block (since block includes columns 0,1). So that's internal.\n\nThus any horizontal match from column0 to column1 is internal, cannot eliminate internal. So column0 cells cannot have external matches (since only other neighbor is down/up). Up is none; down is (i+1,0) which is internal within same block if i is even (pair). However if i is odd (i=1,3,...), then (i,0) down neighbor (i+1,0) belongs to next block row? For i odd, i and i+1 are rows (odd, even) belonging to two different row-pairs: e.g., rows1 and2 belong to different blocks (row-pair 0-1 vs 2-3). Because block rows are pairs (0,1), (2,3), etc. So vertical neighbor across boundary connects two blocks. So for odd i, vertical edge to i+1 is external. Similarly vertical edge to i-1 (if odd?) up neighbor (i-1,0) is even row internal same block.\n\nThus we could design a matching that pairs each odd row cell in column0 with the cell below it (i+1) which is in next block, making external matches, while pairing each even row cell with the cell above? Not possible. Let's attempt pattern: For each pair of rows (2k,2k+1) (block), we could match the lower cell (row 2k+1) vertically downwards to row 2k+2 (next block) external, and match the upper cell (2k) vertically upwards? No upward none. So upper cell still needs match, maybe horizontal internal with column1. So block would have one internal (vertical maybe not) Actually internal could be horizontal for upper cell? No horizontal uses both cells in top row across columns 0-1 (internal). That would pair upper cell with its right neighbor, internal. Then lower cell pairs downwards external. Thus each block in first column would have 1 internal domino (horizontal covering (2k,0)-(2k,1)). That's still 1 internal per block. Could we make 0 internal per block? To have zero internal, both cells in column0 must be matched externally: upper cell matched upward (none) or left (none) or down to row 2k+1 (internal) or down across boundary (needs odd row). Upper cell is even row; its down neighbor row+1 is odd row within same block internal, not external. Up neighbor none. So impossible. Thus each block in first column must have at least 1 internal domino, indeed.\n\nSimilarly last column (99) analogous: each block must have at least 1 internal domino.\n\nThus lower bound = 2*50 = 100 internal dominoes.\n\nNow can we achieve exactly 100? Let's construct tiling achieving 1 internal per block for first and last columns, and 0 internal for all interior columns. Need to ensure overall matching feasible.\n\nWe can design as:\n\n- For interior columns (2..97), use horizontal dominoes crossing block boundaries (odd-even pairs as earlier). This creates no internal within interior blocks because each block’s left column cells (even) match leftwards to previous block? Wait need pattern to give each interior block outdegree 4 (i.e., all 4 cells external), resulting internal 0.\n\nLet's attempt scheme: Use horizontal dominoes covering columns (1,2), (3,4), ..., (97,98). This gives for blocks:\n\nBlock columns (0,1): its column1 cells matched to column2 (external); its column0 cells currently unmatched; we will handle them with vertical internal (as earlier). So block (0,1) has 1 internal.\n\nBlock columns (2,3): its column2 cells matched left to column1 (external), column3 cells matched right to column4 (external) (since domino covering 3-4). Thus block (2,3) has 0 internal.\n\nSimilarly, block (4,5): column4 matched left external, column5 matched right external? Wait domino covering 5-6 exists, not 5-6 unless pattern continues. So we need to ensure each column's cells are matched either left or right external. Using dominoes on every odd-even pair ensures that column j (odd) external to j+1 even; column j (even) may be external to j-1 odd if domino covering (j-1,j). Since dominoes cover (odd, even) pairs where odd is left cell? Actually pair (1,2) means odd left, even right. So column2 gets matched leftwards (external). Column3 then belongs to pair (3,4) meaning column3 matched rightwards to 4 external. Thus block (2,3) gets both columns external (2 left, 3 right). Good.\n\nProceed alternately.\n\nThus interior blocks (except first and last) have 0 internal.\n\nNow need to cover the column0 cells (first column) as described: for each block (rows 2k,2k+1; columns0,1), match the top cell (2k,0) with its right neighbor (2k,1) internal horizontal? That would be internal. The bottom cell (2k+1,0) could be matched vertically downwards to (2k+2,0) external (since across block boundary). But then that uses cell (2k+2,0) which belongs to next block's top cell. This would cause chain; need to ensure matching consistent across rows.\n\nSimpler: match column0 cells vertically within each block (2k,0)-(2k+1,0) internal vertical domino. That's 1 internal per block. No external from column0. That aligns with earlier count 1 internal per block first column.\n\nSimilarly last column internal vertical per block.\n\nThus total internal = 50 (first column) + 50 (last column) = 100.\n\nNow need to ensure that cells in column1 that are matched horizontally with column2 (external) do not conflict with internal vertical dominoes in column0 etc. That's fine as they are separate rows.\n\nNow we need to verify that all cells are covered uniquely.\n\nLet's construct algorithmically for each row i:\n\n- If column0: pair (i,0)-(i+1,0) for i even (vertical internal). Works for i=0,2,...,98.\n\n- For columns 1..98: Use horizontal dominoes pairing (i, odd j) with (i, j+1) where j odd (j=1,3,5,...,97). This covers all cells in columns 1..98 for every row.\n\nCheck coverage: For row i, columns:\nj=0 handled by vertical internal (paired with row i+1 same column).\nj=1 paired with j=2 (since 1 odd) horizontally.\nj=2 paired with 1 (already).\nj=3 paired with 4.\n...\nj=97 paired with 98.\nj=98 paired with 97.\nj=99? column99 not covered yet. We'll need to handle column99 similar to column0 but internal vertical using rows i even.\n\nThus for column99, we can pair (i,99)-(i+1,99) vertical internal for i even.\n\nNow need to ensure that each vertical internal domino uses rows i,i+1 both even-odd pairs within same block column. Since block rows are pairs, that's internal.\n\nNow does this scheme create any conflict at boundaries between rows? For i = 98 (last even row), vertical domino pairs (98,0)-(99,0) etc; that's okay.\n\nThus we have a full tiling.\n\nNow count internal dominoes:\n\n- For each of the 50 blocks in first column: one internal vertical domino covering rows (2k,0)-(2k+1,0). That's 50 internal.\n\n- For each of the 50 blocks in last column: similarly 50 internal. That's 100.\n\n- Are there any internal dominoes elsewhere? The horizontal dominoes cross block boundaries, i.e., for odd j, domino (i,j)-(i,j+1) where j odd connects column j (odd) which is right column of block (j-1,j) to column j+1 (even) which is left column of next block (j+1,j+2). They cross block boundary, thus external. Also vertical dominoes internal only as described. No other internal.\n\nThus internal count = 100. Is it minimal? Need to prove can't go below 100. We argued each block in first and last columns must have at least one internal domino. Hence lower bound 100. Need to ensure no other forced internal. So answer likely 100.\n\nEdge case: Could we reduce internal count by using vertical internal dominoes in first column but maybe combine with external matches to reduce count to 0 for some block by using a more clever pattern that uses some cells from first column matched upward via wrap? No upward external. So each block in first column must have at least one internal domino because the two cells in column0 cannot both be matched externally: reason: in block, there are two columns: 0 and 1. For a cell in column0, its only possible external neighbor is vertically downwards if it's odd row (as earlier). But within the block there are two cells in column0 (rows 2k,2k+1). The upper cell (even row) has no external neighbor (cannot go up, left, right internal, down internal); thus must be internal. So each block requires at least one internal domino covering that upper cell with something (either horizontal with column1 internal, or vertical with lower cell internal). In any case internal domino count at least 1 per block. Similarly for last column, the upper cell of column99 (even row) also has no external neighbor to right, so needs internal. So minimal internal per block at edges is 1. Thus lower bound 100 holds.\n\nThus minimal number of dominoes entirely inside some 2x2 square is 100.\n\nAnswer: 100.", "response": "ANSWER: 100", "answer": 100, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.529945101694466e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999954700651585, "logp_correct": -4.529945101694466e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6919518083388506, "co