81, 10610, 1373, 94774, 8, 889, 2454, 13878, 133589, 665, 4321, 540, 1645, 220, 17, 6000, 16, 11242, 13, 2632, 11085, 13878, 133589, 268, 24878, 6000, 16, 11242, 382, 35964, 7, 1353, 14, 17, 25987, 1434, 13, 65037, 395, 6000, 1353, 8967, 3613, 220, 1434, 11, 3609, 220, 1353, 13, 2632, 6931, 11608, 220, 1353, 364, 84787, 6052, 220, 1353, 364], "n_tokens": 5908, "words": 4031, "band_lo": 2015.5, "band_hi": 8062.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have 100x100 board. Two tilings: one using only dominoes (1x2 or 2x1) covering whole board, and another using only 2x2 squares covering whole board (so board dimensions even, okay). We are asked: What is the least number of dominoes that are entirely inside some 2x2 square? Means in the domino tiling, count dominoes such that the two cells of the domino are both within the same 2x2 block (aligned with grid). We can consider the 2x2 squares of the second tiling? The second tiling partitions board into 2x2 blocks (a grid of 50x50 blocks). Those are axis-aligned 2x2 squares. We want to minimize number of dominoes that lie completely inside one of those blocks. Equivalent: given a domino tiling, count dominoes that are not crossing the boundaries of the 2x2 blocks (i.e., lie within a single block). We can choose domino tiling to minimize that count. Need minimal possible number.\n\nWe have 100x100 board = 10,000 cells. Domino tiling uses 5,000 dominoes. Partition into 2x2 blocks yields 2,500 blocks. Each block contains 4 cells. Within each block, there are possible domino placements: either two horizontal dominoes or two vertical dominoes or one vertical and one horizontal crossing? Actually dominoes entirely inside block must be either two horizontal covering top and bottom rows, or two vertical covering left and right columns, or one vertical covering left column, one vertical covering right column etc. But each domino inside block occupies 2 cells. So a block could contain up to 2 interior dominoes (covering all 4 cells). Could also contain 0 interior dominoes if dominoes cross block boundaries.\n\nGoal is minimize total number of interior dominoes across all blocks. Since each interior domino occupies two cells that lie inside same block, and each block has 4 cells, each interior domino reduces the number of cells that must be matched with dominoes crossing block boundaries. Let's think combinatorial: For each block, we can have 0,1,2 interior dominoes. But can we have exactly 1 interior domino? The remaining 2 cells must be paired with cells outside the block, forming dominoes that cross boundaries. That's possible.\n\nWe want to minimize total interior dominoes subject to overall tiling covering the board.\n\nSuppose we attempt to have 0 interior dominoes in all blocks. Is that possible? That would mean every domino straddles a block boundary. Consider coloring: treat blocks as 2x2 cells. If we draw adjacency graph of blocks via edges across which dominoes may cross. We need a perfect matching of cells such that no edge stays within a block. Is that possible? Let's test with parity constraints.\n\nModel: Each block has 4 cells: coordinates (i,j) where i,j from 1..100. Blocks are groups of (2a-1,2a) rows and (2b-1,2b) columns. Domino can be horizontal connecting (x,y) and (x,y+1). It may be either within same block (if y and y+1 are within same block column pair), meaning y odd/even? For within block, both columns belong to same pair. If y is odd, then y+1 even => same block column. So horizontal domino inside block if it lies within same column pair. Similarly vertical inside block if rows (x,x+1) same block row pair.\n\nOtherwise domino crosses block boundary: either horizontal crossing between column pairs (i.e., y even), vertical crossing between row pairs (x even). So classification.\n\nNow consider board as a checkerboard coloring of cells (black/white). Domino always covers one black, one white. The block contains 2 black, 2 white cells. If we have zero interior dominoes, each block's 2 black cells must be matched with white cells outside its block. That seems plausible.\n\nBut global parity maybe forces some interior.\n\nWe can treat block bipartite graph: each block has two black cells (say NW and SE) and two white cells (NE and SW) depending on orientation. Actually in a 2x2 block, the colors are like:\n(1,1) black, (1,2) white,\n(2,1) white, (2,2) black (assuming usual chessboard). So each block has two blacks (NW and SE) and two whites (NE and SW). In order for zero interior dominoes, each black must pair with a white outside same block; each white must pair with black outside. So we need a perfect matching between blacks and whites across blocks, without using intra-block edges.\n\nConsider a graph with vertices = cells, edges allowed are adjacent cells (horiz/vert). Remove intra-block edges (so we disallow edges that connect two cells within same block). Does this graph still have a perfect matching? If yes, min is 0.\n\nWe need to see if this graph has a perfect matching in the 100x100 grid with those edges removed.\n\nObserve that removing intra-block edges corresponds to deleting all edges that are horizontal between columns (2k-1,2k) within same row, and vertical between rows (2k-1,2k) within same column. So we delete a set of edges that form a 'grid' of size 50x100 vertical edges? Wait compute: For each row, there are 50 intra-block horizontal edges (between col 2k-1 and 2k). For each column, there are 50 intra-block vertical edges (between row 2k-1 and 2k). So we remove many edges but still many remain.\n\nWe need to know if a perfect matching exists. Usually a bipartite planar grid graph with some edges removed may still have perfect matching if no Hall obstruction.\n\nPotential obstruction: Consider parity of each block's black cells along outer boundary? Could be a cycle.\n\nLet's test small case to get insight: 2x2 board (1 block). With domino tiling, you must use an interior domino (since any domino covering board must be within block because board size 2). So min interior =1? Actually board only 2x2, you need 2 dominoes covering four cells; each domino must be interior (since any edge stays inside block). So min interior =2? Wait domino tiling of 2x2 board uses two dominoes either both horizontal or both vertical. Both are interior to the sole 2x2 block. So count =2. So for 2x2 block minimal interior is 2 (max). But for larger board perhaps we can have zeros.\n\nTry 2x4 board (two blocks side-by-side). Blocks: columns 1-2 and 3-4. Can we tile with dominoes such that none are interior? Let's attempt: Use vertical dominoes crossing the vertical boundary between block columns? But interior would be horizontal within each block. We can place four vertical dominoes each covering rows (1,2) each column? Actually vertical dominoes within same column cross row pair within block (since rows 1-2 within same block row pair). That's interior vertical (since both rows 1-2 part of same block row pair). So vertical dominoes are interior to block if column stays within same block column pair. So they'd be interior.\n\nThus to avoid interior, need dominoes that cross either column pair boundary or row pair boundary. That means dominoes must be oriented such that they straddle block boundaries. Options: horizontal domino crossing between column pairs uses edge between column 2 and 3 (boundary). That is allowed. Also vertical domino crossing row pair boundary uses edge between row 2 and 3 (if more rows). For 2 rows only, can't cross row boundary. So with 2 rows, only crossing column boundaries possible. For 2x4 board, we can tile using two horizontal dominoes crossing the middle boundary, and two more horizontal dominoes each within outer halves? Wait edges available: columns: 1-2 (interior), 2-3 (boundary), 3-4 (interior). To avoid interior, we must avoid using edges 1-2 and 3-4. So we need perfect matching using only edges across column boundary (2-3) and perhaps vertical edges within same column (they are interior vertical? Actually block row pair is rows 1-2; vertical edge within column connects rows 1 and 2, which are within same block row pair, but also same block column? It stays inside each block (since column belongs to a block). For column 1, vertical edge stays within block (since columns 1 inside block). So that's interior. So not allowed.\n\nThus for 2x4, we cannot have perfect matching without interior edges because limited edges. Indeed seems impossible: each cell in column1 can only match to column2 (interior) or vertical within column (interior). To avoid interior, must match to column2 via interior? Not allowed. So impossible. So min interior >0.\n\nHence for large board maybe we need some interior dominoes to satisfy constraints.\n\nWe need to find minimal number across 50x50 blocks. This reminiscent of counting discrepancy: each block contributes parity of interior dominoes maybe must be even? Let's think using a flow argument.\n\nDefine each block as a region with 4 cells. Consider total number of vertical dominoes that are interior within a blockGiven adjacency across boundaries.\n\nMaybe treat each block as nodes in a graph where crossing dominoes correspond to edges between adjacent blocks. Dominoes that are interior correspond to edges inside node (like self loops). Want to minimize number of self-loops while achieving degree constraints: each block has 4 cells -> need exactly 4 incident half-domino edges (since each cell matched). Represent each domino crossing block boundary contributes one half-edge from each of the two adjacent blocks. So for a block, number of interior dominoes (each uses 2 cells) contributes 2 half-edges that stay inside block.\n\nLet variable a_i = number of interior dominoes in block i (0,1,2). Then remaining cells = 4 - 2a_i must be matched with neighboring blocks via crossing dominoes, each crossing uses one cell of this block. So each block must have exactly 4 - 2a_i crossing half-edges. Since each crossing domino uses two blocks, sum over all blocks of crossing half-edges = 2 * (# crossing dominoes). Also sum of (4 - 2a_i) over all blocks = 4*2500 - 2*sum a_i = 10000 - 2*sum a_i. This equals 2 * (# crossing dominoes). Total number of dominoes is 5000 = # crossing + sum a_i. So # crossing = 5000 - sum a_i.\n\nCheck consistency: 2*(5000 - sum a_i) = 10000 - 2 sum a_i matches above. So any sum a_i is feasible theoretically.\n\nThus any total number of interior dominoes sum a_i could be any integer between 0 and 2*2500? Actually each block max 2 interior, so sum a_i ≤ 5000. So could be any number? But need integrality constraints due to adjacency possibilities. There might be parity constraints per coloring of blocks chessboard.\n\nConsider bipartite graph of blocks in a checkerboard pattern (since blocks arranged 50x50). Crossing dominoes connect either horizontally adjacent blocks (share vertical boundary) or vertically adjacent blocks (share horizontal boundary). These edges correspond to domino crossing (since crossing must be either horizontal across column boundary within same rows, or vertical across row boundary within same columns). Hence crossing edges only between blocks that are adjacent (share a side). So we need to find a multigraph matching cells.\n\nEach block has 4 cells considered as 4 ports: two ports on each of its four sides? Actually each cell on boundary of block can match with neighbor block via edge across the side. But each side of a block has 2 cells (since block height 2). So each side provides 2 possible crossing positions. So between two adjacent blocks, there are 2 possible crossing domino positions (horizontal crossing across vertical boundary: consists of two horizontal dominoes each using a row's pair across boundary? Wait crossing dominoes are horizontal spanning the boundary: they occupy cells (row r, col 2k) and (row r, col 2k+1). That's one crossing domino using one cell from each block (top row). Similarly second row. So there are 2 possible horizontal crossing dominoes across that vertical adjacency. Similarly vertical adjacency between blocks gives 2 possible vertical crossing dominoes.\n\nThus between each adjacent block pair, there are 2 parallel edges possible (representing up to 2 crossing dominoes). The problem reduces to: we have a grid of 50x50 nodes, each node needs degree d_i = 4 - 2a_i (number of crossing half-edges). Since each crossing edge contributes 1 to degree of each endpoint. There are max 2 parallel edges per adjacency.\n\nGoal: minimize total interior sum a_i, equivalently maximize total crossing edges. Total crossing edges = 5000 - sum a_i. Maximum possible crossing edges limited by capacity of edges: each adjacent pair can carry at most 2 crossing dominoes. How many adjacencies total? For 50x50 grid, number of horizontal adjacencies = (50-1)*50 = 49*50 =2450. Similarly vertical adjacencies =2450. So total adjacency pairs = 4900. Each can have up to 2 crossing dominoes, so max crossing edges capacity = 2*4900 = 9800 crossing dominoes. But we only need 5000 crossing dominoes (if sum a_i=0). Capacity is ample. So maybe we can achieve 5000 crossing dominoes without interior.\n\nBut degree requirement per node: each node needs degree 4 (since a_i=0). Is it possible to assign 4 edges incident to each node using at most 2 per neighboring pair? That's similar to finding a 4-regular multigraph on grid with max 2 per adjacency. Since each node degree 4, and there are 4 possible neighbor directions (up, down, left, right), each can contribute up to 2 edges. So need to allocate 4 edges among up to 4 neighbors (maybe 2 each). This seems feasible: we could place 1 crossing edge on each of the four sides (using one of the two parallel edges). That would give degree 4. So a configuration exists where each block uses exactly one crossing domino on each side. That would correspond to a tiling where every domino crosses a block boundary. Need to ensure that such a set of crossing dominoes corresponds to a valid domino tiling (no conflicts that a cell used twice). Since each side has two cells, we need to choose which row's crossing uses which edge such that cells are matched uniquely.\n\nConsider a block - it has top row two cells (positions top-left, top-right). The left side crossing uses left column cell(s). Actually crossing on left side is vertical domino crossing between this block and left neighbor: uses the left column cells of the block (both rows) and the right column cells of left neighbor. That's two possible vertical crossing dominoes: one using top cell of each block, another using bottom cell. So we can assign up to 2. To give degree 4, block could use both crossing dominoes on left (2) and both on right (2) and none up/down, etc. But need consistency across neighbors: if block uses a crossing domino on its left side, the neighbor must also use that same domino (since it's shared). So we need a global arrangement of dominoes that correspond to a perfect matching of cells, which is equivalent to a domino tiling.\n\nIt seems we can construct a tiling where all dominoes are horizontal crossing between columns pairs (i.e., every domino is horizontal and lies across the vertical line between column pairs). That would mean each row has dominoes pairing column 2k with 2k+1 (boundary), for all k=1..49? Wait there are 50 column pairs (blocks). Crossing boundary occur between block columns: between column pair 1 and 2 (i.e., columns 2 and 3), etc. There are 49 boundaries. If we tile each row with horizontal dominoes crossing every boundary, we get 49 dominoes per row, but need cover 100 columns => 50 dominoes per row normally. Actually each row of 100 cells can be tiled by 50 dominoes. To have all dominoes crossing boundaries, each domino must occupy cells columns (2k,2k+1) for k=1..49 plus maybe something else for edges at boundaries (columns 1 and 100). Edge columns 1 and 100 cannot be part of crossing domino because there is no neighbor block beyond. So cells in column 1 and 100 must be paired somehow, likely vertical dominoes inside leftmost and rightmost block (interior). Thus we cannot avoid interior dominoes at outermost columns.\n\nSimilarly top and bottom rows (rows 1 and 100) cannot cross row boundaries beyond board, so they need interior vertical dominoes within first/last block rows.\n\nThus at least some interior dominoes are forced near outer boundary. Let's compute minimal count.\n\nConsider using horizontal crossing dominoes for all internal column boundaries, and vertical crossing dominoes for internal row boundaries, covering interior. For the outermost cells (first column, last column, first row, last row), we have to pair them perhaps with interior dominoes within the edge block.\n\nEdge blocks: there are 50 blocks along top row (row block 1). Their topmost row cells (row 1) cannot be crossed upward. They must be matched either horizontally within block (interior) or vertically downwards within same block (interior) or horizontally crossing to neighbor block? Horizontal crossing uses same row, which is okay; row 1 cells can be matched horizontally across vertical boundary (still interior? That's crossing between blocks horizontally, but still within row 1). That does not involve interior vertical. So we could match row1 cells horizontally across block boundaries (i.e., between column 2 and 3) etc. So outer rows can still use horizontal crossing dominoes, no need interior. But columns 1 cells cannot cross leftward; they could cross rightward horizontally across column 2? Wait column 1 cells are in leftmost column of block column 1. They could be matched horizontally with column2 (which is within same block) - interior horizontal; or vertically downwards interior vertical; or horizontally crossing to column2? But column2 is still inside same block (since block columns are 1-2). So crossing only occurs between column2 and 3, which involves column2 inside leftmost block and column3 inside second block. So column1 cell cannot be part of a crossing across block boundary because its neighbor is column2 within same block. So column1 cells must be interior (either with column2 horizontally or vertical with row2). Thus each leftmost column cell forces interior dominoes.\n\nSimilarly rightmost column (100) cells must be interior.\n\nThus each of the 100 rows has cells at column1 and column100. That's 200 cells that must be paired interiorly. Each interior domino uses 2 cells, so at least 100 interior dominoes needed for left/right edges.\n\nSimilarly, top row row1 cells at column? Already accounted maybe matched horizontally across internal boundaries, so okay. However row1 cells at column1, etc already counted. No extra.\n\nNow consider bottom row row100 also similar.\n\nBut note that cells at corners (1,1), (1,100), (100,1), (100,100) counted twice? Actually each corner belongs to both left/right edge and top/bottom edge. However interior domino can pair a corner cell with its neighbor either horizontally (to column2) or vertically (to row2). That's still just one interior domino per corner cell used. Counting necessity: each of the 200 edge cells (col1 or col100) need interior domino. That's 100 dominoes covering them (since each domino uses two cells). But could a domino cover both a left edge cell and a right edge cell? Not possible, they are far apart. So 100 interior dominoes minimum.\n\nNow also consider topmost row cells column2..99 (excluding columns1,100). These can be matched horizontally crossing with column3 etc. No issue.\n\nSimilarly leftmost column's two rows (row1 and row100) already part of those 200 cells.\n\nThus lower bound 100 interior dominoes.\n\nIs this achievable? Need to construct a domino tiling where only those 100 interior dominoes are used (i.e., interior to the leftmost and rightmost column blocks) and all other dominoes cross boundaries.\n\nWe have 50 blocks per row. Leftmost column block (columns1-2) for each of the 50 block rows (i.e., each 2-row band). Within each such block, there are 4 cells left column (col1) + 2 rows? Actually each block at left edge covers columns1-2 and rows (2r-1,2r). Its column1 cells are two per block (top and bottom of that 2x2). Those need interior dominoes. Could we pair each column1 cell with its column2 counterpart horizontally (within block) using two horizontal interior dominoes per block (top row and bottom row), thus 2 interior dominoes per block. That would be 2*50 =100 interior dominoes (since there are 50 row blocks). Similarly for right edge block columns99-100 we also need interior dominoes? Wait column100 cells also need interior; could pair each column100 cell with column99 within its block horizontal interior dominoes. That's another 100 interior dominoes? That would double count. But earlier we counted 200 edge cells (col1 and col100) requiring interior dominoes; each interior domino can pair a col1 cell with col2 cell, or col100 with col99. So we need at least 100 dominoes for col1 cells and another 100 for col100 cells? Actually each interior domino uses two cells; a domino covering column1 cell and column2 cell uses one col1 cell, one col2 cell. Similarly domino covering column100 with column99 uses column100 cell. So total interior dominoes at least 100 (col1) + 100 (col100) = 200? Wait earlier we considered 200 edge cells, each domino uses 2 cells, so minimum dominoes = 200/2 =100. But that's assuming we can pair a col1 cell with a col100 cell inside same block, impossible. So each interior domino includes exactly one edge cell (col1 or col100) and one interior cell (col2 or col99). Thus we need one interior domino per edge cell. Since there are 200 edge cells, we need 200 interior dominoes. But each domino accounts for 2 edge cells only if we could pair col1 with col100 which not possible. So indeed we need 200 interior dominoes.\n\nBut maybe we can pair two edge cells within same block: leftmost block has two edge cells (row top and bottom) both column1. They can be paired vertically within block (since rows 2r-1 and 2r). That's a vertical interior domino covering both edge cells (both column1). That's allowed! That would use two edge cells in one domino. Let's see: In the leftmost block (2x2), the two column1 cells are vertically adjacent (rows 2r-1,2r). So we could place a vertical interior domino covering both edge cells of that block. Then we would not need to involve column2 cells. That uses two edge cells per domino, reducing count. So per left edge block, we can have one vertical interior domino covering both column1 cells. Similarly right edge block we can have vertical domino covering both column100 cells. Thus per row block (there are 50 such vertical blocks), we need 1 domino for left edge and 1 domino for right edge => total 100 interior dominoes (2 per 2 rows). That's count = 100 interior dominoes, matching earlier lower bound of 100? Let's recount cells: left edge cells total = 100 rows *1 column =100 cells. They can be paired vertically in groups of 2 within each block (since rows pair). So need 100/2 =50 interior dominoes for left edge. Similarly right edge 50. Total 100 interior dominoes. Yes!\n\nThus lower bound is 100 interior dominoes, feasible if we can tile rest with crossing dominoes.\n\nNow construct: For each of the 50 row blocks (rows groups (2r-1,2r)), place a vertical domino covering the two cells in column1 (rows 2r-1,2r) and another vertical domino covering two cells in column100. That's 2 interior per block row, total 100.\n\nNow we need to tile remaining cells (columns 2-99). That's a 100x98 region (since we removed columns1 and 100). This region can be tiled using only crossing dominoes? Its block structure: inside this region, there are 49 internal column block pairs (columns 2-3, 4-5,...,98-99). The internal columns are even number, and leftmost column of region is column2 which is interior column of leftmost block; but we have removed column1 cells, leaving column2 cells which can be matched horizontally with column3 (crossing boundary) which is fine. Similarly at right side column99 matched with column98 crossing. So we can tile entire interior region using only horizontal dominoes that cross every internal boundary, plus perhaps vertical spanning between row blocks? But we must ensure each cell is matched exactly once.\n\nSimplify: Use horizontal dominoes across each internal boundary for all rows. For each row, we tile columns 2-99 with dominoes of size (col 2k, 2k+1) for k=1..49 i.e., crossing each internal block boundary. This covers all cells in rows 1..100 columns2-99. No interior dominoes used. Works because number of columns is even (98). So each row has 49 dominoes.\n\nThus total dominoes: left edge vertical 50, right edge vertical 50, plus horizontal 100 rows *49 = 4900. Sum = 5000 dominoes. Count interior: only the 100 vertical dominoes (since they are interior to leftmost and rightmost blocks). Are they entirely inside some 2x2 square? Yes, each is inside a 2x2 block (the leftmost/rightmost block). So answer appears 100.\n\nCheck that vertical dominoes are inside a 2x2 square? The block is 2x2 with columns1-2 rows pair. The vertical domino occupies column1 rows pair; still inside the block. So yes.\n\nThus minimal number = 100.\n\nWe need prove cannot be less than 100. Reason: each of the 100 cells in column1 must be matched with an adjacent cell. The only adjacent cells are column2 (horizontal) or row neighbor (vertical). Column2 cell is inside a different 2x2 block? Actually column2 is within same block (columns1-2). So any matching that pairs column1 cell with column2 cell creates interior domino. Matching column1 with vertical neighbor (same column) also interior. Thus any domino covering a column1 cell is interior to the leftmost block. Therefore each domino covering a column1 cell is interior. Since there are 100 cells in column1, at least 50 interior dominoes (if we pair them vertically) but each interior domino can cover at most 2 column1 cells. So minimum interior dominoes covering column1 cells is ceil(100/2)=50. Similarly for column100 gives another 50, total 100. So lower bound 100.\n\nThus answer 100.", "response": "ANSWER: 100", "answer": 100, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.861020561795158e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999971389835309, "logp_correct": -2.861020561795158e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6303684530574668, "cot_lp_min64": -1.7525371153404308, "frac_below_-5": 0.012046148625721072, "digit_lp_min": -3.5297510623931885, "answer_first_mention_lp": -3.576278118089249e-07, "n_cot_tokens": 5894, "lp_end": -0.3613240420818329, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.5497195136049413e-06, "logp_nocot": -11.26153349876401, "p_nocot": 1.2858139811975854e-05, "uplift": 11.261530637743448}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "86bb7301-4f70-47c6-89a9-833b9277ba4c", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Sir Alex plays the following game on a row of $9$ cells. Initially, all cells are empty. In each move, Sir Alex is allowed to perform exactly one of the following two operations: (1) Choose any number of the form $2^j$, where $j$ is a non-negative integer, and put it into an empty cell. (2) Choose two (not necessarily adjacent) cells with the same number in them; denote that number by $2^j.$ Replace the number in one of the cells with $2^j+1$ and erase the number in the other cell. At the end of the game, one cell contains the number $2^n$, where $n$ is a given positive integer, while the other cells are empty. Determine the maximum number of moves that Sir Alex could have made, in terms of $n$. Please provide the value of the maximum number of moves when $n = 10$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Sir Alex plays the following game on a row of $9$ cells. Initially, all cells are empty. In each move, Sir Alex is allowed to perform exactly one of the following two operations: (1) Choose any number of the form $2^j$, where $j$ is a non-negative integer, and put it into an empty cell. (2) Choose two (not necessarily adjacent) cells with the same number in them; denote that number by $2^j.$ Replace the number in one of the cells with $2^j+1$ and erase the number in the other cell. At the end of the game, one cell contains the number $2^n$, where $n$ is a given positive integer, while the other cells are empty. Determine the maximum number of moves that Sir Alex could have made, in terms of $n$. Please provide the value of the maximum number of moves when $n = 10$.", "true_answer": 2025, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "86bb7301-