382, 8975, 13, 2632, 12241, 3779, 23275, 220, 24, 364, 84787, 290, 30248, 3104, 382, 58866, 364, 84787, 2999, 19523, 220, 1323, 20, 364, 4383, 538, 1062, 21568, 316, 679, 945, 1572, 220, 7959, 18, 10898, 756, 2360, 1236, 8663, 483, 2735, 5420, 1424, 23, 889, 2928, 23704, 3518, 12241, 5192, 316, 107009, 2254, 68881, 30, 50557, 2254, 68881, 581, 2804, 679, 72554, 23, 33001, 26, 1934, 68881, 581, 665, 679, 869, 316, 220, 24, 11, 1815, 176901, 10389, 13, 2632, 1062, 10898, 21073, 17664, 3605, 656, 220, 16, 11, 1815, 176901, 10389, 10112, 945, 1572, 220, 16, 13, 2632, 10705, 17664, 3605, 1934, 176901, 314, 2735, 5420, 328, 620, 4215, 11, 1118, 2804, 413, 72554, 23, 395, 2613, 68881, 13, 2632, 2735, 5420, 328, 620, 4215, 2804, 413, 72554, 23, 13, 2632, 581, 6284, 679, 47388, 4215, 483, 2735, 5420, 220, 24, 1934, 176901, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 1721, 2608, 1934, 176901, 741, 3072, 1934, 68881, 11, 2254, 107009, 11, 2735, 5420, 2023, 413, 869, 316, 220, 24, 11, 10954, 25435, 13, 2632, 290, 23292, 382, 484, 1753, 75628, 4215, 1934, 68881, 326, 1934, 722, 4149, 176901, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 290, 91323, 22311, 8, 2804, 679, 2735, 5420, 72554, 23, 11, 6384, 1721, 220, 7672, 19, 483, 2735, 5420, 220, 16, 364, 84787, 1039, 43276, 161470, 364, 198140, 6052, 220, 1323, 20, 13], "n_tokens": 6720, "words": 4573, "band_lo": 2286.5, "band_hi": 9146.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to understand game: Row of 9 cells. Initially empty. Moves: either insert a power of two 2^j into an empty cell, or combine two cells with same number 2^j: replace one cell with 2^{j+1} (note: they write 2^j+1? Wait: \"Replace the number in one of the cells with 2^j+1 and erase the number in the other cell.\" I think they mean replace with 2^{j+1} (since they add exponent 1). Actually \"2^j+1\" ambiguous: could be 2^j + 1, not power of two. But typical merge game (like 2048) uses 2^{j+1}. Let's examine: They said: \"Choose two cells with the same number in them; denote that number by 2^j. Replace the number in one of the cells with 2^j+1 and erase the number in the other cell.\" If they literally mean 2^j+1, then resulting number becomes not power of 2 (except j=0). That seems weird. I think they missed exponent formatting: should be 2^{j+1}. Yes.\n\nThus operation (2) merges two equal powers into the next power.\n\nGoal: end with exactly one cell containing 2^n, all others empty.\n\nWe can make many moves; we are asked for maximum possible number of moves given 9 cells limit. Essentially we want to maximize total moves while eventually produce a single 2^n.\n\nWe can think each insertion move adds a new tile; each merge move reduces tile count by 1 (since two become one). Total number of tiles inserted minus number of merges = final tile count = 1. Let I be number of insertion moves, M be number of merges. Then I - M = 1. total moves T = I + M = (I) + (I-1) = 2I -1. So maximizing moves equivalent to maximizing I (number of insertions) given constraints of cells (max 9 occupied at any time) and the need to achieve final tile 2^n.\n\nNow each insertion can be any power 2^j, not necessarily 2^0. So we can strategically insert high powers directly to reduce merges needed? But we want many moves, so we want as many insertions as possible while still being able to end with 2^n. To maximize moves, we want many insertions, which means many merges as well (since I huge). But merges limited by the need to combine to eventually produce 2^n. Essentially we need to generate enough smaller tiles to combine up chain.\n\nThink of binary representation: To produce 2^n, we need 2 copies of 2^{n-1}, which each need 2 copies of 2^{n-2}, etc. This is like building a binary tree of merges: to get one 2^n, you need 2^n copies of 2^0 if you only start from 2^0. Insertion of larger powers reduces required merges (since you skip some levels), thus reduces total moves. To maximize moves, we want to start from smallest possible: insert only 2^0 (i.e., 1). Because merging from larger powers reduces number of insertions.\n\nThus maximal I occurs when we only insert 1's. But maybe we could insert other powers as well while still overall insert count high? Inserting a larger tile saves us having to insert many small ones, which would reduce I, not beneficial. So best to only insert 1's.\n\nNow we have 9 cells capacity; we can only have at most 9 tiles at any time. So we need to schedule merges to avoid overflow while inserting many 1's.\n\nGoal: produce 2^n eventually, using only insert 1's and merges. This is analogous to the game 2048, maximizing number of moves before achieving a tile 2^n given board size 9 cells (1D). The constraint: we can also insert any power, but we don't want that.\n\nWe need to maximize I under capacity 9: we can think of process: we keep inserting 1's until board is full (9 cells of 1). Then we must start merging to free cells.\n\nWe want to keep board as full as possible most of the time to maximize insertions (since each move after we have full board we must merge before inserting more). So optimal strategy likely maintains at most 9 tiles at all times except maybe during merges we temporarily reduce count then increase after insertion.\n\nGoal is to produce 2^n; this requires total number of 1's inserted I = number of leaves inserted. For binary tree merging to produce single 2^n, we need exactly 2^n 1's inserted (if no larger inserts). Because each merge reduces count by 1, to get final 1 tile we need I - M =1 and also each merge consumes two same-valued tiles at some level; overall, total number of 1's needed is 2^n. Let's verify: Starting with I tiles of 1. After merges we get 1 tile of 2^n. Number of merges M = I -1. But also merges happen across levels, but is there any additional constraint linking I to n? Let's check: each merge increases exponent by 1. Starting from all 1's (exponent 0). The sum of values is conserved? Actually each merge replaces two 2^j (value 2^{j+1}) total value stays 2^{j+1}+0 = 2^{j+1}, but original total of both tiles 2*2^j = 2^{j+1}. So total sum of numbers on board is invariant under merges (since we delete one tile). Likewise, insertion adds value of tile inserted. So total sum after all moves = sum of all inserted numbers = final tile value 2^n. So sum of inserted numbers = 2^n. If we insert only 1's, then we need I = 2^n.\n\nThus maximal I is bounded by the sum constraint: Up to 2^n total sum of inserted numbers, but we could insert larger numbers and still sum to 2^n. To maximize I, we want small numbers, i.e., all 1's. So I_max = 2^n.\n\nThus total moves max T = 2I -1 = 2 * 2^n -1 = 2^{n+1} -1? Wait, 2I-1 = 2*(2^n) -1 = 2^{n+1} -1.\n\nBut is this attainable given 9-cell limit? Need to check if we can insert 2^n 1's while only having max 9 cells at any time and being able to eventually merge to 2^n. This resembles needing to pack binary representation using limited space. For n large (10) maybe possible, but need to see if there is any limitation preventing reaching that maximum due to capacity constraints.\n\nEssentially we need a sequence of insert+merge operations that never exceeds 9 occupied cells, and after 2^{n+1} -1 moves ends with single tile 2^n. Is it always possible for n up to some bound? We need to test for n=10 (2^10=1024). Need to store up to 9 tiles.\n\nWe can think of representation of current multiset of powers as binary representation of total sum of inserted numbers so far (since merges treat as carries). The board holds at most one tile of each exponent? Actually we can have multiple same exponent because they haven't merged yet, but capacity is 9.\n\nGoal is to keep occupancy <=9 while total sum S increases up to 2^n (1024). That is similar to writing binary of S with limited number of bits? Not exactly; each tile corresponds to a 1 in binary representation (with digits maybe greater than 1 if multiple copies). The process of merging is like performing carries when a digit exceeds 1.\n\nMaximum number of tiles at any time equals number of 1's in binary representation of current sum, because after all possible merges (i.e., after each insertion you could optionally merge as much as you like, but you can choose order). However you might keep some digits >1 temporarily, causing more than binary representation count, but would increase tile count up to maybe 9.\n\nGoal: keep tile count <=9 throughout, with final representation 2^n (which has exactly one 1). We can schedule merges to keep tile count low.\n\nEssentially we need to ensure that the binary representation of S never has more than 9 ones (since we can always merge duplicate exponents to reduce count). But if we allow more than one tile per exponent, count may increase more.\n\nThus the question reduces to: what is the maximum total number of insertions (i.e., S total sum) achievable before you must exceed 9 tiles given you always merge whenever you have two of same exponent? That's like you always keep representation in binary (no duplicate exponents). Then number of tiles = popcount(S). Since we can merge any time we have two same. So to maximize moves while staying within capacity, we want to insert 1's and after each insertion, repeatedly merge any pairs as possible (i.e., maintain minimal popcount). That ensures tile count is popcount of current sum. Since you can always decide to merge later, but merging earlier reduces count earlier, giving more room for inserts. So optimal keep popcount <=9.\n\nThus we can insert 1 repeatedly, each time increment S by 1, then reduce popcount via carries (binary addition). The process replicates binary counting, where number of 1 bits (popcount) after each increment is the popcount of the current integer.\n\nThus the maximum number of insertions I before exceeding 9 tiles is the largest integer S such that for all integers from 1 to S, popcount(k) <=9. Once you attempt to go to S+1 where popcount exceeds 9, you'd require >9 tiles briefly (during addition before merges?) Actually binary addition: when you add 1 to a binary number, you may flip many trailing zeros to ones, etc. The number of 1s after addition could be larger than before; however the process of merging corresponds to the binary representation after addition. So you can achieve any S, but at the moment after insertion and full merging, you have popcount(S) tiles. So capacity constraint only cares after merges (since you can merge any time). So you can have intermediate step with more than 9 before merges? When you insert a new 1, you momentarily have maybe popcount(prev) +1 before merging duplicates, which could exceed 9, but you can immediately merge as needed to bring down <=9. So as long as final popcount after merging <=9, you can respect capacity.\n\nThus the maximum total sum S you can reach is the largest integer <=2^n such that popcount(S) <=9 throughout process? Actually final S must be exactly 2^n (1024). So the question: can we reach 2^n while maintaining popcount <=9 at all intermediate steps? Starting from 0 and counting to 2^n, will there be steps where popcount exceeds 9? For n=10, need to go up to 1024. Let's examine maximum popcount for numbers up to 1024. The worst popcount is for numbers of form 2^k -1 (binary all ones). The largest such below 1024 is 1023 = 2^{10} -1, which has popcount 10. That's >9. So at S=1023, popcount=10 >9, which exceeds capacity. Could we avoid ever having S=1023 by using different insertion strategy (like inserting larger powers) to skip that problematic count? Since we need final sum 1024. If we avoid S=1023, we could maybe jump from 1022 to 1024 by inserting a 2 (2^1) instead of 1? Inserting larger values changes sum more than 1 and may avoid hitting popcount 10. So we may be able to achieve final 1024 without ever having a state with popcount >9.\n\nThus we need to design strategy maximizing number of moves while staying within 9 tiles, possibly using insert of larger numbers at critical moments to skip high popcount states.\n\nOur objective is to maximize total moves (insertions plus merges). Since merges are tied to insertions (I-M=1), maximizing moves equals maximizing I. So we need to maximize number of insertion moves while respecting capacity.\n\nSum of inserted numbers = 2^n = 1024. To maximize I, we want as many small numbers as possible, but may need to include some larger numbers to avoid exceeding popcount limit.\n\nThus we need to find the maximal number of summands (positive powers of two) that sum to 1024, such that during the incremental process (some ordering) we never exceed 9 tiles after merges. Equivalent to represent 1024 as a multiset of powers of two (with repetitions) with total count I as large as possible, with the property that there exists an ordering of insertion that never exceeds tile capacity when merging duplicates greedily.\n\nWe can think in terms of binary addition with limited carry depth. Another perspective: Each time we insert a tile 2^j, we increase the count of exponent j by 1. If count becomes 2, we may merge to exponent j+1 (increase count of j+1, possibly further merges). The maximum total number of tiles present at any moment equals maximum over process of sum of counts across exponents (i.e., total tiles after performing merges wherever possible). This is essentially the number of 1's in binary representation of partial sum when using a certain addition order (since merges happen automatically). The ordering may affect intermediate popcounts because if you add a large tile early, you may create carries earlier.\n\nGoal: Maximize number of addends (I) subject to that there exists an ordering where for each partial sum S_k (sum of first k added numbers), popcount(S_k) <=9.\n\nThus we need to choose a multiset of powers of two (with total sum 1024) and an addition order to keep popcount <=9 throughout. Want biggest count of addends.\n\nEssentially we seek the longest sequence of positive powers of two summing to 1024 such that cumulative popcount never exceeds 9.\n\nObservation: Since each addend is power of two, the binary representation of S_k is simply sum of those addends. So we need to produce a sequence where intermediate sums avoid having many 1 bits.\n\nWe can try to construct using greedy: Always add the smallest possible tile (1) unless that would cause popcount >9; if so, add a larger tile that reduces popcount.\n\nThe problem akin to restricted binary addition counting with limited number of 1's, maximizing number of increments.\n\nThis reminiscent of problem: given limit of bits b=9, the maximum number of increments (adding powers of two) to reach total N is something like (b * floor(log2(N)) - something). But let's compute explicitly for N=1024.\n\nWe can think of representing N in base 2 with limited digits (i.e., using at most 9 nonzero bits at any time). Since final sum is power of two, binary representation of N is just 1 followed by zeros. To reach that from zero, we could insert one 2^{10} directly, but that is only 1 insertion. Not maximal.\n\nTo maximize, we will keep many low bits but occasionally need to cause carries to reduce popcount.\n\nEssentially we can think of the process as building up binary counter with at most 9 ones. Starting from 0, we increase by adding 1's; each time the count of ones after addition is at most 9. That's like counting up to N using a counter with at most 9 ones. The number of increments (i.e., number of 1 insertions) before reaching N is exactly N, but constrained by the 9 limit preventing us from reaching N if N >= 2^{10}. However we can use larger increments to skip over states with too many ones.\n\nThus we need to compute maximal number of addends (I). Equivalent to minimal total size of each addend (i.e., minimize average value) while staying within capacity.\n\nWe can formulate dynamic programming: we want to partition 1024 into powers of two with as many parts as possible, such that there exists an ordering respecting popcount limit.\n\nObservation: The process of merging is deterministic independent of order if we always merge when possible: after each insertion, you can repeatedly merge any pairs, but the final representation (popcount) is the popcount of total sum. However intermediate representation after each insertion and after merging is exactly the binary representation of cumulative sum. So the ordering determines the sequence of cumulative sums, which are some increasing sequence from 0 to N, each step adding a power of two. So we need a path from 0 to N in which each step adds a power of two, and the visited numbers have popcount <=9.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: find longest possible path from 0 to N using moves that add a power of two, staying within popcount <=9.\n\nAll numbers visited are distinct (since increasing). We want to maximize steps, i.e., number of visited numbers (excluding 0) = I (since each insertion corresponds to moving to new sum). Note that merges steps are not counted here because after each insertion we merged fully but merges themselves also count as moves in original game. In our counting earlier, each insertion corresponds to a move, and each merge corresponds to a move separate. Wait: In the game, after insertion we may need to perform merges as separate moves. In our previous relation I-M=1 and total moves T = I+M. However our sequence of cumulative sums after each insertion plus automatic merges conflates merges into the same \"turn\". But in actual game, merging two tiles is a separate move. So our \"insert then merge automatically\" approach undercounts merges. However earlier we derived relation T = 2I -1 regardless of merging order because each insertion eventually needs to be matched by a merge eventually (except final tile). So as long as we insert I tiles, we will have M = I-1 merges total. The order doesn't affect total count. So maximizing I suffices.\n\nThus we only need to maximize I, not detailed merge sequence.\n\nThus we need to find maximal number I of addends (powers of two) summing to 1024, such that there exists an ordering of these addends where cumulative sum never exceeds popcount 9.\n\nBut is popcount constraint necessary for order? Because merges are separate moves, you could temporarily have more than 9 tiles before you do merges; however you cannot because you have only 9 cells; you might need to merge before next insertion. So between insertion moves, you can perform arbitrarily many merge moves to reduce tile count to <=9. So capacity constraint is that after each move (either insertion or merge), the number of occupied cells must be <=9. Since you can merge any time, you can always after insertion immediately merge needed pairs (each merge is a move). So while you insert a 1, board count becomes previous count +1 (maybe >9), but you can then merge some pairs, each reducing count by 1, requiring extra moves. Since we are counting moves, those merges count too. So the capacity constraint does not limit I, because you can always perform merges after each insertion to keep count <=9, but merges add moves, which we already count. However we already accounted merges: M = I -1 regardless of schedule. So capacity might impose limitation on how many merges you can do between insertions? Since each merge reduces tile count by 1, you can at most free up cells to allow next insert. So you cannot have more than 9 tiles at any point. Starting with k tiles. To insert another, you need at most 8 before insertion (since after insertion you have at most 9). After insertion you can do merges (maybe many) before next insertion. So essentially the process must ensure that before each insertion the tile count ≤8. Equivalently, you can't have more than 8 tiles before insertion. Since merges after insertion can bring count down.\n\nThus the capacity imposes a constraint on the maximum number of tiles present just before each insertion. Starting from 0, we can insert, then merge, etc.\n\nNow can we always achieve I = 2^n (1024) with capacity 9? At any time we might have many 1s; before merging they could exceed capacity. But we can interleave merges to keep count low. The minimal number of tiles needed to represent a sum S given merges performed as needed is popcount(S). That's the minimal possible tile count after full merging. So before insertion we can have at most popcount(S_prev) tiles (if we already merged everything). That must be ≤8 (since we need free cell for insertion). So condition: for each intermediate sum S (excluding final 1024), we need popcount(S) ≤8. Because after merging, we have popcount(S) tiles, which must be ≤8 to allow next insertion.\n\nThus the sequence of cumulative sums S_k (after each insertion and after all possible merges) must have popcount ≤8 for all intermediate S (not including final which can be 1). Final S=1024 has popcount 1 (≤8).\n\nThus we need a path of adding powers of two where all intermediate sums have popcount ≤8. And we maximize number of steps (I).\n\nThus previous earlier bound of popcount ≤9 (including final) changes to ≤8 before final insertion. Because we need at least one empty cell to insert the next number. After final insertion that creates 1024, we stop, no need free cell.\n\nThus we need to avoid numbers with popcount 9 or greater during build-up.\n\nGoal: maximize I = number of steps (= number of added numbers). Since each step adds some power of two, final sum 1024.\n\nWe can think of representing 1024 as sum of many powers, constructing it incrementally while staying popcount ≤8.\n\nThus we need to find maximal number of addends respecting that any partial sum has popcount ≤8.\n\nNow consider binary counting again: Adding 1 each time would generate sums with popcount equal to number of 1 bits in binary of the integer. For integer up to 1023, popcount reaches 10 at 1023, and also at 511 popcount 9, etc. So many intermediate sums would violate popcount ≤8.\n\nThus we need to skip those problematic sums by using larger addends.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: start at 0; we may add powers of two; we want to maximize number of adds (count) while never creating a number whose binary has >8 ones. Equivalent to we can add any power, which corresponds to flipping a 0 bit to 1 and possibly causing carries (if that bit already 1). The popcount after addition equals popcount(prev) - (number of trailing 1s) +1 because addition of 2^j toggles the j-th bit. If the j-th bit was 0, popcount increases by 1. If it was 1, it becomes 0 and carries, decreasing popcount by 1 each trailing 1 until we find a 0, which then becomes 1 adding 1; net change: popcount(new) = popcount(old) - t +1 where t = number of trailing 1s in binary of old at positions < j. So popcount may decrease.\n\nThus to stay ≤8, you can add a bit where there are many trailing 1s to reduce popcount.\n\nWe want to maximize steps; each step reduces popcount occasionally, allowing many steps.\n\nIdea: Use binary representation with limit of 8 ones. The maximal number of steps is achieved by using smallest addends possible while forcing occasional reductions via carries.\n\nThis is similar to the classic problem of counting number of numbers with popcount ≤8 up to N; steps could be each integer between 0 and N with popcount ≤8, but we may skip numbers with higher popcount. Since we can jump directly over them by adding a larger power. The maximal number of steps equals the size of a chain where each step increments sum by some power of two, staying within allowed set. This looks like we can visit all numbers with popcount ≤8 in increasing order, because each transition from one allowed number to the next allowed number can be achieved by adding a power of two? Not necessarily; consecutive allowed numbers may differ by 1 (which is allowed). But skipping over numbers with popcount 9 may require adding a larger power.\n\nThus the maximum steps is the count of allowed numbers (popcount ≤8) up to 1024, because we could arrange to visit them in increasing order using allowed moves (adding 1 is always allowed as it's power of two). If a number has popcount 9, we cannot be at that number, but we can jump over it using a larger bit addition, which counts as one step (instead of many steps we could have had if we could step through each number). Visiting every allowed number yields the maximum steps because each step corresponds to moving to next allowed number perhaps by adding 1; but adding 1 is always power of two so works. However to go from allowed number A to next allowed number B, which may be A+1 if popcount(A+1) ≤8; else we need to jump. That still counts as one step, but we may lose ability to include numbers in between that are disallowed anyway. So total steps equal number of allowed numbers we can reach, i.e., number of integers in [1,1024] with popcount ≤8. Because we could just increase by 1 whenever next integer also allowed ; otherwise we \"skip\" over disallowed numbers via larger addition (counts as a step). So the maximal number of moves (insertions) equals count of integers from 1 to 1024 inclusive with popcount ≤8.\n\nBecause each such integer corresponds to a insertion move (increase sum to that integer). We can always achieve that by incrementing by 1 when allowed (popcount ≤8) else we use larger increment to jump to next allowed integer; that larger increment still counts as an insertion (adding a power of two). So each allowed integer is visited exactly once; each insertion corresponds to reaching a new allowed integer.\n\nThus I_max = count_{k=1}^{1024} [popcount(k) ≤8].\n\nNow compute this count.\n\nWe need to count numbers from 0 to 1024 (inclusive maybe) with popcount ≤8. Note 1024 = 2^{10}. Binary length up to 11 bits (since 2^{10}=1 followed by 10 zeros). For numbers up to 1024 inclusive, max bits 11 (positions 0..10). Number 1024 has popcount 1.\n\nWe can count using combinatorics: Count numbers with at most 8 ones among up to 10 bits (for numbers < 1024) plus the number 1024.\n\nNumbers from 0 to 1023 are 10-bit numbers (bits 0-9). Count of numbers with popcount ≤8 among 10-bit numbers.\n\nNumber with exactly k ones: C(10, k). So total with ≤8 ones = sum_{k=0}^{8} C(10,k). Excluding 0 because we start from 1. So I = (sum_{k=0}^{8} C(10,k)) - 1 (exclude 0) plus maybe include 1024 (popcount 1). But 1024 is beyond 10 bits; its binary is 1 followed by 10 zeros, effectively 11 bits with a 1 at position 10. Its popcount 1 ≤8, so counts as allowed number as well. So we add 1.\n\nThus total allowed numbers from 1 to 1024 = (sum_{k=0}^{8} C(10,k) - 1) + 1 = sum_{k=0}^{8} C(10,k) .\n\nBecause subtract 0 (k=0) which corresponds to number 0; then add 1024 yields net same.\n\nThus I_max = sum_{k=0}^{8} C(10,k). Compute.\n\nSum of all subsets C(10,k) from k=0 to 10 = 2^{10}=1024.\n\nWe need to exclude k=9 and k=10 terms. C(10,9)=10, C(10,10)=1, total 11. So sum_{k=0}^{8}=1024 -11 = 1013.\n\nThus I_max = 1013.\n\nTherefore total moves T_max = 2I -1 = 2*1013 -1 = 2026 -1 = 2025? Wait compute: 2*1013 = 2026; minus 1 = 2025.\n\nThus answer X = 2025.\n\nCheck plausibility: Total moves = I insertions + M merges with M=I-1 = 1012 merges, total 2025 moves. Seems plausible.\n\nNeed ensure capacity of 9 cells: we required popcount ≤8 before insertion (i.e., after merges). Our count includes numbers with popcount exactly 8 as allowed intermediate sums. At those points there are 8 tiles, leaving one empty cell for next insertion. Good.\n\nWhen we reach number with popcount 8, we can still insert a 1 (or other) which may increase popcount to 9 temporarily before merging; but after insertion we can merge to bring popcount back ≤8. Since we count insert as a move, after insertion we may have popcount 9 (since we haven't merged yet). But is that allowed? After insertion, number of occupied cells = previous popcount (8) +1 =9, allowed (max 9). So okay. Then we perform merges required (maybe multiple) to reduce popcount.\n\nThus capacity constraint satisfied.\n\nEdge case: We may need to ensure that when we jump over a disallowed number (popcount 9 or 10) by adding larger power, the intermediate after insertion may have popcount >9? Let's examine scenario: Suppose current sum S has popcount 8. Adding 2^j where that bit is 0 and no trailing ones (i.e., j > position of any ones), then popcount becomes 9 (exceeds 9?). That's okay because after insertion we have 9 tiles – allowed. After insertion we then need to merge to reduce, but merges may require multiple steps each decreasing count. That is fine. So capacity never exceed 9.\n\nThus the constructed path is feasible.\n\nThus max moves 2025.\n\nCheck if any possibility to have more than 1013 insertions using some numbers with popcount >8 but still staying within capacity due to merging before insertion? Actually before insertion we must have ≤8 tiles; after insertion we can have up to 9, then merges reduce. So any insert increases tile count by 1, then merges reduce maybe more than 1. So overall tile count after merges = popcount of new sum, which must be ≤8 for next insertion. So popcount of new sum must be ≤8. So we cannot have intermediate sum with popcount 9 after merges (i.e., final state after merges). But after insertion, before merging, popcount could be up to 9, that's okay. So the requirement is that every cumulative sum after insertion and after all possible merges (i.e., the canonical representation) must have popcount ≤8, except final 1024 with popcount 1.\n\nThus our counting aligns.\n\nHence answer 2025.", "response": "ANSWER: 2025", "answer": 2025, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.072883321739937e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999989271172538, "logp_correct": -1.072883321739937e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7651356206066802, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3485746711562, "frac_below_-5": 0.014615958240119313, "digit_lp_min": -3.3948261737823486, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 6705, "lp_end": -0.002501931507140398, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -0.0005577438860200346, "logp_nocot": -14.437674033222713, "p_nocot": 5.367818661642499e-07, "uplift": 14.437672960339391}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "b2e73e19-d97e-4d3c-b27f-73de20e8efba", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Fix an integer $n \\geq 2$. An $n \\times n$ sieve is an $n \\times n$ array with $n$ cells removed so that exactly one cell is removed from each row and column. For any positive integer $k$, a stick is a $1 \\times k$ or $k \\times 1$ array. For any sieve $A$, let $m(A)$ denote the minimum number of sticks required to partition $A$. Find all possible values of $m(A)$, since $A$ is different in all possible $n \\times n$ sieves, for $n=100$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Fix an integer $n \\geq 2$. An $n \\times n$ sieve is an $n \\times n$ array with $n$ cells removed so that exactly one cell is removed from each row and column. For any positive integer $k$, a stick is a $1 \\times k$ or $k \\times 1$ array. For any sieve $A$, let $m(A)$ denote the minimum number of sticks required to partition $A$. Find all possible values of $m(A)$, since $A$ is different in all possible $n \\times n$ sieves, for $n=100$.", "true_answer": 198, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "b2e73e19-d97e-4d3c-b27f-73de20e8efba:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 4218, 4792, 25, 2214, 297, 1215, 297, 10586, 483, 9707, 1001, 12486, 4206, 306, 2454, 5225, 326, 6000, 350, 9995, 261, 135200, 328, 11906, 11242, 741, 2632, 480, 382, 1299, 448, 297, 1215, 297, 6796, 483, 261, 135200, 328, 30416, 13, 1416, 1309, 316, 31512, 290, 15448, 11242, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 290, 6796, 40335, 2617, 297, 30416, 8, 1511, 54819, 350, 7385, 2081, 268, 328, 2349, 220, 16, 1215, 372, 503, 372, 1215, 220, 16, 395, 1062, 372, 741, 901, 8736, 665, 413, 1062, 5517, 113653, 503, 94774, 11, 24878, 130997, 11242, 306, 261, 5225, 503, 6000, 13, 3164, 6284, 56631, 326, 2804, 4321, 72