elect any column segment intersecting it (since they'd overlap). So we need a vertex cover in a bipartite graph where vertices are the 2n segments, edges correspond to cells (intersection). We need to select a set of vertices that covers all edges (cells), and the number of selected vertices is the number of sticks (since each selected segment becomes one stick). Additionally, we can only select a vertex if we take the whole segment (i.e., all incident edges). That's fine. So the problem reduces to: Find minimum vertex cover size in bipartite graph G with left vertices = row segments (L_i,R_i) (2n), right vertices = column segments (T_j,B_j) (2n), and edges correspond to cells (where row segment and column segment intersect). This graph is bipartite. Minimum vertex cover size = maximum matching size (Kőnig's theorem). So m(A) equals size of maximum matching in this graph.\n\nGreat! So we need to compute possible values of maximum matching size for such graph derived from permutation. The graph structure: L_i corresponds to cells (i, 1..p(i)-1) intersecting columns j where j<p(i). In column j, the top/bottom segment depends on relation of row index of hole i=j? Actually column j top segment T_j includes rows i with i < row where p(i)=j. Let r(j) = the row i such that p(i)=j (inverse permutation). Then T_j includes rows 1..r(j)-1; B_j rows r(j)+1..n.\n\nThus L_i connects to T_j for j < p(i) and also to B_j for j > p(i) ? Wait cell (i,j) belongs to row-left if j<p(i). For column j, if i < r(j) then cell is in T_j (above hole). If i > r(j) then in B_j. Since we are in left of hole, i's row missing column p(i). For a given cell (i,j) where j<p(i). Determine relation of i to r(j). Since j<p(i), but not known relative. So L_i connects to T_j if i < r(j) else B_j.\n\nSimilarly R_i (cells j>p(i)) connects to T_j if i < r(j) else B_j.\n\nThus graph is determined by permutation p.\n\nGoal: maximum matching size possible values. Graph has 4n vertices, but maximum matching canno