ows hitting column1? Not necessarily. Row empties require hole at column1 or n. We can have hole at column n located in row i (not n) creates empty R_i (right segment empty). So left side empty count at least 1 as well. Thus total empty vertices at least 2 (one on each side). Hence minimum nonempty vertices per side is 2n-2, leading to min matching size 2n-2.\n\nThus possible matching sizes are 2n-2 and 2n? What about 2n-1? Since empties come in pairs maybe matching size cannot be odd difference? Because each side has same number of empties (since total empties left = number of rows where hole at edge = count of holes at column1 plus column n). That's equal to number of columns where hole at top or bottom empty? Not equal.\n\nBut we know at least one empty left (due to column n hole) and at least one empty right (due to column n hole). Could be more.\n\nThus minimal 2n-2. Maximum 2n. Can we achieve 2n-1? Need total empties on smaller side =1 while opposite side =0, but we argued each column n hole creates empty on right side, and also left side empty unless its row = n. If column n hole is at row n, then left side empty also (R_n empty) and right side empty (T_n empty). That's both sides empty count at least 1. So we cannot have empties only on one side. Minimum total empties across both sides is 2? Therefore matching size cannot be 2n-1. So possible values are exactly 2n-2 and 2n? What about intermediate 2n-3 etc? Might be possible if we have more edge holes increasing empties.\n\nNumber of empties on each side equals number of edge holes maybe. Edge holes count = number of rows with hole at column1 or n = also number of columns with hole at row1 or n. These counts are equal? Since mapping is bijection, the number of holes at column1 (which is 1) plus at column n (1) equals total edge columns count =2. Similarly number of rows with hole at row1 is 1 (hole in row1 some column) and row n is 1. So there are exactly 2 holes at edge columns and 2 holes at edge rows? Actually each column edge has exactly one hole somewhere, each row edge has exactly one hole somewhere. So there are exactly 2 edge columns (1,n) each with a hole; they may be in same row or different rows. So total edge columns holes =2. Edge rows (row1, row n) also each have a hole somewhere (maybe at columns 1 or n). So total edge rows holes =2. The total number of edge holes (holes located on perimeter) could be up to 4 but could overlap (if hole at corner counts for both a edge row and edge column). So total edge holes ranged 2 to 4.\n\nEach edge hole contributes empties: For hole at (1,1) creates L1 empty, T1 empty (both sides). Hole at (1,n) creates R1 empty, T_n empty. Hole at (n,1) creates L_n empty, B1 empty. Hole at (n,n) creates R_n empty, B_n empty.\n\nThus each corner hole creates two empties (one left/right, one top/bottom). Non-corner edge hole (e.g., (i,1) with i not 1,n) creates only L_i empty (left side) but column1 has both top and bottom nonempty, so only one empty. Similarly (i,n) creates R_i empty.\n\nThus total empties left side = number of rows with hole at column1 or n = 2 (since exactly two columns 1 and n each have a hole, but could be same row?), could be 1 if both holes are in same row (i.e., row i has both columns1 and n holes impossible because one hole per row). So they must be in different rows, thus left empties count =2 (since each column edge hole is in a distinct row). Therefore left side empties =2 always. Wait if column1 hole at row i, column n hole at row j, i may equal j? cannot because each row has one hole, so i ≠ j. So exactly two rows have edge column holes, each causing an empty left segment (L_i or R_j). Hence left side empties =2 always.\n\nSimilarly right side empties: column1 hole may create empty top segment if its row=1 else none. column n hole creates empty top segment always (since top segment rows before that row, which may be nonempty unless row=1). Actually top segment T_n is empty only if hole at row1, else nonempty. Bottom segment B1 empty only if hole at row n.\n\nThus number of empty right vertices depends on positions of edge column holes relative to row1/n. At minimum, maybe 1? Let's compute possibilities:\n\n- If column1 hole at row i where 1<i<n, then both T1 and B1 are nonempty (no empties from column1).\n- If column n hole at row k where 1<k<n, then T_n nonempty (rows above), B_n nonempty (rows below). So no empty from column n either. Thus total empties right side could be 0! Wait earlier we thought column n hole always creates empty top. But if k>1, then T_n includes rows 1..k-1 nonempty. So not empty. So we can have zero empties right side.\n\nEdge rows: row1 hole at column a (could be internal). This affects column segment empties? Row1 hole at column a creates T_a empty (since rows<1 none). Also column a's bottom nonempty. Similarly row n hole at column b creates B_b empty.\n\nThus empties on right side may also arise from edge rows. Row1 hole creates an empty top segment in its column; row n hole creates empty bottom segment.\n\nThus total empties right side = number of edge rows whose hole column is not the opposite edge? If row1 hole at column1 (corner) then T1 empty (again). So we have at least 2 empties on right side (row1 and row n each cause one empty), unless their columns are also 1 or n causing same empties? Anyway at minimum there are 2 empties on right side as well.\n\nThus total empties left =2 always. Right empties at least 2. Hence minimum nonempty vertices per side = 2n-2 on left, 2n-2 on right. So min matching size = 2n-2. Maximum matching when there are no empties on right side? But we said at least 2 empties from row1 and row n. However if row1 hole at column n, then T_n empty (that's one empty) and row n hole at column1 gives B1 empty (another empty). So still 2 empties. So minimum empties right side is 2. So nonempty right vertices max =2n-2? Wait but earlier we thought could be 2n. Seems contradictory.\n\nLet's count systematically: Right side vertices are column segments: T_j and B_j for each column j.\n\nWhen does T_j become empty? If the hole in column j is in row1 (i.e., r(j)=1). Similarly B_j empty if r(j)=n.\n\nThus number of empty top segments equals number of columns whose hole is in row1. Since each row has exactly one hole, row1's hole column is say a = p(1). So exactly one column (a) has r(a)=1, thus T_a empty. Similarly row n's hole column b = p(n) gives B_b empty.\n\nThus exactly two empty right vertices always: T_{p(1)} and B_{p(n)}. Possibly they coincide if p(1)=p(n) which cannot since rows distinct columns; so distinct. Therefore exactly 2 empties on right side always. Good! So regardless of permutation, right side always has exactly 2 empty vertices. Similarly left side always has exactly 2 empty vertices (L_{i} where p(i)=1 and R_{j} where p(j)=n). Indeed column1 hole at row i = inverse of p(i)=1 -> i = p^{-1}(1). So L_i empty. Column n hole at row j gives R_j empty. Since i ≠ j, two empties.\n\nThus both sides have exactly 2 empty vertices always. Hence each side has 2n-2 nonempty vertices. Therefore any perfect matching can match at most 2n-2 vertices on each side, resulting in matching size ≤2n-2. Wait but earlier we found for n=3 identity matching size 4 = 2n-2 =4. So maximum across permutations is also 2n-2? Could there be larger? Since each side limited to 2n-2 nonempty vertices, maximum matching size ≤2n-2. Therefore m(A) is always exactly 2n-2! But we earlier thought maybe 2n possible, but now corrected: because each side has exactly 2 empty vertices, can't exceed 2n-2. Therefore m(A) is constant = 2n-2 for any n ≥2. Let's test n=2: 2n-2=2 matches earlier. Good. So for n=100, m(A)=2*100 -2 = 198.\n\nThus possible values set is just {198}. So answer integer 198.\n\nReturn format.", "response": "ANSWER: 198", "answer": 198, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.4305110482837335e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999985694899749, "logp_correct": -1.4305110482837335e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6047706923669642, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4775657306153391, "frac_below_-5": 0.011773788843852539, "digit_lp_min": -3.167999267578125, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.00020346954988781363, "n_cot_tokens": 10362, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.537788011773955e-05, "logp_nocot": -7.586504817008965, "p_nocot": 0.0005072508939017881, "uplift": 7.586503386497917}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
