431, 553, 350, 17, 11, 18, 8, 591, 220, 16, 11, 350, 16, 11, 18, 8, 591, 220, 17, 11, 350, 16, 11, 17, 8, 591, 18, 13, 2632, 2454, 25363, 14518, 306, 1920, 22569, 13, 2514, 28726, 97990, 25363, 323, 11, 581, 2023, 10333, 1001, 328, 290, 92927, 25, 2304, 3407, 38360, 261, 17568, 22261, 13, 21050, 328, 4566, 2454, 328, 1373, 163314, 290, 1273, 1920, 11, 581, 2023, 679, 261, 163314, 287, 22261, 350, 13457, 51743, 8, 472, 2254, 11, 287, 163314, 261, 22261, 350, 359, 936, 326, 274, 163314, 261, 326, 323, 350, 152746, 328, 261, 17568, 8, 10112, 30, 3072, 274, 6284, 163314, 8807, 11, 889, 665, 163314, 261, 326, 323, 26, 11165, 350, 64, 30680, 741, 7801, 323, 738, 413, 29703, 328, 11165, 350, 64, 30680, 741, 1328, 8844, 274, 885, 63298, 920, 6664, 323, 11, 25435, 2236, 323, 42308, 254, 274, 13, 8820, 1309, 323, 350, 3086, 97990, 8, 316, 679, 1617, 2316, 63298, 10610, 350, 38397, 5036, 8, 625, 3463, 323, 13, 1328, 738, 2501, 3613, 11165, 2870, 1920, 5036, 23859, 364, 2167, 2804, 6757, 2454, 25363, 2928, 853, 9707, 1001, 11165, 9113, 350, 2845, 1617, 2316, 90839, 741, 2632, 581, 665, 37707, 323, 1511, 1236, 41487, 316, 1520, 6392, 328, 220, 19, 38360, 483, 22569, 10112, 4137, 43061, 2086, 2928, 1078, 297, 14, 18, 30, 730, 261, 6392, 483, 220, 19, 38360, 11, 29400, 2999, 13313, 920, 30, 30456, 413, 220, 17, 10112, 13, 1843, 581, 2023, 2501, 261, 6392, 1919, 3793, 28, 16, 777, 220, 18, 38360, 402, 7848, 13, 2214, 220, 19, 38360, 11, 581, 3572, 11659, 3793, 28, 16, 6961, 4569, 1481, 10389, 7848, 13, 41021, 14326, 364, 42087, 25, 12998, 3793, 314, 8350, 2406, 14, 18, 8, 314, 220, 48412, 30, 12265, 297, 625, 167813, 656, 220, 18, 11, 1636, 6931, 11608, 382, 35964, 2406, 14, 18, 8, 314, 220, 49636, 350, 2345, 4004, 78, 12, 107150, 741, 3072, 4004, 78, 12, 107150, 11608, 8967, 3793, 99731, 21494, 220, 16, 27334, 67, 6932, 16161, 16, 741, 2214, 7848, 9882, 220, 17, 11, 2023, 413, 472, 4465, 472, 5263, 316, 297, 14, 18, 889, 10112, 12629, 316, 35964, 30, 41021, 23864, 11608, 395, 1039, 30248, 7465, 483, 1001, 42329, 25363, 25, 18210, 25, 220, 17, 395, 2454, 25363, 306, 92927, 350, 25035, 10112, 1236, 483, 5018, 11165, 741, 2763, 119504, 25363, 9882, 220, 15, 871, 26735, 220, 16, 13, 2214, 2454, 41487, 25363, 9882, 220, 17, 871, 62274, 220, 16, 14, 18, 2454, 13, 2632, 4215, 314, 220, 16, 659, 350, 667, 24, 44406, 16, 14, 18, 8, 314, 220, 16, 659, 220, 48412, 314, 220, 49636, 13, 2632, 11608, 15248, 47685, 13, 30456, 581, 679, 3793, 314, 220, 48412, 30, 4569, 1481, 1841, 4215, 29298, 72554, 48412, 11, 889, 290, 4215, 328, 147877, 1288, 382, 13213, 4335, 9882, 12545, 13, 2514, 6931, 4215, 11, 581, 1309, 1236, 38360, 483, 9882, 1424, 17, 11, 1118, 38566, 29298, 25, 220, 16, 27334, 67, 10, 16, 8, 13679, 13, 3072, 581, 679, 13213, 7848, 9882, 220, 17, 350, 9577, 11165, 3605, 220, 1323, 15, 813, 4215, 9882, 314, 220, 17, 9, 1323, 15, 314, 220, 13989, 15, 741, 2514, 3357, 4215, 328, 147877, 1288, 4465, 11, 581, 2023, 679, 1236, 38360, 483, 1932, 9882, 11, 326, 11095, 18258, 1991, 483, 4465, 9882, 13, 5551, 4465, 9882, 350, 26167, 220, 15, 8, 7064, 26735, 13, 2632, 581, 1309, 316, 8397, 9882, 220, 15, 13, 99472, 4149, 3793, 1340, 413, 10740, 1572, 35964, 2406, 14, 18, 20707, 41021, 2075, 316, 1921, 4149, 6931, 11608, 2360, 10895, 3712, 885, 177750, 30, 2214, 7465, 483, 7848, 9882, 272, 11, 43061, 2086, 540, 5153, 297, 27334, 67, 10, 16, 741, 21926, 220, 1323, 15, 14, 18, 28, 48412, 13, 2546, 871, 540, 5153, 220, 49636, 350, 21910, 16336, 741, 2632, 3793, 99731, 220, 49636, 13, 2632, 220, 49636, 382, 261, 6931, 11608, 11, 8535, 413, 3760, 13, 19233, 19957, 4149, 3793, 314, 220, 49636, 13, 31526, 45794, 472, 581, 30248, 13, 2632, 6052, 25, 5153, 2086, 328, 14306, 69658, 314, 220, 49636, 364, 84787, 4733, 364], "n_tokens": 5350, "words": 3985, "band_lo": 2454.0, "band_hi": 9816.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to model: there are 2020 inhabitants. Each person chooses (appoints) two distinct other people. The post company then for each person chooses one of those two to send a card to, on behalf of that person. So each inhabitant will send exactly one card to some other inhabitant, chosen among his two appointed ones, by the company. The company wants to maximize the number of sad inhabitants, i.e., those who receive zero cards. We need the minimal possible number of sad inhabitants given that the company is adversarial (maximizing sad). Equivalent: Given the ability of each person to nominate two possible recipients, we can think of a directed graph where each vertex has out-degree 2 (edges to the two nominated recipients). Then the company chooses for each vertex one outgoing edge to activate, resulting in a functional digraph (each vertex out-degree exactly 1). The company chooses to maximize vertices with indegree zero (no incoming active edges). The residents choose their nominations perhaps? Wait statement: \"each inhabitant may appoint two different other inhabitants, among which the company chooses one to whom to send a Christmas card on behalf of that inhabitant.\" It seems inhabitants can decide who they appoint, but we ask for the least possible number of sad inhabitants, given that the company chooses worst-case assignments after we (as designers) can choose the nominations? Usually \"it is known that the company makes the choices in such a way that as many inhabitants as possible will become sad. Find the least possible number of inhabitants who will become sad.\" So we (the town) can arrange appointments (two per inhabitant) to minimize maximum sadness.\n\nThus it's a game: We choose for each vertex a set of two distinct possible recipients. Then adversary chooses one outgoing edge per vertex from each set to maximize number of vertices with indegree zero. What is the minimal possible maximum number of vertices with indegree zero? Equivalent to: For a directed graph where each vertex has out-degree at most 2 (actually exactly 2 possible edges), the adversary will select a subgraph where each vertex has out-degree 1 (choose one of its two edges). We ask: what is the minimum possible number of vertices that can be forced to have indegree zero, when we can design the 2-out alternative edges.\n\nWe need guarantee that no matter which outgoing edge each vertex chooses from its two, at least N - (maximum possible sad) get at least one incoming. We want to minimize (maximum possible sad) i.e., guarantee as many get cards as possible.\n\nGoal: design a 2-regular-out directed graph (a 2-regular out-degree) such that any functional subgraph (choice of one outgoing per vertex) has at least a certain number of vertices with indegree >=1. Find minimal possible maximal sad =? Equivalent to: Find minimal possible of the maximum size of a set S such that there exists a selection with all arcs pointing into complement? Hmm.\n\nLet’s think. Let the selection produce a functional digraph. Vertices with indegree zero are those that are not chosen by anyone. The total number of edges is 2020 (each vertex sends one). So number of vertices with indegree zero = number of vertices not chosen as target. Since each edge hits some vertex, we have sum indegrees = 2020. Let k be number of vertices with indegree zero. Then the remaining 2020 - k vertices have indegree at least 1, sum indegrees among them is 2020, average indegree among non-zero vertices = 2020/(2020 - k). Since each indegree >=1, we have 2020 >= 2020 - k => trivial. Actually we need bound on k: maximum possible k given constraints on nominations? Usually can have up to 1010 sad if each person nominates two others, maybe we can force at least half get cards? Let's test extremes.\n\nIf each person nominates the same two individuals A and B. Then the company could send all cards to A, making B get none, others none. But each sender must choose among A or B, could send to A all, making only A get many cards, others sad. So sad = 2019. That's huge. But we want to minimize maximum sad; we can control nominations to avoid this.\n\nWe need to guarantee that whatever selection, the set of recipients covers many vertices.\n\nEssentially, we have a bipartite covering problem: For each vertex i, we have two possible targets Ti = {a_i, b_i}. The adversary selects one target from each Ti. This yields a multiset of chosen targets; we want guarantee that the set of chosen distinct vertices has size at least some L; then sad = 2020 - L. So we need to design the collection of 2-element subsets of a 2020-set such that any choice of one element from each subset (allow repetitions) produces at least L distinct chosen elements.\n\nSo we need the minimum possible maximal number of distinct chosen elements, given each subset size 2. Equivalent to hitting number: we want to design subsets to force the chosen elements to cover many vertices.\n\nThis is reminiscent of problem of the \"postal company\" maximizing uncovered vertices analogous to set cover with each element can be avoided if not in any chosen. It's like design of a family of 2-element subsets where the transversal number (minimum size of a set intersecting every subset) is as large as possible? Wait: If we think of sad vertices = those not selected. The adversary tries to avoid picking some vertices. For each vertex v, to make it sad, must ensure no subset chooses v. So if we want to prevent a vertex being avoided, we need that for each vertex v, there exists a subset Ti that contains only v and some other? Actually to force v to be selected, we need that there is at least one subset where the only way to avoid v is impossible: but each subset offers two choices, so as long as there is at least one person who nominated v as one of two options, the company can still avoid picking v by choosing the other option for that person. So to guarantee v receives at least one card, we need that for any selection, at least one subset picks v. That means v is in a family such that it's impossible to avoid picking it. This is like a hitting set: we need that the family of subsets is such that any choice function (pick one element from each subset) hits each vertex? That's impossible because you could possibly avoid a given vertex unless there is a subset {v, v}? Not allowed (must be two different others). So you cannot guarantee every vertex gets a card. So some will be sad inevitably.\n\nThe problem asks for least possible number of sad, i.e., we want to minimize the worst-case uncovered vertices we can guarantee.\n\nThis is combinatorial: For n=2020, each vertex must be in at most ?? each person nominates two others, so total nominations = 2*2020 = 4040. So sum of degrees (in terms of being nominated) = 4040, counting each nomination as an edge in underlying bipartite incidence. So each vertex can be nominated multiple times, average nomination per vertex = 4040/2020 = 2. So average indegree potential (i.e., number of times someone could send to you) is 2.\n\nNow the adversary chooses exactly one nomination per person; total 2020 selections. The number of times a vertex gets selected is its actual indegree. The sum over all vertices of indegree = 2020.\n\nWe need guarantee: at least L vertices have indegree >=1. Given total indegree 2020, what's the minimum number of vertices that can have indegree >=1? That's when indegrees are as concentrated as possible: try to give many cards to few vertices. The maximum concentration limited by the number of nominations each vertex has (cannot receive more than number of nominators that nominated them). Since each nominates two others, max inbound potential per vertex ≤ number of people who listed that vertex (call its nomination count). That could be high. In worst case you could have one vertex nominated by many (maybe all 2020 people list it as one of two). Then you could give all 2020 cards to that vertex, making only 1 vertex non-sad? Actually that vertex would get all cards, others sad = 2019. That's allowed because each person also nominates another person but company picks the one that is this central vertex.\n\nThus we can get as many sad as 2019, but we are looking for minimal maximum sad, i.e., we want to design nominations to prevent such concentration. Since each person only nominates two, to avoid concentration we need to distribute nominations relatively evenly. The company can only select among each person's two options, so to concentrate cards they need many people to share a common nominee among their two options. So the worst-case number of sad is determined by the maximum size of a subset X of vertices that can be avoided (not chosen) by selecting the other option for each person.\n\nDefine a set S of vertices we want to potentially keep uncovered. For the company to avoid covering S, for each person i, at least one of his two choices must be outside S (i.e., the person can choose a target outside S). If there exists any person whose both nominations are inside S, then the company must select a target in S (since both options are inside). So to keep S uncovered, we need that each person has at least one nomination in complement of S. That is a necessary condition for S to be possibly all sad. So the maximum sad achievable is the size of the largest set S such that for every person, at least one of his two nominated vertices lies outside S.\n\nWe want to design nominations to minimize the maximum size of such S. Equivalent to: Find minimal possible value of the largest S that is a hitting set complement? Actually we want to avoid large S with that property.\n\nLet V be set of 2020 inhabitants. For each i, we have a 2-element subset N(i) ⊂ V\\{i}. Define a set S ⊂ V. If for all i, N(i) ⊄ S (i.e., N(i) not contained entirely in S), then S can be sad (company can choose for each i a target outside S). Conversely, if there exists i with N(i) ⊆ S, then at least one card must go to S, making some member of S non-sad.\n\nThus the maximal possible sad size is the size of the largest S that is \"2-avoiding\": no N(i) is wholly inside S. This is like the largest subset with no edge (i, j)?? Not exactly because N(i) are unordered pairs not containing i.\n\nWe get to choose the family of N(i) (two-element subsets). We need to make the largest S with that property as small as possible.\n\nThus it's a combinatorial problem: given a universe of size n=2020, choose n subsets of size 2 (each labeled by i) with the condition that i not in its own subset (different others). Want to minimize the size of largest S that doesn't contain any of these subsets entirely.\n\nIn other words, we are covering the universe with a family of 2-subsets such that any large subset S will contain some of these 2-subsets wholly. What is minimal t such that we can guarantee that any subset of size t contains some N(i) whole? Then the maximum sad is t-1 (largest S with no whole N(i)). Actually largest S that avoids containing any N(i) is at most t-1.\n\nThus we need to construct a family of size n, each a 2-subset, to have the property that any subset of size m (some threshold) contains at least one of the 2-subsets as a subset. That's akin to covering number: the family is a \"2-covering\" of the m-subsets. Determine minimal possible maximum size of S with property that it is \"N-free\".\n\nWe can think in terms of graph: create an undirected graph G with vertices V, and for each person i, draw edges between the two nominated persons? Wait N(i) is a set of two vertices (a_i, b_i). So each i defines an unordered pair {a_i,b_i}. So we have n edges possibly with repetitions (could be same edge by multiple i). The condition for S to be \"N-avoiding\" is that S does not contain both endpoints of any edge of G (i.e., S is an independent set in G). Because if edge {a,b} is inside S, that corresponds to N(i)⊆S, which is prohibited. So S must be an independent set of the (undirected) graph formed by edges defined by each person (ignoring loops). Multiple parallel edges don't matter. Also note that i itself is not part of edge, but i could be one of a or b for other edges.\n\nThus the problem reduces: We have 2020 labeled vertices; each vertex i chooses two distinct other vertices a_i and b_i; we add undirected edge (a_i,b_i). The company can keep a set S sad exactly when S is an independent set (no edge fully inside) because each vertex i's edge is between two potential recipients. To avoid any card to S, for each i we need to select the target among at least one outside S; equivalently, the edge {a_i,b_i} must have at least one endpoint outside S, meaning S cannot contain both endpoints of any edge. So yes S independent set.\n\nThus the maximum number of sad inhabitants equals the size of the largest independent set α(G) of graph G (constructed from nominations). The company chooses orientation to avoid giving cards to independent set.\n\nWe, the designer, can choose the edges (with the constraint that each i (as a \"selector\") contributes an edge between two vertices not equal to i). There are n edges total (could have parallel edges). We need to minimize the independence number α(G) given we can place n edges (with possible loops avoided, edges must not use the vertex i as part of edge? Actually edge is between a_i and b_i, neither can be i. So each vertex i cannot appear as endpoint of the edge it creates, but can appear in many other edges.\n\nGoal: Build a graph on 2020 vertices with 2020 edges (allowing multiedges) such that independence number α is minimized. What is minimal possible α?\n\nFor simple graphs (no parallel edges), there's known bound: For a graph with average degree d = 2e/n = 2*2020/2020 = 2, i.e., average degree 2. For any graph, α ≥ n/(d+1) by Caro-Wei bound or Turán. So α ≥ n/(2+1) = n/3 = 673.33..., so at least 674. That's a lower bound. Can we achieve α close to n/3? Maybe we can design a 3-regular? but average degree is 2, not 3. To minimize α we want high degree to force small independence. But limited average degree 2. The extremal case for given average degree is usually a disjoint union of cycles (average degree 2) which has independence number floor(n/2) for even cycles, actually independence number of cycle C_k is floor(k/2). For a collection of cycles, α ~ n/2. That's bigger than n/3.\n\nWhat about graphs with many vertices of degree high and many of degree low? To lower α maybe we want many vertices of degree high, but average is 2: you can have some vertices of high degree and many isolated vertices (degree 0). That would increase α. Not good.\n\nSo the bound α ≥ n/(d+1) may be not tight for d=2? Example of a graph composed of disjoint triangles (3-cycle). Each triangle has independence number 1 (since you can only pick at most 1 vertex from triangle). For 2020 vertices, if we partition into triangles (3-vertex components) and maybe leftover, each triangle yields 1 independent vertex, so total α = number of triangles = floor(n/3). That's exactly n/3 (rounded up). And edges count: each triangle has 3 edges. So total edges = number of triangles *3 = (n/3)*3 = n. Indeed n edges! So we can achieve α = ceiling(n/3). That's probably minimal.\n\nBut need to respect the edge generation constraints: each edge must be associated to a vertex i not part of the edge. For a triangle (a,b,c), we need three edges (a,b), (b,c), (c,a). For each edge, we need a distinct i that is not one of its endpoints. Since each vertex i creates exactly one edge, we need to assign each vertex to be the \"selector\" of some edge not incident to it. In a triangle, each vertex can be assigned to the edge opposite it (i.e., the edge between the other two vertices). Indeed for triangle with vertices 1,2,3, let person 1 select pair {2,3} (edge 2-3). Person 2 selects {1,3} (edge 1-3). Person 3 selects {1,2} (edge 1-2). That's perfect. So triangles correspond to triplet of vertices where each vertex nominates the other two. That yields edges forming triangle. Works.\n\nThus we can partition the set of 2020 inhabitants into as many disjoint triples as possible, each forming a triangle via this scheme. 2020 = 3*673 + 1 (since 3*673 = 2019). Leftover 1 vertex cannot form a triangle alone.\n\nWe have 673 triples, each gives 3 vertices, edges count = 3 per triple => total edges from triples = 3*673 = 2019 edges. We need total edges = 2020 (since each vertex creates an edge). We have one more vertex remaining. This vertex (call v) must create an edge between two other vertices (not itself) possibly already used. Also, need to satisfy that each of other vertices already have their edge defined (they each have their own edge). So we can have v nominate two vertices from somewhere, but must ensure that the resulting graph's independence number doesn't increase beyond what minimal possible.\n\nWe need to add one more edge (since we already have 2019 edges from triples). That edge could be placed anywhere as long as not incident to v (i.e., edge between two other vertices). Adding an edge can only potentially decrease the independence number (or keep same), never increase, because it adds more constraints (more connections). So the independence number of the final graph will be at most the independence number after triples, which was 673 (one per triangle). Adding extra edge might reduce some triangles' independent possibility? Could it reduce to 672 maybe? Let's see.\n\nWe have leftover vertex v that hasn't been assigned as selector. v must nominate two different others, say x and y. Then edge (x,y) is added, possibly joining two vertices that maybe already connected via triangle edges.\n\nIf x and y belong to same triangle, they are already connected (they have edge already). Adding parallel edge doesn't affect independence number (still same). If they belong to different triangles, adding edge merges those components, possibly reducing independence number? Let's analyze.\n\nSuppose we connect vertex a from triangle T1 and vertex b from triangle T2. These two vertices already each have degree 2 within their triangle. Adding edge between them makes a degree 3, b degree 3. Now independent set can't contain both a and b (they're adjacent). But each triangle originally allowed picking exactly one vertex (any of its three) for independence. After adding cross edge, we might have to pick at most one of the two adjacent vertices, but we could still pick from each triangle unless we pick the particular vertices a and b simultaneously. So independence number maybe still 673 because you can select from each triangle a vertex not equal to a (choose other vertex) and from other triangle a vertex not equal b, circumventing the new edge. So doesn't reduce.\n\nThus minimal possible independence number α_min = ceil(n/3) = 674? Wait n=2020, ceil(2020/3)=674 (since 3*673=2019, remainder 1). Our construction gives independent set size at most 673 (since each triangle contributes 1). But we also have one extra vertex v (the leftover). This vertex v could be part of independent set as well because it may not be adjacent to any selected vertex? Actually v hasn't any edges incident to it (since its edge is between others). So v is isolated (degree 0). That means any independent set can include v as well. So the independence number becomes 673 + 1 = 674. Indeed we can pick v plus one vertex from each triangle (any that are not incident to crossing edge). Since v is isolated, it's independent of all others. So α = 674.\n\nThus the maximum sad (company can cause) = α = 674. Therefore the least possible number of sad inhabitants (i.e., the minimal possible maximum sad) is 674.\n\nCheck if we could reduce to 673 by arranging differently such that no isolated vertex exists; i.e., we might integrate the leftover vertex into a component to keep α=673. To avoid isolated vertex, we need to ensure every vertex is part of at least one edge (connected). Since each vertex already is part of some edge as part of triangle, but the leftover vertex is not yet part of any edge (since its edge is between others). However it could be made incident to some edge: we can have two vertices from a triangle nominate (including the leftover vertex) to create edge between leftover vertex and another vertex. But constraint: each vertex i's selected pair cannot include i. So the leftover vertex cannot be part of its own edge, but could be endpoint of other vertices' edges. Indeed many vertices already nominate others. In triangle arrangement, each vertex is endpoint of two edges (edge from itself and edge from the other vertex) actually each vertex appears in the two edges involving the other two selectors. For triangle (1,2,3): Edges are (2,3) from 1, (1,3) from 2, (1,2) from3. So each vertex appears in two edges. To involve leftover vertex v, we could modify one of the triangles: take three vertices a,b,c. Instead of having each of them nominate the other two, we could have a nominate b,c (edge bc) as before, b nominate a,c (ac), and c nominate a and v (instead of a,b) maybe? But c cannot nominate itself, but can nominate a and v; edge (a,v). Then v will be endpoint of edge (a,v). This uses c's nomination set includes v, okay because v ≠ c. Also need v (the leftover) to have its own nomination pair (two others) not including v. This will create another edge between two others potentially.\n\nWe must ensure each vertex still has exactly one edge defined (from its own nominations). So we can incorporate v into some triangle to make component of 4 vertices with edges maybe making independence number still about n/3? In a component with 4 vertices, what's max independent set? Could be 2 maybe. If we could create a component where α=1 per 3 vertices on average. For 4 vertices, we might manage α=1?? That would reduce average. Let's explore.\n\nGoal: achieve α = floor(n/3) = 673? Since n not divisible by 3, best lower bound is ceil(n/3) = 674 (by Caro-Wei). But Caro-Wei bound gives α ≥ Σ 1/(d(v)+1). For average degree 2, could be as low as close to n/3 but maybe equal to ceil? Let's compute bound for our constructed graph with one isolated vertex: degrees: 2 for each vertex in triangles (except maybe some with extra edge). Isolated vertex degree 0 => contribution 1. For each triangle vertex degree 2 => contributes 1/3 each. So sum = 1 + (2019)*(1/3) = 1 + 673 = 674. So bound matches equality. Could we have α = 673? That would require sum contributions ≤673, but the sum of reciprocals is fixed given degree distribution. To lower sum, we need some vertices with degree >2, which reduces contributions: 1/(d+1) smaller. But we have fixed average degree 2 (total edge count 2020 so sum degree = 2*2020 = 4040). To keep sum of reciprocals low, we could have some vertices with high degree, and correspondingly many with low degree. However low degree (including 0) increase contribution. So we need to avoid degree 0. Minimal possible α may be greater than ceil(n/3)? Let's try to see possible lower bound using Turán's theorem? For graph with average degree d, independence number at least n/(d+1). That's 2020/3=673.33 => at least 674 (since integer). So α ≥ 674. So 674 is a lower bound, can't be less. Therefore minimal possible α = 674. Achievable as we constructed. So answer: least number of sad inhabitants = 674.\n\nThus output.", "response": "ANSWER: 674", "answer": 674, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.7881385474538547e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999982118630513, "logp_correct": -1.7881385474538547e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7344882070924214, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4262258383888735, "frac_below_-5": 0.012368815592203899, "digit_lp_min": -3.288342237472534, "answer_first_mention_lp": -6.615896563744172e-05, "n_cot_tokens": 5336, "lp_end": -0.06233393773436546, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.8358061424805783e-05, "logp_nocot": -4.279143810271307, "p_nocot": 0.013854519106780884, "uplift": 4.27914202213276}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "1bf0f514-a492-45b9-8d75-007cd0b742e2", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: We say a $2023$-tuple of nonnegative integers $\\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots a_{2023}\\right)$ is sweet if the following conditions hold:  \n- $a_{1}+a_{2}+\\ldots+a_{2023}=2023$,  \n- $\\frac{a_{1}}{2^{1}}+\\frac{a_{2}}{2^{2}}+\\ldots+\\frac{a_{2023}}{2^{2023}} \\leqslant 1$.  \n\nDetermine the greatest positive integer $L$ such that  \n$$  \na_{1}+2 a_{2}+\\ldots+2023 a_{2023} \\geqslant L  \n$$  \nholds for every sweet 2023 -tuple $\\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots, a_{2023}\\right)$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: We say a $2023$-tuple of nonnegative integers $\\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots a_{2023}\\right)$ is sweet if the following conditions hold:  \n- $a_{1}+a_{2}+\\ldots+a_{2023}=2023$,  \n- $\\frac{a_{1}}{2^{1}}+\\frac{a_{2}}{2^{2}}+\\ldots+\\frac{a_{2023}}{2^{2023}} \\leqslant 1$.  \n\nDetermine the greatest positive integer $L$ such that  \n$$  \na_{1}+2 a_{2}+\\ldots+2023 a_{2023} \\geqslant L  \n$$  \nholds for every sweet 2023 -tuple $\\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots, a_{2023}\\right)$.", "true_answer": 22228, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "1bf0f514-a492-45b9-8d75-007cd0b742e2:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 19957, 4149, 1432, 328, 70288, 4215, 336, 314, 21494, 575, 261, 10853, 350, 72, 591, 220, 16, 316, 478, 1919, 478, 28, 1323, 18, 8, 4335, 31060, 25, 4215, 261, 10853, 314, 478, 11, 326, 21494, 261, 10853, 820, 220, 17, 61, 72, 72554, 220, 16, 11, 483, 261, 10853, 99731, 15, 54912, 13, 27042, 16983, 451, 2238, 484, 336, 99731, 451, 3324, 26, 575, 2560, 4213, 451, 314, 11085, 4149, 336, 1072, 722, 58866, 16336, 52254, 11, 1815, 6052, 382, 484, 11085, 1432, 350, 21910, 80149, 2804, 5060, 395, 722, 12418, 112464, 11,