. k=1:8+2^l=75 =>2^l=67. k=2:16+2^l=75 =>2^l=59. k=3:32+2^l=75 =>2^l=43. k=4:64+2^l=75 =>2^l=11 not power. So none.\n\nb=5: LHS =5*(25-1)=120. RHS =5*2^k + 2^l.\n\nWe can try combos where 5*2^k is power of two times 5. Values: 5,10,20,40,80,160...\n\nAdd a power of two to get 120.\n\nCheck: 5*2^k = 80 (k=4) then need 40, not power. 5*2^k=40 (k=3) need 80 not power. 5*2^k=20 (k=2) need100 not power. 10 need110 not. So none.\n\nThus only solution for a=5 currently is (5,3,7) and its permutations.\n\nNow a=6.\n\nWe can search.\n\nEquation (A): a(b^2 -1) = b 2^k + 2^l.\n\nLet a=6.\n\nTest b=2: LHS =6*(4-1)=18. RHS =2*2^k +2^l.\n\nTry k=1 =>4+2^l=18 =>2^l=14 no. k=2 =>8+2^l=18 =>2^l=10 no. k=3 =>16+2^l=18 =>2^l=2 => l=1 works. So k=3,l=1. Compute c = ab-2^k =6*2-8=12-8=4. Check bc - a =2*4 -6 =8-6=2 =2^1 matches l. ca - b =4*6 -2 =24 -2=22 not power. So fails.\n\nb=3: LHS =6*(9-1)=48. RHS =3*2^k +2^l.\n\nTry k=0:3+2^l=48 =>2^l=45 no. k=1:6+2^l=48 =>2^l=42 no. k=2:12+2^l=48 =>2^l=36 no. k=3:24+2^l=48 =>2^l=24 not power. k=4:48+2^l=48 =>2^l=0 no. So none.\n\nb=4: LHS =6*(16-1)=90. RHS =4*2^k + 2^l.\n\nTry k=0:4+2^l=90 =>2^l=86 no. k=1:8+...=90 =>2^l=82; k=2:16=>74; k=3:32=>58; k=4:64=>26 not power; k=5:128>.\n\nSo none.\n\nb=5: LHS =6*(25-1)=144. RHS =5*2^k + 2^l.\n\nTry k=0:5+2^l=144 =>2^l=139; k=1:10 =>134; k=2:20=>124; k=3:40=>104; k=4:80=>64 =>2^l=64 works! So k=4,l=6 because 2^l=64 => l=6. Then compute c = ab - 2^k =6*5 -16 =30-16=14. Check bc - a =5*14 -6 =70-6=64 =2^6 matches l. ca - b =14*6 -5 =84-5=79 not power. So fails.\n\nThus a=6 no new.\n\na=7.\n\nTest b small.\n\nb=2: LHS =7*(4-1)=21. RHS=2*2^k+2^l.\n\nTry k=0:2+2^l=21 =>2^l=19; k=1:4+...=17; k=2:8+...=13; k=3:16+...=5 not power; k=4:32 >.\n\nNone.\n\nb=3: LHS =7*(9-1)=56. RHS =3*2^k +2^l.\n\nTry k=0:3+2^l=56 =>2^l=53; k=1:6 =>50; k=2:12=>44; k=3:24=>32 (2^5) works! So k=3,l=5. Compute c = ab -2^k =7*3 -8 =21-8=13. Check bc - a =3*13 -7 =39-7=32 =2^5 matches. ca - b =13*7 -3 =91-3=88 not power.\n\nThus fail.\n\nb=4: LHS =7*(16-1)=105. RHS =4*2^k +2^l.\n\nTry k=0:4+...=105 =>2^l=101; k=1:8=>97; k=2:16=>89; k=3:32=>73; k=4:64=>41 not power; k=5:128 too big. None.\n\nb=5: LHS =7*(25-1)=168. RHS =5*2^k+2^l.\n\nk=0:5 =>163; k=1:10=>158; k=2:20=>148; k=3:40=>128 (2^7) works! So k=3,l=7. c = 7*5-8=35-8=27. Check bc - a =5*27 -7 =135-7=128 =2^7 matches. ca - b =27*7 -5 =189-5=184 not power.\n\nThus fail.\n\nb=6: LHS =7*(36-1)=245. RHS =6*2^k+2^l.\n\nk=0:6 =>239; k=1:12=>233; k=2:24=>221; k=3:48=>197; k=4:96=>149; k=5:192=>53 not power. No.\n\nThus a=7 no.\n\nNow perhaps larger a will not give solutions because third condition fails.\n\nFrom patterns, solutions seem to be the ones where the three numbers form arithmetic progression? Let's see (2,6,11) differences 4,5 not equal. (5,3,7) maybe not.\n\nGeneral property? Could correspond to solutions where numbers are consecutive odd? Not sure.\n\nMaybe there are infinite families? Observing earlier with a=2 we got infinite possibilities k=0,m even gave b integer, but only some m gave third condition power of two; we got m=2,4 working. Maybe there is another family for a different a giving few solutions.\n\nWe could try a=3 but we already got only permutation of (2,2,3).\n\nAlright maybe complete list: (2,2,2); permutations of (2,2,3); permutations of (2,6,11); permutations of (3,5,7) (since (5,3,7) same set). Let's check if (3,5,7) works? Compute: a=3,b=5,c=7:\n\nab - c =15-7=8 (2^3)\nbc - a =35-3=32 (2^5)\nca - b =21-5=16 (2^4) -> all powers. Yes works! So set {3,5,7} works.\n\nThus we have solution set {3,5,7} (distinct). Check permutations also work.\n\nNow try to see if there is another set like {4,? ,?}. Perhaps {4,? ,?} corresponds to a=4 earlier no.\n\nPerhaps there is family generated by solving previous linear equations? Let's attempt to param.\n\nFrom earlier example (a,b,c) = (2,6,11). Not all odds.\n\nMaybe we can generate via solving for one variable small and following.\n\nLet's attempt to search for a=3 gave (3,5,7)