1, 540, 5153, 1001, 328, 2617, 27933, 2804, 13574, 1373, 13, 1328, 382, 96811, 328, 7465, 50205, 25, 2454, 11165, 350, 144127, 8, 55089, 656, 30380, 2061, 395, 39612, 364, 10620, 665, 581, 679, 12282, 1919, 2127, 853, 4345, 220, 15, 13, 20, 1606, 306, 1215, 11, 865, 853, 4345, 220, 15, 13, 20, 1606, 306, 342, 11, 326, 2973, 679, 3149, 22253, 306, 15448, 27933, 11, 20547, 10945, 220, 15, 13, 20, 30, 24855, 25, 7649, 39612, 4251, 1215, 395, 71987, 11, 4251, 342, 395, 2127, 57, 11, 4251, 579, 395, 865, 57, 13, 41021, 1746, 1402, 12, 2214, 71987, 25, 13574, 656, 1215, 871, 2127, 853, 1215, 18085, 230, 58, 15, 11, 15, 13, 20, 2155, 865, 853, 1215, 18085, 230, 58, 15, 13, 20, 11, 16, 49887, 12, 2214, 2127, 57, 25, 13574, 656, 342, 871, 2127, 853, 342, 18085, 230, 58, 15, 11, 15, 13, 20, 2155, 865, 853, 342, 18085, 230, 58, 15, 13, 20, 11, 16, 49887, 12, 2214, 865, 57, 25, 13574, 656, 579, 871, 2127, 853, 579, 18085, 230, 58, 15, 11, 15, 13, 20, 2155, 865, 853, 579, 18085, 230, 58, 15, 13, 20, 11, 16, 37441, 10620, 2127, 1936, 1094, 25, 1215, 104733, 15, 13, 20, 11, 342, 104733, 15, 13, 20, 11, 579, 104733, 15, 13, 20, 871, 10945, 72554, 15, 13, 10676, 13, 65037, 865, 25, 2454, 72554, 15, 13, 20, 871, 1217, 220, 15, 13, 10676, 13, 21926, 220, 16, 14, 23, 13, 21926, 6931, 364, 84787, 12282, 15286, 316, 220, 16, 14, 23, 364, 3335, 581, 2075, 316, 1520, 2127, 679, 4410, 22253, 306, 1920, 27933, 11, 581, 1309, 39612, 306, 74016, 44041, 13, 2514, 679, 2127, 4345, 220, 16, 306, 342, 11, 1309, 395, 41244, 71987, 39612, 413, 4251, 1215, 11, 625, 342, 13, 2214, 41244, 2127, 57, 39612, 1217, 4251, 1215, 350, 786, 2973, 1199, 1215, 741, 7801, 1215, 9913, 316, 220, 15, 13, 20, 11, 342, 137479, 11, 579, 10112, 9913, 30, 2214, 865, 57, 41244, 2804, 413, 33930, 4493, 342, 503, 579, 13, 1843, 581, 13574, 4493, 342, 11, 1815, 865, 885, 342, 9913, 220, 15, 13, 20, 11, 2127, 885, 342, 10112, 3149, 13, 3072, 484, 1481, 1520, 865, 10945, 9913, 656, 1920, 27933, 30, 41021, 23864, 1402, 22951, 6791, 929, 25, 71987, 4493, 1215, 11, 2127, 57, 4493, 1215, 11, 865, 57, 4493, 342, 364, 84787, 1402, 12, 2127, 25, 1215, 72554, 15, 13, 20, 350, 4220, 69164, 483, 865, 885, 1215, 936, 860, 38247, 402, 342, 11, 889, 865, 57, 39612, 4493, 342, 47808, 865, 885, 342, 72554, 15, 13, 20, 326, 2127, 885, 342, 137479, 30, 5551, 865, 57, 41244, 164265, 553, 723, 88, 3352, 328, 2127, 60, 8088, 723, 89, 3352, 328, 2127, 60, 10217, 865, 33269, 13, 1843, 39612, 382, 4493, 342, 11, 484, 4748, 342, 49900, 553, 829, 69164, 26, 13972, 2127, 885, 342, 72554, 15, 13, 20, 472, 1775, 350, 21910, 865, 885, 342, 72554, 15, 13, 20, 741, 24305, 1023, 553, 829, 69164, 49900, 3518, 723, 15, 11, 16, 11464, 2454, 72554, 15, 13, 20, 13, 2632, 2127, 885, 342, 1217, 72554, 15, 13, 20, 13, 2632, 2127, 853, 2973, 1215, 104733, 15, 13, 20, 326, 342, 104733, 15, 13, 20, 11, 15021, 579, 14836, 3149, 220, 16, 11, 10945, 72554, 15, 13, 1161, 13, 2632, 2418, 220, 16, 14, 19, 364, 84787, 1062, 102060, 15286, 316, 540, 1645, 1920, 27933, 9913, 316, 220, 15, 13, 20, 395, 2454, 920, 11, 9874, 10945, 72554, 15, 13, 1161, 364, 72816, 98608, 394, 314, 220, 16, 14, 19, 364, 198140, 284, 28, 16, 11, 297, 28, 19, 871, 284, 87683, 314, 20, 364, 4383, 43286, 6011, 25, 220, 16, 14, 19, 2747, 284, 87683, 314, 20, 364, 84787, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 8473, "words": 5026, "band_lo": 2513.0, "band_hi": 10052.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret problem. We have unit cube [0,1]^3. We need two closed convex subsets X,Y inside the cube, each of volume V, such that for each of the three coordinate planes (xy-plane, xz-plane, yz-plane), the orthogonal projections of X and Y onto that plane are disjoint sets (i.e., they do not intersect). Need supremum possible V.\n\nWe need maximum volume each can have while still being separate in each coordinate projection.\n\nObservation: For two subsets to have disjoint projection onto XY-plane means that for any (x,y) there is not both a point in X and a point in Y with same (x,y) coordinates (any z). Equivalent: their shadows onto xy-plane do not overlap.\n\nSimilarly for yz-plane and xz-plane.\n\nThus any point (x,y,z) in X, any point (x',y',z') in Y must differ in at least one coordinate by being projected disjointly? Let's think: If projections onto XY are disjoint, then there is no pair with same (x,y) coordinates. So for any point in X and any point in Y, we cannot have both x and y coordinates equal. Similarly cannot have both x and z equal (xz-plane) and cannot have both y and z equal (yz-plane). So for any point p in X and q in Y, at least two coordinates differ? Actually condition: For each plane, the pair cannot have same pair of coordinates. Suppose p and q share x coordinate: if they also share y, then XY projection would intersect. So to avoid intersection, they must not share both x and y simultaneously. Similarly cannot share both x and z; cannot share both y and z.\n\nThus any pair (p,q) cannot have two coordinates equal simultaneously. So at most one coordinate may be equal. So any pair must differ in at least two coordinates.\n\nGoal: pack two convex bodies of equal volume within unit cube with such separation.\n\nThink about dividing cube into two parts by using a \"grid\" where each point assigned to X or Y based on some rule depending on coordinates, ensuring that if two points have at least two coordinates equal, they cannot be of different sets. Equivalent to: the relation \"belongs to same set if they share at least two coordinates\"? Not exactly.\n\nBut consider constructing X as set of points where x > y and x > z perhaps? Need convex.\n\nBehave like a region near one vertex vs opposite vertex.\n\nPotentially the maximal volume is 1/2? Can we have each volume 1/2? If they are convex and disjoint in projections perhaps not.\n\nWe need to find supremum V such that there exist two convex subsets each volume V meeting condition. Likely something like 1/3 perhaps.\n\nLet's attempt to think about constraints via integration. Consider projection onto xy-plane: The area of projection of X onto xy-plane plus area of projection of Y onto xy-plane cannot exceed 1 (since they are disjoint subsets of unit square). Because they are subsets within unit cube, projection area at most 1; disjoint so sum ≤1.\n\nSimilarly for other planes.\n\nLet A_xy = area of proj_{xy}(X), B_xy = area of proj_{xy}(Y). Then A_xy + B_xy ≤ 1.\n\nBut volume V of X can be expressed as integral over its projection of height (z-range). Since convex, but general bound: V ≤ A_xy * 1 (since cube height is 1). So V ≤ A_xy. Actually the height at each (x,y) is at most 1 (z between 0 and1). So volume ≤ area of projection onto xy. So V ≤ A_xy and similarly V ≤ A_xz and V ≤ A_yz.\n\nThus V ≤ min of three projection areas of X; similarly for Y.\n\nSince we have V bounded by each of its own projection areas, and the sum of the two projection areas for each coordinate plane ≤1, we get 2V ≤ 1 for each plane? Not exactly because V may be less than each projection area but we can bound.\n\nWe have V ≤ A_xy, V ≤ A_xz, V ≤ A_yz. Similarly for Y: V ≤ B_xy, V ≤ B_xz, V ≤ B_yz. And A_xy + B_xy ≤1, etc.\n\nThus we have V ≤ min(A_xy, A_xz, A_yz). To maximize V, we would try to make projection areas as large as possible but still allow both bodies.\n\nSince sum ≤1, best we can have is A_xy = B_xy = 1/2 (if both equal). But then also need each V ≤ 1/2. However also need consistency across three planes.\n\nTherefore V ≤ 1/2.\n\nBut can we achieve V = 1/2? Need convex sets each of volume 1/2 with projection area 1/2 onto each plane, and projections disjoint.\n\nIs there a construction? Perhaps take X = set of points where x + y + z ≤ 1 (a tetrahedron) scaled? Its volume is 1/6th. Not large.\n\nWe need volume 1/2, which is large, so perhaps each set is half cube separated by a plane, but then projections would overlap: Suppose X = {x ≤ 1/2}, Y = {x ≥ 1/2}. Projections onto yz-plane would be whole unit square for both, overlapping, not allowed.\n\nThus need more clever shape.\n\nMaybe the optimal V is 1/3? Let's test constraints more tightly.\n\nConsider that each projection area must be at least V. So sum of both projection areas for each plane is at least 2V. But also must be ≤1. Hence 2V ≤1 => V ≤ 1/2. That's the only bound we have.\n\nBut perhaps there are more constraints because the projection areas of X onto different planes are related (by the body's shape). For convex body within unit cube, there is inequality like \\(\\text{Vol}(X)^{2/3} \\le \\text{area projection onto xy} \\cdot \\text{area proj onto xz} /? Not.\n\nActually Loomis-Whitney inequality: For a measurable set S in R^3, volume satisfies \\(\\text{Vol}(S)^{2} \\le \\text{area}_{xy}(S) \\cdot \\text{area}_{xz}(S) \\cdot \\text{area}_{yz}(S)\\). The inequality states \\( |S|^{d-1} \\le \\prod_{i=1}^{d} |P_i(S)|\\) where P_i are projections onto coordinate hyperplanes (of dimension d-1). For d=3, \\( \\text{Vol}(S)^{2} \\le A_{xy} A_{xz} A_{yz}\\).\n\nThus for each set, V^2 ≤ A_xy A_xz A_yz.\n\nGiven that each A's at most 1, have V^2 ≤ product ≤ (max each). But we also have each A ≥ V.\n\nThus V ≤ 1 obviously.\n\nNow we have additional constraints that for each plane, A_xy + B_xy ≤ 1, etc.\n\nWe need to maximize V such that there exist numbers a1,a2,a3 for X and b1,b2,b3 for Y (the three projection areas) satisfying:\n\n(1) V^2 ≤ a1 a2 a3 (Loomis-Whitney for X)\n(2) V^2 ≤ b1 b2 b3 (for Y)\n(3) a_i + b_i ≤ 1 for i=1,2,3.\n(4) 0 ≤ a_i,b_i ≤1.\n\nGoal to maximize V possible.\n\nSince we can adjust shape, perhaps we can achieve equality of Loomis-Whitney: for convex sets, can we achieve equality? For boxes (axis-aligned rectangular prisms), area projections equal side lengths times 1 etc; volume equals product of side lengths. For a rectangular box, projection areas onto xy etc are product of two side lengths, so V = l_x l_y l_z. Then a1 = l_x l_y, a2 = l_x l_z, a3 = l_y l_z. Then product a1 a2 a3 = (l_x l_y)(l_x l_z)(l_y l_z) = (l_x l_y l_z)^2 = V^2. Hence equality holds for boxes.\n\nThus we can restrict to axis-aligned boxes (rectangular prisms) inside unit cube, convex. Then we need two boxes disjoint in each projection.\n\nBoxes defined by intervals: X = [0,a]×[0,b]×[0,c] maybe anchored at origin; Y could be elsewhere.\n\nBut projections onto xy plane: X projects to rectangle [0,a]×[0,b]; Y's projection must be disjoint in xy-plane. So Y's projection rectangle must be placed in unit square not overlapping. Since both boxes axis-aligned, to maximize volume maybe we place them anchored at opposite corners?\n\nLet X be box of dimensions a,b,c anchored at origin (0,0,0). So its projection onto xy is rectangle of size a × b. Similarly onto xz is a×c, yz b×c.\n\nLet Y be box of dimensions a',b',c' anchored at opposite corner (1-a',1-b',1-c') maybe. Its projection onto xy is rectangle [1-a',1]×[1-b',1] of size a'×b'.\n\nDisjointness: these rectangles must not overlap: so need either a + a' ≤ 1 or b + b' ≤ 1 (or both) to keep them separated. Actually for disjoint rectangles, they must be separated along x or y direction. Since they are in opposite corners, we require a + a' ≤ 1 and b + b' ≤ 1 to guarantee disjointness; otherwise they'd overlap.\n\nSimilarly for other planes: require a + a' ≤1 (for xz), and c + c' ≤1 ; and for yz require b + b' ≤1 and c + c' ≤1.\n\nThus conditions: a + a' ≤1, b + b' ≤1, c + c' ≤1. So each dimension sums ≤1.\n\nVolumes: V = abc, V = a'b'c' (equal). Want maximize abc given constraints a + a' ≤1 etc and a,b,c, a',b',c' ∈ [0,1]. Also equality of volumes.\n\nSince we can choose a',b',c' = 1 - a, 1 - b, 1 - c (largest possible while meeting ≤), because to maximize abc we can set a' = 1-a, etc. Then volume equality gives abc = (1-a)(1-b)(1-c).\n\nThus we need to solve abc = (1-a)(1-b)(1-c) with a,b,c in [0,1]. Maximize V = abc.\n\nLet f(a)=a/(1-a). Then condition gives f(a) f(b) f(c) =1. So product of ratios =1.\n\nLet’s set a = b = c by symmetry for max volume. Then a/(1-a) cubed =1 => a/(1-a) = 1 => a=1/2. So a=b=c=1/2. Then a' = 1/2 also. Volume V = (1/2)^3 = 1/8.\n\nThus we achieve V = 1/8 with two opposite corner boxes of size 0.5 each. Check projection disjoint? Boxes are [0,0.5]^3 and [0.5,1]^3. Their xy projections are [0,0.5]^2 and [0.5,1]^2, disjoint (touch at edge? they share boundary at x=0.5? The borders intersect at line x=0.5 (if closed). The problem says disjoint sets (projections of X and Y onto that plane are disjoint). Since they are closed convex subsets, their projections are closed rectangles; if they touch at edge, that's intersection (nonempty). So we need strict disjointness. We could shrink a little: make a = 0.5 - ε, a' = 0.5 - ε (still sum <1) then volume less than 1/8. So supremum could be 1/8 maybe.\n\nBut is there possibility to exceed 1/8 with non-rectangular shapes? Perhaps can get larger V up to 1/2? Need to explore constraints beyond dimension sums.\n\nWe can consider using shape like a half-cube slab that occupies full length in x but limited in y and z varying. Let's consider building X and Y as \"slanted\" shapes such that projections onto each plane are disjoint but each projection area can be up to 1 (they just need to be disjoint between X and Y, not covering whole plane). Could we have each projection area = 1? No, because two disjoint subsets of unit square cannot both have area 1 (sum would exceed 1). So each projection area ≤1, but they could have area close to 1 while still being disjoint? No—they must be subsets of unit square, disjoint, sum of areas ≤1. So each projection area at most 1, but cannot both be near 1. So each projection area ≤1, and we need each V ≤ each projection area.\n\nThus V cannot exceed max possible projection area for one set, which is ≤1. Not helpful.\n\nBut could we have volume >1/8 by making projection areas for X large (close to 1) but Y's small? Since V same for both, Y's volume must be same as X's, so Y's projection areas also must be at least V, thus need to be also large. So both need each projection area at least V. Since sum of areas ≤1, we have V + V ≤1 => V ≤ 1/2. That bound is weaker than 1/8.\n\nBut 1/2 may be achievable maybe not because geometry constraints linking the three projections simultaneously.\n\nLet's consider theory: For any set S within unit cube, we have inequality between its three projection areas and its volume: Loomis-Whitney: V^2 ≤ a1 a2 a3. Since each a_i ≤ 1 - b_i (since sum ≤1). Since b_i ≥ V (as V ≤ each projection area of Y). So a_i ≤ 1 - V. So we can bound V^2 ≤ (1 - V)^3. Because each a_i ≤1 - V (since a_i + b_i ≤1, b_i ≥ V). Actually we only know a_i ≤ 1 - b_i, and b_i ≥ V. Therefore a_i ≤ 1 - V. So product ≤ (1 - V)^3.\n\nThus V^2 ≤ (1 - V)^3. Solve V^2 ≤ (1 - V)^3 for V ∈ [0,1]. Let's solve equality to find max V.\n\nLet f(V) = (1 - V)^3 - V^2 ≥0.\n\nSet (1 - V)^3 = V^2.\n\nTake cube root? Not simple. Let's solve.\n\nLet x = V. Then (1 - x)^3 = x^2.\n\nTake cubic root: 1 - x = x^{2/3}. So x + x^{2/3} = 1.\n\nLet t = x^{1/3} (so x = t^3). Then equation: t^3 + t^{2} = 1. Multiply by? Write t^2 (t+1) =1. So t^2 (t+1) = 1.\n\nWe need t>0, less than 1. Solve numerically: try t=0.5 => 0.25*(1.5)=0.375<1. t=0.7 => 0.49*1.7=0.833<1. t=0.8 => 0.64*1.8=1.152 >1. So t between 0.7 and 0.8.\n\nt=0.75 => 0.5625*1.75=0.984 <1. t=0.76 => 0.5776*1.76=1.016... approximate. So t≈0.755. Compute: t=0.755 => t^2=0.570... ; t+1=1.755; product=0.570*1.755=1.001. So close. So t≈0.7549.\n\nThus x = t^3 ≈ 0.7549^3. Compute: 0.7549^2 ≈0.5699; times 0.7549 ≈0.430. So V≈0.43.\n\nThus bound V ≤ ~0.43 from inequality.\n\nNow we need to see if construction can approach this bound. Possibly supremum is the solution to V = (1-V)^{3/2}. Wait earlier derived V^2 ≤ (1-V)^3 => V ≤ (1-V)^{3/2}. This bound might be sharp using boxes? Let's test with a = b = c = p, and a' = 1-p. But need equality of volumes: p^3 = (1-p)^3 implying p = 1/2 gives V=1/8, not large. So box construction gave far less.\n\nMaybe we can have shapes where a_i are close to 1 - V while still satisfying volume equality.\n\nWe can attempt to maximize V given constraints: a_i + b_i ≤1, V^2 ≤ a1 a2 a3, V^2 ≤ b1 b2 b3, and a_i,b_i ≥ V.\n\nGiven symmetry, optimum likely have a_i = b_i = 1 - V? Wait a_i + b_i = 1 (tight). If we set a_i = b_i = 1/2, then V ≤ (1/2)^{3/2} = 1/(2√2)≈0.3535. But need also V ≤ a_i: V ≤ 0.5. So bound 0.3535. But we can maybe take a_i larger than b_i like a_i = 1 - V, b_i = V (minimum). Then product a1 a2 a3 = (1 - V)^3, gives same bound as before. So limiting case: one set uses almost whole projection area, other minimal just enough to have volume V. Since both have same volume, both need at least V projection area each inevitably. So minimal projection area for Y is V, thus sum constraint gives a_i ≤ 1 - V and similarly b_i = V (or could be larger). So product for X is ≤ (1 - V)^3, for Y product = V^3 (if each projection area = V). Then need V^2 ≤ V^3 => V≥1? Not possible. Actually we need V^2 ≤ product for Y i.e., V^2 ≤ b1 b2 b3. Since each b_i ≥ V, product ≥ V^3, so condition holds automatically (since V^2 ≤ V^3 for V ≤1). So the restrictive inequality is V^2 ≤ (1 - V)^3, which is same as earlier.\n\nThus theoretical maximum V is solution of equality: V = (1 - V)^{3/2}. Solve: V^{2/3} + V^{1/3} = 1 (which earlier gave t). Equivalent to t^2 (t+1) =1 where t = V^{1/3}. So solution t is root of cubic t^3 + t^2 -1=0.\n\nThus V = t^3 where t solves t^3 + t^2 -1 =0.\n\nFind exact expression? That cubic maybe has a rational root? test t=1: 1+1-1=1 ≠0. t= (sqrt{5}-1)/2 ≈0.618, compute 0.236+0.382-1=-0.382, not zero. So not simple fraction.\n\nThus supremum may be algebraic, not rational? But problem expects rational m/n. So perhaps our bound not tight, and actual supremum rational maybe 1/8 (m=1,n=8 => m+n=9). That's simple.\n\nGiven contest problem (likely AoPS) expects rational answer.\n\nThus maybe supremum is 1/8.\n\nWe need to verify that any volume >1/8 impossible.\n\nNeed stronger bound than earlier. Perhaps using Brunn-Minkowski or other.\n\nConsider each point in X and Y must differ in at least two coordinates. This implies that the \"shadow\" of X onto any 2D line (like coordinate axis?) No.\n\nWe can try to bound total volume of X + Y = 2V. Since for any point (x,y) in projection onto xy-plane, at most one of X or Y can have a point with those (x,y). Then consider integrating the heights: define f_X(x,y) = length of intersection of X with vertical line at (x,y) (i.e., measure in z). Similarly f_Y. For each (x,y), at most one of the two can be positive because otherwise you would have both points sharing (x,y) coordinates (different z). But they could have same (x,y) but different z? Wait condition states projections onto xy-plane are disjoint, meaning there is no (x,y) such that there exists point in X with those coordinates and also point in Y with same (x,y). So indeed cannot share (x,y). So for each (x,y), at most one of f_X, f_Y is non-zero. So sum f_X+f_Y ≤ 1 (since max possible length in z is 1). Then integrate over unit square:\n\n∬ (f_X + f_Y) dx dy = Vol(X) + Vol(Y) = 2V ≤ ∫∫ 1 dx dy = 1. Thus 2V ≤ 1 ⇒ V ≤ 1/2. Wait this just reproduces earlier bound, not 1/8.\n\nBut also similar for other planes gives same bound.\n\nNeed combine constraints from three axes perhaps to get stronger.\n\nConsider sum over all three planes? For each point in cube, think of contributions to three integrals maybe overlapping leading to stronger bound.\n\nLet’s define for each point (x,y,z), there are three lines parallel to axes. Perhaps a combinatorial inequality: For each point, at most one of the three \"pairwise coordinate matches\" can happen across X and Y? Not.\n\nAlternatively use inclusion-exclusion: Since any two points from X and Y must differ in at least two coordinates, maybe we can map each point in X to a unique region in the unit cube that cannot contain any point from Y, resulting in covering volume proportion.\n\nMaybe consider partition of cube into 8 octants based on whether each coordinate is ≤ 0.5 or ≥0.5. The condition that two points differ in at least two coordinates suggests they cannot be in opposite octants? Not.\n\nPotential guess: optimum uses partition of cube into 2 sets where each is convex and occupy opposite corners, giving volume 1/8 each. Could we increase by using shapes that are like prisms that stretch across whole length in one dimension but limited in other two?\n\nLet's test constructing X as full length in x (0 to1), but limited in y and z small such that projections onto yz-plane area = small, meanwhile projections onto xy and xz large. However Y would need disjoint projections onto xy and xz meaning cannot share same x-y rectangle etc.\n\nIf X uses full x range, its projection onto yz-plane is rectangle [0,1] × [0, ...]? Actually projection onto yz-plane discards x; So if X spans full x, its projection onto yz is just the projection of its cross-section in y,z which could be large.\n\nBut Y must have projection onto yz disjoint, so Y's yz projection cannot overlap. So Y must be placed at different y,z region. That may be possible.\n\nPotentially we can set X = [0,1] × [0,a] × [0,b] (a,b small). Volume V = a b. Similarly Y = [0,1] × [1-a,1] × [1-b,1] maybe but needs convex? That would be also a slab.\n\nCheck projections:\n\n- xy projection of X: [0,1]×[0,a] — area a.\n- xy projection of Y: [0,1]×[1-a,1] — area a, disjoint if a + a ≤1? Actually they are separated along y, sum 2a ≤1 -> a ≤0.5.\n\n- xz projection: X: [0,1]×[0,b] area b. Y: [0,1]×[1-b,1] area b, need 2b ≤1 => b ≤0.5.\n\n- yz projection: X: [0,a]×[0,b] area ab. Y: [1-a,1]×[1-b,1] area ab, need 2ab ≤1 => ab ≤0.5.\n\nVolume each = a b. We want maximize V = ab with constraints a ≤ 0.5, b ≤0.5, and ab ≤ 0.5 (redundant). So max V = (0.5)*(0.5)=0.25. This gives V = 1/4 > 1/8.\n\nBut need convex and closed; yes slabs are convex. However check condition about projections onto XY: they are disjoint (since intervals [0,a] and [1-a,1] are disjoint for a<0.5). Similarly for xz and yz. So seems possible V up to 1/4.\n\nBut we must ensure that Y fits inside unit cube: [0,1] in x fine, y from 1-a to1 etc. That's okay.\n\nThus we have a construction with V=1/4 (choose a=b=1/2). Wait a=0.5, b=0.5 leads to Y's y interval [0.5,1]? Actually 1-a = 0.5, so intervals touch at 0.5, making projections intersect at boundary line y=0.5. Need disjoint: need strict inequality a<0.5 . So supremum could approach 0.25 but not achieve. So supremum could be 1/4.\n\nIs there any further constraint like convex sets must have interior? That's fine.\n\nThus we have V arbitrarily close to 1/4. Could we exceed 1/4? Let's analyze bound.\n\nFrom earlier, pick a,b up to 0.5 due to disjointness in xy and xz. So V=ab ≤ (1/2)^2 = 1/4 . Can't exceed because a,b each at most 0.5 from those constraints (since for xy projection each width ≤0.5). So V ≤ 1/4.\n\nThus supremum is 1/4.\n\nFormal proof: Let X project onto xy rectangle of area A_xy. Since projection of Y onto xy must be disjoint, total area ≤1, so A_xy ≤ 1 - A'_xy where A'_xy ≥ V (since V ≤ each projection area). Thus A_xy ≤ 1 - V. But also we have V ≤ A_xy? Not necessary if projection area could be larger than V; we need bound on V via dimensions.\n\nHowever to get V ≤ 1/4, we need more elaborate argument. But our construction demonstrates that supremum at least 1/4. Could it be larger? Suppose X has projection onto xy area α, yz area β, xz area γ. Its volume V ≤ min(α,β,γ). So V ≤ α and α + α' ≤1. Similarly V ≤ β and V ≤ γ.\n\nThus V ≤ min(α,β,γ) and α ≤ 1 - V, β ≤ 1 - V, γ ≤ 1 - V (because other's projection at least V). So V ≤ 1 - V ⇒ V ≤ 1/2 again.\n\nTo improve, need relation among α,β,γ. Using Loomis-Whitney: V^2 ≤ α β γ. Since each ≤ 1 - V, we get V^2 ≤ (1 - V)^3 giving bound ~0.43 bigger than 0.25. So not restrictive enough.\n\nBut maybe we have also inequality relating sum of projection areas? For convex body inside unit cube, I suspect inequality α+β+γ ≤ something? For a convex body of volume >1/4 maybe impossible given disjointness condition.\n\nObserve our construction uses bodies that are \"prisms\" with full extent in x direction (length 1). Could there be bodies with larger volume using similar idea? Suppose we let X extend full in x and also in y partially, but need disjointness for projections onto xy, which only cares about rectangle area. So max area for X's xy projection is 1/2 (so width in y at most 0.5). Similarly xz projection width in z at most 0.5. Thus its volume V ≤ (max length in x)*(max width y)*(max width z) = 1 * 0.5 * 0.5 = 0.25. Given convex shape could have more volume by not being product, but still max volume limited by product of extents in each dimension (by Brunn-Minkowski? Actually convex body's volume ≤ product of its extents along axes? For any set, volume ≤ product of side lengths of bounding box (range of coordinates). Indeed volume ≤ (max x - min x)*(max y-min y)*(max z-min z). For X inside unit cube, those extents are ≤1. But also from projection constraints, we have that range in y and z each ≤0.5 because projection onto xy and xz must be disjoint from Y, which also needs at least some range. But does the condition force each set's range in y <=0.5? Suppose Y's projection onto xy occupies area somewhere else, maybe covering some y range but not necessarily limiting X's entire y range to 0.5; they could be separated along x direction instead. If we separate along x then X could occupy full y range.\n\nBut we need disjointness in all three projections simultaneously, so you need separation in each pair of coordinates. That forces each coordinate pair ranges to be separated: i.e., for XY, the rectangles of X and Y must be separated either in x or y direction. But could be separated differently for each plane. For instance, they could be separated along x for XY, along y for XZ, along z for YZ. That could allow each set to have large extents.\n\nWe need a set of three separations forming maybe a cyclic arrangement.\n\nLet's attempt to design larger volume using such scheme: Let X be region where x small, y small, z any; Y where x large, y large, z any? Their xy projection: X uses small x and y both small; Y uses large x and y both large. That's separated in both x and y, okay. XZ projection (x small, z any) and YZ projection (x large, z any) are separated along x, fine. YZ projection (y large, z any) vs XZ (y small?) Wait yz projection of X uses small y and z any; Y uses large y and z any; separated along y. So each projection separates along coordinate according to which coordinate is small vs large. This suggests X could have x ≤ a, y ≤ b, any z. So X = [0,a]×[0,b]×[0,1]; volume = a b. Y = [1-a,1]×[1-b,1]×[0,1]; volume same = a b. This is similar to previous slab but with full z length, gives V = a b, constraints a + a ≤1 => a ≤0.5, similarly b ≤0.5 => V ≤ 0.25. So same.\n\nCould we get larger by not using full extent in each dimension? Might combine extents differently: For XY projection we separate along x (X having small x, Y large x) but allow both to have full y range. Then X's y range can be 1, thus volume may increase.\n\nLet's try: X: x in [0,a], y in [0,1], z in [0,b]. Y: x in [1-a,1], y in [0,1], z in [1-b,1]. Check projections:\n\n- XY: X projects to rectangle [0,a]×[0,1]; Y to [1-a,1]×[0,1]; disjoint if 2a ≤1 => a ≤0.5.\n\n- XZ: X projects to [0,a]×[0,b]; Y projects to [1-a,1]×[1-b,1]; need disjoint; they are separated both in x and z, okay if a ≤0.5 and b ≤0.5.\n\n- YZ: X projects to [0,1]×[0,b]; Y projects to [0,1]×[1-b,1]; disjoint if 2b ≤1 => b ≤0.5.\n\nThus volume V = a * 1 * b = a b ≤ 0.25 again.\n\nThus maximum appears 1/4.\n\nCould we achieve larger by using non-rectangular convex shape that uses more area in each projection even though extents limited? Projection area bound still product of extents? For convex body, projection area onto xy is at most (extent in x)*(extent in y). Indeed area ≤ width_x * width_y. Thus if width_x ≤0.5, width_y could be 1, giving area ≤0.5*1 =0.5. So XY area could be up to 0.5.\n\nOur volume V is at most product of extents in all three dimensions (width_x * width_y * width_z). If we allow width_x ≤0.5, width_y ≤1, width_z ≤0.5, then max volume = 0.5*1*0.5=0.25. That's still 1/4.\n\nThus whichever coordinate pair we limit to 0.5, the third also limited similarly, leading product ≤0.25.\n\nThus supremum likely 1/4.\n\nNeed to argue cannot exceed 1/4.\n\nProof outline: By pigeonhole principle, in the three coordinate planes, at least one direction (x, y, or z) must be the separating direction for at least two of the three projections. That implies that for that coordinate, the ranges of X and Y are disjoint and each ≤0.5, limiting volume.\n\nFormally, for each projection, the two rectangles are disjoint. In each projection, the separation is either along first coordinate or second. For XY, separation either along x or y. For XZ, separation along x or z. For YZ, separation along y or z. By pigeonhole, some coordinate (say x) is used as separating direction in at least two of the three projections. Therefore the ranges of X and Y in that coordinate are disjoint and each length ≤0.5. Hence for that coordinate, width ≤0.5 for each set. The other two coordinates each have extents at most 1 (since within cube). Therefore volume ≤ 0.5*1*1 =0.5??? Wait need both other coordinates also limited? Actually we have two other coordinates may each be up to 1, giving bound 0.5. Not enough.\n\nBut we need both other coordinates also limited to 0.5 perhaps using similar reasoning with other coordinate being separating in another pair.\n\nBetter: Since each coordinate appears as separating direction in exactly two of the three projections (x appears in XY and XZ), if x is separating direction in both, then its extent for each set ≤0.5. So each coordinate could be limited in at most one set? Need ensure that for each set, at least two coordinates are limited to ≤0.5? Wait X separating from Y in projection XY could be via x, meaning X's x interval and Y's x interval are disjoint, with union length ≤1. So each ≤0.5. If the separation in XY is via x, then X's x-range ≤0.5. Similarly if separation in XZ is also via x, again same bound. So X's x-range ≤0.5. For Y, also ≤0.5. For other coordinates, may not be bounded.\n\nThus worst case each set may have only one coordinate bounded to 0.5, volume up to 0.5. So need stronger.\n\nBut note each set must be separated from the other in all three projections. So for each pair of coordinates, at least one of those coordinates must separate them. This is reminiscent of graph coloring: each edge (projection) labeled by coordinate used for separation.\n\nNow can we have configuration where X has width 0.5 only in x, Y has width 0.5 only in y, and both have full extent in remaining coordinates, resulting volume 0.5? Example: Use separation along x for XY, along y for XZ, along z for YZ. Let's test:\n\n- For XY: separate by x => X has x∈[0,0.5], Y has x∈[0.5,1].\n- For XZ: separate by y => X has y∈[0,0.5], Y has y∈[0.5,1].\n- For YZ: separate by z => X has z∈[0,0.5], Y has z∈[0.5,1].\n\nNow X extents: x≤0.5, y≤0.5, z≤0.5 => volume ≤0.125. Similarly Y: each ≤0.5 => also 0.125. That's 1/8. That's lower.\n\nThus configuration leads to 1/8.\n\nIf we try to make X have large extent in two coordinates, we need separation in projections accordingly. To have X width 1 in y, need for projection XY separation be along x, not y. For projection XZ separation also along x (so both use x). Then x limited to 0.5, y unrestricted, z maybe limited? For YZ projection must be separated via y or z. If we separate via y, then Y's y limited 0.5, X's y maybe full. But that would make Y volume limited by two coordinates? Let's compute:\n\nTake separations: XY via x, XZ via x, YZ via y.\n\nThus:\n\n- X: x ≤0.5 (disjoint with Y's x), no restriction on y, but YZ separation via y implies Y's y ≤0.5 and X's y unrestricted? However YZ projection rectangles are [y range of X] cross [z range of X] vs Y ranges. If separation is via y, that means y intervals are disjoint; thus X's y ≤0.5 as well (since Y's y ≤0.5). Wait they are disjoint intervals within [0,1]; each ≤0.5. So X's y also ≤0.5. So X has both x≤0.5 and y≤0.5, leaving z possibly full 1, volume ≤0.25. So again 1/4.\n\nThus any labeling leads to at most two coordinates limited to 0.5 for each set, giving volume ≤0.25.\n\nTherefore supremum = 1/4.\n\nHence m=1, n=4 => m+n =5.\n\nCheck rational format: 1/4 -> m+n =5.\n\nThus answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 5", "answer": 5, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -5.245197257863765e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999947548164981, "logp_correct": -5.245197257863765e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6220688673954285, "cot_lp_min64": -1.5683482120354135, "frac_below_-5": 0.011939945620049651, "digit_lp_min": -4.141367435455322, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.010393422096967697, "n_cot_tokens": 8459, "lp_end": -0.04154587537050247, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.099436753473128e-06, "logp_nocot": -2.300051664933555, "p_nocot": 0.1002536639901109, "uplift": 2.3000464197362973}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "a9c58011-337c-404d-91a6-708b90bd8b9c", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Consider a graph $G$ with 2019 nodes. Define \"inverting\" a vertex $v$ as follows: for every other vertex $u$, if there was an edge between $v$ and $u$, it is deleted; if there wasn't, it is added. We aim to minimize the number of edges in the graph through several inverting operations (we can invert the same vertex multiple times). Determine the smallest number $M$ such that we can always reduce the number of edges in the graph to be no greater than $M$, regardless of the initial configuration of $G$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Consider a graph $G$ with 2019 nodes. Define \"inverting\" a vertex $v$ as follows: for every other vertex $u$, if there was an edge between $v$ and $u$, it is deleted; if there wasn't, it is added. We aim to minimize the number of edges in the graph through several inverting operations (we can invert the same vertex multiple times). Determine the smallest number $M$ such that we can always reduce the number of edges in the graph to be no greater than $M$, regardless of the initial configuration of $G$.", "true_answer": 1018081, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "a9c58011-337c-404d-91a6-708b90bd8b9c:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 679, 261, 7465, 402, 297, 28, 667, 24, 38360, 11, 9279, 9506, 25, 21800, 22569, 20934, 316, 261, 16180, 25363, 323, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 24966, 177015, 2870, 323, 326, 722, 1273, 38360, 741, 197203, 316, 392, 41694, 289, 1, 540, 25363, 323, 350, 32038, 5542, 472, 1550, 26928, 45236, 30, 50557, 1550, 26928, 45236, 169694, 22569, 2870, 323, 326, 722, 5036, 741, 1328, 9506, 2226, 625, 7902, 22569, 6200, 1273, 38360, 350, 25035, 2617, 20934, 316, 323, 741, 2632, 581, 665, 45605