4787, 6052, 364, 7943, 1309, 316, 413, 3239, 625, 1277, 656, 220, 16, 5192, 316, 113181, 364, 16577, 3605, 1934, 24174, 6160, 395, 658, 2170, 483, 297, 14210, 25, 25151, 261, 314, 8350, 2406, 14, 17, 25987, 1353, 24, 11, 287, 28, 7959, 15, 30, 50557, 297, 28, 667, 24, 14210, 13, 10093, 261, 72162, 382, 8350, 2406, 61, 17, 14, 19, 8, 314, 220, 1353, 24, 9, 7959, 15, 314, 220, 16, 11, 32069, 11, 31445, 350, 21910, 1888, 328, 1920, 54912, 484, 2171, 656, 220, 16, 741, 41351, 8350, 2406, 61, 17, 14, 19, 25987, 1353, 24, 9, 7959, 15, 28, 16, 11, 32069, 11, 31445, 364, 84787, 22569, 6, 1349, 314, 478, 533, 261, 72162, 314, 220, 17, 11, 41227, 11, 19603, 533, 220, 16, 11, 32069, 11, 31445, 314, 220, 16, 11, 25749, 11, 37037, 364, 84787, 581, 665, 16218, 540, 1645, 220, 16, 11, 25749, 11, 37037, 22569, 1934, 24174, 57537, 395, 1062, 7465, 13, 9939, 484, 1354, 13031, 7465, 1919, 6284, 810, 4895, 484, 350, 26232, 7465, 741, 2632, 391, 314, 220, 16, 11, 25749, 11, 37037, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 13], "n_tokens": 4335, "words": 2498, "band_lo": 1249.0, "band_hi": 4996.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have a graph on n=2019 vertices, allowed operation: complement edges incident to a chosen vertex v (i.e., toggle adjacency between v and all other vertices). Equivalent to \"switching\" at vertex v (also known as Seidel switching? Actually Seidel switching flips edges between v and all others). This operation does not affect edges among other vertices (except those incident to v). So we can repeatedly choose vertices and toggle their adjacency edges.\n\nGoal: For any initial graph, we can perform sequence of such operations to achieve a graph with at most M edges, where M is minimal such that guarantee exists. Need worst-case minimal achievable edge count.\n\nObservation: The operation corresponds to complement of the star centered at v (i.e., toggle its incident edges). Note that applying inversion twice on same vertex returns to original.\n\nWe can think of labeling vertices with 0/1 indicating whether we've inverted them (mod 2). After sequence, each vertex may have been inverted an odd number of times; overall effect is same as some set S of vertices inverted exactly once (mod 2). Because operations commute? Inversions at different vertices interactions? Let's calculate resulting adjacency: For two vertices u and v, originally edge status e_{uv} (1 if edge). After toggles: If we invert u (i.e., toggle edges incident to u) we toggle e_{uv}. If we invert v, we toggle e_{uv} again. So final status = e_{uv} XOR (I_u XOR I_v) where I_x = 1 if vertex x inverted odd times.\n\nThus after choosing set S as vertices inverted, final adjacency matrix (excluding loops) A'_{uv}= A_{uv} XOR (1_{u∈S} XOR 1_{v∈S}) . This is equivalent to performing a \"switch\" with respect to S: It complements edges between S and its complement, leaving edges inside S and inside complement unchanged.\n\nThus the set of reachable graphs from a given G is exactly the switching class: all graphs obtained by toggling edges between S and its complement.\n\nGoal: For any initial G, choose S to minimize number of edges in resulting graph.\n\nThus we need to find minimal possible edge count among graphs in switching class of G. Then M is the maximum over all initial G of that minimal edge count. That is, worst-case over G of optimal reduction.\n\nSo we need to compute: For any graph G on n vertices, define f(G)= min_{S⊆V} e(G_S) where G_S is switching of G with respect to S (i.e., edges between S and V\\S complemented). Determine max over G of f(G). Then M = that maximum.\n\nInterpretation: We can choose partition (S, V\\S). Number of edges after switching: edges inside S (unchanged) + edges inside complement (unchanged) + non-edges between S and complement (since they become edges). Let a = |S|, b = n-a.\n\nLet e_in(S) = edges inside S; e_in(T) = edges inside T = V\\S. Let e_between = edges across cut (S,T). Then after switching, edges across become (a*b - e_between). So total edges after switching: e_in(S)+ e_in(T) + (a*b - e_between) = total edges inside both parts plus complement of cut.\n\nOriginal total edges = e_in(S)+ e_in(T) + e_between = m (original).\n\nThus after switching, edges = m - e_between + a*b - e_between = m + a*b - 2 e_between.\n\nSo function of S: edges' = m + a*b - 2 e_between(S). where e_between(S) is number of edges crossing cut (S,T). So minimizing edges' is equivalent to maximizing (2 e_between - a*b); i.e., maximize (2 e_between - a*b). Since m constant.\n\nThus f(G)= m + min_{S}(a*b - 2 e_between) = m - max_{S}(2 e_between - a*b).\n\nDefine cut value: For each partition S, define cut size e_between(S). Want to maximize (2 e_between - a*b). Note that a*b is total possible edges across that cut. So 2 e_between - a*b = e_between - (a*b - e_between) = e_between - nonedges_between.\n\nThus maximizing difference between number of edges and non-edges across cut; i.e., maximize imbalance.\n\nEquivalently, treat each unordered pair across cut contributes +1 if edge, -1 if non-edge. Sum over all across pairs of (+1 for edge, -1 for non-edge)= 2 e_between - a*b.\n\nGoal: find S that maximizes this sum.\n\nObservation: This is reminiscent of maximizing signed cut: assign each vertex label +1 (in S) or -1 (in T), then for each unordered pair (i,j) the contribution weight w_{ij}=+1 if edge exists else -1. Then sum over pairs with opposite signs: sum_{i<j} w_{ij} * (1 - s_i s_j)/2? Actually (1 - s_i s_j)/2 is 1 if opposite signs, 0 if same. So total sum = ½ ∑ w_{ij} (1 - s_i s_j) = ½ ( ∑ w_{ij} - ∑ w_{ij} s_i s_j). Since ∑ w_{ij} is constant ( edges - nonedges). But edges+ nonedges = N = n choose 2. Let total weight W = (#edges)*(+1) + (#nonedges)*(-1) = m - (N - m) = 2m - N.\n\nThus the sum across cut = ½ (W - ∑ w_{ij} s_i s_j). So maximizing across-cut sum is equivalent to minimizing ∑ w_{ij} s_i s_j.\n\nBut maybe easier to think combinatorially.\n\nWe are looking for worst-case G where even after optimal switch we cannot reduce edges below some bound.\n\nWe want M = max_G f(G). Plug formula: f(G)= m - max_S (2 e_between - a*b). Since m can be as high as N = n choose 2. However the expression may reduce m substantially.\n\nWe can also see that after optimal switch, edge count becomes at most ??? maybe floor(N/2)? Might be bound.\n\nLet's consider the possibility that we can always make edges at most floor(N/2). Let's test small n.\n\nFor n=2: single edge possible. Operation toggles that edge if we invert one vertex => edge flips. So we can achieve either 0 or1 edges; M minimal guaranteeing ≤? worst-case initial graph? Suppose start with edge, can invert to zero edges. So we can always achieve ≤0? Not always: start with no edge, we cannot increase to zero? Actually we can also invert to get edge, but we want ≤M. Since we can always achieve 0 edges (by either leaving or inverting whichever reduces?), For empty graph, edge count already 0. So M=0 for n=2? But formula maybe half of N=1? Not zero. So need check.\n\nFor n=3: N=3 edges. Can we always reduce to at most 1 edge? Let's brute think. Suppose complete graph K3 (3 edges). Choose S size 1: a=1,b=2, a*b=2, e_between = edges from that vertex to others =2. Then after switch edges' = m + a*b -2 e_between =3+2-4=1. So we can get to 1. So M≥? worst-case maybe graph with 2 edges (a path). Let's test: G a path of length2 (edges: 1-2,2-3). m=2. Choose S maybe vertex2? a=1,b=2, e_between = edges from 2 to others =2. Then edges' =2+2-4=0. So we can get 0. So worst case might be K3 giving 1. So M=1 = floor(N/2)=1. Good.\n\nFor n=4: N=6 edges. Can we always get ≤3? Let's test worst-case maybe complete graph K4 has m=6. Choose S of size 2: a=2,b=2 a*b=4. e_between = 4 (since complete bipartite between halves). Then edges' =6+4-8=2. So can get to 2 edges. Maybe worse graph leads to higher minimal edges? Let's test empty graph m=0 gives 0. Maybe some graph leads to minimal edges 3? Not sure. Let's try graph with half edges, say a 4-cycle (edges 4). m=4. Need compute best S to minimize edges'. Try S size 2 perhaps opposite vertices. For 4-cycle, vertices 1-2-3-4-1. Choose S={1,3}. Then a*b=4. e_between edges across cut are edges (1-2),(1-4),(3-2),(3-4)=4 edges actually each vertex connected to both of opposite side? In cycle, 1 adjacent to 2 and4 (both in other side), 3 adjacent to 2 and4. That's 4 edges. So e_between=4 => edges' =4+4-8=0. So can get 0. So maybe worst-case is complete graph giving 2 edges. So M maybe floor(N/4)? For n=4, floor(6/3?) Not.\n\nLet's compute formula for complete graph K_n: m = N.\n\nFor K_n, e_between for any S is a*b (since all possible edges). Then edges' = N + a*b - 2 a*b = N - a*b. Minimum over S of N - a*b = N - max_{a} a*(n-a). Max product occurs at a = floor(n/2) or ceil. So min edges' = N - floor(n^2/4). Since N = n(n-1)/2. So minimal edges reachable from K_n is N - floor(n^2/4). Compute for n odd 2019: floor(n^2/4) = floor(2019^2/4). 2019^2 = (2000+19)^2=4,000,000 + 2*2000*19=76,000 + 19^2=361 => 4,076,361. Divide by4 = 1,019,090.25 so floor =1,019,090.\n\nThus N = n(n-1)/2 = 2019*2018/2 = 2019*1009 =? 2000*1009=2,018,000; 19*1009=19,171; sum=2,037,171. So N=2,037,171.\n\nThus minimal edges after optimum switching for K_n = 2,037,171 - 1,019,090 = 1,018,081.\n\nThus for complete graph, we can get down to about half of N (since floor n^2/4 ≈ N/2). Indeed N ≈ n^2/2, floor n^2/4 ≈ N/2. So minimal edges ≈ N/2.\n\nNow is there any graph G for which the minimal achievable edges is larger than this value? We need worst-case max_G f(G). Since K_n gives f(K_n)=N - floor(n^2/4). Could there be graph that is \"more balanced\" meaning switching cannot reduce edges as much? Let's consider complement of K_n (empty graph) gives f=0. So K_n might be worst.\n\nBut need verify that for any G, we can achieve edges ≤ N - floor(n^2/4). That would give M = N - floor(n^2/4).\n\nWe need to show: For any graph G on n vertices, there exists S such that edges' ≤ N - floor(n^2/4). Equivalent to existence of cut with (2 e_between - a*b) ≥ floor(n^2/4) - (N - m)?? Maybe easier: Want edges' = m + a*b - 2 e_between ≤ N - floor(n^2/4). Rearranged: 2 e_between - a*b ≥ m - (N - floor(n^2/4)) = m - N + floor(n^2/4) = (m - N) + floor(n^2/4) = -(N - m) + floor(n^2/4) = floor(n^2/4) - (N - m). Since N - m = number of non-edges.\n\nThus need cut where excess edges over nonedges across cut ≥ floor(n^2/4) - (#nonedges). Since RHS could be negative; we can always meet if RHS ≤ max possible value which is a*b (if all edges). However we need existence for all G.\n\nAlternative approach: For any labeling S, the cut imbalance B = 2 e_between - a*b = (edges) - (nonedges) across cut. This value lies between -a*b and +a*b. The maximum possible over choices S is at least something due to averaging argument (discrepancy). Use probabilistic method: random S each vertex independently with prob 1/2. Then e_between expected = m/2? Not exactly.\n\nLet each unordered pair contributes +1 if edge else -1 if non-edge. Randomly assign each vertex to side uniformly independently. Probability that pair is separated is 1/2. Expected sum across cut = (1/2) * Σ w_{ij} = (1/2) * (2m - N) = m - N/2.\n\nThus there exists S with sum at least m - N/2. So max_{S} B ≥ m - N/2.\n\nThus we can guarantee edges' = m + a*b - 2 e_between = m - B ≤ m - (m - N/2) = N/2.\n\nBut N/2 may be larger than earlier bound N - floor(n^2/4). Let's compute N/2 vs N - floor(n^2/4). Since floor n^2/4 approx n^2/4, N = n(n-1)/2 ≈ n^2/2 - n/2. N - n^2/4 ≈ n^2/4 - n/2 = N/2 - n/2. So N - floor(n^2/4) ≈ N/2 - O(n). So smaller. So our random argument gives guarantee ≤ N/2, which is looser.\n\nWe need tighter bound. Maybe worst-case is K_n, giving edges' = N - floor(n^2/4) which is about N/2. Could there be graph where the best cut imbalance is less than floor(n^2/4) - (N - m) which would make minimal edges larger? Let's examine extremes.\n\nDefine nonedges = N - m = t. Want to bound max_B from below in terms of t. Because B = 2 e_between - a*b = (a*b) - 2 (nonedges between)?? Not helpful.\n\nNote that across cut, total possible pairs a*b = e_between + non_between. So B = e_between - non_between = a*b - 2 non_between.\n\nThus maximizing B equivalent to minimizing non_between (nonedges across cut). Since non_between ≥ 0, maximum B achievable is a*b when we can find cut with no nonedges across (i.e., cut is a complete bipartite subgraph). In a graph, the maximum a*b we can achieve corresponds to the size of a complete bipartite subgraph (K_{a,b}) as a subgraph.\n\nThus max_B = max_{S} (a*b - 2 non_between) = max_{S} (a*b) - 2* min_{S} non_between.\n\nBut maybe we can use Turán-type results to guarantee existence of large bipartite subgraph? For any graph, there is a bipartite subgraph with at least N/2 edges (by taking random partition). That's standard: any graph contains bipartite subgraph with at least half of edges.\n\nHere we need: there exists a cut with at most t_nonedges_between minimal; but nonedges across cut is complement of edges across cut inside bipartite subgraph.\n\nLet edges across cut = e_between, total pairs across cut = a*b. So edges in bipartite subgraph formed by cut = e_between.\n\nWe know there exists cut with e_between ≥ m/2 (expected). So maximum e_between ≥ m/2. So B = 2 e_between - a*b ≥ m - a*b/?? Not helpful.\n\nConsider worst-case for minimizing B: maybe regular graph with half edges random. Might produce B close to 0. Then edges' ≈ m (can't reduce). But we can also choose complement set? Since you can also invert opposite side? Actually S vs complement produce same B with opposite sign? No, B depends on S; but B for S and its complement are same (since a*b same, e_between same). So can't flip sign.\n\nThus worst-case G might be one where all cuts have B small, leading to limited reduction. Need to compute worst-case over G of min_{S} edges'. That equals m - max_S B.\n\nSince m can be up to N. So worst-case likely when m large, because then you start with many edges and reduction depends on cut edge count.\n\nIf m small, you already have few edges, so min edges' small.\n\nThus worst-case may be K_n (or near). Let's check if any other graph could give larger minimal edges than K_n. Suppose graph is complement of K_n, i.e., empty: f=0, much smaller.\n\nSuppose graph is balanced complete bipartite K_{⌊n/2⌋,⌈n/2⌉}. For that graph, maybe after optimal switch you cannot reduce edges much. Let's test n=4, K_{2,2} has edges 4. Compute min edges' via formula: choose S maybe one side of bipartition? Take S equal to one side (size 2). Then a=2,b=2,a*b=4, e_between = edges across cut =4 (since all edges are across). Then edges' = m +4-8 =0. So can reduce to 0. So not worst.\n\nThus K_n seems worst.\n\nHence M = N - floor(n^2/4) for n odd 2019.\n\nCompute exact value:\n\nn=2019.\n\nN = n(n-1)/2 = 2019*2018/2.\n\nLet's compute precisely: 2018/2 = 1009. So N = 2019*1009.\n\nCompute 2000*1009=2,018,000; 19*1009 = 19*1000 +19*9 =19,000 +171 =19,171. Sum = 2,037,171. So N=2,037,171.\n\nNow floor(n^2/4). n^2 = 4,076,361. Divide by 4: 1,019,090.25 => floor = 1,019,090.\n\nThus M = 2,037,171 - 1,019,090 = 1,018,081.\n\nCheck arithmetic: 2,037,171 - 1,019,090 = 1,018,081 correct.\n\nThus answer.\n\nBut need to be sure not off by 1 due to parity.\n\nEdge count after optimal switch for K_n with n odd: Choose a = floor(n/2)=1009, b=1010? Actually n=2019 odd. Max a*b is floor(n^2/4) = 1009*1010 = 1,019,090 (since product of two integers that differ by 1). Indeed floor(n^2/4)=1009*1010=1,019,090.\n\nThus edges' min = N - a*b = 2,037,171 - 1,019,090 = 1,018,081.\n\nThus we can guarantee at most 1,018,081 edges after optimal switches for any graph. Show that there exists graph where cannot go below that (complete graph). So M = 1,018,081.\n\nThus final answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 1018081", "answer": 1018081, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.7683711557056085e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999995231629981, "logp_correct": -4.7683711557056085e-07, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5223437014584009, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2380909746956767, "frac_below_-5": 0.008566797869877286, "digit_lp_min": -3.5297505855560303, "answer_first_mention_lp": null, "n_cot_tokens": 4319, "lp_end": -1.3589766240329482e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.4066597032069694e-05, "logp_nocot": -19.68139338120818, "p_nocot": 2.8345195608273e-09, "uplift": 19.681392904371066}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "bfee799b-646e-4bee-843d-6908a0435b82", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: We say that a $2023$-tuple of nonnegative integers $(a_1,\\ldots,a_{2023})$ is \\textit{sweet} if the following conditions hold:\n\n- $a_1+a_2+\\ldots+a_{2023}=2023$\n- $\\frac{a_1}{2}+\\frac{a_2}{2^2}+\\ldots+\\frac{a_{2023}}{2^{2023}}\\le 1$\n\nDetermine the greatest positive integer $L$ so that $a_1+2a_2+\\ldots+2023a_{2023}\\ge L$ holds for every sweet $2023$-tuple $(a_1,\\ldots,a_{2023})$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: We say that a $2023$-tuple of nonnegative integers $(a_1,\\ldots,a_{2023})$ is \\textit{sweet} if the following conditions hold:\n\n- $a_1+a_2+\\ldots+a_{2023}=2023$\n- $\\frac{a_1}{2}+\\frac{a_2}{2^2}+\\ldots+\\frac{a_{2023}}{2^{2023}}\\le 1$\n\nDetermine the greatest positive integer $L$ so that $a_1+2a_2+\\ldots+2023a_{2023}\\ge L$ holds for every sweet $2023$-tuple $(a_1,\\ldots,a_{2023})$.", "true_answer": 22228, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "bfee799b-646e-4bee-843d-6908a0435b82:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 41776, 336, 314, 4215, 575, 261, 10853, 4335, 31060, 25, 4215, 261, 10853, 314, 297, 314, 1323, 18, 11, 326, 70288, 4215, 486, 314, 4215, 261, 10853, 820, 220, 17, 61, 72, 72554, 220, 16, 13, 27042, 19957, 4149, 336, 1072, 2893, 46069, 16336, 261, 10853, 52718, 31060, 13, 7801, 451, 314, 8350, 328, 484, 11085, 30, 50557, 581, 1309, 16983, 16336, 451, 2238, 484, 336, 5064, 451, 395, 722, 12418, 112464, 26, 575, 2560, 4213, 451, 314, 11085, 336, 5251, 722, 12418, 112464, 350, 21910, 16336, 11, 290, 10574, 16336, 3760, 1572, 503, 12629, 316, 1349, 741, 50557, 1309, 451, 2238, 484, 336, 5064, 451, 3324, 1343, 11, 326, 451, 65414, 13, 2632, 451, 314, 11085, 4149, 336, 350, 21910, 336, 16