 28, 15, 13, 48897, 36354, 20, 9707, 11, 3609, 6049, 220, 16, 11, 3605, 813, 4150, 220, 1494, 10, 1179, 15, 28, 1323, 19, 63152, 478, 656, 16, 13, 2632, 581, 1309, 10389, 3605, 656, 220, 16, 12951, 656, 2360, 1236, 7266, 575, 483, 3760, 6049, 777, 7670, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 220, 16, 14, 24928, 21, 8, 316, 3357, 6049, 2684, 889, 3605, 3760, 13, 54839, 1001, 575, 28, 994, 7670, 350, 33457, 220, 994, 8, 483, 1001, 575, 28, 899, 7670, 350, 33457, 220, 899, 8, 6049, 3343, 25, 6049, 70943, 656, 220, 16, 14, 14397, 23, 533, 220, 16, 14, 24928, 21, 314, 220, 16, 14, 24928, 21, 350, 15, 13, 1302, 25291, 741, 2514, 3357, 6049, 540, 220, 16, 11, 581, 1309, 316, 1147, 3543, 1203, 328, 6049, 220, 16, 14, 24928, 21, 11, 10112, 3613, 575, 28, 899, 30, 50557, 39866, 38566, 6049, 11, 1309, 316, 1147, 6049, 25, 581, 2023, 1147, 3613, 575, 28, 899, 350, 172749, 6049, 220, 16, 14, 24928, 21, 936, 2776, 6049, 35239, 2684, 889, 3605, 21073, 656, 220, 16, 350, 21910, 581, 5768, 5018, 2169, 741, 50557, 316, 3357, 3609, 3605, 220, 1323, 18, 581, 1309, 316, 1604, 483, 220, 1494, 10, 1179, 15, 28, 1323, 19, 11, 1309, 10389, 3605, 656, 16, 2049, 13958, 6049, 220, 16, 13, 21050, 328, 220, 1179, 15, 328, 1490, 994, 11, 581, 2023, 1199, 220, 3204, 24, 328, 1490, 994, 326, 1236, 14083, 328, 7266, 6009, 316, 87064, 6049, 326, 3605, 364, 58369, 8704, 25, 261, 62, 702, 28, 1494, 11, 261, 62, 994, 28, 3204, 24, 11, 10954, 6049, 25, 220, 1494, 14, 7672, 19, 659, 3204, 24, 14, 14397, 23, 314, 350, 1494, 9, 17, 659, 3204, 24, 11876, 14397, 23, 314, 350, 3519, 10, 3204, 24, 11876, 14397, 23, 314, 14397, 22, 14, 14397, 23, 13, 19792, 6623, 6049, 220, 16, 14, 14397, 23, 13, 30456, 1147, 1001, 261, 62, 899, 28, 17, 30, 12265, 2454, 261, 62, 899, 62274, 220, 16, 14, 24928, 21, 13, 13311, 328, 1373, 3644, 220, 17, 14, 24928, 21, 314, 16, 14, 14397, 23, 11, 1147, 3605, 220, 17, 13, 7801, 3609, 3605, 314, 1494, 10, 3204, 24, 10, 17, 28, 1323, 20, 1424, 1323, 18, 13, 19792, 10389, 1920, 32995, 30, 4037, 35737, 364, 84787, 1062, 75420, 591, 290, 24174, 1920, 100248, 12545, 6960, 21073, 3097, 2236, 481, 13284, 12424, 4732, 483, 945, 14818, 4732, 316, 10266, 6049, 364, 198140, 19957, 336, 314, 16427, 2029, 364, 84787, 451, 314, 220, 16427, 2029, 364, 2167, 1757, 6757, 484, 336, 382, 22476, 16336, 350, 278, 382, 741, 2632, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 6162, "words": 3428, "band_lo": 1758.5, "band_hi": 7034.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to minimize S = sum i a_i given constraints: sum a_i = n =2023, and weighted sum W = sum a_i / 2^i ≤ 1. Want minimal possible S over nonnegative integer a_i satisfying constraints. Then L = floor of that minimum? Actually we need greatest integer L such that S >= L for all sweet tuples; i.e., L = minimum S across all sweet tuples (since integer, the largest integer less than or equal to min). Actually need L such that S >= L always true, and L maximal. So L = minimum possible S (since S integer). So compute min of S subject to constraints.\n\nWe have integer variables, but perhaps we can treat as linear programming; extreme points will be integer? Since constraints are integer total sum and weighted sum rational.\n\nGoal: minimize sum_i i a_i given sum a_i = N and sum a_i/2^i ≤ 1.\n\nObservation: Larger i have larger coefficient in S but smaller contribution to weighted sum (since denominator 2^i). To minimize S while meeting sum a_i = N, we'd want to allocate as many a_i as possible to small i (since they contribute less to S) but they also contribute more to weighted sum, limited by ≤1. So we need to allocate numbers to meet sum a_i = N with weighted sum constraint. This is like we have capacity of weighted sum 1; each unit assigned to a_i consumes 1/2^i capacity and adds i to S. Want to achieve total N units (a_i sum) while minimizing total cost i with capacity constraint.\n\nThus we need to choose multiset of N items each with weight w_i = 1/2^i and cost c_i = i. Unlimited supply of each type (i from 1..2023). Must total weight ≤1. Minimize total cost.\n\nThis is a knapSack/coin/water-filling: For minimal cost we would like items with smallest cost per unit weight? Actually cost per weight = i / (1/2^i) = i*2^i. Larger i gives huge cost per weight, so we want low i. So we fill with smallest i as much as possible while respecting weight limit.\n\nThus we first try to allocate a_1 as big as possible, then a_2, etc, until we meet total count N but can't exceed weight 1.\n\nLet’s compute how many units of type i can we assign before exceeding weight.\n\nWeight contributed by a_i = a_i / 2^i. Total weight ≤1.\n\nIf we set a_1 = x, weight = x/2 ≤1 => x ≤2. Since a_i must be integer, max a_1 =2.\n\nTake a_1=2 uses weight 1; then no weight left for others, and total count a_1=2 <2023, not enough. So we can't exceed weight 1, but need total count 2023, far larger. So we cannot satisfy sum a_i=2023 with weight ≤1 using only small i? Indeed because each unit consumes at least 1/2^{2023} weight if we go to i=2023, the total weight for 2023 units minimum achievable is when we put all units on highest i where weight per unit is smallest: weight per unit = 1/2^{2023}. Then total weight = 2023 / 2^{2023} which is far less than 1. So the weight constraint is not the limiting factor? Wait we have inequality ≤1, not equality; we need weight ≤1, not must use all weight. So we can have many units with very small weight each, total weight still ≤1, and sum a_i =2023 satisfied. The real challenge: we need sum a_i =2023, weight constraint is not restrictive because we can allocate many items at high i with tiny weight. However the cost S would be high if we use high i. We want to minimize S by using small i but cannot exceed weight 1. So weight constraint is restrictive: we can only have limited number of low-i items due to weight cap, rest must be high i.\n\nThus we need to allocate as many low i items as allowed by weight ≤1 while still reaching total count 2023, then fill remaining count with highest possible i to keep cost low? Actually high i gives higher cost, we want to minimize cost, so after using low i items up to weight capacity, we need to fill the rest with as small i as possible that still fits weight capacity left. Since after using max allowable low i, we may have remaining weight budget to allocate some higher i.\n\nGoal: minimize total cost, we should allocate items in increasing i order until we reach total count N, but must respect weight budget: total weight cannot exceed 1.\n\nThus we should solve: Choose integers a_i≥0 to minimize sum i a_i subject to sum a_i = N and sum a_i/2^i ≤1.\n\nThis is linear programming; optimum will have at most one fractional variable if we consider relaxation; but we need integer.\n\nBecause weights 1/2^i decreasing geometrically, we can think of using greedy: allocate as many as possible to smallest i until weight budget runs out; then maybe partially allocate to next i.\n\nLet's compute capacity for each i: maximum a_i allowed given remaining weight.\n\nLet’s attempt: Start with i=1, weight per unit = 1/2. Max a_1 = floor(1/(1/2)) =2. Use a_1=2, weight used =1. Then no weight left for others, impossible to reach total count N=2023. So we cannot give weight fully; we need to keep some weight capacity for many other items (since we need many items). So we need to limit a_1 to less than 2 to save weight for many high i items.\n\nThus we need to distribute weight budget across many items. Since each high i uses tiny weight, we can afford many items there.\n\nGoal: minimize sum i*a_i, we want many items at low i, but each consumes relatively large weight, limiting count. There is tradeoff.\n\nThink: We need to achieve N items. Let’s denote total weight w = sum a_i/2^i ≤1. For a given weight w, the minimal possible S given N items? Actually given we must have N items, and total weight w, we want to minimize S: allocate weight to low i items to reduce cost, because cost per weight ratio is low for small i? Let's compute cost per weight = i / (1/2^i) = i*2^i, which grows with i, so lower i have much lower cost per weight. So to minimize S for given weight w, we allocate weight to smallest i possible. Hence the optimal will use weight as much as needed to pack low i items, but we also have requirement of N items, which may force using weight less than 1? Actually we can have weight less than 1. Since weight constraint is ≤1, we can choose any weight up to 1, not forced to use all. So we could try to achieve N items while using minimal weight (so we can allocate many low i items). Wait low i items consume more weight per item, so to have many items we would need more weight; but we have upper bound on weight, so we cannot have too many low i items. We must have total weight at most 1, so the maximum number of items we can allocate using i up to some k is limited.\n\nThus to minimize cost, we allocate as many items as possible to low i while respecting weight ≤1, then the remaining items must be placed at higher i (which have higher cost but low weight). So we need to find distribution that yields N items total.\n\nLet’s formalize: Let’s choose a cutoff K such that we allocate a_i = maximum possible for i ≤ K, and the rest for i = K+1 perhaps fractional.\n\nMaximum number of items we can allocate using i ≤ K given weight limit 1 is: we can set a_i = floor(2^i * (remaining weight)). But to maximize count we would allocate weight to lowest i? Actually to maximize count given weight limit, we would allocate to highest i (lowest weight). But we want minimize cost, which is opposite.\n\nThus we need to solve optimization: min Σ i a_i subject to Σ a_i = N, Σ a_i/2^i ≤1.\n\nConsider Lagrangian: minimize Σ i a_i + λ( Σ a_i/2^i -1) + μ(Σ a_i - N). For optimal, the marginal cost per unit a_i is i + λ/2^i + μ =0 for used i (except boundaries). Since i increases, and 1/2^i decreases, there will be a threshold where i + λ/2^i + μ =0. Since λ≥0? Actually constraint ≤1, so λ ≥0 for optimal (since if sum weight <1, λ=0). But if weight not tight, we could drop λ to 0 earlier, but then the condition reduces to i + μ = 0 for all used i, impossible since i vary. So weight constraint must be tight at optimum; else we could reduce cost by moving weight from higher i to lower i without violating weight. So we expect Σ a_i/2^i = 1 at optimum.\n\nThus λ>0, with equality.\n\nThus we have i + λ/2^i + μ = 0 when a_i >0. So we can rewrite μ = -i - λ/2^i. For two indices i<j both with positive a, we have -i - λ/2^i = -j - λ/2^j ⇒ i + λ/2^i = j + λ/2^j. Define function f(i)= i + λ/2^i. We need f(i)=constant for all used i. Since f(i) is increasing? Let's examine: derivative continuous version: f'(i)=1 - λ ln 2 / 2^i. For large i, second term tiny, so f'(i)≈1>0. For small i, maybe negative if λ large. So f(i) may have a minimum and increasing after. There could be at most two i where equality holds; optimal solution might involve at most two consecutive indices with positive a_i (like classic linear programming with two constraints). So we can expect that optimal uses at most two different i values: some count of lower i (say k) and some count of higher i (maybe k+1). Because with constraints sum count and sum weight, it's a 2D linear system; extreme point uses at most 2 variables positive.\n\nThus we can assume optimal solution uses only two indices p<q with a_p, a_q >0, others 0. Then we have equations:\n\na_p + a_q = N (2023)\n\na_p/2^p + a_q/2^q = 1   (weight tight)\n\nWe also need integer solutions for a_p, a_q nonnegative.\n\nGoal: minimize S = p a_p + q a_q = p a_p + q (N - a_p) = q N + (p - q) a_p.\n\nSince p<q, (p - q) negative, so to minimize S we want a_p as large as possible. So we want to maximize a_p under the constraints (given p,q). So given p<q, the equations let us solve a_p.\n\nFrom weight equation: a_p = 2^p (1 - a_q/2^q). Using a_q = N - a_p.\n\nThus a_p/2^p + (N - a_p)/2^q = 1\n\n=> a_p (1/2^p - 1/2^q) + N/2^q = 1\n\n=> a_p = (1 - N/2^q) / (1/2^p - 1/2^q) = (1 - N/2^q) / ( (2^{q-p} -1)/2^q ) = (2^q (1 - N/2^q)) / (2^{q-p} -1) = (2^q - N)/ (2^{q-p} -1).\n\nThus a_p = (2^q - N)/(2^{q-p} -1). Need integer nonnegative ≤N.\n\nSimilarly a_q = N - a_p.\n\nWe need a_p integer >=0, similarly a_q >=0.\n\nWe also need p ≥1 and q ≤2023.\n\nWe want to maximize a_p (to minimize S). Since a_p expression depends on p,q. For fixed q, denominator decreases as p approaches q (since 2^{q-p} -1 smaller). So a_p larger when p close to q maybe. Let's examine.\n\nWe can try to find q such that 2^q >= N (2023) else numerator negative => a_p negative. So we need q s.t. 2^q > N (since weight 1). Indeed minimal q with 2^q >= N is q = 11 because 2^11=2048 >2023. q=10 gives 1024 <2023 not enough numerator positive. So q must be at least 11.\n\nThus we can take q=11 maybe yields solution with p <11.\n\nCompute for each p from 1 to 10, compute a_p.\n\nFirst compute 2^q =2048. N=2023. Numerator = 2048-2023=25.\n\nDenominator = 2^{q-p} -1 = 2^{11-p} -1.\n\nThus a_p = 25 / (2^{11-p} -1). Need integer. Let's test values:\n\np=10: denominator 2^{1}-1 =1 => a_10 =25. integer.\n\nThen a_q = a_11 = N - a_10 =2023-25=1998.\n\nCheck weight: a_10/2^{10}=25/1024≈0.02441, a_11/2^{11}=1998/2048≈0.97656 sum≈1.001? Let's compute precisely: 1998/2048 = (2048-50)/2048 = 1 - 50/2048 = 1 - 0.0244140625 = 0.9755859375. Add 25/1024 = 0.0244140625 gives exactly 1. So works.\n\nThus solution with p=10, q=11 yields S = p a_p + q a_q =10*25 + 11*1998 = 250 + 21978 = 22228.\n\nNow perhaps we can get lower S using other p,q combos? Since we maximize a_p we want denominator small, which occurs when p is close to q (i.e., p=10 gives denominator 1). That's maximal a_p =25. Could we get more a_p? Denominator 1 yields a_p = numerator = 25. That's max possible because numerator fixed. So a_10 =25 is max low-index count using only two indices with q=11.\n\nCould we get lower S by using q larger than 11? Then numerator = 2^q - N larger, but denominator also larger maybe, but a_p maybe larger? Let's compute.\n\nGeneral formula: a_p = (2^q - N)/(2^{q-p} -1). For given difference d = q-p (positive integer). Let’s write denominator = 2^{d} -1. Numerator = 2^q - N = 2^{p+d} - N.\n\nWe want to maximize a_p = (2^{p+d} - N)/(2^{d} -1). For fixed d, a_p increases with p because numerator scales with 2^{p+d}. However p = q - d. So p also grows as q grows. But we also have constraint that a_p ≤ N.\n\nOur target S = q N + (p - q) a_p = q N - d * a_p (since q-p = d). So S = q N - d * a_p.\n\nPlug a_p expression: S = q N - d * (2^{p+d} - N)/(2^{d} -1).\n\nSince q = p + d, we can express in terms of p,d:\n\nS = (p+d) N - d * (2^{p+d} - N)/(2^{d} -1).\n\nGoal minimize S. Could compute for small d maybe 1 yields best.\n\nWe already have d=1 (q=p+1). Then denominator 2^1 -1 =1, a_p = 2^{p+1} - N. With q = p+1. Need a_p >=0 => 2^{p+1} >= N. Smallest p such that 2^{p+1} >= N gives minimal p? Let's compute. Need 2^{p+1} >=2023. p+1 >=11 => p >=10. So p=10 yields a_10 = 2^{11} - 2023 =2048-2023=25. For p>10, numerator bigger (2^{p+1} larger) gives larger a_p, but also cost p larger maybe affect S.\n\nLet's compute S for p=11, q=12 (d=1). Then 2^{12}=4096. a_11 = 4096 - 2023 = 2073. Then a_q = N - a_p = 2023 - 2073 = negative -> impossible because a_p exceeds N. So need a_p ≤ N. So require 2^{p+1} - N ≤ N => 2^{p+1} ≤ 2N =4046. So p+1 ≤ log2 4046 ≈ 12. (2^{12}=4096 >4046). So p+1 ≤11? Actually 2^{12}=4096 >4046, so p+1 cannot be 12. So p+1 ≤11, giving p≤10. So only p=10 works for d=1.\n\nThus optimal with d=1 is p=10 q=11 giving S=22228.\n\nNow consider d=2: q = p+2. Denominator = 2^2 -1 =3. Numerator = 2^{p+2} - N.\n\nThen a_p = (2^{p+2} - N)/3. Need integer. Also a_p between 0 and N. Also a_q = N - a_p >0.\n\nWe need weight equation to hold (it will).\n\nFind p such that 2^{p+2} >= N (to make a_p nonnegative). So p+2 >=11 => p≥9.\n\nAlso must ensure numerator divisible by 3.\n\nCompute possibilities p=9,10, etc up to maybe 11? But q up to 2023.\n\nCalculate for p=9 (so q=11). Then numerator = 2^{11} - 2023 = 2048-2023=25. a_p = 25/3 not integer. Not allowed.\n\np=10 (q=12): numerator = 2^{12} -2023 =4096-2023=2073. Divide by 3: 2073/3=691. So a_10 = 691, a_12 = N - 691 = 1332. Both nonnegative integers.\n\nCheck weight: a_10/2^{10}=691/1024≈0.67578, a_12/2^{12}=1332/4096≈0.32520 sum≈1.0000? Compute exact: 691/1024 +1332/4096 = (691*4 +1332)/4096 = (2764+1332)/4096=4096/4096=1. Yes.\n\nCompute S = p a_p + q a_q =10*691 +12*1332 = 6910 + 15984 = 22894.\n\nHigher than 22228.\n\np=11 (q=13): numerator 2^{13}=8192 -2023=6169. Divide by 3 => 2056.333 not integer.\n\np=12 (q=14): numerator 16384 -2023 =14361. /3=4787 integer? 14361/3=4787 exactly. a_12=4787 > N (2023) not allowed. So discard.\n\nThus d=2 gives larger S.\n\nThus d=1 seems best so far.\n\nConsider d larger maybe result higher S because penalty d*a_p may be larger.\n\nGiven S = q N - d a_p. Since q = p+d, this is (p+d)N - d a_p = p N + d(N - a_p). Since a_p <= N, N - a_p = a_q >=0, so S >= p N. Since p is at least 1, lower bound S >= N =2023. But we have S ~22228 > that.\n\nBut we can perhaps get smaller S if we use more than two indices? However linear program optimal will be at extreme point with at most 2 positive variables (since 2 constraints). Since integer requirement could make need three, but we may still find integer solution with 2 variables as we did. That should give minimum; if no integer solution with 2 variables yields feasible, we might need 3, but we found a feasible integer solution with 2 variables (p=10,q=11). Since objective is linear, integer optimum may be same as LP optimum if LP optimum is integral. Need to verify LP optimum: Since constraints matrix is totally unimodular? The constraints are sum a_i = N and sum a_i/2^i =1 (maybe equality at optimum) with a_i integer; right-hand sides are integers (N) and 1 expressed as fraction; not integral; tot unimodular property may not guarantee integral. But we found integral solution; need to ensure no better (lower) S possibly using 3 indices with integer values.\n\nWe can attempt to see if any other combination could yield lower S than 22228.\n\nSuppose we use three indices p<r<q maybe, to achieve better S? The extra variable could allow more low-index count with proper weight distribution perhaps decreasing S.\n\nBut intuitively, due to convexity, mixing more types would increase cost because cost function per unit weight is convex? Let's examine: cost per weight ratio i*2^i, which is increasing with i exponentially. The marginal cost per added weight is increasing with i. So to achieve a given total weight (capacity 1) while meeting total item count N, the optimal distribution is to use the cheapest cost per weight items as much as possible, i.e., lower i, until weight limit or count requirement reached. However we also need to meet count N; cheaper per weight items also increase cost per item? Wait lower i have lower cost per weight (good) but also lower cost per item (i) compared to higher i. So both metrics favor low i, but low i consume more weight, limiting count.\n\nThus we can think of using a fractional mix of two consecutive i to exactly meet weight=1 and count N. That's typical water filling; using more than two types would be suboptimal because you could replace an intermediate type with combination of neighboring ones to achieve same weight and count with lower cost maybe.\n\nHence optimum should involve at most two consecutive indices.\n\nThus we can check all possible consecutive indices (p, p+1). For each p, solve a_p + a_{p+1} = N and a_p/2^p + a_{p+1}/2^{p+1}=1 => a_p = 2^{p+1} - N (as before). Need 0 ≤ a_p ≤ N integer. So requirement: 2^{p+1} - N ≥0 => p+1 ≥ ceil(log2 N) =11. So p≥10. Also a_{p+1}= N - a_p = N - (2^{p+1} - N)=2N - 2^{p+1}. Must be >=0 => 2^{p+1} ≤ 2N=4046 => p+1 ≤ floor(log2 4046) =11 (since 2^{12}=4096 >4046). So p+1 ≤11 => p ≤10. So only p=10 works. So unique consecutive pair solution is p=10,q=11.\n\nThus minimal S among any solution using two consecutive is S=22228.\n\nCould there be solution using non-consecutive pair that gives lower S? We computed d=2 gave larger S. Since d>1 leads to larger denominator thus smaller a_p, which reduces weight contribution of cheap items but then we must use higher q, which would increase S more. Likely S increases.\n\nWe can also test d=3 quickly: p=9 (q=12) maybe; compute a_p = (2^{12} - N)/(2^{3} -1) = (4096-2023)/7 =2073/7≈296.14 not integer. p=10,q=13: numerator 8192-2023=6169; denominator 2^{3}-1=7 => a_p=881.285 not integer. p=8,q=11: numerator 2048-2023=25; denominator 2^{3} -1=7 => not integer. So not integer; but even if integer would cause S larger.\n\nThus minimal integer S is 22228.\n\nWe should also verify that there is no solution with more than two positive variables achieving lower S. Let's attempt to see if we could have a_10=24, a_11=some, plus a higher index to adjust weight to exactly 1 while count N=2023. With a_10=24, weight contributed 24/1024=0.0234375, remaining weight needed = 1- that = 0.9765625 = 1998/2048? Actually full weight left is 1 - 24/1024 = (1024-24)/1024 =1000/1024=125/128 =0.9765625. To meet weight using higher i, we could allocate many at i=11 and maybe some at 12 to adjust count while preserving weight. Ideally using a_11 as many as possible yields weight per unit 1/2048 ~0.000488281. To get remaining weight we need a_11 such that a_11/2048 <= remaining weight. Compute max a_11 = floor(remaining weight * 2048) = floor(0.9765625*2048)= floor(2000) =2000? Actually 0.9765625*2048 = 2000 exactly? Since 125/128 * 2048 = 125*16 =2000. So a_11 max =2000, weight =2000/2048=0.9765625 exactly, total weight 1, count so far 24+2000=2024 exceeds N by1. So we need reduce count by 1 perhaps by using some higher i with less weight per unit (i.e., 1/4096) to keep weight same but count less. Replace one i=11 unit (cost 11) with one i=12 unit (cost 12) weight change: weight decreases by 1/2048 - 1/4096 = 1/4096 (0.000244). To keep weight at 1, we need to add something else of weight 1/4096, maybe another i=12? Actually replacing reduces weight, need to add weight: we could add another i=12 (adds weight 1/4096), net weight stays same but count increases by 1 (since we added extra item). Actually to keep total count 2023 we need to start with 24+2000=2024, need reduce count by1 while keeping weight 1. Instead of 2000 of type11, we could use 1999 of type11 and some combination of higher types to compensate weight and count.\n\nLet's attempt: a_10=24, a_11=1999, that's weight: 24/1024 +1999/2048 = (24*2 +1999)/2048 = (48+1999)/2048 =2047/2048. Need additional weight 1/2048. Could add one a_12=2? Since each a_12 contributes 1/4096. Two of them give 2/4096 =1/2048, add count 2. Then total count =24+1999+2=2025 >2023. Need reduce two elsewhere? Not straightforward.\n\nThus any deviation from the optimal two-variable distribution likely increases cost because you replace cheap items with more expensive items to adjust weight.\n\nHence minimal S =22228.\n\nThus L = 22228.\n\nWe should ensure that S is indeed integer (it is). So answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 22228", "answer": 22228, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -7.152555099310121e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999992847447459, "logp_correct": -7.152555099310121e-07, "cot_lp_mean": -0.58379888076798, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4443645777905445, "frac_below_-5": 0.009598177972994957, "digit_lp_min": -5.259156703948975, "answer_first_mention_lp": -3.814689989667386e-06, "n_cot_tokens": 6147, "lp_end": -1.1513755321502686, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.2186455882765586e-06, "logp_nocot": -19.764443454216234, "p_nocot": 2.608622704769224e-09, "uplift": 19.764442738960724}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "df84033e-0ba6-4f5f-b277-9f881d6cf3ef", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: There are $110$ guinea pigs for each of the $110$ species, arranged in a $110 \\times 110$ array. Find the maximum integer $n$ such that, no matter how the guinea pigs are arranged, we can always find a column or a row of $110$ guinea pigs containing at least $n$ different species.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: There are $110$ guinea pigs for each of the $110$ species, arranged in a $110 \\times 110$ array. Find the maximum integer $n$ such that, no matter how the guinea pigs are arranged, we can always find a column or a row of $110$ guinea pigs containing at least $n$ different species.", "true_answer": 11, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "df84033e-0ba6-4f5f-b277-9f881d6cf3ef:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 679, 220, 7920, 15361, 2454, 483, 220, 7920, 92461, 30, 24305, 9710, 25, 392, 5632, 553, 220, 7920, 184484, 92461, 395, 2454, 328, 290, 220, 7920, 15361, 11, 36276, 306, 261, 220, 7920, 87, 7920, 2644, 3692, 2632, 3609, 92461, 314, 220, 7920, 9, 7920, 314, 220, 14503, 504, 13, 11555, 15361, 14518, 9707, 220, 7920, 4238, 350, 21910, 220, 7920, 92461, 2454, 741, 2632, 581, 679, 261, 12248, 220, 7920, 13585, 11, 220, 7920, 15697, 11, 2454, 4206, 261, 24819, 328, 1236, 15361, 13, 1416, 665, 36281, 31157, 10581, 364, 2167, 1309, 290, 10562, 16336, 297, 2238, 484, 306, 1062, 21868, 11, 581, 665, 16218, 24140, 328, 261, 5225, 503, 6000, 15683, 540, 5153, 297, 16913, 15361, 364, 2500, 382, 261, 25307, 68745, 8771, 280, 4792, 69198, 316, 177804, 55043, 30540, 326, 54467, 497, 651, 67428, 364, 2167, 1309, 20486, 83130, 21868, 111215, 290, 10562, 16913, 15361, 306, 1062, 5225, 14, 9380, 13, 7801, 297, 314, 484, 11085, 10562, 659, 30