7, 23909, 455, 14, 1398, 28, 16576, 15, 6145, 364, 12, 220, 17, 10, 21, 25, 220, 23, 0, 27334, 17, 9, 21, 8, 314, 23909, 455, 14, 899, 28, 30447, 15, 6145, 364, 12, 220, 19, 10, 19, 25, 220, 23, 0, 27334, 19, 61, 17, 9, 17, 19406, 314, 23909, 455, 27334, 1125, 9, 17, 25987, 23909, 455, 14, 1398, 28, 16576, 15, 6145, 364, 12, 220, 23, 25, 220, 23, 199519, 23, 28, 33619, 15, 6145, 364, 17434, 25, 220, 11442, 10, 16576, 15, 28, 17537, 20, 26, 659, 30447, 15, 28, 39133, 20, 26, 659, 16576, 15, 28, 41289, 20, 26, 659, 33619, 15, 28, 7920, 1161, 6145, 364, 10620, 478, 314, 89097, 2935, 220, 17, 25, 220, 19, 10, 19, 350, 16576, 15, 8, 659, 220, 23, 350, 33619, 15, 8, 314, 31316, 15, 6145, 364, 84787, 418, 314, 220, 7920, 1161, 12, 31316, 15, 314, 39133, 20, 6145, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 220, 23723, 899, 364, 4383, 484, 3609, 52015, 174595, 314, 3609, 533, 23723, 899, 314, 220, 7959, 3062, 13, 41021, 23864, 52015, 1757, 413, 174595, 483, 860, 13213, 2438, 326, 860, 220, 17, 146397, 4917, 483, 2973, 220, 17, 146397, 326, 14210, 41478, 13, 6789, 1879, 25, 11965, 581, 46716, 174595, 483, 722, 41478, 5064, 18, 350, 1750, 220, 16, 11, 17, 8, 314, 220, 46584, 23, 13, 19792, 1217, 174595, 483, 540, 5153, 1001, 220, 17, 146397, 326, 540, 5153, 1001, 14210, 15839, 350, 18303, 5517, 5064, 18, 741, 6789, 314, 13570, 56734, 3609, 1227, 147657, 314, 220, 19149, 2546, 364, 13518, 147657, 12648, 1511, 3407, 16944, 734, 12, 1606, 1952, 41478, 350, 36, 8, 314, 7920, 1161, 198, 12, 540, 5153, 1001, 14210, 15839, 350, 46, 8, 314, 220, 19149, 2546, 533, 220, 7920, 1161, 314, 220, 22413, 23, 198, 53769, 532, 11, 1236, 679, 261, 220, 17, 146397, 11, 1236, 625, 13, 623, 8104, 2935, 220, 17, 146397, 553, 2617, 483, 14210, 41478, 889, 860, 220, 17, 3452, 119324, 26, 1023, 553, 4279, 60500, 306, 11965, 52015, 920, 220, 46584, 23, 30, 24305, 220, 46584, 23, 6664, 174595, 483, 860, 220, 16, 503, 220, 17, 41478, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 722, 41478, 5517, 5064, 18, 741, 4569, 6664, 2973, 14147, 14210, 41478, 326, 1952, 41478, 5064, 19, 350, 9995, 220, 19, 10, 19, 503, 220, 23, 741, 41351, 1039, 11965, 52015, 920, 6664, 174595, 328, 6009, 25, 350, 23, 936, 350, 20, 11, 18, 936, 350, 19, 11, 19, 741, 21926, 220, 33619, 15, 10, 29994, 23, 10, 16576, 15, 28, 46584, 23, 11, 20238, 364, 10620, 532, 6664, 174595, 483, 14210, 41478, 14836, 2932, 220, 17, 3452, 119324, 13, 21079, 1504, 625, 60500, 306, 220, 46584, 23, 13, 9184, 328, 532, 314, 220, 22413, 23, 364, 84787, 3609, 52015, 314, 220, 46584, 23, 350, 1750, 220, 17, 146397, 8, 659, 350, 8096, 328, 1227, 147657, 483, 540, 5153, 1001, 220, 17, 146397, 326, 540, 5153, 1001, 14210, 15839, 741, 1328, 28236, 314, 532, 533, 350, 18303, 41478, 2935, 220, 17, 146397, 741, 24305, 532, 4279, 6664, 2617, 483, 14210, 41478, 26, 1309, 316, 13574, 8104, 483, 220, 17, 146397, 364, 12845, 532, 17, 314, 1227, 147657, 483, 540, 5153, 1001, 14210, 15839, 326, 540, 5153, 1001, 220, 17, 146397, 364, 19371, 3609, 52015, 314, 220, 46584, 23, 659, 532, 17, 364, 10620, 3609, 52015, 581, 46716, 472, 220, 7959, 3062, 350, 21910, 220, 23909, 455, 12, 23723, 899, 741, 2632, 532, 17, 314, 220, 7959, 3062, 533, 220, 46584, 23, 314, 220, 12307, 15, 364, 84787, 532, 17, 314, 12307, 15, 13, 41021, 14866, 656, 43276, 8516, 10112, 364, 84787, 1039, 48742, 120152, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 25, 220, 23723, 899, 364, 4383, 94610, 25, 8702, 12243, 1424, 6375, 328, 174595, 30, 220, 23723, 899, 14, 23909, 455, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 3384, 13, 21926, 120152, 364, 84787, 10635, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 12482, "words": 8070, "band_lo": 3393.75, "band_hi": 13575.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need number of permutations of 8 elements that can be automorphism group of some tree (i.e., there exists some tree on vertices 1..8 for which that permutation is an automorphism). Equivalent: count permutations that preserve adjacency structure of some tree.\n\nEssentially we need to count permutations that can be realized as graph automorphisms of some tree on eight labeled vertices.\n\nAny permutation can be an automorphism of some graph (like a graph with all edges forming cycles). But with the restriction that the graph is a tree. Not all permutations can be automorphisms of a tree.\n\nWe need to characterize possible automorphism permutations of trees.\n\nObservation: Automorphism group of a tree is a subgroup of the symmetric group. The automorphism group can be any subgroup of S_n that is realized as automorphism group of some tree? Not all subgroups: e.g., a 3-cycle can't be automorphism of a tree? Let's consider. A tree is a bipartite graph (no odd cycles) but automorphisms can be any permutation of vertices that preserves adjacency.\n\nQuestion: For given permutation sigma, does there exist a tree T such that sigma is an automorphism of T? Equivalent: can we construct a tree whose automorphism group contains sigma (maybe additional automorphisms) but at least sigma.\n\nThus count permutations known as \"tree-automorphisms possible\".\n\nWe need to find condition on sigma.\n\nObservation: A tree has a unique center: either a vertex (center) or an edge (bicenter) (the set of vertices minimizing maximum distance). Any automorphism must map center(s) to themselves.\n\nCase 1: Tree with a central vertex c. Then the tree can be viewed as rooted at c. Automorphism must map c to c. Then sigma can be any permutation that fixes c and permutes subtrees rooted at c isomorphically. So sigma's cycle structure must be a product of permutations within each isomorphic branch.\n\nSimilarly for bicenter: central edge (u,v). The tree can be thought of as rooted at edge; automorphisms can swap the two sides possibly.\n\nThus permutation sigma must be such that its cycle structure can be realized as automorphism of rooted tree.\n\nThis seems similar to \"permutations that can be realized as automorphisms of a rooted tree.\" For a rooted tree of depth, automorphism group is a wreath product of symmetric groups corresponding to equal subtrees. The possible permutations are those that respect a hierarchical partition.\n\nThus any permutation can be realized? Let's test small n.\n\nTake n=3. Permutations of 3: identity (ok). Any transposition? Can we have a tree on 3 vertices where a transposition (swap two leaves) is automorphism? Tree on 3 vertices is a path of length2. Automorphism group swaps the two leaves (transposition). Yes.\n\n3-cycle? Can we have a tree where cyclic rotation of three vertices is automorphism? Tree of 3 vertices only path; its automorphism group has order2. So no 3-cycle. So not all permutations.\n\nn=4: possible permutations? Consider star K1,3: center 1, leaves 2,3,4. Automorphism group S3 permuting leaves => any permutation of leaves (including any 3-cycle) fixing center. So any permutation that fixes one vertex and permutes the other three arbitrarily. That includes 3-cycles and transpositions among leaves. Also identity. Additionally, path of length3: vertices 1-2-3-4. Automorphism group is order2 swapping ends (1↔4,2↔3). That's product of two transpositions (1 4)(2 3). So permutations like (1 2)(3 4) not realized? Actually (1 4)(2 3) realized.\n\nAlso there is tree with central edge? That's path of length3 which we just considered. So overall set for n=4 includes many permutations.\n\nWe need count for n=8.\n\nApproach: Enumerate all tree shapes (unlabeled) on 8 vertices, consider all possible automorphism permutations of labeled trees? But we can assign labels arbitrarily. We can choose a tree, then its automorphism group as a set of permutations of vertices (depending on labeling). Since labeling is fixed set V = {1..8}. For each permutation sigma, we ask if there exists at least one tree (some edge set) such that sigma is an automorphism. We can construct such tree tailor-made.\n\nThus we just need to know which permutations are realizable as automorphisms of some tree (some tree possibly arranged to accommodate sigma). So we can design tree based on sigma.\n\nConstruction strategy: Given sigma, can we always construct a tree where sigma is automorphism? Possibly not for permutations whose cycle structure is \"complex\". For example, a 4-cycle? Can we make a tree that rotates four vertices in a cycle while preserving edges? The tree has no cycles, but automorphism can rotate substructures.\n\nOne can realize a permutation of order k by arranging k isomorphic subtrees attached to a common central vertex (or edge) and then permuting them cyclically via rotation of the k subtrees (i.e., sigma acts as a k-cycle on the roots of those subtrees). For a tree, automorphism must map each vertex to a vertex of same degree and same position in structure.\n\nIf we have a central vertex c, and attach k subtrees T1,...,Tk that are pairwise isomorphic. Then there is an automorphism that cyclically permutes the subtrees, giving a k-cycle on the root vertices of each subtree (the vertices adjacent to c). However, to realize a pure k-cycle on those vertices, we need the subtrees to be identical as rooted trees. So a 4-cycle possible by having 4 identical subtrees.\n\nThus any permutation whose cycles can be arranged hierarchically where each cycle corresponds to permuting isomorphic components.\n\nThus condition: the permutation must be realizable as an automorphism of a rooted tree: can be built recursively where each orbit of sigma on vertices of same distance from root forms cycles of equal length and the induced subtrees are isomorphic.\n\nEssentially sigma must be a \"tree automorphism\", meaning its cycle decomposition respects a hierarchical partition where each vertex's children form cycles of equal length.\n\nWe can think of building a tree where sigma acts as given permutation. Starting from cycles of sigma: Suppose sigma has cycles lengths ℓ1,...,ℓm. We need to create a rooted tree where sigma maps each vertex to next in its cycle, preserving adjacency.\n\nIdea: Build a rooted tree whose underlying directed functional graph of sigma (as permutation) defines a cyclic structure; edges must be mapped accordingly.\n\nObserve: For a rooted tree (center vertex fixed), sigma must fix root. So any permutation with a fixed point can be realized? Not always; need that the rest can be partitioned into isomorphic subtrees attached to root. We can attach each orbit of sigma as a \"branch\" that is a cycle of vertices, but tree cannot have cycles. So the orbit must be arranged linearly maybe with extra leaves.\n\nImportant: In a tree, the image of an edge under sigma must be an edge. So adjacency relation must be invariant under sigma.\n\nSuppose sigma is a 4-cycle (a b c d). If we want tree where these vertices are arranged linearly with root fixed? They need to be in same structural position. Consider root r fixed. Attach to r a path of length 2 maybe? But the orbit of a vertex must map to another vertex with same degree and same subtree structure. If we have a leaf attached to r, sigma could map that leaf to another leaf attached to r. So each leaf orbit can be a cycle of length equal to number of leaves in that orbit. So cycles can be realized as permuting leaves attached to same parent. Similarly deeper nodes.\n\nThus any permutation can be realized by constructing a \"tree of orbits\": we can take the permutation's cycle decomposition, and for each cycle assign a depth level such that parent is fixed point, etc.\n\nWe need to ensure that the tree has no cycles: edges appear between parent and child. The sigma maps parent to parent (since must preserve tree), so parent must be fixed or belong to same orbit as its image (i.e., sigma maps parent to another vertex, which would imply that edge between a parent and child becomes edge between sigma(parent) and sigma(child)). For consistency, the parent-child relation must be sigma-equivariant: if v is parent of w, then sigma(v) is parent of sigma(w). This defines that the parent function is a homomorphism with sigma. That means the relation of being parent can be defined via some function p such that p(sigma(v)) = sigma(p(v)). So p commutes with sigma.\n\nThus sigma must act as automorphism of rooted tree where root fixed.\n\nThus we can define a rooted tree using sigma-orbits as levels such that sigma acts level-wise.\n\nConstruct as follows: Represent permutation sigma as product of cycles. Build a rooted tree where each orbit corresponds to a set of vertices at same depth and they are permuted cyclically. Choose a hierarchy tree of orbit representatives. Since sigma is a permutation, it is composed of cycles, we can make each cycle correspond to a set of leaves attached to a fixed parent (maybe also part of cycles). If parent is itself part of larger cycle, we need consistent.\n\nSimplify: Since we can also have center edge with swapping sides, we can realize permutations of even order 2 that swap root vertices.\n\nBut are there permutations impossible? Consider a 2-cycle (swap a and b) with no fixed points: we need a tree where automorphism swaps exactly a and b and fixes others. That's possible: take a tree where a and b are leaves attached to a common neighbor c which is fixed. Then swapping a and b is automorphism. So any transposition possible.\n\nWhat about a product of two disjoint transpositions (a b)(c d) with others fixed? Could realize by having two pairs of leaves attached to same fixed root, each pair swapped. That's fine.\n\nWhat about a 3-cycle without fixed points, e.g., (1 2 3) and others fixed? Need a tree where three vertices rotate. We can have a central fixed vertex r, and attach three leaves a,b,c that are permuted cyclically. The automorphism would rotate leaves, which is possible only if the root's structure treats those three leaves identically (they are all leaves). Indeed the tree has root r with degree 3; automorphism group includes S3 on those leaves. A 3-cycle is present. So yes.\n\nThus any permutation that fixes at least one vertex can be realized.\n\nWhat about a permutation with no fixed points, e.g., a single 8-cycle. Can we realize a tree where sigma is an 8-cycle? Need all vertices be in single orbit, no fixed vertex. Then tree must have no root fixed, so center must be an edge that is swapped via some rotation? But an edge automorphism could swap the two vertices, but an 8-cycle can't be generated by swapping. Could we have a tree whose automorphism group contains an 8-cycle but no fixed points? For a tree, automorphism acting without fixed point must be a “central edge” swapping sides? Actually any automorphism of a tree has either a fixed vertex or a fixed edge (center). Known theorem: Every automorphism of a tree fixes either a vertex or an edge. This is due to tree being a finite tree (no cycles). Indeed any automorphism has a fixed point or fixed edge (by considering the set of vertices minimizing distance). So if permutation has no fixed vertex, it must fix an edge (swap its endpoints possibly). For an automorphism that is an 8-cycle, there would be no fixed vertex; does it have a fixed edge? Possibly the automorphism could map edge (u,v) to itself with u→v, v→u. That still fixes the edge as an unordered set. So central edge with swap? But such automorphism would have order 2. Because swapping endpoints twice returns identity. For higher order, cannot have fixed edge unless order 2? Consider edge fixed as set, but maybe rotation of subtrees attached to edge? Suppose we have central edge with two symmetric subtrees on each side, and automorphism rotates those subtrees cyclically with swapping the two central vertices after half rotation? But the automorphism must map vertices on left side to vertices on right side after some steps. However the map must be graph isomorphism with itself. It's possible to have an automorphism of order >2 that swaps the two sides after some shift? In trees, is there an automorphism having no fixed vertex? The fixed edge case leads to an involution that exchanges the two vertices (if both vertices are mapped to each other) or possibly each vertex fixed individually? But if edge is fixed setwise, mapping could be identity on edge (both vertices fixed). That's fixed vertices. So only possibility without fixed vertex is swapping the two vertices of an edge, which is order 2.\n\nThus any automorphism of a tree must have a fixed vertex or a fixed edge with swapping of the two vertices (order 2). Therefore an automorphism of odd order >1 must have a fixed vertex. So permutations of odd order without fixed point cannot be tree automorphisms. In our case, a single 8-cycle (order 8) is even; could it be realized? It would have to have a fixed edge, leading to order 2 maybe times something? But order 8 would imply that after 4 applications, vertices would map back to themselves? Not possible because swapping edge vertices repeatedly just toggles. So order must be power of 2? Let's analyze.\n\nIf automorphism has no fixed vertex, there exists a unique edge e invariant setwise such that sigma swaps its endpoints. Then sigma^2 fixes both endpoints individually, thus sigma^2 has fixed vertices, and could be realized as earlier. So sigma can be of order 2k where sigma^2 has fixed vertex. Could we have order 4? Suppose sigma: swap u↔v (edge) and also rotate subtrees attached to them such that after two swaps, leaves return? For order 4, sigma^2 fixes u and v but may permute leaves. That's possible. Example: consider central edge u-v; attach identical subtrees A to u and B to v. Let sigma act: swap u with v and also map subtree A to B via an isomorphism that when applied twice equals identity on each side (i.e., order 2). Then overall order could be 2 (if swapping twice returns leaves). To get order 4, need the mapping between subtrees to be of order 2, and swapping adds another factor? Actually if when swapping the sides, we also map subtrees by some automorphism of order 2, then sigma^2 will map each side to itself performing that automorphism twice => identity, resulting sigma of order 2. To have order 4, need sigma^2 not identity but still fixes u,v. Means sigma^2 applies nontrivial automorphism within each side of the edge. Since sigma flips sides, sigma^2 will map each side back to itself applying the isomorphism composed twice (maybe order 2). So sigma could be order 4 if the isomorphism on each side is order 2 (i.e., an involution). Then sigma^2 is the involution on each side; sigma^4 identity.\n\nThus higher order potential.\n\nThus any permutation whose order is power of 2? Not necessarily.\n\nGeneral theorem (maybe): The automorphism group of a tree is a (finite) group that is a direct product of symmetric groups and maybe includes some involutive \"swap of ends\". It is known that tree automorphism groups are exactly the finite groups that are \"tree groups\", which are subgroups of automorphism groups of trees (Cayley tree?). But for finite trees, the group is soluble? Actually groups acting on trees are exactly subgroups of a wreath product of symmetric groups, which are solvable? Might be in general any finite group can be realized as automorphism group of some tree? I think any finite group is the automorphism group of some tree (Cayley tree?). Not sure.\n\nBut we only need which individual permutations can appear, not whole group.\n\nGiven ability to construct tree to realize any permutation that has at most one orbit without fixed point? Let's brute force by computational enumeration: generate all trees on 8 labeled vertices (there are many: number of labeled trees = 8^{6} = 262144 (Cayley's formula). That's manageable. For each tree compute automorphism group and collect all permutations that appear as automorphisms of any tree. Count distinct permutations.\n\nWe can simulate via reasoning, but we can also attempt mental enumeration? Maybe quicker to conceive algorithmically, but as ChatGPT offline we can't compute. Need analytic combinatorial count.\n\nBut perhaps answer is all permutations except those composed entirely of cycles of odd length greater than 1 with no fixed points? Let's test on n=3: permutations: identity, (12), (13), (23), (123), (132). We know (123) not realizable. So condition matches: odd cycles of length 3 with no fixed point fail. Actually there are also permutations like (12) with fixed point? Yes transposition has fixed point (third vertex). Realizable. So rule: any permutation without a fixed point and of odd order cannot be tree automorphism. For n=3, (123) order 3 odd, no fixed point -> not realizable. All else realizable? (132) also order 3, not realizable. True.\n\nNow test n=4: consider a 4-cycle (1234), order 4 even, no fixed points. Is it realizable? Could we construct tree with automorphism of order 4, no fixed vertex? Maybe central edge with swapping and internal rotations? Let's attempt: have central edge u-v. Attach leaves a1,a2 to u and b1,b2 to v such that sigma rotates among a's and b's while swapping u/v. Could produce 4-cycle? For example label leaves a1->b1->a2->b2->a1? That would be sigma moves a1 to b1 (across edge), etc. After 4 steps returns. Does tree mapping preserve adjacency? Edge (u,a1) maps to (v,b1) which exists if we attach b1 to v. Works. Similarly (v,b1) maps to (u,a2). Need edge (v,b1) present; yes. So pattern works if subtrees are simple leaves. Let's define tree: vertices: u,v, a1,a2,b1,b2 (6 vertices). Add edges: u-a1, u-a2, v-b1, v-b2, plus edge u-v. That's a tree with central edge, each side has two leaf children. Define sigma: u→v, v→u (swap), a1→b1, b1→a2, a2→b2, b2→a1. This is 4-cycle on a1,b1,a2,b2 plus swap of u/v (which is a transposition). Full permutation consists of a 4-cycle and a transposition, order lcm(4,2)=4? Actually (u v) is order2, but together with 4-cycle, overall order 4 because (u v) squares to identity. Composition? Since cycles act on disjoint sets, order is lcm(4,2)=4. So overall permutation has order 4 and no fixed vertex (u and v swapped). So this is automorphism of tree. Therefore a 4-cycle without fixed points is possible (plus additional transposition). But the permutation (1234) alone on 4 vertices cannot because need extra swapped vertex not present. However we can embed 4-cycle within larger set where some other vertices get swapped etc. For n=8, a pure 8-cycle could be realized if we have central edge swapping etc with additional vertices? But the cycle includes all vertices; need that mapping swaps two vertices (the central edge) but they are within the 8-cycle; order of whole permutation maybe 8 with a transposition inside cycle??? A single 8-cycle cannot have a transposition as separate factor. So maybe impossible.\n\nThus restriction: a permutation that as a whole has a fixed edge action must decompose with a 2-cycle (or more) representing the edge swap. In general, any automorphism without a fixed vertex must have each orbit either of even length, and there must be a pairing of vertices across the edge such that sigma maps a vertex to its counterpart after odd number of steps? The structure suggests that every orbit of sigma must be of length either arbitrary but must contain vertices that pair up under sigma^{k} where k is half length? Actually consider sigma acting without fixed vertex; there exists edge {x, sigma(x)}? Indeed applying sigma to x yields sigma(x). The edge between x and sigma(x) may be the invariant edge? The central edge must be mapped to itself after sigma: sigma(e) = e as unordered set. That means sigma either fixes each endpoint (then edge fixed) or swaps them. The latter means sigma(x)=y, sigma(y)=x, so they form a 2-cycle. Hence there is a 2-cycle representing the central edge. Thus any automorphism without fixed vertex must contain a 2-cycle. Moreover the rest of vertices can be partitioned into orbits that are mapped accordingly, perhaps mixed with the swapping.\n\nThus a permutation consisting of a single 8-cycle has no 2-cycle, thus cannot be automorphism of tree. So condition: permutation must have at least one fixed point, or contain a 2-cycle (i.e., have an orbit of size 2) that could serve as central edge.\n\nIs that sufficient? Likely yes: given permutation sigma on 8 elements, if it has a fixed point, we can construct a rooted tree with that vertex as root and attach isomorphic subtrees for each cycle. This seems always possible. For permutations without fixed point but containing a 2-cycle, we can treat that 2-cycle as central edge swapped. Then the rest of vertices can be arranged as subtrees attached symmetrically to the two endpoints, respecting sigma action.\n\nThus any permutation on 8 elements that either has a fixed point, or has a 2-cycle, is realizable. The only permutations excluded are those consisting solely of cycles of length >=3 odd, and all cycles have length >=3 (i.e., no fixed points and no 2-cycles). They may include even cycles of length >2 as well? Wait an even cycle of length 4 could be realized without fixed point but includes no 2-cycle; can we realize a 4-cycle alone? Possibly using central edge plus some structure like earlier, but that required additional transposition. The 4-cycle alone would map u→v→... Not contain a 2-cycle, so sigma has no fixed vertex and no 2-cycle. Is such permutation possible as tree automorphism? Let's test n=4 case: permutation (1234) alone without extra vertices. Could we construct a tree on exactly 4 vertices where automorphism is (1234)? Tree on 4 vertices either star (center + 3 leaves) or path of length3. Center star automorphisms are permutations fixing center; cannot be 4-cycle (no fixed point). Path automorphism group is order2. So not possible. So 4-cycle alone not possible. So condition of having a 2-cycle is necessary.\n\nWhat about permutation consisting of two 4-cycles (disjoint) on 8 vertices. No 2-cycle, no fixed point. Could we construct? Need at least a central edge that swaps vertices, but each cycle length even may be realized as combination of swapping and rotation. Possibly each 4-cycle could be constructed using central swap plus other leaf rotations, but would need extra transposition somewhere. Since there is only edge swapped, could the rest be arranged in cycles of length 4 without extra 2-cycle? Let's try: central edge (a,b) swapped. Then the rest 6 vertices need to be arranged into two 4-cycles? That's impossible because cycles need to be disjoint; we only have a,b as 2-cycle. Other vertices cannot be arranged to produce 4-cycles unless they are mapped across the edge and rotate. Maybe we can have two 4-cycles each using both sides? Eg Cycle1: x1 -> y1 -> x2 -> y2 -> x1 where x's on one side, y's on the other. This cycle length 4 involves the swapping between sides. So each such 4-cycle includes the swapping repeatedly. The central edge (a,b) also part of the swapping; that's a 2-cycle. So overall permutation would have that 2-cycle plus maybe other cycles of even lengths (maybe multiple of 2). So a permutation without any 2-cycle cannot be realized.\n\nHence the condition should be: sigma must have at least one cycle of length 1 or 2.\n\nIs that sufficient for all permutations on 8 elements? Let's consider permutation consisting of a 2-cycle and a 3-cycle and a 3-cycle (2+3+3). Has fixed point? No. Contains a 2-cycle, so condition met. Can we construct tree? Use central edge = the 2-cycle vertices. Then need to attach subtrees that are permuted accordingly to produce 3-cycles. Since the central edge swaps sides, the action on the rest must be compatible: sigma maps a vertex on side A to vertex on side B in maybe after some steps. For a 3-cycle, length odd, cannot be achieved purely by swapping sides because after an odd number of applications, vertex would end up on opposite side. But we could have a 3-cycle entirely within one side if sigma after some iterations maps side to itself? However sigma maps side A to side B in one step (since central swap), then after two steps back to side A, etc. So sigma^2 fixes sides. Therefore any orbit under sigma must have even length unless it consists of vertices that are fixed by sigma (length1) or belong to central 2-cycle. Because applying sigma twice fixes each side, so the orbit size must divide 2* (orbit under sigma^2). Actually sigma can have odd length cycles only if includes a fixed vertex (since sigma^2 would have same cycle length). Let's test: Suppose sigma has a 3-cycle not containing any of the central vertices. Then sigma^2 also cycles those three vertices (since (abc)^2 = (acb) also a 3-cycle). So they never become fixed under sigma^2; but central edge swapping does not affect them? However sigma may map side A to side A (if the vertices are on side that is fixed under sigma? But sigma swaps the central vertices only; does it necessarily map other vertices from side A to side B? The parent relation: If vertex w is adjacent to a (on side A) only, then sigma(w) adjacent to sigma(a)=b, so sigma(w) lies on side B. So any vertex not fixed by sigma must move across the edge each application (since sigma maps adjacency). Thus its orbit length must be even. Hence odd-length cycles other than fixed points cannot exist when there is no fixed vertex.\n\nThus condition refined: If sigma has no fixed points, then all cycles must be of even length, and there must be at least one 2-cycle (the central edge). This seems necessary.\n\nIs it sufficient? Likely yes: we can construct a tree where central edge consists of the 2-cycle, and other even cycles are realized by alternating vertices on the two sides.\n\nConstruction: For each even cycle of length 2k, we can arrange vertices alternately on each side of central edge, making a \"path\" of length 2k from one side to the other? Need them to be leaves attached appropriately to maintain degree. We can create a \"ladder\": pick k vertices on left side attached to left center, and k vertices on right side attached to right center, then sigma maps each left vertex to right vertex next in cycle, etc. That works.\n\nThus any permutation with at least one fixed point, or all cycles even with at least one 2-cycle, is realizable.\n\nNow count permutations of 8 elements satisfying that condition.\n\nLet total number of permutations of 8 = 8! = 40320.\n\nWe need to subtract those that are NOT realizable: permutations that have no fixed points, and all cycles are of odd length (i.e., each cycle length odd >=3). Also they must contain at least one cycle odd length (since no fixed point). Also cannot have any even cycles, in particular cannot have any 2-cycle.\n\nThus excluded permutations = permutations of 8 with cycle type consisting solely of odd integers >=3, summing to 8, and none equal to 1 or 2.\n\nPossible partitions of 8 into odd parts >=3:\n\n- 3+5 (both odd)\n- 3+3+... remainder 2 (not allowed)\n- 5+3 same as above\n- 7+1 (has 1 fixed -> allowed, so not excluded)\n- 8 (even) not allowed\n- 3+3+... need 2 left not allowed.\n- 3+3+3 is 9 >8.\n\n- single 5+? leftover 3 yes we have 3+5.\n\n- single 3+? leftover 5 same.\n\n- single 7+? leftover 1 excluded because fixed point.\n\n- single 3+? leftover 5 done.\n\n- single 1 not allowed.\n\nThus only cycle type (3,5) is possible for excluded permutations (no 1 or 2 cycles). Also could be a single cycle of length 8? that's even, but includes even length; does it have a fixed point? no, but includes even length which is not allowed for excluded set because condition requires all cycles odd; but we are excluding only those with no 1 or 2 cycles and all cycles odd. Since 8-cycle has even length, but does it satisfy condition necessary? Let's test: 8-cycle has no fixed point and includes even cycle length, which we earlier argued may be realizable? Wait does an 8-cycle possibly be automorphism? It might be realizable via central edge and alternating? but 8-cycle is even length but includes no 2-cycle. As argued earlier, without 2-cycle it is impossible because need a 2-cycle to serve as central edge. So even cycles without a 2-cycle also impossible. So we need to also exclude permutations with no fixed point and having no 2-cycle (i.e., all cycles lengths are >=3, could be even). So excluded set: permutations with no fixed point and without any 2-cycle. That's equivalent to permutations where all cycles lengths are >=3. (Even lengths allowed). Let's test 4-cycle alone (length4) no fixed point, no 2-cycle. We determined impossible. Good. So we must exclude all permutations with no fixed points and no 2-cycles.\n\nThus condition for realizable: permutation must have either a fixed point or a 2-cycle. That's it. So all permutations except those with neither fixed point nor 2-cycle.\n\nThus count = total permutations - number of permutations with no 1-cycles and no 2-cycles.\n\nWe need count of permutations of 8 with all cycle lengths >=3.\n\nLet's compute using inclusion-exclusion or using Stirling numbers of first kind? Count permutations with no cycles of size 1 or 2.\n\nLet a_n = number of permutations of n with all cycle lengths ≥3.\n\nWe can compute via recurrence: a_n = (n-1)! for n? Not.\n\nBetter use exponential generating function for permutations with allowed cycle sizes. Number = n! * [x^n] exp( sum_{k≥3} x^k/k ). Equivalent to using inclusion-exclusion: total permutations minus those with at least one 1-cycle or 2-cycle.\n\nBut easier: count directly by enumerating cycle type partitions of 8 with parts >=3.\n\nList all partitions of 8 with parts >=3:\n\n- 3+5\n- 4+4\n- 3+3+? left 2 not allowed\n- 6+? left 2 not allowed\n- 8\n- 3+3+3? can't\n- 5+3 (same)\n- 6+? gave 6+?2 not allowed\n- 4+?4 done\n- 3+?5 done\n\n- Also partition 3+3+?2 not allowed.\n\nThus permissible cycle type partitions: (8), (5,3), (4,4). Also (6,? ) + 2 not allowed; (3,3,?2) not allowed; (3,?5) already.\n\nThus permutations with cycles of length >=3 are those with cycle type:\n\n- one 8-cycle\n- product of a 5-cycle and a 3-cycle\n- product of two 4-cycles\n\nNow count each.\n\nNumber of permutations with a single 8-cycle: (8-1)! = 5040 (since number of cycles of length n is (n-1)!).\n\nNumber of permutations with a 5-cycle and a 3-cycle: Choose the 5 elements for the 5-cycle: C(8,5)=56. Number of distinct 5-cycles on those 5 elements = (5-1)! =24. Number of distinct 3-cycles on remaining 3 elements = (3-1)! =2. Since cycles are disjoint and order of cycles doesn't matter (product), total = 56*24*2 = 56*48 = 2688. However note that swapping labeling of cycles doesn't double count because we chose specific set for 5-cycle first; that's fine.\n\nNumber of permutations with two 4-cycles: Partition 8 elements into two unordered sets of 4. Number of ways: (1/2) * C(8,4) = (1/2)*70 =35. For each 4-element set, number of 4-cycles = (4-1)! =6. So for each partition, number of permutations = 6*6 =36. Total = 35*36 = 1260.\n\nCheck totals: 5040 + 2688 + 1260 = 899? Wait compute sum: 5040+2688=7728, +1260=8988.\n\nThus number of permutations with no 1- or 2-cycles = 8988.\n\nTherefore number of realizable permutations = 40320 - 8988 = 31332.\n\nLet's verify reasoning that any permutation with at least a fixed point or a 2-cycle is realizable. Need to ensure there's no other obstruction such as needing the rest to be compatible with tree structure, but we argued construction possible.\n\nConstruction for fixed point case: root fixed. Pair cycles can be realized as swapping leaves attached to root maybe need even cycles? For odd cycles >1 (like 3-cycle) we can attach leaves to root and rotate them using root's symmetry (since root fixed). That works. For cycles of length k, we can attach subtrees that realize cyclic permutation of their roots. Example: have k vertices attached to root as leaves; sigma permutes them cyclically. That's fine, leaves have degree1; root degree includes them. So any cycle can be realized as permutation of leaves attached to root. However they must be disjoint cycles, root fixed, okay.\n\nThus any permutation with a fixed point can be realized by taking the fixed point as root and