, 47605, 21482, 484, 3793, 13, 41351, 581, 665, 14975, 220, 1422, 47605, 777, 2454, 80677, 350, 9577, 220, 8145, 16, 936, 326, 29111, 15448, 220, 11914, 31157, 10581, 350, 62832, 316, 717, 1236, 115502, 220, 1157, 741, 7801, 395, 1062, 3609, 4215, 80677, 428, 11, 1354, 553, 540, 5153, 220, 1422, 47605, 483, 3793, 10853, 314, 428, 11, 326, 1023, 738, 722, 413, 6145, 350, 21910, 7060, 18305, 741, 24305, 1309, 316, 14866, 25, 11965, 24923, 484, 538, 20808, 885, 11915, 9113, 472, 329, 10853, 314, 350, 727, 10853, 533, 336, 10853, 8, 1812, 220, 14075, 11, 1815, 20808, 575, 738, 413, 6145, 165577, 3609, 4215, 42308, 94, 3793, 10853, 13, 41351, 23994, 328, 1617, 2316, 2086, 11, 290, 42006, 38566, 13, 2632, 722, 47605, 12119, 3793, 10853, 29702, 290, 3609, 4215, 80677, 738, 413, 6145, 0, 21926, 2212, 0, 2632, 16218, 540, 5153, 11085, 2086, 328, 47605, 13110, 1062, 80677, 314, 8350, 10564, 14, 14075, 25987, 1422, 364, 84787, 581, 679, 23141, 10772, 25, 25151, 395, 2454, 20808, 261, 123156, 16336, 3793, 10853, 306, 354, 15, 35229, 11, 12307, 16255, 623, 20808, 144619, 290, 4215, 328, 8663, 402, 1273, 47605, 112372, 220, 14075, 11, 2421, 265, 10853, 13, 1225, 134802, 329, 10853, 314, 350, 727, 10853, 533, 265, 10853, 8, 1812, 220, 14075, 350, 4522, 581, 4014, 1534, 316, 220, 16, 485, 14075, 10112, 12847, 741, 12265, 8663, 553, 591, 220, 16, 485, 14075, 11, 889, 112372, 220, 14075, 581, 665, 4665, 220, 15, 472, 220, 14075, 13, 46830, 11155, 364, 19371, 538, 3609, 4215, 353, 314, 4215, 328, 722, 8663, 112372, 220, 14075, 11, 581, 679, 265, 10853, 314, 353, 533, 261, 10853, 350, 4847, 220, 14075, 741, 7801, 329, 10853, 314, 3793, 10853, 533, 350, 51, 533, 261, 10853, 8, 314, 3793, 10853, 533, 353, 659, 261, 10853, 13, 109553, 7060, 329, 10853, 42308, 94, 261, 10853, 350, 4847, 220, 14075, 8, 8967, 3793, 10853, 533, 353, 42308, 94, 220, 15, 871, 353, 42308, 94, 3793, 10853, 13, 2632, 9707, 2617, 47605, 483, 3793, 10853, 314, 353, 11915, 20323, 364, 84787, 19957, 6145, 314, 19957, 3605, 328, 47605, 21482, 2454, 80677, 314, 8350, 10564, 14, 14075, 25987, 1422, 364, 84787, 16218, 220, 1422, 11, 326, 13057, 11608, 382, 1217, 220, 1422, 13, 2632, 372, 28, 1422, 364, 23483, 316, 6757, 8663, 553, 220, 16, 485, 14075, 625, 220, 15, 485, 12307, 13, 7649, 112372, 22573, 54286, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 1422, 364, 94272, 1721, 6052, 6011, 13], "n_tokens": 5758, "words": 4254, "band_lo": 906.0, "band_hi": 3624.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to understand problem: There are N=2024 robots. Each robot sees the numbers on others (i.e., the multiset of numbers assigned to other robots). They each have a deterministic program (function) that, given the observed multiset of numbers, produces a guess for its own number (an integer in 1..113). They all guess simultaneously. We can design these functions (programs) in advance (same for all robots? It says Dexter can set up the programs. Likely each robot can have its own program, possibly different. But they all are predetermined before numbers are assigned.) The assignment is arbitrary (any function from robots to {1..113} possible, possibly many duplicates). We want to guarantee at least k correct guesses for any assignment. Find the maximum k possible.\n\nWe need to compute a lower bound (guarantee) using some combinatorial principle, like pigeonhole, and also show can't guarantee larger.\n\nEssentially, each robot's guess depends only on the multiset of observed numbers (size N-1). Since each robot sees all other numbers, each robot sees the whole assignment except its own number. So the information to each robot is the vector of N numbers minus its own entry. The set of possible observations is the set of N-length sequences where one entry missing. The deterministic algorithm can produce a guess possibly varying with the observed multiset.\n\nGoal: Guarantee at least k correct guesses for any assignment.\n\nThink of this as a covering problem: For any assignment a ∈ {1..113}^N, we have N guesses g_i(a_{-i}) (depend on all others). Count correct = #{i: g_i(a_{-i}) = a_i}. We want to maximize minimal number.\n\nWe can think of each robot's program as a function f_i mapping observed multiset (or vector) to a guess.\n\nWe can view each assignment a as a tuple, and define a selection of indices where guess matches.\n\nWe want to choose the functions to maximize min over a of correct count.\n\nQuestion reminiscent of \"guessing hat colors\" puzzles with common knowledge etc. Typically, with N participants see others' hats, each guess color; you can guarantee at least N/ (colors) correct? Actually in classic puzzle where each sees all other hats and can guess own hat color from q colors, they can guarantee at least floor(N/q) correct using grouping based on sum modulo q. But the classic ensures at least one correct - using modulo. Wait standard: With N hats, each sees all others and needs to guess own color from 2 colors, they can guarantee at least N/2 correct? Let's recall: There is a well known \"hat guessing\" problem where each player sees hats of others and must guess own hat color (say red/blue). The team can guarantee at least N/2 correct by pairing? Actually known results: They can guarantee at least ceiling(N/2) correct using strategy where they pair up, each guesses that their own color is same as partner's, leading to at least one correct per pair. That's for 2 colors and they can guarantee at least N/2. For q colors, they can guarantee at least N/q correct (pair groups size q). Also using modular sum they can guarantee at least one correct. But here we want to maximize minimal total correct, not guarantee at least one.\n\nOur setting: 113 possible numbers (colors). We have N=2024. Want to guarantee at least floor(N/113)? Let's compute: 2024/113 ≈ 17.918. floor = 17. So at least 17? Could be better.\n\nBut we have ability to customize each robot's program (possibly different), maybe we can do better using covering codes.\n\nConsider mapping each possible full assignment vector (of length N) to a set of indices that guess correctly. We want each assignment to have at least k correct.\n\nTotal number of possible assignments = 113^2024 huge. But each robot's program defines for each observation (which is a full vector except its own entry) a guess.\n\nConsider counting total number of (assignment, robot) pairs where guess is correct, think of expectation over uniform random assignment. If we can design functions to maximize minimal count maybe use averaging to bound.\n\nBut need worst-case guarantee, not average. Typically, for such local schemes, the best guarantee equals floor(N/113). Example: Partition robots into groups of size 113 (or near). Within each group, they coordinate to guarantee at least one correct per group: using modulo sum strategy. That yields k = floor(N/113) = 17 (maybe plus remainder?). Since remainder 2024 mod 113 = 2024 - 113*17 = 2024 - 1921 = 103. So there are 17 full groups plus a leftover of 103 robots. For leftover maybe guarantee at least floor(103/113)=0, but we might guarantee at least 1 with other method? Could maybe guarantee at least 1 more? Let's think.\n\nIn typical modulo sum strategy with q colors, you can guarantee at least 1 correct among all N (not per group). But we can adapt to guarantee each group of size q at least 1 correct, by applying sum modulo q to each group individually based on observations within the group (ignoring others). But each robot sees entire set, so they can compute the sum of numbers they see within its group (excluding own). This is possible. Then each robot guesses number that makes group's sum modulo 113 equal to some predetermined constant (like 0). In each group, exactly one robot's guess will be correct (since if we consider the actual numbers, sum of all numbers in group mod 113 = S. The rule: each robot assumes its own number = (c - sum_of_others_mod) mod 113, where c is constant. Then exactly those i for which actual number equals that guess will be correct; but due to modulo, exactly one i? Let's test: Suppose we have group of size m = 113. Let numbers a1..a113. Define constant C (e.g., 0). Robot i sees sum of others S_i = total sum - a_i. It guesses g_i = (C - S_i) mod 113 = (C - (total - a_i)) mod 113 = (C - total + a_i) mod 113. Since total mod 113 = T. Then g_i = (C - T + a_i) mod 113. Therefore g_i = a_i iff (C - T) mod 113 = 0, i.e., C ≡ T (mod 113). So if total sum modulo 113 equals constant C, then every robot's guess equals its own number (all correct). But that's not guaranteed. For a given assignment, total sum may not equal C. Then which robot is correct? Let's compute condition g_i = a_i -> (C - T + a_i) ≡ a_i mod 113 -> C - T ≡ 0 mod 113 -> independent of i. So either all robots are correct if total sum equals C, otherwise none are correct. That's not useful.\n\nWait I recall different strategy: Use modulo sum without groups: each robot guesses that total sum (including its own) modulo q equals predetermined value, so only those whose number makes that hold will guess correctly; exactly one robot per whole set will be correct (since total sum modulo q is fixed, there is exactly one i satisfying that). Indeed for whole set with q colors, we can guarantee at least one correct: each player assumes that the sum of all hats modulo q equals 0, thus they guess a_i = (- sum of others) mod q. Exactly one player will guess correctly (the one whose actual a_i satisfies that equation). Thus k =1. That's classic.\n\nBut we can group into blocks of size 113, then each block guarantee at least one correct if they treat block independently. However each robot can compute sum of other numbers within its block based on seeing all numbers, so yes.\n\nHence we can guarantee at least number of groups = floor(N/113) = 17 correct. For leftover 103 robots, we may also guarantee at least 1 correct using overall modulo maybe, adding extra. Could we guarantee also at least 1 within leftover? That's size less than 113, but we can use another technique: Since less than q colors, we can guarantee at least 0? Usually with q colors and group size < q, you can guarantee at least 1 using trivial? Not necessarily.\n\nMaybe we can guarantee at least ceil(N/113). Because we could treat all N robots with modulo 113 and get at least 1 correct. Then we can also group to get 17 groups each guarantee at least 1, but the overall guarantee is at least max of these methods? Actually you can design program to combine both, maybe guarantee floor(N/113)+1? Let's see if we can guarantee 18.\n\nWe must show upper bound: no strategy can guarantee 18 correct. Show adversarial assignment can limit correct guesses.\n\nWe need to find bound: Since each robot's program maps each observation (multiset of size 2023) to a guess. There are at most 113 possibilities for guess; each robot's decision might be arbitrary.\n\nPotential counting argument: For any fixed set of programs, we can consider all assignments, and consider sum over robots of indicator of correct. Compute average number of correct over uniformly random assignments. By linearity, average per robot is probability its guess equals its actual number. Since the robot's observation is all numbers except its own, which is independent of its own number? For random assignment uniform over 113 values each robot independent, the distribution of observed numbers is independent of its own number, but there may be correlation because they share other robots' numbers. However symmetry suggests that for any deterministic function f_i of observed other numbers, the probability that f_i equals the actual a_i is 1/113, because given the observed others, a_i is uniformly random independent of observation (since uniform). So expected correct per robot = 1/113. Therefore expected total correct = N / 113 ≈ 17.92. This means there exists some assignment where correct ≤ floor(N/113) maybe, but can the adversary force less than ceiling? Since average is N/113, the minimal cannot exceed ceiling of average? The max minimal possible ≤ floor(N/113) maybe N/113+?? Actually by pigeonhole principle, there exists some assignment with at most floor(N/113) correct, since otherwise average > floor. More formally: If minimal > t, then all assignments have > t correct, so average > t, but average = N/113. So minimal ≤ floor(N/113) maybe? Let's compute: If minimal ≥ t+1, then average ≥ t+1, but N/113 ≈ 17.918, so t can be at most 17. So minimal ≤ 17. So guarantee cannot exceed 17. So k_max ≤ 17.\n\nThus k=17 is maximal. Need to show guarantee 17 feasible.\n\nConstruct strategy: Partition robots into 17 groups of 113 each, plus leftover 103 robots maybe ignore. For each group, use modulo sum strategy that guarantees at least one correct per group, as discussed but earlier we realized it may guarantee all or none. Wait earlier we concluded modulo sum yields either all correct or none correct for the whole group. Let's re-evaluate: The group of size 113, we label them 1..113. Define constant C_g maybe equal to 0. Each robot i in group sees numbers of others in group except its own, plus also other groups but they can ignore them. It guesses number such that sum of all numbers in the group (including its own) modulo 113 equals C_g. Then as derived, either total sum ≡ C_g, then all 113 robots guess correctly; else none correct. That's not guaranteeing at least one per group in worst case.\n\nSo we need different strategy to guarantee at least one per group irrespective of numbers. Known approach: For q colors, we can guarantee at least ceil(N/q) correct by pairing? Wait with groups of size q, we can guarantee at least 1 by \"matching\"? Actually we can guarantee at least floor(N/q) using pigeonhole principle: Since there are only q possible numbers, by pigeonhole at least floor(N/q) numbers appear at least once? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, we can use \"pairing strategy\" for q=2 giving N/2 correct. For q>2, pairing into subsets of size q and using a cyclic rule may guarantee at least 1 per subset: e.g., for each subset, assign each robot a distinct guess value from 1..q (or 0..q-1). They each guess that value regardless of observation. Since the actual numbers assigned to the subset may include each possible number multiple times, but we want at least one robot's guess matches its actual number. If we assign each robot a distinct number, pigeonhole principle ensures at least one robot's number equals its guess? Actually if we assign each robot a distinct number from the set of q possible numbers, there are q robots in subset (so each number used exactly once). Then for any assignment of numbers to those q robots, there could be a case where none of the robots have the number they guessed (if the assignment is a derangement of the set). For a set of 113 distinct numbers (1..113), there exist permutations with no fixed points. So not guaranteed.\n\nNeed a deterministic rule that uses observation to guarantee at least one correct per group. Consider the following: For each group of size m=113, each robot can compute the multiset of numbers among the other 112 robots in its group. This multiset gives counts of each value. Since there are 113 possible numbers but only 112 observed numbers, at least one number (call it x) does NOT appear among the observed others. Since each robot sees 112 numbers; if some number occurs 0 times among the others, robot can safely guess that number x; if the actual number of that robot is indeed x, then correct; otherwise the actual number is among those observed, meaning that number appears at least once among the others. Could guarantee that at least one robot's actual number matches the missing number? Let's think.\n\nFor each value v in 1..113, count total occurrences among all 113 robots. The sum of counts =113. There must be at least one value that occurs exactly once? Not necessarily; could have 112 robots with value 1 and one robot with value 2, etc. So there might be values with zero counts? Since total robots =113, we cover at most 113 numbers, could be some missing value.\n\nObservation: For any group of 113 robots, there are at most 113 distinct numbers possible; there will be at least one number that appears at most once (by pigeonhole). Indeed average count =1, so there is a number appearing ≤1 times. If a number appears 0 times, obviously none robot has that number. That's not helpful. We want guarantee at least one robot guesses correctly. Use rule: each robot looks at the multiset of others; if there exists a number that appears an odd number of times (?) Not.\n\nConsider the following classic result: For N players each sees others' hats from q colors, they can guarantee at least floor(N/q) correct using 'quotient' strategy: Partition the set of all possible full assignments into q^{N-1} equivalence classes based on the vector of others? Not.\n\nLet's attempt a direct bound: We need guarantee 17 correct. Using averaging argument we can't guarantee more than floor(N/113)=17. To achieve 17, need strategy where for any assignment, at least 17 correct.\n\nWe could simply guarantee 17 correct by using the classic “global modulo” strategy to guarantee exactly 1 correct, then repeat 17 times across independent partitions? Not independent because same guess for each robot.\n\nBut we could create 17 disjoint groups each using modulo strategy with different constants; then across groups, at least 1 correct per group? Let's think: For each group g, define constant C_g unique values 0..16. Each robot i in group g will assume that sum of numbers in group g modulo 113 equals C_g, and guess accordingly. Then for each group, exactly one robot in that group will be correct when the actual sum modulo 113 equals C_g; otherwise none. So not guarantee.\n\nBut we could set each group's constant equal to the actual sum maybe unknown.\n\nAlternative: Use deterministic function that maps observed multiset to a guess such that for each possible assignment there are at least 1 correct per group due to some combinatorial property like \"ham sandwich\"?\n\nMaybe we can construct using error-correcting codes: Each robot corresponds to a coordinate in a code of length N over alphabet 113. The functions correspond to decoding? The guarantee corresponds to covering radius. We want covering radius such that each word is within 0 distance of at least k coordinates? Not.\n\nLet's think simpler: Since expected correct is about 17.9, we can guarantee at least floor of that i.e., 17 using probabilistic method: Randomly assign each robot a function (maybe simple) and show there exists a set achieving guarantee 17. Could use Yao's minimax: Since average over uniform assignments is N/113, there must exist a deterministic strategy achieving at least that many correct for every assignment? Not, expectation over random strategies may guarantee expected correct >= N/113 for each assignment, then there exists a deterministic strategy with min >= N/113? Not true.\n\nBut we aim for lower bound 17. Let's attempt explicit construction: Use 17 predetermined 'reference' numbers (guess values) and assign each robot to a distinct reference. For each robot i, program guesses the reference number r_i irrespective of observation. Then, for any assignment, number of correct guesses equals number of robots whose actual number equals its reference. Since there are 113 possible numbers, by pigeonhole principle at least ceil(2024/113) = 18 robots share the same number? Wait compute: total robots 2024 across 113 values, average per value ≈ 17.918. So there exists a value that appears at least 18 times (by pigeonhole, because if all values appear at most 17 times, total ≤ 113*17=1921 < 2024). Hence there is a value v occurring at least 18 times. If we set references to cover each value at least once? Not.\n\nIf we set references such that each value appears at least once among the references, then we guarantee at least 1? Actually we can guarantee at least 18 correct by aligning references to the most frequent value? But we cannot adapt to assignment.\n\nWe could define 113 reference numbers each assigned to about 18 robots (since 2024/113 ~ 17.9). Suppose we assign robots into 113 groups (not necessarily equal) each group associated with a specific guess value (the same for all robots in group). Then for any assignment, the number of correct guesses equals sum over values v of (number of robots in group associated with v that actually have value v). That's at least the number of occurrences of v that are in its own group. Since each value appears at some count c_v, and we have allocated r_v robots to guess v (potentially not equal to c_v). The minimum total correct across assignments occurs when each value's actual occurrences are placed in groups guessing a different value. But we can arrange groups to minimize? However we cannot control assignment. If we allocate groups uniformly, the adversary can assign numbers to avoid groups: they could assign each robot a number different from its group's guess, as long as each value appears as many times as needed.\n\nBut can the adversary avoid all matches? If each robot guesses a fixed number (its group), then the assignment can be chosen such that no robot gets its guessed number as long as the multiset of numbers assigned to each group does not contain the group's value. Is that possible given sizes? For each group value v, there are r_v robots guessing v. To avoid matches, we must assign numbers to those robots using only values ≠ v. This is feasible as long as total number of slots for each value allows distributions. This becomes a problem of a Latin transversal? Might be possible to avoid all matches if groups are too many.\n\nBut we can apply Hall's marriage theorem: With 113 values and 2024 robots where each robot prefers one value (its guess). We need to assign each robot a value from 1..113 such that no robot receives its guessed value. This is a derangement problem with repetitions allowed. This is always possible unless some value is guessed by more than total robots minus (count of that value?). Actually you need to avoid assigning that value to any robot that guessed it. Since you can assign same value to many robots as long as they didn't guess it. Since there are 113 values, each appears some number of times in the assignment; the adversary can choose assignment distribution arbitrarily. Could they assign each robot a value different from its guess? For each value v, there are r_v robots that guess v. They need to assign the value v to robots that didn't guess v, i.e., total capacity = total robots - r_v. As long as the number of times value v appears in assignment ≤ total robots - r_v, feasible. Since the adversary can choose the frequencies arbitrarily up to N. They can try to assign value v very many times, perhaps exceeding that capacity, forcing some robot who guessed v to actually have v. The maximum they can assign to v without causing a forced correct is N - r_v (since must avoid r_v robots). So if r_v > N - max_possible? Actually they can assign up to N - r_v copies of v while still avoiding all r_v robots. Since total N =2024, if any r_v > N/2, then they can't avoid? Not.\n\nThus we can guarantee at least min over v of (2r_v - N)+? Because if they assign as many v as possible to avoid matches, the leftover must go to guessed robots. The worst case minimal correct total = sum_v max(0, r_v - (N - c_v)), where c_v is number of v assigned. But they choose c_v. They want to minimize sum of matches, i.e., for each v, keep matches low. Matches for value v is max(0, r_v - (N - c_v)) (since at most N - r_v robots can receive v without a match). To minimize total matches, they'd like to set c_v as high as possible but limited to N - r_v? Actually they can set c_v up to N - r_v to avoid matches, beyond that matches forced. So they may set c_v = N - r_v to avoid matches entirely (matches zero). But sum c_v must equal N. Feasibility: Choose c_v = N - r_v for each v => sum c_v = 113 N - sum r_v = 113 N - N = (113-1)N =112N huge, not N. So can't.\n\nThus need optimization.\n\nBut maybe simple: By pigeonhole, there exists a value v appearing at least 18 times. If we allocate at least 18 robots to guess v, then at least one of those robots will be correct? Not necessarily; we could avoid putting any of those 18 guessed robots to be the ones with that value? Wait if there are at least 18 robots guessing v, and there are at least 18 robots whose actual number is v, it's possible that the sets are disjoint (i.e., none of the guessers are among actual v) as long as total robots ≥ 36 (which is true). So still possible to avoid matches.\n\nThus that line not guarantee.\n\nThus best guarantee is floor(N/113)=17 by average argument; need to construct strategy achieving 17 for all assignments.\n\nConsider using linear algebra: Represent numbers modulo 113 (since 113 prime). Each robot knows the sum of all numbers modulo 113 except its own (call S_i). The robot can compute S_i. Their guess could be some function of S_i. Let's think about using multiple independent equations to guarantee many correct. For each robot i, define guess g_i = a_i' where a_i' = (α_i - S_i) mod 113 for some predetermined coefficient α_i. The condition for robot i to be correct is a_i = (α_i - (total sum - a_i)) mod 113 => a_i = (α_i - total + a_i) => α_i - total ≡ 0 => total ≡ α_i (mod 113). Thus all robots with same α_i will be correct iff total sum equals that α. So we can assign each robot a distinct α_i values (up to 113). Since we have 2024 robots, many will share α. For each possible total sum modulo 113, the robots whose α_i equals that value will all be correct. Thus number of correct = number of robots with α_i = total sum mod 113. Therefore, to guarantee at least k correct, we need to allocate α values such that each residue class appears at least k times. Since there are 113 residues, we can allocate at least floor(2024/113)=17 per residue, and some residues get extra (since remainder 103). So we can guarantee at least 17 correct: because whichever residue total sum is, there are at least 17 robots assigned that α. Indeed we can assign 17 robots per each residue (total 1921), and distribute remaining 103 arbitrarily (maybe to get some residues 18). Then for any total sum residue r, there are at least 17 robots with α_i = r, and they will all be correct (since condition holds). Wait need to verify: earlier derived that if robot's guess defined as g_i = (α_i - S_i) mod 113, then robot i will be correct iff total sum ≡ α_i. Indeed regardless of its own number, the equation reduces. So all robots whose α_i equals the total sum residue will be correct! That's great! So guarantee at least minimum number of robots sharing any residue = floor(N/113)=17.\n\nThus we have explicit strategy: Choose for each robot a predetermined integer α_i in {0,...,112}. The robot computes the sum of numbers on other robots modulo 113, call s_i. It guesses g_i = (α_i - s_i) mod 113 (where we map result to 1..113 maybe shift). Since numbers are from 1..113, but modulo 113 we can treat 0 as 113. We'll adapt.\n\nThen if total sum T = sum of all numbers modulo 113, we have s_i = T - a_i (mod 113). Then g_i = α_i - (T - a_i) = α_i - T + a_i. Equality condition g_i ≡ a_i (mod 113) gives α_i - T ≡ 0 => T ≡ α_i. So exactly those robots with α_i = T guess correctly.\n\nThus minimal correct = minimal count of robots assigned each residue = floor(N/113)=17.\n\nThus guarantee 17, and upper bound is also 17. So k=17.\n\nNeed to ensure numbers are 1..113 not 0..112. Use modulo mapping appropriately.\n\nThus answer: 17.\n\nProvide final answer format.", "response": "ANSWER: 17", "answer": 17, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": ["too_long"], "accepted": false}
{"qid": "54a88897-5371-4eab-a157-046ef830bd20", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Let $m$ and $n$ be positive integers. Fuming Zeng gives James a rectangle, such that $m-1$ lines are drawn parallel to one pair of sides and $n-1$ lines are drawn parallel to the other pair of sides, thus dividing the rectangle into an $m \\times n$ grid of smaller rectangles. Fuming Zeng chooses $m+n-1$ of the $mn$ smaller rectangles and then tells James the area of each of the smaller rectangles. Of the $\\binom{mn}{m+n-1}$ possible combinations of rectangles and their areas Fuming Zeng could have given, let $C_{m,n}$ be the number of combinations which would allow James to determine the area of the whole rectangle. Given that \\[A=\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{C_{m,n}\\binom{m+n}{m}}{(m+n)^{m+n}},\\] then find the greatest integer less than $1000A$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Let $m$ and $n$ be positive integers. Fuming Zeng gives James a rectangle, such that $m-1$ lines are drawn parallel to one pair of sides and $n-1$ lines are drawn parallel to the other pair of sides, thus dividing the rectangle into an $m \\times n$ grid of smaller rectangles. Fuming Zeng chooses $m+n-1$ of the $mn$ smaller rectangles and then tells James the area of each of the smaller rectangles. Of the $\\binom{mn}{m+n-1}$ possible combinations of rectangles and their areas Fuming Zeng could have given, let $C_{m,n}$ be the number of combinations which would allow James to determine the area of the whole rectangle. Given that \\[A=\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{C_{m,n}\\binom{m+n}{m}}{(m+n)^{m+n}},\\] then find the greatest integer less than $1000A$.", "true_answer": 1289, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "5