 9, 31612, 314, 220, 17773, 21, 42308, 94, 220, 41404, 1812, 220, 1353, 15, 364, 84787, 478, 314, 220, 17, 42923, 667, 21, 92, 533, 16, 42308, 94, 220, 41404, 533, 16, 314, 220, 38575, 1812, 220, 1353, 15, 364, 17045, 314, 220, 38575, 364, 4383, 25, 581, 7360, 8213, 2195, 350, 7914, 220, 15, 8, 60500, 472, 3582, 13, 34138, 6664, 480, 13, 9700, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 13], "n_tokens": 8895, "words": 5727, "band_lo": 2863.5, "band_hi": 11454.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse problem: W is infinite periodic word with period = minimal period length = 2^{2016}. That means W repeats a block P of length L = 2^{2016}, which is not composed of a smaller period. So P is a binary string of length L, not having a smaller period.\n\nWe consider all substrings (contiguous) of W (i.e., of infinite periodic sequence). A word U is special if U a, U b, aU, bU all appear as substrings in W.\n\nGiven that there are no special words of length > 2015. So all special words have length ≤ 2015.\n\nGoal: minimal possible number N of special words, given the constraints? So we can design W (choose P) with minimal number of special words, under condition that no special word >2015 length. Then compute minimal possible number of special words overall (including empty word). Then find N mod 1000.\n\nEssentially we need lower bound on number of special words forced by period length 2^{2016} and no long special words. And find minimal achievable.\n\nObservation: For any word U that appears in W, its extensions to left and right may also appear. A word is special if both left and right extensions with each letter a,b appear.\n\nThus, we need to count substrings such that both possible letters can appear on each side.\n\nWe can think of the de Bruijn graph of order k: vertices are words of length k that appear; edges correspond to length-(k+1) substrings. For a word U length m, condition that Ua appears => there is an edge from U to U' where we drop first letter? Actually consider suffix/prefix. For substring U, Ua appears => there exists occurrence of U followed by a. Similarly Ub. This means both possible continuations at the right are present. Similarly aU, bU means both possible left extensions.\n\nThus special words correspond to vertices in the (m)-order de Bruijn graph that have indegree 2 and outdegree 2 (with respect to the subgraph induced by substrings of W). Since alphabet size is 2, max indegree/outdegree each is 2. So special means both indegree and outdegree equal 2 (i.e., both letters possible left and right).\n\nWe have a periodic word with period L with no smaller period: it's a binary sequence that is aperiodic within L (i.e., a primitive word). We're to arrange it to minimize number of “full-degree” vertices of lengths up to 2015, while also ensuring that no vertex of length >2015 has indegree=outdegree=2 (i.e., there are no special longer than 2015). But we can have vertices of length >2015 with indegree/outdegree not both 2 (maybe missing left or right extensions). So the condition says that maximal length of a special word is ≤2015.\n\nGoal: minimize total number of special words (including empty word). Empty word is considered special, count 1.\n\nWe need minimal possible given constraints. Likely minimal N is 2^{2016} perhaps? Not.\n\nThink about constructing P such that the de Bruijn graph is a directed line (paths) rather than branching, to reduce full-degree vertices. The condition that both left and right extensions present requires that both possible extensions appear somewhere, not necessarily at same occurrence. So to avoid a word being special we can make at least one side missing a letter extension.\n\nThus we want to design a primitive word of length L such that for most substrings U up to length 2015, at least one of the four possibilities fails.\n\nWe may use property of binary words: For any substring U, the possible left letters are the letters preceding each occurrence of U in the periodic word; possible right letters similarly.\n\nGiven periodic infinite word with period L, occurrences are at positions modulo L. Each occurrence of U corresponds to an interval of length |U|; the left letter is the character preceding the start (position -1 mod L) and right letter the one after end (position+|U|). So left/right sets are determined by cyclic positions.\n\nThus we can view sliding window of length |U| over the cyclic sequence P; each window yields a substring. For each possible substring U that occurs, its left extensions are characters left of each occurrence; right extensions are characters right of each occurrence.\n\nGoal: minimize number of substrings that have both left letters a,b and right letters a,b.\n\nWe can think of constructing P as a binary necklace of length L that is “almost” like a De Bruijn sequence? Actually De Bruijn sequence of order k contains every length-k word exactly once, meaning each vertex would have outdegree/in-degree 1? Wait De Bruijn graph of order k each node is length k word; edges correspond to length-(k+1). In a De Bruijn sequence of order n, each length-n substring appears exactly once, and each length-(n-1) substring appears twice? Not exactly.\n\nBut we want minimal number of vertices with indegree=outdegree=2. Because a primitive binary word of length L is a Hamiltonian cycle in the de Bruijn graph of order (some m). Actually each length-(m) substring appears some number of times. If we choose sequence that is a \"binary m‑universal cycle\" covering all length m strings maybe many edges.\n\nTo minimize full-degree vertices, maybe we can choose P such that it's a \"binary necklace\" of length L that's a \"run-length limited\" pattern where there are long runs of same letter, which reduces branching.\n\nConsider a word formed by repeating pattern a^{M} b^{M} ... but minimal period large so we need full length L not repeating smaller pattern. We could take a word that consists of L/2 a's followed by L/2 b's? But that would have period 2 (since a...a b...b repeating? Actually if L is even, that word 'a^{L/2} b^{L/2}' has period maybe L? Check: shift by any smaller amount would not preserve. For instance, the word a^{n}b^{n} is primitive if n>0 because it's not repetitions of smaller block (since it has two runs). The minimal period is L because you need full length to rotate to same. Indeed shifting by less than L changes the distribution.\n\nBut our infinite periodic word repeats that block; does it have smaller period? Let's test: P = a^{L/2} b^{L/2}. If we shift by 1 position we get a^{L/2-1}b a b^{L/2-1} which is different, not equal to P. So minimal period is L indeed. So we can use that.\n\nNow determine special words: what substrings appear? There are runs of a's length L/2 and b's length L/2. Any substring that goes across the boundary (some a's then b's) appears. Let's analyze.\n\nFor a substring U consisting only of a's (i.e., a^k). Does it have both left extensions a and b? Its occurrences can be inside the a-run or ending at the boundary etc. Suppose U = a^k where k < L/2. It can appear inside the a-run; the left letter is a (if not at start of the run) except when the occurrence starts at the first a of the run (position 0 mod L); then left letter is the preceding character which is b (since boundary). Similarly right letter is a except when occurrence ends at the last a before b, then right is b. Thus both left extensions a and b appear (provided k ≤ L/2). For k less than L/2, there is at least one occurrence inside run (with left a, right a). Also at positions near boundary we get left b, right b, etc. In fact both left a,b and right a,b appear for any k such that there are occurrences that touch boundary on each side. For a^k where k up to L/2, there exists occurrence ending at the boundary producing right b; also occurrence starting at boundary producing left b. So Ua and Ub both appear? Right side: after a^k we need either a or b. For interior occurrence, right letter a. For occurrence that ends at the boundary (i.e., its last a is the last a before the b block), right letter is b. So both appear. Similarly left side.\n\nThus a^k is special for all k ≤ L/2? Provided both left extensions a and b appear. Yes.\n\nSimilarly b^k are special.\n\nThus we would have many special words length up to L/2 which is huge enormous >2015, contradict condition that no special word of length >2015. So such simple block fails.\n\nWe must design sequence where longest run of same letter before both left/right options appear is ≤2015.\n\nEssentially to avoid special words longer than 2015, for any substring of length >2015, at least one side missing a letter.\n\nThus the word must be such that for any word of length >2015, either only a can appear on left or only b, or only one right letter.\n\nThis suggests constructing a word where each occurrence of long substrings is \"unambiguous\" in context.\n\nPerhaps we can use aperiodic \"binary De Bruijn\" of order 2016: a De Bruijn sequence of order 2016 contains every length-2016 binary word exactly once (as substring). In such sequence, any length-2015 word appears exactly twice? Actually De Bruijn sequence of order n has length 2^n and each n-bit pattern appears exactly once cyclically. For any (n-1)-bit word, there are exactly two occurrences, corresponding to its two possible extensions (with a or b). In a De Bruijn sequence, each length-(n-1) word has both left and right extensions because different occurrences correspond to different extensions? Let's analyze.\n\nStandard De Bruijn sequence B_n: cyclic length 2^n. For each n-bit string s, it appears exactly once as consecutive bits. For each (n-1)-bit word w, there are exactly two n-bit superstrings: wa and wb, which each appear exactly once, and they correspond to the two occurrences of w in the sequence, with different following bits. So w has both right extensions a and b. Similarly left extensions: because occurrences of w correspond to preceding bits which are the first bits of the n-bit windows (the preceding bit). Since each n-bit string is unique, the two occurrences of w have different preceding bits (i.e., the bits that, together with w, form an n-bit string). Thus left extensions also both a and b. Therefore every (n-1)-bit word is special! Indeed indegree and outdegree both 2 for length n-1.\n\nThus in De Bruijn sequence of order 2016, all substrings of length 2015 are special, which violates condition \"no special words longer than 2015\" but length exactly 2015 allowed (since not greater). Actually condition: no special words of length greater than 2015. So length 2015 may be special. That's okay.\n\nThus a De Bruijn sequence reaches the maximal possible number of special words of length 2015 (2^{2015} many). But we want minimal possible number of special words overall while still having period 2^{2016} and primitive. So we want to avoid many specials, but we cannot avoid specials of length up to some? Maybe we must at least have all length <= k words special for some k? Need lower bound.\n\nObservation: For any word of length m, there are at most 2^{m} distinct substrings of that length (since binary). The number of special words of that length is equal to number of vertices with indegree=outdegree=2. For a primitive word of length L, the de Bruijn graph of order m is a subgraph of the full binary graph. The sum over all vertices of outdegree is equal to number of edges = number of length-(m+1) substrings. Since each length-(m+1) substring corresponds to an edge from its prefix (length m) to its suffix. So total edges = number of occurrences of length m+1 substrings (allow duplicates). In a length L cyclic word, there are exactly L occurrences of substrings of any fixed length ≤ L (counting cyclic). So total edges = L (each position gives a length-(m+1) substring). Therefore average outdegree per vertex = L / V_m, where V_m is number of distinct length-m substrings.\n\nSimilarly average indegree = same.\n\nWe need lower bound on number of vertices with both indegree and outdegree 2 given constraints on average maybe.\n\nLet V_m be number of distinct substrings of length m. Since the word is primitive, V_m = L for m = L (full period) maybe? Actually for m < L, V_m can be up to L (since each position gives a substring; could be all distinct). Minimal V_m is at least m+1? Not.\n\nBut we can try to minimize special words by making many vertices with indegree/outdegree 1.\n\nSuppose for each length m we try to have V_m = L (all substrings distinct), then each vertex appears exactly once as a window. Then each vertex has outdegree=1 and indegree=1 because each occurrence leads to unique preceding and following letters (the letters that belong to the sequence). Indeed if every length-m substring occurs exactly once, then there is a unique continuation to left and right, so indegree=outdegree=1. Therefore there would be no special words of length m. However this is impossible for m up to something because total substrings distinct impossible for large m given binary alphabet? Actually with length L=2^{2016}, it's possible to have all substrings of length m distinct for m up to 2016? For a binary string of length L, the maximum number of distinct substrings of length m is min(2^m, L). Since L = 2^{2016} is huge, for m≤2016, 2^m ≤ L, so we could achieve V_m = 2^m (all possible) but cannot exceed L. Distinctness possible, e.g., De Bruijn sequence achieves V_m = 2^m for m ≤ 2016? Actually De Bruijn sequence has each length-m word appear exactly 2^{m} / 2^{? Wait total substrings length m in cycle length 2^{n} is L = 2^{n}. For n=2016, L=2^{2016}. Number of possible length-m binary strings is 2^{m}. Since L >> 2^{m} for m<2016, one could have repeats. But De Bruijn has each length-m substring appear exactly 2^{n-m} times? Let's check: In cyclic De Bruijn sequence order n, each length-m word appears exactly 2^{n-m} times (since there are L substrings total, and each of the 2^{m} distinct words appears equally many times). Yes frequency is 2^{n-m}. So not unique.\n\nBut perhaps we can aim for V_m = L (i.e., each position gives distinct substring) for m up to something less than log2 L = 2016. Since there are only L positions, can have at most L distinct substrings of any length, which is fine. So we can make each length-m substring unique (no repeats) for any m up to L perhaps? But with binary alphabet, number of possible strings of length m is only 2^m; if L > 2^m, we can't have all L substrings distinct because there are only 2^m possibilities. So the max distinct is 2^m. For m up to 2016, 2^m ≤ L, so we could have all substrings distinct (i.e., each possible binary word appears at most once). That would make indegree/outdegree maybe varying.\n\nIf each length-m word appears at most once, then at most one left extension and one right extension; so indegree/outdegree ≤1. Could be 0 for words that don't appear. So there would be no special words of length m.\n\nThus to minimize special words, we can try to build a primitive word where for each length m ≤2015, each possible binary string appears at most once. Is that possible simultaneously for multiple m? Consistency constraints may make it impossible for all m up to 2015.\n\nIf each length-m substring appears at most once, then the word must be a \"binary sequence with no repeats\" for each length. But having a repeat of length m may force repeat of longer lengths? Not necessarily.\n\nNotably a De Bruijn sequence has many repeats, because each word repeats many times.\n\nWe can attempt to design a word that is like a binary \"Lyndon\" word? The lexicographically minimal primitive word of length L? Not.\n\nWe need lower bound: maybe it's mandatory that there are at least 2^{2015} special words (all length 2015) because L=2^{2016} ensures each length 2015 word appears exactly twice (pigeonhole). Let's evaluate.\n\nGiven cyclic binary word of length L = 2^{2016} with minimal period L. Consider substrings of length 2015. There are L such substrings (cyclic). Number of possible distinct length-2015 strings is 2^{2015} which is exactly half of L. Thus by pigeonhole, each such possible string must appear either 0,1, or 2 times. Since total occurrences = L = 2 * 2^{2015}, the average count is 2. Thus each possible length-2015 word appears exactly twice. Because you can't have a word appear 3 times without another appearing less than 2, but possible to have some appear once and some three times balancing? Could happen.\n\nBut given binary alphabet, can you have distribution other than uniform? Let's consider: sum over all words w of count(w) = L = 2^{2016}. Average = 2. Some could be 0, some >2, but due to maximal distinct is 2^{2015}, you need at least 2^{2015} total distinct substrings due to primitive requirement maybe? Not necessarily; you can have fewer distinct substrings length 2015.\n\nThe maximum distinct is 2^{2015}. Minimum distinct could be L - (something). However there is constraint of minimal period L: the word is primitive, meaning the cyclic word is not composed of repeats. That doesn't restrict length-2015 substrings heavily.\n\nBut maybe we can show that every length-2015 word must appear exactly twice, due to balance of indegrees/outdegrees? Let's think.\n\nEach length-2015 substring w occurs some number c_w times. For each occurrence, it contributes one left extension letter (the preceding char) and one right extension. The total number of right extension occurrences across all w is L (each position defines next char for the length-2015 window). That equals sum over w of c_w (since each occurrence). So sum c_w = L.\n\nSimilarly, total number of occurrences of length-2016 substrings (i.e., windows of length 2016) equals L as well; but each length-2016 substring is uniquely defined by a length-2015 prefix plus a right char. So number of distinct length-2016 substrings equals number of distinct pairs (w, right letter). Since each w can be followed by at most 2 letters, total distinct length-2016 substrings ≤ 2 * (number of distinct w). However sum of c_w (which counts occurrences) = L.\n\nIf some w occurs only once, then its right extension is unique; but we must also consider left extensions.\n\nBut we want to minimize number of special w (both sides 2). Perhaps we could have many w with c_w =1, leading to indegree/outdegree possibly 1. But need sum counts = L huge, so need many repeats.\n\nSince total count is L, and number of possible w is at most 2^{2015}, average is 2. So many w must have count 2, some maybe 1, etc. To have many w with count 1 would require other w with count >2 (since average 2). But a w with count >2 would have both left/right maybe >1 leading to a special? Not necessarily, but if a w appears more than 2 times, it must have left letters maybe some repeats.\n\nBut can a word appear 3 times with only one left letter? Possibly if three occurrences have same left letter but different right letters? But each occurrence contributes a particular pair (left, right). With three occurrences, pigeonhole suggests at least one side repeats; could still have at most two distinct left letters total, but could be only one. So may not become special.\n\nBut we need lower bound on number of w where both left letters appear and both right letters appear. Perhaps because of counting constraints, there must be at least something.\n\nI suspect minimal number of special words = number of length ≤2015 words that are forced to be special due to combinatorial reasons, which might be exactly 2^{2015}. But that seems huge; mod 1000 would be 2^{2015} mod 1000.\n\nCompute 2^{2015} mod 1000? 2^φ(125)=2^{100} mod125 = 1. Similarly mod8? 2^{>=3} ≡0 mod8. Use CRT.\n\nBut we need maybe fewer. Let's attempt to derive minimal number.\n\nObservation: For any length-m word w that appears, the set of left letters L_w and right letters R_w are subsets of {a,b}. If |L_w|=2 and |R_w|=2 then w special. So to avoid being special, we need either |L_w|≤1 or |R_w|≤1.\n\nDefine \"good\" words where both sets equal 2.\n\nGoal minimize count of such words length ≤2015.\n\nConsider building Y as a directed path in de Bruijn graph of order 0? Another approach: Use a word that is a \"binary concatenation of a long a-run then a long b-run then ...\"? But earlier those gave specials of many lengths.\n\nMaybe we can construct W as concatenation of the binary representation of numbers 0..(L-1) maybe produce unique contexts.\n\nI think the minimal number of special words might be exactly 2^{2015}+1 (including empty). Let's compute mod 1000.\n\n2^{2015} mod 1000: compute.\n\nCompute modulo 8 and 125.\n\nModulo 8: for n≥3, 2^n ≡0 mod8. Since 2015>3, so mod8 =0.\n\nModulo 125: use Euler φ(125)=100. So 2^{2015} = 2^{ (20*100) + 15 } because 2015 = 20*100 +15. So 2^{2015} ≡ (2^{100})^{20} *2^{15} ≡1^{20}*2^{15} mod125. Compute 2^{10}=1024≡1024-8*125=1024-1000=24. So 2^{10}≡24. Then 2^{15}=2^{10}*2^{5}≡24 *32 = 768 ≡ 768-6*125=768-750=18. So 2^{15}≡18 mod125.\n\nThus we have x≡0 mod8, x≡18 mod125.\n\nFind solution modulo 1000 (lcm 1000). Use CRT.\n\nWe need number N = 2^{2015}.\n\nLet N = 125k +18. Want N ≡0 mod8 => (125k+18) mod8 = (5k+2) mod8 (since125≡5,18≡2). So need 5k+2 ≡0 mod8 =>5k ≡6 mod8 => multiply inverse of5 mod8 which is 5 (since5*5=25≡1). So k ≡5*6=30≡30 mod8 =>30 mod8 =6. So k ≡6 mod8. Let k = 6 +8t. Then N =125(6+8t)+18 = 750+1000t+18 =768 +1000t. So modulo 1000, N ≡768.\n\nThus 2^{2015} mod1000 = 768.\n\nAdd empty word count 1 => N = 2^{2015}+1 ≡ 769 mod1000.\n\nBut is that minimal? Might need also count special words of lengths <2015, maybe forced to exist as well.\n\nIf all length-2015 words are special (2^{2015}), plus empty, maybe also some shorter ones must be special. For instance, if a word of length 2015 is special, then its prefixes/suffixes may also be special? Not necessarily: a prefix of a special word might have both extensions because you can extend it