left letters and also different right letters (so each word gets both sides). That would make all length-2015 words special, as desired.\n\nNow consider a length-2014 word u. In the word, each occurrence corresponds to positions within two occurrences of its length-2015 extensions. Since each length-2015 word extends u by either a or b at the right. For a given u, there are maybe many length-2015 extensions (up to 2). Actually each u can be extended to u a and u b, both are length-2015 words. If both of those exist (they may, because all length-2015 words exist), then u will have right extensions a and b (since you can take occurrence of u that is prefix of ua or ub). Similarly left extensions may arise from words of length 2015 that have u as suffix.\n\nThus any length-2014 word will also be special if both its possible extensions (adding a/b on either side) appear somewhere. Since all length-2015 words exist, both extensions exist, meaning u will get both right extensions. And also left extensions because for each of the two length-2015 words having u as suffix (i.e., a u and b u), the preceding letters could be a or b due to property for those longer words. It seems cascade leads to all shorter lengths being special as well.\n\nHence making all length-2015 words special forces many shorter words special inevitably.\n\nThus minimal number likely larger.\n\nWe need systematic lower bound using counting arguments like number of vertices with indegree=outdegree=2 must be at least something.\n\nConsider the directed graph of order m for each m ≤2015. Each vertex has indegree+outdegree = total edges incident? Not.\n\nObservation: For each occurrence of length-(m+1) substring, it contributes 1 to indegree of its suffix (length m) and 1 to outdegree of its prefix (length m). So sum of indegrees over all vertices at level m equals total occurrences = L. Similarly sum of outdegrees = L.\n\nLet S_m be set of special vertices of length m. For vertex v ∈ S_m indegree=2 and outdegree=2. For non-special vertices, either indegree ≤1 or outdegree ≤1 (or both 0). Let I_v + O_v denote indegree+outdegree. For a special vertex, sum=4. For others, maximum sum≤3? Actually indegree could be 2 and outdegree 1 => sum 3; or 1+2. Or 1+1 =2; etc.\n\nTotal sum over all vertices of (indeg+outdeg) = 2L (since sum indegree = L, sum outdegree = L). So Σ_v (indeg+outdeg) =2L.\n\nLet V_m = number of distinct length-m substrings that appear (i.e., vertices). Let s_m = |S_m| (special count length m). Then we have 2L = Σ_v (indeg+outdeg) ≤ s_m*4 + (V_m - s_m)*3 (since max for non-special is 3). Actually could be 2 for some vertices, but max 3 for non-special because at least one of indegree/outdegree ≤1 so sum≤3. Therefore:\n\n2L ≤ 4 s_m + 3 (V_m - s_m) = 3 V_m + s_m.\n\nThus s_m ≥ 2L - 3 V_m.\n\nSince V_m ≤ min(L, 2^m). For m ≤ 2015, 2^m ≤ L (since L=2^{2016}). So V_m ≤ 2^m.\n\nThus s_m ≥ 2L - 3*2^m.\n\nBut L = 2^{2016}. Therefore s_m ≥ 2 * 2^{2016} - 3*2^m = 2^{2017} - 3*2^m.\n\nBut note s_m cannot exceed V_m = 2^m. So this lower bound is meaningful only when RHS positive.\n\nCompute for m=2015: RHS = 2^{2017} - 3*2^{2015} = 2^{2015}(2^2 -3) = 2^{2015}(4-3)= 2^{2015}. So s_{2015} ≥ 2^{2015}. Since V_{2015} = at most 2^{2015}, we get s_{2015}=2^{2015}. So all possible length-2015 words must be special! Good.\n\nFor m=2014: RHS = 2^{2017} - 3*2^{2014} = 2^{2014}(2^3 -3) = 2^{2014}(8-3)=5*2^{2014}. Since V_{2014} ≤ 2^{2014}, RHS > V_{2014}. But we can't have s_m exceed V_m. The inequality derived may be too weak because we used max 3 for non-special; but maybe some vertices have sum ≤2, making RHS larger (i.e., need more specials). Actually to achieve the total sum 2L we need enough specials; if non-special sums are at most 2 (if either indegree=0 or outdegree=0), the bound becomes stronger. However we used max 3, which gives lower bound on specials; but here lower bound exceeds possible maximum V_m, implying that our assumption of max 3 is insufficient, perhaps we need better bound: For a vertex to be non-special, at least one of indegree/outdegree ≤1. Could both be 2? No. But sum could be 3 (2+1). So bound stands. Since RHS > V_m, we deduce that to satisfy sum, we need more vertices than exist, contradiction. Therefore our assumption that V_m ≤ 2^m perhaps wrong? Actually V_{2014} could be larger because positions L=2^{2016} > 2^{2014}; but number of distinct substrings cannot exceed 2^{2014}. That's fixed. So contradiction implies our earlier inequality must be tightened: maybe some non-special vertices can have sum=4 as well? Not possible because that would be special. So maybe our sum bound must be exactly 2L = Σ (indeg+outdeg). Since each vertex indegree + outdegree at least 2? Not; a vertex could have indegree=0 and outdegree=2 (if appears but preceded always same letter). That's sum=2. So minimal sum per vertex is at least 2? Actually a vertex must have at least one occurrence; then it has at least one left extension (its predecessor) and one right extension (its successor). So indegree ≥1 and outdegree≥1? Wait if a word occurs, there is at least one occurrence => there is a left letter preceding that occurrence, giving an incoming edge from left neighbor (the length-m word formed by dropping first letter of length-(m+1) substring ending at that occurrence). That left neighbor may be same as v or another vertex. So indegree is number of distinct left letters; but could be 1. So indegree≥1. Similarly outdegree≥1. Hence indegree+outdegree ≥2. So sum per vertex at least 2.\n\nThus each vertex contributes at least 2, at most 4, with special being 4. So we have average sum = (2L)/V_m.\n\nThus we can compute needed proportion of specials to achieve average.\n\nLet average = A = 2L / V_m.\n\nGiven that indeg+outdeg is at most 4, at least 2. Let x be fraction of specials. Then overall sum = 4 s + at most 3 (V - s) (if we assume others max 3). For lower bound of s we can use worst-case where non-special vertices have sum=3 (max). That gives smallest s needed. So inequality earlier is correct.\n\nNow for m=2014, V=2^{2014}. Compute RHS = 2L - 3V = 2^{2017} - 3*2^{2014} = 2^{2014}(2^3 -3) =5*2^{2014}. Since RHS > V, lower bound cannot be satisfied; meaning assumption that each non-special vertex can have sum up to 3 is insufficient to meet total sum; we need more sum, but max per vertex is 4, so we need even more specials. Actually to achieve total sum 2L, we need at least certain number of specials s such that 4s + 3(V - s) >= 2L, solving s >= 2L - 3V, as before. If RHS > V, then inequality cannot be met, meaning our earlier assumption that V ≤ 2^m is false? Wait we used V_m = number of distinct substrings length m that appear. Could it be larger (up to L)? For m=2014, L = 2^{2016} > 2^{2014}. But number of possible distinct length-2014 binary strings is 2^{2014}. So V cannot exceed that. So inequality impossible unless our assumption that each non-special sum max is 3 is too high; maybe some non-special vertices can have sum>3? Not possible if not special (cannot have both indegree=2 and outdegree=2); still could have indegree=2 and outdegree=2 but but left and right sets each size 2 but not independent? That is still special by definition (both appear). So not.\n\nThus we see contradiction, meaning that our earlier deduction that s_{2015}=2^{2015} leads to consequences that for shorter lengths, number of specials must also be full. Indeed the inequality shows that for m=2014 we must have s_{2014} ≥ V_{2014} (i.e., all vertices special). So all length-2014 words must also be special. By induction, all lengths down to 0 are special. So N = total number of distinct substrings of all lengths ≤2015 plus empty? Wait empty included. So total specials would be sum_{m=0}^{2015} V_m where V_m is number of distinct substrings length m appearing, which likely is 2^m for each m (since for length 2015 we have all 2^{2015} distinct; similarly for shorter lengths we must have all possible binary strings appear). So total number N = sum_{m=0}^{2015} 2^m = 2^{2016} -1. Plus empty counted? The empty word corresponds to m=0, where 2^0 =1 includes empty? Actually length 0 distinct substrings count is 1 (the empty word). So sum includes empty. So N = 2^{2016} -1? Wait sum_{m=0}^{2015} 2^m = 2^{2016} -1.\n\nThus minimal possible number of special words is at least 2^{2016} -1. Can it be achieved? If we can construct a word where all possible binary strings of length ≤2015 appear, and each is special, that would meet bound and likely minimal (since lower bound forces all). So N minimal = 2^{2016} -1.\n\nWe need to verify that construction exists respecting period minimal L=2^{2016} and no special words longer than 2015 (i.e., length 2016 not special). In such construction, length-2016 substrings may not be special (they may have only one left/right). Since we have L=2^{2016} distinct length-2016 substrings (each position gives possibly repeats). Number of possible length-2016 binary strings is 2^{2016}. If we make the word be a De Bruijn sequence of order 2016, then all length-2016 words appear exactly once, and each length-2015 word is special (as argued). Moreover length-2016 words are not special because each occurs once, giving only one left and one right extension (since each occurrence's left and right letters are determined, but could be same? However each length-2016 word appears exactly once, thus its left extensions are determined by the preceding character which is the first bit of the preceding length-2016 window; but that preceding character could be either a or b, but will be unique, so left set size 1, similarly right set size 1 => not special. So no special words of length 2016. Also longer lengths >2016 repeat due to periodicity maybe also not special.\n\nThus the De Bruijn sequence of order 2016 satisfies condition: no special words longer than 2015? Wait length 2016 is not special; length >2016 also not special because they are repeats of length-2016 pattern? For length >2016, the word will have repeats; but could there be special words longer? likely not because context predetermined. Since sequence is primitive, longest repeats may cause branching? However we need to ensure no special words of length >2015. In De Bruijn sequence, any word of length >2015 appears at most (maybe) 2^{?} times? But could some length 2017 word be special? Let's examine length 2017 substring. Since period length is 2^{2016}, any length >L will repeat pattern, but indegree/outdegree? Not sure.\n\nBut requirement only says there are no special words of length greater than 2015. De Bruijn sequence of order 2016 likely satisfies that because special words correspond to vertices with indegree=outdegree=2, which for length >2015 cannot happen because the graph's branching only occurs up to order n-1 (where each vertex has degree 2). For length n (2016), each vertex has indegree=outdegree=1 (since each n-length word appears exactly once). For longer lengths >n, each vertex corresponds to sequence of length >n, which repeats some n-length pattern, and its indegree/outdegree also 1. So no specials beyond 2015. Good.\n\nThus minimal N = total number of special words = sum_{m=0}^{2015} 2^m = 2^{2016} -1.\n\nCompute N mod 1000.\n\nWe already computed 2^{2015} mod 1000 = 768. Need 2^{2016} = 2 * 2^{2015} ≡ 2*768 = 1536 ≡ 536 mod 1000.\n\nThus N = 2^{2016} -1 ≡ 536 -1 = 535 mod 1000.\n\nAnswer = 535.\n\nCheck: we included empty word (length 0) counted as special. Sum includes it. Good.\n\nThus final answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 535", "answer": 535, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.861018856492592e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999971389852362, "logp_correct": -2.861018856492592e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6767303181611118, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4752081020732817, "frac_below_-5": 0.014525391284765229, "digit_lp_min": -5.006715297698975, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 8881, "lp_end": -8.344646857949556e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -0.0002224197960458696, "logp_nocot": -7.848428685218096, "p_nocot": 0.0003903648726885398, "uplift": 7.848425824199239}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "ad1c76e8-6097-4ffb-ae5c-aa00d586ff69", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Consider polynomials $P$ of degree $2015$, all of whose coefficients are in the set \\{0,1,\\dots,2010\\}. Call such a polynomial \"good\" if for every integer $m$, one of the numbers $P(m)-20$, $P(m)-15$, $P(m)-1234$ is divisible by $2011$, and there exist integers $m_{20}, m_{15}, m_{1234}$ such that $P(m_{20})-20$, $P(m_{15})-15$, $P(m_{1234})-1234$ are all multiples of $2011$. Let $N$ be the number of good polynomials. Find the remainder when $N$ is divided by $1000$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Consider polynomials $P$ of degree $2015$, all of whose coefficients are in the set \\{0,1,\\dots,2010\\}. Call such a polynomial \"good\" if for every integer $m$, one of the numbers $P(m)-20$, $P(m)-15$, $P(m)-1234$ is divisible by $2011$, and there exist integers $m_{20}, m_{15}, m_{1234}$ such that $P(m_{20})-20$, $P(m_{15})-15$, $P(m_{1234})-1234$ are all multiples of $2011$. Let $N$ be the number of good polynomials. Find the remainder when $N$ is divided by $1000$.", "true_answer": 460, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "ad1c76e8-6097-4ffb-ae5c-aa00d586ff69:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 3605, 1823, 174033, 9882, 220, 667, 20, 483, 76338, 306, 354, 15, 35229, 11, 667, 15, 92, 2238, 484, 395, 1753, 16336, 284, 11, 540, 5153, 1001, 328, 290, 3407, 8663, 398, 2145, 14080, 455, 11, 398, 2145, 14080, 1055, 11, 398, 2145, 14080, 7633, 19, 382, 167813, 656, 220, 667, 16, 13, 1958, 1217, 1354, 4310, 4857, 284, 885, 1919, 2454, 43992, 84, 1082, 18305, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 2454, 1432, 382, 23175, 741, 197203, 316, 25, 2214, 722, 284, 11, 398, 2145, 8, 112372, 220, 667, 16, 33063, 316, 290, 920, 336, 314, 354, 455, 11, 1055