, 16290, 28, 1353, 11, 51159, 1812, 220, 1353, 871, 220, 667, 16, 1812, 1353, 314, 220, 994, 13, 2632, 220, 17, 42923, 994, 92, 1812, 10676, 364, 17, 61, 20, 28, 1398, 13, 220, 17, 61, 21, 28, 2220, 13, 220, 17, 61, 22, 28, 8076, 42308, 94, 18, 13, 220, 17, 61, 23, 28, 18, 9, 17, 28, 21, 13, 220, 17, 61, 24, 28, 899, 13, 220, 17, 61, 702, 28, 1494, 13, 220, 17, 61, 994, 28, 3519, 364, 84787, 220, 17, 42923, 994, 92, 42308, 94, 3519, 1812, 10676, 364, 10620, 23864, 355, 314, 220, 18, 42923, 1008, 92, 533, 220, 18, 9, 17, 42923, 1008, 92, 659, 18, 42308, 94, 220, 1709, 533, 18, 9, 3519, 659, 18, 314, 220, 1709, 533, 15363, 659, 18, 314, 350, 1161, 8, 533, 15363, 314, 533, 15970, 42308, 94, 220, 10676, 12, 15970, 30, 24305, 533, 15970, 1812, 10676, 314, 220, 21, 13, 13602, 220, 10676, 12, 15970, 314, 21, 13, 41351, 533, 15970, 42308, 94, 220, 21, 1812, 10676, 364, 4383, 25, 220, 1709, 10, 18, 28, 1161, 11, 220, 1161, 12, 15363, 314, 533, 15970, 11, 1147, 220, 10676, 871, 21, 364, 84787, 355, 42308, 94, 21, 1812, 10676, 11, 326, 355, 42308, 94, 21, 1812, 23, 364, 84787, 355, 42308, 94, 21, 112372, 305, 7871, 7, 23, 11, 10676, 25987, 1353, 15, 350, 21910, 2973, 3644, 220, 21, 741, 2632, 355, 1812, 1353, 15, 314, 220, 21, 364, 19936, 0, 2632, 355, 314, 21, 364, 10620, 478, 1812, 1353, 15, 314, 350, 32, 1812, 1353, 15, 8, 425, 220, 25248, 1812, 1353, 15, 314, 220, 21, 9, 25248, 314, 220, 23488, 15, 1812, 1353, 15, 314, 220, 28623, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 28623, 364, 10948, 30411, 484, 1354, 382, 860, 5018, 7060, 1078, 76338, 2447, 3760, 1572, 503, 12629, 316, 220, 667, 15, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 1023, 553, 36432, 316, 2617, 54912, 11, 625, 56424, 115502, 741, 3072, 3630, 290, 920, 382, 9707, 115502, 220, 15, 485, 667, 15, 11, 2454, 80677, 14518, 9707, 4730, 11, 14531, 2454, 64656, 665, 413, 1062, 80677, 26, 1354, 382, 860, 5018, 44762, 364, 19734, 6757, 484, 8117, 64656, 2447, 2893, 79097, 112372, 220, 667, 16, 1217, 126205, 64656, 72554, 667, 15, 326, 2893, 79097, 11, 1899, 364, 16577, 25, 312, 8117, 64656, 2893, 79097, 30005, 9882, 220, 667, 20, 22476, 11, 889, 1217, 1309, 6062, 113464, 76338, 316, 413, 3518, 220, 15, 485, 667, 15, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 35441, 2684, 11608, 741, 12265, 581, 12871, 285, 314, 329, 659, 350, 87, 42923, 667, 16, 92, 533, 1215, 8, 71, 11, 290, 20547, 76338, 1340, 625, 23517, 413, 306, 484, 3352, 2236, 1023, 553, 46716, 472, 73121, 328, 115502, 26, 889, 2454, 64656, 382, 261, 3259, 3577, 350, 15, 485, 667, 15, 741, 12265, 5856, 306, 3259, 66938, 316, 16336, 5856, 112372, 220, 667, 16, 26, 290, 20547, 64656, 738, 413, 306, 220, 15, 485, 667, 15, 472, 448, 16336, 25389, 290, 80677, 13, 23586, 25435, 364, 84787, 1062, 7158, 328, 329, 326, 312, 1072, 3259, 66938, 316, 5746, 920, 328, 16336, 76338, 220, 15, 485, 667, 15, 364, 198140, 3609, 3605, 478, 472, 5151, 364, 10620, 23864, 1721, 6052, 47056, 220, 1353, 15, 314, 220, 28623, 364, 58369, 4733, 13], "n_tokens": 3300, "words": 1477, "band_lo": 739.25, "band_hi": 2957.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to count polynomials degree 2015 with coefficients in {0,...,2010} such that for every integer m, at least one of the three numbers P(m)-20, P(m)-15, P(m)-1234 is divisible by 2011. And also there exist specific m's where each congruence holds (i.e., each value is realized). Equivalent to: For all m, P(m) modulo 2011 belongs to the set S = {20,15,1234} (mod 2011)? Wait condition: for each m, at least one of P(m)-20, P(m)-15, P(m)-1234 divisible by 2011 => P(m) ≡ 20 or 15 or 1234 (mod 2011). So the image of P modulo 2011 is subset of S (size 3). Additionally, there must exist m achieving each residue (20,15,1234) at least once. So P takes each of those three residues for some integer m.\n\nThus we need to count polynomials with coefficients 0..2010 (i.e., each coefficient modulo 2011 is same range) of degree 2015 such that the function Z → Z/2011 defined by evaluating polynomial mod 2011 only takes values in S and hits each.\n\nSince coefficients are in that set, they are considered as integers not modulo, but modulo 2011 they are just any residues 0..2010 (since 2011 is prime). So each coefficient can be any residue modulo 2011. However the coefficient can't be 2011 (which would be 0 same as 0). So coefficient set is all residues modulo 2011 exactly once. So there are 2011 choices per coefficient. Good.\n\nThus counting polynomials modulo 2011 essentially any polynomial of degree ≤2015 with leading coefficient non-zero (i.e., not 0 mod 2011). Since degree exactly 2015 (leading coefficient not zero mod 2011). So number of polynomials (without condition) is (2011-1) * (2011)^{2015} = 2010 * 2011^{2015}. But we need those satisfying condition.\n\nWe work modulo 2011: consider polynomial f(x) in F_{2011}[x] of degree 2015 with leading coefficient non-zero. Condition: f(F_{2011}) ⊆ S (as subset of F_{2011}) and hits each of the three values.\n\nSince property is about integer m, and modulo 2011 polynomial values for all integers depend only on class modulo 2011 (since coefficients modulo 2011). So condition equivalent to for all a in F_{2011}, f(a) ∈ S, and the image is exactly S (or at least each element appears for some a). Good.\n\nThus count of such polynomials over finite field with degree 2015, leading coeff ≠0, and image contained in S and hits each.\n\nLet’s denote S = {α,β,γ} where α=20, β=15, γ=1234 (mod 2011). Need f: F_q → S surjective onto S.\n\nWe need to count number of functions represented by polynomials degree ≤2015 with leading coeff non-zero and mapping set F_q (q=2011) into S and surjective.\n\nSince q is prime, field size is 2011 > degree, so polynomial is uniquely determined by its values on all q points? Not necessarily; there are many polynomials that agree on all points (difference divisible by x^q - x). Since degree less than q, the polynomial is uniquely determined by values (by interpolation). Here degree is 2015 > q=2011. So degree 2015 > q, thus there could be multiple polynomials giving same function (by adding multiples of x^{q} - x). But we restrict degree exactly 2015 with leading coefficient non-zero. Need to count polynomials of that specific degree satisfying the functional condition.\n\nApproach: Represent any polynomial f(x) of degree ≤2015 as g(x) + (x^q - x)h(x) where deg g < q (≤2010) and h(x) degree ≤ 2015 - q =4 (since q=2011). Because x^q - x has degree q, leading coefficient 1. Then degree of f is max(deg g, deg h + q). Since we require deg f = 2015, we likely need deg h =4 (since q+4=2015) and leading coefficient of h must be non-zero (since deg f determined by h term). Also g can be any polynomial of degree < q.\n\nWe have f(x) = g(x) + (x^{2011} - x)h(x). Where deg g ≤2010, deg h ≤4.\n\nCondition on values mod 2011: for any a∈F_q, note that a^{q}=a (by Fermat), thus a^{q} - a = 0. Hence f(a) = g(a). So the function values are determined solely by g, i.e., the restriction of f to field points is exactly g evaluated at those points. So we need g:F_q→S surjective onto S.\n\nThus we can choose any g of degree ≤2010 with image subset S and surjective. Then choose h of degree at most 4 such that the resulting f has degree exactly 2015 (i.e., coefficient of x^{2015} non-zero). Since (x^{2011} - x)h contributes degree deg h + 2011 unless cancellation with g's leading term. But deg g ≤2010, so highest degree term of f comes from h term. Need leading coefficient of h (coeff of x^{4}) such that resulting leading coefficient of f (coeff of x^{2015}) is non-zero (i.e., not zero mod 2011). Since leading term of (x^{2011} - x)h where h has term a x^4 => leading term a x^{2015}. No other contributions to x^{2015} from g. So condition is a ≠0 (mod 2011). Hence h must have degree exactly 4 with leading coefficient non-zero. So h must be any polynomial of degree 4 with leading coeff ≠0 (i.e., any polynomial degree ≤4 but with degree exactly 4). Number of such h over F_q: (q-1) * q^{4}?? Let's see: choose coefficients for degrees 0..3 arbitrarily (q choices each), and leading coefficient a4 non-zero (q-1 choices). So total q^{4} * (q-1) = (q-1) q^{4}.\n\nThus for each suitable g, there are (q-1) q^{4} possible h giving degree 2015 polynomial f.\n\nNow need to count number of g polynomials of degree ≤2010 (any degree allowed, could be less) that as functions map F_q onto S (surjectively). Note that polynomials and functions coincide for degree < q; there is a one-to-one correspondence between functions F_q→F_q and polynomials of degree ≤ q-1 (unique). So number of such g equals number of functions from F_q to S that are surjective onto S (i.e., each of three values appears at least once). Since there is a unique polynomial of degree ≤2010 representing each function, count is number of surjective functions.\n\nThus we need count of surjective functions from a set of size q=2011 to a set of size 3 (named S), i.e., 3^{q} - 3*2^{q} + 3*1^{q} (by inclusion-exclusion). In general number of onto functions onto k elements = k^n - C(k,1)(k-1)^n + C(k,2)(k-2)^n - ... . Here k=3, n=q.\n\nThus count = 3^{q} - 3*2^{q} + 3*1^{q} - 0? Actually inclusion-exclusion: total functions to 3 elements = 3^q. Subtract those missing at least one element: choose which element missing (3 ways) times functions to remaining 2 elements: 2^q. Add back those missing at least two elements: choose 2 missing (C3,2=3) times functions to remaining 1 element: 1^q =1. Subtract those missing all three (impossible). So onto count = 3^q - 3*2^q + 3.\n\nThus number of g = 3^{2011} - 3*2^{2011} + 3.\n\nNow total number N of good polynomials = (number g) * (q-1) q^{4}.\n\nN = (3^{q} - 3*2^{q} + 3) * (q-1) q^{4}, where q=2011.\n\nWe need N mod 1000.\n\nCompute modulo 1000.\n\nFirst reduce q modulo 1000: 2011 mod 1000 = 11. q-1 = 2010 mod 1000 = 10.\n\nq^{4} mod 1000: 11^4 = 14641. Mod 1000 => 641.\n\nThus (q-1) q^{4} mod 1000 = 10 * 641 = 6410 mod 1000 = 410.\n\nNow we need to compute A = 3^{2011} - 3*2^{2011} + 3 mod 1000.\n\nThen N mod 1000 = (A mod 1000) * 410 mod 1000.\n\nCompute A mod 1000.\n\nWe need powers modulo 1000. Since 1000=2^3*5^3. Use Carmichael λ(1000)=lcm( λ(8)=2, λ(125)=100) = lcm(2,100)=100. Actually λ(2^3)=2, λ(5^3)=100, so λ(1000)=100. So for numbers coprime to 1000, exponent cycle 100. 3 and 2 are coprime to 1000, fine.\n\nThus 3^{2011} mod 1000 = 3^{(2011 mod 100)} because 3^100≡1 mod 1000. Compute 2011 mod 100 = 11. So 3^{2011} mod 1000 = 3^{11} mod 1000.\n\nSimilarly 2^{2011} mod 1000: 2 is not coprime to 1000 (shares factor 2). Need compute directly using modular exponent with modulus 1000. Since powers of 2 modulo 1000 become zero after exponent≥? Actually 2^k mod 1000 eventually periodic after certain point, with factor 8. Since 2^3=8, we can compute.\n\nBut we can compute using Chinese remainder: compute modulo 8 and 125 then combine.\n\nBecause modulus 1000 = 8*125.\n\nFirst compute A modulo 8 and modulo 125, then combine; then multiply by 410 mod 1000.\n\nLet's compute modulo 8:\n\n- 3 mod 8 =3. 3^{any} mod8: 3^1=3, 3^2=9≡1, so period 2. 2011 odd => 3^{2011} ≡3 mod8.\n\n- 2^{2011} mod8: For exponent ≥3, 2^k ≡0 mod8. Since 2011≥3, => 0.\n\nThus A mod8 = 3 - 3*0 + 3 = 3+3 =6 mod8.\n\nNow modulo 125.\n\nWe need compute 3^{2011} mod125, 2^{2011} mod125.\n\nSince gcd(3,125)=1, φ(125)=100, so exponent reduce mod 100: 2011 mod 100 = 11. So 3^{2011} ≡ 3^{11} mod125.\n\nCompute 3^5=243 ≡ 243-125*1=118. 3^6 = 118*3=354 ≡ 354-125*2=104. 3^7=104*3=312 ≡ 312-125*2=62. 3^8=62*3=186 ≡ 61. 3^9=61*3=183 ≡ 58. 3^10=58*3=174 ≡ 49. 3^11=49*3=147 ≡ 22.\n\nThus 3^{11} ≡22 mod125.\n\nNow 2^{2011} mod125. Since gcd(2,125)=1, φ=100, exponent mod 100 => 2011 mod100 = 11. So 2^{11} mod125.\n\n2^5=32. 2^6=64. 2^7=128 ≡3. 2^8=3*2=6. 2^9=12. 2^10=24. 2^11=48.\n\nThus 2^{11} ≡48 mod125.\n\nNow compute A = 3^{...} - 3*2^{...} +3 ≡ 22 -3*48 +3 = 22 -144 +3 = (25) -144 = -119 ≡ 125-119? Wait -119 mod125 = 6. Because 125-119 =6. Indeed -119 ≡ 6 mod125.\n\nCheck: 22+3=25, 25-144 = -119, add 125 =>6.\n\nThus A ≡6 mod125, and A ≡6 mod8.\n\nThus A ≡6 modulo lcm(8,125)=1000 (since both give 6). So A mod1000 = 6.\n\nGreat! So A =6.\n\nNow N mod1000 = (A mod1000) * 410 mod1000 = 6*410 = 2460 mod1000 = 460.\n\nThus answer: 460.\n\nDouble-check that there is no extra condition about coefficients being less than or equal to 2010 (i.e., they are restricted to those integers, not arbitrary residues). But since the set is exactly residues 0..2010, each residue appears exactly once, yes each coefficient can be any residue; there is no extra constraint.\n\nAlso ensure that leading coefficient being non-zero modulo 2011 also satisfies coefficient ≤2010 and non-zero, good.\n\nEdge: h leading coefficient non-zero ensures degree 2015 indeed, but also need whole polynomial coefficients to be within 0..2010 (i.e., satisfy same bound). Since we construct f = g + (x^{2011} - x)h, the resulting coefficients may not necessarily be in that range because they are computed as sums of residues; but each coefficient is a field element (0..2010). Since addition in field corresponds to integer addition modulo 2011; the resulting coefficient will be in 0..2010 as an integer representing the residue. Thus okay.\n\nThus any choice of g and h over field corresponds to unique set of integer coefficients 0..2010.\n\nHence total count N as above.\n\nNow compute final answer remainder 1000 = 460.\n\nLet's output.", "response": "ANSWER: 460", "answer": 460, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.0265558760002023e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999979734461775, "logp_correct": -2.0265558760002023e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4325677375561114, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1684827212058053, "frac_below_-5": 0.006695069993913573, "digit_lp_min": -3.4087541103363037, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 3286, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -7.152555099310121e-07, "logp_nocot": -14.035833448171616, "p_nocot": 8.022597143314436e-07, "uplift": 14.03583142161574}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
