 12378, 77, 6796, 30, 26632, 297, 12378, 77, 6796, 483, 27933, 350, 72, 22712, 741, 38966, 135200, 395, 2454, 575, 25, 2569, 38360, 656, 441, 2238, 484, 350, 72, 71365, 1812, 297, 8, 16281, 10112, 30, 41021, 1746, 395, 297, 28, 19, 2360, 3478, 10853, 4997, 25987, 575, 138193, 1812, 297, 30, 4037, 364, 34013, 1199, 174595, 9113, 656, 25, 3478, 11637, 25, 1562, 8663, 472, 350, 74, 11, 372, 10, 16, 11, 126708, 297, 11, 220, 16, 11, 126708, 372, 12, 16, 8, 889, 1217, 125099, 9262, 395, 2454, 5983, 316, 8397, 95681, 13, 20064, 5542, 9770, 25, 2214, 297, 1952, 11, 665, 334, 65322, 658, 22305, 77, 18103, 92, 54467, 497, 651, 30, 4037, 364, 39520, 581, 679, 77486, 24140, 395, 220, 19, 11, 581, 29645, 24140, 395, 722, 297, 13, 1416, 665, 17717, 656, 66162, 25, 183159, 581, 679, 9770, 395, 297, 13, 10885, 395, 297, 10, 17, 30, 30456, 413, 364, 7943, 395, 6052, 11, 10562, 4149, 382, 220, 1353, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 1353, 364, 58369, 9570, 364], "n_tokens": 3260, "words": 2106, "band_lo": 1305.25, "band_hi": 5221.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have permutations of 1..100. We want a family Q such that for any ordered pair (a,b) where a,b distinct? Actually condition: \"for all 1 ≤ a, b ≤ 100, a can be found to the left of b and adjacent to b in at most one permutation in Q\". Means consider unordered? \"a can be found to the left of b and adjacent to b\" means a appears immediately before b (adjacent) in a permutation. So for each ordered pair (a,b) with a before b adjacent, at most one permutation in Q contains the pattern ... a,b ... So each ordered adjacent pair appears in at most one permutation.\n\nWe need maximum size of a family of permutations such that any ordered adjacent pair (i.e., consecutive elements with left element a and right element b) occurs at most once across all permutations.\n\nWe have n = 100. Each permutation has 99 adjacent ordered pairs (positions 1-2,2-3,...,99-100). So each permutation contributes 99 distinct ordered pairs. Across Q, total number of ordered adjacent pairs used ≤ total number of possible ordered adjacent pairs (i.e., 100*99 possible ordered pairs? Actually ordered pair (a,b) with a ≠ b; there are 100*99 =9900 possibilities. But adjacency also includes direction left->right. So each ordered pair allowed at most once. Therefore, we have bound: |Q| * 99 ≤ 9900 => |Q| ≤ 100. So upper bound 100.\n\nCan we achieve 100? Need a set of 100 permutations where each ordered pair appears at most once across permutations, using all 9900 possible ordered pairs exactly once across 100 permutations (since 100*99=9900). That's like a decomposition of the complete directed graph K_{100} (without loops) into 100 Hamiltonian paths (or directed Hamiltonian permutations) each using each vertex exactly once and using each directed edge exactly once across the set.\n\nWe need a decomposition of the complete directed tournament (complete digraph with both directions for each unordered pair) into 100 Hamiltonian paths (actually permutations correspond to Hamiltonian directed paths covering all vertices, i.e., Hamiltonian path with direction consistent left to right). Equivalent to find a set of 100 permutations such that each ordered pair appears exactly once (a before b adjacent). This is reminiscent of concepts of \"pairwise orthogonal Latin squares\" or \"complete set of Hamiltonian decompositions of K_n\". For directed complete graph K_n (with arcs both ways), there exists a decomposition into (n-1) directed Hamiltonian cycles when n is odd (called round-robin tournament schedule). For n even, you can also decompose the complete digraph into n-1 Hamiltonian cycles plus a 1-factor? Not exactly.\n\nBut we need Hamiltonian paths, not cycles. For even n, there is known decomposition of K_n into n/2 edge-disjoint Hamiltonian cycles? Let's think.\n\nWe want to partition the set of directed edges (ordered pairs) into 100 permutations (paths of length 99). Since each permutation uses exactly n-1 edges (directed). There are n(n-1) edges total, so we need n permutations (since n*(n-1) = n * (n-1) -> number of permutations = n?). Indeed n=100 gives 100 permutations. So we need a decomposition of the directed complete graph K_n (with both directions) into n Hamiltonian paths. Is that possible for any n? Let's search memory: \"complete directed graph decomposition into Hamiltonian paths\" known result: the complete directed graph K_n (with loops removed) can be decomposed into n-1 Hamiltonian cycles when n is odd, and into n-1 Hamiltonian cycles plus a perfect matching? Not sure.\n\nBut we need exactly n paths. Could use the following construction: For each k = 0,...,n-1 define permutation P_k as: order vertices according to (i + k) mod n? That's just a cyclic shift, but that yields the same adjacency pairs just shifted? Let's test: For n=4, permutations:\n\nP0: 1 2 3 4 gives adjacency pairs (1,2),(2,3),(3,4)\nP1: 2 3 4 1 gives (2,3),(3,4),(4,1)\nPairs (2,3) repeats -> not allowed. So cyclic shifts repeat same pairs.\n\nNeed each ordered pair appears exactly once across permutations. This is akin to constructing a \"complete set of permutations such that the list of adjacent ordered pairs forms a Latin square of order n\". Another viewpoint: Consider an n×(n-1) array where rows are permutations, columns positions. Adjacent pair (a,b) corresponds to a appears in column j and b in column j+1. Condition: each ordered pair appears at most once across all rows.\n\nOne known solution: Use permutations corresponding to \"De Bruijn sequence\"? Not.\n\nWe might try to construct using \"round robin tournament schedule\": For n even, create n-1 rounds each containing n/2 matches (unordered pairs). But we need ordered adjacency, not matching.\n\nWe could take each round as a permutation composed of pairing then ordering within each pair? Not.\n\nMaybe the maximum size is 100, and construction exists. Need to argue existence.\n\nConsider the set of permutations defined by: For each a from 1 to n, define permutation σ_a where σ_a(i) = (a*i mod n) with maybe 0 mapping to n. Since n is even, we need modulo n where 0 correspond to n. This is like multiplication by a modulo n. Does this produce each ordered pair exactly once? Let's test small n=3 (odd) using multiplication modulo 3: permutations: a=1 -> 1 2 3, pairs (1,2),(2,3). a=2 -> 1? Actually multiplication: σ_a(i)= (a*i mod n) with 0 replaced by n. For n=3, a=2: mapping i=1→2, i=2→1, i=3→3? Wait 2*3=6 mod3 =0 => n=3. So permutation: 2,1,3, pairs (2,1),(1,3). Combined pairs: (1,2),(2,3),(2,1),(1,3). Missing (3,1) and (3,2). So not covering all 6 possible ordered pairs (n(n-1)=6). Need 3 permutations each with 2 pairs =6 total. We have only 2 permutations? Actually we need n permutations =3. So maybe also a=0? Not permutation. So this set not enough.\n\nMaybe constructing using linear permutations total n permutations: For each k from 0 to n-1, define permutation π_k where π_k(i) = (i + k) mod n (with 0 as n). That's just shifts; we already saw repeats.\n\nConsider permutations based on \"Graeco-Latin squares\". Another approach: Represent each ordered pair (a,b) as a unique permutation index and position: we need to assign each ordered pair to a unique permutation and a unique position j (1 ≤ j ≤ n-1). This is like an edge coloring of the complete directed graph with colors = permutations and each color class forms a Hamiltonian path (i.e., the edges of each color must be a spanning directed path). Equivalent to edge-partition of the complete digraph into n directed Hamiltonian paths. Is this known to be possible for all n? There is result: The complete digraph K_n* (with arcs both ways) can be decomposed into n directed Hamiltonian cycles when n is odd, and into n-1 directed Hamiltonian cycles plus a 1-factor when n even. But path decomposition?\n\nIf we have a directed Hamiltonian cycle, we can break it by removing one edge to get a Hamiltonian path. If we have n cycles (for odd n) each using n edges, we would have n*n edges = n^2 edges, but complete digraph has n(n-1) edges, which is less when n>2. So cycles have extra edges. Not good.\n\nBut we could try: For even n, we can decompose the complete digraph into n-1 directed 2-factors each consisting of cycles covering all vertices (maybe one Hamiltonian cycle plus smaller cycles). Not.\n\nMaybe the maximum size is 99? Let's see if constructing 100 may be impossible due to parity constraints: each vertex appears as left element in some number of adjacent pairs across all permutations. In each permutation, each interior vertex appears as left in exactly one pair (the pair with its right neighbor) and as right in exactly one pair (the pair with its left neighbor). The first vertex appears only as left, the last only as right. So across all permutations, count total occurrences of each element as left neighbor = number of permutations where the element is not last. Similarly right neighbor occurrences = number where not first.\n\nSince each ordered pair (a,b) uses a as left, b as right. Total left-occurrences across all permutations = |Q| * (n-1) (since each permutation contributes n-1 left occurrences). Similarly total right-occurrences = same.\n\nThus each vertex a appears as left some L_a times, and as right some R_a times, with sum_{a} L_a = |Q|(n-1). Each vertex can be left at most (n-1) times because there are n-1 possible right neighbors and each pair distinct, but could be less. Similarly for right.\n\nTo reach the bound |Q| = n, we need each vertex to appear as left exactly n-1 times (since total left occurrences = n*(n-1) and there are n vertices). Similarly each appears as right exactly n-1 times. That implies that each vertex must be first element in exactly 0 permutations? Wait first element appears as left but never right. Actually first element appears as left in its outgoing pair (if n>1). For being left exactly (n-1) times, it must be first element in ? each permutation where it appears not last. Since each permutation has exactly one first element (which appears as left) and one last element (appears as right). Thus total number of times vertices appear as first element = |Q| (since each permutation contributes one first). Similarly last element count = |Q|.\n\nIf each vertex appears as left n-1 times, then total left occurrences = n(n-1) = |Q|(n-1). So |Q| = n indeed. Also each vertex appears as left n-1 times; because each vertex appears in each permutation somewhere, and except when it is last, it contributes a left occurrence. So each vertex must be last exactly once across all permutations, because in each permutation each vertex appears exactly once. Number of times vertex appears as last = #permutations - L_a (since total appearances = |Q|). So if L_a = n-1 = |Q|-1, then last occurrences = 1. Similarly each vertex appears as first exactly once (since right occurrences are n-1, implies first appears once). Indeed for each vertex, right occurrences is n-1 => not first exactly once. So each vertex must be first exactly once, last exactly once across the set of permutations. That's plausible.\n\nThus we need a set of n permutations where each element appears exactly once as first and once as last. This is similar to constructing a \"complete set of permutations\" forming a schedule where each ordered pair appears exactly once and each vertex appears first once and last once.\n\nIs such a decomposition known as a \"pairwise orthogonal Latin square of order n\"? Might be possible for any n.\n\nConstructive method: Represent vertices as elements of Z_n (0..n-1). For each k in Z_n, define permutation π_k: order vertices as (k, k+1, k+2, ..., k-1) i.e., cyclic shift. That repeats adjacency pairs, but maybe we can modify using direction reversal for half? Hmm.\n\nLet's attempt to construct for n=4 to see feasibility. Need 4 permutations each length 4 with 3 pairs each total 12 pairs covering all ordered pairs (12 = 4*3). Let's try to find such set manually.\n\nVertices {1,2,3,4}. Need each ordered pair (a,b) a≠b once. Let's attempt:\n\nPermutation1: 1 2 3 4 -> pairs (1,2),(2,3),(3,4)\nPermutation2: 2 4 1 3 -> pairs (2,4),(4,1),(1,3)\nPermutation3: 3 1 4 2 -> pairs (3,1),(1,4),(4,2)\nPermutation4: 4 3 2 1 -> pairs (4,3),(3,2),(2,1)\n\nNow list all ordered pairs: (1,2) yes, (1,3) yes, (1,4) yes, (2,1) yes, (2,3) yes, (2,4) yes, (3,1) yes, (3,2) yes, (3,4) yes, (4,1) yes, (4,2) yes, (4,3) yes. All covered exactly once! Also each vertex first once? First elements: 1,2,3,4 each appears once. Last elements: 4,3,2,1 each appears once. So feasible for n=4.\n\nThus seems possible for even n. Construction pattern appears: permutations are \"reverse of others\"? The set we gave resembles using all 4 cyclic permutations of a 4-cycle but with some swaps.\n\nMaybe general construction: Represent permutations as rows of a Latin square of order n where each row is a permutation, and the difference between consecutive columns yields each ordered pair.\n\nOne known structure: A \"complete set of mutually orthogonal Latin squares\" gives decomposition of complete directed graph into n permutations? Actually a Latin square of order n can be seen as arranging n symbols in n×n grid such that each symbol appears once per row/col. If we take rows as permutations (order of symbols by column index) then adjacency pairs across columns encode something.\n\nBut we have n permutations (rows) each of length n. Need each ordered pair appears exactly once as (symbol, next column) across rows. Equivalent to constructing a \"directed pairwise balanced design\" with block size 2.\n\nI think it's possible for all n, using construction based on the \"rook tour\" on n×n board? Consider n×n board with coordinates (i,j). Define permutation for each i: order vertices by j such that (i+j mod n) sequence maybe? Let's test for n=4 using π_i(j)= i*j mod n? Not.\n\nMaybe use permutations defined by: π_k: list numbers as (k, k+1, ..., n, 1, ..., k-1) but also alternating direction for each step to avoid repeats. Another known construction: For n even, can decompose K_{n,n} bipartite? Not.\n\nGiven we have exhibited existence for 4, we suspect existence for all n. We can prove by induction: Suppose we have construction for n. Build for n+2? Could be.\n\nBut for answer, maximum possible is 100.\n\nThus answer: 100.\n\nLet's respond.", "response": "ANSWER: 100", "answer": 100, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.9073475456821143e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999980926542733, "logp_correct": -1.9073475456821143e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5667344317358171, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2317267904473361, "frac_below_-5": 0.009858287122612447, "digit_lp_min": -3.651303768157959, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 3246, "lp_end": -2.312633478140924e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.576278118089249e-07, "logp_nocot": -1.6934306323528219, "p_nocot": 0.18388758993075277, "uplift": 1.6934287250052762}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "01109e3e-d9e4-4af6-8ce1-221a7b9b37ad", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Find the least positive integer $n$ such that there exists a polynomial \\[ P(x) = a_{2n}x^{2n} + a_{2n-1}x^{2n-1} + \\dots + a_1x + a_0 \\] with real coefficients satisfying the following properties:\n- For $i = 0, 1, \\dots, 2n$, it holds that $2014 \\leq a_i \\leq 2015$.\n- There exists a real number $\\xi$ such that $P(\\xi) = 0$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Find the least positive integer $n$ such that there exists a polynomial \\[ P(x) = a_{2n}x^{2n} + a_{2n-1}x^{2n-1} + \\dots + a_1x + a_0 \\] with real coefficients satisfying the following properties:\n- For $i = 0, 1, \\dots, 2n$, it holds that $2014 \\leq a_i \\leq 2015$.\n- There exists a real number $\\xi$ such that $P(\\xi) = 0$.", "true_answer": 2014, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "01109e3e-d9e4-4af6-8ce1-221a7b9b37ad:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 5153, 8841, 16336, 297, 2238, 484, 1354, 13031, 261, 9882, 220, 17, 77, 113464, 483, 722, 76338, 2870, 220, 667, 19, 326, 220, 667, 20, 29482, 11, 326, 853, 261, 1374, 6577, 364, 103044, 423, 581, 1682, 261, 113464, 483, 722, 76338, 36144, 2684, 8841, 2086, 11, 13972, 398, 4061, 8, 1424, 15, 395, 722, 1374, 1215, 30, 4037, 23517, 26, 2023, 679, 2232, 6629, 5192, 316, 1215, 2447, 12626, 25, 76338, 8841, 11, 889, 14210, 27742, 7075, 2232, 3343, 395, 12626, 1215, 25, 2214, 1215, 27, 15, 11, 5941, 483, 14210, 51159, 5025, 12626, 350, 21910, 64656, 8841, 741, 2632, 290, 113464, 665, 2304, 12626, 4824, 395, 77799, 4410, 12626, 1215, 2236, 11639, 75754, 2914, 885, 2232, 20102, 402, 113181, 328, 9882, 25, 9882, 220, 17, 77, 1952, 11, 8117, 2914, 261, 22305, 17, 77, 92, 1215, 42923, 17, 77, 92, 8841, 395, 1062, 1374,