21, 8704, 34410, 25, 4871, 220, 15, 220, 16, 220, 17, 220, 18, 220, 19, 13, 19792, 3783, 220, 17, 220, 18, 220, 19, 220, 15, 220, 16, 364, 17333, 25, 25489, 350, 15, 11, 16, 3127, 220, 16, 220, 15, 220, 17, 220, 18, 220, 19, 350, 16, 446, 55961, 350, 15, 11, 17, 3127, 220, 16, 220, 17, 220, 15, 220, 18, 220, 19, 350, 17, 446, 55961, 350, 15, 11, 18, 3127, 220, 16, 220, 17, 220, 18, 220, 15, 220, 19, 350, 18, 446, 55961, 350, 15, 11, 19, 8, 5251, 20404, 6961, 15897, 220, 15, 326, 220, 19, 553, 39069, 350, 21910, 37626, 3127, 1023, 553, 220, 16, 326, 220, 19, 30, 50557, 2208, 2569, 25, 220, 16, 220, 17, 220, 18, 220, 15, 220, 19, 26, 177015, 2870, 220, 19, 326, 220, 16, 4493, 20404, 13, 19792, 316, 5275, 220, 15, 316, 290, 1268, 25, 25489, 220, 15, 326, 220, 19, 350, 57001, 28063, 4493, 20404, 3127, 220, 16, 220, 17, 220, 18, 220, 19, 220, 15, 350, 19, 446, 10620, 581, 679, 220, 16, 220, 17, 220, 18, 220, 19, 220, 15, 26, 1309, 220, 17, 220, 18, 220, 19, 220, 15, 220, 16, 13, 2632, 25489, 220, 16, 326, 220, 17, 25, 220, 17, 220, 16, 220, 18, 220, 19, 220, 15, 350, 20, 446, 55961, 220, 16, 326, 220, 18, 30, 625, 39069, 13, 165972, 581, 4279, 2061, 220, 20, 150699, 326, 625, 4167, 13, 2632, 10112, 1309, 220, 21, 364, 84787, 297, 81061, 14, 17, 350, 20, 8, 7890, 67567, 364, 5808, 20690, 3572, 413, 4215, 22305, 72, 27, 73, 92, 272, 3649, 22712, 8, 1919, 272, 382, 61198, 9324, 10067, 306, 9262, 328, 23769, 10112, 59684, 372, 14793, 77, 10897, 741, 2214, 297, 28, 20, 35155, 28, 17, 2747, 220, 17, 9, 18, 28, 21, 6145, 13, 2632, 1039, 11965, 70924, 3605, 15248, 19957, 150699, 13, 2214, 297, 28, 20, 11, 581, 38398, 220, 21, 150699, 10112, 118877, 364, 84787, 19957, 150699, 402, 15839, 29702, 1349, 12125, 11, 297, 10897, 44406, 77, 533, 1349, 12125, 11, 297, 10897, 915, 30, 50557, 484, 1888, 382, 2684, 115053, 328, 9262, 364, 84787, 6052, 395, 297, 28, 1323, 16, 11, 372, 28, 1353, 15, 350, 21910, 297, 533, 372, 314, 220, 7672, 16, 8, 1888, 314, 220, 1353, 15, 9, 7672, 16, 314, 220, 16, 11, 21199, 11, 1302, 364, 4383, 538, 1062, 3432, 2360, 42218, 2595, 30, 30456, 581, 1199, 26697, 150699, 30, 3004, 11, 3605, 2454, 25489, 364, 84787, 6052, 220, 16, 11, 21199, 11, 1302, 364, 58369, 97196, 13], "n_tokens": 4236, "words": 2759, "band_lo": 1322.0, "band_hi": 5288.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have 2021 people in a circle labeled positions 0..2020. We need to rotate each person 1000 positions left (counterclockwise). That's equivalent to applying a rotation permutation R where each person moves to position i-1000 mod 2021.\n\nWe can only swap adjacent people (neighbors) per move. The minimum number of adjacent swaps needed to achieve a given permutation equals the number of inversions of that permutation when linearizing the circle? For a circle, we need to think: we have a circular arrangement, but adjacent swaps are defined along the circle (including swap between position 0 and 2020?). Usually in a circular table, neighbors are those with indices modulo 2021, so yes, the adjacency is circular. So we can swap any adjacent pair along the circle.\n\nGoal: achieve rotation by 1000 positions. Equivalent to we need to reorder the people accordingly.\n\nWe need minimal number of adjacent transpositions to realize this rotation on a circular arrangement. Since rotation is a cyclic permutation, we can possibly achieve by moving each person step by step? However we can perhaps use the concept of sorting by adjacent swaps which is the bubble sort distance (number of inversions). For a given linear permutation, minimal adjacent swaps to transform identity to that permutation equals inversion count.\n\nBut on a circle, we have flexibility: we can choose a cut point (linearize) to compute. However the adjacency includes the wrap-around edge, which could give a shorter route using that edge.\n\nWe can think of representing arrangement as a string order around circle. Starting order: 0,1,2,...,2020. Desired order after rotation left by 1000: each person moves left 1000, meaning new order around circle (starting at some reference) would be: persons are shifted right by 1000? Let's define positions: position i (0-index) initially has person i. After rotation left by 1000, person i should be at position i-1000 mod 2021 (left means earlier in the circular order). So the new sequence around circle (starting at position 0) would be: who ends up at position 0? Person (0+1000) mod 2021 = 1000. So order after rotation: 1000,1001,...,2020,0,1,...,999.\n\nThus the target permutation (as a linear order from position 0 to 2020) is a cyclic shift of the initial order by 1000 to the left. That's just a rotation of the sequence: shift left by 1000 positions. For linear adjacent swaps (line), moving from original to shifted order would require many swaps: inversion count equals number of pairs (i,j) such that i<j but after shift, i appears after j. For a rotation by k (left), the inversion count is k*(n-k). Indeed for linear arrangement where we move a block of size k from front to back, inversion count is k*(n-k). Here k = 1000, n=2021, n-k = 1021. So inversions = 1000*1021 = 1,021,000.\n\nBut we have circular adjacency, and we also have the ability to consider the wrap-around as adjacent swap. In a circular setting, we might be able to achieve the rotation with fewer moves. Since rotating the entire circle by any amount can be done by rotating the whole table? But swapping adjacent people only.\n\nObservation: The permutation is just a rotation. In a linear line, you need many swaps. In a circle, you could achieve rotation by repeatedly moving one person all the way around via adjacent swaps across the wrap edge. But moving all persons individually would be more.\n\nMaybe we can think about using a single \"cut\" and treat the circle as a linear list with adjacency also between ends; the distance metric is the minimum number of adjacent swaps on a cyclic graph to achieve a permutation. This is known as the sorting distance on a cycle graph. There's known formula: minimal number of adjacent swaps on a cycle equals (inversion count) - (max number of \"crossings\" resolved using the wrap). Actually you can think of picking a cut to linearize the cycle to minimize inversion count. Because you can keep the edge between last and first as an adjacency, allowing one to swap across it. So the distance is the minimum, over all rotations (choice of where you cut), of inversion count of the target permutation relative to that linear order.\n\nWe can cut the circle at any point to linearize; since swaps across the cut are allowed (they are adjacent across the cut). In linear representation, you can still swap across the cut, but that would be considered as nonadjacent in linear sense; however we can treat the cut point as being the location where we allow a special adjacency. But distance defined on cycle graph is the minimal number of swaps along edges of the cycle to achieve the permutation.\n\nWe can think of representing the permutation as a mapping of indices on a cycle graph. The distance equals sum over all persons of minimal distance they must travel along the cycle? Not exactly, because swaps move two people simultaneously.\n\nAlternative approach: Since the permutation is just rotation by 1000, we can achieve it by moving each person 1000 steps left. One way: pick a direction (say left). Perform 1000 rounds where in each round we swap each adjacent pair (i,i+1) simultaneously (parallel moves). But moves are counted individually, not parallel.\n\nIf we rotate the whole circle by one position left via adjacent swaps: we can achieve that by swapping each adjacent pair sequentially: moving person at position 0 around the circle? Actually to rotate left by one, we can take the person at position 0 and move it left through the circle (i.e., to position n-1) by swapping with each neighbor. That's n-1 swaps. Another way: We could move everyone else right by one using n-1 swaps as well. So rotation by 1 requires n-1 swaps minimally? Might be minimal because you need to move each of the n elements at least total distance sum of positions maybe? The total displacement sum of positions is (n-1)+(n-2)+...+1 = n(n-1)/2 but each swap moves two elements one step each, so each swap reduces sum of absolute distances by 2? Not sure.\n\nLet's compute minimal swaps to achieve rotation by k on a cycle. There may be formula: min swaps = k*(n - k). But that's for linear? On a cycle, we can choose direction shorter: rotation by k is equivalent to rotation by n-k in opposite direction. For adjacent swaps, rotating left by k is same as rotating right by n-k. So minimal number of swaps maybe = k*(n - k) - something? Let's test small n to infer.\n\nTake n=4, k=1 (rotate left by 1). Linear inversion count = k*(n-k)=1*3=3. But on a cycle, can we achieve with 3? Let's simulate: initial 0 1 2 3; target 1 2 3 0. Can we do in 3 swaps? Sequence: swap 0 and 1 => 1 0 2 3 (1). swap 0 and 2? not adjacent. need move 0 to end: swap 0 and 2 not adjacent. Actually we need bring 0 around via adjacency: after first swap, arrangement: 1 0 2 3. Now swap 0 and 2 (adjacent) => 1 2 0 3 (2). swap 0 and 3 (adjacent via wrap) => 1 2 3 0 (3). Yes 3 swaps. So same as linear.\n\nWhat about n=5, k=2. Linear inversions = 2*3=6. Can we do less on cycle? Let's try compute maybe we can do 5? Quick mental? Possibly same. Let's test n=5, rotate left by 2: target: 2 3 4 0 1. We can move 0 to position 3 via swaps: steps needed 3 (from index0 to index3 moving left 2? Actually distance along cycle leftwards is 2? Wait moving left means earlier positions: we want 0 to go to position 3 (since shifted left 2). Distance along the circle minimal is 2? From position0 to position3 moving left (decreasing index) passes through 4 then 3: two steps. So each element moves distance min(k, n-k) perhaps. Sum of distances = n * min(k, n-k)? Not exactly.\n\nMaybe formula for total number of swaps needed on a cycle equals sum over elements of distance they need to move in chosen direction, divided by 2 (since each swap moves two elements). For rotation by k left, each element moves left by k positions (mod n). The shortest path distance for each element is min(k, n-k). Since we can choose direction for each maybe? But to achieve uniform rotation, we probably move all elements same direction; could also mix? But final arrangement is rotation; any sequence of swaps results eventually rotating the whole configuration; the net effect is each element moved by k left (or equivalently n-k right). Since each swap swaps adjacent, think of each swap as moving each of the two participants one step in opposite directions relative to their original plan? Hard.\n\nIf we always swap in direction of rotation, each swap moves one element one step towards its target (and the other maybe away). Not efficient.\n\nBetter: we can rotate the whole circle by performing a series of adjacent swaps that pass a single \"hole\". Equivalent to bubble sort of rotation: Doing n-1 swaps moves the first element to the end, effectively rotating left by 1. That's n-1 = 2020 swaps. For k=1000 rotations, we could repeat 1000 times: 1000*(n-1)=1000*2020 = 2,020,000 swaps. That's larger than linear inversion count (1,021,000). So not minimal.\n\nThus minimal might actually be the linear inversion count 1,021,000, since rotation can be achieved by moving the block of k elements (the first k) after the rest using swaps. That's exactly the process: swap each of those k elements across n-k others. In linear case that's k*(n-k). In cycle, perhaps we can do better by moving the other block of size n-k across the k? Since rotation left by k is same as rotation right by n-k. The inversion count for right rotation by n-k would be (n-k)*k which is same product. So same.\n\nThus maybe the minimal equals k*(n-k) = 1,021,000.\n\nBut we need to ensure that the wrap adjacency doesn't reduce count. Could we cut at a different point to reduce inversions? The target permutation is a rotation; any cut yields linear representation that is also a rotation by some amount possibly shifting. For any cut, the resulting linear permutation will be a cyclic shift of target relative to original cut's linear order. Inversion count for any rotation of a rotation? Might be same? Let's test small n=5, k=2. Original order 0 1 2 3 4. Target order after rotation left 2: 2 3 4 0 1.\n\nIf we cut at position 2 (i.e., start linear order at 2), then original linear order becomes 2 3 4 0 1 (if we cut same as target) as identity; inversion count 0. But you can't cut arbitrarily because adjacency across cut is also allowed, but starting state after cut is not identity: you re-label positions? Actually cutting at position 2 means we consider the circular arrangement but we rename positions so that position 2 becomes index 0. Initially the sequence around circle starting at 2 is 2,3,4,0,1. After rotation left 2, the sequence starting at 2 becomes? Let's compute target arrangement starting at position 2 in final arrangement: After rotation, the order around circle is same as original shifted left 2, which is 2,3,4,0,1 (since it's the same as original order!). Wait the permutation is exactly a rotation, so the configuration after rotation is identical to original arrangement, just relabeled positions. If you look at the circle physically, the people have moved, but the circular ordering of people around the table is unchanged! Indeed rotating everyone left by k doesn't change the cyclic order; it's just a rotation of positions, but the relative ordering around the circle is same as before. Since the people are seated, rotating them left by any amount will result in same cyclic order as originally (just each person moved to a new seat, but the sequence around the circle of person labels is unchanged). Let's verify: initial circular order (clockwise) is 0,1,2,...,2020. After moving each to 1000 left, the circular order is still 0,1,2,...,2020 because each person moved but they all shifted equally, so the relative adjacency remains same. Actually check: Person 0 originally sits next to 1 on its right and 2020 on its left. After moving left 1000 seats, person 0 ends up at position -1000 = 2021-1000 = 1021. Who is to its right? Person who was originally at position 1022 (since all shifted left equally). That's person 1022? Wait compute: Person i after shift appears at position i-1000. So the clockwise neighbor of person i after shift is person (i+1) -1000 = i-999, which is person (i-999) maybe not i+1. That seems not preserving original adjacency? Let's test with small n=5, k=2: initial order 0-1-2-3-4. After left 2: each moves left 2 seats. Person0 goes to position -2 = 3. Person1 to position4. Person2 to position0. Person3 to position1. Person4 to position2. The new circular order (starting at position0) is persons: position0=2, position1=3, position2=4, position3=0, position4=1 -> sequence 2,3,4,0,1. That's indeed a rotation of original order (starting at 2). So the cyclic order changed (rotated) but is still same relative adjacency as before (since cyclic order is same up to rotation). So overall cyclic order is unchanged up to rotation; that means the permutation is a cyclic rotation of positions, which is equivalent to rotating the whole circle. So as a cyclic permutation, the arrangement is indistinguishable if you disregard fixed seat labels. Since moves are swaps of neighbors, but adjacency defined by current arrangement, not seat labels? Wait seats are fixed; people swap seats with neighbors. So after rotation, the ordering of people around the table is changed (they occupy different seats). But the cyclic order of people (when reading around the table) is the same as before, just starting at a different place. That's true because a uniform rotation doesn't change the circular permutation (it's just a cyclic shift). So we need to achieve a configuration that is a cyclic shift of the original labeling of seats. Since the cyclic order of people relative to each other is unchanged (the list is same but starting point shifted), perhaps we can achieve this with zero swaps? No, because people need to be at specific seats (positions). The seats are distinguished, so you cannot just rotate the whole table; you need to move people.\n\nThus we need to transform identity permutation to the rotation by k, which as a permutation on seat indices has many cycles? Actually rotation by k is a single cycle of length n if k and n are coprime (which they are, since 2021 is prime? 2021 = 43 * 47? Let's compute: 43*47 = 2021 indeed composite. gcd(1000,2021)=? 2021 mod 1000 =21; 1000 mod 21 = 13; 21 mod13=8;13 mod8=5;8 mod5=3;5 mod3=2;3 mod2=1;2 mod1=0 so gcd=1. So k and n are coprime, thus rotation is a single n-cycle.\n\nNow minimal number of adjacent swaps to realize a given permutation on a cycle graph equals n - number of cycles? Not for adjacent swap metric.\n\nWe can think of each adjacent swap corresponds to transposition of neighboring positions on the cycle graph. Adjacent transpositions generate the symmetric group; the word length of a permutation in the Coxeter group of type \\tilde A_{n-1}? That's the affine symmetric group, but length formula is sum of absolute differences modulo n? For the usual symmetric group (linear) length = inversion count. For the affine type, the length is minimal number of adjacent transpositions on a cycle (including wrap). The length function for affine permutation w is sum_{i<j} min( (w(i)-w(j)) mod n, (w(j)-w(i)) mod n ). Something like that.\n\nGiven rotation by k (shift left by k), the length (minimal adjacent swaps on cycle) should be k*(n-k). Wait compute min distance between original positions i and image w(i)=i-k mod n. The forward distance maybe k. The length might be sum_i distance(i, w(i)) = n * k? But each swap reduces sum distances by 2, so length = n*k/2 = n*k/2 = 2021*1000/2 = 1,010,500. That's close but not same as 1,021,000. Let's test with small n=5, k=2. n*k/2 =5*2/2=5. That's predicted minimal swaps 5. Let's see if possible? Let's attempt to achieve rotation left 2 with 5 swaps maybe. earlier we hypothesized maybe 6. Let's attempt manually: initial 0 1 2 3 4. Need target 2 3 4 0 1.\n\nSequence: swap (0,1): 1 0 2 3 4 (1)\nswap (0,2): 1 2 0 3 4 (2)\nswap (0,3): 1 2 3 0 4 (3)\nswap (0,4) across wrap?? positions 0 and 4 are adjacent (since circular): they are 1 and 4? Actually current order: 1 2 3 0 4; adjacency between 4 and 1 via wrap. Need to move 0 to the end: swap 0 and 4 (adjacent via wrap): 1 2 3 4 0 (4)\nNow we have 1 2 3 4 0; need 2 3 4 0 1. So swap 1 and 2: 2 1 3 4 0 (5)\nswap 1 and 3? not adjacent. Hmm we already used 5 swaps and not done. So maybe need 6.\n\nThus n*k/2 (5) seems insufficient.\n\nSo formula might be sum_{i<j} d(i,j) where d is modular distance moving in direction of rotation maybe yields k*(n-k). For n=5,k=2 -> 2*3=6 correct. So our earlier inversion count matches minimal swaps. For n=5, we attempted 6 swaps maybe achievable.\n\nThus minimal swaps on cycle equals min(k, n-k)*(n - min(k, n-k))? Actually that product is same irrespective of direction.\n\nThus answer for n=2021, k=1000 (since n - k = 1021) product = 1000*1021 = 1,021,000.\n\nCheck if any better using clever method? Could we use parallel swaps? No, count each swap.\n\nThus answer 1,021,000.\n\nLet's finalize.", "response": "ANSWER: 1021000", "answer": 1021000, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.145766444527908e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999978542358576, "logp_correct": -2.145766444527908e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6752481632269145, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3351447413297137, "frac_below_-5": 0.011374407582938388, "digit_lp_min": -2.3502581119537354, "answer_first_mention_lp": null, "n_cot_tokens": 4220, "lp_end": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -4.768370445162873e-07, "logp_nocot": -12.435980061907685, "p_nocot": 3.973035833539052e-06, "uplift": 12.43597791614124}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "18c9e5ac-4c8c-4a57-9b89-de497a09c61c", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Let $a_1$, $a_2$, \\dots, $a_{2015}$ be a sequence of positive integers in $[1,100]$. Call a nonempty contiguous subsequence of this sequence \"good\" if the product of the integers in it leaves a remainder of $1$ when divided by $101$. In other words, it is a pair of integers $(x, y)$ such that $1 \\le x \\le y \\le 2015$ and \\[a_xa_{x+1}\\dots a_{y-1}a_y \\equiv 1 \\pmod{101}. \\] Find the minimum possible number of good subsequences across all possible $(a_i)$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Let $a_1$, $a_2$, \\dots, $a_{2015}$ be a sequence of positive integers in $[1,100]$. Call a nonempty contiguous subsequence of this sequence \"good\" if the product of the integers in it leaves a remainder of $1$ when divided by $101$. In other words, it is a pair of integers $(x, y)$ such that $1 \\le x \\le y \\le 2015$ and \\[a_xa_{x+1}\\dots a_{y-1}a_y \\equiv 1 \\pmod{101}. \\] Find the minimum possible number of good subsequences across all possible $(a_i)$.", "true_answer": 19320, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "18c9e5ac-4c8c-4a57-9b89-de497a09c61c:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 41776, 2086, 328, 1543, 158630, 12119, 1888, 42308, 94, 220, 16, 1812, 220, 7959, 11, 4335, 2454, 261, 10853, 382, 677, 2870, 220, 16, 326, 220, 1353, 29482, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 2893, 79097, 112372, 220, 7959, 741, 12265, 220, 7959, 382, 9197, 11, 2454, 261, 10853, 382, 57364, 1430, 112372, 220, 7959, 26, 581, 665, 2325, 1043, 115502, 112372, 220, 7959, 472, 8632, 328, 67929, 1799, 3566, 328, 2569, 220, 1353, 364, 42087, 25, 29844, 16281, 5517, 297, 28, 667, 20, 11, 5655, 115502, 350, 11741, 79097, 1812, 220, 7959, 8, 316, 41776, 2086, 328,