tterns:\n\n000, 001, 010, 011, 100, 101, 110, 111.\n\nBy symmetry (complement) swapping 0↔1 yields same count. So we only need to consider patterns up to complement: {000,001,010,011}. Also reversal maybe same count? Reverse of pattern also same because automaton symmetric? Might be same.\n\nThus compute counts for patterns: 000, 001, 010, 011.\n\nWe already have 000 (or 111) count =504.\n\nWe have 010 count =351.\n\nNow compute for pattern 001.\n\nPattern P=001.\n\nConstruct automaton.\n\nPrefixes: '', '0', '00'.\n\nState0: no partial match.\n\n- reading 0: suffix '0' matches prefix length1 => state1.\n- reading 1: suffix '1' no match => state0.\n\nState1 (matched '0'):\n\n- reading 0: we have '00' matches prefix length2 => state2.\n- reading 1: we have '01' suffix, longest prefix? prefixes '0','00'. '01' doesn't match. So state0.\n\nState2 (matched '00'):\n\n- reading 0: would get '000', need longest suffix that matches prefix. Suffixes: '0','00','000'. Prefixes: '0','00'. Longest matching is '00' (length2) because last two chars '00'. So reading 0 stays in state2 (since we still have '00' suffix). Actually '000' contains '00' as suffix length2, so allowed.\n\n- reading 1: would get '001' which is forbidden (full match), not allowed.\n\nThus transitions:\n\nState0: 0→1, 1→0\nState1: 0→2, 1→0\nState2: 0→2, 1→dead\n\nNow compute counts.\n\nInitialize (1,0,0)\n\nn=1:\ns0' = from state0 reading1 + from state1 reading1? Wait state1 reading1 -> state0, state2 reading1 forbidden. So s0' = s0 (reading1) + s1 (reading1) =1+0=1\ns1' = from state0 reading0 = s0 =1\ns2' = from state1 reading0 = s1 =0\ntotal =2\n\nn=2:\nprev (1,1,0)\ns0' = s0 + s1 =1+1=2 (reading1 from state0 and state1)\ns1' = s0 =1 (reading0 from state0)\ns2' = s1 =1 (reading0 from state1)\ntotal=2+1+1=4\n\nn=3:\nprev (2,1,1)\ns0' = s0 + s1 =2+1=3\ns1' = s0 =2\ns2' = s1 =1\ntotal=3+2+1=6? Wait but we expect maybe 7? Let's verify: Let's also consider contributions from state2 reading 0 leads to state2; we missed that for s2' update maybe.\n\nOur recurrence for s2' should also consider reading 0 from state2 (stays in state2). Indeed, s2' gets contributions from state1 reading0 (to state2) and state2 reading0 (stays). So s2' = s1 + s2.\n\nSimilarly s0' gets contributions from state0 reading1 and state1 reading1 (both to state0). State2 reading1 is forbidden.\n\ns1' gets from state0 reading0 (to state1). That's it.\n\nThus correct recurrence:\n\ns0' = s0 + s1\ns1' = s0\ns2' = s1 + s2\n\nNow recompute with that.\n\nn=0: (1,0,0)\n\nn=1:\ns0'=1+0=1\ns1'=1\ns2'=0+0=0\ntotal=2 (ok)\n\nn=2:\nprev (1,1,0)\ns0'=1+1=2\ns1'=1\ns2'=1+0=1\ntotal=4\n\nn=3:\nprev (2,1,1)\ns0'=2+1=3\ns1'=2\ns2'=1+1=2\ntotal=7\n\nNow matches expectation.\n\nn=4:\nprev (3,2,2)\ns0'=3+2=5\ns1'=3\ns2'=2+2=4\ntotal=5+3+4=12\n\nn=5:\nprev (5,3,4)\ns0'=5+3=8\ns1'=5\ns2'=3+4=7\ntotal=8+5+7=20\n\nn=6:\nprev (8,5,7)\ns0'=8+5=13\ns1'=8\ns2'=5+7=12\ntotal=13+8+12=33\n\nn=7:\nprev (13,8,12)\ns0'=13+8=21\ns1'=13\ns2'=8+12=20\ntotal=21+13+20=54\n\nn=8:\nprev (21,13,20)\ns0'=21+13=34\ns1'=21\ns2'=13+20=33\ntotal=34+21+33=88\n\nn=9:\nprev (34,21,33)\ns0'=34+21=55\ns1'=34\ns2'=21+33=54\ntotal=55+34+54=143\n\nn=10:\nprev (55,34,54)\ns0'=55+34=89\ns1'=55\ns2'=34+54=88\ntotal=89+55+88=232\n\nThus a_10 = 232 for pattern 001 (or its complement 110). So counts: 000->504, 001->232, 010->351, 011 maybe similar to 001? Let's compute 011.\n\nPattern 011.\n\nPrefixes: '', '0', '01'.\n\nState0: reading0->state1; reading1-> state0 (since prefix starts with 0)\n\nState1 (matched '0'): reading0 ->? suffix '00' -> longest prefix? prefixes: '0','01'. '00' ends with '0', matches prefix length1 -> state1. reading1 -> '01' -> state2.\n\nState2 (matched '01'): reading0 -> '010' which is not forbidden (forbidden is 011). Need longest suffix matching prefix: suffixes '0','10','010'. Prefixes: '', '0','01'. Longest matching is '0' (length1) -> state1. reading1 -> '011' forbidden, not allowed.\n\nTransitions:\n0: 0->1,1->0\n1: 0->1,1->2\n2: 0->1,1->dead\n\nNow recurrences:\n\ns0' = from state0 reading1 = s0\nplus from state? state2 reading? none because reading1 dead. state1 reading1 goes to state2 not state0. So only s0.\n\ns1' = from state0 reading0 + from state1 reading0 + from state2 reading0 = s0 + s1 + s2\n\ns2' = from state1 reading1 = s1\n\nCheck s0 contributions also from any other state? state2 reading? none.\n\nThus recurrence:\n\ns0' = s0\ns1' = s0 + s1 + s2\ns2' = s1\n\nNow compute.\n\nn=0: (1,0,0)\n\nn=1:\ns0'=1\ns1'=1+0+0=1\ns2'=0\ntotal=2\n\nn=2:\nprev (1,1,0)\ns0'=1\ns1'=1+1+0=2\ns2'=1\ntotal=4\n\nn=3:\nprev (1,2,1)\ns0'=1\ns1'=1+2+1=4\ns2'=2\ntotal=7\n\nn=4:\nprev (1,4,2)\ns0'=1\ns1'=1+4+2=7\ns2'=4\ntotal=12\n\nn=5:\nprev (1,7,4)\ns0'=1\ns1'=1+7+4=12\ns2'=7\ntotal=20\n\nn=6:\nprev (1,12,7)\ns0'=1\ns1'=1+12+7=20\ns2'=12\ntotal=33\n\nn=7:\nprev (1,20,12)\ns0'=1\ns1'=1+20+12=33\ns2'=20\ntotal=54\n\nn=8:\nprev (1,33,20)\ns0'=1\ns1'=1+33+20=54\ns2'=33\ntotal=88\n\nn=9:\nprev (1,54,33)\ns0'=1\ns1'=1+54+33=88\ns2'=54\ntotal=143\n\nn=10:\nprev (1,88,54)\ns0'=1\ns1'=1+88+54=143\ns2'=88\ntotal=1+143+88=232\n\nThus a_10 =232 same as pattern 001 (as expected due to symmetry). Good.\n\nThus the maximum a_10 among patterns is 504 (for pattern 000 or 111). So the maximum number of length-10 substrings that can avoid a particular triple is 504.\n\nNow question: there are at most 7 prophetic words of length 3 (distinct). Means we have at most 7 distinct triples present. So we can avoid at least one triple: we can choose the triple missing to be the most permissive? Actually the maximum number of distinct length-10 substrings is achieved when we forbid the triple that yields the largest a_10, which is 504. So the maximum number of prophetic words of length 10 is at most 504.\n\nWe need to see if we can realize 504 distinct substrings of length 10 in a word length 10000 while avoiding only triple 111, i.e., not containing 111 (or 000). Example: we need to construct a word of length 10000 that contains all 504 allowed length-10 substrings (those without 111). Is there a word of length that long containing each allowed length-10 substring at least once? Since the graph of allowed substrings is Eulerian? Need to check if we can have Eulerian trail covering all edges. For pattern '111', the allowed length-10 strings are those with no three consecutive 1's. The corresponding graph of length-9 states (strings of length 9 without 111) will have equal indegree/outdegree because each state can be extended with 0 or 1 except those ending with '11' where adding 1 is forbidden. Let's check indegree/outdegree condition for Eulerian trail: In a directed graph, an Eulerian circuit exists if all vertices with nonzero degree belong to a single strongly connected component and indegree=outdegree for each vertex. For the de Bruijn-like graph of order 10 with forbidden pattern, may have indegree=outdegree for each vertex because the allowed extensions are symmetric? Let's verify: Vertex corresponds to a length-9 string s that avoids 111. Outgoing edges: append 0 always allowed, append 1 allowed unless s ends with '11'. So outdegree is 1 or 2. Ingoing edges: prepend a bit: allowed unless the first two bits of s are '11' and the prepended bit is 1? Actually to have a length-10 string ending with s, we need a preceding 9-bit prefix t such that t[2..] = s[0..7] and t plus last bit forms allowed string (no 111). Equivalent condition: the first two bits of s being '11' restrict incoming 1? Let's compute indegree similarly: For a given vertex s, the possible preceding letters are 0 always allowed, and 1 allowed unless the first two bits of s are '11' (since then adding 1 would create '111' crossing the boundary). So indegree also 1 or 2 mirroring outdegree (depending on last two bits vs first two bits). For many vertices, outdegree may differ from indegree, causing Euler trail but not circuit. However we can still have an Eulerian trail covering all edges (path using each edge once) if at most two vertices have imbalance of 1. Might be possible.\n\nBut we need to cover each of the a_10=504 edges. Since number of edges is relatively small, we can likely construct such a trail within length 10000 (as we need length edges+9 = about 513). Indeed, an Eulerian trail that uses each edge exactly once yields a word of length edges + 9 = 504 + 9 = 513 (because start with first 9 letters then each edge adds one letter). That's far less than 10000, so we can extend further arbitrarily without introducing new triples (we could repeat some letters). So we can definitely achieve 504 distinct length-10 substrings.\n\nThus the maximum possible number of prophetic words of length 10 is 504.\n\nCheck that we can't exceed 504 due to restriction. Since there are only 2^10=1024 possible length-10 strings; those containing the missing triple are forbidden. Number of length-10 strings that contain a particular triple? Could be more than half, but we have counted those that avoid the triple; maximum is when triple is '000' or '111' gives 504 avoidance. That seems plausible as the maximum avoidance number.\n\nThus answer: 504.\n\nLet's double-check count for pattern '111' matches same recurrence as '000' (just complement). So 504.\n\nTherefore final answer: ANSWER: 504.", "response": "ANSWER: 504", "answer": 504, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -9.440936969440372e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999055950867299, "logp_correct": -9.440936969440372e-05, "cot_lp_mean": -0.43665908388616753, "cot_lp_min64": -1.299659194149525, "frac_below_-5": 0.005504587155963303, "digit_lp_min": -6.908924102783203, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 7630, "lp_end": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -8.22540732769994e-06, "logp_nocot": -15.04029893875122, "p_nocot": 2.938198716749979e-07, "uplift": 15.040204529381526}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "3ef238be-047c-4891-b078-2f4ed4bd56ce", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Each cell of a $7 \\times 7$ table is painted with one of several colors. It is known that for any two distinct rows, the numbers of colors used to paint them are distinct, and for any two distinct columns, the numbers of colors used to paint them are distinct. What is the maximum possible number of colors in the table?\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Each cell of a $7 \\times 7$ table is painted with one of several colors. It is known that for any two distinct rows, the numbers of colors used to paint them are distinct, and for any two distinct columns, the numbers of colors used to paint them are distinct. What is the maximum possible number of colors in the table?", "true_answer": 22, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "3ef238be-047c-4891-b078-2f4ed4bd56ce:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 2999, 2086, 328, 10803, 2061, 306, 261, 220, 22, 87, 22, 10586, 483, 7060, 25, 2214, 2454, 5225, 11, 3605, 328, 16913, 10803, 44748, 306, 484, 5225, 26, 1879, 28991, 553, 722, 16913, 5251, 13585, 13, 2632, 13585, 679, 220, 22, 13585, 26, 16913, 28991, 2804, 413, 220, 15, 30, 47219, 220, 16, 869, 316, 10112, 220, 22, 30, 12265, 2454, 5225, 853, 220, 22, 11242, 11, 2086, 328, 16913, 10803, 306, 261, 5225, 665, 413, 591, 220, 16, 316, 220, 22, 350, 76543, 413, 220, 15, 741, 2632, 581, 1309, 220, 22, 16913, 54912, 2870, 220, 16, 326, 220, 22, 29482, 11, 13972, 1023, 2804, 413, 9707, 220, 16, 11, 17, 11, 18, 11, 19, 11, 20, 11, 21, 11, 22, 21482, 316, 13585, 306, 1236, 2569, 13, 65037, 395, 15697, 25, 2454, 6000, 885, 16913, 3089, 3605, 2804, 413, 261, 135200, 328, 220, 16, 485, 22, 364, 42087, 44207, 3609, 16913, 10803, 2061, 10705, 306, 3293,