ed find i where length7 blocks equal.\n\nCheck i=4 maybe? Compare block4-10 (indices 4..10) vs 11..17.\n\nBlock4-10: indices 4:0,5:0,6:1,7:1,8:0,9:0,10:0 => 0 0 1 1 0 0 0\n\nBlock11-17: 11:1,12:1,13:0,14:1,15:1,16:0,17:0 => 1 1 0 1 1 0 0 not equal.\n\nTry i=5 block5-11 vs 12-18: block5-11: 0,1,1,0,0,0,1 ; block12-18: 1,0,1,1,0,0,0 . Not.\n\ni=6 block6-12 vs 13-19: block6-12:1,1,0,0,0,1,1 ; block13-19:0,1,1,0,0,0,1 . Not.\n\ni=7 block7-13 vs 14-20: block7-13:1,0,0,0,1,1,0 ; block14-20:1,1,0,0,0,1,0 . Not.\n\ni=8 block8-14 vs 15-21: block8-14:0,0,0,1,1,0,1 ; block15-21:1,0,0,0,1,0,0 . Not.\n\nLooks maybe not.\n\nThus possible a = 1,3,5 maybe also other odd numbers? Let's test a=9 using data 0..31.\n\nFind i where block9 repeats.\n\nCheck i=0 block0-8 vs 9-17: block0-8: 0 0 0 1 0 0 1 1 0 ; block9-17: 0 0 0 1 1 0 1 1 0 . Not.\n\ni=1 block1-9 vs 10-18: 0 0 1 0 0 1 1 0 0 vs 0 0 1 1 0 1 1 0 0. Not.\n\ni=2 block2-10 vs 11-19: 0 1 0 0 1 1 0 0 0 vs 1 1 0 1 1 0 0 0 1 . Not.\n\ni=3 block3-11 vs 12-20: 1 0 0 1 1 0 0 0 1 vs 1 0 1 1 0 0 0 1 0 . Not.\n\ni=4 block4-12 vs 13-21: 0 0 1 1 0 0 0 0 1 vs 0 1 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 . Not.\n\ni=5 block5-13 vs 14-22: 0 1 1 0 0 0 0 1 1 vs 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 1 1 . Not.\n\nSeems not.\n\nMaybe only a which are of form 2^k -1? 3 (2^2-1), 7 not found, 15 maybe? Not.\n\nMaybe a must be odd and not divisible by 3? Actually 3 works,5 works,1 works. Let's test a=9 not working maybe.\n\nCheck a=11? Need more data.\n\nHypothesis: The possible a are odd numbers not divisible by 3? But 3 works is divisible by 3.\n\nMaybe a must be of form 2^m+1? 5 = 2^2+1. 3=2^1+1. 1=2^0+1? Actually 2^0+1 =2 not 1. So no.\n\nMaybe a are numbers whose binary representation is all ones? 1 (1),3 (11),7 (111) but 7 didn't work. So no.\n\nWe need systematic method: sequence s is automatic, squares can be studied using combinatorics on words. But limited time.\n\nMaybe we can compute all a up to some bound? The problem likely expects finite set of a. Since condition existence for some n; perhaps only a = 1,3,5.\n\nLet's double-check a=5 works with n? we found start i=4 (i corresponds to offset from 0). That means n = something such that n + i corresponds to i? With our construction using large power of two, we used n = power of two, then i corresponds to offset. So n large works. Therefore a=5 is valid.\n\nCheck a=7 maybe there exists n beyond our small range. Could be later.\n\nLet's attempt to algorithmic reasoning: Since s(i) depends only on bits near trailing 1, we can think of building a block of length a where bit patterns repeat; this corresponds to requiring that for each i, the pattern of bits ( ... ) repeats after shift a.\n\nThis is similar to requirement that for all i, the binary representation of i and i+a have same bit immediately above the LSB. This can be reinterpreted as: For each i, consider i expressed as ...b_{k} 1 0...0 (r zeros). Then i+a must have same bit at position k (the one above that 1). This seems related to binary addition without carry affecting those bits.\n\nIf a has binary representation without causing carries that affect bits above the least significant 1 of i, then equality holds. For the condition to hold for all i within range length a, we need a to be such that when adding a to any number i in [0,a-1], the addition does not propagate a carry beyond the first zero after the LSB 1? Actually addition may cause carries.\n\nObservation: For i varying from 0 to a-1, the binary addition i + a may cause carries at various positions, but we need the bit above the LSB of i (call it bit b) to be equal to same bit after addition.\n\nIf a is odd, then i and i+a have opposite parity, so the LSB flips, trailing zeros maybe different.\n\nTest a=3 (binary 11). Works.\n\na=5 (101) works.\n\nMaybe condition is that a's binary representation has exactly two 1s? 3 (11) has two adjacent ones; 5 (101) has two ones separated. a=1 (1) has single 1 but works.\n\nWhat about a=9 (1001) has two ones separated, maybe fails.\n\nLet's test a=9 quickly with more extensive search maybe beyond 32 we could miss block. Could be later.\n\nBut we can reason: For i ranging 0..8, the addition may cause carry into high bits that affect pattern for some i. Might fail globally.\n\nMaybe the condition is that a is of form 2^k or 2^k +/-1? Not.\n\nWe should maybe do more systematic: Since condition must hold for all i in 0..a-1, we can consider binary of a. Analyze the effect of addition on each i.\n\nLet’s denote i = 2^e * m odd. Then i+a = ?\n\nIf a has binary less than 2^e, then addition doesn't affect the LSB of i (since lower e bits are zero in i). However a may have bits within lower e bits causing carries that affect the bit at position e (the 1). This would change the trailing zeros after addition.\n\nFor the equality to hold, we likely need a to be a multiple of 2^e for each e? That would be impossible for varying e across i.\n\nBut we only need equality of the bit above the LSB, not necessarily of v2.\n\nConsider case where a is multiple of 2^{e+1}. Then adding a will not affect bits up to e+1, thus the bit above LSB (position e+1) stays same. So s(i)=s(i+a). Therefore if a is multiple of 2^{e+1} for all e that appear among i in [0,a-1] (i.e., for all e where there exists i with v2(i)=e), then condition holds. The maximum e among i in range is floor(log2 a). To satisfy for all e from 0 to floor(log2 a), we would need a multiple of 2^{e+1} for each e => a multiple of 2^{floor(log2 a)+1} > a, impossible unless a=0. So not.\n\nThus need not strict.\n\nMaybe the equality holds when a's binary representation consists of all 1's? For a=3 (11) works, a=7 maybe would work? Let's test again more thoroughly maybe a=7 works with larger n not covered earlier. Let's try constructing using principle: If a = 2^k -1 (binary k ones), then adding a to i toggles lower k bits (i + (2^k-1)). For i in [0, a-1], i has at most k bits. Then i + (2^k-1) = (i-1) mod 2^k + 2^k -1? Actually overflow may cause carry into bit k.\n\nFor i