75, 105633, 326, 575, 105633, 10, 16, 382, 220, 16, 350, 21910, 5225, 8663, 40840, 741, 23586, 290, 6000, 19504, 395, 13878, 15697, 553, 722, 12629, 316, 220, 16, 11, 24297, 21461, 0, 41351, 17679, 19504, 1481, 722, 413, 220, 16, 11, 625, 16913, 13, 2632, 495, 28336, 364, 84787, 922, 11965, 6000, 7060, 945, 111795, 25, 6000, 17679, 19504, 2804, 1217, 413, 16913, 11, 889, 290, 124437, 3104, 8967, 2684, 17679, 8868, 328, 220, 16, 395, 722, 13878, 38360, 364, 84787, 1039, 57291, 11965, 1078, 16913, 6000, 19504, 1340, 413, 3101, 12035, 30, 24305, 7060, 395, 15697, 25, 8397, 382, 365, 4610, 268, 174494, 4151, 315, 483, 31148, 26697, 316, 342, 75385, 13, 1328, 84230, 316, 6771, 7060, 25, 2214, 1062, 1920, 15697, 11, 538, 1023, 2454, 679, 540, 5153, 1920, 5571, 11, 1023, 2804, 625, 5143, 261, 5355, 17679, 9809, 13, 2632, 581, 1309, 16913, 17679, 19504, 5251, 15697, 472, 1775, 13, 41351, 364, 84787, 1309, 261, 12282, 1919, 2454, 6000, 885, 10610, 328, 13585, 679, 5746, 9809, 4824, 350, 29475, 741, 2632, 1309, 173247, 316, 13585, 25, 2454, 6000, 483, 1920, 5571, 1757, 679, 5746, 17679, 19953, 364, 84787, 581, 1309, 261, 920, 328, 49900, 350, 62473, 395, 13585, 11, 17679, 395, 15697, 8, 2973, 11982, 328, 51957, 24878, 220, 16, 485, 1353, 64699, 13, 1328, 382, 123676, 4792, 13, 2632, 581, 1309, 261, 920, 328, 220, 1353, 5225, 49900, 328, 16913, 51957, 11, 326, 220, 1353, 6000, 49900, 328, 16913, 51957, 11, 326, 1023, 64353, 54286, 540, 5571, 350, 93882, 261, 135200, 328, 143513, 741, 1328, 382, 96811, 328, 75745, 261, 28974, 13749, 328, 2569, 220, 7959, 10112, 364, 88748, 25, 623, 920, 328, 5571, 665, 413, 28989, 472, 22569, 328, 261, 135200, 12248, 2932, 6623, 8104, 44860, 261, 392, 7886, 328, 2893, 107171, 15930, 974, 18858, 69029, 50557, 2454, 5225, 853, 1920, 15697, 11, 2454, 6000, 853, 1920, 13585, 11, 44860, 261, 5801, 328, 220, 1353, 220, 19, 3452, 119324, 30, 1843, 581, 679, 261, 5801, 328, 220, 1353, 829, 69164, 220, 19, 3452, 119324, 350, 4104, 34011, 936, 2454, 45808, 8844, 1920, 13585, 326, 1920, 15697, 11, 9072, 1920, 25511, 19504, 10112, 12629, 1008, 4037, 16913, 364, 1929, 9913, 13, 17158, 6052, 382, 220, 667, 11, 483, 5542, 1534, 6771, 316, 65043, 1104, 662, 42125, 4792, 30, 357, 2411, 10562, 382, 220, 667, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 25, 220, 667, 13], "n_tokens": 8525, "words": 5714, "band_lo": 2503.75, "band_hi": 10015.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to understand problem: We have integer lattice points (x,y) with 1≤x,y≤101, i.e., 101x101 grid = 10201 points. We need to select a subset with no 4 points forming an isosceles trapezium with its base parallel to either x or y axis (including rectangles). Need maximum size.\n\nWe need to avoid any isosceles trapezoid with bases parallel to axes: So base(s) are horizontal or vertical? An isosceles trapezium (trapezoid) with bases parallel to say x-axis: So top and bottom are horizontal lines, legs equal lengths (non-parallel sides). Also includes rectangle (which is a special trapezoid with equal bases). Similarly for vertical base (parallel to y-axis). So we must avoid any four points that are corners of such shape.\n\nEssentially, pick points such that among any two distinct x-values and two distinct y-values there is not an isosceles trapezoid configuration.\n\nLet's define: Choose points (x_i, y_j). Consider two horizontal lines y = a, y = b (a<b). To have a trapezoid with bases parallel to x-axis, we need points (x1, a), (x2, a), (x3, b), (x4, b) such that the (non-parallel) legs are equal length: distance between (x1,a) and (x3,b) equals distance between (x2,a) and (x4,b). Additionally, bases are horizontal so lengths of bases are |x2-x1| and |x4-x3| (maybe not equal). For isosceles trapezoid: the legs are equal, meaning the horizontal offset between top and bottom vertices should be same: i.e., x3 - x1 = x4 - x2 (if legs are slanted equally). But also we consider possibly the legs could be crossing? Typically isosceles trapezoid with bases parallel to x-axis: vertices arranged as (x1,a) left top, (x2,a) right top, (x3,b) left bottom, (x4,b) right bottom with x3 ≤ x4. For isosceles, distances of legs: sqrt((x3 - x1)^2 + (b-a)^2) = sqrt((x4 - x2)^2 + (b-a)^2). So equality reduces to |x3 - x1| = |x4 - x2|. Also need x1 ≤ x2 and x3 ≤ x4.\n\nThus condition: there exist four points with two y-levels a,b and x-values such that x3 - x1 = x4 - x2 (assuming ordering). Also we need distinct points; possibly rectangle corresponds to x3 - x1 = x4 - x2 and also x2 - x1 = x4 - x3 (i.e., both base lengths equal). But any rectangle also satisfies condition because legs equal (vertical), i.e., x3 = x1, x4 = x2, making equality.\n\nBut also we could have trapezoid with bases vertical: analog with y differences.\n\nThus we need to avoid any configuration where we have two rows (y-values) and points in each row such that the horizontal differences between left vertices are equal.\n\nEssentially, for any pair of rows (distinct y), look at the set of x-coordinates selected in each row. We must avoid choosing four points (x1, y1),(x2, y1),(x3, y2),(x4, y2) with x3 - x1 = x4 - x2 . That's equivalent to existence of two pairs (x1,x3) across rows and (x2,x4) across rows where the differences are equal.\n\nThinking combinatorics: Suppose in each row we have a subset S_y ⊆ {1,...,101}. Condition: For any two rows y1≠y2, the set of differences D_y1 = {x - x' | x,x'∈S_{y1}, x> x'}? But we need cross differences: we need equal differences between elements across rows: i.e., there exist a,b from row y1 and c,d from row y2 such that c - a = d - b . Equivalent to (c - d) = (a - b). That is, difference between two points in row y2 equals difference between two points in row y1. So if two rows have any common difference among their internal differences, we can form isosceles trapezoid? Wait careful.\n\nTake x1,x2 in row y1 (top), with x2 > x1. Choose x3 = x1 + delta, x4 = x2 + delta in row y2 (bottom) where delta=c - x1 etc. Then we need those points to exist. So if row y2 contains the translates of row y1's pair by same amount delta, we get trapezoid. However condition earlier: x3 - x1 = x4 - x2 . If we set delta = x3 - x1 = x4 - x2. This implies that the pair (x3, x4) is (x1+delta, x2+delta). So essentially we need two rows where the selected x-set contains two elements that are a translation of another two-element pattern from another row.\n\nBut also can have overlapping x-range not translation; maybe x3 may be less than x1? We can reorder: The leftmost bottom vertex could be left of left top, i.e., x3 < x1, but then x4 - x2 also negative same magnitude; equality still holds in absolute values? Actually earlier we used absolute value. The leg distances equal means |x3 - x1| = |x4 - x2| . Could be negative offset equal magnitude but sign could differ. For simplicity we can consider equality of absolute differences. So condition becomes: there exist two rows y1, y2 and two unordered pairs {x1,x2} in row y1, {x3,x4} in row y2 with |x3-x1| = |x4-x2| . This basically means the multiset of differences between elements of one pair equals another difference (maybe sign). This may be complicated.\n\nBut perhaps known result: Maximum size without any axis-aligned isosceles trapezoid is 2*101 - 1 = 201? Or something?\n\nLet's test small cases via reasoning: Suppose we select at most one point per row or per column to avoid? If we select at most one point per row, then can't have 4 points forming trapezoid because need at least two points in each of two rows. So we could select 101 points (one per row). Similarly one per column.\n\nBut maybe we can do more than 101.\n\nWe can think of constructing a set with each row having many points but ensuring that the set of differences across rows are disjoint.\n\nObservation: For a given row, the set of pairwise differences (positive) determines potential conflicts with other rows. To avoid conflict, the difference sets of any two rows must be disjoint. Because if they share a common difference d, then we could have x1,x2 in row1 with x2 - x1 = d, and x3,x4 in row2 with x4 - x3 = d, giving isosceles trapezoid? Wait need also alignment such that x3 - x1 = x4 - x2? That condition is stronger; just having equal internal differences doesn't guarantee cross equality. Let's test: Suppose row1 has points at 1 and 3 (difference 2). Row2 has points at 5 and 7 (difference 2). Then we can take x1=1,x2=3,x3=5,x4=7. Then x3 - x1 =4, x4 - x2=4, equal! Indeed works: offset delta =4. So any two rows each containing a pair with same width yields an isosceles trapezoid (by aligning leftmost points perhaps). But need also that ordering yields valid quadrilateral: we have x1 < x2 and x3 < x4, and we can set them.\n\nThus condition reduces to: If two rows each have at least two points, and they share a common difference value d between some pair, then we can create trapezoid. Because we can align the left points arbitrarily? Wait we need x3 - x1 = x4 - x2 . If we have pairs (a,b) in row1 with b-a = d, and (c,d') in row2 with d' - c = d. Then we can set x1 = a, x2 = b, x3 = c, x4 = d'. Then we need x3 - x1 = c - a. x4 - x2 = d' - b = (c+d) - (a+d) = c - a . Indeed same. So works irrespective of positions as long as ordering consistent (i.e., a<b and c<d'). So basically any common internal difference leads to trapezoid.\n\nThus the condition to avoid any such trapezoid is: For any two rows, the sets of internal differences must be disjoint. Similarly for columns for vertical orientation (swap roles). Also need to avoid rectangles which correspond to difference zero? Actually rectangle requires x3 = x1 and x4 = x2 (vertical legs). That difference is zero (c-a=0). So having same x coordinate across rows also creates rectangle. That corresponds to having difference d=0? But internal difference zero would require same point repeated not possible. Rectangle condition: need x1 = x3 and x2 = x4, i.e., both columns the same. That is two rows sharing same two columns. That would be two rows each having points at same x-values, which gives difference d = x2 - x1 appears in both rows. So already covered by common difference.\n\nThus problem reduces to constructing a set of points such that for any two distinct rows, the set of differences among selected x's in each row are disjoint. Also similarly for columns (since we must also avoid trapezoid with bases parallel to y-axis). That adds symmetry condition.\n\nGoal maximize total points.\n\nWe can think of using at most one point per row to trivially avoid differences (since set of differences empty). That yields 101 points. Perhaps we can do better by allowing some rows have multiple points but ensure differences across rows are unique.\n\nMaximum number of rows with multiple points could be limited because differences values range from 1 to 100 (positive). There are only 100 possible positive differences. If each row with at least two points contributes at least one difference value, and rows must have disjoint difference sets, then at most 100 such rows can have at least two points. Since there are 101 rows total, maybe we can have 100 rows each with 2 points (different differences each) plus one row with 1 point. That would be 100*2 + 1 = 201 points. This matches earlier guess.\n\nBut also need to consider columns condition analogously. If we fill rows each with points, columns will also have many points, maybe causing conflict with column condition. Need to check that column condition also satisfied: no two columns can share same vertical difference (i.e., y-differences). Since our construction symmetrical maybe works.\n\nIf we construct a set where each row has either 0,1,or2 points, and each difference appears at most once across rows, and similarly each column has at most one difference across columns. Since we have symmetric grid, we can try to have exactly one point per column as well? Let's think.\n\nIf each row has exactly 2 points, then each column would receive points from possibly multiple rows; we need to ensure column condition holds too (differences in y between points in same column across rows). So we must also ensure the set of vertical differences among points sharing a column are unique across columns.\n\nThus a design similar to a Sidon set? Actually need both row differences and column differences be unique across rows/columns.\n\nSimplify: Suppose we select points (i, i) for i=1..101 (diagonal). That's 101 points, any two rows share at most one column? Actually each row has exactly one point, so condition satisfied trivially. Could we add more points while still maintaining uniqueness? Consider adding another diagonal offset: (i, i+1) maybe leads to some rows with two points.\n\nGoal maximize total points; candidate max maybe 202? Let's think of constructing a permutation matrix plus offset? If we select exactly one point per row and per column, we get 101 points (a permutation). Adding an extra point per row would cause some column duplication leading to columns having two points, creating vertical differences that could clash.\n\nPotential bound: Since there are 100 possible non-zero differences in x and also 100 in y, total number of rows with ≥2 points ≤100, total columns with ≥2 points ≤100. Number of points = #rows_one + 2*#rows_two? Not exactly.\n\nLet R0 = rows with 0 points, R1 = rows with 1 point, R2 = rows with 2 points, maybe more (>2) but would generate many differences causing conflicts quickly.\n\nIf a row has k points, it contributes C(k,2) differences. Since total positive differences are at most 100, sum over rows of C(k_i,2) ≤ 100 (since differences sets across rows must be disjoint). Similarly sum over columns of C(l_j,2) ≤ 100, where l_j is number of points in column j.\n\nWe want maximize total N = Σ k_i = Σ l_j.\n\nWe have constraints: Σ C(k_i,2) ≤ 100, Σ C(l_j,2) ≤ 100, where sum of k_i = sum of l_j = N, and there are at most 101 rows/columns each.\n\nOptimization: To maximize N given the constraints, we should use many rows with small k (0 or 1). Since C(0,2)=0, C(1,2)=0, C(2,2)=1, C(3,2)=3, etc. Since total allowed differences small, better have many rows with k=2 (each consumes 1 difference) rather than a few rows with large k (which use many). So to maximize N, allocate differences to rows each with exactly 2 points until we run out. Similarly for columns.\n\nThus we can have at most 100 rows with k=2 (each uses one difference) and the remaining 1 row maybe with k=1 (or 0). That gives N_row = 100*2 + 1*1 = 201 points.\n\nSimilarly, columns also need ≤100 columns with l=2, rest with l=1 maybe.\n\nNow is it possible to have a configuration achieving N=201 that satisfies both row and column constraints simultaneously? Likely yes using a structure of a \"permutation plus a matching shift\". Example: Use each of 100 differences d=1..100 exactly once assigned to a row. For row i (i=1..100), pick two points at columns (i, i+d_i) maybe. Need columns also to have at most one difference repeated.\n\nLet's attempt constructive: For d from 1 to 100, assign row r = d (or any). Choose points (r, r) and (r, r+d) but column index r+d could exceed 101 for large d. So need stay within 1..101.\n\nAlternative: Use rows 1..100, each row i contains points at columns i and i+1 (difference 1). That would reuse difference 1 many times – not allowed. So different differences per row.\n\nCould use rows i with points (i, i) and (i, i+ i)?? Not good.\n\nRemember columns also need differences unique. We may think of using a set that forms a \"graph\" where each row and column degree ≤2, and the edges correspond to points. The condition on differences corresponds to distances (difference of column indices) for rows, similarly for columns.\n\nIf each row has exactly two points, that defines a matching of columns? Actually each row connects two columns, forming an edge between those columns. The difference associated with that row is the distance between the two columns. The requirement that differences across rows are distinct means edges have distinct lengths.\n\nSimilarly, each column's degree (number of points in that column) may be up to 2; the vertical differences correspond to distances between rows for pairs of points in same column. The requirement is that for any two columns, the row differences of pairs in each column must be distinct.\n\nBut if each column also has at most two points, then each column defines an edge between its two rows with a length (row difference). These lengths must also be distinct across columns.\n\nThus we can think of organizing points such that each row and each column has degree ≤2, and the set of row-interval lengths (horizontal edges) are all distinct; same for column-interval lengths (vertical edges) are distinct.\n\nGoal N = total edges = number of points (since each point is incident to its row and column). With degree ≤2, the graph formed on grid maybe collection of cycles and paths.\n\nWe need to maximize edges given constraints of distinct lengths ≤100 each direction. Since we have at most 100 distinct lengths each direction, we could have up to 100 rows with degree 2 (giving 100 horizontal edges) and up to 100 columns with degree 2 (giving 100 vertical edges). However each point belongs to both a row edge and column edge? Actually each point is intersection of a row and column edge? In representation: Row edges connect two columns within same row; column edges connect two rows within same column. The overall configuration corresponds to a collection of points where each point belongs to a row edge (its row's pair) and column edge (its column's pair). So each point is at intersection of a particular row pair (i.e., between its two columns) and a particular column pair.\n\nIf each row has exactly two points, then those two points are in different columns. Each column may have 0,1,or2 points; but if many rows have degree 2, some columns will have higher degree potentially >2 causing column differences multiple pairs.\n\nBut we must maintain column degree ≤2 because otherwise column would have at least three points giving at least two differences in that column possibly causing conflict with other columns.\n\nThus we need a graph with maximum total points such that each row degree ≤2 and each column degree ≤2, and additionally the lengths (differences) of rows are distinct and lengths of columns are distinct.\n\nWe can aim for a configuration that forms a single permutation plus a second permutation shifted such that each column also has degree 2 at most.\n\nSimplify: We can try to achieve N=201 by having 100 rows with exactly 2 points, and one row with 1 point. Then total points=201. For columns, we need also degree distribution that respects constraints. Since total points 201 and 101 columns, average column degree ≈2. Yet with degree ≤2, some columns will have degree 2, some 1, some 0.\n\nNumber of column degree-2 columns =? Let c2 be count of columns with degree 2, c1 columns with degree 1, c0 with 0. Then 2c2 + c1 = 201. Also c2 + c1 + c0 =101. So 2c2 + c1 =201. Subtract first equation from twice second? Let's solve: c1 = 201 - 2c2. Since c1 ≥0, we need 2c2 ≤201 → c2 ≤100. Also c0 =101 - c2 - c1 =101 - c2 - (201 - 2c2) =101 - c2 -201 +2c2 = (c2) -100. So c0 = c2 - 100. Since c0 ≥0, need c2 ≥100. Therefore c2 =100 exactly, then c0=0, c1 =201 -200 =1. So columns distribution must be 100 columns with degree 2, 1 column with degree 1, and 0 columns with degree 0. This matches rows distribution symmetrical: 100 rows degree 2, 1 row degree 1.\n\nThus structure must be a near-perfect 2-regular bipartite graph: each of 100 rows connected to two columns, each of 100 columns connected to two rows, plus an extra row and column each with degree 1, and they must be incident to each other (the single point). This essentially forms a collection of cycles covering 100+100 vertices plus a single edge connecting the degree-1 row and column. That's feasible: we can have a single Hamiltonian cycle within the bipartite 100x100 subgrid, plus one extra point on remaining row/column linking them.\n\nNow we also need distinct horizontal lengths per row and distinct vertical lengths per column. Since each row has exactly two columns, the horizontal length for that row is the absolute difference between its two column indices; we need all 100 rows to have distinct lengths 1..100 (each appears once). Similarly for columns, each column's two rows must have distinct vertical lengths 1..100 (each appears once). This seems achievable by constructing a Latin square like mapping: use points (i, i) and (i, i+ d_i) where d_i = i (or a permutation) but need column degrees ≤2.\n\nIf we set row i has columns i and i+di where di distinct from 1..100. Then column i gets point from row i (first entry). Also column i appears perhaps again as the second entry of some other row j where i = j + d_j. Since each column must have degree 2 except one column that serves as degree 1. This would imply mapping from rows to second column forms a permutation of columns (except maybe one). If di is a permutation of {1..100} then second columns are i+di (mod? but must stay within 1..101). Not easy.\n\nBetter to think of using arithmetic: Let rows be 1..100, columns also 1..100 (ignore 101 for now). Define row i connects to columns i and i+ i (maybe exceed). Not good.\n\nPotential construction: Use grid of size 101; consider rows 1..101, columns 1..101. Use points (i, i) for i=1..101 (diagonal) gives each row and column degree 1 (total 101 points). Now we need add extra 100 points to increase degrees to 2 for 100 rows and columns while preserving distinct differences. For each i from 1..100, add point (i, 101) i.e., second column (column 101). Then each row i gets second point at column 101, giving row i horizontal length |101 - i| = 101 - i, which are distinct (1..100). Good! Row i difference = 101 - i, distinct. Row 101 already has only one point (101,101) remains degree 1. So rows condition satisfied: 100 rows have degree 2 with distinct lengths 1..100, row 101 degree1.\n\nNow column degrees: Column 101 now has 100 points (rows 1..100) plus row 101 also has point (101,101) (so column 101 degree 101!). That's not allowed because column degree exceeds 2 and will create many vertical differences. So not good.\n\nNeed each column degree ≤2.\n\nAlternative: Use second points placed across columns such that each column gets exactly two points (except one column). For rows i, we could pair rows i and something else to share a column.\n\nWe need a matching between rows and columns such that each row i (except one) is paired with two distinct columns, and each column j (except one) is paired with two rows, and the lengths are distinct.\n\nThink of constructing a 100-cycle: sequence of rows and columns alternating: row r1 - column c1 - row r2 - column c2 - ... etc forming a 2*100 cycle. Then each row appears twice (connected to two columns). In such a cycle, each column also appears twice. Horizontal length for row r_k is |c_{k} - c_{k-1}| (difference between the two columns incident to that row). For distinctness we need those differences to be 1..100 unique. Similarly column vertical lengths are differences between adjacent rows.\n\nThus we need to find a Hamiltonian cycle on bipartite complete graph of size 100+100 such that the absolute differences between consecutive vertices on each side are all distinct.\n\nWe can think of ordering rows 0..99 and columns 0..99 with edges (row i, column i) and (row i, column i+1 mod 100) maybe produce differences of 0 and 1 not distinct.\n\nMaybe we can use simple construction: For k=1..100, add points (k, k) and (k, k+1) where indices modulo 101? That gives row differences 1 for many rows, not distinct.\n\nWe need distinct horizontal differences 1..100 per row. So row i must have its two columns differ by i (or some distinct value). So we need to assign for each i a pair of columns (a_i, b_i) with |a_i - b_i| = i, and each column appears in exactly two rows (except maybe one). This is akin to designing a set of 100 intervals of lengths 1..100 covering each integer point (1..101) exactly twice (except endpoints). This resembles constructing a \"perfect difference covering\".\n\nMaybe we can use intervals that nest: For i from 1 to 100, let interval be between column i and column i+1? length 1 each; not distinct.\n\nBut we could use intervals like: for length i, choose interval [i, i+ i]? Not possible exceed.\n\nLet's think of using columns 1..101. Suppose we take row i (i=1..100) with columns i and i+1 (difference 1) for many rows; fails distinct.\n\nAlternative: Use row i with columns i and i+ (101 - i). Difference = 101 - 2i? Not distinct necessarily.\n\nBetter systematic: Use columns as numbers 0..100. For each difference d from 1..100, we need a row whose two columns are (0, d) perhaps. But that would reuse column 0 many times >2.\n\nThus we need each column used at most twice.\n\nHence think of constructing a permutation σ of columns such that each row i connects to column i and σ(i). Then differences are |σ(i) - i| distinct. Since σ is a permutation of columns, each column appears exactly once as second column, and appears also once as first column (since column i appears as first for row i). Therefore each column degree =2 (once as first, once as second), except possibly for column which is the degree-1 column (where row with only one point includes same column as its first/second?). For 100 rows considered, we have 100 columns i (i=1..100) each appear twice, okay. Then row i difference d_i = |σ(i) - i|. Need all d_i distinct and range 1..100.\n\nThus we need a permutation σ on {1..100} such that the absolute differences |σ(i)-i| are a permutation of {1,...,100}. This is a classic problem: Find a permutation where all absolute differences are distinct (a \"deranged difference permutation\"). Such permutations exist: known as \"Skolem sequences\" or \"graceful labeling\" of a path of length 100. Since a path with 101 vertices can be gracefully labeled with vertices 0..100 and edges labeled 1..100. Indeed graceful labeling of a path P_n yields a labeling of vertices with distinct numbers 0..n such that edge differences are 1..n. For a path with n edges, there is a graceful labeling. Here we have 100 edges (since rows correspond to edges). So we can take the graceful labeling of path with vertices 0..100. Label vertices with numbers 0..100 such that edges (i,i+1) have differences 1..100. The labeling of vertices is a permutation of 0..100, and consecutive vertices correspond to edges with distinct differences. In our context, we could let rows correspond to edges between consecutive vertices in the path; columns correspond to vertices. So each edge (row) connects two vertices (columns) with distinct difference. Since each vertex appears in at most two edges (except endpoints appear in one), degree constraints satisfied. Perfect!\n\nThus construction: Take a graceful labeling of path P_100 (with vertices v0,...,v100). Assign column numbers equal to labels (0..100). For each edge e_k (k=1..100), connect the two vertices of that edge: these correspond to a row with two column positions (the two vertex labels). Each edge's difference is k (unique). Each vertex (column) degree: interior vertices degree 2, endpoints degree 1. Thus we have 100 rows degree 2 (edges), and 2 columns degree 1 (the endpoints). Wait we need only one column degree 1, not two. But we also have a row degree 1? In our model we had also one row with degree 1 (the extra row with one point). The graceful labeling path yields exactly two vertices of degree 1 (endpoints). That's two columns with degree 1, and all other columns degree 2. To match earlier requirement of only one column degree 1, we can add the extra row-degree 1 point linking the two degree-1 columns together: add a point at intersection of that row (row101) and one of those columns? Actually to connect two degree-1 columns, we can add a row (the 101st row) with a point in each of the two degree-1 columns? That would be two points, making the row degree 2, column degrees become 2 each. But then row count changes.\n\nInstead we can choose to have 100 rows degree2 and 1 row degree1, and columns 100 degree2 and 1 degree1. The path graceful labeling gives 101 rows? Wait rows correspond to edges (100). We still need an extra row, which could be the 101st row containing the single point at the column which is the unique degree-1 column (maybe we merge the two endpoints into same column by using column 101 as both endpoints?). Not exactly.\n\nBut we can modify: Use path on 101 vertices labeled 0..100. This has 100 edges. The two endpoint vertices have degree1 (columns). We'll have exactly two columns degree1, but we need only one column degree1. However we could also have two rows degree1 (maybe the extra row degree1 plus we have two column degree1). Our earlier analysis forced column distribution to have exactly 100 columns degree2 and 1 column degree1 because total points 201. However we could also have column distribution of 99 degree2 and 3 degree1 etc as long as totals satisfy equations? Let's recompute: Let columns degrees c_j, each ≤2. Sum c_j = N = 201. Suppose we have a columns with degree2, b columns with degree1, rest 0. Then 2a + b = 201 and a + b ≤101.\n\nWe can solve possibilities. Let a = number degree2. Then b = 201 - 2a. Need b ≥0, integer, and a + b ≤101 ⇒ a + 201 - 2a ≤101 ⇒ 201 - a ≤101 ⇒ a ≥100. So a at least 100. Since max a ≤101, possibilities: a=100 gives b=201-200=1, total columns used =101 (good). a=101 gives b= -1 impossible. So only solution a=100,b=1. Thus exactly one column degree1. So path construction gives two degree1 columns, not suitable.\n\nThus we need to adjust: maybe use a \"graceful labeling\" of a tree with 101 vertices where all but one internal vertices have degree 2, and exactly one leaf (degree1). But a tree must have at least two leaves. So impossible. Hence our earlier deduction that column distribution must be exactly 100 columns degree2 and 1 column degree1 is forced. That means we need a graph bipartite where columns side has exactly one leaf. That's impossible because sum of degrees on column side equals sum of degrees on row side = N = 201, which is odd. Since each edge contributes 1 to each side degree sum, total sum of degrees on each side equals N. If one side has odd total degree, number of odd-degree vertices on that side must be even (handshaking lemma). Columns side with degrees ≤2: total sum 201 odd ⇒ number of vertices with odd degree (i.e., degree1) must be odd. Since degree options are 0,1,2; odd degree vertices are those with degree1. So columns must have odd number of degree1 vertices. With total columns ≤101, possible counts: 1,3,5,... But earlier equation gave only 1 possible? Let's recompute with a general a (degree2 count) and b (degree1 count). Then 2a + b =201, a+b ≤101. Solve for possibilities.\n\nb = 201 - 2a. Need b≥0, integer. Also a+b = a + 201 -2a = 201 - a ≤101 ⇒ a ≥100. So a can be 100 or 101.\n\nIf a=100, then b=201-200=1, a+b=101 OK.\n\nIf a=101, then b=201-202=-1 not allowed.\n\nThus only a=100, b=1 solution, so exactly one column degree1.\n\nBut handshaking lemma says sum of degrees of vertices in any graph = 2*edges, which is even. However that's total sum across whole bipartite graph (both sides). Here sum of degrees on column side alone can be odd, because edges counted once per side. That's allowed. Number of odd-degree vertices on that side must be even? Actually any graph, the number of vertices with odd degree in the whole graph is even. So across both sides combined, total number of odd-degree vertices is even. We have column side odd-degree count b=1 (odd). Therefore row side must also have odd number of odd-degree vertices to make total even. Row side has also 1 odd-degree vertex (since we have 100 rows degree2 and 1 row degree1). So total odd-degree vertices =2, which is even. Works.\n\nThus configuration with exactly one column degree1 and one row degree1 is feasible.\n\nNow need to construct such bipartite graph meeting distinct length constraints.\n\nWe can modify graceful labeling by merging the two leaves into same column (so column degree 2) and make a row leaf instead. For example, take path labeling on columns 0..100 (101 columns). The two leaves are columns 0 and 100 (degree1). If we add an extra point (row 101) at column 0 (or 100) making that column degree2, and column 0 now degree2, column 100 becomes degree1 (still leaf). However row 101 degree1 adds the needed odd row leaf. That yields columns: one leaf (col100) degree1, other column 0 now degree2, all others degree2. So column degree distribution matches: exactly one column degree1. Row distribution: we have 100 rows from edges (degree2) plus row101 degree1. That's perfect! Need to ensure that adding point (row101, column0) does not introduce new differences conflicts in column 0 (vertical difference). Column 0 previously had a point at row corresponding to edge incident? Which row corresponded to edge that connects column0 to some other column? In path, edge incident to vertex 0 is between column0 and column a where a is label of adjacent vertex (maybe number something). That edge corresponds to a particular row (say row r0). So column0 already has one point from row r0. Adding new point at row101 adds second point in column0, creating vertical difference Δ = |row101 - r0|. This difference must be unique among column differences (i.e., no other column has same vertical difference between its two points). Since column0 was previously leaf (degree1) now degree2; the vertical difference for column0 is now some value. Need to ensure it is not equal to any other column's vertical difference. We have control by choosing which row we connect to, maybe row101 can be placed to give a new unique vertical difference not used elsewhere. Column100 remains leaf (degree1) no vertical difference.\n\nAll other columns have degree2 as defined by path edges: each interior vertex appears in two edges, thus its vertical differences are the differences between row numbers of the two edges incident to it. In our construction, rows corresponds to edges; each interior vertex belongs to two edges (rows). The vertical difference for that column is the absolute difference between the indices (row numbers) of those two edges. We need these vertical differences to be distinct across columns, and also distinct from the new column0 difference.\n\nIs it possible to label edges (rows) with numbers 1..100 such that the vertical differences (between consecutive edges incident to each interior vertex) are the set 1..100 as well, maybe achieving graceful labeling of a tree's line graph? This seems more complex.\n\nBut maybe the problem only asks for maximum number, not construction proof; we can argue upper bound 201, and existence by known graceful labeling of path plus adding extra point (as above) ensures we can achieve 201? We need to verify column differences distinctness holds.\n\nIn graceful labeling of a path, assign vertex labels (columns) a0,...,a100 such that edge i (between a_{i-1}, a_i) has label i (difference). Edge labels are distinct 1..100. Edge labels correspond to row differences horizontally (good). Now rows are edges i=1..100. For each interior vertex a_k (k=1..99), incident edges are i=k and i=k+1 (since edges numbered sequentially). The vertical distance between rows i=k and i=k+1 is 1 (since row numbers consecutive). Thus the column differences for interior columns are all equal to 1, causing conflict! Indeed vertical differences would all be 1, not distinct. So this fails.\n\nThus my earlier column condition more stringent: column vertical differences must also be distinct, but the graceful path gives same vertical diff of 1 for all interior vertices.\n\nThus our assumption earlier about distinct column differences may be too strict? Wait condition for columns: avoid isosceles trapezoid with bases parallel to y-axis. This translates to similar condition: For any two columns, if they each have at least two points, they must not share a common vertical difference. So we need distinct vertical differences across columns as well. Indeed.\n\nThus need a configuration where each column's pair of rows have unique difference values (vertical). So need analogous to rows: each column with two points should have unique vertical gap.\n\nThus we need a set of intervals (horizontal for rows, vertical for columns) both sets of lengths covering 1..100 uniquely. This is symmetric problem. So we need a set of 100 row intervals of distinct lengths, and 100 column intervals of distinct lengths, and they intersect appropriately at points (forming a permutation of intersections). This is reminiscent of constructing a Latin square of order 101 maybe.\n\nObservation: The set of points can be viewed as edges of a permutation matrix plus additional ones forming a \"grid of non-attacking rooks\"? Actually each row has two columns, each column has two rows, forming a collection of 100 4-cycles? If we have a collection of 100 disjoint 4-cycles (rectangles), each rectangle uses two rows and two columns, providing two horizontal differences maybe equal... Not distinct.\n\nTime limited. Maybe answer is 201, with known result similar to Zarankiewicz problem? I think maximum is 201.\n\nThus final answer: 201.", "response": "ANSWER: 201", "answer": 201, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -8.344648279035027e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999991655355203, "logp_correct": -8.344648279035027e-07, "cot_lp_mean": -0.648530965816144, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4516548189883238, "frac_below_-5": 0.012219480672071436, "digit_lp_min": -3.895812749862671, "answer_first_mention_lp": -9.536697689327411e-06, "n_cot_tokens": 8511, "lp_end": -8.439661905867979e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -6.103329360485077e-05, "logp_nocot": -6.200767159461975, "p_nocot": 0.0020278743364220927, "uplift": 6.200766324997147}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "6346c210-8136-407b-9a45-8db78bbccc0a", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: The natural numbers from $1$ up to $300$ are evenly located around a circle. We say that such an ordering is \"alternate\" if each number is either less than its two neighbors or greater than its two neighbors. We will call a pair of neighboring numbers a \"good\" pair if, by removing that pair from the circumference, the remaining numbers form an alternate ordering. Determine the least possible number of good pairs in which there can be an alternate ordering of the numbers from $1$ to $300$ inclusive.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: The natural numbers from $1$ up to $300$ are evenly located around a circle. We say that such an ordering is \"alternate\" if each number is either less than its two neighbors or greater than its two neighbors. We will call a pair of neighboring numbers a \"good\" pair if, by removing that pair from the circumference, the remaining numbers form an alternate ordering. Determine the least possible number of good pairs in which there can be an alternate ordering of the numbers from $1$ to $300$ inclusive.", "true_answer": 150, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "6346c210-8136-407b-9a45-8db78bbccc0a:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 2475, 8663, 220, 16, 485, 4095, 2846, 261, 22005, 350, 64, 135200, 8, 2238, 484, 290, 30931, 382, 43193, 25, 2454, 3577, 382, 4784, 261, 2698, 11085, 350, 2695, 1572, 2973, 31655, 8, 503, 261, 2698, 10562, 350, 143566, 1572, 2973, 31655, 741, 1328, 382, 1299, 125099, 50342, 326, 139394, 13, 2214, 1952, 2086, 328, 8632, 11, 581, 665, 679, 4387, 125099, 16281, 13, 2214, 14210, 5517, 11, 625, 4149, 13, 7306, 297, 28, 4095, 1952, 11, 813, 4149, 364, 55292, 261, 10610, 328,