5, 13, 2632, 484, 1481, 1520, 1899, 364, 3206, 7890, 3291, 297, 1340, 9578, 1991, 1899, 24702, 364, 39520, 37588, 11, 1058, 4465, 13, 357, 2411, 19957, 499, 314, 220, 5215, 472, 581, 30248, 11965, 7890, 120152, 326, 10112, 24174, 13, 51441, 6052, 220, 5215, 364], "n_tokens": 11814, "words": 7589, "band_lo": 4367.0, "band_hi": 17468.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to place numbers 1..300 around a circle (a permutation) such that the ordering is alternate: each element is either a local minimum (less than both neighbors) or a local maximum (greater than both neighbors). This is like alternating peaks and valleys. For even number of elements, we can have perfect alternating sequence. For odd length, not possible. Here n=300 even, so possible.\n\nDefine a pair of neighboring numbers (adjacent in circle). A pair is \"good\" if removing those two numbers (and connecting the gap) yields the remaining 298 numbers still forming an alternate ordering (i.e., the induced circular ordering of remaining numbers still alternates). Need minimal possible number of such good pairs in any alternating ordering of 1..300.\n\nWe need to find an arrangement where few adjacent pairs have the property that if they are removed, the rest still alternates. Among all alternating permutations around circle, find minimal count of such pairs.\n\nWe need to characterize when a pair is good. Suppose we have an alternating circle. Each vertex is either peak or valley. Since n is even, we must have alternating pattern: peak, valley, peak, valley, ... around circle. So each vertex type alternates. So each edge connects a peak to a valley. Good pair removal should preserve alternation of remaining circle: after removing two adjacent vertices (a peak and a valley), the neighbors of the removed pair become adjacent. Need to check if new adjacency respects alternating condition at the new edge.\n\nOriginal sequence: ... A - X - Y - B ... where X and Y are adjacent (the pair candidate). X is peak, Y valley (or vice versa). After removal, A becomes neighbor of B. The types: A is opposite type of X? Pattern: ... (peak) A - (valley) X - (peak) Y - (valley) B ... Actually if X is peak, then its neighbors are valleys: A and Y are valleys. If X is valley, its neighbors are peaks. Let's define pattern: around circle alternating types. So the two vertices of pair are opposite types. Their neighbors (outside the pair) are also opposite types respectively. After removal, A and B will be adjacent. What are their types? Let's consider starting pattern: ... A (type T1) - X (type T2) - Y (type T3) - B (type T4) ..., where T1 ≠ T2, T2 ≠ T3, T3 ≠ T4. Since T2 opposite T3 (adjacent). Also because alternation, T1 = T3, T2 = T4 (since alternating). Thus A and Y have same type, and X and B have same type. After removal of X and Y, we connect A (type T1) to B (type T4 = T2). So A and B are opposite types? Wait T1 = T3, T2 = T4. So A type = T1 = T3 (same as Y), B type = T2 = T4 (same as X). Since T1 ≠ T2, A and B are opposite types. Good: adjacency of opposite types is required for alternation. But we also need the numeric condition: for each vertex, its value must be less than both neighbors (if valley) or greater than both neighbors (if peak). After removal, the condition for A and B needs to hold with new neighbor. Before removal, A's two neighbors were (previous vertex) and X. After removal, its new neighbor is B. Need to check that A still satisfies its valley/peak condition with new neighbor B and the other neighbor (call it C). Similarly for B.\n\nThus a pair (X,Y) is good if after removing them, the alternating property still holds for all remaining vertices, which boils down to A and B still being correctly relative to their new neighbor B and A respectively.\n\nEssentially, we need that for A (type T1), its relationship with B is correct: If A is valley, we need A < B and also A < its other neighbor C (which already held). Since original: A < X (as valley) and A < C (when C is peak). After removal, we need A < B. Since originally X is a peak (if A is valley). Because pattern: valley A, peak X, valley Y, peak B. Wait let's set explicit pattern: Suppose sequence valley, peak, valley, peak. So let A = valley, X = peak, Y = valley, B = peak. Good. So A (valley) originally less than X (peak) and less than its other neighbor (call C) which is a peak. After removal, A becomes adjacent to B (peak). We need A < B. So condition: the valley A must be less than the peak B (two steps away). Similarly, B (peak) originally greater than Y (valley) and greater than its other neighbor D (valley). After removal, B must be greater than A. So need B > A, which is same as A < B. So the required condition reduces to: the valley A must be less than the peak B (which is across the removed pair). Since A< B is already implied? Not necessarily; A may be less than X but could be greater than B originally (since they are not adjacent). So pair is good iff the valley on one side is less than the peak on the other side (across the removed pair). In other words, in the original circle, for any adjacent peak-valley pair we can denote orientation: an adjacent pair (peak, valley) is such that peak > both neighbors, valley < both neighbors. For a good pair (X,Y) where X is peak, Y valley, we need that the valley preceding X (A) is less than the peak following Y (B). Since A is valley, B is peak. So condition reduces to A < B.\n\nSimilarly if pair orientation is valley then peak (i.e., X valley, Y peak), then the neighboring peak before X is C, neighboring valley after Y is D; after removal the new adjacency is C (peak) and D (valley). Need C > D. So condition: the peak before X must be greater than the valley after Y. Equivalent condition: the two vertices that become adjacent after removal must satisfy the appropriate inequality.\n\nThus we can view each adjacent pair as \"good\" if the element two positions away in same direction has appropriate inequality.\n\nSimplify: Number the circular order as v0, v1,...,v_{n-1} with alternating types. Suppose even indices are peaks, odd indices valleys (or vice versa). Then an adjacent pair (v_i, v_{i+1}) consists of a peak (if i even) and a valley (if i odd). After removing them, the new edge connects v_{i-1} and v_{i+2}. Their types: v_{i-1} same as v_{i+1} (valley), v_{i+2} same as v_i (peak). So need the valley v_{i-1} < peak v_{i+2}. So good pair condition: v_{i-1} < v_{i+2}. Similarly for i odd? Works similarly.\n\nThus we need to count number of indices i such that the element two steps ahead (i+2) is larger than element two steps back (i-1) given that v_{i-1} is valley, v_{i+2} is peak.\n\nGoal: minimize number of good pairs in alternating permutation of numbers 1..300.\n\nThus we need to arrange numbers to have as few occurrences where a valley is less than the peak two steps away.\n\nEquivalently, think of the sequence of peaks (positions even): P0, P1,...,P149 (since 150 peaks). Valleys V0,...,V149 interleaved: order ... V0 P0 V1 P1 V2 P2 ...\n\nWait indexing: suppose we start with a valley V0, then peak P0, valley V1, peak P1, ... total 300. Good pair condition for adjacent pair (P_k, V_{k+1})? Let's compute.\n\nIf we index circularly: ... V_k, P_k, V_{k+1}, P_{k+1} ...\n\nAdjacency pairs:\n- Pair (V_k, P_k) (valley before peak) corresponds to removal leads to new edge between previous peak P_{k-1} and next valley V_{k+1}. Condition for good: P_{k-1} > V_{k+1}. This is about peak preceding valley after removal.\n\n- Pair (P_k, V_{k+1}) (peak then valley) removal leads to new edge between V_k and P_{k+1}. Condition: V_k < P_{k+1}.\n\nThus there are 300 adjacent pairs, half are of each type.\n\nGiven symmetry, we can focus on condition V_k < P_{k+1}. So a good pair of type (peak, valley) corresponds to inequality V_k < P_{k+1}. Since V_k is valley (smaller than its adjacent peaks P_k and P_{k-1}), and P_{k+1} is peak (greater than V_{k+1} and V_k+?) Actually P_{k+1} is adjacent to V_{k+1} and V_{k+1}? Wait P_{k+1} adjacent to V_{k+1} and V_{k+2}. So V_k not adjacent to P_{k+1} originally.\n\nThus to minimize good pairs, we want many instances where V_k > P_{k+1} (i.e., valley larger than later peak), violating condition, making pair not good.\n\nBut we must maintain all valley < its two neighboring peaks; that places constraints.\n\nLet’s formalize: For each k, we have constraints:\n- V_k < P_k and V_k < P_{k-1}\n- P_k > V_k and P_k > V_{k+1}\n\nThus each valley is less than both adjacent peaks; each peak larger than both adjacent valleys.\n\nThus we can think of assigning numbers to peaks and valleys satisfying these inequalities. This is essentially a partial order: each valley less than its neighboring peaks.\n\nGoal: minimize number of k such that V_k < P_{k+1} (i.e., valley smaller than the next peak). Equivalent to maximize number of k where V_k > P_{k+1}. Note that P_{k+1} is adjacent to V_{k+1} and V_{k+2}, and must be > those.\n\nPotentially we could try to arrange numbers so that valleys are relatively large compared to next peaks, but still less than their own neighboring peaks.\n\nObservation: For any k, we have V_k < P_k and also V_k < P_{k-1}. So P_{k-1} and P_k are both greater than V_k. However P_{k+1} could be smaller or larger than V_k.\n\nThus to make V_k > P_{k+1}, we need peaks to be lower than adjacent valleys on one side (but still higher than the valley directly after). This seems plausible if we arrange peaks generally decreasing and valleys increasing in some pattern.\n\nWe need a cyclic arrangement.\n\nLet’s think of a construction: Sort numbers 1..300. Let's assign the smallest 150 numbers to peaks maybe? But peaks must be larger than neighboring valleys, so peaks should be larger numbers, valleys smaller numbers. So natural assignment: assign 150 smallest numbers to valleys, 150 largest numbers to peaks. That would satisfy constraints easily? Let's check: Each valley is among smallest half, each peak among largest half. Then each valley is less than any peak, especially its two neighbors. Condition V_k > P_{k+1} would never hold (since V_k < P_{k+1}). So all such pairs would be good? For pair (P_k, V_{k+1}) we need V_k < P_{k+1}. With this assignment, all V_k < all P_{k+1}. So every such pair is good. That's 150 good pairs (of that type). Similarly, for pair (V_k, P_k), condition P_{k-1} > V_{k+1}. Since all peaks > all valleys, this will also hold, making all 150 other pairs good. So total 300 good pairs. That's maximum, not minimum.\n\nWe need opposite: we want as many non-good pairs as possible. We need many V_k > P_{k+1}. To achieve that, we need valleys larger than some peaks. Thus we can't separate halves; we need interleaving values.\n\nThis resembles constructing alternating permutation that maximizes number of \"inversions\" across distance 2.\n\nDefine sequence of length 300 alternating valley-peak. Let's map valleys to a set A (size 150), peaks to set B (size 150). The constraints: each valley must be less than its neighboring peaks. So each valley must be less than the two peaks adjacent to it. So each valley's two neighboring peaks must be larger than that valley. But valleys could be greater than other peaks not adjacent to them.\n\nThus we can choose to make peaks generally low numbers, but they must still be higher than adjacent valleys, which are lower.\n\nGoal: produce many instances where V_k > P_{k+1}. That means valley V_k is larger than the next peak P_{k+1}. Since P_{k+1} is adjacent to V_{k+1} and V_{k+2}; to keep constraints, we need V_{k+1} and V_{k+2} be even smaller than P_{k+1}. So P_{k+1} could be relatively low among peaks.\n\nIdea: Arrange valleys in descending order, peaks in ascending order, but interleaved such that each valley is paired with a higher peak on its left, but a lower peak on its right. That would make many V_k > P_{k+1}. Similarly also affect the other condition for the other type of pair.\n\nSpecifically, suppose we label indices around circle as V0,P0,V1,P1,...,V149,P149. Choose peaks P_i increasing with i (i from 0..149) low to high perhaps? Wait we want P_i to be lower than preceding valley? For V_k > P_{k+1}, we want V_k relatively large, P_{k+1} relatively small. If peaks increase, then later peaks are larger, making V_k > P_{k+1} less likely for later k. Better to have peaks decreasing: P_0 largest, P_{149} smallest. Then V_k > P_{k+1} for many ks (since P_{k+1} is smaller). However need constraint: each valley V_k must be less than adjacent peaks P_k and P_{k-1}. Since peaks decreasing, P_{k-1} > P_k. So valley V_k must be less than both a larger peak (P_{k-1}) and a smaller peak (P_k). Therefore V_k must be less than the smaller of those two peaks (i.e., P_k). So valley must be less than P_k. So to make V_k large, we need P_k even larger. So peaks need to be significantly larger than valleys they are right of, but can be larger than preceding valley.\n\nThus we could set P_i large numbers descending, valleys somewhat less but still can be bigger than next peak.\n\nConstruction approach: Let’s assign peaks descending from large to smaller, and valleys also descending but offset.\n\nLet’s use numbers 1..300. Let’s assign peaks as the top 150 numbers (151..300) but order them descending around circle. Valleys as bottom 150 numbers (1..150) but order them descending as well? Then V_k > P_{k+1}? Since peaks descending, P_{k+1} is slightly smaller than P_k. Valleys are all less than any peak (since <=150 vs >=151) so V_k < P_{k+1}. So not good.\n\nWe need peaks not necessarily all larger than valleys. Some peaks must be relatively low to allow subsequent valley larger.\n\nBut every valley must be less than its right neighbor peak P_k. So P_k must be > V_k. So each peak must be larger than the preceding valley. That suggests we can pair each valley V_k with a larger peak P_k, but the following peak P_{k+1} could be much smaller than V_k (as long as it's still larger than V_{k+1}). So peaks can alternate high and low.\n\nThus we could create pattern: high peak, low valley (but valley must be < high peak), then low peak (smaller than previous valley?), wait valley preceding low peak must be less than that low peak, which would be contradictory if valley larger. Let's test.\n\nSequence: V0 low, P0 high (P0 > V0). Next valley V1 maybe moderate. Need P0 > V1 also? Actually V1 must be less than both P0 and P1. So V1 must be less than P0 (high) and less than P1 (whatever). So V1 must be small relative to P0, okay.\n\nNow for V0 > P1 to get good pair (P0,V1)? Wait good pair condition involves V0 < P1 (for pair P0,V1). Actually pair (P0,V1) good if V0 < P1. To make not good, we need V0 > P1. That's plausible if V0 moderate, P1 relatively low.\n\nThus design: alternate high peaks and low peaks, with valleys in between selected such that each valley is less than its left peak (high) but may be greater than right peak (low). That will create many non-good pairs.\n\nSimilarly for the other type of pair (V_k,P_k), condition for good is P_{k-1} > V_{k+1}. To make not good, need P_{k-1} < V_{k+1}. That's analogous to valley after being larger than preceding peak.\n\nThus we need many instances where a peak is lower than the valley two steps ahead.\n\nThus likely minimal good pairs may be as low as 0? Is it possible to have zero good pairs? Let's test feasibility. Need for all k, V_k > P_{k+1} (so every pair (P_k,V_{k+1}) not good). Also need for all k, P_{k-1} < V_{k+1} (so pair (V_k,P_k) not good). Wait that second condition is opposite orientation: For pair (V_k,P_k), good if P_{k-1} > V_{k+1}. Non-good if P_{k-1} < V_{k+1}. Since we already require V_{k+1} > P_{k} (from first condition shifting index), we can analyze consistency.\n\nLet's attempt to see if we can have zero good pairs.\n\nCondition set:\nFor all i (mod n): V_i > P_{i+1} . (1)\nAnd for all i: P_{i-1} < V_{i+1}. (2)\n\nNote (2) is same as V_{i+1} > P_{i-1} . Equivalent to V_j > P_{j-2} for all j.\n\nThus we require V_i > P_{i+1} and also V_i > P_{i-2} (since let j=i, then V_i > P_{i-2}). So each valley must be greater than two peaks: the one to its right (i+1) and the one two steps left (i-2). But valley must be less than its neighboring peaks P_i and P_{i-1} (by definition of valley). So each valley must lie between its neighboring peaks (which are larger) but be larger than the next peaks further away.\n\nIs this possible with total ordering of numbers? Possibly if peaks' values alternate high low.\n\nLet's attempt construct with 6 elements (small case) to test feasibility. n=6 even, 3 valleys V0,V1,V2 and peaks P0,P1,P2. Constraints: V0 < P0,P2 ; V1 < P1,P0 ; V2 < P2,P1. For zero good pairs we need V0 > P1 (since V0 > P_{0+1}=P1) and also V0 > P_{0-2}=P? indices mod 3: i-2 = (0-2)=1 mod 3 => P1 again. So same condition. Similarly V1 > P2 , V2 > P0.\n\nThus each valley must be greater than the peak two steps ahead (which is the other peak not adjacent). So set up inequalities:\n\nV0 > P1\nV1 > P2\nV2 > P0\n\nAlong with valley less than its adjacent peaks:\n\nV0 < P0 and V0 < P2\nV1 < P1 and V1 < P0\nV2 < P2 and V2 < P1\n\nCombine: V0 < P0, V0 > P1, V0 < P2\nThus P1 < V0 < min(P0,P2)\n\nSimilarly V1: P2 < V1 < min(P1,P0)\nV2: P0 < V2 < min(P2,P1)\n\nIs there assignment? Let's try pick peaks values: suppose P0=9, P1=5, P2=8. Then constraints:\nV0: 5 < V0 < min(9,8)=8 => V0 could be 6 or7.\nV1: 8 < V1 < min(5,9)=5 impossible. So fails.\n\nWe need ordering such that each peak that's being lower than certain valleys must be lower than them but also higher than other valleys.\n\nObservation: For V1 we need P2 < V1 < min(P1,P0). For this to be possible we need min(P1,P0) > P2. So both P0 and P1 must be greater than P2. Similarly other cycles. This suggests peaks must be ordered cyclically descending: P0 > P1 > P2 > P0 absurd.\n\nThus impossible for 3 peaks. So zero good pairs impossible.\n\nWe need to find minimal number.\n\nProbably the minimal number equals 100? Let's attempt to derive lower bound.\n\nConsider each good pair corresponds to an inequality V_i < P_{i+1}. Define direction: Let's count number of indices where inequality holds versus opposite. For each i, either V_i < P_{i+1} or V_i > P_{i+1} (cannot be equal). Define indicator g_i = 1 if V_i < P_{i+1} (good for pair (P_i, V_{i+1})?). Count of good pairs of that type = number of i where V_i < P_{i+1}. Similarly good pairs of other type correspond to inequality P_{i-1} > V_{i+1}. But note P_{i-1} > V_{i+1} is equivalent to V_{i+1} < P_{i-1}. That's same form shifted: for j = i+1, condition V_j < P_{j-1}. So the total number of good pairs equals number of i where V_i < P_{i+1} plus number where V_i < P_{i-1} (since shifting). But note pair (V_i, P_i) corresponds to condition V_{i+1} < P_i? Wait earlier we derived good for (V_i,P_i) when P_{i-1} > V_{i+1}, i.e., V_{i+1} < P_{i-1}. That's not same index. However total good pairs count G = #{i | V_i < P_{i+1}} + #{i | V_{i+1} < P_{i-1}}. Re-index second sum: let j = i+1 => #{j | V_j < P_{j-2}}. So G = #{i | V_i < P_{i+1}} + #{i | V_i < P_{i-2}}.\n\nThus each valley compares with two peaks: the one two steps ahead (i+1) and two steps behind (i-2). Good pair contributed if inequality holds for either comparison? Actually each comparison corresponds to a distinct adjacent pair (the pair removed). The pair (P_i,V_{i+1}) good iff V_i < P_{i+1}. The pair (V_i,P_i) good iff V_{i+1} < P_{i-1} i.e., V_i < P_{i-2}. So each valley yields potentially two good pairs based on two comparisons.\n\nThus G = count of comparisons where valley less than that peak. Since each valley participates in two comparisons with different peaks, total possible 300 comparisons (since 150 valleys *2). Minimal G is minimal number of these inequalities that must hold given constraints.\n\nNote constraints: For each i, we have V_i < P_i and V_i < P_{i-1}. So V_i is less than its two adjacent peaks. Those are not part of the comparisons for good pairs (they involve peaks two steps away). So the constraints don't directly force any of the comparisons to be less; they could be >.\n\nThus we can try to choose orientation such that for each valley, V_i > both distant peaks (P_{i+1} and P_{i-2}) to make all corresponding pairs not good. But earlier we found for n=6 (3 peaks) impossible globally. Perhaps minimal G > 0.\n\nWe need to find lower bound via cyclic ordering arguments, perhaps using parity or counting of up-down sequences.\n\nConsider labeling peaks order around circle as P0,P1,...,P149. Valleys V0... V149 with constraints V_i < min(P_i, P_{i-1}) and >? not constrained.\n\nWe can view a graph of inequalities between peaks and valleys. To minimize G we want many V_i > P_{i+1} and V_i > P_{i-2}. But note P_{i+1} is adjacent to V_{i+1} and V_{i+2}. Since V_{i+1} must be less than P_{i+1}, we have P_{i+1} > V_{i+1}. Not conflicting with V_i > P_{i+1} could still hold if V_i is larger than P_{i+1}, while V_{i+1} is smaller.\n\nThus we essentially need to arrange numbers such that peaks are low relative to preceding valleys and high relative to following valleys alternating.\n\nPotentially we can achieve a pattern where peaks are monotone decreasing: P0 highest, then P1 lower, etc. Valleys then placed in between to satisfy constraints.\n\nLet's attempt constructive approach for larger n.\n\nTake peaks descending: assign peak values decreasing from 300 downwards: P0 = 300, P1 = 299, ..., P149 = 151 (largest 150 numbers). For valleys, we need each V_i < both P_i and P_{i-1}. Since P_i are descending, P_{i-1} > P_i. So the smaller of the two is P_i. So need V_i < P_i. That's easy. Additionally we want V_i > P_{i+1} (next peak) to make pair not good. Since P_{i+1} is slightly lower than P_i (because descending), we can choose V_i somewhere between P_{i+1} and P_i (i.e., between consecutive peak values). That would satisfy V_i < P_i and V_i > P_{i+1}. Also need V_i > P_{i-2}? Not required for good pairs? Actually V_i also compared to P_{i-2} for other type. Need V_i > P_{i-2} to make second comparison not good. Since P_{i-2} is larger (two steps earlier) because descending, P_{i-2} > P_{i-1} > P_i > P_{i+1}. So V_i > P_{i-2} cannot hold because P_{i-2} larger. Thus V_i will be less than P_{i-2}, causing that second comparison to be good (since V_i < P_{i-2}). So each valley will be less than P_{i-2}, making the pair (V_{i-2}, P_{i-2}) good? Let's compute: the second good condition corresponds to V_i < P_{i-2}. That will be true, making that pair good. Thus each valley contributes at least one good pair (the one where it's compared with P_{i-2}) if peaks are monotone decreasing.\n\nThus total good pairs at least 150? Because each valley will have one of the two comparisons true. Can we arrange peaks to have more alternating so that some valleys may be > both distant peaks, reducing count? Let's see.\n\nGoal to minimize number of true comparisons (V_i < P_{i+1} or V_i < P_{i-2}). For each valley, there are two distant peaks: one on right (i+1) and one two left (i-2). Since the peaks form a cyclic order, each valley is between two adjacent peaks; the distant peaks are the next peak on each side beyond the adjacent one.\n\nIf we think of peaks' ordering around circle, we can attempt to make them such that for many valleys, V_i is larger than both of those distant peaks. But is that possible globally? Consider three consecutive peaks P_{i-1}, P_i, P_{i+1}. The valley V_i sits between P_{i-1} and P_i, must be less than both. To be larger than P_{i+1}, we need P_{i+1} even smaller than V_i. So we need P_{i+1} < V_i < min(P_{i-1}, P_i). That's possible if peaks are not monotonic; they can dip low then rise.\n\nSimilarly, to also be larger than P_{i-2}, we need P_{i-2} even smaller. So need two steps away peaks both smaller than V_i.\n\nThus we can design peaks to have a \"valley\" of low values, then a higher valley V_i between higher peaks.\n\nGiven cyclic constraints, perhaps we cannot have all valleys larger than both distant peaks. There may be a combinatorial lower bound like at least 100 good pairs.\n\nLet's attempt to find lower bound using counting of sign changes.\n\nDefine for each i the comparison C_i = sign of (V_i - P_{i+1}) (+ if V_i > P_{i+1}, - if V_i < P_{i+1}). Similarly D_i = sign of (V_i - P_{i-2}). Good pairs correspond to negative signs (V_i < distant peak). We want minimize number of negatives across all i for both comparisons.\n\nObservation: C_i = - D_{i+1}? Let's check: D_{i+1} compares V_{i+1} with P_{i-1}. Not directly.\n\nMaybe we can use that sum over all valleys of (indicator V_i < P_{i+1}) + (indicator V_i < P_{i-2}) = total G. Also each peak appears in two such comparisons (as P_{i+1} for V_i and as P_{i-2} for V_{i+2}). So each peak is compared with two valleys: the valley before it (V_{i}) and valley after it (V_{i+1}? Actually check indices). The peak P_j is compared with valleys V_{j} (since P_j = P_{i+1} where i=j-1) and V_{j+2} (since P_j = P_{i-2} where i = j+2). So each peak appears in two comparisons.\n\nThus we have a bipartite graph where each edge corresponds to inequality V ? P. We have 300 edges. In each edge, the inequality could be either valley > peak (good? no) or valley < peak (good). Since total order of numbers, for each edge either valley smaller or larger. So we need to assign orientations to edges minimizing number of edges where valley < peak, subject to constraints that each valley is less than its two adjacent peaks (adjacent in circle). Those adjacent edges are not part of the 300 considered edges; they are separate constraints (must be valley < adjacent peaks). That's fine.\n\nThus we have a graph where each valley connects to two nonadjacent peaks (distance 2). We need orientation of these 300 edges (valley vs peak size) minimizing number pointing valley < peak.\n\nBut there may be a global feasibility condition on the directed graph because numbers are totally ordered.\n\nWe can think of constructing linear order of all 300 numbers such that for each edge, orientation matches order.\n\nGoal: minimize count of edges oriented from valley (smaller) to peak (larger). That's like we want as many edges oriented from peak (smaller) to valley (i.e., valley > peak). However any total order must respect transitivity: if A < B and B < C then A < C.\n\nMaybe we can view as a tournament? Not clear.\n\nPerhaps using pigeonhole principle: Since there are equal numbers of peaks and valleys, there must be at least 150 edges where valley < peak? Let's test.\n\nConsider sum of ranks: assign each number a rank 1..300. For each valley V_i, compare to two distant peaks. If V_i is larger than a peak, then its rank is higher.\n\nDefine for each valley count of distant peaks that are larger than it (i.e., V_i < P). Let s_i be 0,1,2. Then total G = sum_i s_i.\n\nOur goal minimize sum s_i.\n\nConstraints: For each valley, we already have two adjacent peaks larger than it, but those are not counted. However those peaks also appear as distant peaks for some other valleys. So each peak is larger than its two adjacent valleys (must be > them) but may be larger or smaller than other valleys.\n\nNow consider sum over all numbers of counts of times they are larger than a valley among these distant edges.\n\nFor each peak, let t_j be number of distant valleys that are smaller than it (i.e., have edge V < P, counting edges where peak > valley). Then total G = total number of edges where valley < peak = sum over peaks t_j (alternatively sum over valleys s_i). Since each edge counted once.\n\nEach peak appears in exactly two such edges (two valleys). So t_j ∈ {0,1,2}. Similarly as s_i.\n\nGoal minimize total edges with valley<peak, equivalently minimize sum t_j.\n\nNow note that the total number of peaks that are larger than both of their distant valleys could be 0? but each peak must be larger than its adjacent valleys (not distant). However for distant valleys, they could be larger than peak.\n\nWe can try to assign each peak to be relatively low (so many distant valleys larger) thereby giving t_j =0 (valley>peak) for both edges, reducing G. However each peak also must be larger than its neighboring valleys (adjacent). That's okay: being low but still higher than its immediate adjacent valleys (which must be even lower). So peaks can be low relative to many other valleys, but high relative to adjacent valleys.\n\nThus we might be able to set t_j =0 for many peaks, maybe for all peaks? If each peak is lower than both of its distant valleys, then each edge is valley>peak, giving G=0! But earlier we argued impossible for n=6. Let's test n=6 case with this idea: peaks P0,P1,P2 need to be lower than both distant valleys. For P0, distant valleys are V1 (since comparisons: V1 with P_{1-2}=P_{-1}=P2? Wait compute: for n=6, each peak appears with two valleys: V_{peak index}??? Let's compute explicit: indices: V0,P0,V1,P1,V2,P2. Distant edges: V0 compared to P_{-2}=P1? Actually earlier rule: V_i compared to P_{i+1} and P_{i-2}. So V0 compared to P1 and P_{-2}=P1? Wait i=0, P_{i+1}=P1, P_{i-2}=P_{-2}=P1? That suggests same peak? Something off because n small leads to overlap. In n=6, distance 2 may cause duplicates. indeed each valley may compare to same peak twice? Let's compute properly: indices modulo 6. Sequence indices: 0 V0,1 P0,2 V1,3 P1,4 V2,5 P2. Then V0 (i=0) compares to P_{i+1}=P1 (index3) and P_{i-2}=P_{-2}= index? -2 mod6 =4 which is V2, not a peak. Something wrong: formula using i referencing valley positions only maybe off.\n\nLet's re-derive correctly. Let indexing of valleys j and peaks j (same index). We earlier defined order V0,P0,V1,P1,... So position numbers: V_j at position 2j, P_j at 2j+1 modulo 2n.\n\nNow for valley V_j, the pair (P_j, V_{j+1}) removal condition uses V_j < P_{j+1}. Indeed index shift: V_j compared to peak P_{j+1}. That's one distant peak (the peak after next valley). The other comparison V_j < P_{j-1}? Let's recalc: pair (V_{j},P_{j}) good condition involves P_{j-1} > V_{j+1}, which is V_{j+1} < P_{j-1}. So valley V_{j+1} compared to peak P_{j-1}. So each valley appears in comparisons with peaks P_{j-1} and P_{j+1}. Indeed each valley V_j compared to peaks P_{j-1} and P_{j+1} (i.e., the peaks that are two steps away). Good.\n\nThus each valley V_j has two distant peaks: previous peak P_{j-1} and next peak P_{j+1}. Good pair counts correspond to V_j < P_{j+1} and V_j < P_{j-1}. So G = #{j | V_j < P_{j-1}} + #{j | V_j < P_{j+1}}.\n\nThus each peak P_j is compared with valleys V_{j-1} and V_{j+1}. So each peak appears in two edges: with preceding and succeeding valleys.\n\nThus earlier reasoning stands: we can aim for each peak to be lower than both V_{j-1} and V_{j+1}, i.e., V_{j-1} > P_j and V_{j+1} > P_j, making both edges non-good. That's possible if the peaks are low values and valleys around them higher (but still less than neighboring peaks? Wait valley V_{j-1} is adjacent to peak P_{j-1} and P_{j-2}. For V_{j-1} > P_j while still < its adjacent peaks P_{j-1} and P_{j-2}. This requires P_j be lower than those adjacent peaks, which is plausible if peaks vary.\n\nThus we can possibly achieve all peaks lower than surrounding valleys (nonadjacent) while still being higher than adjacent valleys (the ones they're directly next to). Since valley V_j is less than peaks P_{j-1} and P_j (adjacent). So V_j is less than its left peak P_{j-1} and right peak P_j. Therefore, for those two valleys, they are less than P_j. So P_j cannot be lower than both V_{j-1} and V_{j+1} if V_{j-1} need to be less than P_{j-2} and P_{j-1}. Wait V_{j-1} adjacent peaks are P_{j-2} and P_{j-1}. So V_{j-1} < P_{j-1} (adjacent) but V_{j-1} could be greater than P_j (nonadjacent). That's fine.\n\nThus it seems possible to have all 300 non-good? Let's try to construct small example n=4 (2 peaks 2 valleys). Sequence V0,P0,V1,P1. Constraints: V0<P0,P1? Wait adjacent peaks: V0 adjacent to P0 (right) and P1 (left, since circle). So V0 < P0 and V0 < P1. Similarly V1 < P1 and V1 < P0.\n\nNow good pairs count: pairs are (P0,V1) good if V0 < P1? Wait compute: good pair (P0,V1) depends on V0 < P1 (since V_before P0). That's always true because V0 < P1 (must be). Similarly (V0,P0) good if V1 < P_{-1}=P1? Actually pair (V0,P0) good if V1 < P_{-1}=P_{?} for 2 peaks index -1 = 1 that is P1. So condition V1 < P1, also always true. Therefore both pairs good, G=4? Actually there are 4 adjacent pairs: V0-P0, P0-V1, V1-P1, P1-V0. All are good inevitably due to constraints. So minimal G for n=4 is 4 (i.e., all). For n=6 we found earlier maybe cannot have zero good pairs, maybe minimal >0. Let's test n=6 via attempt.\n\nWrite variables: V0,P0,V1,P1,V2,P2.\n\nConstraints:\nV0 < P0 and P2.\nV1 < P1 and P0.\nV2 < P2 and P1.\n\nNow compare edges:\n- Pair (P0,V1) good if V0 < P1. (Because V0 is left valley). V0 < P1? Not required by constraints (V0 not adjacent to P1). Could be either.\n- Pair (V1,P1) good if V2 < P0. Condition V2 < P0? Not required.\n- Pair (P1,V2) good if V1 < P2.\n- Pair (V2,P2) good if V0 < P1? Wait careful: pair (V2,P2) corresponds to condition V0 < P0? Actually formula: pair (V_j,P_j) good if V_{j+1} < P_{j-1}. For j=2, need V_{0} < P_{1}. So same as previous condition V0 < P1.\n- Pair (P2,V0) good if V2 < P1.\n- Pair (V0,P0) good if V1 < P2.\n\nThus G = [V0<P1] + [V2<P0] + [V1<P2] + [V0<P1] + [V2<P1] + [V1<P2]? Wait duplicates. Let's compute systematically:\n\nEdges:\n1) (V0,P0): condition V1 < P_{-1}=P2 -> V1<P2.\n2) (P0,V1): condition V0 < P1.\n3) (V1,P1): condition V2 < P0.\n4) (P1,V2): condition V1 < P2.\n5) (V2,P2): condition V0 < P1.\n6) (P2,V0): condition V2 < P1.\n\nSo G = [V1<P2] + [V0<P1] + [V2<P0] + [V1<P2] + [V0<P1] + [V2<P1] = 2[V1<P2] + 2[V0<P1] + [V2<P0]+[V2<P1].\n\nThus we can attempt to assign numbers to make these false where possible.\n\nGoal: make V0>P1, V1>P2, V2>P0 and V2>P1 as well maybe impossible due to transitivity? Let's try assign peaks low numbers, valleys high numbers, but need V0 < P0 and P2 (adjacent). So each valley must be less than its two adjacent peaks, limiting how high it can be.\n\nMaybe minimal G relates to unavoidable comparisons due to cyclic order, yielding lower bound of 100.\n\nGiven time limit, maybe known result: minimal number of good pairs equals 150? Let's test with constructive arrangement achieving 150 good pairs (maybe minimal). Example: take peaks descending high numbers, valleys descending lower numbers placed between, leads to each valley greater than next peak but less than previous peak-2, giving exactly 150 good (the ones where V_i < P_{i-2} maybe). Could be minimal.\n\nLet's attempt to construct arrangement achieving G=150.\n\nTake peaks as descending from 300 down to 151. For valley V_i choose number between P_i and P_{i+1} (i.e., between two consecutive peaks). Since peaks descending, P_i > P_{i+1}. Choose V_i = (P_i + P_{i+1})/2 maybe integer. This ensures V_i < P_i (good) but V_i > P_{i+1}. Then V_i compared to P_{i-1} (previous peak) will be less because P_{i-1} > P_i > V_i, so V_i < P_{i-1}. Thus among two comparisons, V_i < P_{i+1}? false (since V_i > P_{i+1}) ; V_i < P_{i-1}? true, giving exactly one good per valley. So total G = 150 (since each valley contributes exactly one good). This satisfies constraints? Need also V_i < P_{i-1}? yes true. Also need V_i < P_i (adjacent) holds because V_i < P_i by construction. So valid.\n\nThus we achieve G = 150.\n\nCan we do less than 150? Would require some valleys contribute 0 good (both comparisons false), meaning V_i > P_{i-1} and V_i > P_{i+1}. But this would violate V_i < P_i and V_i < P_{i-1}? Wait V_i must be less than P_{i-1} as adjacent? Actually V_i adjacent to P_{i-1} (left) and P_i (right). So V_i must be less than P_{i-1}. So V_i > P_{i-1} impossible. Therefore each valley must be less than P_{i-1} (one of the distant peaks!). Indeed P_{i-1} is a distant peak (previous peak two steps