, 24, 9, 23, 314, 220, 1125, 11, 42507, 198, 17434, 314, 220, 19, 11, 40850, 11, 42507, 13, 24305, 11965, 357, 2242, 220, 1179, 23, 9, 1179, 15, 314, 220, 19, 11, 28653, 11, 1302, 11, 889, 10954, 220, 1179, 23, 9, 1179, 15, 11, 6145, 13, 7801, 220, 1179, 23, 9, 24, 28, 1157, 11, 42507, 11, 4215, 220, 19, 11, 40850, 11, 42507, 15248, 2684, 364, 10620, 478, 14, 17, 314, 220, 17, 11, 30526, 11, 42681, 22476, 350, 21910, 478, 1952, 741, 6208, 220, 17, 11, 30526, 11, 42681, 9, 17, 314, 220, 19, 11, 40850, 11, 42507, 11339, 364, 10620, 13749, 25, 581, 665, 23864, 2360, 12391, 25, 41021, 14866, 2360, 13319, 2595, 25, 220, 17, 11, 30526, 11, 42681, 61, 17, 364, 56734, 220, 17, 11, 30526, 11, 42681, 425, 220, 17, 11, 30526, 11, 42681, 2360, 1701, 86340, 10112, 1402, 17, 11, 30526, 11, 42681, 425, 220, 17, 11, 30526, 11, 42681, 314, 13570, 22951, 220, 17, 11, 30526, 11, 42681, 425, 220, 17, 11, 1302, 11, 1302, 314, 220, 19, 11, 40850, 11, 42507, 11, 1302, 11, 1302, 364, 22951, 220, 17, 11, 30526, 11, 42681, 425, 220, 1422, 11, 42681, 314, 13570, 21378, 220, 1422, 11, 42681, 314, 220, 702, 11, 1302, 659, 220, 22, 11, 1302, 659, 220, 1130, 659, 220, 21, 364, 56734, 1402, 9, 702, 11, 1302, 314, 220, 455, 11, 12536, 11, 9870, 11, 1302, 279, 9, 22, 11, 1302, 314, 220, 17, 11, 30526, 11, 42681, 425, 220, 22, 11, 1302, 314, 220, 1265, 11, 15970, 11, 22581, 11, 1302, 279, 9, 1130, 314, 220, 1910, 11, 33539, 11, 23150, 279, 9, 21, 314, 220, 899, 11, 7672, 11, 22705, 279, 17434, 328, 1879, 25, 220, 455, 11, 12536, 11, 9870, 11, 1302, 659, 220, 1265, 11, 15970, 11, 22581, 11, 1302, 314, 220, 3020, 11, 31868, 11, 38189, 11, 1302, 558, 2578, 220, 1910, 11, 33539, 11, 23150, 2747, 220, 3020, 11, 11727, 11, 7633, 11, 23150, 558, 2578, 220, 899, 11, 7672, 11, 22705, 2747, 220, 3020, 11, 35528, 11, 18566, 11, 31788, 364, 10620, 1147, 316, 290, 220, 19, 11, 40850, 11, 42507, 11, 1302, 11, 1302, 350, 2845, 220, 17, 11, 1302, 11, 1302, 997, 3127, 17565, 314, 220, 19, 11, 47889, 11, 36697, 11, 18566, 11, 31788, 13, 38569, 472, 11965, 13, 9700, 364, 84787, 6052, 364, 8191, 6011, 461, 160761, 25, 220, 31541, 42500, 31088, 41043, 21, 6120], "n_tokens": 2103, "words": 942, "band_lo": 453.25, "band_hi": 1813.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have a 2008x2009 board (grid of unit squares). We remove a pair of squares. After removal, the rest (total squares = 2008*2009 -2) must be tileable by dominoes (1x2). Domino tilings of a rectangular board require board to have even number of squares, which we have: 2008*2009 =? 2008 even, so product even, subtract 2 -> still even. Necessary condition also that board is bipartite: each domino covers one white and one black square in checkerboard coloring. So tileability requires equal number of white and black squares remaining. Initially, 2008x2009 board: Since dimensions m x n where m even (2008) and n odd (2009). For standard checkerboard coloring with alternating colors, total number of squares = even*odd = even. For an even x odd board, there is one more of one color? Let's compute: In a chessboard coloring, each row alternates starting color. For even number of columns? Actually columns = 2009 (odd). Rows = 2008 (even). For each row, if number of columns odd, each row has one extra of starting color. Starting colors alternate per row. Since number of rows even, total counts of each color equal. Reason: For even rows, each pair of rows have opposite starting colors, and each row has one extra of its start color, but these extra cancel across two rows. So total white = total black = total squares/2 = (2008*2009)/2.\n\nThus initially equal numbers. After removing two squares, we need remaining board to have equal numbers (i.e., removed squares must be of opposite colors). Also need that board remains \"connected\"? Not necessary for tiling maybe but some removals cause untileable due to shape (e.g., remove two opposite corners of a chessboard (both same color) impossible; but opposite colors would work? Actually the classical mutilated chessboard problem: remove opposite corners (same color) not tileable, but any removal of opposite colors can be tiled? Not always. For rectangular board with at least 2 rows and 2 columns, maybe any pair of opposite colors yields a domino tiling? Need to consider parity and also maybe connectivity/holes causing parity of regions, but domino tiling condition is more complex: board must be tileable if and only if each connected component has equal numbers of each color? Actually for simply connected region on grid with checkerboard the condition of equal numbers is necessary but not sufficient (e.g., certain shapes). However for a board with two squares removed from a rectangle, there is known result: a board of size m x n with m,n ≥ 2, removal of any pair of squares of opposite colors is tileable (I think it holds for a board with both dimensions even? Not sure.)\n\nLet's test small cases: 2x3 board (6 squares). Remove opposite colors: can we tile? Example coordinates (1,1) white, (1,2) black removed maybe. Remaining 4 squares shape could be disconnected? 2x3 grid, remove two squares maybe leave shape not tileable? Let's try 2x3: labeling colors. Suppose remove (1,1) white and (2,3) black. Remaining squares: shape maybe tileable. In general known theorem: For a rectangular board with at least one side even, removal of any pair of opposite colors results in a tiling (I think due to existence of a Hamiltonian path?). Actually there is a theorem: a board with an even number of squares can be tiled by dominoes iff the board is \"balanced\" (equal numbers of each color) and the board is simply connected? Not exactly.\n\nBetter approach: Count number of opposite-color pairs. Since necessary condition likely also sufficient for such a large board (2008x2009). I suspect answer = number of opposite-color pairs = total pairs of squares of opposite colors.\n\nTotal squares N = 2008*2009. Number of white = N/2 = N/2. Same for black = N/2. Number of unordered pairs consisting of one white and one black = (N/2)*(N/2) = N^2/4.\n\nBut need unordered pairs (pair of squares), order not matter. Selecting a white and a black yields N_w * N_b combos = (N/2)*(N/2). That's count of ordered? Actually selecting one white and one black unordered is exactly that product because each unordered pair uniquely defined by which white and which black. Order doesn't matter; product already counts each unordered pair once (white chosen then black). So total opposite-color pairs = (N/2)*(N/2) = N^2/4.\n\nCompute N = 2008*2009. Let's compute.\n\n2008*2009 = 2008*(2000+9)=2008*2000 + 2008*9 = 4,016,000 + 18,072 = 4,034,072.\n\nCheck: 2008*2009 =? 2008*2009 = (2008*2000) =4,016,000 + (2008*9)=18,072 => 4,034,072. Good.\n\nN/2 = 2,017,036.\n\nNow N^2/4 = (N/2)^2 = 2,017,036^2.\n\nCompute square. Use multiplication.\n\n2,017,036^2.\n\nWe can compute: (2,017,036)*(2,017,036).\n\nLet's do using break: 2,017,036 ≈ 2.017036e6.\n\nCompute precisely:\n\n2,017,036 * 2,017,036.\n\nWe can compute via (2,000,000 + 17,036)^2 = 4,000,000,000,000 + 2*2,000,000*17,036 + 17,036^2.\nFirst term: 4,000,000,000,000.\n\nSecond term: 2*2,000,000*17,036 = 4,000,000 * 17,036 = 68,144,000,000.\n\nThird term: 17,036^2. Compute 17,036^2: (17,000+36)^2 = 289,000,000 + 2*17,000*36 + 36^2 = 289,000,000 + 1,224,000 + 1,296 = 290,225,296.\n\nNow sum: 4,000,000,000,000 + 68,144,000,000 = 4,068,144,000,000. Add 290,225,296 => 4,068,434,225,296.\n\nThus answer maybe 4,068,434,225,296.\n\nBut need to ensure that all opposite-color pairs are tileable. Let's think of counterexample: 1xN board can't be tiled with a missing pair of opposite colors if N is odd? But our board has both dimensions ≥2, so might hold.\n\nThere is known result: In a board with at least one even dimension, removal of any opposite colored squares yields a tiling. Actually algorithm: we can tile board with dominos along rows, leaving a path connecting the two removed squares, and adjust. For even number of columns, we can tile each column vertically, etc. For our board dimensions even*odd, we can tile using horizontal dominoes in each row, except perhaps we need to create a \"channel\". I think it's true.\n\nThus answer is N^2/4.\n\nCompute N^2? Might need to output as integer.\n\nWe have already computed (N/2)^2 = 4,068,434,225,296.\n\nDouble-check arithmetic.\n\nCompute N = 2008*2009.\n\nAlternative compute to verify: 2009*2008.\n\n2009*2000 = 4,018,000\n2009*8 = 16,072\nSum = 4,034,072. Wait earlier I did 2008*2000 = 4,016,000, but that's 2008*2000, correct. Then 2008*9=18,072, sum 4,034,072 matches same.\n\nNow N/2 = 2,017,036 indeed (since N even). Check 2,017,036*2 = 4,034,072 OK.\n\nNow square: we can compute using mental: Let's verify using alternative method: 2,017,036^2.\n\nCompute 2,017,036 * 2,017,036 using long multiplication maybe:\n\n2,017,036 * 2,017,036 = ?\n\nTake 2,017,036 * 2,000,000 = 4,034,072,000,000.\n\nTake 2,017,036 * 17,036 = ?\n\nBreak 17,036 = 10,000 + 7,000 + 30 + 6.\n\nCompute:\n\n*10,000 = 20,170,360,000\n\n*7,000 = 2,017,036 * 7,000 = 14,119,252,000\n\n*30 = 60,511,080\n\n*6 = 12,102,216\n\nSum of these: 20,170,360,000 + 14,119,252,000 = 34,289,612,000.\nAdd 60,511,080 -> 34,350,123,080.\nAdd 12,102,216 -> 34,362,225,296.\n\nNow add to the 4,034,072,000,000 (from 2,000,000 part): Total = 4,068,434,225,296. Same as earlier. Good.\n\nThus answer.\n\nReturn format 'ANSWER: 4068434225296'.", "response": "ANSWER: 4068434225296", "answer": 4068434225296, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.576278118089249e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999996423722521, "logp_correct": -3.576278118089249e-07, "cot_lp_mean": -0.49955828146946896, "cot_lp_min64": -1.154648562707024, "frac_below_-5": 0.010071942446043165, "digit_lp_min": -3.048600673675537, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 2085, "lp_end": -0.0034632247406989336, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.576278118089249e-07, "logp_nocot": -16.13269247300923, "p_nocot": 9.855091950914423e-08, "uplift": 16.132692115381417}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "72cf7ec2-9089-4be6-b4c0-2c4244efc363", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Graphistan has $2011$ cities and Graph Air (GA) is running one-way flights between all pairs of these cities. Determine the maximum possible value of the integer $k$ such that no matter how these flights are arranged, it is possible to travel between any two cities in Graphistan using only GA flights, provided that the absolute value of the difference between the number of flights originating from and terminating at any city is not more than $k$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Graphistan has $2011$ cities and Graph Air (GA) is running one-way flights between all pairs of these cities. Determine the maximum possible value of the integer $k$ such that no matter how these flights are arranged, it is possible to travel between any two cities in Graphistan using only GA flights, provided that the absolute value of the difference between the number of flights originating from and terminating at any city is not more than $k$.", "true_answer": 1005, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "72cf7ec2-9089-4be6-b4c0-2c4244efc363:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 4792, 25, 1416, 679, 220, 667, 16, 15636, 350, 28238, 741, 3274, 382, 261, 5533, 23699, 7465, 350, 83, 41147, 95807, 889, 392, 690, 44845, 27150, 2870, 722, 24702, 328, 1879, 15636, 4050, 125152, 395, 2454, 131528, 10610, 328, 16913, 15636, 11, 1354, 382, 261, 1001, 44845, 15243, 350, 37135, 295, 11165, 8, 29430, 1373, 11, 889, 9262, 665, 413, 4784, 2006, 30, 63659, 2454, 10610, 853, 9707, 1001, 23699, 11165, 350, 690, 44845, 15243, 8, 591, 1001, 5030, 316, 290, 1273, 11, 625, 2973, 13, 2632, 581, 679, 261, 28579, 402, 220, 667, 16, 38360, 364, 2167, 1682, 316, 1646, 10562,