1888, "band_lo": 982.5, "band_hi": 3930.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have 2008x2008 board, each square gets an integer from 1 to N where N = 2008^2. I think each integer used exactly once? It says \"The integers from 1 to $2008^2$ are written on each square of a $2008 \\times 2008$ board.\" I think they mean arranged each integer exactly once onto the board squares. So we have a permutation of numbers 1..N placed on board.\n\nDefine for each row i, let max_i and min_i among its 2008 entries. The difference d_i = max_i - min_i. Similarly for each column j, difference e_j = max column - min column. Then S = sum_{i=1}^{2008} d_i + sum_{j=1}^{2008} e_j, total 4016 numbers.\n\nGoal: maximize S.\n\nWe need to arrange numbers to maximize row-wise and column-wise ranges.\n\nObservations: The total sum of all d_i depends on the distribution extremes in each row. Since each row has 2008 numbers, the max of a row is at most N, min at least 1, but can't have that for many rows because numbers limited.\n\nWe need to allocate large numbers to be max in many rows, and small numbers to be min in many rows, similarly for columns.\n\nEssentially assign for each row i its max a_i and min b_i; then the remaining 2006 numbers in that row are between them (but may affect column constraints). Likewise for columns.\n\nWe need to maximize sum of (a_i - b_i) + sum of (c_j - d_j) where a_i = row i max, b_i = row i min, c_j = column j max, d_j = column j min.\n\nNumbers appear in both a row and column simultaneously.\n\nConsider we want to place the largest numbers as row maxima and column maxima simultaneously perhaps to increase both contributions. Similarly smallest numbers as minima.\n\nSince each number sits at intersection of a row and column, it can serve as max for both its row and column (if placed suitably). Likewise a min for both row and column.\n\nPotentially we could place the very largest number N = 2008^2 at position (row r, column c). Then row r max = N, column c max = N. That contributes N to both sums, but the difference counts max-min, not the max alone. The contributions depend also on the min for that row and column.\n\nGoal: maximize sum of ranges = sum over rows max - sum over rows min + sum over columns max - sum over columns min = (sum rows max + sum columns max) - (sum rows min + sum columns min).\n\nThus we need to maximize total of all row maxima plus column maxima, and minimize total of all row minima plus column minima.\n\nConstraints: each number appears in exactly one row and one column. The row maximum for a given row is the largest number among its 2008 entries, which may also be a column maximum for its column, etc.\n\nWe can think about selecting a set of numbers to be row maxima (one per row) and another set to be column maxima (one per column). Some numbers may be used as both a row max and column max; similarly for minima. We need to assign numbers to cells so that each row and column have designated max/min.\n\nWe can treat as bipartite assignment problem: we have 2008 rows and 2008 columns; each cell (i,j) contains a number. Row i's max will be the max across its row; that is the maximum number among cells in that row. So it's the max across its 2008 cells, which includes each column's cell.\n\nThus, the row max will be the max among the numbers assigned to that row across columns; similarly column max is max across rows.\n\nIf we want high sums we likely want to choose the largest numbers to appear in many rows and columns as maxima. But a single number can be max for at most one row and one column (its row and column). So each large number can contribute at most twice (once to row max sum, once to column max sum) if it is the maximum in its row and column.\n\nSimilarly each small number can be min for both its row and column.\n\nThus we want to pair large numbers to be placed on a \"max\" cell which is unique per row and column; that is effectively we want to choose a permutation matrix of 2008 cells where we place the largest numbers, each being row and column max.\n\nSimilarly we want smallest numbers to be placed on cells that are simultaneously row and column minima (a permutation). The rest of cells can be anything, but they affect that the chosen max/min must indeed be maximum/minimum in their respective row/column, so other numbers in same row/column must be less than the max and greater than the min accordingly.\n\nFeasible plan: Choose a permutation π such that cell (i,π(i)) contains a large number; make that the row i max and column π(i) max. Similarly choose another permutation σ for minima with smallest numbers. Possibly we can use same cells? No, each cell can hold just one number.\n\nThus we need to allocate distinct cells for maxima and minima. Since board is large, we can pick two disjoint permutation matrices.\n\nNow, to maximize sum of row maxima + column maxima, we should assign the top 2008 numbers (the largest) to the max permutation cells? But each occupies both row and column, contributing twice each. The total contribution of each such number x to S is 2x (since appears in both a row max and a column max). However note that S = sum (row max - row min) + sum (col max - col min) = (sum row max + sum col max) - (sum row min + sum col min). So each max contributes +x to row sum and +x to column sum? Actually each max belongs to a particular row; its value counted in sum of row maxima. Also belongs to a column; its value counted in sum of column maxima. So yes contributes +x twice.\n\nSimilarly each min number y contributes -y twice (subtracted).\n\nThus we want to choose 2008 largest numbers to be double-counted positive, and 2008 smallest numbers to be double-counted negative, while ensuring feasibility.\n\nBut there is also the remaining numbers: they neither are row/col max/min. They don't contribute.\n\nThus maximum possible S = 2*(sum of largest 2008 numbers) - 2*(sum of smallest 2008 numbers) = 2*(sum_{k=N-2007}^{N} k - sum_{k=1}^{2008} k). Wait need indices: largest 2008 numbers are N-2007, N-2006, ..., N. Smallest 2008 numbers are 1..2008.\n\nCompute sums.\n\nFirst N = 2008^2 = 2008*2008. Compute: 2000*2008 = 4,016,000; plus 8*2008 = 16,064; total 4,032,064. let's verify: 2008^2 = (2000+8)^2 = 4,000,000 + 2*2000*8=32,000 + 64 = 4,032,064. Yes.\n\nSum of first m = m(m+1)/2. For m=2008, sum_small = 2008*2009/2 = 2008*2009/2. Compute 2008*2009 = 2008*(2000+9)=2008*2000 + 2008*9 = 4,016,000 + 18,072 = 4,034,072. Half gives 2,017,036. So sum_small = 2,017,036.\n\nNow sum of largest 2008 numbers: sum_{k=N-2007}^{N} k = total sum 1..N minus sum 1..(N-2008). Equivalent = sum_{k=1}^{N} k - sum_{k=1}^{N-2008} k.\n\nSum 1..N = N(N+1)/2. N=4,032,064. Compute N(N+1)/2: first N(N+1) = 4,032,064 * 4,032,065. That's large; we might compute difference later.\n\nSum up to N-2008 = (N-2008)(N-2007)/2. Because (M)(M+1)/2 with M=N-2008.\n\nThus sum_large = [N(N+1) - (N-2008)(N-2007)] / 2.\n\nCompute difference: N(N+1) - (N-2008)(N-2007).\n\nLet’s expand: N(N+1) = N^2 + N. (N-2008)(N-2007) = N^2 - (2008+2007)N + 2008*2007 = N^2 - 4015 N + 2008*2007.\n\nSubtract: (N^2 + N) - [N^2 - 4015 N + 2008*2007] = N^2+N - N^2 + 4015 N - 2008*2007 = (1+4015)N - 2008*2007 = 4016 N - 2008*2007.\n\nThus sum_large = (4016 N - 2008*2007) / 2 = 2008 N - (2008*2007)/2.\n\nSince 4016/2 =2008. So sum_large = 2008 N - (2008*2007)/2.\n\nCompute N=4,032,064. Then 2008*N = 2008*4,032,064. Compute: 4,032,064*2000 = 8,064,128,000; plus *8 = 32,256,512; total = 8,096,384,512.\n\nNow compute (2008*2007)/2. First product 2008*2007. Compute 2008*2000 = 4,016,000; 2008*7 = 14,056; total = 4,030,056. Divide by 2 => 2,015,028.\n\nThus sum_large = 8,096,384,512 - 2,015,028 = 8,094,369,484.\n\nNow S_max_possible = 2*(sum_large - sum_small) = 2*(8,094,369,484 - 2,017,036) = 2*(8,092,352,448). Subtract: 8,094,369,484 - 2,017,036 = 8,092,352,448. Multiply by 2 gives 16,184,704,896.\n\nThus theoretical upper bound assuming we can achieve perfectly.\n\nWe need to verify feasibility: can we assign numbers such that each row's max is among the largest 2008 numbers, each column's max also among same set, and each row's min among smallest 2008, each column's min among same set, with the two sets disjoint? Need ensure that each row has both a max and a min (distinct cells maybe) with large and small numbers, and columns similarly. Also need no conflict: a cell cannot be both a max and a min.\n\nWe can design a board as follows: Choose a permutation π for large numbers placed on main diagonal maybe. Place the top 2008 numbers in distinct rows/columns (a permutation). Choose another permutation σ disjoint from π for small numbers (also a permutation). Since board is large enough (2008 positions each row/col), we can place both.\n\nFor each row i, we need that its max is the large number placed somewhere in that row (maybe column π(i)). Ensure that all other numbers in that row are less than that max and greater than its min (the small number placed somewhere else in that row). That's doable if we put the small number as min in column σ(i) (different column) and fill remaining cells with numbers between them (i.e., not exceeding the max nor less than the min). Since we have many numbers, we can choose remaining numbers accordingly.\n\nSimilarly each column j will have its large number as max (if j = π(i) for some i) and its small number as min (if j = σ(k) for some k). Need ensure that for a column that contains a large max cell, also contains a small min cell maybe different row; that works.\n\nThus seems feasible.\n\nWe must also ensure that the large numbers are indeed larger than all other numbers in that row (including the small numbers). Since the smallest large number is N-2007, which is huge, clearly > all small numbers (≤2008) and > all other numbers (which we can keep between). So fine.\n\nSimilarly smallest numbers are indeed minima.\n\nTherefore the bound is achievable.\n\nEdge Cases: need to ensure that rows which receive a large number also have a small number, but the small numbers are distinct per row (since we have 2008 small numbers and 2008 rows). So we can map each row i to a distinct small number via the second permutation; that ensures each row has exactly one small number placed.\n\nThus feasible arrangement: let rows indexed 1..2008. Choose π(i)=i (identity) for large numbers, place L_i (the i-th largest number) at cell (i,i). Choose σ(i)= i+1 mod 2008 for small numbers, place S_i (i-th smallest) at cell (i,σ(i)). Avoid collision when i=σ(i)? Not with shift. Works. Fill rest arbitrarily with remaining numbers.\n\nThus S achieves bound.\n\nThus answer = 16,184,704,896.\n\nCheck arithmetic carefully.\n\nLet's recompute sums to avoid mistake.\n\nSum_small = 2008*2009/2 = (2008/2)*2009 = 1004*2009 = compute: 2009*1000 = 2,009,000; +2009*4 = 8,036; total 2,017,036 matches earlier.\n\nSum_large: could also compute directly as sum of arithmetic series: sum of 2008 terms from N-2007 to N inclusive. Average = ( (N-2007) + N ) /2 = (2N - 2007)/2 = N - 2007/2. Multiply by count 2008: sum_large = 2008*(N - 2007/2) = 2008 N - 2008*2007/2 = 2008 N - (2008*2007)/2 = same as earlier.\n\nNow compute 2008 N. N = 4,032,064.\n\nCompute 4,032,064 * 2008:\n\n4,032,064 * 2000 = 8,064,128,000.\n4,032,064 * 8 = 32,256,512.\nAdd = 8,096,384,512 correct.\n\nNow 2008*2007 =? Let's recompute: 2008*2007 = (2000+8)*(2000+7)=2000*2000 +2000*7 +8*2000 +8*7 = 4,000,000 +14,000 +16,000 +56 =4,030,056. Yes.\n\nHalf = 2,015,028.\n\nThus sum_large = 8,096,384,512 - 2,015,028 = 8,094,369,484.\n\nNow difference = 8,094,369,484 - 2,017,036 = 8,092,352,448.\n\nMultiply by 2 = 16,184,704,896.\n\nThus answer.\n\nCheck if any double counting issues: S = sum rows (max - min) + sum cols (max - min). Our construction gives each row max = large L_i, row min = small S_i. Similarly each column j max = some large L_k (where k such that π(k)=j). Each column min = some small S_m (where σ(m)=j). Since permutations are bijections, each column gets exactly one large and one small, distinct rows.\n\nThus each large number appears once as row max and once as column max, so contributes twice. Each small number contributes twice negative.\n\nThus S = 2*(sum_large - sum_small). Good.\n\nThus answer numeric.\n\nProvide answer format.", "response": "ANSWER: 16184704896", "answer": 16184704896, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.768370445162873e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999995231630692, "logp_correct": -4.768370445162873e-07, "cot_lp_mean": -0.49297546101835515, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1547656501536494, "frac_below_-5": 0.007743045597935188, "digit_lp_min": -3.5297505855560303, "answer_first_mention_lp": null, "n_cot_tokens": 3487, "lp_end": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.4305104514278355e-06, "logp_nocot": -34.14489364100882, "p_nocot": 1.4827266945635551e-15, "uplift": 34.144893164171776}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "89a2a561-f4b5-4c80-b674-b1d6c32286e6", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Find the least positive integer $N$ such that the only values of $n$ for which $1 + N \\cdot 2^n$ is prime are multiples of $12$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Find the least positive integer $N$ such that the only values of $n$ for which $1 + N \\cdot 2^n$ is prime are multiples of $12$.", "true_answer": 556, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "89a2a561-f4b5-4c80-b674-b1d6c32286e6:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 1646, 42173, 8841, 16336, 478, 2238, 484, 395, 1062, 16336, 297, 11, 538, 220, 16, 659, 478, 9, 17, 61, 77, 382, 9197, 1815, 297, 2804, 413, 261, 7598, 328, 220, 899, 13, 158286, 25, 395, 1062, 297, 625, 7598, 328, 220, 899, 11, 220, 16, 10, 45, 9, 17, 61, 77, 382, 40773, 350, 267, 625, 9197, 741, 8820, 1309, 484, 395, 297, 70720, 328, 220, 899, 11, 12951, 1236, 553, 104042, 11, 889, 7060, 5003, 392, 7393, 4824, 328, 297, 395, 1118, 2550, 553, 9197, 553, 70720, 328, 220, 899, 4050, 2632, 722, 9197, 108073, 5659, 1606, 1261, 297, 167813, 656, 220, 899, 13, 1225, 8740, 1841, 484, 722, 70720, 328, 220, 899, 3644, 104042, 26, 1327, 484, 538, 4275, 9197, 11, 297, 382, 7598, 328, 220, 899, 364, 42087, 25, 19957, 478, 364, 175703, 387, 25, 478, 8841, 16336, 2238, 484, 395, 1062, 8841, 16336, 297, 350, 267, 16336, 297, 20974, 15, 95807, 538, 220, 16, 10, 45, 9, 17, 61, 77, 382, 9197, 11, 1815, 220, 899, 144028, 297, 13, 350, 9095, 3095, 297, 2893, 46069, 16336, 741, 2632, 581, 2804, 1520, 220, 16, 10, 45, 9, 17, 61, 77, 40773, 395, 1062, 297, 625, 167813, 656, 220, 899, 364, 2167, 665, 2075, 316, 57352, 2360, 3291, 9197, 37826, 914, 13, 2214, 2454, 80677, 744, 1812, 220, 899, 350, 135903, 220, 15, 936, 581, 1682, 316, 1646