+15*(2+7t) = 1 +30 +105 t =31 +105t.\n\nSo N ≡31 (mod105). Check N mod7: 31 mod7 = 3 good.\n\nNow also need N ≡10 (mod13). Solve 31 +105 t ≡10 (mod13).\n\nCompute 31 mod13 = 5 (since 13*2=26). 105 mod13 = 105-13*8=105-104=1. So equation: 5 + t ≡10 (mod13) => t ≡5 (mod13).\n\nThus t = 5 +13s.\n\nHence N = 31 +105*(5+13s) = 31 +525 +1365 s = 556 +1365 s.\n\nSo smallest positive N is when s=0: N=556.\n\nCheck N coprime to 3,5,7,13? 556 mod3 = 556-3*185=1 good; mod5 => 1 good; mod7 => 556-7*79=556-553=3 good; mod13 => 556-13*42=556-546=10 good not 0.\n\nThus candidate N = 556.\n\nNow verify that for each n not multiple 12, expression composite.\n\nWe should test residues n=1..11.\n\nLet's compute 1+556*2^n for n from 1 to 11 mod small primes:\n\nRecall primes used: For odd n, 3 divides.\n\nCheck n=1 (odd): 1+556*2=1+1112=1113. 1113 /3 = 371 => divisible by 3, composite. Good.\n\nn=2 (even, 2 mod4; also n≡2 mod3): Should be divisible by 5? Wait N≡1 mod5 gave class n≡2 mod4 (yes). So p=5 should divide. Compute 1+556*4=1+2224=2225. Divide by5: 2225/5=445 => composite.\n\nn=3 (odd): divisible by 3. compute 1+556*8=1+4448=4449, 3*1483=4449. Good.\n\nn=4 (even, n≡0 mod4, also n≡1 mod3). Should be divisible by 7 (since N≡3 mod7 => class n≡1 mod3). n=4 mod3 =1, yes. Check: 1+556*16=1+8896=8897. Divide by7? 7*1271=8897 (since 7*1200=8400, plus 7*71=497, total 8897). Indeed divisible by7. Composite.\n\nn=5 (odd): divisible by 3. compute 1+556*32=1+17792=17793. 3*5931=17793.\n\nn=6 (even, n≡2 mod4, also n≡0 mod3). Divisible by 5 (since n≡2 mod4). Check: 1+556*64=1+35584=35585. /5=7117 -> composite. Also maybe 13? Not needed.\n\nn=7 (odd): divisible by3. 1+556*128=1+71168=71169. /3=23723.\n\nn=8 (even, not covered yet by 5 (since 8 mod4=0) nor 7 (8 mod3=2) but covered by 13 (n=8). Should be divisible by13. Let's test: 1+556*256 = 1+142,336? compute 556*256 = 556*200=111200, plus 556*56=31136, sum 142,336. plus 1 = 142,337. Divide by13: 13*10,949 = 142,337? Compute 13*10,000=130,000