, 17, 25363, 286, 625, 39069, 316, 11082, 274, 11, 1617, 31655, 553, 2973, 625, 274, 350, 21910, 2023, 413, 25882, 326, 1273, 9882, 17, 741, 7801, 3566, 50768, 540, 286, 1481, 413, 1617, 1920, 31655, 1118, 621, 625, 10232, 274, 13972, 1481, 413, 829, 69164, 483, 8896, 50768, 540, 274, 13, 18774, 9882, 328, 286, 314, 17, 889, 1001, 13034, 382, 274, 26, 1815, 8896, 10232, 274, 364, 84787, 722, 9882, 12, 17, 38360, 2804, 413, 39069, 316, 274, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 553, 31655, 328, 274, 741, 13602, 538, 261, 9882, 12, 17, 25363, 382, 540, 9324, 1424, 16, 591, 274, 11, 1815, 1617, 3566, 24791, 10232, 274, 13, 2632, 1023, 2804, 413, 8516, 12079, 316, 274, 11, 4137, 22611, 42636, 328, 5517, 17, 350, 66, 533, 286, 533, 25882, 741, 2632, 290, 8165, 382, 9707, 261, 392, 16204, 328, 4679, 72554, 17, 1, 50768, 540, 274, 11, 472, 581, 5207, 295, 364, 84787, 581, 679, 34714, 19545, 26, 15657, 3605, 19957, 1261, 472, 1991, 38360, 472, 4149, 553, 2061, 472, 9882, 12, 17, 22611, 38360, 350, 21309, 8967, 1001, 25882, 777, 1920, 38360, 741, 2632, 483, 3609, 478, 11, 4566, 1215, 9882, 12, 17, 38360, 326, 342, 25882, 38360, 8516, 18618, 11, 126205, 478, 314, 220, 16, 659, 1215, 659, 342, 11, 1919, 2454, 9882, 12, 17, 25363, 62274, 1001, 25882, 350, 1348, 13034, 936, 813, 25882, 3605, 451, 314, 342, 659, 1215, 30, 24305, 2454, 9882, 12, 17, 25363, 286, 1217, 853, 25882, 18618, 11, 1118, 382, 60500, 306, 25882, 3605, 26, 581, 9113, 342, 472, 15657, 8516, 18618, 316, 274, 11, 1215, 472, 9882, 12, 17, 38360, 2454, 483, 1617, 25882, 13, 17565, 15657, 314, 342, 659, 1215, 13, 17511, 478, 25, 220, 16, 659, 1215, 659, 342, 314, 478, 871, 342, 314, 478, 12, 16, 533, 1215, 13, 130434, 451, 314, 1215, 659, 350, 45, 12, 16, 533, 1215, 8, 314, 478, 12, 16, 314, 220, 1323, 17, 30, 4569, 7890, 8201, 2236, 15657, 3503, 80811, 30, 41021, 413, 25120, 364, 7942, 11965, 11047, 2860, 25, 2214, 2454, 13464, 5517, 17, 581, 679, 1001, 9882, 12, 17, 25363, 337, 326, 1001, 25882, 286, 13, 623, 25882, 382, 625, 8516, 18618, 316, 274, 26, 4275, 25882, 328, 337, 13, 2632, 28991, 25, 2086, 328, 9882, 12, 17, 38360, 314, 372, 11, 2086, 328, 15657, 8516, 18618, 316, 274, 314, 451, 16, 11, 2086, 328, 15657, 30482, 591, 9882, 12, 17, 314, 372, 350, 21910, 2454, 13464, 853, 1001, 25882, 741, 17565, 38360, 25, 220, 16, 350, 66, 8, 659, 372, 350, 98659, 17, 8, 659, 451, 16, 350, 37135, 15657, 8, 659, 372, 350, 50921, 540, 1268, 8, 314, 220, 16, 659, 451, 16, 659, 220, 17, 74, 314, 478, 364, 149882, 3609, 314, 451, 16, 659, 372, 364, 1385, 41776, 15657, 11, 44207, 372, 11, 9913, 656, 451, 16, 5064, 15, 871, 372, 5017, 350, 45, 12, 16, 11876, 17, 13, 12265, 478, 314, 220, 1323, 18, 871, 372, 13731, 314, 8350, 2054, 1323, 17, 11876, 17, 25987, 7959, 16, 13, 2632, 1349, 15657, 314, 478, 12, 16, 533, 372, 13731, 314, 220, 1323, 17, 533, 220, 7959, 16, 314, 7959, 16, 364, 84787, 220, 7959, 16, 382, 19957, 364, 4383, 483, 945, 19536, 6931, 11608, 25, 130434, 5064, 35964, 2054, 45, 12, 16, 11876, 17, 20707, 3813, 478, 14210, 11, 350, 45, 12, 16, 11876, 17, 314, 220, 7959, 16, 13, 2632, 6931, 11608, 15248, 364, 84787, 6052, 220, 7959, 16, 364, 10620, 10635, 1721, 6052, 6011, 13], "n_tokens": 4267, "words": 3118, "band_lo": 1948.0, "band_hi": 7792.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret problem: we have a connected simple graph with n=2023 vertices, m=2022 edges (so it's a tree? For a connected graph with n vertices, minimum edges is n-1 = 2022. So m=n-1. Therefore the graph is a tree. Conditions: each pair of vertices at most one edge (true). So we have a tree.\n\nDefine \"island group\" = a set of three vertices such that there exists an island (vertex) among them that is adjacent to each of the other two. So basically a triple where one vertex is adjacent to the other two (forming a \"V\" shape). Not required that the other two be adjacent themselves (would be triangle, impossible in tree). So a \"V\". So any three vertices having a common neighbor among them.\n\nWe are given that any two island groups share at least one common island (vertex). In other words, for any two triples that satisfy that property, their vertex sets intersect.\n\nGoal: minimize number of leaves (vertices of degree 1) in such a tree with given property.\n\nWe need minimal possible number of leaves given constraints.\n\nWe need to find a tree on 2023 vertices, 2022 edges, such that the set of \"V-shaped\" triples (i.e., triples {a,b,c} where a is adjacent to b and c) have the pairwise intersecting property (any two share a vertex). Minimize leaves.\n\nObservations: In a tree, a V-shaped triple corresponds to a vertex of degree at least 2, and picking two of its neighbors. So each vertex v of degree d contributes C(d,2) triples (groups) with center v.\n\nThus the collection of groups is essentially the collection of unordered pairs of distinct neighbors of each vertex.\n\nCondition: any two such groups intersect. Means for any two distinct unordered neighbor-pairs (v, {x,y}) and (u, {p,q}) (with v maybe equals u or not), the sets {v,x,y} and {u,p,q} intersect. Intersection could be at either a leaf vertex, or a center, etc.\n\nWe need to find tree where all such V triples intersect pairwise.\n\nGoal minimize leaves.\n\nInterpretation: Suppose there are two vertices v and u (both possibly degree >=2) each with two neighbors. Then groups centered at v and u might be disjoint if the neighbor sets are disjoint and neither vertex is neighbor of the other's neighbors. Could they intersect at v or u? Intersection would be if the vertex set includes v or u common. For groups centered at different vertices, the only possible common vertex is if one center is neighbor of the other, or one of the leaves is same as a leaf of other group, etc.\n\nBut given tree, it's likely difficult to have many vertices of degree >=2 without violating intersecting condition. Perhaps the intersecting property forces a structure akin to a \"star\": all V groups share the central vertex of the star. The star tree has one central vertex connected to all others (so degree n-1). Then any V-group is formed by the central vertex and any two leaves. All such groups share the central vertex, satisfying condition. Leaves are n-1 = 2022 leaves. But we want minimize leaves, not maximize. This star has maximum leaves.\n\nWe need minimal leaves. Wait star has many leaves degree 1; we want minimal number of degree-1 vertices. Could we have a tree where many vertices have degree >=2 and still satisfy pairwise intersect property? Let's examine.\n\nIf there are two vertices with degree >=2 that are not adjacent, their groups are likely disjoint: pick two neighbors each; sets have no common vertex. Because tree distances >1. So likely must have a \"core\" where all high-degree vertices are arranged like a path maybe with intersecting condition forced to share common vertex.\n\nConsider structure where there is a \"central edge\" connecting two vertices a and b, each having many pendant leaves attached. Let's see groups: groups centered at a involve a plus two of its incident leaves (including possibly b as neighbor?). Actually b is neighbor of a, but degree of a may be d_a. Groups centered at a could be (a, leaf1, leaf2) (both leaves not b) or maybe (a, leaf, b) – but need two distinct neighbors; if one neighbor is b and other is leaf, then the group includes a,b,leaf. Similarly groups centered at b.\n\nNow consider a group centered at a using two leaves of a, set = {a, leaf1, leaf2}. Another group centered at b using two leaves of b, set = {b, leaf3, leaf4}. Intersection? Might be empty unless a = b (no) or one leaf equals the other (unlikely) or maybe if leaf1 = b? but leaf1 cannot be b, because leaf is neighbor of a but not b. So groups could be disjoint: {a, leaf1, leaf2} vs {b, leaf3, leaf4} share nothing (a≠b, leaves distinct). So intersection condition fails. Therefore we cannot have two vertices each with at least two leaf neighbors not shared.\n\nThus to satisfy condition, there cannot exist two vertices each with degree at least 3? Let's analyze.\n\nIf there is a vertex v with degree >=3, that vertex alone creates groups that all share v, that's fine. However if there is another vertex u distinct from v that also has degree >=2, we need any group centered at u to intersect any group centered at v. Since groups centered at v all contain v, any group centered at u must contain v as well to intersect. So each group centered at u must include v. That means for any two neighbors of u, say x,y, the triple {u,x,y} must contain v. So v must be equal to one of {u,x,y}. Since u ≠ v, v must be either x or y, i.e., v must be a neighbor of u. Moreover, for any pair of neighbors of u, at least one of them must be v. This can happen only if u has at most two neighbors, and one of them is v, and the other arbitrary. Because if u has three distinct neighbors, we can pick two that are both not v (if there are two other neighbors) giving a group not containing v. So degree of any vertex other than the special \"central\" one must be at most 2, and if degree 2, one neighbor must be the central vertex.\n\nThus the structure resembles a \"star-like\" with a central vertex v (maybe more than one?), and all other vertices are either leaves attached to v, or form a pendant edge adjacent to v (i.e., a vertex u of degree 2: u connected to v and a leaf). Let's test condition.\n\nLet central vertex c have degree maybe many. For each neighbor u of c, u could be leaf (degree1) or could have its own leaf attached (making a pendant path length2). If we have such a pendant vertex u (degree2) with leaf w, then groups centered at u: need two neighbors of u: they are c and w. The only possible pair is {c, w}. So group is {u, c, w}. This group contains c. So intersect with all groups from c (which contain c). Good.\n\nNow consider groups centered at c: they could be (c, neighbor a, neighbor b). If a and b are both leaves attached directly, fine. If a is leaf attached via intermediate vertex u (i.e., a = w, but w is not neighbor of c), can't be because w is not neighbor of c. Wait group centered at c requires two neighbors of c. Neighbors of c are vertices directly attached: leaves or pendant vertices u. So groups centered at c are of form {c, x, y} where x,y are distinct neighbors of c (could be leaf directly, or degree2 vertex u). So each group contains c.\n\nThus any group includes c => pairwise intersect condition satisfied. Are there any groups centered at other vertices besides c? Only possible are the degree2 vertices u (pendant). They produce groups {u,c,w} which also contain c. So intersect satisfied.\n\nThus structure: a central vertex c (maybe more than one?) with arbitrary attached \"branches\" each of length 1 (leaf) or 2 (leaf beyond a degree2 node). There cannot be longer branches because that would create group centered at interior node of degree 2 where both neighbors are not c, leading to group without c.\n\nConsider a path of length 3: c - u - w - x where x leaf. u degree 2 (c,w). w degree2 (u,x). Groups centered at w would be {w,u,x} which doesn't contain c (unless w=c) -> violates condition. So not allowed.\n\nThus allowed tree is a \"star of height at most 2\": central vertex c, with each incident component being a leaf or a 2-vertex chain ending leaf.\n\nNow we need to count leaves (degree1 vertices) minimum given total vertices =2023.\n\nLet number of leaves directly attached to c be L1. Number of pendant chains of length2: each contributes two vertices besides c: a middle vertex u (degree2) and leaf x (degree1). So each such chain adds 1 leaf (x) and one internal degree2 vertex.\n\nLet k = number of such length-2 chains.\n\nTotal vertices: 1 (c) + L1 + 2k = 2023\n\nLeaves: L1 (direct leaves) + k (leaf at end of each chain) = L1 + k.\n\nGoal minimize leaves L = L1 + k subject to equation and integer nonnegative.\n\nWe can choose to maximize internal vertices (degree2) in the tree; but each internal vertex adds 1 vertex and does not increase leaf count (except the leaf at end). The chain adds exactly 1 leaf per internal vertex (u). So each chain adds 2 vertices and 1 leaf. While direct leaf adds 1 vertex and 1 leaf.\n\nThus leaf count per vertex: direct leaf ratio 1 leaf per vertex; chain ratio 0.5 leaf per vertex (since 2 vertices give 1 leaf). To minimize total leaves, we should use as many chains as possible (since they are more efficient in adding vertices without increasing leaves proportionally). So maximize k.\n\nConstraint: total vertices = 1 + L1 + 2k = N=2023 => L1 = 2022 - 2k.\n\nLeaves L = L1 + k = (2022 - 2k) + k = 2022 - k.\n\nThus to minimize leaves, maximize k, i.e., make L as small as possible: L_min = 2022 - k_max.\n\nWhat is max k? Need L1 >=0, so 2022 - 2k >=0 => k <= 1011.\n\nSince k integer, max k = 1011.\n\nThen leaves L_min = 2022 - 1011 = 1011.\n\nThus minimal number of leaves is 1011.\n\nCheck: with k=1011, L1 = 2022 - 2*1011 = 0. So all branches are length2; central vertex degree = number of branches = k = 1011 (since each branch has an intermediate vertex attached to c). Actually c adjacent to each intermediate vertex u (degree2). So degree of c = k = 1011.\n\nTotal vertices: 1 + 2*1011 = 2023 okay.\n\nLeaves: each chain returns leaf, count = k =1011. Good.\n\nNow verify condition: Any two groups intersect.\n\nGroups: centered at c: choose any two of its neighbors (the degree2 nodes). Set includes c and two degree2 nodes, which does not contain any leaf. Is that okay? Does group need to have one vertex adjacent to other two: c adjacent to those degree2 nodes, yes. So groups from c all share c.\n\nGroups centered at intermediate vertex u_i: neighbors are c and leaf_i. Only pair (c, leaf_i). Group {u_i, c, leaf_i} includes c.\n\nThus all groups contain c, intersect property holds.\n\nThus structure valid.\n\nNow is there any possibility of even fewer leaves with alternative structure, perhaps having two central vertices that share groups? Let's test possibility of having two vertices both high degree and each adjacent to each other? Could we have central edge uv where all groups contain at least u or v but any two groups intersect? Suppose each group must contain either u or v, but there might be a group not containing the other, leading to possible disjoint? Let's attempt to design tree where groups are all centered at u or v, and suppose any group centered at u contains v as one neighbor (so groups are {u, v, leaf}). Then each group includes u and v. Similarly groups centered at v include u and leaf. Then all groups share both u and v, okay. But can we have degree of u more than 2? If u has neighbor a (leaf) besides v, then to form a group centered at u, need two neighbors: v and a, giving group {u,v,a}. That's okay includes v. If u also has another leaf b, then we could choose neighbors a and b (both not v), forming group {u, a, b} which does not contain v, violates intersect with groups centered at v (which may be {v, u, c}) if they don't intersect? Actually they intersect at u? Wait {u,a,b} includes u, and {v, u, c} includes u, so they intersect at u. So maybe intersection condition could still hold if all groups share at least one of the two central vertices. Eg any group centered at u could be of type (v, a) or (a,b). If it's (a,b), it still contains u. For groups centered at v may contain u or not? If group centered at v uses two neighbours that are not u, then group is {v, x, y} not containing u, and may be disjoint with {u,a,b}. Intersection then empty. So to avoid that, any group centered at v must also include u, meaning v must have degree exactly 2 (neighbors u and leaf). Similarly u could have many leaves but then groups formed by two leaves avoid v. So to keep intersect property, at most one of the two vertices can have degree >2? Let's analyze more systematically.\n\nLet’s consider a tree where there is a central edge uv. Suppose degree(v)=2 (neighbors u and leaf). Then groups centered at v are only {v,u,leaf}, contain u. So all groups contain u. Now u could have many leaves. Any group centered at u could be {u, leaf_i, leaf_j} without v, but still contains u. So all groups share u. Thus intersect condition satisfied. That's essentially previous star with central vertex u (c), plus a pendant leaf attached to u via v? Wait v degree2, leaf attached to v. This is a chain of length2: leaf-v-u. But that's already covered by our earlier model where we have u as central, and one neighbor is v which is degree2 internal attaching leaf. Indeed that structure is a branch of length2. So the model already covers possibility of having a leaf attached via a degree2 neighbor.\n\nBut could we have central edge with both vertices degree >=2 but each having multiple leaf neighbors such that any group either includes the other central vertex? For groups centered at u, if we require that any pair of neighbors chosen must include v, then u must have at most one other neighbor besides v (otherwise we could pick two others). So u's degree max 2 (neighbors v and maybe leaf). So central edge cannot both have high degree.\n\nThus the previous model (single central vertex with degree many) seems optimal for minimizing leaves, as we can allocate each extra vertex as an intermediate degree2 node attached to central, each adds 1 leaf per 2 vertices, which is optimal ratio.\n\nCould there be a configuration with zero leaves? Not possible because leaf count at least 1 for a tree. Actually tree with all vertices degree >=2 cannot exist; must have at least two leaves. So min leaves >=2. But constraints produce more leaves.\n\nOur derived lower bound: leaves = L1 + k = N - (something) So can we prove 1011 is minimum? We reasoned that each leaf attached directly contributes 1 leaf per vertex, each degree2 chain contributes 0.5 leaf per vertex. The best ratio is 0.5 leaves per vertex, giving at least ceil(N/2?) Let's compute theoretical lower bound.\n\nConsider any tree satisfying condition. Let's consider the set of vertices of degree >=3 (high-degree). Claim there can be at most one such vertex, call it c. Because if there are two distinct vertices a,b with degree >=3, we can find groups not intersect? Suppose a and b not adjacent. Then choose two neighbors of a not b, and two neighbors of b not a, groups are disjoint. So impossible. If they are adjacent, but each degree >=3 (so each has at least two other neighbors besides each other). Then we could produce a group at a using its two non-b neighbors (contains a not b) and group at b using its two non-a neighbors (contains b not a). Intersection empty. So at most one vertex with degree >=3.\n\nNow consider vertices of degree 2. They form paths between leaves and possibly the central vertex. For a degree-2 vertex w not adjacent to central c, its neighbors are both not c (since could be leaf and other degree2). Then group centered at w would be its two neighbors which do not contain c thus would be disjoint with groups centered at c. Unless degree of w =2 but one neighbor is c; then groups contain c.\n\nThus all degree-2 vertices must be adjacent to c (i.e., are neighbors of c). Because if a degree-2 vertex is at distance >1 from c, then its group wouldn't contain c. So they must be directly connected to c, making pendant chains of length2 (c - w - leaf). So the tree is exactly a \"star of height ≤2\" centered at c, as we reasoned.\n\nThus we have structural proof; leaves count minimal when as many vertices as possible are used as degree-2 pendant vertices (each gives one leaf per two vertices). So with total N, having x degree-2 vertices and y leaf vertices directly attached, satisfies N = 1 + x + y, where each degree-2 vertex contributes one leaf (its neighbor), so leaf count L = y + x? Wait each degree-2 vertex w also has leaf attached, which is counted in leaf count; we defined y as leaves directly attached to c, x as degree-2 vertices each with its leaf. Total leaves = y + x. Using N: 1 + x + y = N => y = N-1 - x. Leaves L = x + (N-1 - x) = N-1 = 2022? That seems wrong because leaves double-count? Let's be careful.\n\nOur earlier parameterization: For each chain length2 we have one degree-2 vertex u and one leaf w. The leaf is not directly attached to c; it's leaf of u. So counts: number of degree-2 vertices = k, number of leaves directly attached to c = L1, number of leaves hanging from degree-2 = k (since each chain has one leaf). Total vertices: 1 (c) + k (degree2) + L1 (direct leaves) + k (leaf at end) = 1 + L1 + 2k = N.\n\nLeaves total = L1 + k.\n\nTo minimize leaves, maximize k, limited by L1 >=0 => k <= (N-1)/2. Since N = 2023 => k_max = floor((2022)/2)=1011. So min leaves = N-1 - k_max = 2022 - 1011 =1011.\n\nThus 1011 is minimal.\n\nCheck with more formal lower bound: Leaves >= ceil((N-1)/2)? With N odd, (N-1)/2 = 1011. So lower bound matches.\n\nThus answer 1011.\n\nNow produce final answer format.", "response": "ANSWER: 1011", "answer": 1011, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.4305109630186053e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999985694900602, "logp_correct": -1.4305109630186053e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6833208519978393, "cot_lp_min64": -1.517561110096608, "frac_below_-5": 0.012229539040451553, "digit_lp_min": -2.12693190574646, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 4252, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.680836794548668e-05, "logp_nocot": -5.704986015334725, "p_nocot": 0.0033293239442869623, "uplift": 5.704984584823762}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "b9e6baf4-176d-4beb-adaf-6dffaa06f4b6", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: In a country, there are $28$ cities, and between some cities, there are two-way flights. Each city has exactly one airport, which can be either small, medium, or big. For every route that:\n1. Contains more than two cities,\n2. Does not repeat any city,\n3. Ends where it begins,\n\nIt must include all types of airports. What is the maximum number of flights in this country?\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: In a country, there are $28$ cities, and between some cities, there are two-way flights. Each city has exactly one airport, which can be either small, medium, or big. For every route that:\n1. Contains more than two cities,\n2. Does not repeat any city,\n3. Ends where it begins,\n\nIt must include all types of airports. What is the maximum number of flights in this country?", "true_answer": 53, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "b9e6baf4-176d-4beb-adaf-6dffaa06f4b6:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 25307, 68745, 4792, 13, 3274, 553, 220, 2029, 15636, 11, 2454, 483, 448, 21292, 1490, 25, 3291, 11, 14093, 11, 3464, 13, 13378, 149617, 7465, 350, 1836, 3635, 741, 30255, 25, 2214, 1062, 4705, 15839, 350, 1750, 24161, 38360, 8, 328, 5517, 1424, 17, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 1062, 15839, 328, 5517, 5064, 18, 8, 2804, 10232, 722, 3407, 6009, 328, 69267, 13, 2632, 1062, 15839, 2804, 679, 540, 5153, 1001, 3291, 11, 1001, 14093, 11, 1001, 3464, 13, 14400, 4552, 3874, 11, 1354, 382, 860, 15839, 2360, 1606, 38360, 328, 540, 1645, 1920, 6009, 13, 1328, 382, 96811, 328, 109718, 39012, 141795, 190565, 350, 267, 287, 19040, 4142, 8, 41478, 306, 261, 220, 18, 15858, 289, 328, 38360, 13, 1416, 1682, 316, 44207, 2086, 328, 22569, 350