a forest of stars? Actually each vertex's earlier adjacency count ≤1 means the graph is a \"pseudoforest\" where each component has at most one cycle? No, because later vertices can have many later neighbors, but earlier neighbors limited to 1. In such an ordering, the underlying undirected graph can be any? Let's think: For any edge between diagonals a and b, whichever is later sees the earlier one as an intersecting earlier diagonal; it counts 1. So each edge contributes to count of later endpoint (maybe both later? only one later). Thus each diagonal can be intersected by many later diagonals, but earlier intersections limited to 1 per diagonal.\n\nThus the ordering can be such that the graph is any; it's about existence of a topological order where each vertex has indegree ≤1, where we orient each edge from earlier to later. That's orientation of graph where each vertex indegree ≤1, which is possible if graph has no directed cycles (since orientation from earlier to later). In any undirected graph, we can assign a total order and orient edges forward, each vertex indegree equals number of neighbors earlier. The condition we desire is that there exists a total order where each vertex has at most one neighbor earlier, i.e., we can label vertices such that each vertex has at most one earlier neighbor.\n\nThis is equivalent to graph being a \"forest of rooted trees where each node has at most one parent\"? Actually that's a rooted forest (each node indegree ≤1). Any orientation of undirected graph that yields a rooted forest is an acyclic orientation where each node indegree ≤1. That's possible iff graph has no undirected cycles? Let's test: a triangle (3-cycle). Can we order vertices v1,v2,v3 such that each vertex has at most one earlier neighbor? Suppose order v1, v2, v3. Edges: v1-v2 (v2 later sees earlier neighbor v1), v1-v3 (v3 later sees earlier neighbor v1), v2-v3 (v3 later sees earlier neighbor v2). So v3 has two earlier neighbors (v1 and v2) violates. Any order will give some vertex with two earlier neighbors? For triangle, minimal earlier neighbor count max is 2. So not possible. Thus graph must be acyclic (no cycles). Even a path of length 2 (3 vertices) is a tree, works: order leaf, middle, other leaf. Middle has one earlier neighbor, other leaf has one earlier neighbor (the middle).\n\nThus condition that there exists order with indegree ≤1 is equivalent to graph being a forest (acyclic). Because orientation of a tree can be made a rooted tree with indegree ≤1. For any forest, we can order via topological order from roots outward.\n\nThus we need to find maximum size of a set of diagonals that contain no cycles in the intersection graph (i.e., the intersection graph must be acyclic). Intersection graph of diagonals where vertices intersect if they cross inside polygon.\n\nSo we need largest number of diagonals such that there is no set of three diagonals each intersecting pairwise (i.e., no cycle). In geometric arrangement of chords of a convex polygon, the intersection graph is actually the comparability of intervals? Might be known: Intersection graphs of chords of a circle are circle graphs. Max size of acyclic subgraph? Equivalent to maximum number of edges in a circle graph that is a forest (i.e., no cycles). This is maximum number of chords we can select with no intersecting cycle.\n\nWhat's a cycle in intersection graph? Example earlier: three diagonals pairwise intersect forming a triangle. In convex polygon, three diagonals can be pairwise intersecting only if they form a \"hexagon\"? Let's think: to have three diagonals pairwise intersect, they must correspond to six distinct vertices with each diagonal connecting opposite vertices, effectively three chords of a hexagon that are the three main diagonals; they intersect each other (like the three diagonals of a convex hexagon). In general, a cycle of length k corresponds to set of k diagonals where each diagonal intersects two others forming a cyclic intersection pattern.\n\nGoal: maximize number of diagonals without having a cycle in intersection graph i.e., no collection of diagonals where each diagonal intersects at least two others in a cyclic arrangement.\n\nPerhaps we can construct a set of diagonals forming a \"non-crossing\" tree plus maybe some crossing but still acyclic. We want as many as possible.\n\nObservation: Intersection graph of chords is a circle graph which is known to be planar? Not necessarily. But forest property may limit count.\n\nMaybe we can think in terms of building incrementally: At each step we add diagonal that intersects at most one prior. That ensures final graph has each vertex indegree ≤1 oriented by addition order. Since adding later diagonal may intersect many earlier ones? Wait condition only restricts each new diagonal intersect at most one already drawn. So later diagonal can intersect many previously drawn? No: It says each newly drawn diagonal intersects no more than one of the already drawn diagonals. So each added diagonal can intersect at most one previous diagonal. Thus each diagonal, when added, intersects at most one earlier diagonal. Therefore overall, each edge in intersection graph corresponds to the later endpoint intersecting exactly that earlier one; thus the graph will have each diagonal intersect at most one earlier: indegree ≤1. However a later diagonal could intersect many later ones (future). But when those later ones are added they must intersect at most one earlier - could be this diagonal, so each later diagonal can intersect this one (as its single earlier intersect). So a diagonal could have arbitrarily many later intersecting diagonals, but each of those later have only that as earlier intersect. So the intersection graph becomes a collection of rooted stars where edges go from earlier to later, forming a forest of outward stars? Essentially each diagonal can be intersected by many later diagonals, but each later diagonal intersects only its \"parent\