, 261, 3149, 45808, 261, 33839, 287, 350, 4522, 261, 72162, 99731, 297, 741, 2214, 45808, 261, 12378, 65, 11, 2086, 328, 38360, 314, 261, 72162, 11, 22569, 314, 350, 64, 12, 16, 11043, 65, 659, 261, 14793, 65, 12, 16, 8, 314, 220, 17, 378, 533, 261, 533, 287, 314, 220, 17, 77, 533, 261, 533, 287, 364, 39520, 297, 11, 581, 1682, 41776, 261, 659, 287, 5401, 316, 692, 99731, 297, 11, 261, 17568, 8841, 54912, 13, 47219, 4215, 21241, 1261, 261, 326, 287, 553, 472, 5263, 472, 4149, 25, 261, 314, 8350, 1858, 17629, 2406, 12098, 287, 314, 35964, 2406, 820, 261, 741, 1328, 59684, 22569, 314, 220, 17, 77, 533, 261, 533, 287, 364, 3335, 297, 382, 625, 261, 4387, 45808, 11, 581, 665, 6954, 45808, 328, 2349, 261, 12378, 65, 15683, 297, 11, 1815, 14836, 5141, 1236, 38360, 25040, 22569, 11223, 316, 3149, 45808, 364, 84787, 65414, 22569, 6960, 220, 17, 77, 533, 35964, 7, 17, 103946, 77, 741, 41021, 23864, 68753, 364, 12845, 261, 314, 8350, 7, 103946, 77, 741, 7801, 287, 314, 35964, 2406, 820, 261, 741, 45438, 261, 659, 287, 150477, 364, 12845, 297, 28, 1353, 13, 44237, 28, 702, 13, 2632, 261, 28, 702, 11, 287, 28, 43552, 7, 1353, 14, 702, 25987, 702, 871, 45808, 220, 702, 87, 702, 65651, 9707, 220, 1353, 13, 7801, 22569, 314, 220, 17, 9, 1353, 533, 702, 533, 702, 314, 220, 1179, 533, 455, 314, 220, 7521, 364, 84787, 10562, 1899, 24702, 314, 220, 7521, 30, 1309, 316, 14866, 484, 2454, 11165, 66938, 316, 261, 1899, 10610, 26, 14531, 2454, 39069, 2438, 75483, 656, 12332, 220, 17, 503, 220, 18, 13, 730, 45808, 328, 513, 4331, 350, 64, 17568, 8, 483, 261, 591, 220, 15, 485, 24, 326, 287, 591, 220, 15, 485, 24, 11, 581, 679, 220, 1353, 8663, 25, 220, 17, 61, 64, 220, 18, 61, 65, 350, 126083, 741, 6117, 4431, 25, 2870, 350, 64, 17568, 8, 326, 350, 64, 10, 16, 17568, 8, 350, 81339, 220, 17, 8, 326, 2870, 350, 64, 17568, 8, 326, 350, 64, 17568, 10, 16, 8, 350, 81339, 220, 18, 741, 21926, 9707, 10586, 22569, 364, 72816, 10562, 1899, 24702, 382, 220, 7521, 13, 19792, 316, 6757, 1354, 382, 860, 3432, 12282, 2360, 2647, 372, 11652, 8117, 316, 945, 22569, 30, 1843, 581, 1199, 7598, 372, 9778, 11, 22569, 6284, 8088, 9778, 11, 813, 3609, 22569, 4215, 5251, 9778, 13, 68506, 6603, 38360, 38566, 4149, 22569, 2236, 22569, 1606, 3518, 2454, 744, 26, 581, 2023, 10112, 679, 1920, 13574, 164265, 2454, 9874, 22569, 11, 889, 3609, 22569, 314, 4215, 328, 350, 17, 77, 10853, 533, 261, 10853, 533, 287, 10853, 8, 72554, 220, 17, 18085, 239, 77, 10853, 533, 350, 12298, 261, 10853, 659, 4215, 287, 10853, 741, 12265, 4215, 261, 10853, 659, 4215, 287, 10853, 99731, 19957, 395, 2454, 19723, 26, 889, 10705, 10112, 581, 2023, 23275, 220, 17, 77, 533, 1349, 4215, 30, 2214, 142423, 30, 357, 29645, 1636, 382, 4590, 45808, 364, 84787, 6052, 220, 7521, 364, 4383, 3291, 297, 316, 14866, 8302, 25, 297, 28, 17, 11, 581, 665, 679, 350, 15, 11, 15, 8, 326, 350, 16, 11, 15, 8, 18051, 220, 17, 871, 22569, 28, 16, 13, 52227, 220, 17, 77, 533, 261, 533, 287, 25, 261, 40464, 5494, 1858, 17629, 17, 25987, 16, 17568, 28, 43552, 7, 17, 14, 16, 25987, 17, 871, 22569, 28, 19, 12, 16, 12, 17, 28, 16, 6145, 364, 77, 28, 18, 25, 261, 28, 16, 17568, 28, 18, 871, 22569, 28, 21, 12, 16, 12, 18, 28, 17, 13, 4101, 581, 679, 3407, 5571, 4137, 1920, 22569, 30, 30456, 413, 13464, 5517, 220, 18, 25, 350, 15, 11, 15, 54876, 16, 11, 15, 54876, 17, 11, 15, 8, 22569, 28, 17, 5882, 364, 16577, 3605, 8535, 23275, 220, 17, 77, 533, 35964, 7, 17, 44237, 297, 741, 2214, 297, 4387, 13749, 11, 495, 29702, 220, 17, 77, 533, 17, 103946, 77, 13, 2214, 297, 28, 1353, 8967, 220, 1179, 12, 455, 28, 7521, 364, 84787, 6052, 364, 94272, 4733, 6011, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 220, 7521], "n_tokens": 1616, "words": 1016, "band_lo": 608.5, "band_hi": 2434.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have 100 distinct positive integers. We define a pair (ordered? presumably unordered) is good if the ratio of the two numbers is either 2 or 3. So |a/b| = 2 or 3 (since positive integers, ratio >1). So pairs where one is twice the other or three times the other.\n\nWe need maximize number of such good unordered pairs among the set of 100 distinct numbers. A number can be in multiple pairs (e.g., if we have numbers that are multiples of each other powers). We need choose a set of 100 distinct positive integers to maximize count of unordered pairs (i,j) where either i=2j or i=3j.\n\nWe need maximize edges in a graph where vertices are numbers, edges correspond to ratio 2 or 3. Need choose 100 vertices (distinct positive integers) to maximize number of such edges.\n\nObservation: If we include numbers that form chains according to multiplication by 2 or 3, we can get many edges. Graph is like a directed graph where edges when one is 2x or 3x of other; treat undirected.\n\nGoal: Find maximum possible edges in such a graph given 100 vertices.\n\nPotentially we can pick numbers that are all powers of 2 times a base? Let's think.\n\nIf we restrict to numbers that are all of form 2^a * 3^b (since only these can be related by factor 2 or 3). If we include numbers outside that set, they cannot be linked to others except maybe with some. So to maximize edges we want numbers closed under multiplication/division by 2 or 3 as much as possible. That suggests we choose the set to be a subset of the lattice of (a,b) exponents.\n\nDefine each number as n = 2^a * 3^b * k where k is not divisible by 2 or 3. If we have two numbers with same k, ratio will be 2^{a1-a2} * 3^{b1-b2}. For ratio to be exactly 2 or 3, we need difference vector (Δa, Δb) either (1,0) or (0,1). So edges only between numbers sharing same k factor.\n\nThus to maximize edges, we should keep all numbers with same k (i.e., k=1 perhaps) to allow many edges. If we include numbers with different k, they cannot be connected to each other except maybe if ratio of k's results in 2 or 3 times power but since k not divisible by 2 or 3, ratio of k's cannot be 2 or 3; thus edges only within each k class.\n\nThus best is to pick all numbers with k=1. So numbers are of form 2^a * 3^b distinct. Choose 100 distinct pairs (a,b). Then edges are between pairs with (a,b) differing by (1,0) or (0,1). So it's like induced subgraph of infinite grid (Z^2) where vertices are chosen set S of 100 points. Edge exists between points that are unit Manhattan distance (up/down/right/left) but only along axes not diagonal.\n\nWe need maximum number of edges in induced subgraph of size 100 on the 2D integer lattice with adjacency defined as Manhattan distance 1 (grid graph). This is a combinatorial geometry problem: maximize edges in a graph on 100 vertices that is subgraph of grid with edges between orthogonal neighbors.\n\nWe can view as we choose 100 lattice points; each point has up to 4 neighbors (if all present). Want maximize total adjacency.\n\nThe maximum edges likely achieved by selecting a shape that is as \"compact\" as possible: a rectangle or as close to square to maximize interior points (4-degree) and minimize boundary points (less edges). For a given number of vertices n, the maximum number of edges in subgraph of Z^2 is at most 2n - ceil(2*sqrt(n)). Actually known result: For induced subgraph of infinite grid, edges ≤ 2n - 2*ceil(sqrt(n)). Let's derive.\n\nConsider a set of vertices with coordinates. Let E be number of edges. For any finite subgraph, we have relationship: sum of degrees = 2E. Each interior vertex can have degree up to 4. Boundary vertices have fewer. The maximal edges occurs when shape is as close to a full rectangle a × b (where a*b ≥ n). For rectangle a×b, number of vertices = a*b, edges = (a-1)*b + a*(b-1) = 2ab - a - b = 2n - a - b.\n\nGiven n, we want minimize a + b subject to ab ≥ n, a,b positive integers. Minimum sum occurs when a and b are as close as possible: a = floor(sqrt(n)), b = ceil(n / a). This yields edges = 2n - a - b.\n\nIf n is not a perfect rectangle, we can fill rectangle of size a×b containing n, then possibly miss some vertices reducing edges compared to full rectangle.\n\nThus maximal edges likely 2n - ceil(2√n). Let's compute approximate.\n\nLet a = floor(√n). Then b = ceil(n / a). Compute a + b minimized.\n\nLet n=100. sqrt=10. So a=10, b=ceil(100/10)=10 => rectangle 10x10 fills exactly 100. Then edges = 2*100 -10 -10 = 200 -20 = 180.\n\nThus maximum good pairs = 180? need to verify that each edge corresponds to a good pair; yes each adjacent point differs by factor 2 or 3. In rectangle of exponents (a,b) with a from 0..9 and b from 0..9, we have 100 numbers: 2^a 3^b (distinct). Edges: between (a,b) and (a+1,b) (ratio 2) and between (a,b) and (a,b+1) (ratio 3). That's exactly grid edges.\n\nTherefore maximum good pairs is 180. Need to ensure there is no better configuration using different k factors leading to more edges? If we use multiple k classes, edges cannot cross classes, so total edges sum across classes. Splitting vertices reduces possible edges because edges only within each class; we could maybe have two separate rectangles each giving edges, but total edges = sum of (2n_i - a_i - b_i) ≤ 2∑n_i - (sum a_i + sum b_i). Since sum a_i + sum b_i ≥ minimal for each cluster; but overall maybe we could exceed 2n - min sum? For convex? I suspect best is single rectangle.\n\nThus answer 180.\n\nCheck small n to verify pattern: n=2, we can have (0,0) and (1,0) ratio 2 => edges=1. Formula 2n - a - b: a=floor(sqrt2)=1,b=ceil(2/1)=2 => edges=4-1-2=1 correct.\n\nn=3: a=1,b=3 => edges=6-1-3=2. Can we have three points making two edges? Could be chain length 3: (0,0),(1,0),(2,0) edges=2 works.\n\nEdge count can't exceed 2n - ceil(2 sqrt n). For n perfect square, this equals 2n -2√n. For n=100 gives 200-20=180.\n\nThus answer.\n\nProvide output format: ANSWER: 180", "response": "ANSWER: 180", "answer": 180, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.9788551043120606e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999802116447489, "logp_correct": -1.9788551043120606e-05, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6094276656231402, "cot_lp_min64": -1.0005143908621985, "frac_below_-5": 0.008739076154806492, "digit_lp_min": -6.263702869415283, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 1602, "lp_end": -0.5826802849769592, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1086402082582936e-05, "logp_nocot": -11.573206901550293, "p_nocot": 9.414995238881511e-06, "uplift": 11.57318711299925}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
