30, 2214, 261, 39383, 1200, 15839, 24878, 722, 38360, 11, 14531, 13, 623, 7060, 581, 24923, 30005, 15839, 5517, 220, 15363, 24878, 722, 38360, 13, 17484, 290, 4322, 484, 2454, 25363, 853, 6741, 44021, 220, 16, 16218, 13422, 30, 623, 135200, 20680, 1340, 679, 7598, 41478, 26, 889, 395, 2617, 483, 4590, 15839, 11, 17804, 722, 38360, 364, 16577, 1890, 25, 10547, 1354, 41477, 1919, 290, 3104, 95681, 261, 25363, 2254, 24878, 722, 30, 4569, 1481, 11095, 316, 261, 13679, 15839, 5517, 94879, 220, 15363, 11, 1118, 581, 52015, 656, 2569, 7060, 364, 84787, 478, 28, 9444, 23, 13, 41021, 14866, 43276, 25, 9184, 328, 15600, 18279, 5517, 220, 899, 483, 2086, 328, 42274, 8039, 81, 642, 316, 220, 899, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 220, 16, 11, 20, 11, 22, 11, 994, 741, 6789, 25, 363, 7, 899, 11, 16, 25987, 899, 26, 363, 7, 899, 11, 20, 25987, 48295, 26, 363, 7, 899, 11, 22, 25987, 48295, 26, 363, 7, 899, 11, 994, 25987, 899, 13, 34138, 28, 9444, 23, 22476, 364, 4383, 484, 581, 60500, 18279, 1919, 457, 28, 15, 503, 220, 899, 350, 47163, 12421, 8, 1481, 3644, 2569, 16, 350, 22023, 741, 79835, 483, 457, 28, 17, 11, 18, 11, 19, 11, 21, 11, 23, 11, 24, 11, 702, 1217, 4790, 364, 84787, 6052, 47056, 220, 37610, 364, 58369, 4733, 13], "n_tokens": 5928, "words": 3675, "band_lo": 1837.5, "band_hi": 7350.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have a clock with 12 numbers. Two hands that can move independently; initially both at 12 (i.e., same position). Moves consist of picking one hand to move clockwise to the next number (increment its position mod 12) while the other stays. So each move changes the ordered pair (position of hand A, position of hand B) by incrementing exactly one coordinate by 1 modulo 12.\n\nWe want sequences of 144 moves such that during the sequence, every possible positioning of the hands appears exactly once, and at the end of the 144 movements, the hands have returned to initial position (both at 12). Also we count number of such sequences N.\n\nInterpretation: The state space = set of ordered pairs (i,j) where i, j ∈ {0,...,11} maybe representing numbers 12 as 0. Number of possible positions = 12*12 =144. They want each possible positioning appears exactly once during the sequence. Starting at (0,0) (12,12). Over a sequence of moves, we visit each of the 144 states exactly once. Since we start at (0,0) and also end at (0,0) after 144 moves, that means we are tracing a Hamiltonian cycle (length 144) in the directed graph defined by allowed moves (increment one coordinate by 1 mod 12). But note that we count the initial state as visited at start, and also at end we return to same state, which would be visited twice unless they consider that the final return may be after all positions visited (i.e., we count the final state as same as initial but not extra). Usually if we have 144 distinct positions, we have 144 steps to go between them if we start at a position and after each move you get a new position; after 144 moves you would have visited 145 positions counting repeats, but they want exactly once each of the 144 positions, including the starting one (so total steps = 144, we start at a position, then we have 144 moves and we end back at start, meaning we visited 144 positions (including the start) because the final position repeats start, not counted as a new position. However we need to verify: If we have 144 distinct positions, start at one, after 1 move we are at second distinct, after 143 moves we are at 144th distinct (the last unvisited), after 144th move we return to start. That's 144 moves and visited each of the 144 states exactly once (though start appears twice: at time 0 and at end). This is consistent.\n\nThus we need number of Hamiltonian cycles of length 144 in the graph (call it G) that start/end at (0,0) and visit all vertices exactly once, where edges are moves that increment one coordinate by 1 modulo 12 (i.e., edges correspond to moving hand A: (i,j) -> (i+1 mod12, j) or moving hand B: (i,j) -> (i, j+1 mod12). This is essentially a directed grid torus (12x12) with edges only in positive direction (right or up) on a torus (i.e., wrap-around). So each vertex has outdegree 2 (right and up). Also indegree 2 (from left and down). So it's a directed 2-regular out-degree graph (a directed de Bruijn-like). It's essentially the Cayley digraph of Z12 × Z12 with generators (1,0) and (0,1).\n\nWe need number of Hamiltonian cycles (directed) that start at origin. Since the graph is regular and vertex-transitive, number of Hamiltonian cycles (directed) may be related to number of Hamiltonian cycles in the undirected version (grid torus) but with orientation constraints.\n\nObservation: Since moves are only clockwise (increment) along each coordinate, the path is monotone in each coordinate modulo 12, but because of wrap-around, you may loop around many times. However since we must visit each vertex exactly once, the sequence of moves defines a Hamiltonian cycle of the directed torus.\n\nThis is essentially a standard problem: counting Hamiltonian cycles in directed toroidal grid with moves east or north (mod m,n). Equivalent to number of \"Hamiltonian cycles\" in a 2D torus digraph known as \"Hamiltonian cycles in de Bruijn graph of dimension 2\"? Not exactly.\n\nBut we can model as a permutation of the 144 vertices where each vertex has exactly one outgoing edge chosen among its two possibilities, and the resulting digraph is a single directed cycle covering all vertices. So we need number of functional digraphs (where each vertex has out-degree 1) constructed from choosing for each vertex one of the two outgoing edges, such that resulting permutation is a single 144-cycle. That's essentially counting number of \"tournaments\"? Not.\n\nAlternative viewpoint: Since each vertex must have exactly one outgoing edge (the move taken when we are at that vertex in the Hamiltonian cycle). In a Hamiltonian cycle, each vertex has exactly one outgoing and one incoming edge (the edges of the cycle). Since the digraph's edges are only the two 'right' or 'up' moves, each vertex's incoming edge must be from left or down. So the cycle is a directed 1-factor of the digraph, i.e., a permutation where each vertex maps to either its right neighbor or up neighbor.\n\nThus the problem reduces to counting permutations σ on the set Z_12 × Z_12 such that for each (i,j), σ(i,j) = (i+1,j) or (i,j+1). Moreover σ must be a single cycle (i.e., its permutation has a single orbit). Also we need that σ starts at (0,0) but any cycle can be rotated; counting cycles with distinguished starting point maybe divides by 144? We'll handle after.\n\nThus N equals number of such permutations that consist of a single directed cycle covering all vertices (i.e., a Hamiltonian cycle) multiplied by perhaps 1 (since starting point fixed) maybe not.\n\nLet’s count number of such \"Hamiltonian permutations\". This resembles counting \"Hamiltonian cycles in the directed 2D torus grid with east/north moves.\" Known result: number = ( (2)^{mn} - ???). Let's think.\n\nWe can think of the cycle as a spanning subgraph where each vertex has out-degree 1 and in-degree 1. This structure corresponds to a collection of vertex-disjoint directed cycles covering all vertices, i.e., a permutation decomposing into cycles, each cycle uses only right/up steps. We want the case where there is exactly one cycle.\n\nCounting permutations with given local choices is similar to counting \"Eulerian orientations\"? Not.\n\nWe can encode by assigning to each vertex a direction: either right (R) or up (U), indicating which outgoing edge it uses. Then the indegree condition must hold: each vertex must have exactly one incoming edge, which comes from left neighbor if that neighbor chose R, or from below neighbor if that neighbor chose U. So each vertex's incoming edge must be satisfied.\n\nThus a global assignment of R/U to each vertex must be such that each vertex receives exactly one incoming edge.\n\nLet’s define for each vertex v a variable a(v) ∈ {R,U} meaning the edge out of v. Then the indegree of v is equal to number of neighbors w such that a(w) points to v. Since each vertex has exactly two potential incoming neighbors: left neighbor (v_left = (i-1,j)) if that neighbor chooses R, and below neighbor (v_down = (i,j-1)) if that neighbor chooses U (mod 12). We need exactly one of those two to point to v.\n\nThus condition: For each v, exactly one of a(v_left) = R and a(v_down) = U holds (i.e., true).\n\nEquivalently, the assignment a must satisfy that for each vertex, the pair of values at its left and down neighbors cannot both be wrong nor both be true; exactly one is true.\n\nThis is reminiscent of a combinatorial object known as a \"perfect matching on a bipartite graph\"? Let's transform.\n\nDefine a Boolean variable at each vertex whether edge points right (R) which we call value 0, and up (U) value 1 perhaps. Then condition at each vertex v: a(v_left) = R (i.e., a(v_left)=0) XOR a(v_down) = U (which means a(v_down)=1) must be true (i.e., exactly one). So XOR condition: (a(v_left) == 0) ⊕ (a(v_down) == 1) = 1. Let's encode using bits.\n\nLet b(v) = 0 for R, 1 for U. Then condition: (b(v_left) == 0) XOR (b(v_down) == 1) = 1.\n\nLet's write statements in terms of b values:\n\nCondition: (b(v_left) = 0) XOR (b(v_down) = 1) = 1.\n\nThat is same as (¬b(v_left)) XOR (b(v_down)) = 1.\n\nXOR of ¬b_left and b_down equals 1 ⇒ ¬b_left XOR b_down = 1 ⇒ (¬b_left) != b_down.\n\nThus condition: b_down = b_left (since NOT of b_left is different from b_down). Wait compute: XOR =1 means they differ. So ¬b_left ≠ b_down ⇒ b_down = b_left. Because if b_down = b_left, then ¬b_left ≠ b_left (since b_left and its negation differ). Yes that satisfies XOR=1. If b_down ≠ b_left, then ¬b_left = b_down? Let's test: Suppose b_left=0, then ¬b_left=1. If b_down=1, then ¬b_left XOR b_down = 1 XOR 1 =0, not allowed. So indeed we require b_down = b_left.\n\nThus condition reduces to: For every vertex v, b(i,j-1) = b(i-1,j). (Indices modulo 12). So the bit value at the below neighbor equals the bit at the left neighbor.\n\nThus the assignment b must be constant along each diagonal direction (southwest-northeast?). Because relation ties coordinates (i-1,j) and (i,j-1). This condition implies that moving (−1,0) then (0,+1) results same as moving (0,+1) then (−1,0). So staying consistent suggests b depends only on i+j (mod something). Let's formalize.\n\nLet’s define c(i,j) = b(i,j). Condition: b(i-1,j) = b(i,j-1) for all i,j (mod 12). Define function f(i,j) = b(i,j). Then for any steps, we can propagate: Starting from (0,0) value b00, then b(1,0) maybe independent? But condition linking across grid will enforce that b(i,j) depends on i+j mod 12 (or maybe on i-j). Let's test.\n\nRewrite condition as b(i,j) = b(i+1,j-1). Shift indices: replace i←i+1, j←j, then condition gives b(i,j) = b(i+1, j-1). So b is constant along lines of slope 1 (i.e., moving east and south). Actually moving east (+1,0) and south (0,-1). So along vector (1,-1). So b is constant on each diagonal where i - j is constant? Let's see: (i+1, j-1) changes i - j by (i+1 - (j-1)) = (i - j) +2. Wait compute: (i+1) - (j-1) = i - j +2. So not constant. Let's test other expression: The condition b(i-1,j) = b(i,j-1) means b at point shifted left equals b at point shifted down. So moving left then down vs down then left yields same value. This suggests b depends only on i+j? Because moving left reduces i by1, moving down reduces j by1; sum i+j reduces by1 either way. Indeed b(i-1,j) and b(i,j-1) both have i+j decreased by1 relative to b(i,j). So condition says the value at positions with sum s-1 is same regardless which coordinate decreased. That indicates the value depends only on i+j modulo 12 (since torus). Let's verify: Suppose define s = i+j (mod 12). Then for any i,j, both (i-1,j) and (i,j-1) have sum s-1 mod 12, so they should have same b value. This consistent with a function g(s) such that b(i,j) = g(i+j mod 12). Does this satisfy condition? Take b(i-1,j) = g(i-1 + j) = g(s-1). b(i,j-1) = g(i + j-1) = g(s-1). They are equal, condition satisfied. Conversely, given the local equality, we can prove by induction that b depends only on i+j mod 12 (since torus is connected via these relations). Indeed the condition ensures that for any two vertices with same i+j mod12 you can reach each other via steps left-down combos.\n\nThus assignments correspond to choosing 12 bits (values) for each sum class s ∈ Z12. So there are 2^{12} possible assignments b.\n\nNow each assignment determines outgoing edge directions for each vertex: if b(i,j)=0 then choose R (right), else choose U (up). Does this automatically guarantee that each vertex also has exactly one incoming edge? The derived condition ensures indegree 1. So any such assignment yields a permutation composed of cycles.\n\nNow what about the cycle structure? For a given assignment, the resulting permutation may consist of multiple cycles. We need those where it's a single cycle of length 144.\n\nThus we need to count number of functions g: Z12 → {0,1} such that the resulting permutation σ defined by σ(i,j) = (i+1,j) if g(i+j)=0 else (i,j+1) if g(i+j)=1, results in a single cycle.\n\nWe can analyze the dynamics. This is reminiscent of a \"rotor router\" type.\n\nObservation: The permutation structure is determined by following the rule: at each position, if the sum s = i+j (mod12) has a value 0 (R) you move east (increment i), else move north (increment j). This is like a deterministic cellular automaton; moving changes sum: moving east increases i so sum s' = (i+1)+j = s+1; moving north increases j so sum s' = i+(j+1) = s+1 as well. Interesting! Regardless of which direction, the sum i+j always increases by 1 modulo 12 after each move. Indeed because each move increments exactly one coordinate, sum increments by 1. Therefore after each move, the value of s cycles through Z12 in order. So after k moves, s = initial s + k (mod12). Since we start at (0,0) with s=0, after t steps we are at a vertex with sum s = t mod12. Therefore the value g(s) at step depends only on step count modulo 12.\n\nThus the trajectory is deterministic: Starting from (0,0), we know each step increments sum. The direction taken at step where sum = s is determined by g(s): if g(s)=0 we go east, else north.\n\nThus the path after 144 steps (12 cycles of sums) will be predetermined by the sequence of 12 bits repeated 12 times? Wait the moves are determined solely by g(s) for each s encountered. Since sum cycles through 0..11 repeatedly, the sequence of moves is periodic with period 12. So the entire 144-step path is just 12 repetitions of the 12-step pattern defined by g(0)...g(11).\n\nNow what is the resulting permutation? Starting from (0,0) and following the moves, you will trace some cycle(s). Since the whole state space will be visited after 144 steps if and only if the path forms a single cycle length 144. But given the dynamics of sum increase, the path is deterministic and repeats after some length L which is a divisor of 144 dependent on g.\n\nSpecifically, consider the net displacement after 12 steps (one full cycle of sums). Let's compute vector displacement after 12 moves: each step either east (1,0) or north (0,1). So total displacement after 12 steps is (E,N) where E = number of s with g(s)=0, N = number with g(s)=1, and E+N =12.\n\nThus after 12 steps, position shifts by (E,N). Since positions are modulo 12, after each 12-step block we add vector (E,N) mod12.\n\nAfter k blocks (each of 12 steps), total displacement = k*(E,N) mod12.\n\nNow the overall orbit length equals the smallest k such that k*(E,N) ≡ (0,0) mod12 (both coordinates). Because after that many blocks, we return to start position; also sum after 12k steps = 0 (since sum cycles), but we return to same coordinates only when the cumulative displacement is 0. The total number of steps visited before returning to origin is 12*k where k is order of vector (E,N) in group (Z12 × Z12). Since we also need to cover all 144 vertices (i.e., length 144), we need k=12 (so 12 blocks). That means order of (E,N) must be 12. i.e., the smallest positive integer k with kE ≡0 mod12 and kN ≡0 mod12 is 12. Equivalent to gcd(E,12) = gcd(N,12) = 12/k? Not.\n\nThe order of (E,N) is lcm(12/ gcd(E,12), 12/ gcd(N,12)). The order equals 12 iff both E and N are coprime to 12? Actually the order dividing 12. For the order to be exactly 12, we need that the vector is not of smaller order. Order d is smallest d such that dE ≡0 mod12 and dN ≡0 mod12. This means 12 | dE and 12 | dN. Since d ≤12. For d=12, automatically 12|12E and 12|12N true. For any smaller d, we would need 12 dividing dE and dN. That requires that 12/gcd(E,12) ≤ d maybe. Let's compute: The condition that dE ≡0 mod12 means 12 / gcd(E,12) divides d. Similarly for N. So the minimal d is lcm(12/gcd(E,12), 12/gcd(N,12)). So the order equals 12 iff lcm(12/gcd(E,12), 12/gcd(N,12)) =12. This requires that each of the two quantities is ≤12 and the lcm is 12. Equivalent to at least one of them is 12, or they are multiples that combine to 12. For lcm to be 12, need that for each prime factor 2^2 *3, the maximum exponent among the two numbers equals that in 12. So we need that for prime 2, max exponent of 2 in (12/gcd(E,12)) and (12/gcd(N,12)) is 2. Similarly for prime 3 exponent 1. This essentially means that at least one of E,N is not divisible by 2, and at least one not divisible by 3? Not exactly.\n\nSimplify: Let gE = gcd(E,12). Then 12/gE is divisor of 12 capturing missing factors. For order 12, we need that for each prime factor p, at least one of 12/gE or 12/gN has full exponent. That is, for p=2 (exponent2), need max exponent of p in those numbers =2. So if both E and N are even, then gE, gN each at least 2, making 12/gE ≤6, 12/gN ≤6, max exponent maybe 1, giving lcm ≤6, not 12. So we need at least one of E,N odd. Similarly for factor 3, we need at least one of E,N not divisible by 3. So overall condition: gcd(E,12) must be 1 or 2 or 3? Let's enumerate possibilities.\n\nCompute possible (E,N) where E+N=12, E,N between 0..12. Need order of vector (E,N) in Z12×Z12 to be 12.\n\nWe can test each E (0..12). N=12-E.\n\nCompute gcd(E,12) and gcd(N,12). Then order = lcm(12/gcd(E,12), 12/gcd(N,12)). Let's tabulate quickly:\n\nE=0 → gcd=12, 12/gcd=1; N=12 → gcd=12, quotient=1; lcm=1 => order 1 (bad). E=1 → gcd(1,12)=1, q=12; N=11 → gcd(11,12)=1, q=12; lcm=12 => order 12 (good). E=2 → gcd=2, q=6; N=10 gcd(10,12)=2, q=6; lcm=6 => order6 (bad). E=3 → gcd=3, q=4; N=9 gcd(9,12)=3, q=4; lcm=4 => order4 (bad). E=4 → gcd=4, q=3; N=8 gcd=4? gcd(8,12)=4, q=3; lcm=3 => order3 (bad). E=5 → gcd(5,12)=1 q=12; N=7 gcd=1 q=12; lcm=12 => order12 (good). E=6 → gcd=6 q=2; N=6 q=2; lcm=2 => order2 (bad). E=7 symmetrical to 5 (good). E=8 same as 4 (bad). E=9 same as 3 (bad). E=10 same as 2 (bad). E=11 same as 1 (good). E=12 same as 0 (bad).\n\nThus good E are those where E is relatively prime to 12 (i.e., gcd(E,12)=1). Because then both E and N = 12-E also relatively prime to 12? Wait if E relatively prime to 12, then N=12-E also relatively prime to 12 (since if a divisor of 12 divides N, then also divides 12 - N = E). So condition reduces to E coprime to 12, i.e., E ∈ {1,5,7,11}. That's 4 possibilities. Indeed we also have N coprime to 12.\n\nThus the vector displacement after 12 steps must be (E,N) where E ∈ {1,5,7,11} and N = 12 - E (which will be {11,7,5,1} correspondingly). So there are 4 valid choices.\n\nNow recall that E is the number of s where g(s)=0 (Right moves). So we need number of g: Z12 → {0,1} with exactly E zeros and N ones, where E ∈ {1,5,7,11}. Since bits indicate direction.\n\nThus N good bit strings of length 12 with exactly E zeros. Count = binomial(12, E). For each E in set {1,5,7,11}, number of strings = C(12, E). Let's compute: C(12,1)=12; C(12,5)=792 (since C(12,5)=792); C(12,7)=C(12,5)=792; C(12,11)=C(12,1)=12.\n\nTotal good assignments = 12 + 792 + 792 +12 = 1608.\n\nBut we must also ensure that the resulting permutation indeed forms a single cycle of length 144, not just that order of displacement after 12 steps is 12. Does order 12 guarantee that the orbit length is 12*12=144? Since each 12-step block moves us to a new vertex, and after 12 blocks we return to origin. Additionally, we need that the trajectory does not repeat earlier (i.e., the smallest block count that returns to start is exactly 12). The order condition ensures that the displacement after k blocks is zero only for k multiple of 12. Since the sum also cycles every 12 steps, after fewer than 12 blocks, we would have sum = 0 mod12? Wait after k blocks, total steps = 12k, sum = 12k ≡ 0 mod12 always. So the sum condition doesn't prevent earlier return. The only way to return earlier is via displacement vector (kE, kN) ≡ (0,0) mod12. Order of vector being 12 ensures smallest k achieving zero is 12. So path returns to start only after 144 steps. Since we have visited 144 distinct vertices (as each step moves to new vertex), the cycle length is 144. So those assignments produce Hamiltonian cycles.\n\nThus number of Hamiltonian cycles (directed) equals 1608? But caution: Does each assignment correspond to a unique directed Hamiltonian cycle? Starting point at (0,0) leads to unique sequence of moves (deterministic). The permutation (global) may produce that same cycle but starting at different vertex yields rotated sequence but same cycle considered same? In counting sequences of 144 movements (starting at (0,0) with fixed initial position), the sequence is determined by the assignment g as described. Indeed given g, the moves are predetermined: at step t, where sum s = t mod12, we perform direction determined by g(s). So the whole sequence is uniquely determined by g. Hence N equals number of good g = 1608.\n\nBut check also possibility of reversals? Not allowed because moves only clockwise incremental one coordinate; can't go backwards.\n\nThus answer N = 1608. Need remainder modulo 1000 => 608.\n\nLet's double-check there are no extra constraints: \"every possible positioning of the hands appears exactly once\" includes states where both hands same number? Yes includes all 144 ordered pairs, including (i,i). In our construction, do we ever have visited all pairs? For a Hamiltonian cycle covering all vertices, yes. The condition we derived ensures cycle length 144 covering all vertices. Does the property that each vertex has indegree 1 guarantee coverage? The permutation formed may have multiple cycles; but for those with single cycle, covers all vertices.\n\nEdge case: Are there assignments where the path repeats a vertex before covering all? That would correspond to a smaller cycle length dividing 144, which we excluded by order condition.\n\nThus N=1608. Let's verify counting: Number of binary strings length 12 with number of zeros coprime to 12 (i.e., 1,5,7,11). Count: C(12,1)=12; C(12,5)=792; C(12,7)=792; C(12,11)=12. Sum=1608 indeed.\n\nCheck that we counted strings where E=0 or 12 (both zero) would give order1 (bad). Cases with E=2,3,4,6,8,9,10 also bad.\n\nThus answer remainder 608.\n\nLet's output.", "response": "ANSWER: 608", "answer": 608, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -6.198864404893811e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999938011548081, "logp_correct": -6.198864404893811e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5709822783774539, "cot_lp_min64": -1.406397049732231, "frac_below_-5": 0.010145417653026716, "digit_lp_min": -2.5805811882019043, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.004078404977917671, "n_cot_tokens": 5914, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.3112935448589269e-05, "logp_nocot": -7.130524538457337, "p_nocot": 0.0008002994895159525, "uplift": 7.130518339592932}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "04ccb950-8039-44df-8993-b89c04d6c999", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials $Q(x,y,z)$ with coefficients in \\{0,1\\} such that there exists a homogeneous polynomial $P(x,y,z)$ of degree 2016 with integer coefficients and a polynomial $R(x,y,z)$ with integer coefficients so that \n\\[P(x,y,z) Q(x,y,z) = P(yz,zx,xy) + 2R(x,y,z)\\]\nand $P(1,1,1)$ is odd. Determine the size of $S$.\n\nNote: A homogeneous polynomial of degree $d$ consists solely of terms of degree $d$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials $Q(x,y,z)$ with coefficients in \\{0,1\\} such that there exists a homogeneous polynomial $P(x,y,z)$ of degree 2016 with integer coefficients and a polynomial $R(x,y,z)$ with integer coefficients so that \n\\[P(x,y,z) Q(x,y,z) = P(yz,zx,xy) + 2R(x,y,z)\\]\nand $P(1,1,1)$ is odd. Determine the size of $S$.\n\nNote: A homogeneous polynomial of degree $d$ consists solely of terms of degree $d$.", "true_answer": 509545, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "04ccb950-8039-44df-8993-b89c04d6c999:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 3605, 328, 15600, 43446, 76593, 1823, 174033, 1486, 4061, 16103, 54920, 8, 395, 1118, 1354, 13031, 16336, 34539, 151031, 9882, 220, 667, 21, 113464, 398, 11, 326, 448, 16336, 113464, 460, 11, 52718, 279, 47, 4061, 16103, 54920, 8, 1486, 4061, 16103, 54920, 8, 314, 398, 14684, 89, 11, 169788, 11, 53634, 8, 659, 220, 17, 460, 4061, 16103, 54920, 1029, 6332, 398, 7, 16, 11, 16, 11, 16, 8, 14210, 13, 57076, 2086, 328, 4149, 1486, 364, 175703, 7060, 112372, 220, 17, 12951, 13, 12265, 76338, 328, 1486, 553, 220, 15, 14, 16, 350, 258, 54912, 741, 7931, 54912, 11, 47685, 18305, 13, 26632, 112372, 220, 17, 25, 2236, 165412, 85