, 1323, 15, 591, 3407, 62835, 741, 3813, 21081, 15897, 350, 68, 1940, 4213, 2475, 8851, 5571, 402, 261, 22005, 328, 21090, 460, 4410, 4951, 2238, 484, 48294, 2870, 26037, 553, 625, 3101, 3582, 8, 581, 665, 8397, 113510, 3851, 13, 2632, 581, 665, 17161, 722, 16913, 42002, 5571, 11, 2454, 55199, 64699, 316, 261, 10610, 364, 84787, 499, 28, 2548, 382, 4149, 13, 4101, 581, 679, 499, 28, 3336, 30, 7801, 10562, 418, 314, 3336, 9, 5320, 28, 13589, 17, 13, 25267, 418, 314, 1323, 15, 12, 3336, 28, 5695, 21, 13, 220, 5695, 21, 1424, 220, 13589, 17, 11, 19786, 13, 2632, 19957, 499, 382, 220, 2548, 364, 7943, 1309, 316, 6757, 860, 5960, 24384, 540, 9324, 220, 1323, 15, 591, 9707, 1920, 62835, 889, 1217, 14836, 1273, 62835, 540, 2684, 9324, 62257, 5192, 316, 33209, 350, 1798, 41454, 5178, 741, 31038, 15897, 8397, 484, 11, 813, 25435, 364, 84787, 6052, 220, 2548, 364, 4383, 11165, 7911, 25, 19792, 9324, 9707, 220, 1323, 15, 2870, 62835, 3760, 1572, 220, 17, 9, 1323, 15, 316, 717, 143513, 13, 1416, 665, 36281, 8851, 5571, 722, 3518, 261, 3291, 7967, 2238, 484, 10610, 7480, 48294, 3760, 1572, 220, 17, 9, 1323, 15, 326, 1424, 15, 13, 21926, 4149, 13, 2214, 220, 2548, 5571, 11, 581, 665, 2475, 1373, 50104, 3518, 261, 3291, 2960, 21090, 10112, 220, 1353, 15, 13, 7801, 1062, 10610, 9324, 464, 1179, 15, 30, 50557, 1309, 464, 220, 17, 9, 1323, 15, 28, 13989, 15, 11, 1118, 382, 4410, 26, 4224, 13, 2632, 2454, 10610, 59684, 1920, 143513, 364, 7943, 1309, 316, 6757, 484, 42002, 5571, 350, 18474, 8, 553, 16913, 395, 2647, 24702, 13, 30456, 107406, 538, 4242, 5571, 139817, 30, 31038, 7526, 15897, 6757, 16913, 364, 84787, 6052, 314, 2548, 364, 94272, 4733, 364], "n_tokens": 1559, "words": 1066, "band_lo": 566.75, "band_hi": 2267.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have 2020 points in plane, each either black or green. For each black point, there are exactly two green points at distance 2020 from that black point (presumably Euclidean distance). We need minimum possible number of green points overall, given constraints.\n\nInterpretation: For each black point B, there exist exactly two greens G1, G2 such that distance(B, Gi) = 2020. Possibly other green points could also be at that distance? The condition says \"there are exactly two green points that are at a distance of 2020 from that black point.\" Means exactly two greens are at distance 2020; not more, not less. Could there be any green points at different distances? Yes.\n\nThus each black point must have degree exactly 2 in a bipartite graph where edges exist if distance =2020.\n\nGoal: minimize total green nodes given 2020 total nodes.\n\nWe can choose number of black points B and green G with B+G=2020, and arrangement of distances such that each black is adjacent to exactly two green nodes, and each green can be adjacent to any number of black nodes (including zero). No condition on greens.\n\nWe need to find configuration possible with minimal G. Equivalent to existence of hypergraph? Basically we need to assign each black two green neighbors; we can reuse same green for many blacks. So minimal G is to have as few greens as possible that can serve as distance-2020 points for many blacks, respecting geometric constraints: each black must be exactly distance 2020 from those two green points.\n\nSo we consider points positioned maybe on circle of radius 2020 from a given green point produces many black points at that distance. For a fixed green point G, the set of points at distance 2020 is circle centered at G radius 2020. We could place many black points on that circle. But each black must also have another green point at distance 2020. So each black must be intersection of two circles of radius 2020 centered at two distinct green points (maybe same) but note they must be distinct green points? Possibly the two green points could be same? \"two green points\" implies distinct.\n\nThus each black lies at intersection of two circles with radius 2020 centered at two greens. Intersection of two circles (with equal radii) is either 0,1 (if coincident) or 2 points (if distance between centers less than 2*2020 and greater than 0). So each black would be one of those two intersection points. So each pair of green points at distance d (0<d<2*2020) gives exactly two possible black locations.\n\nThus configuration: Choose set of green points. For each unordered pair of green points with distance < 2*2020 and not zero, we obtain two black points that are exactly distance 2020 from both. But we might not need all. Also black points could be placed multiple times from multiple pairs? A black could be intersection of many green pairs if more than two greens are at distance 2020 from it, violating condition (must be exactly two). So each black must be distance 2020 from exactly two greens, no more.\n\nThus we need to assign each black to a unique pair of greens such that no green is at distance 2020 from that black other than those two. That implies that for each black, its distance 2020 neighbors are exactly those two greens and no other green.\n\nSo we need to construct set of green points where for each unordered pair (Gi,Gj), we may place up to two black points at the intersection, but ensure that those black points are not also at distance 2020 to any other green. That would happen if there is a third green collinear? Actually if a third green is also at distance 2020 from that intersection point, that would mean the intersection point lies on circle radius 2020 around third green, which would imply its distance to third green also 2020. So to avoid, we must ensure that for each black (intersection of two circles), no third green lies also at distance 2020 away.\n\nThus we need to design geometry such that each distance-2020 circle centered at a green intersects circles of other greens only in pairs that are unique to each pair.\n\nSo the minimal number of green points such that we can produce 2020 - G black points each satisfying condition. Let B = 2020 - G.\n\nWe need B <= maximum number of black points realizable with G greens under constraints. Want G minimal such that maximum B >= 2020 - G.\n\nSo need to compute maximum possible B given G.\n\nAssume we can use each unordered pair of green points to generate exactly two black points (the intersection points), and ensure each such black is exclusive to that pair. Then maximum B = 2 * C(G,2) = G(G-1). Because each pair yields two black points.\n\nThus B_max = G(G-1). Then condition: we need B = 2020 - G ≤ G(G-1).\n\nWe want minimal G satisfying this inequality.\n\nSolve G(G-1) ≥ 2020 - G → G(G-1) + G - 2020 ≥0 → G^2 - G + G - 2020 ≥0 → G^2 - 2020 ≥0 → G ≥ sqrt(2020) ≈ 44.94. So G ≥45.\n\nAlso B must be integer; with G=45, G(G-1)=45*44=1980. Then B = 2020-45 = 1975. Since 1975 ≤ 1980, feasible likely. Need to check if we can realize 1975 black points with 45 greens meeting constraints (some pairs may not be used). Since total possible black max =1980, we can select subset of 1975. That seems plausible. Need also ensure that we can arrange 45 green points such that no three circles intersect at same point (i.e., no point is distance 2020 from three greens). With generic positions (e.g., place green points on a circle of radius R large enough such that distances between centers are not too special) we can avoid coincidences. So we can realize all distinct intersection points, each belonging uniquely to a pair.\n\nThus G=45 is possible. Can we have G=44? Then maximum B =44*43=1892. Required B =2020-44=1976. 1976 > 1892, impossible. So minimal G is 45.\n\nBut need to ensure no black lies at distance 2020 from exactly two greens but also possibly other greens at same distance accidentally due to geometry (collinear etc). Generic positions avoid that, so okay.\n\nThus answer 45.\n\nCheck edge cases: Need distance exactly 2020 between greens less than 2*2020 to get intersections. We can arrange green points all within a small region such that pairwise distances less than 2*2020 and >0. That's possible. For 45 points, we can place them randomly within a small disc radius maybe 1000. Then any pair distance <2000? Actually need < 2*2020=4040, which is large; easy. So each pair yields two intersections.\n\nBut need to ensure that intersection points (black) are distinct for different pairs. Could coincide if four points cyclic? Generic random positions ensure distinct.\n\nThus answer =45.\n\nProvide output.", "response": "ANSWER: 45", "answer": 45, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.5299468638404505e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999954700633964, "logp_correct": -4.5299468638404505e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6678821146082414, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1694477258479141, "frac_below_-5": 0.009061488673139158, "digit_lp_min": -0.9742307066917419, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.25192904472351074, "n_cot_tokens": 1545, "lp_end": -0.00010609064338495955, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.576278118089249e-07, "logp_nocot": -13.172892086207867, "p_nocot": 1.9014532681127511e-06, "uplift": 13.172887556261003}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "062ca0e0-bda9-4b0b-ab46-b0529207261f", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: For integers $0 \\le m,n \\le 2^{2017}-1$, let $\\alpha(m,n)$ be the number of nonnegative integers $k$ for which $\\left\\lfloor \\frac{m}{2^k} \\right\\rfloor$ and $\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2^k} \\right\\rfloor$ are both odd integers. Consider a $2^{2017} \\times 2^{2017}$ matrix $M$ whose $(i,j)$th entry (for $1 \\le i, j \\le 2^{2017}$) is $(-1)^{\\alpha(i-1, j-1)}$. For $1 \\le i, j \\le 2^{2017}$, let $M_{i,j}$ be the matrix with the same entries as $M$ except for the $(i,j)$th entry, denoted by $a_{i,j}$, and such that $\\det M_{i,j}=0$. Suppose that $A$ is the $2^{2017} \\times 2^{2017}$ matrix whose $(i,j)$th entry is $a_{i,j}$ for all $1 \\le i, j \\le 2^{2017}$. Compute the remainder when $\\det A$ is divided by $2017$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: For integers $0 \\le m,n \\le 2^{2017}-1$, let $\\alpha(m,n)$ be the number of nonnegative integers $k$ for which $\\left\\lfloor \\frac{m}{2^k} \\right\\rfloor$ and $\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2^k} \\right\\rfloor$ are both odd integers. Consider a $2^{2017} \\times 2^{2017}$ matrix $M$ whose $(i,j)$th entry (for $1 \\le i, j \\le 2^{2017}$) is $(-1)^{\\alpha(i-1, j-1)}$. For $1 \\le i, j \\le 2^{2017}$, let $M_{i,j}$ be the matrix with the same entries as $M$ except for the $(i,j)$th entry, denoted by $a_{i,j}$, and such that $\\det M_{i,j}=0$. Suppose that $A$ is the $2^{2017} \\times 2^{2017}$ matrix whose $(i,j)$th entry is $a_{i,j}$ for all $1 \\le i, j \\le 2^{2017}$. Compute the remainder when $\\det A$ is divided by $2017$.", "true_answer": 1382, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "062ca0e0-bda9-4b0b-ab46-b0529207261f:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 