edges possibly same ratio set; they will have r_e maybe same as r_f or reciprocal, condition requires r_e = -r_f or -r_f^{-1}. Since both r_e and r_f are from same set {2,6}, does -r_f belong to that set? For r_f=2, -2=9 which is not in {2,6}. So not conflict. Similarly for reciprocal 2^{-1}=6, -6=5 not in set. So indeed edges within same ratio set are compatible. Good.\n\nThus the only forbidden pairs are when one edge belongs to {2,6} ratio set and the other to {5,9} ratio set, or one belongs to {3,4} and other to {7,8}. Additionally edges with ratio set {10} (sum 11) are compatible with all.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: We have a set of 10 vertices and edges (unordered pairs). Each edge belongs to a type according to ratio set: Type A: ratio set {2,6}. Type B: {5,9}. Type C: {3,4}. Type D: {7,8}. Type E: {10} (sum 11). Edge pairs of types (A,B) are incompatible; (C,D) incompatible; all other combinations allowed.\n\nWe want maximum number of edges such that we avoid having any edge of type A together with any edge of type B, etc.\n\nThus we can choose to include edges from any subset of types as long as we don't include both types of a conflicting pair.\n\nGoal maximize total edges count.\n\nThus we need to know how many edges of each type exist among 10 vertices.\n\nCompute edges of type E: pairs (a,b) with a+b=11. Those are (1,10), (2,9), (3,8), (4,7), (5,6). That's 5 edges.\n\nNow need to compute edges of type A (ratio set {2,6}). Means a/b ≡ 2 or 6 mod 11. Since orientation matters but unordered edge just needs ratio either 2 or 6. Similarly B corresponds to ratio 5 or 9.\n\nWe can compute all unordered pairs and assign type.\n\nLet's enumerate vertices 1..10. For each unordered pair {a,b}, compute a*b^{-1} mod 11, and also b*a^{-1} (reciprocal). The set {r, r^{-1}} corresponds to ratio set.\n\nAlternatively, since unordered edge yields ratio set, we can determine which set based on the product a*b modulo 11 maybe? Because a/b ≡ r implies a ≡ r b mod 11 ⇒ a - r b ≡ 0. But not simple.\n\nMaybe compute directly.\n\nI'll write table quickly.\n\nLet’s compute multiplicative inverses mod 11:\n\n1→1\n2→6\n3→4\n4→3\n5→9\n6→2\n7→8\n8→7\n9→5\n10→10\n\nNow for unordered pair {a,b}, compute r = a * inv(b) mod 11 (a/b). Determine which unordered set: if r in {2,6} => type A, if r in {5,9} => type B, if r in {3,4} => type C, if r in {7,8} => type D, if r =10 => type E, but we have already accounted for E. Note that possible r could be other values like 1 (not possible) etc.\n\nLet's compute all pairs (a<b) from 1..10 and categorize.\n\nI'll create list.\n\nWe'll also note that if r is 10 (i.e., a + b =11), that's already accounted.\n\nLet's compute systematically.\n\nI'll make a table.\n\nPairs:\n\n1-2: a=1, b=2. Compute r = 1 * inv(2)=1*6=6. So r=6 which is in {2,6} => type A. Good.\n\n1-3: r = 1*inv(3)=1*4=4 => type C (since {3,4}).\n\n1-4: inv(4)=3 => r=3 => type C.\n\n1-5: inv(5)=9 => r=9 => type B (since {5,9}).\n\n1-6: inv(6)=2 => r=2 => type A.\n\n1-7: inv(7)=8 => r=8 => type D (since {7,8}).\n\n1-8: inv(8)=7 => r=7 => type D.\n\n1-9: inv(9)=5 => r=5 => type B.\n\n1-10: sum 11 => type E.\n\n2-3: inv(3)=4 => r = 2*4=8 => type D.\n\n2-4: inv(4)=3 => r = 2*3=6 => type A.\n\n2-5: inv(5)=9 => r=2*9=18≡7 => type D.\n\n2-6: inv(6)=2 => r=2*2=4 => type C.\n\n2-7: inv(7)=8 => r=2*8=16≡5 => type B.\n\n2-8: inv(8)=7 => r=2*7=14≡3 => type C.\n\n2-9: inv(9)=5 => r=2*5=10 => type E? Wait 10 corresponds to sum 11? Let's check: a=2,b=9 sum=11 yes pair (2,9) is type E (already). Indeed ratio -1. Since r=10. Good.\n\n2-10: inv(10)=10 => r=2*10=20≡9 => type B.\n\n3-4: inv(4)=3 => r=3*3=9 => type B. But also note sum 7 not 11.\n\n3-5: inv(5)=9 => r=3*9=27≡5 => type B.\n\n3-6: inv(6)=2 => r=3*2=6 => type A.\n\n3-7: inv(7)=8 => r=3*8=24≡2 => type A.\n\n3-8: inv(8)=7 => r=3*7=21≡10? 21-11=10 => type E? Check sum: 3+8=11 yes (3,8) sum 11, ratio -1, indeed type E. Good.\n\n3-9: inv(9)=5 => r=3*5=15≡4 => type C.\n\n3-10: inv(10)=10 => r=3*10=30≡8 => type D.\n\n4-5: inv(5)=9 => r=4*9=36≡3 => type C.\n\n4-6: inv(6)=2 => r=4*2=8 => type D.\n\n4-7: inv(7)=8 => r=4*8=32≡10? 32-22=10, yes type E (4+7=11)\n\n4-8: inv(8)=7 => r=4*7=28≡6 => type A.\n\n4-9: inv(9)=5 => r=4*5=20≡9 => type B.\n\n4-10: inv(10)=10 => r=4*10=40≡7 (since 33 is 0, 40-33=7) => type D.\n\n5-6: sum=11 => type E (also ratio -1). (Check r=5*inv6=5*2=10)\n\n5-7: inv(7)=8 => r=5*8=40≡7 => type D.\n\n5-8: inv(8)=7 => r=5*7=35≡2 => type A.\n\n5-9: sum=14 not 11. inv9=5 => r=5*5=25≡3 => type C? Wait r=3 is in {3,4} yes type C.\n\n5-10: inv10=10 => r=5*10=50≡6 (since 44 is 0, 50-44=6) => type A.\n\n6-7: inv7=8 => r=6*8=48≡4 (since 44 is 0, 48-44=4) => type C.\n\n6-8: inv8=7 => r=6*7=42≡9 (since 33 is 0, 42-33=9) => type B.\n\n6-9: sum=15 not 11. inv9=5 => r=6*5=30≡8 => type D.\n\n6-10: inv10=10 => r=6*10=60≡5 (since 55 is 0, 60-55=5) => type B.\n\n7-8: sum=15 not 11. inv8=7 => r=7*7=49≡5 (49-44=5) => type B? Wait 5 is in {5,9} yep type B. But also note ratio set maybe other orientation yields 8? but classification fine.\n\n7-9: inv9=5 => r=7*5=35≡2 => type A.\n\n7-10: inv10=10 => r=7*10=70≡4 (since 66 is 0, 70-66=4) => type C.\n\n8-9: inv9=5 => r=8*5=40≡7 => type D.\n\n8-10: inv10=10 => r=8*10=80≡3 (since 77 is 0, 80-77=3) => type C.\n\n9-10: inv10=10 => r=9*10=90≡2 (since 88 is 0, 90-88=2) => type A.\n\nNow let's count each type.\n\nWe'll tally.\n\nInitialize counters A,B,C,D,E:\n\nGo through list:\n\n1-2 A (+1)\n1-3 C (+1)\n1-4 C (+1)\n1-5 B (+1)\n1-6 A (+1)\n1-7 D (+1)\n1-8 D (+1)\n1-9 B (+1)\n1-10 E (+1)\n\n2-3 D (+1)\n2-4 A (+1)\n2-5 D (+1)\n2-6 C (+1)\n2-7 B (+1)\n2-8 C (+1)\n2-9 E (+1) (already counted)\n2-10 B (+1)\n\n3-4 B (+1)\n3-5 B (+1)\n3-6 A (+1)\n3-7 A (+1)\n3-8 E (+1)\n3-9 C (+1)\n3-10 D (+1)\n\n4-5 C (+1)\n4-6 D (+1)\n4-7 E (+1)\n4-8 A (+1)\n4-9 B (+1)\n4-10 D (+1)\n\n5-6 E (+1)\n5-7 D (+1)\n5-8 A (+1)\n5-9 C (+1)\n5-10 A (+1)\n\n6-7 C (+1)\n6-8 B (+1)\n6-9 D (+1)\n6-10 B (+1)\n\n7-8 B (+1)\n7-9 A (+1)\n7-10 C (+1)\n\n8-9 D (+1)\n8-10 C (+1)\n\n9-10 A (+1)\n\nNow sum each:\n\nLet's count A: from above entries:\n\n1-2,1-6,2-4,3-6,3-7,4-8,5-8,5-10,7-9,9-10 plus maybe others: Also 5-? Wait 5-? we have A also at 5-8 already counted, 5-10 counted, also 4-? we have 4-8 counted, also 3-6,3-7,2-4,1-2,1-6,7-9,9-10. Did we miss any A: from list \"2-?\": 2-4 A yes counted. \"3-6 A\" counted; \"3-7 A\" counted; \"4-8 A\" counted; \"5-8 A\" counted; \"5-10 A\"; \"7-9 A\"; \"9-10 A\". Also maybe \"6-?\": none; \"8-?\": none. Count total: Let's count manually:\n\n1) 1-2\n2) 1-6\n3) 2-4\n4) 3-6\n5) 3-7\n6) 4-8\n7) 5-8\n8) 5-10\n9) 7-9\n10) 9-10\n\nSo A = 10 edges.\n\nNow B count: entries:\n\n1-5,1-9,2-7,2-10,3-4,3-5,4-9,6-8,6-10,7-8,8-? Actually 8-? we have none, 6-8 B counted, 6-10 B, 7-8 B, also 4-? we have B at 4-9 counted, 5-? B? none, 8-? maybe 8-? Actually 8-? B not. Additionally 2-? we also have B at maybe 2-? yes 2-7 and 2-10 counted. Also 3-? we have B at 3-4 and 3-5 counted. 4-? we have B at 4-9. 5-? none. 6-? we have B at 6-8,6-10. 7-? B at 7-8. 8-? none. 9-? none. Total list:\n\n1)1-5\n2)1-9\n3)2-7\n4)2-10\n5)3-4\n6)3-5\n7)4-9\n8)6-8\n9)6-10\n10)7-8\n\nSo B = 10 edges.\n\nNow C count: entries:\n\n1-3,1-4,2-6,2-8,3-9,4-5,5-9,6-7,7-10,8-10,3-? also 4-5 already, plus maybe 2-6 counted, 2-8 counted, 6-7 counted, 3-9 counted, 5-9 counted, 8-10 counted, 7-10 counted, plus 4-5 counted, 1-3 and 1-4. Also 2-6 counted earlier.\n\nLet's list all C edges from the notes:\n\n1-3\n1-4\n2-6\n2-8\n3-9\n4-5\n5-9\n6-7\n7-10\n8-10\n3-? also 3-? we have 3-9 yes; 4-5 yes; also 5-? we have 5-9; 6-? we have 6-7; 7-? we have 7-10; 8-10; also 2-? we have 2-6,2-8; also 3-? perhaps 3-? else maybe 3-? none more.\n\nAlso 4-? maybe 4-5 counted; 5-? none else; 6-? also 6-7 done; 9-? maybe 9 with something? 9-? not.\n\nCount them: 1)1-3, 2)1-4, 3)2-6, 4)2-8, 5)3-9, 6)4-5, 7)5-9, 8)6-7, 9)7-10, 10)8-10. That's 10 edges. Also check if any C missing: 3-? maybe 3-? we have 3-? (3-?) where ratio 4 maybe: Actually 3-9 gave r=4, counted; 4-5 gives r=3, ok; 6-7 r=4; 5-9 r=3; 2-6 r=4; 2-8 r=3; 1-3 r=4; 1-4 r=3; 7-10 r=4; 8-10 r=3. So 10 edges.\n\nNow D count: entries:\n\n1-7,1-8,2-3,2-5,3-10,4-6,4-10,5-7,6-9,8-9, plus maybe 3-? also 3-10 counted, 5-? also 5-7 counted, 6-9 counted, 8-9 counted, also 2-3 counted, 2-5 counted, 4-6 counted, 4-10 counted, 1-7,1-8. Also 3-10 counted. Also 6-? we have 6-9 counted. That's list length: 1)1-7, 2)1-8, 3)2-3, 4)2-5, 5)3-10, 6)4-6, 7)4-10, 8)5-7, 9)6-9, 10)8-9. Also 6-? maybe 6-? also 6-? we have 6-9 counted. Also maybe 3-? 3-10 done. Also 5-? maybe 5-7 done. Also 7-? maybe 7-? we have 7-? 7-? but 7-? with 9 maybe? 7-9 is type A not D. 7-? 7-? 7-? else 7-8 is B. So D count =10.\n\nNow E count =5.\n\nCheck total edges: sum = A10 + B10 + C10 + D10 + E5 = 45 which matches total edges. Good.\n\nThus each type has 10 edges, except E =5.\n\nNow constraints: cannot have both A and B edges together. Similarly cannot have both C and D together. No other restrictions. So we can choose either A or B (or neither) and either C or D (or neither) plus E can always be included.\n\nTo maximize total edges, we should pick the larger between each conflicting pair; they are equal size (10 each). So we can choose either A or B and either C or D. Additionally E edges can be included always.\n\nThus maximum = 10 (from chosen between A/B) + 10 (chosen between C/D) + 5 (E) = 25.\n\nBut need to verify that mixing A with C may cause any hidden conflict? According earlier analysis, conflict only between A-B and C-D (since 10*R mapping). Since A (ratio set {2,6}) is not negation of C's set {3,4} (since 10*{2,6} = {5,9} = B). So A with C are fine.\n\nAlso need check if picking both A and C edges could cause conflict due to overlapping vertices? Not a rule: overlapping vertices does not affect condition (just sum checks). Could there be scenario where a pair of edges share a vertex and still cause product zero? Let's test: Suppose we have edge {a,b} of type A (ratio set {2,6}) and another edge {a,c} sharing a. Does condition (ax + by) with x=a? Wait but edges must be different - they could share a. The condition uses the elements a,b from first edge and x,y from second edge; there is no ordering about which is which; but they may share a vertex; the formulas (ax+by)(ay+bx). If edges share a vertex, say first edge {a,b}, second edge {a,c}. Then formula becomes (a*a + b*c)(a*c + b*a) = (a^2 + bc)(ac + ab). Could this be divisible by 11 even if types are compatible? Possibly? Need to verify.\n\nOur earlier derivation assumed general a,b,x,y distinct? Not required. The condition for conflict derived from existence of orientation such that a x + b y ≡0 mod11. For edges sharing a vertex, we need to check if that could happen even if ratio sets not related.\n\nTake edges {a,b} (type A) and {a,c} (type something). Compute ax + by = a*a + b*c ≡ 0? That's a^2 + bc ≡0. This is not of the form a x + b y = 0 previously derived as a/b = - y/x? That derivation assumed b and x are invertible, but they are nonzero anyway. The equation a x + b y ≡0 can be rearranged: a x ≡ - b y ⇒ a/b ≡ - y/x, provided b and x non-zero (true). So even with shared a, we can still define ratio a/b and y/x where y = c, x = a. The equation becomes a*a ≡ - b c ⇒ a/b ≡ - c/a. So condition becomes ratio a/b = - c/a. That's more restrictive. So possible? Let's evaluate with specific numbers to see if any such pair could cause zero product despite types not conflicting earlier.\n\nWe need to check if there exist edges e (a,b) of type A and f (a,c) of any type such that a/b = - c/a (mod11) i.e., a^2 ≡ - b c (mod11). Since a,b,c in 1..10, distinct maybe.\n\nThis condition might happen even if ratio sets are not negated reciprocals? Let's see.\n\nOur classification earlier used unordered ratio sets; we considered conflict if ratio set of e intersects negative of ratio set of f. But that considered orientation where the first element of each edge is the first element in equation ax+by, i.e., pairing a with x, b with y. For the case where edges share a vertex, we could orient second edge as (x,y) = (a,c) or (c,a). Both possibilities correspond to the previous condition: either a/b = - c/a (if we pick (x,y) = (a,c) giving ax + by = a*a + b*c), or a/b = - a/c (if orient other way? Let's compute: using (x,y) = (c,a), then ax + by = a*c + b*a = a(c+b). Not zero unless c+b ≡0 mod11 which is impossible (since sum <=20). So only orientation leading to potential zero is (x,y) = (a,c).\n\nThus condition reduces to a/b = - c/a.\n\nNow note that -c/a = -(c)*(a^{-1}) = -(c*a^{-1}) . This expression is of form - (c/a). So it's the negative of ratio c/a. So it matches condition r_e = - s_f with r_e = a/b and s_f = c/a (ratio of f when oriented as (c,a) i.e., numerator c denominator a). However the unordered ratio set of f includes both c/a and a/c (its reciprocal). So -s_f is either -c/a or -a/c. So conflict condition r_e = -s_f or r_e = -s_f^{-1}, which are exactly what we defined earlier. Indeed our previous classification considered unordered sets; for any two edges, we can choose orientation (i.e., which element of the second edge aligns with first's a or b). So the condition a/b = -c/a corresponds to r_e = - (c/a), which is -s_f where we pick orientation of f as (c,a). Since r_e belongs to its edge's set R_e, and -s_f belongs to -R_f = 10*R_f. Hence conflict arises exactly when R_e intersects 10*R_f, which we already captured. So even sharing vertices no new conflicts beyond those ratio sets. Good.\n\nThus classification stands.\n\nNow possible to include all edges of type A, all edges of type C, and all edges of type E (5) simultaneously? Need to verify that type A edges are compatible among themselves; we argued they are, because for any two edges both of type A, they have ratio set {2,6}. Could there be conflict between two type A edges? Condition would be r_e = - s_f? Since both r_e, s_f ∈ {2,6} . Their negatives are {9,5} which are not in {2,6}. So no conflict. Also r_e = -s_f^{-1}? s_f^{-1} is also in {2,6} (since reciprocal of 2 is 6, etc). Negative of that is {9,5}. Not in {2,6}. So fine.\n\nSimilarly C edges fine.\n\nThus maximum size 25.\n\nNow we must verify that we can indeed select all 10 edges of type A, all 10 of type C, and 5 sum-11 edges without any conflict.\n\nCheck possible conflict between A and C: R_A = {2,6}. 10*R_A = {9,5} = B set. R_C = {3,4}. Intersection with B? No. So fine.\n\nConflict between A and E? R_E's set is {10}. 10*R_E = {1}. Not intersect. So fine.\n\nConflict between C and E similar.\n\nThus we can take T = (all edges of types A, C, E) size 25. Indeed edges of type A list earlier: we have 10 edges. Type C also 10. Type E 5. That's 25.\n\nThus answer is 25.\n\nBut ensure that there is no other hidden conflict like between A edge and C edge where a x + b y = 0 possible with some orientation that mixes elements differently (not aligning the first element of first edge with first element of second). Our analysis considered arbitrary assignment because we can swap labeling within each edge. The conflict condition is symmetric: existence of labeling such that ax + by ≡0. That exactly corresponds to checking if ratio set R_e equals negative of ratio set of f (or its reciprocal). Which we already captured.\n\nThus maximum is 25.\n\nCheck if we can get higher by perhaps not using full type A or C but mixing some edges from B or D that increase count beyond 10+10+5? Since each type set size is 10, we cannot exceed 10 from each conflict class. If we choose A and B we can't have both. So we would pick at most 10 from that pair. Similarly for C/D. So maximum sum from those two pairs is 20. Add E =5 gives 25. No additional edges left.\n\nThus answer 25.\n\nEdge-case: Could we drop some edges of A and include some from B and D to get more than 10 + 10? But B edges conflict with A only, not with C/D/E. D edges conflict with C only, not with A/B/E. So possibility: Choose some from A and some from B such that no conflict? But any edge from A conflicts with any edge from B? Indeed because R_A intersects 10*R_B? Let's verify: 10*R_B = 10*{5,9} = {6,2} = R_A. So they conflict bidirectionally: For any edge e in A and any edge f in B, we have R_e = R_A, R_f = R_B, and 10*R_f = R_A, so intersection is whole set. Thus every pair A-B is conflicting. So can't have any coexist. So max among them is 10 either way.\n\nSimilarly C vs D all conflict each other. So choose one side max.\n\nThus optimum is choose side with larger cardinality (both equal). So 10+10+5=25.\n\nThus answer 25.\n\nNow produce final answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 25", "answer": 25, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.410737794557917e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999955892719328, "logp_correct": -4.410737794557917e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.49434012225253593, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3798265184160527, "frac_below_-5": 0.00999462654486835, "digit_lp_min": -4.683469772338867, "answer_first_mention_lp": -2.1457441107486375e-05, "n_cot_tokens": 9305, "lp_end": -9.536738616588991e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.0728830375228426e-06, "logp_nocot": -11.762291705003008, "p_nocot": 7.79294512266529e-06, "uplift": 11.762287294265214}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "175ea7da-7f59-446f-951c-973813382af9", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A set $S$ consisting of $2019$ different positive integers has the following property: the product of every $100$ elements of $S$ is a divisor of the product of the remaining $1919$ elements. What is the maximum number of prime numbers that $S$ can contain?\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A set $S$ consisting of $2019$ different positive integers has the following property: the product of every $100$ elements of $S$ is a divisor of the product of the remaining $1919$ elements. What is the maximum number of prime numbers that $S$ can contain?", "true_answer": 1820, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "175ea7da-7f59-446f-951c-973813382af9:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 4218, 4792, 25, 1416, 679, 920, 336, 328, 220, 667, 24, 16913, 8841, 54912, 13, 2214, 1062, 58845, 328, 220, 1353, 8632, 11, 290, 1888, 328, 2617, 220, 1353, 144028, 290, 1888, 328, 290, 15448, 220, 10037, 24, 8632, 13, 197203, 316, 25, 2214, 1062, 58845, 355, 328, 2349, 220, 1353, 11, 1888, 22305, 64, 18085, 230, 32, 92, 261, 1022, 1888, 22305, 65, 18085, 230, 50, 59, 32, 92, 287, 364, 2167, 1682, 10562, 4149, 3605, 328, 9197, 8663, 306, 336, 364, 2167, 1309, 316, 11433, 31060, 402, 9197, 12332, 67929, 1629, 364, 12845, 336, 314, 354, 64, 10853, 16255, 2214, 2454, 9197, 275, 11, 3331, 1617, 51159, 5251, 290, 6062, 920, 25, 1632, 3609, 51159, 457, 1359, 314, 4215, 22305, 72, 92, 323, 1359, 6271, 10853, 741, 8820, 395, 1062, 58845, 355, 2349, 220, 1353, 11, 4215, 22305, 72, 18085, 230, 32, 92, 323, 1359, 6271, 10853, 8, 72554, 4215, 22305, 72, 18085, 231, 32, 92, 323, 1359, 6271, 10853, 8, 314, 457, 1359, 533, 4215, 22305, 72, 18085, 230, 32, 92, 323, 1359, 6