9, 9, 23, 28, 1398, 364, 74, 28, 17, 25, 363, 7, 19, 11, 17, 25987, 21, 13, 135774, 2349, 28, 17, 871, 363, 7, 17, 11, 17, 25987, 16, 13, 261, 3605, 314, 220, 17, 42923, 21, 12, 16, 102838, 17, 61, 20, 28, 1398, 13, 2914, 314, 21, 9, 1398, 28, 8145, 364, 74, 28, 18, 25, 363, 7, 19, 11, 18, 25987, 19, 13, 135774, 2349, 28, 16, 871, 363, 7, 16, 11, 17, 25987, 15, 13, 261, 3605, 28, 17, 42923, 21, 12, 15, 102838, 17, 61, 21, 28, 2220, 13, 2914, 314, 19, 9, 2220, 28, 5780, 364, 74, 28, 19, 25, 363, 7, 19, 11, 19, 25987, 16, 13, 135774, 2349, 28, 15, 871, 363, 7, 15, 11, 17, 25987, 15, 13, 261, 3605, 28, 17, 42923, 21, 102838, 2220, 13, 2914, 314, 16, 9, 2220, 28, 2220, 364, 17434, 314, 220, 16, 659, 220, 1398, 659, 220, 8145, 659, 220, 5780, 659, 220, 2220, 314, 220, 38369, 364, 84787, 2086, 328, 41477, 395, 350, 64, 17568, 8, 1261, 274, 28, 16, 382, 220, 38369, 364, 46687, 12332, 328, 220, 17, 61, 20, 314, 1398, 395, 2240, 165377, 364, 84787, 3609, 483, 274, 28, 16, 25, 220, 38369, 425, 220, 1398, 314, 220, 20750, 1723, 364, 10620, 3609, 2086, 328, 4529, 336, 314, 220, 30413, 21, 659, 220, 20750, 1723, 314, 220, 14028, 5007, 364, 4383, 25, 1328, 1757, 413, 2086, 328, 165377, 336, 52718, 7060, 364, 58369, 14866, 93407, 4295, 25, 3609, 165377, 328, 398, 314, 220, 31467, 2636, 13, 2632, 1078, 220, 2040, 74, 35441, 7060, 11, 7890, 120152, 364, 10620, 581, 2804, 6757, 581, 23399, 20677, 1062, 7060, 1078, 10610, 44748, 306, 9707, 1001, 3577, 328, 336, 25, 581, 33556, 28991, 314, 16, 13, 5339, 43276, 30005, 28991, 625, 220, 16, 13, 41351, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 14028, 5007, 364, 58369, 4733, 364], "n_tokens": 7118, "words": 4154, "band_lo": 1776.5, "band_hi": 7106.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse problem: We have universe U = {1,2,3,4}. P = power set of U, i.e., all subsets of U (including empty set). So |P| = 2^4 = 16. We're looking at subsets S of P, i.e., collections of subsets of U. Condition: \"no two distinct integers a, b ∈ {1,2,3,4} appear together in exactly one element of S\". Means: For any unordered pair {a,b}, consider the number of subsets X ∈ S such that both a and b belong to X. The condition says that number cannot be exactly 1. It can be 0, 2, 3, ...? But max possible is? Since S can contain many subsets. For each pair, we must avoid that they appear together in exactly one member of S. So allowed counts are 0 or at least 2.\n\nThus we need to count number of collections S ⊆ P such that for each pair {a,b}, the count of subsets in S containing both a and b is not equal to 1.\n\nWe need total number of S satisfying this.\n\nThis is like we have 6 pairs (since 4 choose 2 =6). For each pair we must avoid count 1.\n\nThe elements of P are subsets. Let's list them by size: ∅, {1},{2},{3},{4} (size1), {12},{13},{14},{23},{24},{34} (size2), {123},{124},{134},{234} (size3), {1234} (size4). We'll denote each by bitmask.\n\nWe need to consider for each pair (i,j), the subsets containing both i and j are exactly those subsets that have at least those two elements. Which are: All subsets that contain the pair, i.e., subsets of size >=2 that include both. For each pair, the collection of subsets containing it is: the 2-element set {i,j} itself, plus any superset: adding any of the remaining 2 elements. So total number of subsets containing a given pair = 2^{4-2}=4 subsets: {i,j}, {i,j,k}, {i,j,l}, {i,j,k,l}. Indeed for each pair there are 4 subsets.\n\nSpecifically, for pair (1,2): subsets: {12}, {123}, {124}, {1234}. Similarly others.\n\nThus for each pair, within S we must not select exactly one of those four potential subsets. We can select 0,2,3,4 of them.\n\nBut the selections for different pairs are not independent because the same subset may contain multiple pairs simultaneously.\n\nWe need number of S such that for each of the 6 pairs, the count of selected subsets among its 4 associated subsets is not 1.\n\nThis is a combinatorial counting problem. Approach: treat each subset as variable (selected or not). There are 16 variables. Condition for each of 6 pairs is linear modulo? Not linear; it's condition that sum over associated 4 variables ≠ 1.\n\nWe can brute force conceptually maybe compute by inclusion-exclusion? Number of total subsets of P = 2^{16} = 65536. We need to subtract those where there exists at least one pair with exactly one occurrence. Use inclusion-exclusion over the 6 pairs. Since constraints are not symmetric due to overlapping.\n\nWe could attempt to compute using principle of counting via generating functions or using complementary counting via inclusion-exclusion length maybe manageable because each pair condition involves 4 variables.\n\nWe have 6 constraints: For each pair p, define event E_p = \"exactly one of the 4 subsets containing p is in S\". Want count of S with none of E_p.\n\nLet total N = 2^{16}. Want N0 = N - sum|E_p| + sum|E_p ∩ E_q| - ...\n\nWe can compute each term. Need to know intersections: For two pairs p and q, the subsets containing both pairs may overlap.\n\nFirst compute single event size:\n\nGiven a specific pair p (say {1,2}), we need exactly one of its 4 subsets selected, and the other 15 subsets arbitrary (except maybe constraints not considered). So number of S with exactly one of those 4 selected = choose which of the 4 is selected (4 ways) * 2^{16-4}=4 * 2^{12}=4*4096=16384. So |E_p| = 16384.\n\nThere are 6 pairs, so sum|E_p| = 6*16384 = 98304.\n\nNow need pairwise intersections: For two distinct pairs p and q, we need both conditions: each pair appears exactly once among subsets containing that pair. There are cases: pairs may be disjoint (no common elements) or share an element (overlap in one element). Since we have only 4 elements, pairs are either intersecting in one element (share one element) or are complementary/disjoint (e.g., {1,2} and {3,4}). Let's categorize.\n\nList all 6 pairs: 12,13,14,23,24,34.\n\nPairs share one element if they have one element in common: e.g., 12 and 13 share 1. Disjoint if they are {1,2} and {3,4} (only one such disjoint pair). Also {1,3} and {2,4} are disjoint? They share none (1,3 vs 2,4). Wait {1,3} and {2,4} also disjoint. So there are multiple disjoint pairs. Let's list all unordered pairs of pairs:\n\nWe could compute intersection sizes based on overlap.\n\nWe need for each pair of pairs (p,q) compute number of S where each of those two pairs appears in exactly one subset.\n\nIn those constraints, the subsets relevant are those containing p (4 subsets) and those containing q (4 subsets). Overlap between these sets of subsets depends.\n\nIf p and q are disjoint (no common element), then the subsets containing both p and q must contain all four distinct elements -> that's exactly the subset {1,2,3,4} (size 4). Since p and q are disjoint, each subset containing p is any superset containing that pair (two extra elements maybe). Similarly for q. The overlap of the two families is the set of subsets that contain both pairs, i.e., must contain all four elements. Thus the intersection is the singleton {1234}. So the two families intersect in 1 subset.\n\nIf they share one element (i.e., share exactly one integer), say p = {1,2}, q = {1,3}. Then subsets containing p: {12}, {123}, {124}, {1234}. Subsets containing q: {13}, {123}, {134}, {1234}. Intersection are subsets that contain both pairs which means contains {1,2,3} at least => subsets that contain {1,2,3}. The possible supersets: {123}, {1234}. So intersection size = 2.\n\nThus we need to compute counts based on these intersections.\n\nNow for given two pairs, we need to select exactly one subset from each of the respective 4-element families, with the condition that the selected subsets could be the same subset if that subset belongs to both families (i.e., intersection). However the condition \"exactly one of the subsets containing p is selected\" means among the 4 subsets belonging to p, exactly one is selected. Similarly for q. If the selected subset is in the intersection, that would count as the selected element for both conditions simultaneously, which would imply that exactly one subset from each family is selected, but it's the same subset selected for both. That would mean that the same subset is the unique one for each pair, which is permissible? Let's think: If we select subset X = {1,2,3,4}, that belongs to both families. Then for pair p, among its four subsets, we have selected X (so exactly one). Similarly for q, among its four subsets, we have also selected X (so exactly one). This satisfies both events simultaneously indeed. Also we could select different subsets (one from each family not overlapping). Or potentially select a subset from intersection as unique for both; then each family sees exactly one selected element (the same). That's allowed.\n\nThus counting intersect sizes requires careful enumeration.\n\nGeneral approach: For two pairs p,q, with families A (4 subsets for p) and B (4 subsets for q). We need to count selections of subsets (i.e., membership decisions for all 16 subsets) such that |S∩A|=1 and |S∩B|=1. This is similar to counting assignments where among the union A∪B we have certain selections, elsewhere arbitrary.\n\nLet k = |A∩B| (intersection size). For our cases, k = 2 if overlapping in one element, k =1 if disjoint, k = ? if identical pairs (not for distinct). Also if share both elements (same pair) not relevant.\n\nWe need to count number of ways to choose a subset of A∪B such that exactly one element from A is chosen, exactly one from B is chosen. This can be broken into cases:\n\nCase 1: The chosen element for A is also the chosen element for B, i.e., choose a single element from intersection (which counts as the unique for both). Then we must not choose any other element from A∪B. So select one element from intersection (k choices). Then all other elements in A∪B (size = |A| + |B| - k = 8 - k) are not selected. So number of selections for this case = k.\n\nCase 2: The chosen element for A is not in B, and chosen element for B is not in A. So choose one element from A\\B (size = 4 - k) and one element from B\\A (size = 4 - k), distinct. Then no other elements selected. Number of ways = (4 - k)*(4 - k).\n\nCase 3: The chosen element for A is from intersection, while for B we choose a different element from B\\A (or vice versa). But then B would have at least two elements selected (since intersection element also belongs to B). That would violate exactly one for B. So not allowed. Similarly cannot have A's unique from intersection and B's unique also from intersection but distinct – there is only one element in intersection? For k>1 there are multiple intersection elements, we could pick different intersection elements for each pair: pick x ∈ A∩B as A's unique; pick y ∈ A∩B, y ≠ x, as B's unique. Then A would have selected x (plus maybe y also is in A? Since y∈A∩B, it is also in A, that would make A have two selected elements) violating condition. So not allowed. Hence the only possible configurations are the two cases above.\n\nTherefore total number of ways to choose selection on A∪B satisfying both events: k + (4 - k)^2.\n\nNow outside A∪B (the remaining subsets of P) there are 16 - |A∪B| free choices (each can be either in S or not). |A∪B| = |A|+|B|-k = 8 - k. So remaining 16 - (8 - k) = 8 + k subsets free, each with 2 possibilities, giving factor 2^{8 + k}.\n\nThus |E_p ∩ E_q| = (k + (4 - k)^2) * 2^{8 + k}.\n\nNow compute for the two categories:\n\n- Overlapping pairs (share one element): k = 2. Then compute: k + (4 - k)^2 = 2 + (2)^2 = 2 +4 =6. 2^{8 + k} = 2^{10} = 1024. So product = 6 * 1024 = 6144.\n\n- Disjoint pairs: k = 1. Then compute: 1 + (3)^2 = 1 +9 =10. 2^{8+1}=2^{9}=512. Product = 10*512 = 5120.\n\nNow we need count of number of unordered pairs of pairs that are overlapping vs disjoint.\n\nWe have 6 pairs total. Let's list each pair of pairs.\n\nPairs list: 12,13,14,23,24,34.\n\nWe can compute number of unordered pairs of these 6 items = C(6,2)=15.\n\nNow compute how many are disjoint. Pairs are disjoint if they involve {1,2} vs {3,4}, {1,3} vs {2,4}, {1,4} vs {2,3}. That's three disjoint combinations. Indeed there are exactly 3 ways to partition 4 elements into two unordered pairs, each giving a disjoint pair of subsets. So disjoint count =3.\n\nRemaining 15 -3 =12 overlapping.\n\nThus sum over all unordered pairs of |E_p ∩ E_q| = 12 * 6144 + 3 * 5120 = 12*6144 = 73728; 3*5120 = 15360. Sum = 89088.\n\nNow for inclusion-exclusion we need sum over ordered pairs? Inclusion-exclusion uses sum over all distinct pairs p<q of intersection cardinalities; that's what we computed.\n\nNow need triple intersections: |E_p ∩ E_q ∩ E_r| for three distinct pairs p,q,r. Need to categorize based on their relationships.\n\nWe need to consider sets of three pairs from the six. Cases depend on union/intersections of families. This is getting complex but possibly doable.\n\nAlternative approach: Use computational enumeration by reasoning? Could maybe think in terms of each subset of U contributes to certain pairs; our condition prohibits that any pair appears exactly once across selected subsets. Equivalent to: For each pair, the parity (?) Not parity but count cannot be 1.\n\nWe could treat each subset as a vector of 6 pair appearances (binary: whether the subset contains that pair). For each subset of U, we can compute its 6-bit pattern of which pairs are present. For each selected collection S, the sum (over selected subsets) of these vectors yields a vector of counts per pair (0 to any). Condition: each component ≠ 1.\n\nThus we count number of subsets of the 16 vectors (allowing repeats? No each subset can be selected at most once) such that the componentwise sum avoids value 1.\n\nWe could treat this as counting number of subsets of a multiset? Might be tackled by generating function using inclusion-exclusion over each coordinate restrictions as we attempted.\n\nSince each coordinate constraint is about sum not equal to 1. Use principle of counting number of subsets where for each coordinate, sum ∈ {0} ∪ {≥2}. Could use inclusion–exclusion on each coordinate to exclude sum=1 cases, which we already started.\n\nWe now need to compute inclusion-exclusion up to 6.\n\nGeneral term: For a set T of t pairs, we need number of S where for each pair in T, exactly one of its 4 subsets is selected. Also no restriction on other pairs not in T (they can have any counts). However, when multiple pairs are in T, the constraints may be contradictory if they force more than one selection from overlapping families? But our calculations above for two gave number.\n\nFor three pairs, we need to count selections where each of the three families has exactly one selected, respecting overlapping subsets perhaps selected multiple times? But a subset can be selected at most once (it's either in S or not). So if a subset lies in the intersection of multiple families, selecting it would count as the unique for each of those families simultaneously, which may lead to each needed exactly one. For three families A, B, C, we need to count subsets of their union where each of A, B, C have exactly one selected. This is essentially counting matchings in hypergraph? We can attempt using inclusion-exclusion again but might be many cases.\n\nGiven only 4 elements may keep small sets.\n\nMaybe easier to brute compute using logic manually? Not feasible.\n\nPerhaps there is a smarter combinatorial approach: Since each pair appears in exactly 4 subsets, the problem might be equivalent to: Choose any collection S; for each pair, the count is either 0,2,3,4,5,... but maximum possible counts? Since each pair appears in those 4 specific subsets only; no other subsets involve that pair. Because any subset that doesn't contain both elements will not count. Therefore the count per pair is exactly the number of selected subsets among the 4 that contain that pair. Thus the count per pair is limited between 0 and 4. So condition: not equal to 1; allowed 0,2,3,4.\n\nThus each pair's count is from {0,2,3,4}. So we just need to pick a subset of those 4-set families such that for each pair the number selected is not 1.\n\nNow each subset (of U) belongs to multiple pairs. Let's examine each subset's pair incidence vector.\n\nLet's list each subset and the pairs it contains:\n\n- ∅ : none (0 pairs)\n- Singletons: {1} no pair (since need 2 elements). So all size 1 subsets have 0 pairs.\n- Size 2 subsets: each corresponds exactly to one pair. Eg {12} contains pair 12 only. So each size2 subset contributes to exactly 1 pair.\n- Size 3 subsets: contain 3 choose 2 =3 pairs. Eg {123} contains pairs 12,13,23.\n- Size 4 subset {1234} contains all 6 pairs.\n\nThus the vector representation: size2 subsets have weight1; size3 weight3; size4 weight6.\n\nNow we need to count subsets S of these 16 subsets such that for each pair the total count (sum over selected subsets containing that pair) ≠1.\n\nObservation: The size0 and size1 subsets never affect counts; they are free—they can be either included or not independently. There are 1 + 4 =5 such subsets (empty set and four singletons). They don't affect any pair constraints, so each can be independently chosen: factor 2^5 =32 multiplicative factor to final count.\n\nThus we can restrict consideration to the remaining 11 subsets: the six 2-element subsets, four 3-element subsets, and the full set (size4). These affect counts.\n\nNow we need to count selections among those 11 subsets under constraints.\n\nLet's rename variables:\n\nLet a_{ij} for each size2 subset {i,j} (i<j). There are 6 such variables (binary inclusion).\n\nLet b_{ijk} for each size3 subset (4 of them). There are 4 variables.\n\nLet c for the size4 subset.\n\nConstraint for each pair p = (i,j): Count = a_{ij} + sum over b_{ijk} where {i,j} ⊂ {i,j,k} + c. For each pair, there are three size3 subsets containing that pair? Wait each pair appears in exactly two size3 subsets? Check: Pair 12 is contained in subsets {1,2,3} and {1,2,4}. That's exactly two 3-element supersets (adding each of the remaining two elements). So there are exactly 2 size3 subsets containing a given pair. Plus the size2 subset itself, plus the size4 subset.\n\nThus count_{ij} = a_{ij} + b_{ijx} + b_{ijy} + c, where x,y are the other two elements.\n\nThus count = a_{ij} + b_{ijx} + b_{ijy} + c. So sum of up to 4 binary variables.\n\nCondition: this sum ≠ 1.\n\nThus for each pair we have a 4-variable sum not equal to 1. This is nice because the variables involved are specific and the constraints share variables.\n\nNow we can perhaps solve via case analysis on c (the full set). Since c contributes to all pairs simultaneously.\n\nConsider two cases: c = 0 or c = 1.\n\nCase 1: c = 0.\n\nThen each pair count = a_{ij} + b_{ijx} + b_{ijy}. Must not equal 1.\n\nThus possible values for each triple (a_{ij}, two associated b variables) cannot sum to 1. That means either 0,2,3.\n\nNow note that each b variable appears in 3 different pairs (since a 3-element subset consists of three pairs). Let's define variables for the four triples: b_{123}, b_{124}, b_{134}, b_{234}.\n\nb_{123} appears in pairs 12,13,23.\n\nSimilarly others.\n\nNow constraints:\n\nFor pair 12: a12 + b123 + b124 ≠ 1.\n\nPair 13: a13 + b123 + b134 ≠ 1.\n\nPair 14: a14 + b124 + b134 ≠ 1.\n\nPair 23: a23 + b123 + b234 ≠ 1.\n\nPair 24: a24 + b124 + b234 ≠ 1.\n\nPair 34: a34 + b134 + b234 ≠ 1.\n\nWe need to count assignments of binary variables a_{ij} (6) and b_{ijk} (4) satisfying all six constraints.\n\nCase 2: c = 1.\n\nThen each pair count = a_{ij} + b_{ijx} + b_{ijy} + 1. Not equal to 1 => sum must be not equal to 1. Since we already have +1, the sum of the other three must not be 0 (because 0+1 =1). In other words, a_{ij}+b_{ijx}+b_{ijy} ≠ 0. So must be 1,2,3. But note total with c=1 could go up to 4.\n\nThus constraint for each pair when c=1: a_{ij}+b_{ijx}+b_{ijy} ≠ 0.\n\nSo the three-variable sum cannot be 0; can be 1,2,3.\n\nThus basically for each pair at least one of the three variables must be 1.\n\nNow we can attempt to count assignments for each case.\n\nSince we have relatively small number of variables (10) we could brute force by reasoning or counting systematically.\n\nCase 1 (c=0): For each of six equations, the sum of three bits cannot be 1. That forbids patterns where exactly one of those three bits is 1.\n\nWe can try to use inclusion-exclusion again on these constraints? Could be easier to brute compute via enumeration logically. Maybe we can reason about possible assignments for b variables first then determine compatible a's.\n\nLet’s denote the vector of b variables: let x = b123, y = b124, z = b134, w = b234. Each is 0/1.\n\nNow for a given pair, its constraint involves a specific a plus two of these b's.\n\nFor pair 12: a12 + x + y ≠ 1.\n\nSimilarly.\n\nGiven values of x,y,z,w, we need to count assignments of the six a_ij satisfying each constraint.\n\nFor each pair p, the constraint on a_{ij} is: a_{ij} ≠ 1 - (sum of its two b's) (mod??) Not mod; it's about not equal to 1 when adding.\n\nSpecifically, let s = x+y for pair 12. Then allowed values for a12 are any binary such that a12 + s ≠ 1. Since a12 ∈ {0,1}, we can determine possibilities:\n\nIf s = 0: then a12 + 0 ≠ 1 => a12 cannot be 1. So a12 must be 0 (only 1 option).\n\nIf s = 1: then a12 + 1 ≠ 1 => a12 cannot be 0 (since 0+1=1). So a12 must be 1 (only 1 option).\n\nIf s = 2: then a12 + 2 ≠ 1 (obviously, since minimum sum is 2). Both a12 =0 or 1 are allowed (since 0+2=2, 1+2=3). So 2 options.\n\nThus for each pair, given the sum of its two associated b variables (which can be 0,1,2), the a variable is forced (if sum 0 or1) or free (if sum 2). So we can compute total number of a assignments as product over pairs of allowed counts.\n\nNow we need to compute for each assignment of (x,y,z,w) the sums for each of the six pairs:\n\nLet's list pairs with their associated b's:\n\n12: x+y\n13: x+z\n14: y+z\n23: x+w\n24: y+w\n34: z+w\n\nThus s12 = x+y, s13 = x+z, s14 = y+z, s23 = x+w, s24 = y+w, s34 = z+w.\n\nWe need to compute total number of a assignments: For each pair, count = f(s) where f(0)=1 (a forced 0), f(1)=1 (a forced 1), f(2)=2.\n\nThus total a count = 2^{ #pairs where s = 2 }.\n\nBecause when s =2, two choices; when s =0 or1, exactly 1.\n\nThus we need to find, for each (x,y,z,w) ∈ {0,1}^4, how many of the six sums equal 2. Because each sum being 2 occurs when both b's are 1.\n\nSince each sum is sum of two of the variables. So s =2 iff both involved bits are 1.\n\nThus we can define edges of tetrahedron: variables correspond to vertices, pairs correspond to edges between two vertices (since each sum is sum of two variables). Indeed each pair is edge.\n\nThus count of edges where both endpoints are 1 (i.e., edges induced by the set of vertices with value 1). If we set the set of vertices V1 = {variables that are 1}, then number of edges among them is C(k,2) where k = number of 1's among x,y,z,w.\n\nBecause each edge corresponds to a pair of variables. So s=2 exactly for each edge whose both endpoints are 1.\n\nThus for a given k = number of b's set to 1, the number of edges with sum=2 is C(k,2). The other edges have sums 0 (if both 0) or1 (if exactly one 1). Indeed.\n\nThus total number of a assignments = 2^{C(k,2)}.\n\nNow total number of assignments for case c=0 is sum over k=0..4 of (number of b assignments with exactly k ones) * 2^{C(k,2)}.\n\nNumber of b assignments with exactly k ones = C(4,k).\n\nThus case c=0 count = Σ_{k=0}^{4} C(4,k) * 2^{C(k,2)}.\n\nCompute:\n\nk=0: C(4,0)=1, C(0,2)=0 => 2^0=1. term=1.\nk=1: C(4,1)=4, C(1,2)=0 => 2^0=1. term=4.\nk=2: C(4,2)=6, C(2,2)=1 => 2^1=2. term=6*2=12.\nk=3: C(4,3)=4, C(3,2)=3 => 2^3=8. term=4*8=32.\nk=4: C(4,4)=1, C(4,2)=6 => 2^6=64. term=64.\n\nSum = 1+4+12+32+64 = 113.\n\nThus number of assignments for variables (a,b) when c=0 is 113.\n\nRecall we also have 5 free subsets (∅ and singletons) that can be chosen arbitrarily, factor 2^5 =32. Hence total number of S with c=0 is 113 * 32 = 3616.\n\nNow case 2: c = 1.\n\nNow constraints: for each pair, we require a_{ij}+ sum of its two b's ≥1 (i.e., not zero). Equivalent: the triple of bits cannot be all zero. So for each edge, at least one of the three variables (a for that edge, and the two incident b vertices) is 1.\n\nWe need to count assignments of a_{ij} (6), b variables (4) such that for each edge (pair) (i,j), a_{ij} or x_i or x_j (where we denote x corresponding to b variable of triple containing the edge) is 1. Note mapping: each b variable corresponds to a triangle of three edges; but we can think as earlier representation.\n\nSpecifically constraints: For each pair p, we have a_p + b_{p with third element 1} + b_{p with third element 2} >= 1.\n\nThus for each edge, at least one of its adjacent a variable or one of the two incident b-variables is 1.\n\nWe need to count total assignments.\n\nWe can approach by complement: Count assignments where there exists at least one edge where all three are 0 (i.e., violation). Use inclusion-exclusion over edges. But with 6 edges maybe doable.\n\nBut maybe easier: Enumerate over b assignments (x,y,z,w) and count a assignments that satisfy for each edge that at least one of a_{edge} or the incident b's is 1.\n\nGiven fixed b values, for each edge we can determine whether the condition is automatically satisfied (if at least one of the two incident b's =1). If yes, then a_edge can be 0 or 1 (both allowed). If both incident b's are 0, then we require a_edge =1 to satisfy at least one of three. So a_edge forced to 1.\n\nThus for each edge, if s = sum of its two incident b's =0, then a must be 1. If s≥1, a can be 0 or 1 (2 options).\n\nThus total number of a assignments = 2^{#edges where s≥1}. Because for edges with s=0 forced to 1 (only one choice). Note: even if s=2, still a can be 0/1.\n\nThus count = 2^{6 - m}, where m = number of edges with s=0 (i.e., both incident b zeros). Equivalent: edges whose both incident vertices are 0.\n\nGiven the set of b vertices with value 1 (size k), edges with both endpoints 0 are edges among the complement set of size (4 - k). Number of such edges = C(4 - k, 2). So m = C(4 - k, 2). Then number of a assignments = 2^{6 - C(4 - k, 2)}.\n\nNow total assignments for case c=1 is sum over k of number of b assignments with k ones (C(4,k)) times 2^{6 - C(4 - k, 2)}.\n\nCompute for each k:\n\nk=0: C(4,0)=1. Then complement size =4 => C(4,2)=6 => m=6. Then a count = 2^{6-6}=2^0=1. term =1*1=1.\n\nk=1: C(4,1)=4. Complement size=3 => C(3,2)=3. a count = 2^{6-3}=2^3=8. term =4*8=32.\n\nk=2: C(4,2)=6. Complement size=2 => C(2,2)=1. a count = 2^{6-1}=2^5=32. term =6*32=192.\n\nk=3: C(4,3)=4. Complement size=1 => C(1,2)=0. a count=2^{6-0}=2^6=64. term =4*64=256.\n\nk=4: C(4,4)=1. Complement size=0 => C(0,2)=0. a count=2^{6}=64. term =1*64=64.\n\nSum = 1 + 32 + 192 + 256 + 64 = 545.\n\nThus number of assignments for (a,b) when c=1 is 545.\n\nAgain factor of 2^5 =32 for free subsets.\n\nThus total with c=1: 545 * 32 = 17440.\n\nNow total number of valid S = 3616 + 17440 = 21056.\n\nCheck: This should be number of subsets S satisfying condition.\n\nLet's verify plausibility: total subsets of P = 65536. So about 21k satisfy condition, seems plausible.\n\nNow we must ensure we haven't missed any condition about pair appearing in exactly one element of S: we addressed counts =1. Our counting ensures counts not 1. Indeed.\n\nThus answer: 21056.\n\nLet's output.", "response": "ANSWER: 21056", "answer": 21056, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.0728832364748087e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999989271173391, "logp_correct": -1.0728832364748087e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5081635396116845, "cot_lp_min64": -1.447574379033508, "frac_below_-5": 0.010418133183162044, "digit_lp_min": -6.002477169036865, "answer_first_mention_lp": -5.483612312673358e-06, "n_cot_tokens": 7103, "lp_end": -0.002885822905227542, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -24.127614641794935, "p_nocot": 3.322845124451374e-11, "uplift": 24.1276135689117}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "2c5f3b85-8eca-4fe3-82fe-c96318babd13", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: In a $25 \\times n$ grid, each square is colored with a color chosen among $8$ different colors. Let $n$ be as minimal as possible such that, independently from the coloration used, it is always possible to select $4$ columns and $4$ rows such that the $16$ squares of the intersections are all of the same color. Find the remainder when $n$ is divided by $1000$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: In a $25 \\times n$ grid, each square is colored with a color chosen among $8$ different colors. Let $n$ be as minimal as possible such that, independently from the coloration used, it is always possible to select $4$ columns and $4$ rows such that the $16$ squares of the intersections are all of the same color. Find the remainder when $n$ is divided by $1000$.", "true_answer": 601, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "2c5f3b85-8eca-4fe3-82fe-c96318babd13:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 19957, 297, 2238, 484, 1062, 50205, 328, 220, 1161, 1215, 297, 10586, 483, 220, 23, 10803, 49460, 261, 141795, 190565, 220, 19, 87, 19, 1543, 7886, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 220, 19, 13585, 326, 220, 19, 15697, 64353, 289, 8, 1127, 625, 23517, 130997, 30, 3004, 23292, 328, 836, 12143, 536, 11, 1327, 5655, 1062, 220, 19, 13585, 326, 1062, 220, 19, 15697, 13, 2632, 581, 1309, 261, 25307, 68745, 177750, 69198, 316, 137209, 395, 54467, 497, 651, 22725, 7978, 25, 581, 679, 220, 1161, 13585, 11, 297, 15697, 13, 1416, 1309, 16218, 328, 261, 658, 22305, 19, 11, 19, 92, 350, 26232, 54467, 497, 651, 8, 12119, 22569, 11095, 316, 57269, 722, 2684, 3089, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 2454, 42002, 13749, 2684, 3089, 741, 197203, 316, 25, 102060, 2454, 5225, 27211, 10610, 483, 261, 30