elatively prime. After taking nth derivative, we can express as sum over partial fractions? Maybe we can relate via expansions near each root. Since q = x(x-1)(x+1). Distinct linear factors. The behavior near each root can be expressed as series with terms like polynomial over (x-a)^{k}. High order derivative will produce high order poles at each root. The multiplicities of denominator at each root will be n+1 each individually. Indeed denominator power (x-a)^{n+1} after n derivatives. The numerator will be some combination of terms that may have factor (x-a)^{something}.\n\nGiven initial p is not divisible by any of these linear factors (i.e., not zero at a=-1,0,1). So p(a) ≠ 0 for a = -1,0,1? Wait \"no nonconstant factor in common with x^3 - x\" means does not share any of the linear factors (x), (x-1), (x+1). So p(0)=p(1)=p(-1) ≠ 0.\n\nThus near each root a, the original rational function behaves like constant / (x-a) times something (since denominator linear factor). The derivative order increases the pole order. After N derivatives, we have a pole of order N+1 at each a, with residue? The numerator will be like some polynomial whose value at a may be zero to some multiplicity.\n\nSpecifically, near x=0, write p(x) ≈ p(0) + p'(0) x + ... . Then p(x)/x behaves like p(0)/x + regular terms. The (1992)th derivative will be something like (-1)^{1992} * 1992! * p(0) / x^{1993} + lower order poles and regular parts. So the leading term will have coefficient non-zero (since p(0) ≠ 0). So the numerator after taking 1992th derivative, expressed over (x)^1993 times factors (x-1)^{1993} (x+1)^{1993} overall denominator product? Wait denominator is (x(x-1)(x+1))^{1993}. The term near x=0: denominator factor x^{1993} times (x-1)^{1993}(x+1)^{1993}. The numerator may have factor (x-1)^{1993}(x+1)^{1993} times something like constant*1992! * p(0) where also maybe poly of x? Actually to produce a pole of order 1993 at x=0, the numerator may be divisible by (x-1)^{1993}(x+1)^{199