1+1/x)^{1993}= x^{3986} (1+1993/x+...). The third term similarly.\n\nThus leading term coefficient for each is A *1 (since (x-1)^{1993}(x+1)^{1993} leading term coefficient 1); B also 1; C also 1. So leading coefficient of f (degree 3986) is (1992)! * (A + B + C). So we can make leading term vanish by selecting A+B+C=0. That would drop degree possibly to 3985? Let's check next term.\n\nThus we can reduce degree by arranging cancellations.\n\nGoal: minimize degree of f. Since we can also set one coefficient zero, maybe further reduce.\n\nLet's consider using three variables to cancel high-degree terms up to some order. Since each term is a polynomial of degree 3986, we can treat the space spanned by these three polynomials. Their linear combinations can produce polynomials of degree less than 3986 possibly as low as? The dimension of span is at most 3. So we can maybe cancel the top 2 leading coefficients (degrees 3986, 3985). But can we cancel more than that? Probably not, because we have only 3 parameters. Let's compute the asymptotic expansion of each term up to a few orders to see linear independence.\n\nLet’s denote:\n\nT1 = (x-1)^{1993}(x+1)^{1993} = (x^2 -1)^{1993}\nT2 = x^{1993}(x+1)^{1993} = x^{1993}(x+1)^{1993}\nT3 = x^{1993}(x-1)^{1993}\n\nWe need linear combination L = A T1 + B T2 + C T3.\n\nWe can treat as polynomial in x. Want to choose A,B,C not all zero to minimize degree of L. Equivalent to find minimal possible degree of nonzero linear combination of these three polynomials.\n\nThe degree of each is 3986. The leading coefficient for each (x^{3986}) is coefficient 1. So the sum of coefficients times A,B,C gives leading coefficient. To cancel degree 3986 term, impose A + B + C =0.\n\nNext, degree 3985 term might be coefficient from each polynomial: need compute coefficient of x^{3985} in each.\n\nCompute expansions.\n\nLet’s define n = 1993.\n\nT1 = (x^2 -1)^n = sum_{k=0}^n binom(n,k) x^{2k} (-1)^{n-k}. Highest power when k=n => x^{2n}=x^{3986}. Next term k=n-1 => binom(n, n-1) x^{2n-2} (-1)^{1} = n * (-1) x^{3984}. So there is no x^{3985} term because powers are even only. So T1 has only even-degree terms (since even function). So coefficient of x^{3985} is 0.\n\nT2 = x^n (x+1)^n = x^n * sum_{k=0}^n binom(n,k) x^{k} 1^{n-k} = sum_{k=0}^n binom(n,k) x^{n+k}. Degrees from n to 2n.\n\nSimilarly T3 = x^n (x-1)^n = x^n * sum_{k=0}^n binom(n,k) x^{k} (-1)^{n-k} = sum_{k=0}^n binom(n,k) (-1)^{n-k} x^{n+k}.\n\nThus T2 and T3 contain both odd and even powers, depending on n (1993 is odd). The highest degree term corresponds to k=n giving x^{2n}=x^{3986} with coefficient 1 for both T2,T3.\n\nNext degree term 3985 corresponds to k=n-1 => coefficient binom(n, n-1) = n times x^{n + n -1}= x^{2n-1}=x^{3985}. For T2, coefficient = n. For T3, coefficient = binom(n, n-1) * (-1)^{n-(n-1)} = n * (-1)^1 = -n (since n is odd, but sign just -). So coefficient for T3 at x^{3985} is -n.\n\nThus T2 coeff = n, T3 coeff = -n, T1 coefficient = 0.\n\nTherefore linear combination L: coefficient of x^{3985} is A*0 + B*n + C*(-n) = n (B - C). To cancel x^{3985} term, impose B - C =0 => B = C.\n\nNow we have two equations: A + B + C =0 and B = C.\n\nThus substitute C = B, then A + 2B =0 => A = -2B.\n\nThus with parameters up to scaling, we have a nontrivial combination achieving cancellation of top two degrees (3986 and 3985). This yields degree at most 3984 (next term). Let's verify the coefficient of x^{3984} maybe non-zero generically.\n\nThus minimal possible degree may be 3984.\n\nBut maybe we can also cancel x^{3984} term using same three parameters? Let's compute coefficients of x^{3984} for each polynomial.\n\nWe need coefficient for each.\n\nFor T1: coefficient of x^{3984} corresponds to k=n-1 term: binom(n,n-1) x^{2n-2} (-1)^{1} = - n x^{3984}. So coefficient = -n.\n\nFor T2: we need contributions from terms where n+k = 3984 => k = 3984 - n = 3984-1993=1991. Since n =1993, so k =1991. That's two less than n (since n -2). So coefficient = binom(n, n-2) = binom(1993,1991) = binom(1993,2) = n(n-1)/2 = 1993*1992/2 = 1993*996 =? 1993*1000=1,993,000 minus 1993*4=7,972 => 1,985,028. Let's compute exact later. Also there could be contributions from expansion of (x+1)^n?? Wait T2 = x^n (x+1)^n, expansion x^{n} * sum_{k=0}^{n} binom(n,k) x^{k} = sum binom(n,k) x^{n+k}. So coefficient for degree n + k = 3984 => k =3984-n =1991. So that's the only term; coefficient = binom(n,1991) = binom(n,2) = n(n-1)/2 = C2. Sign positive as (x+1)^n has all positive signs.\n\nThus T2 coefficient = binom(n,2) = n(n-1)/2.\n\nFor T3: coefficient for degree 3984 likewise k =1991, coefficient = binom(n,1991) * (-1)^{n -1991} = binom(n,2) * (-1)^{2} = + binom(n,2) (since n - (n-2) =2, even). So same sign positive. So T3 coefficient also + binom(n,2).\n\nThus coefficients:\n\nT1: -n\nT2: + (n choose 2)\nT3: + (n choose 2)\n\nNow L coefficient at degree 3984: = A*(-n) + B*(n choose 2) + C*(n choose 2).\n\nGiven constraints A = -2B, C = B, we substitute:\n\nCoeff = (-2B)*(-n) + B*(nC2) + B*(nC2) = 2Bn + 2B * (nC2) = 2B [ n + (nC2) ].\n\nHence unless B=0, coefficient non-zero (since n + nC2 >0). Therefore degree reduces to 3984 (since leading term canceled up to 3985). So minimal degree maybe 3984.\n\nBut could we achieve degree lower than 3984 by choosing different combination not obeying earlier constraints? We have only three parameters, we can cancel at most two highest-degree coefficients. The degree-3984 term is linearly independent of previous constraints given the coefficients above; we cannot set it zero unless B=0, which would imply A = 0, and then C =0, leading to zero polynomial. So minimum degree is 3984.\n\nThus degree of numerator f after cancellation (gcd may be only constant) is 3984.\n\nBut need to consider that we also have a factorial factor (1992)! which does not affect degree.\n\nThus answer: 3984.\n\nCheck that degree of p originally <1992 works with this selection? Our p is quadratic giving coefficients A,B,C via partial fractions. We need to find a p (degree ≤2) whose partial fraction constants A,B,C satisfy A = -2B, C = B, with B ≠0. Existence? Since mapping from p to (A,B,C) is linear and invertible (since dimension 3). So we can find such p. Also need p not divisible by any linear factor – fine.\n\nThus minimal possible degree of f(x) is 3984.\n\nBut note that total degree of numerator before simplification is 3986. After canceling we could possibly factor out x? Wait we didn't cancel any common factor, but we have reduced degree due to cancellation between terms, not due to common factor. So f degree 3984.\n\nThus answer.\n\nLet's produce final.", "response": "ANSWER: 3984", "answer": 3984, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.7418113859312143e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999972581923728, "logp_correct": -2.7418113859312143e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5981244534262617, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4143875506944603, "frac_below_-5": 0.012505719078847033, "digit_lp_min": -5.504842281341553, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.005251855945971329, "n_cot_tokens": 6557, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.0490362910786644e-05, "logp_nocot": -15.804726777947508, "p_nocot": 1.3680260658610263e-07, "uplift": 15.804724036136122}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "4bcea3e5-789b-4505-9381-88ce0c3a20cf", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Let $k$ be a positive integer. A sequence $a_0, a_1, \\ldots, a_n$, where $n > 0$, of positive integers satisfies the following conditions:\n\n1. $a_0 = a_n = 1$;\n2. $2 \\leq a_i \\leq k$ for each $i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n-1$;\n3. For each $j = 2, 3, \\ldots, k$, the number $j$ appears $\\phi(j)$ times in the sequence $a_0, a_1, \\ldots, a_n$, where $\\phi(j)$ is the number of positive integers that do not exceed $j$ and are coprime to $j$;\n4. For any $i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n-1$, $\\gcd(a_i, a_{i-1}) = 1 = \\gcd(a_i, a_{i+1})$, and $a_i$ divides $a_{i-1} + a_{i+1}$.\n\nSuppose there is another sequence $b_0, b_1, \\ldots, b_n$ of integers such that $\\frac{b_{i+1}}{a_{i+1}} > \\frac{b_i}{a_i}$ for all $i = 0, 1, \\ldots, n-1$. Find the minimum value of $b_n - b_0$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Let $k$ be a positive integer. A sequence $a_0, a_1, \\ldots, a_n$, where $n > 0$, of positive integers satisfies the following conditions:\n\n1. $a_0 = a_n = 1$;\n2. $2 \\leq a_i \\leq k$ for each $i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n-1$;\n3. For each $j = 2, 3, \\ldots, k$, the number $j$ appears $\\phi(j)$ times in the sequence $a_0, a_1, \\ldots, a_n$, where $\\phi(j)$ is the number of positive integers that do not exceed $j$ and are coprime to $j$;\n4. For any $i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n-1$, $\\gcd(a_i, a_{i-1}) = 1 = \\gcd(a_i, a_{i+1})$, and $a_i$ divides $a_{i-1} + a_{i+1}$.\n\nSuppose there is another sequence $b_0, b_1, \\ldots, b_n$ of integers such that $\\frac{b_{i+1}}{a_{i+1}} > \\frac{b_i}{a_i}$ for all $i = 0, 1, \\ldots, n-1$. Find the minimum value of $b_n - b_0$.", "true_answer": 1, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "4bcea3e5-789b-4505-9381-88ce0c3a20cf:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 4792, 25, 3274, 382, 261, 16281, 261, 10853, 52718, 6409, 13, 7801, 1062, 16281, 287, 10853, 350, 18750, 13027, 8, 38971, 11293, 306, 290, 69972, 287, 10853, 820, 261, 10853, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 138920, 690, 11293, 8, 483, 287, 10853, 56424, 54912, 30, 19792, 316, 1646, 11085, 4149, 1432, 328, 287, 2170, 533, 287, 62, 15, 4335, 24140, 328, 2238, 287, 483, 11293, 69972, 13, 20953, 413, 13313, 328, 4857, 261, 30, 52762, 1151, 581, 1309, 316, 11433, 19957, 4149, 9809, 4335, 31060, 328, 261, 364, 2167, 1309, 316, 4218, 261, 10853, 16281, 8866, 13, 30255, 220, 18, 5003, 2454, 16336, 441, 591, 220, 17, 316, 372, 14518, 9707, 16290, 4997, 8, 4238, 6200, 261, 62, 15, 35229, 11, 64, 2170, 13, 8820, 261, 62, 15, 53088, 2170, 28, 16, 11, 1118, 553, 625, 60500, 306, 7060, 220, 18, 13, 2632, 3609, 5517, 297, 10, 16, 29702, 220, 16, 350, 1938, 261, 15, 8, 659, 16, 350, 1938, 448, 8, 659, 4215, 22305, 73, 28, 17, 92, 61, 74, 16290, 4997, 8, 314, 220, 17, 659, 21494, 16290, 4997, 741, 47682, 297, 314, 220, 16, 659, 21494, 16290, 4997, 741, 50557, 297, 10, 16, 314, 220, 17, 659, 21494, 16290, 4997, 8, 15155, 297, 314, 220, 16, 659, 21494, 16290, 4997, 741, 9700, 364, 14232, 220, 19, 25, 2214, 2454, 13878, 575, 11, 261, 10853, 382, 8039, 81, 642, 316, 1617, 31655, 326, 144028, 4215, 328, 31655, 364, 39520, 19261, 5542, 8496, 25, 1328, 382, 392, 34, 2082, 258, 14439, 311, 69, 8165, 1, 45665, 30, 66273, 4322, 25, 121423, 8663, 13768, 4493, 26584, 2027, 26, 40840, 5941, 553, 78186, 110523, 306, 123047, 88, 16281, 13, 30255, 484, 261, 10853, 144028, 261, 22305, 72, 12, 16, 190680, 64, 22305, 72, 10, 16, 92, 326, 106918, 220, 16, 15286, 316, 4322, 484, 261, 22305, 72, 12, 16, 4180, 261, 10853, 11, 261, 22305, 72, 10, 16, 92, 553, 40840, 5941, 306, 1236, 68045, 885, 1320, 16877, 16281, 30, 54458, 11095, 316, 16281, 328, 39104, 4840, 328, 110523, 306, 2569, 328, 11293, 1432, 306, 123047, 88, 16281, 328, 2569, 372, 364, 110355, 7060, 