, 11, 3609, 12241, 220, 17953, 17, 1424, 667, 22, 11, 15021, 86653, 316, 29111, 5018, 24183, 26, 581, 1217, 679, 5018, 12241, 591, 290, 42647, 16336, 350, 3150, 8, 306, 1617, 220, 17, 15697, 56022, 656, 220, 2991, 25, 1354, 220, 3150, 2023, 6442, 869, 316, 220, 3152, 472, 22244, 350, 21910, 1617, 3605, 2023, 413, 869, 316, 220, 3152, 472, 1775, 8, 889, 581, 3572, 1309, 316, 48502, 1617, 15448, 220, 2991, 24183, 22991, 13, 65037, 220, 2991, 853, 220, 9659, 22, 15448, 24183, 1934, 1617, 94900, 26, 1023, 665, 413, 42041, 5251, 722, 15697, 869, 316, 220, 6733, 2454, 5178, 13, 12265, 12241, 4410, 11, 58866, 364, 84787, 6052, 4215, 314, 220, 15363, 17, 364, 56734, 25, 220, 2636, 9, 3150, 314, 220, 17537, 23, 11, 2932, 220, 17, 9, 2991, 314, 220, 9876, 11, 3609, 220, 15363, 17, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 6453, "words": 3720, "band_lo": 1475.25, "band_hi": 5901.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to understand problem: There are 2017 voters each submit a full ranking (a permutation) of numbers 1..38. For each position k (1st place etc), we look at which integer received the most votes for that k-th place. Among ties, choose the smallest integer. Call that integer a_k. Then compute sum over k of a_k. Want the maximum possible value of this sum, given we can arrange the votes (the rankings) arbitrarily, with 2017 voters.\n\nWe can influence which numbers become most frequent in each position. We want to maximize sum of the numbers that are most frequent in each position (choosing smallest in ties), so we likely want larger numbers to be the most frequent in many positions.\n\nBut we are limited by number of voters and permutations: each voter contributes exactly one number in each position, and each number appears exactly once per ranking.\n\nWe need to allocate votes for each position k across numbers 1..38. Let v_{i,k} be number of voters who rank integer i at position k. For each k, sum_i v_{i,k}=2017. Also for each voter, the positions must be consistent (the set of positions assigned to numbers forms a permutation). This corresponds to doubly stochastic constraints: each integer i appears exactly once per voter across positions. So total votes for integer i across all positions is also 2017: sum_k v_{i,k}=2017.\n\nThus we have a 38x38 matrix V with nonnegative integer entries, row sums = column sums = 2017, and entries represent counts of voters ranking i at position k.\n\nGoal: For each column k, define a_k = argmax_i v_{i,k} (choose smallest i among ties). Want to maximize sum a_k.\n\nThus we need to design matrix V with row/col sums fixed to maximize sum of indices of column-wise leaders.\n\nWe can think of giving dominance to large numbers in many columns.\n\nObservation: Since each row sum is 2017, each integer i can be most frequent in at most 2017 columns (if it dominates a column it needs at least > half? not necessarily). Actually to be the most frequent in a column, we need its count > any other count (or equal but then smallest i wins). Since we can make ties, we may force larger numbers to win by ensuring they have at least as many votes as smaller numbers, but tie goes to smaller number, undesirable. To make larger number win, need strictly greater count than any smaller integer.\n\nThus for a column where we want a_k = some large integer L, we must allocate v_{L,k} >= v_{i,k}+1 for all i< L. For i > L we don't care because larger numbers tie? Wait a_k is the integer received the most k-th place votes; if multiple integers tie for max, we pick smallest such integer. So if we want a_k = L, we must ensure that no integer i<L has count equal to max; they must be strictly less than max. Larger integers (i>L) could tie with L, but then smallest among them is L? Actually if i>L ties with equal count, the smallest integer among those with max is L, because L < i. So it's okay if larger integers have same count as L.\n\nThus condition: For all i<L, v_{i,k} <= v_{L,k} -1. For i>L, v_{i,k} <= v_{L,k} (allowed equal).\n\nGoal to maximize sum of L values over columns.\n\nWe have constraints of row sums as well.\n\nWe can think of constructing a matrix where a set of columns are \"owned\" by large numbers.\n\nSince ties are allowed with larger numbers, we could have many numbers share same max count, but then a_k will be the smallest among them, which would be the smallest number in that set, potentially not the large one we desire. So to make a_k = large L, we need to ensure any number smaller than L has strictly less votes.\n\nThus we need to allocate votes to ensure this.\n\nSimplify: Since we can control counts, perhaps we can set v_{i,k}=0 for all i< L, v_{L,k}=t, and distribute remaining 2017-t votes among numbers >L (including L maybe). Need columns sum 2017.\n\nRow sums: each integer i will need to have total across columns = 2017 . So for integer i being used as dominant in many columns, its overall row sum must be 2017; cannot exceed 2017.\n\nThus each integer i can dominate at most 2017 columns if each time we assign all its 2017 votes to that column? Wait row sum is total votes across columns = 2017, not per column. So each integer appears exactly once per voter, so for each voter the integer appears in exactly one column. Over all voters (2017 of them), integer i appears exactly 2017 times across columns.\n\nThus total number of times integer i appears as the kth place vote across all k is 2017. So if we want integer i to be the dominant (most frequent) in many columns, its count per column will be less than or equal to 2017 (since sum across columns is 2017). So we can spread its appearances across many columns.\n\nThus we can regard each integer i has 2017 \"tokens\" to assign to columns, one per voter (for each voter they appear somewhere). We choose distribution across columns. For a column k, we need total 2017 tokens across all i.\n\nGoal: choose distribution to make for each column the smallest integer among those with max tokens be as large as possible.\n\nThis resembles constructing a matrix with row/col sums = 2017, maximizing sum of column-minimax.\n\nIdea: Use extreme assignment: For each column, allocate all 2017 votes to a single integer i (so v_{i,k}=2017, others 0). Then that integer i will dominate column k. Row sums would then be 2017 for each column's integer, but then each integer's row sum become 2017 times number of columns assigned to it, exceeding 2017 unless each integer is assigned to exactly one column. Since there are 38 columns, we can assign each column a distinct integer (i) and allocate all votes to that integer. This would satisfy column sums (2017 each) and row sums: each integer used in at most one column has row sum 2017, and others row sum 0, which violates row sum requirement (each integer must sum to 2017). Row sums must be exactly 2017 for all 38 integers, so each integer must appear 2017 times total. So each integer must be assigned to exactly 2017 total positions across columns. Thus we cannot have zero for a row.\n\nThus we need each integer to be distributed across columns so that row totals are exactly 2017.\n\nThus the problem reduces to a 38x38 matrix with each row sum = each column sum = 2017 (doubly stochastic integer). That's a Latin square-type.\n\nGoal to maximize sum over columns of minimal index achieving column max.\n\nObservation: Since each row sum is fixed, we may think of each column as a permutation of the multiset of size 2017 of integers across voters; but globally over columns, each integer appears exactly 2017 times.\n\nWe can consider constructing a 2017 x 38 matrix of rankings (voters rows). So each column position k across voters: we need to decide which integer appears in that column for each voter. This is same as constructing 2017 permutations.\n\nBut perhaps we can think of assigning each voter ranking as identity (1..38) to simplify? Not yet.\n\nGoal: maximize sum of column winners (the smallest integer among those with max count). To make a_k high, we need that the smaller integers have low counts in that column.\n\nBecause each integer must appear 2017 times across 38 columns, the average count per column per integer is 2017/38 ≈ 53.08. So each integer appears about 53 times per column on average.\n\nWe can try to allocate such that for each column k, the smallest integers have counts below the max, while larger integers have high counts (maybe equal). Since each integer's total across columns is limited, we need to spread them.\n\nPotential strategy: Partition columns into groups, each group has a \"dominant\" integer L which appears many times in those columns (maybe close to 2017), while for other columns L appears less or zero, and smaller integers appear low.\n\nBut row total 2017 prevents giving one integer high counts in many columns.\n\nIf we let integer 38 dominate one column, we could assign many of its 2017 appearances to that column (e.g., 2017). But then its row sum would be satisfied, leaving zero for other columns, making them need other integers to fill. That's fine: row sum is satisfied (exactly 2017). So integer 38 could occupy all votes in column 38 (k=38). That's allowed, other columns then must be filled using other integers each with total 2017 across remaining columns.\n\nThus we can assign each integer i to dominate exactly one column, perhaps the column k=i? That would give a_k = ? smallest integer with max count is i for column i. Then sum = sum_{i=1}^{38} i = 38*39/2 = 741. But we can possibly get larger sum by making some columns have dominant integer larger than column index? Actually a_k is independent of k. We can assign integer 38 to dominate multiple columns if we split its row sum across columns but still high enough to be max.\n\nBut row sum total 2017; to dominate multiple columns, it would need to have high count in each, but the sum over those columns equal at most 2017. So #columns dominated * votes per column = 2017. To dominate a column, the integer must have count > any smaller integer. Minimum count needed to beat smaller integers could be as low as something like floor(2017/2)+1? Wait we could make smaller integers have low counts, but others may have also some counts. If we let integer 38 have count c in a column, we must ensure for each i<38, v_{i,k} ≤ c-1. To satisfy column total 2017, we need sum over all i v_{i,k}=2017, thus at least some other integers' counts. Could allocate the rest to integers >38? none. So we must allocate remaining 2017-c votes to integers (including those >38 none) or to i>38 (none). So the only way is allocate to integers with index ≥38 (i.e., 38) itself? But can't exceed 2017. So if we set v_{38,k}=c, other integers cannot exceed c-1. Since total must be 2017, we need sum over i<38 v_{i,k} = 2017 - c, each ≤ c-1. Is that possible? Yes if (38-1)*(c-1) ≥ 2017 - c. That is 37(c-1) ≥ 2017 - c => 37c -37 ≥ 2017 - c => 38c ≥ 2054 => c ≥ 2054/38 ≈ 54.05 => c ≥55. So as long as c≥55, we can distribute the remaining votes among smaller integers without any reaching c. So integer 38 can dominate a column with only 55 votes, not needing majority. Therefore each integer can dominate multiple columns as long as we allocate enough of their 2017 votes across columns, each with count at least 55 (or the threshold based on numbers count). In general, for integer L to dominate column, its count c must be such that remaining votes can be allocated to other integers respecting their per-integer limits (<c). The worst case for small integers is they could have up to c-1 each; there are (L-1) smaller integers. Also there are larger integers (greater than L) that can have up to c (since tie is okay). To maximize ability to allocate, we can allow larger integers to also have count c, which doesn't affect a_k because smallest max is L.\n\nThus the feasible minimal c for given L is determined by ability to fill remaining demand with at most c each for (38-1) other integers, but with constraints that smaller ones ≤c-1. So we need total capacity: (L-1)*(c-1) + (38-L)*c ≥ 2017 - c (the remaining votes after allocating c to L). This inequality must hold.\n\nWe can solve for minimal c as function of L. Let's compute.\n\nLet S = 2017.\n\nWe allocate x = c to integer L. Need to allocate S - c among other 37 integers, with each integer i< L max c-1, and each i> L max c.\n\nThus capacity = (L-1)*(c-1) + (38-L)*c = (L-1)(c-1) + (38-L)c = (L-1)c - (L-1) + (38-L)c = [(L-1)+(38-L)]c - (L-1) = (37)c - (L-1).\n\nWe need capacity ≥ S - c.\n\nThus (37)c - (L-1) ≥ S - c => 38c ≥ S + (L-1) => c ≥ (S + L -1)/38.\n\nSince c integer, minimal c = ceil((S+L-1)/38).\n\nCheck earlier calculation for L=38: c ≥ ceil((2017+37)/38) = ceil(2054/38)= ceil(54.0526)=55 matches earlier.\n\nThus an integer L can dominate a column with as low as c_min(L) = ceil((2017+L-1)/38). Since L ranges up to 38, c_min varies.\n\nNow each integer i has total of 2017 votes to distribute across columns it may dominate. If it dominates t_i columns, it must allocate at least c_min(i) per column, so t_i ≤ floor(2017 / c_min(i)). Also it can allocate more than minimum in some columns.\n\nGoal: maximize sum over columns of L = a_k. Equivalent to allocate each column a dominant integer L, and we have constraints that each integer i can dominate at most t_i columns (with sum of its allocated counts not exceeding 2017). However we could also allocate extra votes beyond c_min, but that reduces capacity for other columns (since row sum fixed). So for maximizing number of columns dominated by large i, we'd allocate exactly c_min per column to conserve votes.\n\nThus treat each domination uses c_min(i) of its row budget.\n\nLet the number of columns assigned to integer i be d_i (nonnegative integer). Need sum_i d_i = 38 (each column assigned to exactly one dominant integer). Also each integer i's total used votes = d_i * c_min(i) ≤ 2017. Since we choose minimal counts, we may leave remaining unused votes for that integer to be allocated as non-dominant counts in columns (where it's not max). That's okay as long as row sum total =2017; we don't need to use all row budget for domination; extra votes can be distributed arbitrarily as not affecting a_k. So constraint is d_i * c_min(i) ≤ 2017.\n\nThus we want to assign each column a dominant integer i, respecting that each i can dominate at most floor(2017 / c_min(i)) columns. To maximize sum of i's across 38 columns, we would assign as many columns as possible to larger i's, within their capacity.\n\nThus sort integers by i descending, assign as many columns as possible to each.\n\nCompute c_min(i) = ceil((2017 + i -1)/38) = ceil((2016 + i)/38). Let's compute values.\n\nWe can compute floor(2017 / c_min(i)). Let's compute for i=38 down to 1.\n\nFirst compute (2016+i)/38:\ni=38: (2016+38)=2054 /38 =54.0526 => c=55.\ni=37: (2016+37)=2053/38=54.0263 => c=55 as ceil.\ni=36: 2052/38=54.0 => c=54 (since 54.0 exactly? 2052/38 = 54? 38*54=2052 yes. Ceil 54). So c_min(36)=54.\n\ni=35: (2016+35)=2051/38=53.9737 -> ceil 54.\ni=34: 2050/38=53.9474 -> ceil 54.\ni=33: 2049/38=53.9211 -> ceil 54.\ni=32: 2048/38=53.8947 -> ceil 54.\ni=31: 2047/38=53.8684 -> ceil 54.\ni=30: 2046/38=53.8421 -> ceil 54.\ni=29: 2045/38=53.8158 -> ceil 54.\ni=28: 2044/38=53.7895 -> ceil 54.\ni=27: 2043/38=53.7632 -> ceil 54.\ni=26: 2042/38=53.7368 -> ceil 54.\ni=25: 2041/38=53.7105 -> ceil 54.\ni=24: 2040/38=53.6842 -> ceil 54.\ni=23: 2039/38=53.6579 -> ceil 54.\ni=22: 2038/38=53.6316 -> ceil 54.\ni=21: 2037/38=53.6053 -> ceil 54.\ni=20: 2036/38=53.5789 -> ceil 54.\ni=19: 2035/38=53.5526 -> ceil 54.\ni=18: 2034/38=53.5263 -> ceil 54.\ni=17: 2033/38=53.5 -> ceil 54? Actually 53.5 => ceil 54.\ni=16: 2032/38=53.4737 -> ceil 54.\ni=15: 2031/38=53.4474 -> ceil 54.\ni=14: 2030/38=53.4211 -> ceil 54.\ni=13: 2029/38=53.3947 -> ceil 54.\ni=12: 2028/38=53.3684 -> ceil 54.\ni=11: 2027/38=53.3421 -> ceil 54.\ni=10: 2026/38=53.3158 -> ceil 54.\ni=9: 2025/38=53.2895 -> ceil 54.\ni=8: 2024/38=53.2632 -> ceil 54.\ni=7: 2023/38=53.2368 -> ceil 54.\ni=6: 2022/38=53.2105 -> ceil 54.\ni=5: 2021/38=53.1842 -> ceil 54.\ni=4: 2020/38=53.1579 -> ceil 54.\ni=3: 2019/38=53.1316 -> ceil 54.\ni=2: 2018/38=53.1053 -> ceil 54.\ni=1: 2017/38=53.0789 -> ceil 54.\n\nInteresting: For i from 1 up to 35, c_min=54, except for i 36 gave 54, earlier we got 55 for 36? Wait we computed 36 gave exactly 54, yes. For i=35 gave 54, etc. For i=38,37 gave 55. So only top 2 integers need 55 votes per dominance. Others need 54.\n\nNow compute maximum number of columns each integer can dominate: floor(2017 / c_min(i))\n\nFor i=38, c=55 => floor(2017/55) = 36? 55*36=1980; 55*37=2035 >2017. So max 36 columns.\n\nSimilarly for i=37, same 55 => max 36 columns.\n\nFor i=36 down to 1, c=54 => floor(2017/54) = 37? 54*37=1998; 54*38=2052 >2017. So max 37 columns.\n\nThus each integer from 1-36 can dominate at most 37 columns; but we only have total 38 columns to assign, so capacity is huge.\n\nBut note each integer's total usage across columns cannot exceed 2017 votes. If we assign 37 columns to integer i with 54 each, uses 1998 votes, leaving 19 votes to allocate elsewhere (non-dominant). That's fine.\n\nNow we want to assign 38 columns to integers to maximize sum of assigned integers, respecting per-integer max columns (36 for top two, 37 for others). Since capacities are high, we could assign all 38 columns to integer 38, but capacity only 36 columns. So can't exceed 36.\n\nThus we assign as many columns as possible to 38 (36 columns), then remaining columns.\n\nNext largest integer 37 also capacity 36; we could assign up to 36 columns, but we only need total 38 columns. After assigning 36 to 38, we have 2 columns left, we could assign them to 37 (both columns), giving sum = 36*38 + 2*37 = 1368 + 74 = 1442.\n\nBut we need to ensure column count per integer does not exceed 38 total columns obviously. So this seems feasible.\n\nCheck if we can assign 36 columns to 38 with each column using 55 votes for 38, and allocate the rest among other integers abiding by row totals. Since 38 uses 36*55 =1980 votes, leaving 37 votes of its row budget (since total 2017). These remaining 37 votes can be distributed as non-dominant in other columns (the two columns where dominant is 37). That's fine.\n\nNow need to verify feasibility of constructing matrix satisfying all rows sums & column sums simultaneously with this dominance arrangement.\n\nWe need to ensure for columns dominated by 38, the remaining 2017-55=1962 votes per column are allocated to other integers not exceeding caps (i.e., for i<38, each <=54). Since c=55 ensures they can't reach 55. But capacity of each smaller integer per column is at most 54 (c-1). Since we have 37 such integers, total capacity per column: 37*54 = 1998, which is greater than 1962 needed, so possible.\n\nSimilarly for columns dominated by 37 with c=55, same capacity.\n\nThus we should be able to allocate.\n\nWe also must ensure each other integer's total across all columns equals 2017. Since many columns (36+2=38) we have distribution.\n\nWe already used 38's 1980 votes in 36 columns, leaving 37 votes for 38 to allocate somewhere else (non-dominant columns). Similarly 37 uses 2*55=110 votes in its dominated columns, leaving 2017-110=1907 votes for 37 to be allocated elsewhere.\n\nIn total, remaining votes for other integers across all columns total: sum over all integers of remaining row budget = 38*2017 - total used for dominant allocations = (38*2017) - (36*55 + 2*55) = (38*2017) - (38*55) = 38*(2017-55)=38*1962 = 38*1962 = 746? compute: 2000*38=76000; subtract 38*38 =1444; So 76000-1444=74556. Wait check: 1962*38 = (2000-38)*38 = 76000 - 1444 = 74556. This equals total remaining votes to fill across columns (since each column totals 2017, after allocating dominant votes 55 each, remaining per column = 1962, total 38 columns *1962 = same 74556). Good.\n\nThus we need to fill remaining votes with all integers (including 38,37) respecting per-column caps: for each column dominated by 38, 38 already has 55; can still receive additional votes up to total row sum but they'd be non-dominant; but that's okay. No per-column cap for dominant integer beyond row sum? Actually 38 can have up to 55 (since if we give more they'd increase its count but we still have dominance). It could even have more than 55, that's okay, would still be dominant; but we would then exceed its row total quickly. So we may keep exactly 55.\n\nThus we need to verify that each other integer can achieve its needed total 2017 across columns, given per-column caps.\n\nBut we have many columns (38) each with capacity per integer: For i<38, in columns dominated by 38, max per column =54; similarly in columns dominated by 37 also max 54 (since dominant count 55, tie allowed). So each of the 36+2 columns allow each smaller integer up to 54 votes. Over 38 columns total capacity for each such integer = 38*54 = 2052, which exceeds its required total 2017, so feasible.\n\nThus we can allocate counts to meet row sums. Since total capacity exceeds required, we can construct a feasible matrix.\n\nThus arrangement seems possible, achieving sum 1442.\n\nBut can we do better by using some columns dominated by 36 also? Since 36 also can dominate up to 37 columns, but its c=54 (smaller). The per-column remaining votes after dominating with 54 is 2017-54=1963, which is higher capacity but fine. If we reduce number of columns assigned to 38 from 36 to, say, 35 and assign more to 37 and 36 etc, sum might change. Since 38 has highest value, we want as many columns as possible to 38. Capacity limit is 36 columns due to its total votes 2017/55 floor 36. Could we possibly allow 38 to dominate 36 columns with 55 each, and in remaining 2 columns also dominated by 38 but with count larger than 55 (e.g., use remaining 37 votes plus extra from other integers)? Wait row sum for 38 is only 2017; after 36 columns using 55 each total 1980, remaining votes 37. To have 38 dominate a 37th column, we would need at least c such that it beats others. Minimum c is ceil((2017+38-1)/38)=55 again, cannot because we only have 37 votes left. But we could allocate extra votes from other integers to that column (i.e., have other integers also get votes). For 38 to be the maximum, its count must be at least maybe 54? Actually if we give 38 only 37 votes in a column, then smaller integers could also have up to 36 votes each; capacity (37-1)=36 per smaller, times 37 =1332, which plus 37 gives total 1369; far less than 2017 needed, so not possible to fill column total 2017 with such low count for 38 because other integers can't collectively reach needed total if they are limited to at most 36 each? Wait they could exceed 36 because they are larger? There are no larger integers; all others smaller. So each smaller integer max is 36 (c-1). With 37 smaller ints, max total = 37*36 =1332, plus 37 =1369 < 2017. So column total cannot be achieved. So we need c at least 55.\n\nThus 38 can dominate at most floor(2017/55)=36 columns.\n\nSimilarly 37 same.\n\nThus the above maximal sum is likely optimal: assign 36 columns to 38, remaining 2 to 37.\n\nBut could we instead assign 36 columns to 38, 1 column to 37, and 1 column to 36 (value 36) to potentially increase sum? That would be sum = 36*38 + 37 + 36 = 1368 + 73 = 1441, slightly less than 1442. So 2 columns to 37 is better.\n\nWhat about using 35 columns for 38, and 3 columns to 37? Then sum = 35*38 + 3*37 = 1330 + 111 = 1441 also less.\n\nThus optimum likely with max 38 columns count as 36, rest to 37.\n\nCheck if we can assign any column to 38 with count 56 (using more than minimal), which would reduce number of columns it can dominate (since its total votes 2017). Using higher c uses more of its row budget, reducing number of columns dominated, not beneficial for sum.\n\nThus optimum is as above.\n\nBut we need to verify feasibility of constructing the matrix with the exact domination counts (36 columns with 55 votes for 38, 2 columns with 55 for 37). And fill other counts appropriately.\n\nWe already argued capacity per column for others up to 54 each; total required per other integer is 2017. We have 38 columns each capacity 54 for each smaller integer, total capacity 2052 >2017, leaving slack to distribute extra votes; we also have extra capacity from the dominant integer (38) in its 2 columns dominated by 37: there 38 could receive up to 55 as tie (since its count could be up to 55 as well) but we might need to allocate its remaining 37 votes somewhere. Similarly 37 has 1907 remaining votes after its dominance; they can be allocated across all columns up to 54 each etc. Since capacity large, feasible.\n\nThus answer sum = 1442.\n\nCompute: 36*38 = 1368, plus 2*37 = 74, total 1442.\n\nThus final answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 1442", "answer": 1442, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.5762783312520696e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999996423722308, "logp_correct": -3.5762783312520696e-07, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6141865495930278, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4600302383159942, "frac_below_-5": 0.010717614165890028, "digit_lp_min": -5.006716728210449, "answer_first_mention_lp": -2.2649508537142538e-05, "n_cot_tokens": 6438, "lp_end": -1.8247708082199097, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -7.390948667307384e-06, "logp_nocot": -7.354588856454939, "p_nocot": 0.000639650351645312, "uplift": 7.3545884988271055}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "5003e0f2-0bbd-4939-b376-fc66bce68ed4", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A round robin tournament is held with $2016$ participants. Each player plays each other player once, and no games result in ties. We say a pair of players $A$ and $B$ is a dominant pair if all other players either defeat both $A$ and $B$ or are defeated by both $A$ and $B$. Find the maximum number of dominant pairs.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A round robin tournament is held with $2016$ participants. Each player plays each other player once, and no games result in ties. We say a pair of players $A$ and $B$ is a dominant pair if all other players either defeat both $A$ and $B$ or are defeated by both $A$ and $B$. Find the maximum number of dominant pairs.", "true_answer": 2015, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "5003e0f2-0bbd-4939-b376-fc66bce68ed4:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 10562, 2086, 328, 17889, 503, 131528, 24702, 484, 35441, 7060, 13, 30897, 493, 10610, 25, 1920, 7238, 355, 19853, 2238, 484, 395, 1062, 1273, 5033, 363, 11, 4784, 363, 54439, 2973, 355, 326, 418, 11, 503, 2973, 355, 326, 418, 16807, 363, 13, 2632, 1023, 679, 32820, 4376, 4372, 722, 1273, 7238, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 2684, 920, 328, 48185, 1023, 39007, 741, 25563, 11, 2870, 9247, 30, 3004, 7060, 1078, 1043, 2813, 1534, 30, 3164, 2454, 2107, 2454, 1273, 26, 1001, 25327, 11, 290, 1273, 63582, 13, 4569, 8740, 7902, 16083, 3630, 363, 382, 1273, 5033, 11, 625, 355, 503, 418, 13, 2632, 42647, 10610, 553, 7238, 483, 2684, 392, 12245, 1, 9727, 328, 24693, 10217, 290, 2867, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 1023, 553, 171587, 12432, 122861, 25651, 591, 1043, 39830, 3981, 24192, 364, 84787, 581, 679, 28579, 350, 26232, 23699, 7465, 741, 1416, 1309, 44207, 2086, 328, 1315