25363, 8, 8812, 316, 829, 6471, 1340, 679, 1991, 92927, 9176, 11, 889, 10954, 25435, 364, 37253, 25, 14166, 261, 142423, 220, 23909, 23, 6559, 263, 116685, 1365, 261, 6953, 220, 23909, 23, 6559, 263, 60816, 1701, 13, 16834, 38360, 83182, 423, 316, 2501, 65081, 13, 7801, 135805, 10111, 656, 21010, 261, 71638, 591, 2454, 25363, 575, 316, 25363, 575, 10, 17, 350, 38258, 1001, 8, 10112, 20547, 306, 92927, 328, 1490, 350, 16, 11, 220, 667, 22, 11, 220, 16, 20707, 4037, 3239, 364, 135706, 1985, 25, 12265, 41487, 43448, 11095, 316, 16336, 10331, 261, 17568, 22261, 314, 10331, 2846, 22005, 13, 623, 16538, 540, 261, 25363, 29702, 23821, 26922, 5517, 11, 1118, 382, 16336, 10331, 13, 730, 135805, 4672, 581, 4128, 679, 316, 43198, 306, 2684, 22005, 26, 2454, 41487, 44468, 126205, 16336, 10331, 364, 84787, 9770, 148365, 6960, 4149, 395, 65414, 297, 314, 220, 23909, 23, 364, 23483, 1217, 316, 2371, 97666, 395, 297, 29, 23909, 23, 30, 4037, 4149, 5192, 316, 7848, 44762, 26, 1236, 372, 10853, 2804, 413, 1424, 667, 23, 1118, 625, 9279, 472, 13878, 43448, 464, 3478, 13, 2632, 10562, 382, 220, 23909, 23, 364, 84787, 6052, 2127, 314, 220, 23909, 23, 364, 4383, 3291, 297, 1890, 30, 1843, 297, 28, 23909, 24, 11, 7848, 314, 220, 667, 24, 14793, 16, 12, 17, 14, 23909, 24, 8, 314, 220, 667, 24, 533, 350, 23909, 23, 9, 667, 24, 60504, 23909, 24, 16918, 24305, 23864, 25, 355, 314, 220, 667, 24, 533, 220, 23909, 23, 9, 667, 24, 27334, 23909, 24, 8, 6961, 50557, 355, 314, 220, 667, 24, 14793, 16, 12, 17, 18292, 8, 314, 220, 667, 24, 533, 220, 23909, 23, 14, 297, 425, 30, 860, 220, 17, 9, 667, 24, 18292, 314, 220, 23909, 23, 18292, 13, 2632, 355, 314, 220, 667, 24, 533, 220, 23909, 23, 18292, 13, 2214, 297, 28, 23909, 24, 11, 355, 314, 220, 667, 24, 533, 220, 23909, 23, 14, 23909, 24, 42308, 230, 220, 667, 24, 533, 220, 15, 13, 9130, 3384, 42308, 230, 220, 667, 23, 13, 1302, 1161, 1424, 667, 23, 13, 2632, 1236, 372, 10853, 2804, 413, 220, 667, 24, 30, 4037, 9279, 13, 2632, 297, 6284, 23275, 220, 23909, 23, 364, 72816, 6052, 25, 220, 23909, 23, 13], "n_tokens": 3391, "words": 1981, "band_lo": 990.5, "band_hi": 3962.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse problem: There's a regular 2019-gon. A \"chaisri\" figure is a triangle whose three vertices are vertices of the regular 2019-gon. So basically any triangle formed by choosing 3 distinct vertices of that regular polygon. \"Two different chaisri figures may be formed by different regular 2019-gons.\" Means we can consider multiple copies of regular 2019-gon positioned maybe differently? Possibly we can have many regular 2019-gons (maybe congruent and rotated) and choose triangles from each.\n\nNow a \"thubkaew\" figure is a convex polygon that can be dissected into multiple chaisri figures, where each vertex of a dissected chaisri figure does not necessarily lie on the border of the convex polygon.\n\nInterpretation: We have a convex polygon (thubkaew). Inside it we can partition (dissect) the polygon into a set of triangles, each triangle being a \"chaisri figure\" - i.e., each triangle's vertices are vertices of some regular 2019-gon (maybe different for each triangle). The triangles may share vertices interior to the thubkaew polygon (these interior vertices need not be on its border). The question: what is the maximum number of vertices that a thubkaew figure may have? i.e., maximize the number of vertices of the convex polygon while allowing such a triangulation into triangles each taken from possibly different regular 2019-gons.\n\nEssentially: we want a convex polygon that can be triangulated with triangles having vertices in the set of all vertices of some regular 2019-gon. Since we can use multiple regular 2019-gons (maybe with different orientations and positions), we can pick any three points that form an equiangular triple? Wait vertices of a regular 2019-gon lie on a circle, equally spaced. So any triangle formed from three of its vertices is determined by chord lengths corresponding to arcs. In particular, the central angles between consecutive chosen vertices are multiples of 2π/2019. So the triangle's side lengths correspond to chords subtending arcs a, b, c where a+b+c = 2019 (in terms of steps). So the possible triangles are those with edges that are chords of a circle, with vertices equally spaced around that circle (but perhaps not consecutive). However, since we can have multiple copies (different regular 2019-gons), we can place triangles anywhere as long as each triangle's vertices are on some such circle with points equally spaced.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: what is the largest n such that there exists a convex n-gon that can be triangulated into triangles each of which is inscribed in some circle with 2019 equally spaced points (i.e., each triangle's vertices lie on a circle with those points). But the circles can be different for each triangle. The convex polygon's vertices are presumably among the vertices of the triangulation (i.e., the outer boundary). Triangulation interior vertices may be new points not on boundary.\n\nGoal: maximize number of vertices of outer polygon.\n\nObservation: We can use many triangles sharing interior points, but the outer polygon's vertices must be from the union of triangle vertices that lie on boundary. Since we can choose many triangles, perhaps we can get many boundary vertices using many small chords.\n\nBut there may be restriction: no three vertices can be collinear (convex). Each boundary vertex must be a vertex of at least one triangle. Since triangle vertices may be anywhere on circles, we can place them arbitrarily as long as they are part of a triangle that fits the 2019-multiple angle condition.\n\nThus the question essentially: What is the maximum number of points in convex position such that the complete triangulation of the convex polygon can be partitioned into triangles each with vertex angles that are integer multiples of 2π/2019 (since arcs correspond to integer steps). Equivalent to having each triangle be similar to a triangle formed by points on a regular 2019-gon; but due to scaling, any triangle with side lengths proportional to chords of a given circle? Actually chords lengths depend on radius. If we have a regular 2019-gon inscribed in a circle of radius R, the chord length for step k is 2R sin(πk/2019). So triangle formed by steps (a,b,c) has side lengths 2R sin(π a/2019), etc. If we scale radius arbitrarily, we get all similar triangles. So the shape of triangle is determined only by the triple (a,b,c) up to similarity. So each triangle in the dissection must be similar to some triangle from the set of integer step triples summing to 2019.\n\nThus each triangle must have internal angles that are determined by arcs: central angles are 2π a/2019 etc. The inscribed angle subtended by chord of arc a is half of central angle => angle at the opposite vertex is π a/2019. Wait inscribed angle theorem: angle subtended by chord AB at point C on circle equals half of central angle AOB (where O center). The central angle corresponds to arc a steps meaning angle = 2π a/2019. So an inscribed angle is π a/2019. So triangle's angles are multiples of π/2019. Indeed each angle of the triangle is k * π/2019 where k are positive integers adding to π (since sum of angles = π). So the three integers sum to 2019. So any triangle formed from regular 2019-gon has angles that are integer multiples of π/2019, with sum π.\n\nThus each triangle in the triangulation must have all three angles being integer multiples of π/2019.\n\nHence the question: what is the maximum number of vertices a convex polygon can have such that it can be triangulated into triangles each having angles multiples of π/2019? No restriction on side lengths.\n\nNow a convex polygon can be triangulated into n-2 triangles. So we need to assign angles at interior vertices consistent.\n\nEssentially we are looking for a convex polygon which can be tiled by such triangles, i.e., a polygon that admits a triangulation where each triangle's angles are integer multiples of basic unit π/2019.\n\nWe can treat this as a problem of angle partition. At each interior point where triangles meet, the sum of angles around that point is 2π, which must be representable as sum of multiples of π/2019, i.e., 2π = (2019)* (2/2019)π. That's fine, any multiple.\n\nThus the only potential restriction is that the polygon's interior angles themselves must be sums of angles of incident triangles. The outer polygon's interior angles are also sums of some of the triangle angles at boundary vertices. For convex polygon, each interior angle < π. So each outer angle must be a sum of one or more of the triangle's angle pieces at that vertex, with each piece being integer multiple of π/2019.\n\nThus each outer angle must be an integer multiple of π/2019 as well (since sum of multiples). So the polygon's interior angles must all be integer multiples of π/2019. Moreover, each must be less than π.\n\nHence each outer angle = k_i * π/2019 with k_i integer, 1 ≤ k_i ≤ 2018 (since less than π). And sum over i of interior angles = (n-2)π = (n-2)*2019*(π/2019). Therefore sum of k_i = (n-2)*2019.\n\nWe need n vertices, each with integer k_i satisfying 1 ≤ k_i ≤ 2018, and sum k_i = 2019(n-2).\n\nWe need to maximize n possible under those constraints.\n\nEssentially it's a combinatorial problem: find maximal n such that there exist integers k_i between 1 and 2018 inclusive whose sum = 2019(n-2). This is possible if average k_i = 2019(n-2)/n must be ≤2018 and ≥1. The upper bound is critical.\n\nCompute average: A = 2019(n-2)/n = 2019*(1 - 2/n). Need A ≤ 2018 => 2019*(1 - 2/n) ≤ 2018 => divide: 1 - 2/n ≤ 2018/2019 = 1 - 1/2019 => subtract: -(2/n) ≤ -(1/2019) => 2/n ≥ 1/2019 => n ≤ 2*2019 = 4038.\n\nAlso need average ≥1 obviously satisfied for n≥? A = 2019 - 4038/n >=1 => 2019 - 4038/n >=1 => 4038/n <= 2018 => n >= 4038/2018 ≈2. So fine.\n\nThus n max is 4038. But we also need each k_i integer. We need to construct a set for n=4038: we need k_i sum = 2019*(4038-2) = 2019*4036 = compute 2019*4036 = 2019*4000 + 2019*36 = 8,076,000 + 72,684 = 8,148,684. Average = 8,148,684 / 4038 =? Let's compute: 4038 * 2019 =? Since average formula gives 2019*(1 - 2/4038) = 2019*(4036/4038) = 2019* (2018/2019) ? Wait 4036/4038 simplifies: divide numerator denominator by 2 => 2018/2019. So average = 2019 * (2018/2019) = 2018. So each k_i must average 2018. Since max allowed 2018, we need all k_i =2018. That satisfies sum: 4038 * 2018 =? 4000*2018=8,072,000; 38*2018= 38*2000=76,000 plus 38*18=684 => 76,684. Total 8,148,684 matches. So we need each interior angle = 2018 * π/2019 = π - π/2019. That's less than π, okay.\n\nThus polygon with n=4038 vertices each interior angle just slightly less than π, essentially a nearly flat convex polygon, like a thin shape approximating a line? But can such a convex polygon exist? Yes, you can have a convex polygon with all angles close to π, e.g., a nearly straight chain. So existence wise, we need to be able to triangulate it using triangles whose angles are multiples of π/2019 and at each vertex we need one angle of size 2018π/2019 (i.e., large angle). In triangulation, each boundary vertex will belong to some number of adjacent triangles; the sum of those triangle angles at that vertex is the interior angle of polygon: 2018π/2019. Since each triangle angle is at most (2019-2)*π/2019? Actually each triangle angle integer multiple, max can be 2018π/2019 as we saw. So to achieve interior angle of 2018π/2019, we can have a single triangle at that vertex using angle 2018? But triangle interior angles must sum to π, so a triangle with one angle 2018π/2019 is impossible because that alone exceeds π. Wait each triangle angle is integer multiple of π/2019. Largest possible angle in a triangle is less than π, so max integer multiple is 2018π/2019? Actually π corresponds to 2019π/2019, can't have that. So a triangle could have angle 2018π/2019? That is less than π, yes possible. The other two angles must sum to π - 2018π/2019 = π/2019 (i.e., sum of 1 unit). That can be split as (1,0) but cannot be 0. So minimal angle is at least π/2019 (1 unit). So we could have (2018,1,0) not allowed. So need two other angles positive integers summing to 1, impossible because they must be at least 1 each. So max possible angle in integer triple sum 2019 is 2017? Let's test: Suppose angles have integers a,b,c with a+b+c=2019, each at least 1. So max a is 2019-2=2017. So max angle is 2017π/2019. Indeed triangle angles can't be 2018 multiple because then remaining sum would be 1 which cannot be split into two positive integers. So a,b,c >=1. Therefore each triangle angle ≤2017π/2019.\n\nThus boundary interior angle 2018π/2019 cannot be achieved by sum of triangle angles at a vertex because each triangle contributes an angle ≤2017π/2019, but we could have multiple triangles meeting at that vertex: sum of several angles each ≤2017π/2019 could total 2018π/2019 (i.e., 2018 units). Minimum angle per triangle is π/2019 (1 unit). So to get 2018 units, we need at least 2 triangles: e.g., 2017+1 = 2018. So a vertex could have two triangles with angles 2017π/2019 and π/2019. That's feasible.\n\nThus we need a triangulation where each vertex of polygon has interior angle 2018 units and the incident triangles' angles partition that. We could have at each vertex one large 2017 and one small 1 angle. Need to ensure global consistency.\n\nIs such triangulation possible? Consider taking a polygon that is a near straight line, we can triangulate using fan from one interior vertex? Not.\n\nWe might construct using a \"triangulation\" where each polygon vertex is adjacent to exactly two triangles sharing that vertex, with angle contributions (2017,1). The sum is 2018. That seems plausible.\n\nNow need to verify that such a triangulation exists for n=4038. Since each interior vertex (not polygon vertex) internal to dissection may have many triangles meeting, but that's okay.\n\nConstruct: Take a convex 4038-gon approximating a regular 4038-gon scaled long. Connect vertices sequentially to create polygon. Then triangulate by drawing a diagonal from each vertex i to vertex i+2 (skip one) maybe resulting in triangles of type (1, 2017, 1)? Not sure.\n\nSimplify: Since triangle angles correspond to integer steps a,b,c = steps around circle. The angle at a vertex equals opposite arc length, which is integer steps. In triangulation we don't have to embed in same circle; each triangle individually satisfies integer steps.\n\nThus construction existential likely possible for maximal n = 4038.\n\nNeed also to check feasibility for n>4038? Not possible due to average constraint; some k_i must be >2018 which not allowed as interior angles < π. So maximum is 4038.\n\nThus answer X = 4038.\n\nCheck small n case? If n=4039, average = 2019*(1-2/4039) = 2019 - (4038*2019)/(4039?) Wait compute: A = 2019 - 4038*2019/(4039)?? Actually A = 2019*(1-2/n) = 2019 - 4038/ n *? no 2*2019/n = 4038/n. So A = 2019 - 4038/n. For n=4039, A = 2019 - 4038/4039 ≈ 2019 - 0.99975 ≈ 2018.00025 >2018. So some k_i must be 2019? Not allowed. So n cannot exceed 4038.\n\nTherefore answer: 4038.", "response": "ANSWER: 4038", "answer": 4038, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.695483030696778e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999963045237976, "logp_correct": -3.695483030696778e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.596173886109025, "cot_lp_min64": -1.5130869630397008, "frac_below_-5": 0.008886255924170616, "digit_lp_min": -2.466013193130493, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 3376, "lp_end": -0.0015257871709764004, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -0.00013171759201213717, "logp_nocot": -3.6310831448063254, "p_nocot": 0.02648747909028293, "uplift": 3.6310794493232947}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "51f12f85-9bae-4d7e-91a9-05a25c40fafc", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: For a positive integer $N$, let $f_N^{1}$ be the function defined by\n$$f_N(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^N\\frac{N+1/2-n}{(N+1)(2n+1)}\\sin((2n+1)x).$$\nDetermine the smallest constant $M$ such that $f_N(x)\\leq M$\n for all $N$ and all real $x.$ The original answer is in $\\frac{m\\pi}{n}$ format, please give the value of m + n.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: For a positive integer $N$, let $f_N^{1}$ be the function defined by\n$$f_N(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^N\\frac{N+1/2-n}{(N+1)(2n+1)}\\sin((2n+1)x).$$\nDetermine the smallest constant $M$ such that $f_N(x)\\leq M$\n for all $N$ and all real $x.$ The original answer is in $\\frac{m\\pi}{n}$ format, please give the value of m + n.", "true_answer": 5, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "51f12f85-9bae-4d7e-91a9-05a25c40fafc:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 1982, 1072, 478, 326, 1215, 328, 285, 3080, 4061, 741, 38966, 285, 3080, 4061, 25987, 4215, 22305, 77, 28, 15, 92, 61, 45, 261, 2170, 2187, 2054, 17, 77, 10, 16, 115950, 8, 1919, 261, 2170, 314, 350, 45, 10, 16, 14, 17, 533, 297, 11876, 2054, 45, 10, 16, 4898, 17, 77, 10, 16, 8784, 2632, 76338, 8841, 30, 478, 10, 16, 14, 17, 533, 297, 8841, 4609, 297, 5017, 478, 10, 15, 13, 20, 13, 12265, 297, 16336, 869, 316, 478, 11, 4275, 8841, 395, 722, 297, 350, 21910, 478, 10, 15, 13, 20, 533, 478, 314, 220, 15, 13, 20, 1424, 15, 741, 2632, 261, 2170, 29, 15, 70763, 36144, 364, 2167, 1309, 10562, 4149, 1432, 328, 290, 498, 71461, 34114, 4215, 483, 8841, 76338, 326, 14210, 70720, 13, 12265, 2187, 2054, 17, 77, 10, 16, 115950, 8, 72554, 16, 13, 623, 10562, 540, 2454, 1215, 10112, 3760, 1572, 4215, 261, 2170, 2236, 581, 6284, 38328, 679, 722, 2187, 314, 16, 350,