5, 13, 3384, 533, 15, 13, 41142, 18, 314, 15, 13, 19837, 22, 13, 26106, 1572, 3478, 14, 19, 364, 29730, 1215, 2846, 10112, 220, 15, 13, 23, 9920, 350, 171441, 2548, 6793, 741, 2187, 15, 13, 23, 28, 15, 13, 46860, 11, 2187, 17, 13, 19, 28, 15, 13, 39542, 13, 7801, 285, 171441, 15, 13, 3384, 9, 15, 13, 46860, 659, 15, 13, 41142, 18, 9, 15, 13, 39542, 28, 15, 13, 41135, 10, 15, 13, 47485, 28, 15, 13, 45490, 13, 2632, 2928, 3760, 364, 195709, 3760, 1572, 3478, 14, 19, 364, 45, 28, 17, 25, 23864, 10112, 8065, 68753, 2360, 3490, 12391, 30, 41021, 8704, 25, 478, 28, 17, 871, 478, 10, 16, 28, 18, 13, 76338, 25, 297, 28, 15, 25, 350, 17, 10, 15, 13, 20, 12, 15, 25987, 17, 13, 20, 820, 350, 18, 9, 16, 25987, 15, 13, 43243, 18, 13, 297, 28, 16, 25, 350, 17, 13, 20, 12, 16, 25987, 16, 13, 20, 820, 350, 18, 9, 18, 25987, 16, 13, 20, 14, 24, 28, 15, 13, 19341, 22, 13, 297, 28, 17, 25, 350, 17, 13, 20, 12, 17, 25987, 15, 13, 20, 178259, 18, 9, 20, 25987, 15, 13, 20, 14, 1055, 28, 15, 13, 24851, 2546, 13, 2632, 285, 17, 314, 220, 15, 13, 43243, 2187, 1215, 659, 15, 13, 19341, 22, 2187, 18, 87, 659, 15, 13, 24851, 2546, 2187, 20, 87, 13, 3604, 1215, 28, 1345, 14, 17, 8967, 25, 2187, 28, 16, 11, 2187, 18, 1345, 14, 17, 21714, 16, 871, 26735, 533, 15, 13, 19341, 22, 26, 2187, 20, 1345, 14, 17, 28, 16, 871, 659, 15, 13, 24851, 2546, 13, 17565, 28, 15, 13, 43243, 533, 15, 13, 19341, 22, 10, 15, 13, 24851, 2546, 28, 15, 13, 41293, 21, 13, 42961, 1572, 3478, 14, 19, 364, 195709, 2999, 51418, 10112, 1078, 220, 15, 13, 4388, 5967, 30, 18057, 413, 3760, 1572, 3478, 14, 19, 30, 50557, 3478, 14, 19, 28, 15, 13, 45085, 19, 13, 5339, 46716, 5862, 2999, 10112, 3760, 13, 52762, 1151, 98608, 394, 1072, 478, 44142, 316, 3478, 14, 19, 591, 4895, 364, 84787, 42173, 9594, 391, 484, 30227, 285, 3080, 4061, 8, 395, 722, 478, 326, 1215, 382, 3478, 14, 19, 13, 13602, 6284, 23275, 3478, 14, 19, 13, 19792, 316, 413, 3239, 860, 85205, 364, 2167, 665, 37071, 2360, 5854, 73, 2156, 22854, 326, 134833, 25, 12265, 76338, 261, 2170, 8841, 326, 70763, 11, 285, 3080, 382, 290, 35720, 708, 298, 4774, 328, 290, 14210, 44883, 5594, 483, 8841, 22854, 350, 5563, 73, 2156, 20769, 741, 5854, 73, 2156, 22854, 382, 2893, 46069, 11, 326, 137447, 328, 1114, 483, 2893, 46069, 22854, 139241, 98608, 394, 11608, 350, 3228, 72554, 1982, 328, 1114, 741, 12265, 4756, 1114, 350, 79029, 20485, 8, 853, 10562, 3478, 14, 19, 11, 1815, 285, 3080, 4061, 8, 72554, 3478, 14, 19, 13, 31082, 472, 478, 20216, 197798, 326, 1215, 306, 350, 15, 11, 1345, 936, 285, 3080, 44142, 316, 3478, 14, 19, 11, 40036, 31157, 10581, 5263, 4824, 11, 13972, 391, 314, 3478, 14, 19, 364, 84787, 391, 314, 3478, 14, 19, 314, 350, 16, 1345, 11876, 19, 11, 284, 28, 16, 11, 297, 28, 19, 11, 284, 87683, 28, 20, 364, 17045, 25, 220, 20, 364, 58369, 13709, 1721, 13], "n_tokens": 3378, "words": 1585, "band_lo": 1022.25, "band_hi": 4089.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need sup over N and x of f_N(x). Define f_N(x)= sum_{n=0}^N a_n sin((2n+1)x) where a_n = (N+1/2 - n)/((N+1)(2n+1)). So coefficients positive? N+1/2 - n positive until n <= N+0.5. Since n integer up to N, it's positive for all n (since N+0.5 - N = 0.5 >0). So a_n>0 decreasing roughly.\n\nWe need maximum possible value of the trigonometric sum with positive coefficients and odd multiples. Since sin((2n+1)x) ≤1. The maximum at each x maybe less than sum a_n because we cannot simultaneously have all sin =1 (requires x = π/2 + 2πk for all odd frequencies? sin((2n+1)π/2) = sin( (odd)*(π/2) ) = ±1 alternating sign: sin( (2n+1)π/2 ) = (-1)^n. Indeed sin((2n+1)π/2)=(-1)^n. So cannot all be +1. So need to find sup over x of weighted sum.\n\nDefine S_N(x) = sum a_n sin((2n+1)x). This resembles Fourier series of some function? Let's attempt to compute analytic expression. Recognize a_n reminiscent of coefficients of square wave? Actually coefficients of some integral: maybe we can sum series in closed form.\n\nConsider sum_{n=0}^N sin((2n+1)x)/(2n+1) = something like (π/4) for x∈(0,π). Indeed known identity: sum_{k=0}^\\infty sin((2k+1)x)/(2k+1)=π/4 for x∈(0,π). That's square wave Fourier series. For finite N, it's partial sum S_N0(x) = sum_{n=0}^N sin((2n+1)x)/(2n+1). That equals (1/2) * something: maybe related to Dirichlet kernel. Actually partial sum of odd harmonics yields: sum_{n=0}^N sin((2n+1)x)/(2n+1) = (1/2) * [Si((2N+1)x)?? Not exactly]. But we might use known expression: sum_{k=0}^{N} sin((2k+1)x)= sin^2((N+1)x)/sin x. Wait formula: sum_{k=0}^{N} sin((2k+1)x) = sin^2((N+1)x)/sin x. But that's without denominator (2n+1). That's Dirichlet.\n\nOur a_n includes factor (N+1/2 - n)/(N+1). So we can view f_N as weighted average of partial sums? Write a_n = (1/(N+1)) * (N+0.5 - n)/(2n+1). So f_N = (1/(N+1)) * sum_{n=0}^N (N+0.5 - n) * sin((2n+1)x)/(2n+1). Let define b_n = N+0.5 - n = (N+0.5) - n.\n\nWe can rewrite sum_{n=0}^N (N+0.5 - n) * sin((2n+1)x)/(2n+1) = sum_{k=0}^N c_k where c_k = sum_{n=0}^k sin((2n+1)x)/(2n+1) maybe? Because a telescoping sum: Consider cumulative sums. Since N+0.5 - n = sum_{j=n}^{N} 1? Actually N+0.5 - n = (N - n) + 0.5. Not integer.\n\nBut we can think discrete integration: weighted by decreasing linear weight equals partial sums of series. Indeed we have identity: sum_{n=0}^N (N - n) u_n = sum_{k=0}^{N-1} sum_{n=0}^{k} u_n. Because each u_n appears in N-n terms: for each k >= n, counted. So sum_{n=0}^N (N-n) u_n = sum_{k=0}^{N-1} S_k where S_k = sum_{n=0}^k u_n.\n\nApply with u_n = sin((2n+1)x)/(2n+1). Then sum_{n=0}^N (N-n) u_n = sum_{k=0}^{N-1} s_k where s_k = sum_{n=0}^k u_n. Add also 0.5 term: sum_{n=0}^N 0.5 u_n = 0.5 s_N.\n\nThus numerator sum = sum_{k=0}^{N-1} s_k + 0.5 s_N.\n\nThus f_N = (1/(N+1)) * [ sum_{k=0}^{N-1} s_k + 0.5 s_N ].\n\nNow s_k = sum_{n=0}^k sin((2n+1)x)/(2n+1). Known to be approximating π/4 for large k for x∈(0,π). So s_k ≤ π/4 maybe? Actually the infinite sum equals π/4 for 0<x<π. Partial sums converge monotonic? For x∈(0,π), series of positive decreasing terms? sin((2n+1)x) can be positive or negative depending, not monotone. But the sum tends to π/4.\n\nBut we can bound s_k ≤ π/4 maybe worst-case sup over x? The supremum over x of s_k maybe also π/4? Let's test x small: sin((2n+1)x)≈ (2n+1)x, so term ≈ x, sum ≈ (k+1)x, grows with k unbounded as k large but denominator cancels? Wait term sin/(2n+1)≈ x, not decreasing. So for very small x, s_k ≈ (k+1) x which can exceed π/4 if k big enough. However x small also near 0, the infinite series behaves like π/4 for any fixed x>0 as N→∞ due to alternating sign contributions? Actually for small x, the series approximates arctan(tan?) Let's recall formula: sum_{n=0}^\\infty sin((2n+1)x)/(2n+1) = π/4 for x∈(0,π/2) ??? Wait standard Fourier series of square wave: for x∈(-π,π), f(x)= (4/π) Σ_{k odd} sin(kx)/k = sign(sin x). So Σ_{odd} sin(kx)/k = π/4 for 0<x<π, and -π/4 for -π<x<0. Indeed so the sum equals π/4 for 0<x<π (excluding endpoints). So indeed the infinite sum is constant independent of x! That's interesting. Yes the square wave series yields constant π/4 for x∈(0,π). So s_k tends to π/4, and overshoot (Gibbs) near discontinuities (x=0,π). So the maximum value of partial sums may be larger than π/4 due to overshoot. So sup over x and N of s_N could be some constant > π/4.\n\nOur f_N is average of s_k. Since average of values may have max maybe also overshoot but less.\n\nThus need sup_{N,x} f_N(x). Could compute numerically to guess constant.\n\nLet's attempt to approximate behavior near x=0 (or π). For small x, s_N ~ sum_{n=0}^{N} sin((2n+1)x)/(2n+1). Approximate using integral? Using Dirichlet kernel? Known that near discontinuity, partial sums exhibit Gibbs phenomenon with overshoot about 9% above jump. Since the jump of square wave is from -π/4 to π/4, total jump = π/2. The overshoot about 9% of jump amplitude: approx 0.08949 * (π/2) = 0.08949*1.5708≈0.1405. So the maximum of the partial sum near x=0 is about π/4 + 0.1405 = 0.7854 + 0.1405 = 0.9259. That's about 0.9259.\n\nNow f_N is average of s_k, likely smaller overshoot. Might be about same as limit of some integral. Could be maximum maybe π/4 + something smaller.\n\nWe could attempt compute f_N analytically using known closed form formula for partial sum with weights (N+0.5 - n). Maybe it's equal to something like (1/(2(N+1))) * (something like Dirichlet kernel integrated). Might converge to integral of square wave's partial sums, leading to maximum equal to something like π/4? Let's think.\n\nDefine g_N(x) = (1/(N+1)) Σ_{k=0}^{N-1} s_k + (1/(2(N+1))) s_N.\n\nAs N large, the average of first N partial sums plus half of Nth. As N → ∞, s_k → π/4 for most x away from discontinuities, but near 0, s_k oscillates with Gibbs overshoot. The Cesàro mean (Fejér sum) of Fourier series yields convergence to function's value at continuity points, with no overshoot and no Gibbs. Indeed Fejér sum averages partial sums. Our weighting is exactly the Cesàro (C,1) means? For Fourier series, the Fejér sum σ_N(x) = (1/(N+1)) Σ_{k=0}^N S_k(x) where S_k are partial sums of Fourier series (including all harmonics). Here we have only odd harmonics series; but still the averaging yields Fejér kernel for that series, which yields convergence to the midpoint at discontinuities (i.e., average of left/right limits) which is 0 at x=0 because sign function jumps from -π/4 to π/4; the midpoint is 0. So the Cesàro sum at x=0 should be 0, not overshoot. Indeed Fejér sum of square wave yields 0 at the jump.\n\nOur f_N is similar but with extra 0.5 weight on final term, not exactly Cesàro. Fejér sum σ_N = (1/(N+1)) Σ_{k=0}^{N} s_k. Our f_N = (1/(N+1))( Σ_{k=0}^{N-1} s_k + 0.5 s_N ) = σ_N - (0.5/(N+1)) s_N. So it's slightly less than Fejér sum.\n\nThus as N→∞, σ_N(x) → π/4 for 0<x<π and 0 at x=0 (midpoint). So sup limit is π/4. The supremum over all N may be slightly larger than π/4 due to finite N overshoot perhaps? But Fejér sum eliminates overshoot, i.e., it's always ≤ maximum of function? Actually Fejér sum of a bounded periodic function is always between min and max for any x? For convex functions maybe. Not sure.\n\nBut our f_N is slightly less than σ_N, so its max likely ≤ max σ_N. The supremum over N of σ_N equals π/4 (since limit is π/4 and early N may be smaller). So supremum of f_N might be ≤ π/4, maybe slightly less. However at x near 0, σ_N is near 0, not large. So max occurs away from discontinuity, maybe near where the original series sum=π/4. So max may be π/4.\n\nNeed to check any x where f_N exceeds π/4? Let's test simple N small manually.\n\nN=0: f_0(x)= (0+0.5-0)/((1)(1)) sin(x)=0.5 sin x. Max =0.5.\n\nπ/4≈0.785>0.5.\n\nN=1: coefficients: n=0: (1.5)/ (2*1)=0.75? Wait compute: N=1 => denominator (N+1)=2. a0 = (1+0.5-0)/(2*(1)) =1.5/2=0.75. term sin x. n=1: (1+0.5-1)=0.5 numerator, denominator 2*(3)=6 => 0.5/6≈0.08333. So f_1=0.75 sin x + 0.08333 sin3x. Max maybe? Use numeric: Could achieve >π/4? Let's approximate at x≈π/2: sin=1, sin3π/2 = -1. So value =0.75 -0.0833 =0.6667. Less than π/4.\n\nTry x around maybe 0.8 rad (≈45°). sin0.8=0.717, sin2.4=0.675. Then f≈0.75*0.717 +0.0833*0.675=0.538+0.056=0.594. So still less.\n\nSeems less than π/4.\n\nN=2: compute maybe quickly approximate using code mental? Let's attempt: N=2 => N+1=3. coefficients: n=0: (2+0.5-0)=2.5 / (3*1)=0.8333. n=1: (2.5-1)=1.5 / (3*3)=1.5/9=0.1667. n=2: (2.5-2)=0.5 /(3*5)=0.5/15=0.03333. So f2 = 0.833 sin x +0.1667 sin3x +0.03333 sin5x. At x=π/2 gives: sin=1, sin3π/2=-1 => contribution -0.1667; sin5π/2=1 => +0.03333. Total=0.833 -0.1667+0.03333=0.6996. Lower than π/4.\n\nSeems max approaching maybe about 0.78 limit? Should be less than π/4? Actually π/4=0.7854. Our computed near max maybe less. Likely supremum over N tends to π/4 from below.\n\nThus smallest constant M that bounds f_N(x) for all N and x is π/4. Because cannot exceed π/4. Need to be sure no exceeding.\n\nWe can argue using Fejér kernel and positivity: Since coefficients a_n positive and decreasing, f_N is the Cesàro mean of the odd sine series with positive kernel (Fejér-type). Fejér kernel is nonnegative, and convolution of function with nonnegative kernel preserves supremum bound (max ≤ sup of function). Since original function (square wave) has maximum π/4, then f_N(x) ≤ π/4. Moreover as N→∞ and x in (0,π), f_N tends to π/4, achieving arbitrarily close values, thus M = π/4.\n\nThus M = π/4 = (1π)/4, m=1, n=4, m+n=5.\n\nAnswer: 5.\n\nLet's craft final.", "response": "ANSWER: 5", "answer": 5, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.2411137024391792e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999775891141033, "logp_correct": -2.2411137024391792e-05, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5039418707557176, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3019909095932256, "frac_below_-5": 0.008620689655172414, "digit_lp_min": -3.165201187133789, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 3364, "lp_end": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -0.008722420781850815, "logp_nocot": -1.3844216465948875, "p_nocot": 0.25046861728749553, "uplift": 1.3843992354578631}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "53502e51-ad04-4e24-80bc-e656e74186be", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: 162 pluses and 144 minuses are placed in a $30 \\times 30$ table such that each row and each column contains at most 17 signs. (No cell contains more than one sign.) For every plus, count the number of minuses in its row, and for every minus, count the number of pluses in its column. Find the maximum of the sum of these numbers.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: 162 pluses and 144 minuses are placed in a $30 \\times 30$ table such that each row and each column contains at most 17 signs. (No cell contains more than one sign.) For every plus, count the number of minuses in its row, and for every minus, count the number of pluses in its column. Find the maximum of the sum of these numbers.", "true_answer": 2592, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "53502e51-ad04-4e24-80bc-e656e74186be:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 4218, 4792, 364, 2167, 679, 220, 1130, 87, 1130, 10586, 13, 220, 18694, 2932, 17424, 36088, 8, 326, 220, 15363, 40335, 17424, 21382, 741, 11555, 5225, 326, 6000, 8895, 540, 1645, 220, 1422, 17424, 350, 1440, 4784, 1490, 15890, 741, 3004, 4206, 8895, 945, 1572, 1001, 2232, 364, 55292, 395, 2454, 2932, 2232, 25, 3605, 2086, 328, 1349, 5977, 306, 1617, 5225, 13, 2214, 2454, 40335, 2232, 25, 3605, 2086, 328, 2932, 268, 306, 1617, 6000, 13, 34138, 1072, 722, 17424, 13, 19792, 10562, 4149, 4215, 364, 12845, 802, 19536, 750, 13, 9024, 10586, 13585, 575, 28, 16, 485, 1130, 11, 15697, 441, 28, 16, 485, 1130, 364, 55292, 13955, 25, 275, 22305, 72, 22712, 102838, 16, 538, 2932, 540, 350, 72, 22712, 936, 1203, 220, 15, 13, 284, 22305, 72, 22712, 102838, 16, 538, 40335, 540, 350, 72, 22712, 936, 1203, 220, 15, 13, 3004, 2973, 1