0, 220, 23, 40335, 11, 3609, 40335, 314, 220, 1157, 9, 23, 28, 15363, 2733, 15248, 13, 1958, 1023, 89046, 829, 69164, 11242, 364, 84787, 581, 1309, 316, 1646, 261, 220, 15, 12, 16, 12248, 328, 2349, 220, 1157, 87, 1157, 483, 5225, 73121, 314, 6000, 73121, 314, 24, 11, 1118, 13031, 350, 68, 1940, 4213, 1062, 6953, 54467, 497, 651, 7465, 328, 9882, 220, 24, 402, 220, 1157, 10, 1157, 38360, 741, 21926, 4149, 2236, 9882, 272, 28, 24, 72554, 220, 1422, 350, 77, 12, 16, 8, 326, 297, 81685, 382, 1952, 350, 1157, 9, 24, 28, 18694, 1952, 741, 2632, 58866, 364, 84787, 290, 47221, 65414, 11608, 220, 28608, 17, 7890, 138276, 364, 10620, 581, 2804, 6757, 484, 13585, 14, 26893, 625, 2061, 350, 3086, 220, 899, 8213, 13585, 14, 26893, 8, 679, 12421, 17424, 11, 1118, 23440, 31060, 72554, 1422, 364, 84787, 10562, 4215, 382, 220, 28608, 17, 364, 4383, 25, 23864, 777, 5225, 1888, 220, 24, 9, 23, 28, 8540, 11, 777, 6000, 220, 24, 9, 23, 28, 8540, 11, 4215, 5251, 220, 1157, 13585, 659, 220, 1157, 15697, 314, 220, 1157, 9, 8540, 9, 17, 314, 220, 28608, 17, 13, 11377, 364, 84787, 6052, 220, 28608, 17, 364, 2167, 1757, 14866, 860, 3432, 21868, 25, 30456, 581, 23275, 220, 16891, 21, 777, 13585, 656, 4566, 945, 1572, 220, 1157, 13585, 483, 17424, 889, 1236, 13585, 483, 3609, 3760, 1572, 220, 1422, 889, 1888, 10112, 945, 30, 12265, 142423, 581, 49699, 137517, 44207, 13, 41021, 1746, 13319, 25, 183159, 581, 679, 220, 1422, 13585, 483, 220, 1422, 17424, 326, 220, 16, 5225, 483, 3543, 1203, 11, 5178, 13, 3072, 3609, 17424, 13213, 220, 25117, 11, 813, 1062, 21441, 591, 220, 1422, 306, 1236, 5225, 2804, 8985, 656, 11293, 306, 3613, 11615, 220, 1422, 11, 19786, 5192, 316, 2328, 13, 2632, 316, 3357, 2684, 3609, 11, 581, 2804, 679, 1236, 13585, 3760, 1572, 220, 1422, 11, 1236, 5036, 1217, 3760, 1572, 220, 1422, 10705, 7848, 464, 1422, 13, 12265, 1114, 382, 142423, 11, 12847, 591, 8771, 280, 12545, 316, 945, 32879, 38566, 4215, 364, 72816, 220, 16891, 21, 382, 2999, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 220, 28608, 17, 364, 16577, 34496, 25, 10547, 581, 3239, 484, 5225, 1888, 8350, 2025, 10853, 61, 17, 14, 19, 8, 314, 2999, 261, 10853, 287, 10853, 4335, 3609, 328, 261, 10853, 76609, 10853, 314, 260, 10853, 30, 11377, 13, 7801, 4215, 4493, 142423, 472, 49699, 13, 2632, 11608, 18305, 364, 198140, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 3795, "words": 2317, "band_lo": 1268.5, "band_hi": 5074.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to understand problem.\n\nWe have 30x30 grid. 162 plus signs (+) and 144 minus signs (-). Each row and column contains at most 17 signs (of either type combined). No cell contains more than one sign.\n\nDefine for each plus sign: count number of minuses in its row. For each minus sign: count number of pluses in its column. Sum over all signs. Need maximum possible sum.\n\nLet’s formalize. Let grid rows i=1..30, columns j=1..30.\n\nDefine variables: p_{i,j}=1 if plus at (i,j), else 0. m_{i,j}=1 if minus at (i,j), else 0. No both. So p_{i,j}+m_{i,j} ≤1.\n\nTotal pluses: sum p =162. Total minuses: sum m=144.\n\nRow constraints: For each i, sum_j (p_{i,j}+m_{i,j}) ≤17.\n\nSimilarly column constraints: for each j, sum_i (p_{i,j}+m_{i,j}) ≤17.\n\nDefine for each plus at (i,j), its contribution = number of minuses in its row: row i minus count: M_i = sum_j m_{i,j}. So each plus in row i contributes M_i.\n\nSimilarly each minus at (i,j) contributes number of pluses in its column: P_j = sum_i p_{i,j}. So each minus in column j contributes P_j.\n\nTotal sum S = sum_{i,j} p_{i,j} * M_i + sum_{i,j} m_{i,j} * P_j.\n\nBut M_i independent of column j. So sum over pluses = M_i * (number of pluses in row i) = M_i * Pplus_i where Pplus_i = sum_j p_{i,j}.\n\nSimilarly sum over minuses = P_j * Mminus_j where Mminus_j = sum_i m_{i,j} (the number of minuses in column j).\n\nSo S = Σ_i (M_i * Pplus_i) + Σ_j (P_j * Mminus_j).\n\nBut note that M_i = number of minuses in row i; Pplus_i = number of pluses in row i. So first term Σ_i (M_i * Pplus_i). Second term Σ_j (P_j * Mminus_j) where P_j = pluses in column j, Mminus_j = minuses in column j.\n\nThus S = Σ_i (row i plus count * row i minus count) + Σ_j (col j plus count * col j minus count).\n\nDefine for each row i: a_i = #pluses in row i, b_i = #minuses in row i. Similarly for each column j: c_j = #pluses in col j, d_j = #minuses in col j. Constraints: a_i + b_i ≤ 17 for each i. sum_i a_i = 162, sum_i b_i = 144. Also for each column: c_j + d_j ≤ 17; sum_j c_j = 162, sum_j d_j = 144. Also consistency: Row and column counts correspond: a_i = sum_j p_{i,j} etc, but the marginals must be realizable with 0/1 matrix where each cell at most one sign. That's a bipartite matrix with two types? But we can treat plus placements and minus placements separate? Actually pluses and minuses cannot occupy same cell, but that just ensures that row counts a_i + b_i ≤ 30, but we already have ≤17 smaller. So it's okay.\n\nWe need to maximize S = Σ_i a_i b_i + Σ_j c_j d_j.\n\nWe have two independent sets: row contributions and column contributions. However a_i,b_i and c_j,d_j are not independent because placement across rows and columns must be consistent across both signs. Specifically, the matrix of plus placements defines both a_i and c_j. Similarly minus matrix defines b_i and d_j. But pluses and minuses are separate; they can share rows/columns but not cells. However constraints on each row/col regarding total signs also involve both types (a_i + b_i ≤ 17). That's captured.\n\nThus we need to choose nonnegative integers a_i,b_i,c_j,d_j satisfying:\n\nFor each i: a_i + b_i ≤ 17.\n\nFor each j: c_j + d_j ≤ 17.\n\nSum_i a_i = 162, sum_i b_i = 144. Sum_j c_j = 162, sum_j d_j = 144.\n\nAlso need consistency: there exists a 0-1 matrix of plus placements with row sums a_i and column sums c_j, and similarly another matrix for minuses with row sums b_i and column sums d_j, with the condition that plus and minus matrices have disjoint support (no same cell). That could impose additional constraints, but maybe the max sum will be achieved with some feasible arrangement.\n\nSince each row total ≤17; total signs =306 across 30 rows. Average per row ~10.2, so feasible.\n\nGoal maximize sum of products a_i b_i across rows plus c_j d_j across columns.\n\nGiven fixed sums of a_i and b_i, the sum Σ a_i b_i is maximized when the distributions are as \"unequal\" as possible? Let's think: For given total A = sum a_i, B = sum b_i, and per-row cap a_i+b_i ≤ 17, maximize Σ a_i b_i.\n\nConsider each row independently: given row limit L_i =17 (max). Since we have global constraints, we want to allocate a_i,b_i to maximize product. Since product is convex in distribution? Actually for fixed sum a_i+b_i = t_i, product a_i b_i ≤ (t_i/2)^2, maximizing when they are as equal as possible. Wait product a*b with given sum t is maximized when a = b = t/2 (balanced) because product is quadratic concave? Let's check: a*b = a*(t-a) = -a^2 + t a, parabola opening downward, max at a = t/2 giving product t^2/4. So product is maximized when balanced within each row, not extreme. However we also have separate totals A and B; we can choose t_i values up to 17.\n\nThus to maximize Σ a_i b_i under total A,B, we likely want each row to have as many signs as possible (t_i close to 17) and within each row balanced distribution (a_i close to b_i). But we also need total per type sums.\n\nWe have total signs per row across both types sum_i (a_i + b_i) = total plus+minus = 306. Average per row = 10.2. So not all rows at cap 17; we can distribute.\n\nGiven we want to maximize product sum, we need to decide row totals t_i = a_i + b_i. For each row i, given t_i, the max product is floor? Since a_i, b_i integers. The maximum product for integer a_i,b_i with a_i+b_i = t_i is when as close as possible to t_i/2 each, i.e., a_i = floor(t_i/2) or ceil. Product = floor(t_i/2) * ceil(t_i/2) = ⌊t_i^2/4⌋.\n\nThus Σ a_i b_i ≤ Σ ⌊t_i^2/4⌋ where t_i = a_i+b_i ≤ 17.\n\nWe need to choose t_i nonnegative integers ≤17, sum t_i = 306, to maximize Σ floor(t_i^2/4). Also we need to realize global A=162 B=144, meaning sum a_i =162, sum b_i=144. With balanced within each row, a_i will be close to b_i, but total A>B by 18. So overall plus count slightly higher.\n\nThus some rows will have one more plus than minus.\n\nSimilarly columns: we have analogous.\n\nNow columns also have to respect same totals.\n\nMaybe we can achieve the maximum by making rows and columns both \"balanced\" as possible. Since plus/minus placements are separate matrices, the row sums a_i, column sums c_j are the same vector (they are the row/col sums of the same plus matrix). Similarly for minus matrix. So we can't choose rows and columns independently; we need an arrangement where the plus matrix has row sums a_i and column sums c_j; likewise minus matrix has b_i,d_j. But for maximizing sum, maybe we can achieve the theoretical maxima of both row and column contributions simultaneously? Might be possible thanks to doubly stochastic feasible.\n\nThe sum S = Σ_i a_i b_i + Σ_j c_j d_j.\n\nNotice that Σ_i a_i b_i is function of row distributions; Σ_j c_j d_j function of column distributions. Since the same plus numbers give both row and column contributions, they are linked via matrix but perhaps we can find arrangement achieving near optimum.\n\nLet’s attempt to maximize upper bound via row and column independently, ignoring matrix feasibility, then see if reachable.\n\nFirst find optimal t_i distribution for rows to maximize Σ floor(t_i^2/4) given sum t_i =306, t_i ≤17, integer.\n\nSimilarly for columns, we have column totals s_j = c_j + d_j also = number of signs in column j. sum over j s_j =306, each ≤17. So same optimization; max total for columns same as rows.\n\nThus maximum possible S ≤ 2 * maximumRowSum.\n\nCompute maximum of Σ floor(t_i^2/4) for 30 variables with total 306.\n\nObservations: floor(t^2/4) is convex? Let's compute values:\n\nt : floor(t^2/4)\n0:0\n1:0 (1^2/4=0.25 floor 0)\n2:1 (4/4=1)\n3:2 (9/4=2.25 floor2)\n4:4 (16/4=4)\n5:6 (25/4=6.25 floor6)\n6:9 (36/4=9)\n7:12 (49/4=12.25 floor12)\n8:16 (64/4=16)\n9:20 (81/4=20.25 floor20)\n10:25 (100/4=25)\n11:30 (121/4=30.25 floor30)\n12:36 (144/4=36)\n13:42 (169/4=42.25 floor42)\n14:49 (196/4=49)\n15:56 (225/4=56.25 floor56)\n16:64 (256/4=64)\n17:72 (289/4=72.25 floor72)\n\nWe want to allocate total T=306 across 30 rows, average 10.2, but we can have some rows higher up to 17.\n\nSince the function is convex upward (since second differences positive?) Let's check increments: difference between t and t-1:\n\nt=1 diff 0\n2 diff 1\n3 diff 1\n4 diff 2\n5 diff 2\n6 diff 3\n7 diff 3\n8 diff 4\n9 diff 4\n10 diff5\n11 diff5\n12 diff6\n13 diff6\n14 diff7\n15 diff7\n16 diff8\n17 diff8\n\nIncrement increases every two steps, so function is convex.\n\nFor convex function, given fixed sum, the sum of f(t_i) is maximized by making t_i as unequal as possible (i.e., many at extremes) – because convex => Jensen: average of f ≥ f(average) for convex? Actually convex means function lies below line, wait Jensen for convex: f(average) ≤ average of f. So to maximize sum under sum constraint, we want distribution extreme (some high some low). So we want as many rows as possible at max 17, and rest low.\n\nBecause we also have integer t_i.\n\nGoal: maximize Σ floor(t_i^2/4). Use as many 17 as possible. Let's see how many rows can be 17 given total 306: 306 /17 ≈ 18.0. 17*18=306 exactly! So we could set 18 rows with t_i=17, and remaining 12 rows with t_i=0. This would meet total 306, each ≤17. That seems allowed. Then sum of floor(t_i^2/4) = 18 * 72 + 12*0 = 1296.\n\nThus max row contribution ≤1296.\n\nSimilarly columns could also achieve 18 columns with 17 signs each, others 0, giving 1296 column contribution.\n\nThus total S ≤ 2592.\n\nBut we have also need to meet totals of plus and minus counts: each row with 17 total signs must have a_i + b_i =17. Over 18 rows total signs =306, matching total. So the rows with zero signs have none. So all signs placed within those 18 rows. Similarly columns: we need 18 columns with total signs 17, others zero. The signs must be located in intersection of those 18 rows and 18 columns (a 18x18 subgrid). That contains 324 cells, enough for 306 signs with at most one per cell: need 306 distinct cells, possible because 324 >306. So feasible to allocate sign placements? Need to allocate 162 plus and 144 minus within that subgrid such that row totals 17 per row (so each of the 18 rows has 17 signs) and column totals 17 per column.\n\nThus each of the 18 rows has 17 signs, each of the 18 columns has 17 signs. This is like a (0,1)-matrix with row sums 17 and column sums 17 of size 18x18, total ones = 18*17=306, fine. That's a regular bipartite incidence.\n\nNow we also need separate placements for pluses and minuses, each with row sums a_i and column sums c_j, and similarly for minuses. However combined they must sum to full matrix of ones. So we need to split the 306 entries into plus and minus marks, each cell at most one sign. That's just a 2-coloring of 306 positions, with counts 162 plus, 144 minus.\n\nGiven any regular matrix of 1s with each row/col 17, we can label 162 of them plus and 144 minus arbitrarily respecting row a_i counts etc. We still need to maximize row contributions a_i b_i. Given each row has total 17, we want a_i ≈ b_i =8 or 9 (since 17). Balanced gives product 8*9 =72 which is the max floor(17^2/4) =72, indeed. So to achieve product 72 per row we need a_i=8, b_i=9 or vice versa. Since total pluses 162 across 18 rows, average plus per used row =162/18=9. So each row must have exactly 9 plus and 8 minus (or 8 plus 9 minus). Similarly column contributions need product 72 each, requiring column plus/minus distribution 9/8 per column.\n\nThus we need a 0-1 plus matrix with row sums 9 (plus) and column sums 9 (plus) within the 18x18 subgrid (since each row total signs 17, pluses 9, minuses 8). That implies plus matrix is a regular 9-regular bipartite matrix of size 18x18: each row has 9 plus, each column 9 plus, total pluses = 18*9 =162 — matches. Similarly minus matrix will be the complement within the subgrid (since each row/col total signs 17). Since each row has 8 minus, each column 8 minus, total minus = 18*8=144 — matches. And they occupy disjoint cells.\n\nThus we need to find a 0-1 matrix of size 18x18 with row sums = column sums =9, which exists (e.g., any regular bipartite graph of degree 9 on 18+18 vertices). That's possible because degree d=9 ≤ 17 (n-1) and n*d is even (18*9=162 even). So feasible.\n\nThus the theoretical maximal bound 2592 seems attainable.\n\nNow we must ensure that rows/columns not used (the 12 empty rows/columns) have zero signs, which fits constraints ≤17.\n\nThus maximum sum is 2592.\n\nCheck: compute per row product 9*8=72, per column 9*8=72, sum across 18 rows + 18 columns = 18*72*2 = 2592. Yes.\n\nThus answer 2592.\n\nWe should verify no better arrangement: Could we exceed 1296 per rows by having more than 18 rows with signs but some rows with total less than 17 but product maybe more? Since convex we argued extremes maximize. Let's test alternative: Suppose we have 17 rows with 17 signs and 1 row with something else, etc. But total signs fixed 306, so any reduction from 17 in some row must offset by increasing in another beyond 17, impossible due to cap. So to keep same total, we must have some rows less than 17, some others also less than 17 overall average <17. Since function is convex, shift from extremal distribution to more balanced reduces sum.\n\nTherefore 1296 is max.\n\nThus final answer 2592.\n\nEdge verification: Are we sure that row product floor(t_i^2/4) = max a_i b_i given total of a_i+b_i = t_i? Yes. Then sum via convex as argued. So bound holds.\n\nHence answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 2592", "answer": 2592, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -5.960462772236497e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999994039539004, "logp_correct": -5.960462772236497e-07, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5900458753015698, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4070975797305891, "frac_below_-5": 0.012962962962962963, "digit_lp_min": -4.264164447784424, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.23077824711799622, "n_cot_tokens": 3780, "lp_end": -0.43970590829849243, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.7418097943154862e-06, "logp_nocot": -6.50560499087441, "p_nocot": 0.0014950360019247747, "uplift": 6.505604394828133}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
