, 11, 813, 11608, 391, 5017, 8350, 2054, 77, 10, 17, 11876, 17, 8, 314, 8350, 2406, 14, 17, 659, 16, 741, 2632, 36144, 297, 14, 17, 10, 16, 13, 2214, 272, 5263, 316, 297, 14, 17, 11, 11608, 4410, 10112, 6574, 532, 2406, 741, 2214, 272, 1424, 297, 14, 17, 11, 3490, 2349, 382, 532, 2406, 741, 2632, 290, 10562, 265, 7038, 382, 1236, 16336, 12951, 220, 14397, 22, 30, 41021, 23864, 6551, 314, 220, 7672, 19, 14, 14397, 22, 13, 7801, 220, 17, 3356, 314, 220, 14397, 23, 14, 14397, 22, 42308, 230, 220, 16, 13, 1302, 36057, 13, 7801, 220, 17, 3356, 533, 16, 314, 350, 14397, 23, 14, 14397, 22, 8, 533, 220, 16, 314, 220, 16, 14, 14397, 22, 13, 2632, 50915, 6342, 11608, 391, 72554, 8350, 7, 220, 17, 3356, 820, 350, 17, 3356, 533, 16, 8, 1546, 314, 8350, 7, 350, 14397, 23, 14, 14397, 22, 8, 820, 350, 16, 14, 14397, 22, 8, 1546, 314, 8350, 7, 14397, 23, 741, 2632, 391, 72554, 220, 14397, 23, 13, 21926, 591, 50915, 6342, 11608, 538, 272, 1424, 297, 14, 17, 13, 41351, 6551, 28, 220, 7672, 19, 14, 14397, 22, 1424, 220, 15, 13, 20, 350, 5283, 16132, 741, 2632, 11608, 391, 5017, 220, 14397, 23, 364, 3031, 495, 11608, 138276, 30, 3274, 382, 261, 5542, 9770, 25, 15600, 176130, 3490, 350, 65846, 313, 597, 3490, 8, 328, 5517, 297, 314, 220, 17, 61, 81, 533, 16, 853, 2349, 391, 314, 220, 17, 61, 81, 314, 297, 10, 16, 11, 1349, 9324, 314, 220, 17, 42923, 81, 12, 16, 16255, 72230, 9324, 314, 220, 16, 14, 17, 13, 21926, 9707, 220, 15, 13, 20, 13, 3072, 581, 1309, 16132, 12384, 9324, 350, 2345, 220, 16, 741, 1416, 1340, 10266, 5517, 284, 314, 220, 14397, 22, 30, 41021, 2411, 364, 976, 10562, 4149, 2349, 10112, 220, 14397, 23, 13, 4101, 581, 12998, 2349, 220, 14397, 23, 30, 19792, 3490, 5517, 284, 2238, 484, 1349, 9324, 99731, 220, 7672, 19, 76, 14, 14397, 22, 13, 2214, 284, 314, 220, 14397, 22, 11, 495, 14081, 272, 5064, 220, 7672, 19, 13, 21926, 9707, 290, 9324, 328, 176130, 3490, 328, 5517, 220, 14397, 22, 484, 853, 9324, 220, 7672, 19, 350, 21910, 220, 17, 42923, 994, 102838, 14397, 23, 11, 220, 17, 42923, 702, 102838, 7672, 19, 741, 41351, 176130, 3490, 328, 5517, 220, 17, 42923, 81, 40412, 16, 314, 220, 14397, 22, 395, 428, 28, 994, 853, 2349, 391, 28, 17, 42923, 994, 102838, 14397, 23, 3490, 10020, 11, 1349, 9324, 314, 220, 17, 42923, 702, 102838, 7672, 19, 13, 41351, 484, 26427, 9707, 6551, 314, 220, 7672, 19, 14, 14397, 22, 13, 27046, 0, 2632, 581, 665, 12998, 2349, 220, 14397, 23, 483, 284, 28, 14397, 22, 350, 3086, 15600, 176130, 3490, 741, 1328, 15248, 290, 50915, 6342, 11608, 47685, 350, 21910, 50915, 6342, 11608, 382, 17246, 395, 176130, 17913, 741, 23586, 10562, 265, 382, 220, 14397, 23, 364, 7943, 290, 4792, 31064, 10574, 4149, 16336, 265, 2238, 484, 1354, 13031, 448, 16336, 284, 326, 261, 220, 7672, 19, 14, 14397, 22, 130370, 920, 328, 2349, 265, 13, 2632, 6052, 382, 220, 14397, 23, 364, 23483, 316, 6757, 860, 12384, 265, 4149, 395, 1062, 284, 13, 50915, 6342, 11608, 7398, 391, 72554, 8350, 7, 17, 67, 27334, 17, 67, 533, 297, 915, 1919, 297, 66606, 13, 2214, 1039, 3759, 9324, 272, 314, 35964, 7, 3356, 284, 8, 10112, 30, 50557, 7060, 382, 1022, 16611, 91, 5064, 350, 7672, 19, 14, 14397, 22, 8, 284, 13, 12265, 284, 2023, 413, 1062, 16336, 11, 9324, 3759, 382, 540, 5153, 35964, 7, 3356, 284, 741, 2214, 50915, 6342, 80149, 1309, 16336, 48294, 13, 3072, 11608, 5985, 13, 64968, 391, 72554, 8350, 7, 220, 17, 3356, 284, 820, 350, 17, 3356, 284, 533, 284, 8, 1546, 314, 8350, 7, 220, 17, 3356, 820, 350, 17, 3356, 533, 16, 8, 14868, 12265, 284, 81021, 1989, 11, 11608, 13313, 328, 284, 472, 1701, 472, 6551, 29, 16, 14, 17, 13, 2632, 391, 72554, 8350, 7, 220, 17, 3356, 27334, 17, 3356, 12, 16, 8, 1546, 314, 8350, 7, 14397, 23, 741, 2632, 391, 72554, 220, 14397, 23, 13, 31526, 45794, 483, 284, 28, 14397, 22, 13, 2632, 6052, 220, 14397, 23, 364, 84787, 1721, 364], "n_tokens": 1192, "words": 557, "band_lo": 350.5, "band_hi": 1402.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need largest possible integer s such that there exists integer m and a 1024/2047-good set of size s. So fraction δ = 1024/2047 approx 0.5 (actually 1024/2047 ≈ 0.5002). So need subset S of power set of size s where any two distinct subsets differ in at least δ m elements, i.e., Hamming distance at least δ m between characteristic vectors of length m.\n\nThus we need largest possible size of a binary code of length m with minimum Hamming distance d >= δ m, where δ = 1024/2047.\n\nWe can choose m arbitrarily to maximize s. So we need to find the asymptotically maximal size of such a code given relative distance δ, perhaps using Hamming bound or Gilbert-Varshamov bound. Since m can be any integer, we can take the largest possible cardinality over all m. The answer likely is the maximum size of a binary code with relative distance just over 1/2, known as constant weight? Actually distance > m/2. It's known that maximum size is at most O(m) perhaps? For δ > 1/2, the Plotkin bound gives an upper bound of (2δ)/(2δ-1). Wait Plotkin bound: For binary codes with relative distance >1/2, maximum size M <= floor(δ/(2δ-1)). Wait recall: For q-ary codes length n, distance d > n (1 - 1/q), then M <= floor( d / ( d - n (1 - 1/q) ) ). For binary q=2, condition d > n/2. Then M <= floor( 2d / (2d - n) ). Another form: M <= floor( 2δ / (2δ -1) ). Check: d = δ n, then M <= floor( δ/(δ - 1/2) )? Let's compute: For binary case Plotkin: If d > n/2, then M <= floor(2d/(2d - n)). Let's test: suppose d = n/2 +1. Then 2d = n+2, denominator 2d - n =2, so bound M <= floor((n+2)/2) = floor(n/2 +1). So roughly n/2+1. For d close to n/2, bound large maybe ~ O(n). For d > n/2, code size is O(n). So the maximum s probably is some integer perhaps 2047? Let's compute δ = 1024/2047. Then 2δ = 2048/2047 ≈ 1.000488. Then 2δ -1 = (2048/2047) - 1 = 1/2047. So Plotkin bound M ≤ floor( 2δ / (2δ -1) ) = floor( (2048/2047) / (1/2047) ) = floor(2048). So M ≤ 2048. That's from Plotkin bound if d > n/2. Indeed δ= 1024/2047 > 0.5 (just slightly). So bound M <= 2048.\n\nIs this bound attainable? There is a known construction: binary simplex code (Hadamard code) of length n = 2^r -1 has size M = 2^r = n+1, min distance = 2^{r-1}. Relative distance = 1/2. That's exactly 0.5. But we need slightly larger distance (by 1). We may adjust length m = 2047? Let's think.\n\nThe maximum possible size maybe 2048. Can we achieve size 2048? Need code length m such that min distance ≥ 1024m/2047. For m = 2047, this becomes d >= 1024. That's exactly the distance of simplex code of length 2047 that has distance 1024 (since 2^{11}=2048, 2^{10}=1024). Indeed simplex code of length 2^{r}-1 = 2047 for r=11 has size M=2^{11}=2048 codewords, min distance = 2^{10}=1024. Indeed that meets exactly δ = 1024/2047. Perfect! So we can achieve size 2048 with m=2047 (the binary simplex code). This matches the Plotkin bound equality (since Plotkin bound is tight for simplex codes). Thus maximum s is 2048.\n\nBut the problem asks largest possible integer s such that there exists an integer m and a 1024/2047-good set of size s. So answer is 2048.\n\nNeed to ensure no larger s possible for any m. Plotkin bound shows M ≤ floor(2d/(2d - n)) where n=m. For our required distance d = ceil(δ m) maybe? Actually condition is |Δ| >= (1024/2047) m. Since m could be any integer, distance required is at least ceil(δ m). For Plotkin inequality need integer distances. But bound general. Typically M ≤ floor( 2δ m / (2δ m - m) ) = floor( 2δ / (2δ -1) ). Since m cancels, bound independent of m as long as δ>1/2. So M ≤ floor( 2δ/(2δ-1) ) = floor(2048). So M ≤ 2048. Achievable with m=2047. So answer 2048.\n\nThus final.", "response": "ANSWER: 2048", "answer": 2048, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.6689292579030734e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999983310721348, "logp_correct": -1.6689292579030734e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5898353691618768, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1805994683886993, "frac_below_-5": 0.0118946474086661, "digit_lp_min": -4.511050224304199, "answer_first_mention_lp": -1.7881377516459906e-06, "n_cot_tokens": 1177, "lp_end": -0.04109096899628639, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.576272320060525e-06, "logp_nocot": -6.71775144059211, "p_nocot": 0.0012092542392377551, "uplift": 6.717749771662852}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "668e9bd3-6b29-4de8-8a97-612ccf0289d6", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: An 8-by-8 square is divided into 64 unit squares in the usual way. Each unit square is colored black or white. The number of black unit squares is even. We can take two adjacent unit squares (forming a $1 \\times 2$ or $2 \\times 1$ rectangle), and flip their colors: black becomes white and white becomes black. We call this operation a \"step.\" If $C$ is the original coloring, let $S(C)$ be the least number of steps required to make all the unit squares black. Find, with proof, the greatest possible value of $S(C)$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: An 8-by-8 square is divided into 64 unit squares in the usual way. Each unit square is colored black or white. The number of black unit squares is even. We can take two adjacent unit squares (forming a $1 \\times 2$ or $2 \\times 1$ rectangle), and flip their colors: black becomes white and white becomes black. We call this operation a \"step.\" If $C$ is the original coloring, let $S(C)$ be the least number of steps required to make all the unit squares black. Find, with proof, the greatest possible value of $S(C)$.", "true_answer": 32, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "668e9bd3-6b29-4de8-8a97-612ccf0289d6:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 679, 220, 23, 87, 23, 58528, 3935, 40521, 31157, 10581, 5960, 14, 9988, 11, 483, 1952, 2086, 328, 5960, 57269, 350, 786, 113181, 7060, 741, 29600, 25, 5655, 39069, 10610, 350, 7385, 2081, 8, 326, 29130, 2973, 10803, 13, 67160, 25, 1520, 722, 57269, 5960, 13, 19792, 19957, 2086, 328, 10331, 26, 395, 2454, 4871, 50205, 363, 350, 6332, 1952, 2086, 5960, 936, 13174, 336, 5559, 741, 19792, 10562, 4149, 336, 5559, 8, 1072, 722, 363, 52718, 7060, 364, 2167, 