, 328, 290, 1920, 15897, 777, 744, 38328, 13, 21926, 625, 3149, 336, 62, 17, 61, 20, 13, 2632, 1039, 57291, 1340, 413, 1485, 395, 3291, 51957, 2236, 581, 1309, 90457, 14387, 316, 10419, 177015, 13, 730, 5517, 220, 1353, 11, 90457, 14387, 4310, 11, 9874, 945, 29142, 364, 58369, 1746, 3291, 25697, 5517, 220, 1055, 483, 115502, 220, 20, 2454, 328, 2349, 18, 13, 94306, 9506, 25, 5655, 220, 702, 40840, 37806, 13, 2214, 297, 28, 1055, 11, 14387, 1604, 575, 28, 16, 485, 21, 13, 54458, 10419, 4951, 316, 717, 1643, 32297, 328, 39069, 3518, 2454, 744, 13, 63659, 14531, 364, 23483, 316, 7620, 484, 581, 665, 10419, 350, 72, 25032, 10, 20, 8, 11238, 13, 41021, 2075, 316, 12871, 2360, 336, 10853, 326, 336, 22305, 72, 10, 20, 16255, 12265, 336, 10853, 150699, 18125, 723, 72, 485, 72, 10, 19, 60, 483, 723, 72, 10, 20, 485, 72, 10, 24, 1651, 1843, 1815, 581, 25489, 2418, 336, 10853, 30, 21926, 16696, 364, 34013, 2360, 89227, 387, 25, 2244, 10853, 314, 350, 72, 25032, 10, 20, 8, 581, 1682, 13, 1416, 679, 220, 18, 146397, 350, 72, 25032, 10, 20, 25032, 10, 702, 8, 591, 336, 10853, 336, 22305, 72, 10, 16, 16255, 8820, 581, 679, 3613, 220, 18, 146397, 12847, 6544, 13, 623, 185557, 13768, 656, 39069, 220, 18, 3452, 119324, 402, 2543, 665, 10419, 3149, 336, 22305, 455, 111441, 41351, 336, 2170, 382, 13768, 656, 220, 18, 3452, 119324, 350, 16, 220, 17, 220, 18, 936, 350, 17, 220, 18, 220, 19, 936, 60534, 1118, 10419, 355, 2170, 11, 625, 3149, 336, 2170, 13, 19792, 1217, 261, 1643, 4504, 316, 717, 14210, 174595, 13, 12265, 581, 679, 1643, 4504, 350, 72, 25032, 10, 20, 8, 6961, 138743, 581, 665, 717, 1643, 4504, 591, 1888, 328, 1920, 220, 18, 3452, 119324, 30, 2214, 297, 87319, 18, 11, 39069, 220, 18, 3452, 119324, 10419, 355, 2170, 13, 19792, 448, 14210, 135200, 1299, 261, 1643, 4504, 13, 17158, 581, 665, 1217, 717, 261, 1643, 4504, 4493, 336, 10853, 350, 13980, 382, 1888, 328, 220, 20, 1643, 32297, 11, 448, 1952, 135200, 2236, 220, 20, 1643, 32297, 314, 113181, 220, 20, 350, 18303, 741, 50557, 1888, 328, 220, 20, 1643, 32297, 10705, 113181, 382, 14210, 350, 21910, 14210, 2086, 741, 2632, 336, 10853, 382, 14210, 26, 13972, 3566, 6664, 14210, 174595, 11, 13972, 6062, 336, 22305, 455, 92, 143580, 364, 84787, 13768, 3566, 402, 2454, 80677, 744, 382, 22476, 336, 22305, 455, 92, 364, 198140, 2086, 328, 56509, 1082, 9778, 314, 220, 2040, 61, 20, 314, 220, 19, 11, 44400, 11, 7959, 364, 84787, 6052, 364, 58369, 4733, 43831, 13], "n_tokens": 2775, "words": 1654, "band_lo": 774.5, "band_hi": 3098.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the problem. We have 100-digit strings over {1,2}. Operation: pick any 10 consecutive digits, partition into first five and second five (positions i..i+4 and i+5..i+9). Swap the two blocks. So we can permute at block-level.\n\nDefine equivalence relation: two strings are similar if one can be obtained from the other by a sequence of such swaps. We need the size of largest set of strings no two similar i.e., number of equivalence classes. The answer = number of equivalence classes of this relation.\n\nThus we need to compute number of orbits under group generated by these swaps on the set of length-100 binary strings (1/2). This group acts on positions as permutations? Each operation swaps two contiguous blocks of length 5. This is similar to generated by transpositions of adjacent blocks of size 5. The group acts on indices {1..100}. Since swapping blocks of equal size results in permutation of positions: For a particular operation on positions i..i+9, it maps position i+k to i+5+k for k=0..4, and i+5+k to i+k for k=0..4. So it's a permutation that swaps i..i+4 with i+5..i+9, i.e., it's product of 5 transpositions (i,i+5)(i+1,i+6)...(i+4,i+9). So the group G is the subgroup of symmetric group generated by these 5-transposition moves for i=1..91 (since i up to 91 to have 10 digits). We need number of orbits of strings with alphabet {1,2} under G. The action is by permuting positions, so equivalence classes correspond to strings that are same up to permutation by G.\n\nThus number of orbits = number of distinct colorings of 100 positions with colors {1,2} modulo group action = number of orbits of G on 2-colorings = (1/|G|) * sum_{g in G} 2^{c(g)} by Burnside's lemma, where c(g) is number of cycles of permutation g on positions.\n\nBut maybe easier: Since alphabet size 2, the number of orbits equals number of distinct multisets of cycle counts? Actually not. Could maybe find that G acts transitively on positions? The group might be generated by adjacent swaps of distance 5? Let's analyze group structure: The swaps (i,i+5) are not adjacent swaps; they swap positions 5 apart but also swap whole block ranges.\n\nWe can think of the group generated by elementary transpositions τ_i = (i, i+5) for i=1..95? Wait i ranges such that both i and i+5 within 1..100. That's i =1..95.\n\nBut our allowed operation swaps blocks of size 5 simultaneously, i.e., product of transpositions τ_i, τ_{i+1},...,τ_{i+4}. However the group generated by these block swaps might generate each individual τ_i? Let's see if we can get a single transposition using combination of block swaps.\n\nConsider block swap at i and at i+1: swap positions i..i+9 (blocks A (i..i+4) B (i+5..i+9)). Then swap at i+1 (positions i+1..i+10): This may produce effect equivalent to swapping only certain positions. Compute composition? Might be able to generate the transposition (i,i+5) etc. Let's attempt to generate adjacent transpositions of distance 5. Since the block swap is (i i+5)(i+1 i+6)...(i+4 i+9). Compose two overlapping swaps may leave a subset.\n\nDefine S_i = product_{k=0}^{4} (i+k, i+5+k). Then S_i S_{i+1} = ?\n\nWrite S_i = (i,i+5)(i+1,i+6)(i+2,i+7)(i+3,i+8)(i+4,i+9)\nS_{i+1}= (i+1,i+6)(i+2,i+7)(i+3,i+8)(i+4,i+9)(i+5,i+10)\n\nComposition S_i S_{i+1}: many transpositions cancel because they appear twice (they commute because disjoint?) Actually they are disjoint? Overlap: (i+1,i+6) appears in both, so composition results in identity for that transposition (since (a b)(a b)=identity). So product = (i,i+5)(i+5,i+10) after cancellations? Also (i+2,i+7),(i+3,i+8),(i+4,i+9) cancel. So S_i S_{i+1} = (i,i+5)(i+5,i+10). That's a product of two transpositions that are not disjoint? They share i+5. This is a 3-cycle? Actually permutation (i,i+5)(i+5,i+10) = (i i+5 i+10) a 3-cycle. Wait compute: transposition (i,i+5) then (i+5,i+10) gives mapping i -> i+5 -> i+10, i+10 -> i+5 (first transposition leaves i+10 fixed then second maps i+10 to i+5), i+5 -> i (first maps i+5 to i, second then maps i to i? hold on). Let's calculate: apply first (i,i+5): i->i+5, i+5->i. Then apply (i+5,i+10): note now i+5 refers to the element originally i (since after first, i+5 holds i). Actually composition right-to-left: S_{i+1} then S_i? whichever, but still yields a 3-cycle. So group includes 3-cycles of step 5. By generated group, likely the whole group is the subgroup of S_100 generated by transpositions (k, k+5) for all k. Because we can generate those transpositions maybe.\n\nLet's test: Can we get τ_i = (i,i+5) alone? Use S_i S_{i+1} S_i =? maybe yields τ_i. Since we have 3-cycle we may combine.\n\nBut maybe easier: The group generated by S_i (block swaps) may be same as group generated by adjacent transpositions of distance 5, i.e., the symmetric group on the 20 blocks of size5? Wait positions mod 5? Since swapping blocks of length 5 shifts content across 5-step lattice.\n\nObservation: The operation respects residue modulo 5? Let's see: Swapping block A (positions i..i+4) with block B (i+5..i+9) moves each position i+k to i+5+k, which changes its index by +5 (same residue modulo 5). So residues modulo 5 are preserved. Indeed each element stays within its congruence class mod 5. Therefore the group cannot mix residues: it permutes positions within each residue class separately. So there are 5 independent sets each of size 20 (positions 1,6,11,...,96 etc). So group acts as permutations within each of these 5 strands.\n\nThus total positions 100 = 5 * 20. The moves allow swapping within each residue class as we move block windows.\n\nNow can we generate any permutation within each residue class? Likely yes: because the transpositions (i,i+5) swap adjacent elements within the same residue class (since same mod 5). So the group includes adjacent transpositions along each of the 5 sequences of length 20. Since adjacent transpositions generate the full symmetric group on 20 elements, we can permute arbitrarily within each residue class. Need to verify we can produce τ_i = (i,i+5) individually.\n\nWe found S_i S_{i+1} = (i,i+5)(i+5,i+10). That's product of two adjacent transpositions in the same residue class (since i and i+5 same residue). Also we have similar combos allow to isolate a single transposition using conjugation? By generating 3-cycles we can generate full symmetric group; standard fact that adjacent transpositions generate S_n. Since we have ability to generate (i,i+5) and (i+5,i+10) compositions lead to full set of adjacent transpositions, we can generate any symmetric group within each residue class. Let's assume group G = S_{20}^5 (direct product of 5 independent copies). Because moves do not mix residues, and we can achieve any permutation within each class.\n\nThus number of orbits of colorings under G = number of ways to assign colors to each residue class up to permutations within each class. Since within each class, positions are unlabeled; only number of 1's in that class matters (the multiset of colors count). Because any permutation of positions within class can rearrange the 1's arbitrarily; strings are equivalent if they have same count of 1's in each of the 5 residue classes.\n\nThus each equivalence class is determined by a 5-tuple (a1,a2,a3,a4,a5) where a_j is number of 1's among the 20 positions of that class. Since each position can be 1 or 2, a_j can be 0..20. So total number of equivalence classes = 21^5.\n\nTherefore largest set of pairwise non-similar numbers = number of equivalence classes = 21^5.\n\nCompute 21^5 = 21^2=441, 21^3 = 441*21 = 9261, 21^4 = 9261*21 = 194,481? Let's compute: 9261*20=185,220, plus 9261 = 194,481. 21^5 = 194,481*21 = 194,481*20 + 194,481 = 3,889,620 + 194,481 = 4,084,101.\n\nThus answer should be 4,084,101.\n\nCheck if any further constraints: The operation selects arbitrary 10 consecutive digits; first five may change places with second five (swap). It doesn't allow us to rearrange individually within blocks, only whole block swap. Our reasoning gave ability to permute within residue classes; need to be sure we can generate any permutation of each residue class. Using adjacent block swaps we have transpositions (i,i+5) may not be individually realizable, but we argued we can generate them via group.\n\nBut let's test small case: Consider length 10 with residues 5 each of size2. Operation swap two blocks of length 5: that's just swapping entire first half with second half, which swaps each residue class (positions 1 and 6 etc). So group would consist of either identity or full swap of the two positions per class simultaneously. That's not full S_2^5. So our assumption may be false for small lengths because we need overlapping windows to generate adjacency. In length 100, overlapping windows exist, giving more flexibility.\n\nLet's test small scenario length 15 with residues 5 each of size3. Allowed operation: choose 10 consecutive digits. For n=15, windows start i=1..6. Might generate enough to get transpositions of adjacent within each class. Probably yes.\n\nNeed to confirm that we can generate (i,i+5) alone. Let's try to construct using S_i and S_{i+5}. Since S_i swaps blocks [i..i+4] with [i+5..i+9]. If then we swap again S_i? That's identity.\n\nMaybe using conjugation: τ_i = (i,i+5) we want. We have 3-cycle (i,i+5,i+10) from S_i S_{i+1}. Also we have another 3-cycle shift further. The subgroup generated by adjacent 3-cycles on line can generate full S_{20}? Indeed S_n is generated by 3-cycles (1 2 3), (2 3 4),..., which generate A_n, not full S_n. Need also a transposition to get odd permutations. Since we have transposition (i,i+5)?? Possibly we can get transposition from product of two 3-cycles? For n≥3, adjacent 3-cycles generate A_n. Need an odd permutation like a transposition. Maybe we can also get a transposition via S_i (which is product of 5 transpositions, an even permutation because 5 transpositions = parity 5 (odd). Actually product of 5 transpositions overall parity is odd (since odd number). So S_i is odd; thus group includes odd permutations, thus whole S_{20} reachable.\n\nThus generated group on each residue class is indeed S_{20}.\n\nHence number of equivalence classes = 21^5 = 4,084,101.\n\nThus answer.\n\nLet's output formatted.", "response": "ANSWER: 4084101", "answer": 4084101, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.4305104514278355e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999985694905718, "logp_correct": -1.4305104514278355e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.605381520448421, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3000862834253226, "frac_below_-5": 0.009786154403769481, "digit_lp_min": -1.8047633171081543, "answer_first_mention_lp": null, "n_cot_tokens": 2759, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.814689989667386e-06, "logp_nocot": -16.733162923854252, "p_nocot": 5.406045260539761e-08, "uplift": 16.7331614933438}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "90d6ce31-d527-4ad6-865d-69a799e59ef4", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Let $\\mathbb{N}$ denote the natural numbers. Compute the number of functions $f:\\mathbb{N}\\rightarrow \\{0, 1, \\dots, 16\\}$ such that $$f(x+17)=f(x)\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad f(x^2)\\equiv f(x)^2+15 \\pmod {17}$$ for all integers $x\\ge 1$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Let $\\mathbb{N}$ denote the natural numbers. Compute the number of functions $f:\\mathbb{N}\\rightarrow \\{0, 1, \\dots, 16\\}$ such that $$f(x+17)=f(x)\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad f(x^2)\\equiv f(x)^2+15 \\pmod {17}$$ for all integers $x\\ge 1$.", "true_answer": 12066, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "90d6ce31-d527-4ad6-865d-69a799e59ef4:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 3605, 9964, 285, 25, 478, 15155, 354, 15, 35229, 11, 1125, 92, 52718, 5265, 220, 1422, 350, 21910, 11819, 6247, 8663, 11, 889, 7060, 4335, 395, 722, 54912, 1215, 87319, 16, 25, 285, 4061, 10, 1422, 25987, 69, 4061, 8, 1127, 60546, 1812, 220, 1422, 741, 8820, 3099, 7060, 25, 285, 4061, 61, 17, 8, 42308, 94, 285, 4061, 73602, 17, 659, 220, 1055, 350, 4847, 220, 1422, 741, 12265, 26163, 6133, 382, 290, 115502, 112372, 220, 1422, 350, 15, 485, 1125, 741, 2632, 1114, 4824, 553, 115502, 11, 326, 7060, 112372, 220, 1422, 29212, 32409, 2352, 1373, 1812, 220, 1422, 13, 12265, 26163, 6133, 9913, 316, 220, 15, 485, 1125, 11, 581, 665, 4665, 1373, 472, 115502, 364, 30105, 60546, 483, 5265, 220, 1422, 11, 9113, 656, 4824, 402, 115502, 112372, 220, 1422, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 402, 920, 354, 16, 35229, 11, 1422, 92, 10112, 220, 15, 472, 1775, 30, 12265, 6247, 8663, 10112, 1604, 540, 220, 16, 11, 889, 60546, 7060, 395, 722, 1215, 87319, 16, 25, 285, 4061, 10, 1422, 25987, 69, 4061, 741, 2632, 29