 220, 24928, 21, 364, 410, 3638, 9994, 418, 17, 410, 1402, 12, 274, 62, 16, 28, 1125, 11, 274, 62, 1125, 28, 1125, 11, 336, 62, 1125, 28, 16, 558, 12, 274, 62, 19, 19387, 220, 1125, 11, 274, 62, 1311, 19387, 220, 1125, 350, 21910, 220, 16, 15796, 741, 46059, 364, 2701, 220, 19, 126158, 581, 46716, 1402, 66, 62, 17, 326, 274, 62, 1055, 2454, 2804, 413, 44237, 328, 220, 16, 350, 16, 503, 220, 1125, 8, 889, 2617, 483, 1432, 220, 16, 4124, 316, 336, 28, 18, 15796, 11, 813, 19387, 220, 1125, 30, 24305, 1369, 27187, 25, 2214, 4596, 220, 19, 483, 274, 62, 19, 28, 1125, 11, 3953, 220, 17, 326, 220, 1055, 2804, 413, 44237, 328, 220, 16, 25, 3880, 220, 16, 503, 220, 1125, 13, 3072, 1815, 581, 1309, 316, 2371, 1043, 2316, 120140, 364, 3335, 274, 62, 17, 28, 16, 871, 336, 62, 17, 314, 220, 16, 12, 1055, 28, 18, 2893, 12, 45481, 15155, 4596, 220, 17, 885, 3953, 6284, 413, 21482, 13, 19233, 484, 7158, 15796, 13, 65037, 274, 62, 1055, 28, 16, 15796, 13, 2632, 2973, 19387, 220, 1125, 13, 2632, 1606, 1001, 5317, 395, 2454, 11, 3609, 220, 16, 364, 19371, 15657, 1641, 220, 17, 326, 220, 1055, 2454, 679, 220, 17, 14494, 350, 16, 503, 220, 1125, 8, 2236, 1023, 1309, 13749, 29895, 328, 336, 28, 16, 11, 1118, 382, 220, 16, 503, 220, 1125, 13, 21079, 25882, 4824, 220, 16, 503, 220, 1125, 553, 9279, 350, 33574, 553, 625, 8783, 741, 3004, 6544, 31060, 13, 2632, 2454, 25882, 13313, 871, 2454, 10610, 220, 19, 150243, 13, 2632, 3609, 25882, 150243, 314, 19, 9, 19, 28, 1125, 13, 19233, 4596, 220, 19, 126158, 3609, 314, 1125, 13, 9700, 364, 2701, 220, 1311, 126158, 25, 274, 62, 1311, 28, 1125, 16691, 274, 62, 23, 22261, 62, 24, 2804, 413, 44237, 328, 220, 16, 350, 16, 503, 220, 1125, 8, 889, 2418, 220, 16, 15796, 11, 813, 19387, 220, 1125, 2454, 11, 64699, 13, 7801, 15657, 1641, 2454, 679, 220, 17, 3880, 2454, 2747, 220, 19, 150243, 2454, 11, 3609, 220, 1125, 13, 2632, 1888, 220, 1125, 9, 1125, 314, 5780, 364, 84787, 418, 17, 3609, 314, 5780, 364, 52859, 7890, 20434, 364, 10620, 1147, 274, 62, 15, 12332, 328, 220, 17, 25, 3609, 220, 6106, 3618, 364, 4383, 538, 1062, 6544, 31060, 10539, 11819, 6247, 8663, 8601, 540, 220, 16, 2023, 7902, 1432, 540, 1215, 28, 1422, 30, 3072, 60546, 30005, 5519, 9113, 13, 274, 62, 15, 66938, 316, 1215, 28, 1422, 350, 267, 220, 15, 538, 581, 3331, 112372, 936, 2928, 3518, 11819, 147584, 15, 741, 2632, 7158, 9279, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 6106, 3618, 364, 58369, 6757, 1534, 23440, 3518, 16336, 3352, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 12765, "words": 5881, "band_lo": 2884.25, "band_hi": 11537.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to count functions f: N → {0,...,16} satisfying period 17 (since domain natural numbers, but condition given for all integers x≥1: f(x+17)=f(x) – periodic mod 17). Also second condition: f(x^2) ≡ f(x)^2 + 15 (mod 17). Since codomain is the residues modulo 17 (0..16). So function values are residues, and condition modulo 17 essentially constrains them mod 17. Since codomain limited to 0..16, we can treat them as residues.\n\nBecause periodic with period 17, defined by values on residues modulo 17 (i.e., on set {1,...,17} maybe 0 as well? Since natural numbers maybe start at 1, but periodic condition for all x≥1: f(x+17)=f(x). So the values at x=1..17 determine all. Also need values at 0? Domain is N (natural numbers) maybe includes 0? Usually N = {1,2,...}, but clarify: it says for all integers x≥1, so domain N maybe positive. Therefore we need define f on residues modulo 17, but we only have residues 1..17 (mod 17). Note that 17 mod 17 is 0 equivalently, but x can be 17. So we have 17 distinct residue classes: 1,...,16,0 (represented by 17). So need values for these 17 inputs.\n\nWe need consistency with second condition for all x≥1. Since periodic, we can reduce arguments modulo 17 as well? Need to consider x^2 modulo 17. Since f(x^2) depends on x^2 perhaps not reduced modulo 17 directly, but periodic means f(x^2) = f( (x^2 mod 17) or plus multiples of 17). Since we can replace x^2 by its residue class mod 17 (with representative 1..17). So second condition reduces to condition for each residue a (1..17) such that for any x with x ≡ a (mod 17), we have f( (a^2 mod 17) as integer in [1..17]) ≡ f(a)^2 +15 mod 17. Because f depends only on residue mod 17, the equation must hold for each residue a.\n\nThus we need function values c_a = f(a) for a in set S = {0,1,2,...,16} where we use a=0 represent 17. Condition: c_{a^2 mod 17} ≡ c_a^2 +15 (mod 17).\n\nNow we must count assignments c_a ∈ {0,…,16} satisfying this functional equation.\n\nWe need graph of mapping a → a^2 mod 17. Since 17 is prime, the map squares modulo 17. Note that 0 maps to 0 (since a=0). For a≠0, squares map into quadratic residues.\n\nWe need to consider cycles of this mapping, and condition imposes relation.\n\nLet's compute squares modulo 17:\n\nCompute residues:\n\n0^2=0\n1^2=1\n2^2=4\n3^2=9\n4^2=16≡-1≡16\n5^2=25≡8\n6^2=36≡2\n7^2=49≡ -? 49-34=15\n8^2=64≡13 (since 64-51=13)\n9^2=81≡81-68=13? Wait 68 is 4*17, 81-68=13 again. Actually 9^2 same as 8^2? Because 9 ≡ -8 (mod 17), square same.\n\n10^2=100≡100-85=15\n11^2=121 ≡121-102=19? 17*7=119, remainder2. So 11^2 ≡ 2.\n12^2=144 ≡144-136=8\n13^2=169 ≡169-  17*9=153 remainder 16? Wait 9*17=153, 169-153=16.\n14^2=196 ≡196-  17*11=187 remainder9.\n15^2=225 ≡225-  17*13=221 remainder4.\n16^2=256 ≡256-  17*15=255 remainder1.\n\nSo mapping squares:\n\n0→0\n1→1\n2→4\n3→9\n4→16\n5→8\n6→2\n7→15\n8→13\n9→13\n10→15\n11→2\n12→8\n13→16\n14→9\n15→4\n16→1\n\nSimplify: We consider residues 0-16.\n\nNow the condition: c_{square(a)} = c_a^2 +15 (mod 17).\n\nLet’s denote mapping s(a)=a^2 mod17.\n\nWe need to find all assignments c_a satisfying for each a.\n\nObserve that if we apply condition twice: c_{s(s(a))} = c_{s(a)}^2 +15 = (c_a^2 +15)^2 +15 mod 17.\n\nBut also directly c_{s(s(a))} must equal c_a^2' ??? Not needed maybe leads to constraints.\n\nWe can view each connected component of functional digraph of s (directed edges a→s(a)). Since s(a) may have multiple preimages. The condition provides equations on each node in terms of its predecessor(s). For each a, we have equation referencing c_{s(a)} in terms of c_a. Also for any b that has multiple preimages a1,a2, we get possibly different equations for same c_b => constraints.\n\nThus we must ensure consistency for nodes with multiple incoming edges.\n\nLet's compute preimage sets.\n\nWe can produce groups:\n\n0's only preimage: a such that a^2 ≡0 => a≡0 mod17. So only a=0.\n\n1's preimages: a such that a^2≡1 => a≡±1 i.e., 1,16. Indeed s(1)=1, s(16)=1.\n\n2's preimages: a such that a^2≡2 => a ≡6 or11.\n\n4's preimages: a=2,15\n\n8's preimages: 5,12\n\n9's preimages: 3,14\n\n13's preimages: 8,9\n\n15's preimages: 7,10\n\n16's preimages: 4,13\n\nAlso note that s of quadratic residues gives another quadratic residue set.\n\nNow we need for each a, c_{s(a)} = c_a^2 +15.\n\nThus we can propagate values along the mapping.\n\nSince each node may have multiple incoming edges, we must ensure for each node b, for all a with s(a)=b, we have same RHS value.\n\nThus for each b, for any two preimages a1,a2 we must have c_{a1}^2 +15 ≡ c_{a2}^2 +15 (mod 17) => c_{a1}^2 ≡ c_{a2}^2 (mod 17). So squares of the preimage values must be equal modulo 17.\n\nThus condition: For each pair of a with same square, their values must have equal squares. Not necessarily equal values themselves, could be negatives (since mod 17, if u^2 ≡ v^2 then u ≡ ± v). So we need for each b, c values on its preimages must be either equal or negatives (mod 17), but squares equal.\n\nThus we can attempt to assign c_a with constraints.\n\nLet's examine structure components:\n\nWe have fixed points: a such that s(a)=a: they are 0 and 1.\n\nCheck a=0: equation c_0 = c_0^2 +15 mod17 => c_0^2 - c_0 +15 ≡0 mod17. Solve.\n\nSimilarly a=1: c_1 = c_1^2 +15 => c_1^2 - c_1 +15 ≡0 mod17.\n\nAlso note a=... any other fixed point? maybe? Let's check: solve a such that a^2 ≡ a mod 17 => a(a-1) ≡0 => a=0 or 1. So only those.\n\nNow other nodes are not fixed, they map eventually into cycles? Let's see mapping chains.\n\nFrom listing:\n\n2→4 →16 →1 →1 (since s(1)=1). So chain: 2→4→16→1. Also 15→4 as well? Wait s(15)=4, so 15 goes to 4 then same chain.\n\nSimilarly 6 →2 →4→16→1.\n\n11→2.\n\nThus many nodes eventually end up at 1 (the fixed point). Similarly 3→9→13→16→1. And 14→9.\n\n8→13 (since s(8)=13) then 13→16→1. Also 9→13 as above.\n\n5→8→13... etc.\n\n7→15→4... etc.\n\n10→15→...\n\nThus graph is rooted at 1 (fixed) and 0 separate.\n\nThus the equations propagate towards 1: c at deeper nodes determines c at parent via equation.\n\nSpecifically, for each a, c_{s(a)} is determined by c_a: c_{s(a)} = c_a^2 +15. So given values at leaves, you compute upwards. However consistency imposes that if a node has multiple children (preimages), the computed c_{node} from each child must be equal (the squares condition derived). So we must assign values to all nodes such that for each set of preimages, the squares of their assigned values are equal.\n\nWe can think of assigning values to each node in a way that for each node b, all preimages a satisfy c_a ≡ ε_a * t_b where ε_a = ±1 maybe, with same square t_b^2, where t_b is some value modulo 17, and c_b = t_b^2 +15.\n\nBut also those c_a themselves must satisfy their own equations wrt their own parents.\n\nThus we can start from root 1 and 0. Determine allowed c_1 and c_0 that satisfy fixed-point equation and also need to be compatible with squares of preimages.\n\nLet’s solve fixed point equations.\n\nWe need solutions modulo 17.\n\nFunction: solve c^2 - c +15 ≡0 mod 17.\n\nCompute discriminant Δ = 1 - 4*15 = 1 - 60 = -59 ≡ 17 - (59 mod 17). 59 mod 17 = 8 (since 17*3=51). So -59 ≡ -8 ≡ 9 mod 17 (since 17-8=9). So Δ ≡ 9. Square roots of 9 mod17 are ±3 (since 3^2=9, 14^2=196 ≡ 196- 170=26? 26-17=9). So roots are (1 ± 3)/2 mod17. Need division by 2 (inverse 9 because 2*9=18≡1). Compute:\n\nCase +: (1+3)=4; 4 * 9 ≡ 36 ≡ 2 (since 34 =2*17). So c ≡ 2.\n\nCase -: (1-3) = -2 ≡15. 15*9 =135 ≡ 135-119=16. So c ≡16.\n\nThus c_1 ∈ {2,16}. Similarly c_0 must satisfy same equation, so c_0 ∈ {2,16}.\n\nNow need to consider compatibility with preimages.\n\nConsider node 1: preimages are a=1 and 16. Wait also maybe some others? Actually preimages of 1 are 1 and 16 only (since squares giving 1). So we need c_1^2 = c_{16}^2 mod17 (they must have equal squares). That is, squares of c_1 and c_16 must be equal.\n\nBut also the equation for a=16: c_{s(16)} = c_{16}^2 +15 => s(16)=1, so c_1 = c_16^2 +15.\n\nSimilarly, from a=1: c_1 = c_1^2 +15 (which already holds). Both must be consistent.\n\nGiven c_1 known (2 or 16), we can solve for c_16 that satisfies:\n\nc_1 ≡ c_16^2 +15 (mod 17)\n\n=> c_16^2 ≡ c_1 -15 ≡ c_1 +2 (since -15 ≡ 2). Because 17-15=2.\n\nThus c_16^2 ≡ c_1 +2.\n\nCompute possibilities:\n\nIf c_1=2: then c_16^2 ≡ 2+2 =4 => c_16 ≡ ±2 (since 2^2=4, 15^2=225≡... 225-204=21-17=4 also -2). So c_16 ≡ 2 or 15.\n\nIf c_1=16: then c_16^2 ≡ 16+2=18≡1 => c_16 ≡ ±1 => 1 or 16.\n\nNow also need squares of c_1 and c_16 equal? Actually we need c_1^2 ≡ c_16^2? Wait condition earlier from preimages of 1: both preimages are 1 and 16, need c_1^2 ≡ c_16^2 (since they map to same parent). Let's check.\n\nIf c_1=2 => c_1^2 =4. For c_16 possibilities: 2 => square 4, 15 => square 225 ≡ 4 also. So both have square 4, fine.\n\nIf c_1=16 => c_1^2 =256 ≡1. c_16 possibilities: 1 => square 1; 16 => square 1. Good.\n\nThus both cases okay.\n\nNow move to node 0: preimage only 0, so no extra restriction besides fixed-point equation already solved. So c_0 = 2 or 16.\n\nNow propagate up: compute c values for other nodes using relation from children.\n\nBut there may be multiple choices due to ± signs at each branching, as long as square constraints hold.\n\nEssentially we need to assign a value to each node such that for each node b, there exists t_b such that for all a ∈ preimage(b), c_a ∈ {t_b, -t_b} (mod 17) and also c_b = t_b^2 +15.\n\nThus the degrees of freedom are the choices of t_b for each node b (maybe extra constraints deeper). Since a node may also be a preimage for higher node.\n\nLet's process the graph from top (root 1) downward? Actually relation goes from child to parent: given c_a we get c_parent. So the value of parent is determined by child's value (its square). However if node has multiple children, consistency forces their squares equal => they all share same t (up to sign). So effectively each node b must have a designated square t_b^2 (i.e., t_b) which is determined by any of its children, but if node is also child of another node, its value must both satisfy its own parent equation and also be either ± t_b for its own parent? Wait confusion.\n\nBetter approach: treat each node a has value c_a. The condition for edge a→b (where b = s(a)) is: c_b = c_a^2 +15. So c_b is function of c_a alone. Therefore if b has multiple incoming edges from a1,a2,... then we must have c_a1^2 = c_a2^2 = ... leading to same c_b. So essentially the values c_a for all a that map to same b must have equal squares. That is a constraint, but c_b is then determined as that common square +15.\n\nThus we can think of each node b chooses a square value S_b = c_a^2 (same for all its preimages a). Then c_b = S_b +15. Moreover, each a (child) must have a value whose square is S_b. So each child can be either sqrt(S_b) or its negative.\n\nThus degrees of freedom propagate from top (roots) backward: starting from node 1 (already determined). For its children 1 and 16, the squares must be S_1 = c_1 -15 ≡ earlier computed as c_16^2 = c_1 -15, consistent. That's okay.\n\nNow consider node 4: its preimages are a=2 and 15. Both must have squares equal S_4, and then c_4 = S_4 +15.\n\nBut also node 2 is child of 4 (since s(2)=4). So the relationship between c_2 and c_4: c_4 = c_2^2 +15. So S_4 = c_2^2. Similarly from a=15: c_4 = c_15^2 +15, so c_15^2 = c_2^2 => squares equal.\n\nThus we could pick a value for c_2 (any of 0..16) plus sign? Actually we have constraints further: node 2's parent is 4, its child? Node 2 also preimages: 6 and 11 map to 2. So constraints will continue.\n\nThus overall we have a tree (actually each node has up to 2 preimages). The value for each node determines its parent, but not vice versa (since parent value fixed as function of child). So we can choose values for leaves (nodes with no preimages). Which nodes are leaves? Those that have no preimages aside from themselves? Let's check: Are there any nodes that are not square of any other number except themselves? In mapping squares, nodes that are not quadratic residues? Actually all residues except 0 are images of exactly two squares (since for each quadratic residue there are two square roots). But the mapping s(a)=a^2; preimages of a include solutions to x^2 ≡ a. For quadratic residues (including 0,1) there are two preimages (except 0 and 1 have 1 each). For non-residues (i.e., numbers not squares), there are zero preimages (they are not squares of any residue). Looking at list of squares set: squares modulo 17 are {0,1,2,4,8,9,13,15,16}. That's 9 elements. The other 8 residues {3,5,6,7,10,11,12,14} are not squares.\n\nThus for non-residue b, there is no a with s(a)=b. That means condition for those b: we still have equation for each a (preimage none), but we still have equation from a which maps to some b (i.e., each a leads to some b). But nodes that are not squares are never a target. Therefore c_b is not determined by any child's equation (no children). However they still have equation describing their own parent: c_{s(b)} = c_b^2 +15. So they are leaves in opposite direction: they have outgoing edge to parent, but no incoming edges.\n\nThus free choices can be made for c_b at those non-residue nodes (including also maybe 0 and 1 are residues but have themselves as preimages). Leaves of the \"inverse graph\" are nodes with no preimages (non-residues). Since they don't have constraints from multiple children, they can be chosen arbitrarily, but subsequent propagation must satisfy later consistency where their parent may have multiple children (including possibly one leaf and another node). The requirement that squares of children be equal will impose constraints.\n\nThus for each residue b (target) that has two preimages a1,a2, we need the squares of c_{a1} and c_{a2} equal. That means whenever we choose values for a1 and a2 (could be leaves or themselves have children), they must have same square.\n\nTherefore we can treat each pair of preimages {a1,a2} as requiring that c_{a1} = ± c_{a2}. So we can decide a sign relation.\n\nThus overall degrees of freedom: For each unordered pair of elements that are square roots of same quadratic residue (b), we must have c_{a1}=±c_{a2}. Their squares equal S_b. Then c_b = S_b +15, determined.\n\nThus we can approach layer by layer: choose values for \"leaf nodes\" (non-residues) arbitrarily (0..16). Then for each parent node b (which is a quadratic residue), S_b must equal the square of its child value (which are possibly multiple). Since both children squares must be same, we must have that the square values of its two children (if both leaves) must be equal, which imposes constraint on choices for leaves: they must have squares equal. That restricts possibilities.\n\nLet's identify the structure:\n\nQuadratic residues set Q = {0,1,2,4,8,9,13,15,16}. Non-residues N = {3,5,6,7,10,11,12,14}.\n\nMapping s: each element maps to another element (could be any).\n\nLet's list s for each element:\n\n0→0 (fixed)\n1→1 (fixed)\n2→4\n3→9 (3 in N maps to 9∈Q)\n4→16\n5→8\n6→2\n7→15\n8→13\n9→13\n10→15\n11→2\n12→8\n13→16\n14→9\n15→4\n16→1\n\nNow let's group by parents:\n\nParent 0: children: only 0.\n\nParent 1: children: 1,16\n\nParent 2: children: 6,11\n\nParent 4: children: 2,15\n\nParent 8: children:5,12\n\nParent 9: children:3,14\n\nParent 13: children:8,9\n\nParent 15: children:7,10\n\nParent 16: children:4,13\n\nThus a tree directed towards 1 (except 0). Indeed each child heavier.\n\nLeaves (no children) are those which are not in \"children list\": Let's see which elements appear as children? The set of all children: {0,1,16,6,11,2,15,5,12,3,14,8,9,7,10,4,13}. This includes all 0..16 except maybe some? Actually includes every element. So every node has at least one child (except maybe some). Wait leaves are nodes that are not parents of anyone (i.e., have no preimages). Those are non-residues (since they are not squares). Indeed they appear only as children, not as parents (i.e., they have outgoing edge to some parent but no incoming). So yes leaves are N = {3,5,6,7,10,11,12,14}. They have exactly one child (they are a) mapping to a residue parent. Since they have no siblings? They are paired into preimage sets of their parent residue.\n\nThus each non-residue leaf is part of a pair of preimages for some residue: for example, 3 and 14 are preimages of 9.\n\nThus the constraints: for each residue parent b ∈ Q\\{0,1}, its two children are either both non-residues or one non-residue and one residue? Let's check the list:\n\nParent 2: children 6 (non-residue) and 11 (non-residue) both leaves.\n\nParent 4: children 2 (residue) and 15 (non-residue). So mixed.\n\nParent 8: children 5 (non-residue) and 12 (non-residue) both leaves.\n\nParent 9: children 3 (non-residue) and 14 (non-residue) leaves.\n\nParent 13: children 8 (residue) and 9 (residue). Both residues (non-leaves).\n\nParent 15: children 7 (non-),10 (non-) leaves.\n\nParent 16: children 4 (residue) and 13 (residue).\n\nThus we have a hierarchical structure.\n\nWe need to assign values consistent across each parent.\n\nWe could compute number of possible assignments by counting assignments of values to leaves satisfying constraints up the chain.\n\nLet's work bottom-up.\n\nDefine variable for each node a: c_a.\n\nWe know c_0 ∈ {2,16} two choices (independent of rest).\n\nc_1 ∈ {2,16} two choices, which will determine possibilities for its children.\n\nNow consider parent 16: children 4 and 13. Both are residues (higher up). Constraint: c_4^2 ≡ c_13^2 (mod 17). Also c_16 = c_4^2 +15 = c_13^2 +15.\n\nBut c_16 is already determined from earlier: c_16 is either 2 or 15 (depending on c_1). Wait earlier we derived possible c_16 values based on chosen c_1: if c_1=2 then c_16∈{2,15}; if c_1=16 then c_16∈{1,16}. Actually from relation c_1 = c_16^2 +15 gave c_16 possibilities; but also c_16 can be any value that together with c_1 satisfies equations.\n\nThus c_16 is not independently chosen after we chose c_1; it's constrained to those values. So we have multiple possibilities.\n\nSimilarly for c_13: it's a parent of 8 and 9, so its value must satisfy constraints.\n\nLet's attempt systematic counting.\n\nWe can treat each node b, define S_b = c_b - 15 (mod 17). Then c_{parent} = S_child + 15 ??? Wait using relation: c_parent = c_child^2 + 15 => So define for each edge child a → parent b: S_a = c_a^2 => then c_b = S_a +15.\n\nThus S_a = c_a^2.\n\nSo for each node a, we can compute S_a as square of its value.\n\nNow for parent b, all its children a must have same S_a (since c_b determined). So define S_b_child? Actually S of child equals same for both children, denote T_b = common square (value of S for children). Then c_b = T_b + 15.\n\nAdditionally, the children themselves may be parents of other nodes, imposing further structure: for each child a, we also have T_{a}? No, a's own children have squares equal to something else.\n\nThus we can think of a labeling of each node with a value c, with constraint that c_b -15 is a quadratic residue (since it's a square S). Also for each parent b, its children must have c values whose squares equal the same S = c_b -15.\n\nThus we can treat S_b = c_b -15 . Then for each node b, its children a satisfy c_a^2 = S_b.\n\nThus the number of possibilities can be counted recursively: For each node b, we need to assign a square root value(s) to each child a: each child