, 625, 306, 920, 13, 2632, 860, 9368, 364, 6187, 457, 25, 272, 17, 28, 20, 11, 272, 18, 28, 18, 364, 64, 17, 314, 261, 20, 659, 20, 19786, 13, 3004, 9368, 364, 84787, 3609, 9368, 395, 372, 21714, 16, 1890, 25, 220, 16, 10, 17, 314, 18, 9368, 364, 84787, 3609, 174595, 483, 9882, 72554, 19, 314, 2086, 328, 9368, 314, 220, 18, 10, 18, 314, 21, 364, 4383, 538, 1062, 174595, 60500, 7598, 30, 3164, 553, 16913, 2236, 261, 16, 23839, 21, 2647, 364, 84787, 3609, 174595, 484, 3644, 9882, 72554, 19, 382, 220, 21, 364, 84787, 9882, 9707, 220, 20, 3605, 314, 220, 21514, 533, 220, 21, 314, 220, 43518, 364, 7943, 6757, 484, 9882, 2023, 413, 3760, 1572, 220, 19, 10112, 30, 21079, 220, 21, 174595, 3572, 11095, 316, 9882, 72554, 19, 26, 2023, 413, 9882, 72554, 18, 5178, 13, 25758, 52015, 13, 2632, 6052, 220, 43518, 364, 58369, 3503, 30411, 484, 581, 23399, 20677, 1062, 1890, 1919, 372, 28, 15, 889, 261, 16, 533, 261, 21, 314, 15, 19786, 472, 16913, 13, 2632, 22476, 860, 13, 2632, 1606, 220, 21, 174595, 553, 5597, 72554, 19, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 43518, 364, 4383, 94610, 25, 7744, 120152, 1991, 174595, 3644, 3149, 9882, 220, 20, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 220, 43518, 13], "n_tokens": 4853, "words": 2052, "band_lo": 1317.25, "band_hi": 5269.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need number of real-coefficient polynomials P of degree exactly 5 such that P maps the set {1,2,3,4,5,6} onto itself as a permutation (i.e., the values P(1),...,P(6) are a permutation of {1,...,6}). Also degree exactly 5.\n\nWe need count distinct polynomials.\n\nObservation: For a polynomial degree ≤5, given 6 points (x_i, y_i), there is at most one polynomial of degree ≤5 fitting them (unique interpolation). So each permutation corresponds to at most one polynomial of degree ≤5. Some permutations may correspond to degree less than 5, which we must exclude. Also some permutations may not be realizable by any degree ≤5 polynomial.\n\nBut the condition \"degree exactly 5\" suggests we count permutations where the unique interpolating polynomial of degree ≤5 actually has degree 5.\n\nSo we need to count permutations σ of {1,...,6} such that the interpolating polynomial through points (i, σ(i)) has degree exactly 5.\n\nEquivalently, the Vandermonde matrix invertible, there is always a unique degree ≤5 polynomial passing through any 6 data points with distinct x-values. Actually any 6 points with distinct x-values determine a unique polynomial of degree at most 5. Yes. So for any permutation σ, there exists a unique polynomial of degree ≤5 interpolating. The question: does that polynomial ever have degree less than 5? It could be degree ≤4 if the points happen to lie on a lower-degree polynomial. So we subtract those permutations where the points lie on degree ≤4.\n\nThus total permutations = 6! =720. Subtract those where interpolant degree ≤4.\n\nSo count permutations that are \"non-degenerate\". Need number of permutations whose points lie on a degree ≤4 polynomial.\n\nWe need to count permutations σ for which there exists a polynomial q of degree ≤4 such that q(i)=σ(i) for i=1..6.\n\nThat's equivalent to σ being extendable by degree ≤4.\n\nSince we have 6 points, degree ≤4 means points are not generic; they must satisfy a linear relation among the Vandermonde vectors.\n\nWe can think in terms of finite differences: For degree ≤4, the 5th finite difference is zero. For equally spaced x (1..6), define sequence a_i = σ(i). The 5th forward difference Δ^5 a_i =0 for all i (only need one value). So condition: the 5th difference of the sequence is zero.\n\nCompute Δ^1 a_i = a_{i+1} - a_i for i=1..5.\nΔ^2: differences again, etc. The 5th difference is constant (for any sequence length 6) equal to sum with binomial coefficients: Δ^5 a_1 = Σ_{k=0}^{5} (-1)^{5-k} * C(5,k) * a_{k+1} = 0.\n\nThus condition: Σ_{k=0}^{5} (-1)^{5-k} C(5,k) a_{k+1} =0.\n\nCompute coefficients: For n=5, forward difference formula: Δ^5 a_1 = a_6 -5 a_5 +10 a_4 -10 a_3 +5 a_2 - a_1 =0.\n\nSo we need permutations a_i of {1..6} such that a_6 -5 a_5 +10 a_4 -10 a_3 +5 a_2 - a_1 =0.\n\nWe need count of permutations satisfying that equation.\n\nLet’s denote a1..a6 distinct from 1..6.\n\nWe need sum S = a6 -5 a5 +10 a4 -10 a3 +5 a2 - a1 =0.\n\nCount number of permutations meeting this linear equation.\n\nWe could attempt to enumerate logically or find symmetry.\n\nNote that coefficients sum to 0? Sum of coeffs: -1 +5 -10 +10 -5 +1 =0 indeed. So S is linear combination with sum zero.\n\nWe need to count permutations where weighted sum zero.\n\nWe can think of assigning values to positions, compute S.\n\nBrute force mental? Could use algorithmic reasoning. Since 6! =720 relatively small, we could think of using generating functions or symmetry: For random permutation, S's distribution symmetrical? Expect number zero maybe 720/?? Let's compute expected value of S: E[S]= sum coeff_i * E[a_i] = each a_i has average (1+...+6)/6 = 21/6 = 3.5. Then E[S]= ( -1 +5 -10 +10 -5 +1) * 3.5 = 0. So mean zero.\n\nWe need count of permutations where S=0 exactly. Since coefficients are integers, S takes integer values. Could compute number via counting solutions to Σ c_i a_i =0 with distinct a_i between 1..6.\n\nWe could attempt to compute via inclusion/exclusion or using generating functions.\n\nLet’s define positions with coefficients: c1 = -1, c2 = 5, c3 = -10, c4 = 10, c5 = -5, c6 = 1.\n\nSo S = Σ c_i a_i =0.\n\nWe need permutation of numbers 1..6.\n\nWe could think of pairing numbers to satisfy equation. Since coefficients sum zero, any constant shift to all a_i cancels; but numbers are fixed.\n\nMaybe we can consider using the fact that coefficients are antisymmetric: c_i = - c_{7-i}. Indeed c1=-1 = -c6? c6=1, yes. c2=5 = -c5 (-5), c3=-10 = -c4 (10). So symmetry around center.\n\nThus S = Σ_{i=1}^{3} c_i (a_i - a_{7-i}). Because c_i + c_{7-i}=0.\n\nCompute S = -1*(a1 - a6) +5*(a2 - a5) -10*(a3 - a4). So S=0 equivalent to - (a1 - a6) +5 (a2 - a5) -10 (a3 - a4)=0.\n\nOr (a1 - a6) =5 (a2 - a5) -10 (a3 - a4).\n\nSimplify: a1 - a6 =5(a2 - a5) -10(a3 - a4).\n\nSince values are 1..6 distinct. The RHS is integer multiple of 5 and 10; range? Max difference a2 - a5 between -5 and 5. Similarly a3 - a4 between -5 and5.\n\nCompute RHS can take values maybe -55 to 55 but limited by small numbers.\n\nLet's enumerate possibilities of differences. Let d2 = a2 - a5, d3 = a3 - a4.\n\nThen RHS = 5 d2 -10 d3 =5(d2 -2 d3).\n\nThus a1 - a6 must be multiple of 5. Since a1 and a6 are integers from 1..6, their difference is between -5 and 5. The only multiples of 5 in that range are -5,0,5.\n\nSo a1 - a6 ∈{-5,0,5}.\n\nThus we need to find assignments where 5(d2 -2 d3) equals that.\n\nCase 1: a1 - a6 =5 => RHS =5 => d2 -2 d3 =1.\n\nCase 2: a1 - a6 =0 => RHS=0 => d2 -2 d3 =0.\n\nCase 3: a1 - a6 = -5 => RHS = -5 => d2 -2 d3 = -1.\n\nNow d2 = a2 - a5, d3 = a3 - a4 each integer between -5..5 not zero? could be zero if values equal? But distinctness across all six ensures a2 ≠ a5, a3 ≠ a4, but could be zero? difference zero implies equal, impossible, so d2 ≠0 and d3 ≠0 possibly but could be zero if numbers swapped? Wait distinctness of all six means a2 cannot equal a5, so d2≠0. Similarly a3≠a4. So d2 ∈{-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5}. Same for d3.\n\nNow we need integer solutions with values from those sets satisfying d2 -2 d3 = k where k ∈{-1,0,1}.\n\nAlso the values a1,a6 must differ accordingly.\n\nWe also need consistency: all six a's are a permutation.\n\nApproach: enumerate possibilities for (d2,d3) meeting condition, then count permutations that realize those differences and also a1 - a6 appropriate.\n\nSince small set we can compute manually.\n\nLet's list possible pairs (d2,d3) where d2 -2 d3 = k.\n\nConsider k=0: d2 = 2 d3.\n\nSince d2 max 5, d3 possibilities where 2 d3 within allowed range and nonzero.\n\nd3 can be -2, -1,1,2. Because 2*d3 yields -4,-2,2,4. Those are allowed for d2 (must be in allowed set and nonzero). Also d3= -3 gives -6 not allowed; 3 gives 6 not allowed. d3 = -2 => d2 = -4 (allowed). d3=-1 => d2=-2 (allowed). d3=1 => d2=2 (allowed). d3=2 => d2=4 (allowed). So four possibilities for (d2,d3) when k=0.\n\nNow k=1: d2 = 2 d3 +1.\n\nFind integer d3 such that RHS in allowed set.\n\nLet's iterate d3 values -5..5 not zero.\n\nCompute 2 d3 +1:\n\nd3=-5 => -10+1=-9 not allowed.\n-4 => -8+1=-7 not allowed.\n-3 => -6+1=-5 (allowed, d2=-5) ok.\n-2 => -4+1=-3 (allowed)\n-1 => -2+1=-1 (allowed)\n0 not allowed.\n1 => 2+1=3 (allowed)\n2 =>4+1=5 (allowed)\n3 =>6+1=7 not allowed (>5)\n4 =>8+1=9 not allowed\n5 =>10+1=11 not allowed\n\nThus allowed d3: -3,-2,-1,1,2 produce d2 values -5,-3,-1,3,5 respectively (all nonzero within range). So 5 possibilities.\n\nNow k=-1: d2 = 2 d3 -1.\n\nCompute for d3 values:\n\nd3=-5 => -10-1=-11 no\n-4 => -8-1=-9 no\n-3 => -6-1=-7 no\n-2 => -4-1=-5 (allowed)\n-1 => -2-1=-3 (allowed)\n1 => 2-1=1 (allowed)\n2 =>4-1=3 (allowed)\n3 =>6-1=5 (allowed)\n4 =>8-1=7 no\n5 =>10-1=9 no\n\nThus d3 = -2,-1,1,2,3 give d2 = -5,-3,1,3,5 respectively. That's 5 possibilities.\n\nSo total (d2,d3) combos: for k=0:4, k=1:5, k=-1:5 => total 14 combos.\n\nNow for each combo we need to count permutations that realize those differences and also a1 - a6 =5k (since k corresponds). Since a1 - a6 must be 5k.\n\nThus we also need to pick a1 and a6 values from {1..6} distinct such that difference is 5k.\n\nDifferences possible: 5k = 5,0,-5.\n\n- For k=1, a1 - a6 =5 => possibilities: (a1,a6) = (6,1) only because max difference 5. So a1=6, a6=1.\n\n- For k= -1, a1 - a6 = -5 => (a1,a6) = (1,6).\n\n- For k=0, a1 - a6 =0 => a1 = a6, impossible because distinct. So there are no permutations for k=0? Wait a1 and a6 must be distinct, but difference zero would imply equal, not allowed. So k=0 cannot happen. Therefore only k=±1 cases count.\n\nThus we only consider combos where k= ±1, and also a1,a6 fixed as (6,1) for k=1, or (1,6) for k=-1.\n\nNow count permutations for each case.\n\nWe have to assign numbers 1..6 to positions a1..a6 with constraints:\n\n- a1,a6 are fixed as above.\n- The differences d2 = a2 - a5, d3 = a3 - a4 must equal specific values from the allowed pairs.\n\n- Also all six numbers must be used exactly once.\n\nLet's handle k=1 case: a1=6, a6=1.\n\nRemaining numbers to assign to positions 2,3,4,5 are {2,3,4,5}.\n\nWe need a2 - a5 = d2, a3 - a4 = d3 with (d2,d3) from the list for k=1 (5 combos):\n\nList for k=1:\n(d3, d2):\n-3 -> -5\n-2 -> -3\n-1 -> -1\n1 -> 3\n2 ->5\n\nSo pairs:\n(-5,-3) wait careful: earlier we had d3=-3 => d2=-5.\nd3=-2 => d2=-3.\nd3=-1 => d2=-1.\nd3=1 => d2=3.\nd3=2 => d2=5.\n\nThus we need assignments of numbers to indices 2,3,4,5 such that:\n\na2 - a5 = d2,\na3 - a4 = d3,\nusing numbers {2,3,4,5} distinct.\n\nLet's enumerate each pair.\n\nWe can treat unordered: choose a5 then a2 = a5 + d2 must be in set.\n\nSimilarly a4 then a3 = a4 + d3 must be in set.\n\nWe must ensure all four numbers distinct and within {2,3,4,5}.\n\nLet's list the set elements: 2,3,4,5.\n\nWe can brute force each (d2,d3).\n\nCase 1: d2 = -5, d3 = -3.\n\nThen a2 = a5 -5, but values are at least 2, so a5 must be >=7 to keep a2 in set, impossible. So no solutions.\n\nCase 2: d2 = -3, d3 = -2.\n\na2 = a5 -3. Since a5 in {2,3,4,5}, a2 would be -1,0,1,2. Only possible a2=2 when a5=5. So a5=5, a2=2.\n\nNow for d3 = -2: a3 = a4 -2. Values a4 in set remaining after using 5 and2: remaining {3,4}. Check possibilities:\nIf a4=3 => a3=1 not in set.\nIf a4=4 => a3=2 (already used). So no solutions.\n\nCase 3: d2 = -1, d3 = -1.\n\na2 = a5 -1. Choose a5 in {2,3,4,5} such that a2 in set and distinct.\n\nPossible pairs:\na5=3 => a2=2\na5=4 => a2=3\na5=5 => a2=4\n\nNow after choosing a2,a5, remaining numbers for a3,a4 are the two left.\n\nNeed a3 - a4 = -1 => a3 = a4 -1. So a4 must be one higher than a3.\n\nFrom remaining two numbers, they must be consecutive with difference 1 and a3 lower.\n\nCheck each a5 choice:\n\n- a5=3, a2=2 used numbers {2,3}. Remaining {4,5}. Can we assign a3,a4 as 4 and5 with a3 - a4 = -1? That would need a3=4, a4=5 gives -1, works. So one solution.\n\n- a5=4, a2=3 used {3,4}. Remaining {2,5}. Need a3 - a4 = -1. Options: (a3=2, a4=5) diff -3 no. (a3=5, a4=2) diff 3 no. So no.\n\n- a5=5, a2=4 used {4,5}. Remaining {2,3}. Need a3 - a4 = -1. Options: a3=2, a4=3 diff -1 works. So solution.\n\nThus we have 2 solutions for this case.\n\nCase 4: d2 = 3, d3 = 1.\n\na2 = a5 +3. Choose a5 such that a2 within set.\n\nPossible a5 values:\nIf a5=2 => a2=5 (in set)\na5=3 => a2=6 not in set\na5=4 => a2=7 no\na5=5 => a2=8 no\n\nSo only a5=2, a2=5.\n\nRemaining numbers {3,4} for a3,a4. Need a3 - a4 =1 => a3 = a4 +1.\n\nPossible assignments: a4=3 => a3=4 diff 1 works. So a4=3,a3=4.\n\nThus one solution.\n\nCase 5: d2 =5, d3 =2.\n\na2 = a5 +5. Since max value in set is 5, a5 would need to be 0 to get a2=5, not allowed. So no solutions.\n\nThus total solutions for k=1 case: 2 +1 =3 solutions.\n\nNow k=-1 case: a1=1, a6=6. Remaining numbers {2,3,4,5} same set.\n\nNow (d2,d3) pairs for k=-1 (list earlier):\nd3=-2 => d2=-5\nd3=-1 => d2=-3\nd3=1 => d2=1\nd3=2 => d2=3\nd3=3 => d2=5\n\nCorresponding pairs:\n(-5,-2)\n(-3,-1)\n(1,1)\n(3,2)\n(5,3)\n\nCheck each.\n\nCase A: d2=-5, d3=-2. a2 = a5 -5 impossible as before. No.\n\nCase B: d2=-3, d3=-1.\n\na2 = a5 -3. Need a5 such that a2 in set.\n\nTry a5=5 => a2=2 works.\na5=4 => a2=1 not in set.\na5=3 => a2=0 no.\na5=2 => a2=-1 no.\n\nSo only a5=5, a2=2.\n\nNow remaining numbers {3,4} for a3,a4. Need a3 - a4 = -1 => a3 = a4 -1.\n\nOptions: a4=4 => a3=3 works. So one solution.\n\nCase C: d2=1, d3=1.\n\na2 = a5 +1. Choose a5 so within set.\n\nPossibilities:\na5=2 => a2=3\na5=3 => a2=4\na5=4 => a2=5\na5=5 => a2=6 not in set.\n\nThus three possibilities.\n\nNow after picking a5,a2, remaining two numbers for a3,a4.\n\nNeed a3 - a4 =1 => a3 = a4 +1.\n\nCheck each:\n\n- a5=2, a2=3 used {2,3}. Remaining {4,5}. Need a3-a4=1, possible a4=4,a3=5 works. So one solution.\n\n- a5=3, a2=4 used {3,4}. Remaining {2,5}. Need diff 1: options (a4=2,a3=5) diff 3; (a4=5,a3=2) diff -3. No.\n\n- a5=4, a2=5 used {4,5}. Remaining {2,3}. Need a3-a4=1 -> a4=2,a3=3 works. So one solution.\n\nThus total 2 solutions.\n\nCase D: d2=3, d3=2.\n\na2 = a5 +3. Need a5 such that a2 in set.\n\na5=2 => a2=5 works.\na5=3 => a2=6 no.\na5=4 => a2=7 no.\na5=5 => a2=8 no.\n\nSo a5=2, a2=5.\n\nRemaining numbers {3,4}. Need a3 - a4 =2 => a3 = a4 +2.\n\nCheck: a4=3 => a3=5 (already used). a4=4 => a3=6 not in set. So no solutions.\n\nCase E: d2=5, d3=3.\n\na2 = a5 +5 impossible. No solutions.\n\nThus total solutions for k=-1 case: 1+2 =3 solutions.\n\nThus total permutations with degree ≤4 = number of solutions = 3+3 =6.\n\nCheck if any permutations counted multiple? They are distinct because a1/a6 different.\n\nThus total permutations that give degree ≤4 is 6.\n\nThus degree exactly 5 count = 720 - 6 = 714.\n\nBut ensure that degree could be less than 4 maybe? Those 6 permutations might correspond to degree ≤4; could be degree ≤3 etc. Still excluded. So answer 714.\n\nLet's double-check that we haven't missed any case where k=0 but a1 - a6 =0 impossible as distinct. So indeed no. So only 6 permutations are deg ≤4.\n\nThus answer: 714.\n\nCheck sanity: It's plausible many permutations give full degree 5.\n\nThus final answer 714.", "response": "ANSWER: 714", "answer": 714, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.3113017871546617e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999986886990726, "logp_correct": -1.3113017871546617e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.3518077485149074, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3249903794487636, "frac_below_-5": 0.0053730109526761724, "digit_lp_min": -5.006716728210449, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 4839, "lp_end": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.145764938177308e-06, "logp_nocot": -25.195847034454346, "p_nocot": 1.1417806168902367e-11, "uplift": 25.19584572315256}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
