483, 272, 10853, 72554, 478, 533, 575, 11, 483, 272, 10853, 11293, 13, 623, 1636, 382, 316, 5230, 272, 10853, 472, 4410, 472, 4149, 2049, 26513, 11293, 30, 2214, 130999, 284, 11, 581, 1309, 316, 35441, 31060, 395, 722, 575, 869, 316, 284, 13, 623, 19957, 4149, 2006, 316, 5769, 1991, 5941, 382, 316, 920, 272, 10853, 472, 3291, 472, 4149, 316, 625, 23275, 11608, 11, 889, 290, 11608, 70943, 483, 575, 13, 2632, 290, 31060, 5025, 120873, 472, 575, 21073, 364, 9051, 46730, 11, 581, 1309, 272, 10853, 72554, 478, 533, 575, 13, 12265, 272, 10853, 2804, 413, 540, 5153, 575, 350, 21910, 1023, 553, 11293, 591, 540, 5153, 220, 16, 936, 889, 625, 23517, 13, 50557, 6931, 11608, 25, 12265, 272, 10853, 553, 11293, 16913, 8841, 121629, 11, 290, 19957, 4149, 1432, 395, 272, 10853, 382, 575, 350, 2345, 6167, 272, 10853, 48141, 741, 1328, 126205, 272, 10853, 72554, 478, 533, 575, 472, 1701, 472, 575, 72554, 478, 533, 575, 871, 575, 72554, 478, 14, 17, 13, 2632, 581, 665, 679, 575, 869, 316, 8350, 10564, 14, 17, 741, 2632, 284, 72554, 8350, 10564, 14, 17, 741, 2214, 478, 28, 1323, 19, 11, 8350, 10564, 14, 17, 25987, 7959, 17, 13, 2632, 581, 6284, 679, 945, 1572, 220, 7959, 17, 49900, 364, 61317, 25, 183159, 581, 679, 284, 49900, 483, 11293, 51957, 272, 62, 16, 464, 2550, 464, 272, 1285, 13, 12265, 1023, 553, 8841, 54912, 11, 272, 10853, 99731, 575, 13, 8820, 1309, 272, 10853, 72554, 478, 533, 575, 350, 21910, 3561, 29703, 540, 5153, 575, 741, 19233, 575, 72554, 478, 533, 575, 871, 575, 72554, 478, 14, 17, 13, 2632, 284, 72554, 8350, 10564, 14, 17, 741, 31526, 45794, 472, 581, 8274, 483, 272, 10853, 314, 575, 326, 305, 10853, 314, 575, 13, 2632, 10562, 284, 314, 8350, 10564, 14, 17, 8, 314, 220, 7959, 17, 364, 4383, 538, 581, 2023, 679, 284, 1424, 478, 14, 17, 2360, 3561, 76454, 625, 19957, 10112, 12384, 52008, 3763, 7411, 51957, 4849, 30, 41021, 1746, 25, 581, 679, 11608, 272, 10853, 72554, 478, 533, 305, 10853, 13, 12265, 305, 10853, 99731, 575, 350, 86800, 423, 11293, 741, 151191, 305, 10853, 38566, 171857, 5517, 945, 13, 2632, 2360, 12384, 3561, 76454, 4787, 11608, 120873, 11, 625, 10297, 13, 2632, 19957, 3561, 76454, 575, 8967, 65414, 4149, 5517, 11608, 364, 84787, 284, 13731, 314, 8350, 10564, 14, 17, 8, 314, 220, 7959, 17, 364, 16577, 2371, 25, 2214, 478, 1952, 11, 478, 28, 17, 74, 11, 581, 665, 679, 51957, 220, 16, 485, 74, 483, 3561, 76454, 220, 16, 485, 74, 11, 9874, 49900, 723, 72, 11, 17, 72, 60, 1919, 1849, 29703, 314, 220, 17, 72, 72554, 220, 17, 74, 314, 478, 13, 9700, 364, 84787, 6052, 314, 220, 7959, 17, 13], "n_tokens": 2425, "words": 1714, "band_lo": 1874.5, "band_hi": 7498.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have integer endpoints between 1 and 2024 inclusive. So all possible segments are intervals [a,b] with integers a<b? endpoints inclusive? Typically segment with endpoints in set includes the points a and b; length is b-a. Since endpoints from that set, length is integer from 1 to 2023.\n\nNo two segments have same length => each length used at most once. Also no segment is entirely contained in another. Overlap allowed partially.\n\nGoal maximize number of segments.\n\nWe need choose a set of intervals with distinct lengths, none containing another.\n\nObservation: If we sort by left endpoint, to avoid containment, we need monotonic property: If a segment is longer and starts earlier, it may contain later shorter maybe.\n\nCondition: For any two intervals I=[l1,r1], J=[l2,r2] with l1 ≤ l2, we must not have r1 ≥ r2 (i.e., I contains J). So for any pair with left endpoint ordering, right endpoints must be strictly increasing: if l1<l2 then r1<r2 (can't be equal length either). Similarly if l1>l2 then r1>r2. So the set must be \"non-nesting\", i.e., intervals are ordered by both endpoints increasing – they form a chain in the partial order of intervals by inclusion. This is known as intervals are \"non-overlapping in the sense of inclusion\". So they are comparable by left endpoint order leading to same order by right endpoint.\n\nThus we need a set of intervals where both left and right endpoints are strictly increasing. That's essentially a set of points (l_i, r_i) with l_i < r_i, integers, 1≤l_i<r_i≤2024, distinct lengths (r_i - l_i distinct), and monotone increasing sequences.\n\nGoal maximize m.\n\nWe can think of constructing such a chain as long as possible.\n\nLet’s think of choosing intervals such that left endpoints increase by at least 1 each step, and right endpoints increase by at least 1 each step (maybe more).\n\nSince lengths distinct, we need r_i - l_i distinct. Both sequences increasing may help.\n\nWe need to find maximal size of subset of integer points (l,r) in the triangle 1 ≤ l < r ≤ N (N=2024) with strictly increasing l and r, and distinct differences d = r - l.\n\nThis is analogous to constructing longest sequence where (l_i, r_i) is strictly increasing in both coordinates (i.e., a chain). Since total possible points is N(N-1)/2 huge; but we limited by distinct lengths.\n\nIdea: Map each interval to its length d and left endpoint l. Since r = l+d.\n\nIncreasing condition: For i<j, we need l_i < l_j and l_i + d_i < l_j + d_j => l_i - l_j < d_j - d_i. Since l_i<l_j, left side negative. So condition is automatically true if d_j > d_i? Let's see: suppose d_j > d_i, then RHS positive, inequality holds (since left negative). So if lengths are increasing, then any left endpoints increasing will satisfy containment condition? Need to check: we need both endpoints increasing, which is equivalent to l_i<l_j and d_i + l_i < d_j + l_j. If d_j > d_i, then even if l_j is close to l_i, we might still have r_i < r_j? Let's compute r_i = l_i + d_i, r_j = l_j + d_j. Since l_j > l_i, we have r_j - r_i = (l_j - l_i) + (d_j - d_i) > d_j - d_i (since l_j>l_i). If d_j > d_i, then r_j > r_i definitely. So monotone increase of lengths suffices given left endpoints increasing (any increase). So we can just require lengths distinct, and we can arrange them in increasing order of lengths.\n\nThus we can pick intervals with distinct lengths, arrange them sorted by length, and choose left endpoints such that they are strictly increasing and also ensure r_i ≤ N.\n\nGoal is maximize number of intervals, i.e., number of distinct lengths we can fit respecting the capacity of line.\n\nWe need to assign to each chosen length d an integer left endpoint l >=1 such that l + d ≤ N, and the sequence of l's is strictly increasing.\n\nWe can think of constructing a sequence of lengths d_1 < d_2 < ... < d_m, and assign left endpoints l_i forming increasing sequence, with constraints l_i ≥ 1, l_i ≤ N - d_i.\n\nAlso need l_i > l_{i-1}. So minimal possible l_i is at least l_{i-1}+1.\n\nWe can attempt to maximize m by using smallest possible lengths to keep room.\n\nSince d_i are distinct, we can pick the smallest possible lengths: 1,2,3,... up to some k. Let's see if we can assign l_i such that constraints hold.\n\nSet d_i = i (i from 1 to k). Need to choose l_i increasing, each ≤ N - i.\n\nWe can try to set l_i = i (or something). Let's test: l_i = i works? Then r_i = i + i = 2i ≤ N? Need 2i ≤ N => i ≤ N/2 = 1012. So up to i=1012 works. For i=1013, 2*1013=2026 >2024, not allowed.\n\nBut also l_i must be ≤ N - i. For i up to N-1, fine.\n\nBut if we set l_i = i, left endpoints increasing obviously. Up to i=1012, r_i = 2i ≤2024. So we can get m=1012.\n\nCan we get more than 1012 by using non-consecutive lengths or different left endpoints? Since lengths cannot repeat; we could use larger lengths but with earlier left endpoints maybe still fit more total intervals.\n\nGoal is to maximize m, not necessarily using smallest lengths.\n\nObservation: Total left endpoints must be at least 1,2,...,m at minimum (since strictly increasing by at least 1). So l_i ≥ i. Also we need l_i + d_i ≤ N. Therefore we need i + d_i ≤ N -> d_i ≤ N - i.\n\nThus for each i (position in ordering), max length allowed is N - i.\n\nSince we need distinct lengths, we need to assign lengths d_i such that d_i ≤ N - i, all distinct.\n\nWe want maximize m such that we can select m distinct integers d_i each ≤ N - i for i=1..m. Is there any additional restriction? Not really, we can pick any distinct set within these caps.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: find largest m such that there exist m distinct integers d_1,...,d_m with d_i ≤ N - i for each i (and presumably also d_i ≥ 1). Since we can order them in any order? But we also require lengths increasing? Actually we didn't enforce monotonicity; but we can reorder intervals arbitrarily, but our earlier condition about containment requires that when sorted by left endpoints, lengths are increasing (if left endpoints increase). However we can assign lengths not necessarily increasing with i as long as the resulting intervals maintain both endpoints increasing. Since r_i = l_i + d_i. If l_i increase, but d_i might not be monotonic, r_i could still increase: need r_i = l_i + d_i increasing. Since l_i at least i, we can enforce d_i such that r_i is increasing.\n\nSimpler: Choose l_i = i (minimum). Then r_i = i + d_i. Need r_i strictly increasing: i + d_i < (i+1)+ d_{i+1} => d_i - d_{i+1} < 1. Since integers, this means d_i ≤ d_{i+1}. Actually need i + d_i < i+1 + d_{i+1} => d_i - d_{i+1} < 1 => d_i ≤ d_{i+1}. If d_i = d_{i+1}, then inequality becomes i + d_i < i+1 + d_i => true (since LHS = i + d_i, RHS = i+1 + d_i). So equality allowed? Let's check: if d_i = d_{i+1}, lengths equal, but constraint says no equal lengths. So strict inequality needed: d_i < d_{i+1} or d_i = d_{i+1} is not allowed. With equality, lengths same violates condition. So we need d_i < d_{i+1}. So lengths must be strictly increasing if left endpoints increase by 1 each. In general if left increase by at least 1, we still need monotonic non-decreasing; but distinct requirement forces strict increase.\n\nThus we need d_i strictly increasing sequence as well. So we earlier requirement stands: d_i increasing and ≤ N - i.\n\nTherefore the maximum m is the maximum size of a strictly increasing sequence of distinct positive integers where each term ≤ N - i at position i.\n\nThus we need to find largest m such that we can pick d_i = something with d_i ≤ N - i, with d_i increasing. The best is to pick d_i as large as possible while maintaining increasing? For maximizing m, we need to satisfy constraints for all i up to m. The minimal possible way to fit many terms is to set d_i as small as possible to not exceed bound, but the bound decreases with i. So the constraints become tighter as i increases.\n\nSpecifically, we need d_i ≤ N - i. Since d_i must be at least i (since they are increasing from at least 1), but not necessarily. Actually lower bound: Since d_i are increasing distinct positive ints, the minimal possible value for d_i is i (by taking d_i=i). This satisfies d_i ≤ N - i as long as i ≤ N - i => i ≤ N/2. So we can have i up to floor(N/2). So m ≤ floor(N/2). For N=2024, floor(N/2)=1012. So we cannot have more than 1012 intervals.\n\nProof: Suppose we have m intervals with increasing lengths d_1 < ... < d_m. Since they are positive integers, d_i ≥ i. Also need d_i ≤ N - i (since left endpoint at least i). Therefore i ≤ N - i => i ≤ N/2. So m ≤ floor(N/2). Achievable as we saw with d_i = i and l_i = i. So maximum m = floor(N/2) = 1012.\n\nCheck if we could have m > N/2 using left endpoints not minimal maybe larger gaps allow longer lengths later? Let's test: we have bound d_i ≤ N - l_i. Since l_i ≥ i (strictly increasing). Larger l_i reduces allowable length more. So using larger left endpoints makes bound tighter, not helpful. So minimal left endpoints i gives maximal possible length bound.\n\nThus m_max = floor(N/2) = 1012.\n\nEdge check: For N even, N=2k, we can have lengths 1..k with left endpoints 1..k, giving intervals [i,2i] where right endpoint = 2i ≤ 2k = N. Good.\n\nThus answer = 1012.", "response": "ANSWER: 1012", "answer": 1012, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": ["too_short"], "accepted": false}
{"qid": "b0805706-6161-4537-b545-61ca4b3b76ba", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Alice and Bob play a game together as a team on a $100 \\times 100$ board with all unit squares initially white. Alice sets up the game by coloring exactly $k$ of the unit squares red at the beginning. After that, a legal move for Bob is to choose a row or column with at least $10$ red squares and color all of the remaining squares in it red. What is the smallest $k$ such that Alice can set up a game in such a way that Bob can color the entire board red after finitely many moves?\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Alice and Bob play a game together as a team on a $100 \\times 100$ board with all unit squares initially white. Alice sets up the game by coloring exactly $k$ of the unit squares red at the beginning. After that, a legal move for Bob is to choose a row or column with at least $10$ red squares and color all of the remaining squares in it red. What is the smallest $k$ such that Alice can set up a game in such a way that Bob can color the entire board red after finitely many moves?", "true_answer": 100, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "b0805706-6161-4537-b545-61ca4b3b76ba:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 19957, 2086, 328, 3592, 57269, 25998, 813, 484, 1354, 382, 261, 16281, 328, 19523, 395, 22582, 316, 17462, 3089, 6508, 6796, 3592, 13, 22582, 885, 19523, 25, 5655, 261, 5225, 503, 6000, 484, 4279, 853, 540, 5153, 220, 702, 3592, 57269, 11, 326, 1815, 3089, 722, 15448, 57269, 306, 484, 5225, 14, 9380, 3592, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 6954, 290, 6062, 2543, 741, 6311, 484, 11, 290, 2543, 14081, 9637, 3592, 11, 12673, 1991, 167801, 11, 1118, 1340, 11434, 1273, 13585, 14, 26893, 364, 42087, 25, 44045, 10648, 316, 920, 869, 4871,