, 220, 702, 15697, 484, 679, 540, 5153, 220, 16, 3592, 591, 484, 5225, 13, 1843, 581, 679, 428, 13585, 2454, 483, 540, 5153, 220, 702, 167801, 11, 3609, 167801, 99731, 702, 81, 13, 2214, 428, 104733, 24, 11, 3609, 167801, 72554, 2744, 13, 3072, 10112, 581, 2023, 679, 15697, 6977, 25, 1309, 540, 5153, 220, 702, 15697, 2454, 483, 99731, 702, 167801, 11, 9874, 540, 5153, 220, 1353, 167801, 2418, 13, 2632, 538, 581, 679, 3760, 1572, 220, 702, 13585, 6977, 11, 581, 2023, 7665, 679, 15697, 6977, 26, 889, 484, 1217, 10860, 540, 5153, 220, 702, 15697, 6977, 871, 99731, 1353, 167801, 364, 84787, 538, 372, 27, 1353, 11, 581, 6284, 679, 220, 702, 13585, 6977, 11777, 220, 702, 15697, 6977, 13, 7801, 581, 679, 540, 1645, 220, 24, 13585, 6977, 326, 540, 1645, 220, 24, 15697, 6977, 13, 9939, 484, 6284, 4124, 316, 3149, 6796, 364, 2167, 665, 8704, 316, 37071, 2360, 120331, 25, 3331, 1934, 1062, 16281, 328, 19523, 11, 290, 2086, 328, 14255, 13585, 2932, 14255, 15697, 382, 9913, 13, 3072, 10112, 625, 6118, 364, 9095, 3095, 581, 679, 540, 1645, 220, 24, 13585, 25998, 483, 5064, 702, 167801, 13, 7801, 1934, 28447, 1373, 11, 2454, 6000, 8625, 540, 1645, 220, 24, 167801, 11, 625, 4951, 316, 413, 6954, 562, 350, 21910, 4414, 220, 702, 741, 3004, 6000, 665, 413, 14255, 12604, 480, 4279, 1458, 1236, 4871, 167801, 316, 717, 316, 220, 702, 13, 3072, 2454, 6000, 665, 679, 540, 1645, 290, 2086, 328, 167801, 25998, 12989, 306, 480, 11, 1118, 10705, 382, 464, 1353, 326, 7581, 6200, 15697, 13, 12265, 10705, 3760, 1572, 220, 1353, 167801, 326, 2454, 6000, 10860, 540, 5153, 220, 16, 5018, 10112, 316, 7627, 220, 702, 26, 483, 540, 1645, 220, 24, 13585, 876, 36591, 11, 581, 1309, 540, 5153, 1001, 6000, 483, 4871, 3605, 99731, 16, 316, 7627, 220, 702, 30, 889, 2454, 6000, 1481, 1309, 540, 5153, 350, 702, 12, 24, 25987, 16, 167801, 13, 2632, 722, 220, 1353, 15697, 1481, 1309, 540, 5153, 220, 16, 3592, 2454, 316, 5025, 6954, 562, 1934, 13585, 13, 21926, 540, 5153, 220, 1353, 167801, 11, 133882, 13, 19233, 6284, 6954, 722, 15697, 13, 47682, 8535, 6954, 6062, 6796, 364, 84787, 19957, 382, 220, 1353, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 1353, 364, 58369, 10635, 1721, 4733, 13], "n_tokens": 6139, "words": 4441, "band_lo": 1848.0, "band_hi": 7392.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need minimal number of red squares initially so that there is a sequence of moves for Bob to eventually color entire board red. Bob's moves: choose a row or column that already has at least 10 red squares, and then color all remaining squares in that row/column red (i.e., fill the whole line). After that, the line becomes fully red, adding many reds, which may enable other rows/columns.\n\nGoal: Alice wants to set up initial red squares to guarantee that eventually Bob can fill whole board (i.e., there exists a finite sequence of legal moves that ends with whole board red). She wants minimal such k. Actually the problem asks: smallest k such that Alice can set up a game in such a way that Bob can color the entire board red after finitely many moves. So find minimal initial red count that can lead to full board, with optimal placement by Alice.\n\nWe need to think about necessary condition. Bob can only start moves on lines with at least 10 reds. So Alice must arrange at least some rows or columns containing at least 10 reds. Also after some lines get filled, they create many reds in intersecting columns/rows, which may then reach threshold 10.\n\nGoal: With minimal initial reds, we need to trigger a cascade that eventually fills all rows and columns.\n\nObservation: If we fill a row, then each column gets one more red at intersection. So after filling some rows, columns may reach 10 red count.\n\nSimilarly, filling columns adds reds to rows.\n\nThis resembles percolation: we need to achieve at least 10 red squares in each line eventually, but we can use the lines we fill to boost counts.\n\nWe need to find minimal initial red set such that there exists a sequence. Equivalent to find minimal \"seed\" set for which closure under operation (fill rows/columns with >=10 red) yields whole board.\n\nLet's model: board is 100x100. Initially some red cells. Operation: if a row has ≥10 reds, we can add reds to all empty cells of that row. Similarly for columns.\n\nThus closure is monotone. We want to start with k reds such that closure = all cells.\n\nWe need to find minimal size of a set whose \"row/column closure\" reaches whole matrix.\n\nConsider we need to eventually have at least one row with >=10 reds, to start. Suppose Alice colors 10 reds all in same row. Then Bob can fill that row (since has 10 reds). After filling, that row now has 100 reds. Then each column gets one red from that row. So each column now has at least 1 red. Not enough for threshold (needs 10). So can't fill any column yet.\n\nThus need to have more initial reds that after first row fill produce columns reaching ten. For a column to reach 10, need at least 9 other reds in that column before column fill. Those could be from other filled rows.\n\nIf we initially have 10 reds in a row, and also say we have some reds in other rows that share columns with the initial 10 columns. But we haven't filled those rows yet (they have <10). The column counts after first row fill will be 1 each (the filled row). So not enough.\n\nThus we need more than one initial row to get columns to 10. Suppose we initially color 10 reds in each of 10 distinct rows, all in the same set of 10 columns (forming a 10x10 block). Then each of those 10 rows have 10 reds, so Bob can fill each of those rows (maybe sequentially). After filling those 10 rows, each of the 10 columns will have 10 reds from those rows (actually after filling rows, each column gets 100 reds in those rows? Wait each filled row makes those columns have a red in each row: after filling each row, it becomes fully red, so each of the 10 columns will have a red in each of the 10 rows, total 10 reds per column (plus maybe initial other reds). So after filling the 10 rows, each of those columns reaches 10 reds, enabling column fill. Once a column is filled, adds reds to all rows, potentially enabling further rows.\n\nThus initial configuration: a 10x10 subgrid fully colored red (i.e., 100 red squares). That's enough, because then each of its rows have 10 reds, each column 10 reds. Bob can fill all rows (or columns) then entire board. But maybe we can do less than full 10x10 block.\n\nGoal minimal k. Suppose we have a set S of red squares. Let R be set of rows with at least 10 reds initially; C similarly columns.\n\nWe can fill rows in R, making them full. That adds reds to columns. After filling all rows in R, each column j gets added |R| reds (i.e., one per newly filled row) plus its initial reds. So column j will have count = initial_reds_in_column_j + |R|. If that >=10, column can be filled.\n\nThus columns become fillable if initial reds in column >=10 - |R|.\n\nSimilarly after filling some columns, rows get more reds.\n\nThus we can think of iterative process: start with initial counts a_i for rows, b_j for columns. At step, if row i has >=10, set its count to 100 (full). That adds 1 to each column counts (if row was not already full). Actually adds (100 - current reds in that row) new reds, but columns each get +1 if they didn't already have red in that row (they didn't). So column count increments by 1 for each filled row.\n\nTherefore after filling a set of rows R_filled, column j count = b_j + |R_filled| (since each filled row contributes a red in column j). Similarly after filling columns C_filled, row i count = a_i + |C_filled|.\n\nThus the process reduces to: start with initial a_i, b_j counts (0 ≤ a_i ≤100). While there exists row i with a_i >=10 and not yet in R_filled, add i to R_filled (set row full). This increments all column counts by 1. Similarly columns.\n\nWe need to reach state where all rows and columns are filled.\n\nThus the process depends only on numbers of initially red squares in each row/column, not their positions (since only count matters). Indeed because after filling rows, columns count just increments by number of filled rows regardless of which columns were already red in those rows? Wait the initial red squares that are in a particular column also get counted; but after a row is filled, each column gets one new red regardless of previous red count. So positions do not matter beyond counts per row and column, not distribution across rows/columns? However there is a subtlety: when we fill a row, we add reds to all columns across that row: each column gains one red. So indeed column increments are uniform: each filled row adds 1 to each column's red count. Therefore only the number of filled rows matters, not which columns had previous reds.\n\nThus the threshold condition for column j after some rows filled: b_j + |R_filled| ≥10 for ability to fill column. Similarly row condition after columns filled: a_i + |C_filled| ≥10.\n\nThus we can model as possible to eventually fill all rows and columns iff there exists ordering such that starting from given vectors a_i,b_j we can eventually reach all rows and columns.\n\nObservation: This is analogous to a bipartite graph activation process.\n\nWe need minimal total red squares sum_i a_i = k (also = sum_j b_j, same count) such that there exists a sequence leading to full activation.\n\nWe can try to minimize k.\n\nLet r = number of rows initially having at least 10 reds? Actually we can choose to fill any rows/columns that become >=10 after steps.\n\nGoal: achieve all rows and columns. Let's denote after process we will have all rows filled (R_filled = 100) and all columns (C_filled = 100). For column j, final count after all rows filled is b_j + 100 (since each row adds 1). Since 100 ≥ 10, final condition satisfied.\n\nBut the process must be able to progress stepwise.\n\nSimplify: let x = number of rows initially having ≥10 reds (call them ready rows). Similarly y = number of columns initially having ≥10 reds.\n\nWe can start by filling those ready rows and columns. After we fill x rows, each column count increases by x. So any column with initial b_j >= 10 - x becomes fillable (if not already). So after filling rows we may get new columns.\n\nThus we can compute closure: start with initial sets R0 = {i: a_i ≥10}, C0 = {j: b_j ≥10}. Then iterative: if there is a row i not in R yet such that a_i + |C| ≥10, add row i. Similarly for columns.\n\nThus this is monotone. We want final R = all rows, C = all columns.\n\nNow minimal total reds needed. Since each red contributes to both a row count and a column count (counts counted twice in sums). Actually total reds = sum_i a_i = sum_j b_j = k.\n\nWe need to allocate reds to rows and columns counts to enable activation.\n\nWe suspect optimal arrangement is to concentrate reds in a small submatrix of size r × c, with each of those rows have at least 10 reds (by placing 10 reds per such row) and similarly each column have at least 10 reds. The minimal total would be something like 10 * r + (c - something) etc. Let's attempt to find minimal k by constructing and also proving lower bound.\n\nStrategy: Choose a set of r rows and c columns where we will place red squares. Within that r by c submatrix, we can place reds to give each selected row at least 10 reds and each selected column at least 10 reds. Outside this submatrix we put no reds initially.\n\nGoal is to have activation cascade that fills those r rows first (since they have 10 reds). After those rows filled, each column gets +r reds. So columns with initial b_j >= 10 - r become fillable. If we set c columns to have enough initial reds, we can get all columns fillable after rows filled. Then after columns filled, remaining rows get additional reds, possibly achieving 10.\n\nThus maybe we need both r and c ≥10? Not necessarily.\n\nSuppose we take r=10 rows, each with exactly 10 reds placed in distinct columns (maybe overlapping). To give each column at least 10 reds, we would need at least 10 rows each contribute one red per column; that would be 10 rows * 10 columns = 100 reds in a 10x10 block: each of the 10 columns gets 10 reds.\n\nThus we need at least 100 reds to have both a row set of size 10 and column set of size 10 ready initially. That matches earlier block.\n\nCan we do with fewer by using more rows? For example, have 20 rows each with 5 reds (so each row not ready initially). But after some columns fill maybe rows become ready. Need to see cascade.\n\nWe might start with some columns ready instead of rows. Maybe we can have a \"seed\" of size less than 100.\n\nLet's attempt to find minimal k via combinatorial lower bound.\n\nSince each move fills an entire line, after filling, that line contributes 100 reds. The total number of lines is 200 (100 rows + 100 cols). Final board after all filled lines is full.\n\nSuppose we need at least t rows to be initially ready (>=10) to start. Each such row requires at least 10 reds. So initial reds at least 10 t. But also each column may need to become ready later. However columns can become ready after rows are filled: each column needs at least 10 reds; after t rows are filled, each column gets t reds. So if t≥10, columns become ready without any initial reds in columns. Thus if we make t=10 rows initially ready (10 each), total reds =100, then after filling those rows, columns reach 10 and fill—makes whole board. That's 100.\n\nCan we have t=9 rows initially ready (9*10=90 reds) and then start with some columns ready as well? For columns to eventually become ready, after we fill those 9 rows, each column has 9 reds. To reach 10, need at least one column that already had 1 red elsewhere (outside those rows). If we have some columns initially with at least 1 red in other rows, we could reach 10. But those reds are already counted in initial total.\n\nGoal: minimize total reds while having at least some combination.\n\nLet's denote initial numbers R0 = number of rows with ≥10 reds, C0 = number of columns with ≥10 reds.\n\nRows ready give us initial filling of those rows without need extra. Suppose we have R0 = r rows ready. Then need at least 10r reds. Additionally we may have some columns ready with some reds maybe overlapping with those rows (since reds count both). Overlap can reduce total needed.\n\nIf a red lies in a row that is ready and also in a column that is ready, counts toward both. So to minimize total, we could try to have the same reds serve both row and column readiness.\n\nThus maybe we can place reds in a r × c submatrix to make r rows each have at least 10 reds and c columns each have at least 10 reds. Minimal total reds for given r,c is at least max(10r, 10c) but also need each row and column reach 10 within that submatrix: The classic problem of constructing a binary matrix with row sums at least 10 and column sums at least 10, minimizing total ones.\n\nMinimum total ones = max(10r,10c, something about covering?). The minimal edges to satisfy row lower bounds a_i >=10, column lower bounds b_j >=10 is at least max(10r,10c). And can achieve exactly max(10r,10c) by placing 10 reds per row in same c columns if c>=10? Let's test: if r ≤ c, we can set each of the r rows have 10 reds all in the first 10 columns (same columns). Then each of those 10 columns gets r reds (maybe <10). So columns may not reach 10. So need column minimum too.\n\nThus we need both lower bounds.\n\nThe minimum total edges to satisfy row sums >=10 (r rows) and column sums >=10 (c columns) is at least 10r + 10c - M where M is max possible overlap (i.e., each red can satisfy one row and one column). The maximum overlap is limited by the number of rows and columns: each red belongs to one row and one column, counting toward both thresholds. So we can think of constructing bipartite graph with degree at least 10 on each vertex (rows and columns). Minimum edges needed is at least max(10r,10c) *? Actually known result: for bipartite graph with minimum degree d on each side, minimum edges = max(d*r, d*c). But we need each vertex degree at least d =10. Minimal edges is at least max(10r,10c). However is it achievable? Yes, by constructing a regular bipartite graph where each of the larger side has degree 10, and each vertex on the smaller side may have degree >10 possibly. But edges count =10 * max(r,c). Example: if r=5, c=100, need each of 5 rows degree ≥10, each of 100 columns degree ≥10. Minimum edges can't be 10*100 =1000 because each column needs degree 10, sum of column degrees =10*100 =1000, which also sum of row degrees = total edges =1000, meaning average degree per row =1000/5 =200, exceeding limit (max 100 columns). Not possible because each row only has 100 columns. So constraints: degree per row ≤100. So need r*100 >=10*c => r >= 0.1c. Similarly c >=0.1 r. So both sides >=0.1 of other.\n\nThus we need both r,c not too extreme.\n\nOur board is 100x100, each row/col can have at most 100 reds.\n\nThus to achieve column degree 10 each, need at least 10 rows (since each column can get at most 1 red per row). So c <= 100*? Actually to give each column 10 reds, need at least 10 rows with a red in that column each. So need at least 10 rows selected. Similarly each row needs at least 10 columns selected. So both r and c must be at least 10.\n\nThus minimal r=c=10 leads to total edges at least 100 (since each row needs 10 => total 100; each column needs 10 => total 100; they match). There is a construction: a 10x10 full block uses 100 edges, each row degree 10, each column degree 10, works.\n\nThus any solution needs at least 10 rows ready and at least 10 columns ready? Is that necessary? Could we maybe start with fewer ready rows but use columns ready to start? Suppose we have R0 rows with >=10 reds maybe less than 10, but C0 columns with >=10 reds maybe also less. Could still cascade.\n\nBut we earlier derived that to get any column to have >=10 after filling rows, need number of filled rows >=10 or the column must have some initial reds.\n\nIf we have R0 = r rows ready, after filling them we have r reds per column. For a column to become fillable we need b_j + r >=10. So we could have some columns with initial b_j = 10 - r to meet threshold. If r <10, those columns need some initial reds (outside the ready rows). Those reds count toward total.\n\nSimilarly after we fill some columns, rows may become fillable.\n\nThus perhaps we can reduce total reds by having a mix: some rows ready, some columns ready, overlapping.\n\nWe need to find minimal total reds to achieve eventual full activation.\n\nLet’s formalize: Let x be number of rows we will initially have ready (>=10 reds). Let y be number of columns initially ready. Let a_i for other rows be less than 10, etc.\n\nOur activation sequence may start with either ready rows or columns. Suppose we start with x rows ready. Fill them -> each column gets +x. Among columns, some may become ready (i.e., have b_j + x >=10). So we need initial column counts to supplement.\n\nAlternatively we could start with y columns ready, fill them first.\n\nGoal is after some steps all rows and columns are filled.\n\nThis resembles need to achieve that eventually the sum of initial seeds (rows+columns) times some counts covers thresholds.\n\nThink of strategy: Choose a set of r rows and c columns, and place reds only at intersections (i.e., r*c cells) maybe not all. We need each selected row to have >=10 reds (so need at least 10 columns among c to have reds in that row). Thus need c >=10. Similarly each selected column needs >=10 rows, thus r >=10.\n\nThus seems r,c >=10 forced. So minimal r=c=10, leading to at least 100 reds (full block). Could we have rows ready that are not among the set of columns initially ready? But rows ready each need at least 10 reds; those reds could be placed all in the same column perhaps? But column would then have many reds from multiple rows, quickly reaching threshold. However column can have up to 100 reds. So we could have 10 rows each with 10 reds all located in the same single column? Then each of those rows gets 10 reds (threshold). Column would have 10*10 =100 reds? Wait each row has 10 reds all in same column? That's impossible because a row cannot have multiple reds in same column (cells are distinct). So each row's 10 reds must be in 10 distinct columns. So rows share columns maybe.\n\nIf we have 10 rows each with reds in the same 10 columns, we get 10x10 block, each column gets 10 reds (threshold). So aligns with block.\n\nIf we try to reduce number of columns to say 9, each row would need 10 reds but can only use 9 columns, needing at least one column repeated – impossible. So each row needs at least 10 distinct columns. Thus need at least 10 columns overall for any row with 10 reds.\n\nSimilarly need at least 10 rows for a column with 10 reds distinct rows.\n\nThus the minimal number of rows/columns involved is 10 each.\n\nThus total red squares at least 10*10 =100? Could we have initial reds not forming a perfect block but still 100 count? Could be less? Suppose we have 10 rows each with 10 reds, but columns may be more than 10, some columns have less than 10 reds initially. After filling rows, each column gets 10 reds (since 10 rows filled). Even if a column initially had 0, after rows filled it gets 10, becomes ready. So we may not need columns initially to have 10; they get 10 after rows fill because each filled row contributes 1 to each column. That works because each column gets exactly r reds where r is number of filled rows. So if r = 10, columns become ready regardless of initial. So we just need 10 rows ready initially; no requirement about columns. Thus initial reds could be arranged in 10 rows such that each row has at least 10 reds, but not necessarily in the same 10 columns, could be many columns. After filling those rows, each column gets at least 10 reds (since 10 rows contributed). Wait each column gets exactly the number of filled rows that have a red in that column. After rows are filled fully, each column gets a red from each filled row, because the filled row becomes fully red across entire row, i.e., after filling a row, we color all squares in that row red, including all columns. So after row is filled, each column obtains a red in that row, regardless of where original reds were. Yes! Important: row fill adds reds to entire row, all columns. So after filling 10 rows, each column has 10 reds (one per filled row). Thus columns become ready without any initial reds. So we just need 10 rows ready initially.\n\nThus total reds needed: need at least 10 rows each with at least 10 reds initially to qualify as ready rows. That's 100 reds. Could we have fewer than 10 rows ready if we also have some columns ready initially, so that after filling a mixture we reach threshold? Maybe we could have, say, 9 rows ready (9*10=90 reds) and also some columns ready, e.g., 1 column ready requiring 10 reds, but those reds could be among the 90 already counted (overlap). Could we achieve total <100?\n\nLet's attempt to see if we can have total k = 91 maybe? Example: have 9 rows each with 10 reds (90). Also we need at least one column to have >=10 reds to start column fill early. That column could have reds from those 9 rows (9 reds) plus an extra red somewhere else to make 10. So we need at least one additional red not already counted? The extra red could be placed in a row not among the 9, in that column. That adds 1 red, total 91. Now we have 9 ready rows, 1 ready column. Process: We could fill the 9 rows first (each >=10). After filling them, each column gets +9 reds (now each column has at least 9). The column that was initially ready now has its count increased also, reaching at least 10+9=19, but it's already fillable anyway. Now we can fill that column (since it has >=10). Once we fill that column, each row gets +1 red. For the remaining 91 rows (the 100-9 =91 rows not yet filled), each gets +1 from column fill. So those rows now have at most 1 red (if they had none). Still far from 10.\n\nNow after filling the column, we have 9 rows filled + 1 column filled. Now each unfilled row has 1 red (from that column). To become ready, need total reds >=10, i.e., need 9 more. Could we start filling more rows? No rows have >=10 yet. No columns left: each column currently has 9 (from rows) + maybe some from column fill (makes full column, now column is full). Actually after we fill the column, that column is full with 100 reds; each row gets a red there, but column is already filled. Other columns have 9 reds (from the 9 filled rows). To get any other column to become ready, need at least 1 more red in each of those columns => need another row filled to add red to them. But no rows ready.\n\nThus deadlock.\n\nThus 9 rows and 1 column insufficient.\n\nMaybe we need 9 rows and 9 columns ready? Let's compute needed reds. Each of 9 rows needs >=10 reds, each of 9 columns needs >=10 reds. Overlap can reduce total count. The bipartite graph with rows R (size9) each degree 10, columns C (size9) each degree 10, minimum edges = maximum of 10*9=90 and also must satisfy column degrees. Minimum edges needed is at least 90, can we achieve 90 edges? Yes, we can have a 9x10 submatrix where each row has 10 distinct columns, but there are only 9 columns, impossible. So need at least 10 columns to give each row 10 distinct positions. So with 9 rows, we need at least 10 columns to support degree 10 per row. But columns need degree 10 as well, requiring at least 10 rows. So symmetrical.\n\nThus you cannot have both rows and columns less than 10 ready.\n\nThus minimal is to have at least 10 rows ready. That gives 100 reds. This seems minimal.\n\nBut we must ensure that after filling those 10 rows, all columns become ready (they have 10 reds each) and fill, leading to full board. Yes.\n\nThus answer k = 100.\n\nNeed to confirm no configuration with <100 reds can succeed.\n\nProof: Suppose total reds k <100. Then by pigeonhole principle, average reds per row is k/100 <1. So many rows have <=0 reds. But that's not enough. Need formal lower bound: need at least 10 rows with ≥10 reds? Could we have fewer rows but columns with many reds that after column fill fill rows? Need at least 10 rows or 10 columns ready initially. Since each row needs at least 10 distinct columns for its reds, any row with ≥10 reds occupies at least 10 columns. So there are at least 10 columns that have at least 1 red from that row. If we have r rows each with at least 10 reds, total reds ≥10r. For r≤9, total reds ≤90. But maybe we could have columns ready: need at least 10 columns each with ≥10 reds, giving at least 100 reds again. So if we have less than 10 rows ready, we could instead have columns ready; but that also requires at least 10 columns ready => ≥100 reds.\n\nThus if k<100, we cannot have 10 rows ready nor 10 columns ready. Then we have at most 9 rows ready and at most 9 columns ready. Show that cannot lead to full board.\n\nWe can attempt to argue using invariant: consider after any sequence of moves, the number of filled rows plus filled columns is limited. But maybe not needed.\n\nAssume we have at most 9 rows initially with >=10 reds. Then after filling them, each column gets at most 9 reds, not enough to be fillable (since needs 10). No column can be filled unless it already had some initial reds to get to 10. But each column can have at most the number of reds initially placed in it, which overall is <100 and distributed among columns. Since overall less than 100 reds and each column requires at least 1 extra maybe to reach 10; with at most 9 rows prefilled, we need at least one column with initial count ≥1 to reach 10? but each column would need at least (10-9)=1 reds. So all 100 columns would need at least 1 red each to become fillable after rows. That's at least 100 reds, contradiction. Therefore cannot fill all columns. Hence can't fill whole board.\n\nThus minimal is 100.\n\nThus answer: 100.\n\nLet's produce final output.", "response": "ANSWER: 100", "answer": 100, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -9.536740606108651e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999990463263941, "logp_correct": -9.536740606108651e-07, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7178554092428024, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4248776648771582, "frac_below_-5": 0.012571428571428572, "digit_lp_min": -5.009769916534424, "answer_first_mention_lp": -4.0531076592742465e-06, "n_cot_tokens": 6125, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -4.768370445162873e-07, "logp_nocot": -12.87900185585022, "p_nocot": 2.5510593444868846e-06, "uplift": 12.87900090217616}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "b68c6bea-627a-4b1f-bb35-4421470f04b5", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Alice starts with the number $0$. She can apply $100$ operations on her number. In each operation, she can either add $1$ to her number, or square her number. After applying all operations, her score is the minimum distance from her number to any perfect square. What is the maximum score she can attain?\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Alice starts with the number $0$. She can apply $100$ operations on her number. In each operation, she can either add $1$ to her number, or square her number. After applying all operations, her score is the minimum distance from her number to any perfect square. What is the maximum score she can attain?", "true_answer": 94, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "b68c6bea-627a-4b1f-bb35-4421470f04b5:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 44207, 290, 11085, 9324, 591, 1721, 2086, 478, 316, 1062, 4387, 13749, 11, 1934, 9707, 220, 1353, 12084, 30, 50557, 392, 13181, 24667, 722, 12084, 11, 1335, 8429, 382, 290, 11085, 9324, 591, 1335, 2086, 316, 1062, 4387, 13749, 13, 4614, 382, 290, 10562, 8429, 1770, 665, 46958, 16842, 3627, 665, 6096, 869, 316, 220, 1353, 12084, 30, 10503, 25, 392, 13684, 665, 6096, 220, 1353, 12084, 402, 1335, 2086, 13, 730, 2454, 9506, 11, 1770, 665, 4784, 1147, 220, 16, 316, 1335, 2086, 11, 503, 13749, 1335, 2086, 3692, 63659, 9707, 220, 1353, 12084, 350, 21910, 44207, 8429, 10112, 2360, 722, 741, 1416, 665, 13001, 16281, 328, 659, 16, 326, 14685, 4115, 364, 42087, 25, 44207, 9324, 591, 35210, 4387, 13749, 13, 2214, 16336, 478, 11, 9324, 316, 35210, 4387, 13749, 382, 1349, 22305, 74, 92, 1022, 45, 533, 372, 61, 17, 91, 13, 27042, 316, 44207, 484, 13, 197203, 316, 478, 2447, 472, 415