1, 13749, 350, 31467, 2636, 8, 314, 10545, 13, 53583, 316, 2613, 13749, 350, 28219, 61, 17, 314, 220, 32756, 3796, 8, 8868, 314, 220, 32756, 3796, 12, 40673, 1130, 28, 37228, 13, 2632, 1349, 9324, 314, 10545, 13, 2632, 8429, 314, 10545, 364, 84787, 581, 665, 12998, 220, 10545, 364, 8475, 581, 717, 7266, 30, 260, 28, 10545, 2999, 30, 12265, 260, 314, 220, 1353, 533, 261, 533, 287, 13, 2514, 7064, 260, 581, 1309, 10389, 261, 76609, 11, 889, 1309, 19953, 7060, 13, 14720, 261, 28, 17, 11, 287, 28, 18, 8967, 260, 28, 4129, 30, 24305, 261, 28, 17, 17568, 28, 18, 2747, 261, 76609, 28, 20, 11, 260, 28, 4129, 13, 336, 314, 220, 17, 42923, 23, 102838, 5780, 11, 44237, 28, 1125, 11, 19953, 28, 2546, 13, 87451, 220, 2546, 464, 260, 220, 4129, 11, 813, 1934, 12673, 220, 4129, 581, 8088, 1991, 57269, 13, 623, 19957, 9324, 1340, 413, 3760, 13, 41021, 23864, 25, 336, 28, 5780, 11, 19953, 28, 2546, 11, 12673, 220, 4129, 871, 478, 314, 220, 33219, 13, 57076, 35210, 57269, 25, 220, 1157, 61, 17, 28, 31007, 8868, 2092, 11, 220, 858, 61, 17, 28, 30413, 8868, 702, 13, 47219, 382, 220, 702, 13, 2632, 8429, 28, 702, 11, 4150, 3760, 1572, 220, 10545, 364, 84787, 287, 2804, 413, 4410, 4951, 813, 19953, 1424, 260, 13, 623, 7060, 36144, 220, 17, 9, 64991, 6181, 8, 1424, 260, 13, 44237, 6181, 25987, 261, 42923, 17, 42923, 65, 12, 16, 6478, 13, 2632, 1309, 261, 42923, 17, 42923, 65, 12, 16, 6478, 1424, 260, 14, 17, 364, 83, 314, 220, 1353, 533, 261, 533, 287, 42308, 230, 220, 1353, 533, 350, 64, 76609, 741, 2214, 3291, 261, 17568, 11, 260, 5862, 220, 1353, 13, 2632, 581, 1309, 261, 42923, 17, 42923, 65, 12, 16, 6478, 1424, 220, 1434, 33956, 364, 11437, 19957, 261, 17568, 316, 35441, 364, 29730, 261, 28, 18, 17568, 28, 18, 25, 261, 76609, 28, 21, 871, 260, 28, 10545, 13, 44237, 6181, 25987, 18, 42923, 17, 42923, 17, 6478, 28, 18, 42923, 19, 102838, 9989, 1424, 220, 4146, 871, 7060, 23121, 13, 2632, 260, 28, 10545, 4149, 483, 261, 28, 18, 17568, 28, 18, 13, 30731, 314, 10545, 364, 26070, 581, 717, 260, 28, 4129, 30, 19792, 261, 76609, 28, 20, 13, 31061, 25, 350, 64, 17568, 8, 314, 350, 17, 11, 18, 8, 581, 8274, 28336, 19953, 13, 350, 64, 17568, 8, 314, 350, 18, 11, 17, 8, 8967, 261, 76609, 28, 20, 11, 260, 28, 4129, 13, 45438, 44237, 6181, 25987, 261, 42923, 17, 42923, 65, 12, 16, 6478, 314, 18, 42923, 17, 102838, 24, 13, 19792, 1424, 4129, 14, 17, 171441, 4146, 13, 20, 28336, 13, 350, 64, 17568, 8, 9893, 19, 11, 16, 8, 871, 44237, 28, 19, 42923, 16, 102838, 19, 28336, 13, 2632, 260, 28, 4129, 625, 58866, 364, 84787, 10562, 260, 382, 220, 10545, 364, 10620, 581, 2804, 6757, 484, 1934, 12673, 260, 124105, 11, 581, 553, 2928, 3518, 2684, 19953, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 625, 29787, 2613, 13749, 741, 19792, 260, 5017, 19953, 12, 16, 30, 50557, 316, 3357, 9324, 314, 260, 11, 581, 1309, 260, 464, 19953, 13, 12265, 9324, 316, 6931, 13749, 382, 260, 11, 316, 13057, 13749, 382, 19953, 533, 260, 13, 2214, 260, 3760, 1572, 19953, 14, 17, 11, 1349, 9324, 314, 260, 13, 12265, 19953, 395, 261, 28, 17, 17568, 28, 19, 382, 220, 40458, 11, 260, 28, 10545, 464, 220, 5780, 13, 2632, 25435, 13, 23586, 8429, 314, 10545, 364, 26070, 581, 12998, 8429, 220, 4129, 483, 2647, 261, 17568, 1919, 19953, 1424, 4129, 326, 261, 76609, 28, 20, 30, 4037, 4149, 472, 581, 8274, 364, 84787, 10562, 8429, 382, 220, 10545, 364, 4383, 1062, 25697, 483, 261, 76609, 28, 19, 350, 83, 28, 6768, 741, 22483, 25, 350, 64, 17568, 8, 2023, 413, 350, 17, 11, 17, 8, 871, 287, 28, 17, 11, 44237, 6181, 25987, 17, 42923, 17, 102838, 19, 11, 19953, 28, 24, 464, 6768, 28336, 13, 350, 18, 11, 16, 3127, 44237, 28, 18, 11, 19953, 28, 22, 28336, 13, 350, 19, 11, 15, 3127, 860, 57269, 11, 44237, 625, 9113, 11, 889, 581, 2023, 1327, 1147, 124105, 1606, 26, 2999, 9324, 591, 57269, 395, 478, 869, 316, 220, 1353, 382, 540, 1645, 220, 702, 472, 11965, 13, 2632, 625, 364, 84787, 1636, 382, 260, 28, 10545, 364, 72816, 6052, 314, 220, 10545, 364, 94272, 1721, 4733, 13], "n_tokens": 6806, "words": 3729, "band_lo": 1864.5, "band_hi": 7458.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to maximize the minimum distance from final number N to any perfect square, after exactly 100 operations? Actually \"after applying all operations, her score is the minimum distance from her number to any perfect square. What is the maximum score she can attain?\" She can apply up to 100 operations? Word: \"She can apply 100 operations on her number. In each operation, she can either add 1 to her number, or square her number.\" Probably exactly 100 operations (since maximize score maybe using all). We can decide sequence of +1 and squaring.\n\nGoal: maximize distance from nearest perfect square. For integer N, distance to nearest perfect square is min_{k} |N - k^2|. Want to maximize that. Equivalent to N being as far as possible from any perfect square, i.e., in middle of large gaps between squares. Gap between successive squares m^2 and (m+1)^2 is 2m+1. The farthest point is floor ((m^2 + (m+1)^2)/2 ) = m^2 + m. Distance to nearest square is floor((2m+1)/2) = m (if gap odd). For gap length L=2m+1, farthest distance = m. So maximum distance achievable for any N is about sqrt(N)/2? Actually for given N, nearest square distance <= floor(sqrt(N)). The maximum possible distance given bound on N (by operations) will be roughly half the gap.\n\nWe need to determine max possible N after 100 ops? Starting from 0, operations: +1 increments slowly; squaring can explode. Max N achievable is by doing squares as late as possible? Actually to maximize distance we might want N to be around mid of a large gap, requiring large N. So we want to maximize N under 100 operations, then distance roughly sqrt(N)/2. So maximize N.\n\nWe have sequence of +1 and squaring. Starting at 0, squaring 0 stays 0. So initial +1 necessary to get >0. The optimal pattern: apply +1 once, then repeatedly square to exponentiate. But adding more +1 before squaring may yield bigger later after more squares (since addition gets amplified). Classic problem: maximize value after limited operations of increment and square; best to increment early then square many times. So we have 100 operations total. Let’s decide to do a sequence of a increments, then b squares, where a + b = 100. Starting from 0, after a increments we have value = a. Then each square multiplies exponent: after b squares, value = a^{2^{b}}.\n\nIs there any benefit to interleaving increments between squares? That could increase: Suppose we add some, square, then add more, square again. The optimal is to distribute increments among squares perhaps? This is akin to maximizing final value given operations of +1 and squaring; known optimal is to add 1's as early as possible before the first square, then maybe after each square add 1? Let's analyze.\n\nWe can think of representation as a binary tree: each increment could be applied at a certain depth (how many subsequent squares will amplify it).\n\nIf we have increments at different times, an increment performed t steps before the end that is then squared s times (where s = remaining squares) contributes 1 * 2^{s} (since each square doubles exponent? Wait value transforms: Suppose you have current value X. After a square, value becomes X^2, not X*2. So adding 1 earlier will be raised to a large power many times, making huge effect.\n\nExample: Starting value x, after square: x^2. Adding 1 then squaring: (x+1)^2 = x^2 + 2x +1, not just etc. Hard to linearize.\n\nBut known optimal is to add 1 just before each square? Let's test small case: 2 operations. Options: ++ => 2. +- => after +1 =>1 then square =>1. -+ => square 0 =>0 then +1 =>1. So max is 2 using ++. But if we had 3 ops: ++ + =>3. ++ square: after 2 increments get 2 then square =>4. + square +: after 1 inc =>1, square =>1, then + =>2. square ++: square 0 =>0 then ++ =>2. So max =4 using ++ then square. That suggests putting increments before square yields bigger.\n\nFor larger, pattern of many increments, then many squares yields huge value. Interleaving may increase more? Let's test with 4 ops. Strategy A: ++ ++ =>4. ++ + square: start 2 then + =>3 then square =>9. ++ square ++: after 2 inc =>2, square=>4, then ++ =>6. + ++ square: after 1 inc 1, ++ =>3, square=>9. Wait that's different order. Let's enumerate best: Possibly ++ + square gave 9. Could there be larger? ++ ++ (no squares) =4. + square ++ => after 1 inc 1, square 1, ++ =>3. square ++ + => 0 then ++ =>2 then + =>3. So max 9. That equals (a) increments before first square? Here sequence: ++ + square = increments 3 then square, a=3,b=1 gives 3^2=9. So indeed increments first then squares yields a^2. If we had two squares b=2: need 2 squares, rest increments. Suppose a=2,b=2 (total 4). compute: start 2, square =>4, square =>16. So 16 > 9. So pattern: increments first then all squares yields final = a^{2^{b}}.\n\nThus interleaving seems not better because increments earlier gets exponentiated more than later increments plus later squares? Let's test with 5 ops: option: ++ ++ square (a=4,b=1) => 4^2=16. Option: ++ square ++ (a=2,b=2? Actually sequence: ++ (2), square =>4, ++ =>6 final 6 not as big. Option: ++ ++ ++ square (a=6,b=1) =>36. Option: ++ ++ square ++? 2 inc, square =>4, ++ =>5 final5. Option with a=3,b=2 (total 5): inc 3, square =>9, square =>81. That's 81 >36, but total ops 3+2=5. So yes increments then squares yields max.\n\nThus general: maximize N = a^{2^{b}} where a + b = 100, a,b nonnegative integers, a>=0, b integer. Need to maximize that expression.\n\nWe need to choose a and b. As b increases, exponent grows double exponentially but base a decreases. Let's compute approximate.\n\nWe want to maximize log(N) = 2^{b} * ln a. Subject to a = 100 - b.\n\nDefine f(b) = 2^{b} * ln (100 - b). Compute values for b from 0 to 99 (though a must be >=1 else value 0). Use approximation.\n\nLet's evaluate few points.\n\nWe can use computers but will approximate.\n\nCompute for b=0: N=100^1=100.\n\nb=1: a=99, exponent 2, N=99^2 ≈9801.\n\nb=2: a=98, exponent 4, N=98^4. 98^2≈9604, ^4 =9604^2≈92,? 9604^2 ≈92,? compute: 9600^2=92,160,000; plus extra etc approx 92 million. So N ~9.2e7.\n\nb=3: a=97, exponent 8, N=97^8 = (97^4)^2. 97^2=9409; 97^4=9409^2≈88,? 9400^2=88,360,000; So approx 88 million. Then square -> ~7.8e15. So huge.\n\nb=4: a=96, exponent 16, N=96^16 huge.\n\nThus increasing b quickly escalates N enormously; likely optimum is max b (as long as a >= 2 maybe) because exponent grows faster than base loss. However when a gets small, ln a becomes small (maybe zero). Need to find b near 99 maybe a=1. If a=1, N = 1^{2^{b}} =1, not good.\n\nSo we need a >=2 to yield >1. For a=2, b=98, N=2^{2^{98}} astronomically huge. So maximum is achieved with a=2, b=98 (or a=3,b=97 maybe larger?). Compare: N=2^{2^{98}} vs 3^{2^{97}}. Since exponents differ: log2 of N1 = 2^{98}. log2 of N2 = 2^{97} * log2 3 ≈ 2^{97} *1.585. That's = 2^{97} *1.585 = 0.7925 *2^{98}. So N2 is roughly 2^{0.7925 *2^{98}} vs N1 is 2^{2^{98}}. N1 is vastly larger. So a=2 is best with huge exponent.\n\nBut we must ensure a+b=100, a integer >=0. a=2 yields b=98. That's allowed (98 squares). Starting from 0, we need to get to 2 via two increments at start; then 98 squares. However note that squaring zero stays zero; after first increment we have 1 then second increment =>2. Then squares will keep growing: after first square:4, then 16, etc. So N = 2^{2^{98}} . That's massive.\n\nNow the score = distance to nearest perfect square: For N = something huge, what is nearest square distance? N itself might be a perfect square? Let's check: Is 2^{2^{98}} a perfect square? Write N = 2^{2^{98}}. A perfect square has even exponent on prime factors. Here exponent of 2 is 2^{98}, which is even? 2^{98} is power of 2, even for exponent >=1? Actually exponent is integer e = 2^{98}. For a number to be a perfect square, its exponent on each prime must be even. e is even (since 2^{98} is divisible by 2), thus N is a perfect square: N = (2^{2^{97}})^2. Indeed because exponent halved.\n\nSo distance to nearest perfect square is 0! That gives score 0, not maximum.\n\nThus we need N not to be a perfect square; we want N in middle of gap. So we need to avoid final being perfect square. We could adjust by adding some +1 after squares to offset.\n\nWe have 100 operations fixed; we can choose order any. So we can aim to end with a number that is far from any square. There is trade-off: we need a large N but not a perfect square, and also not close to a square.\n\nIdea: Use the maximal N (2^{2^{98}}) and then at final step add 1 (or some small number) to make it not perfect square and maybe at middle. Since we have predetermined number of operations, we could reserve last operation as +1 instead of square, resulting in N = 2^{2^{98}} + 1 (if we use a=2, b=97 squares then +1). However then total ops: a=2 inc, b=97 squares, plus final +1 inc => total increments =3, squares=97, total=100. So N = ((2)^{2^{97}})^{2}? Wait compute: Starting 2, then 97 squares => exponent 2^{97}. So after 97 squares we have 2^{2^{97}}. Then final +1 => N = 2^{2^{97}} + 1. That may be far from nearest square?\n\nWe need to evaluate distance between N and nearest perfect square. Since N is massive, the gap between squares near sqrt(N) is about 2*sqrt(N)+1 ≈ 2*2^{2^{96}} +1 huge. Distance from N to nearest square is at most about half gap ≈ sqrt(N). Since we have +1 shift, the distance could be very small if N is just after a square. Let's consider whether 2^{2^{97}} is a perfect square? Exponent 2^{97} even? Since 2^{97} is even (since 97>=1). So base 2 exponent even => it's a perfect square: 2^{2^{97}} = (2^{2^{96}})^2. Then N = that square + 1. So the distance to the nearest square is min(1, distance to next square -1). The next square after (2^{2^{96}})^2 is (2^{2^{96}}+1)^2 = (2^{2^{96}})^2 + 2*2^{2^{96}} +1. That's difference of 2*2^{2^{96}} +1. So N = square +1 is 1 away from lower square. So distance =1. Score =1.\n\nWe can maybe do better than 1.\n\nIf we end with number that is roughly halfway between squares, distance could be about sqrt(N)/2 which is enormous. To achieve that, we need to choose final number not too close to a perfect square. We can after squares add some offset k to jump into middle. Since we have limited increments (+1 steps), we can only add up to maybe some small amount relative to huge N. But we could incorporate increments earlier before final squares to affect N more significantly, because early increments get exponentiated.\n\nThus we can try to craft N such that it's roughly half way between two squares. The effect of early increments can shift the final huge number by a multiplicative factor, not additive.\n\nConsider we have a increments before squares; each increment adds 1 before exponentiation becomes huge. So maybe we can choose a such that final N is not a perfect square, and its fractional part relative to nearest squares is large.\n\nGoal: Maximize minimal distance = >? Could be as large as ~ sqrt(N)/2.\n\nGiven we can make N arbitrarily large (exponential), the distance can be huge as well, but we are limited by 100 ops, so N is bounded (still huge) but we can possibly achieve huge distance.\n\nWe need to find the maximal possible minimal distance, which likely equals floor( (gap-1)/2 ) where gap = 2*floor(sqrt(N)) +1. With N near the center of a gap, distance ≈ floor(gap/2). So approximate distance ≈ sqrt(N)/? Actually gap ≈ 2 sqrt(N) (since sqrt(N) ≈ m). So distance max ≈ sqrt(N). So we need to maximize sqrt(N) essentially. So we need to maximize N while still able to position value near middle of gap, not being a perfect square.\n\nSo we can choose a and b such that N is not a perfect square; we have control over N = something. Perhaps best to make N = a^{2^{b}} + c where c is not too small. But c cannot be large additive; we can add at most maybe a few (since after squares each +1 is not amplified). So we may need to incorporate the +1 earlier.\n\nBetter: Choose a such that a is odd (so that a^{2^{b}} is a perfect square when exponent even? Actually any integer a, if exponent 2^{b} is even, then result is perfect square: a^{2^{b}} = (a^{2^{b-1}})^2. So any a^{2^{b}} is perfect square for b>=1. Therefore final N must include some non-square component: either we end with a+something after last square, or we have at least one increment after the last square to break perfect square nature. That addition will be small relative to N, causing distance likely small (like 1). To get larger distance we need to have last operation not be a square, but maybe a sequence of squares then a few increments (maybe many) to move into middle of gap. How many increments can we have after the last square? We can use many +1 operations after final square: suppose we have s squares, then t increments afterwards, with t can be up to 100 - (a + s). But early increments also can be after squares? The idea: we need a large number of increments after the last square to move by a sizable amount relative to N (but N is huge, gap huge). However t increments add only t, which is negligible compared to sqrt(N) ~ huge; can't reach middle.\n\nThus maybe we need to avoid having final exponent huge; maybe choose moderate exponent to allow additive increments to be substantial fraction of sqrt(N). So we need to balance.\n\nLet final value N = A^{2^{b}} + t where t is number of increments after last square (0 ≤ t ≤ remaining ops). sqrt(N) ≈ A^{2^{b-1}} (since sqrt of power). Gap ≈ 2*sqrt(N). To make t comparable to sqrt(N), we need t ≈ sqrt(N) i.e., huge, impossible since t ≤ 100. So additive after squares can't give large distance.\n\nThus we need to use increments before squares but not just one block: maybe we can alternate to create N that is not a perfect square and not too close to a perfect square. But any number of the form integer^power where exponent is power of 2 will be a perfect square. However if we have multiple increments interleaved, the final expression will be more complex, not a perfect power, possibly giving large distance.\n\nWe need to consider general expression: starting from 0, sequence of operations: +1 and square. This corresponds to building a number expressed in base-2? Actually it's like building using recursive formula: if we denote f(sequence) as result.\n\nObservation: This process similar to constructing numbers in binary representation when using operation: double (x->2x) vs adding 1; but here squares are more aggressive.\n\nHard to enumerate.\n\nMaybe the maximum minimal distance is 1? Could be that any number reachable after many squares is either a perfect square or one away from a perfect square, due to structure. Not likely.\n\nLet's test small case search: For small total ops, compute max distance. Let's try manually for small n like 3 ops we found max N=9 distance 0. But maybe other sequence gives distance 1? For 3 ops, possibilities: ++ + =>3 distance to nearest square: nearest squares 1,4 --> distance min(2,1)=1. So score 1. The max we found earlier maybe 9 (distance 0). So max score is 1 (since 9 distance 0, 3 distance 1). There might be also 2 distance 0? Actually 2 distance to nearest square = min(|2-1|,|4-2|)=1. So max 1.\n\nFor 4 ops: we have value 16 (distance 0), 9 (distance 0), 15? sequence: ++ ++ + =>5 distance 1; ++ + ++ =>? start2, + =>3, ++ =>5 distance 1. maybe we can achieve distance larger? The gap near sqrt(20)=4.47, squares 16 and 25 gap9, midpoint distance floor(9/2)=4. Could we reach N=20? Possibly? Let's see if we can produce 20 with 4 ops: start 0: + =>1, square =>1, + =>2, square =>4? That's 4 ops gives 4. Not 20. Hard.\n\nProbably max distance for 4 ops maybe 2? Let's brute think: we have limited possibilities; maybe max distance 2? Not known.\n\nGiven complexity, maybe answer is 1 for any number of ops >1? But seems unlikely for larger ops because you can get numbers like 2^16 + something? Could be distance still small because number ends as perfect square +/- small.\n\nConjecture: For any sequence, the result is of form k^2 + r where |r| < k? Not certain.\n\nLet's think: The squaring operation makes the number either perfect square (if the current number is integer) after squaring. Starting with integer, after each square the result is perfect square. Adding 1 to a perfect square yields a number 1 above a square, distance 1 from that square, but could be close to next square? Next square is (k+1)^2 = k^2 +2k+1, distance from k^2+1 to next square is 2k, which is huge if k large. So after you have a huge perfect square, adding 1 yields distance 1 (to lower square) but also far from upper square. The minimal distance remains 1. So adding a few increments after a huge square yields minimal distance = min(r, (2k+1)-r) where r is increments added. For r small, distance = r (since lower square distance). For r large close to gap, distance may become small again near upper square. Max minimal distance achievable after adding t increments is floor(gap/2) when r approx gap/2. But t is limited to at most 100. Gap = 2k+1 ≈ 2 sqrt(N)+1 huge, so t cannot reach middle. Therefore best minimal distance after a huge square is max r we can achieve which is number of increments after that square (t). So distance <= t. Since t ≤ remaining operations after the last square. So to maximize distance, we need to have many increments after the last square, but also need the square to be huge to make gap big enough that those increments are far from both squares? Wait minimal distance after t increments is min(r, gap - r). If r=t (since we start from lower square and add t), distance = t if t <= gap/2, which holds because gap huge. So distance = t. So score equals number of increments after final square (as long as they are less than half gap). So to maximize score, we want to maximize number of +1 operations after the last square.\n\nThus we should make as many +1 after final square as possible. That suggests we should do squares early, then use remaining operations as +1 to increase distance.\n\nSpecifically, we can do a sequence: first we do some increments to get base a (>0), then many squares (b), then remaining t increments (t = 100 - a - b). Score = t (since after final square we have perfect square S = a^{2^{b}}; we add t to get N = S + t; distance to lower square is t; to upper square distance = gap - t which is huge > t). So score = t.\n\nThus maximize t given constraints a≥1? Actually need a ≥1 to avoid zero squares.\n\nWe need at least one increment before any square to get >0. Also need at least one square perhaps? Not necessary to have square; could have all increments => N=100 distance to nearest square maybe higher? Let's compute: N=100, sqrt≈10, nearest squares 100 (10^2) distance 0. So not good.\n\nIf we have zero squares, then distance could be up to about sqrt(N)/2 maybe with N near middle of gap; but we cannot get huge N because max N=100; gap ~ 2*10+1=21, distance max ~10. So score up to 10 possible without squares.\n\nWith squares, we can have t increments after large square, giving score = t. To maximize t, we should minimize a + b while ensuring we have at least one square (b>=1) and a>=1. So t = 100 - a - b. Want to maximize t => minimize a+b. Minimum a=1 (need to get to 1), b=1 (one square). Then t=98. Let's test feasibility: sequence: + (1), square =>1, then 98 increments. Final N = 1 + 98 = 99. Wait after square 1 stays 1. So final N = 1 + 98 =99. That's not huge, but still we have only one square that didn't increase value. Actually a=1 results in perfect square S=1, gap = 3 (between 0^2=0 and 1^2=1? Actually sqrt near 1). The nearest squares: 9? Wait 1 is square; after adding t increments, we have N= 1 + t. For t=98, N=99. The lower square is 1 (distance 98). Upper square is 10^2=100 distance 1. Minimum distance = min(98,1)=1. Because upper square is close. So our earlier assumption fails because gap between squares grows with sqrt(N). After we add t to a small square, we may cross into next squares. Since S is small, adding many increments will cross many squares, making distance small.\n\nThus we need S to be huge so that adding t increments does not reach next square. So we need S big enough that gap > t. gap = 2*sqrt(S) +1 > t => sqrt(S) > (t-1)/2. So we need S > ((t-1)/2)^2.\n\nThus to maximize t, we need to also ensure S large enough. So we need to choose a,b such that S = a^{2^{b}} > ((t-1)/2)^2.\n\nGiven t = 100 - a - b. Want maximize t subject to a^{2^{b}} > ((t-1)/2)^2.\n\nLet's search small a values.\n\nTry a=2, b=1 => S=2^2=4, gap = 2*2+1=5. t = 100-3=97. Need gap> t -> 5 >97 false.\n\na=2,b=2 => S=2^4=16, sqrt=4, gap=9, t=100-4=96, insufficient.\n\nIncreasing b quickly makes S huge, gap huge. Let's test a=2,b=6 => exponent 64, S=2^{64} ≈ 1.84e19, sqrt=2^{32}≈4.29e9, gap≈2*4.29e9+1≈8.58e9. t = 100 - (2+6)=92. Gap >> t, condition satisfied. So t=92 achievable with a=2,b=6.\n\nBut we could increase b to reduce a+b further? a minimal 2, b minimal 1 but gap small. Need b large enough that gap>t. So find minimal b such that gap > 100 - (2+b). Gap = 2*sqrt(S)+1 = 2*a^{2^{b-1}}+1 = 2*2^{2^{b-1}}+1.\n\nWe need 2*2^{2^{b-1}}+1 > 100 - (2+b) ≈ 98 - b.\n\nSince RHS ≤98, left is huge for b≥2: b=2 => 2*2^{2}=2*4=8+1=9 > 96? no. b=3: 2*2^{4}=2*16=32+1=33 > 95? No. b=4: exponent 8 => 2^{8}=256, left =2*256+1=513 > 94. So condition satisfied from b=4 onward.\n\nThus minimal b=4 yields t = 100-(2+4)=94. Gap=513 >94, so distance after t increments = t? Need verify that after adding 94 to 2^{16}=65536? Wait compute S = a^{2^{b}} = 2^{2^{4}}= 2^{16}=65536. Actually b=4 squares, exponent 2^{4}=16. So S=65536 which is perfect square (256^2). Gap = 2*256+1=513. Adding t=94 leads to N=65630. Distance to lower square (65536) =94. Distance to next square (257^2 = 66049) diff = 66049-65630=419. So min distance =94. So score =94.\n\nThus we can achieve 94.\n\nCan we get higher? t=94 max? Since t = 100 - a - b. To increase t we need reduce a+b, but need gap condition. Try a=2, b=3 gives t=95? Wait a=2,b=3 -> a+b=5, t=95. S = 2^{8}=256, sqrt=16, gap=33. Gap 33 < t 95, so after adding 95 we cross many squares. The minimal distance may be less. Let's compute: S=256, gap=33, adding 95 => N = 351. Determine nearest squares: 18^2=324 diff27, 19^2=361 diff10. Minimum is 10. So score=10, far less than 94.\n\nThus b must be large enough so gap > t. The condition roughly 2*sqrt(S) > t. sqrt(S)= a^{2^{b-1}}. So need a^{2^{b-1}} > t/2.\n\nt = 100 - a - b ≈ 100 - (a+b). For small a,b, t near 100. So we need a^{2^{b-1}} > 50 approx.\n\nFind minimal a,b to satisfy.\n\nTry a=3,b=3: a+b=6 => t=94. sqrt(S)=3^{2^{2}}=3^{4}=81 > 47 => condition satisfied. So t=94 possible with a=3,b=3. Score =94.\n\nCould we get t=95? Need a+b=5. possibilities: (a,b) = (2,3) we saw fails gap. (a,b) = (3,2) gives a+b=5, t=95. Compute sqrt(S)= a^{2^{b-1}} =3^{2}=9. Need >95/2≈47.5 fails. (a,b)=(4,1) => sqrt=4^{1}=4 fails. So t=95 not feasible.\n\nThus maximum t is 94.\n\nNow we must ensure that after adding t increments, we are still within same gap (i.e., not reaching next square). Need t <= gap-1? Actually to keep distance = t, we need t < gap. Since distance to lower square is t, to upper square is gap - t. For t less than gap/2, min distance = t. Since gap for a=2,b=4 is 513, t=94 < 256. So okay. Thus score =94.\n\nCould we achieve score 95 with different a,b where gap >95 and a+b=5? Not possible as we saw.\n\nThus maximum score is 94.\n\nCheck any scenario with a+b=4 (t=96). Options: (a,b) could be (2,2) => b=2, sqrt(S)=2^{2}=4, gap=9 <96 fails. (3,1): sqrt=3, gap=7 fails. (4,0): no squares, sqrt not defined, but we could just add increments only; max distance from squares for N up to 100 is at most 10 as earlier. So not.\n\nThus best is t=94.\n\nTherefore answer = 94.\n\nProvide final output.", "response": "ANSWER: 94", "answer": 94, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -6.24637395389982e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999375382112797, "logp_correct": -6.24637395389982e-05, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6464802790859531, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3210371245224621, "frac_below_-5": 0.010011778563015312, "digit_lp_min": -4.562519550323486, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0003564914222806692, "n_cot_tokens": 6792, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -5.960462772236497e-07, "logp_nocot": -21.634805079396756, "p_nocot": 4.01905118259891e-10, "uplift": 21.634742615657217}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "b7042c20-6caa-4bef-baf4-1de16fa5c364", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Peter and Basil together thought of ten quadratic trinomials. Then, Basil began calling consecutive natural numbers starting with some natural number. After each called number, Peter chose one of the ten polynomials at random and plugged in the called number. The results were recorded on the board. They eventually form a sequence. After they finished, their sequence was arithmetic. What is the greatest number of numbers that Basil could have called out?\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Peter and Basil together thought of ten quadratic trinomials. Then, Basil began calling consecutive natural numbers starting with some natural number. After each called number, Peter chose one of the ten polynomials at random and plugged in the called number. The results were recorded on the board. They eventually form a sequence. After they finished, their sequence was arithmetic. What is the greatest number of numbers that Basil could have called out?", "true_answer": 20, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "b7042c20-6caa-4bef-baf4-1de16fa5c364:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 679, 261, 4792, 25, 4325, 178997, 498, 84556, 16596, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 178997, 1823, 174033, 8, 16180, 656, 16104, 326, 82435, 30, 3164, 4525, 328, 4325, 178997, 498, 84556, 16596, 350, 786, 1023, 392, 112245, 328, 672, 575, 2560, 4213, 1023, 679, 4325, 13213, 39966, 55687, 741, 7801, 82435, 11666, 40840, 6247, 8663, 8601, 483, 1236, 6247, 2086, 13, 6311, 2454, 4358, 2086, 11, 16104, 75184, 1001, 328, 290, 4325, 1823, 174033, 540, 7526, 326, 95863, 306, 290, 4358, 2086, 13, 623, 4376, 1504, 19460, 402, 290, 6796, 13, 3164, 17462, 1625, 261, 16281, 13, 6311, 1023, 11684, 11, 1