18, 41951, 22569, 15286, 316, 4137, 16174, 472, 3291, 472, 4149, 2049, 16913, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 220, 16, 11, 17, 11, 18, 35229, 472, 1991, 472, 6118, 4609, 4215, 42373, 220, 2636, 13, 41351, 290, 19957, 4215, 328, 363, 1858, 11, 17, 8, 21241, 1261, 581, 2304, 290, 42173, 16913, 16174, 11, 2236, 363, 1858, 11, 17, 8, 42326, 8065, 483, 265, 13, 2632, 316, 44207, 22569, 11, 581, 1682, 316, 1199, 290, 42173, 4149, 16913, 997, 16174, 364, 84787, 5655, 42173, 16913, 8841, 54912, 484, 4215, 316, 220, 2636, 13, 623, 19957, 4215, 328, 1577, 284, 54912, 314, 284, 2145, 10, 16, 11876, 17, 13, 9764, 42173, 284, 2238, 484, 495, 4215, 5064, 2636, 13, 45438, 284, 28, 23, 8967, 220, 2636, 9707, 350, 21910, 220, 23, 9, 24, 14, 17, 28, 2636, 741, 2632, 290, 24174, 382, 9707, 7881, 16174, 220, 16, 485, 23, 13, 4569, 15248, 1039, 9770, 364, 84787, 1062, 1273, 12545, 328, 16913, 997, 16174, 1481, 679, 12384, 3609, 328, 8812, 12486, 22569, 11, 20547, 306, 30732, 22569, 364, 198140, 10562, 22569, 314, 3609, 4149, 22569, 40335, 8812, 12486, 22569, 395, 16174, 220, 16, 485, 23, 314, 220, 31316, 533, 350, 16, 10, 18, 10, 21, 10, 702, 10, 1055, 10, 2040, 10, 2029, 8, 29702, 220, 31316, 12, 9928, 28, 43112, 364, 84787, 19545, 364, 10620, 2804, 6757, 484, 9882, 9778, 483, 2684, 2349, 6284, 4310, 13, 12265, 581, 2061, 16913, 16174, 11, 18210, 16913, 13, 30255, 23121, 364, 198140, 6052, 220, 43112, 364, 94272, 6052, 6011, 25, 392, 160761, 25, 220, 43112, 4050], "n_tokens": 5012, "words": 3303, "band_lo": 1597.5, "band_hi": 6390.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have 36 participants. Handshake graph: simple undirected graph on 36 vertices. Condition: No two participants with same degree are adjacent. That is, vertices of equal degree form an independent set. We need to maximize number of edges under that constraint.\n\nWe need to find the maximum possible sum of degrees /2 given 36 vertices, simple graph, constraint that vertices with same degree are not adjacent.\n\nWe can think of degree sequence of length 36, each degree between 0 and 35. Additionally, for any degree value d, the vertices with degree d must form an independent set.\n\nGoal maximize total edges.\n\nObservation: If we assign distinct degrees to all vertices, then condition automatically satisfied because no two same degree. But can we have distinct degrees for 36 vertices? Maximum different degrees available: 0..35 (36 values). So yes we could assign each vertex a distinct degree 0-35. Is that realizable as a simple graph? That's the classic \"graphical sequence\" problem (Erdos-Gallai). Sequence 0,1,2,...,35 is not graphical because sum of degrees must be even: sum = (0+35)*36/2 = 35*18 = 630. 630 is even? 630 is even (yes). Need also satisfy Erdős–Gallai inequalities. The sequence 0,1,...,n-1 is graphical for n even? Let's recall known result: The complete graph K_n has degree n-1 for all vertices (not independent). But sequence 0..n-1 is realized by a tournament? Wait.\n\nWe can try construct graph with degrees 0..35. There are 36 vertices. Vertex with degree 35 must connect to all other 35 vertices. That includes vertex of degree 0, which would then have degree at least 1, contradiction. So cannot have both degree 35 and degree 0 in same simple graph because vertex with degree 35 adjacent to all others gives each other vertex at least degree1. So degree 0 cannot coexist with degree35. Actually the condition of independent same-degree doesn't prevent adjacency between different degree vertices. So we need to find a degree sequence where for each d, the vertices with degree d are independent, but we may still have adjacency between degree35 and degree0; but degree0 would then have degree ≥1, contradiction. So indeed can't have both degree0 and degree35 simultaneously because degree35 vertex forces degree0 vertex to be adjacent, making its degree at least 1.\n\nThus we cannot have the full range. Need to pick a set of degrees such that the degree constraints are realizable.\n\nWe need maximize total edges, i.e., maximize sum of degrees/2.\n\nIntuitively, we want many high-degree vertices but they can't be adjacent to each other if they share degree, but can be adjacent to lower degree vertices.\n\nProbably the optimal arrangement: partition vertices into groups by degree, each group independent. So edges only go between groups of different degrees.\n\nGoal maximize edges given that vertices in higher-degree groups must have many neighbors, which must be drawn from lower-degree groups.\n\nWe can think of ordering degrees descending. For a vertex of degree d, it must connect to d vertices among those with different degree.\n\nIf we have groups G_k with degree value k, size n_k.\n\nConstraints:\n\n- For each k, each vertex in G_k must have exactly k neighbors among vertices not in G_k (since no internal edges). So total edges incident to G_k = k * n_k.\n\nBut each edge counted twice across its two endpoints. So sum over k of k n_k = 2E.\n\nBut also each edge connects a vertex of degree a to vertex of degree b, where a ≠ b perhaps (could be same degree but not allowed). So each edge contributes 1 to each side sum.\n\nWe need to ensure feasibility: For each group G_k, the set of possible neighbors includes all other groups. But a vertex cannot have more neighbors than total vertices outside its group: i.e., k ≤ 36 - n_k (since cannot connect to its own group). Also there must be enough total \"available degree slots\" in other groups to accommodate connections.\n\nWe can think of constructing bipartite between each pair of groups with edges such that each vertex of group k gets degree k across all edges to other groups.\n\nThis is a degree-constrained bipartite multigraph problem.\n\nSimplify: Let's assume we assign degrees such that all degree values are distinct i.e., each group size =1. Then condition satisfied trivially. Then each vertex degree d_i distinct. The constraints: For each vertex i with degree d_i, it must connect to d_i other vertices (all distinct). That's possible if we can realize degree sequence as a simple graph. So the problem reduces to find a graphical degree sequence of length 36 maximizing sum of degrees, under the condition that degrees are all distinct (or at least no two equal vertices adjacent, still possible with repeats but independent). Distinct may be optimal due to maximizing sum.\n\nThus we need to find maximal sum of distinct degrees possible for a simple graph on 36 vertices. The maximal sum of distinct degrees cannot exceed sum of 35+34+...+0 = 630 but we must respect realizability.\n\nConstraint: No vertex can have degree 35 if there exists vertex of degree 0 (contradiction). So cannot have both extremes. Similarly, if we have degree 34, then any vertex not adjacent to that vertex will have degree at most? Actually vertex of degree 34 misses exactly one neighbor. That missing neighbor could be degree 0 or any other vertex not connected. If we have degree 0, then that zero-degree vertex is not connected to anyone, in particular not to degree 34 vertex, okay. So degree 34 can coexist with degree 0: The single missing neighbor for degree34 could be the degree0 vertex, fulfilling both. But degree0 still degree0, fine. However degree34 vertex must be adjacent to all 34 other vertices (excluding itself and the zero-degree vertex). The degrees of those 34 vertices must be at least 1 (since they are adjacent to the degree34 vertex). So they cannot be 0. That's okay.\n\nThus we can include degree0 and degree34. But degree35 can't coexist with degree0.\n\nPotentially we can include degree35 if we drop degree0. Could we have degree35 plus many other high degrees? Let's try to construct maximum sum using distinct degrees but ensure realizability.\n\nErdős–Gallai: Sequence d1 ≥ d2 ≥ ... ≥ d_n. For our case, we can choose to drop some low values to allow high values.\n\nGoal maximize sum = total edges*2. So we want highest possible degrees.\n\nWe can think we might be able to realize all degrees from 1 to 35 (i.e., missing 0). That's 35 values, need 36 vertices, so one degree must repeat. But if we repeat a degree, the two vertices of same degree cannot be adjacent. That's permissible.\n\nLet's test possibility of degrees 1..35 each appears once (35 vertices) plus an extra vertex with some degree among these (repeat). The repeated degree vertices must be nonadjacent.\n\nBut first need to check if degree sequence 1..35 (with sum = (1+35)*35/2 = 36*35/2? Wait n=35. Sum = 35*36/2 = 630. Actually same as 0..35 sum minus 0 plus missing? Let's compute: sum 1..35 = 35*36/2 = 630. So same total as 0..35 (because sum0..35 also 630). So having degrees 1..35 uses 35 vertices, sum 630. Adding a sixth vertex (36th) will increase sum further; but max degree per vertex is 35. So extra vertex degree at most 35. However having already vertex degree35 present, if we add another degree35, they'd share same degree and cannot be adjacent, but each degree35 vertex would need to be adjacent to all other 35 vertices (except itself). However they cannot be adjacent to each other (same degree). That's impossible because degree35 vertex needs 35 neighbors (all other vertices). If there are two such vertices, they must be adjacent to each other, contradiction. Thus at most one vertex can have degree 35. So extra vertex must have degree ≤34.\n\nNow sum currently 630. Adding a vertex with degree d adds d to total sum, increasing edges by d/2 (but must keep graphic). However we also increase total vertices to 36, while existing degrees still sum 630. But note that maximum total degree sum for 36 vertices simple graph is 36*35 =1260 (if complete). So 630+ d ≤1260 obviously.\n\nBut need to verify if sequence (degrees 1..35, plus d) is graphical, with constraint that equal-degree vertices independent. Since we may have duplicate degree if d equals some existing value.\n\nWe need to maximize d such that sequence is graphical.\n\nBut perhaps we can do better by also having degree 0? Let's explore systematically using known theorem: The handshake graph with independent equal-degree vertices is known as a \"graph with no adjacency among equal-degree vertices\" similar to \"graphical degree sequence with forbidden edges among equal-degree classes\". Might be complex, but we can attempt constructive upper bound.\n\nUpper bound: Suppose we sort vertices by degree descending: d1 ≥ d2 ≥ ... ≥ d36. For any i, the i-th highest degree vertex cannot be adjacent to any vertex among the first k where those vertices have same degree as it. Since degrees distinct? Not necessary.\n\nBut we can bound using that each vertex of degree d can be adjacent to at most 36 - n_d -1? Actually cannot connect to vertices of same degree within its own group; thus maximum potential neighbors = 36 - n_d (excluding itself and its own group). So d ≤ 36 - n_d.\n\nThus n_d ≤ 36 - d.\n\nFor each degree value d, the number of vertices with that degree <= 36 - d. This ensures feasibility.\n\nNow to maximize sum ∑ d n_d, we would like many vertices with high d, but bound limits n_d.\n\nWe can treat each degree possible from 0 to 35. For each d, max count n_d = 36 - d.\n\nThus we could set n_d = 36 - d for all d perhaps, but sum of n_d across all d would exceed 36 (since sum_{d=0}^{35} (36 - d) = 36*36 - sum d = 1296 - 630 = 666). Too many vertices.\n\nWe need choose distribution such that total vertices =36, while maximizing weighted sum.\n\nThis is a kind of knapSack: maximize total weight = Σ d n_d subject to Σ n_d =36 and n_d ≤ 36 - d, integer ≥0.\n\nSince higher d gives higher contribution per vertex, we should allocate as many vertices as possible to highest d values, respecting constraints.\n\nThus start from d=35 downwards, set n_35 ≤ 1 (since 36 -35 =1). So we can have at most 1 vertex degree35.\n\nTake n_35 =1.\n\nNext d=34: bound n_34 ≤ 2. because 36-34=2. We can allocate up to 2 vertices degree34, but need check feasibility with other constraints (they cannot be adjacent to each other because same degree). That's okay; each needs 34 neighbors among the 34 others not in its group (since group size 2, each vertex cannot connect to the other same-degree vertex, so must connect to remaining 34 vertices, which is possible if those other vertices provide enough degree slots). So we can set n_34 =2.\n\nSimilarly d=33, n_33 ≤ 3. Could we set n_33 =3? They cannot connect among themselves, so each needs 33 neighbors among the 36-3=33 other vertices, which is exactly all others. That's possible if all those others are adjacent to each of them (including the high-degree vertices). Seems feasible.\n\nIn general, we could set n_d = 36 - d for d from some threshold downwards until we fill 36 vertices. But cumulative count will quickly exceed 36.\n\nLet's compute cumulative counts if we start from top: define for k from 35 downwards, sum of (36 - d). Let's compute.\n\nI'll calculate cumulative:\n\nd=35 => 1 (total vertices =1)\nadd d=34 => +2 => total 3\nd=33 => +3 => total 6\nd=32 => +4 => total 10\nd=31 => +5 => total 15\nd=30 => +6 => total 21\nd=29 => +7 => total 28\nd=28 => +8 => total 36 exactly! So after d=28, we have exactly 36 vertices.\n\nThus we can set n_{35}=1, n_{34}=2, n_{33}=3, n_{32}=4, n_{31}=5, n_{30}=6, n_{29}=7, n_{28}=8. That sums to 36.\n\nAll lower degree values (0-27) get 0 vertices.\n\nNow compute total sum of degrees: Σ d * n_d for those d.\n\nCompute:\n\n35*1 =35\n34*2 =68\n33*3 =99\n32*4 =128\n31*5 =155\n30*6 =180\n29*7 =203\n28*8 =224\n\nNow sum: 35+68=103\n+99=202\n+128=330\n+155=485\n+180=665\n+203=868\n+224=1092\n\nSo total degree sum = 1092.\n\nEdges = 1092/2 = 546.\n\nThus we have at least 546 handshakes possible, if such degree distribution is realizable.\n\nNow need to verify feasibility: There is known theorem (the Gale-Shapley? Actually the degree sequence with constraints n_d ≤ 36-d is sufficient? Might be realizable via construction using complement? Let's attempt construct explicitly.\n\nWe can construct using complement graph: Complement's degree of vertex v is 35 - deg(v). In complement, vertices with same original degree have same complement degree. The condition that original graph has no edges between equal-degree vertices translates to complement graph having a (complete) subgraph (clique) among same-degree vertices? Because if two vertices are not adjacent in original, they may be adjacent in complement. Since original forbids adjacency among equal-degree vertices, complement forces them to be adjacent (since they cannot be adjacent; but they could be nonadjacent in complement as well). Actually if two vertices have same degree, original graph cannot have edge between them. So complement graph can either have edge or not; it's free. So not helpful.\n\nWe need to construct graph with those groups sizes.\n\nWe can think to make a \"complete multipartite graph\" where partition classes are the groups of equal degree, with sizes as above. In such a graph, any vertex is adjacent to all vertices not in its own class. Then a vertex in class of size s (degree d) will have degree = total vertices - s = 36 - s. So degree d = 36 - s. Thus class size s = 36 - d. Indeed matches our bound equality. So the construction of a complete multipartite graph where each class corresponds to a degree achieves the maximum possible degree for each class (since each vertex connects to all vertices outside its class). This satisfies condition because vertices within same class are not adjacent (independent). Therefore the degree of each vertex in class of size s is exactly 36 - s = d, consistent.\n\nThus we can set class sizes according to n_d = 36 - d. That's exactly a complete multipartite graph where each degree class is a part. Since we set those classes for d = 28..35, we have part sizes: for d=35, size 1; d=34 size2; d=33 size3; ...; d=28 size8. Indeed each part size = 36 - d.\n\nThus the graph is complete multipartite with eight parts of sizes 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8 (total 36). In such graph each vertex in part of size s has degree 36 - s = d as defined.\n\nTherefore it's realizable. Edge count of complete multipartite graph = total possible edges - sum of edges inside each part (which are absent). Total possible edges in complete graph of 36 vertices: C(36,2) = 630. Subtract edges missing within each part: for each part of size s, missing edges = C(s,2). So edges = 630 - Σ C(s,2) for s =1..8.\n\nCompute Σ C(s,2) for s=1..8:\n\nC(1,2)=0\nC(2,2)=1\nC(3,2)=3\nC(4,2)=6\nC(5,2)=10\nC(6,2)=15\nC(7,2)=21\nC(8,2)=28\n\nSum = 0+1+3+6+10+15+21+28 = 84.\n\nThus edges = 630 - 84 = 546. matches earlier.\n\nThus maximum possible number of handshakes is at most 546? Need to prove that cannot exceed 546.\n\nWe need upper bound proof: Using earlier inequality n_d ≤ 36 - d, sum of degrees = Σ d n_d = Σ (36 - s) s where s is part size? But to produce upper bound, we can use that each vertex of degree d cannot be adjacent to its own n_d -1 vertices of same degree, so d ≤ 36 - n_d. Thus n_d ≤ 36 - d. Then sum of degrees ≤ Σ d (36 - d) over chosen d? Wait we need to choose distribution to maximize sum under constraints n_d ≤ 36 - d and Σ n_d =36. This is an integer linear programming; we can bound total degree sum:\n\nTotal degree sum = Σ d n_d ≤ Σ_{d} d (36 - d) where we allocate n_d up to its maximum. Since we have total 36 vertices, the maximum sum occurs when we fill vertices with the highest possible degrees using their maximum allowed multiplicities until we reach 36 vertices, which is exactly the construction above.\n\nFormal proof: Arrange degrees descending d1≥...≥d36. For i-th vertex (ordered), we have degree di ≤ 36 - i + 1? Actually because among first i vertices there may be repeats? Another common bound: Since vertices with same degree form independent set, each vertex must miss at least (size of its own degree class -1) neighbors. So degree ≤ 36 - size_of_class. But size_of_class ≥ count of vertices with degree at least that value? Might be complex. But we can present argument: Let a_k be number of vertices with degree at least k. Because if a vertex has degree d, then there are at most 36 - d vertices not adjacent to it (including its own class). So the set of non-neighbors size at least n_{d} (same degree group) plus maybe others. This leads to inequality a_k ≤ 36 - k for each k? Let's test: a_k = number of vertices with degree ≥ k. For any vertex with degree d ≥ k, its non-neighbors include at least the other vertices with same degree (d - maybe?) Not directly.\n\nAlternative approach: Complement graph and apply Turán theorem. Since vertices of same degree form independent set, consider partition according to degree. The graph is complete multipartite between parts (maybe some missing edges). To maximize edges, we want all possible edges between parts present; any missing edge between different degree groups reduces total edges without violating condition. So the optimal graph should be complete multipartite with parts being degree equivalence classes.\n\nThus to maximize edges given the independent condition, we should add all possible edges between different degree classes.\n\nThus we have graph being complete multipartite with part sizes s_i. Then each vertex in part i has degree 36 - s_i. Thus the degree value for that part is 36 - s_i, which must be distinct across parts (since parts have different sizes). Different sizes produce different degrees; if two parts had same size they'd have same degree and then vertices between parts would be adjacent (which is allowed as they have same degree? Wait they would have same degree thus should be nonadjacent, but they would be adjacent, violating condition. So parts must have distinct sizes, otherwise they'd have same degree and inter-part edges would violate condition. But we can avoid edges between same-degree parts? Actually in multipartite, all inter-part edges are present. If two parts have same degree, they'd be adjacent across parts (since complete multipartite) which violates condition. Therefore sizes must be distinct (ensuring degrees distinct). Our construction uses distinct sizes 1-8.\n\nThus the problem reduces to partition 36 into distinct positive integers (part sizes) s1<...<sk (since degree descending correspond to smaller sizes for higher degree). The degree of part size s is 36 - s. Total edges = C(36,2) - Σ C(s_i,2). To maximize edges, we need minimize Σ C(s_i,2). For given total sum of sizes =36, sum of squares is minimized when the sizes are as unequal as possible? Actually C(s,2) = s(s-1)/2, convex in s. For fixed sum, minimizing sum of squares (or C(s,2)) occurs by making sizes as equal as possible (by convexity). But we also have distinctness constraint, making them as close as possible, which would increase the sum, not good. Wait we want to minimize total missing edges (within parts). Since C(s,2) convex, to minimize sum of C(s,2) given sum s_i=36 and distinct positive integers, we want sizes as \"balanced\" (near equal). However we also have constraint that sizes correspond to degree values at most 35, meaning sizes ≤36 - min degree. But we also need each part size s_i = 36 - d_i, where d_i are distinct degrees allowed 0..35. Since we want many high degrees, we want small part sizes (since degree = 36 - size). Thus to maximize edges we want many small parts (small size => high degree), but must sum to 36.\n\nThus minimizing within-part edges leads to making sizes as small as possible while distinct, i.e., 1,2,3,... as many as needed until sum reaches 36. Indeed the minimal sum of C(s,2) occurs when we take the smallest distinct sizes, because C(s,2) grows quickly with s. So to maximize edges, we want to use the smallest possible distinct part sizes.\n\nThus choose smallest distinct positive integers that sum to 36. The minimal sum of first m integers = m(m+1)/2. Find smallest m such that this sum >=36. Compute m=8 gives 36 exactly (since 8*9/2=36). So the optimal is exactly parts sizes 1..8. That matches our construction.\n\nThus any other distribution of distinct part sizes would have larger total of internal missing edges, resulting in fewer edges.\n\nHence maximum edges = total possible edges minus internal missing edges for sizes 1..8 = 630 - (1+3+6+10+15+21+28) equals 630-84=546.\n\nThus proof.\n\nNow must ensure that degree classes with same size cannot exist. Since we used distinct sizes, degrees distinct. Condition satisfied.\n\nHence answer 546.\n\nProvide answer format: \"ANSWER: 546\".", "response": "ANSWER: 546", "answer": 546, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.4318426994796027e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999756818686958, "logp_correct": -2.4318426994796027e-05, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6457352326227477, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2018939377607012, "frac_below_-5": 0.01020408163265306, "digit_lp_min": -4.758639812469482, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 4998, "lp_end": -6.079655122448457e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -0.003196133067831397, "logp_nocot": -8.357644010800868, "p_nocot": 0.0002345963897552488, "uplift": 8.357619692373873}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "c3d5b6b5-eac9-4a7a-8908-e924d74d46b1", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Let A be a $2n\\times2n$ matrix, with entries chosen independently at random. Every entry is chosen to be 0 or 1, each with probability 1/2. Find the expected value of $\\det(A-A^{t})$ (as a function of n), where $A^{t}$ is the transpose of $A.$ The original answer is in \\frac{(an)!}{b^{n}n!} format, please give the value of a+b.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Let A be a $2n\\times2n$ matrix, with entries chosen independently at random. Every entry is chosen to be 0 or 1, each with probability 1/2. Find the expected value of $\\det(A-A^{t})$ (as a function of n), where $A^{t}$ is the transpose of $A.$ The original answer is in \\frac{(an)!}{b^{n}n!} format, please give the value of a+b.", "true_answer": 6, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "c3d5b6b5-eac9-4a7a-8908-e924d74d46b1:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 5930, 1432, 328, 174922, 328, 448, 108342, 49964, 12248, 418, 314, 355, 533, 355, 61, 51, 11, 1919, 355, 382, 7526, 220, 15, 12, 16, 13313, 18614, 2454, 483, 34933, 220, 16, 14, 17, 13, 12225, 418, 382, 88378, 2011, 49964, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 418, 61, 51, 314, 533, 33, 11, 483, 12421, 71638, 350, 21910, 261, 62, 3573, 533, 261, 62, 3573, 314, 220, 15, 741, 5011, 103062, 38072, 7251, 418, 22305, 681, 92, 314, 261, 22305, 681, 92, 533, 261, 22305, 4133, 4180, 1118, 6948, 4824, 25, 220, 16, 12, 15, 314, 220, 16, 11, 220, 15, 12, 16, 314, 533, 16, 11, 220, 15, 12, 15, 314, 220, 15, 11, 220, 16, 12, 16, 314, 220, 15, 13, 2632, 12545, 25, 398, 8409, 22305, 681, 102838, 16, 8, 314, 398, 6271, 22305, 681, 102838, 16, 11, 261, 22305, 4133, 102838, 15, 8, 314, 220, 16, 14, 19, 13, 65037, 398, 8409, 22305, 681, 92, 21714, 16, 25