540, 4010, 220, 7959, 16, 13, 183159, 581, 1682, 316, 4736, 1577, 40335, 540, 4010, 220, 7959, 17, 350, 57001, 28063, 8, 1118, 382, 8975, 350, 61115, 220, 16, 741, 7801, 581, 4901, 483, 8302, 328, 31615, 220, 7959, 17, 11, 220, 7959, 19, 11, 220, 7959, 21, 35229, 2632, 52008, 553, 220, 17, 13, 2632, 7060, 23121, 364, 84787, 363, 314, 220, 36919, 382, 138276, 326, 65414, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 36919, 364], "n_tokens": 4904, "words": 3336, "band_lo": 1534.25, "band_hi": 6137.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to find the largest C such that any ±1 sequence length n=2022 has a subsequence of indices spaced at most distance 2 apart (i.e., we can pick indices with gaps 1 or 2) such that absolute sum of selected entries is at least C. We must guarantee existence of such a \"dense\" subsequence (no gaps >2) achieving large absolute sum.\n\nWe can select any number of indices k (>=1) with condition consecutive selected positions differ by at most 2 (i.e., we can skip at most one element between successive selected indices). Equivalent to we can choose a subset without two consecutive unchosen positions.\n\nThus indices selected form a \"no two consecutive gaps\" condition: we can't have a run of at least two consecutive unselected positions. So the complement (unselected positions) cannot contain two consecutive indices. So unselected positions are isolated.\n\nThus we can select almost all indices; at most floor((n+1)/2) unselected? Wait: if unselected cannot be adjacent, max number of unselected is ceil(n/2) when they alternate. But we may want to maximize absolute sum of selected values, maybe we want to select mostly those with same sign.\n\nGoal: guarantee we can achieve sum at least C regardless of arrangement. This is like we can delete a set of positions such that no two deletions are adjacent, and sum of remaining numbers' absolute value is at least C. Equivalent to we may choose to discard some a_i (unselected) under the constraint no two discarded are consecutive. Want to guarantee that after discarding, the remaining sum magnitude is at least C.\n\nWe can think in terms of we can flip sign? Hmm.\n\nLet total sum S = sum a_i (range -2022 to 2022 with same parity as n). If we could select all indices, we get |S| perhaps small. We can also drop some indices to increase absolute sum, because dropping a negative element when S positive can increase sum magnitude? Actually if we aim for large magnitude, we might drop elements that reduce magnitude.\n\nWe can think of we can choose a subset to maximize absolute sum under constraint no two unchosen adjacent. Equivalent to choose to delete a set D with no two consecutive indices; resulting sum = S - 2*sum_{i in D} a_i (since removing a_i subtracts its contribution). Actually total sum of selected = sum_{i not in D} a_i = S - sum_{i in D} a_i. Wait original S = sum_{all}. Removing a_i reduces sum by a_i (not double). So selected sum = S - sum_{i in D} a_i. Since a_i is +/-1, removing a positive reduces sum positive, removing a negative (a_i=-1) increases sum (makes it more positive). So we can control sign.\n\nGoal: guarantee we can make selected sum's absolute value at least C by appropriate choice of D (subject to no adjacency). Equivalent to we can add or subtract up to certain amount.\n\nConsider we want to maximize |S - sum_D a_i|. Since we can choose D, we can try to push the sum towards either +something or -something.\n\nLet define x = sum_D a_i, where D is a subset with no consecutive indices. Then selected sum = S - x. We can choose D to maximize |S - x|. Since a_i = ±1, x can take integer values between -|D| and +|D| depending on composition.\n\nGoal: for worst-case original sequence, the maximal achievable |S - x| (over allowed D) is at least C. So we need lower bound over sequences of the maximum over D (subject to no adjacency) of |S - sum_D a_i|.\n\nWe need largest guaranteed minimal value.\n\nThink in terms of we can \"flip\" signs of selected indices? Another approach: Consider constructing a subsequence using a greedy algorithm: walk through indices, decide to take each element unless we skip two in a row. Equivalent to we may create a subsequence that avoids two consecutive gaps: this is like a path in a graph where we can move step 1 or 2.\n\nAlternative viewpoint: The subsequence corresponds to a walk selecting positions such that each selected index is at most 2 apart. That means we can think of partitioning the index set into blocks of size up to 2 where we choose at least one from each block. Equivalent to you cannot have a block of two consecutive indices both omitted.\n\nThus we can think of we assign each index a weight a_i; we wish to choose from each pair of consecutive positions at least one (i.e., a vertex cover of a path?). Actually the condition that we cannot have two consecutive unchosen is exactly a vertex cover of the complement? If we consider choosing positions which are \"selected\", then \"unselected\" is an independent set with no adjacent vertices. So selected set is a vertex cover (covers all edges). Minimum vertex cover size for path length n is ceil(n/2). Indeed selected size at least ceil(n/2). So we must choose at least ceil(n/2) indices.\n\nThus we must pick at least half the elements (rounded up). Among those, we want sum large. So worst case is when we are forced to pick many opposite signs canceling.\n\nThus we need guarantee on sum of at least m = ceil(n/2)=1011 elements (since n=2022 even, half =1011). Actually for even n, min selected = n/2 = 1011? n=2022, n/2=1011. So we must select at least 1011 elements.\n\nHence worst-case scenario: adversary chooses signs, we can select any subset of size at least 1011 (since you might be forced exactly 1011? You could select more). We want to maximize absolute sum. Given we can select any subset of size at least 1011 with the constraint of no two unselected adjacent (i.e., selected set is a vertex cover), what's the smallest possible attainable max absolute sum?\n\nWe need to find worst-case sequence where any vertex cover sum has limited magnitude.\n\nMaybe symmetric sequence of alternating signs will minimize ability. Consider pattern + - + - ... . If we need to select at least half elements, we could select all +'s? But we can't select all +'s because they are every other index, and we would be leaving the - elements unselected, which are not adjacent (since - are also alternated, no two - are adjacent). That's allowed because unselected set are the - entries; they are not adjacent. So we can choose all +'s (1011 of them) and achieve sum = 1011. So C would be at least 1011 for this pattern.\n\nThus worst case must be more complex.\n\nWe need to find sequence where any vertex cover sum is bounded. This is reminiscent of discrepancy theory, like balanced sequence where any large subset has small sum.\n\nBut with constraint that unselected set is independent (no adjacent). Equivalent to we can delete an independent set of vertices (size at most ceiling(n/2)). The selected sum = total sum - sum over deleted set.\n\nWe can think of we can delete any independent set; we want to make the selected sum as far from zero as possible. So we can delete some +1's to reduce sum etc.\n\nWorst case may be when total sum is zero (balanced) and independent set can delete up to half of each sign, making selected sum small.\n\nConsider sequence of blocks of three maybe? Suppose we have pattern + + - - + + - - ... . Since we cannot delete two consecutive positions, we cannot delete both + in a ++ block because they are adjacent. So at most one of the two consecutive +'s can be omitted. Similarly for -- block. Thus in each block you must keep at least one of each sign? Let's analyze.\n\nSequence of length 2022; we can view as groups of 2 maybe? Since we cannot delete two consecutive, the maximum number we can delete is ceil(n/2) =1011, i.e., we could delete exactly half the indices, but they must be independent (no adjacency), which is achieved by deleting all odd positions, for example. So we can delete any set of positions with at most one per adjacent pair.\n\nThus worst-case for us is when the signs are arranged such that after deleting any independent set, the remaining sum still small in magnitude.\n\nGiven we can decide independent set after seeing sequence, we would likely delete all positions where a_i has sign opposite to the majority we aim for. So if sequence has many + and -. But we are limited to at most one per adjacent pair, so we might not be able to delete all negatives if they appear consecutive.\n\nThus worst-case arrangement will place negatives in long runs, making it impossible to delete many of them (since you can delete at most half of a run). So the adversary could cluster same sign together.\n\nHence we need to find minimal maximal absolute sum given that we can delete independent set up to size floor(n/2). Equivalent to we can keep at least floor(n/2) from each run? Not exactly.\n\nLet's formalize: Let we pick independent set D (deleted). For each maximal run of consecutive equal signs, of length L, the number we can delete from that run is at most ceil(L/2)?? Actually independent set cannot contain adjacent vertices, so within a run of length L, we can delete at most ceil(L/2) elements (choose every other). So we must keep at least floor(L/2) from that run.\n\nGoal: after deletions, the sum of remaining signs may be small. If run has sign +, keeping some +'s adds positive; if run is -, keeping some negatives contributes negative.\n\nThus worst-case for us is to have long runs of opposite signs, making net sum after deletions possibly small.\n\nWe can think of the problem as: given sequence of ±1, we can choose for each run of sign s length L, to keep either ceil(L/2) elements of that sign, or possibly more (since we can also keep the ones we didn't delete). But deletions limited by independent set overall, not per run. However we can choose deletions to maximize sum magnitude: if overall we want positive sum, we would try to delete as many - as allowed (i.e., about half of each - run). But we might also delete some + inadvertently due to adjacency constraints.\n\nThus worst-case sum magnitude is when after optimal deletions, we still have near balance.\n\nCompute bound: Let total number of + be p, - be q = n - p. Original sum S = p - q = 2p - n. After deletions D, selected sum = S - sum_{i in D} a_i = S - ( #(deleted +) - #(deleted -) ). Because a_i = +1 for +, -1 for -. So sum over D a_i = (#deleted +) - (#deleted -). Thus selected sum = (p - q) - (d_plus - d_minus) = (p - d_plus) - (q - d_minus). That's just remaining plus count minus remaining minus count.\n\nWe can choose deletions to increase the difference, i.e., delete more of the minority sign. Let target to maximize absolute value, we will try to delete as many of sign opposite to majority as possible, within independent constraint.\n\nLet majority sign be + if p >= q else -.\n\nWe can delete at most floor(p?) but limited by independent set size.\n\nWe need to find guarantee that we can make remaining difference at least C.\n\nSuppose the sequence arranged so that runs alternate sign, each run length maybe 2 (to maximize deletability). If we have alternating single signs (+ - + - ...), then we can delete all - (odd positions) because they are not adjacent. That would leave all +, sum = 1011 if n even. That's large.\n\nThus worst sequence likely has long runs of same sign, e.g., all + then all -. Then you cannot delete many - because they are clustered but you could delete every other - within the run. Suppose we have p = q = 1011 each. Sequence: first 1011 +'s, then 1011 -'s.\n\nWe can delete at most ceil(1011/2)=506 negatives (since we can delete every other). Additionally, we might also delete some +'s perhaps from first block as needed (but better to delete positives? we want to increase sum magnitude maybe we want to keep many +'s and delete many -'s). So we can delete up to 506 - from second block. Also we can delete up to 506 + from first block if needed (but we don't need). However total deletions limited to 1011 (max independent set). We could delete exactly 506 negatives and also maybe some positives (if we need more deletions to achieve independent size bound? Actually we are allowed to delete up to 1011, not required to max. We can just delete 506 negatives, leaving 1011-506=505 +'s and 1011-506=505 -'s? Wait we kept all +'s (since we didn't delete them), so plus count =1011, minus count =1011-506=505. Selected sum = 1011 - 505 = 506. That's C? Possibly minimal.\n\nCheck if we could do better by also deleting some +'s to perhaps increase sum? Deleting +'s would reduce sum, not increase positive magnitude, so not needed. So maximal achievable sum magnitude for this sequence is 506 (positive). Could we obtain larger magnitude by aiming negative? If we delete many +'s instead, we could get negative sum: we could delete 506 +'s from first block, leaving 505 +'s and 1011 -'s -> sum = 505 - 1011 = -506 magnitude same.\n\nThus for this sequence, maximal achievable |sum| = 506.\n\nThus C cannot exceed 506.\n\nNow question: is there any sequence where max achievable |sum| is less than 506? Could be lower; need to evaluate worst-case optimum.\n\nGeneral scenario: Suppose sequence has runs of lengths L1, L2, ..., Lm with alternating signs. For each run j with sign s_j (±), we can delete at most ceil(Lj/2) elements of that run (nonadjacent). We would delete from runs of the minority sign as much as possible to tilt sum.\n\nLet total plus count P, minus Q. Suppose P≥Q. Then we need to delete up to D_minus = min(ceil of each minus run length?) Actually we can delete at most ceil(Lj/2) from each minus run. So total deletable minus count D_minus = sum over minus runs ceil(L_run/2). After deletions, remaining minus count = Q - d_minus, where d_minus <= D_minus.\n\nWe can also delete some pluses if we need more deletions but that reduces sum.\n\nOur aim to maximize |sum| = (P - d_plus) - (Q - d_minus) = (P - Q) + (d_minus - d_plus). Since P - Q = S initial. We can increase magnitude by making d_minus - d_plus as large as possible (positive direction) up to D_minus (if we delete only minus). So best magnitude = |S| + D_minus (if S positive). However there is a cap that we cannot delete more than total independent set size? But independent set total deletions D = d_plus + d_minus cannot exceed ceil(n/2)=1011. Typically D_minus may be ≤1011 already. But could be bigger? sum of ceil(L_j/2) over all minus runs ≤ number of minus positions perhaps? Actually each minus run length Lj, ceil(Lj/2) ≤ Lj. So sum ≤ Q. Since Q ≤ n. Generally D_minus ≤ Q.\n\nThus best magnitude = S + D_minus (assuming S ≥0). Since S may be negative then we aim reverse sign.\n\nThus worst-case minimal guaranteed magnitude is min over sequences of max(|S| + D_minus, |S| + D_plus) where D_minus = max deletable minority count? Actually we assume we know which sign is majority after seeing sequence; we can aim to maximise magnitude either positive or negative whichever yields larger absolute.\n\nThus the optimal magnitude for given sequence is max( |S| + D_minus, |S| + D_plus )? Wait D_plus is deletable plus count (if we want negative sum). Since we can choose whichever direction yields larger magnitude. So the achievable max magnitude = max( |S| + D_minor ), where D_minor is total deletable count of the minority sign.\n\nSince deletions from minority increase magnitude, deletions from majority decrease magnitude.\n\nThus worst-case sequence will minimize |S| + D_minor.\n\nGiven we can choose direction, the minority sign is the one with smaller count: let m = min(P, Q). Then D_minor = sum_{runs of minority} ceil(L/2). Since minority signs are spaced possibly in runs.\n\nGoal minimize |S| + D_minor = (P - Q positive? Actually |S| = |P - Q| = (max - min) = M - m, where M = max(P,Q). So |S| = M - m.\n\nThus value = (M - m) + D_minor.\n\nSince M + m = n, we have M = n - m. So expression = (n - m - m) + D_minor = n - 2m + D_minor.\n\nThus we need to minimize n - 2m + D_minor over sequences (where m is minority count, D_minor depends on arrangement of minority elements). Since n fixed, minimizing expression means maximize 2m - D_minor.\n\nGiven we can arrange minority elements to reduce D_minor (i.e., making them clustered so you cannot delete many). For a given count m, the maximum deletable minority elements is achieved when they are isolated (alternating) yielding D_minor = m (since each minus isolated run length 1 has ceil(1/2)=1). Minimum D_minor occurs when they are in one consecutive block of length m, giving D_minor = ceil(m/2). Because single run.\n\nThus D_minor is at least ceil(m/2). So expression at worst:\n\nvalue >= n - 2m + ceil(m/2).\n\nWe want the worst-case sequence to make this as small as possible, so we should choose m to minimize that expression. Since we can also adjust distribution.\n\nLet f(m) = n - 2m + ceil(m/2). For n=2022.\n\nCompute for m from 0 to floor(n/2)=1011.\n\nIf m=0 (all same sign), then expression = n - 0 + 0 = 2022. That's huge, not worst.\n\nWe want small.\n\nCompute f(m) approximated as n - 2m + (m+1)/2 floor? Actually ceil(m/2) = (m+1)//2.\n\nSo f(m) = n - 2m + ceil(m/2) = n - (2m - ceil(m/2)) = n - ( (3/2)m +/-). Let's compute exact.\n\nIf m even: m=2t, ceil(m/2)=t. Then f = n - 4t + t = n -3t. So f = n - 3t where t=m/2.\n\nIf m odd: m=2t+1, ceil=m/2 rounded up = t+1. Then f = n - 2(2t+1) + (t+1) = n -4t -2 + t +1 = n -3t -1.\n\nThus for m even f = n -3(m/2) = n - (3m)/2.\nFor m odd f = n -3t -1 = n -3((m-1)/2) -1 = n - (3m -3)/2 -1 = n - (3m)/2 + (3/2) -1 = n - (3m)/2 + 0.5.\n\nBut f must be integer.\n\nNow we need to find m (<=1011) that minimizes f, i.e., maximize term (3m)/2 (or approx 1.5m). So choose m as large as possible, i.e., m=1011 (since minority cannot exceed half). For n even and equal split, m=1011.\n\nCheck parity: 1011 is odd. So m odd case: m=2t+1 => t=505. Then f = n -3t -1 = 2022 - 3*505 -1 = 2022 -1515 -1 = 506.\n\nMatches earlier calculation.\n\nThus minimal guaranteed maximum magnitude is at least 506. And we exhibited a sequence achieving exactly 506 (all + then all -). Therefore C = 506.\n\nNeed to verify that any sequence yields at least 506 (our lower bound). Our derived formula shows minimal value = n - 2m + ceil(m/2). Since m ≤ floor(n/2). The minimal value occurs at m = floor(n/2) when n even, giving f = n - 2*(n/2) + ceil((n/2)/2) = n - n + ceil(n/4) = ceil(n/4). Wait compute: For n even, n=2k. Then m can be at most k. Plug in m=k: if k even? Suppose n=2022, k=1011 (odd). f = n - 2k + ceil(k/2) = 0 + ceil(k/2) = ceil(1011/2) = 506. Yes matches ceil(n/4?) Since n/4 = 505.5, ceil = 506. Indeed formula becomes ceil(n/4) when n divisible? Let's general. For even n, with m = n/2, f = n - n + ceil(n/4) = ceil(n/4). So C = ceil(n/4). That seems plausible.\n\nThus answer C = 506.\n\nCheck that conditions of subsequence: indices spacing at most 2; we argued corresponds to deleting independent set. The selected set must be vertex cover, thus size at least n/2. Our construction of all + then all - gave min max |sum| = 506 and meets condition? Let's ensure we can achieve sum 506 with allowed subsequence. Choose subsequence consisting of all + and half of the - (every other). The gaps between selected indices? Starting with plus block of length 1011. If we select all pluses, then at transition to minus block we need to select some minus indices such that gaps ≤2. Since we have at most one unselected between selected indices. Within minus block, we select every other element, e.g., first minus selected, skip one, select next,... That ensures gaps of 2 between selected (selected indices difference 2). At the boundary between blocks, there is no issue because last selected plus index is at position 1011. Suppose we want to select first minus at position 1012 (adjacent) which is fine (gap 1). Then we continue with pattern of selecting 1012, 1014, 1016,... So gaps are 2. So condition satisfied.\n\nThus C = 506 is attainable and maximal.\n\nThus answer: 506.", "response": "ANSWER: 506", "answer": 506, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.1457659613588476e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999978542363408, "logp_correct": -2.1457659613588476e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6811554880544558, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2640401332085602, "frac_below_-5": 0.014314928425357873, "digit_lp_min": -2.2933707237243652, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0033521433360874653, "n_cot_tokens": 4890, "lp_end": -0.4837140440940857, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.311301275563892e-06, "logp_nocot": -16.398635163903236, "p_nocot": 7.553760963184548e-08, "uplift": 16.398633018137275}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "cdf67949-be0c-4e24-bd77-ac61d2755f19", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Vasya has $n$ candies of several types, where $n > 145$. It is known that for any group of at least 145 candies, there is a type of candy which appears exactly 10 times. Find the largest possible value of $n$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Vasya has $n$ candies of several types, where $n > 145$. It is known that for any group of at least 145 candies, there is a type of candy which appears exactly 10 times. Find the largest possible value of $n$.", "true_answer": 160, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "cdf67949-be0c-4e24-bd77-ac61d2755f19:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 4218, 4792, 25, 32711, 261, 5801, 328, 151267, 350, 34841, 39097, 8, 328, 4919, 6009, 13, 30255, 25, 2214, 1062, 58845, 350, 4323, 8, 328, 540, 5153, 220, 16620, 151267, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 1062, 1543, 34841, 39097, 328, 2349, 99731, 16620, 95807, 1354, 13031, 261, 1490, 484, 14518, 9707, 220, 702, 4238, 3518, 484, 3566, 13, 125152, 25, 2214, 1753, 58845, 336, 328, 290, 6062, 920, 483, 1022, 50, 91, 99731, 16620, 11, 1354, 13031, 261, 42573, 1490, 260, 2238, 484, 306, 336, 11, 290, 3605, 328, 1490, 260, 382, 9707, 220, 702, 13, 19792, 65414, 297, 350, 9577, 151267, 8, 395, 1118, 2238, 261, 5801, 665, 4310, 11, 4335, 297, 29, 16620, 364, 175703, 387, 6771, 316, 25307, 68745, 2364, 14, 8771, 280, 920, 17346, 13, 623, 7060, 382, 5532, 13, 1416, 1309, 316, 1646, 2999, 2349, 328, 261, 3469, 39097, 1919, 1753, 4410, 4951, 58845, 8895, 261, 1490, 51667, 9707, 220, 702, 4238, 364, 2167, 665, 2411, 328, 2454, 1490, 885, 3609, 3605, 5251, 6062, 5801, 25, 2891, 581, 679, 284, 6009, 11, 483, 28991, 261, 10853, 350, 87319, 15, 54912, 741, 2214, 1062, 58845, 328, 15