66, 42002, 8213, 13, 2632, 581, 679, 448, 64353, 289, 3502, 328, 2349, 220, 45764, 21, 402, 220, 1265, 8632, 52718, 7060, 13, 2632, 581, 665, 4736, 1062, 220, 3234, 15, 328, 1373, 11, 2928, 64353, 289, 326, 8213, 42002, 350, 21910, 23201, 1340, 2928, 3357, 97407, 1811, 741, 20953, 6757, 1934, 23201, 2928, 8213, 42002, 26, 538, 581, 6234, 722, 165377, 12486, 261, 5024, 3577, 11, 484, 3577, 1481, 5025, 29983, 13, 3072, 581, 665, 5655, 261, 8441, 484, 2928, 2030, 1348, 2454, 3577, 540, 5153, 4730, 13, 12265, 581, 679, 1991, 11982, 11, 581, 665, 5230, 220, 3234, 15, 484, 2928, 3931, 11, 395, 2454, 3577, 11, 540, 5153, 1001, 920, 12486, 480, 13, 4569, 382, 153639, 364, 84787, 19957, 22208, 2349, 382, 220, 1265, 364, 23483, 316, 7620, 6931, 11608, 25, 1258, 737, 484, 284, 28, 1311, 67567, 13, 1877, 49699, 11, 2999, 64353, 289, 3502, 2349, 483, 220, 1311, 8632, 382, 540, 1645, 220, 17, 42923, 899, 102838, 24928, 21, 464, 3234, 15, 11, 813, 19786, 13, 41351, 11, 290, 13686, 1534, 25, 2214, 1062, 64353, 289, 3502, 328, 165377, 328, 723, 76, 2155, 2349, 72554, 220, 17, 42923, 76, 12, 16, 16255, 65675, 25, 135774, 4014, 24702, 2454, 920, 483, 1617, 21800, 26, 64353, 289, 7060, 109718, 3375, 2973, 261, 920, 326, 1617, 21800, 2447, 306, 3502, 13, 2632, 540, 1645, 1001, 777, 10610, 11, 3609, 72554, 220, 17, 42923, 76, 12, 16, 16255, 19233, 483, 284, 28, 1311, 11, 2999, 382, 220, 24928, 21, 464, 3234, 15, 13, 47682, 284, 87319, 1265, 364, 84787, 19957, 4149, 2086, 328, 8632, 306, 22208, 382, 220, 1265, 364, 17045, 25, 461, 160761, 25, 220, 1265, 6120], "n_tokens": 2650, "words": 1750, "band_lo": 1550.25, "band_hi": 6201.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to find minimal size of union of 5000 distinct finite sets, pairwise intersections nonempty, global intersection empty.\n\nWe need to construct sets to minimize total distinct elements used. Equivalent to find smallest possible universe size with 5000 subsets such that each pair intersect, but no element belongs to all sets.\n\nWe need to assign elements to subsets. Each element can be present in multiple sets. Pairwise intersection condition: for any two sets A_i, A_j, they must share at least one element. This is like a family of sets with property that the system is intersecting but not having a common element (i.e., not a star). Minimum size of union known as problem of intersecting families with no transversal of size 1 (i.e., hitting number). Known extremal problem: For n sets, minimal union size is something like ceiling(log2 n)+1? Not exactly.\n\nWe can think of representing each set as characteristic vector over elements. Pairwise intersection nonempty = for any i≠j, dot product ≥1. No universal element = no coordinate where all vectors have 1.\n\nGoal minimize number of coordinates (elements) such that we can have 5000 distinct 0/1 vectors with pairwise dot ≥1, and no coordinate all 1s.\n\nWe also need distinct sets; vectors distinct.\n\nThis is like size of smallest covering code with covering radius? Might relate to set system with intersection graph being complete.\n\nWe can think of using elements as \"labels\" that encode subsets via incidence. The condition pairwise intersections nonempty means that for any two vectors, they share a coordinate with both 1.\n\nThus the family of characteristic vectors forms a family of subsets of [m] where the family is an intersecting family without a common element, i.e., an intersecting family of subsets of an m-element set, no element belongs to all subsets.\n\nWe need smallest possible m such that there exists an intersecting family of size 5000 with no universal element.\n\nKnown result: For any intersecting family of subsets of [m] without a common element, its size ≤ 2^{m-1} - something? Actually classic Erdős–Ko–Rado theorem: For families of k-element subsets. Not directly.\n\nBut we can consider all subsets that contain element 1, that would guarantee common element. To avoid that, we need more varied.\n\nOne simple construction: Use m = ceil(log2 5000) + 1? Let's try binary representation.\n\nIdea: Let each set correspond to a distinct nonempty subset of a set of m elements, and also include a special element added to ensure pairwise intersections? Wait pairwise intersections must be nonempty; if we assign each set to be a distinct binary vector with at least one 1, but two sets might be disjoint (no common 1). To avoid that, we need that for any two subsets, they intersect. This is exactly a family of subsets that is intersecting.\n\nWhat is the minimal size of ground set needed for an intersecting family of size N? Max size of intersecting family on m elements is 2^{m-1} (all subsets containing a fixed element). That's maximum; but we need at least N, so m must satisfy 2^{m-1} ≥ N? No, because we are not forced to use maximum; we need to achieve N distinct intersecting subsets without common element. The maximum possible for given m without common element may be less than 2^{m-1}. Actually the maximum size of intersecting family without a common element is? Consider families of subsets that are intersecting and not all contain a fixed element. For m ≥ 2, the maximal size is 2^{m-1} - 1? Example: all subsets containing element 1, except the set {1} alone? That still all contain 1, so universal element present, violates condition. Need exclude any element that belongs to all sets. So we cannot have all subsets contain same element. The maximum size might be 2^{m-1}, achieved by taking all subsets of size ≥ m/2? Not sure.\n\nBut we can think to minimize m, we can construct families using \"star plus a missing element\"? Let's attempt constructing with m = k+1 where we have k elements labelled 1..k, plus an extra element x. Use sets of form {x} ∪ S where S is a nonempty subset of {1..k} maybe? Then any two sets intersect at least at x, so pairwise intersection nonempty, but x belongs to all sets – violates global empty intersection. So need not have universal element.\n\nThus we need more clever: Use each set to miss exactly one element among a collection, and also include a common core? But common core would be universal.\n\nIdea: Use elements correspond to pairs of sets? Might need at least ceiling(log2 5000) elements via using each element to separate complements.\n\nConsider representing each set by a binary string of length m such that any two strings have at least one position where both have 1. This is equivalent to set family being intersecting. Complement condition: No position where all strings have 1, i.e., for each position, there exists at least one string with 0.\n\nGoal: minimize m given we need at least 5000 distinct strings with pairwise 1-overlap.\n\nThis is similar to concept of \"clique cover number of complement of hypercube\"? Actually we need size of largest binary code with forbidden all-zero intersection.\n\nWe can think of using all non-zero vectors of length m, but many are disjoint (e.g., unit vectors). So cannot use all.\n\nWe need intersecting property => the family is an intersecting code. Known result: Maximum size of intersecting family of subsets of [m] equals 2^{m-1}. That's achieved by taking all subsets containing a fixed element. That's with universal element, which we cannot have.\n\nIf we forbid universal element, the maximum size is 2^{m-1} - something. Perhaps the maximum size without a universal element is 2^{m-1} - 1 (remove the set that is the universal element itself? Wait universal element is not a set but an element present in all sets. To avoid universal element, we must ensure for each element there is at least one set missing it. So we can still include many subsets containing element 1, as long as some set does not contain element 1. So we can take all subsets containing element 1, plus some additional subsets not containing 1 but still intersecting all others (must intersect elements with 1). Any set not containing 1 must intersect every set containing 1, meaning it must contain at least one other element that is present in each of those sets? But those other sets may vary—they all contain 1, but may differ on other elements. So a set without 1 could be, say, containing element 2, and intersect any set containing 1 because they also might contain 2? Not guaranteed. So to guarantee intersection, a set without 1 must contain all other elements? Not possible. So to have intersecting family without universal element, you cannot have a huge star.\n\nThus maybe the minimal union size grows like log2 N. Let's attempt constructive lower bound.\n\nConsider m elements. There are at most 2^m possible distinct subsets. So m must satisfy 2^m ≥ 5000 → m ≥ ceil(log2 5000) = ceil(12.2877) = 13. So at least 13 elements needed in union (since each element is a possible label). But also need intersecting property. With 13 elements maybe possible.\n\nWe need to find a family of 5000 intersecting subsets of a 13-element set with empty global intersection. Is that feasible? Let's attempt produce large intersecting family without universal element on 13 elements.\n\nMaximum intersecting families without universal element maybe close to 2^{12} = 4096? That's less than 5000. So need larger m.\n\nIf maximum size of intersecting family on m elements is 2^{m-1}, which for m=13 is 2^{12}=4096. That's the maximum possible, even allowing universal element. Since we cannot have universal, maximum may be slightly less, certainly ≤4096. Since we need 5000 >4096, m must be at least 14 (2^{13}=8192). At m=14, max intersecting family size is 2^{13}=8192. So 5000 possible. Need to ensure no universal element can still get 5000. Likely possible.\n\nThus minimal m is 14.\n\nNow need to ensure we can construct 5000 distinct intersecting subsets of a 14-element universe with no element in all sets.\n\nConstruction: Take all subsets that contain element 1, that gives 2^{13}=8192 subsets. Among those, every set contains element 1, so universal element present, not allowed. We need to modify.\n\nIdea: Take all subsets that contain element 1 except remove some sets to break universality, and add some subsets that do not contain 1 but still intersect all remaining sets.\n\nSimpler: Use all subsets of size at least 7 (i.e., majority). In a set of 14 elements, any two subsets of size ≥7 intersect (by pigeonhole). Indeed, two subsets each of size > 14/2 guarantee intersection. Since 7+7=14, they could be disjoint if both size 7 and complementary; but if both size ≥8 then intersect. For size 7, they could be complements thus disjoint. So to guarantee intersecting, we can take all subsets of size ≥8. Number of subsets size ≥8 = sum_{k=8}^{14} C(14,k). Compute.\n\nC(14,8)= C(14,6)=3003 (since 14 choose 6 = 3003). C(14,9)= C(14,5)=2002. C(14,10)=C(14,4)=1001. C(14,11)=C(14,3)=364. C(14,12)=C(14,2)=91. C(14,13)=14. C(14,14)=1. Sum =3003+2002=5005; +1001=6006; +364=6370; +91=6461; +14=6475; +1=6476. That's >5000. Also note any two subsets of size ≥8 intersect because complement size ≤6; two subsets each size≥8 have at most 6 elements missing each, union can't cover all 14 without overlap? Actually two subsets of size 8 could be disjoint? If one has 8 elements, its complement has 6, so second of size 8 cannot be entirely within complement (size 6). So they intersect. So family is intersecting. Does there exist a universal element? Intersection of all subsets of size ≥8 is empty because for each element, we can find a subset missing it (e.g., a subset consisting of the other 13 elements? That's size 13, which includes all but one element; that's in family). So each element is omitted in some subset (the subset of all elements except that one). Thus global intersection empty. So we have an intersecting family of size 6476 on 14 elements satisfying condition. So we can select any 5000 of them, still intersecting and empty intersection (since removal may still keep emptiness). Must ensure after removal still empty intersection; if we remove all subsets missing a particular element, that element would become universal. But we can choose a selection that still omits each element at least once. Since we have many sets, we can pick 5000 that still include, for each element, at least one set missing it. That is doable.\n\nThus minimal union size is 14.\n\nNeed to confirm lower bound: Prove that m=13 insufficient. As argued, max intersecting family size with 13 elements is at most 2^{12}=4096 <5000, so impossible. Indeed, the classic result: For any intersecting family of subsets of [m], size ≤ 2^{m-1}. Proof: Complement map pairs each set with its complement; intersecting condition forbids both a set and its complement being in family. So at most one per pair, total ≤ 2^{m-1}. Therefore with m=13, max is 4096 <5000. Hence m≥14.\n\nThus minimal possible number of elements in union is 14.\n\nAnswer: 'ANSWER: 14'.", "response": "ANSWER: 14", "answer": 14, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -5.602823307526705e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999943971923883, "logp_correct": -5.602823307526705e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5679508177680863, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1185485452102863, "frac_below_-5": 0.006069802731411229, "digit_lp_min": -1.910226583480835, "answer_first_mention_lp": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "n_cot_tokens": 2636, "lp_end": -1.4305104514278355e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -6.556489552167477e-06, "logp_nocot": -5.814531410112977, "p_nocot": 0.002983878215083744, "uplift": 5.8145258072896695}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "e37b245c-0faf-4bd5-86b4-d152e1226053", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Let $\\times$ represent the cross product in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ For what positive integers $n$ does there exist a set $S\\subset\\mathbb{R}^3$ with exactly $n$ elements such that\n$$S=\\{v\\times w:v,w\\in S\\}?$$ Please provide the sum of all possible values of $n$.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Let $\\times$ represent the cross product in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ For what positive integers $n$ does there exist a set $S\\subset\\mathbb{R}^3$ with exactly $n$ elements such that\n$$S=\\{v\\times w:v,w\\in S\\}?$$ Please provide the sum of all possible values of $n$.", "true_answer": 8, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "e37b245c-0faf-4bd5-86b4-d152e1226053:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 1646, 4149, 16174, 328, 261, 920, 336, 306, 460, 61, 18, 2238, 484, 336, 314, 354, 85, 33839, 286, 712, 323, 49598, 35353, 230, 336, 16255, 4569, 382, 11, 336, 382, 12412, 1641, 6167, 8088, 1888, 328, 1062, 1920, 8632, 11, 326, 97318, 1753, 3577, 328, 336, 665, 413, 22171, 472, 261, 8088, 1888, 328, 1920, 350, 2878, 23517, 16913, 16918, 8632, 328, 336, 13, 623, 920, 328, 722, 4149, 8088, 3891, 328, 24702, 591, 336, 29702, 9707, 336, 26, 575, 2560, 4213, 290, 920, 328, 722, 8088, 3891, 382, 9707, 336, 364, 2167, 1309, 8841, 16336, 297, 395, 1118, 2238, 336, 13031, 364, 2167, 1309, 4149, 102745, 1629, 364, 88748, 25, 8088, 1888, 328, 52254, 306, 460, 61, 18, 59684, 261, 9727, 35656, 127861, 316, 2973, 13, 1843, 581, 679, 1062, 1920, 52254, 11, 1043, 8088, 1888, 382, 35656, 127861, 316, 2454, 13, 2632, 336, 2804, 35441, 484, 395, 1062, 323, 49598, 306, 336, 11, 323, 12378, 86, 382, 306, 336, 11, 326, 9436, 1151, 2454, 3577, 328, 336, 79095, 472, 80