, 337, 314, 1161, 364, 2653, 337, 72554, 1161, 11, 12221, 25, 220, 1434, 533, 337, 99731, 30, 220, 1353, 533, 220, 18, 84, 30, 41021, 1746, 337, 28, 16, 25, 220, 3796, 10217, 220, 5170, 871, 1349, 382, 220, 3796, 13, 2632, 395, 3291, 337, 11, 220, 1434, 40100, 382, 13679, 30, 50557, 220, 3796, 464, 220, 5170, 11, 813, 1349, 314, 220, 3796, 13, 2632, 395, 337, 28, 16, 11, 355, 16, 382, 13679, 13, 3604, 337, 28, 1161, 25, 355, 16, 314, 220, 1161, 11, 355, 17, 314, 220, 1161, 12629, 13, 2214, 337, 1424, 1161, 11, 355, 17, 14081, 13679, 364, 84787, 1402, 2653, 220, 16, 72554, 337, 72554, 1161, 25, 631, 3228, 314, 220, 1434, 533, 337, 558, 2653, 220, 2109, 72554, 337, 72554, 2546, 25, 631, 3228, 314, 220, 1353, 533, 220, 18, 84, 364, 4383, 540, 337, 28, 2109, 25, 355, 16, 28, 1494, 11, 355, 17, 28, 1353, 12, 4388, 28, 1709, 871, 1349, 220, 1709, 314, 355, 17, 22476, 364, 10620, 6757, 631, 3228, 99731, 16, 25, 2214, 337, 28, 2546, 11, 355, 17, 314, 220, 1353, 533, 2058, 314, 16, 11, 11339, 364, 10620, 3605, 3609, 7493, 314, 4215, 22305, 84, 28, 16, 107298, 1161, 92, 350, 1434, 533, 337, 8, 659, 4215, 22305, 84, 28, 2109, 107298, 2546, 92, 350, 1353, 533, 220, 18, 84, 3991, 56734, 1577, 4215, 25, 4215, 22305, 84, 28, 16, 107298, 1161, 92, 350, 1434, 533, 337, 8, 314, 220, 1161, 9, 1434, 533, 4215, 22305, 84, 28, 16, 107298, 1161, 92, 337, 314, 220, 10676, 15, 533, 350, 1161, 9, 2109, 14, 17, 25987, 10676, 15, 533, 26249, 28, 36831, 364, 17422, 4215, 25, 23864, 395, 337, 28, 2109, 316, 220, 2546, 364, 2167, 665, 23864, 2454, 2914, 1402, 84, 28, 2109, 25, 220, 1353, 12, 4388, 28, 1709, 198, 2092, 25, 220, 1353, 12, 9989, 28, 858, 198, 2029, 25, 220, 1353, 12, 9928, 28, 1125, 198, 2270, 25, 220, 1353, 12, 6818, 28, 1311, 198, 1130, 25, 220, 1353, 12, 2744, 28, 702, 198, 2911, 25, 220, 1353, 12, 10573, 28, 22, 198, 1398, 25, 220, 1353, 12, 6768, 28, 19, 198, 2546, 25, 220, 1353, 12, 2058, 28, 16, 279, 17434, 314, 220, 1709, 10, 858, 10, 1125, 10, 1311, 10, 702, 10, 22, 10, 19, 10, 16, 314, 13570, 1709, 10, 858, 28, 4987, 198, 10, 1125, 28, 5085, 198, 10, 1311, 28, 2789, 198, 10, 702, 28, 2241, 198, 10, 22, 28, 6818, 198, 10, 19, 28, 8956, 198, 10, 16, 28, 10088, 364, 84787, 3609, 7493, 314, 220, 36831, 659, 220, 10088, 314, 220, 7959, 22, 364, 84787, 1354, 553, 220, 7959, 22, 5571, 306, 415, 484, 553, 7493, 328, 1273, 5571, 306, 415, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 679, 84156, 741, 12265, 2454, 3621, 66938, 316, 261, 5746, 84156, 350, 21910, 26079, 585, 936, 1879, 220, 7959, 22, 5571, 553, 16913, 364, 84787, 65414, 2086, 328, 16913, 4824, 314, 3609, 5571, 533, 7493, 314, 220, 1353, 504, 533, 220, 7959, 22, 314, 220, 46584, 18, 364, 7943, 1309, 316, 3503, 30411, 25, 10547, 1354, 1062, 5571, 484, 679, 1920, 2647, 143645, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 625, 26079, 585, 20707, 3004, 11, 5192, 316, 57364, 4295, 43286, 423, 30005, 105230, 364, 19734, 7477, 484, 5571, 484, 553, 2973, 3621, 328, 261, 2438, 326, 1217, 679, 1043, 2316, 3621, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 7680, 306, 261, 13464, 8, 2928, 60500, 472, 3621, 1606, 4730, 13, 21926, 25435, 25, 1023, 738, 5143, 1432, 483, 1043, 84156, 11, 25040, 16913, 3605, 13, 5339, 43276, 71973, 82, 2454, 2438, 483, 261, 84156, 26, 290, 6577, 15749, 350, 104647, 2935, 84156, 8, 3605, 472, 16913, 13, 2632, 20690, 5882, 364, 10620, 14866, 484, 860, 2438, 1340, 679, 945, 1572, 1001, 84156, 3518, 54912, 26, 17722, 355, 17057, 30680, 8, 314, 350, 87, 16103, 8, 59684, 5746, 7578, 26, 6287, 9443, 7060, 30005, 4784, 220, 15, 503, 220, 16, 16336, 84156, 364, 84787, 6052, 220, 46584, 18, 364, 4383, 11165, 1890, 25, 350, 87, 16103, 8, 3572, 679, 84156, 7539, 415, 889, 2928, 1354, 382, 84156, 3518, 415, 30, 4037, 12331, 364, 84787, 65414, 16913, 4824, 29702, 2086, 328, 8632, 306, 415, 625, 52718, 43992, 84, 1082, 7060, 220, 17, 87, 533, 342, 42308, 94, 220, 15, 1812, 19, 483, 84156, 1217, 306, 415, 13, 5551, 1039, 3605, 9474, 1606, 2617, 7493, 1919, 84156, 382, 1217, 6772, 415, 11, 2360, 6687, 7060, 4493, 337, 30680, 3352, 13, 21926, 6145, 364, 198140, 6052, 25, 220, 46584, 18, 364, 58369, 9570, 13], "n_tokens": 3183, "words": 1854, "band_lo": 2205.0, "band_hi": 8820.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have function f defined on integer pairs. It satisfies functional equation: f(x, y) = f(3x + y, 2x + 2y) for all integers x,y. So f is constant along orbits of transformation T: (x, y) -> (3x + y, 2x + 2y). We need maximal number of distinct values on the grid of (x,y) with 1≤x,y≤100, i.e., the maximum size of set of equivalence classes intersecting this domain, because f can assign distinct values per class (subject to constraints). So we need number of distinct orbits (equivalence classes) intersecting that region. Since f can be arbitrary (any mapping constant on each orbit), the maximal number of distinct values equals number of orbits among those points.\n\nThus we need to analyze transformation T (linear) over integers. Orbits under iteration both forward and backwards? Equation holds for all (x,y). So we have f(x,y) = f(T(x,y)). Also applying transformation again gives f(x,y)=f(T^n(x,y)). Also we may be able to invert? If T is invertible over integers? Compute matrix A = [[3,1],[2,2]]. Determinant = 3*2 - 1*2 = 6-2 =4. Determinant 4, invertible over rationals but inverse matrix is (1/4)*[[2, -1],[-2,3]] . Not integer matrix generally, but may map integers to integers only when vector is divisible appropriately.\n\nHowever functional equation only given forward direction: f(x,y)=f(A*(x,y)). Does not automatically imply f constant on reverse direction unless we can derive using some sequence of forward steps to reach original? Because if T is not invertible on Z, one can't deduce equality backwards. However we can iterate forward arbitrarily large; but may not be able to go backwards. So equivalence relation generated by forward steps: (x,y)~(x',y') if there is a chain of forward applications (or maybe also using identity) that leads from one to other. But since relation is not symmetric necessarily; but property f(x)=f(Tx) forces equality along forward edges; transitivity gives f(x)=f(T^n x) for n≥0. However does it force equality for two points that are not related forward? No.\n\nThus distinct values possible can differ for points not connected by forward transformation chain.\n\nBut we could also consider that we can apply transformation repeatedly to any point, getting a sequence that grows quickly (since matrix entries >1). So each orbit forward is infinite; but in domain of interest limited.\n\nHence the maximal number of distinct values equals number of \"minimal elements\" w.r.t forward relation? Actually each point can be linked to some earlier point maybe? Not necessarily, because we cannot go backward.\n\nThus we can assign arbitrary values to points which are not reachable from any other point in the set via forward steps (i.e., points that are not in the image of T applied to any point within domain?). Wait we need to ensure consistency: For any point p in domain D, its value must equal the value of its forward iterate(s) which may lie outside D; that's okay, since no constraints with other points in D unless they share same forward image chain.\n\nThus we can choose value for each point independently except for points that are linked via forward iteration within D. If point a's forward image b also lies in D, then they must have same value. More generally, if there is a chain a -> ... -> b where each step stays inside D, then values equal. But if forward images leave D then no further constraints.\n\nThus the maximal number of distinct values among points in D equals number of equivalence classes induced by the relation: a ~ b if there exists k,l≥0 such that T^k a = T^l b (i.e., they meet at same point after some forward steps). Since forward iterations converge (?) maybe to unbounded. So they can meet if they have a common forward descendant.\n\nThus we need to compute for each point in D its forward orbit and see whether two points share a common descendant (within integer lattice). If they ever coincide, their values equal.\n\nThus maximal distinct values = number of distinct forward \"tails\" that never merge. In other words the number of ancestors in the directed graph (each node points to its image) that are not coalescing.\n\nSince the transformation is linear and invertible over rationals (determinant nonzero), forward orbits are distinct unless they intersect. Intersection occurs if there exists n,m such that A^n (x1,y1) = A^m (x2,y2). That would mean (x2,y2) = A^{n-m} (x1,y1) (if n≥m) i.e., one is forward image of the other. So essentially orbits intersect if and only if one is forward image of the other, because A is invertible over rationals, thus A^k is injective over Z. So distinct forward images never collide unless they are same point under integer iterations.\n\nThus the equivalence classes are just sets of points that lie on same forward trajectory (i.e., one is forward iterate of another). Since A is injective, two points cannot have same image unless they were same already (injective). So each point has at most one predecessor (maybe none in integers). So the orbits form directed trees (each node with out-degree 1, in-degree 0 or 1). Thus points in D that are on same trajectory are just those where one is forward iterate of another and both lie in D.\n\nHence maximal distinct values = number of points in D that are not forward images of another point in D. Because each such \"root\" can have its own value, and then its forward images in D will share that value.\n\nThus we need to count points (x,y) in [1,100]^2 such that there does NOT exist integer (u,v) in [1,100]^2 with (x,y) = A (u,v). That is, the point is not in the image of A of another point within D.\n\nTherefore answer is number of points in D minus number of points that are images of other points in D (i.e., count of points that have a predecessor within D). Since each image corresponds to a unique predecessor (since A invertible rational implies unique preimage possibly rational not integer). But not every point has integer preimage; need to solve integer solutions of A(u,v) = (x,y). The preimage must be integer pair.\n\nSo we need to find integer solutions to:\n\n3u + v = x\n2u + 2v = y\n\nSolve for u,v integers. From first equation, v = x - 3u. Plug into second: 2u + 2(x - 3u) = y => 2u + 2x -6u = y => -4u + 2x = y => -4u = y - 2x => u = (2x - y)/4 = (2x - y)/4.\n\nv = x - 3u = x - 3*(2x - y)/4 = (4x - 3*(2x - y))/4 = (4x -6x +3y)/4 = (-2x +3y)/4.\n\nSo integer preimage exists iff (2x - y) divisible by 4 and (-2x +3y) divisible by 4 (actually same condition). Since both numerators linear combos.\n\nThus condition: 2x - y ≡ 0 mod 4. Because then u integer, v will also be integer automatically? Let's check: If 2x - y ≡0 (mod4), then compute -2x +3y = -(2x - y) +4y ≡ -(0) +0 ≡0 mod4. So both divisible. So condition is congruence.\n\nThus a point (x,y) has integer predecessor if and only if 2x - y ≡ 0 (mod 4).\n\nNow we also need the predecessor (u,v) to be within [1,100]^2.\n\nGiven (x,y) satisfying congruence, compute u,v via formulas; need check if they fall within range.\n\nThus we need to count points in D that are images of another point in D, i.e., have a predecessor also in D.\n\nSo we need to count pairs (x,y) in [1,100]^2 such that:\n\n(1) 2x - y ≡ 0 mod 4.\n(2) u = (2x - y)/4 is integer between 1 and 100.\n(3) v = (-2x +3y)/4 integer between 1 and 100.\n\nThen number of such points = count of images within D.\n\nThen answer = total points (100*100=10,000) minus that count.\n\nThus compute count.\n\nLet's enumerate possible (x,y). Approach: Loop over possible u,v in D, compute (x,y)=A(u,v) and see if within D; then count distinct images (they will be distinct since A injective). So count number of (u,v) such that (x,y) also lies in D. That's same as count of images.\n\nThus we can count number of (u,v) with x=3u+v ∈ [1,100] and y=2u+2v ∈ [1,100].\n\nCompute constraints. Since u,v ≥1, the minimum x=3*1+1=4, min y=2+2=4. So images stay within at least 4.\n\nUpper bounds: x ≤100 => 3u+v ≤100. y ≤100 => 2u+2v ≤100 => u+v ≤50.\n\nBoth constraints.\n\nWe need count integer pairs u,v≥1 satisfying both.\n\nSince u+v ≤50, and also 3u+v ≤100.\n\nLet's enumerate over u from 1 to maybe 49. For each u, v can be from 1 to min(50 - u, 100 - 3u). Need v ≥1.\n\nDefine Vmax = min(50 - u, 100 - 3u). Also must be ≥1.\n\nWe need to count sum over u of max(0, Vmax).\n\nFind range of u where Vmax≥1.\n\nFirst condition: 50 - u ≥1 => u ≤49.\n\nSecond: 100 -3u ≥1 => 3u ≤99 => u ≤33.\n\nThus u up to 33 ensures both possibilities; beyond 33, 100-3u may be less than 1, making Vmax<1 maybe but 50-u may still be >0; Vmax is min, so if 100-3u <1, Vmax<1, thus no v.\n\nSo u max =33.\n\nNow for u=1..33, compute Vmax = min(50 - u, 100 - 3u).\n\nWe need to determine which of the two is smaller.\n\nCompute both expressions:\n\nA1 = 50 - u (decreases linearly from 49 to 17)\nA2 = 100 - 3u (decreases from 97 to 1)\n\nFind u where they intersect: 50 - u = 100 - 3u => bring terms: -u +3u = 100 -50 => 2u =50 => u =25.\n\nFor u ≤25, compare: 50 - u ≥? 100 - 3u? Let's test u=1: 49 vs 97 => min is 49. So for small u, 50-u is smaller? Actually 49 < 97, so min = 49. So for u=1, A1 is smaller. At u=25: A1 = 25, A2 = 25 equal. For u >25, A2 becomes smaller.\n\nThus:\n\nFor 1 ≤ u ≤25: Vmax = 50 - u.\nFor 26 ≤ u ≤33: Vmax = 100 - 3u.\n\nCheck at u=26: A1=24, A2=100-78=22 => min 22 = A2 indeed.\n\nNow ensure Vmax ≥1: For u=33, A2 = 100 -99 =1, OK.\n\nNow count total images = sum_{u=1}^{25} (50 - u) + sum_{u=26}^{33} (100 - 3u).\n\nCompute first sum: sum_{u=1}^{25} (50 - u) = 25*50 - sum_{u=1}^{25} u = 1250 - (25*26/2)=1250 -325=925.\n\nSecond sum: compute for u=26 to 33.\n\nWe can compute each term:\n\nu=26: 100-78=22\n27: 100-81=19\n28: 100-84=16\n29: 100-87=13\n30: 100-90=10\n31: 100-93=7\n32: 100-96=4\n33: 100-99=1\n\nSum = 22+19+16+13+10+7+4+1 = ?\n\n22+19=41\n+16=57\n+13=70\n+10=80\n+7=87\n+4=91\n+1=92.\n\nThus total images = 925 + 92 = 1017.\n\nThus there are 1017 points in D that are images of other points in D (i.e., have predecessor). Since each image corresponds to a unique predecessor (since injective), these 1017 points are distinct.\n\nThus maximal number of distinct values = total points - images = 10000 - 1017 = 8983.\n\nBut need to double-check: Are there any points that have two different predecessors (i.e., not injective)? No, due to invertibility rationally ensures uniqueness.\n\nAlso note that points that are both image of a point and also have their own image (i.e., appear in a chain) still counted as image only once. That's okay: they will share value with their predecessor, reducing distinct count. Our counting subtracts each point with a predecessor; the root nodes (those without predecessor) count as distinct. So formula works.\n\nNow verify that no point may have more than one predecessor within integers; solve A(u,v) = (x,y) yields unique solution; integrality condition ensures either 0 or 1 integer predecessor.\n\nThus answer 8983.\n\nCheck edge case: (x,y) might have predecessor outside D but still there is predecessor within D? Not relevant.\n\nThus maximal distinct values equals number of elements in D not satisfying congruence condition 2x - y ≡ 0 mod4 with predecessor also in D. However our count considered only those images where predecessor is also inside D, using forward condition via u,v range. That's correct.\n\nHence answer: 8983.\n\nLet's respond.", "response": "ANSWER: 8983", "answer": 8983, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": ["too_short"], "accepted": false}
