0, 18, 10, 16, 11043, 17, 9, 17, 28, 1125, 13, 84705, 314, 47320, 18292, 314, 220, 18, 13, 2632, 15586, 28, 1125, 364, 26070, 1354, 413, 2086, 483, 15586, 28, 1055, 30, 17927, 8302, 30, 15586, 28, 1055, 665, 413, 275, 61, 1265, 350, 23657, 3464, 51159, 8, 503, 275, 61, 19, 2335, 61, 17, 30, 50557, 350, 19, 10, 16, 44406, 17, 10, 16, 25987, 20, 9, 18, 28, 1055, 13, 2632, 297, 314, 275, 61, 19, 2335, 61, 17, 13, 41021, 1746, 42173, 2238, 25, 275, 28, 17, 11, 2335, 28, 18, 871, 297, 28, 17, 61, 19, 9, 18, 61, 17, 28, 1125, 9, 24, 28, 15363, 13, 15586, 28, 350, 19, 10, 16, 44406, 17, 10, 16, 25987, 1055, 13, 45438, 47320, 25, 47320, 7, 17, 61, 19, 25987, 2911, 26, 47320, 7, 18, 61, 17, 25987, 1311, 26, 1888, 314, 23909, 13, 47320, 18292, 314, 23909, 14, 15363, 42308, 230, 17, 13, 50844, 464, 18, 13, 2632, 28336, 364, 34013, 1273, 14083, 275, 61, 17, 2335, 61, 19, 350, 55961, 8, 2684, 18051, 364, 84787, 15586, 28, 1055, 28336, 364, 84841, 28, 1265, 25, 31061, 350, 1311, 10, 16, 8, 9197, 3470, 220, 1311, 51159, 2747, 18051, 5263, 316, 220, 17, 26, 350, 21, 10, 16, 44406, 16, 10, 16, 25987, 22, 9, 17, 28, 1265, 66938, 297, 314, 275, 61, 21, 2335, 13, 41021, 1746, 42173, 25, 220, 17, 61, 21, 9, 18, 28, 2220, 9, 18, 28, 8145, 13, 45438, 47320, 25, 47320, 7, 17, 61, 21, 25987, 12807, 26, 47320, 7, 18, 25987, 19, 26, 1888, 28, 35911, 13, 84705, 28, 35911, 14, 8145, 171441, 17, 13, 43787, 13, 4037, 4951, 364, 84787, 15586, 5017, 1055, 8535, 12998, 18051, 5064, 18, 13, 30456, 1354, 413, 15586, 28, 1311, 30, 18587, 25, 350, 899, 10, 16, 25987, 1311, 51159, 9560, 18051, 464, 17, 364, 84787, 19957, 15586, 382, 220, 1125, 364, 2167, 1217, 1309, 316, 6757, 484, 1062, 2086, 483, 15586, 28, 1125, 484, 382, 540, 430, 29297, 13031, 11, 326, 220, 6106, 5882, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 5153, 2086, 328, 37826, 914, 314, 220, 1125, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 220, 1125, 13], "n_tokens": 6779, "words": 2597, "band_lo": 1343.75, "band_hi": 5375.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need a positive integer n such that its divisor set D can be partitioned into 3 subsets with equal sum. So total sum of divisors sigma(n) must be divisible by 3. And we need to partition into three subsets of equal sum, i.e., each subset sum = sigma(n)/3.\n\nQuestion: what is the minimal number of divisors (i.e., size of D) that such an n can have? We need the smallest possible tau(n) (number of divisors) for which there exists a partition as described.\n\nWe must find minimal tau(n) such that sigma(n) divisible by 3 and a partition exists. Also note trivial case: maybe n=1? Divisors {1}. Can't split into 3 subsets each sum same unless we allow empty subsets? Usually subsets may be empty; but sum of empty set is 0. To have three subsets with equal sum, total sum must be 0, but sigma(1)=1, not possible. So need at least 3 divisors? Could we have 2 divisors? tau=2, divisors {1,n}. Sum = n+1. Must divisible by 3 => n+1 ≡0 mod3 => n≡2 mod3. Need partition into 3 subsets each sum = (n+1)/3. But only two elements. Some subsets may be empty? To get three subsets each sum S, total sum =3S => S = (n+1)/3. We need to assign each divisor to one of three subsets. With two elements, at least one subset empty, whose sum 0 => S must be 0, impossible unless sum zero. So tau must be at least 3.\n\nCheck tau=3: numbers with 3 divisors are squares of primes: n = p^2. Divisors {1,p,p^2}. Sum = 1 + p + p^2 = (p^3-1)/(p-1). Need divisible by 3. Also need partition into three subsets each sum = sigma/3. Since three elements, each subset likely contains exactly one element to have equal sums unless we allow empty but then sums would need zeros. So we require each divisor equals sigma/3. So all three divisors must be equal, impossible unless they are same number. So tau=3 impossible.\n\ntau=4: numbers with 4 divisors: either p^3 or p*q distinct primes. Let's examine possibilities.\n\nWe need to partition 4 elements into 3 subsets. Subset sizes possibilities: (2,1,1) or (3,1,0) etc. But all subsets must have equal sum, thus empty not allowed unless sum zero contradictions. So subsets likely non-empty. With 4 elements, you can have one subset with two elements, others with one each.\n\nLet divisors be a,b,c,d sorted. Need a+b = c = d? Wait total sum sigma = a+b+c+d = 3S. Subset sums: one subset (size2) sum = S, and two singletons each equal S. So we need two singletons each equal S; thus two divisors equal S. Since divisors distinct (unless n is square of prime? but then divisors distinct). So we need two divisors equal same value. Not possible. Unless we allow subset with three elements and one element empty? That would force S = sum of three elements = the remaining element, requiring that element equals sum of the other three, unlikely. But we could have (2,2,0) kind of? But empty not allowed.\n\nThus tau=4 likely impossible.\n\ntau=5: numbers with 5 divisors are p^4. Divisors: 1,p,p^2,p^3,p^4. Need to partition 5 elements into 3 subsets equal sum. Could have sizes (2,2,1). Then the singleton must equal S. The two doubletons also sum S each. So we need two distinct pairs sum to same value, also equal to some divisor. Might be possible.\n\nWe need to search for minimal n. Let's brute reasoning.\n\nLet S = sigma/3.\n\nLet’s denote divisors list D.\n\nWe need a subset of size1 equal to S (i.e., one divisor equals S). So sigma must have a divisor equal to sigma/3.\n\nLet’s compute sigma for p^4: sigma = (p^5-1)/(p-1) = 1 + p + p^2 + p^3 + p^4.\n\nSet S = sigma/3. Need one of the divisors (i.e., 1,p,p^2,p^3,p^4) equal to S.\n\nTry small p.\n\np=2: n=16. Divisors:1,2,4,8,16. Sum =31. Not divisible by 3.\n\np=3: n=81. Divisors:1,3,9,27,81. Sum =121. 121 mod3 = 1, no.\n\np=5: n=625: sum = 1+5+25+125+625 = 781. 781 mod3 = 781- 3*260=781-780=1. Not divisible.\n\nObserving formula: sum = (p^5-1)/(p-1). Compute mod3. Need p mod3? Let's test p≡1 mod3: then p= 3k+1. Evaluate numerator p^5-1 ≡1^5-1=0 mod3, denominator p-1 ≡0 mod3 => division may be ambiguous; but overall sigma may be something mod3? Let's compute directly small p=7: sum =1+7+49+343+2401=2801. 2801 mod3: 2+8+0+1=11 -> 2 mod3, not 0. p=11: sum = 1+11+121+1331+14641=160... let's compute: 1+11=12, +121=133, +1331=1464, +14641=16105. 16105 mod3: sum digits 1+6+1+0+5=13 => 1 mod3. So none divisible. Probably p^4 never yields sigma divisible by 3? Need check condition.\n\nGeneral: sigma(p^4) = (p^5-1)/(p-1). For p ≡ 2 mod3: p=3k+2. Compute modulo 3: p ≡ -1 => p^5 ≡ (-1)^5 = -1 ≡2 mod3. Then numerator p^5-1 ≡2-1=1 mod3. Denominator p-1 ≡ -1-1=-2 ≡1 mod3. So sigma ≡1/1 ≡1 mod3. Not divisible.\n\nFor p ≡1 mod3: p≡1 => p^5-1 ≡0, denominator 0, need compute limit. Use p=1+3k. Write p=1+3k. Use expansion? But maybe sigma divisible by 3? Let's test p=13 (≡1). Sum: 1+13+169+2197+28561=?? 1+13=14, +169=183, +2197=2380, +28561=30941. Mod3: 30941 sum digits 3+0+9+4+1=17 => 2 mod3, not 0. So p^4 impossible.\n\nThus tau=5 not work.\n\ntau=6: numbers with 6 divisors: either p^5, p^2 q (with distinct primes), or p q r? Wait product of three distinct primes gives 8 divisors? Actually number of divisors = (1+1)^3=8. So 6 comes from exponents pattern (5+1)=6 or (2+1)(1+1)=3*2=6. So n = p^5 or n = p^2 q with p,q distinct primes.\n\nWe need to partition 6 elements into 3 subsets each sum S. Could be sizes (2,2,2) or (3,2,1) etc. Most likely (2,2,2) because equal numbers easier. Then we need three disjoint pairs each sum S. This is possible maybe.\n\nSo need sigma divisible by 3 and existence of three pairs with equal sum.\n\nLet's attempt computational reasoning manually.\n\nFirst, consider n = p^2 q where p<q primes.\n\nDivisors: 1, p, p^2, q, p q, p^2 q = n. That's 6. Need sigma = (1+p+p^2)(1+q).\n\nCompute sigma = (1+p+p^2)*(1+q) = (1+p+p^2)*(1+q).\n\nWe need sigma divisible by 3.\n\nCase 1: 3 divides (1+p+p^2) or (1+q).\n\nWe can try small primes.\n\nLet's brute mind few possibilities.\n\nTake p=2. Then (1+2+4)=7. So sigma =7*(1+q) =7*(q+1). Need divisible by 3 -> (q+1) must be multiple of 3. So q ≡2 mod3. Small q=2? distinct so q must be 5? 5≡2 mod3 yes.\n\nTake q=5 => n=2^2*5=20. Divisors:1,2,4,5,10,20. Sum sigma = 1+2+4+5+10+20=42. 42/3 =14 = S. Need partition into 3 pairs each sum 14.\n\nLet's try to pair: List numbers:1,2,4,5,10,20. Need three disjoint pairs sum 14: candidates: 1+13 none; 2+12 none; 4+10=14, good pair (4,10). 5+9 none; 20 too large. 20 can't pair with negative. So need also 1+?=14 ->13 not present; 2+?=12 not present;5+?=9 not;20+?= -6 none. So cannot.\n\nBut perhaps subsets not necessarily size 2 each. Could be (3,2,1) etc. Let's search more.\n\nGoal: find any partition of the 6 numbers into 3 groups equal sum 14. Let's attempt: need groups sum 14.\n\nTry group1: 20 alone too big >14, can't. So 20 must be combined with some negatives? impossible. So tau=6 may need groups sizes where 20 is alone? Actually we could have subsets with multiple elements summing to 14 including 20? can't because 20 > 14.\n\nThus sigma must be at least 3*max divisor? Actually each subset sum S = sigma/3. Since the largest divisor n must be ≤ S otherwise impossible because if n > S, n cannot be placed into any subset (since sum of subset is S). So we need n ≤ S = sigma/3. So sigma ≥ 3n.\n\nBut sigma of n is at most something like n + sum of proper divisors. Typically sigma(n) < something. Let's check condition: need sigma(n) ≥ 3n. For many numbers it's false. Let's compute for n=20 sigma=42, 3n=60, not satisfied, indeed n > S? Actually S=14, n=20 >14, impossible. So condition necessary: sigma(n) ≥ 3n.\n\nThus we need numbers with sigma >= 3n, i.e., abundancy index ≥3.\n\nAbundant numbers with high sigma. Usually perfect numbers have sigma=2n. Need sigma at least 3n, that's a \"multiply perfect\" number with multiplier at least 3. Numbers where sigma = k n are k-multiperfect. Need k≥3. So n must be at least 3-multiperfect (or higher). The smallest known 3-multiperfect number is 120? Wait 120 is perfect? sigma(120) = 360? Actually sigma(120) = sum of divisors of 120 =? 120's divisors sum is 360? Let's compute quickly: I know 120 is abundant with sigma=360? Let’s compute: divisors of 120: 1+2+3+4+5+6+8+10+12+15+20+24+30+40+60+120 =? Sum: pair wise 1+120=121, 2+60=62 =>183, 3+40=43 =>226,4+30=34 =>260,5+24=29 =>289,6+20=26 =>315,8+15=23 =>338,10+12=22 =>360, plus  ? also? we accounted all. Yes sigma=360 = 3*120. So 120 is 3-multiperfect. Great.\n\nThus any n with sigma=3n works: then S = sigma/3 = n. So each subset must sum to n. Since n is a divisor, we could place n alone in a subset (size 1). The other two subsets must also sum to n using the remaining divisors (excluding n). So requirement reduces to partition of the proper divisors (excluding n) into two subsets each sum n.\n\nThus we need a 3-multiperfect number where proper divisors can be split into two groups equal to n.\n\nNow we want minimal tau(n). For 120, tau(120) =? prime factorization: 120 =2^3 *3*5. Number of divisors = (3+1)(1+1)(1+1)=4*2*2=16. That's tau=16. Maybe there is a smaller tau for a 3-multiperfect number.\n\nKnown 3-multiperfect numbers: 120, 360, 720?, let's recall sequence: 120, 360, 720, 840, 1260, 1680? Actually these are abundant. Let's compute sigma / n for each: For 360, sigma? 360 is maybe 3-multiperfect? Let's compute sigma(360). 360 factorization =2^3 *3^2 *5. Divisor count: (3+1)(2+1)(1+1)=4*3*2=24. sigma = product (2^4-1)/(2-1) * (3^3-1)/(3-1) * (5^2-1)/(5-1) = (16-1)=15 * (27-1)/2=26/2=13 * (25-1)/4=24/4=6. So sigma =15*13*6 =15*78=1170. 1170 /360 = 3.25 approx. Not integer, but requirement is multiple of 3? Actually need sigma divisible by 3, not necessarily equal 3n. Need sigma >=3n. For partition feasibility we need n ≤ S = sigma/3. If sigma is greater than 3n, S > n, still ok because n can be in a subset alone (if n ≤ S). So condition is sigma ≥ 3n. That's true for many abundant numbers.\n\nGoal: minimize tau while still possible to partition.\n\nWe need to search for n with small divisor count but sigma ≥ 3n, and can be partitioned into 3 equal-sum subsets.\n\nSmall tau values: Let's test tau=7? Not possible because tau function yields 7 only for p^6? Since (6+1)=7 so n = p^6. Divisors 7 distinct. Could we have partitions? Need sigma divisible by 3. For p^6, sum = (p^7-1)/(p-1). Check modulo 3 similarly likely not 0. Might be but quickly compute p=2: n=64, sigma=1+2+4+8+16+32+64=127, not multiple of 3. p=5 maybe? but likely not.\n\ntau=8: possible forms p^7, p^3 q, p q r distinct (2*2*2). Let's examine small n with tau=8 but also sigma≥3n?\n\nConsider n = p q r (product of three distinct primes). For small primes 2*3*5=30. Divisors count 8. sigma = (1+2)(1+3)(1+5)=3*4*6=72. sigma=72. Then S = sigma/3 =24. Need to partition 8 divisors into 3 subsets each sum 24. Divisors of 30: 1,2,3,5,6,10,15,30. Largest is 30 >24, impossible. So need sigma≥ 3n: 3n =90, sigma=72 less than 90, fails condition, also n> S.\n\nThus need sigma≥3n. For product of 3 distinct primes, sigma = (p+1)(q+1)(r+1). Compare to 3pqr. Usually much smaller than 3n, unless primes are small? Let's test 2*3*7=42. sigma =3*4*8=96. 3n=126, still less. So not good.\n\nNext candidate with tau=8: p^3 q: exponents 3 and 1 yields (3+1)(1+1)=8. Example n=2^3*3=24. Divisors: 1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24. sigma = (1+2+4+8)*(1+3)=15*4=60. 3n=72, sigma < 72. So fails.\n\np^7 (single prime power) tau=8: n=2^7=128. sigma=255, 3n=384, fails.\n\nThus need bigger tau.\n\ntau=9: possibilities p^8, p^2 q^2, p^4 q. Let's examine maybe sigma large enough? For p^2 q^2 (exponents 2,2) n = p^2 q^2. Example 2^2 *3^2 =36. tau = (2+1)*(2+1)=9. sigma = (1+2+4)*(1+3+9)=7*13=91. 3n=108, sigma<108.\n\np^4 q: e.g., 2^4 *3 = 48. tau = (4+1)*(1+1)=10 not 9. Wait exponents 4 and1 produce (4+1)(1+1)=10. So not.\n\np^8: 2^8=256 sigma=511 < 3n.\n\nThus not.\n\ntau=10: forms p^9; p^4 q; p^1 q^2? Wait exponents giving (9+1)=10; (4+1)(1+1)=10; (1+1)(2+1)=2*3=6 not 10. So just p^9 or p^4 q.\n\nExample p^4 q = 2^4*3=48 gives tau=10. sigma: (1+2+4+8+16)*(1+3)=31*4=124. 3n=144, sigma<144.\n\nSo fail.\n\ntau=12: many possibilities: p^11; p^5 q; p^3 q^2; p^2 q r; p q r s (four distinct primes) gives 2*2*2*2=16 not 12. Actually 2*2*3=12 corresponds p^1 q^1 r^2 (exponents 1,1,2). etc.\n\nBut we suspect minimal tau maybe 16 as example of 120 works. However perhaps there's number with tau less than 16 meeting sigma ≥3n.\n\nLet's search.\n\nCompute sigma/n ratio, called abundancy index. Need >=3. Smallest known k-multiperfect number with k=3 is 120 (first 3-perfect). So minimal n with sigma = 3n is 120 (tau=16). For sigma>3n, maybe there are numbers with tau less than 16 yet sigma ≥3n (abundancy >3). Are there any with tau<16? Let's check known highly abundant numbers.\n\nAbundancy index >3 maybe appears later? Let's examine n=96. n=96 = 2^5 *3. tau = (5+1)(1+1)=12. sigma = (2^6-1)/(2-1)*(1+3) =63*4=252. 252/96 = 2.625 <3.\n\nn=108 = 2^2 *3^3. tau = (2+1)(3+1)=3*4=12. sigma = (1+2+4)*(1+3+9+27)=7*40=280. 280/108 ≈2.59.\n\nn=180 = 2^2 *3^2 *5. tau = (2+1)(2+1)(1+1)=3*3*2=18. sigma = (7)*(13)*(6)=7*13*6=546. 546/180 = 3.033... >3. Good. tau=18 >16.\n\nMaybe there is n with tau =15 giving sigma≥3n? tau=15 corresponds to exponents product =15 = 15, 5*3, etc. possibilities: p^14, p^4 q^2, p^2 q^4, p^2 q r (2+1)(1+1)(1+1)=3*2*2=12 not 15. So not many.\n\nBut potentially tau=16 is minimal? Let's think of numbers with sigma/n > =3 but tau less than 16.\n\nWe can try n=84 = 2^2*3*7. tau = (2+1)(1+1)(1+1)=3*2*2=12. sigma = (1+2+4)*(1+3)*(1+7)=7*4*8=224. 224/84 = 2.666... <3.\n\nn=90 = 2*3^2*5. tau =2*3*2=12. sigma =(1+2)*(1+3+9)*(1+5)=3*13*6=234. 234/90 =2.6.\n\nn=96 we already.\n\nn=108 we.\n\nn=120 we 3.\n\nSo seems first where ratio >=3 is at n=120 (tau=16). Might be minimal tau. Need to verify if any number with tau less than 16 could have sigma >= 3n.\n\nSearch systematically small n up to maybe 200.\n\nWe can approximate: sigma generally less than something like n * product (p/(p-1)) small.\n\nFor numbers with small primes, the ratio can be high. The maximal ratio for given size of n is for primorial etc. But we need ratio >=3.\n\nThe product over primes of p/(p-1) diverges logarithmically. For n with many small primes, ratio can exceed 3. Let's compute ratio for n = 2*3*5*7 =210. sigma = (3*4*6*8)=576. Ratio = 576/210 = 2.742... <3.\n\nAdd exponent 2 to one prime: n=2^2*3*5*7 =420. sigma = (1+2+4)=7 * (1+3)=4 * (1+5)=6 * (1+7)=8 => 7*4*6*8=1344. Ratio=1344/420=3.2 >3. tau = (2+1)*2*2*2=3*8=24. So tau>16.\n\nSo seems tau=16 minimal.\n\nBut we also need partition property beyond sigma≥3n. For 120 we need to check that proper divisors can split into two groups each sum 120.\n\nProper divisors of 120: all except 120. List: 1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10,12,15,20,24,30,40,60. Sum = sigma - n = 360-120=240. Need to split into two subsets each sum 120. Does such partition exist? Likely yes because problem expects answer.\n\nLet's try to find: maybe {60,40,20}=120. And remaining sum also 120: 1+2+3+4+5+6+8+10+12+15+24+30 =? compute: (1+2+3+4+5+6+8+10+12+15+24+30)=120? Let's sum: 1+2=3, +3=6, +4=10, +5=15, +6=21, +8=29, +10=39, +12=51, +15=66, +24=90, +30=120. Yes works! So partition exists: groups: {60,40,20} ; {1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10,12,15,24,30}; and {120} as third subset. Good.\n\nThus number 120 works with tau=16.\n\nNow we must ensure there is no atresvido number with less than 16 divisors.\n\nWe need to prove minimal tau is 16.\n\nWe have argued that need sigma ≥ 3n. Show that any n with tau ≤15 has sigma < 3n. Could be proven by bounding.\n\nObservation: sigma(n) ≤ n * product over distinct primes (p/(p-1)). For tau small, n cannot have many small prime factors, thus product limited.\n\nWe can compute max possible sigma/n for given tau perhaps.\n\nBut maybe there exists a number with tau=15 where sigma ≥3n? Let's test candidate: n with many small prime powers: maybe 2^4*3*5 = 240? tau = (4+1)*(1+1)*(1+1) =5*2*2=20 >15.\n\nWhat about tau=14? patterns: (13+1) p^13? very large but ratio small approximates (p/(p-1)). For p=2, sigma/n = (2^{14}-1)/(2^{13}*(2-1)) = (16384-1)/8192 ≈2 - (1/8192) ~ 1.999..., less than 2. Not enough.\n\nThus numbers with high prime power exponent produce ratio <2.\n\nThus need multiple distinct small primes to boost sigma/n.\n\nThe product over distinct primes p/(p-1) for the first k primes approximates e^{γ} log log something; but for few primes: for primes 2,3,5,7: product =2/(1)*3/2*5/4*7/6 = (2)*(1.5)*(1.25)*(1.1667)=4.375. That's >3. But such n includes all those primes at exponent 1 each => tau = 2^k = 2^4=16. Indeed n=210 gave ratio 2.74 because exponents 1 each gave sigma/n = product (p+1)/p = product (1 + 1/p). Wait sigma/n = ∏ (1 + 1/p). That's about product (1+1/p). For primes 2,3,5,7: product (1+1/2)(1+1/3)(1+1/5)(1+1/7) = (3/2)*(4/3)*(6/5)*(8/7) = (3*4*6*8)/(2*3*5*7)= (576)/(210) = 2.7429 indeed.\n\nWe need product >=3, need enough small primes maybe plus exponents.\n\nCheck combination: primes 2,3,5 yield product (3/2)*(4/3)*(6/5)= (3*4*6)/(2*3*5)=72/30=2.4 <3. Add p=7 exponent may increase ratio slightly if exponent >1? e.g., 2^2 *3*5*7? Let's compute n=2^2*3*5*7 = 420? Actually that's 4*3*5*7=420 earlier gave ratio 3.2 >3. Tau = (2+1)*2*2*2=3*8=24. So tau >16.\n\nBut 2*3*5*7*11 maybe ratio product (1+1/p): add 11 gives multiply by 12/11 ≈1.0909 yields 2.7429*1.0909 ≈2.99 still <3. Need include 13 maybe 13 gives 14/13≈1.0769 => product ~3.22. That would tau = 2^5 =32 >16.\n\nThus with squarefree numbers, tau = 2^k. To achieve ratio ≥3, need k≥5? Let's test: product for first five primes 2,3,5,7,11 = (3/2)*(4/3)*(6/5)*(8/7)*(12/11)=? We already have 2.7429*12/11 ≈2.9985 ~ just under 3. So need sixth prime 13 gives >3. So k=6 => tau=64 >16.\n\nThus squarefree numbers require at least 6 distinct primes => tau >= 64. So not minimal.\n\nThus we need combination with exponents giving higher ratio with fewer divisor count.\n\nOur candidate 120 = 2^3 *3*5 gives tau = (3+1)*2*2=16. Ratio = sigma/n = 3. So tau=16.\n\nCould there be number with tau=15? unknown pattern? tau=15 can be p^14 (too big exponent) or p^4 q^2? Actually (4+1)*(2+1)=5*3=15. So n = p^4 q^2. Let's test smallest such: p=2, q=3 => n=2^4*3^2=16*9=144. tau= (4+1)*(2+1)=15. Compute sigma: sigma(2^4)=31; sigma(3^2)=13; product =403. sigma/n =403/144 ≈2.798 <3. So fails.\n\nMaybe other combination p^2 q^4 (swap) same ratio.\n\nThus tau=15 fails.\n\ntau=14: possibilities (13+1) prime power 13 exponent -> ratio close to 2; (6+1)*(1+1)=7*2=14 corresponds n = p^6 q. Let's test smallest: 2^6*3=64*3=192. Compute sigma: sigma(2^6)=127; sigma(3)=4; product=508. Ratio=508/192≈2.645. Not enough.\n\nThus tau <=15 can't achieve ratio >=3. Could there be tau=13? patterns: (12+1)=13 exponent huge ratio <2.\n\nThus minimal tau is 16.\n\nWe also need to ensure that any number with tau=16 that is atresvido exists, and 120 works.\n\nThus answer: least number of divisors = 16.\n\nThus final answer: ANSWER: 16.", "response": "ANSWER: 16", "answer": 16, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.4662643359031335e-05, "p_correct": 0.999985337464137, "logp_correct": -1.4662643359031335e-05, "cot_lp_mean": -0.49609277702682353, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2047060380354822, "frac_below_-5": 0.007390983000739098, "digit_lp_min": -5.1309285163879395, "answer_first_mention_lp": -1.2993727978027891e-05, "n_cot_tokens": 6765, "lp_end": -5.960462772236497e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -4.7801782784517854e-05, "logp_nocot": -6.267746545374365, "p_nocot": 0.0018964974424974265, "uplift": 6.267731882731006}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
