m > 1810). We can cross out some numbers (i.e., choose a submultiset) such that the remaining sum is within [1810 - d, 1810 + d]. We need minimal d such that for any admissible list we can achieve that.\n\nInterpretation: We can delete some numbers (maybe none) to reach sum close to 1810. The admissible list sum > 1810 (strict). Want to guarantee existence of sublist with sum within ±d of 1810.\n\nWe can think of subset sum closeness problem with constraints max element 100. Want worst-case minimal possible closeness guarantee.\n\nWe need minimal d such that any multiset of numbers in [1,100] with total S >1810 has a sub-sum (maybe the complement?) Actually we cross out some numbers, leaving some numbers (i.e., choose a subset to keep). So we can achieve any sum from 0 up to S by subset selection? Not necessarily contiguous due to limited elements.\n\nGoal: find maximal possible minimal distance from 1810 when we can choose subset sum. For each list, define δ(L) = min_{subsets T} |sum(T)-1810|. Since S>1810, we can possibly also consider taking all numbers (sum S) but that's >1810; we could also take empty (0). But we can cross out some numbers, not necessarily need to keep at least one? Yes, can cross out any numbers maybe all; but sum must be >=1810-d? D is positive integer.\n\nWe want smallest d such that for all L, δ(L) ≤ d. So we need sup over all admissible L of δ(L) and then d = ceil of that sup. Since δ(L) integers maybe half??? Actually sum and 1810 integer, δ integer. So d = maximal minimal distance across worst-case list.\n\nThus we need to construct lists that force any subset to be far from 1810. The numbers limited to ≤100.\n\nWe need to find worst-case minimal distance. Equivalent to classic \"postage stamp\" or Frobenius coin? Let's consider that we can decide to keep a subset sum approximating 1810. Since numbers up to 100, ability to fine-tune sum is limited if list has many same numbers maybe. But we can use many small numbers to adjust tightly. The worst-case will be when list consists of numbers that are all multiples of some g >1, which restrict possible sums to multiples of g. Then distance to 1810 may be at least g/2. So d must be at least minimal possible g/2? However numbers are arbitrary up to 100, we can use numbers all multiples of, say, 2 (i.e., all even). Then any subset sum even; 1810 is even, so we can achieve exactly 1810 possibly if total sum >1810 and we can drop some to possibly achieve 1810 exactly? But need subset sum 1810 using evens, possible if we have enough even sum to make 1810. Since evens can sum to any even number up to total sum, as long as there is enough total even sum. However might miss because we may not have enough combination to reach exact 1810 if total sum of evens >1810 but not exactly 1810 due to limited counts.\n\nIf list consists of many 100s (max). Let's think worst-case: using large numbers leaves coarse granularity: each element 100, sum S >1810 implies at least 19 numbers (since 18*100=1800). So minimal S is 1811? Could have 18 numbers: some numbers not 100 to get >1810 with 18 numbers: max sum 1800, insufficient. So need at least 19 numbers. With 19 numbers each ≤100, we can have 18 100s and one small to exceed 1810: sum = 1800 + x >1810 -> x≥111 impossible. So not possible. So actually need at least 19 numbers, but sum of 19 numbers each ≤100 max 1900 so possible. Minimum sum >1810 with 19 numbers: need total at least 1811, average ≈95.3, feasible with many 100s and some lower.\n\nConsider list with 19 numbers: 18 of them 100, last 11? That sum = 1811. That's admissible. Subset sums possible: we can choose any number of 100s plus maybe 11. The reachable sums are of form 100k or 100k+11 where k from 0 to 18. Need within d of 1810. Let's compute closest to 1810: 100*18 =1800, diff 10. Also 100*17+11 = 1711? Wait 100*17+11 = 1711 diff 99. 100*18+11 = 1811 diff 1. But we can't exceed total sum? We have 18 100s and a 11, total 1811. Subset sum can be 1811 (keep all) diff 1. That's within d=1. So worst-case not that.\n\nBut we can forbid the small 11 to adjust? If we had 19 numbers all 100 except one 2? Sum 1802? That's not >1810. Need >1810.\n\nWhat about list composed of numbers all equal to 100 except one 20? Then total = 1820. Subset sums: may be 100k, or 100k+20. We can achieve 1820 exactly (all). diff 10 from 1810? Actually 1820 diff 10. Can we get closer? 100*18=1800 diff 10. 100*17+20=1720 diff 90. 100*18+20=1820 diff 10. So closest distance is 10. So for this list, δ=10. So d must be at least 10.\n\nBut maybe we can construct worse. Use numbers all multiples of m but 1810 not multiple, forcing distance at least remainder. Want maximize minimal distance. Also have total >1810 but we may not be able to reach near because limited count of smaller numbers to adjust.\n\nIf we have many 100s and one 19 (sum 1919?) Wait 18*100+19=1919 >1810. Closest sums: 100*19? not enough. Actually we have 18 100s and a 19. Subset sums: 100k or 100k+19. Want close to 1810. Evaluate k=18 => 1800 diff10. k=17+19 = 1719 diff91. k=18+19 = 1819 diff9 (since we can take all 18 100s + 19 = 1819 diff 9). That's better: diff 9. So δ=9.\n\nGeneral: with 100s and a small r, total = 100n + r >1810, n≥18.\n\nDefine n = floor(1810/100)=18. Then we need r > 10 (since 100*18=1800). So r = 11..100. Then total = 1800+r >1810, r≥11. We can achieve sum = 1800 (by selecting 18 100s) diff = r-10 . also total sum = 1800+r diff = r-10? Actually 1800+r -1810 = r-10. So both have same diff r-10? Wait 1800 is diff 10-r? Actually 1810-1800=10. So difference from 1800 is 10. For r from 11 to 100, diff = |1800 - 1810| =10 fixed. The total sum diff = r-10 (since total >1810). So min diff = min(10, r-10). For r between 11 and 20, r-10 ≤10, so min diff = r-10 (e.g., r=11 gives diff1). For r=11 diff1; r=12 diff2; ... r=20 diff10; for larger r diff becomes 10 (from 1800) vs >10 from total, so min diff stays 10. So worst-case diff is 10 when r≥20. So this yields δ =10.\n\nThus we have a lower bound d ≥10.\n\nCan we force larger? Maybe using numbers that are multiples of 7 etc. Need to find list where any subset sum can't get within <d of 1810.\n\nConsider list that has many copies of a large value nearly 100 but not allowing fine adjustments.\n\nGeneral concept: Since each number ≤100, we can think of representation modulo m. If we have all numbers congruent to a mod m, then any subset sum is congruent to k*a mod m. To get close to 1810, need some multiple's remainder close to 1810 mod m. Minimal distance at least half of m minus something.\n\nWe can design list where all numbers are multiples of 5, say, but 1810 is multiple of 5 (since 5*362). So we can achieve exactly 1810 if total sum includes enough multiples to make that sum (by appropriate combination maybe). However need ability to make exact sum of 5 multiples to reach 1810. Since we can use up to many numbers, maybe possible.\n\nWorst-case likely with numbers all equal to 100 except one number (r) as above gave d=10. Could we produce d larger than 10? Let's test with numbers equal to 99 maybe.\n\nLet list: many 99s. Let's compute n = floor(1810/99) = 18 (since 18*99=1782). Need extra to exceed 1810: need sum >1810 => total >=1811. With 18 99s sum 1782; need extra at least 29 using another number ≤100. Could add 30. So list: 18*99 + 30 = 1782+30=1812. Subset sums possible: any combination of 99s plus maybe 30. Achievable sums are 99k or 99k+30. Want close to 1810. Consider k=18: 1782 diff 28. k=17+30 = 1713 diff 97. k=18+30 = 1812 diff 2. So min diff =2. So not large.\n\nMaybe use numbers all 100 except one 20 gave diff 10. Could we do with all 100 except one 21? Then total 1821 diff from 1810 =11, but 1800 diff10 still ; min diff 10. So still 10.\n\nWhat about using numbers 95 each? n= floor(1810/95)=19 (19*95=1805). Need extra to exceed 1810: need at least 6 more, but we have 20 numbers? Actually 19*95 =1805 <1810. Add another 95 gives 1900 >1810. So we could have 20 numbers of 95 each sum 1900. Subset sums multiples of 95. Nearest to 1810: compute k such that 95k close. 95*19 = 1805 diff5; 95*20=1900 diff90. So δ=5. So less than 10.\n\nThus worst-case seems 10.\n\nBut need to verify if any list can force δ more than 10. Let's think more generally. Since we can delete numbers, we can think of possible subset sums forming a set S. Want to guarantee that there exists sum in interval [1810-d,1810+d]. Equivalent to say the gap between consecutive achievable sums near 1810 is at most 2d. The maximum possible minimal distance across all lists equals the maximum of the minimal distance to 1810 given the achievable sums set.\n\nWe can attempt to bound d ≤10 universally. Show that any admissible list has a subset sum within 10 of 1810. And there exists list where minimal distance =10 (like 18*100 plus 20). So answer d=10.\n\nProof of bound: Since each number ≤100, we can apply greedy removal? Consider taking all numbers, sum S>1810. If S ≤1810+10, already within 10. If larger, we can try to remove some numbers to reduce sum by at most 100 each step, aiming to get within 10. We need to show we can achieve any remainder modulo something? Let's attempt: Starting with S, we can subtract numbers one by one; each subtraction reduces sum by at most 100. Want to get to within 10 of 1810. Since S-1810 >0. Let excess = S-1810. If excess ≤10, done. Otherwise excess ≥11. Since each removed number ≤100, we can try to remove a subset whose total excess is in [11, 100+10]? Actually we need to get new sum between 1810-10 and 1810+10. So we need to subtract a subset T of total t such that S - t ∈ [1800, 1820]. Equivalent t ∈ [S-1820, S-1800]. Width = (S-1800) - (S-1820) =20. So we need a subset sum t within an interval of length 20. Since existing numbers are ≤100, maybe always possible due to some combinatorial argument like postage stamp/pigeonhole with residues modulo something.\n\nDefine the multiset of numbers. Consider all possible subset sums t modulo 100? Not.\n\nAlternative: Use the theorem: For any set of positive integers each ≤100 with total sum >100*ceil(1810/100)=1900? Not.\n\nBetter: Use the idea of the Frobenius coin problem ensures any large enough integer can be expressed as sum of numbers from a set with gcd 1, but we don't have guarantee gcd 1.\n\nBut we can choose to keep numbers, not necessarily remove.\n\nMaybe bound of 10 can be proven via considering the sum modulo 10? Since numbers ≤100, their residues modulo 10 cover 0..9 possibility. If list contains at least one number with each residue? Not guaranteed. However we can use at most 10 numbers?\n\nConsider that we can always adjust sum by at most 10 using numbers of size ≤100 by maybe replacing a 100 with a smaller number? Not.\n\nLet's try to formal proof: Suppose we have admissible list L, total S>1810. If S ≤1820, then we already have sum in interval with d=10 (keep all). If S>1820, excess >10. Since each number ≤100, there exists at least one number a such that removing a reduces the sum to ≤S- a . We try to find a subset whose sum is between excess-10 and excess+10? Actually we want to remove t = S - desired sum where desired sum in [1810-10, 1810+10]. So t must be in [S-1820, S-1800] which length 20. Since each number ≤100, we need to find subset sum t within that interval.\n\nNow consider the set of all possible subset sums of the list. By classic subset sum result, the set contains an interval of consecutive integers of length at least the smallest element? Not.\n\nBut we can use the concept that given numbers up to 100, the subset sums cover all residues modulo 100 beyond some point due to pigeonhole principle on cumulative sums? Suppose we sort numbers arbitrarily and consider partial sums mod 100. With n numbers, there are n+1 partial sums (including 0). Mod 100 there are 100 residues. If n+1 >100 then two partial sums have same residue, difference gives a subset sum multiple of 100. But we need interval length 20, maybe we can use.\n\nGiven that S>1810, we have at least 19 numbers (since max per number=100, 18*100=1800 insufficient). So n≥19. Then n+1=20 which is not >100. So not guarantee.\n\nBut maybe we can construct using 19 numbers each ≤100 there is always a subset sum in any interval of length 100? Not.\n\nLet's try to show d=10 works by case analysis: if there is a number a such that 1810-a is achievable with remaining numbers? Not guarantee.\n\nMaybe we can use the following: Since 1810 = 18*100 + 10. Consider any list with total sum >1810. Consider count of numbers >10? Could reduce.\n\nAlternative: Use complement subset (crossed out). Let T be set crossed out with sum t. Then remaining sum = S - t. Want S - t within [1810-10, 1810+10]. So t ∈ [S-1820, S-1800]. Since S > 1810, the interval length is 20. So we need a subset of numbers whose sum lies in any interval of length 20 (depending on S). Claim: For any multiset of numbers ≤100 with total sum > 1810, there is a subset with sum in any interval of length 20? Not any interval but the specific interval as defined.\n\nWe can use DP style: Since numbers are ≤100, we can achieve any sum up to 100*? Not.\n\nPotential worst-case scenario we found gave minimal distance 10, achieving exactly the bound. So d=10 likely minimal.\n\nThus answer: 10.\n\nI will output accordingly.", "response": "ANSWER: 10", "answer": 10, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.933902235075948e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999960661055027, "logp_correct": -3.933902235075948e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7135220947586347, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4542477813010635, "frac_below_-5": 0.014125799573560768, "digit_lp_min": -4.86458683013916, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 3752, "lp_end": -1.1920922133867862e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -4.768370445162873e-07, "logp_nocot": -1.6973787520546466, "p_nocot": 0.1831630110173744, "uplift": 1.6973748181524115}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "fb06c9ed-f23f-4e56-bc8f-4f62c3e54623", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: For an upcoming international mathematics contest, the participating countries were asked to choose from nine combinatorics problems. Given how hard it usually is to agree, nobody was surprised that the following happened:\n[b]i)[/b] Every country voted for exactly three problems.\n[b]ii)[/b] Any two countries voted for different sets of problems.\n[b]iii)[/b] Given any three countries, there was a problem none of them voted for.\nFind the maximal possible number of participating countries.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: For an upcoming international mathematics contest, the participating countries were asked to choose from nine combinatorics problems. Given how hard it usually is to agree, nobody was surprised that the following happened:\n[b]i)[/b] Every country voted for exactly three problems.\n[b]ii)[/b] Any two countries voted for different sets of problems.\n[b]iii)[/b] Given any three countries, there was a problem none of them voted for.\nFind the maximal possible number of participating countries.", "true_answer": 56, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "fb06c9ed-f23f-4e56-bc8f-4f62c3e54623:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 4792, 25, 3274, 553, 220, 24, 6840, 13, 11555, 4931, 94639, 9707, 220, 18, 6840, 350, 64, 220, 18, 73011, 58845, 741, 30255, 25, 1062, 1920, 8981, 679, 2647, 11982, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 16913, 220, 18, 37663, 7234, 741, 2632, 10562, 2086, 328, 8981, 72554, 2086, 328, 220, 18, 37663, 7234, 328, 220, 24, 314, 363, 7, 24, 11, 18, 25987, 9928, 13, 33202, 7060, 25, 2214, 1062, 3407, 16913, 8981, 11, 1354, 13031, 540, 5153, 1001, 4792, 625, 35666, 656, 1062, 328, 1373, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 62