 1215, 665, 413, 5768, 13, 2632, 65414, 3502, 382, 9707, 2617, 15683, 261, 5355, 3577, 1215, 350, 16204, 741, 2632, 2349, 382, 220, 5007, 364, 10620, 14866, 484, 1062, 3407, 220, 18, 12, 7234, 484, 8397, 261, 5024, 4792, 22476, 35441, 7060, 25, 11377, 2236, 1023, 722, 5141, 484, 4792, 11, 22208, 6284, 413, 722, 220, 24, 364, 84787, 65414, 2086, 328, 8981, 314, 220, 5007, 364, 7943, 581, 2804, 1217, 2371, 538, 1354, 1340, 413, 1273, 11512, 1217, 2349, 220, 5007, 889, 625, 8253, 26, 889, 10562, 382, 220, 5007, 364, 2167, 2804, 1217, 6757, 7060, 117876, 25, 392, 39520, 1062, 3407, 8981, 11, 1354, 673, 261, 4792, 12698, 328, 1373, 35666, 395, 3692, 125152, 1354, 13031, 540, 5153, 1001, 4792, 625, 35666, 656, 1062, 328, 290, 3407, 13, 21926, 23121, 395, 8253, 11512, 11, 472, 5151, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 5007, 364, 58369, 10635, 1721, 6052, 13], "n_tokens": 2786, "words": 1981, "band_lo": 864.5, "band_hi": 3458.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret problem: There are 9 problems. Each country selects exactly 3 problems (a 3-element subset). Condition: any two countries have different sets (i.e., distinct 3-subsets). So maximum number of countries ≤ number of 3-subsets of 9 = C(9,3)=84. Additional condition: For any three distinct countries, there exists at least one problem not voted by any of them (i.e., not in the union of their selected 3-sets). Equivalent: The union of any three 3-sets is not the whole set of 9; i.e., missing at least one element.\n\nWe need to find maximal size of a family F of 3-element subsets of [9] such that (i) all subsets distinct; (ii) for any three distinct subsets A,B,C ∈ F, we have |A∪B∪C| ≤ 8 (i.e., not all 9). equivalently, there is some element not covered by any of the three sets.\n\nWhat is largest possible size? This is a combinatorial design/covering problem.\n\nWe can think complement: Let each subset be 3-element; its complement is a 6-element set. Condition that for any three subsets, there is an element not in any of them, i.e., there exists element x ∈ [9] such that x ∉ A,B,C. Equivalently, the intersection of complements of A, B, C (i.e., 6-sets) is nonempty. So we want a family G of 6-element subsets (complements) such that for any three members of G, their intersection is nonempty. This is reminiscent of the Helly property: family of subsets of a finite set where any three intersect => there is a point common to all? No, Helly's theorem for hypergraphs: For families of subsets where every k intersect => there is a point common to all? Actually Helly's theorem for intervals: but for arbitrary sets not true; we just need condition that triple intersections nonempty. We ask for maximal size of family of 6-sets on 9-point set with property: any three intersect.\n\nGoal: maximize number of 3-sets (or 6-sets) satisfying that condition. Equivalent to find largest family F of 3-subsets of [9] such that any three have union size ≤8.\n\nWe can attempt to use complement view: family of 6-sets with triplewise intersecting property. What is the maximal size? Since each 6-set is complement of a 3-set. So maximizing number of 3-sets is same as maximizing 6-sets.\n\nThis is reminiscent of Erdos–Ko–Rado type results for intersecting families, but there the condition is pairwise intersecting. Here we have triplewise intersecting (i.e., 3-wise intersecting). For subsets of size r in an n-element set, the maximum size of a family with the property that any t members intersect is known: it is given by the Frankl theorem? For t-wise intersecting families.\n\nSpecifically, the problem: for n=9, r=6, t=3 (any three intersect). Need maximum size.\n\nWe suspect that the extremal families are those that contain all 6-sets that contain a particular element: that ensures any three have that element, thus intersect. That's a 1-element kernel. Number of 6-sets containing a fixed element = C(8,5)=56. That's huge but we also still have complement families of size 56 3-sets? Wait complement of each 6-set is a 3-set not containing the fixed element (since if 6-set contains element x, its complement of size 3 does NOT contain x). So the family of 3-sets would be all 3-sets that avoid a particular element. That family size is C(8,3)=56 as well.\n\nBut does this family satisfy condition that any three union misses at least one element? If all 3-sets avoid element x, then certainly any three avoid x, so union lacks x: condition satisfied. So we can have up to 56 countries (choosing all 3-sets that avoid a particular problem). However there is also constraint that each country votes for exactly three problems; allowed. Distinct sets. So 56 seems possible.\n\nBut can we exceed 56? Let's think: If we include any 3-set that contains the previously excluded element x, then we might break condition because we could pick three sets that together cover all 9. Maybe we can still add some while maintaining property, maybe not.\n\nWe need maximum size of family of 3-subsets not covering whole 9 with any three. Equivalent to complement family of 6-sets with pairwise property: any three intersect.\n\nWe have n=9, r=6. The largest intersecting families (pairwise) is C(8,5)=56. For t=3 intersecting, maybe also same bound? Usually the extremal for t-wise intersecting families with r > (t-1)n/t maybe is also a star; I'm not sure.\n\nLet’s try to see if we can have larger than 56. Since total number of 3-sets is 84, we’re missing 28 sets if we take all that avoid a fixed element. Could we add some of those missing 28 while still preserving property? The missing 28 are the 3-sets that contain element x. The complement 6-sets of those missing 28 are the 6-sets that *exclude* element x; i.e., 6-sets not containing x (i.e., subsets of the other 8 elements). If we add a 3-set containing x, its complement is a 6-set missing x. Then the family of 6-sets would contain both many that contain x (the original star) and now some without x. Then you could potentially have three 6-sets whose intersection empty - produce violation.\n\nSpecifically, to violate condition, we need three 3-sets whose union is all 9. That means their complements (6-sets) have intersection empty. So we need three 6-sets with empty intersection. In original star, all 6-sets contain x, so any three intersect at least at x, never empty. Adding a 6-set not containing x creates possibility that intersection of three could be empty if we combine two containing x and one not containing x, their intersection may be empty if the element x is not present in the third, but still the first two both contain x, so intersection includes x (since both contain x). Actually intersection of three sets, two contain x, third does not, then intersection cannot contain x. Could still be nonempty due to other common elements.\n\nGoal ensure intersection of any three 6-sets is nonempty. If we add a set not containing x, we must ensure that for any two other sets (both contain x), their intersection with this new set still has some element. That would require that the new set shares at least one element with any other set that includes x. Since each other set includes x and four other elements (since 6-set size). The new set of size 6 (excluding x) could miss x and include some subset of other 8 elements.\n\nGiven that there are many original sets, it's unlikely we can add any without causing a triple without common element. Let's attempt to see if adding a single 6-set not containing x leads to violation: choose two original 6-sets that intersect only at x (i.e., they share only element x). Are there such pairs? Original star consists of all 6-sets that contain x. Among those, two sets could have intersection exactly {x} (plus maybe other common elements). Since each set selects 5 other elements from remaining 8. So we can choose A = {x} ∪ S where S is a 5-element subset of the other 8. B = {x} ∪ T where T is another 5-element subset. The intersection = {x} ∪ (S∩T). Could be just {x} if S and T are disjoint (possible? 5+5=10>8 so not disjoint). Max intersection size? To have S∩T minimal, we need S and T to overlap as little as possible. Since there are only 8 elements, two 5-element subsets must intersect in at least 2 elements (by pigeonhole). Because 5+5=10, exceed 8 by 2, so intersection size ≥2. Thus any two original 6-sets intersect in at least {x} plus at least 2 other elements, total at least 3 elements. So intersection size ≥3.\n\nThus any two original sets have intersection containing at least 3 elements, not just x. Good.\n\nNow add a new 6-set N that does not contain x. We need for any two original sets A,B, we need A∩B∩N ≠ ∅. Since A∩B already has at least 3 elements (including possibly not x). So as long as N contains at least one of those common elements, triple intersection nonempty. So we need N to intersect every possible intersection of two original sets. The worst-case intersection is the smallest intersection among pairs: size 3 (including x maybe). But we already know the intersection includes at least 2 non-x elements. Let's identify minimal common set of non-x elements across all pairs? For any pair of original sets, they share at least 2 elements among the other 8. However the specific pair could share any pair of the 8 elements. So N would need to contain each possible pair? Actually just need to intersect each specific pair's intersection which is some set of at least 2 elements (maybe variable). To guarantee triple intersection nonempty for every pair, N must contain at least one element from each possible pair intersection. Since there are many possible intersections (choose any 2 of the 8 maybe). Is there a 6-set N (without x) that hits all 2-element subsets of the 8? A 6-set contains 6 of the 8 elements, so it misses 2 elements. Could miss a pair that could be the required intersection for some original pair (i.e., those two elements may be the whole intersection besides x?), but we need at least one element from the intersection, which includes the two elements themselves plus possibly more (since each pair's intersection includes at least two fixed elements; but not necessarily exactly those two). Actually for a given pair of original sets A,B, their intersection consists of x plus the common elements of S and T where S,T are 5-sets of the 8. Intersection size ≥3 including maybe x+2 others. The two non-x common elements can be any subset of size 2 of the 8 (plus maybe more). So it's possible there exists a pair where intersection (excluding x) is exactly a particular chosen pair {a,b}. So N would need to contain at least one of {a,b}. If N omits both a and b, then intersection of A,B,N would be just {x}? Wait N doesn't contain x, so triple would be empty. Indeed if N lacks both a and b and doesn't have x, triple intersection empty. So to avoid this, N must contain at least one element from every 2-subset of the 8. That is impossible because a 6-set misses exactly 2 elements, which correspond to a particular 2-subset; that 2-subset would be missing from N, and then choose A,B whose non-x intersection exactly those two elements would cause empty triple intersection. Are there A,B with intersection being exactly that pair plus x? Let's see if we can choose S,T such that S∩T = that pair only (size 2). Since S and T are 5-element subsets of 8, we can pick S containing that pair plus 3 others, T containing that pair plus the other three distinct from S (so total elements: the pair plus 3+3=6 distinct others, total 8). That's possible: Choose pair {a,b}. Let remaining elements be c,d,e,f,g,h (6 elements). Choose S = {a,b,c,d,e}. T = {a,b,f,g,h}. Both size 5, intersection = {a,b}. So yes possible. Therefore any 6-set N missing a and b would cause violation with A,B as constructed. Hence no 6-set not containing x can be added. So maximal family is exactly those containing a common element x (star). So size is 56.\n\nNow verify that any three 3-sets that avoid a particular problem indeed satisfy condition: Yes because they all miss that problem, union cannot be all 9.\n\nThus maximal number of countries = 56.\n\nBut we must also check if there may be other families also size 56 but not star; but maximum is 56.\n\nWe must also ensure condition iii: \"Given any three countries, there was a problem none of them voted for.\" Means there exists at least one problem not voted by any of the three. That's satisfied for star families, as above.\n\nThus answer: 56.\n\nLet's produce final answer.", "response": "ANSWER: 56", "answer": 56, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.8610207891688333e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999971389833036, "logp_correct": -2.8610207891688333e-06, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6491153982692777, "cot_lp_min64": -1.5726031529142332, "frac_below_-5": 0.012626262626262626, "digit_lp_min": -1.053711175918579, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.009162994101643562, "n_cot_tokens": 2772, "lp_end": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -0.0005581013392657042, "logp_nocot": -10.035786374472053, "p_nocot": 4.380395821445166e-05, "uplift": 10.035783513451264}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "0d64ae77-372a-495d-be3b-3a4a299fa2df", "source": "dapo", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: On the planet Mars, there are $100$ states that are in dispute. To achieve a peace situation, blocs must be formed that meet the following two conditions:\n1. Each block must have at most $50$ states.\n2. Every pair of states must be together in at least one block.\n\nFind the minimum number of blocks that must be formed.\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is the final integer answer.", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: On the planet Mars, there are $100$ states that are in dispute. To achieve a peace situation, blocs must be formed that meet the following two conditions:\n1. Each block must have at most $50$ states.\n2. Every pair of states must be together in at least one block.\n\nFind the minimum number of blocks that must be formed.", "true_answer": 6, "origin": "base", "source_ref": "0d64ae77-372a-495d-be3b-3a4a299fa2df:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 679, 13686, 24878, 4792, 25, 29844, 920, 328, 220, 1353, 8632, 11, 581, 1309, 261, 5801, 328, 165377, 350, 44754, 8, 2454, 328, 2349, 72554, 220, 1434, 11, 2238, 484, 1753, 10610, 328, 8632, 382, 4717, 306, 540, 5153, 1001, 4355, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 290, 5801, 328, 165377, 382, 261, 10610, 24878, 741, 27042, 19957, 2086, 328, 18125, 364, 2500, 382, 29212, 11165, 24878, 328, 5533, 7465, 658, 62, 1353, 656, 567, 11654, 328, 2349, 72554, 220, 1434, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 2454, 4355, 66938, 316, 261, 60301, 15683, 722, 24702, 3518, 484, 4355, 741, 1416, 1309, 316, 4321, 722, 22569, 328, 658, 62, 1353, 483, 567, 11654, 328, 2349, 540, 1645, 220, 1434, 13, 197203, 316, 11165, 24878, 2086, 2360, 567, 11654, 328, 136812, 2349, 364, 42087, 19957, 2086, 328, 567, 11654, 328, 2349, 869, 316, 220, 1434, 316, 4321, 722, 22569, 364, 88748, 25, 1843, 581, 20