5, 21073, 395, 722, 11, 13972, 57325, 395, 484, 52015, 3577, 21073, 656, 16, 11, 2049, 134661, 395, 7360, 8632, 73928, 13, 1328, 18518, 124326, 13, 2514, 17462, 1520, 722, 134661, 12421, 11, 581, 1309, 316, 2364, 14083, 364, 2500, 382, 4811, 8012, 364, 39520, 1058, 11, 12951, 6052, 382, 220, 1179, 15, 30, 41021, 1746, 478, 28, 1179, 15, 11, 2454, 3577, 14518, 306, 260, 28, 1353, 15, 12486, 11982, 13, 37520, 12486, 2349, 314, 350, 21, 9, 1353, 15, 11876, 1179, 15, 28, 18, 13, 2632, 1309, 64353, 289, 3502, 328, 7848, 2349, 220, 18, 13, 30456, 413, 30248, 2360, 722, 165377, 328, 2349, 5064, 18, 350, 33570, 2349, 220, 18, 13, 43335, 8, 2932, 1236, 49015, 364, 34013, 19957, 478, 382, 220, 1179, 15, 22476, 364, 40, 2411, 6052, 1340, 413, 220, 1179, 15, 364, 58369, 4733, 484, 13], "n_tokens": 6522, "words": 4100, "band_lo": 2050.0, "band_hi": 8200.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse problem: There are 6 problems. Each problem solved by exactly 1000 students. Also, it never happens that two students together have solved all 6 problems. That is, for any pair of students, the union of problems they solved is not all 6; i.e., there is at least one problem not solved by either of the two students.\n\nGoal: minimal total number of participants (students) consistent with these constraints.\n\nEach student solves a subset of the 6 problems (could be empty? Typically a participant may solve some problems; can solve none, but they might count as participants). Usually participants meaning those who solved at least one? Probably all participants (students who attempted). It's allowed to solve zero? Might be ok. But to minimize number of participants, we may want each student to solve as many problems as possible to cover the counts. However constraints about pair union limiting.\n\nWe need a set system of students: each student corresponds to a subset of {1..6}. For each problem i, exactly 1000 students have i in their subset. For any two subsets A,B, A ∪ B ≠ {1..6}. i.e., there exists at least one problem missing from both.\n\nGoal: minimize number of subsets (students) meeting those problem occurrence counts.\n\nWe need to find minimal size of family F of subsets of a 6-element set such that each element appears in exactly 1000 subsets, and the family has no pair whose union is whole set.\n\nLet’s think of complement: For each student, let missing set M = complement of solved problems. Then condition \"pair union not all 6\" means for any two students, there exists a problem not solved by either, i.e., there exists an element that is missing from both. Equivalently, for any two students, their missing sets intersect (i.e., share at least one problem they both missed). So the family of missing sets is an intersecting family (pairwise intersecting). Also each problem appears in missing sets for some numbers complement to 1000: total number of students N, each problem i is missed by N - 1000 students (since solved by 1000). So size of missing set family is N, each element appears in exactly N-1000 missing sets.\n\nThus find minimal N such that there exists an intersecting family of subsets of a 6-element set where each element appears in exactly N-1000 subsets. Additionally each missing set could be empty? If a student solved all problems, then missing set empty, but then pairwise intersect condition would fail because empty set intersects nothing (intersection with another empty set is empty). Actually intersecting family requires every pair have non-empty intersection; an empty set cannot intersect others unless all sets include empty which they don't. So missing sets cannot be empty (otherwise condition fails). Therefore each student solves at most 5 problems (at least one missing). So each missing set size ≥1.\n\nWe need to design intersecting family with uniform element frequencies (each element appears in exactly t = N-1000 members). We want minimal N.\n\nThis is related to intersecting families on 6 points. By Erdős–Ko–Rado, maximum size of intersecting family of k-subsets is ... but here sets can have varying sizes.\n\nWe need to find minimal N such that possible frequencies.\n\nSince each missing set size at least 1, and at most 5.\n\nLet’s denote a_i = number of students whose missing set is exactly {i} (size 1). Similarly for larger missing sets.\n\nIntersection condition: Any two missing sets intersect: they cannot be disjoint. So family is intersecting.\n\nOn a 6-element set, intersecting families maximal size? The trivial intersecting family is all subsets containing a fixed element (a star). That is intersecting. Could also include all subsets that contain at least two specific elements? But star is simplest.\n\nSo perhaps we can construct intersecting family as all subsets containing a particular element, say problem 1 missing. That means every missing set includes problem 1. Then they intersect at least at element1. That satisfies condition. Then each problem appears in missing sets: problem 1 appears in all N students, so frequency of problem1 missed is N. For other problems j>1, frequency depends on how many subsets also include j.\n\nBut we need each element appear exactly N-1000 times. So we can try to find such family.\n\nLet’s consider star centered at element 1: all missing sets are subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6} that include 1. Frequency: element1 appears in all N missing sets. So N must equal N-1000? Not possible unless 1000=0. So star not possible unless we add some sets not containing 1 to adjust frequency of element1.\n\nWe need each element to appear exactly t = N-1000 times. So element1 appears N times? Not allowed unless N = N-1000 => 1000=0 contradictory. So we need missing sets not all contain each element equally.\n\nThus we need a family where each element appears same number t. It's a regular intersecting family.\n\nWe need to find minimal N such that a regular intersecting family exists on 6 points with each element degree t = N-1000, and sets have size at least 1.\n\nObservation: For any intersecting family F on [6], its complement sets (solved) have property that any two have union ≠ [6]. Equivalent.\n\nWe need regular intersecting hypergraph.\n\nThis is similar to problem of constructing intersecting family with given degree. Let's think of using 2-element missing sets: size 2. If we use only size-2 missing sets that form a \"pairwise intersecting\" family. For 6 points, the largest intersecting family of 2-sets is size 5 (choose all 2-sets containing a fixed point). That's star again.\n\nBut we need regular degree. Maybe we can consider all 5 subsets missing exactly one problem (i.e., each student solved all but one problem). Missing set size 1. If we take each of the 6 possible singletons some number of times. Pairwise intersect condition: Two singleton missing sets are disjoint if they are different elements, because {i} ∩ {j}=∅ for i≠j. So can't have two different singleton missing sets. So at most one element can be used for singleton missing sets. So can't rely on many singletons.\n\nThus we need larger missing sets to guarantee intersecting.\n\nConsider using all missing sets of size at least 3 maybe? For intersecting, any two must intersect, but two disjoint of size 3 possible (complementary halves). So need to avoid disjoint pairs.\n\nA known construction: take all subsets of size > 3 (i.e., 4,5). Any two such subsets intersect because total size >6? Actually two subsets each of size 4 on a set of 6 have possible to be disjoint? No, two 4-sets must intersect because 4+4 >6, so intersection at least 2. Similarly 5-sets intersect. So family of all subsets of size at least 4 is intersecting.\n\nLet's consider using missing sets of size 4 or 5 (or 6? but 6 missing set means student solved none; can be allowed; then missing set = whole set size6; any two missing sets intersect obviously). Including size6 set also okay. So we can consider using all subsets of size >=4. Let's compute degrees.\n\nTotal number of subsets of size 4: C(6,4)=15. size5: C(6,5)=6. size6: C(6,6)=1. Total N = 22.\n\nNow compute frequency of each element in this family. For each element i, count subsets (size >=4) that contain i.\n\nNumber of subsets of size k containing i: C(5, k-1). So count:\n\nsize4 containing i: C(5,3)=10\nsize5 containing i: C(5,4)=5\nsize6 containing i: C(5,5)=1\nTotal per element = 10+5+1=16.\n\nThus each element appears in 16 missing sets. So t = 16, N =22. Then N-1000 = -978 (negative). Not matching requirement that each problem solved by exactly 1000 students, so N should be >1000.\n\nWe can scale up the construction by taking multiple copies of this family (i.e., replicating each subset multiple times). Since we just need counts of each element (problem) solved 1000 times, we can multiply.\n\nLet each missing set type have multiplicity m (same for all subsets). Then N = 22 m, each element appears in t = 16 m missing sets. Constraint: each problem is solved by 1000 students, i.e., number of students solving problem i = N - t = 22m -16m =6m. So we need 6m = 1000 → m = 1000/6 ≈ 166.666 not integer.\n\nThus cannot achieve exactly 1000 solved using uniform multiplicities.\n\nBut we can assign varying multiplicities to subsets to adjust frequencies per element while keeping intersecting property. Need each element appears N-1000 times. Let N be unknown, t = N-1000.\n\nSince degrees per element identical, we need regular intersecting hypergraph of size N, degree t.\n\nPotentially use design known as \"projective plane of order 2\"? That's 7 points etc. Not our case.\n\nMaybe we can think of complement: solved sets form family where any two have union not full set. Equivalent to missing sets intersecting. So we need intersecting family with given degree.\n\nWe can use the family of all subsets containing at least one of two fixed elements? That would be intersecting because any two subsets could miss both? Wait need intersect.\n\nIf we take all subsets that contain element 1, that's intersecting (star). Then degree of element1 is N (all subsets). Others have some arbitrary frequencies (could be less). To make regular we need each element appear equally. Not possible.\n\nThus need more balanced intersecting family. Balanced intersecting families are known as \"pairwise intersecting families\" with equal degrees: can be constructed as complements of \"pairwise covering families\"? hmm.\n\nAnother possibility: Use all subsets of size 3 that contain a fixed element? Size 3 subsets containing element1: count C(5,2)=10. Intersection property? Two such subsets each contain element1, thus intersect (at least element1). So family of 10 subsets size3 containing 1.\n\nCompute degree: element1 appears in all 10. Others appear each in C(4,1)=4? Wait for element j≠1, number of 3-sets containing both 1 and j? Choose remaining element from remaining 4: C(4,1)=4. So each other element appears 4 times. Not regular.\n\nCould combine multiple stars on each element to balance.\n\nWe can consider all subsets of size 3 that are not complementary pairs? Actually any two 3-sets can be disjoint (if they are complements). To avoid that, we can take all 3-sets that contain element1, plus all 3-sets that contain element2, etc, but then there could be a set containing 1 but not 2 and another set containing 2 but not 1 which intersect? They intersect maybe not if they are {1, a, b} and {2, c, d} could be disjoint if {a,b} ∩ {c,d}=∅ and a,b,c,d are distinct and not containing 1 or2. That's possible with 6 elements? Example: {1,3,4} and {2,5,6} are disjoint. So not intersecting.\n\nThus need more systematic.\n\nObservation: For any intersecting family, its complement family (of complements) is a covering family: every element belongs to at most? Not helpful.\n\nMaybe we can use linear programming to find minimal N: We need N such that there exists nonnegative integer vector x_S for each nonempty subset S (missing set) satisfying:\n\nFor each element i: sum_{S: i ∈ S} x_S = N-1000 = t.\n\nAnd intersecting condition: for any two subsets S,T with x_S>0, x_T>0, we must have S∩T ≠ ∅. That's hard.\n\nBut to minimize N, we want N as small as possible but must be >1000 because each problem solved by 1000, each student solves at most 5, so number of participants N must be at least 1000 (since each problem solved by 1000, a student could solve multiple problems). Could be less than 6000.\n\nWe suspect minimal N close to 1000 maybe? Let's consider lower bound.\n\nLet each student miss at least one problem, so solves at most 5. Total solves counts across all problems = sum over problems solved count = 6*1000 = 6000. Each student contributes at most 5 solves, so N ≥ ceil(6000/5)=1200.\n\nThus N>=1200.\n\nAlso each student must miss at least one, so total missed counts = sum over problems missed = N*? each problem missed N-1000 times, total missed counts = 6(N-1000) = 6N-6000. Also each student misses at least 1, so total missed counts ≥ N. So 6N-6000 ≥ N =>5N ≥6000 => N ≥1200 again. So lower bound 1200.\n\nNow can we achieve N=1200? Then each student misses average = total missed / N = (6N-6000)/N =6-6000/N =6-5 =1. So average missing size =1. Since each missing size ≥1, the only way average 1 is all missing sets size =1. So each student misses exactly one problem. Then each problem missed by N-1000 =1200-1000=200 students. So each problem has exactly 200 students missing it. That would mean the missing sets are exactly the 6 singleton types each with 200 copies. But then intersecting condition fails because two students missing different problems have disjoint missing sets, meaning their missing sets intersect empty, violating requirement. But requirement is that missing sets intersect (i.e., there is a problem both missed). With singletons of different problems they are disjoint, so not allowed. So we need some missing sets of larger size to ensure intersection.\n\nThus N must be greater than 1200.\n\nLet’s think of constructing family where most students miss exactly one problem, but need to avoid any pair of students missing different singleton problems. So perhaps we cannot have two different singleton missing sets coexisting. So at most one problem can have singleton missing sets. That suggests we must have many students missing at least 2 problems.\n\nLet’s consider we choose a distinguished problem, say problem 1, that many students miss only that problem (singleton {1}). Others must have missing sets that intersect with {1}, i.e., must also include 1. Therefore any other student missing set must contain 1. So essentially all missing sets must include problem 1, except the singleton {1} (which also includes 1). Thus the family is a star centered at 1. That satisfies intersection because any two subsets both contain 1. So we can have missing sets that are any subset containing 1 (size≥1). This is intersecting.\n\nNow compute degrees: element1 appears in all N missing sets, so t = N-1000 = N (since element1 appears in all). That gives N-1000 = N => 1000 =0 contradiction. So star centered at 1 cannot satisfy required degree for element1 (must appear N-1000 times, not N). Therefore we need to reduce appearances of element1; i.e., include missing sets not containing 1. But then intersecting condition would be violated unless those other sets intersect with each other and with 1-containing sets (they must intersect with each other, and also intersect with 1-containing sets, meaning they must contain some element that is also in the 1-containing sets). Since 1-containing sets all contain 1, to intersect with them, a set not containing 1 must contain at least one element also present in the other set; could be any element besides 1.\n\nThus we need more complex.\n\nConsider taking intersecting family formed by all subsets of size >=4 (as earlier), which is intersecting and regular (each element occurs 16 out of 22). This gave t/N = 16/22 ≈0.727. Our needed t = N-1000. So proportion t/N = 1 -1000/N. Since N will be > 1000, t/N = 1 -1000/N.\n\nWe can solve for N such that there exists intersecting family with degree ratio equal to that. For our family, ratio 16/22 ≈0.727. So we need 1 - 1000/N ≈0.727 => 1000/N ≈0.273 => N ≈ 3665. So maybe feasible N around 3665 using this base family scaled.\n\nLet's compute N minimal such that we can get integer counts achieving each element degree N-1000, using subsets sizes 4-6 duplication multiples plus maybe extra subsets to adjust degrees.\n\nGeneral method: We can use base intersecting family F0 that is regular (each element appears same number a). Suppose F0 size b, each element degree a. Then by taking m copies plus possibly adding some additional subsets that maintain intersecting and adjust degrees.\n\nThe base of all subsets size >=4 has a=16, b=22, ratio a/b = 16/22 =8/11.\n\nWe need a' = N-1000, b' = N, ratio a'/b' = (N-1000)/N = 1 - 1000/N.\n\nThus need ratio >=8/11? Because we can perhaps add more subsets that increase degree proportion? Let's examine adding a particular subset S (of some size) increases counts for elements in S by 1 and increases N by 1. This changes ratio. For a subset that contains k elements, degree increase per added element is 1 for those elements, others unchanged. So we can adjust degrees.\n\nGoal: find smallest N for which it's possible to have integer solution of degree equations with intersecting condition.\n\nThis seems like a combinatorial design problem. Could brute force small N? Not possible manually.\n\nMaybe the minimal N is 1500? Let's test feasibility. If N=1500, each element appears in t=500 missing sets. Since each missing set must intersect each other, we need a family of 1500 subsets, each element appears 500 times. Average missing size = total missing occurrences / N = 6*500 /1500 =2. So average missing size =2. So many missing sets of size 2.\n\nNeed intersecting family of size 1500 with each element degree 500, average block size 2. For intersecting families of size-2 sets (edges), maximum intersecting size is 5 (star). Can't get 1500.\n\nThus must include larger sizes.\n\nGiven lower bound N≥1200, we suspect minimal N maybe around 1800? Let's try N=1800, then t=800, average missing size = (6*800)/1800 = 2.666.\n\nCould design using all subsets containing at least 3 elements? Let's compute the family of all subsets size >=3. Count: C(6,3)=20, +15+6+1=42. N=42. Degree per element: subsets of size k containing i: sum_{k=3}^6 C(5,k-1) = C(5,2)+C(5,3)+C(5,4)+C(5,5)=10+10+5+1=26. So ratio a/b=26/42=13/21≈0.619. Need ratio 1-1000/N. For N=1800, ratio = 800/1800=0.444. That's lower than 0.619, meaning we need family where each element appears fewer times relative to total N. We can add subsets that are missing many elements (large missing sets) that increase N but increase degree less proportionally if they are small? Actually adding a missing set of size s adds 1 to N and adds 1 to degree of s elements. To lower ratio, we need subsets that contain few elements (small size). So we can add many singletons? But singletons increase degree of that particular element only, harming regularity.\n\nThus we could start with family of all subsets size >=4 (ratio 8/11~0.727) which is higher than needed 0.444, then we need to add many subsets of small size to bring down ratio? Adding a subset of size 1 adds degree to only that element, raising its degree more relative. That's opposite. Adding subset of size 5 adds degree to 5 elements (ratio 5/1=5 >). Adding size 2 adds degree to 2 elements. To lower global ratio, we need to add subsets with small size relative to N increase? Actually ratio = total degree sum / (6*N). Adding a subset of size s adds s to total degree sum, and N increases by 1. New ratio = (prev_sum + s)/(6(N+1)). To reduce ratio, we need s < current ratio*6. Since current ratio > desired, we need to add subsets with s less than current average missing size. Current average missing size = (total sum degrees)/N = 6*a/b = 6* (8/11) = 48/11 ≈4.364. Wait that's average missing size for family >=4 is between 4 and 6. Indeed average size = (4*15+5*6+6*1)/22 = (60+30+6)/22=96/22≈4.364. Adding subsets of size 2 or 3 reduces average size.\n\nThus we could start with base family of size 22 with each element degree 16, then add many subsets of size 2 (or 3) to bring average size down to around needed average for desired N.\n\nGoal N minimal, but still need regular degrees t = N-1000.\n\nLet's formulate unknown: let we have x_k copies of subsets of size k (with some pattern) such that intersecting condition holds. Hard.\n\nGiven complexity, maybe answer known: minimal participants is 1500? Let's test N=1500, average missing size =2. So need intersecting family average size 2. Is there intersecting family with average size 2? Yes, consider taking all subsets that contain element1 and have size 2 (i.e., pairs {1,i} for i=2..6). That's 5 subsets. Intersection each contains 1, good. Average size =2, degree of element1 =5, other elements each degree 1. Not regular.\n\nWe can replicate with permutations? For regularity each element must have degree 500. Could use all 5-element pairs? Not possible because each element appears in many subsets.\n\nI think the minimal N is 1800? Let's compute if we could construct regular intersecting family with N=1800 degree 800.\n\nTotal degree sum = 6*800 = 4800. Average missing size = 4800/1800 = 2.666. So we need family average size ~ 8/3.\n\nPossible by mixing size 3 and size 2 subsets. Suppose we have a family of all triples containing a fixed element? That's intersecting star with triples. Number of triples containing element1: C(5,2)=10. Degrees: element1 degree 10, other each degree 4. Not regular.\n\nMaybe we can use all 3-sets that contain at least 2 of a fixed set of 3 elements? Complexity.\n\nGiven time, maybe answer is 1200 but we earlier argued impossible due to singleton issue, but perhaps intersecting condition only says two students together have solved the 6 problems – meaning union not necessarily covering all 6. Wait maybe condition is \"in no case has it happened that two students together have solved the 6 problems.\" Means there is no pair of students such that the union of the problems they solved equals the set of all 6 problems. That's same as we used.\n\nIf each student solves exactly 5 problems (misses exactly 1), then any two students missing possibly different problems, their union might be all 6 if they miss complementary problems. For union to be all 6, they'd need to miss different problems perhaps? Actually if each solves 5, missing one each. Two students miss problems i and j. Their solved union includes all problems except possibly those both missed. Since each missed only one, if i≠j, then union covers all 6 (because student A solved all except i; student B solved all except j; together they solved everything). So that's prohibited. Therefore we cannot have two students missing different problems. So at most one problem can be missed by any student. So all students must miss the same problem. Then each problem solved count? If all miss same problem, then that problem solved by 0 students, can't be 1000. So not possible. Therefore at least some students miss more than one problem.\n\nThus N >1200.\n\nI suspect minimal N is 1800? Let's check condition: If each student misses exactly 2 problems, then each pair of students missing sets of size 2 must intersect, i.e., any two pairs must share at least one problem. This is equivalent to a family of 2-element subsets that is intersecting, maximum size is 5 (star). So can't have many.\n\nThus we need larger missing sets.\n\nMaybe minimal N is 3600? Because using family of size >=4 gives ratio 8/11; solving N such that N-1000 = (8/11)N => N - (8/11)N = 1000 => (3/11)N = 1000 => N = 1000 * 11/3 ≈ 3666.66, need integer multiple. Since we can multiply the base family, we can achieve N being multiple of 22. Let's set N = 22k. Then degree per element = 16k must equal N-1000 = 22k -1000 ⇒ 16k = 22k -1000 ⇒ 6k = 1000 ⇒ k = 1000/6 ≈166.666 not integer. So not exact.\n\nBut we can add extra subsets to adjust difference. Since 22k close to 3667.\n\nTake k=167 => N=22*167=3674. Then degree from base 16*167=2672. Need target degree t = N-1000 =2674. So short by 2. Need to add extra subsets to increase degree of each element by 2 total (i.e., each element degree need +2). Adding a missing set of size 6 (empty solved) increases degree of all 6 by1, raising N by1. So we could add 2 copies of the full set (size6). That adds 2 to N and 2 to each degree. New N = 3676, degree =2674? Wait compute: Starting N0=3674, degrees each 2672. Add 2 full sets: N=3676, each degree add 2 => 2674. Target degree = N-1000 = 2676? Actually N-1000 = 2676. Let's compute: With N=3676, N-1000=2676. Our degree after adding 2 full sets is 2674, still short by2. Need more.\n\nAdd one more full set gives N=3677, degrees each +1 =>2675, need N-1000=2677, still short 2. Hmm.\n\nWe need to increase degree more than N increase proportionally 1:1 using full set gives equality (adds 1 to each degree, adds 1 to N so N-1000 increases by1 as well). So full sets keep degree deficit constant? Let's compute.\n\nLet deficit d = (N-1000) - degree_i. Initially for base family: degree_i = (16/22)N = (8/11)N. So deficit d = N-1000 - (8/11)N = (3/11)N -1000.\n\nSet N = 22k. Then d = (3/11)(22k) -1000 = 6k -1000.\n\nWe need d=0. So k=1000/6 not integer. Thus we need N not multiple of 22, adjust with extra subsets that change ratio.\n\nLet’s consider adding some subsets of size s to adjust. Adding one subset of size s increases N by1, each of the s elements degree+1. So new deficit for those s elements changes by: new N-1000 = old N+1 -1000 = old N-1000 +1. Their degree increased by1, so deficit unchanged for those elements. For other 6-s elements, N-1000 increased by1 while degree unchanged, so deficit increased by1. So adding subsets of size s reduces deficit for elements in the subset relative to others.\n\nGoal is to reach zero deficit for all elements. Starting with base family where deficits are uniform positive (since N too low). We need to add subsets that contain each element equally to reduce deficits uniformly. Using full set (size6) reduces deficits uniformly to 0? Actually with full set, s=6, all elements get degree+1, N-1000+1, so deficits unchanged. So full set does not affect deficit. So need subsets that are not full to shift deficits.\n\nIf we add a missing set of size 5 (i.e., student solves exactly 1 problem). That adds degree to 5 elements, leaving one element without degree increase while N-1000 increases for all, thus deficit for that excluded element increases by1, while deficits for included elements unchanged. This creates imbalance. To eventually make all deficits zero, we need to design combination.\n\nThis is getting complex.\n\nGiven time, perhaps answer is 2000? Let's test N=2000, each element appears in t=1000 missing sets. Average missing size = (6*1000)/2000=3. So need intersecting family of average size 3. Could be constructed using all subsets of size >=3 (average size 3.619) plus some adjustments.\n\nMaybe minimal N is 2000 indeed.\n\nI think answer may be 2000.\n\nLet's output that.", "response": "ANSWER: 2000", "answer": 2000, "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -5.960463269616412e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999994039538507, "logp_correct": -5.960463269616412e-07, "cot_lp_mean": -0.60833438995843, "cot_lp_min64": -1.5733324560998412, "frac_below_-5": 0.010296603657599507, "digit_lp_min": -4.6642889976501465, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.7958146333662626, "n_cot_tokens": 6507, "lp_end": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -9.536738616588991e-07, "logp_nocot": -15.240661814808846, "p_nocot": 2.4047208740821825e-07, "uplift": 15.240661218762519}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec055vn3qEqKHHTc", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A large gene has dozens of exons, of which the central ones code for folded triple helical repeats that connect the cytoskeleton with sarcolemma and extracellular space. Each exon usually codes for one folded triple alpha helix. The most common mutations of the gene are central exon deletions that create out-of-frame peptides and progressive degenerative organ waste. A solution is to deliver a Morpholino that recognizes the 5' end of the out-of-frame exon in pre-mRNA. The molecule prevents binding of the spliceosome and creates exon skipping and in-frame joining. Several missing exons are well tolerated by an organism. Which structure below is not involved in the proposed therapy?\n\nA) R-loops\nB) polyA tail\nC) antisense\nD) lariat\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A large gene has dozens of exons, of which the central ones code for folded triple helical repeats that connect the cytoskeleton with sarcolemma and extracellular space. Each exon usually codes for one folded triple alpha helix. The most common mutations of the gene are central exon deletions that create out-of-frame peptides and progressive degenerative organ waste. A solution is to deliver a Morpholino that recognizes the 5' end of the out-of-frame exon in pre-mRNA. The molecule prevents binding of the spliceosome and creates exon skipping and in-frame joining. Several missing exons are well tolerated by an organism. Which structure below is not involved in the proposed therapy?\n\nA) R-loops\nB) polyA tail\nC) antisense\nD) lariat\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "rec055vn3qEqKHHTc:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 4928, 13, 7744, 1078, 108342, 1603, 156092, 177870, 484, 118020, 316, 220, 20, 6, 181783, 2702, 328, 448, 842, 13108, 97357, 174616, 316, 7075, 174616, 89472, 11, 20547, 306, 448, 306, 97357, 63989, 13, 623, 4928, 25, 21580, 8866, 4895, 382, 625, 9808, 306, 290, 17994, 17441, 30, 22483, 25, 355, 8, 460, 26299, 4645, 11, 418, 8, 14600, 32, 12742, 11, 363, 8, 108342, 1603, 11, 415, 8, 305, 46557, 364, 2167, 1309, 316, 11433, 1118, 328, 1879, 21207, 382, 625, 9808, 306, 290, 17441, 13, 623, 17441, 8844, 108342, 1603, 48995, 177870, 484, 18125, 181783, 2702, 11, 24297, 174616, 89472, 13, 623, 181783, 57339, 11526, 261, 305, 46557, 47388, 3354, 1014, 155297, 350, 112958, 1808, 553, 11906, 472, 305, 46557, 741, 2632, 305, 46557, 382, 9808, 306, 6078, 1014, 155297, 26, 48995, 177870, 18125, 1014, 155297, 328, 261, 4857, 174616, 11, 889, 1014, 155297, 2928, 21241, 11, 813, 305, 46557, 18704, 21241, 395, 1273, 12676, 1808, 26, 889, 382, 305, 46557, 16515, 9808, 306, 290, 17441, 30, 623, 17441, 20526, 52494, 1014, 155297, 26, 290, 181783, 57339, 11526, 261, 305, 46557, 47388, 395, 290, 3529, 5761, 174616, 30, 50557, 174616, 89472, 4376, 306, 89472, 328, 290, 6508, 174616, 11, 10915, 290, 12676, 1808, 1548, 46993, 484, 174616, 553, 1014, 136115, 4717, 11, 24168, 448, 12676, 263, 305, 46557, 591, 2454, 12676, 263, 30, 2604, 290, 12676, 263, 15683, 290, 28453, 174616, 382, 11906, 472, 261, 305, 46557, 30, 72015, 305, 46557, 8866, 382, 997, 328, 1014, 155297, 11, 813, 9808, 364, 49, 26299, 4645, 553, 59607, 11989, 9555, 32881, 21207, 20680, 3354, 66912, 1261, 290, 6575, 1939, 59607, 32881, 6370, 316, 290, 23224, 6894, 11, 15021, 261, 4590, 52983, 17107, 23224, 7967, 13, 4037, 8516, 162449, 306, 48995, 177870, 108342, 1603, 17441, 13, 2632, 6960, 6052, 382, 355, 8, 460, 26299, 4645, 11, 472, 1023, 553, 625, 997, 328, 290, 17441, 364, 41025, 32, 12742, 382, 261, 6286, 328, 284, 49212, 11, 625, 8516, 9808, 306, 1014, 155297, 889, 12331, 395, 27827, 326, 24005, 13, 623, 17441, 24615, 306, 876, 2126, 49212, 22614, 1014, 155297, 2254, 14600, 9519, 3643, 387, 30, 28763, 32, 12742, 1340, 413, 163614, 889, 625, 997, 328, 17441, 13, 3072, 1118, 1001, 382, 392, 2878, 9808, 69029, 17659, 460, 26299, 4645, 326, 14600, 32, 12742, 3572, 625, 413, 9808, 13, 5551, 48995, 177870, 118020, 316, 876, 2126, 49212, 26, 108342, 1603, 382, 8516, 9808, 350, 34, 741, 451, 46557, 382, 9808, 306, 1014, 155297, 13, 460, 26299, 4645, 553, 625, 19271, 17135, 13, 28763, 32, 12742, 1340, 1217, 625, 413, 9808, 889, 1014, 155297, 21241, 2254, 14600, 9519, 3643, 387, 11, 5495, 14600, 32, 12742, 382, 625, 6118, 395, 1014, 155297, 13, 623, 4928, 1340, 1682, 290, 6052, 484, 14600, 32, 12742, 382, 625, 9808, 306, 1014, 155297, 17441, 13, 21580, 382, 945, 120152, 30, 44130, 48995, 177870, 108342, 1603, 17441, 18125, 1014, 155297, 656, 17156, 316, 876, 2126, 49212, 181783, 6427, 26, 484, 23856, 108342, 1603, 17156, 11, 181783, 57339, 10674, 11, 14338, 2438, 11, 305, 46557, 18704, 26, 14600, 32, 12742, 382, 625, 997, 328, 1014, 155297, 26, 460, 26299, 4645, 553, 1217, 625, 997, 13, 3072, 460, 26299, 4645, 553, 1217, 96152, 13, 21580, 1001, 382, 945, 24525, 625, 9808, 30, 623, 14600, 32, 12742, 382, 945, 1078, 220, 18, 6, 1268, 12323, 11, 625, 1014, 155297, 26, 813, 6052, 418, 364, 58369, 28035, 117551, 25, 392, 34405, 8866, 4895, 382, 625, 9808, 306, 290, 17994, 17441, 16842, 623, 17441, 23856, 108342, 1603, 48995, 177870, 350, 786, 363, 382, 9808, 741, 2856, 7321, 57339, 738, 2928, 2501, 261, 305, 46557, 8866, 1934, 1014, 155297, 350, 786, 415, 9808, 741, 460, 26299, 4645, 25, 1879, 665, 1625, 5192, 316, 66912, 889, 625, 997, 328, 1014, 155297, 503, 48995, 177870, 1114, 13, 28763, 32, 12742, 1217, 625, 997, 328, 1014, 155297, 26, 889, 480, 382, 3333, 402, 284, 49212, 1934, 12323, 11, 625, 28453, 364, 149923, 177870, 118020, 316, 876, 2126, 49212, 2254, 14600, 9519, 3643, 387, 21241, 11, 889, 290, 14600, 32, 12742, 382, 5768, 4849, 1934, 1014, 155297, 11, 625, 8516, 9808, 13, 3072, 290, 4928, 1340, 2411, 484, 14600, 32, 12742, 382, 77671, 316, 181783, 89472, 13, 460, 26299, 4645, 1217, 77671, 13, 21580, 1001, 382, 945, 24525, 625, 9808, 30, 11048, 5846, 4928, 1481, 5230, 14600, 32, 12742, 364, 19048, 290, 17441, 382, 306, 290, 117323, 402, 876, 2126, 49212, 26, 460, 123903, 18704, 382, 261, 66912, 21408, 32881, 26, 625, 12331, 13, 28763, 32, 12742, 382, 1217, 625, 12331, 13, 54458, 413, 261, 28864, 25, 460, 26299, 4645, 1625, 1261, 108342, 1603, 3465, 16183, 32881, 750, 316, 59607, 540, 23224, 25, 49212, 176144, 30, 3072, 48995, 177870, 118020, 59607, 11, 625, 23224, 11, 813, 480, 2023, 350, 366, 480, 95853, 8, 889, 625, 19271, 13, 357, 2411, 6052, 25, 355, 8, 460, 26299, 4645, 364, 84787, 6052, 6011, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 355], "n_tokens": 833, "words": 600, "band_lo": 222.75, "band_hi": 891.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the question. It's about antisense Morpholino that binds to 5' splice site of an out-of-frame exon to cause exon skipping, resulting in an in-frame transcript. The question: Which structure below is not involved in the proposed therapy? Options: A) R-loops, B) polyA tail, C) antisense, D) lariat.\n\nWe need to determine which of these structures is not involved in the therapy. The therapy uses antisense morpholino that blocks splice site, causing exon skipping. The spliceosome forms a lariat intermediate during splicing (introns are removed as lariat). So lariat is involved in normal splicing; morpholino blocks splicing of a specific exon, but splicing still occurs, so lariat formation occurs for other introns; but is lariat specifically involved in the therapy? The therapy manipulates splicing; the spliceosome forms a lariat intermediate for the excised exon? Actually exon skipping results in skipping of the entire exon, meaning the introns flanking that exon are spliced together, producing an intron lariat from each intron? Or the intron containing the targeted exon is removed as a lariat? Nonetheless lariat structure is part of splicing, so involved.\n\nR-loops are RNA-DNA hybrid structures formed during transcription when the nascent RNA hybridizes to the DNA template, leaving a single-stranded DNA region. Not directly implicated in morpholino antisense therapy. So likely answer is A) R-loops, as they are not part of the therapy.\n\nPolyA tail is a feature of mRNA, not directly involved in splicing but relevant for stability and translation. The therapy acts in pre-mRNA nuclear splicing before polyadenylation? PolyA tail may be unaffected but not part of therapy. But which one is \"not involved\"? Both R-loops and polyA tail might not be involved. However morpholino binds to pre-mRNA; antisense is directly involved (C). Lariat is involved in splicing. R-loops are not typical participants. PolyA tail may also not be involved but splicing occurs before polyadenylation, though polyA tail is not needed for splicing. The question may want the answer that polyA tail is not involved in splicing therapy. Which is more plausible? Usually morpholino antisense therapy blocks splicing by binding to pre-mRNA splice sites; that involves antisense binding, spliceosome components, branch point, lariat formation; polyA tail is not part of splicing; R-loops are also not part. But R-loops are also unrelated. Which one is more obviously not involved? The polyA tail is more about 3' end processing, not splicing; so answer B.\n\nLet's examine wording: \"Which structure below is not involved in the proposed therapy?\" The therapy involves antisense morpholino (so C is involved). Spliceosome will still create a lariat structure after splicing (so D involved). R-loops: these can form due to transcription but not part of splicing or morpholino function. PolyA tail also not part of splicing; but it is present on mRNA after processing, not targeted.\n\nMorpholino binds to pre-mRNA before polyadenylation occurs, but the polyA tail is added later after splicing, not directly involved. But the question may think that polyA tail is irrelevant to splice skipping. R-loops also irrelevant. Which one is more obviously not involved? Many exam question would pick polyA tail.\n\nSince the therapy is in the nucleus on pre-mRNA; R-loop formation is a transcription-related hybrid; not relevant. PolyA tail is also not relevant. Might be a trick: R-loops form when antisense oligos hybridize to RNA at DNA:RNA hybrids? But morpholino binds RNA, not DNA, so it could (if it crosses) but not typical. I think answer: A) R-loops.\n\nThus answer format: ANSWER: A", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.0265558760002023e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999105, "logp_correct": -8.95207760671808e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000895, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.8066409643140396, "cot_lp_min64": -1.327631428223496, "frac_below_-5": 0.018292682926829267, "digit_lp_min": -6.9141146923357155e-06, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.31339097023010254, "n_cot_tokens": 820, "lp_end": -0.18761199712753296, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -6.501921490062274, "p_nocot": 0.0015005531231742336, "uplift": 6.501921490062184, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.001501, "B": 0.998081, "C": 0.000261, "D": 0.000158}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
