291, 8, 503, 220, 24, 350, 45235, 741, 25767, 945, 5377, 11, 10112, 6052, 382, 220, 19, 13, 20, 364, 87471, 10112, 1354, 382, 5542, 6301, 25, 1851, 47221, 14376, 314, 220, 1353, 820, 350, 16, 659, 220, 702, 32612, 79, 39, 533, 275, 28611, 8784, 1843, 275, 28611, 10112, 220, 22, 30, 4037, 364, 39520, 290, 9913, 5377, 11, 12951, 1023, 2665, 2360, 6368, 14376, 18051, 483, 275, 39, 326, 20690, 25, 275, 39, 314, 533, 2133, 14684, 1174, 64918, 2214, 220, 1723, 4, 14376, 11, 275, 39, 314, 533, 2133, 7, 15, 13, 19, 25987, 15, 13, 39121, 24, 350, 2878, 306, 3880, 741, 2214, 220, 2744, 4, 14376, 11, 275, 39, 21714, 2133, 15, 13, 24, 28, 15, 13, 42047, 23, 364, 34013, 275, 39, 9809, 25, 26143, 79, 39, 314, 533, 2133, 7, 15, 13, 19, 14, 15, 13, 24, 25987, 15, 13, 31083, 871, 625, 364, 34013, 21700, 2973, 472, 7848, 25, 350, 15, 13, 42047, 23, 10, 15, 13, 39121, 24, 11876, 17, 28, 15, 13, 22687, 24, 33956, 275, 39, 220, 15, 13, 1709, 625, 364, 84787, 10112, 1023, 19429, 275, 39, 35353, 251, 14376, 11, 22573, 220, 15, 12, 1353, 4, 316, 220, 15, 12, 1265, 9318, 25, 275, 39, 314, 350, 68396, 14, 1353, 11043, 1265, 13, 2214, 220, 2744, 4, 2747, 275, 39, 28, 899, 13, 21, 350, 2878, 741, 2214, 220, 1723, 4, 2747, 275, 39, 28, 20, 13, 21, 350, 8722, 316, 220, 19, 13, 20, 30, 3991, 7943, 1721, 20402, 275, 39, 10112, 1934, 12673, 2127, 11, 4122, 402, 220, 1723, 4, 6290, 14376, 26, 275, 39, 314, 350, 68396, 14, 1353, 11043, 1265, 314, 220, 20, 13, 21, 13, 57272, 376, 5317, 382, 220, 19, 13, 20, 10112, 364, 84787, 6052, 418, 8, 220, 19, 13, 20, 364, 67504, 810, 483, 418, 13], "n_tokens": 913, "words": 379, "band_lo": 265.0, "band_hi": 1060.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have a question: \"A pH-responsive reaction proceeding at STP showed a 90 % theoretical yield. The addition of a substance ‘X’ resulted in a 40 % actual yield. The final estimated pH of the system will be\n\nA) 2.3\nB) 4.5\nC) 9.0\nD) 7.0\"\n\nWe need to interpret. pH-responsive reaction: yield changes with pH? Perhaps the yield is proportional to something with pH. The theoretical yield is 90% (maybe at optimal pH). Adding substance X reduces actual yield to 40%, meaning pH shift. Need to estimate pH. Might be using Henderson-Hasselbalch? Or maybe the relationship between yield and pH is linear? Might be a known trick: The factor of yield drop from 90 to 40: ratio 40/90 = 0.444... Assuming yield ∝ [H+]? pH is -log[H+]. If yield proportional to [H+], then [H+] = yield proportion. At optimal, yield 90% corresponds to something? At STP pH maybe neutral 7? If yield 90% at pH maybe? Not sure.\n\nProbably a puzzle: theoretical yield 90% => pH maybe 1 (since 90%?). Adding X reduces to 40% => pH maybe 3? The options are 2.3, 4.5, 9.0, 7.0.\n\nCalculate pH using log: pH = -log10(yield fraction). For 90% theoretical yield = 0.9 => pH = -log10(0.9)=0.0458. Not match. For 40% actual =0.4 => pH =0.398. Not match.\n\nMaybe pH change is given by log of ratio: pH = -log (new yield / old yield) = -log(0.4/0.9)= -log(0.444)=0.352. Not.\n\nMaybe pH is average: (2.3+4.5+9+7)/4=5.7. Not.\n\nPerhaps the answer known: 4.5? Not sure.\n\nCould be that theoretical yield 90% corresponds to pH 2.3? Actual 40% corresponds to pH 7? Use Henderson-Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A-]/[HA]). If adding X reduces yield means shift to more acidic (or basic). Which option plausible? 2.3 is acidic, 9 basic.\n\nIf original yield high (90%) maybe near optimal pH maybe 7.0 giving high yield? Adding X reduces to 40% maybe shift away, maybe pH becomes 2.3 (acidic) or 9 (basic). Without more info, maybe answer is 4.5.\n\nActually maybe there is known relationship: % theoretical yield = 100 / (1 + 10^(pH - pKa)). If pKa maybe 7? Not.\n\nGiven the limited info, perhaps they expect using percent yield ratio with pH and formula: pH = -log(yield%). For 40% yield, pH = -log(0.4)=0.3979 (not in options). For 90% yield, pH=-log0.9=0.0458.\n\nMaybe pH difference: ΔpH = -log(0.4/0.9)=0.352 => not.\n\nMaybe combine both as average: (0.0458+0.3979)/2=0.2219 approx pH 0.22 not.\n\nThus maybe they assume pH ∝ yield, mapping 0-100% to 0-14 scale: pH = (yield/100)*14. For 90% -> pH=12.6 (not). For 40% -> pH=5.6 (close to 4.5?).\n\nBut final estimated pH maybe after adding X, based on 40% actual yield; pH = (yield/100)*14 = 5.6. Closest option is 4.5 maybe.\n\nThus answer B) 4.5.\n\nI'll go with B.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.4689302992774174e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999797, "logp_correct": -2.028214869428713e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000202, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6410867911016545, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1008319941452056, "frac_below_-5": 0.016666666666666666, "digit_lp_min": -4.584985256195068, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.43047580122947693, "n_cot_tokens": 900, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -0.578570663865591, "p_nocot": 0.5606992217416057, "uplift": 0.5785706638655707, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.097435, "B": 0.560699, "C": 0.001785, "D": 0.340081}}, "force_keep": false, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec2K9NTFNw04xewq", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Scientist 1 is studying linkage maps in Drosophila. Specifically, Scientist 1 is working out the linkage between 3 genes in one cross, also known as a three-point testcross. The genes under study are V, CV, and CT. To obtain the required information a trihybrid female and a tester male (triple recessive male) are crossed. Analyzing the information from this cross, the genetic mapping and the genetic map units (m.u.) read as follows:\n\nV - - CT - CV\nV -> CV: 18.5%\nV -> CT: 13.2%\nCV -> CT: 6.4 %\n\nScientist 1 questioned the data, asking, \"Why was the addition of V -> CT and CV -> CT (13.2% + 6.4%) greater than the m.u. for V -> CV (18.5%)?\n\nA) Erred loci placement\nB) Recombinant interference\nC) The gene order was reversed\nD) A double crossover event\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Scientist 1 is studying linkage maps in Drosophila. Specifically, Scientist 1 is working out the linkage between 3 genes in one cross, also known as a three-point testcross. The genes under study are V, CV, and CT. To obtain the required information a trihybrid female and a tester male (triple recessive male) are crossed. Analyzing the information from this cross, the genetic mapping and the genetic map units (m.u.) read as follows:\n\nV - - CT - CV\nV -> CV: 18.5%\nV -> CT: 13.2%\nCV -> CT: 6.4 %\n\nScientist 1 questioned the data, asking, \"Why was the addition of V -> CT and CV -> CT (13.2% + 6.4%) greater than the m.u. for V -> CV (18.5%)?\n\nA) Erred loci placement\nB) Recombinant interference\nC) The gene order was reversed\nD) A double crossover event\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "rec2K9NTFNw04xewq:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 290, 4928, 25, 730, 3407, 37909, 1746, 52400, 11, 581, 679, 48294, 25, 631, 2747, 16178, 25, 220, 1157, 13, 20, 14906, 631, 2747, 24286, 25, 220, 1311, 13, 17, 14906, 16178, 2747, 24286, 25, 220, 21, 13, 19, 15678, 122816, 5359, 4436, 5856, 328, 631, 2747, 24286, 326, 16178, 2747, 24286, 350, 1311, 13, 17, 4, 659, 220, 21, 13, 19, 4, 314, 220, 858, 13, 21, 18929, 10740, 1572, 290, 284, 22262, 13, 395, 631, 2747, 16178, 350, 1157, 13, 20, 64918, 1328, 24832, 484, 4014, 9324, 2870, 631, 326, 16178, 350, 1157, 13, 20, 8, 382, 3760, 1572, 4215, 328, 48294, 2870, 631, 12, 1895, 326, 24286, 10050, 53, 350, 1311, 13, 17, 10, 21, 13, 19, 28, 858, 13, 21, 741, 730, 7937, 23876, 2569, 11, 3609, 9324, 631, 12, 1895, 659, 24286, 10050, 53, 1757, 12629, 631, 10050, 53, 9324, 538, 290, 2569, 382, 631, 12, 1895, 10050, 53, 503, 16178, 12, 1895, 19674, 11, 889, 2105, 4275, 16132, 10740, 25, 5192, 316, 3503, 8088, 21115, 625, 2447, 83982, 30, 50557, 11, 306, 3407, 37909, 22573, 11, 290, 3609, 9324, 2870, 25982, 34553, 350, 53, 326, 16178, 8, 29702, 4215, 328, 48294, 328, 49900, 350, 53, 12, 1895, 2932, 24286, 10050, 53, 8, 40335, 3503, 8088, 21115, 30, 24305, 19271, 25, 2214, 3407, 184088, 355, 11, 418, 11, 363, 483, 2569, 355, 8287, 10050, 11, 4014, 9324, 16520, 314, 1851, 19119, 65, 659, 32540, 3503, 8088, 8, 350, 21910, 3503, 8088, 2870, 16520, 326, 28308, 382, 60500, 472, 2893, 8618, 639, 171164, 395, 25982, 184088, 741, 623, 9324, 18577, 314, 1851, 19119, 65, 350, 26167, 19042, 2870, 355, 326, 363, 2932, 3503, 8088, 741, 3072, 290, 4215, 328, 16520, 659, 28308, 314, 18577, 659, 1851, 3503, 8088, 13, 2632, 4215, 328, 49900, 1424, 25982, 9324, 5192, 316, 3503, 8088, 29298, 13, 623, 16968, 40519, 3503, 8088, 19813, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 61125, 30, 50557, 3503, 8088, 19813, 741, 623, 6052, 6960, 415, 25, 355, 3503, 85119, 2104, 13, 5551, 11, 290, 4928, 25, 392, 13903, 673, 290, 5856, 328, 631, 2747, 24286, 326, 16178, 2747, 24286, 350, 1311, 13, 17, 4, 659, 220, 21, 13, 19, 18929, 10740, 1572, 290, 284, 22262, 13, 395, 631, 2747, 16178, 350, 1157, 13, 20, 18929, 16842, 21926, 2236, 3503, 8088, 21115, 553, 60500, 18370, 350, 21986, 306, 2454, 17116, 8, 889, 625, 306, 290, 25982, 17116, 11, 8117, 290, 4215, 316, 413, 12384, 13, 2632, 415, 364, 7943, 2371, 3880, 25, 355, 8, 4225, 1291, 184088, 29002, 11, 418, 8, 176799, 171164, 61125, 11, 363, 8, 623, 19546, 2569, 673, 57184, 11, 415, 8, 355, 3503, 85119, 2104, 13, 623, 6145, 30547, 382, 3503, 85119, 15286, 316, 150774, 328, 25982, 9324, 30, 50557, 25982, 9324, 350, 53, 316, 16178, 8, 314, 3609, 19119, 180524, 2870, 1373, 6664, 4590, 8088, 21115, 2870, 631, 12, 1895, 326, 24286, 10050, 53, 2932, 3503, 8088, 21115, 26, 6726, 3503, 8088, 21115, 10635, 19119, 180524, 395, 25982, 34553, 11, 813, 1023, 553, 60500, 13, 24305, 19271, 25, 730, 3407, 37909, 1746, 52400, 11, 19119, 180524, 19813, 2870, 25982, 34553, 6664, 4590, 8088, 21115, 306, 4784, 17116, 326, 3503, 8088, 21115, 350, 21910, 1023, 19119, 22066, 25982, 34553, 741, 2632, 9324, 2870, 25982, 34553, 314, 4590, 8088, 21115, 306, 4784, 17116, 659, 3503, 8088, 21115, 13, 623, 4215, 328, 49900, 350, 53, 12, 1895, 659, 24286, 10050, 53, 8, 28991, 4590, 8088, 21115, 2454, 17116, 11, 889, 3503, 8088, 21115, 553, 60500, 306, 2973, 49900, 350, 21910, 1023, 5659, 306, 2973, 49900, 8, 13972, 4215, 314, 25982, 9324, 659, 3503, 85119, 19813, 13, 2632, 4215, 10740, 2236, 3503, 8088, 21115, 13, 2632, 6052, 415, 364, 84787, 6011, 25, 461, 160761, 25, 415, 6120], "n_tokens": 636, "words": 411, "band_lo": 162.5, "band_hi": 650.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse the question: In three-point testcross, we have distances: V -> CV: 18.5%, V -> CT: 13.2%, CV -> CT: 6.4%. Scientist questions why addition of V -> CT and CV -> CT (13.2% + 6.4% = 19.6%) greater than the m.u. for V -> CV (18.5%). This indicates that map distance between V and CV (18.5) is less than sum of distances between V-CT and CT-CV (13.2+6.4=19.6). In proper linear order, total distance V-CT + CT-CV should equal V-CV distance if the order is V-CT-CV or CV-CT-V, but here it's slightly greater: due to double crossovers not being accounted? Actually, in three-point mapping, the total distance between outer genes (V and CV) equals sum of distances of intervals (V-CT plus CT-CV) minus double crossovers? Wait typical: For three loci A, B, C with order A-B-C, map distance AB = % recomb + (% double cross) (since double cross between AB and BC is counted as non-recombinant for outer loci). The distance AC = % recomb (including singles between A and C plus double cross). But the sum of AB + BC = AC + % double cross. So sum of intervals > outer distance due to double cross contributions. The excess reflects double cross frequency (i.e., interference? Actually double cross frequency). The answer likely D: A double crossover event. However, the question: \"Why was the addition of V -> CT and CV -> CT (13.2% + 6.4%) greater than the m.u. for V -> CV (18.5%)?\" That's because double crossovers are counted twice (once in each interval) but not in the outer interval, leading the sum to be larger. So D.\n\nBut check options: A) Erred loci placement, B) Recombinant interference, C) The gene order was reversed, D) A double crossover event. The correct explanation is double crossover leads to underestimate of outer distance? Actually outer distance (V to CV) = total recombination between them includes single crossovers between V-CT and CT-CV plus double crossovers; however double crossovers produce recombination for outer genes, so they are counted. Wait typical: In three-point testcross, recombination frequency between outer genes includes single crossovers in either interval and double crossovers (since they recombine outer genes). So distance between outer genes = single crossovers in either interval + double crossovers. The sum of intervals (V-CT + CT-CV) counts single crossovers each interval, but double crossovers are counted in both intervals (since they happen in both intervals) thus sum = outer distance + double crossover frequency. So sum greater because double crossovers. So answer D.\n\nThus format: 'ANSWER: D'.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.88757495986647e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999436, "logp_correct": -5.6331414972763576e-14, "letter_mass": 0.999997735028621, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.613535535642378, "cot_lp_min64": -0.8920493760272272, "frac_below_-5": 0.008025682182985553, "digit_lp_min": -1.2371017932891846, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.10252219438552856, "n_cot_tokens": 623, "lp_end": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -2.127766158333599, "p_nocot": 0.11910305427377765, "uplift": 2.1277661583335425, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.000803, "B": 0.880059, "C": 3.5e-05, "D": 0.119103}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec2UlKqC6RFHdcro", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: The universe is filled with the Cosmic Microwave Background. Consider the annihilation of high energy \\gamma-rays with a photon from the CMB Radiation into electron-positron, i.e. $\\gamma\\gamma\\rightarrow e^{+}e^{-}$. From what energy \\gamma-rays would have their lifetimes in the universe limited by this process? Knowing that the average photon energy of the CMB is $10^{-3}eV$.\n\nA) 3.9*1e5 GeV\nB) 1.8*1e5 GeV\nC) 2.6*1e5 GeV\nD) 9.5*1e4 GeV\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: The universe is filled with the Cosmic Microwave Background. Consider the annihilation of high energy \\gamma-rays with a photon from the CMB Radiation into electron-positron, i.e. $\\gamma\\gamma\\rightarrow e^{+}e^{-}$. From what energy \\gamma-rays would have their lifetimes in the universe limited by this process? Knowing that the average photon energy of the CMB is $10^{-3}eV$.\n\nA) 3.9*1e5 GeV\nB) 1.8*1e5 GeV\nC) 2.6*1e5 GeV\nD) 9.5*1e4 GeV\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "rec2UlKqC6RFHdcro:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 1646, 290, 24313, 5954, 395, 40570, 22420, 316, 10635, 319, 10, 68, 12, 483, 363, 13874, 135911, 13, 12482, 8558, 659, 8558, 2747, 319, 10, 319, 23609, 623, 24313, 7060, 25, 265, 5064, 220, 19, 284, 4618, 61, 17, 274, 61, 19, 11, 1919, 265, 314, 220, 17, 457, 2728, 4278, 350, 16, 533, 8974, 4114, 8, 306, 9887, 7298, 350, 21116, 5954, 40570, 6110, 7069, 483, 4465, 110057, 135911, 741, 2214, 3189, 13237, 100572, 350, 3228, 936, 12962, 314, 3478, 11, 813, 350, 16, 533, 8974, 4114, 25987, 17, 13, 2632, 265, 314, 220, 17, 457, 2728, 4278, 425, 220, 17, 314, 220, 19, 457, 2728, 4278, 13, 24305, 25120, 25, 2214, 1920, 187122, 483, 70824, 457, 16, 326, 457, 17, 326, 16538, 12962, 2870, 1373, 11, 290, 120331, 265, 314, 220, 17, 457, 16, 457, 17, 350, 16, 533, 8974, 4114, 741, 2632, 24313, 25, 220, 17, 457, 2728, 4278, 350, 16, 533, 8974, 4114, 8, 5064, 350, 17, 284, 4618, 274, 61, 17, 73602, 17, 314, 220, 19, 284, 4618, 61, 17, 274, 61, 19, 13, 2214, 3189, 13237, 11, 8974, 4114, 314, 533, 16, 11, 813, 220, 16, 533, 8974, 4114, 314, 220, 17, 11, 13972, 220, 17, 457, 2728, 4278, 425, 220, 17, 314, 220, 19, 457, 2728, 4278, 5064, 220, 19, 284, 4618, 61, 17, 274, 61, 19, 15155, 457, 2728, 4278, 5064, 284, 4618, 61, 17, 274, 61, 19, 364, 72816, 24313, 40570, 5954, 457, 2728, 16038, 314, 284, 4618, 61, 17, 274, 61, 19, 820, 4278, 13, 7649, 284, 4618, 274, 61, 17, 314, 220, 15, 13, 33539, 2896, 53, 314, 220, 20, 13, 994, 68, 20, 319, 53, 13, 22980, 25, 350, 20, 13, 994, 68, 20, 319, 53, 73602, 17, 314, 220, 17, 13, 8954, 68, 994, 319, 53, 61, 17, 13, 105916, 656, 4278, 350, 34, 13874, 135911, 7848, 5954, 220, 16, 68, 12, 18, 319, 53, 741, 2632, 457, 2728, 16038, 314, 220, 17, 13, 8954, 68, 994, 820, 220, 16, 68, 12, 18, 314, 220, 17, 13, 8954, 68, 1265, 319, 53, 314, 220, 17, 13, 8954, 68, 20, 5053, 53, 13, 21926, 1078, 220, 17, 13, 21, 33839, 220, 702, 61, 20, 5053, 53, 364, 84787, 6052, 363, 364, 4383, 4335, 3880, 25, 363, 8, 220, 17, 13, 21, 9, 16, 68, 20, 5053, 53, 15248, 364, 84787, 6052, 364, 2167, 3572, 7477, 4275, 290, 24313, 13, 3604, 70824, 5151, 484, 11, 290, 4774, 2240, 3104, 14081, 3291, 11223, 316, 53432, 11, 58810, 24644, 364, 72816, 6052, 25, 363, 364], "n_tokens": 440, "words": 178, "band_lo": 78.75, "band_hi": 315.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to find the threshold energy for gamma ray to produce e+e- with CMB photon. Process γ + γ -> e+ e-. The threshold condition: s >= 4 m_e^2 c^4, where s = 2 Eγ ε (1 - cosθ) in lab frame (high energy gamma colliding with low-energy photon). For head-on collisions (max), θ = π, so (1 - cosθ)=2. So s = 2 Eγ ε * 2 = 4 Eγ ε. Wait careful: For two photons with energies E1 and E2 and angle θ between them, the invariant s = 2 E1 E2 (1 - cosθ). So threshold: 2 Eγ ε (1 - cosθ) >= (2 m_e c^2)^2 = 4 m_e^2 c^4. For head-on, cosθ = -1, so 1 - cosθ = 2, thus 2 Eγ ε * 2 = 4 Eγ ε >= 4 m_e^2 c^4 → Eγ ε >= m_e^2 c^4.\n\nTherefore threshold gamma energy Eγ_th = m_e^2 c^4 / ε. Use m_e c^2 = 0.511 MeV = 5.11e5 eV. Square: (5.11e5 eV)^2 = 2.61e11 eV^2. Divide by ε (CMB photon average energy 1e-3 eV). So Eγ_th = 2.61e11 / 1e-3 = 2.61e14 eV = 2.61e5 GeV. That's about 2.6 × 10^5 GeV.\n\nThus answer C.\n\nCheck given options: C) 2.6*1e5 GeV matches.\n\nThus answer.\n\nWe might note it's the threshold. At energies above that, the mean free path becomes small compared to Universe, limiting lifetime.\n\nTherefore answer: C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.5377881929623527e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999988, "logp_correct": -1.233268628901702e-15, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807907259, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.37055196718392924, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9017092827367312, "frac_below_-5": 0.00234192037470726, "digit_lp_min": -0.5231297016143799, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.03925781324505806, "n_cot_tokens": 427, "lp_end": -0.06682723015546799, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -2.6532990665209133, "p_nocot": 0.07041851406454824, "uplift": 2.653299066520912, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.857873, "B": 0.070419, "C": 0.070419, "D": 0.00129}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec2ZTsqazBZ3LTWZ", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: You perform a high-throughput experiment on white lupine to find genes contributing to resistance to the fungal disease anthracnose. As a result, you receive three candidate genes of unknown function – G1, G2, and G3. You create three knock-out mutants, g1, g2, and g3, and a set of double-mutants, g1g2, g1g3, and g2g3. You know that at least one of these genes is a transcription factor acting upstream of (an)other gene(s). You start to test those mutant plants: do they have a higher sensitivity to anthracnose than the wild-type because they cannot produce certain gene products? \nAfter tests with the pathogen, you receive the following results where 100% is the level of resistance to the pathogen in control; 50% is half of the control’s resistance; 25% is a quarter of the control’s resistance; 0% ‒ all plants show signs of infection:\n- resistance of g1: 75% of control\n- resistance of g2: 0% from control\n- resistance of g3: 50% from control\n-resistance of g1g3: 10% from control\n- resistance of g2g3: 0% from control\n- resistance of g1g2: 0% from control\n\nWhich conclusion regarding those genes' interaction can you draw from this experiment?\n\nA) G1 is a transcription factor, G2 and G3 show pleiotropy, G2 is epistatic towards G1\nB) G2 is a transcription factor, G1 and G3 has the same promoter, G3 is epistatic towards G1\nC) G2 is a transcription factor, G1 and G3 show pleiotropy, G1 is epistatic towards G3\nD) G2 is a transcription factor, G1 and G3 show gene redundancy, G1 is epistatic towards G3\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: You perform a high-throughput experiment on white lupine to find genes contributing to resistance to the fungal 