013, 290, 4928, 47384, 6052, 355, 2236, 1023, 2411, 499, 16, 382, 49743, 78314, 328, 499, 17, 326, 499, 18, 30, 41021, 322, 7863, 28899, 483, 484, 39300, 364, 3335, 499, 16, 49743, 78314, 11, 1815, 6266, 328, 499, 16, 1481, 10389, 85328, 12973, 328, 499, 17, 326, 499, 18, 13, 25377, 329, 16, 4590, 7398, 220, 3384, 4, 20918, 350, 25231, 54782, 936, 24832, 10112, 499, 16, 45298, 3759, 13, 5551, 3503, 329, 16, 70, 17, 382, 220, 15, 350, 21910, 499, 17, 8803, 936, 329, 16, 70, 18, 382, 220, 702, 350, 71992, 12421, 741, 1843, 499, 16, 78314, 11, 1815, 329, 16, 70, 17, 3503, 1340, 413, 6771, 316, 329, 17, 11238, 350, 15, 8, 2236, 85328, 499, 17, 6266, 40028, 78314, 30, 730, 61537, 11, 85328, 19546, 382, 5966, 421, 2351, 316, 78314, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 85328, 40028, 78314, 741, 2632, 499, 17, 5966, 421, 2351, 316, 499, 16, 11, 1118, 15248, 355, 25, 392, 38, 17, 382, 5966, 421, 2351, 9979, 499, 16, 4050, 21926, 1343, 13, 623, 1273, 21937, 46235, 25, 499, 17, 12421, 11, 499, 18, 220, 1434, 11, 329, 16, 70, 18, 220, 702, 13, 1843, 499, 16, 78314, 11, 329, 18, 85328, 30, 1843, 499, 18, 85328, 328, 499, 16, 11, 1815, 3503, 329, 16, 70, 18, 1481, 1631, 1299, 329, 18, 350, 1434, 741, 4037, 3981, 13, 2632, 625, 364, 84787, 6052, 6960, 625, 1062, 4335, 30, 30456, 413, 261, 28864, 25, 5655, 415, 472, 1636, 2236, 499, 17, 49743, 11, 499, 16, 326, 499, 18, 2356, 170540, 350, 72620, 936, 499, 16, 5966, 421, 2351, 316, 499, 18, 350, 62832, 1023, 3331, 484, 3503, 21937, 432, 143766, 499, 16, 2236, 220, 702, 4, 6574, 5263, 316, 220, 15, 11, 625, 220, 3384, 11, 889, 10112, 1023, 18135, 220, 702, 4, 472, 5862, 220, 15, 11, 165324, 499, 16, 85328, 328, 499, 18, 30, 186402, 3991, 58369, 23864, 25, 1843, 499, 16, 5966, 421, 2351, 316, 499, 18, 11, 3503, 152720, 314, 499, 16, 350, 3384, 741, 4037, 364, 3335, 499, 18, 5966, 421, 2351, 350, 9995, 418, 936, 3503, 314, 220, 1434, 13, 4037, 364, 3335, 499, 17, 49743, 78314, 11, 5966, 421, 2351, 316, 2973, 26, 889, 625, 13808, 364, 84787, 10112, 290, 15663, 6052, 382, 392, 12851, 328, 290, 5151, 672, 889, 625, 4335, 364, 34013, 581, 4694, 37352, 290, 27179, 392, 789, 62585, 34271, 1, 10915, 484, 261, 4590, 19546, 49166, 7598, 42908, 11, 1118, 2023, 16644, 4436, 4445, 27082, 16513, 679, 13664, 9327, 13, 499, 16, 326, 499, 18, 2356, 5590, 62585, 34271, 350, 21309, 7902, 7598, 85328, 34553, 741, 730, 484, 1890, 5966, 9435, 276, 2870, 499, 16, 326, 499, 18, 10112, 625, 12331, 13, 17796, 363, 5003, 499, 16, 5966, 421, 2351, 9979, 499, 18, 11, 889, 625, 20395, 364, 39520, 89229, 11, 10112, 5230, 5317, 415, 2236, 480, 66396, 170540, 11, 1118, 161470, 483, 113232, 3159, 328, 3503, 107323, 2447, 19256, 1572, 2454, 4590, 11, 34700, 1023, 45298, 87064, 13, 1958, 499, 16, 5966, 421, 2351, 9979, 499, 18, 30, 3072, 10112, 1023, 2411, 5966, 9435, 276, 665, 413, 16817, 1606, 1261, 3503, 853, 6931, 1572, 2973, 350, 106048, 15068, 741, 4037, 19271, 364, 125072, 11, 1309, 6052, 13, 18754, 5655, 5317, 415, 472, 1645, 120152, 25, 499, 17, 49743, 11, 499, 16, 326, 499, 18, 95984, 85328, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 415, 13], "n_tokens": 3248, "words": 2011, "band_lo": 712.0, "band_hi": 2848.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret experiment.\n\nWe have genes G1, G2, G3 unknown function. At least one is a transcription factor (TF) acting upstream of other gene(s). Knockouts g1 etc.\n\nResistance levels (percentage of wild-type control). Let's denote WT =100% resistance.\n\nData:\n\ng1: 75% (mild reduction)\ng2: 0% (complete loss)\ng3: 50% (moderate reduction)\n\nDouble mutants:\n\ng1g3: 10% (very low)\ng2g3: 0%\ng1g2: 0%\n\nGoal: determine which gene is TF, epistatic relationships, etc. Options mention pleiotropy, redundancy, same promoter, etc.\n\nWe need to analyze genetic interactions.\n\nFirst, assess single mutants:\n\n- g2 knockout eliminates resistance completely. So G2 is essential for resistance. Possibly TF upstream of others, if its loss also eliminates downstream gene products. So G2 likely transcription factor.\n\n- g1 knockout reduces resistance to 75% (i.e., 25% loss). So G1 contributes partially.\n\n- g3 knockout reduces to 50% (i.e., 50% loss). So G3 contributes.\n\nNow double mutants:\n\n- g1g2: 0% (same as g2 alone). So loss of G2 masks effect of G1. So G2 is epistatic to G1 (since double = g2 phenotype). Epistasis: gene upstream (TF) typically epistatic to downstream genes. If G2 is TF upstream, then its loss makes downstream irrelevant -> same as g2 alone. So G2 epistatic to G1.\n\n- g2g3: 0% same as g2 alone. So G2 epistatic to G3 as well.\n\n- g1g3: 10% (much lower than either single: g1 75, g3 50). This suggests additive or synergistic effect. So G1 and G3 may act in parallel pathways that both contribute to resistance; when both removed, severe loss but not completely zero (10%). Could be partial redundancy? Actually redundancy would cause double mutant similar to single (if redundant). Here double is more severe than each single, suggests they act in separate pathways that converge; not redundant.\n\nThe options:\n\nA) G1 is a transcription factor, G2 and G3 show pleiotropy, G2 is epistatic towards G1\n\nThis says G1 TF, but data suggests G2 is essential and epistatic. So A likely wrong.\n\nB) G2 is TF, G1 and G3 have same promoter, G3 epistatic towards G1.\n\nSame promoter maybe co-regulated? But doesn't explain epistasis pattern. Data shows G3 not epistatic; double g1g3 is severe, not G3 epistatic. So B wrong.\n\nC) G2 is TF, G1 and G3 show pleiotropy, G1 is epistatic towards G3.\n\nPleiotropy: single gene affecting multiple traits; perhaps not relevant. G1 epistatic towards G3? But double g1g3 (10%) suggests not epistatic (if G1 epistatic, double would look like g1 75%). So C wrong.\n\nD) G2 is TF, G1 and G3 show gene redundancy, G1 is epistatic towards G3.\n\nRedundancy: if redundant, double would be similar to single (no further reduction). Not the case. Also G1 epistatic towards G3 not supported.\n\nHmm none seem right? Let's think.\n\nMaybe we misinterpret epistasis direction: The gene acting upstream is epistatic (its phenotype masks downstream). So G2 (TF) upstream of G1 and G3. Then G2 is epistatic to both G1 and G3. In double mutants g1g2 and g2g3 we see that: g1g2 = 0 same as g2, g2g3 =0 same as g2. So G2 epistatic to both.\n\nAmong options, which mention G2 as TF and epistasis? A says G2 epistatic towards G1 (but says G1 TF). Not correct.\n\nB says G2 TF, G3 epistatic towards G1 (no). C says G2 TF, G1 epistatic towards G3 (no). D says G2 TF, G1 epistatic towards G3 (no). All have G2 TF except A. So they all assume G2 TF. Good. But they differ on interactions.\n\nWhich matches data best? Data: G2 masks both G1 and G3 (since double with G2 always 0). So G2 epistatic to G1 and to G3. None of the options state that. However Option A says G2 epistatic towards G1 (but not G3). Option B says G3 epistatic towards G1. Option C says G1 epistatic towards G3. Option D says G1 epistatic towards G3. So only A mentions G2 epistatic (though only towards G1). Maybe they expect only one epistasis relationship is needed? Let's examine other features: pleiotropy, same promoter, redundancy.\n\nLook at g1g3 10%: indicates they have additive effect, maybe they work in same pathway but both required partially; redundancy would cause no extra effect. So not redundancy.\n\nPleiotropy: gene affecting multiple traits, but we only measure resistance. Could be that G1 and G3 each affect multiple downstream genes (pleiotropy). Not clear.\n\nOption A: G1 is TF (contradicts data). If G1 were TF upstream of G2 and G3, loss of G1 would cause severe effect (should be 0). But g1 has 75% only, not severe. So G1 not TF.\n\nOption B: G2 TF (good). G1 and G3 have same promoter (means co-regulated). If they share promoter, their mutants may be additive? Not sure. G3 epistatic towards G1 would mean double phenocopies g3 (50%). But g1g3 is 10%, not 50. So not.\n\nOption C: G2 TF (good). G1 and G3 show pleiotropy (maybe each affect multiple pathways). G1 epistatic towards G3 => double would look like g1 (75%). Not observed. So no.\n\nOption D: G2 TF (good). G1 and G3 show gene redundancy (they compensate). If redundant, double knockout would be similar to single (maybe 0 if each essential). However g1g3 is 10% not 0. Redundancy would likely make double similar to more severe of singles. That could be 0? Not.\n\nThus none match perfectly. Perhaps we misinterpret epistasis definitions: \"gene A is epistatic to gene B\" means mutation of A masks effect of B (i.e., double = A phenotype). In data, G2 mutation masks both G1 and G3 (since double = 0). So G2 epistatic to G1 and G3. The options only have epistasis mentioned between G1 and G3, not referencing G2 (except A partial). Could be a trick: only one of those is correct given other statements about pleiotropy etc.\n\nCheck each option for consistency besides epistasis.\n\nOption C: G2 TF (makes sense). G1 and G3 show pleiotropy (means each affects multiple traits—maybe affecting same phenotype in multiple ways). G1 epistatic towards G3 (but data opposite). So not.\n\nOption D: G2 TF (makes sense). G1 and G3 show gene redundancy (i.e., they can compensate). If redundant, single mutants show moderate loss (75% and 50% respectively). Redundant genes often when both knocked out give more severe (like synthetic lethality) which is observed (10% vs 0). So could fit: redundancy leads to more severe phenotype when both lost. G1 epistatic towards G3: if redundant, epistasis not typical. However G1 epistatic to G3 would predict double = g1 phenotype (75). Not.\n\nThus maybe the question expects that G2 is TF upstream; G1 and G3 are downstream genes and act additively, showing pleiotropy (affect multiple downstream). Epistasis: G2 epistatic to both. However only option with G2 TF is A, B, C, D (all), but only A says G2 epistatic to G1 (not G3). Maybe they think only G2 epistatic to G1 matters because G2& G3 double already 0 due to G2, but that's also epistasis. Option A says G2 epistatic towards G1; doesn't address G3 but implicit maybe.\n\nOption A also says G1 is TF (contrary) and G2 and G3 show pleiotropy (which maybe) but G1 as TF contradicts data.\n\nThus perhaps none are correct, but we must pick best. Let's examine each statement fully:\n\nA) G1 is a transcription factor, G2 and G3 show pleiotropy, G2 is epistatic towards G1\n\nData: G1 not TF (based on g1 partial phenotype). So A false.\n\nB) G2 is a TF, G1 and G3 have same promoter, G3 is epistatic towards G1\n\nSame promoter maybe explains additive? Not. G3 epistatic to G1 would mean double = g3 (50). Not.\n\nC) G2 is TF, G1 and G3 show pleiotropy, G1 is epistatic towards G3\n\nG1 epistatic to G3 would make double = g1 (75). Not.\n\nD) G2 is TF, G1 and G3 show gene redundancy, G1 is epistatic towards G3\n\nAgain double = g1 (75) not.\n\nThus none matches. Maybe we misread percentages: \"resistance of g1: 75% of control\" maybe means 75% reduction? But they say 100% is level of resistance in control; 50% is half; 25% quarter; 0% all infected. So 75% means 75% of control residual resistance (i.e., not reduced). So yes.\n\nGiven that, G1's loss reduces 25% of resistance.\n\nThus G1 not TF.\n\nHence correct answer should be something with G2 TF. Options B, C, D have that. Need decide which also matches interactions. Which is best describing G1 and G3? The double g1g3 is much lower (10%). This suggests they act in parallel pathways, each contributing partially; the loss of both leads to severe defect, indicating they are not redundant but additive. Pleiotropy is not about interaction; redundancy would give similar phenotype as single if they compensate, not additive severe drop. So not redundancy. Same promoter maybe cause co-regulation, but still double severe. Could be that they are in the same pathway downstream of TF; both needed for full resistance; loss of each gives partial, loss of both near zero. That is not redundancy; they are partially redundant? Actually they are partially redundant: each can partially compensate for the other. If they are redundant, single loss yields little phenotype, double yields strong phenotype. Here single loss yields moderate phenotype (25% loss for G1, 50% loss for G3). That's moderate, not minimal. But could still be redundant partially. Option D says redundancy and G1 epistatic to G3. Epistasis not matching.\n\nPerhaps the key is G1 epistatic over G3 (dominant effects). Let's see: If G1 is upstream of G3, then double would phenocopy G1 (75) – not the case. So not.\n\nThus maybe the correct answer is B? Let's test: G2 TF, G1 and G3 same promoter (means co-regulated). If they share promoter, knocking both might affect promoter usage leading to near zero. G3 epistatic to G1 means double equals G3 (50). Not observed. So no.\n\nThus maybe the intended answer is C? G2 TF, G1 and G3 show pleiotropy (each affect multiple downstream traits) and G1 epistatic towards G3— again not matched.\n\nWait maybe we misinterpret epistasis direction: \"G1 is epistatic towards G3\" could mean G1 masks G3; i.e., phenotype of double equals G1 (which is 75%); but we have 10% – not.\n\nThus none.\n\nMaybe the question expects answer A because they think G1 is TF upstream of G2 and G3? Let's re-evaluate with that interpretation.\n\nIf G1 TF upstream, then loss of G1 would reduce downstream expression of G2 and G3. Yet g1 single shows 75% resistance (some residual), indicates maybe G1 partially required. However double g1g2 is 0 (since G2 lost), g1g3 is 10 (near zero). If G1 upstream, then g1g2 double may be similar to g2 alone (0) because downstream G2 loss masks upstream? In hierarchy, downstream gene is epistatic to upstream (i.e., downstream masks upstream). So G2 epistatic to G1, which matches A: \"G2 is epistatic towards G1\". That's true. The other phenotypes: G2 zero, G3 50, g1g3 10. If G1 upstream, g3 downstream? If G3 downstream of G1, then double g1g3 would look like g3 (50). Not match. So not.\n\nThus answer likely not any given? Could be a trick: choose D as best because G2 TF, G1 and G3 show redundancy (partial), G1 epistatic to G3 (maybe they consider that double phenocopies G1 because 10% ~ close to 0, not 75, but maybe they interpret 10% as near 0, implying G1 downstream of G3? hmm).\n\nLet's compute: If G1 epistatic to G3, double phenotype = G1 (75). Not.\n\nIf G3 epistatic (like B), double = 50. Not.\n\nIf G2 TF upstream, epistatic to both; but not mentioned.\n\nThus maybe the intended answer is \"none of the above\", but not given.\n\nMaybe we misinterpret the phrase \"pleiotropy\" meaning that a single gene influences multiple traits, which could explain why individual knockouts have partial effects. G1 and G3 show pleiotropy (each affect multiple downstream genes). In that case epistasis between G1 and G3 maybe not relevant. Option C says G1 epistatic towards G3, but not observed.\n\nGiven mismatch, maybe pick option D because it mentions redundancy, which aligns with additive effect of double mutant being worse than each single, indicating they partially compensate. And G1 epistatic towards G3? But maybe they think epistasis can be determined only when double has lower than both (synthetic). Not typical.\n\nNevertheless, need answer. I'd choose option D as most plausible: G2 TF, G1 and G3 redundant downstream.\n\nThus answer: D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.4305109061751864e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999887, "logp_correct": -1.132344126487738e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999995231630805, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6436345337190683, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2839264327610174, "frac_below_-5": 0.014528593508500772, "digit_lp_min": -3.8956074714660645, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 3235, "lp_end": -4.768360213347478e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -4.511179439202929, "p_nocot": 0.010985495807548627, "uplift": 4.511179439202918, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 7.4e-05, "B": 5.8e-05, "C": 0.988883, "D": 0.010985}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
