 15883, 6118, 316, 6234, 484, 34398, 25, 532, 17, 659, 363, 2747, 10271, 17, 350, 267, 10271, 741, 1843, 532, 17, 284, 8795, 2061, 382, 9913, 656, 11002, 4527, 30, 623, 11002, 3333, 665, 34881, 540, 1645, 4454, 532, 17, 26, 10112, 1023, 19429, 722, 532, 17, 591, 658, 1388, 46, 18, 137530, 483, 11002, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 11002, 382, 306, 16968, 741, 3072, 11002, 4527, 382, 9913, 350, 17, 13, 1125, 329, 741, 38200, 532, 17, 6118, 316, 45266, 750, 722, 11002, 25, 2214, 1667, 11, 1309, 220, 15, 13, 3218, 9192, 532, 17, 11, 15021, 220, 15, 13, 6733, 9192, 532, 17, 54644, 13, 2214, 102595, 1309, 220, 15, 13, 28653, 20, 9192, 532, 17, 11, 15021, 220, 15, 13, 45545, 20, 9192, 532, 17, 13, 2632, 10112, 1023, 19429, 532, 17, 9637, 137530, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 11002, 382, 306, 16968, 30, 3072, 11002, 14147, 19137, 1606, 220, 17, 13, 1125, 329, 11, 8535, 34881, 722, 532, 17, 13, 2632, 10112, 1023, 19429, 3282, 627, 11002, 27591, 1932, 30, 4037, 364, 4827, 538, 11002, 382, 68837, 350, 2878, 11002, 936, 180942, 364, 58369, 1746, 5317, 656, 2360, 15883, 6118, 12629, 316, 532, 17, 284, 8795, 350, 15, 13, 21, 9192, 8, 425, 220, 899, 314, 220, 22, 13, 17, 329, 15883, 350, 3941, 415, 30, 220, 15, 13, 8540, 329, 625, 220, 22, 13, 17, 741, 2632, 625, 364, 4383, 18051, 25, 5317, 415, 220, 15, 13, 8540, 329, 15883, 66938, 316, 220, 15, 13, 3218, 9192, 363, 13, 21926, 220, 15, 13, 3218, 9192, 425, 220, 899, 314, 15, 13, 8540, 329, 13, 1843, 15883, 66938, 316, 532, 17, 2061, 395, 11002, 95505, 350, 15, 13, 3218, 9192, 532, 17, 395, 1667, 741, 41351, 581, 2491, 532, 17, 23292, 395, 1667, 314, 15, 13, 3218, 9192, 13, 1843, 15883, 38566, 532, 63489, 25, 532, 17, 2747, 220, 17, 532, 63489, 11, 1309, 220, 15, 13, 3218, 9192, 532, 17, 314, 220, 15, 13, 899, 9192, 532, 63489, 30, 4037, 364, 7943, 15883, 6118, 316, 8452, 11002, 64480, 1602, 25, 4527, 328, 15883, 29702, 284, 8795, 328, 532, 17, 484, 122218, 350, 366, 2454, 532, 17, 137530, 483, 220, 17, 34, 316, 1625, 220, 17, 8310, 95807, 35717, 28035, 25, 532, 17, 659, 363, 2747, 10271, 17, 350, 16, 34, 777, 532, 17, 741, 2632, 15883, 284, 8795, 12629, 532, 17, 284, 8795, 2061, 13, 2214, 1667, 11, 532, 17, 2061, 314, 15, 13, 3218, 9192, 11, 15883, 4842, 314, 15, 13, 3218, 9, 899, 314, 15, 13, 8540, 329, 350, 3941, 415, 741, 4569, 15248, 0, 2632, 1023, 19429, 15883, 38566, 290, 34398, 8516, 11, 625, 290, 64480, 13, 2632, 1023, 23864, 532, 17, 2061, 316, 45266, 750, 11002, 350, 52911, 402, 11002, 4842, 741, 41021, 14866, 25, 28548, 4842, 220, 17, 13, 1125, 329, 1667, 350, 366, 11002, 1667, 741, 532, 17, 6118, 314, 220, 15, 13, 3218, 9192, 13, 36897, 4842, 314, 220, 15, 13, 3218, 9, 899, 314, 220, 15, 13, 8540, 329, 871, 5317, 415, 364, 84787, 6052, 6960, 415, 364, 39520, 11002, 104910, 168136, 11, 59897, 1127, 1667, 23440, 13, 2632, 6052, 415, 364, 84787, 5655, 5317, 415, 364], "n_tokens": 2592, "words": 1261, "band_lo": 758.25, "band_hi": 3033.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse problem: 49 g KClO3 decomposes, producing O2. That O2 reacts with 10.8 g impure metal (20% purity) to form metal oxide. Then calculate amount of carbon needed to convert the metal oxide back to pure metal. The metal is amphoteric, abundant in earth crust.\n\nWe need to find which metal: amphoteric, abundant in crust. Candidates: aluminum (amphoteric oxide Al2O3), also Zn (amphoteric?), ZnO is amphoteric? Zn is moderately abundant, but Al is more abundant. Aluminum oxide is amphoteric. Aluminum is abundant in crust (8%). So likely aluminum.\n\nWe have impure metal sample 10.8 g with 20% purity, thus pure metal mass = 20% of 10.8 g = 2.16 g metal, rest 8.64 g impurities.\n\nThe O2 from decomposition of KClO3 reacts with metal to form oxide. Need moles of O2 produced from 49 g KClO3.\n\nReaction: 2 KClO3 → 2 KCl + 3 O2 (upon heating). Balanced: 2 KClO3 → 2 KCl + 3 O2.\n\nMolar mass KClO3: K (39.10) + Cl (35.45) + 3*O (48) = 122.55 g/mol approx. So moles KClO3 = 49 / 122.55 = 0.400 (approx). Let's compute: 122.55*0.4 = 49.02, close. So ~0.4 mol.\n\nFrom stoichiometry: 2 mol KClO3 produce 3 mol O2. So per mol KClO3 produce 1.5 mol O2.\n\nThus O2 moles = 0.4 * 1.5 = 0.6 mol O2.\n\nNow this O2 reacts with metal (pure portion) to form metal oxide. Need reaction: 2 Al + 3/2 O2 -> Al2O3 (balanced). Or Al + 3/2 O2 -> Al2O3? Actually balanced: 4 Al + 3 O2 -> 2 Al2O3 (simplify: 2 Al + 3/2 O2 -> Al2O3). So 1.5 O2 per 2 Al. Moles O2 needed per mole Al = 3/4 = 0.75? Wait compute: from 2 Al + 3/2 O2 -> Al2O3. So 1 mole Al requires (3/4) O2. So O2 per Al = 0.75 mol O2 per mol Al.\n\nAlternatively, if metal is Zn: Zn + 1/2 O2 -> ZnO, O2 per Zn = 0.5.\n\nWe have O2 moles 0.6. Which metal would be fully oxidized? Let's check Al: we have pure Al mass 2.16 g. Moles Al = 2.16 / 26.98 = 0.08 mol. Required O2 = 0.08 * 0.75 = 0.06 mol O2. We have 0.6 mol O2, far excess. So all Al can be oxidized, extra O2 leftover.\n\nIf metal is Zn: Moles Zn = mass/65.38 = 2.16/65.38=0.033 mol. O2 required = 0.033*0.5=0.0165 mol O2, also less than 0.6.\n\nThus O2 abundant, not limiting.\n\nThus we need carbon to reduce metal oxide back to pure metal using carbon (likely in form of carbon as reducing agent: metal oxide + C -> metal + CO/CO2). We need to compute amount of carbon needed to reduce the formed metal oxide (which corresponds to the pure metal mass 2.16 g). Using typical reduction: For Al2O3, carbon reduction is not practical (requires high temperature, produces Al and CO/CO2?). More typical is Al2O3 + 3C -> 2Al + 3CO. Balanced: Al2O3 + 3 C -> 2 Al + 3 CO. So per mole Al2O3, need 3 C atoms (or 3 moles C). So per mole Al (half that) need 1.5 C.\n\nBut is carbon reduction for Al oxide plausible? Probably not, but in problem they ask amount of carbon needed.\n\nThus find moles of metal oxide formed = same as moles of metal (since metal oxide formula contains metal). For Al2O3, 2 Al per oxide. So moles of Al2O3 = moles Al /2 = 0.04 mol. Wait we found moles Al =0.08. So oxide moles =0.04.\n\nCarbon needed = 3 moles per mole Al2O3 = 3*0.04 =0.12 mol C.\n\nMass carbon = 0.12 mol *12.01 = 1.44 g. Not among options.\n\nMaybe they use reaction: Metal oxide + C -> metal + CO2 (like for many metals) e.g., SnO + C -> Sn + CO. For Al? Not typical. Let's examine alternative: If metal is Silicon? Not a metal. Maybe the metal is Aluminum but carbon reduces Al2O3 to Al + CO? Actually carbon reduces Al2O3 -> Al + CO, but stoichiometry is Al2O3 + 3C -> 2Al + 3CO. So carbon needed 3 per 2 Al, i.e., 1.5 per Al, as earlier. So carbon moles = 0.08*1.5=0.12 mol same. mass 1.44 g.\n\nNot match choices (0.36,0.06,0.48,0.72). Maybe they consider only pure metal 20% purity? Wait we already used pure metal mass 2.16 g. Perhaps they consider impure metal sample 10.8 g being oxidized, but only 20% is the metal, rest impurities not reacting, but carbon needed to reduce oxide from whole 10.8 g? No, only metal part forms oxide.\n\nMaybe metal is iron? Fe is abundant but not amphoteric (Fe2O3 is not amphoteric). Amphoteric metal: zinc, aluminum, lead (lead oxide amphoteric), tin? Tin oxide is amphoteric.\n\nBut among abundant in crust, Al, Zn, Pb less abundant. Aluminum is most abundant.\n\nBut answer options are lower, maybe they assume carbon reduces oxide to metal and carbon is converted to CO2 with molar mass 44, but they ask mass of carbon, not CO2.\n\nLet's compute for ZnO reduction: ZnO + C -> Zn + CO. Balanced: ZnO + C -> Zn + CO. So 1 C per ZnO. Moles Zn =0.033, carbon needed =0.033 mol. Mass =0.033*12.01=0.396 g ~0.40 g, close to 0.36 g option (A). Might be rounding.\n\nIf they used atomic weight approximations: Zn = 65.4, carbon 12, so carbon mass = (2.16/65.4)*12 = (0.0330)*12=0.396 g. Option 0.36 g maybe if they used 2.5? Not sure.\n\nIf metal is Aluminum with reduction: Al2O3 + 3 C -> 2 Al + 3 CO. Need 3 C per 2 Al, carbon per Al =1.5. For 0.08 mol Al, carbon =0.12 mol, mass =1.44 g. Not match.\n\nThus target answer likely 0.36 g (close to Zn calculation). Let's compute using more precise numbers: Pure Zn mass = 20% of 10.8 = 2.16 g. Moles Zn = 2.16/65.38 = 0.03304 mol. Carbon needed = 1 mol per Zn = 0.03304 mol. Mass carbon = 0.03304*12.01=0.397 g. Rounded to two sig figs maybe 0.40 g, not in options. Option 0.36 g is a bit lower.\n\nWhat about metal being aluminum but they incorrectly use Al + O2 -> Al2O3? Not; maybe they assume oxide formula M2O3 needing 3 O per 2 M, reducing with carbon: M2O3 + 3C -> 2M + 3CO. For metal atomic weight maybe low.\n\nCheck if they used M = 27 (Al). Then O2 moles not limiting. Carbon needed per Al2O3 is 3 C per 2 Al (1.5). So carbon mass = 2.16 g metal * (1.5 mol C / 1 mol Al) * (12 g/mol) / (27 g/mol) = 2.16 * (1.5*12/27) = 2.16 * (18/27) = 2.16 * 0.6667 = 1.44 g again.\n\nSo not.\n\nMaybe they consider only oxygen produced (0.6 mol) reacts with metal to form oxide, and carbon needed to convert O2 (or oxide) back to metal? Let's think: O2 reacts with metal to make oxide; carbon needed to reduce oxide back: essentially carbon reacts with O2 to form CO2, removing O. The amount of O in oxide equals O2 moles used. So carbon needed to remove that oxygen: O2 + C -> CO2 (or CO). If O2 moles used is limited by metal amount? The metal present can consume at most certain O2; maybe they assume all O2 from KClO3 reacts with metal (i.e., metal is in excess). But metal amount is limited (2.16 g). Compare O2 needed to oxidize all metal: For Al, need 0.06 mol O2, leaving 0.54 mol O2 idle. For Zn need 0.0165 mol O2, leaving 0.5835 mol O2. So maybe they assume O2 fully reacts, i.e., metal is in excess? But metal pure portion only 2.16 g, can't consume all O2. So maybe they assume impure metal density high? Not.\n\nWhat if metal is silicon (not metal), improbable.\n\nLet's test option by using carbon needed equal to O2 moles (0.6 mol) * 12 = 7.2 g carbon (option D? 0.72 g not 7.2). So not.\n\nCheck ratio: option D 0.72 g carbon corresponds to 0.06 mol C. That's 0.06 mol * 12 =0.72 g. If carbon corresponds to O2 used for metal oxidation (0.06 mol O2 for Al). Indeed we found O2 requirement for Al =0.06 mol. If carbon reduces O atoms: O2 -> 2 O atoms, need 0.06 mol O2 = 0.12 mol O atoms? Not.\n\nBut carbon needed to convert metal oxide back: amount of carbon equals moles of O2 that reacted (if each O2 reacts with 2C to form 2CO?), let's examine: O2 + C -> CO2 (1C per O2). So carbon moles equal O2 moles used. For Al, O2 used =0.06 mol, carbon mass =0.06*12 =0.72 g (option D). That matches! So they assume carbon reduces the oxygen directly, not the oxide. So they compute O2 used to oxidize metal (based on metal mass). Let's verify: Metal mass 2.16 g Al (if metal Al). O2 needed = 0.06 mol. Carbon mass = 0.06*12 = 0.72 g => option D.\n\nThus answer likely D.\n\nGiven metal amphoteric, abundant – Al fits. So answer D.\n\nThus choose option D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -8.34461570775602e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999948, "logp_correct": -5.1673074032496585e-15, "letter_mass": 1.000000000000005, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5478998341010837, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2204907818191728, "frac_below_-5": 0.009693679720822024, "digit_lp_min": -4.463869571685791, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.09137049317359924, "n_cot_tokens": 2579, "lp_end": -0.003344539552927017, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -3.3336858075427824, "p_nocot": 0.0356614214004231, "uplift": 3.333685807542777, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.027773, "B": 0.91972, "C": 0.016845, "D": 0.035661}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec2fsnzUuvNtUYK8", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: which of the following molecules has c3h symmetry?\ntriisopropyl borate\nquinuclidine\nbenzo[1,2-c:3,4-c':5,6-c'']trifuran-1,3,4,6,7,9-hexaone\ntriphenyleno[1,2-c:5,6-c':9,10-c'']trifuran-1,3,6,8,11,13-hexaone\n\nA) benzo[1,2-c:3,4-c':5,6-c'']trifuran-1,3,4,6,7,9-hexaone\nB) triisopropyl borate\nC) triphenyleno[1,2-c:5,6-c':9,10-c'']trifuran-1,3,6,8,11,13-hexaone\nD) quinuclidine\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: which of the following molecules has c3h symmetry?\ntriisopropyl borate\nquinuclidine\nbenzo[1,2-c:3,4-c':5,6-c'']trifuran-1,3,4,6,7,9-hexaone\ntriphenyleno[1,2-c:5,6-c':9,10-c'']trifuran-1,3,6,8,11,13-hexaone\n\nA) benzo[1,2-c:3,4-c':5,6-c'']trifuran-1,3,4,6,7,9-hexaone\nB) triisopropyl borate\nC) triphenyleno[1,2-c:5,6-c':9,10-c'']trifuran-1,3,6,8,11,13-hexaone\nD) quinuclidine\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "rec2fsnzUuvNtUYK8:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11433, 1118, 73005, 6200, 290, 3880, 853, 363, 18, 71, 90882, 13, 363, 18, 71, 2438, 3566, 25, 853, 261, 363, 18, 10589, 16300, 326, 261, 25511, 28026, 16499, 350, 1168, 71, 8, 137754, 316, 484, 16300, 13, 1225, 1217, 6664, 261, 336, 18, 75568, 23769, 30, 50557, 363, 18, 71, 6664, 363, 18, 326, 3669, 71, 11, 8117, 316, 336, 18, 350, 34, 18, 425, 3669, 71, 741, 623, 3566, 853, 8632, 25, 457, 11, 363, 18, 11, 363, 18, 61, 17, 11, 3669, 71, 11, 336, 18, 11, 336, 18, 61, 20, 350, 267, 336, 18, 61, 20, 95807, 889, 21924, 1309, 73005, 483, 363, 18, 16300, 326, 25511, 28026, 16499, 889, 860, 363, 17, 51760, 13, 2632, 1118, 328, 290, 4335, 53785, 853, 2238, 90882, 364, 58369, 2411, 1078, 21207, 364, 7, 16, 8, 25062, 276, 467, 2283, 3643, 16641, 379, 25, 8866, 41