2-CH(CH3)2? Wait isoamyl is 3-methylbutyl: CH3-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-. Actually 3-methylbutyl: CH3-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-. That's isoamyl group attached to carbonyl via its terminal CH2? Usually isoamyl ketone (also known as 3-methyl-2-butanone?) Hmm confusion.\n\nLet's parse: methyl isoamyl ketone: CH3COC5H11 where isoamyl is 3-methylbutyl (C5H11). So carbonyl carbon attached to CH3 (as methyl) and isoamyl group (C5). So structure: CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CH(CH3)2? Let's draw isoamyl: CH3-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2- (the carbon attached to carbonyl is the CH2). So carbonyl attached to CH2 group which is attached to CH2 then to CH(CH3)2? Actually isoamyl chain: starting from carbon attached to carbonyl (C1) is CH2, then CH2 (C2), then CH(CH3) (C3) with a methyl substituent, then CH3 (C4) as terminal? Wait isoamyl is 3-methylbutyl: CH3-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-. So carbon attached to carbonyl is CH2 (the CH2- of -CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3?). Actually the chain is CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3 with a CH3 substituent at second carbon counting from carbonyl? Let's number: carbonyl carbon (C=O) attached to CH3 and to CH2 (C1). C1-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3. So the overall structure: CH3-CO-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3. Wait count: isoamyl group has 5 carbons, but we have only 4 after carbonyl? Let's check: isoamyl group is C5H11 = 5 carbons. If attached via CH2, the group includes CH2-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH3? Let's list: CH2 (attached), then CH2, then CH(CH3), then CH3 (makes 4) plus a methyl branch (CH3) on the third carbon, total 5. So chain: -CH2-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH3 with a CH3 branch on the CH (the third carbon). So full formula: CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH3; plus the branch CH3 attached to the CH. So the structure: CH3CO-CH2-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH3, with CH(CH3) meaning carbon bearing a methyl substituent. So we have a ketone with carbonyl adjacent to CH2 (alpha), then CH2 (beta), then a tertiary carbon (gamma) bearing a methyl substituent and a terminal methyl (delta). So basically 2-methyl-3-pentanone? Not exactly.\n\nBut the reaction: hydrogen peroxide and BF3 in diethyl ether. This is reminiscent of Baeyer-Villiger oxidation (peracid oxidation of ketone) but with H2O2 and BF3 as a peracid-like reagent? Actually Baeyer-Villiger uses peracids (mCPBA) to insert O between carbonyl and the more substituted alkyl. Hydroperoxide with BF3 can act as a peracid to oxidize ketone to an ester (or lactone). For a simple ketone, you get an ester: R-CO-R' + H2O2 -> R-CO-O-R' (if migration of R). With BF3, it's a Lewis acid that activates carbonyl to nucleophilic attack by peroxide.\n\nThus methyl isoamyl ketone (CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH3) under Baeyer-Villiger will give methyl isoamyl formate? Actually the migration tendency: more substituted group migrates preferentially. So isoamyl group (more substituted) migrates over methyl, giving isoamyl acetate (i.e., isoamyl acetate = isoamyl ethanoate). Wait if methyl migrates, product is methyl isoamyl peroxide? Let's think: Starting ketone R-CO-R' where R = CH3, R' = isoamyl. In Baeyer-Villiger, insertion of O yields R-CO-O-R' (if R migrates) or R'-CO-O-R (if R' migrates). Actually the O ends up between carbonyl carbon and migrating group; the carbonyl carbon becomes part of an ester where the migrating group is now attached to carbonyl oxygen. The original carbonyl carbon becomes carbonyl of ester attached to the other group (the one that does NOT migrate). So if R (methyl) migrates, product is CH3-CO-O-isoamyl i.e., methyl isoamyl? Wait no: If methyl migrates, then carbonyl carbon is attached to isoamyl via oxygen? Let's illustrate: Starting R-CO-R'; after Baeyer-Villiger, we get R-CO-O-R' (if R migrates) or R'-CO-O-R (if R' migrates). But typical rule: the group migrating becomes attached to carbonyl oxygen, the other stays attached to carbonyl carbon. For example, acetophenone (PhCOCH3) gives phenyl acetate (PhCOOCH3) because phenyl migrates (more substituted). So moving group ends up on the carbonyl side as acyl? Wait phenyl acetate structure: Ph-CO-O-CH3. Here phenyl is attached to carbonyl carbon, methyl attached to oxygen. The migrating group (phenyl) stays attached to carbonyl carbon (it's the acyl part). Actually phenyl migrated from carbonyl carbon to the adjacent oxygen? No.\n\nLet's step: In Baeyer-Villiger, carbonyl O attacks peroxide, forming Criegee intermediate, then migration of R group to peroxide O, cleavage of O-O bond producing ester R-CO-O-R'. The migrating group R moves to the peroxy oxygen, which becomes carbonyl O in ester? Wait final ester R-CO-O-R'. The carbonyl carbon is now attached to R (migrated group) and double-bond O, and single bond O of the ester to R'. The group that does not migrate ends up attached to oxygen. So if methyl migrates, product is CH3-CO-O-isoamyl (acetate with isoamyl as alkoxy). That's methyl isoamyl? Actually CH3-CO-O-isoamyl is isoamyl acetate. Yes isoamyl acetate (banana oil). The other possibility methyl does not migrate, isoamyl migrates, giving isoamyl-CO-O-CH3 = isoamyl formate? Wait isoamyl-CO-O-CH3 would be isoamyl formate is not correct as isoamyl formate is H-CO-O-isoamyl ( formate). Instead isoamyl migrates gives isoamyl-CO-O-CH3 is isoamyl acetate? No.\n\nLet's compute: starting R = CH3, R' = isoamyl. If isoamyl migrates, product = isoamyl-CO-O-CH3 = isoamyl acetate? Actually isoamyl acetate is CH3-CO-O-isoamyl (acetyl group attached to isoamyl). Compare: isoamyl-CO-O-CH3 is isoamyl formate? Wait formate is H-CO-O-... So isoamyl-CO-O-CH3 would be isoamyl acyl (7 carbon) formate? Wait isoamyl carbonyl (R-CO) where R=isoamyl would be isoamyl carbonyl = isoamyl carbonyl (C6). So that would be isoamyl formate? No. The carbonyl attached to isoamyl (RCO) where R is isoamyl: isoamyl CO- is a carboxylic acid derivative, e.g., isoamyl carboxyl? That's isoamyl acetate? Actually acetate is CH3-CO-O-... The acyl group is CH3CO-. So isoamyl acetate: CH3CO-O-isoamyl (methyl migrates). So isoamyl acetate corresponds to migration of methyl.\n\nThus expected product is isoamyl acetate. The most deshielded hydrogen nucleus likely is the O-H? No OH not present. In ester, most deshielded is the methylene protons alpha to carbonyl? Actually in esters the carbonyl-adjacent carbon (alpha to carbonyl) experiences deshielding due to carbonyl. In isoamyl acetate, the carbonyl carbon attached to CH3 (acetyl). So CH3-CO-O-CH2... The most deshielded hydrogens are the CH3 attached to carbonyl? Not; carbonyl carbon is attached to CH3 (acetyl methyl) which appears around 2.0 ppm (triplet?). Let's examine: isoamyl acetate structure: CH3-CO-O-CH2-CH2-CH(CH3)2 (since isoamyl is 3-methylbutyl). So we have:\n\n- Acetyl methyl (CH3CO-) directly attached to carbonyl: a singlet? It's attached to carbonyl carbon, not adjacent to any hydrogens, so no splitting (singlet). But coupling to adjacent carbon? Wait methyl attached directly to carbonyl cannot couple because carbonyl carbon has no hydrogens, but can couple via 3-bond to CH2 of the alkoxy? Actually J coupling through a carbonyl is usually negligible at 1H NMR unless via long-range? Methyl attached to carbonyl is not directly coupled to any protons (no vicinal H). So appears as singlet.\n\n- Methylene next to oxygen (O-CH2-): this CH2 is adjacent to O (no H on O), and next to CH2, so it will couple to the next CH2 (two protons) giving a quartet? Actually CH2 adjacent to CH2: The O-CH2-CH2... The CH2 next to O will couple to the adjacent CH2 (next carbon) which has 2 protons, giving a triplet (n+1, n=2). Also it could couple to oxygen? Not. So expected pattern: triplet.\n\n- Next CH2 (middle) couples to O-CH2 and to CH (a methine) which has 1 H (CH). So it would be more complex (multiplet). But question asks for most deshielded and second most deshielded hydrogen nucleus. Most deshielded will be the CH2 adjacent to carbonyl? Actually the carbonyl carbon is attached to methyl (CH3) and O etc. The most deshielded protons are typically α to carbonyl, which here is the methyl of acetyl (CH3-CO) at about 2.0 ppm. However the CH2 adjacent to O (O-CH2) also appears ~4.0 ppm, which is more downfield (deshielded) because of oxygen. Indeed O-CH2 is more deshielded than carbonyl α-methyl. So the most deshielded would be the O-CH2 protons (~4.1 ppm). That CH2 is a triplet (coupling to next CH2). The second most deshielded would be the carbonyl methyl (~2.1 ppm) appearing as a singlet. So pattern: most deshielded = triplet; second most = singlet. That corresponds to option D: singlet, triplet? Wait order: they ask \"splitting patterns of the most deshielded, and second most deshielded hydrogen nucleus\". So first answer: pattern of most deshielded; second answer: pattern of second most deshielded.\n\nWe think most deshielded = O-CH2 = triplet. Second most deshielded = acetyl methyl = singlet. So the pair: triplet, singlet. Which option matches? Option B: triplet, singlet. Option D: singlet, triplet (reverse). So answer B.\n\nLet's verify there is not another more deshielded hydrogen like O-CH (if isoamyl group includes CH attached to methyl). Actually isoamyl part includes a CH (the one bearing a methyl substituent) attached to CH2 and CH3. That CH is attached to O-CH2? The O-CH2-CH2-CH(CH3)2. The CH is one bond away from CH2 next to O; its chemical shift maybe ~1.5-2 ppm, less deshielded than O-CH2. So yes O-CH2 is most downfield.\n\nThus answer B.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.3378546504536644e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999998368, "logp_correct": -1.6318867412556094e-13, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000001632, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5628753301915921, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2866677798173, "frac_below_-5": 0.013004791238877482, "digit_lp_min": -11.903818130493164, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.006746490485966206, "n_cot_tokens": 2922, "lp_end": -4.362964682513848e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.576278118089249e-07, "logp_nocot": -3.8023773868597055, "p_nocot": 0.022317651046490486, "uplift": 3.8023773868595425, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.022318, "B": 0.022318, "C": 0.006394, "D": 0.948971}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec5eA17Qr1ucNLUf", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Researchers are attempting to detect transits of two Earth-like planets: Planet_1 and Planet_2. They have limited observing time and want to observe the one that has the highest probability of transiting. Both of these planets have already been detected via the RV method, allowing us to know their minimum masses and orbital periods. Although both planets share the same masses, the orbital period of Planet_1 is three times shorter than that of Planet_2. Interestingly, they both have circular orbits. Furthermore, we know the masses and radii of the host stars of these two planets. The star hosting Planet_1 has a mass that is twice that of the host star of Planet_2. As the host of Planet_2 is slightly evolved, both host stars have the same radii. Based on the provided information, the researchers have chosen to observe:\n\nA) Planet_1 is preferred due to its ~2.7 times higher probability to transit.\nB) Planet_2 is preferred due to its ~1.5 times higher probability to transit.\nC) Planet_2 is preferred due to its ~2.25 times higher probability to transit.\nD) Planet_1 is preferred due to its ~1.65 times higher probability to transit.\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Researchers are attempting to detect transits of two Earth-like planets: Planet_1 and Planet_2. They have limited observing time and want to observe the one that has the highest probability of transiting. Both of these planets have already been detected via the RV method, allowing us to know their minimum masses and orbital periods. Although both planets share the same masses, the orbital period of Planet_1 is three times shorter than that of Planet_2. Interestingly, they both have circular orbits. Furthermore, we know the masses and radii of the host stars of these two planets. The star hosting Planet_1 has a mass that is twice that of the host star of Planet_2. As the host of Planet_2 is slightly evolved, both host stars have the same radii. Based on the provided information, the researchers have chosen to observe:\n\nA) Planet_1 is preferred due to its ~2.7 times higher probability to transit.\nB) Planet_2 is preferred due to its ~1.5 times higher probability to transit.\nC) Planet_2 is preferred due to its ~2.25 times higher probability to transit.\nD) Planet_1 is preferred due to its ~1.65 times higher probability to transit.\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "rec5eA17Qr1ucNLUf:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 23864, 40320, 34933, 395, 2454, 17921, 4335, 5542, 9621, 25, 132498, 5265, 11, 8253, 21090, 11, 326, 23976, 166736, 16300, 20102, 402, 8253, 4842, 326, 5265, 350, 15640, 23938, 885, 6914, 3954, 741, 134644, 37626, 503, 24043, 11, 40320, 34933, 42308, 230, 350, 49, 78943, 659, 460, 53840, 292, 11876, 64, 42308, 230, 460, 78943, 820, 261, 350, 72150, 2349, 175463, 741, 8820, 20102, 402, 96595, 536, 889, 37626, 364, 84787, 1309, 18051, 328, 100033, 398, 16, 27120, 17, 364, 39520, 460, 78943, 2684, 395, 2973, 30, 623, 4792, 5003, 2973, 5014, 13701, 679, 2684, 14041, 72, 350, 21910, 5014, 328, 40880, 62, 17, 16132, 48656, 11, 889, 21090, 12629, 741, 2632, 460, 78943, 2684, 364, 84787, 34933, 35353, 251, 220, 16, 820, 261, 13, 261, 16817, 656, 5265, 326, 5014, 8253, 4842, 364, 3879, 8280, 23938, 885, 6914, 3954, 25, 261, 61, 18, 314, 499, 391, 110943, 398, 61, 17, 820, 350, 19, 1345, 61, 17, 8, 350, 258, 13306, 741, 2632, 261, 35353, 251, 350, 44, 110943, 1546, 42923, 16, 14, 18, 92, 425, 398, 42923, 17, 14, 18, 92, 364, 84787, 34933, 35353, 251, 220, 16, 820, 261, 35353, 251, 350, 44, 110943, 1546, 183239, 16, 14, 18, 92, 425, 398, 183239, 17, 14, 18, 92, 364, 39520, 25, 40880, 62, 16, 5265, 314, 398, 26, 40880, 62, 17, 5265, 314, 220, 18, 47, 350, 21910, 3407, 4238, 7411, 3991, 17883, 4842, 25, 391, 16, 314, 220, 17, 425, 391, 17, 350, 16204, 328, 398, 16, 382, 18370, 4842, 328, 8253, 328, 398, 17, 741, 48765, 72, 2684, 364, 56734, 18051, 1402, 47, 16, 50074, 820, 398, 17, 50074, 314, 350, 44, 16, 183239, 16, 14, 18, 92, 425, 398, 16, 183239, 17, 14, 18, 9263, 820, 350, 44, 17, 183239, 16, 14, 18, 92, 425, 398, 17, 183239, 17, 14, 18, 9263, 314, 350, 44, 16, 17712, 17, 8, 183239, 16, 14, 18, 92, 425, 350, 47, 16, 27120, 17, 8, 183239, 17, 14, 18, 92, 364, 39520, 391, 16, 17712, 17, 314, 220, 17, 871, 350, 17, 8, 183239, 16, 14, 18, 92, 314, 220, 17, 183239, 15, 13, 15517, 1008, 92, 314, 220, 16, 820, 220, 17, 42923, 16, 14, 18, 16255, 220, 17, 42923, 16, 14, 18, 92, 42308, 230, 220, 16, 13, 2109, 11, 813, 12332, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 7767, 364, 47, 16, 27120, 17, 314, 220, 16, 14, 18, 11, 813, 350, 16, 14, 18, 8, 183239, 17, 14, 18, 92, 314, 350, 18, 8, 42923, 17, 14, 18, 16255, 220, 18, 42923, 17, 14, 18, 92, 314, 350, 18, 42923, 16, 14, 18, 9263, 61, 17, 42308, 230, 350, 16, 13, 37945, 73602, 17, 42308, 230, 220, 17, 13, 3062, 364, 84787, 18051, 314, 220, 15, 13, 7767, 425, 220, 17, 13, 3062, 42308, 230, 220, 16, 13, 2220, 364, 84787, 40880, 62, 16, 34933, 382, 1078, 220, 16, 13, 3898, 4238, 7266, 1572, 40880, 62, 17, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 364, 4383, 3880, 25, 415, 8, 40880, 62, 16, 382, 20146, 5192, 316, 6574, 16, 13, 3898, 4238, 7266, 34933, 364, 51113, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 13], "n_tokens": 536, "words": 196, "band_lo": 101.75, "band_hi": 407.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to compute transit probability for each planet given known parameters: orbital period, star radius, and semi-major axis depends on star mass and period (Kepler's third law). Assuming circular orbits, transit probability ≈ (R_star + R_planet)/a ≈ R_star / a (planet size negligible). Also depends on eccentricity but circular.\n\nThus need ratio of probabilities P1/P2.\n\nGiven R_star same for both? The problem says both host stars have same radii (since host of Planet_2 slightly evolved, but radius equal). So R_star same.\n\nThus probability ∝ 1 / a. a determined by period and host star mass.\n\nFrom Kepler's third law: a^3 = G M_* P^2 / (4π^2) (in units). So a ∝ (M_* )^{1/3} * P^{2/3}.\n\nThus probability ∝ 1 / a ∝ (M_* )^{-1/3} * P^{-2/3}.\n\nGiven: Planet_1 period = P; Planet_2 period = 3P (since three times longer).\n\nStar mass: M1 = 2 * M2 (star of P1 is twice mass of star of P2). Radii same.\n\nCompute ratio:\n\nP1_prob / P2_prob = (M1^{-1/3} * P1^{-2/3}) / (M2^{-1/3} * P2^{-2/3}) = (M1/M2)^{-1/3} * (P1/P2)^{-2/3}.\n\nGiven M1/M2 = 2 => (2)^{-1/3} = 2^{-0.333...} = 1 / 2^{1/3}. 2^{1/3} ≈ 1.26, so factor ≈ 0.79.\n\nP1/P2 = 1/3, so (1/3)^{-2/3} = (3)^{2/3}. 3^{2/3} = (3^{1/3})^2 ≈ (1.442)^2 ≈ 2.08.\n\nThus ratio = 0.79 * 2.08 ≈ 1.64.\n\nThus Planet_1 probability is about 1.65 times higher than Planet_2.\n\nThus answer D.\n\nCheck options: D) Planet_1 is preferred due to ~1.65 times higher probability.\n\nMatches.\n\nThus answer D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.05310810691617e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999947, "logp_correct": -5.30062662243636e-15, "letter_mass": 0.99999988079073, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.3501230377308645, "cot_lp_min64": -0.8605905231652385, "frac_below_-5": 0.0057361376673040155, "digit_lp_min": -4.589105129241943, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.08542785048484802, "n_cot_tokens": 523, "lp_end": -4.172316494077677e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -2.816466146640626, "p_nocot": 0.059816953991690736, "uplift": 2.8164661466406207, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.935695, "B": 0.003824, "C": 0.000665, "D": 0.059817}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec5gf9RftZN8uzUk", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: The majority of stars in our Galaxy form and evolve in multi-stellar systems. Below are five potential multi-star systems that are presented. How many of these systems can coexist?\n\nW Virginis type star, G2V, M4V, RGB star(1.5Msun) \n\nWD (B5 when in the MS) and A0V\n\nG2V, K1V, M5V\n\nDA4, L4\n\nWD (MS mass of 0.85Msun), K3V, A star with a mass of 0.9Msun in the MS.\n\nA) 1\nB) 4\nC) 2\nD) 3\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: The majority of stars in our Galaxy form and evolve in multi-stellar systems. Below are five potential multi-star systems that are presented. How many of these systems can coexist?\n\nW Virginis type star, G2V, M4V, RGB star(1.5Msun) \n\nWD (B5 when in the MS) and A0V\n\nG2V, K1V, M5V\n\nDA4, L4\n\nWD (MS mass of 0.85Msun), K3V, A star with a mass of 0.9Msun in the MS.\n\nA) 1\nB) 4\nC) 2\nD) 3\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "rec5gf9RftZN8uzUk:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 4928, 25, 392, 976, 14945, 328, 13701, 306, 1039, 25955, 1625, 326, 63070, 306, 12151, 6321, 83101, 7511, 13, 35307, 553, 6468, 6451, 12151, 32191, 7511, 484, 553, 13392, 13, 3253, 1991, 328, 1879, 7511, 665, 151434, 16842, 2632, 581, 679, 6468, 12073, 6451, 12151, 32191, 7511, 13, 6083, 1340, 413, 35805, 4149, 350, 55927, 8, 4335, 31060, 1299, 4842, 69972, 11, 26416, 25699, 11, 5178, 13, 57076, 1495, 1991, 328, 1879, 7511, 665, 151434, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 553, 89183, 4149, 741, 52762, 1151, 6052, 3880, 11095, 316, 2086, 328, 52235, 7511, 25, 220, 16, 11, 17, 11, 18, 11, 19, 364, 2167, 1309, 316, 21646, 2454, 2420, 395, 97666, 25, 7598, 8253, 7511, 2804, 413, 135113, 22105, 503, 4149, 4335, 71974, 26416, 25699, 350, 