12884, 395, 260, 128426, 3643, 3566, 350, 24, 487, 8, 472, 6211, 1347, 2846, 220, 16, 13, 19, 108313, 13, 5551, 1934, 334, 823, 90592, 11, 151067, 382, 11906, 11, 813, 290, 260, 128426, 3643, 3566, 382, 12299, 13, 2632, 290, 1721, 1888, 1757, 625, 679, 484, 12884, 13, 18774, 60592, 334, 823, 90592, 364, 7943, 1934, 44310, 328, 29674, 11896, 11, 151067, 11906, 11, 813, 581, 2665, 860, 260, 8287, 84, 13, 2632, 290, 478, 27029, 1757, 625, 2356, 290, 6211, 1347, 540, 220, 16, 13, 19, 364, 976, 110666, 440, 45023, 2208, 1888, 25, 18057, 413, 3333, 11, 10112, 261, 17524, 1347, 2846, 220, 21, 13, 20, 12, 22, 13, 20, 108313, 13, 32417, 3880, 1606, 355, 350, 51122, 1347, 540, 220, 21, 13, 24, 8, 382, 110666, 7967, 13, 30456, 413, 261, 8831, 1347, 8302, 591, 1209, 22572, 431, 5028, 26369, 76732, 1900, 350, 9995, 220, 16, 11, 19, 22572, 431, 5028, 26369, 8967, 1920, 3503, 1561, 625, 8831, 1347, 741, 2604, 10112, 290, 66669, 853, 261, 220, 16, 11, 17, 11, 18, 8302, 8117, 316, 8831, 1347, 30, 1367, 14994, 364, 976, 4928, 3572, 413, 11493, 484, 1934, 334, 823, 90592, 11, 290, 1606, 620, 12884, 34700, 23201, 328, 151067, 382, 128973, 328, 290, 260, 128426, 3643, 6211, 1347, 13, 2632, 1118, 12884, 382, 20395, 306, 1721, 1888, 30, 17158, 290, 6052, 382, 363, 25, 6211, 1347, 540, 220, 19, 13, 15, 108313, 66938, 316, 290, 10049, 17, 18618, 316, 68141, 350, 45, 12, 3583, 17, 8, 591, 448, 16111, 3643, 3566, 30, 3072, 290, 66669, 382, 21433, 110666, 939, 514, 26, 1934, 151067, 23201, 11, 481, 1340, 679, 261, 478, 13091, 1118, 48248, 11, 625, 6177, 13, 2632, 1606, 110666, 440, 45023, 12760, 11, 1118, 7680, 472, 261, 8831, 1347, 10112, 364, 58369, 2411, 328, 290, 6290, 8866, 25, 392, 45957, 298, 5039, 110666, 21433, 939, 514, 1, 2023, 413, 3543, 1299, 478, 49539, 2469, 563, 74, 49801, 41887, 30, 17158, 290, 939, 514, 382, 21433, 350, 49, 12, 3780, 13833, 39, 12401, 10047, 1299, 448, 448, 41887, 66383, 483, 3613, 32353, 3643, 3566, 13, 53650, 151067, 540, 68141, 25, 3168, 13833, 39, 8287, 432, 30, 50557, 36385, 21433, 939, 514, 483, 151067, 25, 481, 1625, 261, 41933, 129379, 25, 460, 17, 45, 12, 8310, 68091, 29920, 13, 2214, 21433, 939, 514, 11, 481, 717, 478, 8287, 432, 21433, 939, 514, 25, 319, 1940, 4213, 3168, 13833, 39, 12, 3583, 18, 4348, 472, 3168, 13833, 39, 12, 8310, 68091, 29920, 30, 24305, 11, 151067, 36385, 382, 395, 939, 514, 47081, 6502, 2892, 25, 460, 13833, 39, 17, 503, 460, 17, 57321, 5025, 41933, 313, 1381, 350, 49, 13833, 39, 12, 8310, 68091, 29920, 741, 2632, 448, 110666, 21433, 939, 514, 350, 68, 1940, 4213, 448, 41887, 483, 32353, 3643, 22148, 11823, 8, 1481, 413, 4348, 472, 261, 41933, 129379, 25, 3168, 13833, 39, 12, 8310, 68091, 29920, 13, 7801, 23201, 59684, 290, 939, 514, 2418, 364, 976, 1721, 1888, 350, 613, 29065, 8, 382, 1327, 290, 21433, 110666, 939, 514, 350, 3780, 13833, 39, 12401, 741, 730, 415, 17, 46, 11, 290, 48198, 120589, 738, 13041, 350, 2878, 6177, 741, 159165, 2351, 440, 45023, 2356, 17524, 12222, 364, 84787, 290, 478, 27029, 6960, 7398, 110666, 440, 45023, 472, 261, 17524, 1347, 11, 625, 1062, 1273, 29026, 13, 32417, 3880, 11, 1606, 355, 382, 110666, 7967, 350, 21, 13, 24, 108313, 741, 623, 8302, 392, 51122, 1347, 1, 1340, 413, 15693, 3166, 26, 1043, 1746, 1340, 89412, 484, 110666, 440, 45023, 472, 261, 8831, 1347, 65176, 21, 13, 24, 108313, 741, 2632, 6052, 355, 364, 84787, 357, 738, 6052, 355, 13], "n_tokens": 1145, "words": 715, "band_lo": 285.0, "band_hi": 1140.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse the chemistry question: \"An electroactive aromatic secondary amine was boc-protected to avoid the side reaction at room temperature. After further reaction to restore the electroactivity of the final product the 1H NMR in D2O shows a\n\nA) Triplet at 6.9 ppm\nB) Quartet at 1.6 ppm\nC) Singlet at 4.0 ppm\nD) Singlet at 1.4 ppm\"\n\nWe need to determine which signal corresponds to something expected after deprotecting BOC from secondary aromatic amine, in D2O solvent.\n\nLet's think: We start with aromatic secondary amine (e.g., an aniline derivative). We protect it with Boc (tert-butoxycarbonyl) to avoid side reactions. Later we deprotect (remove Boc) to restore the amine (which is electroactive). Then we run 1H NMR in D2O.\n\nThe final product likely contains an aromatic secondary amine (which may be protonated in D2O? but if amine is basic, it may be present as ammonium, exchangeable protons might disappear). In D2O, labile protons (NH) exchange with D, so NH proton not observed. So the signals observed are aromatic protons, maybe some aliphatic side chain.\n\nGiven answer choices, let's examine each:\n\nA) Triplet at 6.9 ppm — aromatic region (6-8 ppm). Triplet suggests aromatic proton pattern? In a mono-substituted benzene, you might see multiplets, not a simple triplet. Could be a para-disubstituted aromatic ring leading to two doublets, not a triplet. Triplet may correspond to a proton adjacent to two equivalent protons (e.g., CH2 next to CH3). So unlikely for aromatic.\n\nB) Quartet at 1.6 ppm — quartet in aliphatic region, typical for CH attached to CH3 (ethyl group). Might be part of a protecting group? The Boc group contains t-butyl (singlet at ~1.4 ppm) not quartet. So quartet at 1.6 could be from CH2 next to CH3 (ethyl). Not typical for Boc.\n\nC) Singlet at 4.0 ppm — a singlet at 4.0 ppm could be from an O-CH2 group (e.g., CH2 attached to oxygen). Maybe from the central carbon of the Boc group after deprotection? Not.\n\nD) Singlet at 1.4 ppm — typical signal for t-butyl group (9 H) as singlet around 1.4 ppm. However after deprotection, Boc is removed, so the t-butyl group is gone. So the final product should not have that signal. Unless incomplete deprotection.\n\nBut after restoration of electroactivity, Boc removed, so we expect no t-Bu. So the NMR should not show the singlet at 1.4.\n\nThe aromatic protons current product: Should be present, maybe a multiplet around 6.5-7.5 ppm. Among options only A (triplet at 6.9) is aromatic region. Could be a triplet pattern from para-disubstituted benzene (like 1,4-disubstituted gives two doublets not triplet). Or maybe the substrate has a 1,2,3 pattern leading to triplet? Unclear.\n\nThe question might be testing that after deprotection, the only new signal indicating removal of Boc is disappearance of the t-butyl singlet. So which signal is observed in final product? Maybe the answer is C: singlet at 4.0 ppm corresponds to the CH2 attached to nitrogen (N-CH2) from an ethyl group? But the substrate is secondary aromatic amine; after Boc removal, you may have a N-H which exchanges, not seen. So only aromatic protons remain, which appear as a triplet maybe.\n\nLet's think of the actual structure: \"Electroactive aromatic secondary amine\" could be something like N,N-dialkylaniline? Maybe the amine is secondary (R-Ph-NH-R'), like an aniline derivative with another alkyl group. Protect Boc at nitrogen: Ph-NH-Boc? Actually protecting secondary amine with Boc: you form a carbamate: R2N-COOtBu. For secondary amine, you get N-Boc secondary amine: e.g., Ph-NH-CH3 protected as Ph-NH-COOtBu? Wait, Boc protecting is for amine nucleophiles: R-NH2 or R2NH become carbamates (R-NH-COOtBu). So an aromatic secondary amine (e.g., aniline with alkyl substituent) would be protected as a carbamate: Ph-NH-COOtBu. Then removal yields the amine again.\n\nThe final product (deprotected) is just the secondary aromatic amine (Ph-NH-R). In D2O, the NH proton will exchange (not seen). Aromatic protons show multiplets.\n\nThus the NMR likely shows aromatic protons as a multiplet, not any other signals. Among options, only A is aromatic region (6.9 ppm). The pattern \"triplet\" may be simplification; their test may deem that aromatic protons as a triplet (~6.9 ppm). So answer A.\n\nThus I will answer A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.1086402082582936e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999984991, "logp_correct": -1.5009193036874195e-12, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000015008, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7379004675408719, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2325668902767148, "frac_below_-5": 0.01678445229681979, "digit_lp_min": -1.5608134269714355, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.4945070147514343, "n_cot_tokens": 1132, "lp_end": -3.576278118089249e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -2.535524883811135, "p_nocot": 0.07922012698795805, "uplift": 2.535524883809634, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.07922, "B": 0.167709, "C": 0.167709, "D": 0.585362}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec7s3hjpMU1sB9J1", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Calculate the pH of a mixture of 300 mL of 0.2 M HCN, 200 mL of 0.2 M HCl, and 350 mL of 0.2 M NaOH. Given that Ka (HCN) = 1.8 x 10^(-5).\n\nA) 5.25\nB) 8.78\nC) 1.33\nD) 4.74\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Calculate the pH of a mixture of 300 mL of 0.2 M HCN, 200 mL of 0.2 M HCl, and 350 mL of 0.2 M NaOH. Given that Ka (HCN) = 1.8 x 10^(-5).\n\nA) 5.25\nB) 8.78\nC) 1.33\nD) 4.74\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "rec7s3hjpMU1sB9J1:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 17950, 1721, 275, 39, 328, 27467, 328, 487, 37266, 350, 33285, 18655, 936, 487, 1388, 350, 9851, 18655, 936, 6888, 45485, 350, 9851, 3611, 741, 9069, 12948, 326, 49573, 1402, 33730, 45, 25, 220, 4095, 284, 43, 425, 220, 15, 13, 17, 391, 314, 220, 15, 13, 4095, 451, 425, 220, 15, 13, 17, 314, 220, 15, 13, 26669, 9192, 487, 37266, 198, 39, 1388, 25, 220, 1179, 284, 43, 425, 220, 15, 13, 17, 391, 314, 220, 15, 13, 1179, 451, 425, 220, 15, 13, 17, 314, 220, 15, 13, 23311, 9192, 487, 1388, 198, 11398, 45485, 25, 220, 11727, 284, 43, 425, 220, 15, 13, 17, 391, 314, 220, 15, 13, 11727, 451, 425, 220, 15, 13, 17, 314, 220, 15, 13, 31355, 9192, 6888, 45485, 279, 10620, 27467, 3609, 10945, 314, 220, 4095, 10, 1179, 10, 11727, 314, 220, 24987, 284, 43, 314, 220, 15, 13, 24987, 451, 364, 7127, 11, 5532, 18655, 57868, 27000, 2860, 25, 487, 1388, 18655, 137530, 483, 6888, 45485, 3611, 2747, 27000, 2860, 364, 44, 8795, 487, 1388, 314, 220, 15, 13, 23311, 11, 6888, 45485, 314, 15, 13, 31355, 13, 2632, 1934, 27000, 2860, 11, 16968, 6888, 45485, 314, 220, 15, 13, 31355, 533, 220, 15, 13, 23311, 314, 220, 15, 13, 24628, 9192, 6888, 45485, 15448, 350, 9851, 3611, 741, 3274, 382, 860, 487, 1388, 3561, 364, 10620, 581, 679, 13586, 18655, 487, 37266, 350, 15, 13, 26669, 9192, 8, 3333, 11, 2932, 16968, 5532, 3611, 220, 15, 13, 24628, 9192, 13, 129157, 25, 487, 37266, 659, 42464, 12, 2747, 37670, 12, 659, 487, 17, 46, 350, 117131, 2860, 741, 2632, 42464, 12, 738, 19069, 483, 13586, 18655, 45298, 364, 2167, 679, 220, 15, 13, 24628, 9192, 42464, 12, 350, 490, 1185, 8, 326, 220, 15, 13, 26669, 9192, 487, 37266, 13, 129157, 109297, 220, 15, 13, 24628, 9192, 487, 37266, 326, 220, 15, 13, 24628, 9192, 42464, 26300, 24168, 220, 15, 13, 24628, 9192, 37670, 12, 326, 15021, 220, 15, 13, 24628, 9192, 487, 37266, 537, 4637, 295, 364, 84787, 1934, 27000, 2860, 11, 581, 679, 1402, 33730, 45, 15448, 314, 220, 15, 13, 26669, 533, 220, 15, 13, 24628, 314, 220, 15, 13, 24628, 9192, 198, 37266, 12, 12790, 314, 220, 15, 13, 24628, 9192, 350, 2845, 27000, 2860, 8, 2932, 10112, 1062, 4871, 37670, 12, 30, 860, 364, 3160, 42464, 12, 3561, 350, 586, 47047, 741, 3004, 5532, 18655, 3561, 364, 5808, 1721, 7578, 8895, 261, 8573, 328, 13586, 18655, 487, 37266, 350, 15, 13, 24628, 9192, 8, 326, 1617, 89227, 379, 3611, 37670, 12, 350, 15, 13, 24628, 9192, 936, 12629, 22586, 871, 275, 39, 314, 275, 28611, 350, 21910, 18051, 314, 16, 741, 275, 28611, 314, 533, 2133, 702, 7, 28611, 8, 1919, 14234, 28, 16, 13, 23, 68, 12, 20, 871, 275, 28611, 314, 533, 2133, 702, 7, 16, 13, 23, 68, 12, 20, 8, 314, 220, 19, 13, 46240, 22, 33956, 364, 84787, 275, 39, 6574, 220, 19, 13, 9876, 13, 17796, 415, 364, 4383, 484, 62151, 328, 192577, 30, 275, 39, 328, 8573, 13313, 328, 27748, 538, 18051, 314, 16, 11, 889, 6290, 275, 39, 1340, 2171, 5192, 316, 171414, 9386, 503, 64723, 8150, 340, 5828, 11, 889, 68753, 275, 39, 314, 275, 28611, 314, 220, 19, 13, 9876, 13, 32417, 3880, 415, 220, 19, 13, 9876, 15248, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 364], "n_tokens": 577, "words": 226, "band_lo": 125.5, "band_hi": 502.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to calculate final pH of mixture of HCN (weak acid), HCl (strong acid), NaOH (strong base). Volumes and concentrations:\n\nHCN: 300 mL * 0.2 M = 0.300 L * 0.2 = 0.060 mol HCN\nHCl: 200 mL * 0.2 M = 0.200 L * 0.2 = 0.040 mol HCl\nNaOH: 350 mL * 0.2 M = 0.350 L * 0.2 = 0.070 mol NaOH\n\nNow mixture total volume = 300+200+350 = 850 mL = 0.850 L.\n\nFirst, strong acid/base neutralization: HCl acid reacts with NaOH base -> neutralization.\n\nMoles HCl = 0.040, NaOH =0.070. So after neutralization, excess NaOH = 0.070 - 0.040 = 0.030 mol NaOH remaining (strong base). There is no HCl left.\n\nNow we have weak acid HCN (0.060 mol) present, plus excess strong base 0.030 mol. Reaction: HCN + OH- -> CN- + H2O (neutralization). So OH- will react with weak acid partially.\n\nWe have 0.030 mol OH- (excess) and 0.060 mol HCN. Reaction consumes 0.030 mol HCN and 0.030 mol OH-, producing 0.030 mol CN- and leaving 0.030 mol HCN unreacted.\n\nThus after neutralization, we have:\n\nHCN remaining = 0.060 - 0.030 = 0.030 mol\nCN- produced = 0.030 mol (from neutralization) plus maybe any initial CN-? no.\n\nNo OH- left (all consumed). No strong acid left.\n\nSo final solution contains a buffer of weak acid HCN (0.030 mol) and its conjugate base CN- (0.030 mol), equal amounts => pH = pKa (since ratio =1). pKa = -log10(Ka) where Ka=1.8e-5 => pKa = -log10(1.8e-5) = 4.7447 approx.\n\nThus pH ~ 4.74. Option D.\n\nCheck that neglect of dilution? pH of buffer independent of concentration if ratio =1, but actual pH may differ due to ionic strength or autoprotolysis, but approximate pH = pKa = 4.74. Among options D 4.74 matches.\n\nThus answer D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.823885577323381e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999831, "logp_correct": -1.6906371479682115e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000169, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.3770134829060286, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9612317155799532, "frac_below_-5": 0.010638297872340425, "digit_lp_min": -4.264163494110107, "answer_first_mention_lp": -3.576278118089249e-07, "n_cot_tokens": 564, "lp_end": -1.744232416152954, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -2.5283430715424555, "p_nocot": 0.07979111899234069, "uplift": 2.5283430715424386, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.459166, "B": 0.001877, "C": 0.459166, "D": 0.079791}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec8JPf9jHanVNzdH", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: The Paranal Observatory is situated in Chile at approximately 24 degrees south latitude and approximately 70 degrees west longitude, making it one of the world's premier observatories. The ESPRESSO spectrograph is widely regarded as the most stable and precise instrument for measuring radial velocity, which is crucial for planet hunting and testing cosmological constants. To ensure optimal observing conditions in terms of airmass, which of the following stars would you recommend for observation?\n\nStar1: RA = 15 deg, Dec = -26 deg, Vmag = 9 mag\nStar2: RA = 2 deg, Dec = +14 deg, Vmag = 7.5 mag\nStar3: RA = 70 deg, Dec = -34 deg, Vmag = 7.0 mag\nStar4: RA = 5 h, Dec = 70 deg, Vmag = 9.0 mag\n\nA) Star4\nB) Star2\nC) Star3\nD) Star1\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: The Paranal Observatory is situated in Chile at approximately 24 degrees south latitude and approximately 70 degrees west longitude, making it one of the world's premier observatories. The ESPRESSO spectrograph is widely regarded as the most stable and precise instrument for measuring radial velocity, which is crucial for planet hunting and testing cosmological constants. To ensure optimal observing conditions in terms of airmass, which of the following stars would you recommend for observation?\n\nStar1: RA = 15 deg, Dec = -26 deg, Vmag = 9 mag\nStar2: RA = 2 deg, Dec = +14 deg, Vmag = 7.5 mag\nStar3: RA = 70 deg, Dec = -34 deg, Vmag = 7.0 mag\nStar4: RA = 5 h, Dec = 70 deg, Vmag = 9.0 mag\n\nA) Star4\nB) Star2\nC) Star3\nD) Star1\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "rec8JPf9jHanVNzdH:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11433, 24174, 77701, 6409, 306, 5941, 328, 261, 2835, 456, 395, 3371, 15134, 133278, 350, 8100, 6574, 533, 1494, 148177, 19395, 6574, 533, 2789, 6793, 741, 623, 261, 2835, 456, 382, 150477, 1261, 8253, 1643, 1348, 5862, 39529, 437, 350, 2885, 1493, 387, 540, 11639, 66032, 741, 26421, 5962, 540, 40320, 314, 220, 2744, 6793, 533, 1022, 8100, 533, 1885, 91, 350, 366, 1885, 853, 2684, 2232, 741, 50557, 66032, 314, 220, 2744, 6793, 533, 350, 56795, 533, 6567, 3273, 8, 395, 1885, 2684, 2232, 30, 5694, 20690, 25, 66032, 540, 40320, 314, 220, 2744, 6793, 533, 1022, 56795, 533, 6567, 3273, 91, 13, 2632, 316, 41776, 261, 2835, 456, 11, 1682, 66032, 472, 1932, 472, 4149, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 1022, 8100, 533, 1885, 91, 472, 3291, 472, 4149, 13, 3371, 15134, 4029, 314, 533, 1494, 172051, 45438, 395, 2454, 8253, 1402, 17883, 16, 5244, 533, 2109, 148177, 9809, 314, 196488, 1494, 533, 21382, 2109, 47976, 314, 196488, 1494, 10, 2109, 91, 314, 220, 17, 172051, 66032, 314, 220, 2744, 533, 220, 17, 314, 220, 2843, 172051, 18956, 1932, 364, 17883, 17, 5244, 659, 1265, 148177, 8868, 314, 196488, 1494, 533, 36088, 1265, 47976, 314, 196488, 3150, 91, 314, 220, 3150, 172051, 66032, 314, 220, 2744, 533, 220, 3150, 314, 220, 6283, 6793, 364, 17883, 18, 5244, 533, 3020, 148177, 8868, 314, 196488, 1494, 533, 21382, 3020, 47976, 314, 196488, 1494, 10, 3020, 91, 314, 220, 702, 172051, 66032, 314, 220, 2241, 6793, 364, 17883, 19, 5244, 220, 2789, 148177, 8868, 314, 196488, 1494, 533, 220, 2789, 91, 314, 196488, 10545, 91, 314, 220, 10545, 172051, 66032, 314, 533, 19, 6793, 350, 74257, 67628, 741, 2632, 625, 81778, 364, 84787, 1636, 261, 2835, 456, 382, 11307, 16, 350, 2843, 6793, 741, 8820, 1309, 3331, 46104, 15001, 316, 1058, 30, 54666, 3563, 5734, 30, 3072, 24174, 261, 2835, 456, 382, 13313, 328, 46104, 26, 1062, 1058, 481, 2023, 12262, 1261, 8253, 1643, 1348, 5862, 54213, 5178, 13, 5551, 46104, 19854, 395, 1261, 4275, 869, 540, 4856, 13, 3371, 15134, 1701, 533, 2789, 148177, 46104, 328, 54213, 47245, 483, 5217, 13, 3072, 290, 4928, 31064, 392, 45212, 24174, 77701, 6409, 306, 5941, 328, 261, 2835, 456, 4050, 2632, 5655, 8253, 483, 11639, 66032, 575, 2560, 4213, 11307, 16, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 415, 364, 4383, 3880, 25, 415, 8, 11307, 16, 364, 84787, 4733, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 415], "n_tokens": 414, "words": 183, "band_lo": 91.0, "band_hi": 364.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to determine optimal observing conditions in terms of airmass for Paranal Observatory (lat ~ -24°, lon ~ -70°). The airmass is minimized when star transits near zenith (culmination at highest altitude). Altitude at transit = 90° - |lat - dec| (if dec has same sign). Actually altitude = 90° - (latitude - declination) for dec same sign? General formula: altitude at transit = 90° - |latitude - declination|. So to minimize airmass, want altitude as high as possible, i.e., |lat - dec| as small as possible. Paranal lat = -24°. Compute for each star:\n\nStar1 Dec -26°, difference = |-24 - (-26)| = |-24+26| = 2°. altitude = 90 - 2 = 88°. Very high.\n\nStar2 Dec +14°, diff = |-24 - (+14)| = |-38| = 38°. altitude = 90 - 38 = 52°.\n\nStar3 Dec -34°, diff = |-24 - (-34)| = |-24+34| = 10°. altitude = 80°.\n\nStar4 Dec 70°, diff = |-24 - 70| = |-94| = 94°. altitude = -4° (never rises). So not observable.\n\nThus best airmass is Star1 (88°). Also need consider RA relative to time? Observability window? But optimal airmass is independent of RA; any time you could schedule when star transits near midnight etc. However RA matters for when it's up at night. Paranal long -70°, RA of midnight varies with season. But the question asks \"ensure optimal observing conditions in terms of airmass\". So choose star with highest altitude i.e., Star1.\n\nThus answer: D.\n\nCheck options: D) Star1.\n\nThus output: ANSWER: D", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.4305109630186053e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999786, "logp_correct": -2.147231072976405e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999991655356838, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.40092861935716556, "cot_lp_min64": -1.0805970409073753, "frac_below_-5": 0.0, "digit_lp_min": -4.315410137176514, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0003363520372658968, "n_cot_tokens": 401, "lp_end": -0.043956954032182693, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -6.751170662232066, "p_nocot": 0.0011695097182438541, "uplift": 6.751170662232044, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1e-06, "C": 0.99883, "D": 0.00117}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec9AH3EU6WzDzsZ6", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Astronomers are studying a star with a 1.8 solar radius and 1.4 solar masses. When the star's surface is not covered by plages, its Teff is 6000K. However, when 30% of its surface is covered by plages, the overall photospheric effective temperature changes by 1000 K. In the stellar photosphere, when examining the ratio of the number of neutral atoms of Si in two energetic levels (level 1 and level 2), astronomers have observed that this ratio changes when the star has plages. What is the factor by which this ratio changes when the star has plages compared to when it does not have plages? Note that the transition between the energy levels under consideration corresponds to nIR wavelength of approximately 14404 Å. Assume that the stellar photosphere is in LTE.\n\nA) ~1.47\nB) ~1.39\nC) ~1.27\nD) ~1.16\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Astronomers are studying a star with a 1.8 solar radius and 1.4 solar masses. When the star's surface is not covered by plages, its Teff is 6000K. However, when 30% of its surface is covered by plages, the overall photospheric effective temperature changes by 1000 K. In the stellar photosphere, when examining the ratio of the number of neutral atoms of Si in two energetic levels (level 1 and level 2), astronomers have observed that this ratio changes when the star has plages. What is the factor by which this ratio changes when the star has plages compared to when it does not have plages? Note that the transition between the energy levels under consideration corresponds to nIR wavelength of approximately 14404 Å. Assume that the stellar photosphere is in LTE.\n\nA) ~1.47\nB) ~1.39\nC) ~1.27\nD) ~1.16\n", "true_answer": 