3, 36839, 22, 9, 16, 13, 17820, 18, 8, 314, 44237, 7, 17, 13, 25397, 8, 314, 16, 13, 40441, 24, 364, 637, 13306, 1919, 41051, 28, 16, 14, 17, 30, 19792, 2371, 13, 623, 37598, 67382, 395, 2127, 64255, 382, 220, 16, 14, 17, 2454, 350, 21910, 723, 55, 64255, 9539, 72, 741, 730, 1039, 16083, 483, 2127, 314, 350, 64, 659, 261, 78115, 11876, 103946, 17, 11, 37598, 67382, 382, 220, 16, 14, 17, 13, 41351, 395, 37598, 11, 2969, 253, 101, 55, 61, 17, 158, 253, 102, 314, 158, 253, 101, 47, 61, 17, 158, 253, 102, 314, 16, 14, 17, 13, 2632, 1039, 46716, 4233, 3528, 1424, 220, 15, 13, 20, 11, 8975, 364, 84787, 33756, 314, 44237, 161017, 631, 8, 314, 44237, 7, 16611, 55, 61, 17, 26143, 47, 61, 17, 8, 314, 16, 13, 40441, 24, 364, 70872, 4475, 110875, 104037, 395, 4590, 40174, 103362, 25, 336, 314, 350, 954, 10, 220, 16, 14, 17, 8, 68859, 7, 954, 10, 16, 14, 17, 8, 533, 350, 954, 12, 16, 14, 17, 8, 68859, 7, 954, 12, 16, 14, 17, 741, 41021, 23864, 364, 954, 10, 15, 13, 20, 314, 220, 16, 13, 34589, 24, 42308, 230, 220, 17, 13, 15, 13, 68859, 7, 17, 8, 314, 15, 13, 48779, 16, 13, 137658, 25, 220, 16, 13, 34589, 24, 9, 15, 13, 48779, 16, 314, 16, 13, 33924, 364, 954, 12, 15, 13, 20, 314, 220, 15, 13, 34589, 24, 42308, 230, 16, 13, 15, 13, 68859, 7, 15, 13, 34589, 24, 8, 42308, 230, 533, 15, 13, 7659, 16, 13, 137658, 25, 220, 15, 13, 34589, 24, 425, 21382, 15, 13, 7659, 16, 25987, 533, 15, 13, 7659, 16, 364, 178819, 25, 336, 314, 220, 16, 13, 33924, 533, 21382, 15, 13, 7659, 16, 8, 314, 220, 16, 13, 33248, 364, 84787, 2893, 13112, 64, 28810, 536, 42308, 230, 16, 13, 3255, 364, 61949, 3880, 25, 363, 8, 220, 16, 13, 3150, 15248, 364, 198140, 6052, 363, 13], "n_tokens": 3095, "words": 799, "band_lo": 314.0, "band_hi": 1256.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need compute non-Gaussianity via relative entropy: S(rho||tau)=Tr(rho ln rho)-Tr(rho ln tau). Actually given delta_b = Tr(rho ln rho)-Tr(tau ln tau). Wait they wrote tau in both traces but typical definition is difference in entropies: S(tau) - S(rho)?? But they gave expression. Anyway need numeric answer for given phi and alpha.\n\nProbably known that Schrödinger cat with these parameters gives certain relative entropy maybe ~0.25 or ~1.38 etc. Let's attempt compute approximate.\n\nWe have state |psi> normalized. The density matrix rho = |psi><psi| (pure). For pure state, S(rho)= -Tr(rho ln rho) = 0 (von Neumann entropy). However they have Tr(rho ln rho) which is 0? Actually for pure state, eigenvalues are 1 and zeros, ln(1)=0, so Tr(rho ln rho)=0. So first term zero.\n\nSecond term: -Tr(tau ln tau) with a minus? They have minus sign? delta_b = [trace(rho* ln(rho))-trace(tau* ln(tau))]. So delta_b = 0 - Tr(tau ln tau) = -Tr(tau ln tau) = S(tau) (since S(tau) = -Tr(tau ln tau)). So delta_b = S(tau). So non-Gaussianity equals the entropy of reference Gaussian state.\n\nReference Gaussian state tau is the Gaussian state with same first and second moments as rho (the cat). For pure states, the Gaussian reference is a thermal (mixed) state maybe.\n\nWe need compute the covariance matrix of the cat state and find its entropy. For a single mode, entropy of a Gaussian state depends only on symplectic eigenvalue ν >=1/2: S = (ν+1/2)ln(ν+1/2)-(ν-1/2)ln(ν-1/2). Actually for a single-mode Gaussian with mean zero, the von Neumann entropy is f(ν) where ν = sqrt(det(V))/ (ħ/2)?? Usually in units ħ=1: ν = sqrt(det(V)) . And S = (ν+1/2)ln(ν+1/2) - (ν-1/2)ln(ν-1/2). If ν = 1/2 (pure Gaussian), S=0.\n\nSo we need compute covariance matrix of cat state.\n\nLet’s compute quadrature operators X = (a + a†)/√2, P = (a - a†)/(i√2). For coherent state |α>, mean values: ⟨X⟩ = √2 Re α, ⟨P⟩ = √2 Im α. For | -α>, means opposite.\n\nOur cat superposition has amplitude α real? α =0.5, real positive. Phase φ = -π/4. So cos φ = cos(-π/4)= √2/2 ≈0.7071, sin φ = sin(-π/4)= -√2/2 ≈ -0.7071.\n\nThus state |psi> = (c|α> + s| -α>)/N where c=0.7071, s=-0.7071.\n\nNormalization N = sqrt(1+ sin(2 φ)*exp(-2 α^2)). Compute sin(2 φ) = sin(-π/2) = -1. So N= sqrt(1 + (-1)*exp(-2*0.25)) = sqrt(1 - exp(-0.5)). exp(-0.5)=0.60653. So N = sqrt(1 -0.60653)= sqrt(0.39347)=0.6275.\n\nNow compute first moments: ⟨a⟩ = ?\n\n|psi> combination gives expectation linear in amplitudes: For pure state superposition, ⟨a⟩ = (|c|^2 ⟨α|a|α⟩ + |s|^2⟨-α|a|-α⟩ + c* s ⟨α|a|-α⟩ + s* c ⟨-α|a|α⟩ ) / N^2.\n\nBut ⟨α|a|α⟩ = α, ⟨-α|a|-α⟩ = -α. Cross terms: ⟨α|a|-α⟩ = ⟨α|-α⟩ (-α)?? Actually a acting on |-α⟩ yields -α | -α⟩. So ⟨α|a|-α⟩ = -α ⟨α|-α⟩. Similarly ⟨-α|a|α⟩ = α ⟨-α|α⟩ = α ⟨α|-α⟩*.\n\nOverlap: ⟨α|-α⟩ = exp(-2|α|^2) = exp(-2*0.25)=exp(-0.5)=0.60653 (real).\n\nThus compute.\n\nLet c = 0.7071, s = -0.7071. |c|^2 =0.5, |s|^2 =0.5.\n\nNumerator: 0.5 * α + 0.5 * (-α) = 0 (they cancel). Cross terms: c* s = 0.7071 * (-0.7071) = -0.5. Then c*s ⟨α|a|-α⟩ = (-0.5)*(-α* overlap) = (-0.5)*(-α*0.6065) = 0.5* α*0.6065 = 0.30325 α.\n\nSimilarly s*c = same (-0.5). Times ⟨-α|a|α⟩ = α*overlap = α*0.6065 => (-0.5)*α*0.6065 = -0.30325 α.\n\nSum cross contributions: 0.30325α + (-0.30325α) =0. So total numerator zero. So ⟨a⟩ =0. Therefore first moments zero.\n\nThus the Gaussian reference will have zero displacement (centered). So covariance determined by second moments.\n\nCompute ⟨a† a⟩ = average photon number.\n\nFor coherent |α> number = |α|^2=0.25. For |-α> same.\n\nCross term: ⟨α|a† a|-α⟩ = α* (-α) ⟨α|-α⟩ + something? Actually a† a acting on |-α>: a| -α> = -α|-α>, then a† gives? Actually a† a |-α> = a† (-α |-α>) = -α a† |-α>. Not straightforward. Simpler: Use formula ⟨β|a† a|γ⟩ = β*γ ⟨β|γ⟩. Indeed because a|γ⟩ = γ |γ⟩ ; ⟨β|a† = β* ⟨β|. So ⟨β|a† a|γ⟩ = β* γ ⟨β|γ⟩. So for β=α, γ=-α: β*γ = α*(-α) = -α^2 = -0.25. Overlap = 0.6065. So cross term = -0.25 *0.6065 = -0.1516.\n\nNow compute expectation:\n\n⟨a† a⟩ = (|c|^2 *0.25 + |s|^2*0.25 + c* s * (-0.1516) + s* c * (-0.1516) ) / N^2.\n\nc*s = -0.5, s*c = -0.5. So sum cross = -0.5*(-0.1516) + -0.5*(-0.1516) = 0.1516*0.5? Wait compute: -0.5 * (-0.1516) = 0.0758. Two terms => 0.1516.\n\nFirst term: |c|^2*0.25 + |s|^2*0.25 = 0.5*0.25 + 0.5*0.25 =0.25.\n\nSo numerator = 0.25 + 0.1516 =0.4016.\n\nN^2 = (0.6275)^2 =0.3935 (matches earlier). So expectation =0.4016/0.3935 =1.0205.\n\nThus average photon number ≈1.02.\n\nNow compute quadrature variances. Need V = [[⟨X^2⟩, ⟨XP+PX⟩/2], ...]. Since first moments zero, variance equals ⟨X^2⟩.\n\nRecall X = (a + a†)/√2.\n\nThus X^2 = (a^2 + a†^2 + a a† + a† a)/2.\n\nWe need ⟨a^2⟩, ⟨a†2⟩ (complex conj), and ⟨a a†⟩ = ⟨a† a⟩ +1.\n\nCompute ⟨a^2⟩.\n\nFor coherent |α>, ⟨a^2⟩ = α^2 = 0.25. For |-α> gives α^2 also? (-α)^2 = α^2 =0.25. Cross: ⟨α|a^2|-α⟩ = α^2 ⟨α|-α⟩ =0.25*0.6065=0.1516.\n\nSimilarly ⟨-α|a^2|α⟩ = α^2 ⟨-α|α⟩ = same 0.1516.\n\nNow compute ⟨a^2⟩ using same weighting.\n\nNumerator: 0.5*0.25 +0.5*0.25 =0.25. Cross terms: c*s *0.1516 + s*c*0.1516 = (-0.5)*0.1516 + (-0.5)*0.1516 = -0.1516.\n\nTotal numerator = 0.25 -0.1516 =0.0984.\n\nDivide by N^2=0.3935 => ⟨a^2⟩ =0.0984/0.3935=0.2502.\n\nInteresting close to 0.25.\n\nThus ⟨a†2⟩ = (⟨a^2⟩)* (since real?) α real => expectation real, so conj same: 0.2502.\n\nNow compute ⟨X^2⟩ = (⟨a^2⟩ + ⟨a†2⟩ + ⟨a a†⟩ + ⟨a† a⟩)/2.\n\nWe have ⟨a† a⟩ =1.0205. Then ⟨a a†⟩ = ⟨a† a⟩ +1 = 2.0205.\n\nSum: ⟨a^2⟩+⟨a†2⟩ = 0.5004.\n\nAdd ⟨a a†⟩ + ⟨a† a⟩ = 2.0205+1.0205=3.0410.\n\nTotal = 3.5414.\n\nDivide by 2 => ⟨X^2⟩ =1.7707.\n\nSimilarly P quadrature variance: P = (a - a†)/(i√2). Then P^2 = -(a^2 + a†2 - a a† - a† a)/2. Expectation yields same because minus signs.\n\nCompute ⟨P^2⟩ = ( -⟨a^2⟩ -⟨a†2⟩ +⟨a a†⟩ +⟨a† a⟩)/2.\n\nPlug numbers: -0.5004 + 3.0410 =2.5406. Divide by 2 => 1.2703.\n\nThus covariance matrix diag (ΔX^2, ΔP^2) = (1.7707,1.2703). Cross terms zero because symmetric and first moments zero, real state.\n\nNow compute symplectic eigenvalue ν = sqrt(ΔX^2 * ΔP^2) = sqrt(1.7707*1.2703) = sqrt(2.247) =1.4989.\n\nIn units where ħ=1/2? Need check. The vacuum variance for X,P is 1/2 each (since [X,P]=i). In our definition with X = (a + a†)/√2, vacuum variance is 1/2. Indeed for vacuum, ⟨X^2⟩ =⟨P^2⟩ =1/2. So our computed variances > 0.5, fine.\n\nThus ν = sqrt(det V) = sqrt(ΔX^2 ΔP^2) =1.4989.\n\nVon Neumann entropy for single-mode Gaussian: S = (ν+ 1/2) ln(ν+1/2) - (ν-1/2) ln(ν-1/2). Let's compute.\n\nν+0.5 = 1.9989 ≈ 2.0. ln(2) =0.6931. Multiply: 1.9989*0.6931 =1.385.\n\nν-0.5 = 0.9989 ≈1.0. ln(0.9989) ≈ -0.0011. Multiply: 0.9989 * (-0.0011)= -0.0011.\n\nSubtract: S = 1.385 - (-0.0011) = 1.386.\n\nThus non-Gaussianity ≈1.39.\n\nAmong options: C) 1.38 matches.\n\nHence answer C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.6569954823353328e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999942, "logp_correct": -5.757070878259464e-15, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000058, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.2750552485704795, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1510072960288333, "frac_below_-5": 0.0022712524334847503, "digit_lp_min": -2.4097862243652344, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.04373663291335106, "n_cot_tokens": 3082, "lp_end": -2.3841830625315197e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -4.768370445162873e-07, "logp_nocot": -5.918640480802583, "p_nocot": 0.002688853240785839, "uplift": 5.918640480802578, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.005692, "B": 0.146806, "C": 0.002689, "D": 0.844812}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec9mLiVJz3PC7NZJ", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A series of experiments are conducted to unravel the function of a novel kinase X in cell survival. Overexpression of a full-length WT kinase X has no effect on cell viability while overexpression of a kinase dead (KD) variant impairs viability minimally. Additionally, a CRISPR experiment is conducted using two sgRNAs designed to the n-terminus of kinase X and two sgRNAs designed to the c-terminus. The two c-terminal sgRNAs are lethal while the two n-terminal ones only have a minimal negative effect on cell viability. When a western blot is run it is observed that all the sgRNAs are equally efficacious against the canonical form of kinase X. Unexpectedly, a smaller molecular weight band is observed to also be strongly depleted by the c-terminal but not the n-terminal sgRNAs. Overexpression of a WT or KD CRISPR-resistant kinase X completely rescues the cell viability decrease caused by the n-terminal and c-terminal sgRNAs. Two different tool compounds designed to inhibit the kinase function of X are strongly lethal. An in vitro kinase panel run for both compounds demonstrates that both compounds strongly inhibit kinase X as well as several other kinases. What is the best explanation of these results?\n\nA) The kinase activity of kinase X is not required for cell survival. A n-terminally truncated variant of kinase X has alternative functions that are essential for cell survival.\nB) The kinase activity of kinase X is required for cell survival. This is demonstrated by the effect of the kinase X inhibitors as well as the rescue of the c-terminal sgRNAs.\nC) Kinase X is required for cell survival. This is demonstrated by the rescue of the c-terminal sgRNAs.\nD) The kinase activity of kinase X is not required for cell survival. The kinase activity of a n-terminally truncated variant of kinase X is essential for cell survival.\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A series of experiments are conducted to unravel the function of a novel kinase X in cell survival. Overexpression of a full-length WT kinase X has no effect on cell viability while overexpression of a kinase dead (KD) variant impairs viability minimally. Additionally, a CRISPR experiment is conducted using two sgRNAs designed to the n-terminus of kinase X and two sgRNAs designed to the c-terminus. The two c-terminal sgRNAs are lethal while the two n-terminal ones only have a minimal negative effect on cell viability. When a western blot is run it is observed that all the sgRNAs are equally efficacious against the canonical form of kinase X. Unexpectedly, a smaller molecular weight band is observed to also be strongly depleted by the c-terminal but not the n-terminal sgRNAs. Overexpression of a WT or KD CRISPR-resistant kinase X completely rescues the cell viability decrease caused by the n-terminal and c-terminal sgRNAs. Two different tool compounds designed to inhibit the kinase function of X are strongly lethal. An in vitro kinase panel run for both compounds demonstrates that both compounds strongly inhibit kinase X as well as several other kinases. What is the best explanation of these results?\n\nA) The kinase activity of kinase X is not required for cell survival. A n-terminally truncated variant of kinase X has alternative functions that are essential for cell survival.\nB) The kinase activity of kinase X is required for cell survival. This is demonstrated by the effect of the kinase X inhibitors as well as the rescue of the c-terminal sgRNAs.\nC) Kinase X is required for cell survival. This is demonstrated by the rescue of the c-terminal sgRNAs.\nD) The kinase activity of kinase X is not required for cell survival. The kinase activity of a n-terminally truncated variant of kinase X is essential for cell survival.\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "rec9mLiVJz3PC7NZJ:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 4218, 12527, 13, 7931, 61248, 112768, 860, 3159, 26, 115765, 16914, 3282, 6498, 120140, 160870, 13, 2632, 141476, 8110, 625, 8731, 30, 115765, 853, 12644, 12626, 3159, 12951, 42647, 126970, 364, 7027, 2291, 8621, 25, 42406, 165180, 316, 478, 12, 26821, 385, 10635, 19957, 12626, 3159, 26, 363, 155991, 42406, 165180, 108292, 13, 2545, 42406, 165180, 27082, 1917, 91323, 3149, 53970, 2127, 29293, 13, 3072, 261, 13679, 58920, 8177, 350, 30641, 135947, 47984, 700, 8, 382, 157056, 656, 363, 155991, 42406, 165180, 889, 625, 478, 155991, 42406, 165180, 13, 2632, 478, 155991, 42406, 165180, 6092, 6047, 26, 1023, 1340, 2928, 7668, 135947, 16737, 30, 24305, 11, 538, 1023, 3783, 478, 12, 26821, 385, 11, 1023, 1481, 7075, 25516, 100209, 6047, 11, 98989, 842, 3149, 53970, 26, 889, 290, 13679, 47984, 700, 1340, 1604, 85328, 328, 478, 13560, 7967, 11, 813, 478, 155991, 42406, 165180, 1481, 625, 7902, 480, 2236, 47984, 700, 71357, 478, 12, 26821, 385, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 8812, 111985, 741, 363, 155991, 42406, 165180, 3783, 7967, 3333, 306, 2973, 3149, 53970, 326, 135947, 47984, 700, 11, 13972, 334, 7817, 2973, 13, 623, 135947, 47984, 700, 382, 8731, 395, 31304, 350, 21910, 1617, 166998, 108292, 741, 7931, 61248, 328, 112768, 503, 115765, 32177, 2973, 478, 326, 363, 42406, 49212, 21937, 46235, 23379, 484, 32177, 5882, 2236, 1072, 23787, 295, 12871, 350, 7027, 2291, 8621, 58799, 8, 6008, 290, 135947, 47984, 700, 30, 24305, 12871, 382, 3149, 53970, 30, 1225, 49094, 1406, 2973, 11, 10915, 290, 8731, 1114, 382, 5181, 656, 290, 1072, 23787, 295, 16737, 115053, 328, 141476, 8110, 13, 2632, 141476, 8110, 625, 3759, 13, 623, 8731, 1114, 382, 12951, 158412, 1114, 328, 135947, 16914, 13, 2632, 6052, 25, 355, 26751, 25, 141476, 8110, 625, 3759, 26, 478, 155991, 132880, 16914, 853, 13319, 9964, 8731, 13, 17796, 415, 5003, 141476, 8110, 328, 478, 155991, 135947, 16914, 382, 8731, 13, 3072, 581, 8274, 141476, 9224, 2928, 49094, 1406, 120140, 11, 813, 8110, 625, 6118, 13, 2632, 355, 382, 1636, 364, 4383, 3880, 25, 355, 8, 141476, 8110, 625, 3759, 26, 261, 478, 155991, 423, 135947, 16914, 853, 13319, 9964, 8731, 13, 4569, 15248, 364, 84787, 6052, 355, 364], "n_tokens": 376, "words": 277, "band_lo": 239.75, "band_hi": 959.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to understand experiment. Overexpression WT no effect; KD variant impairs viability minimally. So kinase activity not essential? KD has slight negative effect perhaps dominant-negative.\n\nCRISPR: sgRNAs to N-terminus produce minimal negative effect; C-terminal sgRNAs lethal. All sgRNAs knock down canonical full-length X equally. But a smaller MW band (likely truncated isoform) is depleted by C-terminal sgRNAs but not N-terminal sgRNAs. So N-terminal sgRNAs cut early; they may still leave truncated protein? Wait, if they target N-terminus, they would cause frameshift early, knocking out full-length; but the smaller isoform may start downstream of N-term region, so N-terminal sgRNAs would not affect it because isoform lacks N-terminus (i.e., internal promoter). C-terminal sgRNAs target region present in both full-length and truncated isoform, thus deplete both. The truncated isoform is essential for survival (since its depletion lethal). Overexpression of WT or KD rescue both N and C sgRNA phenotypes suggests that rescue works because overexpressed construct (CRISPR-resistant) provides the truncated isoform? Wait construct is full-length? It rescues both, meaning the essential function is provided by the overexpressed protein irrespective of kinase activity. So kinase activity not required. The essential function is perhaps scaffold function of truncated variant. So answer: A describes: kinase activity not required; N-terminal trunc variant has alternative functions essential. Option D says kinase activity of N-terminal truncated variant is essential. But we saw kinase dead still rescues viability, so activity not needed. So A is best.\n\nCheck options: A) kinase activity not required; a N-terminally truncated variant has alternative functions essential. That matches.\n\nThus answer A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.0531076592742465e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999514, "logp_correct": -4.862971038395036e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000486, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6539320460823677, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9053007574882637, "frac_below_-5": 0.008264462809917356, "digit_lp_min": null, "answer_first_mention_lp": -1.344942569732666, "n_cot_tokens": 363, "lp_end": -0.0003023882454726845, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -0.12730750278405892, "p_nocot": 0.8804628861767417, "uplift": 0.1273075027840103, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.880463, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.000379, "D": 0.119158}}, "force_keep": false, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rec9ubQihAh6g9bft", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A student regrets that he fell asleep during a lecture in electrochemistry, facing the following incomplete statement in a test:\nThermodynamically, oxygen is a …… oxidant in basic solutions. Kinetically, oxygen reacts …… in acidic solutions.\nWhich combination of weaker/stronger and faster/slower is correct?\n\nA) weaker - slower\nB) stronger – faster\nC) stronger – slower\nD) weaker – faster\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A student regrets that he fell asleep during a lecture in electrochemistry, facing the following incomplete statement in a test:\nThermodynamically, oxygen is a …… oxidant in basic solutions. Kinetically, oxygen reacts …… in acidic solutions.\nWhich combination of weaker/stronger and faster/slower is correct?\n\nA) weaker - slower\nB) stronger – faster\nC) stronger – slower\nD) weaker – faster\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "rec9ubQihAh6g9bft:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 9710, 25, 392, 62256, 4847, 5698, 2905, 11, 34398, 382, 261, 178796, 45266, 493, 306, 9439, 9368, 13, 658, 12559, 2905, 11, 34398, 137530, 178796, 306, 124714, 9368, 6635, 2167, 1309, 316, 6954, 306, 160898, 483, 91592, 14, 9851, 259, 350, 942, 4847, 42675, 8, 326, 16107, 113852, 1611, 350, 6342, 42157, 3991, 5808, 25, 730, 9439, 9368, 11, 34398, 472, 448, 45266, 493, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 290, 21441, 6451, 328, 532, 17, 14174, 123753, 6077, 30, 730, 9439, 7578, 532, 17, 659, 220, 17, 39, 17, 46, 659, 220, 19, 68, 12, 15155, 220, 19, 42464, 12, 741, 623, 7024, 6451, 457, 6793, 395, 532, 17, 659, 220, 17, 39, 17, 46, 659, 220, 19, 68, 12, 15155, 220, 19, 42464, 12, 382, 659, 15, 13, 18793, 631, 350, 16121, 85378, 741, 730, 124714, 7578, 11, 532, 17, 659, 220, 19, 39, 10, 659, 220, 19, 68, 12, 15155, 220, 17, 487, 17, 46, 11, 457, 6793, 314, 659, 16, 13, 25242, 631, 13, 2632, 532, 17, 382, 261, 26929, 45266, 493, 306, 124714, 7578, 1572, 306, 9439, 7578, 350, 21910, 7266, 6451, 4748, 26929, 45266, 493, 741, 623, 9710, 5003, 25, 17576, 368, 5698, 2905, 11, 34398, 382, 261, 178796, 45266, 493, 306, 9439, 9368, 13, 2632, 11223, 316, 124714, 30, 3164, 3810, 91592, 503, 26929, 30, 12265, 532, 17, 382, 91592, 45266, 493, 306, 9439, 1572, 306, 124714, 13, 2632, 6052, 25, 91592, 364, 10620, 121749, 997, 25, 392, 42, 12559, 2905, 11, 34398, 137530, 178796, 306, 124714, 9368, 3692, 129157, 328, 532, 17, 306, 124714, 7578, 350, 9995, 34398, 21441, 316, 3411, 8, 382, 5542, 316, 413, 130262, 350, 69974, 154756, 8, 5192, 316, 1932, 28961, 38358, 11, 39241, 196883, 350, 68, 1940, 4213, 306, 14827, 11242, 741, 730, 9439, 7578, 11, 34398, 21441, 316, 42464, 12, 1217, 89342, 591, 130262, 154756, 11, 889, 4783, 9439, 9368, 3572, 679, 261, 2647, 6251, 30, 50557, 34398, 21441, 382, 54189, 306, 138809, 10217, 124714, 30, 730, 14827, 11242, 11, 138809, 14827, 11242, 679, 16107, 4917, 49, 154756, 1572, 124714, 30, 24305, 25, 730, 8785, 64480, 503, 166695, 14827, 11242, 11, 4917, 49, 306, 124714, 382, 54189, 26, 196883, 6118, 13, 182762, 11, 34398, 21441, 306, 124714, 350, 823, 1013, 13041, 50162, 8, 382, 54189, 1572, 306, 138809, 350, 4522, 1236, 196883, 1101, 741, 3072, 357, 2411, 290, 121749, 6251, 328, 532, 17, 306, 124714, 9368, 382, 54189, 11223, 316, 9439, 13, 623, 4928, 25, 392, 42, 12559, 2905, 11, 34398, 137530, 178796, 306, 124714, 9368, 3692, 2632, 2226, 532, 17, 19069, 16107, 503, 54189, 306, 124714, 30, 85246, 484, 290, 21441, 328, 532, 17, 306, 124714, 382, 54189, 2236, 328, 54189, 154756, 350, 21116, 1072, 135363, 741, 2632, 6052, 1481, 413, 54189, 364, 84787, 581, 679, 91592, 350, 942, 4847, 1276, 8, 326, 54189, 350, 6342, 17492, 741, 17796, 355, 25, 91592, 533, 54189, 364, 4383, 3880, 25, 355, 8, 91592, 533, 54189, 26, 418, 8, 26929, 1127, 16107, 26, 363, 8, 26929, 1127, 54189, 26, 415, 8, 91592, 1127, 16107, 13, 2632, 6052, 355, 364, 84787, 392, 160761, 25, 355, 4050], "n_tokens": 533, "words": 333, "band_lo": 124.5, "band_hi": 498.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the statement: \"Thermodynamically, oxygen is a …… oxidant in basic solutions. Kinetically, oxygen reacts …… in acidic solutions.\"\n\nWe need to fill in blanks with weaker/stronger (thermodynamics) and faster/slower (kinetics).\n\nSo: In basic solutions, oxygen as an oxidant (i.e., the reduction potential of O2/hydroxy? In basic solution O2 + 2H2O + 4e- → 4 OH-). The standard potential E° for O2 + 2H2O + 4e- → 4 OH- is +0.401 V (vs SHE). In acidic solution, O2 + 4H+ + 4e- → 2 H2O, E° = +1.229 V. So O2 is a stronger oxidant in acidic solution than in basic solution (since higher potential means stronger oxidant). The statement says: thermodynamically, oxygen is a …… oxidant in basic solutions. So compared to acidic? They ask weaker or stronger? Since O2 is weaker oxidant in basic than in acidic. So answer: weaker.\n\nNow kinetic part: \"Kinetically, oxygen reacts …… in acidic solutions.\" Reaction of O2 in acidic solution (like oxygen reduction to water) is known to be sluggish (slow kinetics) due to high activation barrier, requiring catalysts (e.g., in fuel cells). In basic solution, oxygen reduction to OH- also suffers from sluggish kinetics, but often basic solutions might have a different rate? Actually oxygen reduction is slower in alkaline vs acidic? In fuel cells, alkaline fuel cells have faster ORR kinetics than acidic? Wait: In solid oxide or PEM fuel cells, ORR in acidic is slower; catalysts needed. Historically, oxygen reduction in acidic (proton exchange membrane) is slower than in alkaline (where some catalysts work). But I think the kinetic rate of O2 in acidic solutions is slower compared to basic. The question: \"Kinetically, oxygen reacts …… in acidic solutions.\" So does O2 react faster or slower in acidic? Considering that the reduction of O2 in acidic is slower because of slower kinetics (high overpotential). So answer would be slower.\n\nThus we have weaker (thermodyn) and slower (kinetic). Option A: weaker - slower.\n\nCheck options: A) weaker - slower; B) stronger – faster; C) stronger – slower; D) weaker – faster. So answer A.\n\nThus \"ANSWER: A\".", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -6.55649341751996e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999802285, "logp_correct": -1.9771484869967748e-11, "letter_mass": 0.9999992847645174, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5612019460091825, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2673588042543997, "frac_below_-5": 0.009615384615384616, "digit_lp_min": -0.8259903788566589, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0008506731828674674, "n_cot_tokens": 520, "lp_end": -4.708655978902243e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -5.393869905048195, "p_nocot": 0.0045443530485030125, "uplift": 5.393869905028423, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.004544, "B": 0.150488, "C": 0.674442, "D": 0.170526}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recAAJoHMW45Lv5je", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: If an equimolar mixture X of two liquids, which decolorizes bromine water, is treated with platinum when heated, then an equimolar mixture Y of two other liquids is formed as a result of disproportionation, which does not decolorize bromine water. Hydrogenation of both mixture X and mixture Y in the presence of platinum under rather severe conditions gives only one substance, a certain hydrocarbon Z (mass fraction of hydrogen is 14.28%), which is a constituent of mixture Y and widely used as a solvent. Substance Z does not react further with hydrogen. There are no conjugated multiple bonds in the molecules of the compounds of mixture X.\nIndicate the total number of hydrogen atoms in two liquids of mixture X.\n\nA) 18\nB) 12\nC) 16\nD) 22\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: If an equimolar mixture X of two liquids, which decolorizes bromine water, is treated with platinum when heated, then an equimolar mixture Y of two other liquids is formed as a result of disproportionation, which does not decolorize bromine water. Hydrogenation of both mixture X and mixture Y in the presence of platinum under rather severe conditions gives only one substance, a certain hydrocarbon Z (mass fraction of hydrogen is 14.28%), which is a constituent of mixture Y and widely used as a solvent. Substance Z does not react further with hydrogen. There are no conjugated multiple bonds in the molecules of the compounds of mixture X.\nIndicate the total number of hydrogen atoms in two liquids of mixture X.\n\nA) 18\nB) 12\nC) 16\nD) 22\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recAAJoHMW45Lv5je:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 4792, 364, 2167, 679, 2801