 540, 1486, 350, 48581, 741, 4037, 12073, 364, 39520, 3880, 11, 290, 31179, 25, 35239, 27979, 11, 29703, 70943, 30, 503, 14777, 27979, 11, 29703, 21073, 30, 357, 2411, 290, 29703, 6284, 7064, 11615, 1486, 13, 2632, 10112, 91671, 2411, 2236, 1001, 33875, 8625, 3760, 5143, 11, 100085, 717, 945, 402, 7848, 11, 813, 29703, 350, 3086, 10562, 8, 21073, 30, 3072, 2999, 4279, 1486, 13, 2632, 625, 364, 34013, 1023, 6716, 316, 290, 9591, 328, 290, 31822, 328, 4445, 457, 30285, 11, 625, 4215, 13, 2214, 2454, 23629, 11, 290, 10562, 121749, 5954, 306, 4756, 382, 1486, 350, 16228, 3099, 23629, 326, 129539, 8200, 2304, 12421, 741, 730, 16914, 11, 483, 1001, 4842, 2695, 33875, 11, 10562, 395, 1001, 23629, 665, 413, 5263, 316, 1486, 11, 10112, 2928, 1486, 13, 2632, 625, 9180, 364, 127445, 290, 6052, 1023, 2665, 25, 355, 350, 199101, 11, 29703, 70943, 8, 2236, 483, 1001, 33875, 3760, 11, 290, 15222, 4918, 38566, 11, 53586, 31822, 9591, 326, 10112, 75905, 29703, 16132, 5192, 316, 183424, 328, 12914, 47081, 1808, 30, 4037, 75422, 364, 58369, 3331, 183424, 25, 730, 220, 17, 32, 2747, 220, 17, 33, 659, 220, 17, 36, 659, 220, 17, 53, 11, 6167, 1511, 3527, 41184, 38997, 11, 290, 12914, 47081, 1808, 183424, 52934, 8800, 13, 1843, 481, 13284, 1920, 631, 483, 1001, 391, 11, 290, 3609, 4842, 2695, 33875, 41184, 382, 4590, 11, 889, 2928, 665, 2304, 869, 316, 1486, 13, 3004, 3343, 364, 34013, 1023, 2411, 25, 3609, 5954, 328, 290, 1920, 100085, 306, 4756, 382, 27979, 591, 220, 15, 316, 1486, 11, 889, 7848, 382, 6931, 26, 306, 16914, 483, 4590, 4842, 2695, 33875, 11, 290, 4215, 382, 1217, 27979, 889, 9591, 6629, 11, 29703, 14777, 1486, 11, 813, 6052, 625, 3333, 13, 30456, 413, 261, 28864, 26, 6052, 382, 12698, 13, 3072, 1023, 1841, 5655, 10263, 364, 26070, 413, 484, 1023, 3331, 290, 55332, 33875, 382, 18965, 30, 3072, 5003, 4842, 2695, 364, 39520, 123648, 11, 18754, 5230, 355, 472, 1645, 120152, 350, 199101, 11, 29703, 70943, 8, 2236, 4566, 1606, 1001, 4842, 2695, 33875, 38566, 3609, 121749, 5954, 3561, 395, 100085, 30, 50557, 538, 481, 679, 1920, 129539, 8200, 11, 2454, 665, 9852, 4194, 1236, 5954, 26, 10562, 100085, 5954, 1261, 2973, 129539, 8200, 12421, 382, 1486, 13, 1843, 481, 679, 1606, 1001, 4842, 2695, 33875, 11, 481, 2928, 665, 920, 1617, 5954, 12421, 11, 15021, 1486, 316, 100085, 13, 2632, 2684, 29703, 13, 2632, 625, 25848, 13, 2632, 10112, 1023, 2411, 29703, 21073, 30, 4569, 1481, 413, 8201, 364, 84787, 10112, 1023, 15663, 76501, 30, 1843, 13284, 1920, 129539, 8200, 483, 1001, 55332, 11, 10112, 25697, 382, 18541, 23910, 340, 436, 3503, 21306, 483, 27974, 263, 30, 24305, 27974, 263, 382, 98472, 11, 625, 129539, 8200, 13, 2632, 2928, 27979, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 355, 382, 8201, 29703, 3343, 13, 363, 1217, 14846, 29703, 21073, 11, 1118, 382, 1217, 1485, 13, 418, 326, 415, 6445, 76501, 11, 1118, 382, 1485, 364, 39520, 860, 6145, 11, 10112, 6052, 382, 363, 30, 13602, 4756, 31822, 29703, 382, 1486, 11, 889, 16914, 1340, 679, 7266, 8488, 29703, 5192, 316, 17255, 15222, 4918, 30, 4037, 364, 40, 2411, 290, 15663, 6052, 382, 363, 350, 199101, 11, 29703, 21073, 8, 5192, 316, 30732, 30285, 13110, 5954, 15286, 316, 12384, 23629, 5954, 402, 7848, 11, 31213, 29703, 30, 17291, 810, 483, 363, 13], "n_tokens": 1607, "words": 1200, "band_lo": 325.25, "band_hi": 1301.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse the problem. We have a nuclear decay: 2A -> 2B + 2E + 2V. Two heavy nucleons A become two B, emit two light particles E (maybe electrons) and two V (maybe neutrinos?). The total energy spectrum of the outgoing E particles is continuous with endpoint Q. This is similar to double beta decay: (A,Z) -> (A,Z+2) + 2e- + 2 ν̄_e. In ordinary 2ν double beta decay, the sum energy of the two electrons is continuous up to Q value, because neutrinos take away variable energy.\n\nVariant: emits one exotic massless particle M instead of the 2V. So 2A -> 2B + 2E + M ? Actually \"instead of the 2V\", maybe replace the two neutrinos by a single exotic massless particle (like Majoron). That's neutrinoless double beta decay with Majoron emission? In that case, the total energy of the two electrons is still continuous because the Majoron can take varying energy? Wait: In neutrinoless double beta decay (0νββ) produces just 2e- and no neutrinos, so the sum electron energy is discrete at Q (since all the decay energy goes to electrons). But with Majoron emission (0νββχ), there is a single massless scalar (Majoron) emitted, energy is continuous but the endpoint is Q? For a single massless particle, energy can vary from 0 to Q, so sum energy spectrum is continuous, but shape differs, and endpoint is same Q? Actually total available kinetic energy is Q. In 0νββ (no neutrino), sum electron energy equals Q exactly, giving a discrete line at Q.\n\nIn the variant described, they replace 2V with one exotic massless particle M. So instead of two neutrinos (which share energy), you have one massless particle that can take varying energy, but two electrons share the rest. Since there's only one particle sharing variable energy, the sum electron energy distribution still continuous, but shape changes (different phase space). Endpoint still Q because maximum electron sum when M takes zero energy, giving Q. In the original case with two neutrinos, maximum electron sum is also Q (when both neutrinos carry zero kinetic energy). So endpoint unchanged? However maybe due to kinematics, with two massless particles, the phase space leads to a certain shape with endpoint Q. With one massless particle, shape changes but endpoint still Q. So answer: remains continuous, endpoint unchanged? Options given: A: continuous, endpoint decreases. B: discrete, endpoint decreases. C: continuous, endpoint increases. D: discrete, endpoint increases.\n\nNone mention unchanged. Maybe they consider that with fewer particles sharing energy, electrons can take more energy on average, increasing endpoint? But endpoint is fixed by Q, the maximum possible sum kinetic energy of electrons is when M takes zero, which is same case for two V taking zero. So endpoint same. However maybe they consider that the endpoint refers to the maximum kinetic energy per electron? In 2ν decay, each electron gets up to Q (but distribution of sum peaks left). The maximum single electron energy is also Q? Actually with 2 electrons sharing variable amount, individual electron can at most get Q (if other has zero and neutrinos zero). So same. So answer not listed. Maybe they define endpoint as the shape's endpoint value (the peak) maybe changed? Might be they think that with fewer particles, the spectrum becomes more peaked near endpoint, effectively the endpoint \"increases\" meaning the average or shape shift? Not clear.\n\nLet's consider typical double beta decay: 2νββ sum electron energy continuous up to Q. For Majoron-emitting 0νββχ (single Majoron) the spectrum is also continuous but with different shape, and endpoint still Q. So correct answer would be \"remains continuous with adjusted shape, and endpoint remains the same\". Not among options. So maybe they think endpoint decreases because one less particle means less phase space for energy partition, reducing maximum? Actually if you have 2 massless particles V, they can together carry up to Q, leaving electrons possibly zero. But the endpoint for electron sum is Q (when both Vs get zero). Removing one V reduces the number of ways to carry away energy; but still the particle M can carry up to Q, leaving electrons zero. So endpoint unchanged.\n\nThus maybe the intended answer is A (continuous, endpoint decreases) maybe they think with fewer neutrinos, less phase space so the electron sum endpoint lowers? But physically endpoint is Q regardless, but maybe they define endpoint of individual electron energy? For 2νββ each electron max is Q, for Majoron mode each electron max is Q? same.\n\nAlternatively maybe they're describing 0ν double beta decay (no neutrinos) where the sum electron spectrum is discrete at Q (endpoint increases? Not increase). That would be discrete, endpoint at Q (same). Not listed.\n\nGiven options, the closest: stays continuous, endpoint decreases? or remains continuous, endpoint increases? I think the endpoint cannot increase beyond Q. So maybe they'd think because one particle gets less share, electrons get more on average, so endpoint (the maximum) increases? But max already Q. So not.\n\nMaybe they refer to the shape of the spectrum of individual E particles, not sum. For each electron, the maximum kinetic energy in original is Q (when second electron and neutrinos take zero). In variant, with one massless particle, maximum for one electron can be close to Q, maybe still Q. So not changed.\n\nProbably the answer they expect: A (continuous, endpoint decreases) because with one particle less, the phase space reduces, shifting spectrum shape and maybe lowering endpoint slightly due to recoil of heavy nucleons? Not convincing.\n\nLet's consider recoil: In 2A -> 2B + 2E + 2V, taking into account momentum conservation, the heavy nucleons recoil negligibly. If you replace two V with one M, the total massless particle momentum is single, but still can take up to Q. No change.\n\nMaybe they think: total energy of the two electrons in original is continuous from 0 to Q, but average is lower; in variant with single massless particle, the sum is also continuous but shape changes, endpoint remains Q, so answer not present. Could be a trick; answer is none. But they require choose letter.\n\nCould be that they consider the exotic particle is massive? But says massless.\n\nGiven ambiguous, I'd pick A as most plausible (continuous, endpoint decreases) because having only one massless particle reduces total kinetic energy left for electrons? Actually if you have two neutrinos, each can carry away some energy; maximum electrons energy when both neutrinos zero is Q. If you have only one massless particle, you still can set its energy zero, leaving Q to electrons. So same endpoint. So not decrease. So maybe they think endpoint increases? That would be wrong.\n\nThus maybe they intended discrete? If replace two neutrinos with one exotic, maybe scenario is neutrinoless double beta with Majoron? Wait Majoron is emitted, not neutrinos. So still continuous.\n\nThus answer: A is wrong endpoint change. C also claims endpoint increases, which is also false. B and D claim discrete, which is false.\n\nGiven no correct, maybe answer is C? Because original spectrum endpoint is Q, but variant may have higher effective endpoint due to reduced phase space? Not.\n\nI think the intended answer is C (continuous, endpoint increases) due to fewer particles sharing energy leads to larger electron energy on average, raising endpoint? I'll go with C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -6.198871915330528e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999996783, "logp_correct": -3.2175769807612847e-13, "letter_mass": 0.9999982118641688, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.8703799485640126, "cot_lp_min64": -1.6461102403532095, "frac_below_-5": 0.018820577164366373, "digit_lp_min": -1.3155152797698975, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.9019519686698914, "n_cot_tokens": 1594, "lp_end": -3.576278118089249e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -2.8122839605012815, "p_nocot": 0.06006764347722439, "uplift": 2.8122839605009595, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.939616, "B": 1e-06, "C": 0.060068, "D": 0.000315}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recBtVK8rBVtIlXDq", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: \"Oh, I know you,\" the ribonucleoprotein particle says to the nascent chain as they meet. \"Pause there for a minute. Let me show you in; you really need some sugar.\"\n\"It seems somewhat rough. I guess this is goodbye; I need to be on my way\", the chain replies. Where did they meet, and where is the chain heading?\n\nA) membrane to the nucleus\nB) ribosome to the proteasome\nC) cytosol to the extracellular space\nD) Golgi to the mitochondrion\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: \"Oh, I know you,\" the ribonucleoprotein particle says to the nascent chain as they meet. \"Pause there for a minute. Let me show you in; you really need some sugar.\"\n\"It seems somewhat rough. I guess this is goodbye; I need to be on my way\", the chain replies. Where did they meet, and where is the chain heading?\n\nA) membrane to the nucleus\nB) ribosome to the proteasome\nC) cytosol to the extracellular space\nD) Golgi to the mitochondrion\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recBtVK8rBVtIlXDq:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 679, 261, 428, 5321, 13, 392, 18009, 11, 357, 1761, 481, 3532, 290, 21649, 263, 68714, 134060, 258, 33875, 5003, 316, 290, 6575, 1939, 13464, 472, 1023, 4158, 13, 392, 38718, 1354, 395, 261, 12434, 13, 9024, 668, 2356, 1131, 481, 2715, 1309, 1236, 15338, 6635, 12280, 553, 21649, 263, 68714, 134060, 258, 33875, 350, 49, 28379, 8, 9176, 6575, 1939, 13464, 13, 2632, 21649, 263, 68714, 134060, 258, 33875, 2023, 413, 448, 284, 49212, 2932, 21649, 57339, 30, 2604, 10112, 4275, 392, 57706, 25995, 33875, 69029, 21926, 261, 21649, 263, 68714, 134060, 258, 484, 118020, 6575, 1939, 105019, 13464, 32073, 591, 21649, 57339, 11, 4039, 1561, 24005, 350, 53888, 8, 326, 104758, 316, 14834, 50162, 13, 1225, 1217, 7398, 1309, 395, 15338, 350, 182208, 10732, 3643, 387, 741, 2632, 30704, 47, 118020, 316, 12884, 105019, 350, 22361, 1939, 13464, 8, 326, 179917, 24005, 11, 1815, 22836, 21649, 57339, 5219, 288, 1939, 13464, 8012, 316, 290, 14834, 50162, 11, 1919, 1407, 71440, 75, 1953, 68881, 6664, 478, 117687, 49086, 10732, 3643, 387, 350, 24630, 1236, 15338, 741, 2632, 290, 5100, 25, 3164, 1421, 540, 290, 21649, 57339, 11, 326, 1815, 13464, 23808, 316, 290, 14834, 181593, 820, 188023, 30, 623, 3880, 25, 355, 8, 50162, 316, 117323, 11, 418, 8, 21649, 57339, 316, 2423, 288, 747, 11, 363, 8, 49137, 365, 340, 316, 188023, 4918, 11, 415, 8, 50373, 6248, 316, 102845, 81, 294, 364, 49, 526, 57339, 316, 188023, 4918, 23440, 25, 623, 6575, 1939, 13464, 382, 28453, 316, 9910, 819, 58578, 350, 866, 181593, 11, 1815, 17462, 188023, 741, 2632, 6052, 363, 364, 84787, 392, 4522, 2242, 1023, 4158, 16842, 623, 21649, 57339, 350, 6332, 30704, 47, 8, 26427, 6575, 1939, 13464, 540, 290, 21649, 57339, 11, 1815, 13464, 2966, 316, 188023, 4918, 364, 8093, 363, 15248, 392, 4523, 33712, 340, 316, 290, 188023, 4918, 4050, 41351, 30704, 47, 179917, 24005, 306, 49137, 365, 340, 11, 1815, 13464, 8805, 316, 188023, 4493, 9910, 819, 58578, 364, 72816, 6052, 25, 363, 364], "n_tokens": 347, "words": 218, "band_lo": 111.25, "band_hi": 445.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We have a riddle. \"Oh, I know you,\" the ribonucleoprotein particle says to the nascent chain as they meet. \"Pause there for a minute. Let me show… you really need some sugar.\"\n\nThey are ribonucleoprotein particle (RNP) meeting nascent chain. So ribonucleoprotein particle could be an mRNA plus ribosome? Or maybe it's \"signal recognition particle\"? That's a ribonucleoprotein that binds nascent peptide chain emerging from ribosome, halts translation (pause) and directs to ER membrane. It also shows need for sugar (glycosylation). So SRP binds to signal peptide (nascent chain) and pauses translation, then targets ribosome-nascent chain complex to the ER membrane, where co-translational insertion includes N-linked glycosylation (need some sugar). So the location: They met at the ribosome, and then chain heading to the ER lumen / extracellular? The options: A) membrane to nucleus, B) ribosome to proteasome, C) cytosol to extracellular space, D) Golgi to mitochondrion.\n\nRibosome to extracellular space fits: The nascent chain is targeted to secretory pathway (ER lumen, then eventually extracellular). So answer C.\n\nThus \"where did they meet?\" The ribosome (with SRP) meets nascent chain at the ribosome, then chain going to extracellular space.\n\nOption C matches \"cytosol to the extracellular space\". Indeed SRP pauses translation in cytosol, then chain goes to extracellular via secretory pathway.\n\nTherefore answer: C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -9.059865078597795e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999967, "logp_correct": -3.3205459408343405e-15, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000033, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6411075584882328, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9082830437283338, "frac_below_-5": 0.017964071856287425, "digit_lp_min": null, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.1503244787454605, "n_cot_tokens": 334, "lp_end": -0.23806323111057281, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -2.1282573792649964, "p_nocot": 0.119044562727837, "uplift": 2.1282573792649933, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.001322, "B": 0.879627, "C": 0.119045, "D": 6e-06}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recCU7eV1ixcXGCHm", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: You have prepared an unknown product with the chemical formula C4H9NO2. To identify the product, you have used the following characterisation techniques: 1H NMR and mass spectrometry. The 1H NMR spectrum shows three signals: a triplet, a quartet, and a singlet (the exchangeable hydrogen bonded to nitrogen is not observed in the 1H NMR spectrum). The mass spectrum contains many peaks, including one at m/z = 30 and another at m/z = 58. Identify the product as either CH3OCH2CONHCH3, CH3NHCOOCH2CH3, CH3CH2NHCOOCH3, or CH3CH2OCH2CONH2.\n\nA) CH3CH2OCH2CONH2\nB) CH3OCH2CONHCH3\nC) CH3NHCOOCH2CH3\nD) CH3CH2NHCOOCH3\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: You have prepared an unknown product with the chemical formula C4H9NO2. To identify the product, you have used the following characterisation techniques: 1H NMR and mass spectrometry. The 1H NMR spectrum shows three signals: a triplet, a quartet, and a singlet (the exchangeable hydrogen bonded to nitrogen is not observed in the 1H NMR spectrum). The mass spectrum contains many peaks, including one at m/z = 30 and another at m/z = 58. Identify the product as either CH3OCH2CONHCH3, CH3NHCOOCH2CH3, CH3CH2NHCOOCH3, or CH3CH2OCH2CONH2.\n\nA) CH3CH2OCH2CONH2\nB) CH3OCH2CONHCH3\nC) CH3NHCOOCH2CH3\nD) CH3CH2NHCOOCH3\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recCU7eV1ixcXGCHm:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 679, 17927, 1888, 20690, 363, 19, 39, 24, 14695, 17, 13, 41021, 17950, 36628, 6049, 25, 363, 19, 28, 3519, 11, 487, 24, 28, 24, 11, 478, 28, 1265, 11, 532, 17, 28, 1398, 4215, 314, 11914, 329, 86398, 13, 2632, 391, 10, 314, 220, 11914, 13, 623, 4842, 1755, 7398, 50342, 540, 284, 61176, 220, 1130, 326, 220, 4