18, 316, 487, 659, 3407, 2647, 32353, 3643, 8896, 13, 2632, 20690, 6664, 540, 5153, 1001, 47612, 402, 549, 46047, 15883, 13, 24855, 25, 220, 17, 11, 18, 2469, 321, 53546, 8293, 1986, 30, 3604, 15883, 220, 17, 25, 18618, 8896, 25, 10049, 18, 11, 10049, 18, 350, 2477, 933, 936, 10049, 18, 350, 2477, 933, 936, 487, 2747, 625, 549, 46047, 13, 220, 18, 2126, 53546, 22803, 1986, 10112, 30, 36897, 220, 18, 18618, 316, 10049, 18, 11, 10049, 17, 3583, 17, 3583, 18, 350, 15630, 3643, 936, 10049, 17, 3583, 18, 350, 53546, 936, 487, 30, 21079, 553, 3407, 2647, 32353, 3643, 8896, 350, 76, 53546, 11, 16111, 3643, 11, 5892, 3643, 8, 2932, 487, 2747, 549, 46047, 13, 2632, 220, 18, 2126, 53546, 22803, 1986, 350, 34, 22, 39, 1125, 8, 382, 549, 46047, 30, 19792, 316, 2371, 90882, 25, 220, 18, 2126, 53546, 22803, 1986, 350, 3583, 18, 12, 3583, 17, 12, 3583, 140797, 18, 14080, 3583, 17, 12, 3583, 17, 12, 3583, 18, 741, 623, 15883, 540, 4010, 220, 18, 18618, 316, 10049, 18, 350, 76, 53546, 936, 10049, 17, 3583, 17, 3583, 18, 350, 15630, 3643, 936, 10049, 17, 3583, 18, 350, 53546, 936, 326, 487, 13, 21079, 553, 722, 2647, 11, 813, 480, 382, 549, 46047, 13, 2632, 363, 22, 39, 1125, 665, 413, 549, 46047, 13, 17796, 415, 853, 363, 22, 39, 1125, 350, 22677, 220, 1353, 741, 2632, 415, 1340, 11095, 316, 261, 549, 46047, 32353, 1986, 350, 18, 2126, 53546, 22803, 1986, 741, 13974, 4842, 1755, 25, 36628, 26485, 220, 1353, 11, 63899, 540, 220, 10018, 350, 62832, 6266, 328, 10049, 18, 936, 220, 5085, 11, 220, 5320, 13, 21926, 19271, 364, 84787, 1832, 2905, 6725, 87455, 31200, 77956, 6960, 66938, 316, 363, 22, 39, 1125, 483, 549, 46047, 6170, 13, 17796, 415, 364, 4383, 1273, 31061, 25, 363, 21, 39, 1265, 2023, 1217, 679, 549, 46047, 382, 22516, 25, 220, 18, 2126, 53546, 60595, 1986, 350, 34, 21, 39, 1265, 8, 1217, 549, 46047, 30, 41021, 28035, 25, 220, 18, 2126, 53546, 60595, 1986, 25, 10049, 18, 12, 3583, 17, 12, 3583, 140797, 18, 14080, 3583, 17, 12, 3583, 18, 13, 36897, 220, 18, 18618, 316, 10049, 18, 11, 16111, 3643, 350, 3583, 17, 3583, 18, 936, 5892, 3643, 30, 50557, 3561, 4307, 10049, 17, 3583, 18, 553, 16111, 3643, 11, 1849, 4307, 10049, 17, 3583, 18, 1217, 16111, 3643, 2684, 11, 813, 625, 549, 46047, 13, 220, 17, 2126, 53546, 60595, 1986, 30, 36897, 220, 17, 18618, 316, 10049, 18, 11, 10049, 18, 350, 2477, 933, 8, 326, 1203, 625, 549, 46047, 13, 2632, 363, 21, 39, 1265, 1340, 625, 679, 549, 46047, 382, 22516, 30, 32711, 1217, 220, 17, 11, 18, 2469, 321, 53546, 8293, 1986, 350, 53095, 936, 220, 17, 11, 17, 2469, 321, 53546, 8293, 1986, 350, 53095, 741, 2632, 10112, 860, 549, 46047, 363, 21, 39, 1265, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 364, 84787, 6052, 6011, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 415], "n_tokens": 1761, "words": 838, "band_lo": 479.75, "band_hi": 1919.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to determine which data set corresponds to an optically active saturated hydrocarbon. Optically active means chiral, must have asymmetry. Saturated hydrocarbon means alkane, no double bonds, no rings? Actually saturated hydrocarbon could be alkane or cycloalkane? Typically saturated means only single C-C bonds, could be cyclic (cycloalkane). But optical activity in hydrocarbons requires a stereogenic centre, typically a carbon attached to four different substituents. In an alkane, you need a quaternary carbon with four different groups. That could be, e.g., 2,3-dimethylbutane? No that has symmetry. Need a carbon with four different alkyl groups. The molecular formula must be CnH2n+2 (acyclic alkane) or CnH2n (cycloalkane). For optical activity, need at least one stereocenter.\n\nWe have percentages, approximate empirical formula.\n\nCalculate formula from C% and H% (assuming only C and H). Use weight percentages: For C: 12.011 g/mol per atom; H: 1.008 g/mol.\n\nSet up: mass of 100 g sample: e.g., for A: 83.33 g C, 16.66 g H. Moles: C: 83.33/12.011 = 6.936 mol. H: 16.66/1.008 = 16.53 mol. Ratio: divide by smallest 6.936 => C 1, H 2.383. Not integer; multiply maybe 4 => C4 H9.53. Not nice. Could be C7H16? Let's test: C7H16 => mass% C = (7*12.011)/(7*12.011+16*1.008)=84.07% C, H=15.93%. That's not A. Try C6H14 => C% = (72.066)/(72.066+14.112)=83.73% C, H% =16.27% (matches C). Actually C6H14 matches composition of C 83.73% and H 16.27% ~ option C. Good. So C is C6H14 (hexane). Option C mass% matches hexane.\n\nA: 83.33% maybe C5H12? Compute C5H12: mass = 5*12.011=60.055; H12=12.096; total=72.151; C% =60.055/72.151=83.2%; H% 16.8% close. So A maybe C5H12 (pentane). B: 82.75 C, 17.25 H. C7H16 gave 84.1% C, 15.9% H. C5H12 83.2 C. So B could be C4H10? Compute C4H10: mass C =48.044; H=10.08; total=58.124; C% =48.044/58.124=82.6% close to 82.75. So B C4H10 (butane). D: 84% C,16% H matches C6H14? earlier we got 83.7 C. C7H16 gave 84.1 C. So D likely C7H16 (heptane). So each corresponds to straight-chain alkanes: pentane, butane, hexane, heptane.\n\nNow optically active saturated hydrocarbon: among alkanes, only those with branched structures can be chiral. Straight-chain alkanes are achiral. However our formulas could also correspond to branched isomers. Need to examine mass spectral fragmentation pattern m/z 72,57,43,29 etc. Which indicates certain fragments. For pentane (C5H12) molecular weight 72. So option A m/z 72 is molecular ion, indicates MW 72, matches C5H12. fragments 57 (C4H9+), 43 (C3H7+), 29 (C2H5+). That suggests normal pentane, maybe branched but still same fragments.\n\nBut optical activity requires chiral center. Which formula could accommodate a chiral carbon? Minimum C must be at least 4 (C4H10) but need four different groups on a carbon. Smallest chiral alkane is 2-butanol (but contains OH, not saturated hydrocarbon). For hydrocarbon, need tetra-substituted carbon with four different alkyl groups. Minimum carbon count? For hydrocarbon, simplest is 2,3-dimethylbutane? No that's symmetrical. The smallest chiral alkane is 2,3-dimethylbutane? Let's think: carbon skeleton with 5 carbons maybe? 2-methylbutane (isopentane) has a chiral center at carbon 2? Actually 2-methylbutane (CH3-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3) carbon 2 attached to CH3, CH3 (two same), H, CH2CH3 -> two identical groups, not chiral. 3-methylpentane? Carbon 3 attached to CH2CH3, CH2CH3 same. So need carbon attached to four different carbon groups. Smallest hydrocarbon with chiral centre probably like 2,3-dimethylpentane? Let's recall known chiral alkanes: 2-bromobutane (but it's not hydrocarbon). For pure hydrocarbon, you need a carbon attached to four distinct alkyl groups, meaning the carbon is quaternary? Actually tetrahedral carbon with four different alkyl groups; any carbon with a hydrogen can't have four alkyl groups (hydrogen is one). So a carbon must be attached to H? Wait chiral center requires four different substituents; H counts as one substituent. So need a carbon attached to H + three different alkyl groups. So formula includes at least one hydrogen on chiral carbon. Example: 2,3-dimethylbutane? At carbon 2: attached groups: CH3, CH3 (identical), CH3 (identical), H -> not chiral. 3-methylhexane maybe? Carbon 3 attached to CH3, CH2CH2CH3 (propyl), CH2CH3 (ethyl), H? Those are three different alkyl groups (methyl, ethyl, propyl) plus H -> chiral. So 3-methylhexane (C7H16) is chiral? Need to check symmetry: 3-methylhexane (CH3-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH3). The carbon at position 3 attached to CH3 (methyl), CH2CH2CH3 (propyl), CH2CH3 (ethyl), and H. Those are all different, so it is chiral. So C7H16 can be chiral. Option D has C7H16 (MW 100). So D may correspond to a chiral alkane (3-methylhexane). Its mass spec: molecular ion 100, fragments at 71 (maybe loss of CH3), 57, 43. That's typical.\n\nThus optically active saturated hydrocarbon likely corresponds to C7H16 with chiral center. Option D.\n\nCheck other possibilities: C6H14 could also have chiral isomer: 3-methylpentane (C6H14) also chiral? Let's examine: 3-methylpentane: CH3-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3. Carbon 3 attached to CH3, ethyl (CH2CH3), propyl? Actually left side CH2CH3 are ethyl, right side CH2CH3 also ethyl same, so not chiral. 2-methylpentane? Carbon 2 attached to CH3, CH3 (identical) and else not chiral. So C6H14 may not have chiral isomer? There's also 2,3-dimethylbutane (sym), 2,2-dimethylbutane (sym). So maybe no chiral C6H14.\n\nThus answer D.\n\nThus answer format: ANSWER: D", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.2291896982551407e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999998911, "logp_correct": -1.0885349831799354e-13, "letter_mass": 0.9999780659714924, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5448156384614731, "cot_lp_min64": -1.0742780309001319, "frac_below_-5": 0.013157894736842105, "digit_lp_min": -4.695237159729004, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 1748, "lp_end": -0.06502673029899597, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -0.4716581524289114, "p_nocot": 0.6239667779910978, "uplift": 0.47165815242880255, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.058038, "B": 0.178769, "C": 0.139226, "D": 0.623967}}, "force_keep": false, "accepted": true}
