910, 6793, 871, 8974, 28, 15, 13, 20, 871, 8785, 16538, 314, 220, 17, 1345, 350, 16, 12, 15, 13, 20, 8, 314, 3478, 13, 63606, 51811, 8785, 16538, 220, 19, 1345, 13, 2632, 33763, 314, 3478, 820, 350, 19, 1345, 8, 314, 220, 16, 14, 19, 13, 3072, 581, 1606, 3331, 15263, 143971, 30, 623, 3609, 15263, 143971, 382, 220, 17, 1345, 11504, 45611, 13, 623, 2328, 3624, 382, 3478, 11, 1118, 382, 6375, 328, 143971, 13, 2632, 33763, 328, 81778, 9753, 314, 220, 15, 13, 20, 328, 143971, 314, 220, 15, 13, 1161, 328, 3609, 51811, 13, 3072, 3880, 25, 392, 28762, 1572, 261, 12965, 1, 350, 33, 8, 503, 392, 28762, 1572, 261, 6914, 11, 889, 945, 1572, 261, 12965, 1, 350, 32, 8, 503, 392, 28762, 1572, 261, 6375, 11, 889, 945, 1572, 261, 6914, 1, 350, 35, 741, 5339, 24923, 33763, 220, 16, 14, 19, 9707, 13, 3072, 5317, 418, 5003, 3760, 1572, 261, 12965, 13, 17796, 363, 382, 6375, 13, 165972, 364, 7943, 10112, 1023, 3810, 33763, 328, 51811, 9753, 350, 9577, 8, 581, 665, 31614, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 1606, 290, 997, 484, 382, 6177, 326, 1217, 625, 3101, 3592, 27472, 295, 741, 12265, 581, 665, 31614, 290, 6508, 15263, 143971, 11, 2928, 6375, 13, 3072, 1023, 6960, 2411, 484, 5192, 316, 112871, 23938, 12847, 11, 1236, 7881, 5025, 49667, 2236, 187122, 553, 3101, 3592, 27472, 295, 575, 2560, 4213, 10112, 625, 198242, 30, 4037, 10185, 364, 3335, 1023, 36988, 581, 665, 1606, 1921, 7967, 1919, 51764, 100209, 125533, 220, 18, 36, 21241, 30, 392, 78601, 5954, 20395, 382, 220, 18, 36, 3692, 4569, 382, 4335, 26, 10112, 28385, 9913, 316, 484, 24125, 93131, 2543, 26, 581, 1606, 3605, 187122, 540, 484, 5954, 30, 623, 4928, 25, 392, 4827, 33763, 328, 290, 51811, 9753, 665, 581, 31614, 1261, 480, 382, 35479, 306, 2238, 261, 2006, 16842, 138743, 1023, 3331, 484, 581, 1606, 11839, 187122, 3518, 484, 24125, 2543, 11, 1118, 1954, 382, 83776, 295, 26, 889, 28385, 328, 2543, 10112, 9913, 316, 4454, 5954, 3352, 5862, 4756, 30, 623, 17898, 5720, 123648, 364, 18683, 2360, 415, 13731, 28, 18, 10175, 15772, 28, 15, 13, 23, 11, 8117, 316, 8974, 4114, 13731, 1919, 415, 20974, 30, 30391, 1023, 3331, 484, 581, 665, 1606, 31614, 187122, 483, 5954, 2870, 457, 326, 220, 18, 36, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 51764, 100209, 295, 1606, 8, 2236, 3592, 27472, 295, 8104, 4895, 457, 553, 7539, 28385, 30, 2632, 33763, 382, 2617, 3793, 2238, 484, 20395, 5954, 72554, 18, 36, 350, 133153, 722, 16918, 50557, 2999, 314, 18, 36, 11, 1349, 10112, 7139, 12421, 30, 3072, 581, 3331, 1606, 997, 1919, 5954, 382, 3518, 723, 36, 11, 18, 36, 137309, 4569, 7967, 382, 8974, 4114, 5064, 15, 13, 20, 472, 5151, 11, 33763, 314, 220, 15, 13, 1161, 328, 3609, 51811, 364, 7943, 6052, 3880, 625, 3931, 9707, 220, 16, 14, 19, 26, 418, 5003, 3760, 1572, 261, 12965, 11, 357, 2411, 91671, 3331, 484, 581, 6284, 1921, 9707, 12965, 2236, 290, 35021, 1919, 8974, 4114, 28, 15, 13, 20, 8967, 20395, 5954, 9707, 457, 11, 889, 581, 7038, 1309, 1424, 36, 11, 813, 16132, 3760, 11, 13972, 3760, 1572, 261, 12965, 13, 2632, 6052, 418, 364, 84787, 357, 2411, 6052, 418, 364, 58369, 3587, 6052, 13], "n_tokens": 2630, "words": 1735, "band_lo": 867.5, "band_hi": 3470.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the problem: an opaque sphere emitting photons of a fixed rest-frame energy E. For a static sphere, we see only half the surface (the hemisphere facing us). For an expanding sphere at constant relativistic velocity in all directions (i.e., the sphere's surface is moving outward radially, with each surface element moving away from the sphere's center). Observed photons are Doppler shifted due to motion of emitting surface relative to observer.\n\nWe see a broadened line with maximum observed photon energy 3E, i.e., blueshifted up to factor 3. That arises from points on sphere moving towards the observer at some angle such that line-of-sight component of velocity yields Doppler factor up to 3.\n\nWe need to find fraction of sphere surface that is observable under this condition? Likely only a smaller part of sphere visible because parts moving away produce redshift less than E? But the problem perhaps: The observed line is only within certain angle where observed Doppler shift factor >= something? Actually we observe the line, but broadened; we see photons from the entire visible hemisphere possibly, but due to relativistic aberration and beaming, only part of the surface contributes photons that reach us with sufficient blueshift up to 3E? Actually we are asked: \"What fraction of the sphere surface can we observe when it is expanding in such a way?\" i.e., the observed photons come only from parts where emitted photons can reach us (i.e., not blocked). But the sphere is opaque, meaning we cannot see through. However the sphere is expanding, maybe parts moving away have their emitted photons unable to overtake the expanding surface? Hmm.\n\nMaybe the concept is that due to relativistic beaming, emission is beamed forward; we only see a smaller patch of surface because photons emitted from edges get beamed away.\n\nSituation: Each surface element moving radially outward; its emitted photons are isotropic in its rest frame? Usually emission isotropic in rest frame. In observer frame, due to relativistic aberration, photons are concentrated in forward direction (i.e., direction of motion). For a surface element moving away from observer, photons directed backward (toward observer) are strongly deboosted and may not escape because the sphere's surface ahead may block them? Actually the sphere is opaque; need line-of-sight to observer not intersect any other part of sphere. For static sphere, observer sees hemisphere. For moving sphere, due to aberration, the apparent visible region is smaller.\n\nConsider any point on sphere, with outward normal velocity vector radial. The condition for observer to see that point is that line from point to observer is not intersected by sphere interior. That's same geometric condition: point must be on near side (visible hemisphere). However due to relativistic beaming, the intensity might be negligible for points near edge because emission is beamed forward away from observer. However the problem asks fraction of sphere surface we can observe, given that maximum observed photon energy is 3E (blueshift). The Doppler factor D = sqrt[(1+β)/(1-β)] for motion directly towards observer (β positive for approach). At angle θ (angle between velocity vector and line-of-sight to observer), the Doppler factor D = 1/γ(1-β cosθ). Actually observed frequency ν_obs = ν_emit * γ (1 - β cosθ)^{-1}.\n\nWe have sphere with expansion velocity β (constant). For point at angle α from line-of-centre (i.e., angle between surface normal and line-of-sight). The surface normal points radially outward, which is same as velocity direction, i.e., direction from center to point. So cosθ = -cosα? Let's define: For observer at +z infinity, sphere center at origin. Point on sphere at polar angle α (0 at near pole). Its outward normal points outward, i.e., radial vector pointing away from center. For near side (α between 0 and π/2) the normal points partially towards observer (i.e., component has negative z direction). The angle between velocity (outward radial) and line-of-sight (toward observer) is π - α? Actually line-of-sight vector from point to observer is roughly opposite to radial direction? For a point on near hemisphere, the line-of-sight direction points outward from sphere towards observer (i.e., roughly opposite to radial direction). The outward radial direction points away from center, which for near side points roughly towards observer (since observer is far away). Wait: radius vector from center to point on near side points roughly towards observer (since observer is far away beyond the sphere). So the velocity vector of that point is outward, which points towards observer for near hemisphere. For far hemisphere, radial vector points away from observer.\n\nThus the angle between velocity vector and direction to observer is θ = α (the same). At near pole α=0, velocity directly towards observer; at edge α=π/2, velocity perpendicular; at >π/2, away.\n\nHence Doppler factor D = 1/[γ(1-β cosα)], where cosα positive for near side (0 to 1). Max blueshift occurs at α=0: D_max = 1/[γ(1-β)] = sqrt[(1+β)/(1-β)].\n\nGiven D_max = 3 (since observed max photon energy =3E). So sqrt((1+β)/(1-β))=3 => (1+β)/(1-β) = 9 => 1+β =9-9β => bring terms: β + 9β =9 -1 =>10β =8 => β = 0.8. So v=0.8c. γ = 1/sqrt(1-β^2) = 1/sqrt(1-0.64)=1/sqrt(0.36)=1/0.6=1.6667.\n\nNow, the observer will see photons from points where Doppler factor yields some energy between some min and max. But we can potentially observe all points on visible hemisphere, albeit with varying shift from redshift to blueshift. However some points may be moving away such that photons cannot escape? Yet for opaque sphere, photons emitted outward (into vacuum) can still reach observer if not blocked. For far side points, the line-of-sight passes through sphere, not seen. So only near hemisphere still visible geometrically.\n\nBut maybe due to relativistic aberration, the apparent visible region (incoming photons) is limited to a cone around direction of motion due to beaming: a moving object appears smaller (searchlight effect). For a relativistically moving sphere, the angular size is reduced (Terrell rotation aside). In observer frame, due to aberration, directions of light from surface are transformed: for a moving sphere approaching, the apparent angular radius is given by sinθ' = sinθ/ (γ(1 - β cosθ)). For a sphere moving directly towards observer, its angular size is increased? Actually aberration compresses angles forward: objects appear concentrated into forward direction, making the apparent angular size larger? Let's recall: For a relativistically moving object, its apparent angular size is increased (relativistic beaming) when moving towards observer, making it appear larger? Hmm.\n\nBut the problem statement: maximum energy observed is 3E, asks fraction of sphere surface we can observe. Perhaps we can only see region where Doppler factor >=1? Because we can only observe photons with energy >= E (maybe they only consider blueshifted portion). However we can see redshifted photons too (lower energy). The spectral line broadens and shifts; we may still detect all photons but the line is broadened both sides. But they state \"maximum energy now 3 times higher (3xE) than the energy of the observed photons from the non-expanding sphere.\" So perhaps the observed line is symmetrically broadened but max is 3E. The question: what fraction of sphere surface can we observe when it is expanding such that the max shift is 3E? Perhaps the fraction is proportional to solid angle of region with cosθ > something leading to D>= something? Not clear.\n\nMaybe they want to know the angular region where Doppler factor >1 (i.e., blueshift). Because redshifted photons might be too low energy or maybe not detected? The observable photons are those with energy up to 3E, but we can still detect lower energy ones. The question ambiguous.\n\nCould be a trick: The fraction of surface that can be seen is independent of expansion; still half, because geometry unchanged; expansion only changes Doppler shift but not visibility. So answer C (half). But they provided other options suggesting other fractions.\n\nBut they give max energy 3E, perhaps using that to determine β. Then perhaps only points moving towards observer enough to produce blueshift up to 3E are those within some cone where cosθ >= something. Let's compute condition for observed photon energy to be at least E (i.e., not redshifted below original). That is D >= 1 => 1/[γ(1-β cosθ)] >=1 => γ(1-β cosθ) <=1 => (1-β cosθ) <= 1/γ. Rearr => β cosθ >= 1 - 1/γ.\n\nPlug numbers: γ=5/3? Actually γ = 1.6667. 1/γ =0.6. So RHS = 1 - 0.6 =0.4. So β cosθ >=0.4 => 0.8 cosθ >=0.4 => cosθ >=0.5. So θ <= 60°. So region within 60° of direction towards observer (i.e., near pole) gives blueshift (energy >=E). That region corresponds to cap of sphere. The area of cap relative to hemisphere? The cap solid angle = 2π (1 - cosθ_max) where θ_max = 60° => cos=0.5 => solid angle = 2π (1-0.5) = π. Whole sphere solid angle 4π. So fraction = π / (4π) = 1/4. But we only consider visible hemisphere? The total visible hemisphere is 2π steradians. The cap area is π, which is half of hemisphere. So fraction of observable surface = 0.5 of hemisphere = 0.25 of total sphere. But options: \"Less than a quarter\" (B) or \"Less than a third, but more than a quarter\" (A) or \"Less than a half, but more than a third\" (D). Our derived fraction 1/4 exactly. But option B says less than a quarter. Option C is half. Hmm.\n\nBut maybe they ask fraction of sphere surface (total) we can observe (i.e., only the part that is seen and also not too redshifted). Since we can observe the entire visible hemisphere, still half. But they likely think that due to Doppler shift, some parts become invisible because photons are too redshifted i.e., maybe not detectable? Not specified.\n\nIf they intend we can only see region where blueshift upto 3E occurs? \"maximum energy observed is 3E.\" That is given; maybe detection limited to that narrow spectral line; we only count photons at that energy? The question: \"What fraction of the sphere surface can we observe when it is expanding in such a way?\" Possibly they consider that we only detect photons within that narrow line, which now is broadened; but detection of line maybe limited to certain energy range near original? The phrasing ambiguous.\n\nHowever using D_max=3 gave β=0.8, leading to cosθ_max where D>=? Perhaps they consider that we can only observe photons with energy between E and 3E (i.e., blueshifted only) because redshifted ones below E are outside detection? So fraction is those α such that observed energy ≤3E (that's all?) Actually max =3E, min maybe approach zero? But we consider only part where energy is within [E,3E]? That region is cosθ >=0.5 as above, fraction = 0.25 of total sphere.\n\nBut answer options not include exactly 1/4; B says less than a quarter, I think they'd consider that we cannot see exactly quarter because the boundary where cosθ=0.5 gives observed energy exactly E, but we probably need >E, so slightly less, thus less than a quarter. So answer B.\n\nThus I think answer B.\n\nLet's provide answer.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.3589787386081298e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999489, "logp_correct": -5.109698852795723e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999982118638981, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7713501830152514, "cot_lp_min64": -1.671727464132431, "frac_below_-5": 0.012609858616736722, "digit_lp_min": -1.5020525455474854, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.03649061545729637, "n_cot_tokens": 2617, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -4.020586212614292, "p_nocot": 0.01794244376911407, "uplift": 4.020586212614241, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.218584, "B": 0.017942, "C": 0.000542, "D": 0.762932}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recIDiDKKrN61Auyr", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Your overhear two chemists talking to each other as they leave a synthetic organic chemistry lab. One asks the other \"So, how did it go?\" The second chemist replies, \"Not well - my compounds are on top of each other.\"\n\nWhat is the second chemist most likely referring to?\n\nA) The compounds they are working with are bonding to each other through non-covalent/van der Waals interactions.\nB) The compounds they are working with have similar optical rotations.\nC) The compounds they are working with have similar boiling points.\nD) The compounds they are working with have similar polarities.\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Your overhear two chemists talking to each other as they leave a synthetic organic chemistry lab. One asks the other \"So, how did it go?\" The second chemist replies, \"Not well - my compounds are on top of each other.\"\n\nWhat is the second chemist most likely referring to?\n\nA) The compounds they are working with are bonding to each other through non-covalent/van der Waals interactions.\nB) The compounds they are working with have similar optical rotations.\nC) The compounds they are working with have similar boiling points.\nD) The compounds they are working with have similar polarities.\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recIDiDKKrN61Auyr:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 41751, 8138, 373, 25, 392, 2874, 1775, 533, 922, 43556, 553, 402, 2344, 328, 2454, 1273, 3692, 52762, 1151, 30795, 316, 75539, 24297, 90457, 50342, 306, 188419, 30, 730, 37001, 19448, 9887, 11, 392, 263, 2344, 328, 2454, 1273, 1, 2023, 6716, 316, 124719, 36206, 1919, 25522, 56631, 5192, 316, 6771, 161727, 13, 2604, 2023, 6716, 316, 392, 187970, 553, 75875, 1, 1299, 140997, 306, 6000, 188419, 30, 41021, 2411, 364, 8093, 355, 25, 2893, 3452, 25066, 299, 14, 8005, 1227, 27969, 1288, 30722, 25, 43556, 402, 2344, 328, 2454, 1273, 35805, 140997, 10112, 5192, 316, 140997, 30722, 13, 3072, 17898, 5720, 392, 263, 2344, 328, 2454, 1273, 1, 10112, 30795, 316, 90457, 50342, 306, 7322, 188419, 503, 10067, 1819, 6000, 5192, 316, 6771, 79667, 5571, 30, 17796, 363, 5003, 6771, 79667, 5571, 8117, 316, 24710, 39612, 656, 3601, 98447, 13, 17796, 415, 25, 6771, 39618, 1629, 8117, 316, 24710, 306, 39612, 4493, 188419, 11, 43556, 650, 1331, 4717, 350, 263, 2344, 741, 17796, 418, 25, 37876, 141136, 6771, 625, 19271, 364, 976, 27179, 392, 3697, 4795, 553, 402, 2344, 328, 2454, 1273, 1, 3572, 413, 261, 7989, 402, 392, 2898, 152588, 50342, 1, 306, 261, 140420, 21598, 13, 50901, 39618, 1629, 15286, 316, 90457, 50342, 306, 124719, 14, 30190, 13, 4381, 10741, 5571, 6771, 15286, 316, 3601, 98447, 24710, 13, 21580, 382, 945, 29166, 30, 730, 37001, 19448, 73786, 11, 125216, 4783, 4493, 6000, 188419, 26, 538, 43556, 679, 6771, 161727, 11, 57956, 2461, 4717, 7109, 263, 2344, 328, 2454, 1273, 163947, 71500, 415, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 415, 13], "n_tokens": 279, "words": 209, "band_lo": 101.0, "band_hi": 404.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the joke/pun: \"Not well - my compounds are on top of each other.\" Likely refers to similarity causing overlapping peaks in chromatography? In synthetic organic lab, \"on top of each other\" could refer to TLC plates where spots overlap due to similar polarity. Or could refer to \"structures are stacked\" like stacking in column chromatography? Let's think.\n\nOption A: non-covalent/van der Waals interactions: compounds on top of each other physically stacking maybe due to stacking interactions. But phrasing \"on top of each other\" maybe refers to overlapping peaks in gas chromatography or moving through column due to similar boiling points? Option C says similar boiling points leading to difficulty separation by distillation. Option D: similar polarities leading to difficulty in separation via chromatography, compounds elute together (on top). Option B: optical rotations similar not typical.\n\nThe phrase \"compounds are on top of each other\" might be a pun on \"overlapping peaks\" in a chromatogram. Similar polarities leads to overlapping peaks in TLC/GC. Boiling points similar leads to distillation difficulty. Which is more apt? In synthetic organic labs, purification often via column chromatography; if compounds have similar polarity, they'll run together (\"on top of each other\").\n\nGuess D.\n\nThus answer: D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.182837463100441e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999956, "logp_correct": -4.4128418610350334e-15, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000044, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.8996838571350758, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1245723006505548, "frac_below_-5": 0.022556390977443608, "digit_lp_min": null, "answer_first_mention_lp": -8.320462075062096e-05, "n_cot_tokens": 266, "lp_end": -3.4570634852570947e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -1.5332679452308247, "p_nocot": 0.21582919559886593, "uplift": 1.5332679452308202, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.025777, "B": 0.005076, "C": 0.753318, "D": 0.215829}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recIMcQfaQNa6vzsT", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Astronomers are studying a system of three exoplanets (Planet1, Planet2, and Planet3) with circular orbits discovered through the TTV method. They have found that the ratio of the equilibrium temperatures between Planet1 and Planet2 is approximately 1.4, and between Planet2 and Planet3, it is about 2.3. They have also found that the ratio of the masses between Planet1 and Planet2 is approximately 1.15, and between Planet2 and Planet3, it is about 1.35. By what factor is the orbital period of Planet3 larger than that of Planet1, if the albedo for all three planets is equal to 0.3 (similar to that of Earth)?\n\nA) ~ 4.4\nB) ~ 3.2\nC) ~ 10.4\nD) ~ 33.4\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Astronomers are studying a system of three exoplanets (Planet1, Planet2, and Planet3) with circular orbits discovered through the TTV method. They have found that the ratio of the equilibrium temperatures between Planet1 and Planet2 is approximately 1.4, and between Planet2 and Planet3, it is about 2.3. They have also found that the ratio of the masses between Planet1 and Planet2 is approximately 1.15, and between Planet2 and Planet3, it is about 1.35. By what factor is the orbital period of Planet3 larger than that of Planet1, if the albedo for all three planets is equal to 0.3 (similar to that of Earth)?\n\nA) ~ 4.4\nB) ~ 3.2\nC) ~ 10.4\nD) ~ 33.4\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recIMcQfaQNa6vzsT:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 4792, 364, 2167, 679, 3407, 513, 166310, 1719, 483, 37626, 503, 24043, 11, 16988, 656, 353, 15216, 350, 2623, 278, 31922, 35357, 741, 1416, 679, 69972, 328, 87639, 28295, 25, 353, 28373, 16, 820, 353, 28373, 17, 42308, 230, 220, 16, 13, 19, 326, 353, 28373, 17, 820, 353, 28373, 18, 42308, 230, 220, 17, 13, 18, 13, 8820, 4842, 69972, 25, 391, 16, 820, 391, 17, 42308, 230, 220, 16, 13, 1055, 326, 391, 17, 820, 391, 18, 42308, 230, 220, 16, 13, 2467, 13, 19792, 12332, 328, 132498, 5265, 328, 40880, 18, 15001, 316, 40880, 16, 364, 39520, 434, 137684, 12629, 350, 15, 13, 18, 741, 2214, 66223, 483, 2684, 434, 137684, 11, 87639, 12088, 20102, 402, 9324, 591, 8253, 11, 8253, 885, 82370, 536, 11, 5178, 13, 64495, 20690, 25, 353, 28373, 314, 723, 350, 16, 533, 355, 8, 451, 78943, 820, 350, 1125, 3478, 3669, 261, 61, 17, 8, 6045, 42923, 16, 14, 19, 92, 395, 37626, 503, 24043, 350, 141027, 5661, 23769, 11, 5178, 8476, 2632, 353, 28373, 35353, 251, 451, 42923, 16, 14, 19, 92, 261, 183239, 16, 14, 17, 16255, 134644, 2684, 8253, 11, 451, 9594, 13, 2632, 353, 28373, 35353, 251, 261, 183239, 16, 14, 17, 16255, 23586, 18051, 328, 48294, 314, 350, 51, 28373, 18051, 8, 183239, 17, 16255, 2632, 581, 665, 1646, 69972, 328, 23976, 166736, 51760, 25, 261, 16, 820, 261, 17, 314, 350, 51, 17, 820, 353, 16, 73602, 17, 314, 350, 16, 14, 16, 13, 19, 73602, 17, 30, 24305, 353, 16, 26742, 17, 314, 16, 13, 19, 871, 353, 17, 26742, 16, 314, 220, 16, 14, 16, 13, 19, 314, 15, 13, 43518, 13, 2632, 261, 16, 23839, 17, 314, 350, 51, 17, 26742, 16, 73602, 17, 314, 350, 15, 13, 43518, 73602, 17, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 6231, 13, 2632, 261, 17, 382, 12384, 1572, 261, 16, 350, 21910, 353, 7266, 395, 220, 16, 741, 2632, 261, 17, 23839, 16, 314, 220, 16, 14, 15, 13, 6231, 42308, 230, 220, 16, 13, 6768, 364, 151907, 353, 17, 26742, 18, 314, 30, 29844, 353, 17, 26742, 18, 314, 220, 17, 13, 18, 30, 50557, 18051, 2870, 40880, 17, 326, 40880, 18, 382, 1078, 220, 17, 13, 18, 13, 52762, 1151, 353, 17, 26742, 18, 314, 220, 17, 13, 18, 350, 21910, 220, 17, 140032, 1572, 220, 18, 741, 7801, 261, 17, 23839, 18, 314, 350, 51, 18, 26742, 17, 73602, 17, 314, 350, 16, 14, 17, 13, 18, 73602, 17, 42308, 230, 350, 15, 13, 33488, 73602, 17, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 13589, 13, 2632, 261, 18, 23839, 17, 314, 220, 16, 14, 15, 13, 13589, 42308, 230, 220, 20, 13, 2270, 364, 84787, 21700, 25, 261, 18, 23839, 16, 314, 350, 64, 18, 23839, 17, 44406, 64, 17, 23839, 16, 8, 314, 220, 20, 13, 2270, 425, 220, 16, 13, 6768, 42308, 230, 220, 702, 13, 2991, 364, 10620, 132498, 5265, 398, 35353, 251, 261, 42923, 18, 14, 17, 92, 350, 15640, 23938, 885, 6914, 3954, 741, 2632, 398, 18, 27120, 16, 314, 350, 64, 18, 23839, 16, 8, 42923, 18, 14, 17, 92, 314, 350, 702, 13, 2991, 8, 42923, 16, 13, 20, 16255, 45438, 25, 44237, 7, 702, 13, 2991, 8, 42308, 230, 220, 18, 13, 25050, 26, 1815, 7602, 18, 30, 50557, 350, 702, 13, 2991, 8, 42923, 18, 14, 17, 92, 314, 350, 702, 13, 2991, 8, 42923, 16, 92, 425, 44237, 7, 702, 13, 2991, 8, 314, 220, 702, 13, 2991, 425, 220, 18, 13, 25050, 42308, 230, 220, 2546, 13, 19, 364, 84787, 398, 18, 1078, 220, 2546, 4238, 398, 16, 13, 1328, 15248, 6052, 415, 364, 976, 4842, 69972, 1340, 413, 5018, 2164, 625, 6118, 11, 10112, 316, 2371, 57291, 484, 8253, 382, 2684, 5178, 13, 30456, 413, 1023, 4525, 581, 1309, 3609, 57556, 395, 8280, 23938, 889, 114609, 57556, 175463, 15001, 8253, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 364, 4383, 8663, 25, 353, 16, 26742, 17, 28, 16, 13, 19, 871, 261, 16, 23839, 17, 314, 350, 51, 17, 26742, 16, 73602, 17, 314, 350, 16, 14, 16, 13, 19, 73602, 17, 314, 350, 15, 13, 43518, 73602, 17, 28, 15, 13, 6231, 813, 261, 17, 314, 220, 16, 13, 6768, 261, 16, 364, 51, 17, 26742, 18, 28, 17, 13, 18, 871, 261, 17, 23839, 18, 314, 350, 16, 14, 17, 13, 18, 73602, 17, 314, 220, 15, 13, 13589, 11, 813, 261, 18, 314, 220, 20, 13, 2270, 261, 17, 314, 220, 20, 13, 2270, 9, 16, 13, 6768, 261, 16, 314, 220, 702, 13, 2991, 261, 16, 364, 47, 35353, 251, 261, 42923, 18, 14, 17, 16255, 2632, 12332, 314, 350, 702, 13, 2991, 8, 42923, 16, 13, 20, 92, 314, 2794, 7, 16, 13, 20, 9, 2943, 702, 13, 2991, 741, 68859, 702, 13, 2991, 171441, 220, 17, 13, 33025, 13, 425, 16, 13, 20, 314, 220, 18, 13, 37516, 871, 319, 42923, 18, 13, 37516, 102838, 2546, 13, 19, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 364, 94272, 6011, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 415], "n_tokens": 857, "words": 279, "band_lo": 147.5, "band_hi": 590.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the problem.\n\nWe have three exoplanets with circular orbits, discovered by TTV (transit timing variations). We have ratios of equilibrium temperatures: T_eq1 / T_eq2 ≈ 1.4 and T_eq2 / T_eq3 ≈ 2.3. Also mass ratios: M1 / M2 ≈ 1.15 and M2 / M3 ≈ 1.35. Need factor of orbital period of Planet3 relative to Planet1.\n\nGiven albedo equal (0.3). For planets with same albedo, equilibrium temperature depends on distance from star, star's luminosity, etc. Temperature formula: T_eq = [ (1 - A) L_star / (16 π σ a^2) ]^{1/4} for circular orbits (assuming fast rotation, etc.) So T_eq ∝ L^{1/4} a^{-1/2}. Assuming same star, L constant. So T_eq ∝ a^{-1/2}. Thus ratio of distances = (T_eq ratio)^{-2}. So we can find ratios of semi-major axes: a1 / a2 = (T2 / T1)^2 = (1/1.4)^2? Wait T1/T2 =1.4 => T2/T1 = 1/1.4 =0.714. So a1/a2 = (T2/T1)^2 = (0.714)^2 ≈ 0.51. So a2 is larger than a1 (since T higher for 1). So a2/a1 = 1/0.51 ≈ 1.96.\n\nSimilarly T2/T3 =? Given T2/T3 = 2.3? Actually ratio between Planet2 and Planet3 is about 2.3. Likely T2/T3 = 2.3 (since 2 hotter than 3). Then a2/a3 = (T3/T2)^2 = (1/2.3)^2 ≈ (0.435)^2 ≈ 0.189. So a3/a2 = 1/0.189 ≈ 5.29.\n\nThus combine: a3/a1 = (a3/a2)*(a2/a1) = 5.29 * 1.96 ≈ 10.37.\n\nNow orbital period P ∝ a^{3/2} (Kepler's third law). So P3/P1 = (a3/a1)^{3/2} = (10.37)^{1.5}. Compute: sqrt(10.37) ≈ 3.219; then ^3? Actually (10.37)^{3/2} = (10.37)^{1} * sqrt(10.37) = 10.37 * 3.219 ≈ 33.4.\n\nThus P3 about 33 times P1. This matches answer D.\n\nThe mass ratios may be extra information not needed, maybe to check assumption that star is same etc. Could be they thought we need total masses for Kepler but planetary masses negligible relative star.\n\nThus answer D.\n\nCheck numbers: T1/T2=1.4 => a1/a2 = (T2/T1)^2 = (1/1.4)^2 = (0.714)^2=0.51 so a2 = 1.96 a1.\n\nT2/T3=2.3 => a2/a3 = (1/2.3)^2 = 0.189, so a3 = 5.29 a2 = 5.29*1.96 a1 = 10.37 a1.\n\nP ∝ a^{3/2}. So factor = (10.37)^{1.5} = exp(1.5*ln10.37). ln10.37≈ 2.338. *1.5 = 3.507 => e^{3.507}=33.4.\n\nThus answer D.\n\nProvide format: ANSWER: D", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.6689287960502952e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999885, "logp_correct": -1.1482049454800169e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807907361, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.3749164683933766, "cot_lp_min64": -1.328154437891662, "frac_below_-5": 0.005924170616113744, "digit_lp_min": -1.9094371795654297, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.12081398069858551, "n_cot_tokens": 844, "lp_end": -0.10600172728300095, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -6.578414867187514, "p_nocot": 0.0013900509648006089, "uplift": 6.578414867187503, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.436741, "B": 0.560786, "C": 0.001083, "D": 0.00139}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recIN1LyuCWtFwrJ4", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A particle of mass m moves in a 1D potential given by V(x) = -α δ(x), where δ(x) is the usual Dirac delta function, and α is some positive constant. The particle is bound. Find the value of x0 such that the probability of finding the particle with |x|< x0 is exactly equal to 1/2.\n\nA) ln(2) [ℏ^2/2mα]\nB) ln(4) [ℏ^2/2mα]\nC) ln(4) [ℏ^2/mα^2]\nD) ln(2) [ℏ^2/mα^2]\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A particle of mass m moves in a 1D potential given by V(x) = -α δ(x), where δ(x) is the usual Dirac delta function, and α is some positive constant. The particle is bound. Find the value of x0 such that the probability of finding the particle with |x|< x0 is exactly equal to 1/2.\n\nA) ln(2) [ℏ^2/2mα]\nB) ln(4) [ℏ^2/2mα]\nC) ln(4) [ℏ^2/mα^2]\nD) ln(2) [ℏ^2/mα^2]\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recIN1LyuCWtFwrJ4:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 11608, 2608, 328, 22728, 18800, 6451, 631, 314, 533, 727, 6551, 4061, 741, 66273, 11608, 2608, 20485, 2706, 25, 80339, 4061, 8, 314, 44237, 7, 1664, 8, 319, 183239, 1664, 91, 87, 91, 4180, 1919, 3610, 314, 284, 3793, 820, 41051, 61, 17, 350, 1938, 631, 314, 533, 727, 6551, 4061, 8784, 24305, 2371, 25, 2214, 18800, 6451, 631, 314, 533, 329, 6551, 4061, 8, 483, 329, 29, 15, 11, 11608, 2608, 5954, 457, 314, 533, 350, 15026, 61, 17, 60504, 17, 41051, 61, 17, 741, 1958, 20485, 2706, 25, 80339, 4061, 8, 314, 44237, 7, 1664, 8, 319, 183239, 1664, 91, 87, 91, 92, 1919, 3610, 314, 12912, 14, 5762, 61, 17, 13, 2632, 3793, 66938, 316, 329, 364, 84787, 34933, 27591, 25, 1022, 15927, 91, 61, 17, 314, 3610, 319, 183239, 17, 1664, 91, 87, 91, 16255, 19792, 1215, 15, 2238, 484, 35353, 104, 22305, 12, 87, 15, 107298, 87, 15, 92, 3610, 319, 183239, 17, 1664, 91, 87, 91, 92, 29472, 314, 220, 16, 14, 17, 364, 19048, 123676, 25, 314, 220, 17, 35353, 104, 62, 15, 42923, 87, 15, 92, 3610, 319, 183239, 17, 1664, 1215, 92, 29472, 314, 220, 17, 58, 533, 350, 1664, 27334, 17, 1664, 915, 319, 183239, 17, 1664, 1215, 53940, 62, 15, 42923, 87, 15, 92, 314, 220, 17, 58, 533, 350, 16, 14, 17, 8, 319, 183239, 17, 1664, 1215, 53940, 62, 15, 42923, 87, 15, 92, 314, 220, 17, 58, 66319, 16, 14, 17, 4898, 68, 183239, 17, 1664, 1215, 15, 92, 533, 220, 16, 8, 6045, 314, 66319, 68, 183239, 17, 1664, 1215, 15, 92, 533, 16, 8, 314, 220, 16, 533, 319, 183239, 17, 1664, 1215, 15, 92, 364, 1984, 314, 220, 16, 14, 17, 871, 220, 16, 533, 319, 183239, 17, 1664, 1215, 15, 92, 314, 220, 16, 14, 17, 871, 319, 183239, 17, 1664, 1215, 15, 92, 314, 220, 16, 14, 17, 871, 533, 17, 1664, 1215, 15, 314, 68859, 7, 16, 14, 17, 8, 314, 533, 2943, 17, 871, 1215, 15, 314, 350, 2943, 220, 17, 60504, 17, 1664, 3991, 1664, 314, 284, 3793, 820, 41051, 61, 17, 13, 2632, 1215, 15, 314, 350, 2943, 220, 17, 8, 820, 350, 17, 425, 284, 3793, 820, 41051, 61, 17, 8, 314, 350, 2943, 220, 17, 8, 41051, 61, 17, 820, 350, 17, 284, 3793, 3991, 84787, 6052, 25, 68859, 7, 17, 8, 723, 5762, 61, 17, 820, 350, 17, 284, 3793, 15710, 575, 2560, 4213, 5317, 355, 364, 4383, 1273, 3880, 25, 418, 853, 68859, 7, 19, 8, 1118, 29702, 220, 17, 68859, 17, 8117, 316, 2684, 30, 68859, 7, 19, 8, 41051, 61, 17, 27334, 17, 76, 727, 8, 314, 350, 17, 68859, 17, 8, 41051, 61, 17, 27334, 17, 76, 727, 8, 314, 68859, 17, 41051, 61, 17, 27334, 76, 727, 8, 1118, 382, 3503, 1039, 13, 4037, 6145, 13, 415, 6771, 483, 284, 727, 61, 17, 79744, 364, 84787, 6052, 355, 13], "n_tokens": 505, "words": 139, "band_lo": 57.5, "band_hi": 230.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need bound state of attractive delta potential V = -α δ(x). Known bound state wavefunction: ψ(x) = sqrt(κ) e^{-κ|x|}, where κ = m α / ħ^2 (for V = -α δ(x)). Wait check: For delta potential V = - g δ(x) with g>0, bound state energy E = - (mg^2)/(2 ħ^2). And wavefunction: ψ(x) = sqrt(κ) e^{-κ|x|} where κ = mg/ħ^2. So α corresponds to g.\n\nThus probability density: |ψ|^2 = κ e^{-2κ|x|}. Need x0 such that ∫_{-x0}^{x0} κ e^{-2κ|x|} dx = 1/2.\n\nSince symmetric: = 2 ∫_0^{x0} κ e^{-2κ x} dx = 2[ - (κ/(2κ)) e^{-2κ x}]_0^{x0} = 2[ - (1/2) e^{-2κ x}]_0^{x0} = 2[ -(1/2)(e^{-2κ x0} - 1) ] = -(e^{-2κ x0} -1) = 1 - e^{-2κ x0}.\n\nSet = 1/2 => 1 - e^{-2κ x0} = 1/2 => e^{-2κ x0} = 1/2 => -2κ x0 = ln(1/2) = -ln2 => x0 = (ln 2)/(2κ).\n\nκ = m α / ħ^2. So x0 = (ln 2) / (2 * m α / ħ^2) = (ln 2) ħ^2 / (2 m α).\n\nThus answer: ln(2) [ħ^2 / (2 m α)] i.e., option A.\n\nCheck other options: B has ln(4) which equals 2 ln2 leading to same? ln(4) ħ^2/(2mα) = (2 ln2) ħ^2/(2mα) = ln2 ħ^2/(mα) which is double our. Not correct. D similar with mα^2 nonsense.\n\nThus answer A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.145764938177308e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999996365, "logp_correct": -3.6345607359811244e-13, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000003635, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.24818771768979364, "cot_lp_min64": -0.47453413659030375, "frac_below_-5": 0.0020325203252032522, "digit_lp_min": -0.9742205739021301, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 492, "lp_end": -1.4543427823809907e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -3.0488223705036828, "p_nocot": 0.04741472850925639, "uplift": 3.0488223705033195, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.047415, "B": 0.95235, "C": 0.000118, "D": 0.000118}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recIOlKBsOeEcgkA1", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A eukaryotic cell evolved a mechanism to turn macromolecular building blocks into energy. The process occurs in mitochondria, which are cellular energy factories. In the series of redox reactions, the energy from food is stored between the phosphate groups and used as a universal cellular currency. The energy-laden molecules are shuttled out of the mitochondrion to serve in all cellular processes. You discovered a new anti-diabetes drug and want to investigate whether it has an effect on the mitochondria. You set up a range of experiments with your HEK293 cell line. Which of the experiments listed below will not help you discover the mitochondrial role of your drug:\n\nA) Confocal fluorescence microscopy after Mito-RTP staining of the cells\nB) Transformation of cells with recombinant luciferase and luminometer reading after 5 μM of luciferin addition to the supernatant\nC) Flow cytometry after labeling with 2.5 μM 5,5',6,6'-Tetrachloro-1,1',3, 3'-tetraethylbenzimidazolylcarbocyanine iodide\nD) Differential centrifugation extraction of mitochondria followed by the Glucose Uptake Colorimetric Assay Kit\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A eukaryotic cell evolved a mechanism to turn macromolecular building blocks into energy. The process occurs in mitochondria, which are cellular energy factories. In the series of redox reactions, the energy from food is stored between the phosphate groups and used as a universal cellular currency. The energy-laden molecules are shuttled out of the mitochondrion to serve in all cellular processes. You discovered a new anti-diabetes drug and want to investigate whether it has an effect on the mitochondria. You set up a range of experiments with your HEK293 cell line. Which of the experiments listed below will not help you discover the mitochondrial role of your drug:\n\nA) Confocal fluorescence microscopy after Mito-RTP staining of the cells\nB) Transformation of cells with recombinant luciferase and luminometer reading after 5 μM of luciferin addition to the supernatant\nC) Flow cytometry after labeling with 2.5 μM 5,5',6,6'-Tetrachloro-1,1',3, 3'-tetraethylbenzimidazolylcarbocyanine iodide\nD) Differential centrifugation extraction of mitochondria followed by the Glucose Uptake Colorimetric Assay Kit\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recIOlKBsOeEcgkA1:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11410, 1118, 12527, 738, 7116, 1652, 8275, 290, 148596, 5430, 328, 290, 7231, 13, 623, 7231, 382, 10329, 103062, 17345, 11, 581, 1682, 148596, 9327, 13, 22483, 1402, 32, 8, 20524, 2567, 134145, 116480, 1934, 391, 2955, 12401, 7683, 122176, 13, 391, 2955, 12401, 7683, 382, 261, 148596, 50162, 6451, 72578, 30, 50557, 391, 2955, 30021, 5952, 30, 391, 2955, 49, 7683, 30, 1328, 1481, 10224, 148596, 4842, 8138, 132479, 533, 10297, 364, 33, 8, 80554, 328, 11242, 483, 198585, 34623, 13325, 618, 326, 77273, 22008, 6085, 1934, 220, 20, 4403, 44, 328, 34623, 13325, 258, 5856, 316, 290, 2539, 32511, 493, 13, 1328, 16005, 188023, 34623, 13325, 258, 30, 1843, 34623, 13325, 618, 382, 22171, 166258, 423, 11, 12673, 34623, 13325, 258, 316, 2539, 32511, 493, 738, 3763, 23385, 2028, 69973, 4122, 402, 87485, 8374, 30, 50557, 34623, 13325, 618, 21416, 8844, 87485, 326, 34623, 13325, 258, 316, 10635, 4207, 26, 41068, 23385, 2028, 69973, 24832, 44013, 87485, 8374, 11, 1118, 40519, 148596, 1114, 13, 5551, 11, 290, 92679, 19282, 34623, 13325, 258, 188023, 423, 26, 538, 34623, 13325, 618, 382, 6772, 11242, 11, 34623, 13325, 258, 2804, 8088, 50162, 11, 889, 4275, 2061, 316, 9714, 87485, 13, 1328, 2023, 3644, 3609, 44013, 87485, 11, 625, 4857, 148596, 5430, 11, 889, 2928, 12331, 364, 34, 8, 33537, 49137, 11851, 1934, 102060, 483, 40509, 1099, 350, 62272, 4829, 53546, 81, 36538, 19980, 16111, 3643, 119989, 8, 1118, 16005, 148596, 50162, 6451, 2747, 10297, 364, 35, 8, 192034, 101185, 846, 387, 48166, 328, 102845, 18784, 12661, 656, 290, 4956, 143201, 179350, 814, 6822, 29890, 2740, 4744, 356, 23793, 13, 4956, 143201, 108958, 92679, 16005, 108958, 328, 48142, 1511, 11242, 11, 625, 102845, 18784, 13, 65680, 480, 402, 42329, 102845, 18784, 8740, 1520, 8512, 2236, 102845, 18784, 621, 625, 108958, 48142, 8516, 13, 2632, 484, 1481, 625, 1652, 11433, 148596, 5430, 13, 2632, 6052, 415, 364, 84787, 4733, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 415], "n_tokens": 337, "words": 218, "band_lo": 134.75, "band_hi": 539.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to identify which experiment will NOT help discover the mitochondrial role of the drug. The drug is anti-diabetes, we want mitochondrial effects. Options:\n\nA) Confocal fluorescence microscopy after Mito-RTP staining. Mito-RTP is a mitochondrial membrane potential dye? Actually MitoTracker Red? MitoRTP? This would assess mitochondrial mass/potential - helpful.\n\nB) Transformation of cells with recombinant luciferase and luminometer reading after 5 μM of luciferin addition to the supernatant. This measures extracellular luciferin? If luciferase is expressed intracellularly, adding luciferin to supernatant will allow luminescence based on ATP levels? Actually luciferase reaction uses ATP and luciferin to produce light; measuring luminescence indicates cellular ATP levels, which reflects mitochondrial function. However, the assay adds luciferin extracellularly; if luciferase is inside cells, luciferin must cross membrane, but it's used to measure ATP. This could give total cellular ATP, not specific mitochondrial role, but still relevant.\n\nC) Flow cytometry after labeling with TMRE (tetramethylrhodamine ethyl ester) which measures mitochondrial membrane potential -> helpful.\n\nD) Differential centrifugation extraction of mitochondria followed by the Glucose Uptake Colorimetric Assay Kit. Glucose uptake assay measures uptake of glucose into cells, not mitochondria. Doing it on isolated mitochondria doesn't make sense because mitochondria do not uptake glucose directly. So that would not help determine mitochondrial role. So answer D.\n\nThus output: ANSWER: D", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.5033933752638404e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999394, "logp_correct": -6.05836482699552e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999984502817478, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4854336656263619, "cot_lp_min64": -0.8113077709701134, "frac_below_-5": 0.0030864197530864196, "digit_lp_min": 0.0, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.031714484095573425, "n_cot_tokens": 324, "lp_end": -0.08225823938846588, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -5.006744738639016, "p_nocot": 0.006692654223283496, "uplift": 5.006744738638956, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 2e-06, "B": 0.993278, "C": 2.7e-05, "D": 0.006693}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
