3503, 15135, 33209, 3572, 413, 2684, 402, 2973, 18089, 11, 813, 290, 10705, 73005, 2928, 853, 90882, 13, 2214, 1489, 350, 40890, 8, 3503, 15135, 11, 290, 22148, 84, 1094, 402, 2684, 4307, 3572, 2338, 16499, 30, 5477, 625, 3239, 364, 125072, 19271, 6052, 395, 6771, 4928, 3572, 413, 220, 19, 3891, 13, 41021, 2371, 3880, 25, 220, 19, 11, 17, 11, 23, 11, 21, 364, 3335, 581, 3931, 457, 113185, 11, 6960, 220, 19, 350, 42498, 6584, 11, 80228, 27268, 10610, 60500, 472, 1920, 26, 2932, 469, 9590, 22516, 291, 30354, 6584, 11, 30354, 27268, 60500, 472, 16913, 871, 10954, 220, 19, 741, 3072, 581, 1217, 679, 34837, 1118, 19282, 10112, 220, 17, 945, 871, 3609, 220, 21, 13, 17796, 220, 21, 382, 3333, 364, 4827, 1078, 3463, 2973, 4801, 368, 107790, 350, 42498, 14, 3642, 8, 2454, 483, 457, 113185, 350, 17, 2454, 8, 8967, 220, 19, 11, 2932, 41158, 368, 3747, 350, 17190, 8, 1118, 2023, 679, 1606, 1001, 350, 4613, 78, 8, 5192, 316, 16499, 11, 3609, 220, 20, 30, 4037, 448, 5317, 364, 4827, 1078, 1217, 17227, 484, 290, 8601, 12334, 60582, 27467, 853, 2973, 469, 9590, 119448, 11, 8117, 316, 3407, 4149, 3891, 25, 350, 36, 14080, 11, 350, 57, 14080, 11, 326, 3886, 78, 30, 21926, 220, 17, 30, 4037, 3981, 364, 8093, 220, 23, 7890, 3101, 1932, 350, 59464, 2973, 457, 113185, 395, 2454, 328, 4242, 56804, 25, 80228, 11, 30354, 11, 34837, 11, 30704, 43276, 37741, 10112, 220, 23, 741, 3072, 34837, 326, 30704, 553, 2684, 11, 813, 625, 364, 84787, 6960, 6052, 382, 220, 21, 13, 41021, 19429, 2454, 328, 80228, 326, 30354, 10635, 457, 326, 1489, 314, 220, 19, 13, 623, 41158, 368, 3747, 34837, 28135, 2973, 457, 326, 1489, 314, 220, 17, 13, 17565, 220, 21, 13, 4569, 15248, 5317, 415, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 364], "n_tokens": 3033, "words": 2025, "band_lo": 1012.5, "band_hi": 4050.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to analyze the problem: Racemic 3-methylpent-1-ene is treated with Grubbs catalyst. How many possible products are there (excluding ethene)? The options: 4,2,8,6.\n\nInterpretation: 3-methylpent-1-ene: a five-carbon chain with a double bond at position 1; methyl substituent at carbon 3. So structure: CH2=CH-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3. That's 1-ene with methyl at carbon 3 (counting from double bond end). The substrate is racemic (i.e., chiral center at C3). So there is a (R) and (S) enantiomer present.\n\nGrubens catalyst (a ruthenium carbene) does olefin metathesis. Reaction of a terminal alkene like 1-ene usually leads to self-metathesis forming internal alkene plus ethylene (the question says exclude ethene). So self-metathesis of 3-methylpent-1-ene would give two possible products? Let's examine.\n\nSelf-metathesis: two molecules of the alkene interact; the double bond cleavage and recombination produce a new internal alkene product containing the carbon skeleton combined. For terminal alkene, product is a dimer (C5 + C5 = C10) internal alkene with ethylene as the other product.\n\nBut because the substrate is chiral (has stereocenter at C3), there could be diastereomeric products depending on which enantiomer reacts with which (i.e., homodimer of (R)-(R) vs (S)-(S) vs heterodimer (R)-(S)). Also the resulting double bond can have E/Z geometry. Also there could be positional isomers? Let's think.\n\nGeneral self-metathesis of a terminal alkene RCH=CH2 gives product: RCH=CH-R (internal alkene) plus ethylene. The internal alkene is symmetrical if both R same. For asymmetric R, there could be two possible internal alkenes i.e., the R group can be attached at either side of double bond (but they are same due to symmetry?). In metathesis, each original alkene’s two carbons recombine: they break the double bonds of each and recombine crosswise. For symmetrical, you get product where the two alkyl groups are now attached across the double bond.\n\nThe product for 3-methylpent-1-ene would be: combine two molecules: each has chain: CH2=CH-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3. Let's name carbons: 1 (CH2), 2 (CH), 3 (CH with methyl), 4 (CH2), 5 (CH3). The double bond is between C1 and C2. After metathesis, the new C=C bond connects C2 of one molecule with C2 of another? Let's recall mechanism: For terminal alkene, the generated product is a new internal alkene: R-CH=CH-R where R is the rest of chain beyond the double bond. So each original alkene contributes its internal carbon (C2) to the double bond. The external carbon (C1) becomes ethylene after cross. So product is dimer where double bond is between the C2 carbons of two molecules. So the resulting product is a 9-carbon internal alkene? Actually each fragment beyond C2 includes the rest of chain (C3-C5). So new alkene: (C3-C4-C5) attached to C2? Let's compute: each original: CH2=CH-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3. The part after the double bond includes C2 onward: CH-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3? Wait C2 is CH (sp2) originally; after reaction becomes sp2 carbon of internal double bond; attached to C3-5 as side chain.\n\nThus product would be: (CH-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3) =C= (CH-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3). So two identical fragments coupling at the former internal carbon, giving a C= C internal alkene with each side a butyl chain bearing a methyl at second carbon from the double bond? Let's draw: The fragment after C2: CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3 (a 3-carbon chain with methyl at carbon 1 of that fragment). Actually after C2, the carbon is attached to CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3 (i.e., a sec-butyl group with methyl branched at the carbon adjacent to the double bond). So the product is: (CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3)C= C(CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3). This internal alkene is symmetrical (same substituents on both sides). So the product is meso? But the substituents are identical, resulting double bond is between two identical groups, thus not chiral (unless geometric isomerism). Since the substituents are same, the E/Z could be possible? For internal alkene with identical substituents on each side (same groups) the stereochemistry is not defined? Actually for alkene with two identical substituents on each carbon (i.e., each carbon has two identical groups), the alkene is not stereogenic (no E/Z). In this case each carbon of the double bond has two substituents: one is a hydrogen? No, after metathesis, the internal carbon originally had a hydrogen? Let's check: Original C2 is CH (sp2) attached to C1 (CH2) and C3 (CH(CH3)). In product, C1 leaves as ethylene, so the double bond carbon now attached to C3 chain and double-bonded to other carbon, and also still attached to hydrogen? Actually earlier C2 had one hydrogen (CH). Yes it is CH with H, C3 group, and double bond to C1. After change, it loses bond to C1 replaced by double bond to other C2', still has the same hydrogen. So each double bond carbon has one hydrogen and the large substituent (C3-5). Thus each carbon has substituents: H and a large alkyl. Therefore each carbon is attached to two different substituents, and the alkene can have cis/trans (E/Z). Indeed internal alkene with each carbon bearing H and R gives stereochemistry. Since both sides are identical (R same), the geometry possibilities are only one? Actually if both sides are identical, the two possible configurations are the same? For a symmetric internal alkene (R-CH=CH-R) where each carbon is attached to H and R, the trans and cis are identical? Wait trans vs cis refers to relative positions of the higher priority groups. Since both sides have same groups, there is no distinction; the molecule is meso? Let's test: For example, 2-butene (CH3-CH=CH-CH3) has cis and trans isomers (cis: both CH3 on same side; trans: opposite). The groups are same on each carbon (CH3 vs H). So cis/trans are distinct. So here also we have R groups identical, so we can have cis and trans isomers.\n\nThus product can be either (E)- or (Z)-2-(3-methylbutyl)ethen? Actually name: 7-methyl-? Wait need carbon count. The dimer gives a 9-carbon chain? let's count: each fragment after C2 includes C3-5 (3 carbons) plus the double-bond carbon itself. So total C per fragment chain = 4? double bond carbon plus three more = 4. Two fragments give 8 carbons, but they share double bond carbons, so total carbon count = 8? Actually each fragment includes the internal carbon that becomes part of double bond. Two fragments together give 2 internal carbons + each 3 chain beyond = 2+3+3 = 8. So product is C8 alkene (octene) with methyl substituents at positions? Let's not worry.\n\nNow consider that starting material is racemic, there are R and S enantiomers. Self-metathesis can involve:\n\n1) R + R (homodimer) forms product with certain stereochemistry (the R configurations at the chiral centers of each fragment become part of the product? After reaction, the chiral centers (C3) remain unchanged. The product will have two stereocenters (one on each side of the double bond). If both fragments are R, then the product will have two R centers (RR). Similarly SS gives SS. Those two are enantiomers of each other (RR vs SS). So that would give a pair of enantiomers (count as 2 stereoisomers). But if we consider they are not resolved (racemic mixture provides both), we count both distinct products.\n\n2) R + S heterodimer gives product with one R and one S center (RS). That could be meso if there is symmetry? The product would have R at one side and S at the other; due to molecular symmetry, that could be a meso compound if the molecule has an internal plane of symmetry. Since the two halves are mirror images, the product could be meso (identical to its mirror). So RS leads to only one stereoisomer (meso) not a pair.\n\nNow also consider E/Z geometry of the double bond. For each diastereomeric combination (RR/SS, RS) there could be E or Z. However, need to see if geometry is compatible with chiral centers? The double bond geometry may influence relative configuration? For symmetrical product with identical substituents, both E and Z are possible for each of the RR/SS (i.e., two enantiomeric pairs each having E and Z). And for RS (meso) also could have E or Z? In meso case, E/Z produce distinct diastereomers? For 2-butene meso is not term; cis-2-butene is (R,R)?? Actually in 2-butene, with two stereocenters? Wait 2-butene doesn’t have chiral centers. Let's think.\n\nOur product has chiral centers at carbon 4? Actually the carbon bearing methyl (original C3) remains chiral. So each side of the double bond has a chiral center adjacent. The geometry of double bond could affect relationship between these chiral centers (like diastereomers). So overall we may have multiple stereoisomers.\n\nWe need total number of distinct products (excluding ethylene). Likely answer 6? Let's compute possibilities.\n\nAssume we have homodimer R+R gives diastereomer possibilities: Could give both E and Z. Each is chiral (since both centers R,R but overall may have meso? No, symmetrical but chiral centers same leads to a pair of enantiomers? Actually the molecule may have a plane of symmetry if double bond geometry is trans? Let's consider: If both sides are R, the molecule may have chirality overall? Might be meso? We need to enumerate.\n\nBetter approach: Use combinatorial counting: Two chiral centers (C*) each can be R or S => 4 configurational combos: RR, SS, RS, SR. But RS and SR are same because the molecules are symmetrical? Actually product may be unsymmetrical? The two fragments are identical, so swapping halves is same molecule. So only three distinct configurations: RR (and SS enantiomer), RS (meso). But the racemic starting mixture yields both RR and SS (enantiomeric pair). So count them as two products? Usually they count distinct stereoisomers ignoring enantiomeric pairs? Usually they count each as separate product (since they are different). The question likely counts enantiomers as separate products? Maybe they count them as two. Then we also need E/Z for each.\n\nThus for RR, there are E and Z (two). For SS also two. But RR and SS are enantiomers; each has its own E/Z forms. So total distinct stereoisomers = 4 (RR_E, RR_Z, SS_E, SS_Z) + RS_E, RS_Z maybe (but RS_E and RS_Z may be enantiomeric? Actually RS could be meso? Let's determine: RS is a meso form if there is internal symmetry; geometry may destroy that symmetry? For the molecule to be meso, it must have plane of symmetry; cis vs trans could break this? For 2,3-butanediol, meso is when R,S with appropriate arrangement. Usually meso applies for molecules where the stereocenters are opposite and the molecule is symmetrical. The double bond geometry might affect symmetry.\n\nConsider the dimer: symmetrical about the central C=C double bond. Each side mirror image. For RS configuration, the molecule is meso irrespective of E/Z? The double bond geometry might be same on both sides, so the overall molecule still has symmetry. For Z (cis) double bond, the substituents on same side might break plane? I'm not sure.\n\nNevertheless typical answer for similar question might be 4 products. Let's check options: 4,2,8,6.\n\nIf we include E/Z, likely 4 (RR_E, RR_Z pair counted as two; plus enantiomeric SS_E, SS_Z counted as distinct => that's 4). But we also have RS which adds maybe 2 more => total 6. Option 6 is present.\n\nWhat about including both homodimers (RR/SS) each with E/Z (2 each) gives 4, plus heterodimer (RS) which could have only one (meso) due to plane, total 5? Not an option.\n\nWhat about also considering that the starting racemic mixture has both enantiomers, leading to three possible products: (E)-, (Z)-, and meso? That's 2? Not match.\n\nOption 8 seems too high (consider both E/Z for each of four configurations: RR, SS, RS, SR counting separately maybe 8). But RS and SR are same, so not.\n\nThus likely answer is 6. Let's assume each of RR and SS produce E and Z = 4. The heterodimer RS produces both E and Z = 2. Total 6. That matches option D.\n\nThus answer D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.969590397900902e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999519, "logp_correct": -4.8035322385774105e-14, "letter_mass": 1.000000000000048, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7810252274316623, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4965856371459552, "frac_below_-5": 0.017218543046357615, "digit_lp_min": -5.2553911209106445, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0002338612830499187, "n_cot_tokens": 3020, "lp_end": -0.16848516464233398, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -1.0637772062856765, "p_nocot": 0.34514964364902273, "uplift": 1.0637772062856286, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.209344, "B": 0.002326, "C": 0.443181, "D": 0.34515}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recJYLOV2gf7p1Mx2", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Grover iterator is a quantum gate that amplifies the amplitude of the target state while reducing the amplitude of the non-target states. What are the eigenvalues of the Grover iterator G in terms of θ?\n\nA) cos(θ/4)+isin(θ/4), cos(θ/4)−isin(θ/4)\nB) cos(θ)+isin(θ), cos(θ)−isin(θ)\nC) cos(θ/2)+isin(θ/2), cos(θ/2)−isin(θ/2)\nD) cos2θ+isin2θ, cos2θ−isin2θ\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Grover iterator is a quantum gate that amplifies the amplitude of the target state while reducing the amplitude of the non-target states. What are the eigenvalues of the Grover iterator G in terms of θ?\n\nA) cos(θ/4)+isin(θ/4), cos(θ/4)−isin(θ/4)\nB) cos(θ)+isin(θ), cos(θ)−isin(θ)\nC) cos(θ/2)+isin(θ/2), cos(θ/2)−isin(θ/2)\nD) cos2θ+isin2θ, cos2θ−isin2θ\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recJYLOV2gf7p1Mx2:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 30890, 16453, 445, 30787, 499, 314, 350, 17, 91, 82, 3784, 82, 91, 533, 357, 8, 532, 11, 1919, 532, 382, 87026, 484, 169694, 3783, 13, 623, 10561, 7222, 306, 290, 1920, 51578, 4918, 1014, 11768, 656, 3783, 1022, 86, 29, 326, 1617, 35656, 127861, 6392, 553, 319, 42923, 32438, 575, 220, 17, 4114, 92, 12951, 30, 50557, 7024, 25, 13174, 2187, 7, 4114, 8, 314, 44237, 6103, 33677, 8, 1919, 391, 382, 2086, 328, 9368, 13, 7801, 290, 2608, 1934, 2454, 16453, 445, 43155, 155086, 656, 16538, 220, 17, 4114, 306, 290, 16499, 11, 813, 10561, 7222, 328, 499, 553, 319, 42923, 32438, 575, 220, 17, 4114, 16255, 3072, 290, 3880, 4335, 25, 418, 382, 8974, 12962, 58882, 575, 2187, 12962, 314, 319, 42923, 32438, 575, 12962, 16255, 363, 25, 319, 42923, 32438, 575, 12962, 14, 17, 16255, 415, 25, 8974, 17, 4114, 58882, 575, 2187, 17, 4114, 314, 319, 42923, 32438, 575, 220, 17, 4114, 16255, 2632, 10561, 7222, 553, 319, 42923, 32438, 575, 220, 17, 4114, 92, 575, 2560, 4213, 8974, 17, 4114, 58882, 575, 2187, 17, 4114, 871, 5317, 415, 13, 5551, 1236, 25382, 13174, 16453, 445, 43155, 472, 23769, 656, 220, 17, 4114, 11, 10561, 7222, 319, 42923, 32438, 575, 220, 17, 4114, 16255, 2632, 6052, 415, 13], "n_tokens": 229, "words": 117, "band_lo": 73.75, "band_hi": 295.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to recall Grover iterator G = (2|s><s| - I) O, where O is oracle that flips target. The eigenvalues in the two-dimensional space spanned by target |w> and its orthogonal component are e^{± i 2θ} perhaps? Actually standard: define sin(θ) = sqrt(M/N) where M is number of solutions. Then the state after each Grover iteration rotates by angle 2θ in the plane, so eigenvalues of G are e^{± i 2θ}. But the options given: B is cos θ ± i sin θ = e^{± i θ}. C: e^{± i θ/2}. D: cos2θ ± i sin2θ = e^{± i 2θ}. So eigenvalues are e^{± i 2θ} i.e., cos2θ ± i sin2θ => option D. However some references define Grover iteration as rotation by 2θ, eigenvalues e^{± i 2θ}. So answer D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -6.556489999809401e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999728, "logp_correct": -2.719659412889434e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807907519, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.42907891324627295, "cot_lp_min64": -0.5401529915218876, "frac_below_-5": 0.0, "digit_lp_min": -1.5026779174804688, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 216, "lp_end": -0.00565109308809042, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -13.061971606500519, "p_nocot": 2.12450524941447e-06, "uplift": 13.061971606500492, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 2e-06, "B": 0.060086, "C": 0.93991, "D": 2e-06}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recJpyGtGIsxulevT", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: If uncertainty in space of electron's location, which is travelling with speed v= 2* 10^8 m/s along x-direction is Δx=0.1 nm . Based on the infromation estimate the minimum uncertainty in the energy ΔE of electron.\n\nA) ~10^(-17) J\nB) ~10^(-18) J\nC) ~10^(-19) J\nD) ~10^(-16) J\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: If uncertainty in space of electron's location, which is travelling with speed v= 2* 10^8 m/s along x-direction is Δx=0.1 nm . Based on the infromation estimate the minimum uncertainty in the energy ΔE of electron.\n\nA) ~10^(-17) J\nB) ~10^(-18) J\nC) ~10^(-19) J\nD) ~10^(-16) J\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recJpyGtGIsxulevT:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 17950, 11085, 44942, 306, 5954, 26143, 36, 4335, 4010, 44942, 26143, 87, 314, 15, 13, 16, 43183, 314, 220, 16, 68, 12, 702, 284, 13, 131282, 26023, 483, 7733, 323, 314, 220, 17, 68, 23, 284, 5031, 4251, 1215, 13, 7649, 1679, 11186, 9667, 44942, 30540, 25, 26143, 87, 26143, 79, 5064, 41051, 14, 17, 13, 2632, 26143, 79, 42308, 230, 41051, 27334, 17, 26143, 87, 741, 7801, 38871, 26143, 36, 316, 26143, 79, 4493, 121749, 5954, 30, 2214, 35925, 6207, 30, 323, 314, 220, 17, 68, 23, 284, 5031, 13, 23451, 328, 4207, 274, 314, 220, 18, 68, 23, 284, 5031, 11, 813, 323, 6574, 220, 15, 13, 36352, 66, 11, 35925, 6207, 13, 3072, 12951, 1199, 2893, 6004, 3492, 6207, 112838, 30, 44130, 121749, 5954, 457, 314, 275, 61, 17, 27334, 17, 76, 741, 19743, 44942, 306, 41184, 15286, 316, 44942, 306, 5954, 25, 26143, 36, 42308, 230, 350, 79, 14, 284, 8, 26143, 79, 350, 21910, 272, 36, 6662, 79, 314, 275, 5712, 741, 21926, 2684, 472, 323, 26143, 79, 13, 13602, 275, 314, 8558, 284, 