7, 49, 1118, 19282, 220, 17, 487, 484, 553, 9986, 1028, 326, 12648, 472, 8831, 1347, 350, 366, 1023, 553, 23458, 741, 4569, 1481, 2501, 18636, 260, 30, 50557, 1920, 23458, 12063, 12885, 696, 10635, 261, 8831, 1347, 350, 77, 28, 17, 8, 9874, 8831, 1347, 11, 625, 155715, 13, 2632, 272, 12490, 1481, 413, 9986, 1028, 3503, 83, 350, 2845, 10049, 8, 12648, 656, 3561, 13034, 3503, 83, 326, 1849, 13034, 8831, 1347, 350, 2845, 22148, 11823, 741, 4569, 15248, 272, 12490, 0, 24305, 3561, 13034, 3503, 83, 350, 34, 18, 1001, 487, 936, 1849, 13034, 8831, 1347, 350, 17, 487, 591, 10049, 17, 49, 936, 326, 1217, 1849, 13034, 1273, 4307, 363, 20, 3503, 83, 30, 19792, 2569, 25, 10112, 3503, 83, 591, 3561, 11, 1815, 3503, 83, 591, 1849, 15883, 11, 1815, 8831, 1347, 591, 22148, 11823, 30, 7744, 120152, 484, 20395, 272, 12490, 66938, 316, 9986, 1028, 3503, 83, 350, 13627, 656, 13034, 15883, 220, 18, 350, 16, 487, 8, 9874, 3503, 83, 8, 326, 1815, 87130, 656, 22148, 11823, 10049, 17, 49, 885, 1920, 12063, 12885, 696, 472, 8831, 1347, 11, 2049, 15883, 20, 885, 47612, 350, 16, 487, 8, 10112, 44297, 9035, 30, 16454, 364, 39520, 1606, 418, 15248, 1991, 11, 6960, 6052, 418, 364, 84787, 1721, 6052, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 418], "n_tokens": 8089, "words": 4867, "band_lo": 2538.75, "band_hi": 10155.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the NMR multiplicities of the hydrogens on the original heptane backbone. Starting heptane C1–C7 chain. Substitutions replace some hydrogens on those carbons with groups (alkyls). Each carbon is sp3 as part of chain. For each carbon i, we have originally certain number of H: C1 CH3 (3 H), internal carbons CH2 (2 H), C7 CH3 (3 H). After substitution, some H replaced by other groups. NMR patterns (multiplicities) are given for the signals due to the hydrogens on each carbon (i.e., all protons attached to that carbon produce a signal with some splitting pattern). The multiplicities: t, dq, dt, ddt, ddd, ddt, d. So for C1: triplet; C2: double of quartets (i.e., a doublet of quartets). C3: dt (doublet of triplets); C4: ddt (doublet of doublets of triplets? Actually ddt likely doublet of doublet of triplets? but pattern specific); C5: ddd; C6: ddt; C7: d (doublet).\n\nWe need to decide which substitution pattern yields these coupling patterns.\n\nGeneral rule: multiplicity from NMR is determined by number of neighboring protons (n) giving n+1 multiplicity (ignoring coupling constants differences). However when the neighboring groups have nonequivalent sets of protons, we can get multiplets like doublet of triplets etc.\n\nFor a carbon bearing multiple hydrogens (like CH2) the pattern is due to coupling of each H on that carbon to neighboring hydrogens. For a CH2 where each H is diastereotopic (different chemical environment), they can couple to each other (geminal) and also to neighboring protons giving complex pattern.\n\nBut here we are told that the multiplicities given are for the signals due to the hydrogens on carbon i. Possibly each carbon could be CH3, CH2, CH, or carbon with no H (quaternary). Multiplicity of the signal depends on the number of neighboring protons (i.e., on adjacent carbon's protons). For a CH3 attached to CH2, would be a triplet (n=2). For a CH2 attached to CH3 and CH2 would be a quartet (n=3) if all neighboring protons are equivalent. But we see more complex patterns.\n\nAlso introduction of substituent groups CH2R, CHR2, CR3 replace hydrogens, altering the number of protons on the carbon and also neighboring counts.\n\nLet's map each carbon after substitution: we need to determine how many H remain on each carbon, and what are the adjacent carbons (and whether those have hydrogen(s) that couple).\n\nAssume the substituents R are non-proton groups (no H to couple). So CH2R means carbon attached to \"R\" which has no hydrogens? Probably R is non-hydrogen, maybe a carbon with no H (like phenyl) or hetero. It says hydrogens replaced by groups where R is non-hydrogen. So the substituent attaches via carbon (the group). That means the new substituent brings new carbon(s) with hydrogens possibly beyond, but those aren't considered for splitting of the backbone hydrogens? However the replaced hydrogen is gone, so that carbon now has one less hydrogen. For CH2R, the carbon now has one R (non-H) and one H? Actually \"–CH2R\" means a two‑hydrogen carbon attached to R (which lacks H). But being attached to the backbone carbon via the carbon of CH2R? At the point of substitution: the replaced hydrogen is replaced by a CH2R group attached to that carbon. The carbon in backbone becomes attached to the CH2 of the substituent. So the CH2R carbon itself may have hydrogens but they are not directly coupled to the backbone carbon? They are two bonds away, so they are not directly coupled (3-bond coupling yes). Actually the backbone carbon is attached to CH2R carbon via a sigma bond; the CH2R carbon's hydrogens are one bond away from the carbon attached to them? For example, if you have -CH(Cl)-CH2CH3, the CHCl carbon couples to CH2 hydrogens (2 bonds away? Actually three-bond coupling: CHCl-H coupling to CH2's hydrogens: CHCl*(the carbon with hydrogen) sees protons on adjacent carbon (the CH2). So these will count as neighboring protons. So substitution changes the coupling patterns because R substituent may have hydrogens that can couple.\n\nBut the problem likely expects a simpler reading: The groups –CH3, –CH2R, –CHR2, –CR3 added onto the carbon replace hydrogens, but the carbon's own hydrogens (if any) will couple to the neighboring carbons based on the number of hydrogens on each adjacent carbon. The substituent groups themselves may have hydrogens that affect coupling of the backbone carbon's protons? Possibly yes. However the multiplicities given are for each carbon's hydrogens only; the pattern arises from coupling to protons on adjacent carbons (including substituent groups attached).\n\nThus we need to find substitution pattern that yields the observed multiplicities list.\n\nLet's compute expected multiplicity given neighbor H counts (assuming all couplings same J).\n\nIf carbon i has ni hydrogens (say 1 for CH, 2 for CH2, 3 for CH3). The multiplicity of its signal (if all hydrogens are equivalent) from neighboring protons (sum of hydrogens on adjacent carbons) will be (n_adj + 1) pattern. But if the carbon has multiple hydrogens, they could couple to each other leading to splitting into doublet of ... However pattern described like dt, ddt etc is typical for CH2 where each of the two a,b hydrogens have different couplings: they are diastereotopic due to nonequivalence of adjacent groups, giving each a doublet (from geminal coupling) further split by neighboring protons giving triplet, etc.\n\nSimplify: The multiplicities likely correspond to carbon's hydrogens (some may be multiple hydrogens). For CH3 (3 H) you'll see quartet from adjacent CH2 (2 H) etc. But the pattern of 't' for carbon 1 suggests it is CH3 with a neighboring CH2 (2 H) (i.e., typical triplet). So carbon 1 likely unchanged (CH3) attached to carbon 2 which has 2 H (CH2). So C1 is CH3, giving triplet (t). That matches option where carbon 1 is no change (CH3). All options have carbon 1: no change. Good.\n\nCarbon 7 is a doublet (d). End carbon 7 maybe CH (one H) attached to a carbon with one H (CH). A single neighbor (n=1) gives doublet. So carbon 7 must be CH (i.e., CH; has one hydrogen) attached to a carbon that has one hydrogen (i.e., carbon 6 is CH). Additionally maybe coupling to R substituents? But doublet suggests only one neighboring hydrogen. So C7 is CH (one H) with only one adjacent H.\n\nThus carbon 6 must have a single hydrogen (CH). Are there possibilities where carbon 6 is CH with substitution? Options show carbon 6: –CHR2 in A, B, D; –CHR2 in C also? Option C has carbon6 –CHR2 yes. All have CH (since CHR2 has one H). So carbon 7's neighbor (C6) is CHR2, which has one H, giving doublet. Good.\n\nNow consider carbon 2 at left side. It shows double of quartets (dq). That likely corresponds to a CH (one H) coupling to two sets of neighboring protons: one set of 3 equivalent (quartet) and another set of 1 (double). But the carbon maybe CH (i.e., has one H) and has two neighboring carbons each with distinct number of hydrogens.\n\nAlternatively, if carbon 2 is CH2 (two hydrogens) each will couple to neighboring protons (C1's 3, C3's maybe something) leading to quartet of doublet (i.e., doublet of quartets). But pattern \"dq\" meaning doublet of quartets: each hydrogen of CH2 sees two different couplings: geminal coupling (to other hydrogen) gives doublet, and coupling to 3 equivalent protons of C1 gives quartet. That yields a doublet of quartets. This is typical for CH2 adjacent to CH3. In n-alkanes, the CH2 next to CH3 appears as a quartet (from 3 H on CH3) but is split into doublet by geminal coupling? Actually geminal coupling between two H on same CH2 gives small J, leading to doublet of quartets indeed. So carbon 2 likely is CH2 (2 H). Good.\n\nThus C2 must have 2 hydrogens (CH2). In options: C2 no change (CH2) for A and B, also maybe with CH2R in D. Option C C2 no change as well. So all options have C2 as \"no change\" except D where C2: –CH2R (so also CH2). Both have 2 H.\n\nNow carbon 3 shows dt (doublet of triplets) meaning likely CH (one H) coupling to two sets: 1 neighboring H (doublet) and 2 neighboring H (triplet). So carbon 3 is CH attached to carbon 2 (CH2) and carbon 4 (which maybe CH2). However carbon 2's hydrogens are 2, carbon 4 maybe ... But CH would see 2 + ? Let's think: a CH with one hydrogen attached to two neighboring carbons: one carbon with 1 hydrogen gives doublet; other carbon with 2 hydrogens gives triplet. So dt pattern matches CH attached to CH (one H) and CH2 (two H). That suggests that carbon 3 is CH, carbon 2 is CH2 (present), carbon 4 is CH2 (maybe). Good.\n\nHence carbon3 must be CH (i.e., have one hydrogen). Options: In A carbon3 –CR3 (no H) -> can't give dt. B also –CR3. C carbon3 –CH2R (2 H). D carbon3 no change (CH2). So none have CH. Wait dt requires a CH with one H. Options may aim to have substitution pattern produce a CH: e.g., –CHR2 has one H, –CH2R has 2 H, –CR3 has 0 H, no change on interior carbon 3 would be CH2 (2 H). So we need CH.\n\nBut we may get dt also from CH2 where each of the diastereotopic H experiences different neighbor couplings, giving one doublet (geminal) further split by another set? dt for CH2? Example: CH2 attached to CH3 (3 H) and CH (1 H). The two H are diastereotopic; each will appear as doublet of triplets perhaps? But the observed overall signal might be more complex (ddt). Typically CH2 next to CH and CH3 gives a multiplet that appears as multiplet not simply dt. The pattern dt maybe for CH.\n\nThus we need carbon3 to have a single H: CH (i.e., CHR2). However none of the options assign –CHR2 to carbon3; only carbon6 and 7 etc. So maybe my interpretation wrong: dt could arise from CH2 with geminal coupling (doublet) and coupling to 2 neighboring H (triplet). That would be a doublet of triplets (two couplings). For CH2, each H experiences geminal with each other (doublet), and also coupling to two equivalent hydrogens on adjacent carbon resulting in triplet. So overall each hydrogen appears as doublet of triplets; the overall signal may be overlapping, but maybe described as dt. So carbon2 being CH2 gave dq (doublet of quartet). Similarly carbon3 being CH2 could give dt (doublet of triplet). However we need that carbon3's neighbors: one side has 3 H? Actually to get triplet (n=2) need two equivalent H on adjacent carbon. So carbon4 must be CH2 (2 H). Good. So carbon3 could be CH2 (no change) but options: A has –CR3 (no H) not matching. B same. C has –CH2R (still 2 H) okay could give dt. D no change means CH2 as well.\n\nThus options C and D have CH2 at C3; C has CH2R substitution (still CH2), D no change (CH2). Both give 2 H.\n\nNow carbon4 shows ddt (doublet of doublet of triplets). That suggests CH2 (two H) where each H is split by geminal (doublet), by neighbor with 1 H (doublet) and by neighbor with 2 H (triplet). So carbon4 must be CH2 attached to carbon3 (CH2) and carbon5 which has maybe CH (one H)?? Let's compute: For CH2 attached to CH2 (2 H) and CH (1 H) gives coupling pattern: each H experiences geminal (doublet) and coupling to 2 H (triplet) and 1 H (doublet) resulting in ddt. That matches ddt.\n\nThus carbon4 likely CH2, adjacent to carbon3 (2H) and carbon5 (1H). So carbon5 must be CH (one H). Let's see options.\n\nCarbon5 observed pattern: ddd (doublet of doublet of doublet) meaning likely CH (one H) attached to three neighboring protons sets each 1 H? Actually a CH with three non-equivalent neighboring set each 1 hydrogen would give doublet of doublet of doublet. So carbon5 must be CH attached to three carbon neighbors each bearing a single hydrogen (i.e., CH). In our chain, carbon5 attached to carbon4 (CH2) with 2 H, that gives a triplet not doublet. So maybe carbon5 is CH attached to carbon4 (CH2) and carbon6 (CH) and maybe substituent R with a hydrogen? But R is non-hydrogen. However if carbon5 is attached to carbon4 (CH2) gives 2 H (triplet). That would not yield ddd. So perhaps carbon5 is CH attached to carbon4 CH (1 H) and carbon6 CH (1 H) and maybe also to substituent (CH)?? hmm.\n\nAlternative: Carbon5 could be CH attached to carbon4 (CH) with 1 H, carbon6 (CH) with 1 H, and carbon5 also attached to some group that has one hydrogen (maybe a -CH). But substituents are attached to the carbon itself, not attached as neighbor.\n\nBut we might have misorientated: The given multiplicities are for each carbon's hydrogens, not for the carbon itself (maybe may be multi-proton). For carbon5, ddd could be a CH2 that couples to three different neighbor protons: geminal doublet + two different neighbors each 1 H? But a CH2 with geminal coupling (doublet) and two neighboring each 1 H would give doublet of doublet of doublet? That's plausible. For a CH2 attached to carbon4 (CH) (1 H) and carbon6 (CH) (1 H). So each of its H is split by geminal (doublet), by one H on carbon4 (doublet), by one H on carbon6 (doublet): overall ddd. That matches if carbon5 is CH2, not CH.\n\nBut the pattern ddd is typical for CH2 where each hydrogen couples to three non-equivalent protons (including its geminal partner). So carbon5 could be CH2 attached to two CH (each 1 H). That fits.\n\nSo carbon5 is CH2, adjacent to carbon4 (CH) and carbon6 (CH). Good.\n\nThus carbon4 must be CH (one H). Let's check pattern for carbon4 earlier: we thought CH2 gave ddt. But maybe CH would give ddt? For CH (one H) coupling to neighbor CH2 (2 H) gives triplet, to neighbor CH (1 H) gives doublet, to neighbor R? Not doublet of doublet of triplet pattern. Hard.\n\nLet's re-evaluate systematically using n+1 rule focusing on proton counts on neighboring carbons ignoring geminal.\n\nFor a carbon with one H (CH): multiplicity = (n+1) where n = total neighboring hydrogens (on adjacent carbons). If there are H on two sides that are chemically different, the multiplet will be combination of J values leading to e.g., doublet of triplet (if n=1+2). So CH attached to CH3 (3 H) would be quartet; attached to CH2 (2 H) would be triplet etc.\n\nFor carbon with two H (CH2): each H also couples geminal (1 H) giving doublet, plus neighboring protons giving splitting, leading to doublet of (n+1) etc.\n\nThus pattern naming: dq could be doublet of quartets from CH2 (geminal doublet; neighbor CH3 (3 H) gives quartet). dt: doublet of triplets (CH2 geminal + neighbor CH2 (2 H) gives triplet). ddt: doublet of doublet of triplets (CH2 geminal + neighbor CH (1 H) doublet + neighbor CH2 (2 H) triplet). ddd: doublet of doublet of doublets (CH2 geminal + neighbor CH (1) + neighbor CH (1)). ddt for carbon6 similar to carbon4 maybe.\n\nGiven symmetrical chain ends, we likely have pattern: C1 CH3 -> t (triplet from neighbor CH2). C2 CH2 -> dq (geminal + neighbor CH3)