blet of quartet) indicates CH2 geminal splitting plus neighbor 3 H (like usual CH2 next to CH3). So that matches for CH2 next to CH3. In normal heptane, C2 adjacent C1 CH3 (3 H) and C3 CH2 (2 H) -> would be quartet of doublets? Actually geminal splitting is small; you could get quartet of doublets (qd) perhaps. So dq fits.\n\nC3 dt: CH2 geminal plus neighbor C2 (2 H) gives triplet? Wait C2 has 2 H, so would give triplet. So dt is doublet of triplet matching CH2 next to CH2. Good.\n\nC4 ddt: CH2 geminal plus neighbor C3 (2 H) gives triplet and neighbor C5 (2 H) gives another triplet? That would lead to doublet of triplet of triplet (dtt?) Not ddt.\n\nMaybe the pattern notation order not important.\n\nNevertheless, perhaps the answer is to identify which substitution pattern (A-D) yields the given multiplicities. We can test each option: assign to each carbon the type (CH3 etc) and calculate expected multiplicities using simple neighbor H counts.\n\nLet's model: For each carbon, compute number of attached hydrogens left (H_i). Substitutions known: –CH3 adds a CH3 group attached to the carbon; that carbon retains same H count? Actually adding a CH3 as substituent replaces a hydrogen on the backbone with a carbon that has 3 H. That carbon's own H count remains as before (maybe no change). The backbone carbon loses a hydrogen, gaining bond to the substituent carbon (which itself carries hydrogens). So the backbone carbon's H count reduces by 1.\n\nThus for each carbon i, its hydrogen count after substitution = original (CH3 =3 for ends, CH2=2 for internal) minus number of substitutions at that carbon (0,1, maybe 2? but we can only replace up to original hydrogens). Options indicate only one substitution per carbon (if any). So H_i = original - 1 if substituted; original remains if no change.\n\nNow determine neighbor H counts for each carbon i, based on adjacent backbone carbons H_j (after substitution). The substituent groups attached may also have hydrogens that are adjacent (one bond away) to the backbone carbon i, thus they count as neighboring hydrogens (as they are on the substituent carbon, directly attached). For –CH3, the substituent carbon has 3 H, which are directly attached to the substituent carbon, which is attached to the backbone carbon; that's a 2-bond distance? The backbone carbon's hydrogen would couple to those 3 hydrogens (3-bond coupling). So they contribute to splitting.\n\nThus for each substitution type:\n- No change: no substituent, so no extra neighbor H from substituent.\n- –CH3: adds 3 H on adjacent carbon (the substituent). So adds 3 neighboring H.\n- –CH2R: substituent carbon is CH2 attached to R (non-H). So adds 2 H (adjacent).\n- –CHR2: substituent carbon is CH attached to two R groups (non-H). Adds 1 H.\n- –CR3: substituent carbon is quaternary (no H). Adds 0 H.\n\nSo each substitution contributes that many H to the neighboring count for the backbone carbon.\n\nNow we can compute expected multiplicity for each carbon i: The number of distinct coupling groups = sets of equivalent neighboring hydrogens. If all neighboring hydrogens are equivalent (e.g., from CH3 group all three equivalent), they give a (n+1) splitting. If there are multiple distinct groups (e.g., from left neighbor different than right neighbor), the pattern is nested: each group splits previous pattern. So the multiplicity descriptor in order likely corresponds to smallest coupling first? Not essential.\n\nWe'll compute the pattern type based on counts of distinct groups with given numbers.\n\nLet's assign for each carbon i:\n- adjacent left carbon i-1 (if exists): its attached H count = H_{i-1}\n- adjacent right carbon i+1: H_{i+1}\n- substituent H count = S_i (0,1,2,3 depending)\nTotal neighboring hydrogens = H_{i-1} + H_{i+1} + S_i (but they may be in separate coupling groups). Distinct groups are each side plus substituent group. So pattern of multiplicity: sort of coupling groups sizes: e.g., for carbon2: left neighbor C1 has H1=3, right neighbor C3 H3=2, substituent none. So three groups: 3,2 maybe? But carbon2 is CH2 (2 H). Its own geminal coupling (1) forms doublet (separate). So overall pattern would be doublet (geminal) of (something) of (something). That is dbl of quartet of triplet? But the observed is dq (doublet of quartet). That suggests only left neighbor 3 H is significant, right neighbor 2 H maybe equivalent? but not seen.\n\nGiven complexity, maybe the substitution pattern that matches observed multiplicities are those where each carbon has distinct neighbor hydrogen counts that produce the described multiplicities. Let's evaluate each option quickly.\n\nOption A:\nC1: no change -> CH3, H1=3, S1=0\nC2: no change -> CH2, H2=2, S2=0\nC3: –CR3 -> original CH2 lost 1 H, becomes CH (since original 2 -1 =1). So H3=1, S3=0 (since substituent adds 0 H)\nC4: –CH2R -> original CH2 (2) minus 1 =1 H (CH). plus substituent adds 2 H. So H4=1, S4=2.\nC5: –CH2R similar: original CH2 ->1 H, S5=2.\nC6: –CHR2 -> original CH2 ->1 H, S6=1.\nC7: –CHR2 -> original CH3 (3) -> lose 1 => CH2 stay 2? Wait carbon 7 originally CH3 (3 H). Replace one H with –CHR2 group, leaving 2 H (CH2). Plus substituent adds 1 H. So H7 = 2, S7=1.\n\nNow compute neighbor patterns:\n\nC1 (CH3) has 3 H (itself). Its signal multiplicity due to coupling to neighboring H on C2 (which is CH2, H2=2). No substituent on C1. So it should be triplet (t). Good matches.\n\nC2 (CH2) geminal doublet (d). Neighbor left C1 H=3 (quartet) neighbor right C3 H=1 (doublet) and maybe substituent none. So expected pattern: doublet (geminal) of quartet of doublet = dqd? Observed dq only. Might be simplified as dq if one coupling dominates? Hard.\n\nC3 is CH (1 H). Couplings: left neighbor C2 (2 H) gives triplet, right neighbor C4 (1 H) gives doublet, plus maybe substituent none. So pattern would be doublet of triplet (dt) or triplet of doublet. Observed dt for carbon3. That matches! Good.\n\nC4 is CH (1 H). Couplings: left neighbor C3 (1 H) => 