 11, 889, 643, 1340, 1217, 3343, 889, 10705, 5954, 1008, 50557, 323, 28, 15, 140664, 28, 15, 316, 323, 28, 15, 140664, 28, 16, 5954, 220, 17, 33, 314, 220, 24, 68, 12, 1860, 643, 13679, 13, 2632, 4436, 1023, 118850, 30, 30391, 290, 73005, 382, 4801, 263, 53079, 30, 889, 4335, 2127, 28, 455, 11, 865, 28, 17, 11, 41158, 263, 53079, 350, 11741, 79097, 14223, 1491, 741, 5551, 10112, 23769, 2935, 62205, 382, 625, 9279, 395, 135911, 57036, 5192, 316, 38962, 41184, 8441, 10652, 39241, 26143, 41, 314, 58882, 16, 11, 889, 1217, 135911, 2804, 9852, 220, 16, 41051, 328, 38962, 41184, 26, 10954, 25435, 13, 2632, 152029, 14463, 4149, 364, 7943, 12951, 290, 4928, 47384, 481, 316, 3331, 1606, 15765, 1953, 14463, 2236, 1023, 10175, 38962, 19813, 112349, 11, 326, 7747, 395, 41184, 6118, 316, 6160, 316, 2613, 2608, 392, 6332, 23120, 4149, 5954, 4050, 17158, 1023, 19429, 2613, 15765, 1953, 2608, 3630, 10954, 290, 2613, 392, 33546, 3211, 1, 328, 290, 142698, 170747, 68244, 23769, 13, 623, 152029, 8374, 553, 945, 23595, 109235, 889, 14836, 9474, 13574, 2359, 13, 623, 27179, 392, 27920, 9827, 34195, 2359, 350, 267, 2893, 6335, 35460, 80067, 2359, 22445, 6664, 2973, 15712, 889, 19271, 18864, 14463, 382, 15765, 1953, 18864, 350, 85, 28, 15, 316, 220, 16, 8, 38033, 656, 152029, 59824, 350, 47, 48317, 34625, 741, 3072, 290, 23120, 5954, 2543, 328, 290, 15765, 1953, 8177, 382, 290, 152029, 14463, 483, 643, 28, 15, 609, 16, 42268, 323, 28, 15, 609, 16, 30, 50557, 1354, 553, 1486, 12, 36005, 30, 2214, 41158, 263, 53079, 11, 15890, 14463, 5954, 314, 41051, 112349, 659, 220, 17, 33, 13, 623, 23120, 3609, 5954, 382, 316, 21700, 15765, 1953, 350, 183409, 493, 8, 483, 643, 19957, 2238, 484, 8441, 9607, 23121, 25, 26143, 41, 314, 58882, 16, 11, 813, 14463, 591, 350, 85, 28, 15, 140664, 28, 15, 8, 316, 350, 85, 28, 16, 140664, 28, 16, 8, 8967, 5954, 26143, 36, 314, 41051, 112349, 659, 220, 17, 33, 13, 21926, 2928, 6574, 5762, 112349, 11, 2236, 418, 382, 2009, 13679, 13, 143985, 41184, 1815, 42308, 230, 41051, 112349, 820, 66, 314, 220, 16, 13, 19, 68, 12, 2029, 478, 71191, 13, 2632, 5317, 415, 66938, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 364, 72816, 6052, 25, 415, 13], "n_tokens": 2360, "words": 1103, "band_lo": 314.25, "band_hi": 1257.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret problem: diatomic molecule modeled using quantum rotation-vibration (non-rigid rotor) . Fundamental state presumably v=0, J=0 (v vibrational quantum number, J rotational quantum number). Next state with lowest possible energy: either vibrational excitation (v=1, J=0) or rotational (v=0, J=1), whichever lower energy. Need photon momentum required to cause transition. Momentum p = h/λ = ħk. Energy needed ΔE = ħω_photon = hc/λ. But momentum magnitude p = ΔE / c (photon). So we need ΔE minimal.\n\nCompute rotational energy spacing: E_J = B J (J+1) where B = ħ^2 / (2I) (in energy units). For J=0 to 1: ΔE_rot = 2B (since J=0 -> J=1: ΔE = B*1*2 = 2B). Need I = μ r^2 where μ reduced mass = mX mY/(mX+mY). Use masses: 20 amu, 2 amu. μ = (20*2)/(22) = 40/22 = 1.81818 amu. Convert amu to kg: 1 amu = 1.66054e-27 kg. μ = 1.81818*1.66054e-27 = 3.019e-27 kg.\n\nBond length r = 2 Å = 2e-10 m. So I = μ r^2 = 3.019e-27 * (2e-10)^2 = 3.019e-27 *4e-20 = 1.2076e-46 kg·m^2.\n\nNow B = ħ^2/(2I). ħ = 1.0545718e-34 J·s. ħ^2 = 1.112e-68. Divide by (2I): 2I = 2.4152e-46. So B = 1.112e-68 / 2.4152e-46 = 4.603e-23 J.\n\nΔE_rot = 2B = 9.206e-23 J.\n\nNow vibrational energy spacing: quantum harmonic oscillator: E_v = ħ ω (v+1/2). Transition v=0 to v=1: ΔE_vib = ħ ω. Given ω = 4e14 rad/s. So ΔE_vib = ħ ω = 1.0545718e-34 * 4e14 = 4.2183e-20 J.\n\nComparing: rotational spacing ~9.2e-23 J, vibrational spacing ~4.2e-20 J, about 460 times larger. So the lowest possible energy transition is rotational: from (0,0) to (0,1). So ΔE = 9.2e-23 J.\n\nPhoton momentum p = ΔE / c (c=3e8 m/s). p = 9.206e-23 / 3e8 = 3.0687e-31 kg·m/s (N·s). Wait units: J/(m/s) = kg·m/s.\n\nBut answer options are ~10^{-27} N*s, four orders magnitude larger. Something off: maybe we need to include rovibrational transition with selection rule ΔJ = ±1 etc. photon carries angular momentum? Also momentum of photon is maybe approximated by p = h/λ, but same as ΔE/c. Our computed p ~3e-31, far smaller. Let's check numbers: Could we have misapplied I? Use bond length maybe half distance? For diatomic rotation about center of mass, each atom distance from CM: r_X = (μ/m_X) * R? Actually moment of inertia I = μ r^2 where r is bond length (distance between masses). That's correct.\n\nOur I: μ ~3e-27 kg, r=2e-10 m, yields I~1.2e-46 kg*m^2. Rotational constant B in frequency units often expressed in cm^-1; typical B is few cm^-1 for light molecules. Compute B/hc to check: B/h = (ħ^2/(2I))/h = (ħ/(2I))/ (h/ħ?) Let's compute B in J: 4.6e-23 J. Convert to cm^-1: divide by hc (h=6.626e-34, c=3e8): hc = 1.987e-25 J·m. Convert J to per cm: 1 cm^-1 = (h c)/ (100)??? Actually energy per cm^-1: (h c) * (100). But simpler: wavenumber = energy / (hc). So B/(hc) = 4.603e-23 / (1.986e-25) = 231.8 cm^-1. That's typical for HCl? Not unrealistic. Then ΔE_rot = 2B = 463.6 cm^-1 correspond to 13.9 meV ~2.2e-21 J? Wait our calculation gave 9e-23 J which equates to 9e-23/(1.986e-23) = 4.5 cm^-1. So discrepancy factor 50. Let's recalc: hc = 1.98644586e-23 J·cm (since h=6.626e-34 J*s, c=2.998e10 cm/s => hc=1.986e-23 J·cm). Right earlier I used meters; need careful.\n\nUsing J to cm^-1: 1 cm^-1 = (h c)/ (100?) Actually c in m/s, hc = 1.986e-25 J·m. Multiply by 100 cm/m gives 1.986e-23 J·cm. So yes 1 cm^-1 corresponds to 1.986e-23 J.\n\nNow our B =4.603e-23 J. Divide by 1.986e-23 = 2.32 cm^-1. That seems plausible for heavy diatomic with long bond. Then ΔE_rot = 2B=9.206e-23 J => 4.64 cm^-1. This seems reasonable. So our earlier B/hc gave 231 due to mistake. So values okay.\n\nNow photon momentum: 9.2e-23 J / c (3e8) = 3.07e-31 kg·m/s. Options are 10^-28, three orders bigger. Possibly they use wavelength corresponding to rotational transition frequency f = ΔE/h? Let's compute p = h/λ = h ν /c = ΔE /c same. So still same.\n\nMaybe we misinterpret \"momentum\" as angular momentum change? Photon carries angular momentum ħ. The change in angular momentum for rotation J=0 to J=1 is √(J(J+1))ħ? Actually rotational quantum J corresponds to magnitude √(J(J+1))ħ. Transition ΔJ=1 changes angular momentum magnitude roughly ħ. So maybe they ask momentum magnitude of photon? But they'd give ħ ≈ 1.05e-34 J·s; that's not momentum.\n\nBut the given options are ~10^-27 N*s, which is ~10^-27 kg·m/s. Our result 3e-31 is far smaller.\n\nLet's double-check calculation: ΔE_rot = 2B = (ħ^2/I). Since B = ħ^2/(2I). So ΔE = ħ^2/I. Compute ħ^2 = (1.055e-34)^2 = 1.113e-68. Divide by I = 1.208e-46 = 9.21e-23 J. matches.\n\nOkay.\n\nNow maybe fundamental state means v=0, J=0, and next state means v=1, J=0 (first vibrational). That gives ΔE ~4.2e-20 J, photon momentum = 4.2e-20 /3e8 = 1.4e-28 kg·m/s. That matches option D (1.4e-28). Indeed 1.4e-28 N*s is there. Let's compute: 4.218e-20 / 3e8 = 1.406e-28. Option D is 1.4*10^{-28}. Yes.\n\nTherefore they consider the next state with lowest possible energy is vibrational? Wait rotational energy is lower (9e-23 J) which would give p~3e-31, way lower than vibrational. But maybe selection rules forbid pure rotational transitions for heteronuclear diatomic? Actually heteronuclear diatomic molecules have permanent dipole moment, so rotational transitions are allowed via IR absorption. So they are allowed. However the \"quantum rotation-vibration model (or non-rigid rotor model)\" maybe describes combined rovibrational energy expression: E(v,J) = ħ ω (v+1/2) + B J(J+1) - D J^2 (J+1)^2 ... So fundamental state (v=0,J=0). Next state with lowest possible energy might be the one where vibrational quantum number increases by 1, but J may also change but overall energy... Actually v=0,J=0 to v=0,J=1 energy 2B = 9e-23 J smaller. So why they disregard? Perhaps the molecule is homonuclear? but given X=20, Y=2, heteronuclear (non-zero dipole). However maybe rotation without vibration is not allowed for photon absorption due to angular momentum selection rule requiring ΔJ = ±1, but also photon must carry 1 ħ of angular momentum; that's okay. So rotational transition possible.\n\nBut perhaps the question expects you to consider only vibrational transition because they gave angular frequency ω, and asked for momentum needed to switch to next state \"with lowest possible energy\". Maybe they assume next vibrational state since that's the next \"energy level\" of the harmonic oscillator ignoring rotation. The rotational levels are more closely spaced but possibly considered separate model. The phrase \"rotation-vibration model (or non-rigid rotor model)\" includes both aspects but typical fundamental transition is vibrational fundamental (v=0 to 1) accompanied by rotational transitions (P,R branches). But the lowest energy line of the vibrational band is the rotational transition with J=0->1 accompanying v=0->1? Actually there are Q-branch? For heteronuclear, combined transition energy = ħ ω + 2B. The lowest total energy is to combine vibrational (dominant) with J minimal such that selection rules satisfied: ΔJ = ±1, so transition from (v=0,J=0) to (v=1,J=1) gives energy ΔE = ħ ω + 2B. That's still ~ħ ω, because B is much smaller. Photon momentum then ≈ ħ ω /c = 1.4e-28 N*s. So option D corresponds.\n\nThus answer D.\n\nTherefore answer: D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -8.702239938429557e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999943, "logp_correct": -5.672967317691857e-15, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000058, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5547461481444145, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2903286871604465, "frac_below_-5": 0.011930123561994035, "digit_lp_min": -3.2882637977600098, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 2347, "lp_end": -1.6569954823353328e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -4.768370445162873e-07, "logp_nocot": -4.5963236062457895, "p_nocot": 0.010088858263763259, "uplift": 4.596323606245784, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.428989, "B": 0.550833, "C": 0.010089, "D": 0.010089}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recOkA2zsy4GSMD9R", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: An equimolar mixture of salts A and B weighing 7.20 g was heated to 200°C without air. In this case, only a mixture of gases was formed, which, without cooling, was passed successively through tubes filled with anhydrous Mg(ClO4)2 (№1), Ca(OH)2 (№2) solution, and red-hot copper (№3). Results: the weights of tubes №1 and №3 increased by 3.60 g and 0.80 g, respectively (CuO formed in tube №3). The weight of the second tube has not changed. As a result, only 2.24 liters of gas C (standard temperature and pressure) remained.\nIndicate the total number of all atoms in salts A and B.\n\nA) 15\nB) 13\nC) 19\nD) 17\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: An equimolar mixture of salts A and B weighing 7.20 g was heated to 200°C without air. In this case, only a mixture of gases was formed, which, without cooling, was passed successively through tubes filled with anhydrous Mg(ClO4)2 (№1), Ca(OH)2 (№2) solution, and red-hot copper (№3). Results: the weights of tubes №1 and №3 increased by 3.60 g and 0.80 g, respectively (CuO formed in tube №3). The weight of the second tube has not changed. As a result, only 2.24 liters of gas C (standard temperature and pressure) remained.\nIndicate the total number of all atoms in salts A and B.\n\nA) 15\nB) 13\nC) 19\nD) 17\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recOkA2zsy4GSMD9R:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 290, 38395, 4792, 364, 39520, 25, 14400, 321, 11559, 27467, 328, 126238, 355, 326, 418, 11, 4842, 3609, 220, 22, 13, 455, 329, 13, 3164, 553, 44191, 316, 220, 1179, 26557, 2935, 3693, 13, 12817, 59246, 20680, 350, 1750, 114919, 81192, 623, 59246, 11, 2935, 34328, 11, 10292, 1819, 52305, 1402, 