69570, 591, 42464, 15883, 1954, 25, 15883, 220, 16, 382, 10049, 17, 45485, 11, 15883, 220, 17, 382, 10049, 483, 101385, 22148, 11823, 13, 21926, 6145, 364, 84787, 54377, 1094, 25, 31200, 6270, 387, 12, 92382, 387, 8844, 79683, 18, 5366, 4867, 37, 326, 1815, 487, 17, 46, 17, 11, 42464, 12, 350, 45235, 741, 2632, 418, 1481, 413, 6794, 37, 11, 79683, 18, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 25, 355, 314, 220, 17, 2126, 53546, 22803, 12, 16, 12, 1900, 11, 418, 314, 6794, 37, 11, 79683, 18, 364, 4383, 1273, 3880, 25, 355, 314, 220, 17, 2126, 53546, 22803, 15134, 659, 91279, 11, 487, 17, 1481, 10389, 100539, 133496, 316, 16589, 316, 3644, 220, 17, 2126, 53546, 22803, 270, 12, 16, 12, 340, 30, 5952, 90598, 100539, 133496, 8967, 9107, 16589, 540, 15883, 220, 16, 350, 48581, 13464, 741, 220, 17, 2126, 53546, 22803, 15134, 21441, 8967, 220, 17, 2126, 53546, 22803, 270, 12, 16, 12, 340, 22476, 350, 32214, 133496, 540, 15883, 220, 16, 741, 4569, 1481, 1217, 14376, 2684, 1888, 13, 5551, 1023, 679, 418, 314, 91279, 11, 487, 17, 350, 23498, 20234, 387, 21441, 741, 21926, 120152, 13, 3072, 1023, 10175, 1217, 487, 17, 46, 17, 11, 42464, 12, 472, 5018, 54377, 1094, 306, 290, 10705, 21416, 26, 889, 21441, 328, 100539, 133496, 8740, 1309, 487, 17, 46, 17, 37469, 39, 23609, 623, 21416, 7060, 66396, 392, 32, 659, 418, 350, 39, 17, 46, 17, 11, 42464, 59437, 155217, 1888, 4050, 2632, 418, 382, 261, 191833, 484, 4849, 483, 487, 17, 46, 17, 326, 42464, 12, 59684, 1888, 13, 21926, 19271, 395, 31200, 6270, 387, 12, 92382, 387, 25, 79683, 18, 14, 4867, 37, 1815, 95505, 483, 487, 17, 46, 17, 14, 11398, 45485, 13, 2632, 22476, 418, 382, 79683, 18, 306, 6794, 37, 364, 84787, 6145, 6052, 415, 364, 17045, 6011, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 415], "n_tokens": 1244, "words": 684, "band_lo": 309.25, "band_hi": 1237.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the reaction: A + B (H2O2, OH-) -> 2-methylhexan-1-ol.\n\nWe need to find what starting material A and reagent B (with H2O2, OH- present) give 2-methylhexan-1-ol. Likely this is oxidation of alkene to diol via hydroboration-oxidation? Wait that uses BH3 then H2O2, OH- to produce alcohols (anti-Markovnikov). Hydroboration-oxidation converts alkenes to alcohols with anti-Markovnikov addition of water (adds OH to less substituted carbon). For an alkene, A when treated with BH3 (in THF) then H2O2, OH- gives an alcohol.\n\nGiven product is 2-methylhexan-1-ol: That is a primary alcohol at carbon 1; chain six carbons with methyl at C2. So the alcohol is CH3-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH2-OH? Wait naming: 2-methylhexan-1-ol. Number chain from the OH carbon as carbon 1 (primary). So chain: HO-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH3. So carbon skeleton: 6 carbon chain with methyl substituent at carbon 2. The double bond precursor would be an alkene which after hydroboration yields anti-Markovnikov addition: OH at the less substituted carbon (the terminal carbon) and H at the more substituted carbon. So we need an alkene with double bond at the terminal position (C1 = C2) such that after hydroboration, OH attaches to C1 (less substituted). Starting alkene: 2-methylhex-1-ene (i.e., CH2=CH-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH3). Yes that's a terminal alkene with substituent at C2 (methyl). Hydroboration-oxidation yields addition of OH to terminal carbon: CH3-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH2-OH? Wait let's map: Starting 2-methylhex-1-ene: numbering from double bond: CH2=CH-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH3. Let's label carbon numbers: C1 = CH2 (terminal), C2 = CH (connected to C1 and C3, also has methyl substituent). C3 = CH (bearing methyl substituent). Actually the double bond is between C1 and C2. The substituent methyl is at C2, giving 2-methyl. So after hydroboration, H adds to more substituted carbon (C2) and B (later OH) adds to less substituted carbon (C1). So OH ends on carbon 1 => CH3-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH2-OH? Wait originally C1 becomes CH3OH? Let's sketch: Starting: CH2=CH-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH3. After anti-Mark addition: CH3-CH(OH)-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH3? Wait need to think: Hydroboration attaches BH2 to the less substituted carbon (C1) and H to more substituted carbon (C2). Then oxidation replaces B with OH, giving OH on C1. So final product: HO-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH3? Wait there is only one methyl substituent at C2 of original. After reaction, C2 becomes CH with H added and retains methyl substituent; C1 becomes CH2OH. So product: HO-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH3? Let's check: Original chain: C1 CH2 (double), C2 CH (double) with methyl substituent, C3 CH2, C4 CH2, C5 CH2, C6 CH3. After addition, C1 becomes CH2OH; C2 becomes CH (now saturated) with methyl substituent and attached to C1 and C3 and H. So chain becomes HO-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH3 = 2-methylhexan-1-ol indeed. Because numbering from OH carbon now: carbon 1 is CH2OH, carbon 2 is CH with methyl substituent. That's correct.\n\nThus reagents: hydroboration-oxidation uses BH3·THF and then H2O2, OH- (basic). So B would be THF, BH3.\n\nThus answer D: A = 2-methylhex-1-ene, B = THF, BH3.\n\nCheck other options: A = 2-methylhexanal + Pt, H2 would reduce aldehyde to alcohol to give 2-methylhexan-1-ol? Reducing aldehyde gives primary alcohol at carbon 1 (same chain). 2-methylhexanal reduction gives 2-methylhexan-1-ol indeed (aldehyde at carbon 1). That would also yield same product. However they have B = Pt, H2 (hydrogenation reduction). That's plausible. But they gave also H2O2, OH- as extra reagents in the overall reaction; but reduction of aldehyde doesn't need H2O2/OH-. The reaction condition mentions \"A + B (H2O2, OH-) ---> product\". So B is a reagent that later with H2O2 and OH- yields product. That's typical for hydroboration-oxidation: BH3/THF then oxidation with H2O2/NaOH. So indeed B is BH3 in THF.\n\nThus correct answer D.\n\nAnswer format: ANSWER: D", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.4107358689871035e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999997862, "logp_correct": -2.1378916771120672e-13, "letter_mass": 0.9999960661091271, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4989276875313152, "cot_lp_min64": -0.99479753456969, "frac_below_-5": 0.007311129163281885, "digit_lp_min": -0.7159232497215271, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.5500664114952087, "n_cot_tokens": 1231, "lp_end": -0.050448477268218994, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.576278118089249e-07, "logp_nocot": -1.3008015622945956, "p_nocot": 0.27231342935237646, "uplift": 1.3008015622943818, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.272313, "B": 0.448969, "C": 0.006404, "D": 0.272313}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recPyDDO11h9okNQk", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: We have a solution (at 25°C) containing Fe(III) and Fe(II) ions. The concentration Fe(III) ion is equal to 0.01 M and the concentration of Fe(II) ion is equal to 0.01 M. The solution also contains sulfuric acid and the solution pH is 1.0. What will be the resulting redox potential of the solution if we add as much orthophosphate acid to it so that total phosphate ions concentration in the solution is 0.1 M? The E0 for the Fe(III)/Fe(II) system is 0.77 V and the complex formation constant of the Fe(III)-H2PO4^- complex is 10^21. At pH 1.0 and at 0.1 M total total phosphate ions concentration, the equilibrium concentration of H2PO4^- ions is 6.97x10^-3 M.\n\nA) +0.26 V\nB) -0.34 V\nC) +0.52 V\nD) +0.40 V\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: We have a solution (at 25°C) containing Fe(III) and Fe(II) ions. The concentration Fe(III) ion is equal to 0.01 M and the concentration of Fe(II) ion is equal to 0.01 M. The solution also contains sulfuric acid and the solution pH is 1.0. What will be the resulting redox potential of the solution if we add as much orthophosphate acid to it so that total phosphate ions concentration in the solution is 0.1 M? The E0 for the Fe(III)/Fe(II) system is 0.77 V and the complex formation constant of the Fe(III)-H2PO4^- complex is 10^21. At pH 1.0 and at 0.1 M total total phosphate ions concentration, the equilibrium concentration of H2PO4^- ions is 6.97x10^-3 M.\n\nA) +0.26 V\nB) -0.34 V\nC) +0.52 V\nD) +0.40 V\n", "true_answer": "B", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recPyDDO11h9okNQk:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 23864, 3592, 1233, 6451, 350, 81446, 8, 395, 5854, 18, 81454, 5563, 17, 10, 7167, 17227, 8012, 18704, 328,