080, 15, 14, 8017, 314, 220, 455, 13, 36324, 17, 364, 56734, 2360, 13319, 20690, 364, 76, 16, 284, 17, 314, 220, 2467, 9, 1434, 314, 220, 16080, 15, 364, 56734, 350, 16080, 15, 8, 42923, 18, 14, 20, 33132, 68859, 220, 16080, 15, 42308, 230, 220, 22, 13, 38080, 13, 425, 15, 13, 21, 314, 220, 19, 13, 19050, 23, 13, 2794, 314, 319, 42923, 19, 13, 19050, 23, 102838, 220, 2843, 13, 20, 364, 44, 3201, 42923, 16, 14, 20, 102838, 8017, 42923, 15, 13, 17, 16255, 68859, 8017, 28, 19, 13, 37945, 22, 9, 15, 13, 17, 28, 15, 13, 11399, 20, 13, 2794, 28, 17, 13, 36948, 364, 84787, 391, 1303, 33, 314, 220, 2843, 13, 20, 820, 220, 17, 13, 36948, 314, 220, 2636, 13, 19, 30298, 373, 364, 162661, 23864, 14380, 3099, 2595, 364, 84787, 52189, 79, 57556, 25, 220, 1311, 13, 20, 10217, 220, 2636, 13, 19, 364, 10620, 23864, 18051, 350, 44, 1303, 32, 820, 391, 1303, 33, 8, 42923, 20, 14, 18, 92, 364, 44, 1303, 32, 820, 391, 1303, 33, 314, 220, 1311, 13, 3519, 820, 220, 2636, 13, 19, 314, 220, 15, 13, 2991, 350, 76945, 741, 41021, 23864, 25, 220, 1311, 13, 3519, 14, 2636, 13, 19, 28, 15, 13, 24734, 364, 10620, 51159, 220, 20, 14, 18, 314, 220, 16, 13, 19837, 22, 364, 84787, 350, 15, 13, 2991, 8, 42923, 16, 13, 19837, 22, 16255, 45438, 68859, 25, 68859, 220, 15, 13, 2991, 314, 533, 15, 13, 45087, 13, 137658, 656, 220, 16, 13, 19837, 22, 314, 533, 16, 13, 44872, 13, 1771, 113908, 25, 319, 183239, 16, 13, 44872, 102838, 15, 13, 10037, 364, 84787, 415, 6829, 820, 415, 3201, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 858, 364, 84787, 6052, 363, 6574, 15, 13, 858, 364, 4383, 3880, 25, 363, 8, 6574, 15, 13, 858, 13, 9700, 364, 84787, 6052, 13], "n_tokens": 1233, "words": 365, "band_lo": 188.0, "band_hi": 752.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse problem: Two binary black hole systems, distances DA and DB. System A with masses m1=10, m2=25 Msun. System B with masses 35, 50 Msun. Observations show strain amplitude and frequency of gravitational waves from both systems are same. Need ratio DA/DB.\n\nWe need expression for strain amplitude h and frequency f for inspiral binary. The strain amplitude at leading order (quadrupole) is h ∝ (G^2/c^4)*(M_c)^{5/3} (π f)^{2/3} / D, where M_c is chirp mass = (μ)^{3/5} M^{2/5}, where M = m1+m2, μ = m1 m2 / M. Alternatively, h ∝ (M_c)^{5/3} f^{2/3} / D.\n\nAlso frequency evolves with time due to chirp; but the observed frequency at some moment maybe the same for both.\n\nIf both observed f and h are same for the two systems, we have:\n\nh_A = h_B => (M_cA)^{5/3} f^{2/3} / D_A = (M_cB)^{5/3} f^{2/3} / D_B\n\nSince f same, cancel: (M_cA)^{5/3} / D_A = (M_cB)^{5/3} / D_B => D_A / D_B = (M_cA / M_cB)^{5/3}.\n\nThus ratio of distances = (M_cA / M_cB)^{5/3}.\n\nNow compute chirp masses.\n\nCompute for system A:\n\nm1=10, m2=25. M_A = 35 Msun. μ_A = (10*25)/35 = 250/35 = 7.142857... Msun.\n\nChirp mass M_cA = μ^{3/5} M^{2/5}. Compute.\n\nLet’s compute logs maybe.\n\nFirst compute μ_A = 7.142857.\n\nCompute μ^{3/5}. Raise μ to power 0.6. Use approximation.\n\nTake ln: ln μ ≈ ln 7.142857 = about 1.964 (since e^1.964=7.13). Multiply by 0.6 => 1.1784. Exponentiate => e^{1.1784}=3.24.\n\nNow M_A = 35. ln 35 ≈ 3.555. Multiply by 0.4 (2/5) => 1.422. Exponentiate => e^{1.422}=4.15.\n\nThus M_cA ≈ 3.24 * 4.15 = 13.44 Msun.\n\nAlternatively compute directly formula M_c = (m1 m2)^{3/5} / (m1+m2)^{1/5}. Wait there is alternative: M_c = (m1 m2)^{3/5} / (m1+m2)^{1/5}. Let's verify: M_c = (μ)^{3/5} M^{2/5}; but μ = m1 m2 / M. So μ^{3/5} = (m1 m2)^{3/5} / M^{3/5}. Then multiply by M^{2/5} gives (m1 m2)^{3/5} / M^{1/5}. Yes.\n\nSo compute (m1 m2)^{3/5} / (M)^{1/5}.\n\nCompute m1 m2 = 10*25=250.\n\nTake 250^{3/5}. 250^{0.6}. ln 250 ≈ 5.5215. *0.6 = 3.3129. exp = 27.45.\n\nM =35, M^{1/5}=35^{0.2}. ln35=3.555, *0.2=0.711. exp=2.036.\n\nThus M_c =27.45 / 2.036 =13.48. Good matches earlier.\n\nNow system B:\n\nm1=35, m2=50. M_B =85 Msun. μ_B = (35*50)/85 = 1750/85 = 20.5882.\n\nCompute using alternative formula.\n\nm1 m2 = 35*50 = 1750.\n\nCompute (1750)^{3/5}: ln 1750 ≈ 7.468. *0.6 = 4.4808. exp = e^{4.4808}= 88.5.\n\nM_B^{1/5}=85^{0.2}. ln85=4.4427*0.2=0.8885. exp=2.431.\n\nThus M_cB = 88.5 / 2.431 = 36.4 Msun.\n\nAlternatively compute previously second method.\n\nThus chirp masses: 13.5 vs 36.4.\n\nNow compute ratio (M_cA / M_cB)^{5/3}.\n\nM_cA / M_cB = 13.48 / 36.4 = 0.37 (approx). Let's compute: 13.48/36.4=0.370.\n\nNow exponent 5/3 = 1.6667.\n\nThus (0.37)^{1.6667}. Compute ln: ln 0.37 = -0.994. Multiply by 1.6667 = -1.657. Exponential: e^{-1.657}=0.191.\n\nThus D_A / D_B ≈ 0.19.\n\nThus answer C ~0.19.\n\nCheck options: C) ~0.19. Good.\n\nThus answer.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.8729025871143676e-05, "p_correct": 0.999999999999999, "logp_correct": -9.525599030137917e-16, "letter_mass": 1.000000000000001, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.28035554962708026, "cot_lp_min64": -0.7326514427431796, "frac_below_-5": 0.002459016393442623, "digit_lp_min": -3.412623643875122, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.30860990285873413, "n_cot_tokens": 1220, "lp_end": -3.4570634852570947e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -4.838739762842826, "p_nocot": 0.007917025096534044, "uplift": 4.838739762842825, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.555026, "B": 0.432255, "C": 0.007917, "D": 0.004802}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recSBcGLPatKb3Ygu", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: We want to observe a decay of a single atom (atomic nucleus). It is possible to obtain this atom (e.g., from a beta decay of another atom) and start monitoring right away. We know from previous experiments that the decay probability of this atom is 32% within 100 minutes.\nSo far, 50 minutes have passed and the atom has not decayed. What is the probability it will decay in the next 150 minutes?\n\nA) 40%\nB) 52%\nC) 44%\nD) 48%\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: We want to observe a decay of a single atom (atomic nucleus). It is possible to obtain this atom (e.g., from a beta decay of another atom) and start monitoring right away. We know from previous experiments that the decay probability of this atom is 32% within 100 minutes.\nSo far, 50 minutes have passed and the atom has not decayed. What is the probability it will decay in the next 150 minutes?\n\nA) 40%\nB) 52%\nC) 44%\nD) 48%\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recSBcGLPatKb3Ygu:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11433, 34933, 484, 448, 34376, 738, 57549, 306, 2613, 220, 5215, 5438, 4335, 484, 480, 853, 47203, 220, 1434, 5438, 11, 4335, 484, 10705, 34933, 328, 1885, 22983, 3518, 220, 1353, 5438, 382, 220, 1398, 15678, 134644, 116799, 57549, 350, 37835, 2695, 936, 575, 2560, 4213, 9594, 57549, 6251, 17667, 13, 7801, 398, 77281, 356, 3518, 220, 1353, 1349, 8, 314, 220, 16, 533, 319, 183239, 1727, 425, 220, 1353, 92, 314, 220, 15, 13, 1398, 871, 319, 183239, 1353, 17667, 92, 314, 220, 15, 13, 4625, 871, 17667, 314, 66319, 16, 14, 1353, 8, 68859, 220, 15, 13, 4625, 364, 10620, 581, 3810, 34933, 484, 480, 1885, 1044, 306, 17116, 350, 1434, 11, 220, 1179, 8, 5438, 30, 5055