, 9439, 536, 350, 146921, 472, 3611, 741, 19792, 316, 3331, 89227, 379, 18655, 275, 28611, 364, 12, 12978, 270, 12, 16, 12, 19980, 350, 11289, 434, 20982, 2351, 939, 514, 741, 13974, 89227, 379, 18655, 275, 28611, 6574, 702, 13, 21, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 3611, 9386, 36748, 3991, 12, 139573, 110815, 25, 8916, 9439, 5192, 316, 92094, 145058, 328, 89227, 379, 18655, 350, 79, 28611, 328, 1704, 270, 315, 27556, 6574, 1311, 13, 21, 741, 2632, 1704, 270, 110815, 382, 26929, 3611, 1572, 32353, 3643, 939, 514, 364, 12, 12978, 270, 12, 16, 42231, 514, 350, 32038, 4358, 5892, 15134, 770, 514, 30, 50557, 10112, 10049, 18, 3583, 17, 3583, 28, 57321, 741, 3133, 514, 68141, 9439, 536, 6771, 316, 448, 41887, 30, 357, 2411, 770, 514, 350, 1148, 17, 8, 9439, 536, 3760, 1572, 939, 514, 11, 275, 28611, 328, 89227, 379, 18655, 2846, 220, 18, 12, 19, 30, 50557, 770, 514, 350, 49, 17, 34, 28, 57321, 8, 382, 1078, 275, 28611, 328, 89227, 379, 18655, 6574, 18, 12, 19, 30, 4569, 7890, 6752, 13586, 3611, 350, 9995, 448, 311, 2028, 6574, 20, 741, 2632, 4275, 91592, 1572, 939, 514, 364, 12, 12978, 294, 83042, 88832, 350, 62832, 10049, 18, 3583, 17, 34, 7, 28, 57321, 8, 57321, 17, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 41762, 514, 741, 186204, 2028, 553, 5532, 31148, 11, 275, 28611, 328, 89227, 379, 18655, 6574, 899, 13, 20, 350, 88722, 316, 1704, 270, 110815, 741, 12978, 294, 83042, 88832, 382, 448, 41762, 514, 11, 9439, 889, 3760, 1572, 1704, 270, 110815, 10112, 16132, 3760, 13, 44130, 41762, 514, 275, 28611, 6574, 899, 13, 20, 11, 1704, 270, 110815, 6574, 1311, 13, 20, 13, 2632, 2569, 328, 9439, 9386, 350, 33285, 376, 316, 57093, 3127, 5892, 270, 12, 16, 42231, 514, 350, 50812, 8, 464, 5892, 270, 12, 16, 12, 19980, 350, 72, 8, 464, 5892, 294, 83042, 88832, 350, 349, 8, 464, 1704, 270, 110815, 350, 3573, 741, 2632, 363, 25, 117876, 464, 575, 464, 20801, 464, 28354, 364, 4383, 6052, 3880, 25, 17796, 355, 25, 363, 314, 117876, 464, 575, 464, 20801, 464, 28354, 350, 6763, 741, 17796, 418, 25, 2684, 363, 314, 117876, 464, 575, 464, 20801, 464, 28354, 350, 6763, 741, 17796, 363, 25, 363, 314, 28354, 464, 20801, 464, 117876, 464, 575, 350, 127306, 741, 17796, 415, 25, 363, 314, 28354, 464, 20801, 464, 117876, 464, 575, 350, 127306, 741, 2632, 355, 326, 418, 2973, 679, 363, 314, 117876, 464, 575, 464, 20801, 464, 28354, 364, 10620, 581, 2804, 5230, 1118, 6052, 350, 32, 11, 418, 11, 363, 11, 415, 8, 15248, 722, 3407, 8896, 13, 22483, 1402, 32, 8, 355, 314, 117876, 464, 28354, 464, 20801, 464, 575, 11, 418, 314, 28354, 464, 117876, 464, 20801, 464, 575, 11, 363, 314, 117876, 464, 575, 464, 20801, 464, 28354, 279, 33, 8, 355, 314, 117876, 464, 20801, 464, 28354, 464, 575, 11, 418, 314, 575, 464, 117876, 464, 28354, 464, 20801, 11, 363, 314, 117876, 464, 575, 464, 20801, 464, 28354, 279, 34, 8, 355, 314, 117876, 464, 28354, 464, 20801, 464, 575, 11, 418, 314, 28354, 464, 117876, 464, 20801, 464, 575, 11, 363, 314, 28354, 464, 20801, 464, 117876, 464, 575, 279, 35, 8, 355, 314, 117876, 464, 20801, 464, 28354, 464, 575, 11, 418, 314, 575, 464, 117876, 464, 28354, 464, 20801, 11, 363, 314, 28354, 464, 20801, 464, 117876, 464, 575, 279, 2167, 16817, 6145, 355, 314, 117876, 464, 28354, 464, 20801, 464, 575, 11, 6145, 418, 314, 28354, 464, 117876, 464, 20801, 464, 575, 11, 6145, 363, 314, 117876, 464, 575, 464, 20801, 464, 28354, 364, 84787, 5317, 355, 15248, 9707, 13, 17796, 363, 853, 355, 326, 418, 6145, 889, 363, 8201, 13, 2632, 6052, 382, 355, 364, 84787, 4733, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 355], "n_tokens": 1919, "words": 969, "band_lo": 484.75, "band_hi": 1939.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse the problem: There are three groups A, B, C. Need to arrange in increasing order of acidic (B) and basic strength (A & C). That is: For group A, order of basic strength (since A is about basic compounds) increasing. For group B, order of acidic strength increasing. For group C, also basic strength? The question states: \"Arrange the following in increasing order of their acidic (B) and basic strength (A & C).\" So group A and C are basic compounds; group B is acidic compounds. Need to pick correct order among four answer choices.\n\nWe must evaluate relative basicities for compounds in sets A and C, and acidity for set B.\n\nLet's do each.\n\nSet A compounds: i = cyclohexanamine, ii = aniline, iii = 4-nitroaniline, iv = 4-ethylaniline. All are aromatic or aliphatic amines. Basicity depends on availability of lone pair on N. Aniline's lone pair conjugated with benzene ring, reduces basicity compared to aliphatic. Electron-withdrawing groups decrease basicity; electron-donating increase.\n\nCyclohexanamine: aliphatic secondary? Actually cyclohexanamine is primary amine attached to cyclohexyl, not aromatic, so its lone pair not delocalized => relatively strong base (pKa of conjugate acid about 10.6). Aniline: conjugation, less basic (pKa ~5). 4-nitroaniline: nitro is strong -I, -M withdrawing, further reduces basicity (pKa ~1?). Actually pKa of anilinium ~5, of 4-nitroaniline ~1. So weaker base. 4-ethylaniline: ethyl is electron-donating via hyperconjugation, increases basicity relative to aniline but still less than aliphatic? It should be slightly more basic than aniline (pKa maybe 5.3). So order of increasing basic strength (weakest to strongest): weakest: 4-nitroaniline (iii). Next: aniline (ii). Next: 4-ethylaniline (iv). Strongest: cyclohexanamine (i). So A: iii < ii < iv < i. Let's verify answer choices: Option A and C both list A = iii < ii < iv < i. Option B and D list A = iii < iv < ii < i. So we choose those with correct A: A or C.\n\nNow Set B acids: i = 1,2,3,4-tetrakis(trifluoromethyl)cyclopenta-1,3-diene, ii = cyclohexa-1,3-diene, iii = cyclopenta-1,3-diene, iv = hydroquinone.\n\nWe need to arrange increasing acidic strength (weakest acid to strongest acid). Let's evaluate acidity (pKa). These are all hydrocarbons or phenols. Hydroquinone (benzene-1,4-diol) is a phenol; its pKa ~9.85 (first). It's relatively acidic compared to alkenes. Cyclohexa-1,3-diene is a conjugated diene in a six-membered ring (cyclohexadiene). It's a hydrocarbon; acidity of sp2 C-H (like cyclohexadiene) is low (pKa ~44?). Cyclopenta-1,3-diene similarly maybe ~41. 1,2,3,4-tetrakis(trifluoromethyl) cyclopenta-1,3-diene has four CF3 groups, strongly electron-withdrawing, stabilizing the conjugate base (an anion at a carbon). Likely much more acidic than others, maybe pKa ~20? Need relative order.\n\nThus weakest acid (least acidic) likely cyclohexa-1,3-diene? Compare cyclohexadiene vs cyclopentadiene (no, cyclopentadiene is more acidic due to aromaticity of cyclopentadienyl anion (10 pi, aromatic). Cyclopenta-1,3-diene is not cyclopentadiene (which is 1,3-cyclopentadiene). Actually cyclopenta-1,3-diene is same as cyclopentadiene (it has two double bonds). Cyclopentadiene is known to be relatively acidic (pKa ~15). Wait pKa of cyclopentadiene ~15. So that's more acidic than phenol? Phenol pKa ~10. So phenol is stronger acid (lower pKa) than cyclopentadiene? Actually lower pKa means stronger acid, so phenol (pKa 10) > cyclopentadiene (pKa 15) in acidity. So order: weakest acid is cyclohexa-1,3-diene (non-aromatic diene) maybe pKa ~44, then cyclopenta-1,3-diene (pKa 15), then hydroquinone (pKa 9.85), then tetrakis(CF3) cyclopenta-diene (maybe pKa lower than phenol, maybe ~8? but CF3 groups strongly withdraw; might be even stronger acid maybe pKa ~5?). Let's assume it's the strongest acid among given.\n\nThus increasing acidity: ii (cyclohexa-1,3-diene) < iii (cyclopenta-1,3-diene) < iv (hydroquinone) < i (tetrakis CF3 diene).\n\nCheck answer options: Need B = ii < iii < iv < i.\n\nOption A: B = ii < iii < iv < i (yes). Option C: B = ii < iii < iv < i (yes). So both A and C have same B that matches our reasoning.\n\nNow Set C compounds: i = propan-1-amine, ii = guanidine, iii = propan-1-imine, iv = propionimidamide. Need increasing basic strength.\n\nLet's examine each's basicity (strength as base). Need to consider conjugate acid pKa.\n\n- Propan-1-amine (primary aliphatic amine). Its conjugate acid pKa ~10.6 (i.e., base strength moderate).\n\n- Guanidine: highly basic due to resonance stabilization of conjugate acid (pKa of guanidinium ~13.6). So guanidine is stronger base than alkyl amine.\n\n- Propan-1-imine (also called propanal imine? Actually maybe CH3CH2CH=NH). Imine nitrogen basicity similar to aniline? I think imine (sp2) basicity less than amine, pKa of conjugate acid around 3-4? Actually imine (R2C=NH) is about pKa of conjugate acid ~3-4? That seems quite weak base (like anilines ~5). So it's weaker than amine.\n\n- Propionimidamide (maybe CH3CH2C(=NH)NH2, i.e., amidine). Amidines are strong bases, pKa of conjugate acid ~12.5 (similar to guanidine). Propionimidamide is an amidine, basic but less than guanidine maybe slightly less. Usually amidine pKa ~12.5, guanidine ~13.5. So order of basic strength (weakest to strongest): propan-1-imine (iii) < propan-1-amine (i) < propionimidamide (iv) < guanidine (ii). So C: iii < i < iv < ii.\n\nCheck answer options: Option A: C = iii < i < iv < ii (yes). Option B: same C = iii < i < iv < ii (yes). Option C: C = ii < iv < iii < i (different). Option D: C = ii < iv < iii < i (different). So A and B both have C = iii < i < iv < ii.\n\nNow we must pick which answer (A, B, C, D) matches all three groups. Options:\n\nA) A = iii < ii < iv < i, B = ii < iii < iv < i, C = iii < i < iv < ii\n\nB) A = iii < iv < ii < i, B = i < iii < ii < iv, C = iii < i < iv < ii\n\nC) A = iii < ii < iv < i, B = ii < iii < iv < i, C = ii < iv < iii < i\n\nD) A = iii < iv < ii < i, B = i < iii < ii < iv, C = ii < iv < iii < i\n\nWe determined correct A = iii < ii < iv < i, correct B = ii < iii < iv < i, correct C = iii < i < iv < ii.\n\nThus option A matches exactly. Option C has A and B correct but C wrong. So answer is A.\n\nThus output: ANSWER: A", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.7683710135970614e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999095, "logp_correct": -9.047284971948535e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999997615815682, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.357793476886237, "cot_lp_min64": -0.7006339670066755, "frac_below_-5": 0.0026232948583420775, "digit_lp_min": -4.029845237731934, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0002661589242052287, "n_cot_tokens": 1906, "lp_end": -0.02680623158812523, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -10.410284117980039, "p_nocot": 3.0121115576681284e-05, "uplift": 10.410284117979948, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 3e-05, "B": 0.148038, "C": 3e-05, "D": 0.851901}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recV1dB3itJjvXzLx", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Performing observations at the La Silla Observatory in Chile is truly enjoyable and unforgettable. However, aside from the enjoyable aspects, there is also some work to be done, such as scheduling observations. Depending on the available time, one must plan the observations. Unfortunately, things don't always go as planned when scheduling observations. Sometimes, strong winds come from the (preferentially) North. To protect the optical elements, one should avoid pointing the telescope toward strong winds.\n\nIn the event that the wind comes from the North, which of the following targets would be the best backup option?\n\nA) Star2: RA = 2 h, Dec = +14 deg\nB) Star4: RA = 5 h, Dec = +65 deg\nC) Star1: RA = 15 deg, Dec = -45 deg\nD) Star3: RA = 70 deg, Dec = -14 deg\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Performing observations at the La Silla Observatory in Chile is truly enjoyable and unforgettable. However, aside from the enjoyable aspects, there is also some work to be done, such as scheduling observations. Depending on the available time, one must plan the observations. Unfortunately, things don't always go as planned when scheduling observations. Sometimes, strong winds come from the (preferentially) North. To protect the optical elements, one should avoid pointing the telescope toward strong winds.\n\nIn the event that the wind comes from the North, which of the following targets would be the best backup option?\n\nA) Star2: RA = 2 h, Dec = +14 deg\nB) Star4: RA = 5 h, Dec = +65 deg\nC) Star1: RA = 15 deg, Dec = -45 deg\nD) Star3: RA = 70 deg, Dec = -14 deg\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recV1dB3itJjvXzLx:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 4792, 13, 32711, 11215, 591, 7180, 26, 581, 1682, 316, 8397, 45521, 121536, 15256, 5532, 46549, 13, 2632, 581, 1682, 261, 3783, 7567, 4194, 591, 7180, 9262, 15001, 316, 290, 8664, 7295, 350, 4579, 336, 5561, 741, 623, 121536, 1757, 2438, 4194, 591, 290, 11215, 9262, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 9979, 6800, 30, 1843, 11215, 591, 7180, 11, 8397, 45521, 16173, 5323, 26, 2438, 15019, 5323, 382, 7703, 13, 2632, 5655, 3783, 483, 30910, 6567, 3273, 350, 46069, 6567, 3273, 8, 6960, 13, 54666, 7295, 40745, 25, 3038, 336, 5561, 382, 540, 6574, 533, 2270, 13, 18, 5597, 40745, 350, 145118, 741, 2632, 22836, 483, 6567, 3273, 4150, 15019, 10112, 6931, 66032, 30, 3072, 290, 6056, 25, 25474, 5317, 484, 382, 4150, 591, 16173, 2322, 62832, 8253, 483, 30910, 6567, 3273, 813, 121536, 5571, 945, 9979, 15019, 364, 2167, 553, 4335, 46104, 306, 5123, 503, 18210, 30, 22483, 25, 355, 46104, 28, 17, 312, 350, 1130, 5597, 936, 5244, 659, 1265, 5597, 33426, 26, 418, 46104, 28, 20, 312, 11, 5244, 659, 3898, 5597, 1869, 16173, 26, 363, 46104, 28, 1055, 5597, 350, 13980, 382, 220, 16, 71, 95807, 5244, 533, 2548, 5597, 15019, 26, 415, 46104, 28, 2789, 5597, 65176, 19, 71, 1723, 76, 936, 5244, 533, 1265, 5597, 15019, 889, 625, 472, 4150, 15019, 364, 5808, 1636, 25474, 6960, 8253, 483, 1645, 30910, 6567, 3273, 25, 11307, 16, 483, 5244, 533, 2548, 5597, 13, 21926, 4150, 15019, 11, 23821, 316, 16173, 11215, 13, 2632, 6052, 363, 364, 4383, 538, 1062, 1273, 12332, 25, 46104, 625, 6118, 13, 2632, 6052, 363, 364, 84787, 3322, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 363], "n_tokens": 287, "words": 191, "band_lo": 104.5, "band_hi": 418.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret problem. There's wind from North; we want to avoid pointing telescope toward strong winds. So we want a target located away from North direction relative to the observatory (La Silla). The telescope should point away from the wind direction, i.e., towards South? If wind from North, avoid pointing northwards; point southwards is safe. So choose target with southern declination (negative declination) likely. Observatory latitude: La Silla is at ~ -29.3 deg latitude (south). So targets with declination far south maybe lower altitude? But the idea: backup option that is far from north—maybe star with southern declination so telescope points more towards south.\n\nWe are given RA in hours or degrees? Options: A RA=2 h (30 deg), Dec +14 deg northern; B RA=5 h, Dec +65 deg very north; C RA=15 deg (which is 1h?), Dec -45 deg south; D RA=70 deg (~4h40m), Dec -14 deg south but not as far south.\n\nSo best backup likely star with most southern declination: Star1 with Dec -45 deg. That's far south, opposite to north wind. So answer C.\n\nCheck if any other factor: RA not needed. So answer C.\n\nThus response: ANSWER: C", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.622602153223852e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999997867, "logp_correct": -2.1325876372644908e-13, "letter_mass": 0.9999979734463907, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7164729868962773, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2206530360336103, "frac_below_-5": 0.014598540145985401, "digit_lp_min": -0.5894854664802551, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.036273129284381866, "n_cot_tokens": 274, "lp_end": -0.0832146555185318, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -2.7295288371427917, "p_nocot": 0.0652500258152895, "uplift": 2.7295288371425785, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.083783, "B": 0.425484, "C": 0.06525, "D": 0.425484}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recVE8cUNHpHZIAvL", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Very large number of neutrinos produced by the Sun reach the Earth (very large flux of neutrinos, defined as the number of neutrinos per cm^2, per second).\n\nLet us assume that, hypothetically, the pp-III branch suddenly stopped in the core of the Sun about 8 and a half minutes ago, while all other reactions remained as they were.\n\nWhat would be the approximate ratio of the flux between two bands of neutrino energies of 700-800 KeV (band 1) and 800-900 keV (band 2). \n\nFlux (band 1) / flux (band 2) is:\n\n(Note: we are talking about stopping the pp-III branch, not pp-II, pp-I or any other. It’s not a typo or something like that.)\n(Note 2: solar neutrino flavor changes happen, but do not play a role here.)\n\nA) 10.\nB) 0.1 (10^-1).\nC) 1.\nD) 0.01 (10^-2).\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Very large number of neutrinos produced by the Sun reach the Earth (very large flux of neutrinos, defined as the number of neutrinos per cm^2, per second).\n\nLet us assume that, hypothetically, the pp-III branch suddenly stopped in the core of the Sun about 8 and a half minutes ago, while all other reactions remained as they were.\n\nWhat would be the approximate ratio of the flux between two bands of neutrino energies of 700-800 KeV (band 1) and 800-900 keV (band 2). \n\nFlux (band 1) / flux (band 2) is:\n\n(Note: we are talking about stopping the pp-III branch, not pp-II, pp-I or any other. It’s not a typo or something like that.)\n(Note 2: solar neutrino flavor changes happen, but do not play a role here.)\n\nA) 10.\nB) 0.1 (10^-1).\nC) 1.\nD) 0.01 (10^-2).\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recVE8cUNHpHZIAvL:3", "cot_tokens": [216