{"qid": "recWSIn23pglAdv4f", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Astronomers are searching for exoplanets around two stars with exactly the same masses. Using the RV method, they detected one planet around each star, both with minimum masses similar to that of Neptune. The orbital inclinations of the two planets are both equal to 30 degrees. The stars themselves have masses similar to that of our Sun. Planet #1 was found to be in a circular orbit. For planet #2, it was found that the radius ratio at apoapsis and periapsis is equal to 1.5.\n\nPlanet #1 was detected from a periodic shift of up to 4 milliangstroms in a spectral line at a given wavelength. The periodic wavelength shift of the same spectral line in the spectrum of the host star of planet #2 corresponds to 1.77 MeV.\n\nHow many times longer is the orbital period of planet #2 compared to that of planet #1?\n\nA) ~ 0.6\nB) ~ 1.2\nC) ~ 1.6\nD) ~ 0.2\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Astronomers are searching for exoplanets around two stars with exactly the same masses. Using the RV method, they detected one planet around each star, both with minimum masses similar to that of Neptune. The orbital inclinations of the two planets are both equal to 30 degrees. The stars themselves have masses similar to that of our Sun. Planet #1 was found to be in a circular orbit. For planet #2, it was found that the radius ratio at apoapsis and periapsis is equal to 1.5.\n\nPlanet #1 was detected from a periodic shift of up to 4 milliangstroms in a spectral line at a given wavelength. The periodic wavelength shift of the same spectral line in the spectrum of the host star of planet #2 corresponds to 1.77 MeV.\n\nHow many times longer is the orbital period of planet #2 co