16, 357, 19220, 741, 41351, 11, 139817, 21207, 3605, 472, 1001, 9882, 13, 2632, 1888, 357, 19220, 314, 220, 16, 350, 7393, 290, 12796, 741, 17796, 415, 382, 220, 16, 13, 6208, 1273, 3880, 25, 220, 15, 11, 18, 11, 20, 11, 16, 26, 6052, 415, 66938, 316, 220, 16, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 16, 350, 3941, 415, 741], "n_tokens": 3175, "words": 1892, "band_lo": 590.0, "band_hi": 2360.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to understand the reaction: 2-formyl-5-vinylcyclohex-3-enecarboxylic acid (so a cyclohexene ring with a formyl group at C2, a vinyl substituent at C5, and a carboxylic acid at... the naming \"cyclohex-3-ene-1-carboxylic acid\"? Actually \"cyclohex-3-enecarboxylic acid\" meaning carboxylic acid attached to the ring (maybe at position 1?). The base name: cyclohex-3-ene-1-carboxylic acid? Let's parse: 2-formyl-5-vinylcyclohex-3-enecarboxylic acid. So the parent is cyclohex-3-ene carboxylic acid (carboxyl at position 1). At position 2 there is a formyl group (CHO), at position 5 there is a vinyl group (CH=CH2). So we have a cyclohexene with three unsaturations? Let's draw.\n\nNumbering: Carboxylic acid group at C1. Double bond at C3-C4 maybe (since cyclohex-3-ene). Formyl at C2 (adjacent). Vinyl at C5.\n\nThus the structure: ring: C1 (CO2H), C2 (CHO), C3=C4 double bond, C5 (CH=CH2), C6 saturations.\n\nNow conversion with red phosphorus and excess HI. This reagent reduces carboxylic acids to alkyl iodides (HI reduces carboxylic acid to alkyl iodide via decarboxylation? Actually HI + red P is used to convert alcohols to iodides, also reduces carboxylic acids to alkanes? Wait: HI + red P reduces acid to alkane (via decarboxylation?) For example, CH3COOH + HI (P) => CH3I? Actually the famous reaction: iodination with red phosphorus reduces carboxylic acids to alkyl iodides (RCOOH + HI → RI + CO2 + H2O). Yes, the decarboxylation (Hunsdiecker? Not exactly). I believe HI+P reduces acids to alkyl iodides (produces iodoalkane). Also reduces aldehydes to hydrocarbons? Actually HI reduces aldehydes to hydrocarbons (HI + red P reduces aldehydes to alkanes). Known as the HI-P reduction (e.g., conversion of aromatic aldehydes to iodo-? Not sure). In general, aldehydes can be reduced to alkanes via HI (HI reduces to alkyl iodide then further to alkane). Similarly, alkenes might undergo addition of HI (hydroiodination) under excess HI (Markovnikov) to saturate double bonds. Also iodination of alcohols to iodides. Excess HI may also lead to substitution of oxygen groups with I.\n\nThus the substrate has three functionalities: carboxylic acid → reduces to alkyl iodide (decarboxylation?). Actually decarboxylates generating a shorter chain? Wait, the substituent is on a ring – so removal of CO2 would open the ring? Let's think: R-COOH + HI → R-I + CO2 + H2O. So the carbonyl carbon is lost as CO2, the side chain becomes iodide attached to the carbon formerly attached to the carboxyl carbon? In a cyclic acid, R is the alkyl part attached to COOH. So you remove the CO2, and the carbon where COOH was attached becomes attached to I (the same carbon? Actually the carbon bearing the carboxyl group becomes the carbon attached to I after decarboxylation? Example: cyclohexanecarboxylic acid + HI → cyclohexyl iodide + CO2 + H2O. So the ring stays intact, just the carboxyl carbon is removed, leaving a hydrogen replaced by I at that position. So the ring becomes cyclohexyl iodide.\n\nThus here, the acid will become I substituent at C1 (replacing CO2H). So now we have a cyclohexane ring with iodine at C1, formyl at C2, vinyl at C5, and double bond at C3- C4 (still present maybe). But next, excess HI may reduce aldehyde to alkyl iodide (or fully to alkane). For aldehyde: R-CHO + HI → R-CH2I (first reduction to iodo-alkane). However, under excess HI and red P, the aldehyde may be reduced to alkane. I think conversion: RCHO + HI → RCH2I (via reduction of C=O). Then the iodo group can be further reduced to RCH3 by HI? Actually HI reduces iodoalkane to alkane? I think HI can convert RI to R H (hydrogen) via hydroiodic acid reduction and formation of H2? Not typical. But known that HI reduces alkyl iodides to alkane (by removal of iodine and addition of H). Example: CH3I + HI leads to CH4? Not typically; but HI can be used with red P to reduce to hydrocarbon: e.g., C6H5COCH3 + HI + P gives C6H5CH3 (deoxygenation). So the overall transformation: aldehyde to alkane (with loss of oxygen, replaced by hydrogen). So the net effect is removal of O (hydrogenation). Similarly, double bonds undergo addition of HI (hydroiodination) saturating them. With excess HI, both sides will be iodinated then reduced further to hydrogen. The overall product becomes fully saturated alkane with iodides? Let's recall: Reaction of alkenes with excess HI gives addition of HI until saturation, giving poly-iodinated product. Then the iodine can be displaced by hydrogen via reduction (HI to H2?). But with excess HI, eventually you'll get fully saturated alkane (no iodine) because HI reduces iodo groups? Actually cleavage of C–I by HI? Possibly not. In practice, HI is a reducing agent that converts C–I to C–H via hydrogen. In presence of red phosphorus, the HI is regenerated (P + H2O = H3PO3?). I'd better recall that the reaction of alkenes with HI in presence of red phosphorus (or large excess) gives saturated alkanes (the identical to catalytic hydrogenation). Example: C2H4 + HI (excess) → C2H6? Not typical. But I think the principle: In presence of HI and red phosphorus, alkenes get reduced to alkanes (HI adds across double bond to give alkyl iodide, then the iodide is reduced by more HI to alkane, producing I2). This is known as \"hydroiodic acid reduction\" often used to remove oxygen. So overall each unsaturation (C=C, C=O, COOH) is removed (i.e., hydrogenated). So final product would be a saturated cyclohexane with extra side chains eventually saturated: formyl becomes CH3 (since aldehyde converted to methyl), vinyl becomes ethyl (since CH=CH2 reduces to CH2CH3). And the carboxylic acid carbon is removed leaving I at C1 which further reduced to H (so just hydrogen). So net final product: cyclohexane with substituents? Let's see: original skeleton: ring positions: C1 (attached to CO2H) becomes H after decarboxylation? Actually after reduction, the carbonyl carbon is lost as CO2, so the ring loses a carbon: originally cyclohexane ring with carboxyl substituent at same carbon (external carbon). So after decarboxylation, the ring remains 6-membered; the substituent removed, leaving hydrogen at that position. So the site becomes a hydrogen, not iodine in final product (if iodine further removed). So final product becomes cyclohexane with no substituents? Wait, we also have formyl at C2 becomes CH3 attached at C2 (i.e., methyl substituent). Vinyl at C5 becomes ethyl substituent. Double bond at C3-4 saturated to single bond.\n\nThus product would be a cyclohexane with a methyl at C2 and ethyl at C5. Plus maybe other hydrogens.\n\nNow we need index of hydrogen deficiency (i.e., degree of unsaturation) of product.\n\nIndex of hydrogen deficiency (IHD) = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2. For product, we can calculate based on structure.\n\nFirst find molecular formula of product.\n\nOriginal molecule formula? Let's compute.\n\nParent cyclohex-3-ene carboxylic acid: cyclohexane base C6H10 (one double bond reduces H by 2). Carboxylic acid adds CO2H => adds C1 O2 H1 overall? Actually CO2H adds COOH group: adds C1 O2 H1 to the skeleton (replaces a hydrogen). So formula for cyclohex-3-ene-1-carboxylic acid: C7H? Let's compute. Cyclohexane: C6H12. One double bond: C6H10. Carboxyl substituent replaces a hydrogen (since carbon attached to COOH already part of ring). So add CO2H (C1 O2 H1) but lose one H from ring (since substitution). So total: C6H10 - H (one) + C1O2H1 = C7H? (10-1+1) =10? Wait: C6H10 - H = C6H9 + C1O2H1 => C7H10 O2. Check typical formula: cyclohex-3-ene-1-carboxylic acid (C7H10O2). Good.\n\nNow add formyl (CHO) at C2 substituting H. Formyl group adds CHO, replacing a hydrogen of ring. So add C1 H1 O1, remove H1. Net +C1 O1 (H unchanged). So formula becomes C8H10 O3? Wait O current 2 plus 1 =3. H remains 10. So C8H10O3.\n\nAdd vinyl (CH=CH2) at C5 substituting H. Vinyl group adds C2 H3 (since CH=CH2). Actually formula of vinyl group (C2H3) substituting a hydrogen: net add C2 H2? Let's compute: group has C2H3, but you replace a hydrogen on ring, so add C2 H2 (since you lose one H from ring). So net add C2 H2.\n\nThus final starting material: C8+2= C10? Wait original C8 (including formyl). Add C2 => C10. H: start H10 (after formyl) + H2 =12. O: 3 unchanged. So C10H12O3.\n\nAlso there's a double bond (C=C) already accounted in unsaturation. The vinyl adds double bond as part of its structure.\n\nThus starting formula: C10H12O3.\n\nNow product after HI/P reduction: Carboxyl removed as CO2, so lose C1 O2 (CO2) and lose the carbonyl O? The carboxyl carbon lost; So product loses C1 O2 (since CO2) and also maybe loses an oxygen from O? Actually the CO2 leaves as CO2 (C1 O2) from the acid. So subtract C1 O2. Also the hydrogen atoms? Carboxyl group had OH; when decarboxylated, we lose OH and the carbon. That yields H loss? The CO2 elimination leaves behind a H (or I that later becomes H). The net effect is losing C1 O2 plus maybe 0 H? Typically RCOOH + HI → RI + CO2 + H2O. So the reactants produce RI (adds I) and H2O (water). So net the acid provides R (alkyl part) losing CO2 and H2O (so overall loss of C1 O2 and H2O? Actually product RI has same alkyl R as acid (the group attached to carbonyl). The acid had CnH? plus CO2H. After reaction, we produce R–I, CO2, H2O. So the atoms of acid: C (n+1) O2 H(2n+1?) Not needed.\n\nThus from our starting molecule: we remove the external carbon of carboxyl (C1) and both oxygens; also we lose one hydrogen as part of H2O formed (the OH hydrogen). Also we add iodine maybe later removed. So net atomic composition after final reduction (to hydrocarbon) lose C1 O2, but also lose 2 hydrogens? Because water includes OH hydrogen from acid and one hydrogen from HI? This is complicated.\n\nBut we can compute final formula based on deduced structure: cyclohexane ring (C6) with methyl (CH3) at C2 and ethyl (CH2CH3) at C5.\n\nThus base cyclohexane C6H12. Add methyl substituent adds CH3 (C1 H3) but replaces a hydrogen (since substitution) net +C1 H2. So C7 H14? Wait base C6H12, after substituting a hydrogen with CH3, you add C1 H2 (since you lose one H from ring). So new formula: C7 H14.\n\nAdd ethyl substituent (CH2CH3) = C2 H5, substitute a hydrogen: net +C2 H4. So final: C9 H18.\n\nTotal formula C9H18. Check unsaturation: degree of unsaturation = (2C+2 - H)/2 = (2*9+2 - 18)/2 = (18+2-18)/2 = 2/2 =1. Also no halogens or nitrogens.\n\nThus IHD = 1.\n\nBut we must verify if any other unsaturation remains: maybe one double bond remains (the original double bond maybe not reduced fully?). Let's think again: Starting had internal double bond at C3-4. Under excess HI, that would be reduced to single bond (adds HI). The iodine may be removed to H. So saturated.\n\nThe vinyl group had the double bond; reduced to ethyl.\n\nFormyl carbonyl reduced to methyl.\n\nThus all unsaturation eliminated. But the ring still is saturated; but we have one degree of unsaturation from the ring (since a cycloalkane counts as 1 IHD). Indeed, cyclic structures count as one degree. So product IHD = 1 (only the ring). Option D is 1. Check other options: 0,3,5,1; answer D corresponds to 1.\n\nThus answer: 1 (option D).", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -6.318073239697242e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999725, "logp_correct": -2.74923222887197e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807907522, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7922603896877429, "cot_lp_min64": -1.429505478581575, "frac_below_-5": 0.02118912080961417, "digit_lp_min": -2.930043935775757, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.003969172947108746, "n_cot_tokens": 3162, "lp_end": -0.0031216247007250786, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -5.303576346879159, "p_nocot": 0.004973774119483087, "uplift": 5.303576346879131, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 3e-06, "B": 0.947834, "C": 0.04719, "D": 0.004974}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recXvQ6gWAmyakrpD", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Two planets have been detected orbiting a star using both the transit and radial velocity (RV) methods. The star has a mass of 1.5 times that of the Sun, a radius 1.2 times that of the Sun, and an effective temperature (Teff) of 6300 K. Planet1 has a mass equivalent to 7 Earth masses and a radius 1.7 times that of Earth, while Planet2 has a mass equivalent to 5 Earth masses and a radius 1.3 times that of Earth. Interestingly, both planets are in circular orbits. Planet1 induces a periodic shift of up to 0.03 Å in the forbidden line [OI] at 6300 Å, while the gravitational impact of Planet2 on the same line results in a periodic Doppler shift of up to 0.04 Å. Assuming that both planets possess the same albedo, what is the ratio of the equilibrium temperatures between Planet1 and Planet2?\n\nA) ~ 1.05\nB) ~ 1.30\nC) ~ 0.53\nD) ~ 0.98\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Two planets have been detected orbiting a star using both the transit and radial velocity (RV) methods. The star has a mass of 1.5 times that of the Sun, a radius 1.2 times that of the Sun, and an effective temperature (Teff) of 6300 K. Planet1 has a mass equivalent to 7 Earth masses and a radius 1.7 times that of Earth, while Planet2 has a mass equivalent to 5 Earth masses and a radius 1.3 times that of Earth. Interestingly, both planets are in circular orbits. Planet1 induces a periodic shift of up to 0.03 Å in the forbidden line [OI] at 6300 Å, while the gravitational impact of Planet2 on the same line results in a periodic Doppler shift of up to 0.04 Å. Assuming that both planets possess the same albedo, what is the ratio of the equilibrium temperatures between Planet1 and Planet2?\n