, 20049, 11, 22476, 12951, 261, 3120, 17624, 14041, 72, 13, 41021, 1921, 538, 484, 59684, 120152, 28295, 30, 30456, 413, 3648, 364, 7943, 581, 1309, 18051, 328, 87639, 28295, 13, 12265, 353, 28373, 35353, 251, 350, 43, 78943, 820, 261, 61, 17, 8, 42923, 16, 14, 19, 92, 35353, 251, 261, 183239, 16, 14, 17, 16255, 2632, 18051, 353, 16, 26742, 17, 314, 44237, 6271, 17, 820, 261, 16, 741, 1843, 261, 16, 382, 220, 858, 8571, 11, 261, 17, 1481, 413, 46716, 60314, 13, 41021, 23864, 261, 17, 364, 44, 1359, 17, 314, 20, 391, 6584, 314, 17, 13, 51187, 68, 1161, 14826, 13, 22980, 25, 350, 17, 13, 51187, 68, 1161, 73602, 17, 314, 350, 17, 13, 51187, 61, 17, 8, 319, 1434, 314, 23, 13, 45967, 319, 1434, 314, 23, 13, 45967, 68, 1434, 364, 38, 425, 391, 1359, 17, 61, 17, 314, 21, 13, 49636, 68, 12, 994, 425, 23, 13, 45967, 68, 1434, 314, 20, 13, 47159, 68, 1723, 364, 22671, 96399, 25, 391, 110943, 323, 17, 61, 17, 483, 323, 17, 28, 9659, 19, 13, 23, 284, 5031, 11, 323, 17, 61, 17, 28, 18, 13, 46156, 68, 21, 364, 44, 110943, 323, 17, 61, 17, 314, 17, 13, 50425, 20, 68, 1130, 425, 18, 13, 46156, 68, 21, 314, 16, 13, 38296, 68, 2991, 364, 84787, 261, 17, 314, 20, 13, 47159, 68, 1723, 820, 16, 13, 38296, 68, 2991, 28, 20, 13, 32451, 68, 18, 284, 314, 220, 20, 13, 20, 8571, 13, 2632, 261, 17, 13679, 1572, 261, 16, 11, 1118, 4787, 8512, 2236, 7266, 30061, 364, 84787, 261, 16, 1424, 261, 17, 11, 18051, 261, 17, 23839, 16, 314, 220, 20, 13, 20, 14, 858, 13, 17, 314, 15, 13, 32630, 364, 19371, 353, 16, 26742, 17, 314, 44237, 6271, 17, 23839, 16, 8, 314, 44237, 7, 15, 13, 32630, 25987, 15, 13, 41404, 13, 2632, 353, 16, 26742, 17, 42308, 230, 15, 13, 6733, 46465, 16, 936, 10915, 40880, 16, 51050, 1572, 40880, 17, 13, 623, 6052, 3880, 25, 6574, 15, 13, 6798, 66938, 316, 5317, 363, 13, 2632, 6960, 6052, 363, 364, 4383, 395, 58384, 25, 2684, 434, 137684, 11, 8253, 2684, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 363, 13], "n_tokens": 2615, "words": 1171, "band_lo": 224.0, "band_hi": 896.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need ratio of equilibrium temperatures T_eq ∝ [ (1 - A) L_star / (16πσ a^2) ]^{1/4} assuming same albedo, same emissivity. So ratio depends on (L_star / a^2)^{1/4} for each planet. Since L_star same for both, need ratio of a (semi-major axis). Compute a from radial velocity and transit? Data: We have planet masses etc and observed Doppler shift of forbidden line? Actually they observe shift of [OI] line due to planet? Might give velocity amplitude: shift Δλ = (v/c) λ. So v = c Δλ / λ. For Planet1 shift 0.03 Å at λ=6300 Å. v1 = c *0.03/6300. c=3e5 km/s (speed of light in km/s?) Actually c=3e5 km/s = 3e8 m/s. Use km/s. Δλ/λ =0.03/6300 =4.7619e-6. So v1 = 3e5 km/s *4.7619e-6 = 1.4286 km/s. Planet2 shift 0.04 Å => v2 = c*0.04/6300 = 3e5 *6.3492e-6 = 1.9048 km/s.\n\nThese are the radial velocity amplitudes of star due to each planet? The line shift is observed in star's spectrum; planet induces reflex motion of star. So amplitude K = v (projected). For circular orbit, K = (2π a_star / P). But we can also get orbital semi-major axis from K using relation: K = (2π G / P)^(1/3) * (M_p sin i) / (M_*^{2/3}) . However we don't have period. Maybe we can find a directly using M_p and K? For circular orbit, star's orbital speed v_star = (M_p/M_*) sqrt(G M_*/a) = (M_p / M_*) sqrt(G M_*/a) = (M_p / sqrt(M_* a)) sqrt(G). Actually star and planet orbit barycenter: v_star = (M_p / M_*) * v_planet where v_planet = sqrt(G M_*/a). So v_star = M_p/M_* * sqrt(G M_*/a)= (M_p / sqrt(M_* a)) sqrt(G). Rearranged: a = G M_* (M_p / (v_star sqrt(M_*)))^2? Let's derive: v_star = (M_p / M_*) sqrt(G M_*/a) = (M_p / sqrt(M_* a)) sqrt(G). Solve for a: v_star = M_p sqrt(G) / sqrt(M_* a). Square: v_star^2 = M_p^2 G / (M_* a). => a = M_p^2 G / (M_* v_star^2). Wait check: rearranged: a = M_p^2 G / (M_* v_star^2). Yes.\n\nThus we can compute a for each planet using M_p and v_star (K). Need masses in kg. M_* =1.5 M_sun. M_sun=1.989e30 kg => M_* = 2.9835e30 kg.\n\nPlanet masses: Earth mass M_E =5.972e24 kg. So M_p1 =7 M_E=4.1804e25 kg. M_p2=5 M_E=2.986e25 kg.\n\nCompute G=6.674e-11 SI. Need v in m/s: v1=1.4286 km/s =1428.6 m/s. v2=1904.8 m/s.\n\nCompute a1 = G * M_p1^2 / (M_* v1^2). Let's compute numbers.\n\nFirst M_p1^2: (4.1804e25)^2 = (4.1804^2) e50 =17.473 e50 =1.7473e51.\n\nG * M_p1^2 =6.674e-11 *1.7473e51 =1.166e41 (since -11+51=40, need compute: 6.674*1.7473=11.66; so 11.66e40 =1.166e41).\n\nDenominator: M_* v1^2 = 2.9835e30 * (1428.6^2). 1428.6^2 ≈2.042e6 (since 1400^2=1.96e6; compute precisely: 1428.6*1428.6 =? 1428.6*1400=2,000,040; plus 1428.6*28.6 ≈40,854; total ~2,040,894). So approx 2.041e6.\n\nMultiply by M_*: 2.9835e30 *2.041e6 = 2.9835*2.041 e36 =6.088 e36.\n\nNow a1 = 1.166e41 / 6.088e36 = 1.916e4 m? Wait exponent: 41-36=5, so 1.166/6.088 *10^5 =0.1916*10^5 =1.916e4 meters! That's 19 km - obviously wrong; orbital radius should be ~0.1 AU ~1e10 m. Something off: my formula maybe missing factor.\n\nLet's double-check derivation: orbital velocity of star v_star = (M_p / M_*) * v_orb where v_orb = sqrt(G (M_*+M_p)/a) ≈ sqrt(G M_*/a). So v_star = (M_p / M_*) sqrt(G M_*/a) = (M_p / M_*) sqrt(G M_*/a). Compute: sqrt(G M_*/a). So v_star = M_p sqrt(G) sqrt(M_*/a) / M_*.\n\nSimplify: v_star = M_p sqrt(G) / (M_* ) * sqrt(M_*/a) = M_p sqrt(G) / (M_* ) * sqrt(M_*) / sqrt(a) = M_p sqrt(G) sqrt(M_*) / (M_* sqrt(a)) = M_p sqrt(G) / ( sqrt(M_*) sqrt(a) ). Because M_* = sqrt(M_*) * sqrt(M_*). So v_star = M_p sqrt(G) / ( sqrt(M_*) sqrt(a) ). So v_star = M_p sqrt(G / (M_* a)). Square both sides: v_star^2 = M_p^2 G / (M_* a). Solve for a: a = M_p^2 G / (M_* v_star^2). Same as earlier. So formula seems correct.\n\nBut numbers giving too small radius suggests maybe using v in m/s but G in SI units leads to a in meters; we got 1.9e4 m, which is unrealistic. Let's recalc more carefully.\n\nCompute M_p1 = 7 M_E = 7*5.972e24 = 4.1804e25 kg (ok). Square: (4.1804e25)^2 = 4.1804^2 e50; 4.1804^2 ≈17.47. So =1.747e51 kg^2. Good.\n\nG M_p1^2 = 6.674e-11 *1.747e51 = 1.166e41 (kg^2 m^3 s^-2?). Units: m^3 kg^-1 s^-2 * kg^2 = kg m^3 s^-2.\n\nDenominator: M_* v1^2 = 2.9835e30 kg * (1428.6 m/s)^2. v1^2 = 2.041e6 (m^2/s^2). Multiply: 2.9835e30 *2.041e6 = 6.090e36 kg m^2 / s^2.\n\nNow a = (1.166e41) / (6.09e36) = 1.915e4 (units: (kg m^3 s^-2)/(kg m^2 s^-2) = m). So indeed ~19 km. That's too small because the star's reflex velocity from a small planet at that distance would be larger? Actually with such small orbital radius, reflex velocity would be much larger; not consistent. So perhaps our relationship uses v_star = (2π a_star)/P, not directly the orbital radius. The amplitude we measure is the star's velocity along line-of-sight due to orbital motion; this depends on orbital period which is linked to a via Kepler's law. The formula earlier may be missing factor of (2π a_*)/P. However the earlier derivation using Newtonian dynamics should be correct for circular orbit: gravitational force provides centripetal force for star's motion around barycenter: M_* v_star^2 / a_* = G M_* M_p / a^2 (where a_* = (M_p / M_*) a). Let's try deriving from barycentric relation.\n\nStar and planet distances from barycenter: a_* = a * M_p / (M_* + M_p) ~ a * M_p / M_* (since M_p << M_*). Planet distance a_p = a * M_* / (M_* + M_p) ≈ a.\n\nCentripetal acceleration for star: v_*^2 / a_* = G M_p / a^2. Because star feels gravity from planet: acceleration = G M_p / a^2. And v_*^2 / a_* = G M_p / a^2.\n\nThus v_*^2 = a_* G M_p / a^2 = (a M_p / M_*) * G M_p / a^2 = G M_p^2 / (M_* a). So same formula. So our calculation is correct. This implies that given planet mass and observed reflex velocity, orbital radius is extremely small, which seems unrealistic for exoplanets causing such small RV amplitude ~1-2 km/s? Wait typical RV amplitude for Earth around Sun is ~0.09 m/s, not km/s. Here we have 1 km/s which is huge, implying close-in orbit. To get about 1 km/s for a 7 Earth-mass planet, the orbital radius would be tiny, indeed perhaps a few solar radii. Let's see if that yields plausible temperatures? Could be hot.\n\nBut we need ratio of equilibrium temperatures. Since T_eq ∝ (L_star / a^2)^{1/4} ∝ a^{-1/2}. So ratio T1/T2 = sqrt(a2 / a1). If a1 is 19 km, a2 would be computed similarly. Let's compute a2.\n\nM_p2 =5 M_E =2.986e25 kg. Square: (2.986e25)^2 = (2.986^2) e50 =8.916 e50 =8.916e50.\n\nG * M_p2^2 =6.674e-11 *8.916e50 =5.953e40.\n\nDenominator: M_* v2^2 with v2=1904.8 m/s, v2^2=3.629e6.\n\nM_* v2^2 =2.9835e30 *3.629e6 =1.082e37.\n\nThus a2 =5.953e40 /1.082e37=5.502e3 m = 5.5 km. So a2 smaller than a1, which makes sense because higher velocity.\n\nThus a1 > a2, ratio a2/a1 = 5.5/19.2 =0.287.\n\nThen T1/T2 = sqrt(a2/a1) = sqrt(0.287)=0.536. So T1/T2 ≈0.54 (<1), meaning Planet1 cooler than Planet2. The answer options: ~0.53 corresponds to option C. So likely answer C.\n\nCheck for assumptions: same albedo, star same.\n\nThus answer: C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": ["too_long"], "accepted": false}
{"qid": "recYCMJWH7IpcdqwE", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Which sequence of reactions from the following options would lead to the high-yield synthesis of 2-(tert-butyl)-1-ethoxy-3-nitrobenzene, beginning with benzene?\n\nA) i) HNO3/H2SO4 ; ii) Fe/HCl ; iii) tert-butyl chloride/AlCl3 ; iv) HNO3/H2SO4 ; v) NaNO2/HCl ; vi) SO3/H2SO4 ; vii) dilute H2SO4 ; viii) H3O+, H2O/Heat ; ix) NaOH/EtBr\nB) i) tert-butyl chloride/AlCl3 ; ii) SO3/H2SO4 ; iii) HNO3/H2SO4 iv) Fe/HCl ; v) NaNO2/HCl ; vi) HNO3/H2SO4 ; vii) H3O+, H2O/Heat ; viii) dilute H2SO4 ix) NaOH/EtBr\nC) i) tert-butyl chloride/AlCl3 ; ii) HNO3/H2SO4 ; iii) Fe/HCl ; iv) HNO3/H2SO4 ; v) NaNO2/HCl ; vi) H3O+, H2O/Heat ; vii) NaOH/EtBr ; viii) SO3/H2SO4 ; ix) dilute H2SO4\nD) i) tert-butyl chloride/AlCl3 ; ii) HNO3/H2SO4 iv) ; iii) SO3/H2SO4 ; iv) NaNO2/HCl ; v) dilute H2SO4 ; vi) H3O+, H2O/Heat ; vii) NaOH/EtBr ; viii) Fe/HCl ; ix) HNO3/H2SO4\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Which sequence of reactions from the following options would lead to the high-yield synthesis of 2-(tert-butyl)-1-ethoxy-3-nitrobenzene, beginning with benzene?\n\nA) i) HNO3/H2SO4 ; ii) Fe/HCl ; iii) tert-butyl chloride/AlCl3 ; iv) HNO3/H2SO4 ; v) NaNO2/HCl ; vi) SO3/H2SO4 ; vii) dilute H2SO4 ; viii) H3O+, H2O/Heat ; ix) NaOH/EtBr\nB) i) tert-butyl chloride/AlCl3 ; ii) SO3/H2SO4 ; iii) HNO3/H2SO4 iv) Fe/HCl ; v) NaNO2/HCl ; vi) HNO3/H2SO4 ; vii) H3O+, H2O/Heat ; viii) dilute H2SO4 ix) NaOH/EtBr\nC) i) tert-butyl chloride/AlCl3 ; ii) HNO3/H2SO4 ; iii) Fe/HCl ; iv) HNO3/H2SO4 ; v) NaNO2/HCl ; vi) H3O+, H2O/Heat ; vii) NaOH/EtBr ; viii) SO3/H2SO4 ; ix) dilute H2SO4\nD) i) tert-butyl chloride/AlCl3 ; ii) HNO3/H2SO4 iv) ; iii) SO3/H2SO4 ; iv) NaNO2/HCl ; v) dilute H2SO4 ; vi) H3O+, H2O/H