butyl group at position 2 (i.e., ortho to ethoxy group), ethoxy at position 1, nitro at position 3 relative to ethoxy? Let's define: the main substituents: ethoxy group (–OCH2CH3) is at carbon 1, tert-butyl at carbon 2 (ortho), nitro at carbon 3 (meta to ethoxy, ortho to tert-butyl). So final pattern is 1-ethoxy, 2-tert-butyl, 3-nitro (i.e., a 1,2,3-trisubstituted benzene with those groups). Sequence must involve electrophilic aromatic substitution, then nitration, sulfonation, etc. Sequence must address regiochemical control and allows transformations like converting sulfonic acid to hydroxyl (or something) to introduce ethoxy? Let's think.\n\nGoal: ethoxy substitution (alkoxy). Typically introduced by ethylation via ethyl sulfonate (Friedel-Crafts alkylation with ethyl chloride yields ethyl group, not ethoxy). Ethoxy can be introduced via ethylation of phenol (then O-ethylation). But starting benzene, perhaps first perform ethoxy via ethylation of benzene?? Actually ethoxy is not a typical substituent by electrophilic aromatic substitution (EAS); you can get ethyl (C2H5) via Friedel-Crafts alkylation, then oxidize to ethoxy? Not typical. Another path: first introduce ethyl via Friedel-Crafts ethylation, then convert to ethoxy via oxidation? Wait, ethoxy group is alkoxy attached to aromatic via oxygen; Typically introduced via phenol O-alkylation. So we might need to first sulfonate to get a sulfonic acid, then convert to phenol (via hydrolysis of sulfonic acid to phenol). Then O-ethylate phenol to give ethoxy. That seems plausible: benzene -> sulfonation (SO3/H2SO4) to give benzenesulfonic acid (para or ortho?), then treat with NaOH/EtBr to give phenoxy ethyl? Actually NaOH/EtBr would ethylate the phenoxide after conversion of sulfonic acid to phenol (maybe via hydrolysis of sulfonic acid to phenol after reduction? Not sure). Another transformation: sulfonic acid can be converted to phenol via heating with NaOH (sulfonic acid to phenol by substitution: hydrolysis of sulfonic acid). The sequence often: sulfonation, then alkaline fusion to phenol (sulfonic acid -> phenol). So steps: sulfonation (SO3/H2SO4) then NaOH/heat to replace SO3H with OH (forming phenol). Then ethylate phenol with EtBr (NaOH, EtBr) to give ethoxy phenyl (anisole with ethyl). So we need steps: sulfonate, alkaline fusion to phenol, ethylate.\n\nNow we also need tert-butyl group (ortho to ethoxy) and nitro group at meta relative to ethoxy (i.e., para to tert-butyl? Actually arrangement: ethoxy at 1, t-Bu at 2 (ortho), nitro at 3 (meta to ethoxy, ortho to t-Bu). So after installing ethoxy, we need to introduce t-Bu ortho to ethoxy (i.e., ortho directing group: ethoxy is an ortho/para director (activating). So Friedel-Crafts alkylation with t-BuCl/AlCl3 would likely give ortho and para products relative to ethoxy. para might be hindered? Reaction may give both ortho and para; but we want ortho (2). Might be feasible.\n\nThen nitration: nitro group is meta to ethoxy (since ethoxy directs ortho/para, but we need meta relative to ethoxy). However nitro is also deactivating meta director. After introducing t-Bu (activating ortho/para), nitration will be directed to positions ortho/para to t-Bu (activating). We need nitro at position 3 (which is ortho to t-Bu). t-Bu directs ortho/para, so ortho would be 3 relative to ethoxy? Yes, t-Bu already at 2; its ortho positions are 1 (occupied by ethoxy) and 3 (desired nitro). So nitration in presence of t-Bu will preferentially give nitro at 3 (ortho to t-Bu) which is also meta to ethoxy due to steric/activation? So nitration after t-Bu makes sense.\n\nThus overall plan: start benzene → sulfonation → alkaline fusion → phenol → ethylation (ethoxy) → Friedel-Crafts t-Bu → nitration.\n\nNow look at options to see sequence matches.\n\nOption C sequence: i) tert-butyl chloride/AlCl3 ; ii) HNO3/H2SO4 ; iii) Fe/HCl ; iv) HNO3/H2SO4 ; v) NaNO2/HCl ; vi) H3O+, H2O/Heat ; vii) NaOH/EtBr ; viii) SO3/H2SO4 ; ix) dilute H2SO4\n\nLet's