14732, 37663, 5028, 26369, 448, 41887, 350, 7893, 630, 7553, 1900, 741, 623, 62751, 19328, 98785, 58322, 38566, 1669, 3952, 88832, 316, 939, 514, 350, 1935, 14526, 15883, 3643, 741, 2632, 415, 382, 14732, 37663, 5028, 26369, 448, 41887, 364, 10620, 1023, 3810, 25, 82478, 290, 3992, 47017, 306, 2569, 328, 70763, 322, 11896, 395, 290, 22165, 328, 36762, 418, 316, 36762, 415, 25, 575, 2560, 4213, 1118, 14732, 37663, 5028, 26369, 2974, 4151, 291, 47017, 350, 78149, 47017, 8, 1261, 10805, 1511, 939, 4105, 350, 33, 8, 326, 1815, 57190, 62751, 19328, 98785, 58322, 316, 939, 514, 350, 35, 8, 679, 44689, 9837, 13, 623, 22148, 84, 1094, 7902, 322, 11896, 328, 939, 617, 306, 62751, 19328, 98785, 58322, 13, 623, 21416, 6960, 54870, 4493, 18704, 328, 478, 2118, 69160, 88832, 1815, 98785, 58322, 26, 23629, 53232, 164194, 8896, 2023, 7902, 290, 132038, 14174, 3859, 20234, 129564, 30, 41021, 2411, 364, 976, 6251, 2887, 321, 6906, 5983, 14836, 382, 290, 18704, 328, 478, 2118, 69160, 88832, 350, 2345, 21416, 328, 939, 617, 483, 2866, 17, 306, 3611, 741, 4569, 5983, 23856, 334, 823, 1013, 387, 328, 939, 617, 68141, 350, 2345, 42464, 59437, 316, 1625, 448, 47150, 939, 617, 1118, 1815, 20985, 2866, 17, 13, 131282, 53232, 164194, 22148, 84, 1094, 402, 110666, 12796, 738, 20644, 23629, 27591, 591, 290, 939, 617, 11, 4137, 478, 3760, 9439, 11, 13972, 334, 823, 1013, 387, 54189, 30, 5551, 290, 132038, 328, 290, 939, 617, 120589, 1340, 413, 11202, 350, 8229, 124714, 8, 5192, 316, 23629, 53232, 164194, 3566, 11, 4137, 334, 823, 1013, 387, 11087, 13, 3072, 290, 47081, 6502, 311, 94919, 328, 290, 68141, 316, 10192, 2866, 17, 1340, 413, 39943, 13, 623, 10705, 6251, 3159, 6960, 57191, 1381, 483, 23629, 2469, 263, 1365, 8896, 11293, 6251, 350, 8229, 47081, 6502, 112176, 939, 617, 68141, 741, 2632, 30612, 25, 23629, 2469, 263, 1365, 22148, 11823, 350, 76, 53546, 8, 1424, 23629, 166781, 350, 23498, 298, 6077, 16918, 50557, 31200, 6077, 382, 24742, 1555, 14, 16151, 61739, 4493, 92094, 889, 14732, 4010, 382, 625, 92094, 36752, 11, 889, 15333, 85716, 585, 3159, 350, 1585, 40, 8, 1118, 382, 23629, 53232, 164194, 13, 2632, 14732, 4559, 123753, 6077, 382, 4771, 23629, 53232, 164194, 350, 4500, 128373, 741, 5287, 3643, 350, 44234, 8, 382, 23629, 53232, 164194, 4493, 92094, 350, 6460, 14732, 38566, 92094, 741, 179863, 382, 27203, 23629, 53232, 164194, 13, 2632, 6200, 22148, 84, 1094, 14732, 25, 101385, 350, 22130, 1365, 4493, 22725, 542, 63159, 387, 11, 13586, 659, 40, 8, 4605, 31200, 6077, 350, 11154, 1127, 40, 8, 70763, 23629, 27591, 26, 1625, 3643, 350, 11154, 1127, 40, 11, 10112, 1127, 44, 3760, 8488, 8, 4605, 27054, 298, 350, 9851, 1127, 40, 11, 1127, 44, 741, 2632, 322, 11896, 70763, 2569, 25, 101385, 1424, 31200, 6077, 1424, 1625, 3643, 1424, 27054, 298, 364, 84787, 290, 2569, 328, 47017, 350, 3086, 4756, 2974, 4151, 291, 18655, 8, 1118, 1934, 22165, 316, 939, 617, 738, 2622, 2684, 2569, 395, 62751, 19328, 98785, 58322, 13, 2632, 30612, 70763, 322, 11896, 25, 220, 18, 2126, 53546, 3128, 4151, 291, 18655, 350, 17, 8, 11639, 11, 1815, 220, 18, 4559, 123753, 6077, 3128, 4151, 291, 18655, 350, 16, 936, 1815, 220, 18, 16674, 3643, 3128, 4151, 291, 18655, 350, 19, 936, 1815, 220, 18, 5219, 278, 298, 3128, 4151, 291, 18655, 350, 18, 741, 4569, 66938, 316, 5317, 363, 25, 220, 17, 29, 19, 29, 16, 29, 18, 30, 24305, 581, 2059, 220, 17, 1424, 220, 16, 1424, 220, 19, 1424, 220, 18, 13, 21580, 5317, 15248, 30, 41021, 12221, 364, 8093, 355, 25, 220, 16, 29, 17, 29, 19, 29, 18, 350, 23498, 298, 6077, 1424, 101385, 1424, 1625, 3643, 1424, 27054, 298, 8, 4037, 53139, 558, 8093, 418, 25, 220, 17, 29, 16, 29, 19, 29, 18, 350, 76, 53546, 1424, 31200, 6077, 1424, 1625, 3643, 1424, 27054, 298, 8, 1328, 15248, 1039, 30612, 558, 8093, 363, 25, 220, 17, 29, 19, 29, 16, 29, 18, 350, 76, 53546, 1424, 1625, 3643, 1424, 31200, 6077, 1424, 27054, 298, 8, 4037, 53139, 558, 8093, 415, 25, 220, 17, 29, 16, 29, 18, 29, 19, 350, 76, 53546, 1424, 31200, 6077, 1424, 27054, 298, 1424, 1625, 3643, 8, 4037, 53139, 364, 84787, 6052, 418, 364, 4383, 21568, 25, 17484, 31200, 6077, 14732, 1330, 472, 23629, 53232, 164194, 27203, 30, 42464, 14732, 20644, 82, 4493, 1127, 40, 11, 889, 1217, 1340, 47612, 15135, 30, 5551, 15001, 316, 10049, 46, 30, 10049, 46, 382, 1127, 40, 350, 427, 1127, 44, 889, 14732, 38566, 741, 179863, 57093, 1127, 40, 13, 2632, 2569, 101385, 1424, 31200, 6077, 1424, 1625, 3643, 1424, 27054, 298, 120152, 364, 84787, 6052, 382, 418, 13], "n_tokens": 1546, "words": 907, "band_lo": 835.5, "band_hi": 3342.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse the problem. There is meta-substituted benzoic acid reacts with a mild base (the base gives a brown precipitate with Nessler's reagent) to form a salt which on heating produces compound B. So what base is that? Nessler's reagent is used to detect ammonia (NH3); brown precipitate indicates presence of NH3 (or maybe ammonium). The base that gives brown precipitate with Nessler's reagent is likely ammonium hydroxide (NH4OH) i.e., aqueous ammonia. So base is NH4OH (weak base). Reaction of benzoic acid with ammonia forms ammonium benzoate salt. Heating ammonium benzoate gives benzoyl chloride? Actually heating ammonium salts of carboxylic acids gives the corresponding amide? Let's recall: Heating ammonium salts of carboxylic acids yields amides (via loss of water). Example: ammonium acetate upon heating gives acetamide. So ammonium benzoate upon heating yields benzamide. So compound B is the amide derived from the meta-substituted benzoic acid: meta-substituted benzamide. So B is meta-substituted benzamide.\n\nNow B combined with compound C and a base gives compound D. Compound C is one of the two elements in periodic table that is a liquid at room temperature. Those are mercury (Hg) and bromine (Br). So C is either mercury or bromine. Which one would react with benzamide + base to give something? Likely bromine (Br2) as a halogen can undergo Sandmeyer-type? Hmm. Reaction of amide with bromine in presence of base? Another possibility is that C is mercury? Mercury (Hg) forms Hg(II) salts? Not typical.\n\nThink of the classic reaction: Formation of isoquinoline? No.\n\nMaybe B is an aniline? Wait, amide not aniline. However, if B is meta-substituted benzoic acid after converting to amide, reacting with Br2 and base can give 2,4,6-tribromo derivative? Not.\n\nAlternatively, could B be the amide which on reaction with bromine in presence of base yields nitrile? No.\n\nLet's consider other base: The base is \"mild base\" that gives brown precipitate with Nessler's reagent, that is NH4OH, giving ammonium hydroxide, which precipitates brown if ammonia is present. So the salt is ammonium salt of benzoic acid (ammonium benzoate). On heating, forms an amide (benzamide). So B is meta-substituted benzamide.\n\nNow B + C + base → D. Maybe C is mercury (Hg). Mercury oxide (HgO) in base? Not.\n\nBut think of the Hoffmann rearrangement? That's conversion of amide to amine using bromine and base (NaOH). Indeed, the Hofmann (or Hofmann bromamide) rearrangement: primary amide reacts with bromine (Br2) and base (NaOH) to give a primary amine with one fewer carbon (decarboxylation). Wait, the Hofmann rearrangement of amide with Br2 and NaOH yields an amine (R-NH2) with loss of CO2. Yes, that's classic: R-CO-NH2 + Br2 + 4 NaOH → R-NH2 + Na2CO3 + 2 NaBr + 2 H2O.\n\nSo C is bromine (Br2). Good. So B (meta-substituted benzamide) + Br2 + base gives D, which is meta-substituted aniline (aminobenzene). The Hofmann rearrangement reduces carboxamide to amine (removes carbonyl). So D is meta-substituted aniline.\n\nNow they ask: Arrange the following acids in order of decreasing reactivity for the conversion of compound B to compound D: i.e., which meta-substituted benzoic acids (starting acids) when turned into amides (B) and then undergo Hofmann rearrangement to amine (D) have varying rates. The substituents affect reactivity of amide in Hofmann rearrangement. The reaction likely proceeds via formation of N-bromoamide then rearrangement; electron-withdrawing groups could affect the acidity/hydrogen abstraction? Let's think.\n\nThe rate-limiting step possibly is the formation of N-bromoamide (by reaction of amide with Br2 in base). That step involves deprotonation of amide nitrogen (by OH-) to form anionic amide which then attacks Br2. Electron-withdrawing substituents on aromatic ring will withdraw electron density from the amide, making N less basic, thus deprotonation slower? However the acidity of the amide proton may be increased (more acidic) due to electron-withdrawing group, making deprotonation easier. But the nucleophilicity of the nitrogen to attack Br2 may be decreased. The overall rate effect likely correlates with electron-donating groups increasing rate (more nucleophilic amide nitrogen). So ranking: electron-donating substituent (methyl) > electron-neutral (hydroxy?) Actually hydroxy is ortho/para donor via resonance but meta position is not resonance donation, but there's inductive effect (–I) which is electron-withdrawing. So meta-hydroxy is actually electron-withdrawing (slightly). Formyl (CHO) is electron-withdrawing via resonance (though meta reduces resonance). Nitro is strongly electron-withdrawing. So among substituents meta: methyl (donating via hyperconjugation, weak +I) ; hydroxy (meta –I) decreasing electron density; formyl (meta –I, maybe –M less effective) ; nitro (strong –I, –M). So reactivity decreasing order: methyl > hydroxy > formyl > nitro.\n\nThus the order of acids (the original benzoic acid) which after conversion to amide will follow same order for Hofmann rearrangement. So ranking decreasing reactivity: 3-methylbenzoic acid (2) highest, then 3-hydroxybenzoic acid (1), then 3-formylbenzoic acid (4), then 3-nitrobenzoic acid (3). That corresponds to option C: 2>4>1>3? Wait we said 2 > 1 > 4 > 3. Which option matches? Let's compare.\n\nOption A: 1>2>4>3 (hydroxy > methyl > formyl > nitro) Not ours.\nOption B: 2>1>4>3 (methyl > hydroxy > formyl > nitro) This matches our ranking.\nOption C: 2>4>1>3 (methyl > formyl > hydroxy > nitro) Not ours.\nOption D: 2>1>3>4 (methyl > hydroxy > nitro > formyl) Not ours.\n\nThus answer B.\n\nCheck possibility: Does hydroxy meta act as electron-withdrawing strongly? OH meta withdraws via –I, but also may hydrogen bond? However relative to CHO? CHO is –I (and –M but meta reduces). Nitro strongest –I. So order methyl > hydroxy > formyl > nitro plausible.\n\nThus answer is B.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -8.344646857949556e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999855, "logp_correct": -1.447940194175071e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000144, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.561602697033947, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1902724736240273, "frac_below_-5": 0.007175472928897586, "digit_lp_min": -3.8958287239074707, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 1533, "lp_end": -7.176141662057489e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -2.8720282982011747, "p_nocot": 0.056584040799753633, "uplift": 2.87202829820116, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.056584, "B": 0.056584, "C": 0.885123, "D": 0.001709}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recZbxrocrxh9YENH", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Calculate the enthalpy of formation of (CH3)2C=CH-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH=C(CH3)2. Given,\nEnthalpy of atomization of carbon = 1000 kJ/mol.\nBond energy of H-H = 100 kJ/mol\nBond energy of C-C = 200 kJ/mol\nBond energy of C=C = 300 kJ/mol\nBond energy of C-H = 400 kJ/mol\n\nA) 1900 kJ/g\nB) 67.4 kJ/mol\nC) 11.44 kJ/g\nD) 11200 kJ/mol\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Calculate the enthalpy of formation of (CH3)2C=CH-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH=C(CH3)2. Given,\nEnthalpy of atomization of carbon = 1000 kJ/mol.\nBond energy of H-H = 100 kJ/mol\nBond energy of C-C = 200 kJ/mol\nBond energy of C=C = 300 kJ/mol\nBond energy of C-H = 400 kJ/mol\n\nA) 1900 kJ/g\nB) 67.4 kJ/mol\nC) 11.44 kJ/g\nD) 11200 kJ/mol\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recZbxrocrxh9YENH:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 1121, 5512, 5823, 328, 18704, 328, 4335, 19448, 73005, 13, 138743, 1023, 1682, 2360, 15135, 70824, 316, 717, 1121, 5512, 5823, 328, 18704, 591, 8632, 306, 7024, 2608, 350, 34, 1858, 11, 127765, 936, 487, 17, 7791, 8784, 623, 4335, 15135, 70824, 553, 34376, 2860, 70824, 30, 41021, 11310, 364, 1568, 58428, 5823, 328, 18704, 382, 26143, 39, 1337, 6793, 314, 21494, 26143, 39, 1337, 6793, 7, 141761, 8, 533, 21494, 15135, 70824, 30, 44130, 26143, 39, 1337, 20680, 591, 8632, 13, 7649, 34376, 2860, 25, 2130, 58428, 5823, 328, 34376, 2860, 328, 15883, 314, 220, 1353, 15, 372, 41, 86398, 350, 34, 8785, 316, 363, 7791, 8, 63489, 741, 487, 13091, 15135, 5954, 314, 220, 1353, 372, 41, 86398, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 487, 17, 2747, 220, 17, 39, 741, 2632, 18704, 1121, 5512, 5823, 591, 8632, 25, 623, 21416, 25, 8632, 350, 34, 8785, 11, 487, 17, 7322, 8, 871, 73005, 13, 17511, 343