46, 11, 939, 514, 220, 2548, 13, 2109, 625, 197465, 364, 58369, 23864, 2360, 162134, 25, 55688, 690, 220, 17, 60074, 555, 270, 690, 220, 15, 13, 43890, 11, 4842, 220, 3519, 70, 871, 220, 4635, 13, 24, 284, 43, 13, 4820, 514, 26157, 3643, 19980, 1217, 220, 15, 13, 43890, 871, 220, 3519, 13, 20, 14, 15, 13, 43890, 314, 220, 4621, 13, 20, 284, 43, 13, 2632, 2973, 6574, 4621, 284, 43, 13, 22483, 625, 364, 34013, 1023, 1199, 162134, 25, 16262, 690, 314, 220, 15, 13, 2241, 329, 5712, 43, 11, 939, 514, 314, 220, 15, 13, 7189, 329, 5712, 43, 13, 7801, 38028, 25, 16262, 690, 220, 3519, 14, 15, 13, 2241, 28, 1910, 284, 43, 11, 939, 514, 220, 3519, 13, 20, 14, 15, 13, 7189, 28, 5007, 13, 19, 284, 43, 13, 17796, 355, 16262, 690, 220, 1434, 13, 3898, 350, 30330, 741, 17796, 418, 16262, 690, 220, 5007, 13, 1910, 364, 84787, 625, 3239, 364, 58369, 23864, 2360, 162134, 591, 3880, 25, 1843, 581, 19429, 290, 6145, 6052, 382, 1919, 2973, 38028, 553, 10740, 1572, 220, 2548, 326, 3760, 1572, 220, 8876, 11, 326, 2569, 10112, 16262, 690, 1424, 939, 514, 30, 4037, 23517, 364, 34405, 5317, 15248, 290, 25702, 57556, 30, 41021, 23864, 4842, 591, 10945, 2360, 19271, 162134, 25, 10112, 1023, 2061, 27591, 328, 16262, 690, 314, 220, 15, 13, 10545, 329, 5712, 43, 350, 68, 1940, 4213, 68232, 178229, 690, 741, 7801, 10945, 314, 220, 3519, 14, 15, 13, 10545, 28, 6231, 284, 43, 26, 939, 514, 27591, 220, 15, 13, 7189, 871, 220, 3519, 13, 20, 14, 15, 13, 7189, 28, 5007, 284, 43, 13, 4569, 15248, 5317, 355, 350, 1434, 13, 3898, 11, 5007, 13, 3062, 741, 2632, 10112, 1023, 2061, 16262, 690, 27591, 220, 15, 13, 46519, 13, 4569, 7890, 120152, 538, 16262, 690, 382, 110666, 350, 86583, 178229, 690, 8, 1118, 853, 220, 20, 10049, 17, 30, 6986, 292, 178229, 690, 853, 21937, 3643, 12796, 625, 10049, 17, 8896, 13, 2632, 625, 364, 39520, 1039, 11965, 106545, 11, 290, 1888, 27524, 326, 57556, 10175, 220, 3519, 329, 2454, 13, 623, 6052, 3880, 6960, 24923, 2360, 162134, 25, 16262, 690, 27591, 220, 15, 13, 4129, 11, 939, 514, 27591, 220, 15, 13, 7189, 15286, 316, 38028, 6574, 1434, 13, 21, 827, 220, 5007, 13, 16, 11, 1118, 382, 5317, 355, 13, 17796, 355, 7890, 120152, 364, 84787, 6052, 6960, 355, 13], "n_tokens": 4551, "words": 2234, "band_lo": 896.25, "band_hi": 3585.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the problem. It asks: a chemist wants to synthesize a Schiff base using a ketone and a primary amine. The desired Schiff base contains 10 methylene groups (5 from amine and 5 from ketone), 1 methine group (belongs to amine), and the IHD value of 3. Reaction yield is 57%. Need to produce 50 grams of the Schiff base, what quantities of reactants should be employed? Options give volumes in mL for each reactant.\n\nWe need to compute needed amounts based on molecular weight, stoichiometry, yield.\n\nFirst, figure out the structures: Schiff base is formed by condensation of a ketone and a primary amine, removing water, forming an imine (C=N). General formula: R-CO-CH2... + R'-NH2 -> R-CH=N-R' + H2O.\n\nBut we need specifics: The Schiff base contains 10 methylene groups (CH2) total, split equally between amine and ketone (5 each). And one methine group (CH) belonging to amine (maybe attached to the N?). IHD (index of hydrogen deficiency) value 3.\n\nWe need to deduce the molecular formula of the product, then its molar mass, then calculate moles needed for 50g at 57% yield: need more theoretical moles to account for yield.\n\nThen we need to know densities of reactants to convert moles to volume (mL). The answer options give volumes around 45-61 mL, so densities likely around 0.8-1.0 g/mL.\n\nWe need to guess which ketone and amine are being used. Probably simple: hexanone? Wait: 5 methylene groups from ketone suggests a chain of 5 CH2 attached to carbonyl? The ketone might be 5-methylene? Let's think: Ketone part provides a carbonyl carbon (C=O) and perhaps the rest of chain. The amine provides the other side.\n\nThe product contains 10 CH2 groups (5 from each). Also 1 CH (methine) from amine. Likely the amine is something like isobutyl? Hmm.\n\nLet's determine possible building blocks: Let the ketone be a straight chain with 5 CH2 groups and a carbonyl. Example: 1-pentanone (CH3-CH2-CH2-CH2-CO-CH3) actually has 4 CH2s? Wait: 2-pentanone is CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CH3 gives 2 CH3 and 2 CH2. Not 5 CH2. Maybe the ketone is a longer chain.\n\nIf ketone provides 5 CH2: That means the ketone chain contains 5 CH2 groups, plus carbonyl carbon, plus maybe some substituents. For example, 6-carbon ketone where carbonyl at one end? That would be a ketone like hexanone with carbonyl internal but still have CH2 groups.\n\nLet's suppose the ketone is 5-hexanone? Actually 2-hexanone: CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH3. That has 4 CH2 not 5.\n\nConsider 7 carbon ketone: 2-heptanone: CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH3. That has 5 CH2 groups (positions 3-6). Yes 2-heptanone has 5 CH2 groups. So ketone likely is 2-heptanone (C7 H14 O). It has 5 CH2 groups + 2 CH3 groups? Actually 2-heptanone: CH3-CH2-CH2-CH2-CO-CH2-CH3? Wait the carbonyl is at C2: CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH3. Then CH2 groups: positions 3,4,5,6 (four), plus maybe one more? Let's count: structure: carbon numbers: 1 CH3, 2 carbonyl carbon (C=O), 3 CH2, 4 CH2, 5 CH2, 6 CH2, 7 CH3. So there are 4 CH2 groups, not 5. So maybe 3-heptanone? That would be CH3-CH2-CO-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH3 -> CH2 groups: 2,4,5,6 = 4 again.\n\nThus need a ketone with 5 CH2 groups: maybe 3-octanone? Actually 4-octanone: CH3-CH2-CH2-CO-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH3? That's still 4 CH2 groups.\n\nBetter to think of ketone as a linear chain where carbonyl carbon counts as a C but not CH2. The total number of carbons contributed by ketone chain maybe = 5 CH2 + 1 carbonyl carbon + maybe 1 substituent (maybe CH3). That would be 5 CH2 + carbonyl + maybe a CH3 on other side: total 7 carbons.\n\nSame for amine: provides 5 CH2 and 1 CH (methine). Primary amine maybe something like (CH3)2CH-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH2-NH2? The methine likely attached to the N as part of a secondary carbon (like isopropyl). Could be 2-aminopentane? Actually primary amine with a methine indicates the carbon attached to the NH2 is a secondary carbon (i.e., CH). That would be a branched amine like isobutylamine? Eg. (CH3)2CH-CH2-NH2: the carbon attached to NH2 is CH2 (primary). So not.\n\nMaybe they refer to the carbon bearing the N after condensation becomes a CH (the imine carbon). That methine belongs to amine side after condensation. Indeed in Schiff base, the carbon from the amine carbon (the carbon that had the amine) becomes a carbon attached to N after dehydration: Actually the amine contributes the nitrogen and its carbon attached to N becomes the carbon of the imine? Wait mechanism: primary amine attacks carbonyl carbon, then eliminates water; the C that becomes C=N is the carbonyl carbon, not the amine carbon. The amine nitrogen attaches to carbonyl carbon. So the amine side provides just the nitrogen and maybe some substituents attached to N, not a carbon.\n\nThus the description is confusing.\n\nPerhaps it's a known exam problem: sum of carbon atoms from each reactant? The product has 10 CH2 groups: 5 from each reactant. So each reactant must contain 5 CH2 groups. The amine also has a CH (methine). So the amine component has 5 CH2 + 1 CH + NH2? So its formula: C7? Because 5 CH2 = 5*CH2 = C5H10, plus CH = CH = C1H1, total C6H11? plus NH2 (NH2 adds N and H2). That would be C6H13N.\n\nKetone component: 5 CH2 plus carbonyl carbon (C=O) plus maybe something else? Likely a methyl group attached on other side? If the ketone is symmetrical about carbonyl? Not necessarily. The product will have the carbonyl carbon now part of imine, losing O as water? Actually water lost includes the carbonyl oxygen and two hydrogens (one from amine). So the ketone contributes a carbonyl carbon which becomes part of C=N in product.\n\nThus the product's carbon count = carbons from both reactants (minus none). So total carbons = (ketone carbons) + (amine carbons). Ketone has 5 CH2 (5 C) + carbonyl carbon (1) + maybe other substituent like methyl? Not stated. But they said 5 CH2 belong to ketone, implies no additional CH2 besides those. Could have additional CH3 but they'd count as methylene? No.\n\nProbably the ketone is 6-carbon: CH3-CO-(CH2)5? That would have 5 CH2 plus a CH3 group (not counted). But then they'd have 5 CH2 indeed.\n\nSimilarly amine: maybe 6-carbon: (CH3)2CH-(CH2)5-NH2? That would have 5 CH2 plus a CH (the methine). Plus maybe CH3 groups? The methine is attached to two CH3 groups? Could be isopropyl attached to chain. So amine formula maybe C7H15N (two CH3 + CH + 5 CH2). That gives 2*CH3 = 2*C1H3 = C2H6 plus CH (C1H1) plus 5 CH2 = C5H10 => total C8? Let's count: C2+1+5=8 C. So amine C8? That's larger.\n\nBut we need actual volumes answer; we need densities. The options show opposite volumes: if ketone volume > amine volume for some options, etc. Which pair yields matched molar amounts? Let's compute theoretical masses given densities typical: For ketone maybe density 0.81 g/mL (e.g., 2-heptanone, density ~0.81). For primary amine maybe density ~0.86 g/mL (e.g., hexylamine). Let's approximate.\n\nLet's guess the ketone is 2-heptanone (C7H14O, MW 114.18 g/mol, density 0.81 g/mL). The amine maybe 6-aminohexane? Actually hexylamine (1-hexylamine) C6H15N, MW 101.19 g/mol, density 0.81 g/mL too? maybe 0.81.\n\nNow product would have C13? Wait combine C7 + C6 = C13, plus O removed as water (O from ketone, 2 H from amine). So product formula C13H? plus N. But we need 10 CH2 (20 H) + 1 CH (1 H) = 21 H from hydrocarbons plus maybe additional hydrogens on N? The imine C=N has no H on N. So total hydrogens maybe 21 +? plus maybe one hydrogen on carbon of imine? Actually the carbonyl carbon becomes C attached to N, no H there. So the product's hydrogen count equals the sum of hydrogens from both reactants minus water (2 H). So sum H from ketone + amine -2 = product H.\n\nIf ketone C7H14O: H14. Amine C6H15N: H15. Sum = 29. Minus 2 = 27 H. But product expected CH2 (10*2 =20) + CH (1*1 =1) =21 H plus maybe other substituents? There's also maybe 6 more H on other carbons like methyl groups etc. So 27 H is plausible.\n\nThus product formula C13H27N? Wait nitrogen present count: 1 N. So C13H27N? plus maybe O? No O removed. So IHD: For C13H27N, IHD = (2C+2+N-H)/2 = (26+2+1-27)/2 = (29-27)/2 =1. Yet given IHD =3, so product has more unsaturation: imine counts as one IHD (C=N), aromatic? Maybe includes carbonyl? But no carbonyl left. So there are 2 additional rings or double bonds... maybe product includes aromatic ring? Not consistent.\n\nThus maybe the product includes a benzene ring (IHD 4). Hmm.\n\nGiven IHD =3 includes imine (=1) plus maybe a double bond? Or two rings? Could be a cyclic imine? Not enough info.\n\nMaybe the problem is purely stoichiometric: 5 CH2 from each reactant => each reactant has 5 CH2 groups. So each reactant has 5 CH2 = 5*14 =70 g per mole of CH2 (CH2 weight 14); plus additional groups: amine also has one CH (14? Actually CH is 13). Ketone has carbonyl carbon and oxygen (C=O weight 28). So compute molar masses of each reactant.\n\nLet's assume ketone: 5 CH2 (C5H10) + carbonyl (C=O) i.e., CO (C1O1) => total C6H10O? Wait there is also maybe one leftover H on carbonyl carbon? Carbonyl carbon has no H. So ketone composition: C6 H10 O (plus maybe something else like CH3? not counted). Its molar mass: C6 (12*6=72) + H10 (10) + O16 = 98 g/mol. Density unknown.\n\nAmine: 5 CH2 (C5H10) + 1 CH (CH) = C1H1 => total C6H11 + NH2 => N1H2 => overall C6H13N? Wait add N and extra H2: H total = 11+2=13. So C6H13N. Molar mass: C6=72, H13=13, N=14 => 99 g/mol.\n\nThus both reactants ~100 g/mol. Volumes given ~50 mL, densities ~2 g/mL? Not; maybe densities ~0.8 g/mL so 50 mL corresponds to ~40 g. That's about 0.4 mol. So product 50 g with MW maybe ~200? That would be about 0.25 mol. So need 0.25/0.57=0.44 mol theoretical. Multiply by 100 g/mol =44 g each. In volume ~44 g/0.8 =55 mL. So volumes around 50-60 mL. So plausible.\n\nThus need to compute exactly: product molecular weight? Need to determine product composition.\n\nProduct formed from ketone (C6H10O) + amine (C6H13N) - H2O (18) = C12H23N? Wait sum: C6+6=12; H10+13=23; O+N -> O1 N1. Subtract H2O removes 2 H and O. So product = C12 H21 N? Let's compute: Starting O from ketone=1, subtract O from water =0, so product no O. Hydrogen: 10+13=23 minus 2 =21. So C12H21N. MW: C12=144, H21=21, N=14 => 179 g/mol.\n\nBut also product includes the imine (C=N) which is counted. IHD for C12H21N: formula IHD = (2*C+2+N - H)/2 = (24+2+1-21)/2 = (27-21)/2 =6/2=3. Good! So matches IHD=3. Great! So we have correct composition.\n\nThus product MW = 179 g/mol.\n\nTarget mass = 50 g => moles needed theoretical = 50 / 179 = 0.2793 mol actual needed product. Since yield 57%, need to start with more theoretical product amount: required moles of reactants = desired moles / 0.57 = 0.2793/0.57 = 0.4896 mol. Assuming 1:1 stoichiometry, need 0.4896 mol of each reactant.\n\nNow compute mass of each reactant required (theoretical). Ketone MW 98 g/mol. Mass = 0.4896 * 98 = 48.0 g. Amine MW 99 g/mol. Mass = 0.4896*99 = 48.5 g.\n\nNow need to convert mass to volume based on densities. Densities not given but can infer from answer options. Option A: Ketone 50.65 mL, Amine 56.08 mL. That implies density of ketone = mass/volume = 48.0 g / 50.65 mL = 0.948 g/mL. Amine density = 48.5 g / 56.08 mL = 0.866 g/mL.\n\nOption B: Ketone 56.60 mL -> density 48.0/56.6 =0.848, amine 50.13 mL -> density 48.5/50.13=0.968.\n\nOption C: Ketone 45.68 => 48/45.68=1.05, amine 61.05 => 48.5/61.05=0.795.\n\nOption D: Ketone 61.47 => 48/61.47=0.781, amine 45.26 => 48.5/45.26=1.07.\n\nWhich densities are realistic? Typical ketone density around 0.8-0.9 g/mL. Primary amine densities similarly ~0.8-0.9 for aliphatic amines (e.g., hexylamine density 0.816 g/mL). So both should be around 0.8-0.9. Option A gives ketone density ~0.95 (high), amine ~0.87 (reasonable). Option B gives ketone 0.85 (good), amine 0.97 (high). Option C gives ketone density 1.05 (unlikely >1), amine 0.795 (good). Option D gives ketone 0.78 (good), amine 1.07 (unlikely >1). So the more realistic combination would be both ~0.8-0.9. Options with both near that: Option B ketone 0.85, amine 0.97 - amine a little high but maybe if amine is more dense like p-toluidine? 1.3? but not.\n\nOption A ketone 0.95 high but possible if ketone aromatic like acetophenone density 1.03. But our ketone is aliphatic, so 0.95 may be high.\n\nOption D ketone 0.78 (good), amine 1.07 (unlikely for simple aliphatic). Option C ketone 1.05 (unlikely), amine 0.795 (good). So the best plausible is option B (both around 0.85-0.97). Considering typical primary amine density: e.g., n-hexylamine density 0.822 g/mL. So 0.97 seems too high. But maybe amine is more branched leading to higher density like 1-phenylpropylamine? Not.\n\nGiven limited info, perhaps the exam expects to use known densities: For 2-heptanone density = 0.815 g/mL. For hexylamine density = 0.815 g/mL as well. Then volume = mass/density -> 48g/0.815 = 58.9 mL each. None of the options exactly but option D ketone 61.47, amine 45.26 not symmetrical.\n\nLet's compute using densities: Ketone 2-heptanone 0.815, mass 48g => 58.9 mL. Amine hexylamine also 0.815 => 48.5/0.815 = 59.5 mL. So both ~59 mL. Options not.\n\nMaybe they use densities: ketone = 0.80 g/mL, amine = 0.86 g/mL. Then volumes: ketone 48/0.80=60 mL, amine 48.5/0.86=56.4 mL. Option A ketone 50.65 (lower). Option B ketone 56.60.\n\nThus not sure.\n\nLet's compute using densities from options: If we assume the correct answer is where both volumes are greater than 45 and less than 63, and order maybe ketone > amine? Not necessarily.\n\nWhich option matches the calculated masses? Let's compute mass from volume using typical densities: maybe they used density of ketone = 0.94 g/mL (e.g., acetophenone). Then volume = 48/0.94=51 mL; amine density 0.86 => 48.5/0.86=56 mL. That matches option A (50.65,56.08). So maybe they used ketone density 0.945. That seems plausible if ketone is aromatic (acetophenone) which has 5 CH2? Acetophenone has phenyl ring not CH2 groups. So not.\n\nGiven our earlier deduction, the product composition and masses gave 48 g each. The answer options likely derived using densities: ketone density 0.95, amine density 0.86 leads to volumes ~50.6 & 56.1, which is option A. Option A seems plausible.\n\nThus answer likely A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.31130149583214e-06, "p_correct": 0.999999999998469, "logp_correct": -1.5309728825131655e-12, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807922557, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5834243037793512, "cot_lp_min64": -1.5661914966963195, "frac_below_-5": 0.011458792419568091, "digit_lp_min": -6.79127836227417, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.40602609515190125, "n_cot_tokens": 4538, "lp_end": -0.00010001159535022452, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.576278118089249e-07, "logp_nocot": -2.8241495223100124, "p_nocot": 0.05935911897431785, "uplift": 2.8241495223084816, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.059359, "B": 0.723142, "C": 0.010315, "D": 0.207184}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recatj4YXzbOaq3Ar", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Consider a system with Hamiltonian operator $H = \\varepsilon \\vec{\\sigma}.\\vec{n}$. Here, $\\vec{n}$ is an arbitrary unit vector, $\\varepsilon $ is a constant of dimension energy, and components of $\\vec{\\sigma}$ are the Pauli spin matrices. What are the eigenvalues of the Hamiltonian operator?\n\nA) +\\hbar/2, -\\hbar/2\nB) +\\varepsilon \\hbar/2, - \\varepsilon \\hbar/2\nC) + \\varepsilon, -\\varepsilon\nD) +1, -1\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Consider a system with Hamiltonian operator $H = \\varepsilon \\vec{\\sigma}.\\vec{n}$. Here, $\\vec{n}$ is an arbitrary unit vector, $\\varepsilon $ is a constant of dimension energy, and components of $\\vec{\\sigma}$ are the Pauli spin matrices. What are the eigenvalues of the Hamiltonian operator?\n\nA) +\\hbar/2, -\\hbar/2\nB) +\\varepsilon \\hbar/2, - \\varepsilon \\hbar/2\nC) + \\varepsilon, -\\varepsilon\nD) +1, -1\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recatj4YXzbOaq3Ar:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 10561, 7222, 328, 487, 314, 4278, 425, 3669, 5366, 77, 13, 6041, 6824, 75935, 25, 3669, 5366, 77, 853, 10561, 7222, 58882, 16, 350, 21910, 3669, 5366, 77, 382, 1299, 17464, 11019, 4251, 297, 9262, 483, 10561, 7222, 58882, 16, 741, 50557, 17464, 11019, 336, 314, 350, 5762, 14, 17, 8, 3669, 13, 2632, 3669, 5366, 77, 10561, 7222, 553, 58882, 16, 13, 137658, 656, 4278, 8967, 10561, 7222, 58882, 4278, 13, 2632, 6052, 363, 13, 3072, 25120, 25, 1236, 1340, 2411, 3931, 41051, 14, 17, 12332, 13, 487, 382, 9113, 472, 4278, 3669, 5366, 77, 11, 860, 41051, 13, 2632, 10561, 7222, 553, 58882, 4278, 13, 17796, 363, 15248, 13, 2632, 6052, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 363, 13], "n_tokens": 134, "words": 60, "band_lo": 26.0, "band_hi": 104.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need eigenvalues of H = ε * σ·n. Pauli matrices: σ·n has eigenvalues ±1 (since σ·n is like spin operator along n direction with eigenvalues ±1). Actually spin operator S = (ħ/2) σ. So σ·n eigenvalues are ±1. Multiply by ε gives eigenvalues ± ε. So answer C. But careful: some may think include ħ/2 factor. H is defined as ε σ·n, no ħ. So eigenvalues are ± ε. Option C matches. So answer: ANSWER: C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.7762033110102493e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999879, "logp_correct": -1.2070004352005177e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999996423722642, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.44051439719382224, "cot_lp_min64": -0.5349348934261043, "frac_below_-5": 0.0, "digit_lp_min": -2.169585604860913e-05, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.054127324372529984, "n_cot_tokens": 121, "lp_end": -1.6689286894688848e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -0.20141350174363465, "p_nocot": 0.817574293252478, "uplift": 0.20141350174362257, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.182425, "C": 0.817574, "D": 0.0}}, "force_keep": false, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recb2M22zaD3tL6Qc", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: You have prepared an unknown compound. To identify the product, you have used the following characterisation techniques: FTIR and 1H NMR. The FTIR spectrum shows a very broad absorption peak at 3000 wavenumbers. A strong absorption peak is also observed at 1700 wavenumbers. Several peaks were observed in the 1H NMR spectrum, none of which are correspond to vinyl-hydrogens. One of the signals in the 1H NMR is a doublet of triplets of quartets whilst a different signal is a doublet of triplets of triplets. Identify the compound as either, CH3CH2C(H)(CH3)C(H)(CH3)COOH, CH3CH2C(H)(C2H5)C(H)(C2H5)COOH, CH3C(H)(CH3)C(H)(CH3)CH2COOH, or CH3C(H)(C2H5)C(H)(C2H5)CH2COOH\n\nA) CH3C(H)(CH3)C(H)(CH3)CH2COOH\nB) CH3C(H)(C2H5)C(H)(C2H5)CH2COOH\nC) CH3CH2C(H)(C2H5)C(H)(C2H5)COOH\nD) CH3CH2C(H)(CH3)C(H)(CH3)COOH\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: You have prepared an unknown compound. To identify the product, you have used the following characterisation techniques: FTIR and 1H NMR. The FTIR spectrum shows a very broad absorption peak at 3000 wavenumbers. A strong absorption peak is also observed at 1700 wavenumbers. Several peaks were observed in the 1H NMR spectrum, none of which are correspond to vinyl-hydrogens. One of the signals in the 1H NMR is a doublet of triplets of quartets whilst a different signal is a doublet of triplets of triplets. Identify the compound as either, CH3CH2C(H)(CH3)C(H)(CH3)COOH, CH3CH2C(H)(C2H5)C(H)(C2H5)COOH, CH3C(H)(CH3)C(H)(CH3)CH2COOH, or CH3C(H)(C2H5)C(H)(C2H5)CH2COOH\n\nA) CH3C(H)(CH3)C(H)(CH3)CH2COOH\nB) CH3C(H)(C2H5)C(H)(C2H5)CH2COOH\nC) CH3CH2C(H)(C2H5)C(H)(C2H5)COOH\nD) CH3CH2C(H)(CH3)C(H)(CH3)COOH\n", "true_answer": "B", "or