is CH3-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH(CH3)-COOH. So chain: ethyl group then two chiral centers then acid. This is 3,4-dimethylpentanoic acid? Let's count: carbonyl carbon is #1 (COOH). Next carbon (alpha) is CH (attached to CH(CH3) and CH3?), actually structure: COOH attached to CH (the second C(H)(CH3)). Wait orientation: COOH is at end, attached to a carbon that is C(H)(CH3). That carbon also attached to previous carbon which is C(H)(CH3). That carbon attached to CH2CH3 side chain? Let's delineate:\n\nSequence:\n\n- Carbon 1: carbonyl carbon (C=O).\n- Carbon 2: C(H)(CH3) (alpha carbon) attached to carbonyl and to carbon 3 and CH3.\n- Carbon 3: C(H)(CH3) attached to carbon 2, CH3, and CH2CH3? Actually the leftmost group is CH3CH2 attached to carbon 3. So carbon 3 has substituents: CH3, CH2CH3, H, and bond to carbon 2.\n\nThus the total skeleton: 4 carbon chain (including acid carbon). Base chain maybe but anyway.\n\nNow Option B: CH3C(H)(C2H5)C(H)(C2H5)CH2COOH\n\nMol: CH3-CH(C2H5)-CH(C2H5)-CH2-COOH. So two ethyl substituents on the two middle carbons.\n\nOption C: CH3CH2C(H)(C2H5)C(H)(C2H5)COOH\n\nMol: CH3CH2-CH(C2H5)-CH(C2H5)-COOH. So acid carbon attached directly to chiral carbon that has ethyl substituents.\n\nWe must use NMR splitting pattern: signals described: doublet of triplets of quartets (dtq) and doublet of triplets of triplets (dtt). This indicates complex coupling to three nonequivalent neighboring protons: e.g., CH attached to three sets of protons: maybe CH attached to CH3 (3 protons) (triplet), CH2 (2 protons) (quartet), and another CH (1 proton) (doublet). The multiplicities combine: the observed pattern dtq suggests coupling to three sets: a doublet from 1 H neighbor, a triplet from 2 H neighbor, a quartet from 3 H neighbor. Similarly dtt: doublet of triplet of triplet (two different 2-H couplings) etc.\n\nThus the molecule must have a CH with neighbors: a CH (one proton) and a CH2 (2 H) and CH3 (3H). The pattern dtq suggests that; dtt suggests CH coupled to two nonequivalent CH2 groups (2 each) and a CH (1). Let's map.\n\nWe need to locate which carbon atoms have that arrangement.\n\nConsider Option D: CH3CH2-CH(CH3)-CH(CH3)-COOH. Let's label carbons:\n\nCα (adjacent to COOH) = carbon bearing COOH and CH (one H) and CH3 substituent and attached to next carbon (CH). So this carbon (call C2) is CH, attached to: COOH (no H), CH attached to CH (C3) (one H), and a methyl group (CH3). That gives coupling to CH (1 H) and to CH3 (3 H). No CH2 neighbor. So splitting would be doublet of quartet (d q) maybe, not triple.\n\nC3 (second CH) attached to CH3, CH2CH3? Wait left side is CH(CH3) attached to CH2CH3 group (CH2CH3). Actually the left group is CH3CH2- attached to C3? The connectivity: CH3CH2-CH(CH3)-CH(CH3)-COOH. So C3 attached to: left side CH2 of ethyl (CH2), CH3 substituent, and the CH (C2). So its neighbor protons are: CH2 (2 H), CH3 (3 H), CH (1 H). That's exactly dtq. Good. So one of the signals (dtq) corresponds to C3.\n\nNow what about the other signal dtt? Which carbon would have coupling to three sets including two CH2 groups? Could be the CH2 of ethyl (CH2 adjacent to CH3). That CH2 is attached to CH3 (3 H) and CH (C3) (1 H). That's coupling to 3 and 1 -> quartet of doublet maybe? Not dtt. Another carbon is the CH of the ethyl (actually CH2CH3 is CH3-CH2). CH2 in ethyl (adjacent to CH3) is CH2 attached to CH3 (3H) and CH (1H). Not dtt.\n\nMaybe the other signal dtt corresponds to the CH adjacent to COOH (C2) which couples to CH (1 H) and CH3 (3 H). That's doublet of quartet again, not dtt.\n\nThus D has one dtq, but not dtt.\n\nLet's examine Option B: CH3-CH(C2H5)-CH(C2H5)-CH2-COOH.\n\nLabel carbon chain: Starting from leftmost CH3 attached to C2 (CH) which also has ethyl substituent. Then C3 has ethyl substituent and attached to CH2-COOH.\n\nC2 (CH) neighbor protons: attached to CH3 (3H), ethyl substituent (CH2CH3) which includes CH2 (2H) as first carbon? Actually ethyl substituent attached via its carbon adjacent to the central carbon (CH2). So C2 is attached to CH3 (3 H), CH2 (2 H) of ethyl substituent, and to C3 (CH) (1 H). That's dtq again.\n\nC3 (CH) neighbor: attached to CH2 of its ethyl (2 H), CH2-COOH (2 H), and C2 (1 H). That's dtt (doublet of triplet of triplet) because couplings to 1H (doublet), 2H (triplet), 2H (another triplet). Yes! So B would exhibit both dtq and dtt signals, matching description.\n\nOption C: CH3CH2-CH(C2H5)-CH(C2H5)-COOH. Here the carbon bearing COOH is CH attached to C2 (CH with ethyl) and another ethyl substituent? Actually COOH attached to C3? Let's map: carbonyl carbon C1, alpha carbon is CH attached to ethyl substituent (C2) and previous carbon? Wait chain: COOH attached to C* (which bears ethyl groups?). The SMILES: CH3CH2C(H)(C2H5)C(H)(C2H5)COOH. Starting left: CH3CH2- C*(H)(C2H5)- C*(H)(C2H5)- COOH. So the carbon bearing COOH (alpha carbon) is the rightmost chiral carbon attached to ethyl group, another carbon (the left chiral carbon) and H plus COOH. That carbon (Cα) has neighbors: left chiral carbon (CH) (1 H) and ethyl substituent (CH2) (2 H). So couplings: doublet (from CH) and triplet (from CH2) => doublet of triplet (dt). Not dtq. The other chiral carbon (Cβ) has neighbors: ethyl substituent (=CH2, 2 H), CH2 (from ethyl leftmost group? Wait left neighbor is CH2CH3 chain). The leftmost ethyl is CH3-CH2 attached to Cβ? Actually the leftmost CH3CH2 is directly attached to Cβ (i.e., it's an ethyl substituent). So Cβ attached to CH2 (2 H) of that ethyl, CH2 of its own ethyl (2 H) and H (1 H). That's dtt. So we have dtt for one, dt for other. Not dtq.\n\nOption A: CH3-CH(CH3)-CH(CH3)-CH2-COOH. Here similar to D but with CH2 at the end vs COOH. Let's analyze: C3 (middle CH) attached to CH3 (3H), CH2 (2H) (from the following CH2-COOH), and CH (C2) (1H). That's dtq. The CH2-COOH carbon neighbor is CH (2 H?). Actually CH2 attached to COOH and CH (C3) and CH3? Wait it's CH2 attached to CH (C3) and COOH (no H). So CH2 coupling only to CH (1 H) (doublet) and maybe not else => doublet. Not dtt. So only dtq.\n\nTherefore only option B gives both signals: one dtq, one dtt. So answer B.\n\nThus ANSWER: B.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.0265558760002023e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999757, "logp_correct": -2.4255298313257748e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000242, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5747697215775952, "cot_lp_min64": -1.0914063704492634, "frac_below_-5": 0.01091617933723197, "digit_lp_min": -6.973382949829102, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.011050336994230747, "n_cot_tokens": 2565, "lp_end": -6.544376083184034e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -5.876845170860179e-05, "logp_nocot": -3.430342259460755, "p_nocot": 0.03237585793273424, "uplift": 3.4303422594607307, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.019637, "B": 0.032376, "C": 0.113003, "D": 0.834984}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recbgfwNuxJkBuisz", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Consider the fission of $^{236}U$ into two equal mass prompt fission fragments and zero free neutrons. What is the value of proton number Z giving the most stable nucleus with the same mass number? What is the energy released by each fragment to reach that stable nucleus by a series of beta decay?\n\nA) Z=50, Energy= 18 MeV\nB) Z=51, Energy= 20 MeV\nC) Z=47, Energy=5 MeV\nD) Z=48, Energy= 16MeV\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Consider the fission of $^{236}U$ into two equal mass prompt fission fragments and zero free neutrons. What is the value of proton number Z giving the most stable nucleus with the same mass number? What is the energy released by each fragment to reach that stable nucleus by a series of beta decay?\n\nA) Z=50, Energy= 18 MeV\nB) Z=51, Energy= 20 MeV\nC) Z=47, Energy=5 MeV\nD) Z=48, Energy= 16MeV\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recbgfwNuxJkBuisz:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 290, 35438, 4792, 25, 454, 2727, 328, 601, 12, 26277, 1511, 1920, 12629, 4842, 15226, 285, 2727, 63899, 326, 12421, 2240, 18541, 50465, 13, 2632, 601, 12, 26277, 76848, 1511, 1920, 63899, 2454, 483, 4842, 2086, 355, 314, 220, 26277, 14, 17, 314, 220, 14642, 13, 2632, 2454, 19018, 382, 355, 28, 14642, 13, 3164, 553, 107935, 65545, 25998, 350, 21910, 8601, 483, 601, 12, 26277, 11, 1118, 853, 1489, 28, 10088, 11, 478, 28, 15363, 741, 68506, 6603, 29293, 25, 2454, 19018, 1481, 679, 1489, 16679, 220, 4217, 350, 21910, 220, 10088, 14, 17, 28, 4217, 936, 326, 478, 171441, 8540, 350, 21910, 220, 15363, 14, 17, 28, 8540, 741, 2632, 4871, 63899, 25, 355, 28, 14642, 11, 1489, 93, 4217, 11, 478, 28, 8540, 13, 2632, 1023, 553, 107935, 65545, 15001, 316, 22105, 103932, 25986, 483, 484, 355, 13, 19792, 316, 1646, 290, 1645, 22105, 117323, 483, 2684, 4842, 2086, 355, 28, 14642, 25, 290, 1489, 1118, 8967, 65414, 17156, 5954, 777, 47081, 263, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 4251, 21306, 27827, 2543, 741, 7649, 23976, 12, 7175, 138477, 4842, 20690, 503, 1631, 869, 22105, 103932, 25986, 25, 2214, 355, 93, 14642, 11, 22105, 103932, 25986, 553, 25, 19571, 12, 14642, 350, 57, 28, 1434, 8, 22105, 11, 1217, 109094, 12, 14642, 350, 57, 28, 3519, 8, 382, 22105, 30, 41021, 30890, 13, 50557, 22105, 103932, 25986, 2846, 4842, 220, 14642, 25, 220, 14642, 24010, 350, 57, 28, 1434, 8, 382, 22105, 26, 220, 14642, 6352, 350, 57, 28, 6283, 8, 382, 625, 22105, 350, 6352, 12, 14642, 382, 103093, 741, 220, 14642, 57548, 350, 57, 28, 3519, 8, 382, 625, 22105, 26, 357, 2411, 220, 14642, 57548, 382, 85238, 350, 13895, 1044, 741, 220, 14642, 135846, 350, 57, 28, 4217, 8, 382, 625, 22105, 13, 2632, 290, 1645, 22105, 483, 355, 28, 14642, 382, 1489, 28, 1434, 350, 99780, 741, 2632, 6052, 6960, 1489, 28, 1434, 13, 7801, 290, 5954, 9917, 656, 2454, 19018, 316, 7627, 484, 22105, 117323, 656, 261, 5594, 328, 21306, 57549, 25, 623, 4871, 19018, 1458, 1489, 93, 4217, 26, 316, 717, 316, 1489, 28, 1434, 11, 1309, 316, 7064, 1489, 656, 220, 19, 4493, 21306, 180136, 57549, 350, 611, 141437, 2747, 120589, 659, 23629, 659, 4624, 514, 64165, 2081, 741, 11555, 21306, 57549, 30979, 1236, 5954, 12629, 316, 290, 4842, 9809, 2870, 10005, 326, 13857, 117323, 350, 26167, 121749, 5954, 328, 21306, 326, 4624, 514, 64165, 2081, 741, 111766, 19271, 1486, 159509, 2569, 220, 17, 12, 19, 2896, 53, 777, 21306, 13, 2214, 220, 19, 1885, 1044, 11, 3609, 10112, 6574, 220, 23, 12, 899, 2896, 53, 13, 3072, 290, 6052, 3880, 25, 2214, 1489, 28, 1434, 5954, 28, 1157, 2896, 53, 350, 3941, 355, 741, 4569, 1481, 413, 1078, 220, 19, 13, 20, 2896, 53, 777, 21306, 11, 120152, 395, 12914, 165125, 13, 2632, 6960, 6145, 6052, 25, 355, 364, 58369, 14866, 538, 1062, 1273, 1489, 5317, 120152, 25, 1489, 28, 3519, 350, 57548, 8, 1481, 413, 220, 17, 21306, 1885, 1044, 591, 1489, 28, 4217, 316, 1489, 28, 3519, 26, 5954, 10112, 220, 1125, 2896, 53, 30, 220, 23, 2896, 53, 777, 57549, 11, 136304, 13, 1489, 28, 4146, 350, 8541, 8, 5954, 220, 20, 2896, 53, 30, 21926, 1001, 21306, 30, 4037, 3981, 13, 1489, 28, 6231, 350, 139974, 8, 5954, 220, 455, 2896, 53, 30, 25211, 1841, 220, 20, 21306, 1885, 1044, 364, 84787, 6052, 355, 364, 58369, 97196, 13], "n_tokens": 588, "words": 331, "band_lo": 260.0, "band_hi": 1040.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse the physics problem: Fission of U-236 into two equal mass prompt fission fragments and zero free neutrons. So U-236 splits into two fragments each with mass number A = 236/2 = 118. So each fragment is A=118. They are neutron-rich initially (since starting with U-236, which has Z=92, N=144). Splitting equally: each fragment would have Z approximately 46 (since 92/2=46), and N≈72 (since 144/2=72). So initial fragments: A=118, Z~46, N=72. So they are neutron-rich relative to stable isotopes with that A. Need to find the most stable nucleus with same mass number A=118: the Z which gives maximal binding energy per nucleon (i.e., along beta stability line). Use semi-empirical mass formula or look up stable isotopes: For A~118, stable isotopes are: Sn-118 (Z=50) stable, also Cd-118 (Z=48) is stable? Let's recall. Actually stable isotopes around mass 118: 118Sn (Z=50) is stable; 118Te (Z=52) is not stable (Te-118 is radioactive). 118Cd (Z=48) is not stable; I think 118Cd is unstable (decays). 118Pd (Z=46) is not stable. So the most stable with A=118 is Z=50 (Tin). So answer likely Z=50. Then the energy released by each fragment to reach that stable nucleus by a series of beta decay: The initial fragment had Z~46; to get to Z=50, need to increase Z by 4 via beta-minus decay (neutron -> proton + electron + antineutrino). Each beta decay releases some energy equal to the mass difference between mother and daughter nucleus (including kinetic energy of beta and antineutrino). Approx typical Q-values order 2-4 MeV per beta. For 4 decays, total maybe ~ 8-12 MeV. But the answer options: For Z=50 energy=18 MeV (option A). That would be about 4.5 MeV per beta, plausible for heavy nuclei. So likely correct answer: A.\n\nLet's verify if any other Z option plausible: Z=48 (Cd) would be 2 beta decays from Z=46 to Z=48; energy maybe 16 MeV? 8 MeV per decay, unrealistic. Z=47 (Ag) energy 5 MeV? That's one beta? Not match. Z=51 (Sb) energy 20 MeV? Would require 5 beta decays.\n\nThus answer A.\n\nLet's finalize.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.410734163684538e-06, "p_correct": 0.999999999999321, "logp_correct": -6.790253402037539e-13, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000006792, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6028219165867994, "cot_lp_min64": -0.8931381904220218, "frac_below_-5": 0.010434782608695653, "digit_lp_min": -2.5125467777252197, "answer_first_mention_lp": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "n_cot_tokens": 575, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -2.9068458606507717, "p_nocot": 0.054647825175962145, "uplift": 2.9068458606500926, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.054648, "B": 0.854836, "C": 0.000417, "D": 0.090099}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recbuZJ4pr9nhKoOH", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Astronomers are studying a star with a 1.4 solar radius and 1.2 solar masses. When the star's surface is not covered by dark spots, its Teff is 6000K. However, when 40% of its surface is covered by spots, the overall photospheric effective temperature decreases to 5500 K. In the stellar photosphere, astronomers have observed that the ratio of ionized to neutral atoms of Mg decreases when the star has spots. What is the factor by which this ratio changes when the star does not have spots compared to when it has spots? Assume the energy of ionization corresponds to a wavelength of approximately 1448 Å, and that the stellar photosphere is in LTE.\n\nA) ~ 6.3\nB) ~ 3.9\nC) ~ 5.2\nD) ~ 8.2\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Astronomers are studying a star with a 1.4 solar radius and 1.2 solar masses. When the star's surface is not covered by dark spots, its Teff is 6000K. However, when 40% of its surface is covered by spots, the overall photospheric effective temperature decreases to 5500 K. In the stellar photosphere, astronomers have observed that the ratio of ionized to neutral atoms of Mg decreases when the star has spots. What is the factor by which this ratio changes when the star does not have spots compared to when it has spots? Assume the energy of ionization corresponds to a wavelength of approximately 1448 Å, and that the stellar photosphere is in LTE.\n\nA) ~ 6.3\nB) ~ 3.9\nC) ~ 5.2\nD) ~ 8.2\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recbuZJ4pr9nhKoOH:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 23864, 12332, 3343, 306, 26485, 2110, 316, 27000, 18051, 328, 63974, 350, 74116, 357, 2747, 63974, 9839, 16918, 336, 5412, 42006, 13, 84705, 4