aromatic carboxylic acid; the rest of chain is released as acetone? Actually the oxidation of side chain yields -COOH and the rest becomes carbonyl fragments like acetone. So tert-butyl not present.\n\nThus maybe the answer that contains tert-butyl rather than isobutyl suggests that isobutyl after oxidation becomes tert-butyl? Not feasible. Unless we misread: isobutyl chloride (i.e., (CH3)2CHCH2Cl) after Friedel-Crafts may rearrange via carbocation rearrangement to give tert-butyl (i.e., (CH3)3C+). In Friedel-Crafts alkylation, secondary carbocation can rearrange to more stable tertiary. Isobutyl chloride under AlCl3 can generate sec-butyl cation which could rearrange to tert-butyl cation, leading to t-butyl substituted benzene. Indeed, the isobutyl chloride yields a secondary carbocation (CH3-CH(CH3)-CH2+). This can undergo hydride shift to give tertiary cation (CH3)3C+, leading to tert-butylbenzene. So product A may be tert-butylbenzene not isobutylbenzene. Good! So A is actually t-butylbenzene (para? but only one group). Then second alkylation with isopropyl chloride could give p-isopropyl-tert-butylbenzene (tert-butyl para to isopropyl). Thus final product may retain t-butyl substituent. That matches options with tert-butyl.\n\nThus our revised view: The isobutyl chloride leads to t-butyl group due to carbocation rearrangement. So product similar to t-butylbenzene (A). Second Friedel-Crafts with isopropyl chloride yields t-butyl para- isopropyl benzene.\n\nNow oxidation with KMnO4 will oxidize the isopropyl group (since it's a secondary benzylic carbon) to a carboxylic acid? Actually the carbon attached to ring is a secondary carbon (CH). Oxidation would cleave the side chain leaving COOH attached, but the methyl groups may be released as CO2? Possibly convert to a COOH directly; the other branch (CH3) go away. So we get p-tert-butylbenzoic acid (i.e., only t-butyl remains). Thus the isopropyl side is removed and replaced by COOH. Then further conversion to acid chloride, amide, reduction yields -CH2NH2 at that para position (relative to t-butyl). Then methylation yields -CH2NMe2 (dimethylamino). Finally NaNH2 may deprotonate or cause something else but likely the product remains dimethylaminomethyl attached to t-butyl phenyl.\n\nThus final product H would be p-tert-butylbenzyl-dimethylamine (i.e., 4-(dimethylaminomethyl)-tert-butylbenzene). Which option matches? Option D: 1-(4-(tert-butyl)-2-methylphenyl)-N,N-dimethylmethanamine. That has a methyl on ring (2-methyl) besides t-butyl, not matching. Option C: includes isopropyl. Option B includes phenyl attached to dimethyl ethylamine. Option A includes aniline plus dimethylamino; not.\n\nNone of the provided options directly is t-butyl + CH2NMe2. The closest is D with t-butyl and methyl (rather than none). But maybe after oxidative cleavage, the isopropyl becomes methyl (since the carbon attached to ring becomes a carbonyl then reduced to CH2 giving methyl? No.)\n\nIf isopropyl oxidation yields a carboxyl, then reduction to amine gives CH2NH2. After methylation get CH2NMe2. So that side becomes CH2NMe2. The remaining t-butyl remains unchanged. So final product: p-(tert-butyl)-benzyl-dimethylamine. This matches structure: 4-(tert-butyl)-benzyl-dimethylamine, which could be written as 4-(tert-butyl)phenyl-CH2NMe2. That would be something like \"1-(4-(tert-butyl)phenyl)-N,N-dimethylmethanamine\". That is exactly option D? Let's read option D: 1-(4-(tert-butyl)-2-methylphenyl)-N,N-dimethylmethanamine. Option D includes an extra 2-methyl substituent on phenyl. Without that, it's 1-(4-(tert-butyl)phenyl)-N,N-dimethylmethanamine. Option D adds 2-methyl. Not correct.\n\nOption C: 1-(5-(tert-butyl)-2-isopropylphenyl)-N,N-dimethylmethanamine. That has both t-butyl and isopropyl plus dimethylamino, not our product.\n\nOption B: 2-(4-(tert-butyl)phenyl)-N,N-dimethylethan-1-amine. That has an ethyl chain (CH2CH2NMe2) attached to phenyl with t-butyl. Could our product be derived via NaNH2 elimination causing a shift from CH2NMe2 to CH2CH2NMe2? Not likely.\n\nOption A: 5-(tert-butyl)-2-((dimethylamino)methyl)aniline: Contains an aniline (NH2) group additionally.\n\nThus none perfectly matches our expected product, but perhaps after NaNH2 step, the benzylic amine is eliminated to give a phenyl with an aniline? Actually NaNH2 might cause an intramolecular rearrangement (Hofmann) converting -CH2NMe2 to -NHMe? Not.\n\nLet's examine NaNH2 with an arylmethyltrimethylammonium could cause removal of a methyl group leading to benzyne generation? Not.\n\nMaybe after the methylation step, the amine is tertiary (N,N-dimethyl) which cannot be further alkylated. NaNH2 could act as a base to deprotonate the benzylic position, leading to elimination of dimethylamine to give benzyne; then benzyne could be attacked by the remaining dimethylamine (if there are two) leading to substitution at ortho position, eventually giving an aniline when nitrogen attaches to aromatic ring directly (forming Ar-NMe2). That could produce an aniline substituent? Option A includes an aniline (NH2) not NMe2.\n\nGiven the difficulty, perhaps the correct answer is D because it is the only one that matches a para-t-butyl phenyl with dimethylaminomethyl (though includes extra methyl maybe from isopropyl oxidation?).\n\nThus I will choose D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -9.536740890325746e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999083, "logp_correct": -9.16859505336176e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999996423723438, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6954229091516766, "cot_lp_min64": -1.7247900407417731, "frac_below_-5": 0.015431627196333078, "digit_lp_min": -6.920282363891602, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 6545, "lp_end": -2.264974000354414e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.933898824470816e-06, "logp_nocot": -1.441709366881228, "p_nocot": 0.23652310816481356, "uplift": 1.4417093668811363, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.052775, "B": 0.067765, "C": 0.642936, "D": 0.236523}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recdJbKDkjUhZi2B2", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Suppose AGNs emitted all their energy output in the form of electromagnetic radiation isotropically (e.g., without prominent jets), and we had perfect detectors that could measure this radiation across the spectrum without obstacles. \nSuppose that we can measure the mass of the supermassive black hole in AGNs almost exactly, as well as the distance of AGN.\n\nThen, we examine the plot with mass of the AGN black hole on the horizontal axis, and bolometric luminosity of AGN on the vertical axis. \nIf hypothetically: \nAll AGNs with a Schwarzschild black hole, lie on a power-law line with practically no scatter; all AGNs with a Kerr black hole with a spin a=1 (very close to 1) and prograde rotation, also lie on a power-law line with practically no scatter.\nHow are the two lines positioned with respect to each other?\n\nA) The 2nd line (Kerr line) is above the 1st line (by a factor of 5 to 10).\nB) The 2nd line (Kerr line) is above the 1st line, by a factor of 50 to 100.\nC) The 2nd line (Kerr line) is bellow the 1st line by a factor of 5 to 10.\nD) The 2nd line (Kerr line) is bellow the 1st line by a factor of 50 to 100.\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Suppose AGNs emitted all their energy output in the form of electromagnetic radiation isotropically (e.g., without prominent jets), and we had perfect detectors that could measure this radiation across the spectrum without obstacles. \nSuppose that we can measure the mass of the supermassive black hole in AGNs almost exactly, as well as the distance of AGN.\n\nThen, we examine the plot with mass of the AGN black hole on the horizontal axis, and bolometric luminosity of AGN on the vertical axis. \nIf hypothetically: \nAll AGNs with a Schwarzschild black hole, lie on a power-law line with practically no scatter; all AGNs with a Kerr black hole with a spin a=1 (very close to 1) and prograde rotation, also lie on a power-law line with practically no scatter.\nHow are the two lines positioned with respect to each other?\n\nA) The 2nd line (Kerr line) is above the 1st line (by a factor of 5 to 10).\nB) The 2nd line (Kerr line) is above the 1st line, by a factor of 50 to 100.\nC) The 2nd line (Kerr line) is bellow the 1st line by a factor of 5 to 10.\nD) The 2nd line (Kerr line) is bellow the 1st line by a factor of 50 to 100.\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recdJbKDkjUhZi2B2:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 195922, 63585, 3814, 25, 19215, 45, 350, 8240, 188095, 478, 1734, 20110, 8, 82370, 536, 54502, 316, 5960, 22985, 4842, 11, 1259, 264, 1141, 6251, 11, 326, 17456, 13, 2214, 261, 4335, 79683, 4842, 11, 290, 10562, 7271, 34114, 82370, 536, 382, 9913, 656, 290, 457, 1183, 8303, 82370, 536, 451, 6584, 1183, 314, 220, 16, 13, 2109, 68, 3150, 350, 44, 17712, 41133, 8, 15606, 5031, 13, 5551, 1374, 82370, 1629, 4783, 1543, 15684, 1183, 8303, 11, 14537, 402, 1259, 264, 1141, 6251, 326, 14041, 1799, 17456, 13, 48765, 1799, 17456, 20102, 402, 17464, 25, 395, 261, 2893, 12, 8150, 1365, 104192, 166845, 79683, 11, 17456, 6574, 15, 13, 45315, 350, 20, 13, 22, 4, 328, 2867, 4842, 5954, 328, 1259, 3431, 295, 7165, 741, 2214, 29045, 2146, 58477, 440, 9013, 118766, 79683, 350, 64, 93, 15, 13, 34589, 936, 17456, 869, 316, 6574, 15, 13, 1398, 350, 1398, 64918, 2632, 12332, 6574, 20, 12, 21, 7064, 306, 17456, 13, 2632, 395, 2684, 1259, 264, 1141, 6251, 350, 50019, 5732, 384, 936, 290, 82370, 536, 382, 6574, 20, 12, 21, 4238, 7266, 395, 29045, 2146, 58477, 79683, 30667, 2893, 29290, 25650, 364, 84787, 290, 6301, 2870, 7271, 34114, 82370, 536, 326, 5960, 22985, 4842, 395, 261, 4335, 457, 1183, 8303, 18051, 30, 1843, 581, 19429, 1023, 553, 1259, 264, 1744, 540, 2684, 457, 1183, 8303, 18051, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 2684, 451, 26049, 6584, 1183, 936, 1815, 4842, 2887, 394, 8200, 536, 18075, 382, 23876, 326, 13313, 328, 17464, 13, 3072, 2105, 11, 581, 19429, 581, 553, 41068, 290, 6290, 20395, 7271, 34114, 82370, 536, 10217, 4842, 25, 2647, 17464, 15286, 316, 2647, 17456, 11, 1118, 1340, 10635, 62813, 12847, 13, 64968, 290, 10562, 20395, 82370, 536, 395, 4335, 4842, 2023, 413, 7266, 395, 118766, 79683, 11, 10112, 12332, 6574, 17, 12, 18, 30, 623, 47221, 10562, 14041, 1799, 17456, 9809, 2870, 104192, 166845, 350, 20, 13, 22, 18929, 326, 65414, 118766, 350, 4689, 4, 395, 261, 28, 16, 30, 50557, 290, 5436, 8310, 21090, 395, 261, 28, 16, 440, 9013, 8967, 17456, 220, 15, 13, 4689, 30, 24305, 25, 98255, 395, 261, 28, 15, 25, 220, 15, 13, 45315, 11, 395, 261, 28, 15, 13, 34589, 25, 220, 15, 13, 1398, 26, 395, 261, 28, 16, 350, 3086, 22730, 2905, 8, 6574, 15, 13, 4689, 30, 730, 37681, 195922, 63585, 6409, 11, 17464, 261, 93, 15, 13, 34589, 382, 290, 748, 24020, 5967, 5192, 316, 135911, 19374, 26, 17456, 220, 15, 13, 1398, 13, 2632, 12332, 6574, 20, 12, 22, 364, 84787, 290, 6052, 6960, 355, 25, 118766, 2543, 5151, 104192, 166845, 2543, 656, 12332, 328, 220, 20, 12, 702, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 461, 160761, 25, 355, 6120], "n_tokens": 469, "words": 256, "band_lo": 128.5, "band_hi": 514.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the astrophysical context: AGN (Active Galactic Nuclei) luminosity relates to black hole mass, accretion rate, and efficiency. For a given BH mass, the maximum bolometric luminosity is limited by the Eddington luminosity L_Edd = 1.26e38 (M/Msun) erg/s. However real luminosities often sub-Eddington, depending on accretion rate and radiative efficiency. Radiative efficiency depends on spin: for a non-rotating Schwarzschild BH, efficiency ~0.057 (5.7% of rest mass energy of accreted matter). For maximally spinning prograde Kerr BH (a~0.998), efficiency up to ~0.32 (32%). So factor ~5-6 increase in efficiency. So for same accretion rate (mass inflow), the luminosity is ~5-6 times higher for maximally spinning BH versus non-spinning.\n\nThus the relationship between bolometric luminosity and black hole mass for a given Eddington ratio? If we assume they are accreting at same Eddington ratio (i.e., same L/L_Edd), then mass-luminosity relation is linear and independent of spin. But here, we assume we are measuring the actual observed bolometric luminosity vs mass: different spin leads to different efficiency, which may produce systematic shift. Typically the maximum observed luminosity for given mass could be higher for Kerr BH, maybe factor ~2-3? The theoretical maximum radiative efficiency difference between Schwarzschild (5.7%) and maximal Kerr (42% for a=1? Actually the ISCO radius for a=1 prograde gives efficiency 0.42? Wait: Efficiency for a=0: 0.057, for a=0.998: 0.32; for a=1 (theoretically) ~0.42? In realistic astrophysical conditions, spin a~0.998 is the Thorne limit due to photon capture; efficiency 0.32. So factor ~5-7.\n\nThus the answer likely A: Kerr line above Schwarzschild line by factor of 5-10.\n\nThus answer: 'ANSWER: A'.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.5299434461298915e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999996154, "logp_correct": -3.845479833363089e-13, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807911092, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6841200770788783, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4916654102186158, "frac_below_-5": 0.006578947368421052, "digit_lp_min": -4.269619941711426, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.5924425721168518, "n_cot_tokens": 456, "lp_end": -0.0072005996480584145, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -2.126956258626719, "p_nocot": 0.11919955487515202, "uplift": 2.1269562586263344, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.1192, "B": 0.880772, "C": 2.1e-05, "D": 7e-06}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recdhG9IdiPi8yZPM", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Teduglutide is a 33 aa peptide derived from the human glucagon-like peptide-2 (processed from the larger GCG gene). It is produced in E. coli and is on the market as a long-term treatment for short bowel syndrome. GLP-2 receptor is detected in various tissues, including the brain, and you wonder if you could repurpose the drug to treat Alzheimer's disease. You will use rTg4510 mice as the model organism and prepare your peptide in-house. The sequence for the teduglutide has a 2A -> G mutation; otherwise, you will rely on the DNA sequence for the GLP-2:\n5'CACGCCGATGGTTCCTTCTCTGATGAGATGAACACAGTTCTTGATAATCTTGCCACCCGGGACTTTATAAACTGGTTGCTTCAGACCAAAATTACTGAC3'\n\nYou will clone it into the commercially available pGEX-5x-3 vector containing Factor Xa cleavage site between the multiple cloning site and the GST tag for affinity purification. PCR product of which primer pair below you cannot digest into sticky ends for ligation into the vector?\n\nA) forward 5' GTAGGATCCCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC 3' and reverse 5' CTGGTCGACCTAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG 3'\nB) forward 5' TAAGCAGAATTCCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC 3' and reverse 5' CTGACTCGAGCTAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG 3'\nC) forward 5' AACCCGGGCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC 3' and reverse 5' TCTAGCGGCCGCTCAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG 3'\nD) forward 5' GTAGGATCCCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC 3' and reverse 5' CTGACTCGAGTCAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG 3'\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Teduglutide is a 33 aa peptide derived from the human glucagon-like peptide-2 (processed from the larger GCG gene). It is produced in E. coli and is on the market as a long-term treatment for short bowel syndrome. GLP-2 receptor is detected in various tissues, including the brain, and you wonder if you could repurpose the drug to treat Alzheimer's disease. You will use rTg4510 mice as the model organism and prepare your peptide in-house. The sequence for the teduglutide has a 2A -> G mutation; otherwise, you will rely on the DNA sequence for the GLP-2:\n5'CACGCCGATGGTTCCTTCTCTGATGAGATGAACACAGTTCTTGATAATCTTGCCACCCGGGACTTTATAAACTGGTTGCTTCAGACCAAAATTACTGAC3'\n\nYou will clone it into the commercially available pGEX-5x-3 vector containing Factor Xa cleavage site between the multiple cloning site and the GST tag for affinity purification. PCR product of which primer pair below you cannot digest into sticky ends for ligation into the vector?\n\nA) forward 5' GTAGGATCCCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC 3' and reverse 5' CTGGTCGACCTAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG 3'\nB) forward 5' TAAGCAGAATTCCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC 3' and reverse 5' CTGACTCGAGCTAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG 3'\nC) forward 5' AACCCGGGCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC 3' and reverse 5' TCTAGCGGCCGCTCAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG 3'\nD) forward 5' GTAGGATCCCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC 3' and reverse 5' CTGACTCGAGTCAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG 3'\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recdhG9IdiPi8yZPM:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11433, 1118, 13240, 10610, 738, 625, 10635, 71681, 1888, 484, 665, 413, 3807, 14155, 1511, 48068, 17095, 395, 9275, 387, 1511, 275, 38, 3922, 12, 20, 87, 12, 18, 9727, 364, 2167, 1309, 316, 3331, 38247, 6427, 306, 9727, 391, 9310, 13, 275, 38, 3922, 12, 20, 87, 12, 18, 391, 9310, 853, 25, 106559, 36525, 350, 43469, 1228, 4433, 936, 49394, 18164, 350, 16328, 1228, 12447, 936, 2127, 1555, 40, 350, 1895, 17879, 2971, 936, 7851, 40, 350, 38, 12447, 38, 2436, 936, 4037, 40, 350, 38, 17879, 38, 4433, 30190, 936, 5178, 13, 50557, 275, 38, 3922, 12, 20, 87, 12, 18, 7598, 158581, 2702, 350, 44, 9310, 8, 16281, 25, 2550, 49394, 18164, 350, 16328, 1228, 12447, 936, 4037, 40, 350, 38, 17879, 38, 4433, 30190, 936, 2127, 1555, 40, 350, 1895, 17879, 2971, 936, 7851, 40, 350, 38, 12447, 38, 2436, 936, 106559, 36525, 350, 43469, 1228, 4433, 936, 5178, 13, 6