, 1228, 38, 30016, 12447, 1895, 12447, 4050, 41351, 290, 13240, 853, 2550, 34, 355, 363, 499, 499, 363, 499, 355, 353, 499, 499, 353, 353, 363, 363, 353, 353, 363, 13, 21926, 20238, 6384, 290, 4756, 853, 168383, 38, 4433, 1008, 7477, 3099, 363, 10217, 499, 30, 41021, 2371, 25, 4756, 25, 168383, 38, 4433, 38, 1228, 1008, 13240, 25, 168383, 43469, 17879, 1228, 1008, 2632, 1354, 382, 261, 9809, 25, 4756, 1458, 13520, 34, 30, 50557, 4756, 25, 168383, 499, 363, 363, 499, 11503, 499, 1008, 24305, 2338, 25, 168383, 499, 363, 363, 499, 11503, 13, 99979, 25, 168383, 499, 499, 363, 499, 11503, 13, 2632, 28847, 44560, 1001, 3611, 26, 10112, 4275, 316, 3931, 290, 220, 17, 32, 609, 38, 52929, 30, 623, 52929, 382, 540, 4010, 30, 138743, 290, 220, 17, 32, 609, 38, 6629, 1001, 52438, 18655, 26, 889, 77671, 364, 10620, 18199, 13240, 25, 6664, 392, 38, 12447, 38, 2436, 1, 7851, 40, 2702, 30, 48128, 13240, 25, 24286, 43469, 12447, 38, 2436, 1895, 2971, 12447, 2971, 10227, 1228, 15741, 98457, 30016, 1895, 38, 13, 61633, 392, 38, 12447, 38, 2436, 1, 350, 15865, 40, 8, 1934, 24286, 38, 30, 50557, 392, 1895, 43469, 12447, 38, 2436, 4050, 11377, 499, 12447, 38, 2436, 382, 7851, 40, 2702, 13, 9700, 13, 623, 2867, 12742, 6960, 21800, 328, 1268, 328, 10898, 13, 2632, 355, 7890, 8975, 364, 33, 8, 6687, 25, 42685, 2971, 34, 106084, 38858, 4433, 2436, 43469, 17879, 1228, 38, 30016, 12447, 1895, 12447, 13, 61633, 392, 16328, 1228, 12447, 1, 49394, 18164, 1934, 12951, 42685, 2971, 34, 30, 50557, 392, 32, 38858, 4433, 1, 997, 6664, 49394, 18164, 350, 16328, 1228, 12447, 741, 57144, 25, 42685, 2971, 34, 106084, 1228, 12447, 2550, 24305, 49394, 18164, 2702, 382, 34221, 1228, 12447, 13, 623, 13240, 6664, 392, 16328, 1228, 12447, 1, 91138, 656, 392, 34, 2971, 4050, 2632, 6687, 13240, 853, 49394, 18164, 2702, 13, 48128, 25, 24286, 38, 13467, 17879, 2971, 1895, 2971, 12447, 2971, 10227, 1228, 15741, 98457, 30016, 1895, 38, 6664, 392, 1895, 17879, 2971, 1, 2127, 1555, 40, 13, 9700, 364, 34, 8, 6687, 25, 126863, 4433, 43469, 30190, 2436, 43469, 17879, 1228, 38, 30016, 12447, 1895, 12447, 13, 61633, 392, 4433, 17879, 43469, 1, 336, 809, 40, 30, 50557, 336, 809, 40, 382, 132759, 43469, 38, 350, 1751, 2202, 741, 7306, 581, 679, 392, 4433, 17879, 43469, 4050, 21926, 336, 809, 40, 11, 1118, 28135, 112015, 17095, 11, 625, 48068, 13, 48128, 25, 353, 1895, 2971, 17879, 38, 4433, 38, 1895, 34, 2971, 12447, 2971, 10227, 1228, 15741, 98457, 30016, 1895, 38, 6664, 392, 43469, 4433, 30190, 1, 4037, 40, 2702, 350, 38, 17879, 38, 4433, 30190, 741, 50557, 392, 43469, 4433, 30190, 1, 382, 997, 328, 4037, 40, 889, 3149, 382, 499, 17879, 38, 4433, 30190, 26, 13240, 6664, 392, 43469, 4433, 30190, 4050, 54458, 625, 413, 3149, 2702, 13, 8820, 6664, 392, 51, 1895, 2971, 34, 1, 1118, 382, 2127, 3103, 40, 350, 51, 1895, 106084, 741, 50557, 392, 51, 1895, 2971, 34, 1, 382, 2127, 3103, 40, 350, 51, 1895, 106084, 8, 12486, 2174, 355, 13, 2632, 10112, 625, 7937, 2702, 2747, 8535, 31146, 13, 2632, 363, 6960, 68230, 364, 35, 8, 6687, 25, 23677, 2971, 38, 1228, 93857, 2436, 43469, 17879, 1228, 38, 30016, 12447, 1895, 12447, 350, 48581, 472, 355, 741, 48128, 25, 24286, 38, 13467, 17879, 2971, 12447, 2971, 12447, 2971, 10227, 1228, 15741, 98457, 30016, 1895, 38, 8895, 392, 1895, 17879, 2971, 1, 2127, 1555, 40, 25435, 13, 2632, 415, 7890, 8975, 364, 84787, 363, 382, 68230, 2236, 6687, 8844, 336, 809, 40, 350, 1751, 2202, 8, 625, 48068, 11, 326, 18199, 1340, 679, 60592, 4037, 40, 503, 2127, 3103, 40, 364, 15143, 25, 392, 178974, 1888, 328, 1118, 13240, 10610, 4895, 481, 6284, 31146, 1511, 48068, 17095, 395, 9275, 387, 1511, 290, 9727, 16842, 2632, 6052, 363, 13], "n_tokens": 1155, "words": 538, "band_lo": 324.5, "band_hi": 1298.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to determine which primer pair will not produce PCR product that can be digested into sticky ends for ligation into pGEX-5x-3 vector.\n\nWe need to consider restriction sites in vector MCS. pGEX-5x-3 MCS has: BamHI (GGATCC), EcoRI (GAATTC), XhoI (CTCGAG), SalI (GTCGAC), NotI (GCGGCCGC), etc. Actually pGEX-5x-3 multiple cloning site (MCS) sequence: ... EcoRI (GAATTC), NotI (GCGGCCGC), XhoI (CTCGAG), SalI (GTCGAC), BamHI (GGATCC), etc. Factor Xa cleavage site is between GST and MCS.\n\nGoal: clone PCR product into vector using sticky ends. Primers include restriction sites at 5' ends for digestion.\n\nWe need to pick primer pair that cannot be digested into sticky ends (i.e., primers either lack appropriate restriction sites or produce incompatible ends). Let's examine each.\n\nFirst need to know the sequence of the insert: DNA sequence given: 5' CACGCCGATGGTTCCTTCTCTGATGAGATGAACACAGTTCTTGATAATCTTGCCACCCGGGACTTTATAAACTGGTTGCTTCAGACCAAAATTACTGAC 3'\n\nWe want to amplify that insert with primers adding restriction sites.\n\nPrimers in options:\n\nA) forward: GTAGGATCCCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC\nReverse: CTGGTCGACCTAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG\n\nWe need to see restriction sites in forward: sequence includes \"GGATCC\" = BamHI site. The forward primer: GTAGGATCCCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC. After GTAG, we have GGATCC (BamHI). Good. Also includes start of insert: CAC... Actually after BamHI and maybe extra nucleotides, then CACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC matches the beginning of the given sequence? Let's check given sequence start: CACGCCGATGGTTCCTT... Wait given start: CACGCCGATGGTTCCTTCTCT... So forward primer after restriction site should have the start of the gene: \"CACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC\". Indeed the primer has ...C A C G G C G A T G G T T C C T T C. That's matching except the original has CACGCC... note second C vs G? Let's check: original: CACGCCGAT... primer: CACGGCGAT... So there is a difference: original had CGC? Actually original: CAC G C C G AT G... Wait break: CAC G C C G AT. Primer: CAC G G C G AT. So mismatched one base; maybe it's to include the 2A->G mutation? The mutation is at position? Possibly the 2A->G changes one amino acid; but irrelevant.\n\nNow reverse primer: includes \"GTCGAC\" SalI site? Reverse primer: CTGGTCGACCTAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG. Contains \"GTCGAC\" (SalI) after CTG? Actually \"CTGGTCGAC\". Yes GTCGAC is SalI site. Good. The rest tail likely complement of end of insert. So A seems fine.\n\nB) forward: TAAGCAGAATTCCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC. Contains \"GAATTC\" EcoRI after perhaps TAAGC? Actually \"AATTCC\" part includes EcoRI (GAATTC). Sequence: TAAGCAGAATTC ... Wait EcoRI site is GAATTC. The primer includes \"GAATTC\" preceded by \"CAG\". So forward primer has EcoRI site. Reverse: CTGACTCGAGCTAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG includes \"CTCGAG\" XhoI. Good.\n\nC) forward: AACCCGGGCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC. Contains \"CCCGGG\" SmaI? Actually SmaI is CCCGGG (blunt). Here we have \"CCCGGG\". That's SmaI, which produces blunt ends, not sticky. Reverse: TCTAGCGGCCGCTCAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG includes \"GGCCGC\" NotI site (GCGGCCGC). Actually \"GGCCGC\" is part of NotI but full is GCGGCCGC; primer includes \"GGCCGC\". Might not be full site. Also includes \"TCTAGC\" which is XbaI (TCTAGA). Actually \"TCTAGC\" is XbaI (TCTAGA) missing last A. So maybe not proper site -> can't digest. So C likely problematic.\n\nD) forward: GTAGGATCCCACGGCGATGGTTCCTTC (same as A). Reverse: CTGACTCGAGTCAGTCAGTAATTTTGGTCTG contains \"CTCGAG\" XhoI okay. So D seems fine.\n\nThus C is problematic because forward uses SmaI (blunt) not sticky, and reverse may have incomplete NotI or XbaI.\n\nQuestion: \"PCR product of which primer pair below you cannot digest into sticky ends for ligation into the vector?\" So answer C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.0967218258883804e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999997532, "logp_correct": -2.468299680315547e-13, "letter_mass": 0.9999920130415753, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.47229065784949126, "cot_lp_min64": -1.0946304885542752, "frac_below_-5": 0.00788091068301226, "digit_lp_min": -0.00798072200268507, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.7503616213798523, "n_cot_tokens": 1142, "lp_end": -0.00573240639641881, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -3.872967717391465, "p_nocot": 0.0207965594421599, "uplift": 3.872967717391218, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.093204, "B": 0.197312, "C": 0.020797, "D": 0.688687}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "receXuVoSto1gUcVJ", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: We are planning to construct a ground based radio telescope with the purpose of conducting a galaxy-redshift survey aimed at studying dark energy. This telescope will map large-scale structures, such as galaxy clusters, at various redshifts, utilizing the collected data to constrain the equation of state related to dark energy. We want to determine the appropriate frequency coverage for this telescope which is suitable for such experiment. Which combination of the following frequency coverages is suitable for constraining dark energy equation of states ?\na) 500 - 700 MHz\nb) 300 - 450 MHz\nc) 1 GHz - 1.2 GHz\nd) 20 - 80 MHz\ne) 100 - 400 MHz\n\nA) b , e\nB) a , e\nC) a , c\nD) b , d\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: We are planning to construct a ground based radio telescope with the purpose of conducting a galaxy-redshift survey aimed at studying dark energy. This telescope will map large-scale structures, such as galaxy clusters, at various redshifts, utilizing the collected data to constrain the equation of state related to dark energy. We want to determine the appropriate frequency coverage for this telescope which is suitable for such experiment. Which combination of the following frequency coverages is suitable for constraining dark energy equation of states ?\na) 500 - 700 MHz\nb) 300 - 450 MHz\nc) 1 GHz - 1.2 GHz\nd) 20 - 80 MHz\ne) 100 - 400 MHz\n\nA) b , e\nB) a , e\nC) a , c\nD) b , d\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "receXuVoSto1gUcVJ:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 4928, 25, 75745, 261, 7740, 8235, 11828, 121536, 316, 621, 261, 66184, 57898, 27472, 15901, 350, 32551, 24473, 8866, 8, 316, 168046, 8883, 5954, 42006, 328, 2608, 13, 21580, 19813, 13422, 382, 13127, 30, 1225, 6960, 30795, 316, 45039, 220, 2040, 9256, 2543, 11, 1118, 853, 2867, 19813, 220, 18330, 15, 74297, 13, 2214, 3592, 27472, 295, 220, 2040, 9256, 2543, 591, 129137, 540, 5890, 3592, 1116, 13870, 11, 581, 1309, 6931, 64338, 21824, 316, 3592, 27472, 295, 2543, 25, 33756, 314, 220, 18330, 15, 820, 350, 16, 10, 89, 741, 2214, 19271, 66184, 3592, 27472, 45453, 395, 8883, 5954, 11, 581, 3572, 3783, 579, 869, 316, 10112, 6574, 17, 30, 2214, 579, 28, 15, 316, 220, 17, 11, 64338, 591, 220, 18330, 15, 74297, 1917, 316, 220, 18330, 15, 27334, 16, 10, 17, 25987, 39987, 74297, 13, 2632, 36144, 220, 3234, 12, 10165, 15, 77140, 13, 2214, 6931, 64338, 350, 68, 1940, 4213, 220, 1353, 12, 4812, 74297, 8, 66938, 316, 7266, 3592, 1116, 13870, 350, 89, 93, 17, 13, 20, 12, 1311, 741, 4569, 1340, 413, 3101, 1932, 3592, 27472, 395, 66184, 45039, 28385, 350, 8293, 10112, 34636, 22573, 741, 3072, 290, 4928, 6960, 10648, 290, 19813, 3352, 484, 17804, 3592, 27472, 295, 220, 2040, 9256, 2543, 395, 579, 93, 15, 12, 17, 350, 42249, 5954, 31060, 741, 4569, 6664, 220, 3234, 12, 9534, 74297, 350, 89, 6574, 220, 16, 12, 16, 13, 23, 8, 326, 10112, 220, 4095, 12, 14869, 74297, 350, 89, 93, 17, 12, 18, 13, 22, 741, 623, 4335, 3880, 25, 261, 8, 3234, 12, 9534, 74297, 11, 287, 8, 4095, 12, 14869, 74297, 11, 274, 8, 16, 81534, 12, 16, 13, 17, 81534, 350, 89, 93, 15, 13, 1157, 12, 15, 13, 4689, 936, 272, 8, 455, 12, 2241, 77140, 350, 89, 93, 1125, 12, 2789, 936, 319, 8, 1353, 12, 4812, 77140, 350, 89, 93, 17, 13, 20, 12, 1311, 741, 21580, 43835, 553, 13127, 30, 2214, 8883, 5954, 540, 4465, 6487, 2126, 6642, 379, 3592, 27472, 11, 581, 1309, 220, 3234, 12, 9534, 74297, 326, 10112, 1217, 220, 16, 79295, 12, 16, 13, 17, 81534, 350, 71992, 2345, 741, 2632, 6960, 261, 827, 274, 364, 84787, 6052, 363, 364, 12845, 802, 3503, 30411, 19271, 45453, 25, 319, 1940, 4213, 10049, 10309, 36208, 220, 4812, 12, 6685, 74297, 395, 579, 28, 15, 13, 23, 12, 17, 13, 20, 13, 2632, 220, 3234, 12, 9534, 74297, 22476, 2061, 395, 32338, 46, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 363, 8, 261, 1366, 274, 13], "n_tokens": 436, "words": 210, "band_lo": 105.0, "band_hi": 420.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the question: constructing a ground-based radio telescope to do a galaxy-redshift survey (large-scale structure) to constrain dark energy equation of state. Which frequency coverage is suitable? It likely refers to HI 21 cm line, which has rest frequency 1420 MHz. For redshifted 21 cm line from galaxies at various redshifts, we need lower frequencies corresponding to redshifted line: ν = 1420 / (1+z). For typical galaxy redshift surveys for dark energy, we might target z up to maybe ~2? For z=0 to 2, frequencies from 1420 MHz down to 1420/(1+2)=473 MHz. So roughly 500-1400MHz. For lower frequencies (e.g., 100-400 MHz) corresponds to higher redshifts (z~2.5-13). That may be too high redshift for galaxy HI detection (but maybe intensity mapping). But the question likely wants the frequency range that covers redshifted 21 cm line for z~0-2 (dark energy constraints). That includes 500-700 MHz (z ~ 1-1.8) and maybe 300-450 MHz (z~2-3.7). The given options: a)500-700 MHz, b)300-450 MHz, c)1GHz-1.2GHz (z~0.18-0.42), d)20-80MHz (z~16-70), e)100-400MHz (z~2.5-13). Which combinations are suitable? For dark energy at low-to-moderate redshift, we need 500-700 MHz and maybe also 1 GHz-1.2GHz (nearby). So likely a & c.\n\nThus answer C.\n\nLet’s double-check typical surveys: e.g., CHIME operates 400-800 MHz for z=0.8-2.5. So 500-700 MHz indeed used for BAO.\n\nThus answer: C) a , c.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.1682443066263204e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999889, "logp_correct": -1.1070737878910006e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807907358, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6161030006012156, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9142136103732734, "frac_below_-5": 0.01182033096926714, "digit_lp_min": -2.356174945831299, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.049449071288108826, "n_cot_tokens": 423, "lp_end": -0.0048085288144648075, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -7.627416518559341, "p_nocot": 0.00048691717629266104, "uplift": 7.627416518559331, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.119145, "B": 0.880367, "C": 0.000487, "D": 2e-06}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "receYQxnUBvxPcvbQ", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Astronomers are currently observing a star with a radius equal to that of the Sun. One  hemisphere of the star is covered in dark spots with a filling factor of 20%. The star has an effective temperature (Teff) of 6000K, and the spots exhibit a temperature difference of 1000K. As only one hemisphere is spotty, photometric time-series observations will reveal periodic variations in brightness due to rotational modulation. Interestingly, this situation can closely resemble the presence of an exoplanet. To produce the same amplitude signal in the star's light curve (if the star was not covered by spots!), what should be the radius of a hypothetical exoplanet relative to the radius of the host star (i.e. Rpl/Rstar)?\n\nA) ~0.32\nB) ~0.39\nC) ~0.11\nD) ~0.07\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Astronomers are currently observing a star with a radius equal to that of the Sun. One  hemisphere of the star is covered in dark spots with a filling factor of 20%. The star has an effective temperature (Teff) of 6000K, and the spots exhibit a temperature difference of 1000K. As only one hemisphere is spotty, photometric time-series observations will reveal periodic variations in brightness due to rotational modulation. Interestingly, this situation can closely resemble the presence of an exoplanet. To produce the same amplitude signal in the star's light curve (if the star was not covered by spots!), what should be the radius of a hypothetical exoplanet relative to the radius of the host star (i.e. Rpl/Rstar)?\n\nA) ~0.32\nB) ~0.39\nC) ~0.11\nD) ~0.07\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "receYQxnUBvxPcvbQ:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 23864, 79673, 328, 59110, 35930, 5192, 316, 25522, 24878, 1001, 143971, 483, 220, 455, 4, 28447, 12332, 11, 12088, 220, 1353, 15, 42, 6931, 1572, 8309, 11842, 350, 6718, 15, 42, 2747, 10021, 220, 3234, 15, 42, 741, 623, 20395, 36547, 47245, 472, 8253, 155086, 11, 14253, 9809, 2870, 1261, 10021, 1206, 143971, 22060, 765, 10217, 2893, 12, 22607, 1206, 143971, 350, 27252, 189592, 1273, 143971, 382, 10021, 14450, 741, 2632, 79673, 382, 9809, 306, 3609, 36547, 2870, 10021, 1206, 326, 537, 1148, 25910, 2325, 59625, 316, 7848, 36547, 30, 50557, 79673, 328, 4207, 27636, 5192, 316, 23769, 28, 350, 37, 13731, 533, 454, 15950, 11876, 37, 39367, 30, 44130, 4961, 34114, 120880, 79673, 382, 25689, 6487, 12, 42583, 15001, 316, 4774, 13, 2214, 40320, 13636, 11, 4275, 350, 49, 40250, 31718, 78943, 73602, 17, 13, 2632, 1023, 3810, 25, 2514, 10635, 290, 2684, 79673, 12884, 306, 8253, 885, 4207, 27636, 350, 366, 290, 8253, 1458, 860, 25522, 936, 1412, 17921, 21090, 15001, 1715, 84787, 1309, 316, 23864, 15001, 36547, 3343, 591, 25522, 25, 4296, 10021, 1206, 143971, 22060, 765, 11, 581, 1921, 36547, 6931, 26, 1261, 23821, 143971, 22060, 11, 581, 1921, 3149, 36547, 13, 623, 9440, 306, 36547, 382, 5192, 316, 12486, 59110, 591, 25522, 11223, 316, 537, 1148, 25910, 8309, 11842, 13, 623, 10021, 13422, 33763, 402, 484, 143971, 25, 220, 455, 4, 328, 484, 143971, 350, 2878, 6062, 8253, 8, 382, 13083, 483, 25522, 13, 2632, 10705, 10021, 3624, 15001, 316, 3609, 71974, 9753, 540, 1062, 12823, 1261, 10021, 1206, 4307, 22060, 765, 382, 220, 15, 13, 17, 425, 220, 15, 13, 20, 314, 220, 15, 13, 16, 328, 3609, 9753, 3624, 13, 13602, 1606, 6375, 51811, 382, 15263, 540, 261, 1058, 350, 10665, 295, 3624, 382, 261, 18044, 741, 3072, 581, 1309, 316, 3331, 20395, 36547, 591, 290, 15263, 18044, 13, 3604, 1062, 4335, 1058, 11, 481, 1921, 6375, 290, 8253, 885, 9753, 350, 3086, 143971, 20511, 765, 741, 4296, 484, 143971, 6664, 25522, 24878, 220, 455, 4, 328, 1617, 3624, 11, 290, 15263, 49179, 3624, 6664, 25522, 483, 12088, 220, 3234, 15, 42, 326, 2867, 220, 6718, 15, 42, 364, 9095, 3095, 21219, 59110, 5251, 2454, 143971, 326, 103932, 71571, 36138, 13, 623, 20395, 36547, 99864, 316, 21781, 34636, 1072, 49179, 18044, 13, 2214, 261, 51811, 11, 84794, 8883, 7299, 25203, 11, 2454, 3624, 3577, 62274, 36875, 2146, 316, 1617, 34636, 364, 84787, 290, 20395, 36547, 1261, 10021, 1206, 4307, 22060, 25, 454, 18491, 346, 314, 350, 16, 533, 285, 8, 425, 357, 78943, 659, 285, 425, 357, 18491, 346, 1366, 1919, 285, 382, 136350, 3624, 328, 15263, 18044, 13083, 656, 25522, 13, 12265, 25522, 553, 402, 143971, 483, 28447, 12332, 220, 455, 4, 328, 484, 143971, 11, 326, 15263, 143971, 29702, 484, 143971, 11, 1815, 285, 314, 220, 15, 13, 17, 350, 21910, 290, 143971, 382, 9637, 15263, 741, 24305, 25, 623, 15263, 143971, 382, 9707, 290, 10021, 1206, 143971, 1261, 4275, 20511, 26, 1815, 3518, 484, 143971, 220, 455, 4, 3624, 382, 10021, 13, 2632, 285, 314, 220, 15, 13, 17, 328, 15263, 18044, 364, 5958, 290, 1273, 143971, 22060, 11, 860, 25522, 11, 285, 314, 220, 15, 364, 84787, 36547, 9809, 350, 3228, 533, 1349, 8, 314, 454, 11119, 1148, 25910, 533, 454, 18491, 25910, 314, 357, 78943, 533, 43900, 16, 533, 285, 8, 357, 78943, 659, 285, 357, 18491, 346, 60, 314, 285, 350, 40, 78943, 533, 357, 18491, 346, 3991, 36637, 79673, 11223, 316, 537, 1148, 25910, 36547, 350, 267, 4774, 8, 314, 26143, 37, 820, 454, 11119, 1148, 25910, 314, 285, 350, 40, 78943, 533, 357, 18491, 346, 11876, 40, 78943, 314, 285, 350, 16, 533, 357, 18491, 346, 60329, 78943, 3991, 10620, 357, 382, 36547, 777, 7670, 3624, 314, 47320, 353, 61, 19, 350, 18474, 4681, 741, 2632, 18051, 314, 350, 51, 18491, 346, 26742, 78943, 73602, 19, 314, 350, 3234, 15, 14, 6718, 15, 73602, 19, 364, 56734, 25, 220, 3234, 15, 14, 6718, 15, 314, 220, 20, 14, 21, 314, 220, 15, 13, 43243, 2546, 13, 7602, 19, 314, 350, 15, 13, 43243, 18, 61, 17, 73602, 17, 314, 350, 15, 13, 49555, 19, 73602, 17, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 41522, 13, 50557, 220, 15, 13, 43243, 61, 17, 314, 220, 15, 13, 49555, 26, 13749, 484, 314, 220, 15, 13, 41522, 364, 84787, 357, 18491, 346, 60329, 78943, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 3519, 364, 5808, 220, 16, 533, 18051, 314, 220, 15, 13, 37162, 364, 69, 314, 220, 15, 13, 17, 364, 84787, 26143, 37, 27092, 314, 220, 15, 13, 17, 425, 220, 15, 13, 37162, 314, 220, 15, 13, 11914, 21, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 12204, 350, 702, 13, 19, 4, 9440, 741, 21926, 25689, 6487, 12, 42583, 79673, 30, 50557, 591, 2999, 350, 373, 1148, 25910, 8, 316, 1349, 350, 22607, 1206, 8, 382, 220, 702, 13, 19, 4, 9809, 13, 80333, 13636, 1481, 413, 350, 49, 528, 31718, 16204, 73602, 17, 13, 2632, 920, 350, 49, 528, 31718, 16204, 73602, 17, 314, 220, 15, 13, 12204, 871, 460, 528, 31718, 16204, 314, 44237, 7, 15, 13, 12204, 8, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 29840, 364, 56734, 44237, 220, 15, 13, 12204, 25, 44237, 220, 15, 13, 16, 314, 220, 15, 13, 27420, 11, 261, 3546, 7266, 25, 10112, 220, 15, 13, 29840, 364, 84787, 6052, 6574, 15, 13, 1398, 66938, 316, 5317, 355, 364, 4383, 3880, 25, 355, 6574, 15, 13, 1398, 15248, 364, 84787, 6052, 314, 355, 364, 8191, 6011, 13], "n_tokens": 930, "words": 510, "band_lo": 224.75, "band_hi": 899.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to compute amplitude of brightness variation due to spots covering one hemisphere with 20% filling factor, temperature 1000K lower than photosphere (6000K -> spot 5000K). The observed flux varies as star rotates, showing difference between when spotty hemisphere faces us vs non-spotty hemisphere (presumably other hemisphere is spot-free). So amplitude is difference in total flux between spotty and unspotted view normalized to average flux? Actually amplitude of light curve due to rotation= (F_max - F_min)/F_mean? Usually photometric modulation amplitude is peak-to-peak relative to mean. For transit depth, it's (R_pl/R_star)^2. So they ask: To produce the same amplitude signal in star's light curve (if the star had no spots), what planet radius relative?\n\nThus need to compute relative flux change from spots: When spotty hemisphere faces us, we see flux lower; when opposite hemisphere faces, we see full flux. The drop in flux is due to missing brightness from spots compared to unspotted photosphere. The spot coverage fraction on that hemisphere: 20% of that hemisphere (not whole star) is covered with spots. So overall spot area relative to total stellar surface at any instant when spotty side faces us is 0.2 * 0.5 = 0.1 of total surface area. Because only half sphere is visible at a time (projected area is a disk). But we need to consider observed flux from the visible disk. At any given time, you see half the star's surface (the hemisphere facing us). When that hemisphere includes spots covering 20% of its area, the visible projected area includes spots with temperature 5000K and rest 6000K.\n\nAssume uniform brightness across each hemisphere and isotropic radiation. The observed flux proportional to integrated intensity over projected disk. For a sphere, limb darkening ignored, each area element contributes proportionally to its intensity.\n\nThus the observed flux when spotty side faces: F_spot = (1 - f) * I_star + f * I_spot , where f is fractional area of visible disk covered by spots. Since spots are on hemisphere with filling factor 20% of that hemisphere, and visible hemisphere equals that hemisphere, then f = 0.2 (since the hemisphere is fully visible). Wait: The visible hemisphere is exactly the spotty hemisphere when it's facing; then within that hemisphere 20% area is spot. So f = 0.2 of visible disk.\n\nWhen the other hemisphere faces, no spots, f = 0.\n\nThus flux difference (max - min) = F_unspotted - F_spotted = I_star - [(1 - f) I_star + f I_spot] = f (I_star - I_spot).\n\nRelative amplitude compared to unspotted flux (or mean) = ΔF / F_unspotted = f (I_star - I_spot)/I_star = f (1 - I_spot/I_star).\n\nNow I is flux per unit area = sigma T^4 (blackbody). So ratio = (T_spot/T_star)^4 = (5000/6000)^4.\n\nCompute: 5000/6000 = 5/6 = 0.83333. ^4 = (0.8333^2)^2 = (0.6944)^2 ≈ 0.482. Actually 0.833^2 = 0.694; square that = 0.482.\n\nThus I_spot/I_star ≈ 0.48.\n\nSo 1 - ratio = 0.518.\n\nf = 0.2.\n\nThus ΔF/F = 0.2 * 0.518 = 0.1036 ≈ 0.104 (10.4% drop). That's peak-to-peak amplitude? Actually from max (unspotted) to min (spotty) is 10.4% difference. Transit depth would be (Rpl/Rstar)^2. So set (Rpl/Rstar)^2 = 0.104 => Rpl/Rstar = sqrt(0.104) ≈ 0.322.\n\nCompute sqrt 0.104: sqrt 0.1 = 0.316, a bit higher: maybe 0.322.\n\nThus answer ~0.32 corresponds to option A.\n\nCheck options: A ~0.32 matches.\n\nThus answer = A.\n\nReturn format.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -7.986990567587782e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999998918, "logp_correct": -1.0826409787013568e-13, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000001084, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6076546040141365, "cot_lp_min64": -1.0862353217017922, "frac_below_-5": 0.007633587786259542, "digit_lp_min": -1.7014133930206299, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 917, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -7.29804993947505, "p_nocot": 0.0006768574019755738, "uplift": 7.298049939474942, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.000677, "B": 0.028781, "C": 0.953086, "D": 0.017456}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "receo3UUzRvWCqyfA", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: The reaction between cyclohexanone and piperidine can result in the formation of a cyclic imine, also known as a Schiff base. This type of reaction is a classic example of imine formation, which involves the condensation of a ketone (cyclohexanone in this case) with a primary amine (piperidine) in the presence of an acid catalyst.\nWhat will be the favorable acid used (A) and the final product (B) of the following reaction?\nCyclohexanone + piperidine + (A, acrylaldehyde, H3O+) ---> B\n\nA) A = TsOH, B = 1-(2-(3-oxopropyl)cyclohexylidene)piperidin-1-ium\nB) A = TsOH, B = 3-(2-oxocyclohexyl)propanal\nC) A = HCl, B = 3-(2-oxocyclohexyl)propanal\nD) A = HCl, B = 1-(2-(3-oxopropyl)cyclohexylidene)piperidin-1-ium\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: The reaction between cyclohexanone and piperidine can result in the formation of a cyclic imine, also known as a Schiff base. This type of reaction is a classic example of imine formation, which involves the condensation of a ketone (cyclohexanone in this case) with a primary amine (piperidine) in the presence of an acid catalyst.\nWhat will be the favorable acid used (A) and the final product (B) of the following reaction?\nCyclohexanone + piperidine + (A, acrylaldehyde, H3O+) ---> B\n\nA) A = TsOH, B = 1-(2-(3-oxopropyl)cyclohexylidene)piperidin-1-ium\nB) A = TsOH, B = 3-(2-oxocyclohexyl)propanal\nC) A = HCl, B = 3-(2-oxocyclohexyl)propanal\nD) A = HCl, B = 1-(2-(3-oxopropyl)cyclohexylidene)piperidin-1-ium\n", "true_answer": "B", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "receo3UUzRvWCqyfA:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 679, 261, 21416, 25, 27789, 78, 22803, 270, 690, 659, 275, 19629, 110815, 659, 350, 32, 11, 59978, 3643, 