121, 30, 24305, 581, 1309, 451, 187706, 14210, 22476, 13, 2632, 4763, 364, 19226, 6824, 25, 1309, 336, 10, 43, 14210, 350, 21910, 353, 28, 15, 741, 336, 28, 18, 350, 18303, 936, 451, 28, 17, 350, 28689, 8, 871, 4215, 314, 20, 14210, 871, 1899, 364, 84787, 355, 7890, 9279, 364, 8093, 418, 25, 220, 18, 35, 18, 659, 285, 13, 220, 18, 35, 18, 871, 220, 17, 50, 10, 16, 28, 18, 871, 336, 28, 16, 13, 451, 28, 17, 350, 35, 741, 643, 28, 18, 13, 4339, 5478, 33875, 25, 285, 871, 305, 28, 18, 364, 97431, 41184, 25, 1309, 643, 3080, 45, 314, 305, 314, 18, 30, 643, 3080, 45, 314, 18, 11, 305, 28, 18, 1899, 364, 192949, 25, 451, 187706, 314, 17, 10, 18, 28, 20, 14210, 871, 4763, 364, 19226, 6824, 25, 336, 10, 43, 314, 16, 10, 17, 28, 18, 14210, 871, 4763, 364, 84787, 418, 9279, 364, 8093, 363, 25, 220, 18, 47, 15, 659, 265, 13, 220, 18, 47, 15, 871, 336, 28, 16, 11, 451, 28, 16, 350, 47, 936, 643, 28, 15, 13, 4339, 5478, 265, 871, 305, 28, 15, 364, 23483, 643, 3080, 45, 314, 305, 871, 220, 15, 314, 15, 8975, 364, 192949, 25, 451, 187706, 314, 16, 10, 15, 28, 16, 14210, 871, 4763, 364, 19226, 6824, 25, 336, 10, 43, 314, 16, 10, 16, 28, 17, 1952, 871, 40723, 2236, 1309, 14210, 13, 2632, 363, 103060, 6041, 6824, 364, 8093, 415, 25, 220, 18, 50, 16, 659, 275, 13, 220, 18, 50, 16, 871, 336, 28, 16, 11, 451, 28, 15, 350, 50, 936, 643, 28, 16, 13, 265, 30, 4339, 5478, 275, 871, 305, 28, 16, 364, 97431, 41184, 25, 643, 3080, 45, 28, 16, 11, 305, 28, 16, 4763, 364, 192949, 25, 451, 187706, 28, 15, 10, 16, 28, 16, 14210, 4763, 364, 19226, 6824, 25, 336, 10, 43, 28, 16, 10, 15, 28, 16, 14210, 4763, 364, 84787, 1606, 363, 625, 27927, 364, 84787, 6052, 363, 364, 4383, 12698, 1273, 679, 6086, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 461, 160761, 25, 363, 6], "n_tokens": 1479, "words": 744, "band_lo": 373.0, "band_hi": 1492.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to determine which transition is not permitted given constraints: initial NN bound system in 1S0 state (spin singlet S=0, L=0, J=0, isospin? Not given but final NN T=0. The particle X emitted with intrinsic parity -1, and of orbital angular momentum l (lowercase letter s,p,d,f denote l=0,1,2,3). Need to conserve total angular momentum, parity, and also Pauli constraint: T(NN)= S(NN) + L(NN) +1 (mod 2). Final NN state characterized by spin S(NN), orbital L(NN), total J = coupling of S+L (or vector). The emitted particle X carries angular momentum l relative to NN? We'll assume total J initial = 0, so J_final (NN) + l must combine to total J=0 (vector sum). Since initial J=0, final total angular momentum must be 0. That implies the NN pair's total J (call J_NN) must equal l (to couple to 0). Actually coupling of two angular momenta J_NN and l to total 0 requires J_NN = l and they are antiparallel (coupling with opposite). In terms of magnitude, must be equal. So allowed if J_NN = l.\n\nParity: Intrinsic parity of X = -1. The parity of final state = (parity of NN pair)*(intrinsic parity of X)*(-1)^l (because orbital angular momentum between X and NN gives factor (-1)^l). The NN pair's intrinsic parity? Each nucleon has intrinsic parity +1, so two nucleons product +1. Their relative orbital between them L(NN) contributes factor (-1)^{L(NN)}. So parity final = (-1)^{L(NN)} * (-1) * (-1)^l = (-1)^{L(NN)+l+1}. Initial parity: NN bound state parity = (-1)^{L_initial}= (-1)^0 = +1. So parity conservation: (-1)^{L(NN)+l+1}=+1 => L(NN)+l+1 even => L(NN)+l odd? Wait: if exponent is even -> +1. So L+ l +1 = even => L + l = odd. So L(NN)+l must be odd.\n\nNow also Pauli constraint: For two nucleons (identical fermions) total wavefunction antisymmetric under exchange. Isospin T(NN)=0 is symmetric? Need to recall: nucleons are fermions, total wavefunction = space * spin * isospin * other (like flavor). For T=0, isospin wavefunction is antisymmetric? Let's recall: For two nucleons, isospin I=0 state is antisymmetric under exchange; I=1 is symmetric. Actually for two nucleons, the antisymmetric combination (p n - n p)/√2 corresponds to I=0, which is antisymmetric in isospin. So T=0 is antisymmetric. The overall wavefunction must be antisymmetric. So the product Spin * Space * Isospin must be antisymmetric. Since isospin is antisymmetric, the combined spin*space must be symmetric. That means S(NN) + L(NN) must be even (since exchange of two nucleons changes sign according to (-1)^{L+S}). Indeed requirement: (-1)^{L+S} = +1 (symmetric). So L+S even.\n\nThe Pauli formula given: T(NN) = S(NN) + L(NN) + 1 (mod 2). Let's test: For T=0, we need S+L+1 even? Since 0 = S+L+1 (mod2) => S+L+1 ≡ 0 mod2 => S+L odd? Wait compute: if RHS mod2 =0 => S+L+1 even => S+L odd. So that condition says S+L must be odd when T=0. That seems opposite of my earlier reasoning. Let's double-check: The formula is given: T(NN) = S(NN) + L(NN) + 1 (mod 2). So rearranged: S + L + 1 ≡ T (mod2). For T=0, S+L+1 ≡0 => S+L odd. So they adopt that convention. Let's trust the given formula. So for T=0, S+L must be odd.\n\nNow we must examine each proposed final NN state, given term symbol e.g., 7D1. Interpret 7D1: 2S+1 =7 => S =3 (since 2S+1=7 => S=3). D => L=2. J=1. So final NN: S=3, L=2, J=1. The emitted particle wave: p means l=1.\n\nNow check constraints: (1) angular momentum: we need J_NN = l for total J=0. J_NN =1, l=1 => ok.\n\nParity: need L(NN)+l odd (since L+l must be odd). L=2 (even), l=1 (odd) => sum =3 odd => condition satisfied? Wait we need L+l odd indeed. So ok.\n\nPauli: need S+L odd (since T=0). S=3 (odd), L=2 (even) => sum =5 odd => good.\n\nThus A seems allowed.\n\nOption B: 3D3 + f. 3D3 => 2S+1=3 => S=1. L=2 (D). J=3. Emitted particle: f => l=3.\n\nAngular momentum: need J_NN = l =3? J_NN =3, l=3 good.\n\nParity: L+l =2+3=5 odd => ok.\n\nPauli: S+L =1+2=3 odd => ok.\n\nThus B allowed.\n\nOption C: 3P0 + s. 3P0 => S=1, L=1 (P), J=0. Emitted s => l=0.\n\nNeed J_NN = l => 0 =0 fine.\n\nParity: L+l =1+0=1 odd => ok.\n\nPauli: S+L =1+1=2 even => violation because need odd. So C violates Pauli.\n\nOption D: 3S1 + p. 3S1 => S=1, L=0 (S), J=1. s? Emitted p => l=1.\n\nAngular momentum: J_NN=1, l=1 ok.\n\nParity: L+l=0+1=1 odd ok.\n\nPauli: S+L=1+0=1 odd ok.\n\nThus only C not permitted.\n\nThus answer C.\n\nCheck none other have issues.\n\nThus answer: 'ANSWER: C'", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.2186462419758755e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999879, "logp_correct": -1.204954427116425e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999970197767617, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4440140879658934, "cot_lp_min64": -0.8244247114425032, "frac_below_-5": 0.008867667121418827, "digit_lp_min": -1.2234482765197754, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 1466, "lp_end": -2.264974000354414e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -2.8698289798824925, "p_nocot": 0.05670862406614213, "uplift": 2.8698289798824805, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.197933, "B": 0.326336, "C": 0.056709, "D": 0.419023}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recgXxEgllSGEpELP", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Which of the following stars can be detected with both the ESPRESSO spectrograph at the Paranal Observatory and the HIRES spectrograph at the Keck Observatory? Please refer to the links below for more information about the observatories. It should be noted that with ESPRESSO, stars with apparent V magnitudes brighter than 17 mag can be observed, while with HIRES, stars brighter than 16 V mag can be observed. For the sake of simplicity, let's disregard the pointing limits of the telescopes (such as maximum and minimum elevations) as well as the altitudes of the telescopes.\n\nLinks:\nParanal Observatory: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paranal_Observatory\nW. M. Keck Observatory: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W._M._Keck_Observatory\n\n\nStar1 - RA = 15 deg and DEC = -75 deg, absolute V magnitude of 15.5 mag and located at 10 pc distance from us.\n\nStar2 - RA = 30 deg and DEC = 55 deg, apparent V magnitude of 16.5 mag and located at 5 pc distance from us.\n\nStar3 - RA = 11 h and DEC = 48 deg, apparent V magnitude of 15.5 mag, E(B-V) = 0.6 mag and located at 15 pc distance from us. Note that the total absorption in the V band is related to the color excess in B-V with a coefficient equal to 3.1.\n\nStar4 - RA = 85 deg and DEC = -48 deg, absolute V magnitude of 15.5 mag, E(B-V) = 0.4 mag and located at 10 pc distance from us. Note that the total absorption in the V band is related to the color excess in B-V with a coefficient equal to 3.1.\n\nStar5 - RA = 10 h and DEC = 60 deg, absolute V magnitude of 16.5 mag and located at 5 pc distance from us.\n\nA) Star4 and Star5\nB) Star1 and Star4\nC) Star2 and Star3\nD) Star3 and Star5\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Which of the following stars can be detected with both the ESPRESSO spectrograph at the Paranal Observatory and the HIRES spectrograph at the Keck Observatory? Please refer to the links below for more information about the observatories. It should be noted that with ESPRESSO, stars with apparent V magnitudes brighter than 17 mag can be observed, while with HIRES, stars brighter than 16 V mag can be observed. For the sake of simplicity, let's disregard the pointing limits of the telescopes (such as maximum and minimum elevations) as well as the altitudes of the telescopes.\n\nLinks:\nParanal Observatory: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paranal_Observatory\nW. M. Keck Observatory: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W._M._Keck_Observatory\n\n\nStar1 - RA = 15 deg and DEC = -75 deg, absolute V magnitude of 15.5 mag and located at 10 pc distance from us.\n\nStar2 - RA = 30 deg and DEC = 55 deg, apparent V magnitude of 16.5 mag and located at 5 pc distance from us.\n\nStar3 - RA = 11 h and DEC = 48 deg, apparent V magnitude of 15.5 mag, E(B-V) = 0.6 mag and located at 15 pc distance from us. Note that the total absorption in the V band is related to the color excess in B-V with a coefficient equal to 3.1.\n\nStar4 - RA = 85 deg and DEC = -48 deg, absolute V magnitude of 15.5 mag, E(B-V) = 0.4 mag and located at 10 pc distance from us. Note that the total absorption in the V band is related to the color excess in B-V with a coefficient equal to 3.1.\n\nStar5 - RA = 10 h and DEC = 60 deg, absolute V magnitude of 16.5 mag and located at 5 pc distance from us.\n\nA) Star4 and Star5\nB) Star1 and Star4\nC) Star2 and Star3\nD) Star3 and Star5\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recgXxEgllSGEpELP:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11433, 1118, 13701, 665, 413, 28357, 483, 2973, 28612, 14704, 46, 350, 53, 5017, 220, 1422, 3284, 8, 326, 45039, 10002, 350, 53, 5017, 220, 1125, 3284, 741, 2632, 1309, 36582, 631, 49895, 395, 2454, 8253, 350, 180297, 1511, 35