 321, 53546, 350, 3941, 363, 741, 2632, 21700, 483, 418, 2447, 220, 18, 2126, 53546, 12, 19, 5219, 278, 298, 22803, 71578, 13445, 554, 15286, 316, 5317, 363, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 134962, 363, 13], "n_tokens": 6526, "words": 3653, "band_lo": 1863.0, "band_hi": 7452.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to decide which products are major for two given reactions.\n\nFirst reaction: 2-ethyl-2,6-dimethylcyclohexan-1-one + ethyl acrylate (t-BuOK) -> product A. Likely a Michael addition? t-BuOK deprotonates the carbonyl alpha position giving enolate which attacks ethyl acrylate (Michael addition) giving a β-keto ester. The cyclohexanone is substituted: at C1 carbonyl, at C2 ethyl, at C6 methyl? Actually 2-ethyl-2,6-dimethylcyclohexan-1-one: numbering - carbonyl at C1, so substituents at C2 (ethyl and methyl) and at C6 (methyl). So there is steric hindrance around the carbonyl alpha positions: C2 is already substituted with ethyl and methyl (tetrasubstituted). The other alpha carbon is C6 methyl (adjacent to carbonyl). The enolate could form at either side? Actually enolate carbonyl alpha positions are C2 and C6 (C1 carbonyl). The enolate will be less hindered at C6 because C2 is heavily substituted (ethyl + methyl). So base abstracts the more accessible α-proton at C6? However C6 carbon bearing a methyl; its α-hydrogen? At C6 there is a methyl attached to carbon 6, but the carbon 6 itself is attached to carbonyl carbon (C1)? Actually cyclohexanone numbering: C1 carbonyl, then C2 next carbon, etc. So the alpha carbons are C2 and C6. At C2 we have ethyl substituent (and also a methyl at C2? The name says 2-ethyl-2,6-dimethyl - meaning at carbon 2 there is ethyl, and also methyl at carbon 2? 2,6-dimethyl indicates methyl at both C2 and C6, plus ethyl at C2. So C2 is gem-dimethyl? Actually can't have both ethyl and methyl at same carbon? Yes you can: carbon can have two substituents besides the ring attachments. So C2 has ethyl and methyl substituents. So it's tertiary carbon (attached to two alkyl groups and the ring). So C2 already fully substituted; no α-hydrogen there (because carbon attached to carbonyl has substituents but may still have a hydrogen? Carbon valence 4: attachments are C1 (carbonyl), C3 (ring), ethyl (C chain), methyl. That's 4 bonds. So no H at C2. So only α-hydrogen is at C6 (which has only a methyl as substituent). So enolate forms at C6.\n\nThus we get enolate attack on ethyl acrylate at β-carbon (Michael addition). The product will be a β-keto ester where the new chain (CH2CH2COOEt) attaches to C6 position. The product name: ethyl 3-(3-ethyl-1,3-dimethyl-2-oxocyclohexyl)propanoate? Let's see. The cyclohexanone after addition will have carbonyl at C1 still, now attached at C6 (which becomes C3 of new substituent? Actually the Michael addition creates a bond between C6 carbon and β-carbon of acrylate (the β carbon is the second carbon of acrylate). The chain becomes -CH2-CH2-COOEt attached to C6. The numbering of product: \"ethyl 3-(3-ethyl-1,3-dimethyl-2-oxocyclohexyl)propanoate\" Means the propanoate is attached to cyclohexyl group at position 3 (the 3 of propanoate). That matches typical Michael addition of cyclohexanone to ethyl acrylate gives ethyl 3-(cyclohexanone-2-yl)propanoate? Wait typical Knoevenagel vs Michael? The product may be named as ethyl 3-(...cyclohexyl)propanoate where the cyclohexyl is attached to the 3 position of propanoate. The options A and C have that description, whereas B and D have \"3-(3-ethyl-3,5-dimethyl-4-oxocyclohexyl)\" meaning the carbonyl moved? Let's analyze substitution pattern: Our cyclohexanone after addition still has carbonyl at C1 (now becomes 2-oxocyclohexyl?). But after Michael addition, the carbonyl remains at C1 (now 2-oxocyclohexyl? numbering of substituent maybe different). The product substituent description must reflect where the carbonyl is: In Options A and C they have \"...-1,3-dimethyl-2-oxocyclohexyl\". That means carbonyl at position 2 of cyclohexyl (i.e., cyclohexan-2-one). But original carbonyl was at C1; after addition, the carbonyl stays at same carbon relative to substituent? But naming of substituent may shift because you attach at carbon adjacent to carbonyl (C3?). Wait.\n\nLet's draw: Original cyclohexanone carbonyl at C1. The enolate at C6 attacks acrylate, forming bond between C6 and β-carbon of acrylate (let's call carbon 3 of acrylate chain). After addition, the new carbon chain attaches to C6, making the ring have carbonyl at C1 unchanged, substituents: at C2 ethyl+ methyl; at C6 the new side chain. So the cyclohexyl moiety attached to propanoate is attached via C6, which is adjacent to carbonyl (C1). So the carbonyl is one carbon away from attachment point (C1). In numbering of the substituent (as a cyclohexyl substituent), the carbon bearing the attachment is considered position 1 of that substituent. If carbonyl is at adjacent carbon (C2), then we have 2-oxocyclohexyl. Indeed the substituent is 2-oxocyclohexyl. Also there are substituents: ethyl at carbon? Original ethyl at C2 of original ring corresponds to which position in substituent after we rotate? If attachment point is C6 originally, then counting around ring may place the ethyl at position? Let's figure: Ring positions relative to attachment point (C6 becomes position 1). Then carbonyl carbon (original C1) becomes position 2, ethyl and methyl originally at C2 become position 3 (since moving one step). Methyl at C6 originally becomes attached carbon (position 1) which likely becomes a methyl substituent? Actually C6 had a methyl substituent originally as part of dimethyl. That methyl is attached to the same carbon that is now attachment point; upon forming bond to side chain, the methyl is still there, making the attachment carbon quaternary (bearing methyl, the ring, side chain, and the next carbon). However the product naming options show \"3-ethyl-1,3-dimethyl-2-oxocyclohexyl\" vs \"3-ethyl-3,5-dimethyl-4-oxocyclohexyl\". Differences: where carbonyl is (2-oxocyclohexyl vs 4-oxocyclohexyl) and methyl positions (1,3-dimethyl vs 3,5-dimethyl). Need to check which is correct.\n\nOur ring after addition: Let's assign numbering for the substituent (the cyclohexyl attached to propanoate). Position 1 = carbon bearing the side chain (formerly C6). This carbon also has a methyl substituent (the original 6-methyl). It also is attached to the rest of ring: position 2 is carbonyl carbon (C1). Position 3 is the carbon that originally was C2 (bearing ethyl and methyl). So position 3 has ethyl and methyl substitution. Position 4 is C3 (no substituents). Position 5 is C4; position 6 is C5. There may be a methyl at original C6 again? Already accounted as substituent at position 1. So the substituent has methyl at position 1, ethyl and methyl at position 3. So total dimethyl at positions 1 and 3, ethyl at 3 as well. So we have \"1,3-dimethyl-3-ethyl-2-oxocyclohexyl\". That matches option A and C (they have 1,3-dimethyl and 3-ethyl). Option A and C differ in product B, not A. Options B and D have \"3,5-dimethyl-4-oxocyclohexyl\". That would be if carbonyl shifted to position 4 relative to attachment, which is not the case. Thus A (or C) for product A likely correct.\n\nNow second reaction: 1-nitropropane + (KOH, (E)-but-2-enenitrile, H2O) -> B. Likely a Henry condensation (nitroaldol) between nitroalkane and conjugated nitrile? Actually (E)-but-2-enenitrile is crotononitrile (CH3-CH=CH-CN). With KOH, nitropropane can deprotonate to nitronate and add to the β-carbon of the α,β-unsaturated nitrile (Michael addition) giving nitroalkyl nitrile product. Then aqueous workup may lead to something? Possibly nitroaldol then dehydration to nitroalkene? but typical conditions: KOH, nitroalkane, α,β-unsaturated carbonyl/nitrile leads to Michael addition, then upon hydrolysis can convert nitro to carbonyl? Not sure. Let's examine: 1-nitropropane is CH3-CH2-CH2-NO2? Actually nitropropane: CH3-CH2-CH2-NO2 (nitro group attached to propyl chain). The nitro can be deprotonated at α to nitro (the carbon bearing nitro). That carbon is attached to nitro and two hydrogens originally? In 1-nitropropane, nitro attached to C1 (the terminal carbon). So the deprotonated carbon is the carbon next to nitro? Actually the acidic hydrogen is on carbon α to nitro group (the carbon bearing the nitro). So the anion is CH2=NO2? Wait structure: CH3-CH2-CH2-NO2; the carbon attached to NO2 is C1 (CH2(NO2)). That carbon has two hydrogens. Deprotonation gives nitronate anion CH2(-)-NO2. This attacks β-carbon of crotononitrile (CH3-CH=CH-CN). The Michael addition yields a new bond between the nitronate carbon and the β-carbon (CH). So product after workup (acidic) yields a nitroalkane attached to nitrile chain: CH3-CH2-CH2-NO2-CH2-CH2-CH(CN)-? Wait: add to β-carbon giving chain: nitro carbon attaches to β carbon (the central CH of crotononitrile). So final saturated chain: Nitro carbon becomes attached to carbon that originally was CH (beta). Then you have at the alpha carbon to nitrile (the carbon bearing CN) is now saturated. So product is 2,3-dimethyl-4-nitrobutanenitrile? Let's count.\n\nCrotononitrile: CH3-CH=CH-CN (E). Number: α-carbon (adjacent to CN) is CH (connected to CN and double bond). β-carbon is CH next to it (with CH3 substituent). Wait double bond between Cα (C2) and Cβ (C3). With CH3 at Cβ (trans). So after addition of nitronate at β-carbon (C3), the double bond becomes single, giving chain: CH3-CH(CN)-CH2-CH2-NO2? Actually need to map.\n\nBetter: Write crotononitrile as CH3-CH=CH-CN. Let's label: carbon 1 = nitrile carbon (C≡N), carbon 2 = α-carbon (the one attached to nitrile, double-bonded). carbon 3 = β-carbon (attached to CH3). So structure: CH3-CH=CH-CN, where CH3 attached to carbon 3.\n\nMichael addition of nitronate (CH2NO2-) to β-carbon (C3) results in addition: the nitronate carbon attaches to C3, converting C3 from sp2 to sp3, and the double bond shifts, giving new single bonds: product is CH3-CH(-)CH(NO2CH2CH3?) Wait we also have the nitronate carbon now attached to C3; the nitronate carbon retains the nitro group.\n\nThus product skeleton: CH3-CH=CH-CN + CH2NO2- => CH3-CH-CH(NO2CH2-?) Wait nitronate adds as CH2NO2 attaches via its carbon (the one bearing NO2). So final product: CH3-CH-CH(NO2CH2-?)? Actually the nitronate carbon becomes attached to C3; the nitro group remains attached to that carbon. So we have CH3-CH-CH(NO2)CH2? Let's draw: C3 originally CH (with CH3 substituent). After addition, C3 becomes CH (with CH3 still attached) now also attached to the nitronate carbon (CH2NO2). So product: CH3-CH(??)-CH(CH3)(CH2NO2)-C≡N? Wait C2 is connected to CN and double bond originally; after addition, the double bond is gone, and C2 becomes saturated as CH (attached to CN and to C3). So final chain: CN-CH-CH(CH3)-CH2NO2? Let's more concretely: starting crotononitrile: CH3-CH=CH-CN (the order along chain from left CH3 to CN). After Michael addition, the nucleophile adds to the β-carbon (the second CH, the one attached to CH3). The double bond shifts electrons to the α-carbon, creating an anion that picks up a proton in workup? Actually typical Michael adds nucleophile to β carbon, and the α-carbon becomes an enolate which is protonated. So product after aqueous workup will have saturated chain: CH3-CH(-)CH2- (??). Let's step:\n\nCrotononitrile (E): CH3-CH=CH-CN.\n\nNucleophile (nitronate) attacks the β-carbon (CH). Electron pair moves to α-carbon (C attached to CN) forming carbanion on α-carbon. Then protonation gives saturated nitrile.\n\nThus final product (after protonation) is: CH3-CH(Nu)-CH2-CN, where Nu = CH2NO2 (the nitroalkyl group). Wait the substitution occurs at β-carbon, so product: CH3-CH(CH2NO2)-CH2-CN. Additionally there may be stereochemistry; but major product due to sterics: nitronate attacked from less hindered side? Not relevant.\n\nThus product B expected is 3-methyl-4-nitrobutanenitrile? Let's check naming: The chain length now is 4 carbons from nitrile (C3?). Let's count: Starting from nitrile carbon as carbon 1 (C≡N). Then carbon 2 is the α-carbon (CH2 after protonation). Carbon 3 is β-carbon (now bearing CH3 and CH2NO2 groups). So overall chain: CN-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH2NO2. That's 4 carbon chain (butanitrile). Substituents: methyl at carbon 3, nitro at carbon 4? No nitro at carbon 4 is attached to CH2 of nitro group? Actually nitro group is attached to carbon (CH2NO2) which is carbon 4 (the terminal carbon). So structure is 3-methyl-4-nitrobutanenitrile. That's option C or D (which have B = 3-methyl-4-nitrohexanenitrile? Wait hexanenitrile would have 6 carbon chain). Option C: B = 3-methyl-4-nitrohexanenitrile. That's 6 carbon chain, not our 4. Option D same. Option A and B have B = 2,3-dimethyl-4-nitrobutanenitrile. Our product has methyl at carbon 3 only, not at carbon 2. So which is correct? Let's recalc: Did the nitronate carbon become carbon 4 attached to nitro? Indeed CH2NO2 attached, that carbon is bearing nitro and is carbon 4. So chain C1 nitrile, C2 CH2, C3 CH (with methyl), C4 CH2NO2. That's 4 carbon atoms excluding nitrile carbon? Actually nitrile carbon counts as carbon 1. So total chain length = 4 (butanenitrile). Substituents: methyl at C3. Nitro at C4 (terminal). Also there is possibility of extra methyl at C2? Not.\n\nThus product is 3-methyl-4-nitrobutanenitrile (not among options). Options list \"2,3-dimethyl-4-nitrobutanenitrile\" (A,B) and \"3-methyl-4-nitrohexanenitrile\" (C,D). Something off.\n\nMaybe my chain counting wrong because the nitronate adds to the α-carbon rather than β? Let's check typical reaction of nitroalkane with α,β-unsaturated nitrile under basic conditions gives addition at the β-carbon (Michael). That yields nitroalkane overall length extended by 3 carbons? Let's examine: Nitropropane (CH3CH2CH2NO2). The α carbon to nitro is carbon bearing NO2 (C1). After deprotonation, this carbanion attacks β-carbon of crotononitrile, which is carbon 3 (with CH3). The resulting chain length of the product from nitrile carbon to nitro carbon is longer: nitrile carbon (C1 of crotononitrile) - CH (α) - CH (β) - CH2-NO2 (nitro carbon). That's total of 4 carbons after nitrile carbon? Wait nitrile carbon is part of crotononitrile. The added carbon from nitropropane is CH2 (the nitro carbon). But nitropropane also contains CH2CH3 groups attached to nitro carbon (i.e., the nitro carbon attached to CH2CH3? Actually nitropropane is CH3-CH2-CH2-NO2, nitro carbon is the terminal carbon attached to NO2 (C1). This carbon has CH2CH3 as attachments? No, the nitro carbon is attached to the chain CH2-CH2-CH3 and NO2 only. So after addition, the nitro carbon will be attached to β-carbon and retains the chain CH2-CH3? Wait nitro carbon already attached to CH2 (next carbon) as part of nitropropane. The nitro carbon is attached to CH2 (C2) which is attached to CH3 (C3). So after reaction, the nitro carbon becomes central point connecting to β-carbon plus the rest of nitropropane (C2 and C3). So product will have additional carbon chain beyond nitro carbon: CH2-CH3 as substituents on nitro carbon? Let's draw nitropropane: CH3-CH2-CH2-NO2 with carbon numbering: C1 (nitro carbon) attached to NO2, attached to CH2 (C2). C2 attached to CH2 (C3). C3 attached to CH3? Wait that's total 3 carbons. Actually nitropropane (1-nitropropane) = CH3CH2CH2NO2; the nitro carbon is the carbon bearing NO2 (terminal). So chain: C3-CH2- C2-CH2- C1 attached to NO2? Actually reversed: Starting from nitro carbon (C1) attached to CH2 (C2) attached to CH2 (C3) attached to CH3. That's 3 carbons before nitro, but nitro carbon counts as carbon as well (makes 3 total?). Wait total carbons in nitropropane = 3 (propane). The nitro carbon is carbon 1, then carbon 2 (CH2), carbon 3 (CH3). So nitro carbon attached to CH2 which attached to CH3. So after Michael addition, the nitro carbon attaches to β-carbon of crotononitrile; it also retains the CH2-CH3 substituents (two carbon substituents) making the nitro carbon a substituted carbon (maybe tertiary). So final product chain includes those two carbons as side chain? Actually the nitro carbon becomes a quaternary carbon (connected to β-carbon, NO2, CH2, maybe H? Wait nitro carbon originally had two bonds: to NO2, to CH2 (C2). It also had two hydrogens (since CH2NO2? Wait carbon attached to NO2 is CH2? In 1-nitropropane, nitro carbon is CH2NO2? Let's verify formula: 1-nitropropane: CH3-CH2-CH2-NO2. The carbon attached directly to NO2 is a carbon of the chain; the NO2 is attached to that carbon via N-O? Actually nitro group is –NO2 attached to carbon (C–NO2). So carbon bearing NO2 is a carbon attached to N (which is double-bond O and single O). That carbon is also attached to two hydrogens (CH2) and to CH2 next. So nitro carbon is CH2 (not CH). Good. So nitro carbon is CH2 with NO2 and CH2 chain.\n\nThus after deprotonation, acidity is from hydrogen alpha to nitro: the carbon adjacent to nitro (the carbon bearing NO2) has electrons? Actually more acidic hydrogen is the one on carbon bearing the nitro? Both α hydrogens? Usually nitroalkanes have acidic α-hydrogens on carbon adjacent to nitro, i.e., the carbon bearing the nitro (the carbon bearing the nitro group) has acidic hydrogen(s). So deprotonation gives nitronate on that carbon (CH-). So the nucleophile carbon is the carbon bearing nitro (now carbanion), which will bond to β-carbon. So after addition, the nitro carbon becomes attached to β-carbon, retains its other substituent (CH2-CH3). So product: β-carbon attached to CH(N O2)-CH2-CH3? Wait the nitronate carbon is now carbon bearing NO2 and attached to CH2CH3 (propyl side chain). After addition, it gets also attached to β-carbon, and still has one hydrogen left (originally CH2, lose one H to become CH). So that carbon is now tertiary? Actually CH(NO2)-CH2-CH3 attached to β-carbon.\n\nThus product will have a side chain of three carbons (nitro carbon plus CH2CH3) attached to β-carbon, making overall skeleton longer.\n\nLet's construct final product: Starting from nitrile carbon (C1). Chain: C1 (nitrile) - C2 (α) - C3 (β) now attached to nitro substituent carbon (Cα of nitro prop). Additionally C3 still has methyl substituent from original crotononitrile. So product is: CN-CH2-CH(CH3)-C(CNO2)(CH2CH3). That's a nitrogen? Actually C(CNO2)(CH2CH3) is carbon bearing nitro group and ethyl substituent. This carbon is carbon 4 (attached to nitrile chain). So overall skeleton is not linear; it's branched at carbon 4.\n\nBut naming options: \"2,3-dimethyl-4-nitrobutanenitrile\": that would be chain of 4 carbons nitrile at carbon1, nitro at carbon4, methyls at carbon2 and 3 (both). This corresponds to linear chain with nitro at terminal and methyls at 2 and 3 (like CH3-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2NO2). That does not account for extra ethyl group from nitropropane (but nitropropane only contributed CH2-CH3 as substituent attached to nitro carbon; that would be extra carbon chain beyond nitro carbon, leading to longer chain). However perhaps the nitro carbon after addition loses the ethyl substituent because the deprotonated carbon is the one bearing nitro and the CH2-CH3 chain is not considered substituents? Wait the carbon bearing nitro originally is CH2 attached to CH2CH3. After deprotonation, the carbon becomes CH(-) attached to NO2 and CH2CH3 (still). After attack, that carbon attaches to β-carbon, so now carbon attached to nitrile chain, NO2, CH2CH3, and possibly hydrogen (since lost one H). That gives a carbon with four substituents (quaternary) - which is not possible because carbon valence 4; the substituents are nitrile chain carbon, NO2, CH2CH3, and H (makes 4). So the product is not linear but branched at C4 with ethyl side chain. The naming of such product would not be \"butanenitrile\" but maybe \"2-ethyl-3-methyl-4-nitrobutanenitrile\"? But not in options.\n\nMaybe the reaction scenario is a Henry (nitroaldol) condensation between nitropropane (as nucleophile) and crotononitrile (as electrophile), followed by dehydration (Loss of water) to give nitroalkene? Not.\n\nAlternate possibility: The reaction is a crossed nitroaldol (Michael) leading to a β-nitro nitrile product that after workup gives 2,3-dimethyl-4-nitrobutanenitrile. Perhaps they consider nitropropane as providing only CH2NO2 (nitro group) and the remainder of propyl chain becomes methyl groups? Let's examine: Nitropropane (CH3-CH2-CH2NO2). The nitronate is at carbon bearing NO2 (C1). After attack, that carbon becomes attached to β-carbon. The remaining chain CH2-CH3 attaches as substituent on that carbon (makes it 2-ethyl). If after protonation, the nitro carbon may lose the ethyl substituent via fragmentation? Not typical.\n\nMaybe I misinterpret; the reaction may be a condensation giving a 1,3-dinitro product, but options show only nitro at position 4.\n\nLet's evaluate possible major product based on steric hindrance and product stability: For the Michael addition, the nitro anion will attack the less hindered face of the unsaturated nitrile. The unsaturated nitrile has a methyl at β-carbon (C3). Attack at the β-carbon occurs, generating a new stereocenter. Steric hindrance may prefer formation of the product where the nitronate adds anti to the methyl (trans). But product naming doesn't address stereochemistry.\n\nNow the options for B: either 2,3-dimethyl-4-nitrobutanenitrile (dimethyl at positions 2 and 3) or 3-methyl-4-nitrohexanenitrile (hexanenitrile chain, maybe representing branched product). The hexanenitrile indicates longer chain (6 carbon). Could result if the nitropropane adds via its terminal carbon, and the remaining chain merges resulting in overall chain length of 6? Let's compute length for that: nitrile carbon (1), α-carbon (2), β-carbon (3), nitro carbon (4), plus its attached CH2 (5) and CH3 (6). That would be hexanenitrile (6 carbons). Indeed, if we consider the chain as linear by including the ethyl substituent of nitropropane as continuation, i.e., the nitro carbon attaches to β-carbon and its CH2CH3 chain becomes part of the main chain? That would generate a hexanenitrile with nitro at carbon 4 (the former nitro carbon) and methyl at carbon 3 (original methyl of crotononitrile). So product would be 3-methyl-4-nitrohexanenitrile. This matches options C or D.\n\nThus the chain is linear: nitrile carbon (C1), next carbon is α (C2) after protonation becomes CH2; next carbon is β (C3) bearing methyl (original methyl); next carbon is nitro carbon (C4) attached to NO2; next carbon is CH2 (C5) from nitropropane; final carbon is CH3 (C6). So overall hexanenitrile. This seems plausible as the product after protonation and not counting branching. Indeed the bond formed is between β-carbon and the carbon bearing NO2; the rest of nitropropane chain continues as substituents, making linear chain.\n\nThus B major product should be 3-methyl-4-nitrohexanenitrile. Option C or D. Difference between C and D is the product A selection: both C and D have same A (option C: A = ethyl 3-(3-ethyl-1,3-dimethyl-2-oxocyclohexyl)propanoate, B = 3-methyl-4-nitrohexanenitrile) and D is same A? D says A = ethyl 3-(3-ethyl-3,5-dimethyl-4-oxocyclohexyl)propanoate, B = 3-methyl-4-nitrohexanenitrile. So we must decide which A is correct. As argued earlier, A should be with 1,3-dimethyl (option C). So combine with B being 3-methyl-4-nitrohexanenitrile leads to option C.\n\nThus answer: OPTION C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -9.536738616588991e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999977, "logp_correct": -2.2982200812842297e-15, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000022, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6314514464180969, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2392461855480406, "frac_below_-5": 0.012590204206970674, "digit_lp_min": -9.319664001464844, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0001255195093108341, "n_cot_