, 17, 11, 11519, 36, 314, 363, 533, 487, 14, 17, 659, 478, 14, 17, 659, 16, 314, 220, 21, 533, 220, 702, 14, 17, 659, 16, 314, 220, 21, 533, 20, 659, 16, 314, 17, 13, 13311, 3503, 15135, 173691, 13, 30456, 413, 1001, 15883, 3643, 350, 99089, 472, 220, 16, 8, 326, 1001, 363, 93363, 350, 99089, 472, 220, 16, 741, 4569, 15248, 44053, 4368, 220, 12536, 15, 350, 34, 28, 46, 8, 659, 220, 16461, 15, 350, 34, 93363, 741, 11899, 532, 13091, 11245, 350, 6830, 3952, 88, 459, 18655, 741, 2632, 363, 21, 39, 702, 46, 17, 23440, 364, 4383, 1273, 80232, 1402, 34, 21, 39, 899, 46, 25, 11519, 36, 314, 220, 21, 533, 899, 14, 17, 659, 16, 28, 21, 533, 21, 659, 16, 314, 16, 13, 12817, 1001, 11519, 36, 11, 1481, 413, 4784, 363, 93363, 503, 363, 28, 46, 11, 625, 2973, 13, 58989, 679, 2973, 15883, 3643, 326, 32353, 1900, 11, 12604, 1001, 382, 997, 328, 2684, 20483, 3566, 1299, 469, 340, 30, 4037, 6960, 364, 34, 21, 39, 899, 46, 17, 25, 11519, 36, 314, 21, 533, 899, 14, 17, 659, 16, 314, 16, 1217, 350, 21910, 532, 625, 60500, 741, 1328, 1481, 3763, 1001, 11519, 36, 11, 889, 581, 679, 11716, 395, 1920, 364, 34, 21, 39, 702, 46, 25, 11519, 36, 314, 21, 533, 702, 14, 17, 659, 16, 314, 220, 21, 533, 20, 659, 16, 314, 17, 11, 2684, 472, 363, 21, 39, 702, 46, 17, 889, 53426, 448, 532, 13, 3072, 581, 1309, 532, 13091, 11245, 326, 15883, 3643, 532, 871, 540, 5153, 1001, 532, 395, 15883, 3643, 326, 10112, 532, 13091, 2684, 532, 395, 18655, 11, 1118, 382, 1920, 532, 13, 2632, 363, 21, 39, 702, 46, 350, 690, 532, 8, 8535, 679, 2973, 15883, 3643, 326, 42464, 13, 30456, 413, 469, 690, 350, 1750, 42464, 8, 889, 11245, 220, 4095, 15, 532, 13091, 24791, 4310, 13, 2632, 1636, 5769, 363, 21, 39, 702, 46, 17, 364, 84787, 6052, 7158, 355, 364, 4383, 4842, 19018, 25, 284, 61176, 220, 2548, 66938, 316, 363, 17, 39, 20, 46, 10, 350, 2548, 8, 503, 10271, 17, 39, 350, 2548, 741, 2214, 363, 21, 39, 702, 46, 17, 11, 189000, 665, 3644, 484, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 355, 364], "n_tokens": 822, "words": 417, "band_lo": 212.5, "band_hi": 850.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret spectral data.\n\nFTIR: very broad absorption at 3000 cm^-1 indicates O-H stretch (broad) likely alcohol or carboxylic acid. Broad peak at 3000 (maybe 3200-3500). Two other strong absorption peaks at 1700 and 1650 cm^-1. 1700 cm^-1 typical C=O (carbonyl) of ketone, aldehyde, acid, ester. 1650 could be C=C alkene stretch (vinyl). So we have both carbonyl and C=C, plus O-H. Could be an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl (enone) like enal or enone, which shows both C=O (~1680) and C=C (1640-1680) peaks. The broad O-H indicates maybe a carboxylic acid, which also gives C=O (~1710) and O-H broad. But carboxylic acid also shows C=C if unsaturated acid.\n\n1H NMR shows vinyl hydrogens, consistent with C=C. Also O-H may show.\n\nMass fragment at m/z 45. What fragment is 45? Could be C3H5O+, or CH3CHO+ (acetyl, 43), but 45 could be COOH? Let's think: 45 could be CH3CO+ (acetyl) is 43. C2H5O+ is 45 (C2H5O·?). Actually C2H5O+ (C2H5O+ = 45). Or COOH radical (CO2H) is 45? Let's compute: CO2H+ (C O2 H) mass = 12 + 32 + 1 = 45. Yes, the fragment of a carboxylic acid often gives m/z 45 (CO2H+). So suggests carboxylic acid.\n\nThus likely molecule is unsaturated carboxylic acid with formula C6H10O2? Let's calculate degrees of unsaturation: For C6H10O2, DBE = C - H/2 + N/2 +1 = 6 - 10/2 +1 = 6 -5 +1 =2. Two double bond equivalents. Could be one carbonyl (counts as 1) and one C=C (counts as 1). That matches FTIR 1700 (C=O) + 1650 (C=C). Plus O-H broad (carboxylic acid). So C6H10O2 fits.\n\nCheck other formulas:\n\nC6H12O: DBE = 6 -12/2 +1=6 -6 +1 =1. Only one DBE, would be either C=C or C=O, not both. Can't have both carbonyl and alkene, unless one is part of same functional group like enol? Not likely.\n\nC6H12O2: DBE =6 -12/2 +1 =1 also (since O not counted). This would allow one DBE, but we have evidence for two.\n\nC6H10O: DBE =6 -10/2 +1 = 6 -5 +1 =2, same as C6H10O2 but lacking an O. But we need O-H broad and carbonyl O => at least one O for carbonyl and maybe O-H same O for acid, which is two O. So C6H10O (one O) can't have both carbonyl and OH. Could be enone (no OH) but broad 3000 O-H wouldn't exist. So best fit C6H10O2.\n\nThus answer choice A.\n\nCheck mass fragment: m/z 45 corresponds to C2H5O+ (45) or CO2H (45). For C6H10O2, cleavage can give that.\n\nThus answer: A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -5.245195097813848e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999977003, "logp_correct": -2.2997177307741425e-12, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000023, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5172579443729345, "cot_lp_min64": -1.0268648306059767, "frac_below_-5": 0.007416563658838072, "digit_lp_min": -1.2234807014465332, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0008105330052785575, "n_cot_tokens": 809, "lp_end": -0.04038984701037407, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -0.39065566825884845, "p_nocot": 0.6766130952979873, "uplift": 0.39065566825654874, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.676613, "B": 0.319609, "C": 0.003551, "D": 0.000227}}, "force_keep": false, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "rechxBmTTAWysQUYs", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Satellites in low Earth orbit have detect thousands of astronomical objects, with localization precision in the range of about 100-200 arc minutes. The distribution of astronomical objects is isotropic.\n\nWhich set of given distances from the Sun, could be possible for these objects?\n\nA) 1 thousand light years, or 100 thousand light years.\nB) 1 million light years, or 1 billion light years.\nC) 10 light minutes, or 100 light seconds.\nD) 10 light years, or 100 light years.\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Satellites in low Earth orbit have detect thousands of astronomical objects, with localization precision in the range of about 100-200 arc minutes. The distribution of astronomical objects is isotropic.\n\nWhich set of given distances from the Sun, could be possible for these objects?\n\nA) 1 thousand light years, or 100 thousand light years.\nB) 1 million light years, or 1 billion light years.\nC) 10 light minutes, or 100 light seconds.\nD) 10 light years, or 100 light years.\n", "true_answer": "B", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "rechxBmTTAWysQUYs:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 4928, 13, 3196, 28426, 25, 102749, 306, 4465, 16464, 61353, 11839, 13369, 328, 178600, 11736, 11, 483, 99224, 27303, 1078, 220, 1353, 12, 1179, 26922, 5438, 13, 54420, 382, 103932, 71571, 13, 21580, 920, 328, 4335, 48294, 591, 290, 11628, 2023, 413, 4149, 395, 1879, 11736, 30, 20953, 3331, 484, 99224, 27303, 350, 1353, 12, 1179, 26922, 5438, 8, 382, 1078, 220, 16, 13, 22, 316, 220, 18, 13, 18, 18210, 11, 1118, 382, 9560, 13, 4569, 7890, 1299, 91671, 1606, 413, 3741, 316, 2698, 750, 11736, 483, 484, 38962, 27303, 13, 1843, 103932, 71571, 11, 316, 11839, 13369, 11, 48294, 2804, 413, 2238, 484, 38962, 17365, 66938, 316, 1236, 9811, 9318, 13, 3072, 4928, 7038, 1078, 392, 796, 85720, 69029, 89460, 306, 451, 10411, 665, 11839, 11736, 4493, 3543, 1299, 147319, 387, 30, 50557, 59698, 13, 30391, 495, 382, 1078, 11628, 2011, 54406, 350, 95807, 889, 357, 2411, 1023, 4595, 1078, 392, 25649, 8706, 933, 11736, 1, 5862, 11628, 11, 28385, 4493, 41656, 30, 164973, 27303, 220, 1353, 12, 1179, 26922, 5438, 314, 1078, 220, 16, 13, 21, 6793, 316, 220, 18, 13, 18, 172051, 21926, 81371, 13, 2214, 48294, 25, 538, 290, 11736, 553, 3518, 36790, 2420, 11, 319, 1940, 4213, 5862, 11628, 350, 702, 4207, 5438, 6574, 9324, 16464, 6968, 373, 382, 220, 23, 4207, 5438, 741, 2632, 220, 702, 4207, 5438, 6574, 9324, 328, 67062, 30, 3072, 103932, 71571, 12545, 1481, 1815, 413, 5862, 11628, 10112, 30, 5551, 103932, 71571, 12545, 591, 11628, 136304, 538, 11736, 553, 3518, 3120, 43920, 30, 623, 103932, 34271, 23379, 4150, 51041, 11, 15674, 45482, 37247, 30, 3072, 28385, 27303, 328, 3120, 18210, 1481, 11095, 316, 9560, 2915, 45131, 26, 316, 679, 1991, 108091, 2620, 103932, 2283, 2905, 11, 290, 11736, 2804, 413, 4150, 4951, 484, 290, 38962, 27303, 2928, 59684, 261, 23939, 9811, 8088, 43621, 30, 118045, 2411, 1023, 1682, 4410, 48294, 25, 220, 16, 5749, 15280, 503, 945, 30, 3072, 220, 1353, 12, 1179, 26922, 5438, 382, 9560, 11, 8117, 316, 9560, 163252, 44942, 25, 540, 220, 16, 5749, 15280, 11, 220, 17, 6793, 66938, 316, 6574, 2467, 11, 1302, 15280, 5251, 13, 4569, 2023, 4321, 1991, 11736, 7890, 8975, 13, 5551, 28385, 328, 13369, 23379, 484, 28385, 5967, 382, 625, 9913, 656, 38962, 17365, 889, 656, 59110, 13, 623, 4335, 3880, 25, 355, 25, 220, 16, 74, 15280, 503, 220, 1353, 74, 15280, 350, 47163, 3518, 10282, 5781, 16638, 741, 418, 25, 220, 16, 44, 15280, 503, 220, 16, 33, 15280, 350, 3082, 8298, 37247, 741, 363, 25, 220, 702, 4207, 5438, 350, 79475, 17624, 2420, 936, 503, 220, 1353, 4207, 12068, 350, 96488, 3760, 741, 415, 25, 220, 702, 15280, 503, 220, 1353, 15280, 350, 98556, 2698, 17528, 3991, 34405, 920, 328, 48294, 2023, 413, 4149, 30, 2214, 103932, 71571, 12545, 11, 11525, 2804, 413, 4150, 4951, 484, 17307, 8224, 103932, 71571, 1127, 575, 2560, 4213, 29212, 15674, 45482, 37247, 11, 11615, 10282, 5781, 16638, 30, 3604, 48294, 328, 6574, 16, 391, 423, 290, 28714, 14518, 103932, 71571, 13, 3604, 220, 702, 12, 1353, 15280, 11, 12545, 382, 625, 103932, 71571, 2322, 54412, 553, 306, 11091, 37247, 16499, 13, 2632, 6960, 6052, 418, 350, 16, 5749, 4207, 2101, 503, 220, 16, 12842, 4207, 2101, 741, 5551, 220, 16, 391, 423, 382, 1078, 9324, 316, 1958, 511, 8602, 382, 220, 17, 13, 20, 391, 423, 13, 11048, 129137, 11615, 391, 6344, 13, 2632, 103932, 71571, 12545, 328, 11525, 1481, 413, 15674, 45482, 37247, 11, 120152, 13, 164973, 328, 220, 1353, 12, 1179, 26922, 5438, 382, 81371, 11, 889, 2928, 665, 29006, 316, 484, 7967, 13, 2632, 6052, 418, 364, 84787, 9570, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 418], "n_tokens": 626, "words": 406, "band_lo": 284.0, "band_hi": 1136.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the question. Described: satellites in low Earth orbit detect thousands of astronomical objects, with localization precision about 100-200 arc minutes. Distribution is isotropic. Which set of given distances from the Sun could be possible for these objects? Must consider that localization precision (100-200 arc minutes) is about 1.7 to 3.3 degrees, which is huge. That seems like they'd only be able to localize objects with that angular precision. If isotropic, to detect thousands, distances must be such that angular resolution corresponds to some physical scale. But question probably about \"parallax\"? Satellite in LEO can detect objects via something like occultation? Actually unclear. Perhaps this is about Sun-synchronous (?), but I think they talk about \"astronomical objects\" near Sun, detection via imaging? Localization precision 100-200 arc minutes = about 1.6° to 3.3°. That's coarse. For distances: if the objects are within Solar system, e.g., near Sun (10 light minutes ~ distance Earth-Sun is 8 light minutes). So 10 light minutes ~ distance of Mercury? But isotropic distribution would then be near Sun maybe? However isotropic distribution from Sun unrealistic if objects are within few AU? The isotropy suggests far distant, extragalactic? But detection precision of few degrees would correspond to huge error circles; to have many detections isotropically, the objects must be far enough that the angular precision still yields a decent physical cross-section? Honestly think they want large distances: 1 million ly or more? But 100-200 arc minutes is huge, leading to huge positional uncertainty: at 1 million ly, 2° corresponds to ~35,000 ly across. That could cover many objects seems fine. However detection of thousands suggests that detection limit is not limited by angular resolution but by brightness. The given options: A: 1k ly or 100k ly (both within Milky Way). B: 1M ly or 1B ly (intergalactic). C: 10 light minutes (inside solar system), or 100 light seconds (much less). D: 10 ly or 100 ly (within local neighborhood).\n\nWhich set of distances could be possible? For isotropic distribution, sources must be far enough that sky looks isotropic – i.e., essentially extragalactic, beyond Milky Way? At distances of ~1 Mly the universe appears isotropic. At 10-100 ly, distribution is not isotropic—stars are in galactic plane. So likely answer B (1 million light years or 1 billion light years). However 1 Mly is about distance to Andromeda is 2.5 Mly. Many galaxies beyond Mpc. So isotropic distribution of sources would be extragalactic, plausible. Localization of 100-200 arc minutes is coarse, but still can locate to that region. So answer B.\n\nThus respond: ANSWER: B", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.4570661000543623e-06, "p_correct": 0.999999999999553, "logp_correct": -4.4699961590386134e-13, "letter_mass": 0.9999976158202266, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -1.0252118394055603, "cot_lp_min64": -1.6496258730594702, "frac_below_-5": 0.03099510603588907, "digit_lp_min": -2.6207115650177, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.10769221931695938, "n_cot_tokens": 613, "lp_end": -0.04911908134818077, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -7.008928150824323, "p_nocot": 0.0009037767818233501, "uplift": 7.008928150823876, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.002784, "B": 0.000904, "C": 0.005201, "D": 0.991112}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "reciHTTRFPWrbhWXA", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: \"Scientist aims to detect two genes using multiplex PCR by using two sets of primers.\nDNA bands were visualized by putting the gel on a UV transilluminator and clear bands were visualized having a size of 600 bp and another one at 40 bp. Then, he used QIAxcel Advanced System and saw three bands: at 590 bp, at 605 bp, and also at 40 bp\nAll of the following statements are incorrect about the previous experiment except:\"\n\nA) Although the scientist detected the two genes, he has to redo the experiment and redesign suitable primers\nB) The two genes were successfully detected at 600 bp and at 40 bp\nC) Multiplex PCR is unsuccessful in the amplification of the two genes so we have to use QIAxcel Advanced System\nD) Although the scientist used two sets of primers, he detected three genes (at 590 bp, at 605 bp, and also at 40 bp), indicating the high efficiency of these primers.\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: \"Scientist aims to detect two genes using multiplex PCR by using two sets of primers.\nDNA bands were visualized by putting the gel on a UV transilluminator and clear bands were visualized having a size of 600 bp and another one at 40 bp. Then, he used QIAxcel Advanced System and saw three bands: at 590 bp, at 605 bp, and also at 40 bp\nAll of the following statements are incorrect about the previous experiment except:\"\n\nA) Although the scientist detected the two genes, he has to redo the experiment and redesign suitable primers\nB) The two genes were successfully detected at 600 bp and at 40 bp\nC) Multiplex PCR is unsuccessful in the amplification of the two genes so we have to use QIAxcel Advanced System\nD) Although the scientist used two sets of primers, he detected three genes (at 590 bp, at 605 bp, and also at 40 bp), indicating the high efficiency of these primers.\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "reciHTTRFPWrbhWXA:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 4928, 13, 3164, 12886, 261, 57204, 6700, 316, 11839, 1920, 34553, 2360, 170660, 71681, 483, 1920, 11982, 328, 147812, 13, 23224, 29650, 11256, 2110, 656, 16019, 8063, 402, 29490, 1643, 492, 12276, 1246, 25, 5364, 29650, 540, 220, 6718, 52357, 326, 220, 1723, 52357, 13, 7801, 1023, 2061, 1486, 7068, 87, 4610, 29017, 1219, 350, 15558, 67068, 194486, 3599, 276, 8, 326, 8274, 3407, 29650, 25, 220, 36351, 52357, 11, 220, 37142, 52357, 11, 326, 220, 1723, 52357, 364, 2167, 679, 20673, 355, 11989, 13, 19792, 316, 11410, 290, 9710, 484, 382, 6145, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 290, 392, 25035, 1, 117551, 25, 2545, 328, 290, 3992, 20673, 553, 25570, 1078, 290, 5880, 12527, 6384, 25, 2632, 581, 1309, 290, 1001, 484, 382, 6145, 741, 41021, 30532, 2454, 364, 32, 8, 392, 25731, 290, 57204, 28357, 290, 1920, 34553, 11, 501, 853, 316, 109462, 290, 12527, 326, 92463, 13127, 147812, 4050, 1328, 5003, 1309, 316, 109462, 2236, 147812, 625, 13127, 13, 2763, 495, 6145, 30, 623, 4871, 8063, 7398, 29650, 540, 220, 6718, 326, 220, 1723, 11, 1118, 3981, 5930, 16174, 10112, 30, 5551, 1486, 7068, 87, 4610, 7398, 1920, 29650, 2846, 220, 6718, 350, 36351, 326, 220, 37142, 8, 34700, 10112, 59007, 16416, 168100, 503, 13240, 6051, 409, 30, 623, 14324, 328, 1920, 29650, 5862, 220, 6718, 23379, 484, 147812, 1340, 413, 36766, 1920, 6771, 2349, 3891, 11, 10112, 625, 4857, 13, 54458, 21611, 13240, 2364, 6086, 11, 889, 2226, 501, 1309, 316, 109462, 30, 138743, 13, 3072, 9710, 5003, 392, 25731, 290, 57204, 28357, 290, 1920, 34553, 11, 501, 853, 316, 109462, 290, 12527, 326, 92463, 13127, 147812, 4050, 21926, 261, 6445, 484, 290, 12527, 382, 625, 15048, 13, 2763, 484, 1343, 30, 30456, 413, 9474, 6145, 364, 33, 8, 392, 976, 1920, 34553, 1504, 12836, 28357, 540, 220, 6718, 52357, 326, 540, 220, 1723, 52357, 4050, 2763, 484, 1343, 30, 623, 8063, 14414, 2617, 29650, 11, 889, 1486, 7068, 87, 4610, 7398, 220, 36351, 326, 220, 37142, 11, 625, 261, 4590, 220, 6718, 8177, 13, 4569, 23379, 290, 11965, 28385, 1340, 413, 94365, 13, 2632, 6445, 3572, 413, 45298, 6145, 30, 3072, 10705, 10112, 1485, 2236, 290, 20395, 29650, 1504, 123648, 364, 34, 8, 392, 44291, 3179, 71681, 382, 91036, 306, 290, 168100, 328, 290, 1920, 34553, 813, 581, 679, 316, 1199, 1486, 7068, 87, 4610, 29017, 1219, 4050, 4569, 23379, 290, 71681, 9363, 326, 1309, 316, 1199, 1486, 7068, 87, 4610, 7665, 13, 21926, 625, 36574, 25, 1486, 7068, 87, 4610, 382, 261, 28385, 2420, 11, 625, 168100, 13, 623, 71681, 1888, 382, 2684, 26, 1486, 7068, 87, 4610, 1327, 140814, 13, 2632, 1485, 364, 35, 8, 392, 25731, 290, 57204, 2061, 1920, 11982, 328, 147812, 11, 501, 28357, 3407, 34553, 350, 266, 220, 36351, 52357, 11, 540, 220, 37142, 52357, 11, 326, 1217, 540, 220, 1723, 52357, 936, 34700, 290, 1932, 17456, 328, 1879, 147812, 3692, 5535, 48105, 3407, 34553, 30, 50557, 1354, 553, 1606, 1920, 3783, 34553, 13, 623, 5018, 8177, 5862, 220, 6718, 6960, 59007, 16416, 13, 2632, 625, 1932, 17456, 13, 2632, 1485, 364, 84787, 6960, 290, 6145, 350, 3086, 392, 25035, 1243, 1606, 6145, 9710, 8, 382, 355, 13, 5551, 1309, 316, 14866, 161791, 25, 623, 4928, 5003, 392, 2594, 328, 290, 3992, 20673, 553, 25570, 1078, 290, 5880, 12527, 6384, 25, 4050, 2632, 581, 1309, 5230, 290, 1001, 484, 382, 6145, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 625, 25570, 741, 95819, 2418, 364, 32, 5003, 92463, 147812, 6118, 13, 623, 14324, 328, 3407, 29650, 23379, 13240, 2364, 6086, 13, 2632, 4275, 6145, 484, 501, 4414, 316, 92463, 13, 2632, 355, 382, 625, 25570, 26, 4275, 290, 6145, 9710, 13, 2632, 6052, 355, 364, 84787, 4733, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 355, 13], "n_tokens": 637, "words": 460, "band_lo": 230.0, "band_hi": 920.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the question. They describe a scientist trying to detect two genes using multiplex PCR with two sets of primers. DNA bands visualized by putting gel on UV transilluminator: clear bands at 600 bp and 40 bp. Then they used QIAxcel Advanced System (capillary electrophoresis) and saw three bands: 590 bp, 605 bp, and 40 bp.\n\nWe have statements A-D. Need to identify the statement that is correct (i.e., the \"except\" wording: All of the following statements are incorrect about the previous experiment except: So we need the one that is correct). Let's analyze each.\n\nA) \"Although the scientist detected the two genes, he has to redo the experiment and redesign suitable primers\". This says need to redo because primers not suitable. Is this correct? The initial gel shows bands at 600 and 40, which match expected sizes maybe? However QIAxcel shows two bands around 600 (590 and 605) indicating maybe nonspecific amplification or primer dimers? The presence of two bands near 600 suggests that primers may be generating two similar size products, maybe not specific. Might indicate primer design issues, but does he need to redo? Possibly. But statement says \"Although the scientist detected the two genes, he has to redo the experiment and redesign suitable primers\". That's a claim that the experiment is not reliable. Is that true? Could be considered correct.\n\nB) \"The two genes were successfully detected at 600 bp and at 40 bp\". Is that true? The gel showed those bands, but QIAxcel shows 590 and 605, not a single 600 band. That suggests the earlier detection may be inaccurate. So claim might be partially correct? But overall maybe false because the observed bands were ambiguous.\n\nC) \"Multiplex PCR is unsuccessful in the amplification of the two genes so we have to use QIAxcel Advanced System\". That suggests the PCR failed and need to use QIAxcel instead. That's not logical: QIAxcel is a detection system, not amplification. The PCR product is same; QIAxcel just resolves. So false.\n\nD) \"Although the scientist used two sets of primers, he detected three genes (at 590 bp, at 605 bp, and also at 40 bp), indicating the high efficiency of these primers.\" Detected three genes? Actually there are only two target genes. The extra band near 600 likely nonspecific. So not high efficiency. So false.\n\nThus likely the correct (the \"except\": only correct statement) is A. However need to verify nuance: The question says \"All of the following statements are incorrect about the previous experiment except:\". So we need pick the one that is correct (i.e., not incorrect). Evaluate again.\n\nA says redesign primers needed. The presence of three bands suggests primer design issues. So it's correct that he needs to redesign. So A is not incorrect; it's the correct statement. So answer A.\n\nThus output: ANSWER: A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.7418097943154862e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999997702, "logp_correct": -2.297711466045766e-13, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000002298, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5611281087175485, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9560744025125247, "frac_below_-5": 0.004807692307692308, "digit_lp_min": -0.030076447874307632, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.45394614338874817, "n_cot_tokens": 624, "lp_end": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -10.250072030130259, "p_nocot": 3.535495413673927e-05, "uplift": 10.25007203013003, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 3.5e-05, "B": 0.999928, "C": 2e-06, "D": 3.5e-05}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "reciaJPlR6Z1IHN3y", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: DFT calculations have been run for the following compounds: 2-styrylcyclohexane-1-carbaldehyde (compound 1), 2-styrylcycloheptane-1-\ncarbaldehyde (compound 2), and 2-styrylcyclooctane-1-carbaldehyde (compound 3) to predict the equilibrium ratio of the syn vs anti (with regards to substituents on the rings) isomers of the compounds. The experimentally observed ratio of anti:syn for each compound is, 8:1, 3:1, and 1:1 for respectively. DFT predicted Boltzmann factors for each are: 8.6:1, 5.2:1, and 4.8:1 respectively. The calculated values were arrived at through structure optimization and energy calculation for each isomer of each compound. Why is the calculated result accurate for the first compound, and progressively less accurate for the\nothers?\n\nA) It is difficult for DFT optimization algorithms to find the minimum energy state of molecules that have many degrees of freedom, such as compounds 2 and 3.\nB) DFT doesn't accurately model solvent interaction effects, which are more significant on molecules that have more degrees of freedom, such as compounds 2 and 3.\nC) Compounds 2 and 3 do not have any isomer that is strongly favored, indicating that the isomers are very close in energy, and DFT methods do not have the required precision to accurately model such small energy differences (< 1 kcal/mol).\nD) These DFT energy calculations of the Boltzmann factor only sample the lowest energy conformation, and don't take into account other conformations, which contribute more significantly for compounds 2 and 3.\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: DFT calculations have been run for the following compounds: 2-styrylcyclohexane-1-carbaldehyde (compound 1), 2-styrylcycloheptane-1-\ncarbaldehyde (compound 2), and 2-styrylcyclooctane-1-carbaldehyde (compound 3) to predict the equilibrium ratio of the syn vs anti (with regards to substituents on the rings) isomers of the compounds. The experimentally observed ratio of anti:syn for each compound is, 8:1, 3:1, and 1:1 for respectively. DFT predicted Boltzmann factors for each are: 8.6:1, 5.2:1, and 4.8:1 respectively. The calculated values were arrived at through structure optimization and energy calculation for each isomer of each compound. Why is the calculated result accurate for the first compound, and progressively less accurate for the\nothers?\n\nA) It is difficult for DFT optimization algorithms to find the minimum energy state of molecules that have many degrees of freedom, such as compounds 2 and 3.\nB) DFT doesn't accurately model solvent interaction effects, which are more significant on molecules that have more degrees of freedom, such as compounds 2 and 3.\nC) Compounds 2 and 3 do not have any isomer that is strongly favored, indicating that the isomers are very close in energy, and DFT methods do not have the required precision to accurately model such small energy differences (< 1 kcal/mol).\nD) These DFT energy calculations of the Boltzmann factor only sample the lowest energy conformation, and don't take into account other conformations, which contribute more significantly for compounds 2 and 3.\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "reciaJPlR6Z1IHN3y:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 5655, 1118, 30547, 1636, 14066, 395, 290, 70763, 18580, 328, 415, 7114, 35086, 472, 290, 12796, 2349, 21073, 364, 2167, 679, 3407, 43556, 25, 27789, 78, 22803, 1986, 11, 27789, 78, 273, 555, 1986, 11, 27789, 3782, 308, 1986, 483, 261, 26407, 93031, 326, 100539, 133496, 22148, 11823, 13, 623, 31381, 10329, 31023, 1276, 18051, 8805, 591, 220, 23, 25, 16, 350, 9851, 35627, 395, 10329, 8, 316, 220, 18, 25, 16, 316, 220, 16, 25, 16, 11, 10915, 472, 290, 12796, 8625, 12384, 11, 290, 55114, 9809, 33870, 28664, 13, 415, 7114, 35996, 10329, 31023, 1276, 18051, 7271, 29918, 19328, 11652, 25, 220, 23, 13, 21, 25, 16, 350, 8722, 316, 220, 23, 25, 16, 936, 220, 20, 13, 17, 25, 16, 350, 16121, 31381, 220, 18, 25, 16, 936, 220, 19, 13, 23, 25, 16, 350, 16121, 31381, 220, 16, 25, 16, 741, 2632, 415, 7114, 1072, 376, 90695, 35627, 395, 10329, 472, 290, 9809, 14081, 13679, 364, 13903, 30, 63659, 2236, 1261, 5954, 19504, 553, 3291, 46465, 16, 143871, 86398, 936, 415, 7114, 10664, 5025, 38899, 326, 35086, 5025, 3760, 15048, 13, 17796, 363, 5003, 484, 13, 22483, 355, 1078, 24710, 12129, 113963, 395, 1991, 18210, 328, 19165, 1340, 413, 12331, 889, 1023, 2242, 1646, 113963, 26, 889, 945, 13460, 37, 1340, 7075, 6086, 483, 19627, 1953, 43881, 13, 17796, 415, 5003, 1023, 1606, 10176, 23120, 5954, 406, 4690, 11, 625, 19627, 1953, 33081, 11, 1118, 19854, 945, 395, 12384, 35188, 483, 945, 29142, 11, 8117, 316, 290, 10664, 13, 4569, 1217, 120152, 364, 58369, 21646, 25, 623, 19504, 306, 4624, 10217, 9041, 70824, 5025, 3291, 13, 415, 7114, 19271, 10664, 6574, 16, 12, 17, 143871, 86398, 13, 2632, 395, 3291, 19504, 11, 35086, 59410, 13, 8820, 17791, 35188, 679, 1991, 19627, 929, 26, 68244, 19627, 1953, 89652, 665, 7075, 10664, 13, 623, 12527, 6960, 306, 7578, 6664, 1991, 19627, 929, 26, 415, 7114, 4122, 402, 4590, 19627, 259, 1340, 4694, 65215, 13, 2632, 415, 382, 1217, 120152, 364, 34405, 382, 945, 6960, 6052, 30, 623, 4928, 25, 392, 13903, 382, 290, 25702, 1534, 16360, 395, 290, 1577, 36762, 11, 326, 118980, 3760, 16360, 395, 290, 5036, 16842, 3164, 1562, 4149, 14716, 13, 17796, 415, 20866, 19627, 1953, 43881, 26, 1877, 12796, 2349, 12384, 11, 945, 19627, 929, 871, 945, 2915, 13, 17796, 363, 5003, 415, 7114, 625, 31687, 4951, 395, 3291, 5954, 19504, 13, 17659, 120152, 364, 7943, 7477, 1023, 23864, 17967, 29918, 19328, 12332, 591, 415, 7114, 70824, 395, 2454, 382, 22516, 1934, 34658, 13, 1843, 2454, 382, 22516, 853, 1991, 19627, 409, 11, 1023, 1606, 2304, 290, 23120, 5954, 19627, 259, 777, 382, 22516, 11, 625, 290, 17967, 29918, 19328, 82808, 2240, 5954, 1072, 722, 19627, 409, 13, 2214, 27789, 78, 22803, 1986, 11, 12951, 19627, 1953, 23659, 9913, 26, 395, 12384, 35188, 11, 1991, 4465, 110057, 19627, 409, 1340, 12847, 15001, 70824, 13, 47682, 35996, 18051, 69772, 1381, 13, 4569, 7890, 6960, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 364, 58369, 5230, 415, 13], "n_tokens": 514, "words": 337, "band_lo": 176.5, "band_hi": 706.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to choose which explanation best accounts for the decreasing accuracy of DFT predictions as the ring size increases.\n\nWe have three compounds: cyclohexane, cycloheptane, cyclooctane with a styryl and aldehyde substituent. The experimental anti:syn ratio goes from 8:1 (strong preference for anti) to 3:1 to 1:1, meaning as the ring gets larger, the energetic difference diminishes. DFT predicted anti:syn ratio boltzmann factors: 8.6:1 (close to 8:1), 5.2:1 (vs experimental 3:1), 4.8:1 (vs experimental 1:1). So DFT overestimates preference for anti as the difference becomes smaller.\n\nWhy? Probably because when energy differences are small (<1 kcal/mol), DFT errors become comparable and predictions become less reliable. Option C says that. Options A about difficulty finding minima for many degrees of freedom may be relevant but they did find minima; but more DOF may cause issues with conformational sampling. Option D says they only sample lowest energy conformation, not conformational ensemble, which matters more for larger rings with more flexibility, leading to the errors. That also plausible.\n\nLet's evaluate: The differences in ant vs syn energies become small. DFT typical errors ~1-2 kcal/mol. So for small differences, predictions uncertain. Also bigger rings have many conformations; ignoring conformational averaging can cause errors. The experiment likely in solution includes many conformations; DFT based on single conformer may misrepresent. So D is also plausible.\n\nWhich is more likely answer? The question: \"Why is the calculated result accurate for the first compound, and progressively less accurate for the others?\" They list possible answers. Option D addresses conformational sampling; As ring size larger, more conformations => more error. Option C says DFT not precise enough for small energy differences. Both plausible.\n\nBut note they compute Boltzmann factor from DFT energies for each isomer after optimization. If each isomer has many conformers, they only take the lowest energy conformer per isomer, not the Boltzmann averaged free energy over all conformers. For cyclohexane, perhaps conformational landscape limited; for larger rings, many low-energy conformers may shift relative energies. Hence predicted ratio deviates. That seems likely.\n\nThus answer D.\n\nLet's pick D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.6093124941107817e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999856, "logp_correct": -1.4474517058105533e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000144, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7973498685156712, "cot_lp_min64": -1.376651089771265, "frac_below_-5": 0.017964071856287425, "digit_lp_min": -0.23569868505001068, "answer_first_mention_lp": -6.603976362384856e-05, "n_cot_tokens": 501, "lp_end": -7.152555099310121e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -4.264163502427887, "p_nocot": 0.014063626403401343, "uplift": 4.264163502427873, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.985936, "D": 0.014064}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recj7NQlHcKW1rRME", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: We dissolve stochiometric AgCl in an ammonia solution (T = 25 °C). We assume that after dissolution, the total silver concentration of the solution will be 0.01 M. What is the concentration of the ammonia solution? Ks for AgCl is 1.56x10^-10, β1 = 2.1x10^3, β2 = 1.7x10^7.\n\nA) 0.214 M\nB) 0.194 M\nC) 0.223 M\nD) 0.201 M\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: We dissolve stochiometric AgCl in an ammonia solution (T = 25 °C). We assume that after dissolution, the total silver concentration of the solution will be 0.01 M. What is the concentration of the ammonia solution? Ks for AgCl is 1.56x10^-10, β1 = 2.1x10^3, β2 = 1.7x10^7.\n\nA) 0.214 M\nB) 0.194 M\nC) 0.223 M\nD) 0.201 M\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recj7NQlHcKW1rRME:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 17722, 87639, 13, 129157, 25, 5582, 1388, 1858, 8, 104771, 234, 5582, 10, 659, 2190, 12, 483, 658, 1148, 314, 220, 16, 13, 5007, 68, 12, 702, 13, 730, 14324, 328, 48198, 18, 11, 5582, 10, 11526, 76136, 25, 5582, 10, 659, 48198, 18, 104771, 234, 5582, 10564, 39, 18, 16161, 483, 15772, 16, 314, 220, 17, 13, 16, 68, 18, 350, 159046, 18704, 9594, 81192, 63659, 5983, 7480, 25, 15772, 16, 314, 723, 8541, 10564, 39, 18, 16161, 18500, 5194, 8541, 10, 2696, 57321, 18, 4636, 887, 15772, 17, 314, 723, 8541, 10564, 39, 18, 8, 17, 10, 18500, 5194, 8541, 10, 2696, 57321, 18, 144683, 17, 741, 50557, 10705, 18704, 38252, 395, 220, 16, 326, 220, 17, 9275, 3460, 25, 15772, 16, 314, 220, 17, 13, 16, 68, 18, 11, 15772, 17, 314, 220, 16, 13, 22, 68, 22, 350, 159046, 395, 1920, 741, 4569, 4748, 723, 8541, 10564, 39, 18, 8, 17, 10, 18500, 5194, 8541, 10, 2696, 57321, 18, 144683, 17, 8, 314, 220