, 18, 13, 30895, 68, 12, 20, 8, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 7672, 51742, 13, 105916, 656, 220, 15, 13, 29460, 19, 8967, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 8034, 17, 391, 13, 94868, 6577, 1481, 413, 12626, 364, 84787, 2240, 138900, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 8034, 391, 13, 21926, 6052, 418, 364, 4383, 538, 581, 1757, 3331, 3609, 138900, 30, 44130, 581, 19429, 16968, 138900, 11, 889, 1023, 3810, 27748, 328, 138900, 7578, 350, 114830, 3609, 4871, 27748, 741, 3072, 37367, 2360, 2240, 48198, 18, 27748, 1340, 413, 5263, 2236, 8012, 18704, 109297, 1236, 48198, 18, 11, 889, 3609, 1481, 413, 5263, 2236, 3464, 4527, 15001, 316, 5582, 350, 15, 13, 2290, 391, 741, 5551, 290, 4527, 8012, 295, 25, 723, 8541, 10564, 39, 18, 16161, 60, 314, 220, 18, 13, 30895, 68, 12, 20, 425, 1215, 42308, 230, 220, 18, 13, 30895, 68, 12, 20, 425, 15, 13, 8034, 42308, 230, 220, 21, 13, 2467, 68, 12, 21, 391, 13, 723, 8541, 10564, 39, 18, 8, 17, 143272, 314, 220, 15, 13, 25799, 17, 1215, 61, 17, 314, 15, 13, 25799, 17, 425, 15, 13, 41227, 21, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 24141, 5170, 391, 13, 2632, 6787, 722, 5582, 472, 1320, 5518, 514, 13, 8160, 12948, 220, 17, 425, 15, 13, 24141, 5170, 314, 15, 13, 32069, 10545, 391, 48198, 18, 13, 8820, 20049, 4527, 591, 1767, 13750, 8012, 109297, 220, 21, 13, 2467, 68, 12, 21, 6574, 175463, 13, 2632, 3609, 48198, 18, 47047, 6574, 15, 13, 32069, 10545, 391, 13, 19233, 3609, 4871, 48198, 18, 2804, 413, 2240, 659, 11608, 314, 220, 15, 13, 8034, 659, 15, 13, 32069, 10545, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 21401, 391, 13, 4569, 15248, 5317, 355, 350, 15, 13, 21401, 391, 741, 623, 4928, 25, 392, 4827, 382, 290, 27748, 328, 290, 138900, 7578, 16842, 30456, 4774, 3609, 27748, 328, 48198, 18, 22257, 350, 29615, 8012, 387, 741, 44130, 91671, 3810, 395, 4871, 27748, 6118, 316, 12998, 3609, 5582, 27748, 220, 15, 13, 2290, 391, 13, 2632, 6052, 6960, 220, 15, 13, 21401, 391, 350, 3941, 355, 741, 41021, 14866, 483, 8663, 25, 4871, 48198, 18, 314, 363, 23814, 13, 6311, 87639, 11, 2240, 314, 1215, 13, 35812, 109297, 1236, 13, 2514, 12998, 4842, 10636, 25, 363, 23814, 314, 1215, 659, 723, 8541, 10564, 39, 18, 16161, 60, 659, 220, 17, 58, 8541, 10564, 39, 18, 8, 17, 10, 37441, 56734, 25, 1215, 314, 220, 15, 13, 8034, 17, 11, 723, 8541, 10564, 39, 18, 16161, 9539, 21, 13, 2467, 68, 12, 21, 11, 220, 17, 58, 8541, 10564, 39, 18, 8, 17, 10, 9539, 220, 17, 9, 15, 13, 24141, 5170, 314, 15, 13, 32069, 10545, 13, 34138, 314, 220, 15, 13, 8034, 17, 10, 15, 13, 1302, 19538, 2467, 10, 15, 13, 32069, 10545, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 21401, 1055, 391, 13, 2632, 6052, 355, 13, 2632, 5655, 355, 13], "n_tokens": 1452, "words": 498, "band_lo": 249.0, "band_hi": 996.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to solve equilibrium. Reaction: AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag+ + Cl- with Ksp = 1.56e-10. In presence of NH3, Ag+ forms complexes: Ag+ + NH3 ⇌ Ag(NH3)+ with β1 = 2.1e3 (overall formation constant?). Probably stepwise: β1 = [Ag(NH3)+]/([Ag+][NH3]) . β2 = [Ag(NH3)2+]/([Ag+][NH3]^2). Actually overall formation constants for 1 and 2 ligands: β1 = 2.1e3, β2 = 1.7e7 (overall for two). That means [Ag(NH3)2+]/([Ag+][NH3]^2) = 1.7e7. Also we might have species Ag(NH3)+ and Ag(NH3)2+. With high ammonia, maybe only diammine complex dominates.\n\nWe have total Ag concentration = 0.01 M = [Ag+] + [Ag(NH3)+] + [Ag(NH3)2+].\n\nWe also have chloride concentration: from dissolution stoichiometric means each AgCl yields Cl- same as Ag+ total? But when Ag+ complexed, AgCl dissolution still produces Cl- free (assuming chloride does not complex). So total Cl- = total Ag = 0.01 M? Actually dissolution of AgCl(s) yields Ag+ (free or complex) and Cl-. The total amounts equal. So [Cl-] = 0.01 M (assuming no other sources). However some Ag+ may stay as free; but we need solubility equilibrium: Ag+ (free) * Cl- = Ksp. So [Ag+] free = Ksp / [Cl-] = 1.56e-10 / 0.01 = 1.56e-8 M. That's the free Ag+ concentration.\n\nNow we need to find [NH3] free such that mass balance for Ag holds. With K formation constants: [Ag(NH3)+] = β1 [Ag+][NH3] and [Ag(NH3)2+] = β2 [Ag+][NH3]^2.\n\nLet x = [NH3] free. Known [Ag+] = a = 1.56e-8. Then [Ag(NH3)+] = β1 a x = 2.1e3 * 1.56e-8 * x = (2.1e3 * 1.56e-8) = 3.276e-5? Let's calculate: 1.56e-8 * 2.1e3 = 1.56*2.1e-5 = 3.276e-5. So = 3.276e-5 * x.\n\nSimilarly [Ag(NH3)2+] = β2 a x^2 = 1.7e7 * 1.56e-8 * x^2 = (1.7*1.56)e-1? 1.7*1.56 = 2.652. Times 10^{-1} = 0.2652. So = 0.2652 * x^2.\n\nNow sum: total Ag = a + 3.276e-5 x + 0.2652 x^2 = 0.01.\n\nSince a is negligible, we have 0.2652 x^2 + 3.276e-5 x - 0.01 ≈ 0.\n\nSolve quadratic: coefficients: A=0.2652, B=3.276e-5, C=-0.01.\n\nx = [-B ± sqrt(B^2 -4AC)]/(2A). Compute discriminant: B^2 ≈ (3.276e-5)^2 = 1.073e-9. 4AC = 4*0.2652*(-0.01) = -0.010608. So minus 4AC = B^2 - 4AC = 1.073e-9 - (-0.010608) = 0.01060800107 approx 0.010608. sqrt ≈ sqrt(0.010608) ≈ 0.1030 (since 0.103^2=0.010609). So x = [-3.276e-5 ± 0.1030]/(0.5304). Positive root: (0.1030 - 3.276e-5) ≈ 0.102967. Divide by 0.5304 gives ≈ 0.1942 M. Negative root would be negative.\n\nThus free ammonia ≈ 0.194 M. That's answer B.\n\nCheck if we should consider total ammonia? Usually we assume excess ammonia, but they ask concentration of ammonia solution (probably total initial concentration). But solving using free NH3 concentration may be close because complex formation consumes some NH3, but total would be close because big amount relative to Ag (0.01 M). However the amount complexed: [Ag(NH3)+] = 3.276e-5 * x ≈ 3.276e-5 *0.194 ≈ 6.35e-6 M. [Ag(NH3)2+] = 0.2652 x^2 =0.2652 *0.0376 ≈ 0.00997 M. So almost all Ag as diammine. Consumes 2 *0.00997 =0.01994 M NH3. Also tiny amount from monoa complex consumes 6.35e-6 ~ negligible. So total NH3 consumed ~0.01994 M. Therefore total initial NH3 must be free + bound = 0.194 +0.01994 ≈ 0.214 M. That matches option A (0.214 M). The question: \"What is the concentration of the ammonia solution?\" Could mean total concentration of NH3 originally (before complexation). Usually they'd ask for initial concentration needed to achieve total Ag concentration 0.01 M. So answer likely 0.214 M (option A). Let's verify with numbers: initial NH3 = C_total. After equilibrium, free = x. Complex consumes some. To achieve mass balance: C_total = x + [Ag(NH3)+] + 2[Ag(NH3)2+].\n\nCompute: x = 0.1942, [Ag(NH3)+]=6.35e-6, 2[Ag(NH3)2+]= 2*0.00997 =0.01994. Sum = 0.1942+0.00000635+0.01994 ≈ 0.21415 M. So answer A. So choose A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.264974000354414e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999959097, "logp_correct": -4.090277505372451e-12, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000040905, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4150043635602286, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4301422823836196, "frac_below_-5": 0.010423905489923557, "digit_lp_min": -2.127002716064453, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 1439, "lp_end": -2.9802276912960224e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -0.6844959149382412, "p_nocot": 0.5043443979852001, "uplift": 0.684495914934151, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.504344, "B": 0.392784, "C": 0.01523, "D": 0.087642}}, "force_keep": false, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recjP3AhnRaZ44JIR", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: You want to quantify the upregulation of a transcript from the mouse hypertrophic muscles and compare it to the levels in the wild-type using a quantitative polymerase chain reaction. You isolate the tissues and carefully prepare the RNA for the cDNA synthesis. You proceed with the qPCR in a two-step process. For the second step, you need to design a pair of primers that would allow for the specific detection of the transcript in question. You identified the transcript's genomic sequence that is 23.7 kb long. The ATG is at 6867 bp, following a 128 bp 5'UTR that stretches over an intron. The second exon ends at 8272 bp. There are three more exons; the last exon contains the stop at 23276 bp and is around 500 bp long. Between the second and the third is the gene's largest intron of 7367bp in length. The third is the largest exon, 2126 bp long.\nConsidering their length of 20 bp and an appropriate annealing temperature, how do you position your primer pair for optimal transcript quantification?\n\nA) forward around 16100 bp and reverse around 23200 bp on the genomic DNA\nB) forward around 6900 bp and reverse around 8200 bp on the genomic DNA\nC) forward around 1 bp and reverse around 6900 bp on the genomic DNA\nD) forward around 8200 bp and reverse around16100 bp on the genomic DNA\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: You want to quantify the upregulation of a transcript from the mouse hypertrophic muscles and compare it to the levels in the wild-type using a quantitative polymerase chain reaction. You isolate the tissues and carefully prepare the RNA for the cDNA synthesis. You proceed with the qPCR in a two-step process. For the second step, you need to design a pair of primers that would allow for the specific detection of the transcript in question. You identified the transcript's genomic sequence that is 23.7 kb long. The ATG is at 6867 bp, following a 128 bp 5'UTR that stretches over an intron. The second exon ends at 8272 bp. There are three more exons; the last exon contains the stop at 23276 bp and is around 500 bp long. Between the second and the third is the gene's largest intron of 7367bp in length. The third is the largest exon, 2126 bp long.\nConsidering their length of 20 bp and an appropriate annealing temperature, how do you position your primer pair for optimal transcript quantification?\n\nA) forward around 16100 bp and reverse around 23200 bp on the genomic DNA\nB) forward around 6900 bp and reverse around 8200 bp on the genomic DNA\nC) forward around 1 bp and reverse around 6900 bp on the genomic DNA\nD) forward around 8200 bp and reverse around16100 bp on the genomic DNA\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recjP3AhnRaZ44JIR:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 4928, 13, 7744, 1078, 35623, 147812, 395, 2335, 178974, 316, 119267, 261, 63989, 13, 1416, 679, 261, 19546, 483, 1991, 513, 1808, 326, 4410, 12676, 1808, 13, 2214, 24174, 2335, 178974, 28385, 328, 63989, 11, 147812, 1757, 19861, 174616, 15801, 263, 79550, 82, 316, 8397, 136503, 23224, 168100, 11, 503, 413, 7567, 3518, 513, 1808, 889, 10635, 4022, 939, 23124, 263, 13, 8820, 147812, 306, 2647, 513, 1808, 33930, 656, 12676, 1808, 10635, 4410, 1888, 306, 136503, 23224, 11, 625, 12430, 13, 2632, 290, 24174, 382, 316, 2364, 147812, 484, 159736, 448, 174616, 15801, 263, 79550, 11, 503, 3518, 39069, 513, 1808, 483, 3291, 939, 23124, 263, 350, 1353, 12, 1179, 52357, 741, 623, 3880, 3644, 15897, 402, 136503, 23224, 13, 17796, 418, 25, 6687, 2846, 220, 37855, 15, 52357, 350, 71992, 11503, 38, 1604, 8, 326, 18199, 2846, 220, 34175, 15, 52357, 350, 42235, 1268, 328, 3099, 174616, 741, 4569, 1481, 160160, 290, 3099, 174616, 350, 76945, 6574, 9300, 15, 52357, 5517, 16918, 50557, 3099, 174616, 17095, 540, 220, 48514, 17, 52357, 26, 1604, 540, 220, 42639, 22, 10, 8076, 28, 41293, 20, 30, 24305, 11503, 38, 540, 220, 42639, 22, 11, 220, 20, 6, 2260, 49, 220, 8076, 52357, 83793, 1072, 448, 12676, 263, 13, 2632, 1604, 328, 22458, 16281, 540, 220, 42639, 22, 1008, 3099, 174616, 17095, 540, 220, 48514, 17, 11, 10915, 5517, 6574, 10165, 15, 52357, 13, 21926, 7542, 4410, 395, 2335, 178974, 26, 62227, 939, 23124, 263, 2349, 6574, 2789, 12, 1179, 52357, 13, 17796, 415, 25, 6687, 2846, 220, 34175, 15, 52357, 326, 18199, 2846, 220, 18881, 504, 52357, 1127, 10954, 5251, 12676, 263, 2870, 3099, 326, 6914, 350, 191832, 12676, 263, 220, 45498, 22, 52357, 741, 4569, 1481, 160160, 4410, 220, 22, 13, 18, 68515, 136503, 1888, 625, 1899, 11, 889, 538, 274, 79457, 11, 480, 1481, 413, 5251, 174616, 15801, 263, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 174616, 17, 15801, 263, 18, 35021, 8, 20547, 306, 4022, 939, 23124, 263, 350, 21910, 860, 12676, 263, 741, 4569, 1481, 413, 9064, 25, 147812, 66335, 174616, 17, 15801, 263, 18, 79550, 11, 1888, 4022, 13, 5551, 290, 6687, 540, 220, 34175, 15, 382, 540, 1268, 328, 174616, 17, 11, 18199, 540, 220, 18881, 504, 1118, 382, 3518, 290, 6914, 174616, 350, 13980, 382, 4410, 220, 19584, 21, 52357, 741, 623, 9324, 306, 274, 79457, 2870, 2617, 15897, 1481, 413, 1078, 350, 60279, 174616, 1604, 314, 1934, 12676, 263, 26, 49328, 6914, 174616, 13217, 540, 220, 48514, 17, 10, 45498, 22, 10, 16, 6574, 18369, 1723, 30, 24305, 3099, 174616, 17095, 220, 48514, 17, 11, 12676, 263, 328, 220, 45498, 22, 8805, 316, 220, 18369, 3255, 350, 48514, 17, 10, 45498, 22, 28, 18369, 3255, 741, 35108, 174616, 1815, 13719, 591, 220, 18369, 1723, 316, 10112, 220, 19227, 3898, 350, 19584, 21, 5517, 741, 2632, 4010, 220, 18881, 504, 382, 3518, 6914, 174616, 5862, 1604, 13, 2632, 13240, 10610, 6687, 540, 220, 34175, 15, 350, 419, 328, 174616, 17, 936, 18199, 540, 220, 18881, 504, 350, 5236, 328, 174616, 18, 8, 738, 160160, 5251, 174616, 15801, 263, 181783, 79550, 11, 9874, 1888, 6574, 350, 490, 263, 17, 1268, 316, 174616, 18, 1604, 8, 3291, 10112, 464, 1179, 52357, 538, 147812, 12989, 5862, 79550, 13, 4569, 382, 261, 19271, 2364, 364, 8093, 355, 25, 6687, 220, 18881, 504, 350, 258, 174616, 18, 8, 18199, 220, 22280, 504, 350, 258, 2174, 174616, 11, 5862, 5666, 741, 4569, 1481, 19861, 7598, 513, 1808, 350, 490, 263, 18, 316, 2174, 174616, 8, 3463, 4410, 12676, 263, 1858, 8, 1424, 30, 3274, 382, 12676, 263, 2870, 220, 18, 326, 220, 19, 5178, 13, 4037, 24174, 364, 8093, 363, 25, 6687, 220, 16, 52357, 350, 62832, 78314, 328, 19546, 8, 18199, 220, 37855, 15, 350, 42235, 1604, 26163, 263, 741, 4569, 1481, 19861, 1991, 12676, 1808, 364, 84787, 1636, 382, 415, 25, 147812, 66335, 174616, 17, 15801, 263, 18, 79550, 59684, 4857, 28385, 328, 284, 49212, 350, 66, 79457, 8, 326, 860, 136503, 168100, 5192, 316, 4410, 12676, 263, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 415, 364, 4383, 290, 4928, 25, 392, 136225, 1043, 5517, 328, 220, 455, 52357, 326, 448, 11081, 87177, 7330, 12088, 11, 1495, 621, 481, 4010, 634, 13240, 10610, 395, 24174, 63989, 8237, 3166, 16842, 1225, 10648, 316, 8397, 136503, 23224, 71956, 26, 5655, 147812, 66335, 174616, 15801, 263, 79550, 13972, 939, 23124, 263, 1888, 3291, 13, 4569, 15248, 5317, 415, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 415, 13], "n_tokens": 758, "words": 453, "band_lo": 259.0, "band_hi": 1036.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the question. It's about designing primers for qPCR to quantify a transcript. We have a gene with many exons and large introns. For optimal qPCR detection of transcript, primers should span exon-exon junctions to avoid genomic DNA amplification, or be located within exons but produce short amplicon. Also primers in different exons separated by introns produce large product in genomic DNA, not efficient. So the optimal is to design primers that flank an exon-exon junction, or within adjacent exons with small amplicon (100-200 bp). The options give positions on genomic DNA. Option B: forward around 6900 bp (near ATG start) and reverse around 8200 bp (around end of second exon). That would amplify the second exon (approx ~1300 bp length?) Actually second exon ends at 8272 bp; start at 6867+128=6995? Wait ATG at 6867, 5'UTR 128 bp stretches over an intron. So start of coding sequence at 6867... second exon ends at 8272, meaning length ~1400 bp. That's rather large for qPCR; optimum amplicon size ~70-200 bp. Option D: forward around 8200 bp and reverse around 16100 bp – that's across intron between second and third (largest intron 7367 bp). That would amplify large 7.3 kb genomic product not good, but if cDNA, it would be across exon-exon (i.e., exon2-exon3 boundary) resulting in short amplicon (since no intron). That would be ideal: primers spanning exon2-exon3 junction, product short. However the forward at 8200 is at end of exon2, reverse at 16100 which is within the third exon (which is large 2126 bp). The distance in cDNA between those positions would be about (third exon start = after intron; assuming third exon starts at 8272+7367+1 ~15640? Wait second exon ends 8272, intron of 7367 goes to 15639 (8272+7367=15639). Third exon then runs from 15640 to maybe 17765 (2126 length). So position 16100 is within third exon near start. So primer pair forward at 8200 (end of exon2), reverse at 16100 (start of exon3) will amplify across exon-exon splice junction, giving product ~ (exon2 end to exon3 start) small maybe <200 bp if primers placed near junction. That is a typical design.\n\nOption A: forward 16100 (in exon3) reverse 23200 (in last exon, near stop). That would span multiple exons (exon3 to last exon) including large intron(s) >? There is intron between 3 and 4 etc. Not optimal.\n\nOption C: forward 1 bp (maybe upstream of gene) reverse 6900 (around start codon). That would span many introns.\n\nThus best is D: primers spanning exon2-exon3 junction yields specific detection of mRNA (cDNA) and no genomic amplification due to large intron.\n\nThus answer: D.\n\nCheck the question: \"Considering their length of 20 bp and an appropriate annealing temperature, how do you position your primer pair for optimal transcript quantification?\" It wants to avoid genomic DNA contamination; choose primers spanning exon-exon junction thus amplicon product small. That matches option D.\n\nThus answer: ANSWER: D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.099438004028343e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999881, "logp_correct": -1.1824024978270904e-14, "letter_mass": 0.999999523163081, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7277654856136304, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1998266489681377, "frac_below_-5": 0.0174496644295302, "digit_lp_min": -3.6247167587280273, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.5213249921798706, "n_cot_tokens": 745, "lp_end": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -11.000576049583346, "p_nocot": 1.669208255302101e-05, "uplift": 11.000576049583334, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.000553, "B": 0.999424, "C": 6e-06, "D": 1.7e-05}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recjgMJaMxz4ESDF2", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Compounds that have the same molecular formula but are different in their structural arrangement are known as isomers. Isomers have two types, constitutional isomers and stereoisomers. Constitutional isomers have the same molecular formula but differ in their structures. In stereoisomers, molecules are connected in the same way, but their arrangements in space are different.\nWhich of the following organic moieties show optical isomerism?\n\n1. dimethyl 6,6'-dinitro-[1,1'-biphenyl]-2,2'-dicarboxylate\n2. methyl 2-hydroxypropanoate\n3. benzophenone\n4. dimethyl fumarate\n\nA) 3 and 4\nB) 1, 2 and 4\nC) 2 and 3\nD) 1 and 2\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Compounds that have the same molecular formula but are different in their structural arrangement are known as isomers. Isomers have two types, constitutional isomers and stereoisomers. Constitutional isomers have the same molecular formula but differ in their structures. In stereoisomers, molecules are connected in the same way, but their arrangements in space are different.\nWhich of the following organic moieties show optical isomerism?\n\n1. dimethyl 6,6'-dinitro-[1,1'-biphenyl]-2,2'-dicarboxylate\n2. methyl 2-hydroxypropanoate\n3. benzophenone\n4. dimethyl fumarate\n\nA) 3 and 4\nB) 1, 2 and 4\nC) 2 and 3\nD) 1 and 2\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recjgMJaMxz4ESDF2:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11410, 1118, 328, 290, 4335, 43556, 2356, 37876, 382, 22516, 2367, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 679, 469, 9590, 119448, 741, 57076, 538, 2454, 36762, 382, 549, 46047, 350, 5429, 261, 51516, 39088, 6170, 11, 16300, 11, 16499, 11, 5178, 8476, 326, 71357, 90882, 484, 1481, 1520, 480, 5023, 46047, 364, 2167, 679, 220, 19, 43556, 1402, 16, 13, 6051, 53546, 220, 21, 11, 21, 44302, 67, 3287, 298, 44861, 16, 11, 16, 44302, 65, 488, 5447, 3643, 27107, 17, 11, 17, 44302, 67, 14670, 3952, 3643, 379, 279, 2500, 382, 261, 54467, 5447, 3643, 483, 92838, 8302, 25, 540, 15897, 220, 17, 11, 17, 6, 350, 390, 1555, 8, 1354, 553, 1669, 3952, 3643, 379, 8896, 350, 288, 6051, 53546, 893, 409, 8, 326, 540, 15897, 220, 21, 11, 21, 6, 27054, 298, 8896, 13, 2632, 4275, 220, 17, 11, 17, 44302, 67, 14670, 3952, 3643, 379, 6051, 53546, 893, 409, 11, 220, 21, 11, 21, 44302, 67, 3287, 298, 13, 623, 54467, 5447, 3643, 123167, 52189, 9443, 25, 54467, 5447, 3643, 82, 483, 24742, 1555, 22148, 84, 1094, 665, 413, 549, 46047, 5192, 316, 36432, 23769, 11, 8117, 316, 178325, 276, 22516, 2367, 350, 3374, 933, 382, 22516, 2367, 8, 538, 290, 92838, 8302, 382, 3975, 1818, 106856, 2238, 484, 290, 73005, 382, 625, 2539, 321, 40419, 402, 1617, 28026, 3621, 13, 730, 495, 1890, 11, 290, 92838, 8302, 382, 197465, 30, 41021, 28035, 25, 54467, 5447, 3643, 10089, 11, 1920, 35188, 12079, 220, 16, 12, 16, 6, 350, 3086, 11082, 15135, 741, 5934, 5028, 84, 1094, 25, 402, 2454, 21937, 3643, 12796, 11, 540, 4010, 220, 17, 15001, 316, 290, 15135, 11, 1354, 382, 261, 1669, 3952, 3643, 379, 119989, 26, 540, 4010, 220, 21, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 24742, 1555, 23821, 4307, 8, 1354, 382, 261, 27054, 298, 13, 2632, 2454, 12796, 853, 1920, 24742, 1555, 22148, 84, 1094, 25, 1001, 402, 2454, 4307, 328, 290, 15135, 30, 50557, 220, 17, 326, 220, 21, 553, 2973, 24742, 1555, 15897, 15001, 316, 290, 54467, 5447, 3643, 15135, 350, 17, 382, 39069, 402, 1001, 4307, 11, 220, 21, 382, 39069, 402, 290, 1273, 4307, 741, 2632, 2454, 21937, 3643, 12796, 382, 829, 431, 5028, 26369, 24742, 1555, 316, 290, 16300, 11, 483, 2647, 22148, 84, 1094, 350, 6830, 3952, 3643, 379, 10217, 27054, 298, 741, 623, 92838, 8302, 382, 3975, 1818, 106856, 25, 1001, 24742, 1555, 3566, 382, 10271, 17, 5507, 11, 290, 23821, 24742, 1555, 382, 9319, 17, 13, 1328, 15286, 316, 36432, 23769, 326, 290, 73005, 382, 549, 46047, 350, 266, 2283, 276, 22516, 741, 2632, 14531, 11, 37876, 382, 22516, 2367, 364, 17, 13, 101385, 220, 17, 4559, 123753, 6077, 15630, 3137, 379, 13, 21926, 101385, 220, 17, 4559, 123753, 6077, 15630, 3137, 379, 25, 10049, 18, 12, 3583, 7, 45485, 14080, 8310, 46, 3583, 18, 30, 50557, 5892, 3137, 379, 483, 31200, 6077, 540, 363, 17, 11, 101385, 119989, 13, 2632, 290, 15883, 24578, 42464, 382, 261, 51516, 39088, 6170, 350, 174185, 316, 10049, 18, 11, 487, 11, 42464, 11, 326, 10271, 2760, 68, 741, 11377, 549, 46047, 13, 2632, 37876, 382, 22516, 2367, 364, 18, 13, 76732, 178229, 690, 25, 14223, 5447, 3643, 16262, 690, 11, 3168, 12, 8310, 12, 3780, 13, 3004, 549, 46047, 6170, 26, 175798, 26, 197465, 26, 625, 549, 46047, 13, 3004, 37876, 382, 22516, 2367, 364, 19, 13, 6051, 53546, 165454, 379, 25, 1643, 12, 16, 11, 17, 12, 6051, 53546, 119989, 328, 165454, 291, 18655, 350, 2623, 12, 2082, 1900, 741, 623, 3503, 15135, 382, 175798, 26, 1643, 382, 22516, 853, 363, 17, 90882, 26, 625, 549, 46047, 13, 5551, 81322, 382, 119448, 350, 45546, 379, 8, 1217, 625, 549, 46047, 13, 2632, 860, 37876, 382, 22516, 2367, 364, 84787, 43556, 14253, 37876, 382, 22516, 2367, 25, 220, 16, 326, 220, 17, 13, 4569, 66938, 316, 5317, 415, 364, 4383, 6052, 3880, 25, 415, 8, 220, 16, 326, 220, 17, 13, 14531, 364, 84787, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 674, "words": 346, "band_lo": 306.75, "band_hi": 1227.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to identify which of the given compounds show optical isomerism (i.e., have enantiomers). Determine if each compound is chiral (has a stereogenic center, axis, plane, etc.) and lacks symmetry that would make it achiral.\n\nWe have 4 compounds:\n\n1. dimethyl 6,6'-dinitro-[1,1'-biphenyl]-2,2'-dicarboxylate\n\nThis is a biphenyl with substitution pattern: at positions 2,2' (ortho) there are carboxylate groups (as dimethyl esters) and at positions 6,6' nitro groups. So it's 2,2'-dicarboxylate dimethyl esters, 6,6'-dinitro. The biphenyl axial chirality: biphenyls with ortho substituents can be chiral due to restricted rotation, leading to atropisomerism (optical isomerism) if the substitution pattern is unsymmetrical such that the molecule is not superimposable on its mirror image. In this case, the substitution pattern is symmetrical? Let's examine: biphenyl core, two rings connected 1-1' (the central bond). Substituents: on each phenyl ring, at position 2 relative to the bond, there is a carboxylate ester; at position 6 (i.e., ortho opposite side) there is a nitro. So each ring has two ortho substituents: one on each side of the bond? Actually 2 and 6 are both ortho positions relative to the biphenyl bond (2 is adjacent on one side, 6 is adjacent on the other side). So each phenyl ring is disubstituted ortho to the axis, with different substituents (carboxylate vs nitro). The substitution pattern is unsymmetrical: one ortho group is CO2Me, the opposite ortho is NO2. This leads to restricted rotation and the molecule is chiral (atropisomer). So yes, optical isomerism.\n\n2. methyl 2-hydroxypropanoate. That's methyl 2-hydroxypropanoate: CH3-CH(OH)-COOCH3? Actually propanoate with hydroxy at C2, methyl ester. So the carbon bearing OH is a stereogenic center (attached to CH3, H, OH, and COOMe). Yes chiral. So optical isomerism.\n\n3. benzophenone: diphenyl ketone, Ph-CO-Ph. No chiral center; planar; symmetrical; not chiral. No optical isomerism.\n\n4. dimethyl fumarate: trans-1,2- dimethyl ester of fumaric acid (trans-alkene). The double bond is planar; trans isomer has C2 symmetry; not chiral. However cis isomers (maleate) also not chiral. So no optical isomerism.\n\nThus compounds showing optical isomerism: 1 and 2. That corresponds to option D.\n\nCheck answer options: D) 1 and 2. yes.\n\nThus answer.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.2755313036905136e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999925, "logp_correct": -7.49669852983685e-15, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000075, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4494377612939998, "cot_lp_min64": -0.7384119153925122, "frac_below_-5": 0.006051437216338881, "digit_lp_min": -2.3778295516967773, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 661, "lp_end": -0.004198190290480852, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -0.5759794843831958, "p_nocot": 0.5621539780141185, "uplift": 0.5759794843831882, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 1.8e-05, "B": 0.437806, "C": 2.3e-05, "D": 0.562154}}, "force_keep": false, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "reckNe2WSDCGPgkU3", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A particle in 1-D harmonic oscillator potential initially starts out in the state: ψ(x,0)= 4ψ0 (x)+3ψ2 (x), where ψ0 and ψ2 are the ground and second excited state of the harmonic oscillator, respectively. Note that ψ(x,0) is not normalized.\nWhat is the probability density at some later time |ψ(x,t)|^2?\n\nA) |ψ(x,t)|^2 = 1/25 [16 |ψ0|^2 + 9 |ψ2|^2 -12 ψ0ψ2 sin(5ωt)]\nB) |ψ(x,t)|^2 = 1/25 [16 |ψ0|^2 + 9 |ψ2|^2 -24ψ0ψ2 sin(2ωt)]\nC) |ψ(x,t)|^2 = 1/25 [16 |ψ0|^2 + 9 |ψ2|^2 +24ψ0ψ2 cos(2ωt)]\nD) |ψ(x,t)|^2 = 1/25 [16 |ψ0|^2 + 9 |ψ2|^2 +12ψ0ψ2 cos(5ωt)]\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A particle in 1-D harmonic oscillator potential initially starts out in the state: ψ(x,0)= 4ψ0 (x)+3ψ2 (x), where ψ0 and ψ2 are the ground and second excited state of the harmonic oscillator, respectively. Note that ψ(x,0) is not normalized.\nWhat is the probability density at some later time |ψ(x,t)|^2?\n\nA) |ψ(x,t)|^2 = 1/25 [16 |ψ0|^2 + 9 |ψ2|^2 -12 ψ0ψ2 sin(5ωt)]\nB) |ψ(x,t)|^2 = 1/25 [16 |ψ0|^2 + 9 |ψ2|^2 -24ψ0ψ2 sin(2ωt)]\nC) |ψ(x,t)|^2 = 1/25 [16 |ψ0|^2 + 9 |ψ2|^2 +24ψ0ψ2 cos(2ωt)]\nD) |ψ(x,t)|^2 = 1/25 [16 |ψ0|^2 + 9 |ψ2|^2 +12ψ0ψ2 cos(5ωt)]\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "reckNe2WSDCGPgkU3:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 1646, 290, 1058, 7863, 94254, 20485, 2706, 13, 29844, 4871, 2608, 80339, 4061, 11, 15, 25987, 19, 80339, 15, 4061, 8, 659, 220, 18, 80339, 17, 4061, 741, 4037, 59625, 13, 16394, 10561, 71154, 25, 80339, 2170, 4061, 8, 483, 5954, 457, 2170, 314, 350, 77, 10, 16, 14, 17, 8, 5762, 2806, 13, 6688, 26416, 25, 80339, 2170, 4061, 26927, 25987, 80339, 2170, 4061, 8, 319, 183239, 72, 36, 2170, 260, 14, 5762, 102838, 80339, 2170, 4061, 8, 319, 183239, 72, 350, 77, 10, 16, 14, 17, 8, 112349, 260, 16255, 350, 19048, 41051, 81021, 1989, 741, 2632, 20485, 2706, 540, 1058, 260, 25, 80339, 4061, 26927, 25987, 19, 80339, 15, 319, 183239, 72, 7, 16, 14, 17, 8, 112349, 260, 92, 659, 220, 18, 80339, 17, 319, 183239, 72, 7, 20, 14, 17, 8, 112349, 260, 16255, 13602, 297, 28, 17, 871, 297, 10, 16, 14, 17, 314, 220, 17, 10, 16, 14, 17, 314, 220, 20, 14, 17, 364, 10620, 23864, 34933, 27591, 25, 1022, 15927, 91, 61, 17, 314, 80339, 9, 80339, 314, 350, 19, 80339, 15, 319, 183239, 72, 112349, 260, 14, 17, 92, 659, 220, 18, 80339, 17, 319, 183239, 72, 20, 2806, 260, 14, 17, 9263, 9, 350, 19, 80339, 15, 319, 183239, 72, 112349, 260, 14, 17, 92, 659, 220, 18, 80339, 17, 319, 183239, 72, 20, 2806, 260, 14, 17, 92, 3991, 56734, 89227, 379, 25, 220, 19, 80339, 15, 9, 319, 42923, 10, 72, 112349, 260, 14, 17, 92, 659, 220, 18, 80339, 17, 9, 319, 42923, 10, 72, 20, 2806, 260, 14, 17, 92, 364, 10620, 1888, 1402, 28, 220, 1125, 1022, 15927, 15, 91, 61, 17, 659, 220, 24, 1022, 15927, 17, 91, 61, 17, 659, 350, 19, 9, 18, 8, 80339, 15, 9, 80339, 17, 319, 183239, 72, 350, 20, 2806, 533, 112349, 8, 260, 14, 17, 111441, 24305, 8088, 5941, 25, 591, 1577, 2914, 328, 89227, 379, 4238, 3099, 2914, 328, 4756, 25, 350, 19, 80339, 15, 9, 319, 42923, 72, 112349, 260, 14, 17, 67254, 18, 80339, 17, 319, 183239, 72, 20, 112349, 260, 14, 17, 9263, 314, 220, 899, 80339, 15, 9, 80339, 17, 319, 183239, 72, 350, 20, 12, 16, 8, 112349, 260, 14, 17, 102838, 899, 80339, 15, 9, 80339, 17, 319, 183239, 72, 220, 19, 112349, 260, 14, 17, 102838, 899, 80339, 15, 9, 80339, 17, 319, 183239, 72, 17, 112349, 260, 92, 364, 151907, 1273, 8088, 2914, 25, 350, 18, 80339, 17, 9, 319, 42923, 72, 20, 112349, 260, 14, 17, 67254, 19, 80339, 15, 319, 183239, 72, 112349, 260, 14, 17, 9263, 314, 220, 899, 80339, 17, 9, 80339, 15, 319, 42923, 10, 72, 17, 112349, 260, 16255, 34138, 25, 220, 899, 58, 80339, 15, 9, 80339, 17, 319, 183239, 72, 17, 112349, 260, 190680, 80339, 17, 9, 80339, 15, 319, 42923, 72, 17, 112349, 260, 53940, 314, 220, 1494, 907, 58, 80339, 15, 9, 80339, 17, 319, 183239, 72, 17, 112349, 260, 53940, 314, 220, 1494, 1022, 15927, 15, 12439, 15927, 17, 91, 8974, 7, 17, 112349, 260, 659, 15222, 30, 3072, 80339, 15, 326, 80339, 17, 553, 1374, 9964, 350, 13187, 10561, 44580, 395, 44188, 741, 2632, 80339, 15, 9, 15927, 17, 314, 80339, 15, 80339, 17, 350, 13187, 741, 23586, 8088, 2914, 314, 220, 1494, 80339, 15, 80339, 17, 8974, 7, 17, 112349, 260, 741, 2632, 3609, 1022, 15927, 91, 61, 17, 314, 220, 1125, 91, 15927, 15, 91, 61, 17, 659, 220, 24, 91, 15927, 17, 91, 61, 17, 659, 220, 1494, 80339, 15, 80339, 17, 8974, 7, 17, 112349, 260, 741, 6549, 1309, 115461, 12332, 25, 4871, 625, 59625, 11, 889, 581, 665, 12332, 350, 159046, 79673, 117867, 741, 623, 20485, 2706, 49895, 6664, 12332, 591, 10705, 64656, 13, 3072, 581, 665, 23864, 115461, 12332, 25, 478, 314, 220, 19, 61, 17, 659, 220, 18, 61, 17, 314, 220, 1125, 10, 24, 314, 1161, 2236, 35656, 263, 3568, 10561, 71154, 13, 2632, 290, 59625, 2608, 1481, 413, 350, 16, 14, 20, 4898, 19, 80339, 15, 659, 18, 80339, 17, 741, 623, 4335, 20485, 2706, 382, 625, 59625, 11, 889, 34933, 27591, 1757, 413, 46716, 2360, 290, 2684, 80339, 4061, 26927, 8, 472, 4335, 11, 625, 59625, 30, 64968, 34933, 27591, 591, 261, 20485, 2706, 2804, 413, 59625, 11, 889, 1023, 6960, 1682, 290, 59625, 3926, 13, 623, 3880, 3931, 12332, 220, 16, 14, 1161, 10705, 11, 20434, 483, 115461, 12332, 220, 16, 14, 20, 117867, 13, 41351, 722, 3880, 679, 12332, 220, 16, 14, 1161, 7539, 13, 2632, 2304, 59625, 25, 80339, 46119, 314, 350, 16, 14, 20, 4898, 19, 80339, 15, 659, 18, 80339, 17, 741, 7801, 1022, 15927, 91, 61, 17, 314, 350, 16, 14, 1161, 13637, 1125, 91, 15927, 15, 91, 61, 17, 659, 220, 24, 91, 15927, 17, 91, 61, 17, 659, 220, 1494, 80339, 15, 80339, 17, 8974, 7, 17, 112349, 260, 58800, 4569, 15248, 7158, 363, 350, 6332, 2932, 2232, 8974, 17, 2806, 260, 741, 17796, 363, 25, 2550, 659, 1494, 80339, 15, 80339, 17, 8974, 7, 17, 112349, 260, 741, 11377, 364, 84787, 6052, 363, 13], "n_tokens": 865, "words": 279, "band_lo": 122.75, "band_hi": 491.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to find the time-evolved wavefunction. Given initial state ψ(x,0)=4 ψ0(x) + 3 ψ2(x). Not normalized. Energy eigenstates: ψ_n(x) with energy E_n = (n+1/2)ħω. Time evolution: ψ_n(x,t)= ψ_n(x) e^{-iE_n t/ħ}= ψ_n(x) e^{-i (n+1/2) ω t}. (Since ħ cancels). So wavefunction at time t: ψ(x,t)=4 ψ0 e^{-i(1/2) ω t} + 3 ψ2 e^{-i(5/2) ω t}. Because n=2 => n+1/2 = 2+1/2 = 5/2.\n\nNow compute probability density: |ψ|^2 = ψ* ψ = (4 ψ0 e^{-i ω t/2} + 3 ψ2 e^{-i5ω t/2})* (4 ψ0 e^{-i ω t/2} + 3 ψ2 e^{-i5ω t/2}).\n\nCompute conjugate: 4 ψ0* e^{+i ω t/2} + 3 ψ2* e^{+i5ω t/2}.\n\nNow product:\n\n= 16 |ψ0|^2 + 9 |ψ2|^2 + (4*3) ψ0* ψ2 e^{-i (5ω - ω) t/2}? Wait cross terms: from first term of conjugate times second term of original: (4 ψ0* e^{i ω t/2})(3 ψ2 e^{-i5 ω t/2}) = 12 ψ0* ψ2 e^{-i (5-1) ω t/2}=12 ψ0* ψ2 e^{-i 4 ω t/2}=12 ψ0* ψ2 e^{-i2 ω t}.\n\nSimilarly other cross term: (3 ψ2* e^{i5 ω t/2})(4 ψ0 e^{-i ω t/2}) = 12 ψ2* ψ0 e^{+i2 ω t}. Sum: 12[ ψ0* ψ2 e^{-i2 ω t}+ ψ2* ψ0 e^{i2 ω t}] = 24 Re[ ψ0* ψ2 e^{-i2 ω t}] = 24 |ψ0||ψ2| cos(2 ω t + phase? But ψ0 and ψ2 are real functions (real eigenfunctions for HO). So ψ0*ψ2 = ψ0 ψ2 (real). Thus cross term = 24 ψ0 ψ2 cos(2 ω t). So total |ψ|^2 = 16|ψ0|^2 + 9|ψ2|^2 + 24 ψ0 ψ2 cos(2 ω t). Now need normalization factor: initial not normalized, but we can factor (overall amplitude squared). The wavefunction magnitude includes factor from overall coefficient. But we can compute normalization factor: N = 4^2 + 3^2 = 16+9 =25 because orthonormal eigenstates. So the normalized state would be (1/5)(4 ψ0 +3 ψ2). The given wavefunction is not normalized, but probability density should be computed using the same ψ(x,t) as given, not normalized? Typically probability density from a wavefunction must be normalized, but they likely want the normalized version. The options include factor 1/25 overall, consistent with normalization factor 1/5 squared. Indeed all options have factor 1/25 outside. So take normalized: ψ_norm = (1/5)(4 ψ0 +3 ψ2). Then |ψ|^2 = (1/25)[16|ψ0|^2 + 9|ψ2|^2 + 24 ψ0 ψ2 cos(2 ω t)]. That matches choice C (with plus sign cos2ω t). Option C: ... +24 ψ0 ψ2 cos(2 ω t). Yes.\n\nThus answer C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.9802293397551693e-06, "p_correct": 0.99999999999991, "logp_correct": -8.998993757506105e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999994039539905, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.3743994985762041, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9763935241363608, "frac_below_-5": 0.007042253521126761, "digit_lp_min": -4.155186653137207, "answer_first_mention_lp": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "n_cot_tokens": 852, "lp_end": -6.079655122448457e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -0.4989873697003173, "p_nocot": 0.607145162115711, "uplift": 0.49898736970022733, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.063993, "B": 0.105506, "C": 0.607145, "D": 0.223356}}, "force_keep": false, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "reckYqR92oJkqJXkE", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Let us assume stars are uniformly distributed in the sky. In this scenario, how the number of stars per unit range of parallax (plx) changes with plx?\n\nA) ~ 1/plx^1\nB) ~ 1/plx^2\nC) ~ 1/plx^3\nD) ~ 1/plx^4\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Let us assume stars are uniformly distributed in the sky. In this scenario, how the number of stars per unit range of parallax (plx) changes with plx?\n\nA) ~ 1/plx^1\nB) ~ 1/plx^2\nC) ~ 1/plx^3\nD) ~ 1/plx^4\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "reckYqR92oJkqJXkE:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 2411, 1078, 21219, 12545, 328, 13701, 306, 4918, 11, 625, 306, 290, 17307, 30, 623, 4928, 5003, 13701, 161240, 7581, 306, 290, 17307, 13, 3072, 581, 1682, 2086, 777, 7670, 3352, 328, 686, 85720, 13, 3371, 85720, 6404, 350, 258, 26922, 47723, 8, 6574, 220, 16, 6662, 1919, 272, 306, 8420, 8102, 350, 40614, 741, 2214, 261, 21219, 44273, 27591, 11, 2086, 328, 13701, 2870, 48294, 272, 326, 272, 10, 1183, 382, 99864, 316, 10945, 20059, 220, 19, 1345, 272, 61, 17, 18636, 13, 15629, 316, 686, 85720, 275, 314, 220, 16, 6662, 11, 813, 272, 314, 220, 16, 8138, 11, 18636, 314, 533, 16, 8138, 61, 17, 21546, 13, 623, 2086, 777, 7670, 686, 85720, 49895, 478, 2407, 8, 21546, 35353, 251, 272, 61, 17, 18636, 314, 350, 16, 8138, 61, 17, 8, 425, 350, 16, 8138, 61, 17, 8, 21546, 314, 220, 16, 8138, 61, 19, 21546, 350, 733, 7443, 2232, 741, 2632, 478, 2407, 8, 35353, 251, 220, 16, 8138, 61, 19, 13, 2632, 6052, 415, 364, 7943, 1023, 2059, 392, 87552, 423, 7581, 306, 290, 17307, 1, 3572, 4774, 161240, 402, 51811, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 9262, 1606, 936, 889, 625, 85692, 12545, 13, 5551, 1023, 3810, 2086, 777, 7670, 3352, 328, 686, 85720, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 9324, 741, 1416, 1309, 21219, 306, 10945, 13, 2632, 6052, 415, 364, 84787, 1721, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 415], "n_tokens": 247, "words": 145, "band_lo": 105.5, "band_hi": 422.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to think about uniform distribution of stars in space, not in the sky? The question says stars uniformly distributed in the sky. But we want number per unit range of parallax. Parallax pi (in arcseconds) ~ 1/d where d in parsecs (distance). For a uniform spatial density, number of stars between distances d and d+dd is proportional to volume shell 4π d^2 dd. Convert to parallax p = 1/d, so d = 1/p, dd = -1/p^2 dp. The number per unit parallax magnitude N(p) dp ∝ d^2 dd = (1/p^2) * (1/p^2) dp = 1/p^4 dp (ignoring sign). So N(p) ∝ 1/p^4. So answer D.\n\nBut they said \"uniformly distributed in the sky\" might mean uniformly on sphere (i.e., direction only), but not radial distribution. However they ask number per unit range of parallax (i.e., distance). We need uniform in volume. So answer D.\n\nThus final: ANSWER: D", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -2.2649744266800553e-06, "p_correct": 0.999999999999982, "logp_correct": -1.800640501080467e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999997615814957, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5434995060677524, "cot_lp_min64": -0.8635311670074368, "frac_below_-5": 0.0, "digit_lp_min": -0.25330570340156555, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.005815017037093639, "n_cot_tokens": 234, "lp_end": -0.5805096626281738, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -7.70187039668869, "p_nocot": 0.00045198100801140616, "uplift": 7.701870396688673, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 5e-06, "B": 0.182342, "C": 0.817201, "D": 0.000452}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "reckbjNKYmpQlU5TY", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A photon h\\nu propagates in the Oz direction of the laboratory frame (R). It collides elastically with a particle m at rest in (R). We denote $\\alpha=\\frac{h\\nu}{mc^{2}}$, and v=\\beta c the velocity of the COM frame (R') of the system photon-particle relative to (R). What is the expression of \\beta?\n\nA) \\alpha\\times\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+\\alpha}}\\right)\nB) \\alpha\\times\\left(\\frac{1}{1+\\alpha}\\right)\nC) \\alpha\\times\\left(\\frac{1}{1-\\alpha}\\right)\nD) \\alpha\\times\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\alpha}}\\right)\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A photon h\\nu propagates in the Oz direction of the laboratory frame (R). It collides elastically with a particle m at rest in (R). We denote $\\alpha=\\frac{h\\nu}{mc^{2}}$, and v=\\beta c the velocity of the COM frame (R') of the system photon-particle relative to (R). What is the expression of \\beta?\n\nA) \\alpha\\times\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+\\alpha}}\\right)\nB) \\alpha\\times\\left(\\frac{1}{1+\\alpha}\\right)\nC) \\alpha\\times\\left(\\frac{1}{1-\\alpha}\\right)\nD) \\alpha\\times\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\alpha}}\\right)\n", "true_answer": "B", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "reckbjNKYmpQlU5TY:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 1646, 290, 30061, 328, 290, 6170, 13108, 2126, 456, 350, 14170, 8, 7298, 328, 290, 135911, 659, 18965, 33875, 2420, 13, 730, 9887, 7298, 25, 135911, 853, 41184, 275, 156429, 314, 312, 954, 4308, 314, 350, 71, 954, 11876, 66, 13, 13974, 5954, 457, 156429, 314, 312, 954, 13, 71474, 4842, 284, 25998, 540, 2867, 25, 5954, 457, 1285, 314, 36958, 61, 17, 11, 41184, 220, 15, 364, 8270, 5954, 25, 457, 23814, 314, 36958, 61, 17, 659, 312, 954, 314, 36958, 61, 17, 350, 16, 659, 3793, 741, 17565, 41184, 25, 275, 23814, 314, 312, 954, 820, 274, 314, 350, 71, 954, 11876, 66, 314, 350, 727, 36958, 61, 17, 11876, 66, 314, 3793, 284, 274, 364, 84787, 3609, 41184, 314, 3793, 284, 274, 11, 3609, 5954, 314, 284, 274, 61, 17, 350, 16, 10, 727, 3991, 976, 30061, 328, 28289, 7298, 350, 3086, 30061, 328, 290, 7298, 1919, 3609, 41184, 314, 220, 15, 8, 382, 4335, 656, 15772, 314, 275, 23814, 274, 820, 457, 23814, 30, 50557, 395, 261, 2420, 11, 290, 30061, 328, 290, 6170, 13108, 2126, 187863, 7298, 15001, 316, 9887, 382, 4335, 656, 1402, 6331, 314, 1022, 79, 23814, 91, 274, 820, 457, 23814, 364, 7943, 1309, 12332, 25, 730, 6247, 13306, 350, 66, 28, 16, 936, 290, 220, 19, 2126, 187863, 3609, 382, 350, 36, 23814, 11, 275, 23814, 741, 623, 12110, 30061, 382, 15772, 314, 275, 23814, 820, 457, 23814, 350, 258, 13306, 274, 28, 16, 741, 11271, 7443, 274, 25, 15772, 314, 275, 23814, 274, 820, 457, 23814, 364, 58369, 23864, 1402, 79, 23814, 314, 3793, 284, 274, 364, 84787, 275, 23814, 274, 314, 3793, 284, 274, 61, 17, 364, 36, 23814, 314, 284, 274, 61, 17, 350, 16, 10, 727, 3991, 5808, 15772, 314, 3793, 284, 274, 61, 17, 820, 723, 76, 274, 61, 17, 350, 16, 10, 727, 15710, 314, 3793, 820, 350, 16, 10, 727, 3991, 84787, 6052, 25, 418, 364, 4383, 290, 3880, 25, 418, 314, 3793, 425, 350, 16, 27334, 16, 10, 727, 8784, 11377, 15248, 364, 84787, 6052, 418, 13], "n_tokens": 360, "words": 186, "band_lo": 102.25, "band_hi": 409.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to find the velocity of the center-of-mass (CM) frame of the photon + massive particle system. In lab frame: photon has momentum p_gamma = hν/c = (hν)/c. Its energy E_gamma = hν. Particle mass m initially at rest: energy E_m = mc^2, momentum 0.\n\nTotal energy: E_total = mc^2 + hν = mc^2 (1 + α). Total momentum: p_total = hν / c = (hν)/c = (α mc^2)/c = α m c.\n\nThus total momentum = α m c, total energy = m c^2 (1+α).\n\nThe velocity of CM frame (the velocity of the frame where total momentum = 0) is given by β = p_total c / E_total? Actually for a system, the velocity of the center-of-momentum frame relative to lab is given by:\n\nβ = |p_total| c / E_total.\n\nBut need factor: In natural units (c=1), the 4-momentum total is (E_total, p_total). The boost velocity is β = p_total / E_total (in units c=1). Restoring c: β = p_total c / E_total.\n\nLet's compute:\n\np_total = α m c.\n\nThus p_total c = α m c^2.\n\nE_total = m c^2 (1+α).\n\nSo β = α m c^2 / [m c^2 (1+α)] = α / (1+α).\n\nThus answer: B.\n\nCheck the options: B = α * (1/(1+α)). Yes matches.\n\nThus answer B.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -5.9604471900343015e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999925, "logp_correct": -7.593628150754827e-15, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807907321, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.431762389619478, "cot_lp_min64": -0.7030699085747991, "frac_below_-5": 0.005763688760806916, "digit_lp_min": -0.0007120219524949789, "answer_first_mention_lp": -1.2896838188171387, "n_cot_tokens": 347, "lp_end": -0.0038165838923305273, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -2.8119778358977388, "p_nocot": 0.060086034475594526, "uplift": 2.811977835897731, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.939904, "B": 0.060086, "C": 1e-06, "D": 1e-05}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "reckl6PkaEMWM50yZ", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: When saturated epoxides are reacted with organocuprates, the alkyl group of the reagent is always introduced at less hindered carbon of the epoxide ring. In the disubstituted epoxides, inversion of configuration occurs at a carbon to which Nu is added. Identify the product when (1R,3R,4R,6S)-1,3,4-trimethyl-7-oxabicyclo [4.1.0] heptane is reacted with Me2CuLi:\n\nA) (1S,4R,5S)-2,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol\nB) (1R,2S,4R,5R)-1,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol\nC) (1R,2R,4R,5R)-1,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol\nD) (1R,4R,5R)-2,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: When saturated epoxides are reacted with organocuprates, the alkyl group of the reagent is always introduced at less hindered carbon of the epoxide ring. In the disubstituted epoxides, inversion of configuration occurs at a carbon to which Nu is added. Identify the product when (1R,3R,4R,6S)-1,3,4-trimethyl-7-oxabicyclo [4.1.0] heptane is reacted with Me2CuLi:\n\nA) (1S,4R,5S)-2,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol\nB) (1R,2S,4R,5R)-1,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol\nC) (1R,2R,4R,5R)-1,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol\nD) (1R,4R,5R)-2,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "reckl6PkaEMWM50yZ:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 30532, 290, 21416, 25, 2681, 119120, 638, 379, 350, 5507, 17, 21372, 36348, 8, 483, 261, 87455, 5966, 69233, 350, 65, 3869, 76776, 741, 623, 5966, 69233, 382, 220, 22, 12, 1233, 378, 3869, 565, 78, 58, 19, 13, 16, 13, 15, 60, 273, 555, 1986, 483, 22148, 84, 1094, 25, 350, 16, 49, 11, 18, 49, 11, 19, 49, 11, 21, 50, 14080, 16, 11, 18, 11, 19, 15461, 321, 53546, 13, 2632, 261, 89643, 565