 cyclohexane), typical product after opening yields a cyclohexanol with methyl substituents.\n\nOur substrate: 1,3,4-trimethyl-7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptane. That's a bicyclic epoxide with 3 methyl substituents at carbons 1,3,4.\n\nWe need product after nucleophilic attack by Me- from organocuprate, which opens the epoxide at less hindered carbon, and inversion at that carbon. Reaction yields an alcohol (the oxygen becomes OH after protonation). So the final product is a cyclohexanol ring with methyls.\n\nThe answer choices show tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol with various stereochemistry. So the epoxide opening yields a cyclohexanol (6-membered ring). So the bicyclo[4.1.0]heptane opens to a cyclohexane.\n\nThus we need to map the original stereocenters to the product.\n\nLet's attempt to draw the bicyclic epoxide as a cyclopropane fused to a cyclobutane? Wait 7 carbons total: epoxide (C1-O-C6) fused to a four-carbon chain connecting C1 to C6 (bridge of 4 atoms). The other bridge of length 1 is maybe a CH2 bridging? Actually bridging counts: path lengths not including bridgeheads. So there is a 4-atom bridge between the two bridgeheads, a 1-atom bridge, and a 0-atom bridge (direct bond). This corresponds to a strained system: maybe a cyclobutane fused to cyclopropane? Better to use known skeleton: Bicyclo[4.1.0]heptane is a cyclohexane fused to an epoxide? Let's search memory: \"3-oxabicyclo[2.2.1]heptane\" is norbornane oxide. For [4.1.0] it's maybe \"oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptane\" also known as \"bicyclo[4.1.0]heptane oxide\". This is a seven-member skeleton similar to \"oxabicyclo[2.2.1]heptane\" but with zero-length bridge.\n\nOk, maybe easier: The opening leads to a trans relationship? The nucleophile attacks less hindered carbon (the less substituted carbon of epoxide). In disubstituted epoxides, inversion at carbon attacked.\n\nOur epoxide is disubstituted? It has methyl substituents at carbons 1 and 4? Actually the epoxide carbon atoms are the bridgeheads (maybe C1 and C6). Substituents: we have methyl at C1 (R), at C3, at C4. So the epoxide carbon at C1 is tertiary (has methyl substituent). The other carbon (C6) maybe not substituted (has hydrogen). So nucleophile attacks C6 (less hindered). Inversion at C6 (which originally has H). So resulting product will have methyl from Me2CuLi attached to C6 (which becomes a carbon bearing methyl). The oxygen becomes OH on C1 after ring opening (and retains configuration? That carbon does not invert). So we need to assign stereochemistry.\n\nWe need to convert bicyclic to cyclohexanol where original bridgehead C1 becomes carbon bearing OH (position 1). The opposite carbon (C6) becomes carbon bearing methyl (new Me from nucleophile). The other carbons (C2-5) correspond to original chain of 4 atoms, plus C3 and C4 have methyl groups.\n\nThus final ring is cyclohexane with methyls at positions maybe 1 (OH carbon), 2? Actually substituents: original methyls at C1,3,4. And new methyl at C6 after nucleophilic attack. In cyclohexane numbering likely 1 is OH carbon, then adjacent are 2,3,4,5,6.\n\nThus product is tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol with methyls at 2,2,4,5 per some choices. Let's examine options:\n\nA) (1S,4R,5S)-2,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol: methyls at C2 (gem-dimethyl), C4, C5.\n\nB) (1R,2S,4R,5R)-1,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol: methyls at 1? Wait 1 is OH carbon; there is also methyl maybe? The name \"1,2,4,5-tetramethyl\" indicates methyl substituents at C1,2,4,5. But OH already at C1, so C1 also has methyl (makes tertiary alcohol). C2,4,5.\n\nC) (1R,2R,4R,5R)-1,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol: same substitution set but different stereochemistry.\n\nD) (1R,4R,5R)-2,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol: methyls at C2 gem-dimethyl, plus C4,5.\n\nOur substrate had methyl at C1 (bridgehead) which becomes carbon bearing OH (C1). That carbon originally had methyl; after opening, does it retain that methyl? Yes. So product has methyl at C1 from original. Also original had methyl at C3 and C4 which become some positions? After opening, need to map.\n\nLet's assign numbering of original bicyclic: Bridgeheads 1 and 6 (connected by epoxide). The 4-atom bridge perhaps goes: 1-2-3-4-5-6? Actually a 4-atom bridge from 1 to 6 includes atoms 2,3,4,5 (four atoms). The 1-atom bridge includes atom maybe labeled something else? Wait there are total 7 atoms (including O). Bridgeheads 1 and 6, plus O as third vertex maybe? The O is part of 0-length bridge? So the 0-length bridge is the direct bond between 1 and 6 (makes cyclopropane ring with O). The 1-atom bridge could be an atom directly connecting 1 and 6 (makes a cyclobutane?). So there is also a CH2 connecting 1 and 6 (maybe carbon???). That could be carbon 7? But O is at position 7 maybe.\n\nNevertheless, the carbon skeleton after opening yields a cyclohexane ring containing atoms: original 4-atom bridge plus the attacked carbon becomes part of chain.\n\nMaybe simpler: Reaction of epoxide opening under organocuprate yields trans product (like anti opening). For an epoxide attached to a cycloalkyl (i.e., 1,2-epoxy cyclohexane), opening yields trans-1,2-diol after nucleophile addition.\n\nOur product options all have methyls distributed as gem-dimethyl at C2 or not. Which pattern matches original substitution?\n\nOriginal methyls: at C1,3,4. After opening, C1 retains methyl (makes 1-methyl). C3 and C4 become maybe vicinal methyls at positions 4 and5? Let's attempt to draw.\n\nTake cyclohexane ring numbering: start at carbon bearing OH (C1). Going clockwise, C2 corresponds to original bridge carbon maybe C2 of the 4-atom bridge. C3 corresponds to original C3 (which had methyl). C4 corresponds to original C4 (which had methyl). C5 corresponds to original C5 (maybe unsubstituted). C6 corresponds to original carbon 6 (attack site) which after addition gets methyl from Me.\n\nThus final methyl distribution: C1 (original methyl), C3, C4, C6 (new methyl). That's 1,3,4,6-tetramethyl not matching any answer (they have 2,2 gem). But perhaps numbering different; after opening, the carbon previously bearing methyl at C1 could become C2 with gem-dimethyl? Let's verify more concretely.\n\nBetter to sketch using actual structure with CHEM.\n\nBut maybe recall known product: When 1,3,4-trimethyl-7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptane is opened, the product is (1R,2R,4R,5R)-1,2,4,5-tetramethylcyclohexan-1-ol (option C). Let's test logic: It includes methyl at C1 (original), C2 (new from Me nucleophile), C4, C5 (original positions maybe). So C2 is now methyl (from nucleophile) but gem-dimethyl at C2 not present; it's just a single methyl at C2. That matches our expectation that nucleophile adds to carbon 6, which becomes C2 after ring expansion? Might be.\n\nOption C stereochemistry all R (except 1 is R). Option B has 1R,2S etc.\n\nWe need to decide which stereochemistry results.\n\nWe need to examine original stereocenters: (1R,3R,4R,6S) in the epoxide.\n\nOpening at carbon 6 (S configuration) leads to inversion: so the new configuration at carbon derived from 6 will be R? Because inversion changes S to R. So the carbon that becomes bearing Me (after attack) will have configuration R.\n\nThus carbon that corresponds to carbon 6 in product should be R.\n\nWhich carbon number in product is that? Likely C2 (the carbon bearing Me from nucleophile). Thus C2 should be R.\n\nNow look at options where carbon 2 is R: Option C has 2R. Option B has 2S. So likely Option C.\n\nNow need to check other stereocenters: Original methyl-bearing carbons at 3R and 4R. After opening, their relative positions maybe become C4 and C5? need to see if they retain configuration.\n\nIf original 3 becomes product C4? Let's see mapping: the 4-atom bridge from 1 to 6 comprises atoms 2,3,4,5 (if we number along). So after opening, the chain becomes 1-2-3-4-5-6 (linear). When the epoxide opens, the bond between 1 and 6 breaks, making a chain 1-2-3-4-5-6 with OH on 1 and Me on 6. Closing the chain into a cyclohexane, the original order may map to ring.\n\nThus product numbering maybe: C1 = original 1 (with OH), C2 = original 2, C3 = original 3 (has methyl), C4 = original 4 (has methyl), C5 = original 5, C6 = original 6 (now bearing Me). Substituted methyls at C1, C3, C4, C6 = 1,3,4,6-tetramethyl pattern. But answer options show 1,2,4,5 pattern etc. Which indicates a different mapping.\n\nMaybe original numbering of ring after opening changes due to preferred numbering (lowest numbers). The OH carbon is 1. The methyl at original 1 is at carbon bearing OH, which is 1. The methyl from nucleophile attaches to carbon adjacent (maybe 2). So there will be methyl at 2. The original methyl at C3 maybe becomes at C4 after renumbering due to shift of atoms because carbon 6 becomes 2.\n\nLet's check mapping after opening: potential new ring connectivity: Starting with original epoxide (C1-O-C6). Attack at C6 opens bond to O, forming O- (which becomes OH) attached to C1 still. The C6 now bonds to C2 (the start of the 4-atom bridge) after ring opening? Actually the original ring includes C6-C5-C4-C3-C2-C1... Wait the 4-atom bridge is 1-2-3-4-5-6? That includes C2-C3-C4-C5 of length 4 atoms between 1 and 6, yes. So after opening, the bond O-C6 breaks, O remains attached to C1; C6 remains attached to C5 as before, and now also to nucleophile. The original O-C1 bond also remains? Epoxide had O attached to both C1 and C6; after opening, O remains attached to C1 and becomes OH after protonation, and C6 loses bond to O. So new chain: C1-OH; C1 attached to C2 (via original bond). The chain goes C1-C2-C3-C4-C5-C6 (which now has Me). That's a six-membered chain which will close to form cyclohexane (C1 to C6 not directly bonded; they are ends of chain? Actually they are both ends: C1 at one end, C6 at other end; they are already connected via bond? Wait there is original bond between C6 and C5 and also the bond between C6 and C1 is broken; C1 still bonded to C2 and O, not to C6. So to make a cyclohexane, there must be a bond between C1 and C6? But there isn't after opening; the chain is open. However the final product is a cyclohexanol not an open chain; the cyclohexane ring is formed by the chain C1-C2-C3-C4-C5-C6 forming a ring; that is the original 6-carbon skeleton (without O). Indeed before opening, C1 and C6 were connected via the epoxide bridge; after opening, they are not directly bonded; but the chain already comprises those atoms in order: C1-C2-C3-C4-C5-C6 is a chain; that chain can be considered as a cyclohexane if we include a bond between C6 and C1? Actually the ring is defined by the atoms being connected in a closed loop: C1 is bonded to C2, C2 to C3, C3 to C4, C4 to C5, C5 to C6, and C6 to ??? Not to C1. So it's not a ring. Wait but the original skeleton is bicyclic: there is also a 1-atom bridge connecting C1 and C6 (the \"1\" bridge). That 1-atom bridge is a carbon (maybe C7?) that we haven't accounted for. Let's revisit: bicyclo[4.1.0] has three bridges: 4 atoms, 1 atom, and 0 atoms. The 0 atom bridge is the O (forming epoxide). The 1 atom bridge is a carbon connecting the two bridgeheads directly (a CH2). So there is also a C (call it C7) that links C1 to C6 directly. That carbon remains after opening. So after opening, C1 is still bonded to that carbon (C7). That carbon also bonds to C6 (since it originally formed the 1-atom bridge). So after opening, the chain now includes that carbon as part of the ring, making a six-membered ring.\n\nThus the 1-atom bridge (CH2) is part of the final ring. So final ring consists of atoms: C1, C7, C6, C5, C4, C3, C2? That's 7? Hmm.\n\nProbably easier: The product is indeed cyclohexanol with 4 methyls as given. So we rely on stereochemical reasoning: The attacked carbon inverts (S to R). At other stereocenter (original C1) does it retain configuration? Opening does not invert at C1. So its configuration remains R (original 1R). So product carbon bearing OH (C1) has R configuration. So product should have 1R.\n\nNow looking at options: B, C, D have 1R; A has 1S. So exclude A.\n\nNow we have that 2R (attacked carbon) should be R. Option C has 2R. Option B has 2S (incorrect). Option D lack 2 because gem-dimethyl at 2 (2,2 teg). But there is no explicit 2 stereocenter because gem-dimethyl leads to quaternary carbon maybe not chiral? 2 (bearing two methyls) might be achiral (if two identical substituents). Indeed if carbon 2 has two methyl groups, it's not stereogenic. So D cannot correspond to our case because attacked carbon gets a single methyl, not gem-dimethyl.\n\nThus Option C fits: 1R,2R,4R,5R.\n\nNow need to verify that other stereocenters (4,5) are R as original 3R,4R maybe map to 4R,5R. That seems plausible.\n\nThus answer C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.1920922133867862e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999909, "logp_correct": -9.039800194684909e-15, "letter_mass": 1.000000000000009, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6722077496735123, "cot_lp_min64": -1.46776085813201, "frac_below_-5": 0.014814814814814815, "digit_lp_min": -7.753767013549805, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 4185, "lp_end": -0.1164497584104538, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -2.6141813773901643, "p_nocot": 0.0732277100252516, "uplift": 2.6141813773901554, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.03459, "B": 0.892096, "C": 0.073228, "D": 8.6e-05}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "reckwNMHJYPkcbXNZ", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Imagine a radioactive nuclei X(Z,A) can decay into Y(Z-2, A-4) by emitting an alpha particle with partial half life 3.0 minutes. X(Z,A) can also decay into Q(Z+1,A) by decaying a $\\beta^-$ with partial half life 0.098 minutes. If the initial number of X nuclei were 5*10^34 then what is the activity of $\\alpha$ decay after 10 minutes? Note, here Z is proton number and A is mass number.\n\nA) 1.911*10^31 Bq\nB) 3.719 Bq\nC) 117.555 Bq\nD) 113.837 Bq\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Imagine a radioactive nuclei X(Z,A) can decay into Y(Z-2, A-4) by emitting an alpha particle with partial half life 3.0 minutes. X(Z,A) can also decay into Q(Z+1,A) by decaying a $\\beta^-$ with partial half life 0.098 minutes. If the initial number of X nuclei were 5*10^34 then what is the activity of $\\alpha$ decay after 10 minutes? Note, here Z is proton number and A is mass number.\n\nA) 1.911*10^31 Bq\nB) 3.719 Bq\nC) 117.555 Bq\nD) 113.837 Bq\n", "true_answer": "B", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "reckwNMHJYPkcbXNZ:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 17950, 8110, 328, 17406, 57549, 1934, 220, 702, 5438, 4335, 4871, 2086, 328, 2127, 165125, 478, 15, 314, 220, 20, 68, 3020, 13, 3274, 553, 1920, 57549, 32057, 483, 13664, 6375, 2887, 2264, 25, 353, 22305, 16, 14, 17, 11, 727, 102838, 18, 13, 15, 1349, 11, 353, 22305, 16, 14, 17, 11, 6331, 102838, 15, 13, 43616, 1349, 13, 17565, 57549, 9594, 17667, 23814, 314, 17667, 62, 727, 659, 17667, 62, 6331, 13, 17667, 10853, 314, 68859, 17, 820, 353, 22305, 16, 14, 17, 25032, 16255, 2632, 17667, 62, 727, 314, 68859, 17, 14, 18, 13, 15, 1349, 85848, 16, 13, 17667, 62, 6331, 314, 68859, 17, 14, 15, 13, 43616, 1349, 85848, 16, 364, 56734, 17667, 62, 727, 25, 68859, 17, 28, 15, 13, 48779, 18902, 13, 820, 18, 314, 15, 13, 22806, 45354, 777, 12434, 364, 1727, 62, 6331, 25, 220, 15, 13, 48779, 18902, 820, 15, 13, 43616, 314, 220, 22, 13, 42507, 20, 777, 12434, 350, 76945, 741, 41351, 220, 15, 13, 43616, 5438, 6574, 220, 20, 13, 2843, 12068, 13, 2632, 15772, 57549, 2009, 16107, 364, 8270, 17667, 314, 220, 22, 13, 23071, 3152, 777, 12434, 364, 3695, 328, 2127, 63489, 540, 1058, 260, 28, 702, 1349, 25, 478, 2025, 25987, 45, 15, 425, 2794, 8087, 1727, 260, 741, 17667, 306, 777, 12434, 11, 260, 28, 702, 1349, 13, 17667, 260, 314, 220, 22, 13, 23071, 3152, 9, 702, 314, 9912, 13, 40314, 20, 13, 2794, 8087, 9912, 13, 40314, 20, 8, 382, 11069, 3291, 13, 41021, 23864, 25, 319, 85848, 9912, 6574, 220, 16, 13, 15, 68, 12, 1398, 350, 21910, 68859, 7, 702, 25987, 17, 13, 23723, 39008, 11, 813, 2142, 702, 3454, 85848, 9912, 25987, 533, 9912, 14, 17, 13, 23723, 21, 314, 533, 2911, 13, 10018, 871, 6574, 17, 68, 12, 1398, 741, 2632, 478, 2025, 8, 42308, 230, 478, 15, 425, 220, 17, 68, 12, 1398, 13, 478, 15, 28, 20, 68, 3020, 871, 478, 42308, 230, 220, 20, 68, 3020, 425, 220, 17, 68, 12, 1398, 314, 220, 16, 68, 18, 314, 1078, 220, 1353, 15, 63489, 3561, 13, 18956, 3291, 364, 26549, 8110, 540, 1058, 260, 382, 355, 62, 727, 2025, 8, 314, 17667, 62, 727, 425, 478, 2025, 741, 13602, 8110, 395, 484, 6766, 29702, 17667, 62, 727, 478, 2025, 741, 1416, 665, 1217, 3331, 484, 33763, 328, 1885, 1044, 591, 2127, 484, 810, 4493, 17406, 382, 17667, 62, 727, 14, 1727, 23814, 13, 3072, 581, 4279, 679, 478, 2025, 741, 2632, 23864, 1402, 1727, 62, 727, 314, 15, 13, 22806, 45354, 777, 1349, 13, 15629, 316, 777, 3099, 395, 418, 80, 350, 13895, 1044, 777, 3099, 741, 220, 16, 1349, 314, 1910, 265, 11, 813, 17667, 62, 727, 350, 82, 85848, 16, 8, 314, 15, 13, 22806, 45354, 14, 1910, 314, 220, 15, 13, 11822, 24987, 23, 265, 85848, 16, 364, 45, 2025, 8, 42308, 230, 30, 41021, 23864, 945, 36960, 25, 2794, 8087, 9912, 13, 40314, 20, 8, 314, 13570, 2167, 665, 23864, 2360, 1932, 27303, 25, 7649, 112838, 319, 85848, 9912, 314, 220, 16, 13, 15363, 68, 12, 1398, 30, 50557, 319, 85848, 2789, 314, 220, 17, 13, 36137, 68, 12, 2911, 11, 319, 85848, 18, 314, 220, 15, 13, 45354, 7767, 11, 1888, 220, 16, 13, 1161, 68, 12, 1398, 13, 41021, 23864, 483, 2142, 702, 25, 533, 9912, 820, 220, 17, 13, 23723, 39008, 314, 533, 2911, 13, 10018, 13, 220, 702, 85848, 2911, 13, 10018, 314, 220, 702, 183239, 15, 13, 10018, 92, 425, 702, 183239, 2911, 102838, 15, 13, 7866, 425, 702, 183239, 2911, 102838, 16, 13, 4129, 68, 12, 1398, 13, 2632, 6574, 17, 68, 12, 1398, 13, 9700, 364, 84787, 478, 171441, 20, 68, 3020, 425, 16, 13, 4129, 68, 12, 1398, 314, 220, 24, 13, 3384, 68, 17, 42308, 230, 220, 47392, 63489, 364, 10620, 8110, 25, 355, 62, 727, 314, 17667, 62, 727, 350, 82, 85848, 16, 8, 425, 478, 42308, 230, 15, 13, 11822, 24987, 23, 425, 220, 47392, 314, 220, 18, 13, 46970, 265, 85848, 16, 13, 2632, 1078, 220, 18, 13, 3384, 418, 80, 13, 21580, 15248, 5317, 418, 350, 18, 13, 47555, 418, 80, 741, 163508, 19504, 5192, 316, 112605, 364, 84787, 6052, 418, 364, 4383, 10112, 581, 1309, 316, 3331, 484, 1934, 220, 702, 1349, 11, 290, 2086, 328, 2127, 70943, 11, 889, 290, 8110, 395, 17406, 382, 17667, 62, 727, 478, 2025, 8, 472, 4167, 13, 21926, 6145, 364, 84787, 6052, 418, 364], "n_tokens": 760, "words": 260, "band_lo": 144.0, "band_hi": 576.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to calculate activity of alpha decay after 10 minutes given initial number of X nuclei N0 = 5e34. There are two decay modes with partial half-lives: T_{1/2,α}=3.0 min, T_{1/2,β}=0.098 min. Total decay constant λ_total = λ_α + λ_β. λ_i = ln2 / T_{1/2,i}. So λ_α = ln2/3.0 min^-1. λ_β = ln2/0.098 min^-1.\n\nCompute λ_α: ln2=0.693147. /3 =0.231049 per minute.\n\nλ_β: 0.693147 /0.098 = 7.0725 per minute (approx). Indeed 0.098 minutes ~ 5.88 seconds. So β decay much faster.\n\nTotal λ = 7.30355 per minute.\n\nNumber of X atoms at time t=10 min: N(t)=N0 * exp(-λ t). λ in per minute, t=10 min. λ t = 7.30355*10 =73.0355. exp(-73.0355) is extremely small. Let's compute: e^-73 ~ 1.0e-32 (since ln(10)=2.302585, so log10(e^-73)= -73/2.3026 = -31.71 => ~2e-32). So N(t) ≈ N0 * 2e-32. N0=5e34 => N ≈ 5e34 * 2e-32 = 1e3 = about 1000 atoms left. Very small.\n\nAlpha activity at time t is A_α(t) = λ_α * N(t). Because activity for that mode equals λ_α N(t). We can also consider that fraction of decays from X that go via alpha is λ_α/λ_total. But we already have N(t). So compute:\n\nλ_α =0.231049 per min. Convert to per second for Bq (decays per second). 1 min =60 s, so λ_α (s^-1) =0.231049/60 = 0.0038508 s^-1.\n\nN(t) ≈? Let's compute more precisely: exp(-73.0355) = ?\n\nWe can compute using high precision: Use approximation e^-73 = 1.144e-32? Actually e^-70 = 2.515e-31, e^-3 = 0.04979, product 1.25e-32. Let's compute with log10: -73 / 2.302585 = -31.71. 10^-31.71 = 10^{-0.71} *10^{-31}=0.195 *10^{-31}=1.95e-32. So ~2e-32. Good.\n\nThus N≈5e34 *1.95e-32 = 9.75e2 ≈ 975 atoms.\n\nNow activity: A_α = λ_α (s^-1) * N ≈0.0038508 * 975 = 3.754 s^-1. So about 3.75 Bq. Which matches option B (3.719 Bq). Slight differences due to rounding.\n\nThus answer B.\n\nCheck maybe we need to consider that after 10 min, the number of X decreases, but the activity for alpha is λ_α N(t) as done. That's correct.\n\nThus answer B.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.4662635294371285e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999098, "logp_correct": -9.016896521588549e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000904, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.39564325644960613, "cot_lp_min64": -0.855903605858868, "frac_below_-5": 0.004016064257028112, "digit_lp_min": -7.073816299438477, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 747, "lp_end": -0.003416774095967412, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -5.504219579537558, "p_nocot": 0.0040695633123883175, "uplift": 5.504219579537468, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.99579, "B": 0.00407, "C": 9.6e-05, "D": 4.5e-05}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recl0GPPYEAK1PZax", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Consider a system of 3 spin 1/2 particles with a Hamiltonian, H, given by A*(S1*S2+S1*S3+S2*S3), where A is a constant and Si are the spin operators of particle i. The spin-state can be represented by psi=1/sqrt(3)*(up1*up2*down3+up1*down2*up3+down1*up2*up3), where upi and downi represent the spin-up and spin-down state of particle i. Is this state an eigenfunction of H? If it is, what is its energy? If it is not, what is the expectation value of E?\n\nA) It is an eigenstate of H, with eigenvalue 3/4*A*hbar^2\nB) It is an eigenstate of H, with eigenvalue 2/3*A*hbar^2\nC) It is not an eigenstate of H, with expectation value 2/3*A*hbar^2\nD) It is not an eigenstate of H, with expectation value 3/4*A*hbar^2\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Consider a system of 3 spin 1/2 particles with a Hamiltonian, H, given by A*(S1*S2+S1*S3+S2*S3), where A is a constant and Si are the spin operators of particle i. The spin-state can be represented by psi=1/sqrt(3)*(up1*up2*down3+up1*down2*up3+down1*up2*up3), where upi and downi represent the spin-up and spin-down state of particle i. Is this state an eigenfunction of H? If it is, what is its energy? If it is not, what is the expectation value of E?\n\nA) It is an eigenstate of H, with eigenvalue 3/4*A*hbar^2\nB) It is an eigenstate of H, with eigenvalue 2/3*A*hbar^2\nC) It is not an eigenstate of H, with expectation value 2/3*A*hbar^2\nD) It is not an eigenstate of H, with expectation value 3/4*A*hbar^2\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recl0GPPYEAK1PZax:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11433, 538, 290, 4335, 2608, 382, 10561, 5294, 328, 487, 314, 355, 350, 50, 16, 5366, 50, 17, 659, 336, 16, 5366, 50, 18, 659, 336, 17, 5366, 50, 18, 741, 2214, 3407, 17464, 12, 16, 14, 17, 30285, 11, 3609, 17464, 665, 413, 220, 18, 14, 17, 350, 179521, 292, 8, 503, 220, 16, 14, 17, 350, 9489, 83, 741, 623, 4335, 2608, 382, 123676, 1641, 13041, 30, 41021, 28035, 25, 60982, 314, 350, 91, 38816, 38816, 38040, 29, 659, 1022, 38816, 38040, 38816, 29, 659, 1022, 38040, 38816, 38816, 29, 11876, 103946, 18, 13, 1328, 382, 123676, 1641, 13041, 328, 1062, 1920, 30285, 30, 6208, 132194, 30285, 220, 16, 326, 220, 17, 25, 1022, 38816, 38816, 38040, 29, 14081, 1022, 38816, 38816, 38040, 29, 350, 48581, 936, 1022, 38816, 38040, 38816, 29, 14081, 1022, 38040, 38816, 38816, 17084, 1118, 382, 3333, 26, 1022, 38040, 38816, 38816, 29, 14081, 1022, 38816, 38040, 38816, 34397, 2632, 14531, 123676, 13, 1328, 382, 123676, 1641, 1062, 135200, 350, 3086, 4215, 328, 722, 9407, 483, 1920, 869, 11, 1001, 1917, 741, 21926, 290, 336, 28, 18, 14, 17, 11, 284, 28, 10, 16, 14, 17, 2608, 30, 41351, 3609, 17464, 336, 28, 18, 14, 17, 853, 4242, 284, 9407, 25, 1022, 38816, 38816, 38816, 17084, 350, 91, 38816, 38816, 38040, 29, 10, 91, 38816, 38040, 38816, 29, 10, 91, 38040, 38816, 38816, 29, 11876, 103946, 18, 11, 350, 91, 38816, 84409, 29, 10, 91, 38040, 38816, 38040, 29, 10, 91, 84409, 38816, 29, 11876, 103946, 18, 11, 1022, 84409, 38040, 34397, 2632, 290, 4335, 2608, 382, 290, 336, 28, 18, 14, 17, 11, 284, 28, 10, 16, 14, 17, 13, 19233, 480, 382, 448, 10561, 5294, 328, 3609, 17464, 117867, 336, 87201, 61, 17, 483, 10561, 1594, 336, 6181, 10, 16, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 314, 350, 18, 14, 17, 4898, 20, 14, 17, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 314, 350, 1055, 14, 19, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 364, 10620, 487, 314, 355, 350, 50, 16, 5366, 50, 17, 659, 336, 16, 5366, 50, 18, 659, 336, 17, 5366, 50, 18, 741, 1416, 1761, 395, 478, 32803, 11, 581, 679, 336, 87201, 61, 17, 314, 350, 50, 16, 10, 50, 17, 10, 50, 18, 73602, 17, 314, 336, 16, 61, 17, 10, 50, 17, 61, 17, 10, 50, 18, 61, 17, 659, 220, 17, 6181, 16, 5366, 50, 17, 659, 336, 16, 5366, 50, 18, 659, 336, 17, 5366, 50, 18, 741, 2214, 17464, 12, 16, 14, 17, 11, 2454, 7832, 61, 17, 314, 350, 18, 14, 19, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 13, 2632, 1402, 50, 87201, 61, 17, 314, 220, 18, 425, 350, 18, 14, 19, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 659, 220, 17, 21494, 22305, 72, 27, 73, 92, 7832, 5366, 168578, 314, 350, 24, 14, 19, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 659, 220, 17, 21494, 364, 84787, 21494, 314, 350, 50, 87201, 61, 17, 533, 350, 24, 14, 19, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 11876, 17, 364, 637, 1039, 2608, 336, 87201, 61, 17, 314, 350, 1055, 14, 19, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 13, 2632, 21494, 314, 3536, 1055, 14, 19, 8, 533, 350, 24, 14, 19, 74514, 17, 41051, 61, 17, 314, 3536, 21, 14, 19, 11876, 17, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 314, 350, 21, 14, 23, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 314, 350, 18, 14, 19, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 364, 84787, 487, 25177, 402, 495, 2608, 8967, 355, 425, 21494, 314, 355, 425, 350, 18, 14, 19, 8, 5762, 61, 17, 13, 2632, 10561, 1594, 314, 350, 18, 14, 19, 8, 355, 41051, 61, 17, 364, 84787, 480, 382, 448, 10561, 5294, 483, 10561, 1594, 220, 18, 14, 19, 355, 41051, 61, 17, 13, 4569, 15248, 5317, 355, 13, 2632, 6052, 25, 355, 364, 58369, 3503, 30411, 484, 860, 2232, 6086, 13, 11377, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 355, 13], "n_tokens": 653, "words": 206, "band_lo": 129.0, "band_hi": 516.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to determine if the given state is eigenstate of H = A (S1·S2 + S1·S3 + S2·S3). For three spin-1/2 particles, total spin can be 3/2 (quartet) or 1/2 (doublet). The given state is symmetric under exchange? Let's examine: psi = (|↑↑↓> + |↑↓↑> + |↓↑↑>)/√3. This is symmetric under exchange of any two particles? Check swapping particles 1 and 2: |↑↑↓> becomes |↑↑↓> (same), |↑↓↑> becomes |↓↑↑>, which is present; |↓↑↑> becomes |↑↓↑>. So yes symmetric. This is symmetric under any permutation (the sum of all states with two up, one down). That's the S=3/2, m=+1/2 state? Indeed total spin S=3/2 has four m states: |↑↑↑>, (|↑↑↓>+|↑↓↑>+|↓↑↑>)/√3, (|↑↓↓>+|↓↑↓>+|↓↓↑>)/√3, |↓↓↓>. So the given state is the S=3/2, m=+1/2. Therefore it is an eigenstate of total spin squared S_tot^2 with eigenvalue S(S+1)ħ^2 = (3/2)(5/2)ħ^2 = (15/4)ħ^2.\n\nNow H = A (S1·S2 + S1·S3 + S2·S3). We know for N spins, we have S_tot^2 = (S1+S2+S3)^2 = S1^2+S2^2+S3^2 + 2(S1·S2 + S1·S3 + S2·S3). For spin-1/2, each Si^2 = (3/4)ħ^2. So:\n\nS_tot^2 = 3 * (3/4)ħ^2 + 2 Σ_{i<j} Si·Sj = (9/4)ħ^2 + 2 Σ.\n\nThus Σ = (S_tot^2 - (9/4)ħ^2)/2.\n\nIn our state S_tot^2 = (15/4)ħ^2. So Σ = ((15/4) - (9/4))/2 ħ^2 = ((6/4)/2)ħ^2 = (6/8)ħ^2 = (3/4)ħ^2.\n\nThus H acting on this state gives A * Σ = A * (3/4)ħ^2. So eigenvalue = (3/4) A ħ^2.\n\nThus it is an eigenstate with eigenvalue 3/4 A ħ^2. That matches option A. So answer: A.\n\nLet's double-check that no sign issues. Yes.\n\nThus answer: A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.3378546504536644e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999997283, "logp_correct": -2.7160863243907234e-13, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000002716, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.23858230770148497, "cot_lp_min64": -0.48237263896321103, "frac_below_-5": 0.003125, "digit_lp_min": -0.1002158597111702, "answer_first_mention_lp": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "n_cot_tokens": 640, "lp_end": -3.6954811548639555e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -9.00684257253485, "p_nocot": 0.0001225682460452077, "uplift": 9.006842572534579, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.000123, "B": 4e-06, "C": 0.993181, "D": 0.006692}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recl1UtgTVKishAq4", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: acetic acid is treated with bromine, pyridine, and acetic anhydride with heating, forming product 1.\n\n1 is heated with ethanol and a small amount of sulfuric acid, forming product 2.\n\n2 is treated with sodium cyanide, forming product 3.\n\n3 is then treated with excess sodium hydride and 1,5-dibromopentane, forming final product 4.\n\nhow many distinct hydrogen signals will be observable in the 1H NMR spectrum of 4? (some of them maybe very close in chemical shift and thus not practically distinguishable, but the desired answer is the number of chemically distinct hydrogens)\n\nA) 12\nB) 10\nC) 5\nD) 8\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: acetic acid is treated with bromine, pyridine, and acetic anhydride with heating, forming product 1.\n\n1 is heated with ethanol and a small amount of sulfuric acid, forming product 2.\n\n2 is treated with sodium cyanide, forming product 3.\n\n3 is then treated with excess sodium hydride and 1,5-dibromopentane, forming final product 4.\n\nhow many distinct hydrogen signals will be observable in the 1H NMR spectrum of 4? (some of them maybe very close in chemical shift and thus not practically distinguishable, but the desired answer is the number of chemically distinct hydrogens)\n\nA) 12\nB) 10\nC) 5\nD) 8\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recl1UtgTVKishAq4:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 6879, 20747, 290, 8866, 10331, 364, 4459, 25, 1375, 17492, 18655, 350, 3583, 18, 8310, 45485, 741, 14031, 780, 483, 112546, 514, 11, 80887, 110815, 11, 326, 1375, 17492, 448, 23498, 2037, 483, 25419, 13, 1328, 382, 261, 42348, 12, 11825, 31916, 17216, 296, 106479, 350, 86749, 57, 8, 4039, 10478, 387, 