1, 71, 1174, 295, 326, 7680, 472, 261, 6211, 1347, 30, 50557, 11, 21833, 28, 3583, 1388, 1481, 7167, 316, 2190, 350, 690, 8, 9874, 261, 3503, 83, 350, 32551, 643, 741, 17158, 1217, 88996, 316, 21833, 30, 21833, 382, 220, 15, 478, 27029, 6725, 30, 220, 4146, 39091, 350, 40, 28, 20, 14, 17, 11, 4465, 50280, 8, 813, 625, 364, 84787, 581, 3572, 679, 1001, 731, 71, 1174, 295, 120589, 350, 3086, 41933, 1900, 8, 3503, 83, 5192, 316, 88996, 316, 2190, 11, 326, 3613, 731, 71, 1174, 295, 120589, 32995, 10112, 591, 4166, 961, 267, 120283, 19018, 350, 62832, 261, 31149, 514, 39069, 316, 532, 741, 4569, 2023, 1217, 413, 3503, 83, 5192, 316, 88996, 316, 3543, 1203, 350, 62832, 78186, 10049, 741, 2632, 8302, 3503, 83, 11, 3503, 83, 364, 84787, 6052, 355, 364, 39520, 9913, 1058, 11, 357, 738, 5230, 6052, 355, 13], "n_tokens": 2719, "words": 1537, "band_lo": 768.5, "band_hi": 3074.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to understand the reaction sequence and the final product 3, then determine NMR multiplicities of the two most deshielded (i.e., most downfield) hydrogens.\n\nWe have a synthetic scheme: starting with dichlorotitanocene, reacting with methyllithium yields orange product 1 (some organotitanium complex). Then heating with camphor gives product 2. Then zinc dust plus trichloroacetyl chloride gives product 3.\n\nWe must identify structure 3. In organometallic chemistry, dichlorotitanocene is Cp2TiCl2. Reaction with MeLi (actually methyllithium) can give Cp2TiMe2 (dimethyl) after two equivalents? \"Methyllithium is slowly added to a solution of dichlorotitanocene\". If 2 equivalents, produce (C5H5)2TiMe2 (titanocene dimethyl). The orange product probably is Cp2TiMe2 (Ti(IV) with two methyl groups). Indeed Cp2TiMe2 is known as a bright orange compound, Ti(IV) dimethyl.\n\nThen heating with camphor (C10H16O) which is a ketone (bicyclic) would undergo insertion into Ti-Me bonds? Or maybe it reduces to produce Ti(III) species? Camphor is a source of CO? Actually camphor can be used to generate Ti(III) species by oxidative addition? Not sure. But there is known reaction: Cp2TiCl2 + 2 MeLi => Cp2TiMe2 (orange). Then oxidation with camphor yields Cp2Ti(μ-O)TiCp2? Wait.\n\nCamphor is a ketone, could undergo cycloaddition with Ti-Me to give a titanocene carboalkoxylate? Might produce a titanocene oxo (Ti=O) after elimination of CH4? In some literature, Cp2TiMe2 can react with ketones to give titanocene alkoxides via insertion of the Ti–C bond into the C=O, forming a Ti–O–C(=O)R? Actually, methyltitanocene reacts with carbonyl compounds to give alkoxytitanium species (e.g., Cp2Ti(OR)CH3). Not certain.\n\nBut perhaps the reaction with camphor leads to a titanocene alkylidene \"titanocene acetylacetonate\"? Need to think.\n\nGoal: final product 3 after reduction with Zn dust (giving low-valent Ti) and trichloroacetyl chloride (Cl3CCOCl). This seems reminiscent of a McMurry-type coupling: low-valent titanium (Ti(III) or Ti(II)) reacts with acid chloride to give a carbonyl compound with chloro substituents? Actually, Zn/TiCl4 reduces to low-valent Ti species used in McMurry coupling of carbonyls to alkenes. Zn and TiCl4 produce \"low-valent Ti\" (Ti(0) maybe). Reaction of low-valent Ti (e.g., TiCl2) with trichloroacetyl chloride could give a gem-dichloroalkene? Hmm.\n\nBut the product of this sequence is known likely to be \"trichloroacetyl cyclopentadienyl titanium complex\"? The question is about 1H NMR of product 3, focusing on two most deshielded hydrogens. Likely they are hydrogens attached to a carbon adjacent to carbonyl groups (e.g., CHCl2?). Or methine protons in a β-diketone?\n\nMaybe we should identify 3 as \"trichloroacetyl cyclopentadienyl titanium(III) chloride\"? But NMR of titanium complexes? Perhaps the product is \"trichloroacetylindenyl\"? Not.\n\nLet's think: After step 2, product 2 likely is a titanocene(III) complex with a cyclopentadienyl and a carbonyl ligand (e.g., Cp2Ti(=O)?), but camphor may provide an oxygen atom to give Cp2TiO (titanocene(IV) oxo). Actually, known compound: Cp2TiCl2 reduced by Zn gives low-valent Ti(III) species that can do O-atom transfer to give Cp2Ti=O (titanocene(IV) oxo). Camphor is used as an oxygen atom donor to convert Cp2TiCl2 to Cp2Ti(=O)Cl? Wait.\n\nMaybe the reaction: Cp2TiMe2 + camphor => Cp2Ti(O-CH2-...) Not.\n\nWe need to recall known synthetic route to \"titanocene(IV) methylidene\"? No.\n\nBut the final step: adding trichloroacetyl chloride to low-valent Ti can produce a \"titanocene alkylidene\" after reductive coupling of the acid chloride to a carbene? Actually, Ti(II) can generate a Ti–C bond via oxidative addition to C-Cl bond of trichloroacetyl chloride, giving a Ti–C(O)CCl3 moiety which upon further steps leads to \"titanocene(IV) dichloromethyl\", maybe leading to a proton NMR signal for CHCl2 (highly deshielded ~ 6 ppm?). The most deshielded hydrogens could be on cyclopentadienyl ring (around 5.5-6 ppm). Usually Cp protons appear around 5.5-6 ppm as singlet. The question likely expects that the two most downfield protons are the Cp protons which are in a singlet (if equivalent) or doublet etc depending on substitution.\n\nIf product 3 has asymmetric substitution on the Cp rings causing non-equivalent protons, you could have doublets.\n\nConsider isomers: product 3 may have two cyclopentadienyl ligands: Cp (C5H5) each with 5 protons. In many Cp complexes, the protons are equivalent giving singlet. But if one Cp is substituted (e.g., Cp*?), not.\n\nBut steps didn't introduce any substitution on Cp. So product 3 likely still has Cp2Ti(x) with some other ligands. The most deshielded protons are the Cp protons (around 5.8 ppm). In Cp2TiCl2, the Cp protons appear as singlet (5.00 ppm). For asymmetric complexes (e.g., CpTiCl3) they may show multiple signals.\n\nIf product 3 is unsymmetrical, perhaps the two types of Cp protons are magnetically non-equivalent leading to doublets due to coupling to Ti? Wait 1H NMR of Ti complexes often show the Cp protons as singlet because Ti has no 1H coupling.\n\nBut the question expects something about doublet of doublets etc, suggesting coupling to neighboring hydrogens (e.g., CH2 group). The two most deshielded hydrogens may be diastereotopic methylene protons.\n\nLet’s consider that product 3 might be a \"trichloroacetyl cyclopentadienyl titanium (III) dichloride\"? But no H.\n\nMaybe product 3 is a \"trichloroacetyl cyclopentadiene dimer\"? Hmm.\n\nMaybe we can find known reaction: Cp2TiMe2 + camphor => Cp2Ti(O-camphor)Me? Actually, camphor can undergo insertion: Ti–Me inserts into C=O to give Cp2Ti(O-CH(camphor)Me). Heating maybe yields a Ti-oxo (Cp2Ti=O) after β-hydride elimination. In fact, Cp2TiMe2 + 2 equivalents of camphor yields Cp2TiO (titanocene oxo) + other products. The Ti=O double bond is strongly deshielding.\n\nThen Zn reduces Ti=O to Ti(III) species which reacts with trichloroacetyl chloride to give a \"titanium alkyl chloride\" maybe generating a \"chlorocarbene\"? Actually, Ti(III) reduces the acyl chloride generating a Ti–C(O)CCl3 species that could undergo α-elimination to give a Ti=CHCl2 (a dichloromethylidene). This would have a =CHCl2 proton (very downfield ~ 8-10 ppm) like a vinylic proton coupling to chlorine (J_HCl). This might give doublet due to coupling to one Cl (I=3/2), leading to a quartet? Actually, coupling of H to a single Cl gives a doublet (because 2I+1 = 4? Wait chlorine has I=3/2, leads to a quartet of 2:1:1:2 relative intensities. Typically described as a doublet of doublets? But there are two equivalent chlorines maybe.\n\nIf the product is CHCl2 group, the proton will be coupled to two equivalent Cl (spin 3/2 each) leading to a (2I+1)^2 =16 lines, often approximated as a triplet of doublets? Could be described as a \"doublet of doublet of doublets\" perhaps.\n\nBut the answer choices limited: doublet, doublet; doublet, multiplet; doublet of doublets, doublet of doublets; singlet, multiplet. So they think the two most deshielded protons each appear as certain pattern.\n\nPerhaps the product 3 is a \"trichloromethyl\" attached to a carbon with two hydrogens (CHCl2) adjacent to carbonyl? The two most deshielded protons then are the CHCl2 (one proton) and maybe a vinylic proton of an alkene formed (e.g., =CH-). They could both be doublets due to J coupling to each other.\n\nThus pattern doublet, doublet.\n\nLet's test: Suppose product 3 is a gem-dichloroalkene CHCl=CH-? That has two vinylic protons, each coupling to each other (doublet) and possibly to Cl (splitting). The most deshielded would be the vinylic near carbonyl maybe ~7 ppm.\n\nHence answer could be A) doublet, doublet.\n\nBut also could be doublet of doublets for each due to coupling to Cl and to each other: then C.\n\nGiven typical NMR, vinylic proton coupling to adjacent vinylic gives doublet, plus coupling to chlorine gives additional splitting; that yields doublet of doublets (J_H-H and J_H-Cl). So each proton would be a doublet of doublets -> answer C.\n\nThe question says there are several possible isomers of 3, but answer same for all. So irrespective of geometry (E/Z), the coupling pattern of each of the two most deshielded hydrogens is the same.\n\nIf the product is CHCl=CHCl (dichloroethene) but attached to Ti? Wait.\n\nTrichloroacetyl chloride is CCl3COCl. Reaction with low-valent Ti might give CCl3CO–Ti? Perhaps after reductive coupling, you get a \"Ti–C(O)CCl3\" which can eliminate CO to give CCl2=Ti? Not.\n\nBut known reaction: low-valent Ti (e.g., TiCl2) with trichloroacetyl chloride yields \"TiCl3\" plus \"CCl2\" (carbene) which forms \"Ti=CHCl\" (a titanium carbene). Actually, organotitanium carbenes (titanocenes) known as \"Ti=CR2\". For example, Cp2Ti=CH2 (titanocene methylene). Reaction of low-valent Ti with trichloroacetyl chloride may generate Ti=CHCl (Fischer-type) or Ti=CHCl2? Not.\n\nIf product is a titanocene alkylidene: Cp2Ti=CHCl (or =CHCCl3). The proton on the carbene carbon would be deshielded and appear as a singlet? Actually, Ti=CHCl would couple to Cl (one) giving a doublet (large J). Maybe also coupling to Ti? Ti is 0 NMR active? 47Ti (I=5/2, low abundance) so not.\n\nThus we might have one deshielded proton (the carbene) doublet due to coupling to Cl, and another deshielded proton elsewhere maybe from camphor-derived fragment (maybe a methine adjacent to O). That could also be doublet due to coupling to something else (maybe neighboring CH). So pattern doublet, doublet.\n\nThus answer A.\n\nGiven limited time, I will pick answer A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.0251946150674485e-05, "p_correct": 0.999999999992997, "logp_correct": -7.002968224179226e-12, "letter_mass": 1.000000000007003, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7642706301061839, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1614885977922569, "frac_below_-5": 0.015890613451589063, "digit_lp_min": -8.414020538330078, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.4403100311756134, "n_cot_tokens": 2706, "lp_end": -1.9073468138230965e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -8.003431312788782, "p_nocot": 0.000334313523293356, "uplift": 8.00343131278178, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.000334, "B": 0.001924, "C": 0.996575, "D": 0.001167}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "reclkg2VdPopXQr1G", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: The carnivore plants fascinate researchers as, unlike other plants, they can perform fast movements. Recent research revealed that a carnivore plant, Dionaea muscipula, utilizes calcium signalling to close a trap. When sensory hairs in D. muscipula experience mechanical impact, it triggers a flow of calcium ions which enters leaf cells and closes the trap. Several fluorescent proteins can be used to study the sequence of molecular events leading to the trap closing. Which of these biotechnological approaches would not be of any help in studying calcium dynamics?\n\nA) bimolecular fluorescence complementation of yellow fluorescent protein to monitor Ca2+-Ca2+ interactions in vivo\nB) yellow fluorescent protein fused to cationic tails to reflect membrane electrostatic properties\nC) yellow fluorescent protein fused to specific lipid-binding domains to visualize membrane phosphoinositides\nD) green fluorescent protein fused with calmodulin and calmodulin-interacting domains\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: The carnivore plants fascinate researchers as, unlike other plants, they can perform fast movements. Recent research revealed that a carnivore plant, Dionaea muscipula, utilizes calcium signalling to close a trap. When sensory hairs in D. muscipula experience mechanical impact, it triggers a flow of calcium ions which enters leaf cells and closes the trap. Several fluorescent proteins can be used to study the sequence of molecular events leading to the trap closing. Which of these biotechnological approaches would not be of any help in studying calcium dynamics?\n\nA) bimolecular fluorescence complementation of yellow fluorescent protein to monitor Ca2+-Ca2+ interactions in vivo\nB) yellow fluorescent protein fused to cationic tails to reflect membrane electrostatic properties\nC) yellow fluorescent protein fused to specific lipid-binding domains to visualize membrane phosphoinositides\nD) green fluorescent protein fused with calmodulin and calmodulin-interacting domains\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "reclkg2VdPopXQr1G:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 11433, 1118, 7139, 1481, 7116, 1652, 27382, 45948, 40596, 364, 2167, 679, 4242, 3880, 1402, 32, 8, 157032, 114688, 134145, 2149, 20004, 350, 25983, 11058, 8, 328, 865, 20174, 316, 10660, 17241, 17, 44764, 30942, 17, 10, 30722, 306, 32383, 13, 12512, 11058, 23856, 1920, 149071, 328, 865, 20174, 138672, 316, 37394, 484, 20255, 26, 1261, 1023, 20255, 11, 134145, 322, 68389, 4201, 13, 392, 30942, 17, 44764, 30942, 17, 10, 30722, 69029, 152895, 82778, 4128, 8516, 20255, 483, 2454, 1273, 26, 1023, 19129, 37394, 5178, 13, 2632, 12512, 11058, 1481, 625, 10660, 45948, 40596, 364, 33, 8, 865, 20174, 138672, 316, 274, 387, 291, 125813, 316, 12140, 50162, 29674, 4267, 8359, 13, 1328, 2023, 3644, 5377, 1078, 50162, 6451, 6629, 5192, 316, 45948, 111986, 12951, 11, 889, 10112, 625, 8516, 45948, 364, 34, 8, 865, 20174, 138672, 316, 4857, 104828, 125087, 45513, 316, 85009, 50162, 43768, 1979, 25967, 4105, 13, 3168, 32274, 1979, 25967, 4105, 553, 9808, 306, 75242, 11, 10112, 45948, 8232, 364, 35, 8, 147223, 138672, 483, 2005, 4847, 27405, 326, 2005, 4847, 27405, 63516, 79900, 45513, 13, 21926, 261, 45948, 18562, 350, 68, 1940, 4213, 499, 30942, 9125, 741, 4569, 9567, 1481, 1652, 5012, 45948, 364, 2167, 1309, 1118, 1481, 625, 1652, 540, 722, 25, 52762, 1151, 355, 2236, 12512, 11058, 395, 17241, 17, 44764, 30942, 17, 10, 30722, 8740, 1520, 8512, 26, 45948, 82778, 4128, 20255, 483, 2454, 1273, 13, 17796, 418, 10112, 2023, 86512, 12140, 6629, 306, 50162, 8363, 5192, 316, 45948, 111986, 11, 889, 2023, 413, 10297, 13, 17796, 363, 382, 1078, 104828, 75242, 11, 625, 45948, 8516, 26, 889, 665, 413, 997, 328, 76998, 13, 25758, 10112, 3760, 2823, 13, 3072, 4928, 25, 392, 34405, 328, 1879, 4424, 55823, 8339, 25707, 1481, 625, 413, 328, 1062, 1652, 306, 27382, 45948, 40596, 16842, 623, 20486, 382, 355, 2236, 4275, 1078, 17241, 17, 44764, 30942, 17, 10, 22391, 1118, 8740, 4310, 26, 12512, 11058, 58355, 16737, 9666, 60941, 30722, 625, 26485, 30722, 13, 2632, 6052, 355, 364, 84787, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 355], "n_tokens": 354, "words": 251, "band_lo": 151.75, "band_hi": 607.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need determine which approach would NOT help studying calcium dynamics.\n\nWe have four options:\n\nA) bimolecular fluorescence complementation (BiFC) of YFP to monitor Ca2+-Ca2+ interactions in vivo. BiFC involves two halves of YFP fused to proteins that interact; when they interact, fluorescence reconstitutes. \"Ca2+-Ca2+ interactions\"? Calcium ions don't directly interact with each other; they bind proteins etc. So BiFC would not monitor calcium dynamics.\n\nB) YFP fused to cationic tails to reflect membrane electrostatic properties. This could give info about membrane potential changes due to calcium influx perhaps, but maybe not directly calcium.\n\nC) YFP fused to specific lipid-binding domains to visualize membrane phosphoinositides. Phosphoinositides are involved in signaling, maybe calcium release.\n\nD) GFP fused with calmodulin and calmodulin-interacting domains. That's a calcium sensor (e.g., GCaMP). That certainly would help study calcium.\n\nWe need which would not help at all: Likely A because BiFC for Ca2+-Ca2+ interactions doesn't make sense; calcium ions don't interact with each other. Option B maybe could indirectly reflect changes in membrane charge due to calcium influx, but could be helpful. Option C is about lipid signaling, not calcium directly; but can be part of cascade. Still maybe less direct. But question: \"Which of these biotechnological approaches would not be of any help in studying calcium dynamics?\" The worst is A because it's about Ca2+-Ca2+ interaction which doesn't exist; BiFC monitors protein-protein interactions not ion interactions. So answer A.\n\nThus: ANSWER: A", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.5497195136049413e-06, "p_correct": 0.999999999999753, "logp_correct": -2.469620469863829e-13, "letter_mass": 1.000000000000247, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5058428831354419, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9056510247904619, "frac_below_-5": 0.008797653958944282, "digit_lp_min": -0.0010587330907583237, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.11023639142513275, "n_cot_tokens": 341, "lp_end": -0.01958765834569931, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -0.6652432958700002, "p_nocot": 0.5141484223007219, "uplift": 0.6652432958697533, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.514148, "B": 0.242867, "C": 0.242867, "D": 0.000119}}, "force_keep": false, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recm3maIWPaegjbAS", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Consider a pion-nucleon system. The isospin of pion (π) is denoted by t1 and that of nucleon is denoted by t2. Total isospin of the two body system is t. Now, m1 and m2 be the eigenvalues of the Z component of the isospins of nucleon and pion respectively, and m be that of the coupled state. The coupled state is denoted by |t1,t2,t,m>. The eigenstates common to T1^2, T2^2, T1z and T2z is denoted by |t1,t2,m1,m2>. If the system is in coupled state |1,1/2,3/2,3/2>, what  are the correct probabilities of getting the system as (π^+,n),  (π^-,n),  (π^+,p), (π^-,p) and  (pi^o,p) respectively? Note, in the options, p means proton and n means neutron.\n\nA) 0, 1/2, 1/2, 0, 0\nB) 1/3, 2/3, 0, 0, 0\nC) 1/3, 0, 1/3, 0, 1/3\nD) 0, 0, 1, 0, 0\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Consider a pion-nucleon system. The isospin of pion (π) is denoted by t1 and that of nucleon is denoted by t2. Total isospin of the two body system is t. Now, m1 and m2 be the eigenvalues of the Z component of the isospins of nucleon and pion respectively, and m be that of the coupled state. The coupled state is denoted by |t1,t2,t,m>. The eigenstates common to T1^2, T2^2, T1z and T2z is denoted by |t1,t2,m1,m2>. If the system is in coupled state |1,1/2,3/2,3/2>, what  are the correct probabilities of getting the system as (π^+,n),  (π^-,n),  (π^+,p), (π^-,p) and  (pi^o,p) respectively? Note, in the options, p means proton and n means neutron.\n\nA) 0, 1/2, 1/2, 0, 0\nB) 1/3, 2/3, 0, 0, 0\nC) 1/3, 0, 1/3, 0, 1/3\nD) 0, 0, 1, 0, 0\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recm3maIWPaegjbAS:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 23864, 31623, 6229, 332, 13112, 17619, 76338, 395, 382, 5149, 258, 88996, 364, 47, 294, 382, 5149, 258, 260, 16, 314, 220, 16, 350, 51122, 1347, 3127, 9407, 25, 3478, 61, 10, 350, 40, 18252, 314, 659, 16, 936, 3478, 61, 15, 350, 40, 18252, 314, 220, 15, 936, 3478, 85848, 350, 40, 18252, 314, 533, 16, 1029, 45, 68714, 263, 382, 5149, 258, 260, 17, 314, 220, 16, 14, 17, 350, 9489, 83, 3127, 120589, 275, 25, 357, 18252, 314, 659, 16, 14, 17, 11, 107935, 297, 25, 357, 18252, 314, 533, 16, 14, 17, 279, 8270, 382, 5149, 258, 260, 314, 220, 18, 14, 17, 350, 78601, 741, 18595, 82644, 2608, 1022, 83, 16, 28, 16, 11, 260, 17, 28, 16, 14, 17, 26, 260, 28, 18, 14, 17, 11, 284, 28, 18, 14, 17, 34397, 2632, 3609, 357, 18252, 314, 284, 314, 220, 18, 14, 17, 364, 43605, 484, 284, 314, 284, 16, 659, 284, 17, 13, 2632, 31061, 25, 581, 1309, 43835, 1919, 284, 16, 350, 79, 294, 8, 659, 284, 17, 350, 77, 68714, 263, 8, 314, 220, 18, 14, 17, 364, 4827, 553, 4149, 6392, 9407, 30, 284, 16, 665, 413, 659, 16, 11, 15, 8202, 16, 26, 284, 17, 665, 413, 659, 16, 14, 17, 11, 533, 16, 14, 17, 13, 2514, 4215, 316, 220, 18, 14, 17, 11, 581, 1309, 1402, 12, 284, 16, 314, 659, 16, 11, 284, 17, 314, 659, 16, 14, 17, 15155, 8967, 3478, 61, 10, 350, 40, 18252, 28, 10, 16, 8, 326, 120589, 350, 40, 18252, 28, 10, 16, 14, 17, 741, 21926, 3478, 61, 10, 275, 558, 12, 1062, 1273, 30, 1843, 284, 16, 28, 15, 1815, 284, 17, 1481, 1309, 220, 18, 14, 17, 11, 19786, 13, 284, 16, 314, 659, 16, 326, 284, 17, 314, 659, 16, 14, 17, 382, 290, 1606, 14083, 364, 84787, 290, 47087, 2608, 483, 3609, 357, 28, 18, 14, 17, 11, 284, 28, 18, 14, 17, 382, 6462, 1022, 1345, 61, 10, 275, 17084, 860, 187694, 10978, 13, 2632, 34933, 395, 3478, 61, 10, 275, 382, 220, 16, 11, 5036, 220, 15, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 364, 7943, 2371, 484, 3880, 415, 5003, 392, 15, 11, 15, 11, 16, 11, 15, 11, 15, 1243, 66938, 316, 350, 1345, 61, 63549, 77, 8, 34933, 220, 15, 11, 350, 1345, 61, 26300, 77, 8, 220, 15, 11, 350, 1345, 61, 63549, 79, 8, 220, 16, 11, 350, 1345, 61, 26300, 79, 8, 220, 15, 11, 350, 1345, 61, 15, 14835, 8, 220, 15, 13, 1328, 15248, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 364, 7943, 10112, 1354, 382, 161791, 1078, 30931, 25, 1023, 12073, 350, 1345, 61, 63549, 77, 8, 1577, 11, 350, 1345, 61, 26300, 77, 8, 3099, 11, 350, 1345, 61, 63549, 79, 8, 6914, 11, 350, 1345, 61, 26300, 79, 8, 19062, 11, 350, 1345, 61, 15, 14835, 8, 29598, 364, 198140, 415, 364, 84787, 1721, 13], "n_tokens": 503, "words": 171, "band_lo": 84.75, "band_hi": 339.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to compute Clebsch-Gordan coefficients for isospin coupling.\n\nPion isospin t1 = 1 (triplet): states: π^+ (I_z = +1), π^0 (I_z = 0), π^- (I_z = -1)\n\nNucleon isospin t2 = 1/2 (doublet): proton p: I_z = +1/2, neutron n: I_z = -1/2\n\nTotal isospin t = 3/2 (maximum). Coupled state |t1=1, t2=1/2; t=3/2, m=3/2>. So total I_z = m = 3/2.\n\nRemember that m = m1 + m2. So possibilities: we need combinations where m1 (pion) + m2 (nucleon) = 3/2.\n\nWhat are possible component states? m1 can be +1,0,-1; m2 can be +1/2, -1/2. To sum to 3/2, we need:\n\n- m1 = +1, m2 = +1/2 → gives π^+ (I_z=+1) and proton (I_z=+1/2). That's π^+ p.\n- any other? If m1=0 then m2 would need 3/2, impossible. m1 = +1 and m2 = +1/2 is the only combination.\n\nThus the coupled state with total I=3/2, m=3/2 is simply |π^+ p>, no admixture. So probability for π^+ p is 1, others 0.\n\nThus answer D.\n\nBut check that options D says \"0,0,1,0,0\": corresponds to (π^+,n) probability 0, (π^-,n) 0, (π^+,p) 1, (π^-,p) 0, (π^0,p) 0. This matches.\n\nThus answer D.\n\nBut maybe there is nuance about ordering: they listed (π^+,n) first, (π^-,n) second, (π^+,p) third, (π^-,p) fourth, (π^0,p) fifth.\n\nHence D.\n\nThus final.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.907347240148738e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999858, "logp_correct": -1.4157740958379051e-14, "letter_mass": 0.999999761581492, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4065991276415653, "cot_lp_min64": -0.652351675545009, "frac_below_-5": 0.006122448979591836, "digit_lp_min": -0.4740963578224182, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.04260438680648804, "n_cot_tokens": 490, "lp_end": -5.960462772236497e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -7.753108075624354, "p_nocot": 0.00042940583854650876, "uplift": 7.75310807562434, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.002471, "B": 0.000203, "C": 0.996897, "D": 0.000429}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recmI7EiLv72PxmYK", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A supernova occurred in our Galaxy. Part of the supernova ejecta is moving directly towards the center of the Galaxy. Both the ejecta part and the Galaxy are inertial frames of reference. From the reference frame of the ejecta, the center of the Galaxy is moving at 60 000 km/s (sixty thousand kilometers per second) towards it.\nWhat distance the ejecta travels in the Galaxy reference frame when 50 seconds pass in the ejecta reference frame?\n\n(Note: Gravity can be ignored, and rotation and revolution of the Galaxy can be ignored.)\n\nA) 3 000 000 km.\nB) 2 940 000 km.\nC) 3 060 000 km.\nD) 2 880 000 km.\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A supernova occurred in our Galaxy. Part of the supernova ejecta is moving directly towards the center of the Galaxy. Both the ejecta part and the Galaxy are inertial frames of reference. From the reference frame of the ejecta, the center of the Galaxy is moving at 60 000 km/s (sixty thousand kilometers per second) towards it.\nWhat distance the ejecta travels in the Galaxy reference frame when 50 seconds pass in the ejecta reference frame?\n\n(Note: Gravity can be ignored, and rotation and revolution of the Galaxy can be ignored.)\n\nA) 3 000 000 km.\nB) 2 940 000 km.\nC) 3 060 000 km.\nD) 2 880 000 km.\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recmI7EiLv72PxmYK:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 3582, 37575, 2409, 1058, 168176, 13, 457, 728, 64, 19523, 540, 220, 1910, 11, 1302, 8571, 5031, 15001, 316, 66184, 13, 21926, 7733, 323, 314, 220, 1910, 11, 1302, 8571, 5031, 314, 220, 21, 68, 19, 8571, 5031, 314, 220, 21, 68, 22, 284, 5031, 30, 24305, 8571, 316, 284, 25, 220, 1910, 11, 1302, 8571, 28, 21, 68, 22, 284, 13, 23451, 328, 4207, 274, 314, 220, 18, 68, 23, 284, 5031, 314, 220, 4095, 11, 1302, 8571, 5031, 13, 2632, 323, 314, 220, 15, 13, 17, 66, 350, 21910, 220, 1910, 11, 1302, 14, 4095, 11, 1302, 314, 220, 15, 13, 17, 741, 2632, 40570, 314, 220, 16, 820, 44237, 7, 16, 533, 323, 61, 17, 4308, 61, 17, 8, 314, 220, 16, 820, 44237, 7, 16, 533, 220, 15, 13, 3000, 8, 314, 220, 16, 820, 44237, 7, 15, 13, 6768, 8, 314, 220, 16, 820, 220, 15, 13, 48822, 24, 1008, 42308, 230, 220, 16, 13, 19164, 8947, 13, 2632, 7937, 1058, 306, 186904, 64, 7298, 350, 1348, 2316, 2867, 7298, 8, 382, 220, 1434, 265, 13, 730, 66184, 7298, 11, 10067, 16523, 13719, 54189, 25, 26143, 83, 5439, 280, 314, 8558, 26143, 824, 314, 220, 16, 13, 19164, 8947, 9, 1434, 314, 220, 6231, 13, 31403, 265, 13, 623, 9324, 52741, 306, 66184, 7298, 314, 323, 425, 26143, 83, 5439, 280, 314, 220, 15, 13, 17, 66, 425, 220, 6231, 13, 31403, 265, 314, 220, 15, 13, 17, 425, 220, 4095, 11, 1302, 8571, 5031, 425, 220, 6231, 13, 31403, 265, 314, 220, 1910, 11, 1302, 8571, 5031, 425, 220, 6231, 13, 31403, 265, 314, 220, 18, 11, 41957, 11, 34216, 8571, 13, 111766, 220, 18, 11, 26669, 11, 1302, 8571, 350, 3941, 363, 741, 2632, 6052, 363, 364, 4383, 25, 30456, 1354, 413, 5517, 118639, 30, 3072, 581, 1327, 1309, 9324, 52741, 306, 66184, 7298, 26, 15929, 70188, 2360, 66184, 1058, 364, 162661, 11, 2360, 37575, 2409, 328, 27260, 1986, 536, 30, 623, 186904, 64, 7298, 27432, 66184, 10067, 9979, 480, 540, 220, 1910, 11, 1302, 8571, 5031, 13, 730, 484, 7298, 11, 480, 16005, 220, 1434, 265, 11, 290, 66184, 19523, 484, 9324, 25, 272, 6, 314, 323, 425, 26143, 824, 314, 220, 1910, 11, 1302, 425, 220, 1434, 314, 220, 18, 11, 1302, 11, 1302, 8571, 13, 3072, 5192, 316, 5517, 118639, 328, 48294, 26489, 306, 10067, 7298, 30, 5551, 290, 4928, 31064, 25, 392, 4827, 9324, 290, 186904, 64, 50316, 306, 290, 25955, 9682, 7298, 1261, 220, 1434, 12068, 1853, 306, 290, 186904, 64, 9682, 7298, 16842, 2632, 1309, 9324, 52741, 306, 66184, 7298, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 7937, 9324, 741, 1416, 679, 7937, 1058, 10217, 30380, 1058, 9809, 364, 84787, 6052, 363, 364, 4383, 3880, 25, 363, 382, 220, 18, 11, 26669, 11, 1302, 8571, 1118, 15248, 1039, 46716, 364, 84787, 6052, 364], "n_tokens": 490, "words": 219, "band_lo": 72.75, "band_hi": 291.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret special relativity time dilation. Ejecta moves at 60,000 km/s relative to galaxy. That's speed v = 60,000 km/s = 6e4 km/s = 6e7 m/s? Wait km to m: 60,000 km=6e7 m. Speed of light c = 3e8 m/s = 300,000 km/s. So v = 0.2c (since 60,000/300,000 = 0.2). So gamma = 1 / sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2) = 1 / sqrt(1 - 0.04) = 1 / sqrt(0.96) = 1 / 0.9799... ≈ 1.02062. So proper time in ejecta frame (its own rest frame) is 50 s. In galaxy frame, moving clock runs slower: Δt_gal = γ Δτ = 1.02062*50 = 51.031 s. The distance traveled in galaxy frame = v * Δt_gal = 0.2c * 51.031 s = 0.2 * 300,000 km/s * 51.031 s = 60,000 km/s * 51.031 s = 3,061,860 km. Approx 3,060,000 km (option C). So answer C.\n\nCheck: Could there be length contraction? But we just need distance traveled in galaxy frame; we're calculating using galaxy time.\n\nAlternatively, using relativity of simultaneity? The ejecta frame sees galaxy moving towards it at 60,000 km/s. In that frame, it measures 50 s, the galaxy moves that distance: d' = v * Δτ = 60,000 * 50 = 3,000,000 km. But due to length contraction of distances measured in moving frame? However the question asks: \"What distance the ejecta travels in the Galaxy reference frame when 50 seconds pass in the ejecta reference frame?\" So need distance traveled in galaxy frame (i.e., proper distance). We have proper time vs coordinate time difference.\n\nThus answer C.\n\nCheck options: C is 3,060,000 km which matches our computed.\n\nThus answer.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.0967194612021558e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999749, "logp_correct": -2.513265801809499e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000253, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.41205111244391485, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9435401411333493, "frac_below_-5": 0.010482180293501049, "digit_lp_min": -1.9102240800857544, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 477, "lp_end": -3.814624506048858e-05, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -6.255113011106185, "p_nocot": 0.0019206088936550804, "uplift": 6.25511301110616, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.003167, "B": 0.9949, "C": 0.001921, "D": 1.3e-05}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recmYAiujjjtSXXZV", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Estimate the ionization energy of Uranium with atomic number 92, if all the electrons were bosons. Assume that an electron feels the nuclear charge shielded by the charge of half the other electrons in the shell.\n\nA) 35.1 keV\nB) 22.7 keV\nC) 39.2 keV\nD) 28.8 keV\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Estimate the ionization energy of Uranium with atomic number 92, if all the electrons were bosons. Assume that an electron feels the nuclear charge shielded by the charge of half the other electrons in the shell.\n\nA) 35.1 keV\nB) 22.7 keV\nC) 39.2 keV\nD) 28.8 keV\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recmYAiujjjtSXXZV:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 24572, 26485, 2860, 5954, 328, 127440, 2883, 350, 57, 28, 10088, 8, 49328, 722, 100085, 1504, 26134, 1808, 13, 4744, 50546, 25, 23629, 16591, 22614, 8363, 1489, 8488, 314, 1489, 533, 350, 16, 14, 17, 44406, 45, 12, 16, 8, 6961, 392, 74977, 295, 656, 290, 8363, 328, 6375, 290, 1273, 100085, 306, 290, 20059, 4050, 63659, 4665, 2454, 23629, 27432, 8488, 22614, 8363, 1489, 137288, 314, 1489, 533, 220, 15, 13, 20, 14793, 45, 12, 16, 8, 1919, 478, 382, 3609, 100085, 350, 10088, 741, 2632, 1489, 137288, 314, 220, 10088, 533, 220, 15, 13, 20, 14793, 8956, 8, 314, 220, 10088, 533, 220, 2548, 13, 20, 314, 220, 4217, 13, 20, 13, 69578, 2860, 5954, 116685, 780, 656, 47612, 22085, 20690, 25, 457, 314, 350, 57, 137288, 61, 17, 8, 425, 220, 1311, 13, 21, 319, 53, 350, 1938, 7740, 2608, 741, 45438, 25, 220, 4217, 13, 20, 61, 17, 314, 220, 22705, 17, 13, 1161, 13, 4238, 220, 1311, 13, 21, 319, 53, 314, 220, 22705, 17, 13, 1161, 425, 220, 1311, 13, 21, 314, 35717, 57388, 25, 220, 22705, 17, 13, 1161, 9, 702, 314, 220, 22705, 1709, 13, 20, 26, 425, 18, 314, 220, 46295, 21, 13, 3384, 26, 425, 15, 13, 21, 314, 220, 16891, 22, 13, 2467, 26, 4215, 314, 220, 22705, 1709, 13, 20, 10, 46295, 21, 13, 3384, 10, 16891, 22, 13, 2467, 314, 220, 31394, 11, 30, 24305, 4215, 25, 220, 22705, 1709, 13, 20, 10, 46295, 21, 13, 3384, 314, 220, 28637, 3114, 13, 1161, 26, 659, 16891, 22, 13, 2467, 314, 220, 32078, 3218, 13, 21, 319, 53, 13, 21926, 220, 2270, 13, 19, 1610, 53, 364, 5360, 25, 220, 2029, 13, 23, 1610, 53, 5263, 13, 2632, 6052, 415, 364, 4383, 112605, 25, 10112, 1199, 220, 4217, 8488, 8363, 30, 220, 4217, 61, 17, 28, 20350, 21, 425, 1311, 13, 21, 314, 220, 32630, 4388, 319, 53, 314, 2029, 13, 23, 1610, 53, 13, 2632, 415, 13], "n_tokens": 346, "words": 98, "band_lo": 208.0, "band_hi": 832.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to estimate ionization energy of Uranium (Z=92) assuming all electrons were bosons. Assumption: electron feels nuclear charge Z effective = Z - (1/2)*(N-1)?? \"shielded by the charge of half the other electrons in the shell\". Probably treat each electron sees effective nuclear charge Z_eff = Z - 0.5*(N-1) where N is total electrons (92). So Z_eff = 92 - 0.5*(91) = 92 - 45.5 = 46.5. Ionization energy approximated by hydrogen-like formula: E = (Z_eff^2) * 13.6 eV (for ground state). Compute: 46.5^2 = 2162.25. times 13.6 eV = 2162.25 * 13.6 = let's multiply: 2162.25*10 = 21622.5; *3 = 6486.75; *0.6 = 1297.35; sum = 21622.5+6486.75+1297.35 = 293,? Wait sum: 21622.5+6486.75 = 28109.25; +1297.35 = 29406.6 eV. That's 29.4 keV.\n\nOptions: 28.8 keV close. So answer D.\n\nCheck rounding: maybe use 46 effective charge? 46^2=2116 *13.6 = 28778 eV =28.8 keV. So D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": ["too_short"], "accepted": false}
{"qid": "recmkvk6EFAmqyMxR", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Particles are collided at the center of a spherical detector producing new type of particles that travel uninterrupted at ultra-relativistic velocities highly centered around Lorentz factor of ~20. On average, one third of these fast-decaying particles reaches the detector inner walls. \nThe radius of the detector is 30 meters.\n\nWhat Lorentz factor is needed in order to have about two thirds of these particles reaching the detector inner walls?\n\nA) 54\nB) 68\nC) 40\nD) 28\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Particles are collided at the center of a spherical detector producing new type of particles that travel uninterrupted at ultra-relativistic velocities highly centered around Lorentz factor of ~20. On average, one third of these fast-decaying particles reaches the detector inner walls. \nThe radius of the detector is 30 meters.\n\nWhat Lorentz factor is needed in order to have about two thirds of these particles reaching the detector inner walls?\n\nA) 54\nB) 68\nC) 40\nD) 28\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recmkvk6EFAmqyMxR:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 290, 4792, 13, 1328, 382, 261, 35438, 4792, 1078, 30285, 591, 6170, 328, 51811, 13, 3164, 6410, 29274, 100122, 3492, 6207, 157421, 483, 52124, 83928, 12332, 40570, 6574, 455, 13, 2160, 7848, 11, 1001, 6914, 328, 1879, 5661, 57524, 22983, 30285, 42373, 290, 51124, 12694, 20562, 13, 623, 21090, 382, 220, 1130, 284, 13, 3164, 3810, 25, 4614, 52124, 83928, 12332, 382, 6118, 316, 679, 1078, 1920, 188197, 328, 1879, 30285, 29787, 290, 51124, 12694, 20562, 30, 22483, 364, 175703, 387, 25, 71474, 853, 1236, 73104, 24644, 350, 178297, 24644, 95807, 1885, 1044, 134422, 13, 730, 9887, 7298, 11, 1058, 168176, 3658, 24644, 656, 40570, 13, 730, 51811, 21090, 220, 1130, 76, 11, 1023, 1309, 316, 6410, 9324, 316, 9688, 25, 220, 1130, 284, 13, 20922, 540, 7733, 323, 171441, 66, 350, 361, 1787, 100122, 3492, 6207, 741, 623, 34933, 328, 29787, 290, 9688, 382, 290, 31304, 34933, 1072, 290, 6410, 1058, 260, 314, 460, 820, 323, 42308, 230, 220, 1130, 284, 820, 274, 314, 220, 16, 68, 12, 30, 274, 314, 220, 18, 68, 23, 284, 5031, 2747, 260, 314, 220, 1130, 820, 220, 18, 68, 23, 314, 220, 16, 68, 12, 22, 265, 314, 220, 1353, 18343, 13, 50557, 220, 1130, 14, 18, 68, 23, 314, 220, 16, 68, 12, 22, 265, 314, 220, 15, 13, 16, 10449, 13901, 314, 220, 1353, 18343, 13, 2632, 6118, 24644, 306, 9887, 7298, 25, 15586, 119577, 314, 40570, 425, 15586, 62, 15, 13, 623, 31304, 34933, 314, 2794, 8087, 260, 820, 15586, 119577, 741, 1843, 40570, 28, 455, 8967, 1001, 6914, 30153, 25, 2794, 8087, 83, 27334, 455, 15586, 15, 915, 314, 220, 16, 14, 18, 13, 7801, 581, 665, 17722, 395, 15586, 15, 350, 267, 40570, 6118, 395, 34933, 220, 17, 14, 18, 741, 1416, 665, 23864, 364, 12845, 275, 314, 2794, 8087, 83, 27334, 75284, 15586, 15, 8784, 2214, 40570, 28, 455, 11, 275, 314, 220, 16, 14, 18, 13, 2632, 2794, 8087, 83, 27334, 455, 15586, 15, 915, 314, 220, 16, 14, 18, 871, 533, 83, 27334, 455, 15586, 15, 8, 314, 68859, 7, 16, 14, 18, 8, 314, 533, 2943, 220, 18, 871, 260, 27334, 455, 15586, 15, 8, 314, 68859, 220, 18, 13, 2632, 15586, 15, 314, 260, 820, 350, 455, 68859, 220, 18, 741, 6549, 581, 1682, 275, 314, 220, 17, 14, 18, 25, 2794, 8087, 83, 27334, 75284, 13000, 15586, 15, 915, 314, 220, 17, 14, 18, 871, 533, 83, 27334, 75284, 13000, 15586, 15, 8, 314, 68859, 7, 17, 14, 18, 8, 314, 68859, 220, 17, 533, 68859, 220, 18, 314, 533, 2943, 7, 18, 14, 17, 741, 2632, 260, 27334, 75284, 13000, 15586, 15, 8, 314, 68859, 7, 18, 14, 17, 741, 77165, 15586, 15, 12973, 25, 260, 27334, 75284, 13000, 425, 260, 178259, 455, 68859, 18, 915, 314, 68859, 7, 18, 14, 17, 8, 871, 47633, 260, 25, 350, 455, 68859, 18, 11876, 75284, 13000, 314, 68859, 7, 18, 14, 17, 741, 2632, 40570, 13000, 314, 350, 455, 68859, 18, 8, 820, 68859, 7, 18, 14, 17, 3991, 56734, 9076, 2905, 25, 68859, 18, 42308, 230, 220, 16, 13, 43616, 38189, 31868, 13, 68859, 7, 18, 14, 17, 8, 314, 68859, 16, 13, 20, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 29818, 36575, 11003, 13, 137658, 220, 455, 9, 16, 13, 43616, 38189, 31868, 314, 220, 2040, 13, 41079, 23481, 4388, 13, 105916, 656, 220, 15, 13, 29818, 36575, 11003, 871, 40570, 13000, 42308, 230, 220, 6733, 13, 17, 13, 21926, 5317, 355, 220, 6733, 364, 84787, 6052, 355, 364, 4383, 538, 68753, 25, 14531, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 355, 364], "n_tokens": 615, "words": 269, "band_lo": 132.25, "band_hi": 529.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse the problem. This is a physics problem about particles from center of sphere. They travel ultra-relativistic velocities with Lorentz factor gamma ~20. On average, one third of these fast-decaying particles reaches the detector inner walls. The radius is 30 m. They ask: What Lorentz factor is needed to have about two thirds of these particles reaching the detector inner walls? Options.\n\nInterpretation: Particle has some finite lifetime (proper lifetime?), decays exponentially. In lab frame, time dilation extends lifetime by gamma. In sphere radius 30m, they need to travel distance to wall: 30 m. Travel at speed v≈c (ultra-relativistic). The probability of reaching the wall is the survival probability over the travel time t = R / v ≈ 30 m / c = 1e-? c = 3e8 m/s -> t = 30 / 3e8 = 1e-7 s = 100 ns. Actually 30/3e8 = 1e-7 s = 0.1 microsecond = 100 ns. So needed lifetime in lab frame: tau_lab = gamma * tau_0. The survival probability = exp(- t / tau_lab). If gamma=20 gives one third survive: exp(-t/(20 tau0)) = 1/3. Then we can solve for tau0 (or gamma needed for probability 2/3). We can compute.\n\nLet p = exp(-t/(gamma tau0)). For gamma=20, p = 1/3. So exp(-t/(20 tau0)) = 1/3 => -t/(20 tau0) = ln(1/3) = -ln 3 => t/(20 tau0) = ln 3. So tau0 = t / (20 ln 3). Now we want p = 2/3: exp(-t/(gamma_new tau0)) = 2/3 => -t/(gamma_new tau0) = ln(2/3) = ln 2 - ln 3 = -ln(3/2). So t/(gamma_new tau0) = ln(3/2). Plug tau0 expression: t/(gamma_new * t /(20 ln3)) = ln(3/2) => Cancel t: (20 ln3)/gamma_new = ln(3/2). So gamma_new = (20 ln3) / ln(3/2).\n\nCompute numerically: ln3 ≈ 1.098612289. ln(3/2) = ln1.5 ≈ 0.405465108. Multiply 20*1.098612289 = 21.97224578. Divide by 0.405465108 => gamma_new ≈ 54.2. That's option A 54.\n\nThus answer A.\n\nCheck if approximate: yes.\n\nThus answer: A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -6.794906312279636e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999516, "logp_correct": -4.8403489218550106e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000486, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.42379763475725934, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9657769439281747, "frac_below_-5": 0.009966777408637873, "digit_lp_min": -1.2072044610977173, "answer_first_mention_lp": -4.625213477993384e-05, "n_cot_tokens": 602, "lp_end": -0.07354874908924103, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -1.7859267937885228, "p_nocot": 0.16764161976842887, "uplift": 1.7859267937884744, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.167642, "B": 0.751318, "C": 0.033011, "D": 0.04803}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recnawZPumSCLMVgp", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Identify compound E in the following reaction scheme.\nCompound A, when reacted with HCN, produces compound B.\nCompound B, upon reduction with H2 in the presence of Pd, forms compound C.\nCompound C, upon reaction with HNO2, forms compound D.\nCompound D, upon elimination of an inert gas, forms compound E.\nHints:\na) Compound A, when reacted with an unknown phosphorous ylide, forms 1,2-dimethyl-4-(propan-2-ylidene)cyclopentane.\nb) The IR spectrum of compound A exhibits a strong peak at around 1750 cm^-1 whereas, IR spectrum of compound E exhibits strong peak at around 1715 cm^-1.\n\nA) 2,2,3,4-tetramethylcyclobutan-1-one\nB) 3,4-dimethylcyclohexan-1-one\nC) 4-methylcycloheptan-1-one\nD) 2,3,4-trimethylcyclopentan-1-one\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Identify compound E in the following reaction scheme.\nCompound A, when reacted with HCN, produces compound B.\nCompound B, upon reduction with H2 in the presence of Pd, forms compound C.\nCompound C, upon reaction with HNO2, forms compound D.\nCompound D, upon elimination of an inert gas, forms compound E.\nHints:\na) Compound A, when reacted with an unknown phosphorous ylide, forms 1,2-dimethyl-4-(propan-2-ylidene)cyclopentane.\nb) The IR spectrum of compound A exhibits a strong peak at around 1750 cm^-1 whereas, IR spectrum of compound E exhibits strong peak at around 1715 cm^-1.\n\nA) 2,2,3,4-tetramethylcyclobutan-1-one\nB) 3,4-dimethylcyclohexan-1-one\nC) 4-methylcycloheptan-1-one\nD) 2,3,4-trimethylcyclopentan-1-one\n", "true_answer": "B", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recnawZPumSCLMVgp:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11410, 36762, 457, 1934, 261, 5594, 328, 94732, 364, 2167, 679, 26