53546, 936, 363, 18, 101385, 11, 363, 19, 101385, 13, 2632, 290, 12796, 853, 4242, 22148, 84, 1094, 25, 1920, 402, 363, 17, 11, 1001, 2454, 402, 363, 18, 326, 363, 19, 13, 6311, 144217, 483, 261, 342, 97067, 484, 19282, 261, 314, 3583, 12, 3583, 140797, 18, 8, 17, 10112, 30, 623, 1888, 382, 261, 27789, 467, 299, 1986, 350, 6201, 220, 20, 94547, 295, 8, 483, 101385, 82, 540, 363, 16, 11, 17, 350, 38397, 101385, 82, 936, 326, 540, 363, 19, 448, 382, 467, 2283, 3643, 315, 1900, 13, 1328, 7890, 120152, 364, 84787, 355, 6960, 382, 5317, 355, 364, 10620, 1934, 94732, 11, 457, 6960, 413, 261, 12384, 12796, 16262, 690, 483, 30732, 101385, 8896, 30, 24305, 290, 3880, 1562, 1606, 220, 19, 43556, 13, 32417, 1373, 1606, 355, 382, 27789, 630, 28528, 690, 13, 2632, 581, 553, 316, 11410, 457, 350, 13980, 382, 625, 355, 741, 1225, 2023, 413, 418, 11, 363, 11, 503, 415, 3880, 350, 33, 25, 27789, 78, 22803, 270, 690, 26, 363, 25, 27789, 78, 273, 555, 270, 690, 26, 415, 25, 27789, 467, 78520, 690, 741, 33432, 24603, 3572, 5659, 4251, 290, 16281, 25, 46049, 220, 19, 94547, 295, 350, 12360, 630, 28528, 690, 8, 355, 11, 1934, 487, 37266, 5856, 11, 21441, 11, 5178, 4213, 1721, 77324, 328, 133844, 7322, 15286, 316, 12796, 24603, 316, 27789, 467, 299, 12, 503, 27789, 78, 22803, 270, 690, 364, 136225, 77324, 328, 133844, 7322, 350, 30641, 478, 17, 8, 591, 415, 15286, 316, 261, 3503, 15135, 484, 2023, 57190, 98785, 58322, 316, 12384, 12796, 30, 17158, 581, 717, 27789, 467, 78520, 690, 350, 3941, 415, 8, 503, 27789, 78, 22803, 270, 690, 350, 3941, 418, 741, 27067, 402, 23712, 12847, 591, 220, 16080, 15, 316, 220, 19603, 20, 11, 290, 12796, 36689, 3760, 696, 25, 27789, 630, 28528, 690, 350, 16080, 15, 936, 27789, 467, 78520, 690, 350, 19249, 20, 936, 27789, 78, 22803, 270, 690, 350, 19603, 20, 741, 2632, 12847, 591, 220, 16080, 15, 316, 220, 19603, 20, 23379, 12796, 2349, 11202, 316, 220, 21, 13, 2632, 457, 6960, 27789, 78, 22803, 270, 690, 350, 3941, 418, 741, 17796, 415, 27789, 467, 78520, 690, 1481, 413, 2846, 220, 20898, 15, 10112, 11, 625, 220, 19603, 20, 13, 2632, 418, 15248, 290, 23712, 364, 19734, 418, 853, 1920, 101385, 22148, 84, 1094, 26, 1934, 12796, 24603, 581, 3572, 679, 1373, 540, 15897, 220, 18, 11, 19, 350, 181629, 8512, 741, 2632, 6052, 6960, 418, 364, 84787, 5655, 5317, 418, 13], "n_tokens": 2240, "words": 1216, "band_lo": 747.25, "band_hi": 2989.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to identify compound E after a series of transformations.\n\nWe have a reaction scheme. Starting from compound A, reacting with HCN gives B. Then hydrogenation (Pd) reduces B to C. Then reaction with HNO2 yields D. Then elimination of an inert gas gives E.\n\nWe have hints: A when reacted with an unknown phosphorus ylide gives 1,2-dimethyl-4-(propan-2-ylidene)cyclopentane.\n\nIR: A has strong peak at ~1750 cm-1, typical for a carbonyl (C=O) maybe a ketone or ester. 1750 suggests strained cyclic ketone (like cyclobutanone ~ 1760 cm-1). E has strong peak at 1715 cm-1, typical for a less strained ketone (e.g., cyclohexanone ~ 1715). So A is probably a strained small ring ketone (maybe cyclobutanone). The product E after elimination of an inert gas is less strained, maybe cyclohexanone.\n\nLook at answer options: they are all cycloketones with methyl substituents: A list: (A) 2,2,3,4-tetramethylcyclobutan-1-one (i.e., cyclobutanone with 4 methyl substituents). (B) 3,4-dimethylcyclohexan-1-one. (C) 4-methylcycloheptan-1-one. (D) 2,3,4-trimethylcyclopentan-1-one.\n\nWhich of these matches the IR shift? Starting A: 1750 cm-1 suggests cyclobutanone (due to ring strain). Among options, only A is cyclobutanone (2,2,3,4-tetramethylcyclobutan-1-one). So likely compound A is that. Then through transformations, E could be a larger ring ketone: 3,4-dimethylcyclohexan-1-one (B), 4-methylcycloheptan-1-one (C), 2,3,4-trimethylcyclopentan-1-one (D). Which matches final IR ~1715 (typical for cyclohexanone). So perhaps E = 3,4-dimethylcyclohexan-1-one (option B). Let's verify the reaction sequence.\n\nStep 1: A + HCN → B. HCN adds to carbonyl? For a ketone, nucleophilic addition of cyanide yields cyanohydrin. So B would be cyanohydrin of A: i.e., a hydroxy nitrile.\n\nStep 2: B reduction with H2/Pd reduces nitrile to primary amine (maybe also reduces C=C?). Usually H2/Pd reduces nitrile to primary amine (R-CH2-NH2). But can it also reduce the OH? Not. So C would be 2-amino-? Actually B is a cyanohydrin: R-CO-CH(OH)CN? Wait for a ketone A, cyanohydrin addition: carbonyl carbon becomes C(OH)(CN). So structure: A: cyclobutanone with substituents; add HCN → B: same ring with a hydroxy and cyanide attached to the carbon that was carbonyl (now sp3). So B is a β-hydroxy nitrile (actually α-hydroxy nitrile). Then reduction of nitrile to amine gives a β-amino alcohol? So C would be a β-amino alcohol: the carbon attached to OH and NH2.\n\nStep 3: C reacts with HNO2 (nitrous acid). This can convert a primary amine to diazonium? Typically HNO2 with primary aliphatic amine gives diazonium? Actually aliphatic amines with nitrous acid give nitrosamines then decompose to produce N2 and an alcohol (or carbonyl). Classic: primary aliphatic amine + nitrous acid → alcohol + N2 + H2O (deamination). The reaction is called \"deamination\" where the C-N bond is broken, forming carbonyl? Let’s recall: R-CH2-NH2 + HNO2 gives R-CHO (aldehyde) + N2 + H2O. In this case we have a β-amino alcohol, where the amine is at same carbon as OH? Actually C is a carbon bearing both OH and NH2 (geminal). Reaction with nitrous acid probably converts the geminal amino alcohol into a carbonyl? Possibly yields a carbonyl discarding N2 and water leading to a ketone with loss of the carbon bearing OH? Let's think: Starting B: α-hydroxy nitrile, after reduction to amine gives α-amino alcohol. Reaction with nitrous acid leads to formation of a carbonyl and loss of N2. For instance, α-amino alcohol with nitrous acid gives α-keto aldehyde? Not sure.\n\nBut subsequent step: D elimination of inert gas yields E. Likely D is a gem-dihalide? Actually \"elimination of an inert gas\" suggests loss of N2 (inert gas), giving double bond maybe? Wait N2 is inert; elimination of N2 from a diazonium yields a carbocation that can form double bond (like in the formation of alkenes from diazonium). But D formed from HNO2 might be a diazonium derivative (if amine was primary aromatic). However we have aliphatic amine, nitrous acid leads to nitrosamine -> decompose to N2 gas, forming carbonyl (R-CH=O). So elimination of inert gas (N2) could yield a keto or unsaturated.\n\nThus D might be an isocyanate? Hmm.\n\nBut maybe the route is: A (ketone) -> B (α-hydroxy nitrile) -> C (α-amino alcohol) -> D (α-aziridine? no). Actually nitrous acid converts the amino group to a leaving group (diazo?). Then loss of N2 yields a double bond (alkene) extension, producing larger ring via rearrangement perhaps leading to ring expansion. The given hint about phosphorus ylide reacting with A to give 1,2-dimethyl-4-(propan-2-ylidene)cyclopentane. That product is a cyclopentane with a terminal isopropylidene substituent (C=C(CH3)2). That resembles a Wittig reaction of a carbonyl with a phosphonium ylide to give an alkene. So: unknown phosphorus ylide reacts with A (a carbonyl) to give that product. So Wittig of A yields that alkene. The product is a cyclopentane (5-membered) derived from a four-membered ring? Wait A is cyclobutanone with substituents. Wittig reaction would convert carbonyl C=O to C=CHR. If we have cyclobutanone and Wittig gives a cyclobutene? But product is cyclopentane, meaning ring expansion occurred. Perhaps the ylide is a rearrangement causing a homologation – maybe a Wittig-Horner? Actually there is known \"Ring expansion via Wittig reaction of cyclobutanone with a stabilized ylide to give cyclopentene\" known as \"homologation\". The reaction of cyclobutanone with a phosphorus ylide can generate cyclopentane after a [2+1] insertion? I recall that a Wittig with phosphorus ylide of type Ph3P=CH-COCH3 can give cyclopentene via methylene insertion (the \"Grob\" like). But not sure.\n\nNevertheless, the product described: 1,2-dimethyl-4-(propan-2-ylidene)cyclopentane. That is a cyclopentane ring bearing two methyl groups at positions 1 and 2, and an isopropylidene (=C(CH3)2) at position 4. So the original A must have been a cyclobutanone with substituents that after ring expansion and alkene formation gave that.\n\nIf we take A as 2,2,3,4-tetramethylcyclobutan-1-one, does Wittig with some ylide give that product? Let's test: Cyclobutanone carbonyl at carbon1. Melded substituents: positions 2,2 are gem-dimethyl? Actually 2,2,3,4-tetramethyl means methyls at C2 (gem-dimethyl), C3 methyl, C4 methyl. So the ring has four substituents: two on C2, one each on C3 and C4. After reacting with a ylide that adds a =CH-CH(CH3)2 maybe? The product is a cyclopentane (now 5-membered) with methyls at C1,2 (two methyls), and at C4 an isopropylidene. This seems plausible.\n\nThus A likely is option A.\n\nNow after transformations, E likely be a larger ring ketone with fewer methyl groups? Wait the options list only 4 compounds. Among them only A is cyclobutanone. So we are to identify E (which is not A). It could be B, C, or D options (B: cyclohexanone; C: cycloheptanone; D: cyclopentanone). Ring expansion might happen along the sequence: Starting 4-membered (cyclobutanone) A, after HCN addition, reduction, etc., final elimination of inert gas leads to ring expansion to cyclopent- or cyclohexanone.\n\nConsidering elimination of inert gas (likely N2) from D leads to a double bond that could undergo rearrangement to larger ring? Maybe we get cyclopentanone (option D) or cyclohexanone (option B). Based on IR shift from 1750 to 1715, the ring strain lessens: cyclobutanone (1750), cyclopentanone (1725), cyclohexanone (1715). So shift from 1750 to 1715 suggests ring size increased to 6. So E likely cyclohexanone (option B). Option D cyclopentanone would be around 1730 maybe, not 1715. So B matches the IR.\n\nAlso B has two methyl substituents; after ring expansion we might have them at positions 3,4 (makes sense). So answer likely B.\n\nThus choose option B.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -7.152555099310121e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999999999980995, "logp_correct": -1.9004487907378476e-12, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000019005, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6763718333333574, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3243598741087013, "frac_below_-5": 0.014369106421194431, "digit_lp_min": -3.077148199081421, "answer_first_mention_lp": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "n_cot_tokens": 2227, "lp_end": -0.0028532531578093767, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -3.576278118089249e-07, "logp_nocot": -5.505188544368427, "p_nocot": 0.004065621958486653, "uplift": 5.5051885443665265, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.000907, "B": 0.004066, "C": 0.000202, "D": 0.994825}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recnjViFrqlZNL3fY", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Large clouds of neutral hydrogen in deep space absorb the light from background quasars at Lyman alpha wavelength ~1216 Angstrom. Estimate the lower limit on the redshift of this objects such that they can be detected with optical ground-based telescopes?\n\nA) 1.2\nB) 1.9\nC) 3\nD) 2.4\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Large clouds of neutral hydrogen in deep space absorb the light from background quasars at Lyman alpha wavelength ~1216 Angstrom. Estimate the lower limit on the redshift of this objects such that they can be detected with optical ground-based telescopes?\n\nA) 1.2\nB) 1.9\nC) 3\nD) 2.4\n", "true_answer": "B", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recnjViFrqlZNL3fY:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 4928, 13, 1225, 31064, 25, 27976, 45881, 328, 27000, 47612, 306, 8103, 4918, 43623, 290, 4207, 591, 6357, 91631, 1904, 540, 451, 56598, 17406, 79731, 6574, 14503, 21, 5775, 55109, 13, 110243, 290, 6931, 5967, 402, 290, 3592, 27472, 328, 495, 11736, 2238, 484, 1023, 665, 413, 28357, 483, 37876, 7740, 8235, 109816, 25986, 1715, 3223, 933, 7740, 8235, 109816, 25986, 11839, 36144, 220, 11727, 15, 12, 9752, 15, 5775, 55109, 350, 267, 10112, 220, 4812, 15, 12, 6685, 15, 49805, 8, 4335, 72500, 22815, 13, 2632, 581, 1309, 316, 12847, 290, 451, 56598, 149586, 57036, 2543, 591, 220, 14503, 21, 49805, 316, 540, 5153, 290, 37876, 5734, 1424, 6574, 11727, 15, 49805, 350, 18789, 5967, 741, 2632, 290, 3592, 27472, 3759, 25, 20395, 17667, 314, 17667, 70749, 425, 350, 16, 10, 89, 741, 2632, 350, 16, 10, 89, 8, 314, 17667, 89360, 14, 1727, 70749, 13, 17511, 17667, 89360, 6574, 11727, 15, 49805, 350, 183722, 741, 2632, 579, 15950, 314, 220, 11727, 15, 14, 14503, 21, 533, 220, 16, 314, 1078, 220, 17, 13, 2843, 533, 16, 314, 220, 16, 13, 2843, 13, 24305, 23864, 25, 220, 11727, 15, 14, 14503, 21, 314, 220, 17, 13, 38879, 13, 40335, 220, 16, 314, 220, 16, 13, 38879, 13, 2632, 1078, 220, 16, 13, 24, 364, 84787, 6052, 418, 350, 16, 13, 24, 741, 10473, 8663, 25, 220, 16, 13, 17, 1481, 11095, 316, 17667, 89360, 6574, 220, 28399, 19, 49805, 350, 29203, 741, 4037, 143580, 591, 7740, 13, 220, 17, 13, 19, 1481, 11095, 316, 220, 14503, 21, 9, 18, 13, 19, 28, 93, 35751, 19, 49805, 1118, 382, 1217, 6772, 37876, 26, 889, 6931, 5967, 382, 484, 11085, 3592, 27472, 316, 5624, 2543, 1511, 198242, 37876, 11, 813, 220, 16, 13, 24, 13, 2632, 163814, 866, 25, 418, 364, 13462, 316, 7620, 25, 290, 4928, 5003, 392, 32551, 45881, 328, 27000, 47612, 2550, 43623, 290, 4207, 591, 6357, 91631, 1904, 540, 451, 56598, 17406, 79731, 6574, 14503, 21, 49805, 13, 110243, 290, 6931, 5967, 402, 290, 3592, 27472, 328, 495, 11736, 2238, 484, 1023, 665, 413, 28357, 483, 37876, 7740, 8235, 109816, 25986, 16842, 623, 57036, 8698, 7680, 472, 392, 43, 56598, 149586, 19458, 1, 306, 474, 50450, 96858, 26, 290, 4465, 57898, 27472, 100594, 382, 1261, 451, 56598, 149586, 19523, 1511, 290, 72500, 100594, 65176, 15415, 15, 12, 11727, 15, 49805, 741, 2632, 6052, 382, 2846, 220, 17, 13, 623, 6052, 3880, 25, 220, 16, 13, 17, 11, 220, 16, 13, 24, 11, 220, 18, 11, 220, 17, 13, 19, 13, 2632, 220, 16, 13, 24, 382, 35210, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 418, 13], "n_tokens": 454, "words": 220, "band_lo": 53.25, "band_hi": 213.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the question. It asks: Large clouds of neutral hydrogen in deep space absorb the light from background quasars at Lyman alpha wavelength ~1216 Angstrom. Estimate the lower limit on the redshift of this objects such that they can be detected with optical ground-based telescopes?\n\nOptical ground-based telescopes detect roughly 3500-9000 Angstrom (or maybe 4000-8000 Å) given atmospheric transmission. So we need to shift the Lyman-alpha absorption line from 1216 Å to at least the optical window > ~3500 Å (blue limit). So the redshift required: observed λ = λ_rest * (1+z). So (1+z) = λ_obs/λ_rest. Using λ_obs ~3500 Å (lowest). So z_min = 3500/1216 - 1 = about 2.88 -1 = 1.88. Wait compute: 3500/1216 = 2.877. minus 1 = 1.877. So about 1.9.\n\nThus answer B (1.9). Other numbers: 1.2 would correspond to λ_obs ~ 2614 Å (UV). Not reachable from ground. 2.4 would correspond to 1216*3.4=~4134 Å which is also inside optical; but lower limit is that minimum redshift to bring line into detectable optical, so 1.9. So ANSWER: B.\n\nJust to confirm: the question says \"large clouds of neutral hydrogen ... absorb the light from background quasars at Lyman alpha wavelength ~1216 Å. Estimate the lower limit on the redshift of this objects such that they can be detected with optical ground-based telescopes?\" The absorption lines appear as \"Lyman-alpha forest\" in quasar spectra; the low-redshift cutoff is when Lyman-alpha moves into the atmospheric cutoff (~3200-3500 Å). So answer is around 2. The answer options: 1.2, 1.9, 3, 2.4. So 1.9 is nearest.\n\nThus answer: B.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": ["too_long"], "accepted": false}
{"qid": "recnsAVZoGayGIj3g", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Which of the following sequences is correctly ordered by their age? Assume that all the standard and non-standard physical processes (such as rotational history, mixing processes, and initial conditions) are the same for all the stars. The stars are single and not part of a multi-stellar system.\n\na) RGB star with [Fe/H] = 0 dex and M = 5 Msun\nb) Star with Teff = 5700 K, [Fe/H] = 0 dex, logg = 4.2 dex, and M = 1 Msun\nc) RGB star with [Fe/H] = 0 dex and M = 1 Msun\nd) Star with Teff = 5760 K, [Fe/H] = 0 dex, logg = 4.45 dex, and M = 1 Msun\ne) AGB star with [Fe/H] = 0 dex and M = 1 Msun\n\nA) a < d < b < e\nB) c < d < b < e\nC) a < b < e < c\nD) b < d < c < e\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Which of the following sequences is correctly ordered by their age? Assume that all the standard and non-standard physical processes (such as rotational history, mixing processes, and initial conditions) are the same for all the stars. The stars are single and not part of a multi-stellar system.\n\na) RGB star with [Fe/H] = 0 dex and M = 5 Msun\nb) Star with Teff = 5700 K, [Fe/H] = 0 dex, logg = 4.2 dex, and M = 1 Msun\nc) RGB star with [Fe/H] = 0 dex and M = 1 Msun\nd) Star with Teff = 5760 K, [Fe/H] = 0 dex, logg = 4.45 dex, and M = 1 Msun\ne) AGB star with [Fe/H] = 0 dex and M = 1 Msun\n\nA) a < d < b < e\nB) c < d < b < e\nC) a < b < e < c\nD) b < d < c < e\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recnsAVZoGayGIj3g:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11433, 6145, 5744, 30931, 328, 4335, 13701, 13, 134450, 2684, 35438, 11, 4871, 6409, 11, 2684, 60970, 536, 220, 15, 80642, 13, 2545, 553, 4590, 13701, 13, 19792, 316, 12221, 20355, 4122, 402