, 2928, 402, 15861, 11, 480, 39519, 5073, 5025, 261, 1543, 6248, 493, 11, 813, 5744, 3760, 1572, 3716, 2864, 5744, 13, 3604, 17624, 60970, 536, 11, 290, 11628, 382, 220, 19, 13, 21, 499, 3866, 11, 2928, 402, 15861, 483, 2142, 70, 2846, 220, 19, 13, 3336, 13, 2632, 495, 8253, 2023, 413, 6771, 316, 11628, 5744, 13, 2632, 22512, 1572, 290, 46144, 8253, 350, 66, 741, 2632, 30931, 6200, 287, 326, 272, 25, 2973, 553, 2758, 16281, 119149, 26, 287, 382, 2142, 70, 220, 19, 13, 17, 350, 3834, 6248, 493, 936, 813, 12787, 1572, 272, 350, 53939, 402, 15861, 741, 2632, 287, 12787, 1572, 272, 364, 68, 8, 355, 9068, 8253, 11, 391, 28, 16, 30298, 373, 13, 6311, 46144, 11, 355, 9068, 382, 4849, 9402, 11, 889, 5744, 382, 16132, 12787, 1572, 46144, 2236, 1058, 1934, 10511, 328, 46144, 316, 355, 9068, 382, 19919, 4022, 350, 134460, 220, 702, 82, 328, 3673, 81, 741, 2632, 5744, 328, 355, 9068, 8253, 16132, 10740, 1572, 274, 350, 38233, 936, 889, 29212, 2684, 2569, 350, 47163, 2890, 741, 623, 9809, 25, 355, 9068, 8253, 1340, 413, 16132, 12787, 1572, 46144, 8253, 2236, 4275, 4849, 306, 26416, 13, 2632, 274, 464, 319, 30, 50557, 12787, 4748, 12384, 5744, 13, 2632, 30931, 591, 62525, 316, 34331, 25, 62525, 314, 261, 350, 20, 30298, 373, 46144, 741, 13886, 10112, 272, 350, 16, 30298, 373, 15861, 741, 7801, 287, 350, 16, 30298, 373, 1543, 6248, 493, 741, 7801, 274, 350, 16, 30298, 373, 46144, 741, 7801, 319, 350, 2971, 33, 8, 472, 34331, 364, 4383, 3880, 1402, 32, 8, 261, 464, 272, 464, 287, 464, 319, 350, 68892, 274, 8, 625, 6145, 364, 33, 8, 274, 464, 272, 464, 287, 464, 319, 350, 66, 62525, 889, 274, 382, 34331, 26, 8201, 3991, 34, 8, 261, 464, 287, 464, 319, 464, 274, 350, 2143, 25, 62525, 261, 11, 1815, 287, 11, 1815, 319, 11, 1815, 274, 34331, 741, 3072, 581, 679, 272, 1217, 364, 35, 8, 287, 464, 272, 464, 274, 464, 319, 350, 151559, 376, 287, 30, 889, 287, 382, 12787, 1572, 272, 30, 50557, 581, 2411, 272, 22512, 1572, 287, 741, 2632, 415, 5003, 287, 22512, 1572, 272, 11, 1118, 79993, 82, 2142, 70, 13, 8820, 12486, 261, 350, 20, 30298, 373, 741, 4662, 328, 1879, 3981, 364, 62034, 581, 4694, 37352, 30931, 25, 10112, 1023, 3810, 2569, 656, 5744, 889, 625, 23517, 3931, 722, 30, 623, 3880, 1562, 1606, 220, 19, 4732, 2454, 30, 41021, 1729, 290, 4928, 13, 1225, 5003, 392, 34405, 328, 290, 3992, 45665, 382, 20323, 17889, 656, 1043, 5744, 30, 134450, 2550, 623, 13701, 553, 4590, 1008, 4050, 623, 3880, 1562, 45665, 483, 220, 19, 4732, 2454, 13, 3072, 1354, 553, 220, 20, 13701, 55089, 261, 7863, 13, 30391, 2454, 16281, 6664, 220, 19, 328, 1373, 26, 12486, 290, 1001, 484, 8740, 5769, 30, 54458, 1309, 316, 4736, 1118, 30931, 6200, 290, 4242, 4335, 8632, 15248, 5744, 30931, 395, 2617, 4242, 11, 483, 290, 29598, 92895, 350, 62832, 5192, 316, 625, 6118, 741, 41021, 28035, 2454, 5317, 364, 8093, 355, 25, 261, 464, 272, 464, 287, 464, 319, 350, 282, 3025, 842, 274, 741, 4569, 1481, 2569, 62525, 261, 350, 20, 30298, 373, 46144, 8, 464, 272, 350, 16, 30298, 373, 15861, 8, 464, 287, 350, 3834, 6248, 493, 8, 464, 319, 350, 2971, 33, 741, 1328, 15248, 1039, 57927, 6384, 581, 2411, 274, 350, 38233, 220, 16, 30298, 373, 8, 23440, 2870, 287, 326, 319, 350, 267, 1934, 319, 741, 12265, 274, 382, 12486, 11, 10112, 1023, 19429, 484, 274, 382, 12787, 1572, 319, 350, 786, 319, 22512, 1572, 274, 8, 1423, 41021, 2411, 25, 382, 355, 9068, 8253, 12787, 1572, 46144, 30, 50557, 355, 9068, 21241, 1934, 46144, 11, 813, 5744, 382, 16132, 10740, 13, 2632, 319, 1757, 413, 12787, 1572, 274, 350, 267, 12629, 741, 2632, 30931, 1481, 413, 2550, 274, 464, 319, 22476, 13, 17796, 355, 12486, 274, 889, 2569, 261, 464, 272, 464, 287, 464, 319, 20434, 483, 1039, 16281, 11, 538, 274, 1481, 413, 1934, 319, 25, 261, 464, 272, 464, 287, 464, 319, 464, 274, 13, 12265, 274, 625, 12073, 3572, 413, 25435, 13, 17796, 355, 7890, 120152, 364, 8093, 418, 25, 274, 464, 272, 464, 287, 464, 319, 13, 4569, 12528, 274, 350, 16, 30298, 373, 46144, 8, 472, 62525, 11, 1118, 382, 8201, 364, 8093, 363, 25, 261, 464, 287, 464, 319, 464, 274, 13, 10735, 25, 261, 350, 151559, 8, 464, 287, 350, 3834, 6248, 493, 8, 464, 319, 350, 2971, 33, 8, 464, 274, 350, 38233, 741, 4569, 19237, 319, 22512, 1572, 274, 11, 1118, 382, 8201, 350, 2971, 33, 4849, 741, 8820, 12486, 272, 364, 8093, 415, 25, 287, 464, 272, 464, 274, 464, 319, 13, 4569, 19237, 287, 22512, 1572, 272, 350, 3834, 6248, 493, 22512, 1572, 15861, 741, 71062, 364, 84787, 17796, 355, 8224, 1636, 13, 2632, 6052, 314, 355, 364, 84787, 4733, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 355], "n_tokens": 1244, "words": 795, "band_lo": 449.5, "band_hi": 1798.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to determine correct age ordering of given stars. Assume same physics, initial conditions, same metallicity 0 dex. All are single stars. Need to compare ages based on their evolutionary stage (main sequence, RGB, AGB) and mass.\n\nGenerally, higher mass stars evolve faster. So a 5 Msun star on RGB is relatively young compared to 1 Msun stars that are still on main sequence, etc.\n\nWe have objects:\n\na) RGB star, M=5 Msun. At red giant branch, a 5 Msun star must have already left main sequence. Its total age is short, perhaps ~100 Myr? Very young.\n\nb) Star with Teff=5700K, logg=4.2, M=1 Msun. That's a main-sequence star? Teff ~5700 K (like Sun 5778K), logg 4.2 is somewhat lower than Sun (4.44), suggests slightly evolved subgiant? Could be near turnoff: maybe near end of main sequence. Age ~ a few Gyr, near main sequence turnoff for 1 Msun at solar metallicity ~10 Gyr? Actually solar 1 Msun has main sequence lifetime ~10 Gyr. At logg 4.2, likely older than Sun (4.44) -> maybe ~8-10 Gyr? Not sure.\n\nc) RGB star, M=1 Msun. That's a low mass star on red giant branch, age similar to main-sequence lifetime (10 Gyr) plus some evolution, so age ~10-12 Gyr.\n\nd) Star with Teff=5760K, logg=4.45, M=1 Msun. That's a main-sequence star similar to Sun (logg ~4.44), likely younger than c and e but maybe similar to Sun's age ~4.6 Gyr? Actually age could be anywhere up to 10 Gyr; but if still on MS, it hasn't yet become a subgiant, so age less than turnoff age. At solar metallicity, the Sun is 4.6 Gyr, still on MS with logg around 4.44. So this star could be similar to Sun age. So younger than the RGB star (c). So ordering among b and d: both are main sequence-ish; b is logg 4.2 (subgiant), so older than d (still on MS). So b older than d.\n\ne) AGB star, M=1 Msun. After RGB, AGB is later stage, but age is slightly older than RGB because time after tip of RGB to AGB is relatively short (few 10s of Myr). So age of AGB star slightly greater than c (RGB), but essentially same order (both old). The difference: AGB star may be slightly older than RGB star because it's later in evolution. So c < e? Actually older means larger age. So ordering from youngest to oldest: youngest = a (5 Msun RGB). Next maybe d (1 Msun MS). Then b (1 Msun subgiant). Then c (1 Msun RGB). Then e (AGB) as oldest.\n\nCheck options:\n\nA) a < d < b < e (missing c) not correct.\n\nB) c < d < b < e (c youngest but c is oldest; wrong).\n\nC) a < b < e < c (order: youngest a, then b, then e, then c oldest). But we have d also.\n\nD) b < d < c < e (youngest b? but b is older than d? Actually we think d younger than b). So D says b younger than d, which contradicts logg. Also missing a (5 Msun). None of these match.\n\nPerhaps we misinterpret ordering: maybe they ask order by age but not necessarily include all? The options list only 4 items each? Let's read the question. It says \"Which of the following sequences is correctly ordered by their age? Assume ... The stars are single...\". The options list sequences with 4 items each. But there are 5 stars labeled a-e. Perhaps each sequence includes 4 of them; missing the one that doesn't fit? Might need to select which ordering among the four given elements matches age ordering for those four, with the fifth omitted (maybe due to not needed). Let's examine each option.\n\nOption A: a < d < b < e (leaving out c). That would order youngest a (5 Msun RGB) < d (1 Msun MS) < b (subgiant) < e (AGB). This matches our reasoning except we think c (RGB 1 Msun) fits between b and e (or after e). Since c is missing, maybe they assume that c is older than e (so e younger than c) ? Let's think: is AGB star older than RGB? Actually AGB occurs after RGB, so age is slightly greater. So e should be older than c (or equal). So ordering would be ... c < e indeed. Option A missing c but order a < d < b < e consistent with our sequence, if c would be after e: a < d < b < e < c. Since c not listed might be okay. Option A seems plausible.\n\nOption B: c < d < b < e. That orders c (1 Msun RGB) as youngest, which is wrong.\n\nOption C: a < b < e < c. Order: a (young) < b (subgiant) < e (AGB) < c (RGB). That puts e younger than c, which is wrong (AGB later). Also missing d.\n\nOption D: b < d < c < e. That puts b younger than d (subgiant younger than MS). Wrong.\n\nThus Option A looks best. So answer = A.\n\nThus output: ANSWER: A", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -8.344646857949556e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999892, "logp_correct": -1.0805438414552132e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000109, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6799521137006093, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2588260153779611, "frac_below_-5": 0.01462225832656377, "digit_lp_min": -1.1992769241333008, "answer_first_mention_lp": -6.198863957251888e-06, "n_cot_tokens": 1231, "lp_end": -0.034996919333934784, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -11.35021968054998, "p_nocot": 1.1766904380952258e-05, "uplift": 11.35021968054997, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 1.2e-05, "B": 0.904638, "C": 2e-06, "D": 0.095348}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recoKZlD5PwM9zbk6", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: During a quantum communication task of a quantum dialogue for a two-mode entangled squeezed continuous variable state under noiseless quantum channel such that the two modes generated are maximally correlated given the condition, r-> inf for the squeezing operator S(r). What would be the Bell basis measurement outcome after a homodyne detection is performed, if Alice encodes the same value as Bob (alpha) in both quadratures x & p using a displacement operator D(alpha)? (Here alpha = x1 + i p1)\"\n\nA) (0,2*p1)\nB) (2*x1, 0)\nC) (0,0)\nD) (2*x1, 2*p1)\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: During a quantum communication task of a quantum dialogue for a two-mode entangled squeezed continuous variable state under noiseless quantum channel such that the two modes generated are maximally correlated given the condition, r-> inf for the squeezing operator S(r). What would be the Bell basis measurement outcome after a homodyne detection is performed, if Alice encodes the same value as Bob (alpha) in both quadratures x & p using a displacement operator D(alpha)? (Here alpha = x1 + i p1)\"\n\nA) (0,2*p1)\nB) (2*x1, 0)\nC) (0,0)\nD) (2*x1, 2*p1)\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recoKZlD5PwM9zbk6:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 290, 4928, 13, 7744, 1078, 27979, 11076, 48889, 32647, 2360, 261, 1920, 40174, 1121, 103612, 126627, 2608, 11, 483, 48413, 180314, 428, 15155, 35353, 252, 11, 9874, 29045, 2146, 86223, 32057, 350, 36, 8621, 8, 2238, 484, 1215, 6829, 314, 1215, 3201, 326, 275, 6829, 314, 533, 79, 3201, 30, 44130, 395, 1920, 40174, 126627, 37598, 25, 1215, 16, 533, 1215, 17, 15155, 220, 15, 11, 275, 16, 659, 275, 17, 15155, 220, 15, 472, 428, 15155, 35353, 252, 13, 3072, 1023, 2891, 29045, 2146, 86223, 13, 730, 1062, 1890, 1023, 31288, 2684, 1432, 17406, 306, 2973, 39966, 3351, 1215, 827, 275, 2360, 81657, 11019, 415, 82669, 741, 44045, 3219, 3821, 290, 2684, 1432, 472, 22582, 350, 15453, 8, 306, 2973, 39966, 3351, 11, 813, 2973, 6096, 415, 7, 727, 8, 402, 1043, 32057, 30, 623, 27666, 382, 27073, 9571, 27666, 1934, 4801, 1558, 611, 28385, 13, 730, 16178, 27073, 27666, 11, 481, 21700, 290, 1920, 32057, 402, 261, 220, 1434, 25, 1434, 36650, 146455, 326, 1815, 9714, 1215, 402, 1001, 4733, 326, 275, 402, 290, 1273, 13, 623, 24693, 11095, 316, 350, 87, 16, 533, 1215, 17, 11, 275, 16, 659, 275, 17, 8, 395, 48413, 180314, 46307, 13, 1843, 2973, 829, 72793, 553, 2684, 3793, 314, 1215, 16, 659, 575, 275, 16, 11, 1815, 1934, 36650, 146455, 290, 9809, 1215, 16, 533, 1215, 17, 314, 220, 15, 350, 21910, 2684, 8, 326, 4215, 275, 16, 659, 275, 17, 314, 220, 17, 275, 16, 350, 21910, 2684, 741, 2632, 27666, 59684, 350, 15, 11, 220, 17, 79, 16, 741, 4569, 15248, 5317, 355, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 355, 13], "n_tokens": 287, "words": 175, "band_lo": 126.25, "band_hi": 505.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse the question. It's about continuous variable quantum dialogue using a two-mode entangled squeezed state, with infinite squeezing r → ∞, giving maximally correlated modes (EPR) such that x_A = x_B and p_A = -p_B? Usually for two-mode squeezed vacuum: x1 - x2 → 0, p1 + p2 → 0 as r → ∞. But they say maximally correlated. In any case they encode same value alpha in both quadratures x & p using displacement operator D(alpha). Alice encodes the same value as Bob (alpha) in both quadratures, so both apply D(α) on their modes? The measurement is Bell basis measurement after homodyne detection. In CV Bell measurement, you combine the two modes on a 50:50 beam splitter and then measure x on one output and p on the other. The outcomes correspond to (x1 - x2, p1 + p2) for infinite squeezing correlation. If both displacements are same α = x1 + i p1, then after beam splitter the difference x1 - x2 = 0 (since same) and sum p1 + p2 = 2 p1 (since same). So measurement yields (0, 2p1). That matches option A.\n\nThus answer: A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -5.960446742392378e-06, "p_correct": 0.999999999999653, "logp_correct": -3.4698569731741027e-13, "letter_mass": 1.000000000000347, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4730455405786738, "cot_lp_min64": -0.7555709350686595, "frac_below_-5": 0.0036496350364963502, "digit_lp_min": -0.7624682784080505, "answer_first_mention_lp": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "n_cot_tokens": 274, "lp_end": -0.00040451448876410723, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -10.500590274014623, "p_nocot": 2.752020009546909e-05, "uplift": 10.500590274014277, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 2.8e-05, "B": 1e-05, "C": 0.000553, "D": 0.99941}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recoOD3zBJsXtLjBv", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A chemist is synthesizing an RNA nucleoside phoshoramidite from a β-D-ribonucleoside.\n\nFirst, they install the DMTr protecting group on the 5'-OH. Next, they treat the 5’-ODMTr ribonucleoside with 1.1 eq of TBDMSCl; two products are formed (products 1 and 2). They isolate the two products and take the 2D COSY NMR spectrum of each.\n\nImportant features of the COSY spectrum of product 1 are: \n*A correlation between a 1H doublet at approx. 6 ppm (Ha) and a 1H doublet-of-doublets at approx. 4.5 ppm (Hb)\n*A correlation between Hb and a broad singlet at approx. 5.5 ppm\n*A correlation between Hb and another 1H doublet-of-doublets at approx. 4.5 ppm (Hc)\n\nThey discard product 2 and take product 1 through to the final nucleoside phosphoramidite by treating it with 2-cyanoethyl N,N-diisopropylchlorophosphoramidite. They then use this phosphoramidite to synthesize an RNA oligo using standard solid-phase methodology.\n\nIf they compare their synthetic RNA oligo with a naturally occurring RNA oligo with exactly the same types and sequence of bases, what is the relationship?\n\nA) Constitutional isomers, but not regioisomers\nB) Diastereoisomers\nC) Regioisomers\nD) Identical\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A chemist is synthesizing an RNA nucleoside phoshoramidite from a β-D-ribonucleoside.\n\nFirst, they install the DMTr protecting group on the 5'-OH. Next, they treat the 5’-ODMTr ribonucleoside with 1.1 eq of TBDMSCl; two products are formed (products 1 and 2). They isolate the two products and take the 2D COSY NMR spectrum of each.\n\nImportant features of the COSY spectrum of product 1 are: \n*A correlation between a 1H doublet at approx. 6 ppm (Ha) and a 1H doublet-of-doublets at approx. 4.5 ppm (Hb)\n*A correlation between Hb and a broad singlet at approx. 5.5 ppm\n*A correlation between Hb and another 1H doublet-of-doublets at approx. 4.5 ppm (Hc)\n\nThey discard product 2 and take product 1 through to the final nucleoside phosphoramidite by treating it with 2-cyanoethyl N,N-diisopropylchlorophosphoramidite. They then use this phosphoramidite to synthesize an RNA oligo using standard solid-phase methodology.\n\nIf they compare their synthetic RNA oligo with a naturally occurring RNA oligo with exactly the same types and sequence of bases, what is the relationship?\n\nA) Constitutional isomers, but not regioisomers\nB) Diastereoisomers\nC) Regioisomers\nD) Identical\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recoOD3zBJsXtLjBv:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 8789, 13, 46049, 483, 15772, 11989, 6335, 53047, 68714, 617, 11, 3319, 220, 20, 44302, 45485, 483, 34483, 1410, 350, 22215, 1068, 37496, 371, 536, 75, 741, 7801, 4665, 220, 20, 44302, 46, 11989, 44, 1410, 21649, 263, 68714, 365, 617, 483, 196022, 6359, 1388, 350, 96944, 2118, 27040, 582, 321, 53546, 82, 1810, 75, 122304, 8, 220, 16, 13, 16, 18955, 13, 196022, 6359, 1388, 265, 1810, 115713, 2240, 153176, 3643, 82, 11, 13729, 290, 220, 17, 44302, 45485, 326, 220, 18, 44302, 45485, 13, 3813, 58810, 191833, 11, 481, 3572, 717, 69244, 2011, 1810, 4247, 4784, 540, 220, 17, 6, 503, 220, 18, 6, 9874, 1920, 3891, 13, 7451, 220, 16, 10112, 265, 1810, 4247, 540, 220, 17, 6, 42464, 350, 267, 220, 18, 3426, 109376, 56, 1238, 25, 10694, 3503, 83, 6574, 21, 108313, 6960, 448, 22516, 291, 487, 30, 50557, 220, 21, 108313, 382, 19271, 395, 487, 16, 6, 306, 21649, 918, 30, 730, 47081, 365, 4105, 11, 487, 16, 6, 14518, 1078, 220, 20, 13, 24, 12, 21, 13, 16, 108313, 11, 3503, 83, 5192, 316, 88996, 316, 487, 17, 6120, 195354, 540, 220, 19, 13, 20, 108313, 18636, 88996, 316, 487, 16, 6, 326, 487, 18, 6, 10112, 220, 17, 6, 42464, 30, 23494, 6211,