382, 876, 12, 55960, 540, 1617, 220, 18, 44302, 45485, 30, 41021, 30890, 25, 730, 3465, 71461, 190787, 56188, 11, 290, 43768, 123345, 315, 651, 382, 402, 290, 220, 18, 44302, 23498, 96462, 3643, 328, 290, 37780, 190042, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 290, 220, 17, 44302, 45485, 382, 4348, 11, 220, 20, 44302, 45485, 4348, 483, 34483, 1410, 741, 623, 43768, 123345, 315, 651, 141543, 316, 290, 220, 20, 44302, 45485, 328, 290, 10606, 13464, 30, 50557, 7024, 47081, 365, 617, 43768, 123345, 315, 651, 25, 290, 47081, 365, 617, 885, 220, 20, 44302, 45485, 382, 4348, 483, 34483, 1410, 11, 290, 220, 17, 44302, 45485, 382, 4348, 350, 51, 9191, 6359, 5178, 936, 326, 290, 220, 18, 44302, 45485, 382, 28358, 316, 43768, 123345, 315, 651, 350, 17, 3452, 88, 3137, 53546, 478, 49539, 103062, 276, 467, 2283, 3643, 43768, 123345, 315, 651, 741, 623, 220, 18, 44302, 45485, 11526, 290, 43768, 651, 12665, 5119, 484, 29972, 316, 290, 220, 20, 44302, 45485, 328, 290, 10606, 13464, 1934, 95505, 13, 2632, 1023, 1309, 290, 220, 18, 44302, 45485, 2240, 395, 43768, 123345, 315, 651, 18704, 13, 2632, 591, 11965, 11, 1023, 265, 1810, 9459, 1001, 328, 290, 220, 17, 6, 503, 220, 18, 6, 42464, 11, 15021, 290, 1273, 2240, 395, 43768, 123345, 315, 651, 27835, 266, 2860, 13, 12265, 1888, 220, 16, 853, 220, 17, 44302, 45485, 3333, 350, 65, 12086, 6211, 1347, 936, 290, 2240, 42464, 382, 540, 220, 17, 787, 625, 220, 18, 15290, 889, 1023, 1815, 4665, 483, 43768, 123345, 315, 651, 191833, 11, 1118, 1481, 8452, 290, 2240, 42464, 350, 17, 1542, 316, 43768, 123345, 315, 651, 13, 4569, 1481, 3644, 261, 47081, 365, 617, 43768, 123345, 315, 651, 1919, 290, 43768, 651, 382, 18618, 540, 290, 220, 17, 44302, 45485, 13, 4569, 1481, 10635, 448, 59607, 3465, 6235, 1919, 290, 88996, 21241, 4493, 220, 17, 44302, 45485, 7542, 1572, 220, 18, 44302, 45485, 13, 4569, 1481, 10419, 261, 94240, 1919, 290, 144641, 382, 540, 290, 8201, 4010, 25, 7665, 328, 261, 220, 18, 44302, 20, 6, 43768, 13578, 5119, 144641, 350, 98373, 918, 316, 21649, 918, 4493, 220, 18, 44302, 45485, 328, 37780, 47081, 365, 617, 316, 220, 20, 44302, 45485, 328, 10606, 13464, 936, 35174, 679, 261, 220, 17, 44302, 20, 6, 43768, 13578, 5119, 350, 9995, 2491, 306, 1236, 24752, 51667, 60292, 2305, 30, 50557, 1354, 553, 220, 17, 44302, 20, 6, 3461, 1461, 306, 1236, 60292, 2305, 2238, 472, 4454, 39073, 60292, 2305, 741, 623, 4928, 31064, 12221, 37001, 59607, 3465, 6235, 483, 24752, 51667, 59607, 3465, 6235, 483, 2684, 3611, 16281, 13, 51240, 1715, 3335, 290, 37001, 8844, 220, 17, 44302, 45485, 316, 3461, 11, 1815, 12764, 679, 261, 2647, 45849, 350, 1802, 726, 276, 22516, 8, 889, 2684, 51516, 94347, 540, 2454, 549, 46047, 6170, 350, 3086, 21649, 918, 51516, 94347, 73928, 741, 7744, 625, 261, 1320, 3588, 68, 22516, 350, 1750, 620, 51516, 432, 299, 409, 17882, 741, 7744, 261, 61106, 276, 22516, 350, 127306, 4010, 328, 43768, 13578, 5119, 15135, 741, 134373, 382, 119448, 483, 2684, 45849, 889, 625, 2684, 30, 50557, 61033, 382, 119448, 553, 2684, 36628, 20690, 889, 2647, 45849, 26, 61106, 276, 119448, 553, 261, 1490, 328, 61033, 382, 22516, 1919, 38389, 4010, 75483, 402, 2684, 68360, 13, 2632, 6052, 6960, 363, 8, 3955, 726, 276, 119448, 364, 4383, 290, 3880, 25, 355, 8, 134373, 382, 119448, 11, 889, 625, 61106, 276, 119448, 350, 8677, 324, 4090, 741, 418, 8, 91994, 28310, 20671, 119448, 350, 1750, 741, 363, 8, 3955, 726, 276, 119448, 350, 6763, 741, 415, 8, 24859, 933, 350, 1750, 3991, 84787, 6052, 363, 13], "n_tokens": 1188, "words": 635, "band_lo": 317.5, "band_hi": 1270.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the situation. Starting with β-D-ribonucleide, protect 5'-OH with DMTr (dimethoxytrityl). Then treat 5'-O-DMTr ribonucleoside with TBDMSCl (tert-butyldimethylsilyl chloride) 1.1 eq. TBDMSCl silylates free hydroxyls, typically the 2'-OH and 3'-OH. With limiting reagent, you might get mono-silylation either at 2' or 3' giving two products. Product 1 maybe silylation at 2' OH (or 3'). COSY data: Ha doublet ~6 ppm likely anomeric H? Actually 6 ppm is typical for H1' in ribose? In nucleosides, H1' appears about 5.9-6.1 ppm, doublet due to coupling to H2'. Hb at 4.5 ppm dd coupling to H1' and H3' maybe 2' OH? Broad singlet at 5.5 ppm could be OH proton (2'OH or 3'OH). Correlation between Hb and OH indicates OH attached to same carbon? Actually 2' OH coupling to H2'? Typically exchangeable, but in COSY you can see cross-peak if OH not exchanging quickly. So product with coupling between Hb and OH suggests OH on same carbon as Hb? Wait: Hb is at 4.5 ppm dd, could be H2' (attached to carbon with OH). The OH on that carbon would be 2'-OH. So product 1 retains 2'-OH (broad singlet) and silylates the 3'-OH. Therefore product 1 is 3'-O-TBDMS protected (and 5'-O-DMTr). The final phosphoramidite is made by reacting with 2-cyanoethyl N,N-diisopropylchlorophosphoramidite; that reacts at the free 2'-OH? Wait, typical RNA phosphoramidite preparation uses 2'-O-tert-butyldimethylsilyl (TBDMS) and 5'-O-DMTr, and the phosphoramidite is installed at the 3'-OH (the primary alcohol) which is left free and then reacted with the phosphoramidite to give a 3'-phosphoramidite for solid-phase synthesis? Actually for RNA synthesis, the phosphoramidite is attached to the 3'-OH of the nucleoside (the C5' of the nucleoside? Wait nucleosides have ribose with 5'-OH free for linking to next nucleotide. In phosphoramidite method, the nucleoside is pre-activated at its 3'-OH? Let's recall: In oligonucleotide synthesis, the phosphoramidite is on the 3'-hydroxyl of the incoming nucleotide (i.e., the 2'-OH is protected, 5'-OH protected with DMTr). The phosphoramidite attaches to the 5'-OH of the growing chain? Actually standard nucleoside phosphoramidite: the nucleoside's 5'-OH is protected with DMTr, the 2'-OH is protected (TBDMS etc), and the 3'-OH is converted to phosphoramidite (2-cyanoethyl N,N-diisopropyl phosphoramidite). The 3'-OH forms the phosphite triester that couples to the 5'-OH of the growing chain after oxidation. So they need the 3'-OH free for phosphoramidite formation. So from earlier, they silylate one of the 2' or 3' OH, leaving the other free for phosphoramidite derivatization. Since product 1 has 2'-OH present (broad singlet), the free OH is at 2', not 3'; but they then treat with phosphoramidite reagent, which would convert the free OH (2') to phosphoramidite. That would give a nucleoside phosphoramidite where the phosphite is attached at the 2'-OH. That would produce an RNA oligo where the coupling occurs via 2'-OH rather than 3'-OH. That would generate a backbone where the linkage is at the wrong position: instead of a 3'-5' phosphodiester linkage (ribose to ribose via 3'-OH of incoming nucleoside to 5'-OH of growing chain), you'd have a 2'-5' phosphodiester (like found in some naturally occurring RNAs? Actually there are 2'-5' linkages in some RNAs such as certain viral RNAs). The question asks compare synthetic RNA oligo with naturally occurring RNA oligo with same base sequence. Relationship?\n\nIf the synthetic uses 2'-OH to link, then you'll have a different connectivity (regioisomer) but same stereochemistry at each chiral center (the ribose stereochemistry unchanged). It's not a diastereomer (no new stereocenters introduced). It's a regioisomer (different position of phosphodiester bond). Constitutional isomers with same connectivity but not same? Actually constitutional isomers are same molecular formula but different connectivity; regioisomers are a type of constitutional isomer where substitute position differs on same skeleton. So answer likely C) Regioisomers.\n\nCheck the options: A) Constitutional isomers, but not regioisomers (contradiction). B) Diastereoisomers (no). C) Regioisomers (yes). D) Identical (no).\n\nThus answer C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.883489311893527e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999899, "logp_correct": -1.006254191831043e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807907347, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.584277856635365, "cot_lp_min64": -1.0116993004913155, "frac_below_-5": 0.011914893617021277, "digit_lp_min": -3.121060609817505, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.019675912335515022, "n_cot_tokens": 1175, "lp_end": -0.0002169373765354976, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -11.163098961381154, "p_nocot": 1.4188213668231597e-05, "uplift": 11.163098961381143, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 4.4e-05, "B": 0.037325, "C": 1.4e-05, "D": 0.962617}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recooG6bivTUJpDBz", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: The 1H NMR spectrum of a solution containing a 1:1 mixture of two aromatic compounds with the molecular formula C10H14 exhibits two signals (two singlets) at around 6.7 ppm in a 1:1 ratio. There are also three signals at around 2.2 ppm (all singlets) in a 2:1:1 ratio. Which two compounds are present in the mixture from the following options: 1,2,4,5-tetramethylbenzene, 1,2,3,5-tetramethylbenzene, 1,2,3,4-tetramethylbenzene, and 1,4-diethylbenzene.\n\nA) 1,2,4,5-tetramethylbenzene and 1,2,3,5-tetramethylbenzene\nB) 1,2,3,5-tetramethylbenzene and 1,4-diethylbenzene\nC) 1,2,3,4-tetramethylbenzene and 1,2,3,5-tetramethylbenzene\nD) 1,2,4,5-tetramethylbenzene and 1,2,3,4-tetramethylbenzene\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: The 1H NMR spectrum of a solution containing a 1:1 mixture of two aromatic compounds with the molecular formula C10H14 exhibits two signals (two singlets) at around 6.7 ppm in a 1:1 ratio. There are also three signals at around 2.2 ppm (all singlets) in a 2:1:1 ratio. Which two compounds are present in the mixture from the following options: 1,2,4,5-tetramethylbenzene, 1,2,3,5-tetramethylbenzene, 1,2,3,4-tetramethylbenzene, and 1,4-diethylbenzene.\n\nA) 1,2,4,5-tetramethylbenzene and 1,2,3,5-tetramethylbenzene\nB) 1,2,3,5-tetramethylbenzene and 1,4-diethylbenzene\nC) 1,2,3,4-tetramethylbenzene and 1,2,3,5-tetramethylbenzene\nD) 1,2,4,5-tetramethylbenzene and 1,2,3,4-tetramethylbenzene\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recooG6bivTUJpDBz:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 478, 27029, 1238, 13, 13236, 10978, 328, 1920, 110666, 43556, 11, 2973, 363, 702, 39, 1265, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 2454, 382, 363, 702, 3