 741, 17796, 355, 533, 1125, 13, 15, 372, 41, 625, 3981, 13, 2632, 6052, 6960, 415, 533, 17, 13, 8540, 143871, 364, 58369, 3503, 30411, 2360, 533, 1265, 13, 15, 372, 41, 777, 9192, 3411, 30, 6083, 85146, 3644, 26143, 39, 314, 533, 5085, 372, 41, 777, 9192, 3411, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 533, 1311, 13, 21, 143871, 741, 17511, 484, 581, 3508, 533, 994, 13, 4689, 372, 41, 13, 30456, 413, 1023, 1199, 533, 5085, 13, 16, 372, 41, 777, 9192, 871, 533, 994, 13, 4689, 372, 41, 8967, 6052, 355, 30, 3004, 355, 382, 533, 1125, 13, 15, 372, 41, 887, 4037, 3981, 364, 34013, 1023, 1199, 1121, 5512, 5823, 777, 17463, 328, 487, 10, 27000, 2110, 30, 2214, 5532, 18655, 55305, 4275, 533, 5085, 13, 16, 372, 41, 777, 17463, 328, 487, 10, 326, 42464, 12, 144217, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 777, 17463, 3411, 741, 2632, 581, 553, 6145, 364, 8093, 415, 533, 17, 13, 8540, 143871, 29702, 533, 994, 13, 19, 372, 41, 11, 15248, 1039, 46716, 533, 994, 13, 4689, 372, 41, 889, 22171, 306, 143871, 13, 2632, 6052, 415, 364, 84787, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 415], "n_tokens": 806, "words": 369, "band_lo": 183.25, "band_hi": 733.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need enthalpy of neutralization of mixture. We have acid and base solutions mixed. Need total heat released (q) = ΔH_neut * moles of water formed? Usually enthalpy of neutralization per mole of water formed ~ -57.1 kJ/mol (or -13.6 kcal/mol) for strong acid–strong base in aqueous solution. Here we have mixed acid strengths: HCl (strong monoprotic), H2SO4 (diprotic strong first proton, second partially strong). Ba(OH)2 provides OH- 2 per mole. Need to compute limiting reagent and water formed.\n\nCompute moles of H+ from acids:\n\n- HCl: 0.5 L * 0.2 M = 0.1 mol HCl => 0.1 mol H+\n\n- H2SO4: 0.3 L * 0.3 M = 0.09 mol H2SO4. First dissociation gives 0.09 mol H+. Second dissociation: second Ka is about 1.2e-2? Actually second dissociation is relatively strong but not fully, but in strong acid concentrations typical 0.3 M maybe ~0.05? However for enthalpy typical problems treat both protons fully dissociated for strong acid approximations. Let's assume both protons fully release H+. So H+ from H2SO4 = 2 * 0.09 = 0.18 mol.\n\nTotal H+ = 0.1 + 0.18 = 0.28 mol.\n\nMoles of OH- from Ba(OH)2: 0.2 L * 0.5 M = 0.1 mol Ba(OH)2; each gives 2 OH- => 0.2 mol OH-.\n\nNow neutralization: OH- reacts with H+ to form water. Limiting reagent = OH- 0.2 mol vs H+ 0.28 mol. So 0.2 mol water formed. (Remaining H+ = 0.08 mol).\n\nEnthalpy of neutralization: ΔH per mole water = -57.1 kJ (approx). Using that, total q = 0.2 mol * (-57.1 kJ/mol) = -11.42 kJ. Convert to kcal: 1 kcal = 4.184 kJ. So -11.42 kJ = -2.73 kcal.\n\nOptions: A -16.0 kJ, B -3.80 kcal, C -11.42 kcal, D -2.72 kcal. Our calculated -11.42 kJ corresponds to -2.73 kcal. Option D is -2.72 kcal close, but also option C is -11.42 kcal (they treat units maybe kcal?). Wait option C says -11.42 kcal, not kJ. That's too large (converted). Option A -16.0 kJ not match. So answer likely D -2.72 kcal.\n\nLet's double-check using -14.0 kJ per mol water? Some textbooks give ΔH = -57 kJ per mol water (i.e., -13.6 kcal). Using that we got -11.42 kJ. Could be they use -57.1 kJ per mol => -11.42 kJ gives answer A? No A is -16.0 kJ . Not match.\n\nMaybe they use enthalpy per mole of H+ neutralized? For strong acid-base it's -57.1 kJ per mole of H+ and OH- reacting (i.e., per mole water). So we are correct.\n\nOption D -2.72 kcal equals -11.4 kJ, matches our computed -11.42 kJ but expressed in kcal. So answer D.\n\nThus: ANSWER: D", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.072883144104253e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999918, "logp_correct": -8.222074518326151e-15, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807907329, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.4973893175664052, "cot_lp_min64": -1.0689478020760532, "frac_below_-5": 0.008827238335435058, "digit_lp_min": -2.9596498012542725, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.030109994113445282, "n_cot_tokens": 793, "lp_end": -0.010193313471972942, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -5.028588918793352, "p_nocot": 0.006548043876198965, "uplift": 5.028588918793344, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.357511, "B": 0.357511, "C": 0.27843, "D": 0.006548}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recqFDvYKKRmbnoUb", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A vector field f(r) falls off radially as 1/r^2 from the origin, i.e. the magnitude of the field is f(r) = 1/r^2. The field has non-zero component only along  the radial direction in spherical coordinates. Evaluate the volume integral of the divergence of the field, ∇.f inside the volume of a sphere of radius R centred at the origin. Here, ∇ and f both are implied as vectors. Which answer below is the most appropriate one for the answer of the integral?\n\nA) 4 π\nB) 4/3 π R\nC) 1\nD) 0\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A vector field f(r) falls off radially as 1/r^2 from the origin, i.e. the magnitude of the field is f(r) = 1/r^2. The field has non-zero component only along  the radial direction in spherical coordinates. Evaluate the volume integral of the divergence of the field, ∇.f inside the volume of a sphere of radius R centred at the origin. Here, ∇ and f both are implied as vectors. Which answer below is the most appropriate one for the answer of the integral?\n\nA) 4 π\nB) 4/3 π R\nC) 1\nD) 0\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recqFDvYKKRmbnoUb:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 23864, 35353, 255, 62, 53, 350, 18085, 229, 5366, 69, 8, 272, 53, 395, 9727, 3259, 285, 314, 350, 16, 17447, 61, 17, 8, 2381, 13274, 90, 81, 16255, 730, 109563, 27933, 11, 173993, 328, 85692, 3259, 355, 4729, 8, 2381, 13274, 90, 81, 92, 382, 350, 16, 17447, 61, 17, 8, 35353, 224, 14, 18085, 224, 81, 350, 81, 61, 17, 355, 4729, 8784, 7306, 355, 314, 220, 16, 17447, 61, 17, 11, 1815, 428, 61, 17, 355, 314, 220, 16, 11, 66383, 5899, 83, 428, 382, 220, 15, 13, 2632, 173993, 382, 220, 15, 25058, 6384, 14836, 540, 12839, 1919, 3259, 382, 48540, 13, 2632, 290, 10945, 30089, 1072, 51811, 76651, 12839, 382, 220, 15, 13, 3072, 581, 1309, 3931, 26735, 591, 48540, 536, 13, 7649, 18586, 1824, 885, 177750, 25, 35353, 255, 62, 53, 35353, 229, 5366, 69, 272, 53, 314, 35353, 106, 62, 50, 285, 5366, 67, 50, 314, 35353, 106, 62, 50, 350, 16, 17447, 61, 17, 8, 2381, 13274, 90, 81, 92, 5366, 350, 81, 61, 17, 2187, 4114, 272, 4114, 272, 4106, 2381, 13274, 90, 81, 9263, 314, 35353, 106, 62, 50, 2187, 4114, 272, 4114, 272, 4106, 314, 220, 19, 1345, 13, 2632, 30089, 29702, 220, 19, 1345, 13, 21926, 6052, 355, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 220, 19, 1345, 13], "n_tokens": 234, "words": 102, "band_lo": 79.5, "band_hi": 318.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to compute ∭_V (∇·f) dV for vector field f = (1/r^2) \\hat{r}. In spherical coordinates, divergence of radial field A(r) \\hat{r} is (1/r^2) ∂/∂r (r^2 A(r)). Here A = 1/r^2, then r^2 A = 1, derivative wrt r is 0. So divergence is 0 everywhere except possibly at origin where field is singular. So the volume integral over sphere excluding origin is 0. But we need include contribution from singularity. Use Gauss's theorem: ∭_V ∇·f dV = ∮_S f·dS = ∮_S (1/r^2) \\hat{r}· (r^2 sinθ dθ dφ \\hat{r}) = ∮_S sinθ dθ dφ = 4π. So integral equals 4π. That's answer A.\n\nThus answer: 4π.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.814689989667386e-06, "p_correct": 0.999999999999632, "logp_correct": -3.680730778013794e-13, "letter_mass": 1.000000000000368, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.26387479851296897, "cot_lp_min64": -0.5453634598181616, "frac_below_-5": 0.004524886877828055, "digit_lp_min": -0.06796859204769135, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0007122601964510977, "n_cot_tokens": 221, "lp_end": -0.0876159518957138, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -2.579188807233139, "p_nocot": 0.07583549628449186, "uplift": 2.5791888072327707, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.075835, "B": 0.000274, "C": 2.5e-05, "D": 0.923866}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recqP4J5FzKIlQyB9", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: You have prepared an unknown unsaturated compound. To identify the product, you have used the following characterisation techniques: Mass Spectrometry, FTIR, and 1H NMR. The mass spectrum shows the molecular ion peak to be at m/z = 98. The FTIR spectrum shows a strong absorption peak at 1725 wavenumbers. Several peaks were observed in the 1H NMR spectrum including a quartet at 5.3 ppm and a triplet at 9.7 ppm. Identify the product as either 3-methyl-3-penten-2-one, 2-methyl-3-pentenal, 3-methyl-3-pentenal, or 3-methyl-3-penten-2-ol.\n\nA) 2-methyl-3-pentenal\nB) 3-methyl-3-penten-2-ol\nC) 3-methyl-3-pentenal\nD) 3-methyl-3-penten-2-one\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: You have prepared an unknown unsaturated compound. To identify the product, you have used the following characterisation techniques: Mass Spectrometry, FTIR, and 1H NMR. The mass spectrum shows the molecular ion peak to be at m/z = 98. The FTIR spectrum shows a strong absorption peak at 1725 wavenumbers. Several peaks were observed in the 1H NMR spectrum including a quartet at 5.3 ppm and a triplet at 9.7 ppm. Identify the product as either 3-methyl-3-penten-2-one, 2-methyl-3-pentenal, 3-methyl-3-pentenal, or 3-methyl-3-penten-2-ol.\n\nA) 2-methyl-3-pentenal\nB) 3-methyl-3-penten-2-ol\nC) 3-methyl-3-pentenal\nD) 3-methyl-3-penten-2-one\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recqP4J5FzKIlQyB9:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11410, 36762, 4335, 1238, 25, 391, 10, 314, 220, 5080, 13, 44053, 4368, 5532, 57036, 540, 220, 19249, 20, 9256, 12, 16, 350, 34, 28, 46, 21638, 19271, 395, 15883, 3643, 11, 220, 12536, 15, 12, 16080, 15, 13, 2214, 100539, 133496, 6574, 20898,