 382, 290, 6145, 6052, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 363, 13], "n_tokens": 1541, "words": 786, "band_lo": 825.25, "band_hi": 3301.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to identify compound given data: M+ = 98. FTIR strong absorption at 1725 cm-1 (C=O stretch typical for carbonyl, 1700-1750. For aldehyde ~1730? Actually aldehyde carbonyl ~1720-1740, ketone ~1715. Also C=O in conjugated enone shifts down? 1715. The presence of quartet at 5.3 ppm (probably vinylic hydrogen coupled to adjacent CH3? Wait quartet suggests coupling to 3 equivalent protons (n+1=4). Chemical shift 5.3 ppm is vinylic region (alkene). So likely CH=CH-CH3 type CH (vinylic) coupled to CH3 (allylic?). Actually coupling of a vinylic proton to a methyl group would be through 3-bond coupling? Example: CH2=CH-CH3, the terminal CH2 protons couple to CH3 (triplet). But here we have quartet, so proton coupled to 3 protons. So likely CH (vinylic) attached to CH3 group: CH=CH-CH3 (internal alkene). The vinylic proton appears at 5.3 ppm, quartet of 2:1? Could be CH=CH-CH3 where CH (attached to CH3) couples to the 3 protons of CH3 giving quartet. The 3-proton CH3 is a doublet due to coupling to the CH. That suggests a methyl attached to double bond. So we have a methyl group attached to vinylic carbon.\n\nTriplet at 9.7 ppm: typical for aldehyde proton (~9-10 ppm) appears as a singlet often, but can be a triplet if coupled to adjacent CH2. Aldehyde adjacent to CH2 (–CH2–CHO) results in quartet? Actually aldehyde proton couples to neighboring protons (if any). An aldehyde next to a CH2 would appear as a quartet (because n=2, n+1=3 gives triplet? Wait, aldehyde proton couples to CH2 (2 equivalent protons) giving a triplet. If adjacent to CH, then n=1 giving doublet. Here observed triplet at 9.7 ppm consistent with aldehyde proton coupling to CH2 (two protons). So we likely have an aldehyde group attached to CH2.\n\nThus the product is an unsaturated aldehyde with a methyl substituent on the double bond, and the carbonyl is aldehyde (C=O) giving IR 1725 and aldehyde proton at 9.7 ppm triplet.\n\nLet's examine options:\n\nA) 2-methyl-3-pentenal. Structure: Wait \"3-pentenal\" is a 5-carbon chain aldehyde with double bond at position 3? Actually 3-pentenal: chain of 5 carbon; aldehyde at C1, double bond between C3 and C4? Let's draw: CHO-CH2-CH=CH-CH3? That's 3-pentenal (also called pent-3-enal). 2-methyl version adds methyl at carbon 2 (alpha to aldehyde). So structure: CHO-CH(CH3)-CH=CH-CH3. That contains an aldehyde carbon attached to CH (bearing methyl). The aldehyde proton would be adjacent to CH (one proton) so would be a doublet not triplet. In our NMR we have triplet aldehyde at 9.7 ppm, indicating two neighboring protons. So not A.\n\nB) 3-methyl-3-penten-2-ol. This is an allylic alcohol, carbonyl is OH (C-O). IR strong absorption at 1725 would be carbonyl? Alcohol O-H stretch ~3300, not 1725. So not.\n\nC) 3-methyl-3-pentenal. Let's draw: pent-3-enal with methyl at carbon 3? Actually 3-methyl-3-pentenal: chain 5 carbon, aldehyde at C1, double bond at C3? Wait naming: \"3-pentenal\" already includes aldehyde at carbon1, double bond between C3 and C4. Add methyl at carbon3 (the same carbon involved in double bond). So structure: CHO-CH2-C(=CH2)-CH3? No, need to sketch.\n\nPent-3-enal: CHO-CH2-CH=CH-CH3. For 3-methyl-3-pentenal: methyl at C3 (the double bond carbon). So CHO-CH2-C(CH3)=CH-CH3? Actually C3 is the carbon of double bond (the one closer to aldehyde). So structure: CHO-CH2-C(CH3)=CH-CH3. So the double bond carbon (C3) is substituted with methyl. So we have CHO-CH2- C*(CH3)=CH-CH3. The aldehyde is at C1, adjacent CH2 (C2). The aldehyde proton couples to CH2 (2 protons) -> triplet, matching observed. Good.\n\nNow we need quartet at 5.3 ppm: which proton is that? Likely the vinylic proton on C4 (CH). The vinylic proton adjacent to CH3 (on C5) would be coupled to 3 protons (methyl) giving quartet. Indeed C4 is CH, attached to CH3 (C5) and double-bonded to C3 which has methyl substituent. So the C4 vinylic proton couples to the methyl (3H) to give quartet, chemical shift around 5.3 ppm typical. That fits.\n\nCheck other signals: methyl attached to double bond (C3) likely appears as doublet at ~1.7? But not described.\n\nMolecular weight: Structure formula: Let's compute: CHO-CH2-C(CH3)=CH-CH3.\n\nNumber atoms: Carbon count: 5 (pent) plus extra methyl substituent at C3 => total 6 carbons. Let's verify: C1 (aldehyde carbon) = 1, C2 (CH2) = 1, C3 (double bond carbon with methyl) = 1, methyl substituent = 1, C4 (vinylic CH) = 1, C5 (terminal CH3) =1. So total C = 6. Hydrogens: Aldehyde (CHO) = 1 H, C2 CH2 =2, C3 has no H (substituted with CH3 and double bond), methyl on C3 =3, C4 CH =1, C5 CH3 =3. Total H = 1+2+3+1+3 =10? Wait that's 10? Let's add: 1 (aldehyde) +2 (CH2) =3, +3 (methyl) =6, +1 (CH vinylic) =7, +3 =10. So C6H10O. Molecular weight: C6=72, H10=10, O=16 => total 98. Yes matches M+ =98. Good.\n\nThus C is the correct answer.\n\nThus answer: C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": ["too_short"], "accepted": false}
{"qid": "recqTvvfE8ldJbknb", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: An astronomer wants to study the profile of the H_alpha emission for an active galaxy. She has data for 4 galaxies with spectral coverage coincident with the \"i\" Sloan band. The inearly-approximated distances to the galaxies are approximately 1 Mpc,  7 Mpc, 70 Mpc, and 700 Mpc. Which galaxy should she choose to study?\n\nA) The galaxy at ~ 70 Mpc\nB) The galaxy at  ~ 7 Mpc\nC) The galaxy at ~ 1 Mpc\nD) The galaxy at ~ 700 Mpc\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: An astronomer wants to study the profile of the H_alpha emission for an active galaxy. She has data for 4 galaxies with spectral coverage coincident with the \"i\" Sloan band. The inearly-approximated distances to the galaxies are approximately 1 Mpc,  7 Mpc, 70 Mpc, and 700 Mpc. Which galaxy should she choose to study?\n\nA) The galaxy at ~ 70 Mpc\nB) The galaxy at  ~ 7 Mpc\nC) The galaxy at ~ 1 Mpc\nD) The galaxy at ~ 700 Mpc\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recqTvvfE8ldJbknb:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 4928, 25, 78453, 259, 10648, 316, 5012, 8855, 328, 487, 727, 55890, 395, 448, 6725, 66184, 11, 1238, 395, 220, 19, 129137, 483, 93131, 13422, 31733, 2477, 483, 575, 179369, 8177, 13, 623, 48294, 25, 220, 16, 11, 220, 22, 11, 220, 2789, 11, 220, 9534, 391, 6344, 13, 21580, 66184, 1757, 1770, 5655, 1715, 49377, 3592, 27472, 25, 487, 727, 2543, 2867, 79731, 314, 220, 40673, 13, 18, 43183, 13, 179369, 575, 112277, 17804, 36144, 220, 9534, 12, 34175, 43183, 350, 62832, 741, 3604, 7266, 3592, 27472, 11, 487, 727, 19523, 1511, 3592, 761, 29650, 13, 2214, 220, 16, 391, 6344, 350, 5818, 3566, 8, 175463, 3592, 27472, 579, 93, 15, 11, 487, 727, 382, 540, 220, 40673, 43183, 11, 1118, 382, 625, 306, 575, 112277, 350, 72, 8177, 6574, 16772, 43183, 741, 3604, 220, 22, 391, 6344, 60314, 175463, 3592, 27472, 871, 625, 306, 575, 8177, 13, 3604, 220, 2789, 391, 6344, 11, 9324, 66938, 316, 1236, 3592, 27472, 12951, 6574, 15, 13, 28653, 30, 41021, 23864, 68753, 3592, 27472, 25, 2360, 487, 15, 28, 2789, 8571, 5031, 17712, 6344, 11, 9324, 220, 2789, 391, 6344, 8967, 30061, 6574, 32088, 15, 8571, 5031, 871, 579, 93, 15, 13, 28653, 13, 7801, 20395, 79731, 17667, 89360, 314, 17667, 70749, 425, 350, 16, 10, 89, 8, 42308, 230, 220, 40673, 13, 18, 425, 220, 16, 13, 28653, 314, 220, 19837, 43183, 11, 2928, 625, 575, 8177, 13, 3604, 220, 9534, 391, 6344, 11, 30061, 6574, 3796, 11, 1302, 8571, 5031, 11, 579, 93, 15, 13, 17666, 11, 17667, 89360, 6574, 220, 40673, 13, 18, 425, 220, 16, 13, 17666, 42308, 230, 220, 50846, 43183, 11, 1118, 25709, 3518, 575, 8177, 350, 5463, 6574, 16772, 43183, 741, 2632, 290, 66184, 540, 6574, 9534, 391, 6344, 1481, 679, 1617, 487, 727, 58644, 1511, 575, 8177, 13, 23586, 1770, 1757, 5230, 290, 1001, 540, 220, 9534, 391, 6344, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 364, 4383, 161791, 25, 623, 4928, 5003, 392, 258, 6047, 12, 14671, 8411, 780, 48294, 69029, 3627, 853, 1238, 395, 220, 19, 129137, 483, 93131, 13422, 31733, 2477, 483, 290, 575, 179369, 8177, 13, 21580, 66184, 1757, 1770, 5655, 316, 5012, 290, 8855, 328, 487, 727, 55890, 30, 3627, 4414, 487, 727, 316, 413, 3518, 8177, 13, 2632, 14531, 11, 290, 4150, 129300, 1001, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 415, 364, 8191, 43831, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 415], "n_tokens": 414, "words": 217, "band_lo": 99.75, "band_hi": 399.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the question: astronomer wants to study profile of Hα emission for an active galaxy, data for 4 galaxies with spectral coverage coincident with i Sloan band. The distances: 1, 7, 70, 700 Mpc. Which galaxy should she choose?\n\nConsider redshift: Hα line rest wavelength = 656.3 nm. Sloan i-band covers roughly 700-820 nm (maybe). At higher redshift, Hα moves into redder bands. For 1 Mpc (local group) negligible redshift z~0, Hα is at 656 nm, which is not in i-band (i band ~750 nm). At 7 Mpc similarly negligible redshift => not in i band. At 70 Mpc, distance corresponds to some redshift perhaps ~0.016? Let's compute approximate redshift: using H0=70 km/s/Mpc, distance 70 Mpc gives velocity ~4900 km/s => z~0.016. Then observed wavelength λ_obs = λ_rest * (1+z) ≈ 656.3 * 1.016 = 666 nm, still not i band. At 700 Mpc, velocity ~49,000 km/s, z~0.163, λ_obs ~ 656.3 * 1.163 ≈ 763 nm, which falls within i band (center ~750 nm). So the galaxy at ~700 Mpc would have its Hα shifted into i band. Thus she should pick the one at 700 Mpc.\n\nThus answer D.\n\nCheck nuance: The question says \"in early-approximated distances\"? She has data for 4 galaxies with spectral coverage coincident with the i Sloan band. Which galaxy should she choose to study the profile of Hα emission? She needs Hα to be within band. So yes, the farthest one.\n\nThus answer: D.\n\nReturn formatted: ANSWER: D", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.1920923199681965e-06, "p_correct": 0.999999999999991, "logp_correct": -9.027364596678796e-15, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807907337, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5578755242777388, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9082415501637939, "frac_below_-5": 0.012468827930174564, "digit_lp_min": -1.340234637260437, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.16696971654891968, "n_cot_tokens": 401, "lp_end": -0.005487497895956039, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -11.377849341612254, "p_nocot": 1.1446239136509094e-05, "uplift": 11.377849341612245, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.119093, "B": 0.879986, "C": 0.000909, "D": 1.1e-05}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recqisVCkgd2ojfRy", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: \"The alkene metathesis is a reaction promoted by a metal catalyst and takes two alkenes and stitches them together like this:\nbut-1-ene + but-1-ene --- metathesis catalyst ---> (E)-hex-3-ene\nWhat is/are the possible product/s of the following reaction?\n1-(3-methylbut-3-en-1-yl)-2-vinylbenzene ---- metathesis catalyst ---> ?\n\n1. 3-methyl-1,2-dihydronaphthalene\n2. 7-vinyl-1H-indene\n3. 1-propyl-1,4-dihydronaphthalene\n4. Ethene \"\n\nA) 1 and 4\nB) 1 and 2\nC) 2 and 4\nD) 1 only\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: \"The alkene metathesis is a reaction promoted by a metal catalyst and takes two alkenes and stitches them together like this:\nbut-1-ene + but-1-ene --- metathesis catalyst ---> (E)-hex-3-ene\nWhat is/are the possible product/s of the following reaction?\n1-(3-methylbut-3-en-1-yl)-2-vinylbenzene ---- metathesis catalyst ---> ?\n\n1. 3-methyl-1,2-dihydronaphthalene\n2. 7-vinyl-1H-indene\n3. 1-propyl-1,4-dihydronaphthalene\n4. Ethene \"\n\nA) 1 and 4\nB) 1 and 2\nC) 2 and 4\nD) 1 only\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recqisVCkgd2ojfRy:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 4218, 290, 66669, 25, 220, 16, 18825, 18, 2126, 53546, 8293, 12, 18, 17888, 12, 16, 12, 3643, 14080, 17, 9827, 258, 3643, 44624, 1900, 364, 58369, 6174, 25, 77214, 1900, 12796, 483, 22148, 84, 1094, 540, 15897, 220, 16, 326, 220, 17, 30, 220, 16, 18825, 18, 2126, 53546, 8293, 12, 18, 17888, 12, 16, 12, 3643, 8, 575, 2560, 4213, 261, 4307, 13464, 18618, 316, 76732, 1900, 540, 15883, 220, 16, 25, 290, 13464, 382, 220, 18, 2126, 53546, 8293, 12, 18, 17888, 12, 16, 12, 3643, 11, 1118, 382, 1131, 41021, 8420, 25, 220, 18, 2126, 53546, 8293, 12, 18, 17