{"qid": "recrDtPYx9Z9lfjzw", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Among the following quantities, which one would an astronomer consider last (i.e. worst quantity) when estimating the ages of solar-type stars? Ignore observational constraints such as weak/absent spectral lines.\n\n[Al/Zr]\n[Mg/Sr]\n[Na/O]\n[K/Ce]\n\nA) [K/Ce]\nB) [Al/Zr]\nC) [Na/O]\nD) [Mg/Sr]\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Among the following quantities, which one would an astronomer consider last (i.e. worst quantity) when estimating the ages of solar-type stars? Ignore observational constraints such as weak/absent spectral lines.\n\n[Al/Zr]\n[Mg/Sr]\n[Na/O]\n[K/Ce]\n\nA) [K/Ce]\nB) [Al/Zr]\nC) [Na/O]\nD) [Mg/Sr]\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recrDtPYx9Z9lfjzw:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 25, 623, 4928, 25, 392, 61949, 290, 3992, 49540, 11, 1118, 1001, 1481, 448, 78453, 259, 3331, 2174, 350, 72, 2560, 13, 20486, 17245, 8, 1261, 127789, 290, 20355, 328, 17624, 20769, 13701, 30, 84342, 172525, 31060, 2238, 472, 13586, 14, 6071, 299, 93131, 8698, 6635, 2167, 679, 4242, 50280, 69972, 25, 723, 2348, 113185, 81, 2155, 723, 74116, 16565, 81, 2155, 723, 11398, 37469, 2155, 723, 42, 18828, 68, 37441, 34405, 1481, 413, 290, 20486, 32003, 395, 71974, 5744, 30, 63659, 4122, 402, 1495, 27203, 2617, 50280, 69972, 3343, 483, 1058, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 47081, 365, 53589, 57432, 326, 188095, 15377, 26416, 364, 20932, 5304, 328, 13701, 4783, 8844, 15377, 98331, 25, 4454, 50280, 69972, 157241, 27203, 483, 5744, 11, 1299, 723, 56, 17712, 70, 2155, 723, 56, 14, 2348, 2155, 723, 75073, 17712, 70, 2155, 723, 33892, 14, 5563, 2155, 5178, 13, 64968, 44854, 265, 110793, 8632, 350, 56, 11, 36013, 11, 1489, 81, 8, 10217, 17406,