, 5744, 32003, 25, 12787, 13701, 1932, 658, 18828, 68, 26, 22512, 4465, 364, 7943, 1118, 1481, 413, 20486, 30, 63659, 1001, 1919, 2973, 8632, 679, 6771, 7142, 4238, 66, 3459, 11, 4137, 18051, 19919, 9594, 13, 723, 11398, 37469, 60, 2973, 591, 18965, 13701, 26, 6888, 1217, 1236, 21433, 59684, 889, 3760, 1058, 75298, 1572, 265, 110793, 13, 2632, 723, 11398, 37469, 60, 1340, 413, 12530, 364, 7943, 1217, 3331, 484, 1667, 326, 1489, 81, 2973, 679, 29298, 591, 2973, 18965, 326, 355, 9068, 13701, 10112, 945, 37588, 11, 889, 2928, 2356, 35930, 364, 42, 10217, 14228, 25, 14228, 265, 110793, 37265, 4849, 11, 889, 658, 382, 12790, 306, 41504, 9839, 326, 1217, 10112, 37265, 656, 7003, 64868, 30, 4037, 2009, 13, 2632, 18051, 1340, 2928, 157241, 364, 84787, 6052, 6960, 363, 25, 723, 11398, 37469, 60, 20486, 364, 84787, 5230, 10263, 363, 364, 4383, 3880, 25, 355, 28, 42, 18828, 68, 11, 418, 28, 2348, 113185, 81, 11, 363, 28, 11398, 37469, 11, 415, 28, 74116, 16565, 81, 13, 2632, 6052, 363, 364, 8191, 6011, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 363], "n_tokens": 714, "words": 497, "band_lo": 223.5, "band_hi": 894.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret: The question: \"Among the following quantities, which one would an astronomer consider last (i.e. worst quantity) when estimating the ages of solar-type stars? Ignore observational constraints such as weak/absent spectral lines.\"\n\nWe have four abundance ratios: [Al/Zr], [Mg/Sr], [Na/O], [K/Ce].\n\nWhich would be the worst indicator for stellar age? Probably based on how strongly those abundance ratios change with time, i.e., nucleosynthetic origins and Galactic chemical evolution.\n\nAge dating of stars often uses chemical clocks: certain abundance ratios correlate strongly with age, like [Y/Mg], [Y/Al], [Sr/Mg], [Ba/Fe], etc. Typically lighter s-process elements (Y, Sr, Zr) vs alpha elements (Mg, Al) are used because Y, Sr, Zr are produced in low-mass AGB and increase over time, while Mg, Al are from core-collapse SN, so their ratios decline with age. So [Mg/Al] maybe less used. But known good age indicators: [Y/Mg] is good, [Y/Al], [Sr/Mg], [Ba/Mg], [C/N] for giants, etc. So among the given options, which ratio is least correlated with age? Let's consider each:\n\n[Al/Zr] – Al is alpha-ish, Zr is s-process (second-peak) maybe similar to Y. So that ratio maybe reasonable? Actually [Al/Zr] = log(Al/Zr) relative to solar. Since Zr increases with time due to s-process, older stars have lower Zr (higher Al/Zr). So it's a plausible age indicator. Not as strong as [Y/Mg] but still possibly.\n\n[Mg/Sr] – Mg is alpha, Sr is light s-process. This ratio is classic chemical clock: [Sr/Mg] or its inverse [Mg/Sr] correlates with age (higher Mg/Sr for older stars). So it's good.\n\n[Na/O] – Na is odd-Z element mainly from massive stars but also some AGB contributions; O is alpha. Ratio may not vary strongly with time, as both produced by core-collapse SNe, O more primary. So likely not as good.\n\n[K/Ce] – K is odd-Z, produced in SN II, Ce is heavy s-process (second-peak) from low-mass AGB. Ce increases over time. So ratio [K/Ce] might be a decent age indicator: older stars high K/Ce; younger low.\n\nBut which would be worst? Probably one where both elements have similar production timescales, making ratio relatively constant. [Na/O] both from massive stars; Na also some secondary yields but less time-dependent than s-process. So [Na/O] may be poor.\n\nBut also consider that Al and Zr both have contributions from both massive and AGB stars maybe more complexity, but still show variation.\n\nK vs Ce: Ce s-process contributed later, but K is produced in SN II and also maybe contributed by Type Ia? Not much. So ratio may still correlate.\n\nThus answer likely C: [Na/O] worst.\n\nThus pick letter C.\n\nCheck options: A=K/Ce, B=Al/Zr, C=Na/O, D=Mg/Sr. So answer C.\n\nReturn format: ANSWER: C", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -6.318081887002336e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999056, "logp_correct": -9.437340147501255e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999960661090076, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7422369207550361, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4450341810857026, "frac_below_-5": 0.012838801711840228, "digit_lp_min": null, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.006477668881416321, "n_cot_tokens": 701, "lp_end": -0.028481027111411095, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -6.2624350027511015, "p_nocot": 0.0019065975693795837, "uplift": 6.262435002751007, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.987642, "B": 0.001907, "C": 0.001907, "D": 0.008545}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recrTL3i1VEPCfDGy", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Consider a one-dimensional spin chain, in which any pair of spins are separated by m lattice constants. The correlation function between these two spins varies exponentially with the m. The corresponding length scale depends upon elements of the transfer matrix between two nearest neighbors of the spin chain. If the nearest neighborhood coupling is J, then the ratio of the largest and the second largest eigenvalue of the transfer matrix at the equilibrium temperature T is given by (consider the Boltzmann constant k = 1)\n\nA) tanh(J/T) or coth(J/T), depending upon whether T is less than T_c or greater than T_c, where T_c is the critical temperature of phase transition\nB) tanh(J/T)\nC) 0.5 exp(2J/T)\nD) coth(J/T)\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Consider a one-dimensional spin chain, in which any pair of spins are separated by m lattice constants. The correlation function between these two spins varies exponentially with the m. The corresponding length scale depends upon elements of the transfer matrix between two nearest neighbors of the spin chain. If the nearest neighborhood coupling is J, then the ratio of the largest and the second largest eigenvalue of the transfer matrix at the equilibrium temperature T is given by (consider the Boltzmann constant k = 1)\n\nA) tanh(J/T) or coth(J/T), depending upon whether T is less than T_c or greater than T_c, where T_c is the critical temperature of phase transition\nB) tanh(J/T)\nC) 0.5 exp(2J/T)\nD) coth(J/T)\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recrTL3i1VEPCfDGy:1", "cot_tokens": [976, 4928, 25, 392, 49377, 261, 1001, 51578, 17464, 13464, 11, 306, 1118, 1062, 10610, 328, 32803, 553, 33930, 656, 284, 102427, 38252, 13, 623, 46307, 1114, 2870, 1879, 1920, 32803, 47245, 134422, 483, 290, 284, 13, 623, 21824, 5517, 9318, 20102, 7557, 8632, 328, 290, 8890, 12248, 2870, 1920, 35210, 31655, 328, 290, 17464, 13464, 13, 1843, 290, 35210, 17528, 88996, 382, 643, 11, 1815, 290, 18051, 328, 290, 10574, 326, 290, 3099, 10574, 10561, 1594, 328, 290, 8890, 12248, 540, 290, 87639, 12088, 353, 382, 4335, 656, 350, 59464, 290, 17967, 29918, 19328, 9594, 372, 314, 220, 16, 1029, 5360, 734, 32, 8, 8323, 71, 25268, 26742, 8, 503, 178684, 25268, 26742, 936, 14537, 7557, 5588, 353, 382, 3760, 1572, 353, 1303, 503, 10740, 1572, 353, 1303, 11, 1919, 353, 1303, 382, 290, 12383, 12088, 328, 15222, 14463, 198, 33, 8, 8323, 71, 25268, 26742, 446, 34, 8, 220, 15, 13, 20, 2794, 7, 17, 41, 26742, 446, 35, 8, 178684, 25268, 26742, 1029, 2167, 1309, 18051, 17667, 16, 14, 1727, 17, 30, 2214, 220, 16, 35, 2763, 289, 2359, 483, 35210, 13034, 88996, 643, 11, 8890, 12248, 10561, 7222, 553, 17667, 62, 32438, 314, 319, 42923, 41, 26742, 92, 58882, 319, 183239, 41, 26742, 92, 314, 220, 17, 8974, 71, 25268, 26742, 8, 326, 220, 17, 44064, 25268, 26742, 20707, 41021, 30890, 25, 2214, 2763, 289, 483, 860, 3259, 11, 8890, 12248, 8632, 25, 353, 22305, 82, 10853, 20379, 22305, 72, 10, 16, 6478, 314, 2794, 7, 6331, 643, 265, 10853, 265, 22305, 72, 10, 16, 9263, 483, 265, 314, 58882, 16, 13, 2632, 12248, 1402, 58, 2794, 7, 6331, 41, 8, 256, 2794, 8087, 6331, 41, 446, 220, 2794, 8087, 6331, 41, 8, 220, 2794, 7, 6331, 41, 8, 32229, 108275, 7222, 25, 17667, 16, 314, 2794, 7, 6331, 41, 16161, 8067, 8087, 6331, 41, 8, 314, 220, 17, 8974, 71, 7, 6331, 41, 936, 17667, 17, 314, 2794, 7, 6331, 41, 14080, 8067, 8087, 6331, 41, 8, 314, 220, 17, 44064, 7, 6331, 41, 741, 84705, 17667, 17, 14, 1727, 16, 314, 8323, 71, 7, 6331, 41, 741, 3072, 1023, 3810, 18051, 328, 10574, 326, 3099, 10574, 10561, 1594, 13, 121064, 382, 17667, 16, 314, 220, 17, 8974, 71, 7, 6331, 41, 8, 1424, 17667, 17, 395, 8841, 643, 30, 2214, 15772, 41, 29, 15, 11, 8974, 71, 1424, 44064, 11, 14531, 17667, 16, 29, 1727, 17, 13, 2632, 18051, 17667, 17, 14, 1727, 16, 314, 8323, 71, 7, 6331, 41, 741, 3072, 10112, 1023, 4774, 17667, 16, 14, 1727, 17, 314, 178684, 7, 6331, 41, 741, 12265, 18051, 328, 10574, 316, 3099, 10574, 314, 17667, 16, 14, 1727, 17, 314, 178684, 7, 6331, 41, 741, 41021, 2371, 25, 17667, 16, 14, 1727, 17, 314, 350, 17, 8974, 71, 60504, 17, 44064, 8, 314, 178684, 13, 2632, 6052, 2023, 413, 178684, 25268, 26742, 741, 17796, 415, 364, 7943, 1236, 35562, 1199, 17667, 13731, 14, 1727, 62, 17, 314, 178684, 25268, 26742, 741, 623, 4928, 25, 392, 81339, 328, 290, 10574, 326, 290, 3099, 10574, 10561, 1594, 328, 290, 8890, 12248, 540, 290, 87639, 12088, 353, 382, 4335, 656, 2550, 4050, 3164, 9289, 27018, 2569, 11, 889, 392, 191832, 326, 290, 3099, 10574, 1, 23379, 17667, 13731, 820, 17667, 62, 17, 13, 2632, 6052, 415, 25, 178684, 25268, 26742, 741, 17796, 355, 66396, 8323, 71, 503, 178684, 14537, 402, 353, 15001, 190405, 11, 889, 220, 16, 35, 2763, 289, 853, 860, 73104, 190405, 350, 57015, 540, 353, 28, 15, 741, 2632, 625, 355, 13, 17796, 418, 8323, 71, 382, 18051, 17667, 17, 14, 1727, 16, 350, 153422, 741, 17796, 363, 7890, 8201, 364, 84787, 6052, 415, 13], "n_tokens": 624, "words": 353, "band_lo": 138.0, "band_hi": 552.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "The question: \"Consider a one-dimensional spin chain, in which any pair of spins are separated by m lattice constants. The correlation function between these two spins varies exponentially with the m. The corresponding length scale depends upon elements of the transfer matrix between two nearest neighbors of the spin chain. If the nearest neighborhood coupling is J, then the ratio of the largest and the second largest eigenvalue of the transfer matrix at the equilibrium temperature T is given by (consider the Boltzmann constant k = 1)\n\nOptions:\nA) tanh(J/T) or coth(J/T), depending upon whether T is less than T_c or greater than T_c, where T_c is the critical temperature of phase transition\nB) tanh(J/T)\nC) 0.5 exp(2J/T)\nD) coth(J/T)\n\nWe need ratio λ1/λ2? For 1D Ising model with nearest neighbor coupling J, transfer matrix eigenvalues are λ_± = e^{J/T} ± e^{-J/T} = 2 cosh(J/T) and 2 sinh(J/T)? Let's recall: For Ising with no field, transfer matrix elements: T_{s_i,s_{i+1}} = exp(β J s_i s_{i+1}) with s = ±1. So matrix:\n\n[ exp(βJ)   exp(-βJ)\n  exp(-βJ)  exp(βJ) ]\n\nEigenvalues: λ1 = exp(βJ)+exp(-βJ) = 2 cosh(βJ), λ2 = exp(βJ)-exp(-βJ) = 2 sinh(βJ). Ratio λ2/λ1 = tanh(βJ). But they ask ratio of largest and second largest eigenvalue. Largest is λ1 = 2 cosh(βJ) > λ2 for positive J? For βJ>0, cosh > sinh, yes λ1>λ2. So ratio λ2/λ1 = tanh(βJ). But maybe they mean λ1/λ2 = coth(βJ). Since ratio of largest to second largest = λ1/λ2 = coth(βJ). Let's check: λ1/λ2 = (2 cosh)/(2 sinh) = coth. So answer could be coth(J/T). Option D.\n\nBut some texts use λ_max/λ_2 = coth(J/T). The question: \"ratio of the largest and the second largest eigenvalue of the transfer matrix at the equilibrium temperature T is given by ...\". They didn't specify order, but \"largest and the second largest\" suggests λ_max / λ_2. So answer D: coth(J/T). Option A mentions tanh or coth depending on T relative Tc, but 1D Ising has no finite Tc (critical at T=0). So not A. Option B tanh is ratio λ2/λ1 (inverse). Option C seems wrong.\n\nThus answer D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.1920858014491387e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999829, "logp_correct": -1.7051040439506488e-14, "letter_mass": 1.000000000000017, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.24396201736962558, "cot_lp_min64": -0.5755790439613733, "frac_below_-5": 0.0016366612111292963, "digit_lp_min": -0.47429993748664856, "answer_first_mention_lp": -1.311301275563892e-06, "n_cot_tokens": 611, "lp_end": -8.344646857949556e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -3.5523710013642575, "p_nocot": 0.028656614170556666, "uplift": 3.5523710013642407, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.022318, "B": 0.948977, "C": 4.9e-05, "D": 0.028657}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recreOOuMnQkSzybb", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: The reaction of silver and oxygen to silver oxide Ag2O has a standard Gibbs free energy (deltaG) of -22 kJ/mol (25 centigrade, 101300 Pa). When placing 23.2 g of solid Ag2O in 1 L of nitrogen gas and closing the vessel, the compound partly decomposes back to the elements. What is the partial pressure of oxygen gas in the vessel under the mentioned standard conditions? (Consider the gases as ideal gases.)\n\nA) 14 Pa\nB) 2763 Pa\nC) 87 Pa\nD) 1875 Pa\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: The reaction of silver and oxygen to silver oxide Ag2O has a standard Gibbs free energy (deltaG) of -22 kJ/mol (25 centigrade, 101300 Pa). When placing 23.2 g of solid Ag2O in 1 L of nitrogen gas and closing the vessel, the compound partly decomposes back to the elements. What is the partial pressure of oxygen gas in the vessel under the mentioned standard conditions? (Consider the gases as ideal gases.)\n\nA) 14 Pa\nB) 2763 Pa\nC) 87 Pa\nD) 1875 Pa\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recreOOuMnQkSzybb:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 1646, 87639, 13664, 9890, 328, 532, 17, 4335, 124780, 25, 220, 17, 8541, 1858, 8, 659, 220, 16, 14, 17, 532, 17, 7791, 8, 34937, 242, 5582, 17, 46, 1858, 741, 50557, 21416, 44860, 5582, 17, 46, 25, 220, 17, 5582, 1858, 8, 659, 220, 16, 14, 17, 532, 17, 7791, 8, 15155, 5582, 17, 46, 1858, 741, 26143, 38, 6793, 314, 533, 1709, 372, 41, 86398, 13, 2214, 87639, 9594, 658, 79, 314, 2794, 8087, 16611, 38, 6793, 27334, 10750, 8784, 129157, 27922, 44252, 11851, 25, 2214, 18704, 21416, 11, 658, 314, 220, 16, 27334, 79, 11678, 17, 8, 42923, 16, 14, 17, 92, 1423, 41021, 77630, 364, 97692, 25, 220, 17, 5582, 1858, 8, 659, 220,