5897, 402, 290, 110666, 12796, 328, 1888, 350, 32297, 220, 18, 326, 220, 23, 741, 391, 53546, 8896, 553, 23629, 2469, 263, 1365, 11, 7064, 1486, 350, 148939, 110666, 145058, 8, 889, 1217, 10112, 9338, 5954, 328, 1888, 13, 5551, 1023, 1217, 9338, 5954, 328, 8601, 1320, 23498, 298, 39085, 60314, 30, 52155, 123648, 364, 105622, 11, 95505, 316, 110666, 382, 25394, 656, 110666, 145058, 13, 5934, 5028, 84, 1094, 484, 48089, 100085, 1340, 175738, 750, 290, 110666, 2420, 3760, 30, 50557, 110666, 536, 5954, 382, 19919, 9594, 26, 22148, 84, 1094, 3159, 382, 36748, 364, 7943, 3331, 95505, 25, 1320, 23498, 298, 36762, 382, 45298, 87455, 350, 11741, 31325, 78306, 741, 623, 945, 110666, 290, 1888, 11, 290, 10740, 12987, 9578, 13, 623, 945, 110666, 290, 8601, 3785, 11, 290, 3760, 3343, 13, 2632, 1309, 316, 3331, 110666, 536, 328, 8601, 1320, 23498, 298, 36762, 25, 1023, 1340, 679, 1236, 110666, 35188, 138672, 483, 87455, 12796, 13, 60461, 101385, 402, 68141, 1340, 10389, 110666, 536, 328, 8601, 2236, 478, 382, 9808, 30, 4037, 3239, 364, 62034, 290, 5153, 48214, 95505, 382, 395, 36762, 220, 17, 11, 2236, 290, 478, 2126, 53546, 92838, 38566, 290, 8285, 328, 290, 1320, 23498, 298, 12796, 316, 5025, 110666, 5192, 316, 11504, 291, 54538, 103771, 326, 10112, 8841, 8363, 2449, 56070, 30, 65037, 11, 478, 2126, 53546, 8896, 1340, 413, 95505, 106854, 350, 33574, 2023, 413, 45266, 2110, 9247, 741, 3072, 34398, 95505, 1340, 3783, 290, 1320, 23498, 298, 15897, 26, 478, 2126, 53546, 1340, 85762, 364, 198140, 6052, 6960, 355, 350, 17, 741, 41021, 3503, 30411, 25, 17796, 418, 382, 220, 19, 11, 483, 6051, 53546, 402, 41933, 1808, 13, 4569, 1340, 1217, 48089, 100085, 11, 4137, 290, 1888, 945, 22105, 11, 889, 1217, 8601, 3785, 10112, 945, 22105, 350, 48770, 316, 22725, 542, 63159, 387, 8, 13972, 3760, 12987, 9578, 30, 38200, 220, 17, 10217, 220, 19, 25, 478, 2126, 53546, 945, 27203, 23629, 2469, 263, 1365, 4493, 92094, 30, 50557, 478, 2126, 53546, 38566, 68141, 885, 8285, 316, 1083, 2567, 750, 82462, 10610, 30, 730, 110666, 41158, 40212, 10934, 11, 68141, 62274, 1001, 10610, 316, 110666, 34839, 292, 26, 478, 2126, 53546, 4787, 480, 474, 23633, 815, 30, 24305, 11, 48781, 26540, 382, 261, 7701, 89, 378, 7553, 1900, 138672, 316, 76732, 1900, 26, 27054, 12885, 696, 553, 1299, 80887, 110815, 20769, 350, 1148, 17, 483, 82462, 10610, 625, 997, 328, 110666, 34839, 292, 741, 391, 53546, 387, 328, 68141, 59684, 478, 2126, 53546, 274, 387, 30, 50557, 478, 2126, 53546, 80887, 110815, 11526, 478, 2126, 53546, 79, 3866, 315, 27556, 350, 2904, 5861, 19577, 741, 3072, 2105, 581, 679, 220, 16, 11, 702, 15897, 10112, 15883, 30, 623, 64882, 25, 220, 16, 11, 702, 2469, 321, 53546, 12, 20, 11, 21, 2469, 2431, 123753, 3128, 4151, 22840, 60, 66, 3148, 26540, 13, 138312, 220, 16, 326, 220, 702, 1340, 11095, 316, 15883, 63489, 402, 290, 1320, 23498, 298, 12796, 350, 32297, 180638, 741, 4037, 13695, 364, 39520, 19271, 19448, 57927, 11, 478, 2126, 53546, 387, 38566, 9439, 536, 326, 1340, 85762, 95505, 13, 2632, 6052, 355, 364, 84787, 1721, 13], "n_tokens": 984, "words": 571, "band_lo": 285.5, "band_hi": 1142.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to determine which oxidation is least favorable (i.e., hardest). The oxidation of dihydro heterocycles to aromatic systems removes two hydrogens (forming double bonds). Favorability depends on aromatic stabilization gained minus any destabilization in starting material? Also substituent effects: electron-donating substituents (alkyl) may stabilize the dihydro form via hyperconjugation, making oxidation less favorable (less driving force). Also fused aromatic systems: the product aromaticity may be higher for some.\n\nWe have:\n\n1: 5,6-dihydrobenzo[c]cinnoline -> oxidized to benzo[c]cinnoline (aromatic). No substituents.\n\n2: 1,10-dimethyl-5,6-dihydrobenzo[c]cinnoline -> oxidized to 1,10-dimethylbenzo[c]cinnoline. Two methyl groups at N positions? 1 and 10 (positions likely nitrogen atoms? Cinnoline is a diazabenzene fused ring. Methyl attached to nitrogens may be N-methyl (N-alkyl). N-methyl may destabilize oxidation because removal of hydrogens not at N? The oxidation likely removes two hydrogens from the saturated ring (positions 5,6). The methyl groups are on nitrogens, may be electron-donating, stabilizing the dihydro form? Also oxidation may generate a N-oxide? Not. Probably the oxidation is less favorable for a compound with electron-donating groups that reduce aromatic stabilization gain. Also steric hindrance may affect.\n\n3: 4,5-dihydronaphtho[8,1,2-cde]cinnoline -> oxidized to naphtho[8,1,2-cde]cinnoline (larger fused aromatic system; product more aromatic stabilization, thus oxidation more favorable). So 3 likely most favorable, not least.\n\n4: 3,8-dimethyl-5,6-dihydrobenzo[c]cinnoline -> oxidation to 3,8-dimethylbenzo[c]cinnoline. Methyl groups at carbon positions on the aromatic ring of product (positions 3 and 8). Methyl groups are electron-donating, increase Q (increase aromatic stabilization) but also maybe raise energy of product. However they also raise energy of starting dihydro analog similarly? Effects ambiguous.\n\nGenerally, oxidation to aromatic is driven by aromatic stabilization. Substituents that donate electrons may destabilize the aromatic system less? Actually aromaticity energy is relatively constant; substituents effect is moderate.\n\nBut consider oxidation: dihydro compound is partially saturated (non-aromatic). The more aromatic the product, the greater driving force. The more aromatic the starting material, the less change. So need to consider aromaticity of starting dihydro compound: they may have some aromatic rings fused with saturated ring. Adding methyl on nitrogen may reduce aromaticity of starting because N is involved? Not sure.\n\nPerhaps the least favorable oxidation is for compound 2, because the N-methyl substitution reduces the ability of the dihydro ring to become aromatic due to steric hindrance and maybe positive charge repulsion? Similarly, N-methyl groups may be oxidation-sensitive (they could be oxidized themselves). But oxygen oxidation may target the dihydro positions; N-methyl may hinder.\n\nHence answer likely A (2). Let's double-check: Option B is 4, with dimethyl on carbons. That may also donate electrons, making the product more stable, but also starting material maybe more stable (due to hyperconjugation) thus less driving force? Compare 2 vs 4: N-methyl more strongly electron-donating via resonance? Actually N-methyl reduces nitrogen's ability to delocalize lone pair? In aromatic heterocycles, nitrogen contributes one pair to aromatic sextet; N-methyl makes it quaternary? Wait, cinnoline is a diazabenzene fused to benzene; nitrogens are like pyridine-type (sp2 with lone pair not part of aromatic sextet). Methylation of nitrogen yields N-methyl cation? Actually N-methyl pyridine forms N-methylpyridinium (positively charged). But here we have 1,10 positions maybe carbon? The naming: 1,10-dimethyl-5,6-dihydrobenzo[c]cinnoline. Positions 1 and 10 may correspond to carbon atoms on the dihydro ring (positions bridging). Not obvious.\n\nGiven typical organic reasoning, N-methylation reduces basicity and may hinder oxidation. So answer A.\n\nThus final.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -9.417489309271332e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999954793, "logp_correct": -4.520658560101056e-12, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000045206, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.737731444663003, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3431481123646707, "frac_below_-5": 0.012358393408856848, "digit_lp_min": -0.9548580050468445, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.006018373183906078, "n_cot_tokens": 971, "lp_end": 0.0, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -2.861222061674595, "p_nocot": 0.05719881705455872, "uplift": 2.861222061670074, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.057199, "B": 0.027019, "C": 0.021042, "D": 0.89474}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recuWKCp09FbG4l8F", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Microbiologist found that the minimum inhibitory concentration and mutant prevention concentration of erythromycin for Klebsiella pneumoniae were 4 mg/L and 64 μg/mL respectively.\n\nHe also found that the minimum inhibitory concentration and mutant prevention concentration of amikacin for the same Klebsiella pneumoniae were 8 mg/L and 16 μg/mL respectively.\n\nHe also found that the minimum inhibitory concentration and mutant prevention concentration of ciprofloxacin for the same Klebsiella pneumoniae were 8 mg/L and 128 μg/mL respectively.\n\nHe also found that the minimum inhibitory concentration and mutant prevention concentration of cefepime for the same Klebsiella pneumoniae were 2 mg/L and 64 μg/mL respectively.\n\nMinimum inhibitory concentration breakpoints (for Enterobacteriaceae) for erythromycin, amikacin, ciprofloxacin, and cefepime are 8 μg/mL, 16 μg/mL, 16 μg/mL, and 4 μg/mL respectively.\n\nWhich of the following will be the ideal antibiotic to be used against Klebsiella pneumoniae?\n\nA) Cefepime\nB) Ciprofloxacin\nC) Erythromycin\nD) Amikacin\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Microbiologist found that the minimum inhibitory concentration and mutant prevention concentration of erythromycin for Klebsiella pneumoniae were 4 mg/L and 64 μg/mL respectively.\n\nHe also found that the minimum inhibitory concentration and mutant prevention concentration of amikacin for the same Klebsiella pneumoniae were 8 mg/L and 16 μg/mL respectively.\n\nHe also found that the minimum inhibitory concentration and mutant prevention concentration of ciprofloxacin for the same Klebsiella pneumoniae were 8 mg/L and 128 μg/mL respectively.\n\nHe also found that the minimum inhibitory concentration and mutant prevention concentration of cefepime for the same Klebsiella pneumoniae were 2 mg/L and 64 μg/mL respectively.\n\nMinimum inhibitory concentration breakpoints (for Enterobacteriaceae) for erythromycin, amikacin, ciprofloxacin, and cefepime are 8 μg/mL, 16 μg/mL, 16 μg/mL, and 4 μg/mL respectively.\n\nWhich of the following will be the ideal antibiotic to be used against Klebsiella pneumoniae?\n\nA) Cefepime\nB) Ciprofloxacin\nC) Erythromycin\nD) Amikacin\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recuWKCp09FbG4l8F:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 5655, 9064, 91561, 4122, 402, 80257, 11, 100957, 11, 2338, 14011, 13, 47477, 69519, 853, 80257, 4895, 117129, 350, 97480, 953, 1430, 8, 326, 1217, 853, 23120, 107323, 8441, 5734, 350, 44, 7349, 533, 80257, 741, 42961, 100957, 382, 3432, 316, 7332, 20918, 364, 39520, 1238, 1402, 36, 1102, 404, 166053, 25, 80257, 314, 220, 19, 12912, 26049, 350, 19320, 13306, 12912, 26049, 16918, 19842, 4859, 220, 23, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 13, 15629, 12912, 26049, 316, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 25, 220, 16, 12912, 26049, 314, 220, 16, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 13, 2632, 80257, 314, 220, 19, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 11, 117129, 220, 23, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 871, 67498, 364, 44, 7349, 314, 220, 2220, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 364, 13973, 493, 8441, 5734, 350, 6359, 54, 8, 314, 100957, 533, 80257, 314, 220, 1910, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 364, 6275, 507, 18829, 25, 80257, 220, 23, 12912, 26049, 314, 220, 23, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 13, 19842, 4859, 220, 1125, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 871, 67498, 364, 44, 7349, 314, 220, 1125, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 13, 15861, 54, 314, 220, 23, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 364, 34, 488, 47121, 183994, 25, 80257, 220, 23, 12912, 26049, 314, 220, 23, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 13, 19842, 4859, 220, 1125, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 871, 67498, 364, 44, 7349, 314, 220, 8076, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 13, 15861, 54, 314, 220, 6106, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 364, 34, 1079, 1027, 642, 25, 80257, 220, 17, 12912, 26049, 314, 220, 17, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 13, 19842, 4859, 220, 19, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 871, 67498, 364, 44, 7349, 314, 220, 2220, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 13, 15861, 54, 314, 220, 8947, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 364, 5808, 722, 553, 67498, 13, 47477, 1481, 413, 7231, 483, 42173, 15861, 54, 575, 2560, 4213, 23120, 100957, 15001, 316, 80257, 11, 316, 41776, 8441, 328, 41078, 167543, 13, 4569, 382, 939, 507, 18829, 350, 6359, 54, 220, 23, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 741, 8820, 3331, 484, 939, 507, 18829, 885, 100957, 382, 23120, 350, 1125, 4403, 70, 5712, 43, 741, 2632, 6052, 415, 364, 84787, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 415, 13], "n_tokens": 378, "words": 170, "band_lo": 135.0, "band_hi": 540.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to choose ideal antibiotic based on MIC, MPC, breakpoints. Ideal ideally has MIC below breakpoint (susceptible) and also has lowest mutant selection window (MPC - MIC). Lower MPC is better to prevent resistance.\n\nGiven data:\n\nErythromycin: MIC = 4 mg/L (note units mg/L?) Breakpoint 8 μg/mL. Convert mg/L to μg/mL: 1 mg/L = 1 μg/mL. So MIC = 4 μg/mL, breakpoint 8 μg/mL => susceptible.\n\nMPC = 64 μg/mL.\n\nMutant selection window (MSW) = MPC - MIC = 60 μg/mL.\n\nAmikacin: MIC 8 mg/L = 8 μg/mL. Breakpoint 16 μg/mL => susceptible.\n\nMPC = 16 μg/mL. MSW = 8 μg/mL.\n\nCiprofloxacin: MIC 8 mg/L = 8 μg/mL. Breakpoint 16 μg/mL => susceptible.\n\nMPC = 128 μg/mL. MSW = 120 μg/mL.\n\nCefepime: MIC 2 mg/L = 2 μg/mL. Breakpoint 4 μg/mL => susceptible.\n\nMPC = 64 μg/mL. MSW = 62 μg/mL.\n\nSo all are susceptible. Ideal would be drug with smallest MSW i.e., lowest MPC relative to MIC, to minimize selection of resistant mutants. That is amikacin (MSW 8 μg/mL). Also consider that amikacin's MPC is lowest (16 μg/mL). So answer D.\n\nThus: ANSWER: D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -7.867791168791882e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999397, "logp_correct": -6.028522458659388e-14, "letter_mass": 0.999998569490632, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.27772261914512103, "cot_lp_min64": -0.7052229040659496, "frac_below_-5": 0.00821917808219178, "digit_lp_min": -1.9102287292480469, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0415530800819397, "n_cot_tokens": 365, "lp_end": -4.768370445162873e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -6.752037607578021, "p_nocot": 0.0011684962566079731, "uplift": 6.752037607577961, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.997965, "B": 0.000709, "C": 0.000158, "D": 0.001168}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recuYEkVjdVUwA3c0", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Consider this annihilation process\n\n$p+\\bar{p}\\rightarrow2A^{+}+2A^{-}$\n\nThe antiproton is slowly moving and $m_{A}c^{2}=300MeV$.\n\nWhat is the velocity of particle A?\n\nA) 0.91c\nB) 0.96c\nC) 0.86c\nD) 0.77c\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Consider this annihilation process\n\n$p+\\bar{p}\\rightarrow2A^{+}+2A^{-}$\n\nThe antiproton is slowly moving and $m_{A}c^{2}=300MeV$.\n\nWhat is the velocity of particle A?\n\nA) 0.91c\nB) 0.96c\nC) 0.86c\nD) 0.77c\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recuYEkVjdVUwA3c0:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 290, 4792, 13, 1689, 14533, 21718, 25, 120589, 659, 4624, 64669, 1013, 2747, 220, 17, 355, 10, 659, 220, 17, 355, 12, 350, 27252, 189592, 33875, 355, 483, 8363, 659, 503, 533, 741, 623, 4624, 64669, 1013, 382, 22936, 10067, 13, 2632, 581, 679, 275, 350, 50019, 284, 1359, 8, 326, 2381, 2990, 90, 79, 92, 350, 50019, 284, 1359, 8, 29212, 540, 2867, 25651, 591, 3291, 121749, 5954, 30, 392, 69974, 423, 10067, 1, 10915, 1617, 121749, 5954, 382, 175463, 11223, 316, 2867, 4842, 13, 2632, 3609, 4871, 5954, 42308, 230, 220, 17, 284, 1359, 274, 61, 17, 350, 21910, 275, 540, 2867, 30, 50557, 290, 120589, 382, 82769, 540, 2867, 30, 44130, 261, 120589, 3783, 382, 85030, 326, 4624, 64669, 1013, 19523, 22936, 26, 290, 121749, 5954, 175463, 13, 2632, 3609, 5954, 306, 28289, 7298, 350, 21048, 7298, 8, 33956, 220, 17, 284, 1359, 274, 61, 17, 314, 220, 17, 425, 220, 47050, 2896, 53, 314, 220, 14780, 21, 2896, 53, 364, 15519, 25, 1920, 355, 10, 326, 1920, 355, 12, 26, 2454, 10610, 82769, 2684, 4842, 284, 6829, 350, 82383, 472, 220, 4095, 2896, 53, 26, 2867, 4842, 5954, 741, 2632, 220, 19, 30285, 2454, 4842, 284, 6829, 11, 3609, 2867, 4842, 5954, 314, 220, 19, 425, 220, 4095, 314, 220, 6106, 15, 2896, 53, 13, 623, 2867, 328, 4871, 5954, 8805, 1511, 121749, 5954, 328, 355, 30285, 364, 2167, 1309, 30061, 328, 33875, 355, 350, 27252, 189592, 2454, 2684, 7733, 11, 23821, 24921, 5178, 741, 134450, 103932, 71571, 30, 12265, 3609, 41184, 2804, 413, 12421, 350, 366, 4871, 41184, 12421, 741, 730, 9887, 7298, 483, 275, 540, 2867, 326, 4624, 64669, 1013, 10067, 22936, 11, 41184, 3291, 889, 116685, 780, 12421, 11, 813, 1721, 3609, 41184, 12421, 25, 290, 4205, 64, 328, 290, 4242, 355, 30285, 4215, 316, 12421, 13, 63659, 1023, 3063, 842, 5357, 194494, 2905, 25, 2454, 355, 10, 23821, 355, 12, 24702, 13, 3072, 581, 1327, 1309, 49895, 328, 7733, 591, 5954, 364, 8270, 121749, 5954, 2839, 25, 457, 45740, 533, 2867, 57556, 1721, 314, 220, 14780, 21, 533, 220, 6106, 15, 314, 220, 46116, 2896, 53, 364, 2500, 121749, 5954, 382, 7581, 6200, 4242, 355, 30285, 29293, 350, 141027, 90882, 741, 2632, 121749, 5954, 777, 33875, 25, 220, 46116, 14, 19, 314, 220, 20335, 2896, 53, 364, 10620, 2454, 33875, 853, 2867, 4842, 284, 6829, 314, 220, 4095, 2896, 53, 13, 623, 3609, 5954, 777, 33875, 457, 314, 8558, 284, 6829, 274, 61, 17, 314, 284, 6829, 274, 61, 17, 659, 20666, 314, 220, 4095, 659, 220, 20335, 314, 220, 39662, 2896, 53, 279, 84787, 8558, 314, 220, 39662, 820, 220, 4095, 314, 220, 16, 13, 41472, 18, 364, 3879, 8558, 11, 7733, 25, 15772, 314, 44237, 7, 16, 533, 220, 16, 14, 2728, 61, 17, 8, 314, 44237, 7, 16, 533, 220, 16, 27334, 16, 13, 41472, 18, 61, 17, 8784, 45438, 8558, 61, 17, 314, 220, 17, 13, 24954, 13, 220, 16, 14, 2728, 61, 17, 314, 220, 15, 13, 24928, 18, 13, 220, 16, 533, 484, 314, 220, 15, 13, 36351, 22, 13, 44237, 314, 220, 15, 13, 31612, 13, 2632, 15772, 42308, 230, 220, 15, 13, 3732, 66, 13, 4569, 15248, 5317, 415, 350, 15, 13, 3732, 66, 741, 2632, 6052, 415, 364, 4383, 116685, 929, 25, 4871, 121749, 5954, 175463, 25, 4624, 64669, 1013, 22936, 10067, 2747, 10112, 3291, 121749, 5954, 11223, 316, 2867, 4842, 26, 25435, 364, 72816, 6052, 415, 364], "n_tokens": 592, "words": 308, "band_lo": 124.75, "band_hi": 499.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to parse the problem. Annihilation: proton + antiproton -> 2 A+ + 2 A- (presumably particle A with charge + or -). The antiproton is slowly moving. So we have p (mass m_p) and \\bar{p} (mass m_p) essentially at rest aside from small kinetic energy? \"slowly moving\" meaning its kinetic energy is negligible compared to rest mass. So total initial energy ≈ 2 m_p c^2 (since p at rest? Actually the proton is presumably at rest? Usually a proton target is stationary and antiproton moves slowly; the kinetic energy negligible. So total energy in CM frame (lab frame) approx 2 m_p c^2 = 2 * 938 MeV = 1876 MeV.\n\nFinal: two A+ and two A-; each pair presumably same mass m_A (given as 300 MeV; rest mass energy). So 4 particles each mass m_A, total rest mass energy = 4 * 300 = 1200 MeV. The rest of initial energy goes into kinetic energy of A particles.\n\nWe need velocity of particle A (presumably each same speed, opposite directions etc). Assume isotropic? Since total momentum must be zero (if initial momentum zero). In lab frame with p at rest and antiproton moving slowly, momentum small but approximated zero, so final total momentum zero: the momenta of the four A particles sum to zero. Probably they come out symmetrically: each A+ opposite A- pairs. But we just need magnitude of speed from energy.\n\nTotal kinetic energy available: E_initial - rest masses final = 1876 - 1200 = 676 MeV.\n\nThis kinetic energy is distributed among four A particles equally (assuming symmetry). So kinetic energy per particle: 676/4 = 169 MeV.\n\nNow each particle has rest mass m_A = 300 MeV. The total energy per particle E = γ m_A c^2 = m_A c^2 + KE = 300 + 169 = 469 MeV\n\nThus γ = 469 / 300 = 1.5633.\n\nFrom γ, speed: β = sqrt(1 - 1/γ^2) = sqrt(1 - 1/(1.5633^2)). Compute γ^2 = 2.444. 1/γ^2 = 0.4093. 1 - that = 0.5907. sqrt = 0.768. So β ≈ 0.77c. That matches option D (0.77c). So answer D.\n\nCheck approximations: initial kinetic energy negligible: antiproton slowly moving -> maybe small kinetic energy compared to rest mass; okay.\n\nTherefore answer D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -1.919251371163e-05, "p_correct": 0.999999999999993, "logp_correct": -6.959428748640641e-15, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807907317, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6174144456950518, "cot_lp_min64": -1.040319311588961, "frac_below_-5": 0.008635578583765112, "digit_lp_min": -1.3739283084869385, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 579, "lp_end": -0.031568266451358795, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "logp_nocot": -10.160367826137625, "p_nocot": 3.86730405949755e-05, "uplift": 10.160367826137618, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.148026, "B": 0.85183, "C": 0.000105, "D": 3.9e-05}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recuhnGik8v2nOK3g", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: A coating is applied to a substrate resulting in a perfectly smooth surface. The measured static contact angles of this smooth coating are 104° and 76° for water and oil respectively. The coating formulation is then modified and when now applied to the same type of substrate, a rough surface is produced. For the rough surface, the advancing and receding water contact angles are measured to be 133° and 125° respectively. What would be the best estimate of the static water and static oil contact angles of the rough surface, and would water droplets roll off of the surface at low or high tilt angles.\n\nA) Water = 129°, Oil = 98°, low tilt angle\nB) Water = 128°, Oil = 48°, high tilt angle\nC) Water = 129°, Oil = 98°, high tilt angle\nD) Water = 128°, Oil = 48°, low tilt angle\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: A coating is applied to a substrate resulting in a perfectly smooth surface. The measured static contact angles of this smooth coating are 104° and 76° for water and oil respectively. The coating formulation is then modified and when now applied to the same type of substrate, a rough surface is produced. For the rough surface, the advancing and receding water contact angles are measured to be 133° and 125° respectively. What would be the best estimate of the static water and static oil contact angles of the rough surface, and would water droplets roll off of the surface at low or high tilt angles.\n\nA) Water = 129°, Oil = 98°, low tilt angle\nB) Water = 128°, Oil = 48°, high tilt angle\nC) Water = 129°, Oil = 98°, high tilt angle\nD) Water = 128°, Oil = 48°, low tilt angle\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recuhnGik8v2nOK3g:0", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 4218, 3701, 16538, 402, 19595, 9753, 13, 29844, 74857, 326, 1369, 16624, 3411, 43448, 25, 74857, 314, 220, 16518, 148177, 1369, 16624, 314, 220, 10676, 172051, 623, 1633, 3701, 16538, 382, 12190, 116685, 780, 472, 290, 7848, 328, 74857, 326, 1369, 16624, 350, 3086, 87639, 3701, 16538, 741, 2632, 1633, 3411, 42308, 230, 350, 16518, 10, 10676, 11876, 17, 314, 220, 16891, 172051, 4569, 15248, 3880, 355, 503, 363, 364, 10620, 1633, 7396, 3701, 16538, 25, 581, 679, 13241, 46423, 7396, 16538, 314, 220, 7947, 172051, 117948, 2816, 738, 7902, 7396, 16538, 14537, 402, 486, 188745, 503, 39591, 396, 8287, 1362, 399, 32662, 13, 12265, 19595, 9753, 382, 12063, 155702, 350, 15373, 13180, 1424, 220, 2744, 741, 2214, 7396, 11, 1118, 10112, 3760, 39618, 11, 581, 1309, 316, 17946, 3343, 13, 1843, 9753, 382, 19595, 326, 12063, 155702, 11, 290, 7396, 3701, 16538, 1340, 7064, 30, 50557, 538, 9753, 382, 19595, 326, 3411, 382, 1932, 13180, 350, 23498, 155702, 936, 7396, 6960, 1217, 14081, 945, 12063, 155702, 30, 3072, 7396, 11340, 1744, 20102, 402, 9753, 5954, 10674, 13, 41021, 3331, 486, 188745, 42006, 25, 8974, 4114, 9, 314, 428, 8974, 4114, 11, 1919, 428, 1424, 16, 13, 2214, 261, 12063, 155702, 89075, 16538, 1424, 2744, 148177, 8974, 4114, 382, 12626, 11, 57388, 656, 428, 350, 1424, 16, 8, 4787, 8974, 4114, 9, 945, 12626, 11, 13972, 12962, 9, 12384, 350, 8229, 12063, 155702, 741, 2632, 19595, 2816, 21073, 290, 36582, 3701, 16538, 395, 12063, 155702, 30625, 350, 15373, 741, 2214, 7396, 11, 89075, 16538, 220, 7947, 6793, 46465, 2744, 6793, 8, 382, 12063, 21107, 112176, 350, 37372, 1744, 741, 8974, 4114, 314, 8974, 220, 7947, 6793, 314, 220, 15, 13, 24865, 30, 50557, 8974, 220, 7947, 314, 220, 15, 13, 24865, 30, 41021, 23864, 25, 8974, 220, 1910, 28, 15, 13, 20, 11, 8974, 220, 2789, 6574, 15, 13, 32615, 11, 8974, 220, 3384, 6574, 15, 13, 28608, 11, 8974, 220, 7947, 6574, 15, 13, 24472, 13, 69952, 13, 137658, 656, 428, 1424, 16, 8967, 12384, 8841, 8974, 11, 1118, 66938, 316, 13679, 16538, 350, 8229, 11340, 1744, 741, 2632, 19595, 2816, 1481, 25848, 7396, 3701, 16538, 11, 4137, 480, 945, 11340, 1744, 2322, 106739, 6931, 1572, 220, 7947, 148177, 10112, 2846, 220, 3519, 6793, 472, 306, 3880, 418, 26018, 364, 84787, 1633, 7396, 16538, 6960, 2009, 6931, 25, 220, 3519, 172051, 2632, 3880, 418, 503, 415, 364, 10620, 3411, 6517, 24884, 8465, 18990, 25, 18166, 68195, 16538, 4748, 4224, 8465, 18990, 165324, 4465, 3701, 16538, 136156, 10187, 13, 487, 42320, 10187, 314, 74857, 533, 1369, 16624, 314, 220, 16518, 12, 10676, 314, 220, 23, 172051, 21926, 19919, 4465, 13, 4569, 23379, 4465, 68195, 16538, 3759, 395, 8465, 18990, 13, 2214, 3411, 1932, 136156, 10187, 1481, 1309, 1932, 68195, 13, 7306, 136156, 10187, 382, 3291, 11, 813, 4465, 68195, 16538, 13, 2632, 6052, 1481, 413, 3411, 220, 16891, 148177, 7396, 220, 3519, 148177, 4465, 68195, 16538, 314, 5317, 415, 30, 24305, 7396, 16538, 581, 2059, 6574, 3519, 148177, 3411, 220, 16891, 148177, 4465, 68195, 16538, 871, 15248, 5317, 415, 350, 27874, 314, 220, 8076, 148177, 18353, 314, 220, 3519, 148177, 4465, 68195, 16538, 741, 17796, 415, 8967, 3411, 220, 8076, 6793, 5263, 4951, 11, 7396, 220, 3519, 148177, 4465, 68195, 13, 17796, 418, 2684, 889, 1932, 68195, 16538, 13, 2632, 5655, 415, 364, 17045, 25, 415, 13], "n_tokens": 573, "words": 326, "band_lo": 158.75, "band_hi": 635.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to understand contact angle on rough surface. Given advancing and receding water angles: advancing = 133°, receding = 125°. The static contact angle is generally approximated as the average of advancing and receding (the equilibrium contact angle). So static water ≈ (133+125)/2 = 129°. That matches options A or C.\n\nNow static oil contact angle: we have smooth coating oil angle = 76°. Roughness will affect oil angle depending on Wenzel or Cassie-Baxter regime. Since rough surface is hydrophobic (water CA > 90). For oil, which maybe less polar, we need to predict change. If surface is rough and hydrophobic, the oil contact angle may increase? Actually if surface is rough and water is high CA (hydrophobic), oil likely also becomes more hydrophobic? But oil wetting depends on surface energy components. Let's consider Wenzel equation: cosθ* = r cosθ, where r >1. For a hydrophobic intrinsic angle >90°, cosθ is negative, multiply by r ( >1) makes cosθ* more negative, thus θ* larger (more hydrophobic). So roughness increases the apparent contact angle for hydrophobic surfaces (water). For oil, intrinsic angle 76° (<90°) is hydrophilic (wetting). cosθ = cos 76° = 0.241? Actually cos 76 = 0.241? Let's compute: cos 60=0.5, cos 70 ~0.342, cos 75 ~0.259, cos 76 ~0.242. Positive. Multiply by r >1 gives larger positive cos, which corresponds to smaller angle (more wetting). So roughness would decrease oil contact angle, making it more wetting—thus lower than 76°, maybe around 48° as in options B/D.\n\nThus static oil angle likely much lower: 48°. So options B or D.\n\nNow water droplet roll-off: Low tilt angle means easy roll-off implying low contact angle hysteresis. Hysteresis = advancing - receding = 133-125 = 8°. That's relatively low. That suggests low tilt angle required for roll-off. For water high hysteresis would need high tilt. Here hysteresis is small, so low tilt angle. So answer would be water 129°, oil 48°, low tilt angle = option D? Wait oil angle we said ~48°, water 129°, low tilt angle => matches option D (Water = 128°, Oil = 48°, low tilt angle). Option D gives water 128° close enough, oil 48°, low tilt. Option B same but high tilt angle. So choose D.\n\nAnswer: D.", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -8.583032467868179e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999909, "logp_correct": -9.058231277273791e-15, "letter_mass": 1.000000000000009, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6030807117088747, "cot_lp_min64": -1.1137101133297993, "frac_below_-5": 0.010714285714285714, "digit_lp_min": -1.0631372928619385, "answer_first_mention_lp": -3.540453326422721e-05, "n_cot_tokens": 560, "lp_end": -5.960446742392378e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -10.626953100183748, "p_nocot": 2.4253415620151078e-05, "uplift": 10.626953100183739, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.880775, "B": 1e-06, "C": 0.1192, "D": 2.4e-05}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recuyeuT5rQ6qDt8F", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Five binary compounds of fluorine with element Y are known. The bright-red substance A1 decomposes at 293 K into A2 (ɷF=31,96%) and fluorine. A1 oxidizes xenon under normal conditions. A3 can also interact with xenon. A3 is obtained by fluorination of element Y with fluorine. By adding Y in a 1:1 molar ratio to a hot colorless concentrated solution of A4, A5 can be obtained. In water, A5 decomposes with the formation of two substances. Indicate the range in which the molecular weight of the substance A4 falls.\n\nA) 160-180\nB) 110-130\nC) 140-160\nD) 220-240\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Five binary compounds of fluorine with element Y are known. The bright-red substance A1 decomposes at 293 K into A2 (ɷF=31,96%) and fluorine. A1 oxidizes xenon under normal conditions. A3 can also interact with xenon. A3 is obtained by fluorination of element Y with fluorine. By adding Y in a 1:1 molar ratio to a hot colorless concentrated solution of A4, A5 can be obtained. In water, A5 decomposes with the formation of two substances. Indicate the range in which the molecular weight of the substance A4 falls.\n\nA) 160-180\nB) 110-130\nC) 140-160\nD) 220-240\n", "true_answer": "B", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recuyeuT5rQ6qDt8F:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 8420, 290, 14204, 291, 15377, 37488, 13, 3274, 553, 6468, 15600, 43556, 328, 82128, 514, 483, 3577, 865, 5542, 13, 2632, 865, 382, 1236, 3577, 484, 11526, 6468, 82128, 4105, 13, 88495, 31061, 25, 8632, 483, 1991, 82128, 4105, 1299, 101145, 350, 46661, 19, 11, 38371, 21, 11, 38371, 17, 95807, 165321, 350, 32637, 18, 11, 52605, 20, 11, 398, 17, 37, 19, 95807, 130129, 350, 1388, 37, 11, 2190, 37, 1