it we add N and H2, replacing a H: C7H6O + NH2 - H = C7H7NO? Actually starting C7H6O plus NH2 gives C7H7NO? plus N1 H2 = C7H8NO? Let's do: benzaldehyde C7H6O (C7 H6 O). Add NH2 substituting a hydrogen on ring: gives C7H6O + NH2 - H = C7H7NO? because we add N and one H net? Let's write formula for p-aminobenzaldehyde: C7H7NO? Actually known: p-aminobenzaldehyde formula is C7H7NO? Let's check: aromatic ring C6H4, plus CHO (CHO) adds C1H1O, plus NH2 adds N1H2: total C7 H (4+1+2)=7? Wait C6H4 is base. Add CHO: C1 H1 O1 => C7 H5 O1. Add NH2: N1 H2 => C7 H7 N1 O1. Yes formula C7H7NO? Actually total H =5+2=7. So C7H7NO. But our formula C8H9NO: extra CH2? Could be p-hydroxybenzaldehyde? That is C7H6O2. Not match.\n\nOur NMR: aromatic doublets 2H each suggests para-disubstituted phenyl (AA'BB') pattern. So we have one phenyl ring with para substituents: likely an aldehyde (CHO) and something else. The aldehyde proton at 9.72 t (triplet) suggests aldehyde adjacent to CH2 group? Triplet indicates coupling with two equivalent protons (CH2). So aldehyde is attached to CH2-CH2? Wait aldehyde proton couples to adjacent CH2 (vicinal) giving triplet if CH2 is next to aldehyde. So structure possibly -CHO-CH2-? Actually the aldehydic proton couples to one set of equivalent protons (2H) via 3-bond coupling -> appears as a quartet if it couples to CH2? No, if CH2 protons are equivalent, aldehyde couples to both => yields triplet (n+1 where n=2) i.e., triplet. So aldehyde attached to CH2 which is attached to something else.\n\nWe also have a doublet at 3.66 (2H), likely CH2 adjacent to something electronegative (O or N). Coupling pattern: doublet indicates coupling to a single proton? But it's 2H as doublet, perhaps -CH2- adjacent to CH (vinylic) giving doublet? Could be -CH2 attached to double bond carbon (CH) where J~? But they gave as d, might be due to coupling to aldehyde proton? Wait aldehyde proton also couples to CH2 gives triplet, CH2 couples to aldehyde (1 set) and maybe also to another proton? But they gave doublet maybe coupling to aldehyde? However aldehyde already observed as triplet due to CH2, the CH2 would be coupled to aldehyde (1 neighbor) and maybe to another CH? If it also coupled to another proton, pattern could be quartet. Since they observed doublet, maybe CH2 is attached to nitrogen? but N coupling small.\n\nAlternatively the 3.66 d (2H) could be -CH2-OH (hydroxymethylene) coupling to OH (broad?) not typical. However broad singlet at 6.27 (2H) likely phenolic OH (exchangeable) appear as broad singlet around 5-6 ppm characteristic? Actually phenol OH appears around 4-6 ppm broad. So 2H OH suggests two phenolic OH groups.\n\nThus final product likely has two phenol groups (bis-phenol). Options with hydroxy phenyl groups: A) 3-hydroxy-2,4-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)butanal (has two phenol rings). B) 4-(4-hydroxyphenyl)but-3-enal (one phenol). C) 2,4-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)but-2-enal (two phenol). D) 2,4-diphenylbut-3-enal (no OH). So our NMR shows 2 phenolic OH (broad 2H). So candidates A or C.\n\nWe need to consider product structure that results from diazotization of starting amine (aryl amine) converting to phenol (Sandmeyer hydrolysis). The starting material must be a p-aminophenyl-substituted aldehyde? Actually starting formula C8H9NO suggests maybe p-aminocinnamaldehyde? Let's think.\n\nIf we have 4-aminophenylacetaldehyde? That would be C6H4(NH2)-CH2-CHO: formula C8H9NO? Let's compute: phenyl C6H4, plus CH2-CHO adds C2H3O? Actually CH2-CHO = C2H3O. Add NH2 = N1H2. Total C8 H? C6+2=8 carbon. Hydrogens: phenyl C6H4 (4 H). CH2-CHO has CH2 (2 H) and aldehyde H (1) =3. So total H =4+3=7. Plus NH2 H2 =9. So C8 H9 N1 O1. Yes matches C8H9NO. Good. So starting compound is p-aminophenylacetaldehyde (para-aminophenylacetaldehyde) i.e., 4-aminophenylacetaldehyde. NMR: aldehyde proton (9.72 t, coupling to CH2). CH2 at 3.66 d (2H) coupling to aldehyde (makes doublet). Aromatic AA'BB' pattern doublets 2H each at 6.98 and 6.51. Broad singlet 6.27 (2H) maybe NH2 (exchangeable). Wait NH2 appears around 5-6 broad? Could be. So yes initial NH2 gives broad signal 2H.\n\nThus after reaction: diazotization then hydrolysis -> replace NH2 with OH giving phenol. So we get p-hydroxyphenylacetaldehyde (4-hydroxyphenylacetaldehyde). Then basic conditions KOH heat may cause some condensation (maybe aldol) because we have phenol not reactive. But options show C8? Actually final products seem larger (C? more). Options A, B, C, D are C? Let's count.\n\nOption A: 3-hydroxy-2,4-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)butanal. That's a C? Let's calculate: two phenyl rings (C6 each) =12 C; plus butanal chain C4 =16 carbons. So larger. So starting material must undergo condensation with another molecule (maybe self-condensation). In basic conditions, aldehyde could undergo aldol condensation with another molecule of same aldehyde, leading to dimer (C16). Indeed in base, aldehydes (non-enolizable?) but p-hydroxyphenylacetaldehyde has alpha CH2 (next to aldehyde) which is acidic. So it can enolize forming enolate, attack another aldehyde carbonyl -> aldol. That yields beta-hydroxyaldehyde (aldol product). Then perhaps dehydration? But options include hydroxy but not dehydro (except B, C, D have enal). Option B: 4-(4-hydroxyphenyl)but-3-enal (C10?). Count: phenyl C6 + chain C4 =10. That's product of aldol dehydration (enone). Option C: 2,4-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)but-2-enal (C? two phenyl plus chain). That's dimer with conjugated double bond. Option A: hydroxy aldol (no dehydration) with extra OH at beta carbon (3-hydroxy). That corresponds to aldol product before dehydration.\n\nWe need which occurs under conditions: After diazotization/hydrolysis, we have phenol aldehyde which under basic heat may undergo aldol condensation. If conditions are aqueous KOH, heat, typical Aldol condensation leads to β-hydroxyaldehyde which can dehydrate to α,β-unsaturated aldehyde (enals). The presence of base and heat often favors dehydration. But the product options include both dehydrated (B,C,D) and non-dehydrated (A). Which is more likely? In aqueous KOH heated, dehydration may occur but not always. Also presence of phenolic OH may affect.\n\nWe have two possible pathways: The aldehyde may undergo self-aldol to give 2,4-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)but-2-enal (C). That is a bis-phenol product (two phenyl rings). Option C has conjugated double bond at 2-3 positions (but-2-enal). Option B is monomer enal (no dimer), maybe result of intramolecular condensation? Actually 4-(4-hydroxyphenyl)but-3-enal is product from intramolecular aldol of phenylacetaldehyde after oxidation of terminal? Wait not.\n\nLet's consider mechanism: Starting phenylacetaldehyde (para-hydroxy). Under base, it can undergo Cannizzaro? No because it has alpha H, so less likely. It can undergo self-aldol to give β-hydroxyaldehyde then dehydration to give α,β-unsaturated aldehyde. The dimer would have two phenyl groups at ends. Option C corresponds to α,β-unsaturated aldehyde after dehydration (C=C between C2 and C3?). Let’s draw: Starting CH2-CHO attaches to CH2-CHO of another molecule forming C-C bond between alpha carbon of one and carbonyl carbon of another => β-hydroxyaldehyde: HO-CH-CH2-CH2-CHO? Actually need draw.\n\nStart: Ph-CH2-CHO (para-hydroxy phenyl). Enolate of CH2-CHO attacks carbonyl of another Ph-CH2-CHO, giving after aldol: Ph-CH(OH)-CH2-CH2-CHO (where the new bond between α-carbon of nucleophile (CH2) and carbonyl carbon of electrophile). That yields 4-carbon chain linking two phenyls at ends: phenyl-CH(OH)-CH2-CH2-CHO. Then dehydration gives phenyl-CH=CH-CH2-CHO (conjugated). That's 4-(4-hydroxyphenyl)but-3-enal (option B). Wait chain length? Count: phenyl-CH=CH-CH2-CHO is 4 carbon chain from phenyl carbon (attached carbon is carbon 1). That's but-3-enal (double bond between C3? Let's number: aldehyde carbon is C1. Next CH2 is C2. Next CH is C3 (connected to phenyl). Actually better: CHO-CH2-CH=CH-Ph? Not same. Let's re-evaluate.\n\nIf condensation occurs between two molecules, product is bis-phenyl. Option B is monophenyl. So maybe condensation is intramolecular after formation of phenoxy? Not.\n\nBut maybe the phenolic OH after diazotization leads to ortho/para? Not.\n\nGiven the reagents sequence: 1) NaNO2 + HCl forms diazonium. 2) H2O hydrolyzes to phenol (replaces NH2 with OH). 3) aq. KOH, Heat leads to aldol condensation between two molecules of the resulting phenylacetaldehyde, giving dimer (since base condenses aldehydes). So final product would be a dimer (bis-phenyl). Options A and C are dimers. The difference: A is aldol product (β-hydroxy) not dehydrated; C is dehydrated α,β-unsaturated aldehyde (enone). Under heat, dehydration likely occurs, giving conjugated enal (C). Option A would be result if no dehydration. Usually heating with base leads to dehydration. So I think answer is C.\n\nCheck structural details: 2,4-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)but-2-enal. That has two phenol groups each para-hydroxy. And chain: CHO-CH=CH-CH(OH)-Ph? Wait need correct connectivity: The aldol would give beta-hydroxy aldehyde: Ph-CH(OH)-CH2-CH2-CHO. Dehydration removes water between OH and adjacent hydrogen on the carbon bearing OH (the carbon bearing OH is α to phenyl). That yields Ph-CH=CH-CH2-CHO (trans). That's 4-(4-hydroxyphenyl)but-3-enal (monophenyl). Not bis. So my earlier mapping off.\n\nLet's draw dimer correctly: Two phenylacetaldehyde: Enolate of one (alpha carbon next to aldehyde) attacks carbonyl of second, producing β-hydroxy aldehyde where the OH is on the attacked carbonyl carbon (the second molecule). The resulting skeleton: Ph-CH2-CH(OH)-CH2-CHO-Ph? Wait need integrate.\n\nTake nucleophile: molecule A enolate at α carbon (CH2 of Ph-CH2-CHO). This carbon attacks carbonyl carbon of molecule B (CHO). So new bond between A's α carbon and B's carbonyl carbon. After attack, B carbonyl becomes alkoxide, which after protonation gives β-hydroxy. So final skeleton: Ph-CH2-CH(-OH)-CH2-Ph-CHO? Actually B still has its own α carbon? B's original aldehyde carbonyl is now attached to A's α carbon and becomes an alcohol (HO-). The B's α carbon is still CH2 attached to phenyl. So product: Ph-CH2-CH(-OH)-CH2-CHO (where the terminal CHO is from B? Wait B's carbonyl is now an alcohol, not an aldehyde. The aldehyde function is now at the former carbonyl carbon? Let's assign: B had CHO. After attack, carbonyl carbon becomes tetrahedral with O- and becomes seat of new C-C bond. It becomes a β-hydroxy carbon but still holds O- which after protonation becomes OH (secondary alcohol). So product no longer has aldehyde? The nucleophile's carbonyl is still an aldehyde (since it was not attacked). So product retains one aldehyde at A side, and a secondary alcohol at B side. That yields a β-hydroxy aldehyde with two phenyl groups at ends: phenyl-CH2-CHO (A) unchanged? Actually A's aldehyde remains; A's α carbon (CH2) now attached to B's carbonyl carbon which now is an alcohol; B's phenyl attached to its own CH2 that is now at β position relative to aldehyde. So structure: Phenyl-CH2-CHO (on A side) attached via its CH2 to a carbon bearing OH and attached to CH2-Phenyl (on B side). So chain length: phenyl-CH2-CHO (C1) -CH2- C(OH)-CH2- phenyl (C5?). This matches option A: 3-hydroxy-2,4-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)butanal. Let's map: Butanal chain (4 carbons) with aldehyde at carbon 1, hydroxy at carbon 3, phenyl groups at carbons 2 and 4? Option A says 3-hydroxy-2,4-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)butanal. That seems to fit the aldol product: aldehyde at C1, phenyl at C2 (the alpha carbon bearing phenyl from A), phenyl at C4 (the carbon bearing phenyl from B) and OH at C3 (beta). Yes that's the β-hydroxyaldehyde dimer.\n\nOption C: 2,4-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)but-2-enal is the dehydrated version where OH eliminated, forming double bond between C2 and C3 (i.e., conjugated enal). That's expected after dehydration.\n\nGiven that reaction includes heating in KOH, dehydration may happen, giving enal (C). Usually aldol condensation under basic heating yields the α,β-unsaturated carbonyl (enone). So answer C.\n\nThus final product is C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.218646035918482e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999745, "logp_correct": -2.5548037911743393e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807907502, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6115030778062711, "cot_lp_min64": -1.3138931301428727, "frac_below_-5": 0.01059705350219695, "digit_lp_min": -4.429952144622803, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.00017093151109293103, "n_cot_tokens": 3869, "lp_end": -0.7272368669509888, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -5.960462772236497e-07, "logp_nocot": -3.0640479995593526, "p_nocot": 0.04669827749049626, "uplift": 3.064047999559327, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.01042, "B": 0.93796, "C": 0.046698, "D": 0.004922}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recw4rROcnHKNZhtK", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: You identified a new quorum-sensing peptide in the bacteria Lactobacillus acidophilus. You are highly interested if its role in intercellular communication is conserved among the eukaryotes. You treat yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae with the peptide and interestingly observe the formation of the shmoo. You want to learn about the proteome of the active chromatin in the shmoo and recover it by chromatin immunoprecipitation followed by mass spectrometry. The least of proteins from which complex below will you observe in your assay?\n\nA) pre-replication complex\nB) nucleosome histone complex\nC) enhancer protein complex\nD) pre-initiation complex\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: You identified a new quorum-sensing peptide in the bacteria Lactobacillus acidophilus. You are highly interested if its role in intercellular communication is conserved among the eukaryotes. You treat yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae with the peptide and interestingly observe the formation of the shmoo. You want to learn about the proteome of the active chromatin in the shmoo and recover it by chromatin immunoprecipitation followed by mass spectrometry. The least of proteins from which complex below will you observe in your assay?\n\nA) pre-replication complex\nB) nucleosome histone complex\nC) enhancer protein complex\nD) pre-initiation complex\n", "true_answer": "A", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recw4rROcnHKNZhtK:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 4928, 25, 1416, 17800, 620, 179068, 2011, 28218, 105019, 591, 170254, 630, 359, 120706, 11, 4665, 64016, 11, 7075, 641, 126202, 18704, 13, 27042, 316, 4484, 1078, 2423, 747, 328, 6725, 33474, 17294, 306, 641, 126202, 326, 18552, 480, 656, 33474, 17294, 28152, 467, 9285, 488, 7949, 12661, 656, 4842, 1755, 13, 21580, 8012, 328, 37394, 738, 481, 31614, 290, 5153, 328, 306, 92679, 30, 2632, 29212, 6200, 76136, 12073, 11, 1118, 382, 5153, 8668, 483, 6725, 33474, 17294, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 66912, 2146, 6725, 741, 18001, 33474, 17294, 738, 679, 47081, 57339, 3124, 3617, 350, 47421, 25058, 936, 876, 125639, 23408, 8012, 350, 49212, 52756, 618, 9839, 108195, 8, 395, 6725, 179996, 11, 188650, 16737, 8012, 350, 12727, 4840, 175224, 12727, 4840, 540, 8818, 39961, 936, 876, 8618, 79, 2389, 8012, 350, 28202, 47548, 8, 1118, 382, 8668, 483, 23224, 87568, 57432, 11, 625, 23517, 6725, 3354, 641, 126202, 18704, 26, 10112, 6931, 14324, 13, 2632, 290, 5153, 20395, 382, 876, 8618, 79, 2389, 8012, 13, 2632, 6052, 415, 30, 24305, 355, 382, 876, 8618, 79, 2389, 8012, 26, 415, 382, 876, 125639, 23408, 8012, 13, 623, 4928, 14494, 25, 355, 8, 876, 8618, 79, 2389, 8012, 11, 418, 8, 47081, 57339, 3124, 690, 8012, 11, 363, 8, 188650, 16737, 8012, 11, 415, 8, 876, 125639, 23408, 8012, 13, 623, 5153, 738, 413, 876, 8618, 79, 2389, 8012, 2236, 4275, 625, 66912, 31999, 889, 87568, 12839, 47548, 13, 2632, 5655, 355, 13, 2632, 6052, 25, 163814, 866, 25, 355, 13], "n_tokens": 270, "words": 178, "band_lo": 118.75, "band_hi": 475.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the question: We identified new quorum-sensing peptide from Lactobacillus, treat yeast, cause shmoo formation. Want to learn about proteome of active chromatin in shmoo and recover it by chromatin immunoprecipitation followed by mass spec. Which complex of proteins will you observe the least of in assay? So essentially among complexes listed, which is least associated with active chromatin (i.e., transcriptionally active). Active chromatin will have nucleosome histones (present everywhere), pre-initiation complex (RNA polymerase II PIC) for active promoters, enhancer protein complex (activators/coactivators at enhancers), pre-replication complex (origin licensing) which is associated with DNA replication origins, not necessarily active during shmoo formation; maybe lower presence. So the least observed is pre-replication complex. So answer D? Wait A is pre-replication complex; D is pre-initiation complex. The question choices: A) pre-replication complex, B) nucleosome histone complex, C) enhancer protein complex, D) pre-initiation complex. The least will be pre-replication complex because it's not transcription-specific but replication origin licensing. So choose A. So answer: ANSWER: A.", "response": "ANSWER: A", "answer": "A", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.0531076592742465e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999376, "logp_correct": -6.240550417048792e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000624, "p_letters": {"A": 1.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6755326853962792, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9077737809216854, "frac_below_-5": 0.01556420233463035, "digit_lp_min": null, "answer_first_mention_lp": 0.0, "n_cot_tokens": 257, "lp_end": -0.00043418517452664673, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -5.008634700269001, "p_nocot": 0.006680017309001832, "uplift": 5.008634700268939, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.00668, "B": 0.991402, "C": 0.000427, "D": 0.001491}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recwW1A85nfyQpReG", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: In a specific region of the sky, astronomers have observed that the number of stars varies with parallax as 1/plx^5. How does the number of stars in that region of the sky change with distance (per unit range of distance, r)?\n\nA) ~ r^4\nB) ~ r^5\nC) ~ r^2\nD) ~ r^3\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: In a specific region of the sky, astronomers have observed that the number of stars varies with parallax as 1/plx^5. How does the number of stars in that region of the sky change with distance (per unit range of distance, r)?\n\nA) ~ r^4\nB) ~ r^5\nC) ~ r^2\nD) ~ r^3\n", "true_answer": "D", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recwW1A85nfyQpReG:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 2411, 25, 623, 2086, 328, 13701, 20395, 47245, 483, 686, 85720, 472, 220, 16, 90732, 87, 61, 20, 13, 3371, 85720, 350, 528, 87, 8, 382, 38721, 1151, 99864, 316, 9324, 25, 633, 87, 350, 18843, 47723, 8, 314, 220, 16, 6662, 5295, 350, 4841, 66, 741, 2632, 633, 87, 35353, 251, 220, 16, 17447, 13, 19233, 220, 16, 90732, 87, 61, 20, 314, 350, 16, 27334, 528, 87, 61, 20, 915, 314, 350, 16, 60504, 350, 16, 17447, 73602, 20, 1546, 314, 428, 61, 20, 13, 2632, 20395, 2086, 10217, 686, 85720, 382, 478, 81738, 87, 8, 35353, 251, 220, 16, 90732, 87, 61, 20, 35353, 251, 428, 61, 20, 13, 623, 4928, 25, 392, 5299, 2226, 290, 2086, 328, 13701, 306, 484, 7967, 328, 290, 17307, 3343, 483, 9324, 350, 543, 7670, 3352, 328, 9324, 11, 428, 8, 16842, 2632, 1023, 3810, 290, 2086, 777, 7670, 9324, 11, 575, 2560, 4213, 272, 45, 6662, 428, 30, 2604, 1327, 478, 472, 1114, 328, 428, 30, 44130, 261, 12545, 25, 9184, 777, 7670, 9324, 382, 99864, 316, 428, 61, 17, 4238, 4918, 27591, 395, 21219, 12545, 11, 8117, 316, 478, 35353, 251, 428, 61, 18, 75628, 13, 3072, 1023, 10175, 172525, 55881, 483, 686, 85720, 13, 41021, 8420, 25, 3164, 20395, 484, 290, 2086, 328, 13701, 47245, 483, 686, 85720, 472, 220, 16, 90732, 87, 61, 20, 13, 4569, 2023, 413, 290, 66898, 3605, 777, 7670, 686, 85720, 13, 1843, 2086, 777, 7670, 686, 85720, 17116, 272, 45, 6662, 81738, 87, 8, 35353, 251, 220, 16, 90732, 87, 61, 20, 13, 7801, 581, 665, 7118, 316, 9324, 25, 633, 87, 314, 220, 16, 17447, 11, 326, 272, 81738, 87, 8, 314, 533, 16, 17447, 61, 17, 1498, 13, 2632, 272, 45, 314, 350, 74, 425, 220, 16, 90732, 87, 61, 20, 8, 272, 81738, 87, 8, 314, 372, 425, 350, 81, 61, 20, 8, 425, 21382, 16, 17447, 61, 17, 1498, 8, 314, 533, 74, 428, 61, 18, 1498, 13, 2632, 777, 7670, 9324, 11, 290, 2086, 328, 13701, 350, 67, 45, 94580, 8, 35353, 251, 428, 61, 18, 13, 2632, 6052, 415, 364, 4383, 290, 17898, 5720, 25, 392, 3086, 2086, 328, 13701, 47245, 483, 686, 85720, 472, 220, 16, 90732, 87, 61, 20, 13, 3253, 2226, 290, 2086, 328, 13701, 306, 484, 7967, 328, 290, 17307, 3343, 483, 9324, 350, 543, 7670, 3352, 328, 9324, 11, 428, 8, 16842, 4569, 22476, 23379, 1023, 1682, 272, 45, 94580, 13, 2632, 6052, 415, 65176, 428, 61, 18, 741], "n_tokens": 434, "words": 233, "band_lo": 116.5, "band_hi": 466.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to think: The number of stars observed varies with parallax as 1/plx^5. Parallax (plx) is inversely proportional to distance: plx (arcseconds) = 1/distance (parsec). So plx ∝ 1/r. Therefore 1/plx^5 = (1/(plx^5)) = (1)/( (1/r)^5 ) = r^5. So observed number vs parallax is N(plx) ∝ 1/plx^5 ∝ r^5. The question: \"How does the number of stars in that region of the sky change with distance (per unit range of distance, r)?\" So they ask the number per unit distance, i.e., dN/d r? Or just N as function of r? Usually a distribution: Number per unit distance is proportional to r^2 times space density for uniform distribution, leading to N ∝ r^3 cumulative. But they gave observational scaling with parallax. Let's parse: They observed that the number of stars varies with parallax as 1/plx^5. That could be the differential count per unit parallax. If number per unit parallax interval dN/d(plx) ∝ 1/plx^5. Then we can transform to distance: plx = 1/r, and d(plx) = -1/r^2 dr. So dN = (k * 1/plx^5) d(plx) = k * (r^5) * (-1/r^2 dr) = -k r^3 dr. So per unit distance, the number of stars (dN/dr) ∝ r^3. So answer D.\n\nCheck the phrasing: \"the number of stars varies with parallax as 1/plx^5. How does the number of stars in that region of the sky change with distance (per unit range of distance, r)?\" That indeed suggests they want dN/dr. So answer D (~ r^3).", "response": "ANSWER: D", "answer": "D", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.458328112377785e-05, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999817, "logp_correct": -1.825853006209897e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000182, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 1.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.44436689958612025, "cot_lp_min64": -1.0387589090528992, "frac_below_-5": 0.011876484560570071, "digit_lp_min": -0.7660540342330933, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.04571240022778511, "n_cot_tokens": 421, "lp_end": -0.09146612882614136, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -12.50071333713014, "p_nocot": 3.723995759927937e-06, "uplift": 12.500713337130122, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.999287, "B": 0.00071, "C": 0.0, "D": 4e-06}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recxTy0x7M6T9ikc0", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: α-β unsaturated carbonyls have a much more electrophilic carbon double bond. These double bonds act as a good Lewis acid and react with nucleophiles to produce enolate ions. In this case, when a nucleophile attacks the β position, it produces a resonance-stabilized intermediate. When a nucleophile attacking the β-carbon is enolate, such reaction is known as the Michael reaction.\nWhat are the reactants and major final products of the following Michael addition reactions?\ndimethyl malonate + methyl (E)-3-(p-tolyl)acrylate + (NaOEt, EtOH) -->(A)\n1-(cyclohex-1-en-1-yl)piperidine + (E)-but-2-enenitrile + (MeOH, H3O+) --> (B)\nC + but-3-en-2-one + (KOH, H2O) ---> 2-(3-oxobutyl)cyclohexane-1,3-dione\n\nA) A = trimethyl 2-(p-tolyl)propane-1,1,3-tricarboxylate, B = 3-(2-oxocyclohexyl)butanenitrile, C = 2-hydroxycyclohexane-1,3-dione\nB) A = trimethyl 3-(p-tolyl)propane-1,1,2-tricarboxylate, B = 3-(2-hydroxycyclohex-1-en-1-yl)butanenitrile, C = 2-hydroxycyclohexane-1,3-dione\nC) A = trimethyl 2-(p-tolyl)propane-1,1,3-tricarboxylate, B = 3-(2-oxocyclohexyl)butanenitrile, C = cyclohexane-1,3-dione\nD) A = trimethyl 3-(p-tolyl)propane-1,1,2-tricarboxylate, B = 3-(2-hydroxycyclohex-1-en-1-yl)butanenitrile, C = cyclohexane-1,3-dione\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: α-β unsaturated carbonyls have a much more electrophilic carbon double bond. These double bonds act as a good Lewis acid and react with nucleophiles to produce enolate ions. In this case, when a nucleophile attacks the β position, it produces a resonance-stabilized intermediate. When a nucleophile attacking the β-carbon is enolate, such reaction is known as the Michael reaction.\nWhat are the reac