o C_a. So the carbon bearing two esters is adjacent to the chain (one carbon away). The aryl is attached to that same carbon. So in three-carbon parent chain (propane), the di-ester carbon could be carbon1 (if we choose that carbon as central? Not typical). Let's draw a propane with carbon atoms numbered 1-3. Suppose carbon 1 has two esters and the aryl attached to carbon 2, carbon3 has one ester. That would not place aryl on the di-ester carbon. Option with aryl on carbon 3 (trimethyl 3-(p-tolyl)propane-1,1,2-tricarboxylate) has aryl at carbon 3, di-ester at carbon 1, one ester at carbon 2. That's also not right.\n\nThus none perfectly match, but maybe they consider the malonate carbon as the carbon bearing one ester (because one of the two esters counted as part of \"propane\" while the other is considered as substituent? Not.\n\nMaybe we need to re-evaluate the product orientation: Maybe after addition, the original carbonyl of acrylate becomes a ketone (via enolate protonation at carbonyl of malonate) resulting in different distribution.\n\nLet's check typical Michael addition product of dimethyl malonate to methyl acrylate: The product is dimethyl 2-(acetyloxy)propionate? Not.\n\nSearch memory: Reaction gives dimethyl 2-(acetoxy)but-3-ene? I'm not sure.\n\nLet's quickly consult mental recall: Michael addition of diethyl malonate to methyl acrylate yields diethyl 2-(carboxymethyl)butanoate (i.e., a diester with chain of four carbons). The product structure: EtO2C-CH2-CH(CO2Et)-CH2-CO2Et? Wait that would be tri-ester again.\n\nActually maybe the malonate enolate attacks β-carbon and after protonation, the carbonyl of the acrylate remains as carbonyl (ester) and the malonate carbon becomes a carbonyl (still), leading to 1,5-dicarbonyl chain: CO2Me-CH2-CH2-CO-CH2-CO2Me? Not.\n\nGiven time, choose option C (since B matches, C matches reactant). Need pick between A option C or D based on likely product; I'd guess option C (trimethyl 2-(p-tolyl)propane-1,1,3-tricarboxylate) because aryl likely ends up at carbon 2 (the central carbon) due to connectivity after addition (maybe the chain collapses). So final answer: option C.\n\nThus answer: ANSWER: C", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -4.768370942542788e-07, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999931, "logp_correct": -6.855598325000576e-15, "letter_mass": 0.9999996423722589, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.547531556316678, "cot_lp_min64": -1.4196514666264406, "frac_below_-5": 0.008327781479013991, "digit_lp_min": -5.083821773529053, "answer_first_mention_lp": -2.398494243621826, "n_cot_tokens": 6004, "lp_end": -0.0014296083245426416, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -1.1508953316390116, "p_nocot": 0.31635340133447104, "uplift": 1.1508953316390047, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.66972, "B": 0.00166, "C": 0.316353, "D": 0.012266}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recxm2fjqvae5dDYW", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Which of the following is true about the operating principle of a thermoradiative cell?\n\nA) The cell is directly heated, causing it to emit thermal radiation. This causes an accumulation of minority carriers. Current occurs due to diffusion of carriers from regions of high to low carrier concentration.\nB) The cell is directly heated, causing it to emit thermal radiation. This causes an accumulation of minority carriers, which causes a built-in field. Current occurs due to drift of the carriers due to the built-in field.\nC) The cell is directly heated, exciting charge carriers. When the charge carriers recombine, this causes a depletion of minority carriers. Current occurs due to diffusion of carriers towards the depleted region.\nD) The cell is directly heated, exciting charge carriers. When the charge carriers recombine, this causes a depletion of minority carriers. Current occurs due to drift of carriers towards the depleted region.\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Which of the following is true about the operating principle of a thermoradiative cell?\n\nA) The cell is directly heated, causing it to emit thermal radiation. This causes an accumulation of minority carriers. Current occurs due to diffusion of carriers from regions of high to low carrier concentration.\nB) The cell is directly heated, causing it to emit thermal radiation. This causes an accumulation of minority carriers, which causes a built-in field. Current occurs due to drift of the carriers due to the built-in field.\nC) The cell is directly heated, exciting charge carriers. When the charge carriers recombine, this causes a depletion of minority carriers. Current occurs due to diffusion of carriers towards the depleted region.\nD) The cell is directly heated, exciting charge carriers. When the charge carriers recombine, this causes a depletion of minority carriers. Current occurs due to drift of carriers towards the depleted region.\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recxm2fjqvae5dDYW:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 4218, 17576, 267, 3040, 1799, 4206, 9506, 30540, 13, 355, 17576, 267, 3040, 1799, 4206, 382, 29212, 261, 18199, 162874, 4206, 25, 44191, 4206, 14041, 1381, 187122, 316, 261, 112085, 5870, 11, 36766, 25093, 13, 7744, 261, 10609, 6018, 4122, 402, 14041, 1799, 19119, 180524, 13, 623, 30540, 25, 623, 4206, 382, 44191, 26, 100085, 15209, 591, 1459, 1082, 316, 193960, 26, 1023, 665, 19119, 22066, 14041, 11594, 11, 175975, 187122, 350, 130918, 36138, 741, 13602, 290, 4206, 382, 140032, 1572, 5870, 11, 2776, 55890, 1424, 57036, 11, 8117, 316, 261, 2776, 92901, 135911, 36547, 13, 623, 19119, 180524, 38566, 51704, 32678, 27748, 30, 50557, 306, 261, 275, 5219, 79550, 11, 1641, 117961, 350, 59605, 936, 16968, 49777, 553, 13768, 11, 8117, 316, 65642, 106155, 326, 8113, 4200, 3259, 24297, 57604, 13, 730, 17576, 267, 3040, 1799, 4206, 11, 25419, 15286, 316, 11202, 32678, 13986, 11, 8117, 316, 11202, 51704, 32678, 27748, 5862, 166998, 7967, 30, 623, 8113, 4200, 6451, 30826, 51704, 49777, 5251, 79550, 11, 8117, 316, 2208, 23821, 316, 50971, 13, 21580, 6496, 15248, 30, 41021, 30890, 25, 44137, 267, 3040, 1799, 4206, 24615, 1299, 261, 162874, 4206, 889, 483, 12229, 117961, 21009, 656, 35031, 36138, 98472, 92901, 13, 623, 5897, 382, 44191, 813, 484, 480, 14041, 1381, 187122, 26, 290, 4206, 77813, 1101, 591, 135911, 55890, 13, 623, 8363, 7704, 382, 4493, 57604, 306, 290, 8113, 4200, 11194, 3259, 350, 9995, 50971, 1641, 6687, 22115, 30, 50557, 306, 50971, 11, 4207, 18518, 23629, 136084, 24702, 26, 1023, 553, 33930, 656, 8113, 4200, 3259, 11, 36766, 2208, 350, 4541, 2976, 741, 67533, 10514, 1217, 62274, 5192, 316, 27748, 117960, 13, 2214, 261, 9159, 4206, 11, 25419, 18518, 49777, 306, 290, 29247, 11, 51704, 32678, 65642, 316, 166998, 7967, 15286, 316, 19119, 180524, 11, 24168, 187122, 484, 21544, 13, 623, 2776, 2208, 382, 23821, 316, 50971, 350, 72, 2560, 4213, 3470, 13986, 889, 483, 10609, 3422, 741, 165972, 364, 58369, 28035, 3880, 1402, 32, 8, 41998, 15155, 22845, 35031, 36138, 15155, 70787, 328, 51704, 49777, 13, 14536, 5192, 316, 65642, 350, 21116, 316, 4465, 27748, 3991, 33, 8, 38569, 889, 2208, 5192, 316, 57604, 328, 49777, 5192, 316, 8113, 4200, 3259, 364, 34, 8, 41998, 179518, 49777, 13, 4296, 1023, 19119, 22066, 11, 495, 16431, 166998, 328, 51704, 49777, 13, 14536, 5192, 316, 65642, 9979, 157056, 7967, 364, 35, 8, 38569, 889, 2208, 5192, 316, 57604, 364, 34405, 382, 6145, 30, 21580, 27004, 382, 42647, 30, 730, 261, 17576, 267, 3040, 1799, 4206, 11, 290, 32678, 19119, 180524, 146484, 187122, 26, 290, 2273, 38566, 51704, 49777, 26, 495, 15286, 316, 261, 40553, 484, 30826, 65642, 328, 51704, 49777, 591, 29247, 316, 166998, 7967, 350, 4522, 19119, 180524, 21241, 741, 2632, 290, 2208, 382, 65642, 2887, 74176, 13, 23586, 6052, 6960, 363, 364, 58369, 14866, 25, 730, 162874, 4206, 1641, 117961, 11, 13986, 18518, 16968, 49777, 26, 51704, 49777, 89284, 316, 79550, 326, 553, 77302, 656, 3259, 11, 24168, 2208, 13, 730, 35031, 4206, 11, 25419, 18518, 49777, 161240, 26, 19119, 180524, 540, 79550, 334, 789, 2822, 51704, 49777, 5862, 79550, 11, 9278, 40553, 13, 2632, 65642, 15256, 290, 79550, 19854, 13, 623, 8113, 4200, 3259, 1217, 14837, 12967, 49777, 5251, 11, 889, 290, 9107, 7075, 328, 2208, 382, 65642, 5192, 316, 40553, 13, 11048, 35562, 2608, 484, 17576, 267, 3040, 1799, 11242, 553, 29212, 290, 18199, 328, 50971, 25, 1023, 19866, 472, 261, 10609, 6018, 1919, 290, 14041, 1799, 19119, 180524, 540, 290, 79550, 382, 8731, 11, 326, 290, 2208, 382, 25394, 656, 65642, 328, 49777, 316, 290, 166998, 7967, 1919, 1023, 19119, 22066, 326, 22845, 187122, 13, 2632, 363, 7890, 11081, 364, 84787, 6052, 25, 363, 364, 4383, 17898, 5720, 25, 392, 5958, 290, 8363, 49777, 19119, 22066, 11, 495, 16431, 261, 166998, 328, 51704, 49777, 3692, 11377, 19119, 180524, 52683, 49777, 11, 15021, 166998, 7967, 328, 51704, 49777, 13, 392, 6576, 21241, 5192, 316, 65642, 328, 49777, 9979, 290, 157056, 7967, 3692, 11377, 65642, 364, 84787, 6052, 363, 13], "n_tokens": 678, "words": 526, "band_lo": 263.0, "band_hi": 1052.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to understand thermoradiative cell operation principle. A thermoradiative cell is essentially a reverse photovoltaic cell: heated cell radiates photons to a colder environment, generating electricity. It's a heat engine based on radiative recombination. The principle: The cell is heated; electrons excited from valence to conduction; they can recombine radiatively, emitting photons (thermal radiation). Because the cell is hotter than environment, net emission > absorption, leading to a net outward photon flux. The recombination reduces minority carrier concentration? Actually in a p-n junction, under illumination (PV), excess carriers are generated, leading to diffusion currents and built-in field causing drift. In thermoradiative cell, heating leads to increased carrier generation, leading to increased minority carrier concentration near depletion region? The built-in potential drives minority carriers across junction, leading to current opposite to PV. Which description matches? Let's recall: Thermoradiative cell acts like a photovoltaic cell but with external illumination replaced by thermal radiation emitted outward. The device is heated so that it radiates photons; the cell extracts work from photon emission. The charge transport is via drift in the built-in electric field (like PV under forward bias? Actually in PV, light creates electron-hole pairs; they are separated by built-in field, generating current (drift). Diffusion also contributes due to concentration gradients. For a TR cell, heating creates carriers in the bulk, minority carrier diffusion to depletion region leads to recombination, producing photons that escape. The net current is opposite to PV (i.e., power generation but with heat input). Hmm.\n\nLet's examine options:\n\nA) Heat → emit thermal radiation → accumulation of minority carriers. Current due to diffusion (high to low concentration).\n\nB) Same but current due to drift of carriers due to built-in field.\n\nC) Heat excite carriers. When they recombine, this causes depletion of minority carriers. Current due to diffusion towards depleted region.\n\nD) Same but current due to drift.\n\nWhich is correct? Which mechanism is dominant? In a thermoradiative cell, the carrier recombination emits photons; the process reduces minority carriers; this leads to a gradient that drives diffusion of minority carriers from bulk to depletion region (where recombination occurs). So the current is diffusion-limited. Thus answer likely C.\n\nLet's verify: In photovoltaic cell under illumination, generation creates excess carriers; minority carriers diffuse to junction and are swept by field, producing current. In thermal cell, heating creates carriers uniformly; recombination at junction depletes minority carriers near junction, creating gradient. So diffusion toward the junction matters. The built-in field also sweeps carriers across, but the primary cause of current is diffusion due to gradient. Many texts state that thermoradiative cells are essentially the reverse of PV: they operate as a heat engine where the radiative recombination at the junction is essential, and the current is driven by diffusion of carriers to the depletion region where they recombine and emit photons. So C seems appropriate.\n\nThus answer: C.\n\nCheck phrasing: \"When the charge carriers recombine, this causes a depletion of minority carriers.\" Yes recombination removes carriers, leaving depletion region of minority carriers. \"Current occurs due to diffusion of carriers towards the depleted region.\" Yes diffusion.\n\nThus answer C.", "response": "ANSWER: C", "answer": "C", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.4570639328990183e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999053, "logp_correct": -9.463431622336373e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999998807908194, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.0, "C": 1.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.7650190666437023, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2132096508890609, "frac_below_-5": 0.021052631578947368, "digit_lp_min": null, "answer_first_mention_lp": -3.58813522325363e-05, "n_cot_tokens": 665, "lp_end": -3.099436753473128e-06, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -9.271364217771744, "p_nocot": 9.408008053648591e-05, "uplift": 9.27136421777165, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.003116, "B": 0.978862, "C": 9.4e-05, "D": 0.017928}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
