25, 363, 21, 39, 20, 350, 18173, 8, 659, 10049, 46, 350, 32214, 133496, 8, 314, 363, 22, 39, 21, 46, 13, 5934, 5028, 5773, 25, 12577, 220, 17, 487, 402, 12796, 350, 786, 71973, 220, 17, 487, 8, 1815, 1147, 42464, 350, 172749, 532, 326, 487, 741, 9352, 20690, 25, 363, 22, 39, 20, 46, 17, 1388, 30, 50557, 1217, 2190, 19282, 860, 47612, 13, 2632, 1721, 25, 363, 22, 39, 20, 1388, 46, 17, 30, 2932, 1412, 1078, 5018, 532, 591, 42464, 871, 532, 3605, 220, 17, 350, 32214, 133496, 15883, 3643, 532, 659, 21937, 12362, 42464, 741, 2632, 20690, 25, 363, 22, 39, 20, 1388, 46, 17, 13, 41021, 17950, 4842, 25, 363, 22, 314, 220, 9928, 13, 15, 11, 487, 20, 314, 20, 13, 15, 11, 2190, 314, 220, 2467, 13, 2548, 11, 532, 17, 314, 220, 1398, 13, 15, 13, 17565, 6574, 18369, 13, 2548, 13, 9700, 15248, 220, 18369, 364, 8093, 418, 25, 220, 19, 15539, 9547, 60006, 4151, 291, 18655, 13, 52227, 25, 363, 22, 39, 20, 1388, 46, 17, 30, 24305, 2974, 4151, 291, 18655, 25, 363, 22, 39, 21, 46, 17, 13, 54839, 487, 483, 2190, 11, 12577, 1001, 487, 11, 1147, 2190, 25, 363, 22, 39, 20, 1388, 46, 17, 13, 38569, 20690, 0, 2632, 2684, 4842, 364, 8093, 363, 25, 220, 17, 15539, 9547, 60006, 4151, 291, 18655, 2684, 20690, 364, 8093, 415, 25, 92567, 3643, 91061, 8527, 700, 379, 13, 52227, 25, 532, 28, 8310, 1388, 18618, 316, 21937, 3643, 13, 21926, 363, 22, 39, 20, 1388, 46, 17, 30, 50557, 21937, 3643, 91061, 8527, 700, 379, 382, 363, 22, 39, 20, 1388, 46, 17, 30, 41021, 3605, 25, 21937, 3643, 314, 363, 21, 39, 20, 11, 2932, 15883, 3643, 15883, 350, 34, 28, 46, 8, 19282, 1001, 363, 326, 1001, 532, 11, 2932, 532, 18618, 316, 21937, 3643, 11, 2932, 2190, 18618, 316, 15883, 3643, 15883, 13, 2632, 3609, 20690, 363, 22, 39, 20, 1388, 46, 17, 30, 2932, 5018, 532, 30, 24305, 21937, 3643, 91061, 8527, 700, 379, 382, 363, 21, 39, 20, 30738, 12, 8310, 1388, 25, 10954, 363, 22, 39, 20, 1388, 46, 17, 30, 41021, 3605, 532, 25, 1354, 553, 1920, 532, 350, 22407, 37496, 532, 326, 15883, 3643, 532, 741, 2632, 532, 17, 13, 11377, 2684, 20690, 13, 2632, 722, 4242, 3880, 679, 2684, 36628, 20690, 363, 22, 39, 20, 1388, 46, 17, 11, 4842, 220, 18369, 364, 23483, 316, 1199, 23712, 326, 478, 27029, 13, 23712, 25, 11245, 25689, 220, 11727, 15, 12, 17820, 15, 9256, 85848, 16, 23379, 532, 13091, 47612, 2118, 1415, 295, 350, 22407, 12362, 42464, 503, 1669, 3952, 88, 459, 18655, 741, 41631, 27261, 25689, 540, 220, 19249, 15, 9256, 85848, 16, 19271, 15883, 3643, 328, 18655, 503, 100539, 133496, 30, 4004, 3952, 88, 459, 18655, 363, 28, 46, 14518, 2846, 220, 19603, 15, 12, 19313, 15, 30, 42136, 7714, 88, 613, 14518, 6574, 19249, 15, 12, 20750, 15, 30, 50557, 100539, 133496, 363, 28, 46, 6574, 19249, 15, 12, 20750, 15, 395, 87455, 100539, 23498, 268, 26, 110666, 100539, 133496, 6574, 12536, 15, 9256, 85848, 16, 13, 4004, 3952, 88, 459, 18655, 363, 28, 46, 6574, 19603, 15, 9256, 85848, 16, 13, 2632, 2973, 4149, 364, 99246, 532, 13091, 21638, 220, 11727, 15, 12, 17820, 15, 382, 44596, 328, 1669, 3952, 88, 459, 18655, 350, 65, 12086, 326, 5532, 8, 5192, 316, 47612, 86667, 13, 92567, 340, 1217, 11245, 889, 12190, 220, 15415, 15, 12, 31081, 15, 483, 191063, 13, 623, 3352, 220, 11727, 15, 12, 17820, 15, 23379, 18655, 364, 45, 27029, 25, 220, 16, 39, 478, 27029, 7398, 261, 6211, 1347, 540, 220, 994, 13, 15, 108313, 350, 16, 39, 8, 1127, 19271, 395, 1669, 3952, 88, 459, 18655, 120589, 350, 77020, 562, 8, 14518, 2846, 220, 702, 12, 1311, 108313, 13, 92567, 12362, 42464, 665, 413, 11076, 889, 4783, 220, 19, 12, 22, 108313, 13, 2632, 220, 994, 108313, 382, 18655, 364, 19371, 110666, 440, 45023, 25, 1920, 3503, 1561, 2454, 74479, 220, 17, 39, 540, 220, 23, 13, 3286, 326, 220, 22, 13, 8540, 108313, 11, 19271, 395, 1209, 22572, 431, 5028, 26369, 76732, 1900, 350, 8857, 6, 12708, 1542, 8302, 25, 1920, 11982, 328, 23458, 24742, 1555, 440, 45023, 2454, 220, 17, 39, 11, 9874, 3503, 1561, 483, 643, 6574, 23, 63844, 13, 4569, 15248, 1209, 15539, 9547, 60006, 4151, 291, 18655, 350, 19, 15539, 9547, 60006, 4151, 291, 18655, 741, 2604, 2023, 1217, 3981, 220, 18, 15539, 75, 8527, 12, 17, 4559, 123753, 6077, 44624, 32214, 133496, 30, 4569, 382, 625, 197465, 26, 1481, 3644, 2647, 87130, 8302, 350, 62832, 220, 16, 39, 2454, 741, 2632, 290, 31822, 23379, 1209, 92838, 364, 5808, 6960, 1209, 15539, 9547, 60006, 4151, 291, 18655, 350, 3941, 418, 741, 623, 100539, 133496, 1481, 679, 100539, 133496, 120589, 2846, 220, 24, 12, 702, 108313, 350, 22456, 1347, 8, 625, 220, 994, 11, 326, 110666, 8302, 625, 123676, 13, 92567, 3643, 91061, 8527, 700, 379, 853, 860, 42464, 11, 813, 860, 220, 994, 108313, 13, 2632, 6052, 418, 364, 84787, 163814, 866, 25, 418, 13], "n_tokens": 1304, "words": 654, "band_lo": 274.5, "band_hi": 1098.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret data. Molecular ion m/z 156. Isotopic pattern: m/z 158 peak 32% relative - suggests chlorine (Cl-35 and Cl-37 ratio 3:1, i.e., 75% and 25% for M and M+2). Here 32% of base peak, close to 1/3, consistent with chlorine. So compound contains one chlorine.\n\nMolecular weight 156 includes Cl. Let's calculate formulas. For chlorine (35.45 average), but using isotopic patterns, assume 35Cl. So M = 156 (including 35Cl). Subtract 35 to get 121 remaining for C, H, O. Need possible formulas. Let's test options.\n\nOption A: 3-Chloro-2-hydroxybenzaldehyde. Molecular formula? Benzaldehyde = C7H6O, add OH (extra O and H) and Cl. So C7? Actually benzaldehyde is C7H6O (C6H5CHO), add OH at ortho => C7? Wait structure: C6H4Cl(OH)CHO? That would be C7H5ClO2? Let's count: Base benzaldehyde C7H6O. Replace H with Cl and OH? Actually starting with benzaldehyde (C6H5-CHO). Add OH to ring (replace a hydrogen). Add Cl to ring (replace another hydrogen). So total formula C7H5ClO2? Check: carbons: 7 (6 ring + 1 aldehyde carbon). Hydrogens: original benzaldehyde has C6H5-CHO -> C7H6O (hydrogens: 5 on ring + 1 on aldehyde). After substitution: two ring H replaced with OH and Cl; OH adds one H, Cl no H. So ring hydrogens reduce by 2, but OH adds H. So net hydrogens: originally 5 ring H, after substitution 3 ring H left + OH H = 4? plus aldehyde H = 1, total 5? Wait count: Original: C6H5 (ring) + CHO (aldehyde) = C7H6O. Substitutions: lose 2 H on ring (so subtract 2 H) then add OH (adds O and H). Net formula: C7H5O2Cl? Actually also Cl adds no hydrogen. So final: C7H5ClO2? plus what about extra O from OH => O count 2 (aldehyde carbonyl O + phenolic OH). So formula: C7H5ClO2. Let's calculate mass: C7 = 84.0, H5 =5.0, Cl = 35.45, O2 = 32.0. Total ~156.45. Good matches 156.\n\nOption B: 4-chlorobenzoic acid. Formula: C7H5ClO2? Wait benzoic acid: C7H6O2. Replace H with Cl, lose one H, add Cl: C7H5ClO2. Same formula! So same mass.\n\nOption C: 2-chlorobenzoic acid same formula.\n\nOption D: Phenyl chloroformate. Formula: O=COCl attached to phenyl. That's C7H5ClO2? Actually phenyl chloroformate is C7H5ClO2? Let's count: phenyl = C6H5, plus carbonyl carbon (C=O) adds one C and one O, plus O attached to phenyl, plus Cl attached to carbonyl carbon. So total formula C7H5ClO2? plus extra O? Wait phenyl chloroformate is C6H5-O-COCl: that's C7H5ClO2? Let's count O: there are two O (phenoxy O and carbonyl O). So O2. Yes same formula. So all four options have same molecular formula C7H5ClO2, mass 156.\n\nNeed to use IR and NMR. IR: broad peak 3500-2700 cm^-1 suggests O-H hydrogen-bonded (phenolic OH or carboxylic acid). Strong sharp peak at 1720 cm^-1 typical carbonyl of acid or aldehyde? Carboxylic acid C=O appears around 1710-1760? Aldehyde appears ~1720-1740? Actually aldehyde C=O ~1720-1740 for saturated aldehydes; aromatic aldehyde ~1700 cm^-1. Carboxylic acid C=O ~1710 cm^-1. So both possible.\n\nBroad O-H stretch 3500-2700 is characteristic of carboxylic acid (broad and strong) due to hydrogen bonding. Phenol also broad but generally 3200-3550 with sharper. The range 3500-2700 suggests acid.\n\nNMR: 1H NMR shows a singlet at 11.0 ppm (1H) – typical for carboxylic acid proton (exchangeable) appears around 10-13 ppm. Phenolic OH can be variable but often 4-7 ppm. So 11 ppm is acid.\n\nThen aromatic protons: two doublets each integrating 2H at 8.02 and 7.72 ppm, typical for para-disubstituted benzene (AA'BB') pattern: two sets of equivalent ortho protons each 2H, giving doublets with J ~8 Hz. That matches para-chlorobenzoic acid (4-chlorobenzoic acid). Or could also match 3-chloro-2-hydroxybenzaldehyde? That is not symmetrical; would give different splitting pattern (maybe 1H each). So the spectrum suggests para substitution.\n\nSo likely para-chlorobenzoic acid (option B). The aldehyde would have aldehyde proton around 9-10 ppm (singlet) not 11, and aromatic pattern not symmetric. Phenyl chloroformate has no OH, so no 11 ppm. So answer B.\n\nThus ANSWER: B.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -3.93390013186945e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999523, "logp_correct": -4.7778731927830385e-14, "letter_mass": 0.9999996423722999, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5260365691965198, "cot_lp_min64": -1.0709204570923747, "frac_below_-5": 0.0077459333849728895, "digit_lp_min": -2.120595693588257, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.0022103183437138796, "n_cot_tokens": 1291, "lp_end": -5.960462772236497e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": -1.1920928244535389e-07, "logp_nocot": -5.007928800008668, "p_nocot": 0.006684734399661172, "uplift": 5.00792880000862, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 5.1e-05, "B": 0.006685, "C": 0.992103, "D": 0.001162}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recyHQ53mlTIKDB7v", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: \"Scientist inoculated Escherichia coli into Luria Bertani broth with 0.1 mg/ml acridine orange and incubated at 200°C. Before adding acridine orange, the zone of inhibition for ampicillin, azithromycin, and meropenem was 25 mm, 23 mm, and 5 mm respectively.\n\nAfter adding acridine orange, there was neither bacterial growth nor zone of inhibition.\n\nInhibition zone diameter cut-off for Enterobacteriaceae for ampicillin, azithromycin, and meropenem was set to 17 mm, 15 mm, and 10 mm respectively.\n\nWhich of the following explains the previous scenario?\"\n\nA) Escherichia coli was sensitive to meropenem\nB) High temperature killed Escherichia coli\nC) Acridine orange cannot be used for plasmid curing\nD) Acridine orange successfully cured plasmids shown by inhibition of bacterial growth and zone of inhibition.\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: \"Scientist inoculated Escherichia coli into Luria Bertani broth with 0.1 mg/ml acridine orange and incubated at 200°C. Before adding acridine orange, the zone of inhibition for ampicillin, azithromycin, and meropenem was 25 mm, 23 mm, and 5 mm respectively.\n\nAfter adding acridine orange, there was neither bacterial growth nor zone of inhibition.\n\nInhibition zone diameter cut-off for Enterobacteriaceae for ampicillin, azithromycin, and meropenem was set to 17 mm, 15 mm, and 10 mm respectively.\n\nWhich of the following explains the previous scenario?\"\n\nA) Escherichia coli was sensitive to meropenem\nB) High temperature killed Escherichia coli\nC) Acridine orange cannot be used for plasmid curing\nD) Acridine orange successfully cured plasmids shown by inhibition of bacterial growth and zone of inhibition.\n", "true_answer": "B", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recyHQ53mlTIKDB7v:2", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 25697, 13, 41021, 8420, 1402, 93994, 421, 79122, 13314, 457, 13, 115791, 1511, 75813, 92688, 483, 220, 15, 13, 16, 12912, 103324, 59978, 110815, 26205, 326, 74381, 780, 540, 220, 1179, 26557, 13, 21926, 11069, 1932, 12088, 350, 1179, 26557, 8, 533, 625, 4149, 395, 70302, 8266, 11, 1481, 15874, 11242, 13, 8820, 290, 6496, 25, 392, 13036, 12673, 59978, 110815, 26205, 11, 290, 15070, 328, 102987, 395, 939, 23450, 91754, 11, 6717, 437, 166053, 11, 326, 4361, 3843, 347, 673, 220, 1161, 8957, 11, 220, 1860, 8957, 11, 326, 220, 20, 8957, 24364, 3692, 2632, 290, 4871, 34102, 21611, 151250, 25, 66520, 220, 1161, 8957, 1424, 6092, 18990, 220, 1422, 2747, 67498, 26, 13417, 437, 298, 220, 1860, 8957, 1424, 220, 1055, 2747, 67498, 26, 9708, 3843, 347, 220, 20, 8957, 464, 220, 702, 2747, 41078, 364, 8188, 12673, 59978, 110815, 26205, 11, 1354, 673, 26349, 70302, 8266, 11777, 15070, 328, 102987, 13, 125152, 860, 70302, 8266, 540, 722, 13, 730, 261, 7024, 7231, 151250, 1746, 11, 538, 860, 70302, 8266, 11, 290, 34102, 8535, 413, 26489, 2236, 1354, 382, 860, 36755, 316, 1921, 34102, 30, 50557, 1023, 2059, 392, 1750, 70302, 8266, 11777, 15070, 328, 102987, 4050, 4569, 2023, 4774, 290, 9674, 14769, 9141, 11, 10112, 5192, 316, 1932, 12088, 13, 623, 25697, 1217, 6664, 392, 637, 109225, 15070, 32514, 6092, 18990, 395, 12240, 109002, 72, 123377, 395, 939, 23450, 91754, 11, 6717, 437, 166053, 11, 326, 4361, 3843, 347, 673, 920, 316, 220, 1422, 8957, 11, 220, 1055, 8957, 11, 326, 220, 702, 8957, 24364, 3692, 5006, 553, 13853, 364, 34405, 30547, 30, 22483, 1402, 32, 8, 457, 13, 115791, 673, 24438, 316, 4361, 3843, 347, 13, 4037, 20434, 2236, 24438, 1481, 3644, 15070, 1424, 702, 26, 2105, 15070, 673, 220, 20, 8957, 2254, 137277, 13, 2632, 625, 6145, 364, 33, 8, 6597, 12088, 17372, 457, 13, 115791, 13, 3604, 220, 1179, 26557, 9504, 62013, 32829, 26, 1217, 1481, 7332, 8266, 326, 13972, 860, 15070, 13, 1328, 382, 120152, 364, 34, 8, 176225, 110815, 26205, 6284, 413, 2061, 395, 126840, 315, 137277, 13, 4037, 6145, 2236, 11943, 59978, 110815, 26205, 382, 22476, 2061, 395, 126840, 315, 137277, 11, 889, 290, 4928, 25697, 25, 1934, 12673, 11, 860, 8266, 533, 1217, 12088, 62013, 11, 813, 8535, 1746, 126840, 315, 137277, 364, 35, 8, 176225, 110815, 26205, 12836, 103577, 126840, 3375, 10147, 656, 102987, 328, 70302, 8266, 326, 15070, 328, 102987, 13, 4569, 5003, 137277, 15286, 316, 102987, 328, 8266, 326, 15070, 30, 363, 4788, 1481, 7075, 6266, 328, 20918, 126840, 3375, 11, 4137, 32829, 945, 67498, 11, 625, 860, 8266, 13, 8820, 15070, 328, 102987, 1481, 413, 3333, 13, 2632, 625, 415, 364, 84787, 6052, 418, 13, 5551, 1309, 316, 3503, 30411, 25, 623, 8633, 328, 74381, 780, 540, 220, 1179, 26557, 382, 136304, 11, 889, 10112, 1023, 14506, 220, 455, 26557, 30, 3072, 4335, 3880, 11, 418, 7890, 1636, 364, 84787, 163814, 866, 25, 418, 13], "n_tokens": 507, "words": 336, "band_lo": 153.25, "band_hi": 613.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the scenario. Let's parse:\n\nScientist inoculated E. coli into LB broth with 0.1 mg/ml acridine orange and incubated at 200°C. That's extremely high temperature (200°C) - not possible for bacterial growth, would kill cells. Also the description: \"Before adding acridine orange, the zone of inhibition for ampicillin, azithromycin, and meropenem was 25 mm, 23 mm, and 5 mm respectively.\" So the initial zones indicate susceptibility: Amp 25 mm > cut-off 17 -> susceptible; Azithro 23 mm > 15 -> susceptible; Meropenem 5 mm < 10 -> resistant.\n\nAfter adding acridine orange, there was neither bacterial growth nor zone of inhibition. Means no bacterial growth at all. In a standard drug susceptibility test, if no bacterial growth, the zones can't be measured because there is no lawn to see zones? Actually they said \"no bacterial growth nor zone of inhibition\". That could mean the culture died completely, maybe due to high temperature. The scenario also includes \"Inhibition zone diameter cut-off for Enterobacteriaceae for ampicillin, azithromycin, and meropenem was set to 17 mm, 15 mm, and 10 mm respectively.\" These are standards.\n\nWhich explanation? Options:\n\nA) E. coli was sensitive to meropenem. Not consistent because sensitive would give zone >10; here zone was 5 mm before curing. So not correct.\n\nB) High temperature killed E. coli. At 200°C definitely kills bacteria; also would prevent growth and thus no zone. This is plausible.\n\nC) Acridine orange cannot be used for plasmid curing. Not correct because although acridine orange is indeed used for plasmid curing, but the question scenario: after adding, no growth - also temperature kills, so can't test plasmid curing.\n\nD) Acridine orange successfully cured plasmids shown by inhibition of bacterial growth and zone of inhibition. That says curing leads to inhibition of growth and zone? Curing would cause loss of resistance plasmids, making bacteria more susceptible, not no growth. Also zone of inhibition would be present. So not D.\n\nThus answer B. However need to double-check: The mention of incubated at 200°C is unrealistic, but maybe they meant 20°C? But given options, B seems best.\n\nThus ANSWER: B.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -7.867782187531702e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999975359, "logp_correct": -2.464164981538932e-12, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000024643, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.6835778022654346, "cot_lp_min64": -1.2814674509103483, "frac_below_-5": 0.01417004048582996, "digit_lp_min": -0.6823087930679321, "answer_first_mention_lp": -0.1337386816740036, "n_cot_tokens": 494, "lp_end": -2.3841855067985307e-07, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -4.264166125510275, "p_nocot": 0.014063589513398986, "uplift": 4.264166125507812, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.0, "B": 0.014064, "C": 3e-06, "D": 0.985934}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recyk2HkNvlEyZzXs", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: You study an Archaea living in extreme environmental conditions using combined genomic and transcriptomic approaches. After thorough data analysis, you discovered a target gene, which may be related to the tolerance of your organism to harsh environmental conditions. However, the specific molecular function of this gene is unknown. Which strategy should you choose to reveal the exact function of the gene?\n\nA) study an organism using RNA interference mechanism for target gene to obtain gain-of-function phenotype\nB) study an organism with diminished transcriptional activity or an amplified number of pre-mRNAs of a target gene\nC) study an organism using CRISPR-Cas9 constructions with an inactive endonuclease fused with a green fluorescent protein\nD) study a wild-type organism and estimate the expression of a target gene under a range of environmental conditions\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: You study an Archaea living in extreme environmental conditions using combined genomic and transcriptomic approaches. After thorough data analysis, you discovered a target gene, which may be related to the tolerance of your organism to harsh environmental conditions. However, the specific molecular function of this gene is unknown. Which strategy should you choose to reveal the exact function of the gene?\n\nA) study an organism using RNA interference mechanism for target gene to obtain gain-of-function phenotype\nB) study an organism with diminished transcriptional activity or an amplified number of pre-mRNAs of a target gene\nC) study an organism using CRISPR-Cas9 constructions with an inactive endonuclease fused with a green fluorescent protein\nD) study a wild-type organism and estimate the expression of a target gene under a range of environmental conditions\n", "true_answer": "B", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recyk2HkNvlEyZzXs:3", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 18135, 290, 4928, 13, 7744, 1078, 448, 70121, 280, 19546, 17927, 1114, 13, 21580, 10772, 316, 26477, 6354, 1114, 30, 22483, 1402, 32, 8, 59607, 61125, 316, 9579, 11621, 13108, 55676, 152720, 13, 59607, 72, 382, 27082, 4653, 11, 15286, 316, 6266, 13108, 55676, 11, 625, 11621, 13108, 55676, 13, 8820, 188439, 12932, 1340, 625, 679, 59607, 72, 364, 33, 8, 25926, 45843, 483, 109578, 66912, 280, 8110, 503, 154399, 2086, 328, 876, 2126, 165180, 328, 3783, 19546, 13, 21926, 123648, 364, 34, 8, 7649, 14964, 2291, 8621, 10050, 288, 24, 483, 63416, 1268, 263, 1734, 2032, 138672, 483, 147223, 13, 4569, 382, 272, 36667, 24, 13112, 20174, 316, 2698, 750, 23224, 17156, 11, 12951, 316, 1921, 17156, 6427, 889, 625, 1114, 364, 35, 8, 25926, 12333, 20769, 45843, 326, 24572, 12973, 1641, 3352, 328, 16449, 6409, 13, 4569, 1481, 3644, 46307, 889, 625, 1114, 364, 976, 1636, 7139, 316, 11433, 1114, 382, 316, 2501, 27082, 14373, 503, 1072, 61248, 326, 31614, 152720, 13, 12265, 70121, 280, 19546, 17927, 11, 14964, 2291, 8621, 61125, 350, 7027, 2291, 8621, 72, 8, 2360, 9224, 8066, 24, 665, 166802, 66912, 26, 889, 363, 8844, 63416, 1268, 263, 1734, 2032, 138672, 483, 147223, 533, 10954, 395, 11256, 6396, 17156, 11, 625, 20483, 8450, 13, 17796, 355, 5003, 59607, 72, 395, 11621, 13108, 55676, 152720, 11, 889, 59607, 72, 13729, 27082, 4653, 395, 6266, 13108, 55676, 11, 625, 11621, 13108, 55676, 13, 2632, 10112, 4275, 8201, 364, 8093, 418, 66396, 392, 67, 10528, 2753, 66912, 280, 8110, 503, 448, 154399, 2086, 328, 876, 2126, 165180, 1, 1127, 484, 382, 1299, 27082, 4653, 14, 2898, 61248, 13, 2632, 418, 7890, 261, 21081, 7139, 25, 5012, 45843, 483, 17255, 66912, 503, 11202, 876, 2126, 165180, 350, 2898, 61248, 741, 4569, 1481, 26477, 1114, 4493, 152720, 6629, 364, 84787, 6052, 418, 13], "n_tokens": 320, "words": 228, "band_lo": 162.25, "band_hi": 649.0, "fmt_ok": true, "correct": true, "reasoning": "We need to interpret the question. It's about an archaeal gene unknown function. Which strategy to reveal exact function? Options:\n\nA) RNA interference to obtain gain-of-function phenotype. RNAi is knockdown, leads to loss-of-function, not gain-of-function. Also archaea may not have RNAi.\n\nB) Study organism with diminished transcriptional activity or amplified number of pre-mRNAs of target gene. That's ambiguous.\n\nC) Use CRISPR-Cas9 with inactive endonuclease fused with GFP. That is dCas9-GFP to localize DNA binding, perhaps to see binding sites but not function.\n\nD) Study wild-type organism and estimate expression under range of environmental conditions. That would give correlation but not function.\n\nThe best approach to determine function is to create knock-out or overexpression and observe phenotype. Since archaeal gene unknown, CRISPR interference (CRISPRi) using dead Cas9 can repress transcription; but C uses inactive endonuclease fused with GFP - that's for visualizing binding, not functional analysis. Option A says RNAi for gain-of-function phenotype, but RNAi typically knockdown for loss-of-function, not gain-of-function. So maybe it's wrong.\n\nOption B mentions \"diminished transcriptional activity or an amplified number of pre-mRNAs\" – that is like knockdown/overexpression. So B seems a generic approach: study organism with reduced transcription or increased pre-mRNAs (overexpression). That would reveal function via phenotype changes.\n\nThus answer B.", "response": "ANSWER: B", "answer": "B", "strict": null, "judged": false, "reject_reasons": [], "force": {"logp_answer": -8.22540732769994e-06, "p_correct": 0.9999999999999071, "logp_correct": -9.293463788915126e-14, "letter_mass": 1.0000000000000928, "p_letters": {"A": 0.0, "B": 1.0, "C": 0.0, "D": 0.0}, "tgt_gap": 0.0, "cot_lp_mean": -0.5482682862994172, "cot_lp_min64": -0.9279690533897378, "frac_below_-5": 0.009771986970684038, "digit_lp_min": -1.2874520507466514e-05, "answer_first_mention_lp": -9.536738616588991e-07, "n_cot_tokens": 307, "lp_end": -0.0015886317705735564, "lp_final": 0.0, "lp_prefix": 0.0, "logp_nocot": -4.839123276876264, "p_nocot": 0.00791398938846197, "uplift": 4.839123276876171, "p_letters_nocot": {"A": 0.075086, "B": 0.007914, "C": 0.914733, "D": 0.002267}}, "force_keep": true, "accepted": true}
{"qid": "recz3MfYHPRhsEQkW", "source": "gpqa", "type": "plain", "value": null, "frame": "-", "term": null, "placement": "user", "position": null, "system": null, "user": "Question: Compound X, which has the following IR and NMR data, reacts with red phosphorus and HI. Identify the final product.\nIR: 3400–2500 cm-1, 1720 cm-1, 1610 cm-1, 1450 cm-1\n1H NMR: 10.5 ppm (bs, 1H), 8.0 ppm (d, 2H), 7.2 ppm (d, 2H), 2.9 ppm (m, 1H), 1.7 ppm (m, 2H), 1.4 ppm (d, 3H), 0.9 ppm (t, 3H).\n\nA) 1-isobutyl-4-methylbenzene\nB) 2-(4-ethylphenyl)propanoic acid\nC) 1-(sec-butyl)-4-methylbenzene\nD) 4-(sec-butyl)benzoic acid\n\n\nYour response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: X' (without quotes) where X is your chosen letter", "instruction": null, "requirement": null, "qblock": "Question: Compound X, which has the following IR and NMR data, reacts with red phosphorus and HI. Identify the final product.\nIR: 3400–2500 cm-1, 1720 cm-1, 1610 cm-1, 1450 cm-1\n1H NMR: 10.5 ppm (bs, 1H), 8.0 ppm (d, 2H), 7.2 ppm (d, 2H), 2.9 ppm (m, 1H), 1.7 ppm (m, 2H), 1.4 ppm (d, 3H), 0.9 ppm (t, 3H).\n\nA) 1-isobutyl-4-methylbenzene\nB) 2-(4-ethylphenyl)propanoic acid\nC) 1-(sec-butyl)-4-methylbenzene\nD) 4-(sec-butyl)benzoic acid\n", "true_answer": "C", "origin": "base", "source_ref": "recz3MfYHPRhsEQkW:1", "cot_tokens": [2167, 1309, 316, 11410, 36762, 2127, 591, 23712, 33677, 27029, 11, 1815, 1921, 21416, 483, 3592, 165321, 326, 45039, 350, 13980, 38566, 1669, 3952, 88, 459, 47017, 316, 32353, 3643, 4039, 617, 30, 50557, 3592, 398, 659, 45039, 382, 2061, 395, 55111, 16589, 82, 316, 124900, 4105, 350, 36525, 936, 326, 395, 25040, 1669, 3952, 88, 459, 47017, 316, 32353, 3643, 124900, 4105, 350, 19962, 45039, 11, 3592, 398, 8, 533, 290, 45039, 166041, 398, 2595, 38566, 1669, 3952, 88, 459, 47017, 316, 32353, 3643, 124900, 4105, 350, 613, 6830, 3952, 3643, 1799, 21441, 8, 55111, 1669, 3952, 88, 459, 47017, 316, 21824, 32353, 3643, 124900, 4105, 350, 3086, 42348, 12, 11825, 31916, 17216, 296, 106